window.ZGCLAB_DETAILS["news-27"]=[{"id":"news-3280","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"联合远征军：从北欧到乌克兰","title_en":"The Joint Expeditionary Force: From Northern Europe to Ukraine","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）评论文章分析由英国主导、十个北欧国家组成的联合远征军（JEF）成立十周年峰会。峰会聚焦对乌克兰的支持，泽连斯基表示乌方愿加入JEF，但短期内成员资格前景渺茫。JEF国家承诺2025年提供120亿欧元军事援助，并加强对俄“影子舰队”的打击。文章指出JEF在海底基础设施防护、欧洲防务分担及跨大西洋关系方面可发挥更大作用。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary analyzes the 10th anniversary summit of the UK-led Joint Expeditionary Force (JEF), a 10-nation northern European grouping. The summit focused on support for Ukraine, with Zelenskyy expressing readiness to join, though near-term membership prospects appear slim. JEF nations pledged EUR 12 billion in military assistance for 2025 and agreed to target Russia's shadow fleet. The author argues the JEF can do more on undersea infrastructure protection, European defense burden-sharing, and strengthening the transatlantic bond as Trump returns to office.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:10:18.232499+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/joint-expeditionary-force-northern-europe-ukraine","content":"The Joint Expeditionary Force: From Northern Europe to Ukraine\nPhoto: Jürgen Randma / Government Office of Estonia\nCommentary\nby\nSean Monaghan\nPublished December 19, 2024\nRemote Visualization\n“Ukraine is ready to engage more and join JEF as a member.” This statement by Ukrainian president Volodymyr Zelenskyy was made during his address on Tuesday to the 10-nation Joint Expeditionary Force, or JEF. The leaders of the northern European group met this week for their JEF Summit in Tallinn, Estonia. While the prospects of Ukraine joining the JEF soon appear slim, the group is increasingly focused on Ukraine. More broadly, the summit also signaled the JEF’s intent to do more to counter growing threats to the region’s critical underwater infrastructure and continue to take on more of the burden of defending Europe as president-elect Donald Trump prepares to reenter the White House.Ten Years Of The JEFThis week’s summit in Tallinn marked ten years of the JEF. Established in 2014, the JEF—which is led by the United Kingdom and includes Denmark, Estonia, Finland, Iceland, Latvia, Lithuania, the Netherlands, Norway and Sweden—emerged from NATO’s Framework Nations Concept. The concept encourages small groups of like-minded nations to work together to overcome barriers to defense cooperation. Each coalition is led by a “framework” nation.Over the last decade, the JEF has become the quiet workhorse of defense and security in northern Europe. The JEF was established as part of NATO’s adaptation to Russia’s invasion of Crimea in 2014. Since becoming fully operational in 2018, it has become an important layer in the European security landscape. In 2022, the JEF ramped up reassurance and deterrence missions in the region after Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine.At last year’s summit JEF leaders took action to combat emerging threats to the region’s critical undersea infrastructure. They activated, for the first time in the group’s history, a “JEF Response Option” (JRO) in which member nations deployed 29 vessels and 11 aircraft to patrol one million square miles of sea in Northern European waters. This was followed by the second JRO in June, “Nordic Warden,” which saw 28 ships and 6 aircraft deployed to track 33 vessels of interest.From Northern Europe To UkraineKyiv might not be a full member of the JEF, but the focus of this summit was squarely on Ukraine. As the summit declaration stated: “Russia’s illegal and brutal war of aggression against Ukraine [remains] our foremost concern.”The JEF nations have long been at the vanguard of support for Ukraine. According to the Kiel Institute’s latest data, the top seven donor nations by percent of GDP given to Ukraine are JEF nations. As a recent report by RUSI points out: “Support for Ukraine has become a major political output of the JEF. The 10 JEF members have committed $11.1 billion more aid to Ukraine than the 18 remaining European NATO members.” After February 2022, the JEF ramped up reassurance and deterrence missions in the region and provided a political forum for northern European leaders to coordinate their support for Ukraine. President Zelenskyy has joined several meetings of JEF leaders, while Ukraine was invited to observe JEF exercises.The summit declaration thus highlighted the JEF nations’ “unwavering and longstanding support for a sovereign Ukraine and its people for as long as it takes.” It also cited the EUR 12 billion in military assistance already allocated by JEF nations for 2025 and pledged “to further expand our training and equipment offer to Ukraine.” It committed the JEF to deeper cooperation with Ukraine’s defense industry, a strategy that would also boost overall European defense capacity.At the summit, JEF leaders also reaffirmed support for Zelenskyy’s Victory Plan and “reiterated that a comprehensive, just, and lasting peace requires full and unconditional withdrawal of all Russian forces and military assets from the entire territory of Ukraine within its internationally recognised borders.” To help achieve this goal the summit declaration described how the JEF is “committed to strengthening Ukraine’s hand by further degrading Putin’s war machine and reducing his sources of revenue.” One example was the agreement before the summit between the JEF nations, plus Germany and Poland, to target Russia’s “shadow fleet” of ships used to illegally transport oil, arms, and grain in violation of international sanctions.Another avenue of support highlighted by the declaration was Ukraine’s recent participation in the JEF’s flagship exercise, Joint Protector. During October and November, Ukrainian officials joined over 300 JEF personnel in a forward deployed headquarters in Liepāja, Latvia. The exercise tested the JEF’s ability to respond quickly to a crisis. It involved a “near-real scenario” in which JEF forces deliver several concurrent missions. Crucially, the exercise also allowed the JEF to learn from Ukraine. As Luke Pollard, UK minister for the Armed Forces, explained, “The participation of our Ukrainian partners highlights the exercise’s importance, ensuring that we can learn from Ukraine’s hard-fought combat experience.”Although Ukraine has moved much closer to the JEF over the past year, the prospects of President Zelenskyy’s aspiration to “join JEF as a member” remain slim. Membership appears limited to its “core regions” of the High North, North Atlantic, and Baltic Sea. Some have argued this is “short sighted” and that if “JEF truly prioritizes regional security and innovation, it cannot afford to ignore Ukraine’s contribution.” Even so, given their location, more likely candidates for JEF membership would be Germany and Poland. But Germany’s constitution forbids its armed forces from operating with coalitions of the willing. With the region’s largest army, Poland may be too big and land-centric for the air- and maritime-focused JEF. Officials from smaller nations also worry about being sidelined, “As soon as you have a Pole in the room, the discussion is all about Poland.”The Next Ten YearsAlthough the JEF will remain focused on supporting and learning from Ukraine for the foreseeable future, it is also focused on the enduring challenge Russia poses to European security over the next decade, beyond the war and beyond Putin. As the declaration stated, “We committed to a long-term policy to constrain, contest, and counter Russia’s aggression and threats to regional security, extending beyond its current regime and its war against Ukraine.” This broad, long-term commitment to European security matches the JEF’s stated ambition to deliver “sub-threshold peacetime responses through to full-spectrum interventions during times of crisis or conflict, both in its core regions and beyond.”As Ed Arnold of RUSI points out, such a high level of ambition will require more resources. The JEF has been focused on securing undersea infrastructure, but even with a significant boost in activity and force presence over the last 12 months, it was unable to prevent the most recent cable damage incident. In part, this is because such hybrid threats are difficult to deter, but it also reflects the scale of the challenge.As NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte described in his first speech last week, the scale of NATO’s defense and deterrence challenge is much larger still. According to Rutte, a revanchist Kremlin with a wartime economy means that “We are not ready for what is coming our way in four to five years. . . . What is happening in Ukraine could happen here too.” This requires a much greater ambition for defense spending and industrial capacity. As Rutte put it, “It is time to shift to a wartime mindset. And turbo-charge our defence production and defence spending.”In practice, this means transforming NATO with a focus on European capabilities and investing in a credible warfighting force. The JEF is well-placed to contribute to both objectives. As well as being the leading supporters of Ukraine, the JEF nations are also some of the top defense spenders in NATO. In the summit declaration, JEF nations committed to “spend well beyond 2 [percent] of GDP”—even if the summit hosts would have preferred a higher target. They also made a “European commitment to invest robustly into defence” and to “[galvanize] European defence industry”—both of which would boost both Ukraine and NATO’s own defense and deterrence.The Transatlantic BondThe final paragraph of the summit declaration emphasized the “transatlantic bond” between Europe and the United States and looked forward “to working closely with the incoming U.S. administration on our shared security and defence interests.” The JEF is well placed to strengthen transatlantic relations, too. President-elect Trump has reiterated the United States’ commitment to NATO—even if he did so in his own style, by suggesting this remains contingent on European allies “paying their bills.” The JEF gives its members a platform to do both; it demonstrates how northern European nations are taking charge of security in their region in a forum that includes ten NATO allies and is closely linked to NATO institutions by design. As Dan Baer and Sophia Besch of Carnegie put it, “Ramping up the Joint Expeditionary Force would contribute to European security and prepare the continent for a second Trump administration.”By the time JEF leaders meet in Oslo, Norway, for their next summit in May, President Trump will have been in office for 100 days. That is a long time in international politics but a blink of an eye for the 10-year-old JEF. With a turbulent period ahead for European security and a burgeoning relationship with Ukraine, the next decade will be a busy one for the JEF.Sean Monaghan is a visiting fellow in the Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nEurope,\nUkraine,\nDefense and Security, and\nUkraine War\nImage\nSean Monaghan\nFormer Visiting Fellow, Europe, Russia and Eurasia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nEurope, Russia, and Eurasia Program\nEuropean Defense and Security\nTransforming European Defense\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"联合远征军：从北欧到乌克兰\n照片：Jürgen Randma / 爱沙尼亚政府办公室\n评论\n作者\nSean Monaghan\n发表于2024年12月19日\n远程可视化\n“乌克兰已准备好参与更多事务，并以成员身份加入联合远征军（JEF）。”乌克兰总统弗拉基米尔·泽连斯基周二在向由10个国家组成的联合远征军发表讲话时作出了这一声明。本周，这个北欧集团的领导人在爱沙尼亚塔林举行了联合远征军峰会。尽管乌克兰近期加入联合远征军的前景似乎渺茫，但该组织正日益聚焦于乌克兰。更广泛地说，此次峰会还表明，面对该地区关键水下基础设施所遭受的日益增长的威胁，以及当选总统唐纳德·特朗普准备重返白宫，联合远征军有意采取更多行动应对这些威胁，并继续承担更多保卫欧洲的责任。\n联合远征军十周年\n本周在塔林举行的峰会标志着联合远征军成立十周年。联合远征军成立于2014年，由英国领导，成员包括丹麦、爱沙尼亚、芬兰、冰岛、拉脱维亚、立陶宛、荷兰、挪威和瑞典，其起源是北约的“框架国家概念”。该概念鼓励志趣相投的小型国家群体协作，克服防务合作障碍。每个联盟由一个“框架”国家领导。\n过去十年间，联合远征军已成为北欧防务与安全领域默默发挥作用的主力。联合远征军的成立，是北约应对俄罗斯2014年入侵克里米亚所作调整的一部分。自2018年全面投入运作以来，它已成为欧洲安全格局中的重要一环。2022年俄罗斯全面入侵乌克兰后，联合远征军加强了该地区的安抚与威慑任务。\n在去年的峰会上，联合远征军领导人采取行动，应对该地区关键海底基础设施面临的新兴威胁。他们首次启动该组织历史上的“联合远征军响应选项”（JRO），成员国部署29艘舰船和11架飞机，在北欧水域一百万平方英里的海域展开巡逻。随后，6月启动了第二次JRO“北欧哨兵”，部署28艘舰船和6架飞机，跟踪33艘重点关注船只。\n从北欧到乌克兰\n基辅或许还不是联合远征军的正式成员，但本次峰会的重点明确放在乌克兰。正如峰会声明所说：“俄罗斯对乌克兰发动的非法且残酷的侵略战争[仍然]是我们最为关切的问题。”\n联合远征军国家长期以来一直处于支持乌克兰的前沿。根据基尔研究所的最新数据，按对乌克兰援助占国内生产总值的比例计算，排名前七的捐助国均为联合远征军成员。正如皇家联合军种防务研究所（RUSI）最近的一份报告指出：“支持乌克兰已成为联合远征军的一项主要政治成果。10个联合远征军成员向乌克兰承诺的援助，比其余18个欧洲北约成员多111亿美元。”2022年2月之后，联合远征军加强了该地区的安抚与威慑任务，并为北欧领导人协调对乌克兰的支持提供了政治平台。泽连斯基总统参加了联合远征军领导人的数次会议，而乌克兰也获邀观摩联合远征军演习。\n因此，峰会声明强调，联合远征军国家“将坚定不移、长期支持主权乌克兰及其人民，无论需要多久”。声明还提到，联合远征军国家已为2025年拨付120亿欧元的军事援助\n并承诺“进一步扩大我们向乌克兰提供的培训和装备”。它承诺让JEF与乌克兰国防工业开展更深入合作，这一战略也将提升欧洲整体的防御能力。在峰会上，JEF领导人还再次确认支持泽连斯基的胜利计划，并“重申，全面、公正和持久的和平要求俄罗斯从乌克兰国际公认边界内的全部领土上全面、无条件撤出所有俄军及军事资产”。为帮助实现这一目标，峰会声明说明，JEF“致力于进一步削弱普京的战争机器并减少其收入来源，从而增强乌克兰的谈判筹码”。其中一个例子是，峰会前JEF成员国以及德国和波兰达成协议，针对俄罗斯的“影子船队”采取行动；这些船只被用于非法运输石油、武器和粮食，违反国际制裁。声明强调的另一项支持途径，是乌克兰近期参加JEF旗舰演习“联合保卫者”。10月和11月期间，乌克兰官员与300多名JEF人员一起，在拉脱维亚利耶帕亚的一个前沿部署司令部中参加演习。此次演习测试了JEF迅速应对危机的能力。演习包含一个“接近真实的场景”，JEF部队在其中执行数项同时展开的任务。至关重要的是，演习还让JEF得以向乌克兰学习。正如英国武装部队国务大臣卢克·波拉德所解释的那样：“乌克兰伙伴的参与凸显了此次演习的重要性，确保我们能够汲取乌克兰历经艰苦战斗所积累的经验。”尽管过去一年乌克兰已大幅拉近与JEF的关系，但泽连斯基总统“以成员身份加入JEF”的愿望前景仍然渺茫。成员资格似乎仅限于其“核心区域”——北极高纬地区、北大西洋和波罗的海。一些人认为这“目光短浅”，并认为如果“JEF真正优先考虑区域安全与创新，就不能忽视乌克兰的贡献”。即便如此，鉴于地理位置，更有可能成为JEF成员的是德国和波兰。但德国宪法禁止其武装部队与意愿联盟开展行动。波兰拥有该地区最大的军队，但对以空中和海上力量为重点的JEF而言，它可能过于庞大且过于陆向。较小国家的官员也担心自己被边缘化：“房间里一旦有了波兰人，讨论就全是波兰。”\n未来十年\n尽管在可预见的未来，JEF仍将专注于支持乌克兰并向其学习，但它也在关注俄罗斯在未来十年、超越战争且超越普京本人对欧洲安全构成的持久挑战。正如声明所述：“我们承诺采取长期政策，遏制、抗衡并反制俄罗斯的侵略和对地区安全的威胁，这一政策将超越其当前政权及其对乌克兰的战争。”这一对欧洲安全的广泛长期承诺，与JEF所宣称的目标相一致，即“在其核心区域及其他地区，从低于门槛的和平时期响应，到危机或冲突时期的全谱系干预”。正如皇家联合军种研究所（RUSI）的埃德·阿诺德指出的那样，如此高的雄心将需要更多资源。JEF一直专注于保障海底基础设施，但即使活动和兵力部署大幅增加，\n在过去12个月中，它未能阻止最近发生的电缆损坏事件。部分原因在于，这类混合威胁难以遏制，但这也反映出挑战的规模之大。正如北约秘书长马克·吕特上周在其首次演讲中所描述的那样，北约面临的防御与威慑挑战规模要大得多。据吕特所说，一个拥有战时经济的复仇主义克里姆林宫意味着：“我们还没有准备好应对四到五年后将要发生的事情……乌克兰正在发生的事情也可能发生在这里。”这要求在国防开支和工业能力方面有更大的雄心。正如吕特所说：“是时候转变为战时思维了，并为我们的国防生产和国防开支按下涡轮增压键。”\n\n在实践中，这意味着改造北约，重点发展欧洲能力，并投资建设一支可信的作战力量。联合远征军（JEF）完全有条件为这两个目标作出贡献。JEF国家不仅是支持乌克兰的主要力量，也是北约国防开支最高的国家之一。在峰会宣言中，JEF国家承诺“将支出提高到远超国内生产总值（GDP）2%”——即使峰会东道国原本希望设定更高的目标。它们还作出了“欧洲方面大力投资国防”的承诺，并表示要“推动欧洲国防工业发展”——这两项举措都将增强乌克兰以及北约自身的防御与威慑能力。\n\n跨大西洋纽带\n\n峰会宣言的最后一段强调了欧洲与美国之间的“跨大西洋纽带”，并期待“与即将上任的美国政府就我们共同的安全与防务利益开展密切合作”。JEF同样完全有条件加强跨大西洋关系。候任总统特朗普重申了美国对北约的承诺——尽管他以自己的方式这样做，暗示这仍取决于欧洲盟友“支付账单”。JEF为其成员提供了兼顾这两点的平台；它展示了北欧国家如何在一个包括10个北约盟国、并在制度设计上与北约机构紧密相连的论坛中，承担起本地区安全的责任。正如卡内基的丹·贝尔和索菲娅·贝施所说：“扩大联合远征军将有助于欧洲安全，并为欧洲大陆迎接第二届特朗普政府做好准备。”\n\n到明年5月，JEF领导人在挪威奥斯陆举行下一次峰会时，特朗普总统上任已有100天。对于国际政治而言，这是一段很长的时间，但对成立已有10年的JEF来说却只是转瞬之间。随着欧洲安全即将进入动荡时期，以及与乌克兰关系不断发展，未来十年对JEF而言将十分忙碌。\n\n肖恩·莫纳汉是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）欧洲、俄罗斯与欧亚项目的访问学者。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2024 战略与国际研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n欧洲，\n乌克兰，\n国防与安全，以及\n乌克兰战争\n\n图片\n肖恩·莫纳汉\n欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚事务前访问学者\n项目\n项目与计划\n欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚计划\n欧洲防务与安全\n转型中的欧洲防务\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-19T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-59","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"金正恩的“第二次并进”：六方会谈协议21年之后","title_en":"Kim’s Second Byungjin: 21 Years After the Six-Party Talks Agreement","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）发表评论，纪念2005年六方会谈《九一九共同声明》21周年，回顾其分阶段无核化框架及2008年后进程中断的原因，认为该声明仍具参考价值。文章提出金正恩正推行“第二次并进”，即推进核武同时寻求与容忍其核地位的国家关系正常化，并评估特朗普与金正恩再次会晤的可能与限度。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary marks 21 years since the 2005 Six-Party Talks Joint Statement, reviewing its step-by-step denuclearization framework and why momentum collapsed after 2008. It argues Kim Jong-un is pursuing a 'second byungjin'—advancing nuclear forces while normalizing ties with states that tolerate them—and assesses the prospects and limits of another Trump-Kim summit.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:48:22.880747+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/kims-second-byungjin-21-years-after-six-party-talks-agreement","content":"Kim’s Second Byungjin: 21 Years After the Six-Party Talks Agreement\nPhoto: KCNA VIA KNS/AFP/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nSydney Seiler\nPublished September 23, 2026\nIt has been 21 years since the six parties with the deepest interest in the peace and security of the Korean Peninsula concluded the September 19 Joint Statement of the Six-Party Talks in 2005. In exchange for North Korea’s commitment to denuclearization, the other five parties agreed to provide security assurances, diplomatic recognition, and energy and economic assistance in an incremental, action-for-action manner. The agreement was less than perfect, glaringly so in its silence on North Korea’s uranium enrichment program, but disablement actions such as the destruction of the iconic cooling tower at Yongbyon’s graphite-moderated reactor gave hope that a viable path to denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula existed. The agreement and the subsequent documents outlining first- and second-phase actions also allowed Pyongyang to probe what benefits it would receive for moving down that path.Momentum was lost, unfortunately, in 2008, as it became increasingly evident that there could be no authentic and credible talks without North Korea’s uranium enrichment program, which Pyongyang omitted from the declaration it submitted to the Six-Party Talks in June 2008. Its determination not to address the highly enriched uranium (HEU) program showed in its refusal to grant the verification measures necessary to understand and eventually dismantle it. Privately, on the sidelines of the talks, North Korean diplomats told U.S. officials that denuclearization would no longer be a legitimate diplomatic topic for negotiation. Efforts by the Obama administration to nudge Pyongyang back to the Six-Party Talks while advancing U.S.-North Korea relations were rebuffed, and from 2012 onward North Korea has consistently refused meaningful dialogue on its nuclear program.Nevertheless, the September 19 Joint Statement retains enduring value for three main reasons:It provides a fundamental framework for an incremental, step-by-step approach to denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula.It provides a framework for compensating North Korea’s denuclearization steps with security assurances, economic and energy aid and assistance, and normalization of relations among all the member statesIt demonstrates, as did the Leap Day Understanding of 2012 and the progress made and then lost in U.S.-North Korea summitry in 2018–2019, that North Korea’s entire uranium enrichment program must be on the table. There can be no authentic and credible discussion of denuclearization, arms control, or even peace so long as North Korea robustly and unabatedly expands its inventory of weapons-usable nuclear material through undeclared uranium enrichment.Implications for TodayIn the broadest sense, North Korea’s walking away from the Six-Party Talks and from its commitments under the Joint Statement in late 2008 was a clear proclamation of its lack of interest in denuclearization and, by extension, in any conciliatory, non-confrontational relationship with the United States. The “action statements” of February and October 2007 laid out agreed denuclearization steps by North Korea, the economic and energy assistance North Korea would receive, and expedited paths toward normalization of U.S.-North Korea and Japan-North Korea relations. They demonstrated both the practicality and the proportionality of denuclearization-driven diplomacy. These reciprocal, step-by-step actions were built to address real North Korean needs, build confidence, and show Pyongyang an alternative path to peace and prosperity through denuclearization. Normalized relations, reduced tensions, economic aid and assistance, and even security guarantees were not enough to keep Pyongyang interested in the process. That is most likely to remain the case going forward, whether the goal is complete denuclearization, a mere halt to the program, or something in between.That is in part why arguing for a “peace first” approach is met with skepticism today. A peace-first approach might reduce tensions and create an environment in which incremental reductions in the North Korean nuclear threat become possible, but it has been tried repeatedly. Implementation of the September 19 Joint Statement rested on a step-by-step approach that front-loaded numerous benefits for North Korea. In walking away from the Six-Party Talks beginning in 2008, Pyongyang rejected that approach. It has since passed up opportunities to make serious progress toward normalization with the United States under three different presidents (Obama, Trump, and Biden), each of whom front loaded benefits North Korea would enjoy while recognizing that the heavy lifting of complete and verifiable denuclearization would take time. Pyongyang has simply been uninterested in exploring what a better relationship with the United States, or with South Korea for that matter, might yield in security, economic, and diplomatic benefits.Kim’s Second ByungjinWhat is Kim Jong-un seeking at this time? Since late 2017, when the November launch of the Hwasong-15 intercontinental ballistic missile was messaged as having “completed the state’s nuclear force,” North Korean rhetoric and behavior have pointed toward a diplomacy that locks in international acceptance of North Korea as a “responsible nuclear power” and moves beyond sanctions and pressure. Just as Pyongyang confidently proclaimed in 2013 that it would pursue “byungjin” (simultaneous development of both nuclear and economic development), it could now be said to be pursuing a “second byungjin” that advances its nuclear program while also seeking to normalize relations with countries willing to tolerate (if not necessarily accept) North Korea as a nuclear power. Pyongyang believes it can have both, which complicates any peace-first approach that purports to slow the growth of the North Korean threat.North Korea remains committed to securing nuclear and conventional forces that clearly establish its dominance over South Korea. That shows both in its openly aggressive rhetoric aimed at Seoul over the past few years and in its development of capabilities messaged as a first-strike option against South Korea with tactical nuclear weapons. In the context of the repeated authoritative North Korean statements warning of continued qualitative and quantitative growth of its nuclear force as well as Pyongyang’s actions, there is little room for optimism that Kim would consider ways to reduce or limit the size of his arsenal, create mechanisms for crisis management or trust building, or seek rapprochement with South Korea.Can Kim and Trump Meet Halfway?Kim Jong-un most likely believes there is value in diplomacy with the United States that falls short of a formal proclamation by Washington that denuclearization is no longer a goal. When the time is right, Pyongyang can easily drop or suspend its requirement that the United States cease insisting on denuclearization. Washington has comparable flexibility: The State Department can continue to assert that denuclearization is the goal, the president can avoid language that would antagonize Kim, and the two leaders can meet in the spirit of advancing peace in a dangerous region. Pyongyang has at times stated that it needs no one’s “recognition” of an established reality. The benefits of securing a meeting with President Trump could be seen as more valuable than dogmatic adherence to its goal of locking in some nuclear power title.The president could take an approach that troubles both his supporters and his detractors, perhaps a sign he is on the right track. Advocates of a “realistic” acknowledgment of North Korea as a “nuclear power” and of abandoning the complete, verifiable, and irreversible denuclearization (CVID) formulation greatly overestimate the value to Pyongyang of such a statement, particularly measured against the harm it would do to the United States’ North Korea policy and to U.S. cooperation with allies such as South Korea. Equally wrong is the worry that all hope for denuclearization vanishes with any meeting between the two leaders in which denuclearization takes a back seat to relationship building and broad-brush discussion of the need for peace and stability on the Korean Peninsula.Given the cards he has been dealt, dealt not by his predecessors but by Pyongyang, there is value in President Trump’s current approach to North Korea, both in his pursuit of summit-level dialogue with Kim Jong-un and in the cautious pace he has set in the face of Pyongyang’s absolute rejection of U.S. overtures. There seems to be more intentionality and pragmatism in play than critics of the president’s outreach acknowledge. That said, defenders of his approach outside the administration have not made a compelling case that the potential gain from a Trump-Kim summit would be worth the risk. The temptation to point fingers at the efforts of previous administrations for failing to resolve the North Korea nuclear issue sooner must be resisted. Lessons from past summits, including from Singapore and Hanoi, should be discussed in a more objective and non-political manner. A more sophisticated discussion may be worth considering related to reinvigorating pressure on North Korea from China and Russia, since both have greater interests with the United States that might incentivize a cooperative approach, in spite of setbacks in such efforts over the past decade.Pyongyang Said It Plainly in 2009As the Six-Party Talks collapsed in the final months of 2008, the North Korean Foreign Ministry stated on January 17, 2009, days before President Obama’s inauguration:\"This is a revelation of the true colors of the U.S. as a big power which regards the normalization of the relations as a gift to the DPRK and a distortion of the essence of the nuclear issue on the Korean Peninsula. The U.S. is miscalculating if it considers the normalization of the DPRK-U.S. relations as a reward for the DPRK's nuclear abandonment. It is the reality on the Korean Peninsula that we can live without normalizing the relations with the U.S. but not without nuclear deterrent. We have lived for decades without normalizing the relations with the U.S. and live on still with dignity.\"Two key points are worth considering. First, nothing has changed in regard to North Korea’s posture toward the United States. Second, from the U.S. perspective, the inextricable ties between North Korea’s nuclear capability and instability on the Korean Peninsula make it impossible to find a path to U.S.-North Korea détente or inter-Korean reconciliation independent of the nuclear issue. Rhetoric emphasizing CVID is not an affront to Pyongyang or a maximalist and unnecessary obstacle complicating efforts to engage, reduce tension and threat, and achieve peace. The logic behind the United States’ insistence that authentic and credible progress on denuclearization be achieved for peace to be promoted is unchanged. The problem is that the two sides’ fundamental goals are antithetical and irreconcilable: Pyongyang intends to build a nuclear arsenal capable of decisively dominating and subjugating South Korea, and the United States pursues denuclearization, peace, and prosperity for the whole Korean Peninsula.The Good News: Deterrence Is WorkingRecent rhetoric from Pyongyang, Moscow, and Beijing shares a common theme: growing concern about and anger toward the U.S. military presence and U.S. actions in the region, closer U.S.-South Korea coordination on nuclear issues within the Nuclear Consultative Group, the remilitarization of Japan, and trilateral U.S.-Japan-South Korea cooperation. That is encouraging. Concern in these three capitals contradicts premature proclamations of the United States’ demise in East Asia. It reflects frustration and fear that efforts by China, Russia, and North Korea to dominate their respective spheres are not working, particularly North Korea’s costly efforts to intimidate and subjugate South Korea. The strengthening of U.S.-Japan-South Korea cooperation, driven in large part by the surge in North Korea’s nuclear and missile threat over the past few years, has negative implications for China’s security and intentions vis-à-vis Taiwan. Russia has long been concerned about how North Korea’s actions sustain a Northeast Asian demand signal for U.S. military presence.All that said, it is difficult to deny that, through no fault of their own, neither President Obama nor President Biden was an attractive dialogue partner for Kim. President Trump is uniquely positioned to engage Kim with sufficient credibility, even if such talks yield limited fruit. His cards are not the greatest, but he seems to know it. Whatever the outcome, the United States would do well to remind both China and Russia that North Korean behavior damages their national interests and will continue to do so until the three parties can cooperate once again, as during the Six-Party Talks, to lead North Korea down a different path than the one it has been on.Sydney Seiler is a non-resident senior adviser with the Korea Chair at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. The views expressed in this submission are the author’s and do not imply endorsement by the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, the U.S. Intelligence Community, or any other U.S. government agency.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nKorea,\nAsia, and\nDefense and Security\nImage\nSydney Seiler\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Korea Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nKorea Chair\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"金正恩的第二次并进路线：六方会谈协议签署21年后\n照片：KCNA VIA KNS/AFP/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nSydney Seiler\n发表于2026年9月23日\n距对朝鲜半岛和平与安全最有切身利益的六方于2005年达成《六方会谈9月19日共同声明》，已经过去21年。作为朝鲜承诺实现无核化的交换，其他五方同意以渐进、逐步对等的方式，提供安全保证、外交承认以及能源和经济援助。该协议并不完美，尤其是对朝鲜铀浓缩计划只字未提，这一点十分明显；但采取的弃核措施——例如摧毁宁边石墨减速反应堆标志性的冷却塔——让人看到，朝鲜半岛无核化存在一条可行路径。该协议以及随后概述第一阶段和第二阶段行动的文件，也让平壤得以探究，沿着这条道路前进将获得哪些好处。\n\n遗憾的是，2008年势头丧失了，因为越来越明显的是，如果没有涉及朝鲜铀浓缩计划，就不可能进行真正且可信的会谈；平壤在2008年6月向六方会谈提交的声明中遗漏了这一计划。朝鲜拒绝应对高浓缩铀（HEU）计划，表现在它拒绝批准为了解并最终拆除该计划所必需的核查措施上。在会谈期间的私下场合，在会场外，朝鲜外交官告诉美国官员，无核化将不再是一个可以进行谈判的正当外交议题。奥巴马政府一边推进美朝关系、一边推动平壤重返六方会谈的努力遭到拒绝；从2012年起，朝鲜一直拒绝就其核计划进行有意义的对话。\n\n尽管如此，9月19日共同声明仍因三个主要原因而具有持久价值：\n它为朝鲜半岛无核化的渐进、分步骤方式提供了基本框架。\n它提供了一个框架，以安全保证、经济和能源援助与支持，以及所有成员国之间关系正常化，来补偿朝鲜采取的无核化步骤。\n正如2012年的“闰日谅解”以及2018—2019年美朝峰会外交中取得后又失去的进展所表明的那样，它证明朝鲜整个铀浓缩计划必须被摆上谈判桌。只要朝鲜通过未申报的铀浓缩活动，持续而强力地扩大其可用于制造武器的核材料库存，就不可能真正、可信地讨论无核化、军备控制，甚至和平。\n\n对今天的影响\n从最广泛的意义上说，朝鲜在2008年末退出六方会谈并放弃其在共同声明下所作的承诺，清楚表明它对无核化缺乏兴趣，也由此表明，它对与美国建立任何和解性、非对抗性的关系同样缺乏兴趣。2007年2月和10月的“行动声明”列出了朝鲜同意采取的无核化步骤、朝鲜将获得的经济和能源援助，以及推动美朝和日朝关系正常化的加速路径。它们既展示了由无核化驱动的\n外交。这些互惠、循序渐进的行动旨在应对朝鲜的实际需求，建立信心，并向平壤展示一条通过无核化走向和平与繁荣的替代道路。关系正常化、缓和紧张局势、经济援助与支持，甚至安全保障，都不足以让平壤继续对这一进程保持兴趣。今后最可能仍将如此，无论目标是实现完全无核化、仅仅暂停核计划，还是介于两者之间的某种目标。部分正因为如此，如今主张“和平优先”方针会遭到怀疑。和平优先方针或许能够缓和紧张局势，并营造一种环境，使逐步降低朝鲜核威胁成为可能，但这种方针已经被反复尝试。落实《9月19日共同声明》所依据的就是循序渐进的方针，预先为朝鲜安排了诸多好处。平壤从2008年开始退出六方会谈时，拒绝了这一方针。此后，在三位不同总统（奥巴马、特朗普和拜登）任内，朝鲜都放弃了与美国实现关系正常化并取得实质进展的机会；这三位总统都把朝鲜可以享有的好处置于前面，同时承认完成全面且可核查的无核化需要时间。平壤根本没有兴趣去探索，与美国或韩国建立更好关系究竟能带来哪些安全、经济和外交利益。金正恩的“第二次并进”金正恩此时正在寻求什么？自2017年底以来，尤其是11月发射“火星-15”洲际弹道导弹并宣称“国家核力量已经完成”之后，朝鲜的言论和行为一直指向这样一种外交：锁定国际社会对朝鲜作为“负责任核大国”的接受，并摆脱制裁和压力。正如平壤在2013年自信地宣称将追求“并进”（核发展与经济发展同步推进）一样，如今也可以说，它正在追求“第二次并进”：推进核计划，同时寻求与那些愿意容忍（即便未必接受）朝鲜作为核大国存在的国家实现关系正常化。平壤相信二者可以兼得，这使任何声称要放缓朝鲜威胁增长的和平优先方针都更加复杂。朝鲜仍致力于确保拥有能够明确确立其对韩国优势的核力量和常规力量。这既体现在过去几年针对首尔的公开挑衅性言论中，也体现在其发展被宣传为可使用战术核武器对韩国实施先发制人打击的能力上。鉴于朝鲜一再发表权威声明，警告其核力量将继续在质量和数量上增长，再加上平壤采取的行动，几乎没有理由乐观地认为金正恩会考虑缩减或限制其核武库规模、建立危机管理或互信机制，或寻求与韩国和解。金正恩和特朗普能否各让一步？金正恩很可能认为，与美国开展外交具有一定价值，即使这种外交未达到华盛顿正式宣布无核化不再是目标的程度。时机成熟时，平壤可以轻易放弃或暂停其要求\n即美国停止坚持无核化。华盛顿也有相应的灵活性：国务院可以继续声称无核化是目标，总统可以避免使用会激怒金正恩的措辞，而两位领导人可以本着推动这个危险地区和平的精神会面。平壤有时曾表示，对于既成事实，它不需要任何人的“承认”。与其教条式地坚持锁定某种核大国地位这一目标，确保与特朗普总统会面的好处可能被视为更有价值。总统可以采取一种令其支持者和批评者都感到不安的做法，这或许正表明他走在正确的道路上。那些主张“现实地”承认朝鲜是“核大国”、放弃“完整、可核查且不可逆的无核化”（CVID）表述的人，大大高估了这种声明对平壤的价值，尤其是与其将对美国的朝鲜政策以及美国与韩国等盟友的合作造成的损害相比。同样错误的是担心：只要两位领导人的任何会面中，无核化让位于关系建设，以及对朝鲜半岛和平与稳定必要性的概括性讨论，所有无核化的希望就会消失。\n\n鉴于他所拿到的牌——不是他的前任们发的，而是平壤发的——特朗普总统目前对朝鲜的做法具有一定价值，既体现在他寻求与金正恩进行峰会级别的对话上，也体现在面对平壤对美国接触的绝对拒绝时，他所设定的谨慎步调上。与总统接触的批评者所承认的相比，其中似乎有更多的意图性和务实性。话虽如此，政府外部为其做法辩护的人，并未令人信服地证明，特朗普与金正恩峰会可能带来的收益值得承担相应风险。必须抵制把未能更早解决朝鲜核问题归咎于历届政府努力的诱惑。应当以更加客观、非政治化的方式讨论历次峰会的经验教训，包括新加坡和河内峰会。或许值得考虑进行更深入的讨论，探讨如何重新推动中国和俄罗斯向朝鲜施压，因为两国与美国都有更大的利益关系，这可能会激励它们采取合作方式，尽管过去十年这类努力屡遭挫折。\n\n平壤在2009年已经直截了当地说明了这一点\n\n随着六方会谈在2008年最后几个月崩溃，朝鲜外交部于2009年1月17日、奥巴马总统就职前几天发表声明：“\n\n这是暴露美国作为一个大国的真实面目：它把关系正常化视为送给朝鲜的礼物，并歪曲朝鲜半岛核问题的本质。如果美国认为朝美关系正常化是朝鲜放弃核武器的奖励，那它就算错了。朝鲜半岛的现实是，我们可以在没有同美国关系正常化的情况下生活，却不能没有核威慑。几十年来，我们没有同美国实现关系正常化，但仍然有尊严地生活着。”\n\n有两点值得考虑。第一，就朝鲜对美国的立场而言，什么都没有改变。第二，从美国的\n从这一角度看，朝鲜的核能力与朝鲜半岛不稳定之间存在着不可分割的联系，这使得不可能找到一条独立于核问题的美朝缓和或南北和解之路。强调“完全、可核查、不可逆地弃核”（CVID）的言辞，并非对平壤的冒犯，也不是使接触、降低紧张与威胁以及实现和平的努力复杂化的极端且不必要的障碍。美国坚持必须在无核化方面取得真实且可信的进展，才能推动和平，其背后的逻辑并未改变。问题在于，双方的根本目标相互对立且无法调和：平壤意图建立一支能够决定性地主宰并征服韩国的核武库，而美国则追求整个朝鲜半岛的无核化、和平与繁荣。\n好消息：威慑正在发挥作用\n平壤、莫斯科和北京近期的言论有一个共同主题：对美国在该地区的军事存在和行动、美国与韩国在核问题磋商小组框架内加强核事务协调、日本重新军事化以及美日韩三边合作日益感到担忧和愤怒。这令人鼓舞。这三个首都的担忧与过早宣称美国在东亚衰落的说法相矛盾。它反映出一种挫败感和恐惧：中俄朝试图主导各自势力范围的努力并未奏效，尤其是朝鲜付出高昂代价恐吓并征服韩国的努力。美日韩合作的加强，很大程度上是由过去几年朝鲜核与导弹威胁的激增所推动的，这对中国的安全以及其在台湾问题上的意图具有负面影响。俄罗斯长期以来一直担心，朝鲜的行动维持了东北亚对美国军事存在的需求信号。\n尽管如此，很难否认，奥巴马总统和拜登总统并非出于自身过错，都不是对金正恩具有吸引力的对话伙伴。特朗普总统处于独特位置，可以以足够的可信度与金正恩接触，即使此类会谈收效有限。他手中的牌并不算最好，但他似乎知道这一点。无论结果如何，美国都应提醒中俄两国，朝鲜的行为损害其国家利益，而且这种情况将持续下去，直到三方能够像六方会谈期间那样再次合作，引导朝鲜走上一条不同于其一直走的道路。\nSydney Seiler是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）韩国事务主席的非驻会高级顾问。本文中表达的观点属于作者本人，并不意味着得到国家情报总监办公室、美国情报界或任何其他美国政府机构的认可。\n战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）评论由该中心制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n© 2026年战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n朝鲜半岛，\n亚洲，\n以及\n国防与安全\n图片\nSydney Seiler\n韩国事务主席项目高级顾问（非驻会）\n项目与计划\n韩国事务主席项目\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-09-23T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3499","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"借莫桑比克大选之机调整德尔加杜角冲突策略","title_en":"Leveraging the Mozambican Election to Change Strategy on the Cabo Delgado Conflict","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心非洲项目发表评论文章，分析2024年莫桑比克大选与德尔加杜角省伊斯兰国关联武装冲突的互动关系。文章回顾了该武装从地方叛乱演变为伊斯兰国分支、后又衰退与复苏的过程，梳理各总统候选人的应对主张，并指出选举舞弊指控、经济机会匮乏与治理薄弱可能影响冲突走向，建议利益相关方推动就业、人道援助与安全部门改革。","summary_en":"A CSIS Africa Program commentary examines how Mozambique's 2024 general election interacts with the Islamic State-linked insurgency in Cabo Delgado. It traces the group's evolution from a local insurgency into an ISIS affiliate and its subsequent decline and resurgence, reviews candidates' proposals, and argues that fraud allegations, weak economic opportunity, and poor governance could shape the conflict's trajectory, urging stakeholders to support jobs, humanitarian aid, and security-sector reform.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:25:38.314337+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/leveraging-mozambican-election-change-strategy-cabo-delgado-conflict","content":"Leveraging the Mozambican Election to Change Strategy on the Cabo Delgado Conflict\nPhoto: ALFREDO ZUNIGA/AFP via Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nEmilia Columbo\nPublished September 26, 2024\nThe 2024 general election in Mozambique on October 9 will take place in a Cabo Delgado province that has undergone significant changes since the 2019 general election. In the past five years, voters in this province have witnessed the evolution of a local insurgency, the Ahlu Sunna Waljama’a (ASWJ), into an Islamic State affiliate often referred to as Islamic State-Mozambique. Cabo Delgado’s voters have watched this group grow in size, capability, and geographic presence, but have also witnessed the ASWJ’s subsequent decline and resurgence. Throughout this conflict, Cabo Delgado’s residents have experienced severe economic disruption, infrastructure destruction, and the displacement of over a million people, but since 2021, they have also seen nearly half of those displaced return to their areas of origin, accompanied by some efforts to rebuild areas that have been under insurgent control.Over the past five years, this conflict has evolved significantly, including the dramatic 2021 attack on the town of Palma, which resulted in a halt in the continent’s largest liquified natural gas exploration project and the introduction of foreign troops to the province. This has made the conflict a key talking point in the leading candidates’ platforms, such as FRELIMO candidate Daniel Chapo, independent candidate Venancio Mondlane, and Mozambique Democratic Movement (MDM) candidate Lutero Simango, each pledging to see the conflict end during their presidency.However, changes in elite calculus toward the costs of the conflict, along with the allocation of political and financial resources needed for long-term stabilization, are likely to remain obstacles to even the most well-intentioned plans. As the new president prioritizes the demands of his elite and popular base during this transitional period, stakeholders may have an opportunity to weigh in on how the new administration can approach these challenges and develop a comprehensive way forward to put Mozambique on the road to resolving this conflict.How the Insurgency Has Influenced the ElectionIn many ways, the ASWJ’s impact on the 2024 election began shortly after the 2019 election concluded. What government officials initially dismissed as “bandits” and “evildoers” evolved in less than two years into a group that could execute sophisticated, multipronged attacks and exert a measure of control over key territories, like the port city Mocimboa da Praia. This period of ascendence for the group was characterized in part by a concerted effort to eliminate the tools and symbols of state governance, such as destroying infrastructure, impeding travel, and driving away civilians, including administrative officials and community leaders. The insurgents accompanied these actions with messages to the public condemning the government of the ruling party, FRELIMO, for profiting at the expense of the poor, particularly the Muslim population, while offering their vision of a sharia-based government as an alternative. The deployment of Rwandan and Southern African Development Community (SADC) forces in 2021 helped bolster security in certain areas of the province, encouraging displaced residents to begin their return. However, they were met with limited state services and, more recently, renewed insurgent threats, further casting the FRELIMO government in a negative light to these potential voters.Indeed, the developments over the past five years have elevated security in northern Mozambique into a key electoral issue, with each candidate presenting a plan to resolve the conflict. FRELIMO candidate Daniel Chapo, for instance, visited Tanzania, which has roughly 300 soldiers stationed in Cabo Delgado, to reaffirm the two countries’ historical and political ties. Chapo reportedly visited Rwanda as well, suggesting a prioritization of these two partnerships and an intent to sustain their support on the ground. In August, Chapo also advocated for “robust investment” in Mozambique’s security services to advance the conflict’s resolution and called for greater investment in the province’s tourism industry.Opposition party presidential candidates have also weighed in on the conflict, offering their own perspectives on a potential solution that in part focuses on addressing the underlying drivers of the insurgency, such as poor economic opportunities and political alienation. Venancio Mondlane has argued that eliminating corruption and restoring integrity in government are essential to defeating the insurgency. Meanwhile, the MDM candidate Lutero Simango has emphasized negotiations as a path forward, saying he would be willing to talk “with the devil, if necessary” to end the conflict. Both of these candidates have also emphasized the importance of generating employment for Mozambican youth and ensuring that wealth generated in the province remains in the province. In particular, Simango has focused on increasing the processing of raw materials in the province rather than exporting them abroad during his campaign.How the Election Will Likely Influence the Trajectory of the ASWJ InsurgencyThe conduct and outcome of the election will likely serve as key factors in the conflict’s trajectory in the post-election period. Allegations of electoral fraud that result in a FRELIMO victory—while not uncommon in Mozambican elections—would reinforce the ASWJ’s narrative of a government indifferent to the will and needs of the people. If electoral irregularities further alienate the public and deepen distrust in the state, civilian indifference toward supporting the government’s security efforts could grow even if the allegations of electoral fraud are insufficient to prompt legitimate support for the insurgency.The new government’s execution of its campaign promises to alleviate insecurity and foster greater economic opportunities in northern Mozambique may also influence both public confidence in the state and the trajectory of the conflict. The general agreement among candidates is that stability has not been fully restored to the province and that attention to issues like economic opportunity is vital. This message will likely resonate among voters in the province who are seeking to reestablish their lives. However, even the most earnest candidate will need to overcome the same political and resource constraints that have helped shape the current government’s approach, obstacles that even the FRELIMO candidate, Chapo, would likely struggle to manage.Opportunities for StakeholdersThe consensus among political analysts following Mozambican politics is that Chapo will win the election, creating a unique opportunity for stakeholders to potentially help shape his approach to Cabo Delgado. Mozambican presidents have a long tradition of shifting course on key issues upon taking office, and Chapo himself has indicated an intent to promote greater investment and economic growth in northern Mozambique. Furthermore, his own background as someone who experienced displacement as a child, combined with his reputation for seeking to improve the lives of the general public during his tenure as governor, may suggest a predisposition to address more seriously some of the underlying issues fueling the insurgency. Stakeholders could potentially contribute to a rethinking of Cabo Delgado through several avenues:Prioritizing Cabo Delgado: The conflict in Cabo Delgado is only one of many issues the new government will face. Voters, especially young voters, are expecting greater attention to employment and personal security, seeking a government that will tackle kidnappings. Ensuring that a region that has long been isolated from Maputo and FRELIMO elites is included will be a crucial step toward ensuring the necessary changes to bring about long-term stability in this region.Leveraging candidates’ statements on supporting greater economic opportunities in northern Mozambique: Multiple United Nations Development Programme studies of former African jihadists noted unemployment and underemployment as key recruitment drivers. Indeed, studies of the Mozambican insurgency have also noted the significant role financial incentives have played in recruitment, suggesting that greater attention toward providing equitable educational and employment opportunities has the potential to reduce the appeal of the insurgency as an alternate income source.Collaborating on greater humanitarian support: Shifting dynamics in the insurgency in Cabo Delgado have contributed to a more complex humanitarian picture. Some displaced communities have returned to their areas of origin despite the lack of support resources, while outbreaks of violence continue to displace other communities. Addressing these evolving needs is further complicated by the presence of bureaucratic constraints that at times hinder the effective delivery of support, such as slow visa issuances to aid workers.Mozambique’s slate of presidential candidates has shown a greater awareness of some of the key issues at play in the conflict, issues that transcend historical geographic, ethnic, and religious divides. Greater employment opportunities for youth will be important to voters from Maputo to Palma. Improving training to the security services to better serve the people’s security needs, such as combating kidnappings and organized crime, would likely improve the quality of life across the country. Translating that line of thought into concrete action, however, will be the greatest challenge, but one that would signal the beginning of a long, but potentially fruitful, process toward improving stability in Cabo Delgado.Emilia Columbo is a senior associate (non-resident) with the Africa Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSub-Saharan Africa,\nDefense and Security,\nConflict and Humanitarian Crises,\nHuman Mobility,\nInternational Development,\nGeopolitics and International Security, and\nTerrorism and Irregular Warfare\nImage\nEmilia Columbo\nFormer Senior Associate (Non-resident), Africa Program\nPrograms & Projects\nAfrica Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nPeople, Power, Politics","content_cn":"利用莫桑比克选举改变对卡布德尔加杜冲突的战略\n照片：ALFREDO ZUNIGA/AFP via Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nEmilia Columbo\n发表于2024年9月26日\n莫桑比克将于10月9日举行2024年大选，届时卡布德尔加杜省相较于2019年大选以来已经发生了重大变化。在过去五年中，该省选民目睹了当地叛乱组织“阿赫卢·孙纳·瓦尔贾马”（ASWJ）逐渐演变为一个伊斯兰国附属组织，通常被称为“伊斯兰国—莫桑比克”。卡布德尔加杜的选民看着这一组织在规模、能力和地理存在范围上不断壮大，但也见证了ASWJ随后经历的衰落与复兴。在这场冲突期间，卡布德尔加杜居民经历了严重的经济中断、基础设施破坏以及超过一百万人流离失所；但自2021年以来，他们也看到近一半流离失所者返回原籍地，同时一些曾被叛乱分子控制的地区开始进行重建。过去五年间，这场冲突发生了显著演变，其中包括2021年对帕尔马镇的猛烈袭击，导致非洲大陆最大的液化天然气勘探项目暂停，并促使外国军队进入该省。这使冲突成为主要候选人竞选纲领中的关键议题，例如解放莫桑比克阵线（FRELIMO）候选人丹尼尔·查波、独立候选人韦南西奥·蒙德拉内，以及莫桑比克民主运动（MDM）候选人卢泰罗·西曼戈，他们都承诺在任期内结束冲突。然而，精英阶层对冲突代价的盘算发生变化，加之长期稳定所需政治和财政资源的分配问题，可能仍将成为即便是最有诚意的计划面临的障碍。在这一过渡时期，新总统优先满足其精英和民众基础的要求之际，各利益攸关方或许有机会就新政府如何应对这些挑战、制定全面的前进方案，并使莫桑比克走上解决这场冲突的道路发表意见。\n\n叛乱如何影响选举\n在许多方面，ASWJ对2024年选举的影响在2019年选举结束后不久便开始显现。政府官员最初将其斥为“土匪”和“恶人”，但不到两年时间，这一组织便发展成能够实施复杂的多路袭击，并对莫辛布瓦-达普拉亚港口城市等关键地区施加一定程度控制的团体。该组织崛起时期的部分特征，是有组织地摧毁国家治理的工具和象征，例如破坏基础设施、阻碍出行以及驱逐平民，其中包括行政官员和社区领袖。叛乱分子还伴随这些行动向公众传递信息，谴责执政党解放莫桑比克阵线（FRELIMO）政府以牺牲穷人、尤其是穆斯林人口为代价牟利，同时提出以沙里亚法为基础的政府愿景作为替代方案。卢旺达和南部非洲发展共同体（SADC）部队于2021年的部署帮助加强了该省部分地区的安全，促使流离失所居民开始返乡。然而，他们面对的是有限的国家服务，以及近来重新出现的叛乱威胁，进一步\n将弗雷利莫政府描绘成对这些潜在选民不利的形象。事实上，过去五年发生的事已使莫桑比克北部的安全问题成为关键选举议题，每位候选人都提出了化解冲突的计划。例如，弗雷利莫党候选人丹尼尔·查波访问了坦桑尼亚；目前约有300名坦桑尼亚士兵驻扎在德尔加杜角省。此行旨在重申两国之间的历史和政治联系。据报道，查波还访问了卢旺达，这表明他优先重视这两个伙伴关系，并有意维持两国在当地的支持。8月，查波还主张对莫桑比克安全部门进行“强有力的投资”，以推动冲突解决，并呼吁加大对该省旅游业的投资。\n\n反对党总统候选人也对冲突发表了看法，提出了各自对潜在解决方案的观点，其中一部分着眼于解决叛乱的根本驱动因素，例如经济机会匮乏和政治疏离。韦南西奥·蒙德拉内认为，消除腐败、恢复政府廉正是击败叛乱的关键。与此同时，莫桑比克民主运动党候选人卢特罗·西芒戈强调谈判是前进之路，并表示为结束冲突，他愿意“必要时与魔鬼谈判”。这两位候选人还都强调，为莫桑比克青年创造就业机会，并确保该省创造的财富留在本省的重要性。西芒戈尤其着重于在竞选期间增加该省原材料的加工，而不是将其出口到国外。\n\n选举将如何可能影响“圣战组织”叛乱的走向\n\n选举的过程及结果很可能成为影响选举后时期冲突走向的关键因素。若有关选举舞弊的指控最终导致弗雷利莫获胜——尽管这在莫桑比克选举中并不罕见——将进一步强化“圣战组织”关于政府漠视人民意愿和需求的叙事。如果选举违规行为进一步疏远公众并加深对国家的不信任，即使有关选举舞弊的指控不足以促成对叛乱的真正支持，平民对支持政府安全行动的冷漠也可能加剧。\n\n新政府兑现其竞选承诺、缓解莫桑比克北部的不安全局势并创造更多经济机会，也可能影响公众对国家的信心以及冲突的走向。候选人普遍认为，该省的稳定尚未完全恢复，而关注经济机会等问题至关重要。这一信息很可能会在该省寻求重建生活的选民中引起共鸣。然而，即使是最为真诚的候选人，也必须克服那些塑造现政府应对方式的同样政治和资源限制；即便是弗雷利莫候选人查波，也很可能难以应对这些障碍。\n\n利益相关者的机会\n\n关注莫桑比克政治的政治分析人士普遍认为，查波将赢得选举，这为利益相关者提供了一个独特机会，使其有可能帮助塑造他对德尔加杜角省的应对方式。莫桑比克总统在就任后改变关键议题方针由来已久。\n政府，而且查波本人也表示有意促进对莫桑比克北部的更多投资和经济增长。此外，他本人童年时期曾经历流离失所，再加上他在担任省长期间致力于改善普通民众生活的声誉，这可能表明他倾向于更加认真地处理助长叛乱的一些根本问题。利益相关者可以通过以下几种途径，促使人们重新思考德尔加杜角：\n\n优先关注德尔加杜角：德尔加杜角的冲突只是新政府将面对的众多问题之一。选民，尤其是年轻选民，期待政府更加关注就业和人身安全，希望政府能够打击绑架活动。确保这个长期以来与马普托及解放阵线精英阶层相隔绝的地区被纳入其中，将是确保必要变革、为该地区带来长期稳定的关键一步。\n\n利用候选人关于支持莫桑比克北部扩大经济机会的表态：联合国开发计划署对前非洲圣战分子的多项研究指出，失业和就业不足是招募的重要驱动因素。事实上，对莫桑比克叛乱的研究也指出，经济激励在招募中发挥了重要作用，这表明更加重视提供公平的教育和就业机会，有可能降低叛乱作为替代收入来源的吸引力。\n\n合作提供更多人道主义支持：德尔加杜角叛乱局势的变化，使人道主义形势更加复杂。一些流离失所的社区尽管缺乏支持资源，仍已返回原居地；与此同时，暴力事件的持续又迫使其他社区流离失所。不断变化的需求本身就难以应对，而官僚主义限制的存在进一步增加了难度，这些限制有时会妨碍支持的有效提供，例如向援助工作人员签发签证的速度缓慢。\n\n莫桑比克总统候选人阵营表现出对冲突中一些关键问题更高的认识，而这些问题超越了历史形成的地理、族群和宗教分歧。从马普托到帕尔马，为青年提供更多就业机会对选民而言都十分重要。改善对安全部门的培训，使其更好地满足民众的安全需求，例如打击绑架和有组织犯罪，可能会提高全国各地的生活质量。然而，将这一思路转化为具体行动将是最大的挑战，但这也将标志着一个漫长却可能富有成效的进程的开始，从而改善德尔加杜角的稳定局势。\n\nEmilia Columbo 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心非洲项目的高级（非驻地）副研究员。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且不涉及专有信息。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n撒哈拉以南非洲，\n国防和\n安全、\n冲突与人道主义危机、\n人口流动、\n国际发展、\n地缘政治与国际安全，以及\n恐怖主义与非常规战争\n图片\nEmilia Columbo\n前高级助理（非驻），非洲项目\n项目与计划\n非洲项目\n地缘政治与外交政策\n人民、权力、政治","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-09-26T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3472","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"让关税再次伟大：特朗普总统是否有法律授权对美国贸易伙伴和中国实施新关税？","title_en":"Making Tariffs Great Again: Does President Trump Have Legal Authority to Implement New Tariffs on U.S. Trading Partners and China?","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表评论文章，分析特朗普若再次当选是否有法律授权实施全面关税。文章梳理了多项潜在法律依据，包括《1962年贸易扩展法》第232条、1974年贸易法第301条、国际紧急经济权力法、第122条国际收支授权以及1930年关税法第338条。作者指出，尽管宪法将关税权授予国会，但国会已广泛授权总统在特定条件下加征关税，且法院历来对总统在外交和贸易领域的裁量权高度尊重，因此法律挑战难度较大。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary assesses whether former President Trump would have legal authority to impose sweeping new tariffs if reelected. It reviews potential statutory bases, including Section 232 of the Trade Expansion Act of 1962, Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, the International Emergency Economic Powers Act, Section 122 balance-of-payments authority, and Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930. The authors argue that although the Constitution grants tariff power to Congress, it has delegated extensive authority to the president, and courts have historically deferred to executive branch discretion on foreign affairs and trade, making legal challenges difficult.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:22:36.166774+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/making-tariffs-great-again-does-president-trump-have-legal-authority-implement-new-tariffs","content":"Making Tariffs Great Again: Does President Trump Have Legal Authority to Implement New Tariffs on U.S. Trading Partners and China?\nPhoto: David McNew/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nWarren Maruyama,\nLyric Galvin,\nand\nWilliam Alan Reinsch\nPublished October 10, 2024\nIntroductionIf former president Donald Trump wins back the White House, U.S. trading partners are likely to face a barrage of renewed tariff threats. Trump has announced that, if reelected, he will impose a 10–20 percent across-the-board tariff on imports into the United States, as well as an additional 60 percent tariff on all imports from China.Trump has multiple potential sources of legal authority that could support his tariffs, if reelected, including Section 232, Section 301, the International Emergency Economic Powers Act, Section 122 Balance of Payments Authority, and even older statutes like Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930. Vice President Kamala Harris has blasted Trump’s tariff plan, arguing that it would represent a $3,900 sales tax on Americans and drive up prices and inflation.That tariffs have reemerged as a central policy debate between the two presidential hopefuls has not only rattled allies and trading partners but also parts of the business community, which have been left in the political wilderness as both political parties compete to bash China and repudiate trade liberalization.A key question is whether a president has the authority to implement the types of across-the-board tariffs being discussed by Trump. The U.S. Constitution plainly grants the power to impose tariffs to Congress, not the president: “The Congress shall have Power To lay and collect Taxes, Duties, . . . To regulate Commerce with foreign Nations” (Article 1, Section 8). However, Congress has delegated extensive authorities that allow the president to impose tariffs if certain statutory conditions are met.While some analysts have tried to reassure investors and markets by asserting that Trump would lack the legal authority to implement his tariffs plans, this reflects an overly optimistic view of the limits of presidential tariff authority. As of today, there are multiple legal authorities that Trump could rely on to justify the imposition of increased tariffs, including many that Trump already availed himself of during his presidency. These include Sections 232 and 301, the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA), Section 122 Balance-of-Payments Authority, and Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930. While Section 232 requires an investigation by the Department of Commerce and Section 301 requires an investigation and determination by the Office of the U.S. Trade Representative (USTR), these procedural niceties could be accomplished in relatively short order by cabinet officials, particularly since undue delay could put them at risk of getting fired. Regardless, any investigation or public comment period would allow anticipation to build, enhancing Trump’s negotiating leverage, which is likely one of the main points of the exercise.Section 232Section 232 of the Trade Expansion Act of 1962 (known as the Trade Expansion Act) grants the president broad power to adjust imports if they are found to be a threat to U.S. national security, including through the imposition of tariffs.Trump has used Section 232 to impose 10–25 percent tariffs in the past. After the Department of Commerce determined in 2018 that the quantities and circumstances of steel and aluminum imports “threaten to impair U.S. national security,” then president Trump applied 25 percent tariffs on imports of steel and 10 percent tariffs on aluminum. While this tariff originally applied to all countries, exemptions were granted to Brazil, South Korea, Canada, Mexico, and Argentina in exchange for quota arrangements, and Australia was exempted. While the Biden administration kept the Section 232 tariffs, it reached separate agreements with the European Union, Japan, and the United Kingdom on tariff-rate quota arrangements.While some argue that it would be a stretch for Trump to claim that all imports are a threat to U.S. national security under Section 232, the courts have routinely deferred to presidents on foreign affairs and trade policy, and legal challenges to the Section 232 tariffs haven’t gone anywhere. USP Holdings, Inc. v. United States affirmed the Court of International Trade determination upholding the Trump administration’s Section 232 tariffs on steel imports, and American Institute for International Steel (AIIS) v. United States affirmed the Court of International Trade determination that Section 232 did not violate the Constitution’s Separation of Powers.Section 301Section 301(b) of the Trade Act of 1974 gives the president broad authority to take all appropriate action, including retaliatory tariffs, to obtain the removal of any act, policy, or practice of a foreign government that is unjustified, unreasonable, or discriminatory, and that burdens or restricts U.S. commerce. If the USTR determines that the alleged conduct is unfair or violates U.S. rights under trade agreements, then it can decide what action to take subject to the direction of the president. Section 301 authorizes the USTR to (1) impose duties or other import restrictions, (2) withdraw or suspend trade agreement concessions, or (3) enter into binding agreements with foreign governments to eliminate the conduct in question or provide compensation.Again, Trump used this authority to impose tariffs during his presidency. While the USTR initiated six investigations during the Trump administration, two investigations resulted in the imposition of tariffs. The first was on U.S. imports from China, due to China’s technology transfer, intellectual property, and innovation policies, and led to 25.0 and 7.5 percent tariffs on roughly $370 billion worth of imports from China. The second was on imports from the European Union caused by EU subsidies on large civil aircraft, which led to additional tariffs of 15–25 percent on roughly $7.5 billion worth of imports from the European Union. Trump also launched 301 investigations into digital services taxes on a number of countries, as well as currency practices and timber from Vietnam.Section 301 tariffs are an additional tool that could permit President Trump to impose a 10–20 percent tariff on all imports to the United States, as well as a 60 percent tariff on imports from China, as long as he finds that the acts of all U.S. trading partners are unfair. International Emergency Economic Powers ActThe IEEPA provides broad presidential authority to deal with international economic emergencies, which Trump would almost certainly be prepared to find. Indeed, Trump nearly became the first president to use IEEPA to impose tariffs in 2019, when he threatened a 5 percent across-the-board tariff on Mexican goods unless the country stemmed illegal migration into the United States. A deal was struck in 2019, rendering reliance on IEEPA unnecessary, but the option remains. While some argue that using IEEPA in this manner is contrary to the intent of the statute, the language is broad: “Any authority granted to the President by section 1702 of this title may be exercised to deal with any unusual and extraordinary threat . . . if the President declares a national emergency with respect to such threat.” IEEPA has been used to target specific countries as well as broader concerns involving export controls, human rights, security, and election interference. It is not a stretch to imagine a President Trump expanding IEEPA to address large U.S. trade deficits.More importantly, IEEPA is the successor to the Trading with the Enemy Act, which President Nixon invoked in 1971 to justify a 10 percent import tariff due to a balance-of-payments crisis and which the U.S. Court of Customs and Patent Appeals upheld in Yoshida v. United States.Section 122 Balance-of-Payments AuthorityThe president’s balance-of-payments authority in Section 122 of the Trade Act of 1974 allows the president to impose an additional 15 percent tariff on imports for 150 days “Whenever fundamental international payments problems require special import measures to restrict imports—(1) to deal with large and serious United States balance-of-payments deficits, (2) to prevent an imminent and significant depreciation of the dollar in foreign exchange markets.” This authority was specifically added by Congress after President Nixon used the Trading with the Enemy Act (IEEPA’s predecessor) to impose a 10 percent surcharge on U.S. trading partners to address rising U.S. trade deficits and the overvaluation of the dollar under the Bretton Woods gold standard. However, this approach would limit the tariffs to a period of only 150 days (unless extended by Congress). Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930Finally, there are a number of older authorities that could be used to justify an increase in tariffs on imports from allies and adversaries alike. Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930, for example, allows the president to impose additional tariffs up to 50 percent on any country that discriminates against U.S. products. Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930 would also allow the president to block imports completely for any country that increases their discrimination against U.S. products. However, this provision hasn’t been used for over 70 years. Legal ChallengesThe use of any of these authorities to impose tariffs would likely be challenged by importers or other stakeholders. However, despite the Supreme Court’s recent decision in the June 2024 case Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, any legal challenges to future Trump tariffs likely would face a steep uphill climb. The courts, including the Supreme Court, traditionally have been reluctant to interfere with the president’s exercise of foreign affairs and tariff powers. For instance, in United States v. Curtiss-Wright Export Corp., the Supreme Court found that the president has certain inherent powers in foreign affairs that do not require an affirmative grant of statutory authority from Congress. In J. W. Hampton, Jr. & Co. v. United States, it found that presidential authority under Section 315 of the Tariff Act was a valid constitutional delegation of authority as long as it sets out “intelligible principle.” Federal Energy Administration v. Algonquin SNG, Inc. upheld Section 232(b) tariffs on imported oil, after finding that Section 232 sets out an “intelligible principle” to guide presidential decisionmaking. Lastly, the Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit decided in Maple Leaf Fish Co. v. United States that courts have “a very limited role” in reviewing presidential trade actions “of a highly discretionary kind,” such as Section 201, and such actions can only be set aside if they involve “a clear misconstruction of the governing statute, a significant procedural violation, or action outside delegated authority.”The Maple Leaf Fish Co. case is particularly important since the Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit has jurisdiction over most trade law appeals. While precedent plays less of a role under the current Supreme Court, these past cases suggest that the courts are likely to take an especially deferential approach to executive branch decisions involving foreign policy, national security, and international economic policymaking, recognizing that it falls outside their normal purview and expertise. While the inevitable appeals to any litigation will take years to wind their way through the judicial system, from a business perspective, the real issue would be the immediate effect on costs, trade flows, and supply chains, and not on what might eventually be the legal outcome three years down the road.While Congress always has the option of passing legislation to amend or revoke some of the president’s delegated tariff-setting authority, it’s difficult to see that happening with a veto-proof majority in the current political climate.ConclusionIf Trump wins back the White House, U.S. trading partners are likely to face a barrage of renewed tariff threats, which has left many companies and trading partners struggling to better understand what measures may be coming. During Trump’s first term, he used tariffs for a variety of purposes, including diversifying supply chains (e.g., China), addressing global excess capacity (e.g., steel and aluminum), and for leverage in negotiations with Europe, China, Canada, and Mexico. Trump also has a longstanding view that U.S. average tariff rates are too low, a vestige of past General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade rounds, the 1994 Uruguay Round Agreements, and the World Trade Organization, which locked in U.S. tariffs through negotiated tariff “bindings.” If he is reelected, there appear to be few practical or legal barriers to Trump making good on his campaign promise.Trump’s decisionmaking process is also highly adaptable, so the ultimate outcomes are less clear. He likes to throw out provocative ideas that grab attention but sometimes abandons or adapts them if there’s too much of a backlash. More than anything else, he is a dealmaker who uses provocative threats, including tariffs, for negotiating leverage and attention. Whether the tariff threats would become reality is anyone’s guess, but as China, U.S. trading partners, and foreign steel and aluminum makers found out in Trump’s first term, it would be foolish to write them off.Warren Maruyama served as general counsel of the Office of the U.S. Trade Representative during the administration of President George W. Bush and as associate director for International Economic Policy on the White House policy staff during the administration of President George H.W. Bush. Lyric Galvin graduated magna cum laude from the Georgetown University Law Center and now practices international trade and investment law. William A. Reinsch holds the Scholl Chair in International Business at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nTrade and International Business, and\nEconomic Security\nWarren Maruyama\nFormer general counsel, Office of the U.S. Trade Representative\nLyric Galvin\nPractitioner, international trade and investment law\nImage\nWilliam Alan Reinsch\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Economics Program and Scholl Chair in International Business\nPrograms & Projects\nEconomics Program and Scholl Chair in International Business\nEconomic Security and Technology","content_cn":"让关税再次伟大：特朗普总统是否拥有对美国贸易伙伴和中国实施新关税的法律权限？\n图片：David McNew/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nWarren Maruyama、\nLyric Galvin、\n和\nWilliam Alan Reinsch\n发表于2024年10月10日\n引言如果前总统唐纳德·特朗普重新赢得白宫，美国贸易伙伴很可能会面临新一轮关税威胁。特朗普已宣布，如果再次当选，他将对进入美国的进口商品普遍征收10%至20%的关税，并对所有来自中国的进口商品额外征收60%的关税。特朗普有多个潜在的法律授权来源可支持其关税措施（如果再次当选），包括第232条、第301条、《国际紧急经济权力法》、第122条国际收支平衡授权，甚至还有1930年《关税法》第338条等更早的法规。副总统卡玛拉·哈里斯猛烈抨击特朗普的关税计划，认为这将相当于对美国人征收3,900美元的销售税，并推高物价和通胀。关税重新成为两位总统候选人之间的核心政策争论，不仅令盟友和贸易伙伴感到不安，也令部分商界感到震动；在两党竞相抨击中国、否定贸易自由化之际，这些商界人士已被抛入政治荒野。一个关键问题是，总统是否有权实施特朗普正在讨论的此类普遍关税。美国宪法明确将征收关税的权力授予国会，而非总统：“国会有权征收和收取税赋、关税……管理与外国的商业”（第一条第八节）。然而，国会已经授予总统广泛权限，只要满足某些法定条件，总统即可征收关税。尽管一些分析人士试图通过断言特朗普缺乏实施其关税计划的法律权限来安抚投资者和市场，但这反映出对总统关税权限限制的过度乐观看法。截至目前，特朗普可以依赖多项法律授权来为提高关税提供正当性，其中许多授权他在担任总统期间已经使用过。这些包括第232条和第301条、《国际紧急经济权力法》（IEEPA）、第122条国际收支平衡授权，以及1930年《关税法》第338条。虽然第232条要求商务部进行调查，第301条要求美国贸易代表办公室（USTR）进行调查并作出裁定，但这些程序上的细节可以由内阁官员在相对较短时间内完成，尤其是因为过度拖延可能使他们面临被解雇的风险。无论如何，任何调查或公众评论期都会让预期升温，从而增强特朗普的谈判筹码，而这很可能正是此举的主要目的之一。第232条1962年《贸易扩展法》（称为《贸易扩展法》）第232条赋予总统广泛权力：如果认定进口对美国国家安全构成威胁，包括通过征收关税，总统可以调整进口。特朗普过去曾利用第232条征收10%至25%的关税。2018年，商务部认定钢铁和铝进口的数量及情形“构成威胁\n以损害美国国家安全”，随后特朗普总统对钢铁进口征收25%的关税，对铝进口征收10%的关税。虽然这项关税最初适用于所有国家，但巴西、韩国、加拿大、墨西哥和阿根廷因接受配额安排而获豁免，澳大利亚则获得豁免。拜登政府虽然保留了第232条关税，但分别与欧盟、日本和英国就关税配额安排达成了协议。尽管有人认为，特朗普若声称所有进口商品在第232条下都对美国国家安全构成威胁未免牵强，但法院通常会对总统在外交事务和贸易政策方面的决定给予尊重，而针对第232条关税的法律挑战也一直没有进展。USP Holdings, Inc. v. United States确认了美国国际贸易法院维持特朗普政府对钢铁进口征收第232条关税的裁定；American Institute for International Steel (AIIS) v. United States则确认了美国国际贸易法院关于第232条并未违反宪法权力分立原则的裁定。\n\n第301条\n\n1974年《贸易法》第301(b)条赋予总统广泛权力，可以采取包括报复性关税在内的一切适当行动，以促使外国政府取消任何无正当理由、不合理或具有歧视性的行为、政策或做法，只要这些行为、政策或做法对美国商业造成负担或限制。如果美国贸易代表办公室（USTR）认定被指控的行为不公平，或违反了美国在贸易协定下的权利，那么它可以在总统指示下决定采取何种行动。第301条授权USTR：（1）征收关税或采取其他进口限制；（2）撤回或暂停贸易协定项下的让步；或（3）与外国政府签订具有约束力的协议，以消除相关行为或提供补偿。特朗普再次利用这一权力在其任期内征收关税。特朗普政府期间，USTR启动了六项调查，其中两项调查导致征收关税。第一项针对美国从中国进口的商品，原因是中国的技术转让、知识产权和创新政策，并导致对价值约3700亿美元的中国进口商品征收25.0%和7.5%的关税。第二项针对从欧盟进口的商品，起因是欧盟对大型民用飞机的补贴，并导致对价值约75亿美元的欧盟进口商品额外征收15%至25%的关税。特朗普还对多个国家的数字服务税，以及越南的货币政策和木材问题，发起了第301条调查。第301条关税是另一项工具，只要特朗普认定美国所有贸易伙伴的行为均不公平，就可能据此对所有进口美国的商品征收10%至20%的关税，并对中国进口商品征收60%的关税。\n\n《国际紧急经济权力法》\n\n《国际紧急经济权力法》（IEEPA）赋予总统广泛权力，以应对国际经济紧急情况，而特朗普几乎肯定会准备认定存在这种情况。事实上，2019年特朗普威胁称，除非墨西哥遏制非法移民进入美国，否则将对所有墨西哥商品统一征收5%的关税；他当时几乎成为首位利用IEEPA征收关税的总统。2019年双方达成协议，因此无需依赖IEEPA\n不必要，但这一选项仍然存在。尽管有人认为以这种方式使用《国际紧急经济权力法》（IEEPA）违背该法的意图，但其措辞十分宽泛：“本标题第1702条授予总统的任何权力，均可用于应对任何异常和非同寻常的威胁……如果总统就此类威胁宣布国家紧急状态。”IEEPA曾被用于针对特定国家，也被用于处理涉及出口管制、人权、安全和选举干预等更广泛的问题。设想特朗普总统扩大IEEPA的适用范围，以应对美国巨额贸易逆差，并非牵强之举。更重要的是，IEEPA是《与敌贸易法》的继承法；尼克松总统曾于1971年援引该法，以国际收支危机为由对进口征收10%的关税，美国海关与专利上诉法院在 Yoshida v. United States 一案中维持了这一做法。\n\n《第122条：国际收支授权》\n\n《1974年贸易法》第122条规定的总统国际收支权力，允许总统在“当根本性的国际收支问题要求采取特殊进口措施，以限制进口——（1）应对美国严重且巨大的国际收支逆差；（2）防止美元在外汇市场即将发生且幅度重大的贬值”时，对进口商品加征15%的额外关税，期限为150天。国会是在尼克松总统利用《与敌贸易法》（IEEPA的前身）对美国贸易伙伴征收10%的附加费，以应对不断上升的美国贸易逆差以及布雷顿森林黄金标准下美元被高估的问题之后，专门增加了这项授权。然而，这种做法会将关税期限限制在仅150天（除非国会予以延长）。\n\n《1930年关税法》第338条\n\n最后，还有一些较早的授权，可以用来为提高对盟友和对手进口商品的关税提供依据。例如，《1930年关税法》第338条允许总统对任何歧视美国商品的国家加征最高达50%的额外关税。《1930年关税法》第338条还允许总统对任何加剧其对美国商品歧视的国家完全禁止进口。然而，该条款已有70多年未被使用。\n\n《法律挑战》\n\n利用上述任何一项授权征收关税，都可能受到进口商或其他利益相关方的挑战。然而，尽管最高法院最近在2024年6月的 Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo 案中作出了裁决，未来针对特朗普关税的任何法律挑战很可能仍将面临艰难的上诉之路。包括最高法院在内的法院，传统上一直不愿干涉总统行使外交事务和关税权力。例如，在 United States v. Curtiss-Wright Export Corp. 案中，最高法院认定，总统在外交事务方面拥有某些固有权力，无需国会以成文法明确授予。在 J. W. Hampton, Jr. & Co. v. United States 案中，最高法院认定，《关税法》第315条赋予总统的权力属于符合宪法的有效授权，只要其中规定了“可理解的原则”。Federal Energy Administration v. Algonquin SNG, Inc. 案维持了对进口石油征收的第232(b)条关税，理由是第232条规定了一个\n以“可理解的原则”指导总统决策。最后，联邦巡回上诉法院在 Maple Leaf Fish Co. v. United States 案中裁定，对于“高度自由裁量性质”的总统贸易行动（例如第201条行动），法院在审查中“发挥的作用非常有限”；只有在涉及“对管辖性法规的明显误解、重大程序违规，或超出授权范围的行动”时，才可以撤销此类行动。Maple Leaf Fish Co. 案尤其重要，因为联邦巡回上诉法院对大多数贸易法上诉案件拥有管辖权。虽然在现任最高法院时期，判例所发挥的作用较小，但这些过去的案件表明，法院很可能会对涉及外交政策、国家安全和国际经济政策制定的行政部门决定采取特别尊重的态度，并认识到这些事务超出了法院通常的职权范围和专业能力。虽然任何诉讼不可避免的上诉都需要数年时间才能在司法系统中逐步推进，但从企业角度看，真正的问题将是对成本、贸易流动和供应链的即时影响，而不是三年后最终可能出现的法律结果。国会始终可以选择通过立法来修订或撤销总统部分经授权的关税制定权，但在当前政治环境下，很难想象能够获得足以推翻否决的多数票来实现这一点。\n\n结论\n\n如果特朗普重新赢得白宫，美国贸易伙伴很可能再次面临一连串的关税威胁，这让许多企业和贸易伙伴努力试图更好地了解可能出台哪些措施。在特朗普第一任期内，他出于多种目的使用关税，包括使供应链多元化（例如针对中国）、应对全球产能过剩（例如钢铁和铝），以及在与欧洲、中国、加拿大和墨西哥的谈判中施加筹码。特朗普还长期认为，美国平均关税税率过低，这是过去《关税与贸易总协定》各轮谈判、1994年乌拉圭回合协议以及世界贸易组织所遗留下来的结果；这些机制通过谈判达成的关税“约束”锁定了美国关税。如果他再次当选，特朗普似乎几乎没有实际或法律上的障碍来兑现竞选承诺。\n\n特朗普的决策过程也具有高度适应性，因此最终结果并不明朗。他喜欢抛出能够吸引注意力的挑衅性想法，但如果引发的反弹过大，有时会放弃或调整这些想法。最重要的是，他是一名交易撮合者，会利用包括关税在内的挑衅性威胁来获取谈判筹码并吸引关注。关税威胁是否会成为现实，谁也无法确定；但正如中国、美国贸易伙伴以及外国钢铁和铝生产商在特朗普第一任期内所发现的那样，轻率地将这些威胁置之不理是愚蠢的。\n\nWarren Maruyama 曾在乔治·W·布什总统执政期间担任美国贸易代表办公室总法律顾问，并在乔治·H·W·布什总统执政期间担任白宫政策班子国际经济政策副主任。Lyric Galvin 以优异成绩毕业于乔治城大学法律中心，如今从事国际贸易与投资法业务。William A. Reinsch 担任……的 Scholl 国际商业讲席教授\n华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家私营、免税机构，专注于国际公共政策问题。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的意见。\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n贸易与国际商业，以及\n经济安全\nWarren Maruyama\n美国贸易代表办公室前总法律顾问\nLyric Galvin\n国际贸易与投资法实务专家\n图片\nWilliam Alan Reinsch\n经济项目及肖尔国际商业主席高级顾问（非驻任）\n项目与计划\n经济项目及肖尔国际商业主席\n经济安全与技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-10-10T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-316","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"错失的机遇：关键矿产与美越全面战略伙伴关系","title_en":"Missed Opportunity: Critical Minerals and the U.S.-Vietnam Comprehensive Strategic Partnership","summary_cn":"该评论回顾2023年美越将关系提升为全面战略伙伴关系时宣布的半导体与关键矿产合作，指出后续落实有限。文章分析关键矿产的战略意义、中国在开采与精炼环节的主导地位，以及越南稀土储量被下调、开采量偏低、腐败案件与环保争议等制约因素，并讨论关税摩擦、中越关系升温及美国对大国竞争关注下降等趋势对双边合作前景的影响。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary reviews the semiconductor and critical minerals cooperation announced when the U.S.-Vietnam relationship was elevated to a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership in 2023, arguing that follow-through has been minimal. It examines the strategic importance of critical minerals, China's dominance in mining and refining, and Vietnam's constraints—including downgraded rare earth reserve estimates, low extraction volumes, corruption cases, and environmental and labor concerns. It also assesses how tariff frictions, warming Vietnam-China ties, and reduced U.S. emphasis on great-power competition shape prospects for deeper cooperation.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:56:36.605001+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/missed-opportunity-critical-minerals-and-us-vietnam-comprehensive-strategic-partnership","content":"Missed Opportunity: Critical Minerals and the U.S.-Vietnam Comprehensive Strategic Partnership\nPhoto: NHAC NGUYEN/AFP/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nHunter Marston\nPublished August 26, 2026\nIntroductionIn September 2023, President Joe Biden traveled to Hanoi and met the late General Secretary Nguyen Phu Trong, marking the 10th anniversary of the U.S.-Vietnam comprehensive partnership. The two leaders surprised observers by skipping the intermediate “strategic partnership” tier altogether and elevating relations directly to a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership, the top rung of Hanoi’s diplomatic hierarchy.At the center of the announcement was a new semiconductor partnership, under which Washington pledged $2 million in seed funding to help build resilient chip supply chains and expand Vietnamese manufacturing capacity. The joint readout also highlighted a further $1.6 billion investment by U.S. firm Amkor Technology to build a factory in Bac Ninh province and two design centers in Ho Chi Minh City.Since then, progress has stalled. Working with Vietnamese authorities to more precisely measure the country’s mineral deposits, the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) revised its estimates of Vietnam’s rare earth elements, previously estimated at roughly 22 million metric tons in 2024—then the world’s second largest—to 3.5 million metric tons in its 2025 report. In October 2025, the two countries reached a framework trade agreement reducing U.S. tariffs on Vietnamese goods from 46 to 20 percent, but the deal included no provisions on critical minerals, and Hanoi has stressed it remains only a framework.Despite this pattern, follow-through on the 2023 U.S.-Vietnam critical minerals commitments has been minimal. General Secretary To Lam met Silicon Valley leaders during a 2024 visit to the UN General Assembly, but little concrete progress has followed, and it remains unclear how Vietnam fits into the Trump administration’s broader Indo-Pacific strategy. Hanoi, shaken by the administration’s unilateral imposition of tariffs, has favored pragmatic engagement over confrontation, continuing to seek cooperation wherever possible. For example, Vietnam opted to join President Trump’s signature Board of Peace, and To Lam attended the initiative’s inaugural meeting in Washington in February.The Strategic Significance of Critical MineralsCritical minerals underpin advanced manufacturing—including laptops, solar panels, electric vehicles (EVs), and fighter jets—making them a fiercely contested strategic asset. China holds roughly 70 percent of global rare earth mining capacity and 90 percent of refining capacity, giving Beijing significant leverage over global supply chains.Beijing has repeatedly used this leverage as political pressure, restricting exports of gallium and germanium in 2023 in response to U.S. chip export controls and curbing exports of seven rare earths in 2025 after President Trump’s “Liberation Day” tariffs. These moves have pushed affected countries toward stockpiling and diversification. In February 2026, the United States hosted a Critical Minerals Ministerial with 54 countries—though notably not Vietnam—building on the “Pax Silica” framework and the 14-member Minerals Security Partnership.Global demand for critical minerals is projected to grow nearly sixfold by 2040 as the clean energy transition accelerates. The United States has responded with legislation such as the Inflation Reduction Act and CHIPS and Science Act and has folded critical minerals provisions into recent trade agreements with Malaysia, Thailand, Indonesia, Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, and Ukraine—several granting the United States first right of refusal on raw ore exports.U.S.-Vietnam Alignment on Critical MineralsThree factors have historically driven U.S.-Vietnam strategic alignment. First, China’s repeated incursions into Vietnamese waters between 2014 and 2019 pushed Vietnam toward deeper security cooperation with the United States, building trust that spilled over into other domains. Second, both countries share an interest in de-risking supply chains from China, motivating Vietnamese investment in semiconductors, EVs, and solar manufacturing, underpinning the 2023 semiconductor MOU. Third, deepening trade ties make Washington indispensable to Hanoi’s goal of reaching upper-middle-income status by 2030 and advanced economy status by 2045. The United States purchases nearly 30 percent of Vietnam’s exports, and the Communist Party’s developmental ambitions rest heavily on industries dependent on critical minerals. Hanoi has invested considerable resources in expanding assembly, testing, and packaging capabilities in semiconductors.The 2023 U.S. investment pledges were a meaningful first step, but the United States could do considerably more, for instance, utilizing the legislative powers of the CHIPS Act and financing from the Development Finance Corporation and Export-Import Bank of the United States to help Vietnam build downstream rare earth separation and refining infrastructure. Bringing Hanoi into minilateral frameworks such as the Minerals Security Partnership and Pax Silica would further embed Vietnam in a coalition capable of counterbalancing Chinese dominance.Vietnam's Strengths and Weaknesses as a Critical Minerals SupplierVietnam retains real strategic assets: a favorable location and labor costs that make it attractive for “China+1” investment, relative political stability, and a growing semiconductor sector. But its position in rare earths has weakened considerably. The 2025 USGS revision dropped Vietnam from second after China to sixth in global rare earth reserve rankings—behind Brazil, India, Australia, and Russia—based on updated government data reclassifying proven reserves. Vietnam’s remaining strengths lie elsewhere: It holds roughly 18 percent of global reserves of bauxite, the raw ore from which aluminum is extracted, and it is the world’s second-largest producer of tungsten, a mineral central to defense manufacturing. It also has meaningful deposits of lanthanum and neodymium used in EV magnets.Vietnam’s 2023 plan targeted processing over 2 million tons of rare earths annually by 2030, yet actual extraction reached only 600 tons in 2023 and 300 tons in 2024. Corruption prosecutions—including the 2023 arrests of executives at Thai Duong Group and Vietnam Rare Earth JSC and the 2025 sentencing of a former deputy minister to three years in prison—have frozen operations and chilled foreign investment, notably at the promising Dong Pao mine. In response, Vietnam has updated its legal framework, passing a new Law on Geology and Minerals in 2024 and a 2025 law restricting exports of rare earth ores and refined products to encourage in-country processing rather than shipping to China.Expanding extraction also raises environmental and labor concerns. Past bauxite mining in Vietnam’s Central Highlands drew sustained pushback from civil society due to environmental concerns, the government’s lack of transparency, and the Chinese mining company’s use of laborers from China rather than Vietnam. The case of Indonesia offers a cautionary tale. In 2014, Jakarta passed legislation restricting the export of raw nickel ore, requiring international firms to develop domestic refining capabilities within Indonesia. While the move was widely heralded as a success for Jakarta, it has done little to benefit Indonesia’s own labor force while deepening the country’s dependence on Chinese technology to export nickel. Furthermore, it has exacerbated environmental destruction and social tensions between local communities and mining companies.U.S.-Vietnam Relations Going ForwardFour trends complicate deeper cooperation:U.S. tariffs and scrutiny of transshipment have unsettled bilateral trade relations. The October 2025 framework agreement kept 20 percent tariffs in place, though these were later reduced to 10 percent following a Supreme Court ruling in February 2026 and subsequently readjusted to 12.5 percent after Section 301 investigations into unfair trade practices. The framework agreement also saw Vietnamese purchases of Boeing aircraft and investment pledges but left critical minerals and transshipment unaddressed.Smaller states such as Vietnam face mounting pressure to align with one great power or another in the U.S.-China technology competition, even as the Trump administration’s more transactional, less values-driven foreign policy reduces some of Vietnam’s traditional anxieties about U.S.-driven regime pressure.Vietnam-China ties have warmed substantially: Consider Xi Jinping’s 2023 and 2025 visits to Hanoi, as well as To Lam’s trip to Beijing in 2024—his first overseas visit after becoming general secretary. The two countries have signed dozens of new economic agreements and deliberately compartmentalized maritime disputes in the South China Sea in favor of broader strategic stability.Fourth, the relative calm in U.S.-China relations under the second Trump administration—reflected in a 2025 National Security Strategy and 2026 National Defense Strategy that both downplay great power competition and omit Vietnam entirely—has diminished Southeast Asia’s strategic salience in the United States’ eyes.Periodic senior-level contacts, including two phone calls between Trump and To Lam and a visit by a Vietnamese delegation to Washington in February 2026, which resulted in $37.2 billion in agreements, have failed to inject the necessary momentum to jumpstart the bilateral partnership. Nor has this engagement translated into any tangible progress on critical minerals or waved away reliance on Chinese refining capabilities. With no further high-level meetings scheduled, deeper cooperation in this domain appears unlikely in the near term.ConclusionThe 2023 Comprehensive Strategic Partnership marked a high point in U.S.-Vietnam relations, built on shared concerns about Chinese assertiveness in the South China Sea, supply chain security, and the political will of leaders on both sides. Yet the semiconductor and critical minerals cooperation at its core has seen little follow-through, as tariff disputes and wider uncertainty regarding the U.S. commitment to Asia have consumed the bandwidth of policymakers in both capitals.Looking ahead, warming Sino-Vietnamese ties and the United States’ reduced emphasis on great power rivalry suggest that the case for U.S.-Vietnam critical minerals cooperation will rest on economic and commercial logic rather than strategic competition. Even where interests converge, overcoming China’s commanding lead in refining capacity remains a formidable challenge. Whether the Comprehensive Strategic Partnership fulfills its early promise will depend on whether Washington and Hanoi can move past current frictions and convert shared strategic interests into concrete, resilient cooperation.Hunter Marston is an adjunct fellow (non-resident) in the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. and the director of the Southeast Asia Program at the Lowy Institute in Sydney.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSoutheast Asia\nImage\nHunter Marston\nAdjunct Fellow (Non-resident), Southeast Asia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nSoutheast Asia Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"错失的机遇：关键矿产与美越全面战略伙伴关系\n照片：NHAC NGUYEN/法新社/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nHunter Marston\n发表于2026年8月26日\n引言\n2023年9月，乔·拜登总统前往河内，会见已故总书记阮富仲，纪念美越全面伙伴关系建立10周年。两位领导人令观察人士感到意外，他们完全跳过了中间的“战略伙伴关系”层级，直接将两国关系提升为全面战略伙伴关系，这是河内外交层级中的最高级别。\n\n公告的核心是一项新的半导体伙伴关系，根据该伙伴关系，华盛顿承诺提供200万美元的种子资金，帮助建立具有韧性的芯片供应链并扩大越南制造能力。联合声明还强调，美国企业安靠科技将进一步投资16亿美元，在北宁省建设一家工厂，并在胡志明市建设两个设计中心。\n\n自那以后，进展陷入停滞。在与越南当局合作更精确地测量该国矿产储量后，美国地质调查局（USGS）在2025年报告中将越南稀土元素储量估计从2024年约2200万吨（当时为全球第二大）下调至350万吨。2025年10月，两国达成框架性贸易协议，将美国对越南商品征收的关税从46%降至20%，但协议未包含任何有关关键矿产的条款，河内并强调这仍只是一项框架协议。\n\n尽管存在这种模式，2023年美越关键矿产承诺的后续落实仍极为有限。2024年访问联合国大会期间，总书记苏林会见了硅谷领导人，但此后几乎没有取得具体进展，而且越南如何融入特朗普政府更广泛的印太战略仍不明确。河内因政府单方面加征关税而受到震动，更倾向于务实接触而非对抗，并继续寻求一切可能的合作。例如，越南选择加入特朗普总统倡议的“和平委员会”，苏林于2月在华盛顿出席了该倡议的首次会议。\n\n关键矿产的战略意义\n关键矿产是先进制造业的基础，包括笔记本电脑、太阳能电池板、电动汽车（EV）和战斗机，因此成为各方激烈争夺的战略资产。中国约占全球稀土采矿产能的70%和精炼产能的90%，使北京对全球供应链拥有重大影响力。\n\n北京一再将这种影响力用作政治施压手段：2023年，为回应美国的芯片出口管制，中国限制镓和锗的出口；2025年，在特朗普总统实施“解放日”关税后，中国又限制七种稀土的出口。这些举措推动受影响国家进行囤积和多元化布局。2026年2月，美国主办了一场有54个国家参加的关键矿产部长级会议——但越南明显未参加——该会议以“硅和平”框架和由14个成员组成的矿产安全伙伴关系为基础。\n\n随着清洁能源转型加速，预计到2040年全球对关键矿产的需求将增长近六倍。美国已通过《通胀削减法案》和《芯片与科学法案》等立法作出回应，并已将\n将关键矿产条款纳入近期与马来西亚、泰国、印度尼西亚、巴基斯坦、沙特阿拉伯和乌克兰达成的贸易协定，其中几项协定赋予美国对原矿出口的优先购买权。\n\n美国—越南关键矿产协调\n\n历史上有三个因素推动了美国与越南的战略协调。第一，中国在2014年至2019年间多次侵入越南水域，促使越南加强与美国的安全合作，建立起的信任也扩展到其他领域。第二，两国都希望降低供应链对中国的风险敞口，这推动越南投资于半导体、电动汽车和太阳能制造业，并为2023年半导体谅解备忘录奠定基础。第三，不断深化的贸易关系使华盛顿成为河内实现2030年迈入中高收入国家、2045年迈入发达经济体目标不可或缺的伙伴。美国购买了越南近30%的出口，而越南共产党的发展抱负在很大程度上依赖于关键矿产相关产业。河内已投入大量资源，扩大半导体组装、测试和封装能力。\n\n2023年美国作出的投资承诺是有意义的第一步，但美国还可以做得更多，例如利用《芯片法案》的立法权限，以及美国国际开发金融公司和美国进出口银行的融资，帮助越南建设下游稀土分离和精炼基础设施。将河内纳入矿产安全伙伴关系和“硅和平”等小多边框架，将进一步把越南嵌入一个能够制衡中国主导地位的联盟。\n\n越南作为关键矿产供应国的优势与劣势\n\n越南仍保有真正的战略资产：有利的地理位置和使其对“中国+1”投资具有吸引力的劳动力成本、相对的政治稳定性，以及不断增长的半导体产业。但其在稀土领域的地位已大幅削弱。美国地质调查局2025年的修订依据越南政府重新分类已探明储量后的最新数据，将越南在全球稀土储量排名中的位置从仅次于中国的第二位下调至第六位，落后于巴西、印度、澳大利亚和俄罗斯。越南剩余的优势在其他领域：其拥有约占全球18%的铝土矿储量，铝就是从这种原矿中提取的；同时它还是全球第二大钨生产国，而钨是国防制造业的核心矿产。越南还拥有规模可观的镧和钕矿藏，这些矿产用于电动汽车磁体。\n\n越南2023年的计划目标是到2030年每年加工超过200万吨稀土，但实际开采量在2023年仅为600吨，2024年仅为300吨。腐败案件的起诉——包括2023年逮捕泰阳集团和越南稀土股份公司高管，以及2025年判处一名前副部长三年监禁——冻结了相关业务，也打击了外国投资，尤其是对前景看好的东包矿的投资。作为回应，越南更新了法律框架，于2024年通过新的《地质与矿产法》，并于2025年通过限制稀土矿石和精炼产品出口的法律，以鼓励在国内加工，而不是运往中国。\n\n扩大开采还引发环境和劳工方面的担忧。过去，越南中部高原的铝土矿开采因环境担忧而持续遭到公民社会的反对，\n政府缺乏透明度，以及这家中国矿业公司使用来自中国而非越南的劳工。印度尼西亚的案例提供了一个警示。2014年，雅加达通过立法限制出口镍原矿，要求国际企业在印尼境内发展本土精炼能力。尽管此举被广泛誉为雅加达的成功，但它几乎未能惠及印尼自身的劳动力，反而加深了该国对中国技术出口镍的依赖。此外，它还加剧了环境破坏，以及当地社区与矿业公司之间的社会紧张关系。\n\n美越关系的未来走向\n\n四项趋势使深化合作变得复杂：\n\n美国关税以及对转运的审查，已扰乱双边贸易关系。2025年10月的框架协议维持了20%的关税，不过在2026年2月最高法院作出裁决后，这些关税后来被降至10%，随后又在根据第301条款对不公平贸易做法展开调查后调整为12.5%。该框架协议还包括越南购买波音飞机和投资承诺，但未处理关键矿产和转运问题。\n\n越南等较小国家面临着越来越大的压力，须在美中技术竞争中与某一大国保持一致；与此同时，特朗普政府更具交易性、更少以价值观为导向的外交政策，减轻了越南对美国推动政权施压的一些传统焦虑。\n\n越中关系显著升温：可以看看习近平2023年和2025年对河内的访问，以及苏林2024年赴北京之行——这是他成为总书记后的首次出访。两国签署了数十项新的经济协议，并有意将南海海洋争端与更广泛的战略稳定分隔处理。\n\n第四，第二届特朗普政府时期美中关系相对平静——这一点体现在2025年《国家安全战略》和2026年《国防战略》中，两者都淡化了大国竞争，且完全未提及越南——已降低了东南亚在美国眼中的战略重要性。\n\n包括特朗普与苏林两次通话，以及越南代表团于2026年2月访问华盛顿在内的定期高层接触，虽促成了372亿美元的协议，却未能注入启动双边伙伴关系所需的动力。这类接触也没有转化为关键矿产领域的任何实质性进展，或消除对中国精炼能力的依赖。由于没有安排进一步的高层会晤，该领域更深入的合作在短期内似乎不太可能。\n\n结论\n\n2023年全面战略伙伴关系标志着美越关系的高点，其基础是双方对中国在南海日益强势、供应链安全的共同关切，以及双方领导人的政治意愿。然而，作为其核心的半导体和关键矿产合作几乎没有后续行动，因为关税争端以及围绕美国对亚洲承诺的更广泛不确定性，已耗尽两国首都决策者的精力。\n\n展望未来，中越关系升温以及美国降低对大国竞争的重视表明，美越关键矿产合作的理由将建立在经济和商业\n逻辑，而非战略竞争。即使在利益趋同之处，要克服中国在精炼产能方面的主导性领先优势，仍是一项艰巨挑战。全面战略伙伴关系能否兑现其早期承诺，将取决于华盛顿和河内能否跨越当前摩擦，将共同的战略利益转化为具体且有韧性的合作。\n\n亨特·马斯顿是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心东南亚项目的兼职研究员（非驻地），也是悉尼洛伊研究所东南亚项目主任。\n\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作；该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策议题的私人免税机构。其研究不偏袒任何党派，也不具专属性。CSIS不持有具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应被理解为仅代表作者本人（或作者们）。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n东南亚\n图片\n亨特·马斯顿\n兼职研究员（非驻地），东南亚项目\n项目与计划\n东南亚项目\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-08-26T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2566","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"现代化卫星频谱规则是美国太空领导力的关键","title_en":"Modernizing Satellite Spectrum Rules Is Key to U.S. Space Leadership","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表评论文章，以19世纪电报与电话之争为类比，讨论美国联邦通信委员会正在审议的等效功率通量密度规则。现行规则制定于GEO卫星主导商业市场的时代，将保护GEO系统的负担加在NGSO星座上。作者认为，随着低轨星座在商业、民用和军事领域作用日益关键，修改规则可让低轨星座更高效运营、服务更多用户并改善太空安全，同时有助于美国企业与中国低轨星座在全球市场竞争。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary uses the nineteenth-century telegraph-versus-telephone contest as an analogy for the FCC's ongoing proceeding on Equivalent Power Flux Density rules governing spectrum sharing between NGSO and GEO satellite systems. The current rules, adopted when GEO satellites dominated the commercial sector, place the burden on NGSO systems to protect GEO operators. The authors argue that revising the rules would let LEO constellations operate more efficiently, serve more customers, improve space safety, and help U.S. operators compete globally against Chinese LEO constellations.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:28:31.421530+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/modernizing-satellite-spectrum-rules-key-us-space-leadership","content":"Modernizing Satellite Spectrum Rules Is Key to U.S. Space Leadership\nPhoto: NicoElNino/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nMatt Pearl\nand\nClayton Swope\nPublished July 30, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nIn 1843, the U.S. government provided Samuel Morse a $30,000 grant to build a demonstration telegraph line between Washington and Baltimore. Within 50 years, the main U.S. telegraph company, Western Union, became a business powerhouse, with profit margins close to 40 percent. Its decline, however, was foreshadowed by Alexander Graham Bell’s invention in 1876 of the telephone—an innovation that left an even more profound impact on telecommunications than Morse’s telegraph. For a time, telegraphy offered competitive advantages, such as providing a written record and easier long-distance connections over the telephone, but those advantages gradually disappeared as new technologies were introduced by the telephone industry. Instead of viewing the rapid adoption of the telephone as an opportunity, the U.S. government saw it as a threat to the status quo and took steps to protect the telegraph industry. However, despite decades of U.S. government policy interventions to hold back the tide of innovation and allow a stagnant business model to take precedence over a revolutionary new one, the U.S. telegraph went the way of the dodo bird when Western Union sold off its telegraph business in 1988.A similar struggle—between the status quo and innovation—is again playing out, which will shape how we communicate for decades and influence the success of U.S. companies in their competition with Chinese peers on the global stage. In recent years, low Earth orbit (LEO) satellite constellations operating in non-geostationary orbit (NGSO) have emerged as the modern equivalent of the telephone—an innovative commercial communications technology that has profound strategic implications. While these constellations were deployed for commercial purposes, they have proven equally useful for civilian and military applications. The same satellites that provide broadband to households, particularly in exurban and rural areas, have been critical to enabling Ukraine to defend itself against Russia’s invasion. Moreover, just as was the case with the telegraph and telephone, the policy choices that the United States government makes in the coming years will play a critical role in determining whether it continues to lead in a technology that will help to determine the course of great power competition.Most immediately, the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) is in the process of deciding whether to change rules regarding Equivalent Power Flux Density that govern spectrum sharing between newer NGSO satellites—which do not stay stationary relative to the earth—and traditional geostationary orbit (GEO) satellite systems—which stay in a stationary position relative to earth because they operate at a high orbit of approximately 35,786 kilometers. While it’s easy to ignore an obscure technical debate at the FCC, what the commission decides in this proceeding will have significant implications for the United States’ economic and national security.The current sharing rules were adopted at the turn of the twenty-first century, when GEO satellites dominated the commercial satellite sector, and the resulting rules put the burden on NGSO systems to protect GEO systems. While these rules were adopted when the hit social app was MySpace and the greatest threat to humanity was the Y2K bug, GEO operators have argued in the past that we shouldn’t even study changing them. In recent decades, the satellite industry has evolved, with LEO constellations playing an increasingly critical role. Further, satellite technology has also matured, with advances such as adaptive coding and modulation making GEO satellites more robust to interference. All these developments have undermined the rationale for the current rules—the rules can be changed without any impact on GEO operators.The benefit of changing the rules is enormous. Revising the rules will allow LEO constellations to operate more efficiently—more customers could be served by the same number of satellites. This means that constellations already in orbit will be able to serve a greater number of customers across the United States and, if similar revisions are adopted elsewhere, around the world. More efficient operation also improves space safety because the number of satellites needed to over a certain level of service decreases and, thus, reduces the number of objects in orbit that could one day pose debris generation risks. Like other nations and regions, such as Europe, that look to enact space regulation to improve space safety, they should consider how revising spectrum rules can play a key role in such initiatives.The FCC’s decision in this proceeding will help to decide the future of the United States’ LEO constellations. While the United States has a good start, with several satellite operators launching massive constellations, the People’s Republic of China is currently in the process of deploying competing constellations. If the FCC decides to adjust the rules to put NGSO constellations on more equal footing with GEO constellations, this will significantly bolster LEO operators’ position in the United States market, enabling them to serve more areas with faster and more reliable satellite service than they do today. The additional revenue that LEO operators would make in this market could help to fund their global ambitions, enabling them to compete with PRC LEO operators in other markets. Further, it would help to level the playing field between incumbents and new entrants, enabling LEO operators with innovative technologies to compete with incumbents based on merit, instead of being held back by outdated technical rules.Matt Pearl is the director of the Strategic Technologies Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Clayton Swope is the deputy director of the Aerospace Security Project and a senior fellow in the Defense and Security Department\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSpace and Aerospace,\nEconomic Security, and\nTechnology\nImage\nMatt Pearl\nDirector, Strategic Technologies Program\nImage\nClayton Swope\nDeputy Director, Aerospace Security Project and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nAerospace Security Project\nDefense and Security\nEconomic Security and Technology\nStrategic Technologies Program","content_cn":"现代化卫星频谱规则是美国太空领导力的关键\n图片：NicoElNino/Adobe Stock\n评论文章\n作者\nMatt Pearl\n和\nClayton Swope\n发表于2025年7月30日\n远程可视化\n1843年，美国政府向塞缪尔·莫尔斯提供了3万美元拨款，用于建设一条连接华盛顿和巴尔的摩的示范性电报线路。不到50年，美国主要的电报公司西联汇款（Western Union）便成为商业巨头，利润率接近40%。然而，亚历山大·格雷厄姆·贝尔于1876年发明电话，预示了它的衰落——这项创新对电信业产生的影响甚至比莫尔斯的电报更为深远。有一段时间，电报业凭借提供书面记录以及比电话更便捷的长途连接等优势保持竞争力，但随着电话行业引入新技术，这些优势逐渐消失。美国政府没有把电话的快速普及视为机遇，反而将其视为对现状的威胁，并采取措施保护电报业。然而，尽管美国政府数十年来通过政策干预阻挡创新浪潮，让停滞的商业模式优先于革命性的新模式，美国电报业最终还是像渡渡鸟一样走向灭亡；1988年，西联汇款出售了其电报业务。类似的斗争——现状与创新之间的斗争——如今再次上演，它将塑造我们未来几十年的通信方式，并影响美国企业在全球舞台上与中国同行竞争的成败。近年来，在非地球静止轨道（NGSO）运行的低地球轨道（LEO）卫星星座，已经成为现代版的电话——一种具有深远战略影响的创新型商业通信技术。虽然这些星座最初是出于商业目的部署的，但事实证明，它们同样适用于民用和军事用途。为家庭提供宽带服务的同一批卫星，尤其是在城市远郊和农村地区，已成为帮助乌克兰抵御俄罗斯入侵的关键。此外，正如电报与电话时代的情形一样，美国政府未来几年的政策选择，将在决定美国能否继续引领这项有助于决定大国竞争走向的技术方面发挥关键作用。眼下，美国联邦通信委员会（FCC）正在决定是否修改有关等效功率通量密度的规则；这些规则管理新型NGSO卫星——它们相对于地球并不保持静止——与传统地球静止轨道（GEO）卫星系统之间的频谱共享；后者由于运行在约35,786公里的高轨道上，因此相对于地球保持固定位置。虽然人们很容易忽视FCC内部一场晦涩的技术争论，但委员会在这一程序中作出的决定，将对美国的经济安全和国家安全产生重大影响。现行共享规则是在21世纪初采纳的，当时GEO卫星主导商业卫星领域，由此形成的规则要求NGSO系统承担保护GEO系统的责任。尽管这些规则是在热门社交应用还是MySpace、人类面临的最大威胁还是千年虫（Y2K）之时制定的，GEO\n过去，运营商曾主张我们甚至不应研究如何改变这些规则。近几十年来，卫星产业不断发展，低地球轨道（LEO）星座发挥着日益关键的作用。此外，卫星技术也日趋成熟，自适应编码和调制等进步使地球同步轨道（GEO）卫星能够更好地抵御干扰。所有这些发展都削弱了现行规则的合理性——改变规则不会对GEO运营商产生任何影响。\n\n改变规则的益处是巨大的。修订规则将使LEO星座能够更高效地运行——相同数量的卫星可以服务更多客户。这意味着，已经在轨的星座将能够服务美国各地更多客户；如果其他地区也采取类似修订，还能服务世界各地更多客户。更高效的运行还有助于提升空间安全，因为达到一定服务水平所需的卫星数量会减少，从而降低轨道上可能在未来带来碎片产生风险的物体数量。与欧洲等其他寻求制定空间监管规则以改善空间安全的国家和地区一样，他们应考虑修订频谱规则如何在此类举措中发挥关键作用。\n\n美国联邦通信委员会（FCC）在本次程序中的决定将有助于决定美国LEO星座的未来。尽管美国已经有了一个良好的开端，已有数家卫星运营商正在发射大规模星座，但中华人民共和国目前正部署具有竞争力的星座。如果FCC决定调整规则，使非地球同步轨道（NGSO）星座与GEO星座处于更加平等的地位，这将显著巩固LEO运营商在美国市场的地位，使其能够比现在覆盖更多地区，并提供更快、更可靠的卫星服务。LEO运营商在这一市场获得的额外收入可以帮助其为全球布局提供资金，使其能够在其他市场与中华人民共和国的LEO运营商竞争。此外，这将有助于为现有企业与新进入者创造公平的竞争环境，使采用创新技术的LEO运营商能够凭借实力与现有企业竞争，而不是因过时的技术规则而受到限制。\n\n马特·珀尔（Matt Pearl）是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）战略技术项目主任。克莱顿·斯沃普（Clayton Swope）是航空航天安全项目副主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员。\n\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应被理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n空间与航空航天、\n经济安全与技术\n\n图片\n马特·珀尔\n战略技术项目主任\n\n图片\n克莱顿·斯沃普\n航空航天安全项目副主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员\n\n项目与计划\n航空航天安全项目\n国防与安全\n经济安全与技术\n战略技术项目","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-07-30T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-847","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"下一场加勒比危机？评估美国对古巴的军事选项","title_en":"The Next Caribbean Crisis? Assessing U.S. Military Options Toward Cuba","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）评论文章评估美国对古巴可能采取的军事选项。文章以美国抓捕马杜罗后的加勒比紧张局势为背景，分析五种情景：持续施压、古巴崩溃、领导层斩首、有限空袭及最不可能的全面入侵，并讨论各自可能性与风险。作者指出，美国缺乏连贯的“变革理论”，误判或突发事件可能引发双方都不愿看到的冲突升级。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary assesses possible U.S. military options toward Cuba amid rising Caribbean tensions after the capture of Maduro. It examines five scenarios—continued pressure campaign, Cuban collapse, leadership decapitation, limited air offensive, and the least likely full invasion—along with their likelihoods and risks. The authors argue the United States lacks a coherent theory of change, and that miscalculation or an incident could spark escalation neither side truly wants.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:18:45.824397+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/next-caribbean-crisis-assessing-us-military-options-toward-cuba","content":"The Next Caribbean Crisis? Assessing U.S. Military Options Toward Cuba\nPhoto: HUGE PERALTA/AFP/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nChristopher Hernandez-Roy,\nMark F. Cancian,\nand\nHenry Ziemer\nPublished June 3, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nFive months have passed since U.S. forces stormed into Caracas to capture Venezuelan dictator Nicolás Maduro, and tensions are again rising in the Caribbean—this time with Cuba. Almost immediately after the Maduro operation, President Trump halted Venezuelan oil deliveries to Cuba, telling the island’s leaders to “make a deal” or face the consequences. Mexico, which since 2023 has been a key supplier of fuel to the island nation, also shut off its deliveries under U.S. pressure, leaving Cuba in dire straits and strangling its tourism industry.Against this backdrop, the United States has engaged in touch-and-go negotiations with various figures within the Cuban communist regime, while at the same time issuing an executive order to further increase economic pressure on the island. Statements from U.S. officials have not offered much strategic clarity, oscillating between maximalist comments by President Trump, who mused that he “will have the honor of taking Cuba,” to more conciliatory moves by the State Department, which announced it would be prepared to authorize a $100 million aid package in exchange for “meaningful reforms.”Approaching an Endgame?More recently, the United States has been settling into a pattern similar to that which presaged Operation Absolute Resolve against Maduro. The recent unsealing of an indictment against Raúl Castro and other regime figures over the 1996 shootdown of U.S. civilian planes creates a rationale for characterizing his capture as a law enforcement operation. A similar justification was used by the Department of Justice to argue that Maduro’s capture on drug and arms trafficking charges represented an extraterritorial application of domestic law, not a foreign military intervention.Along with rhetorical measures, the United States has stepped up military surveillance overflights of Cuba, gathering intelligence on the capabilities and dispositions of the country’s Revolutionary Armed Forces (FAR). In late May, CIA Director John Ratcliffe met with Cuban leaders, bringing along a paramilitary officer who was involved in capturing Maduro and killing the Cuban military personnel who were guarding the Venezuelan leader. It was a sign of what could come if negotiations are unsuccessful. Although the crisis in the Persian Gulf continues to smolder, threatening to reignite at any moment, the United States has enough military assets to operate simultaneously in the Western Hemisphere. The USS Nimitz carrier strike group entered the Caribbean in May, and the 1,300 Marines of the 24th Marine Expeditionary Unit (MEU) will replace the 22d MEU, which has been in the Caribbean since January.\nImage\nChristopher Hernandez-Roy\nActing Director and Senior Fellow, Americas Program\nImage\nMark F. Cancian\nSenior Adviser, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nHenry Ziemer\nFellow, Americas Program\nPrograms & Projects\nAmericas Program\nDefense and Security\nDemocratic Resilience: Democratic Backsliding and Authoritarian Regimes in Venezuela, Nicaragua, and Cuba\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nCivil-Military Relations and Defense Cooperation\nRemote Visualization\nWhile the ultimate objective of U.S. Cuba policy remains unclear, the possibility of military action is present. Therefore, it is worthwhile to examine a range of possible scenarios involving the use of force against Cuba. The following sections examine five such scenarios, their relative likelihoods, and the risks involved.Scenario 1: Pressure CampaignThe first scenario involves the continuation of the U.S. policy toward Cuba adopted in January 2026, which uses restrictions on oil imports as leverage to induce the regime to accept major changes. This is fundamentally an attritional strategy for the United States, relying on pressure from Cuba’s deteriorating economic position over time.Militarily, this would be easy to execute. Cuba has essentially no navy and its few friends—Russia, Iran, and China—are not in a position to intervene. Tankers, the likely targets of a blockade, are easily tracked and intercepted. Military forces may not even be required if the United States convinces other countries not to send oil. There would likely be some low-level blockade running, but quantities would not be significant enough to affect the outcome. Blockades don’t need to stop every ship to be effective. The political challenge to a blockade is twofold: The first challenge occurs if a nation or NGO decides to run the blockade, forcing a public confrontation. The second challenge is that the suffering in Cuba makes the United States look like a bully. Both of these dynamics are already at play to an extent. In particular, Russia has proven willing, to some degree, to challenge the de facto U.S. blockade, delivering an oil shipment to Cuba in March, a move that likely reflected U.S. desire not to enter into a showdown with the war in Iran still raging, as well as a calculated strategy to keep some fuel headed to the island to stave off total collapse. However, recent reports that a second shipment of Cuba-bound Russian oil has now turned back could suggest that the economic pressure campaign may be ramping up.Scenario 2: Cuba CollapsesThe second scenario involves an internal collapse in Cuba that results in a humanitarian crisis and power vacuum, prompting the United States to intervene to restore order. The Cuban regime has persevered through periods of immense deprivation, and even today seems firmly entrenched in the face of U.S. pressure. In addition, recent protests have largely been spontaneous responses to economic deprivation, lacking a cohesive political program capable of challenging the regime’s dominance. Nevertheless, a sudden deterioration cannot be ruled out. This scenario would likely be difficult to predict, involving a rapid and simultaneous unraveling of various parts of the Cuban state, as well as mass protests and civil unrest.General Colin Powell once described these situations as embodying the Pottery Barn rule: “You break it; you own it.” If there were a collapse but without disorder, U.S. military intervention might be limited to a few ports and airfields to facilitate the flow of humanitarian supplies and ensure their proper distribution. A complete breakdown of law and order and a decision to impose stability by occupying the island would require large ground forces. A rule of thumb from the stability operations literature is that one member of the security forces is needed for every 50 citizens if there is no active insurgency. If half of those forces come from indigenous organizations like the police, then half would come from the United States and any coalition that the United States might put together. With a Cuban population of 10 million, that implies an external force of 100,000. The Trump administration is unlikely to pursue such an option, while a regional coalition would be equally reticent to step up. In nearby Haiti, even with U.S. prodding and financing, the international community was unable to deploy 2,500 troops for the now-defunct Multinational Security Support Mission, and it has more recently committed 5,500 troops to a UN-backed Gang Suppression Force, a fraction of what would be needed in Cuba.Scenario 3: Leadership DecapitationThe third scenario most closely resembles Operation Absolute Resolve, with a special forces operation or a kinetic strike aimed at capturing or killing senior Cuban leadership. Potential targets would include the recently indicted Raúl Castro, President Díaz-Canel, or both, as a means of signaling U.S. resolve and capabilities to the remaining Cuban leadership.Leadership decapitation is hard to do despite recent operations that make it look easy. The U.S. seizure of Maduro required extensive preparations, a weak and unprepared adversary, and inside information. U.S. and Israeli decapitation of the Iranian government required exquisite intelligence, including with sources on the ground. Putting boots on the ground for a seizure operation is extremely risky because of the possibility of casualties and capture. A kinetic strike would, therefore, be more likely. It still requires superb intelligence to know where the targets will be at a particular moment and to avoid collateral damage. The United States does have the military assets in theater to execute such a strike.The first U.S. action in any kinetic operation would likely be the reinforcement of the U.S. base at Guantanamo Bay, which is currently essentially undefended. In the 1960s, the base was heavily defended with mines and infantry, but over the decades, those defenses have dissipated as the threat of Cuban attack has diminished.If a decapitation attack were contemplated, it should be borne in mind that Cuba is not Venezuela. The Cuban Communist Party is far better institutionalized than Venezuela’s kleptocratic regime. While internal divisions are undoubtedly present, the edifice is unlikely to collapse with the removal of a single high-level figure. Furthermore, Cuba lacks an organized domestic opposition capable of putting pressure on the regime from within.Even if a decapitation strike were successful, there is the problem of what happens next. Like Iran, the Cuban government has been fortifying itself for decades against possible kinetic strikes from the United States. As occurred in Iran, the most likely immediate outcome would not be quick regime collapse but instead a hardline response led by the Communist Party, the Revolutionary Armed Forces, intelligence services, and the military-economic mega-conglomerate Grupo de Administración Empresarial S.A., or GAESA.Scenario 4: Limited Air OffensiveIn this scenario, the United States would carry out airstrikes against a number of military and intelligence targets in Cuba to pressure the regime into accepting major changes and to degrade Cuba’s ability to oppose future U.S. military action.With the Nimitz in the Caribbean, the United States has the capability to launch several days of punitive strikes. While the size of the military force in the Caribbean remains well below the heights it reached in December 2025 and early January 2026, Cuba is close enough to several air bases within the continental United States that could supplement naval aviation. Airstrikes would likely focus on drone and intelligence facilities, consistent with U.S. concerns about Cuban behavior. The U.S. military would likely also strike Cuban air defenses, especially in a longer campaign. Cuban air defenses are weak, having been neglected for decades, but reducing them to impotence would allow the United States to fly over Cuban territory and use more plentiful and less expensive munitions rather than scarce and expensive long-range missiles. This dynamic has been present in the Iran war, where the coalition eliminated the remnants of Iranian air defenses in the first four or five days and was then able to overfly Iran with near impunity.The political problem remains the same as in the previous scenario: What if the Cuban government continues to resist? Assuming no large-scale nationwide uprising that threatens the regime (there are further parallels to Iran here), airstrikes alone are unlikely to topple the regime.Scenario 5: Runaway EscalationThe final scenario involves an unforeseen event—such as an accident where the cause was not immediately apparent or an attack, authorized or not, on U.S. forces in the Caribbean by Cuba—that would lead to U.S. military retaliation. Like the second scenario, it would be difficult to anticipate this prior to the triggering incident.A risk inherent to the U.S. strategy of saber-rattling rhetoric and increased military deployments around Cuba is the potential for miscalculation on either side and the outbreak of hostilities that neither party truly wants. In October 1962, U.S. pilot Rudolf Anderson was killed when his U-2 surveillance plane was shot down conducting reconnaissance over Cuba. At the time, diplomacy between Washington, Havana, and Moscow prevailed, but the incident could have triggered a darker trajectory, driven by tensions that were running white-hot. Sixty-four years earlier, the USS Maine had been sent to Havana to protect U.S. interests as the Cuban independence movement from Spain gained strength. The ship’s explosion, the cause of which is still debated today, led directly to the Spanish-American War. An incident between U.S. and Cuban forces, especially if there were U.S. casualties, might spark escalation. The United States might respond with leadership decapitation or limited air strikes, as described above.The least likely outcome is a U.S. invasion. Cuba is unable to impose costs upon the United States militarily and economically in the same way as Iran. The Cuban armed forces are weak, their equipment is woefully out of date, and their long-range strike capabilities are severely limited. While reports that the country has acquired hundreds of Russian military drones suggest that Cuba may be seeking to acquire an asymmetric capability against the United States, the 300 units allegedly received are orders of magnitude less than what would be needed to credibly close the Straits of Florida or threaten U.S. critical infrastructure on the mainland; Guantanamo, however, could be vulnerable. The FAR maintains around 50,000 active soldiers, about 39,000 reservists, and nearly 90,000 paramilitary personnel, and could also threaten combat in urban areas and insurgency in rural areas, both of which would be politically difficult for the United States. As in the second scenario, achieving the overwhelming force favored by U.S. planners would likely require an invasion force of at least 100,000 personnel, with air and naval support. That would take months to put in place and be visible long before operations began.Theories of VictoryTo deny the United States a quick victory either on the battlefield or at the negotiating table, Cuba could seek to delay negotiations at least until the U.S. midterm elections, when a potential change in Congress could create more favorable conditions for the country. Cuba has long recognized that the vast disparity in military and economic might necessitates an asymmetric strategy to resist its northern neighbor, and it hopes that U.S. willingness to fight runs out before the regime collapses. Such a strategy is not without risks, though, chief among them the second scenario described above, involving a spontaneous collapse and subsequent humanitarian crisis.The United States has real strategic interests at play in Cuba: unwinding the island’s authoritarian partnerships that allow it to serve as an outpost for Chinese intelligence; preventing Cuba from supporting Russia’s war in Ukraine with mercenaries; opening the Cuban economy; and creating a political opening to offer a path to return for many Cubans who have fled the island for U.S. shores.However, the United States will struggle to achieve any of these objectives without a coherent theory of change, which has often been lacking in U.S. messaging around Cuba. In a worst-case scenario, the United States could find itself drawn into a military confrontation that could stretch U.S. forces and endanger the lives of both U.S. personnel and ordinary Cubans, with no clear path to democratization or economic recovery.Christopher Hernández-Roy is a senior fellow and acting director of the Americas Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS). Mark F. Cancian is a retired Marine colonel and senior adviser with the CSIS Defense and Security Department. Henry Ziemer is a fellow with the CSIS Americas Program.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAmericas,\nThe Caribbean, and\nDefense and Security\nRelated Content\nImage\nPreparing for the Consequences of Collapse in Cuba\nCritical Questions\nby\nCSIS Scholars\n— April 9, 2026","content_cn":"下一个加勒比危机？评估美国对古巴的军事选项\n照片：HUGE PERALTA/法新社/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nChristopher Hernandez-Roy、\nMark F. Cancian\n和\nHenry Ziemer\n发表于2026年6月3日\n远程可视化\n自美国军队突袭加拉加斯、抓获委内瑞拉独裁者尼古拉斯·马杜罗以来，五个月已经过去，加勒比地区的紧张局势再次升温——这一次的对象是古巴。马杜罗行动后几乎立即，特朗普总统叫停了向古巴输送委内瑞拉石油，并告诉这个岛国的领导人，要“达成协议”，否则就承担后果。自2023年以来一直是这个岛国关键燃料供应方的墨西哥，也在美国压力下停止供货，使古巴陷入困境，并扼杀了其旅游业。在此背景下，美国一直与古巴共产主义政权内部的各路人物进行时断时续的谈判，同时发布行政命令，进一步加大对该岛的经济压力。美国官员的声明并未提供多少战略清晰度，在特朗普总统的最大主义言论与国务院更为和解的举措之间摇摆不定。特朗普曾沉思说，他“将有幸接管古巴”；而国务院则宣布，准备以“有意义的改革”为交换，批准一项1亿美元的援助计划。\n\n接近终局？\n\n最近，美国逐渐形成了一种与马杜罗行动前夕相似的模式。近期，针对劳尔·卡斯特罗及其他政权人物因1996年击落美国民用飞机一事的起诉书被解封，这为将抓捕卡斯特罗描述为执法行动提供了理由。司法部曾以类似理由辩称，以贩毒和军火贩运罪名抓捕马杜罗，代表的是国内法的域外适用，而非外国军事干预。除了言辞上的措施，美国还加强了对古巴的军事侦察飞行，搜集该国革命武装力量（FAR）的能力和部署情报。5月下旬，中央情报局局长约翰·拉特克利夫会见了古巴领导人，并带去一名准军事人员；此人曾参与抓捕马杜罗，并杀死负责守卫这位委内瑞拉领导人的古巴军人。这是谈判失败时可能发生之事的信号。尽管波斯湾危机仍在持续闷烧，随时可能再次引燃，但美国拥有足够的军事资产，能够同时在西半球开展行动。尼米兹号航母打击群于5月进入加勒比海，拥有1300名海军陆战队员的第24海军陆战队远征部队（MEU）将接替自1月起一直驻扎加勒比海的第22海军陆战队远征部队。\n图片\nChristopher Hernandez-Roy\n美洲项目代理主任兼高级研究员\n图片\nMark F. Cancian\n国防与安全部高级顾问\n图片\nHenry Ziemer\n美洲项目研究员\n项目与计划\n美洲项目\n国防与安全\n民主韧性：委内瑞拉、尼加拉瓜和古巴的民主倒退与威权政权\n地缘政治与外交政策\n军民关系与防务合作\n远程可视化\n虽然美国对古巴政策的最终目标仍不明确，但军事行动的可能性依然存在。因此，值得\n审视一系列可能涉及对古巴使用武力的情景。以下各节将审视其中五种情景、它们的相对可能性以及所涉及的风险。\n\n情景1：施压行动\n\n第一种情景涉及延续美国于2026年1月对古巴采取的政策，即利用限制石油进口作为杠杆，促使该政权接受重大改变。这对美国而言从根本上是一种消耗战略，依靠古巴经济状况随着时间推移不断恶化所产生的压力。\n\n在军事上，这很容易执行。古巴基本上没有海军，而它为数不多的朋友——俄罗斯、伊朗和中国——也无力进行干预。油轮很可能是封锁的目标，容易被跟踪和拦截。如果美国说服其他国家不要运送石油，甚至可能不需要动用军事力量。可能会有一些低水平的突破封锁行动，但数量不会大到足以影响结果。封锁不需要拦截每一艘船才能奏效。\n\n封锁在政治上面临双重挑战：第一个挑战是某个国家或非政府组织决定突破封锁，从而迫使双方公开对峙。第二个挑战是，古巴的苦难会让美国看起来像个恶霸。这两种态势在一定程度上都已经出现。尤其是，俄罗斯已被证明在某种程度上愿意挑战美国的事实封锁，于3月向古巴运送了一批石油。这一行动可能反映出，在伊朗战争仍在激烈进行之际，美国不希望陷入对峙，同时也可能是经过计算的策略，即让一些燃料继续运往该岛，以避免其彻底崩溃。然而，近期有关第二批运往古巴的俄罗斯石油现已返航的报道，可能表明经济施压行动正在升级。\n\n情景2：古巴崩溃\n\n第二种情景涉及古巴内部崩溃，导致人道主义危机和权力真空，促使美国介入以恢复秩序。古巴政权历经极度匮乏时期仍然坚持下来，即使在今天，面对美国的压力，它似乎仍然根基稳固。此外，近期的抗议活动在很大程度上是对经济匮乏的自发回应，缺乏能够挑战该政权主导地位的统一政治纲领。尽管如此，也不能排除局势突然恶化的可能性。这种情景可能难以预测，因为它会涉及古巴国家各个部分迅速而同时地瓦解，以及大规模抗议和内乱。\n\n科林·鲍威尔将军曾把这类情况描述为体现了“陶器谷仓规则”：“你打碎了它；你就拥有它。”如果发生崩溃但没有出现混乱，美国的军事干预可能仅限于控制几个港口和机场，以便利人道主义物资流动并确保其得到适当分配。如果法律和秩序全面崩溃，并决定通过占领该岛来实施稳定，就需要大规模地面部队。稳定行动文献中的一条经验法则是，如果不存在活跃的叛乱，每50名公民就需要一名安全部队成员。如果这些部队中有一半来自警察等本土组织，那么另一半将由美国及其可能组建的任何联盟提供。古巴人口为1000万，这意味着一\n10万人的外部力量。特朗普政府不太可能采取这一选项，而地区联盟同样不愿挺身而出。在附近的海地，即使有美国的推动和资金支持，国际社会也未能为如今已经解散的多国安全支助任务部署2500名部队；最近，国际社会又为一个由联合国支持的帮派镇压部队承诺部署5500名部队，这只是古巴所需兵力的一小部分。\n\n情景3：斩首式领导层打击\n\n第三种情景与“绝对决心”行动最为相似，即通过特种部队行动或动能打击，试图抓捕或杀死古巴高级领导层。潜在目标包括最近被起诉的劳尔·卡斯特罗、迪亚斯-卡内尔总统，或两人同时作为向古巴其余领导层展示美国决心和能力的一种手段。\n\n尽管近期的行动让人觉得斩首式领导层打击很容易实施，但实际上这很难做到。美国抓捕马杜罗需要广泛的准备、一个虚弱且毫无准备的对手，以及内部情报。美国和以色列对伊朗政府实施斩首式打击，需要极其精确的情报，包括来自当地人员的情报。为抓捕行动派遣地面部队极其危险，因为存在人员伤亡和被俘的可能性。因此，动能打击更有可能发生。但这仍然需要卓越的情报，以确定目标在特定时刻的位置，并避免附带损害。美国确实拥有在该战区执行此类打击所需的军事资产。\n\n美国在任何动能行动中的第一步，可能是增援关塔那摩湾的美国基地，该基地目前基本上不设防。20世纪60年代，该基地以雷区和步兵进行重兵防守，但数十年来，随着古巴发动攻击的威胁减弱，这些防御逐渐瓦解。\n\n如果考虑实施斩首式打击，应牢记古巴不是委内瑞拉。古巴共产党比委内瑞拉的盗贼统治政权制度化程度高得多。虽然内部无疑存在分歧，但移除一名高层人物不太可能导致整个体系崩溃。此外，古巴缺乏有组织的国内反对派，无法从内部向政权施压。\n\n即便斩首式打击取得成功，也会面临接下来会发生什么的问题。与伊朗一样，古巴政府几十年来一直在加固自身，以抵御美国可能发动的动能打击。正如伊朗发生的情况一样，最可能的直接结果不会是政权迅速崩溃，而是由共产党、革命武装力量、情报机构以及军事经济巨型企业集团——企业管理集团股份公司（Grupo de Administración Empresarial S.A.，简称GAESA）——领导的强硬回应。\n\n情景4：有限空中攻势\n\n在这一情景下，美国将对古巴境内若干军事和情报目标实施空袭，迫使该政权接受重大改变，并削弱古巴反对美国未来军事行动的能力。\n\n随着“尼米兹”号位于加勒比海，美国具备发动持续数日惩罚性打击的能力。尽管加勒比海的军事力量规模仍远低于2025年12月和2026年1月初达到的高峰，但古巴距离美国本土的数个空军基地很近，这些基地可以补充\n海军航空兵。空袭可能会重点打击无人机和情报设施，这与美国对古巴行为的担忧相一致。美国军方也可能打击古巴的防空力量，尤其是在一场更长期的行动中。古巴防空力量薄弱，几十年来一直被忽视，但将其削弱到无力反击的程度，将使美国能够飞越古巴领土，使用数量更多、成本更低的弹药，而不是稀缺且昂贵的远程导弹。在伊朗战争中就出现过这种态势：联军在最初四五天内消灭了伊朗防空力量的残余，随后便能够近乎不受惩罚地飞越伊朗领空。\n\n政治问题仍与此前情景相同：如果古巴政府继续抵抗怎么办？假设没有威胁到政权的大规模全国性起义（这里也与伊朗存在进一步的相似之处），仅凭空袭不太可能推翻该政权。\n\n情景五：失控升级\n\n最后一种情景涉及一件无法预见的事件——例如一起起因未能立即查明的事故，或古巴对美国在加勒比地区的部队发动的一次袭击（无论是否经过授权）——这将导致美国进行军事报复。与第二种情景一样，在触发事件发生之前，很难预料到这种情况。\n\n美国对古巴采取咄咄逼人的言辞并增加周边军事部署，其内在风险在于，双方都可能出现误判，进而爆发任何一方真正都不希望发生的敌对行动。1962年10月，美国飞行员鲁道夫·安德森在驾驶U-2侦察机对古巴进行侦察时，飞机被击落身亡。当时，华盛顿、哈瓦那和莫斯科之间的外交努力占了上风，但这一事件本可能引发更黑暗的发展轨迹，因为当时的紧张局势已达到白热化程度。64年前，随着古巴脱离西班牙独立的运动日益壮大，美国缅因号战列舰被派往哈瓦那，以保护美国利益。该舰爆炸，其原因至今仍有争议，但这一事件直接导致了美西战争。美国与古巴部队之间的事件，尤其是在造成美国人员伤亡的情况下，可能引发升级。美国可能会采取斩首行动或有限空袭，正如上文所述。\n\n最不可能出现的结果是美国入侵。古巴无法像伊朗那样在军事和经济上对美国施加代价。古巴武装力量薄弱，其装备严重过时，远程打击能力也极为有限。尽管有报道称该国已获得数百架俄罗斯军用无人机，这表明古巴可能正寻求获取针对美国的不对称能力，但据称收到的300架无人机，比起要真正封锁佛罗里达海峡或威胁美国本土关键基础设施所需的数量，少了几个数量级；不过，关塔那摩可能较为脆弱。古巴革命武装力量约有5万名现役士兵、约3.9万名预备役人员和近9万名准军事人员，还可能在城市地区发动战斗、在农村地区发动 insurgency，这两种情况在政治上都会令美国难以应对。与第二种情景一样，要实现美国规划人员所偏好的压倒性武力，可能需要一支至少10万人的入侵部队，并配以空中和海上支援。那\n这将需要数月时间才能落实，并且在行动开始前很久就会显现出来。\n\n胜利理论\n\n为了不让美国在战场上或谈判桌上迅速取胜，古巴可以寻求将谈判至少拖延到美国中期选举之后；届时国会可能发生的变化或许会为该国创造更有利的条件。古巴长期以来一直认识到，军事和经济实力之间的巨大差距要求采取非对称战略来抵抗其北方邻国，并希望美国作战的意愿在该政权垮台之前耗尽。不过，这种战略并非没有风险，其中最主要的风险就是上述第二种情景，即自发性崩溃及随后的​​人道主义危机。\n\n美国在古巴有切实的战略利益：解除该岛的威权伙伴关系，使其无法继续充当中国情报机构的前哨；阻止古巴派遣雇佣兵支持俄罗斯在乌克兰的战争；开放古巴经济；以及创造政治空间，为许多逃离该岛、前往美国海岸的古巴人提供回归途径。\n\n然而，如果没有一个连贯的变革理论——而这在美国围绕古巴的信息传递中往往有所欠缺——美国将难以实现上述任何目标。在最坏情况下，美国可能会被卷入一场军事对抗，这场对抗可能使美国军队不堪重负，并危及美国人员和普通古巴人的生命，同时又没有明确的民主化或经济复苏路径。\n\nChristopher Hernández-Roy是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）美洲项目的高级研究员兼代理主任。Mark F. Cancian是退役海军陆战队上校，也是CSIS国防与安全部的高级顾问。Henry Ziemer是CSIS美洲项目的研究员。\n\n《评论》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私营、免税机构。其研究是无党派且非专有的。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n美洲、\n加勒比地区，以及\n国防与安全\n\n相关文章\n图片\n为古巴崩溃的后果做准备\n关键问题\n作者\nCSIS学者\n— 2026年4月9日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-06-03T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3484","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"NRO的故事：与克里斯·斯科莱斯博士的对话","title_en":"The NRO Story: A Conversation with Dr. Chris Scolese","summary_cn":"这是CSIS于2024年10月3日举办活动的文字记录，由国际安全项目主办。CSIS情报、国家安全与技术项目主任艾米莉·哈丁和航空航天安全项目主任卡莉·宾根与NRO主任克里斯·斯科莱斯博士对话，讨论NRO的使命、从胶片回收舱到现代卫星系统的技术演进、商业航天合作、劳动力招募以及NRO在情报界和国防部中的双重角色。斯科莱斯还谈及NRO在自然灾害等民用领域的支持作用及未来展望。","summary_en":"This is a transcript of a CSIS event hosted on October 3, 2024, by the International Security Program. Emily Harding, director of the Intelligence, National Security, and Technology Program, and Kari Bingen, director of the Aerospace Security Project, spoke with NRO Director Dr. Chris Scolese about the NRO's mission, its technological evolution from film-return capsules to modern satellite systems, commercial space partnerships, workforce recruitment, and its dual role serving the intelligence community and the Department of Defense. Scolese also discussed NRO's civil support in natural disasters and its future outlook.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:23:31.663921+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/nro-story-conversation-dr-chris-scolese","content":"The NRO Story: A Conversation with Dr. Chris Scolese\nPhoto: CSIS\nTranscript\n— October 3, 2024\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Transcript\n334kb\nImage\nPhoto: CSIS\nThe NRO Story: A Conversation with Dr. Chris Scolese\nOctober 3, 2024 • 1:00 – 2:00 pm EDT\nHosted by International Security Program\nThis transcript is from a CSIS event hosted on October 3, 2024. Watch the full video here.Emily Harding: OK, everybody. Thank you so much for being here today. I’m Emily Harding. I’m the director of the Intelligence, National Security, and Technology Program here at CSIS.And we are here today to talk about the power of presence in space. In the very recent past, a persistent presence in orbit was the sole domain of national governments. But over the last decade or so, we’ve seen a transformation and the start of a generational change. There is now a far wider range of nations sending stuff into orbit. Commercial providers have become a necessary part of nations’ national security picture.And today we’re talking about NRO, which is a real American national treasure. It has been there for the duration, but so quietly that most Americans had no idea they existed. Today, however, they’re starting to come out of the shadows in a way that we have not seen before.And we have with us today Dr. Chris Scolese, the director of NRO. He started his career as a naval officer working on nuclear propulsion. But from the sea, he turned to space and never went back. He had a long career at NASA, including becoming NASA’s chief engineer, acting administrator, and director of the Goddard Space Flight Center. And then, in 2019, he became the director of NRO, and he was the first Senate-confirmed person to hold that position. And that really was meant to communicate the seriousness with which the Senate took NRO’s mission, and also the oversight that they thought was required of this massive program that was so important to the country. I had the privilege of helping out with that nomination process, and it was – it was really interesting to see.Both Dr. Scolese and my colleague Kari Bingen, who will introduce herself here in just a second, are super big brains when it comes to space policy, so I have dubbed myself the generalist on this stage who can try to translate some of the really exquisite capabilities they’re going to be talking about for a more generalist audience.But first, NRO has brought us a video showing off what they do.(A video presentation is shown.)Kari A. Bingen: My name is Kari Bingen. I’m the director of the Aerospace Security Project here at CSIS. And it’s a real privilege to have you, Dr. Scolese.I am struck by that video. And just think about it for a moment. The National Reconnaissance Office is an organization that’s been around over 60 years, but for a lot of that time didn’t exist. The government didn’t acknowledge its existence until 1992. So to be able to sit here and see a video like that is pretty, pretty spectacular.And I also just want to emphasize, too, it is – it is incredibly unique to have a director of the National Reconnaissance Office here at CSIS talking publicly about the organization and about the mission. We’ve had a chance to talk at some trade association events and whatnot, but there’s a different audience here. And so we very much appreciate you coming out and talking to that broader audience and educating us.I was fortunate to meet Dr. Scolese back in the 2018-2019 timeframe was you were getting ready to go through your confirmation process that Emily mentioned. And as we were thinking about it in the Department of Defense, where I was at the time, we wanted to ensure that that first Senate-confirmed director was not political; was focused on technical soundness, sound management, and very apolitical in the job. Because when you think about acquiring and operating satellites, that’s just not a political thing. So we were so privileged that Dr. Scolese said yes, you know, I will sign up for this mission and continue to serve the nation. So we’ve very thankful that you did.We have about 30 minutes of Q&A that both Emily and myself will do with Dr. Scolese, and then we’ll open it up to audience questions both in person here and online. So if you’re online, go to the event website. There’s a button there to click to ask a question. We’ll get it here. And then also, if you’re in the audience, there’s a QR code here for the folks in person. So we do encourage your questions.With that, I’m going to turn it back over to Emily.Ms. Harding: So the floor is yours, sir. Tell us a little bit about NRO and the mission that you serve. If you had to explain to somebody who had never heard of NRO before what you do, how would you describe it?Dr. Chris Scolese: Certainly. Well, first, thanks for having me here. And you don’t have to call me Dr. Scolese; please call me Chris. I’d appreciate that.So the NRO, as has been mentioned, it’s been around for about 60 years, a little more than 60 years. For about half that time, we were a secret. And there was an important reason for us to be a secret at that time. But our fundamental mission hasn’t changed over that 60 years. Our job is to go off and collect from the vantage point of space what’s happening on the Earth and deliver that to the analyst, the policymaker, the warfighter, the first responder, whoever needs that information. And we have continued to do that over those 60 years.And over that time, we have consistently innovated. We have consistently kept up with the times. Oftentimes, we have led technology because countries and people want to prevent us from seeing or finding out what they’re doing, so we have to stay ahead that way. We have technology and we need to stay there so that we can make our systems affordable and capable. And at the same time, we’ve also taken advantage of using, as you said, Emily, the capabilities that already exist. If it exists, why recreate it? With a strong commercial industry, we’re taking, you know, a lot of advantage of that.And the other thing that’s really a difference in the last 30 years is the NRO used to have a contained workforce, if you will, and now we actually recruit and we bring in people. If you’re going to do that, you have to tell people you exist – (laughter) – or else they can’t find you.So we have adapted over time. We’ve driven innovation. We’ve driven technology. We’ve taken advantage of it. And you know, we’ve stayed ahead of the competition, if you will.Ms. Harding: So your role is one of both an intelligence agency and also a defense agency. And your point about workforce, if I’m right, once upon a time NRO pulled personnel directly from CIA and elements of the IC, and then also from the Defense Department. But now that you’re doing this open recruiting, you need a much broader swath of people. So can you talk a little bit about that dual role as an intelligence agency and also a defense agency?Dr. Scolese: Oh, absolutely. You know, the – if you’re operating in space, you can see the whole globe. Therefore, that information should be made available to anybody that needs that information, so the IC or the DOD. It’s efficient to have one organization go off and do that within, you know, certain boundaries, of course, that we need to go off and look at. And that’s the role that the NRO has played.And really, the difference has been how quickly do you need the data, and how much data do you need, and how varied is that data. You know, in the 1960s, when we were created, dropping buckets from the sky was fine. The world wasn’t changing that fast, or that need. There was one adversary at that time, the Soviet Union, that we had to deal with. As we progressed, we needed information faster and faster, and that’s true today. People would like information instantaneously, like they can – you know, like you can get with Twitter or X or whatever it is, right? And that’s our job, to deliver that information faster and to deliver the information that’s needed, which means different phenomenologies from what we had in the 1960s, which were mostly, you know, film capsules coming back with exquisite pictures of whatever it is that was – that was being looked at. Today, we have to look more broadly than that because there’s more complicated techniques.So our fundamental job has stayed the same. And it – and we serve well both the Department of Defense and the intelligence community, and to the extent that they need it the civil communities, for doing that. And I think it’s an efficient use of resources. It’s a vantage point that takes advantage of, you know, holistically, the whole Earth. And it allows us to distribute that information quickly, and it allows us to coordinate with all those groups to deliver the information that, ultimately, is needed.Ms. Harding: So before I turn it over to Kari to talk about some of the generational change, I remember the old TV show “24” and laughing when they were like, redirect the satellites from my cellphone. (Laughter.) It was like, if only we could – we could do that. But we’re getting closer and closer to that real-time look-in that you’re talking about.I’m going to ask you to explain, though, dropping buckets from the sky, because that’s a literal thing that used to happen. So what did that mean?Dr. Scolese: So in the 1960s it was very difficult to take pictures and beam them to Earth. I mean, today we have cellphones and, you know, anybody that was probably born after 1990 has no idea what film is. And the – so the most efficient way to get it was to put the camera in space; have the film with the camera; take the picture; roll up the film when you’re done taking all the pictures; put it in a – in a bucket, essentially a reentry vehicle; and drop it down to Earth with a parachute. And if you ever get a chance, go to YouTube and watch it. It’s CORONA capture of a film capsule coming down. An airplane would catch it in the air, and then it would go and get – the film would get processed and those imageries would be distributed. But I think there’s one other thing that we should mention. The first really capable NRO satellite that delivered was called GRAB. It was about 20 inches. And it was – so it would be considered a cubesat today. And that one did transmit information to the ground directly, but it wasn’t images; it was locating where radars are. Ms. Harding: Massive leap forward. Let’s talk about our leap forwards today.Ms. Bingen: Well, it’s interesting as you’re talking about CORONA, there was a fantastic history that the NRO historian has put out there on CORONA and other programs. One of our team here at CSIS, Christina Nordby, actually found an NRO cartoon series.Dr. Scolese: Yes.Ms. Bingen: Legit cartoon series about the history of the NRO. So I commend that reading to folks. But you go from the dropping film cannisters to building, you know, these very big, exquisite systems. Now, as the threat has evolved, the technology has evolved, you’ve talked about we are in a moment of generational change with our overhead architecture. Can you talk through what – explain, really, what that is. And you’ve been out there in the press talking about a proliferated satellite architecture that NRO is pursuing. Talk to us about what you’re doing there. Dr. Scolese: Sure. There’s a lot of things that are happening today. You know, the first thing I’ll say is, you know, today, as we speak, we have satellites ranging in size from cubesats to the large, exquisite satellites that you’re talking about. That allows us to deliver the information that is needed in the best possible way. We’re also engaging, you know, commercial entities so that they can fill in gaps and take over part of the mission so that we can focus on those things that are extremely difficult to go off and do. And the proliferated architecture is something that kind of addresses all of these things. We need to have persistence or fast revisit. Persistence you get by going into higher orbits, but that means you need much, much larger apertures in order to accomplish whatever the mission is, to go off and do that. Or you can proliferate your architecture, put more satellites up there so that a satellite is always coming over an area within a given reasonable amount of time that’s needed by the users. And that’s what we’re doing with a proliferated architecture. That’s enabled by a really rich commercial industry that’s building hundreds or thousands of satellites that allowed us to take those satellites, adapt them to our use at low cost, and apply whatever sensor is needed to go off and acquire the information that’s needed at whatever revisit time is required. So it gives us that capability, so it allows us to revisit, provide the information at a very high rate of speed and deliver it, you know, directly to the user. The other thing it allows us to do is it allows us protection of that architecture. We all know that there are countries – you know, Russia and China are examples – that have developed ASATs that can take out satellites. Well, if you have a hundred satellites up there, you have to have at least a hundred missiles in order to take them down, and that gets to be much, much more complicated in order to deal with that. So it gives us a degree of resilience that we didn’t have before. The other thing it gives us is an opportunity to refresh at a higher rate so that we’re constantly improving the technology as either the technology comes along or the threat changes or the mission changes to some extent. So that allows us to move at a much, much higher pace than we have in the past. Ms. Bingen: What’s interesting is a lot of us in the aerospace community, we tend to focus on satellites. But when you’re getting to a point where you’ve got hundreds up there, some of the manual processes that we use, making sure analysts are looking at every single image that comes through, like, that model for me just is – you can’t sustain that model. So can you also talk about the – apply that generational-change thinking to what are we doing on – I’ll say on the ground side, the tasking, the processing, the analysis with some of these tools like AI technologies will bring?Dr. Scolese: Yeah, absolutely. So it is changing. It has changed because, again, we can deliver information faster with the systems that we have if we can, you know, reduce that data very quickly into a – into a product that is needed. But you’re right, once you go to a proliferated architecture and you’re talking – you’re going from a few to – a few satellites to tens of satellites to now hundreds of satellites, you have to change a lot of things. And we’re in the process of doing that.So operating a proliferated architecture means that it’s no longer possible to go off and for an individual sitting at a control center to say I know what this satellite is doing. So we have to have the machines to go off and help us there.We need artificial intelligence, machine learning, automated processes to help us do that. Fortunately, Kepler helps us, you know, when it comes to that. But when it comes to tasking the satellites, asking them what to do, and figuring out which satellite is the best one to do it or which combination is, that’s where we’re taking advantage of, you know, advanced processing techniques, some of the techniques in artificial intelligence and machine learning, to do that.And then you’re right, also now we’ve already been bringing down a lot of data, particularly in some parts of our constellation, so we have a head start on being able to go off and pull out from large databases the signals that our functional managers or our users need that they can go off and use very quickly. And we’re going to be applying that to the proliferated architectures as well.Ms. Harding: Yeah. So let’s keep talking about that innovation theme. Kari mentioned AI. We’ve done some work here on the possibility of on-orbit processing so you’re not sending huge amounts of data down to ground stations; you can figure out on orbit what of these images is actually the most important and get it down quicker or to a more direct ground station. So talk about what you see ahead for this kind of con stellation and what fun toys we should look forward to in the near future.Dr. Scolese: (Laughs.) Well, certainly, yes, on-orbit processing, we do it today, but we need to do more of it. And with the advanced processors that are out there, it’s going to allow us to do even more so we can deliver the information that’s needed. But I think the real value is going to be coming from merging various datasets to deliver a product. Whether you do that on orbit or you do it on the ground – it’s probably easier to do it on the ground – but what you want to do is to get the information that’s needed to the ground so that there you can even speed that up. So we’re going to be using on-orbit processing more as we go forward. And you know, the processing chips have gotten smaller and more capable and more available, so that’s going to really allow us to go off and do it. We just need to figure out how to make it work in space. Ms. Harding: Details, details. (Laughs.) We know you’ll get there. We were talking in the green room a little bit about quantum sensing, which I think is just so cool when we can make it work. Do you want to talk a little bit about where you are on that? Dr. Scolese: Well, we’re all in the – you know, in the early phases of doing quantum sensing, particularly remote quantum sensing. And the advantage of this is you’re using fundamental physics to go off and do detections, which allows you to get information, you know, faster because you don’t have as much processing that’s needed. You’ll know what the differences are. It’s very difficult to spoof those things because you’ll know it. That’s one of the things that are there. And it has applications from, you know, communications, which is probably the furthest along, to measuring, you know, the gravitational differences on the Earth, or any planet for that matter. So there’s broad applications to it, and we’re in the process of working through all of that. And we’re working with – in that particular case, since it is very early research, we do have an advanced technology group, and they’re the ones that are working it. And they’re working with not only industry, but also with academia to go off and develop those capabilities. And we’ve set up a research lab that will allow us – we were talking about going a little bit more public. Our research lab, we can do unclassified research. A lot of this is very fundamental research, so we need to have that opportunity to be able to go off and do that. In fact, next week we’re going to have an unclassified conference at the NRO talking about the technologies that we need, and offering the opportunity for others to come in in an unclassified arena to share their ideas and build the relationships that we need. We’ve routinely had classified conferences, but this is to open it up even broader. Ms. Harding: So talk a little bit about NRO’s role as the fast follower in partnerships like this where we see the commercial sector or the academic sector actually pushing forward the bounds of the science and then NRO finds itself taking advantage of those discoveries and trying to partner with those groups. How are you envisioning trying to push NRO into that partnership role? Dr. Scolese: It was a natural role. I mean, it kind of started out that way. You know, if you read the history books, there was a lot of that happening. You look at some of the pioneers, they called them, in the NRO, they came from industry that were, you know, researchers like Edwin Land, you know, the Polaroid camera. You know, you wouldn’t – you wouldn’t imagine that. Richard Garwin, who was a researcher at IBM, as well as, you know, university professor. So it’s in our DNA to go off and work with the community at the – at the leading edge of technology. And it’s a two-way street. They’re developing capabilities, whether it’s, you know, new math models or new technologies, and we want to be engaged in working with them. Which is one of the reasons we’ve established this – unclassified opportunities for getting together. We’ve had it, but it’s been in – you know, tighter confines. This way, it opens it up so that we can – we can bring in even more. And at the same time, we have gone off and demanded and pushed technologies that ultimately end up on the other side, in the commercial sector or otherwise. So I would say it’s in our DNA to go off and have that relationship and make it work, and we’re continuing to go off and do that. The neat thing now is there’s a lot more capability out there than there was when the NRO first started, when, as one of you said, it was largely government-run. You know, space was largely government. And most of the others was in individual technologies, not space technologies. That’s changed, so now we have that additional benefit coming to us. Ms. Harding: So before I turn it over to Kari to talk about some of the intergovernmental challenges that we have, I want you to talk a little bit about hard targets and how you’re using those partnerships, how you’re using those advances in technology to go after the truly difficult intelligence questions, places where you cannot get people on the ground but you can see them from space. Dr. Scolese: Well, you think you can see them from space – (laughter) – because they try and hide them, and that requires us to go off and do all the things that we talked about: the proliferated architectures, now you can’t hide because you’re constantly being looked at; greater persistence, again you can’t hide because we’re constantly looking. But there’s many other things. There’s camouflage, if you think about it. There’s lots of techniques that can be used. Which means we have to go off and look at, you know, very different phenomenologies. And we’ve developed capabilities and are developing capabilities that will allow us to defeat those types of activities. And you know, quantum sensing is one of them. You can’t really hide from, you know, fundamental physics. Ms. Harding: That’s a great tagline for NRO: You can’t hide from fundamental physics. (Laughter.) Kari? (Laughs.) Ms. Bingen: So I’m going to hit on an issue that I worked on when I was in the Pentagon and I know you’re in the middle of it day in, day out, which is this Title 10/Title 50 issue. So for those that aren’t following, Title 10 is the Department of Defense’s authorities/missions. Title 50 is the intelligence community’s authorities and missions. And you know, if I think about it from a DOD perspective, they’re starting – they’re putting more missions into space. GMTI – so ground-moving target indicator – is a great example of that. That is a mission that’s long been done by aircraft. But as the adversary gets better at their capabilities to keep us out it’s pushing us further and further back, those aircraft are vulnerable, so we’re going to space. But that’s a battlespace awareness mission; it’s not necessarily a traditional intelligence mission. But yet, you all are building the – that capability for our nation. So, you know, you were talking about this generational change. How is that community wrestling with and thinking about, as more of these missions go to space, it’s not business as usual – it’s not the traditional way that we’ve tasked these systems, processed them, analyzed them – because speed is of the essence getting that information right to, say, the warfighter? So how are you thinking through those – some of those issues that are manifesting, I think, with this, but there’s probably more to come? Dr. Scolese: Yeah. You know – you know, that’s really a good question, and you’ve given me a thing that will – that makes me think about it. You know, building the satellites and operating from the vantage point of space doesn’t care about Title 10 or Title 50. It’s when it hits the ground and who uses it, right? So I actually think of these problems as a datacentric problem. We have to deliver the data at the speed and with the characteristics that the user – and it can be any user – needs. So GMTI is, obviously, going to go off and need information on where an object is and where it is going, right, in real time. We need to deliver that data there. Others will be delivering, also, data. That data has to be fused into a product that is used. This was the same problem that we had back when they were moving this to airborne, when now all of a sudden you’re remotely sensing it where, you know, the operator in the aircraft can’t necessarily see the object on the ground without the benefit of equipment. So, you know, we’re there, and we’re in the process of figuring out how do we bring all that data down, and how do we assimilate it in such a way that we can deliver, in this particular case, to the warfighter the information they need that’s going to keep that object, you know, tracked with high reliability? So I think of it more as a datacentric problem. We can collect the data. That data could be used Title 10, Title 50. It can be used in the civil applications, wherever it needs to be. It’s how you, ultimately, fuse that data, assuming that you’re delivering it at the quality and at the time rate of – the time that they need it and the rate of time that they need it, and you’re able to provide the discrimination to detect the object. Ms. Bingen: Well, and on ground-moving target indicator, GMTI, you now have this operational challenge presented to you of, you know, we have to track these vehicles, and we’re tracking them across multiple satellites. These satellites are handing off to each other. So you now have to be able to do all of that in real time. You also have this other dimension which, you know, you’re collecting electro-optical color images, you’re collecting radar images, you’re collecting signals; you have to fuse that all together in real time. My hypothesis, though, is those technology challenges are solvable. And your engineers are doing a phenomenal job there, and I’ll want you to talk about that. But some of the challenges where – you know, when you’re doing those kind of tasking handoffs, or you’re having to tip and queue between a signals collection system and an imaging system, there are policy challenges, there are cultural challenges. So can you talk through some of those pieces? Dr. Scolese: Sure. Yeah. So we’re used to doing hard things, generally hard technical things. Policy things tend to be even harder – (laughs) – as you well know. And you know, you’re right; I mean, the technical challenges, we are in the process of solving those and we are making good progress. Some of those have been solved. Some of them, still work to be done. The policy ones, we’re still in the process of working through all of those things. And they range from the mundane – how do we, you know, make sure that the information we’re giving to the user, the warfighter, is accurate, and who’s going to make – you know, who is going to do that in the field; I mean mundane in the sense of, you know, you would expect us to go off and do that – and some of it is more complicated, along the lines that you’re talking about, how do we integrate all of these different systems in a way that will allow all of that data to come down in a format that is usable to be put together, so that – so that we can deliver the product that’s needed.And we are in the process of continuing to work through that. Again, we’re making a lot of progress there. We’re going through it. We put prototypes of the various satellites up there. That’s allowed us to see what they can do. It’s also allowed us to participate in exercises where we can go off and test those various things. We’re also doing tabletop exercises and gaming to allow us to go off and test things at the edges and say, what if, this? And then, you know, OK, now we need to adjust, you know, X, Y, or Z. And that’s helping us to identify are there policy areas or gaps that we need to go off and address? From the building of the satellites, there’s really no gaps there. From bringing the data down, there’s really no gaps other than you have to make sure that you get it. But putting all the data together and making that a viable product at the end, that’s where we’re going off and doing it. We’re making good progress. The exercises are helping us an awful lot as we do that to get that done. And of course, that’s just a small subset of what’s up there. You know, in – by roughly December of this year – from last June till December of this year we’ll have probably launched 100 satellites. So we are going from the demo phase to the operational phase, where we’re really going to be able to start testing all of this stuff out in a more operational way. Ms. Bingen: So that’s a challenge to the policymaking community, to get the policies – the authorities fixed, so that as those operational capabilities come online it’s seamless for the users.Ms. Harding: And that is the hardest challenge of all. Ms. Bingen: Oh my gosh, yeah. (Laughter.)Ms. Harding: Absolutely. I love listening to a very smart engineer talk through a really complicated problem. You make it sound like it’s so straightforward and logical. Like, we do this, and then we do this, and then it works. If only policy was that straightforward. You used the word “mundane,” which I find really funny because even a mundane day at NRO – (laughter) – is still a pretty exciting day, with all the amazing work that you guys are doing. I want to remind the audience to send in your questions. We’ve already got some online – actually, a bunch coming in from online. But scan the QR code and send them in, and we will ask Dr. Scolese, Chris, what he thinks about these questions. Before we do that though I want to hit on the topic of trust in intelligence, which is something near and dear to my heart. After a long career working in the intelligence world, I now have the privilege to be more out in public talking about what the intel space does, trying to debunk some of the worst myths, misconceptions. And your entry onto sort of a more public role in bringing NRO into the light, I think, is a great opportunity to talk about this.Balancing classification with transparency. How are you envisioning that as a way ahead for the NRO? And then this new era of openness, one way that we saw this really be a huge intelligent success was the Biden administration’s decision to release a lot of information about the impending Russian invasion of Ukraine. Some of that was satellite imagery. Some of that was other intelligence. But the fact of being able to point to evidence that nobody could ignore, that this was a thing that was about to happen, I think, really prepared the world for what was going to be a ground war in Europe that we haven’t seen for decades. So talk a little bit about the way that the IC can operate more in the light, and also just why the American people should be really proud of the work that you’re doing. Dr. Scolese: Well, I mean, you know, our job is to deliver information. And ours is – again, you know, we’re collecting photons and electrons and we need to turn that into a product, which is a lot easier to do in some respects from figuring out intention. Our job is to give unequivocal, clear data that comes from the satellite. So it’s an image or a signal. And we take those and make sure it works. So we’re checking calibration. We’re checking, you know, the satellite’s ability to point and deliver data, all of those things. So it’s really, you know, objective evidence of what’s going on.The point you made is an example of how we’ve come to work very effectively with our commercial partners. Combining both the systems that we need to protect the information from and using the commercial imagery, we were able to share all of that data and be able to say, hey – well, not us, actually. The NRO doesn’t do that. But because we had that ability, we were able to give that information as unclassified information, and that was able to be shared with everybody. At the same time, we’re also, you know, looking at our products and saying, you know, what really needs to be classified? I mean, it’s a broad question that is being asked more and more. And we’re part of answering that question.Ms. Harding: Mmm hmm, for sure.Ms. Bingen: If I can jump in on commercial – and I know you’re getting ready to ask some questions here too.Ms. Harding: No, go for it. Ms. Bingen: Is we at CSIS earlier this week unveiled a report called “Gold Rush: The 2024 Commercial Remote Sensing Global Rankings.” So we took inspiration from the Olympics. We did it in partnership with United States Geospatial Intelligence Foundation, Taylor Geospatial Engine, Taylor Geospatial Institute. But one of the reasons why we wanted to assess these worldwide commercially available remote sensing systems was to see what the state of competition looks like. And what was surprising in our results is, of all of the winners in different categories of electronic imaging, radar, hyperspectral, et cetera, the sector’s moving really fast.Roughly half the systems on the podium were ones launched in the last three to four years. China is making incredible inroads in their technologies that they’re now selling commercially. So for us, there was this broader strategic narrative of, you know, what can the government do from a policy regulatory perspective, as a customer, as an investor, to ensure that our companies stay in the lead in the years to come? So all of that is to kind of come back to you. I mean, the NRO is buying data and, you know, leveraging these commercial capabilities. Where do you see that going? And what role is NRO playing in that – the viability, really, of the commercial sector? Dr. Scolese: So, you know, our role is to buy the pixels or the data that’s coming back from those satellites, and deliver it to the community, principally our partners at NGA and NSA, but broadly – more broadly than that as well. And today we have a relationship with, I think, all of the U.S. providers that are in the report that you were talking about. And they span a range from we’re just getting to know each other, they may have a new capability or a new idea that they want to bring online so that’s kind of an early phase type of a thing, to demonstrations, which we’re doing, for instance, with the radar community right now that’s proving out to be very well, to contracts where we’re saying, yes, we want your data, you fill a niche, unquestionably, and you can take over this role from the national means, the government-developed systems.So we cover that range. And we want to work with the commercial community to go off and share what we need. And we do that with our partners, again, at NGA and NSA. To say, these are the types of things that we need so they have an idea of where we’re at. And then if they can develop it, and we’ll work with them to develop it – as I said, we’ve got the range all the way from I’m thinking about it, to I’ve got some examples of what I want to do to, you know, we’re pretty far along that we want you to really try it out, to, yeah, it’s working, and we can – you know, we can count on it. That gives us the opportunity to go off and help nurture that community.And over – you know, since we started this, about 2018, I think, time frame, we’ve grown the budget, and you know how hard it is to grow a budget, by about 50 percent. So, you know, that shows, you know, a real commitment to working it. And it shows a commitment by the government, right? Because the NRO can propose anything, but there’s many other organizations that have to agree that there’s value there as well. So, you know, the U.S. government is indicating that. And we want to continue to grow it even more as we’re going forward.Ms. Bingen: That’s a good demand signal. Dr. Scolese: Yeah. Ms. Harding: Yeah. I just want to put another plug for Kari’s team’s wonderful report, the 2024 Commercial Remote Sensing Global Rankings, which we lovingly refer to as the satellite Olympics. So it’s a really good report. And you can look through it quickly at who’s accomplishing what, in what areas. And you can kind of see the gaps that we as a whole community still need to fill. And I think one thing that really came across to me from the report is the collaboration with allies and how important that collaboration with allies is.Which actually takes me to our first question, from a CSIS intern, Ianni Nikas: How will cooperation with international allies work for NRO? Dr. Scolese: Well, we’re doing a lot more with our international partners. We’ve always sort of had a relationship with the international community. And that’s growing, just like I mentioned commercial is growing. There is great capability that exists with our partners. And we want to take advantage of it. And they would like to work with us. So that’s a – that’s a great relationship. So we are greatly expanding that relationship. And I think it – the fact that we’re doing that makes people that want to keep us from doing things, makes them worry.And to think about it, you know, we have now – typically we have launched from Florida and California. Over the last four or five years, we’ve launched from New Zealand with our partners there. We’ve launched from the U.K. And we’ve launched from Wallops Island, Virginia. So, you know, that shows international partnership. It also shows a diversification of our launch capabilities, that we can – you know, we can talk about. Because usually it has a little symbol on the rocket, so I can’t say it’s not NRO. (Laughter.) But, you know, I mean that’s just an indication of how much more broadly our international cooperation has grown. Ms. Harding: Yeah. We have a question from Vivienne Machi from Aviation Week, that I’m actually going to throw to Kari first because I think it speaks to your background in commercial space as well as in the government areas.Ms. Bingen: I thought I was the moderator today.Ms. Harding: (Laughs.) Not going to get off that easy. So on Tuesday at CSIS, representatives of U.S. commercial remote sensing companies reflected upon the current challenges inherent to working with the U.S. government. You shared recently that the NRO is working with a broader swath of industry partners these days, including startups and new space companies. What does the NRO need from commercial space companies that it can’t already do? And what needs to be done, regulatory, policy-wise, for you to work with these vendors? So the reason I’m forcing Kari to answer this question first is because she’s been on both sides of this equation. Ms. Bingen: Man, I thought I was going to be the questioner. (Laughter.) No, it’s a great question. And one of the areas that we emphasized in the report was the concept of integration. And, Chris, you mentioned this quite a bit in your discussion today is, you know, so long as commercial is thought of as the separate thing, it’s going to be a cost center, or folks are not going to want to use it. But if you can bring it in – you know, as you start with the baby steps, the ones that you’re thinking about, all the way up to the ones that you’ve got a deep partnership with, is if you can get those capabilities, experimenting with them, integrating them into your tasking cycles, your operations, into the platforms that your analysts use, it’s going to be much more seamless and integrated to them. And they’ll see the value of what commercial brings. But I think on the government side – and I apologize, I’m going to take some of your talk here is – you know, there are things that the government demands that maybe the commercial market doesn’t demand, or in a different way. So some of the security pieces, the supply chain pieces. Yes, the commercial sector cares about that, but the government really, really cares about that. But I’ll tee it up for you. Dr. Scolese: I think Kari covered it well.Ms. Harding: I knew she would. Dr. Scolese: So the only thing that I’ll add, you know, from the commercial sector, is, you know, we’ve been talking about space systems, launchers. And – well, we’ve been talk about spacecraft delivering data. It’s broader than that. So everything Kari said. And we definitely want all of that, but to expand it. Commercial launch has helped us incredibly. Commercial spacecraft are allowing us to do a lot of things. It enabled our proliferated architecture. There’s also, you know, the data centers that we need, because we always talk about space. The cloud services are critical to helping us disaggregate our ground systems so that we don’t have one ground system operating everything. We can now disaggregate. The developments in data processing, data science, artificial intelligence, those are all areas that we can take advantage of. And they have a lot of crossover, you know, between commercial and what we need on the government end. So I would just expand what Kari said to include those. So those are some of the things that we could we – could go off and take advantage of. Ms. Harding: Great. OK.From Jorge Carlos Arias Delgado from Florida International University: What has been one of the highlights of your career as an engineer and as the director of the NRO?Dr. Scolese: (Laughs.) Well, as the director of the NRO I guess I can’t tell you. (Laughter.)But, you know, actually, as one that does actually cover everything, you know, we do – and throughout my career, I’ve been very fortunate. I’ve been able to do some really neat things – build satellites that have looked at the Earth and left the solar system. You know, that’s pretty cool. But, you know, the other thing that I’ve really been very, very fortunate to have is to work with, you know, just great people. You know, just outstanding individuals. And I see them coming in every day. I see it in our interns and everybody else. You know, they’re a team. Every organization I’ve been in, it’s been a team. People are driving with the same goals to deliver whatever it is we’re asked to deliver, and finding new and clever ways to go off and do it. So I would say that’s been, you know, the real highlight of my career, great people doing great things. Ms. Harding: So, on that front, we have several questions that are coming in that are about that very question. If I wanted to work at the NRO, what are you looking for? What do you want in your people?Ms. Scolese: Well, we clearly want smart, dedicated people that – in all fields. Because, I mean, we have to build, and design, and operate satellites. So clearly, you know, that’s our engineers, our scientists, our mathematicians. But we also have to know how much they cost, otherwise we won’t get our money, and we won’t know where our money is going, which would be bad. We need contracting officers. We need people that know how to hire people. So we need – we need all disciplines, basically, coming in to work at the – at the NRO. But the main thing we really want are people who are really innovative and really committed to going off and doing things that have never been done before and making them a reality. I mean, that’s really what our job is, to go off and deliver the information that’s needed, because technology is going to change, and we have to keep up with it. But, more importantly, as long as there are people that want to do harm to the United States or our allies, there are also people who are capable of challenging us, either technologically or with better ways of hiding things. And we need to stay ahead of that. So we’re always going to be busy. We’re always going to be innovating. So there’ll never be a boring day at the NRO. (Laughter.) Ms. Bingen: Can you – I’m going to pick up on Jorge’s question. I know you can’t tell us the details, but give us a hint. Like, what’s really at the leading edge? What excites you about what the NRO is doing, and where it’s really pushing the envelope for these – that young electrical engineer, or social scientist that wants to come work at NRO? What are they going to be doing?Dr. Scolese: Cool stuff. (Laughter.) You know, it’s very difficult. I mean, you know, from a system standpoint about what we deliver is very hard. But from a technology standpoint, you can definitely say things. I mean, think about what we need in terms of optics. I mean, y’all know we’re looking at the Earth, right? I mean, you take a picture, we’ve been taking pictures. Got to have mirrors. You got to have lenses. You got to have stuff like that. You don’t have to build them all the same way. There are lots and lots of different ways of doing it.I mean, look at Hubble Space Telescope, and look at James Webb, right? Hubble is a big monolithic mirror. James Webb is a bigger segmented mirror, right? We need to do those kinds of things. You know, think about the processing that we were talking about. Think about putting an NVIDIA chip, an AI system, into space, right? Those are things that that we have to go off and think about. You know, the detectors that we use need to be ever-more sensitive. Which means, you know, solid state – solid state physics is absolutely critical to what we’re doing. We want to make things smaller. So that’s necessary.And think about the materials that we that we have to go off and use. Now you go off and you talk about those things. You got to have materials that are radiation hardened. They got to be able to take, you know, a relatively rough ride in the rocket. They have to deploy. So we need lots and lots of different capabilities out there. And we operate in a pretty severe regime. And then think about on the ground, where we’ve got to build networks that are, you know, reliable, but also hopefully invincible to cyberattacks. Because that’s the other gift that will keep on giving, right? We’re constantly going to be, you know, having to go off and deal with that. And then think about programming. I mean, we always – you know, anybody that’s been an engineer or a scientist, or probably anybody nowadays, has programmed. But we got to get reliable programs that we can trust, right? So how do we go off and do that? So those are some of the areas that, without saying how we’re putting them all together, we’re working in. And we need to go off and really get really, really great people that want to go off and do that. Ms. Harding: That’s one of my favorite things about the IC, and working in and with the IC, is that people say it’s impossible. You can’t do that. And they say, OK, hold my beer. (Laughter.) We’re going to figure it out. We’re going to make it happen.So we have two questions that are on the subject of working with allies, especially allies that are not part of the Five Eyes construct. Jim Schwenk from Tawas USA says: Can you share your thoughts related to working with allied partners that are not within the Five Eyes, especially with all the additional allied space commands being available to enhance supply chain and resiliency through their industrial base? And then from our very own Jacob Bradley, who’s one of the interns here at CSIS: Do you feel like your information-sharing and technology-sharing capabilities with our allies and partners lacks? So you’ve got the how are we cooperating together, and then how are we sharing information out as well? Dr. Scolese: Yeah. So, you know, our supply chain is global now. I mean, there’s no question about it. And, you know, we’d like to work with our allies because we can, you know, have much higher confidence and trust in what’s going on there. So definitely, you know, we’re working across the spectrum, data sharing where we can. You mentioned, you know, the fact that there are a number of space commands. More importantly, there’s a lot of satellites that are – that are up there in space.The better we can coordinate our operations in space, while it’s very big – (laughs) – there are still – there are still challenges that we – that we need to deal with, to avoid conjunctions. And the more we share, and we are, that information – typically, we work with Space Command that has that as their responsibility, space management, to get that done. But that also includes all of our allies and partner space development ones that want to operate responsibly in space, so that we can – we can go off and operate there. I think that pretty much covers it.And then what was the other one, challenges in? Ms. Harding: Sharing information back out.Dr. Scolese: Yeah. I mean, we do share information. Now, again, it’s not the NRO. That’s not our job. I mean, we provide the information. But I do know that, you know, we do share. You have to ask –Ms. Harding: You promised, we do share.Dr. Scolese: Yes, we do share. You have to – you have to ask my colleagues at NGA and NSA how that’s done.Ms. Harding: Right. One more from the audience before I turn it back over to Kari for sort of some closing thoughts, and we could have covered 15,000 more topics, so anything else that we’ve sort of forgotten in our hour today.But Cal Biesecker from Defense Daily says: Regarding the 100 satellites that will have been launched since last December, are all of these NRO owned? And what will these new satellites enable you to do?Dr. Scolese: So the answer is yes. They are NRO-owned, if you – if you mean it the sense that, is NRO going to be operating them? The answer is yes. And what are they going to be doing? They are part of the proliferated architecture to go off and get us reasonably high-resolution imagery of the Earth at a high rate of speed. Ms. Harding: At a high rate of speed. This is the part about you can’t hide. Yeah, we’re constantly – this idea of persistence. Where you can get constant imagery of what’s going on in a particular place on the planet is just so important, especially if you are, like I was, an intel analyst sitting at their desk trying to figure out, like, has a thing happened yet? It’s invaluable. But over to Kari for final questions and thoughts.Ms. Bingen: Well, and I am just – you know, when you just take a step back and think, I mean, this is a community that has long had few – little inventory to meet a whole variety of different needs. And now we’re getting to the point where you’re talking dozens to hundreds of satellites, where, in many respects, supply is less of an issue. I now start to think, gosh, that’s much more like Uber than it is, you know, the traditional way of doing business, right? (Laughter.) So how do we think about some of those different kind of models going forward? Or that we’re going to be so heavily reliant on the processing technology that you mentioned, artificial intelligence, automation, all those things.We’re going to end – I’m going to give you the last word here, but I want to mention a couple of things. And, Chris, you mentioned at the very beginning, which is, you know, it’s the National Reconnaissance Office. There’s much that you – a tremendous amount that you do for providing national intelligence to senior leadership, providing tactical intelligence to warfighters. I remember being – doing trips – when we’ve done congressional trips to Iraq and Afghanistan, and those folks on the edge were receiving data from NRO satellites. But you also have a – really a civil mission there as well, as you’re supporting such a range of customers as well that are not within your traditional national security circle. So I’m going to throw that out at you.But then also just mention, in the spirit of really being more transparent and talking about what the organization does, is we plan to follow this discussion up with some of your leadership team as well, and different aspects of the NRO mission. So we’re really looking forward to hosting other NRO leaders in the future here within the towers. But, OK, so last words is: Remind us of what you do beyond national security. And then also, looking forward, what does NRO look like in the next 10 to 20 years? Let’s end on that. Dr. Scolese: OK. Well, that’s pretty good. So I’ll go back to it’s all about the data that we deliver, and making that available to the appropriate users. So to the civil community, I mean, if you think about some of the challenges that they have and where they need information quickly and reliably, it’s if a natural disaster occurs. And typically, you have limited communications, limited ability to move around.And you want to know, to a first order, how bad it is and where are the ways that you can move in order to get resources to the people and the communities that need them. If you have the data and you’re delivering it at the pace that it’s needed, you can do that quickly. That’s part of what the NRO does. As well as supporting, you know, other activities. You know, we have a limited role in providing data for climate change and what have you. But I would say that’s a perfect example of where, you know, we’re in there. You know, what’s the future going to look like in 10 or 20 years? You know, I’m always, you know, reminded about, you know, people that predict the future. Arthur C. Clarke had a good quote; look it up if you don’t know it. It usually involves, you know, getting it wrong. But you know, I believe the NRO will – there’s still going to be a need to understand what’s going on on this planet, what are humans doing and what is the planet doing. And you know, we’re going to need to continue to adapt to deal with that, and some of the things that we talked about are going to be there. But the one thing I do know is it’ll be a different generation of people that are up there – or, out there; maybe up there, too – that will be doing this, and you know, we need to continue to have, you know, really outstanding people going in there and making sure that we can deliver the information that’s going to be needed in 10 or 20 years, which is going to be different information to that’s needed today.Ms. Bingen: And Emily and I have both been fortunate we’ve been able to walk the halls of the organization that you lead. And to your point, you know, these engineers, these scientists, the others that work there, I mean, they’re excited to be there. The mission and the technology is really cool. So thank you for all of your leadership and everything that you’re doing to keep our nation safe. And if we can also – we’d like to thank some of the staff that supported this event today – so Christina Nordby, Kendra LaFave, and our fantastic streaming and broadcasting team here. So again, Dr. Scolese, thank you. Dr. Scolese: Thank you. Ms. Harding: Thank you. (Applause.) (END.)\nTags\nNorth America,\nSpace and Aerospace,\nTechnology,\nDefense and Security, and\nDefense Strategy and Capabilities\nPrograms & Projects\nInternational Security Program\nAerospace Security Project\nIntelligence, National Security, and Technology Program\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"NRO的故事：与克里斯·斯科尔斯博士的对话\n照片：CSIS\n文字记录\n— 2024年10月3日\n可用下载\n下载文字记录\n334kb\n图像\n照片：CSIS\nNRO的故事：与克里斯·斯科尔斯博士的对话\n2024年10月3日 • 美国东部时间下午1:00 – 2:00\n由国际安全项目主办\n本文字记录来自CSIS于2024年10月3日举办的一场活动。观看完整视频请点击此处。Emily Harding：好的，各位。非常感谢大家今天来到这里。我是Emily Harding。我是CSIS这里的情报、国家安全与技术项目主任。今天我们要谈的是太空中存在的力量。在不久以前，在轨道上持续存在还只是各国政府的专属领域。但在过去十年左右，我们见证了转型以及一代人的变化的开始。如今，向轨道发射设备的国家范围广泛得多。商业供应商已经成为各国国家安全图景中不可或缺的一部分。\n今天我们要谈的是NRO，这是美国真正的国家瑰宝。它一直存在，从未缺席，只是如此低调，以至于大多数美国人都不知道它的存在。然而如今，他们开始以我们以前从未见过的方式走出阴影。\n今天我们请来了NRO主任克里斯·斯科尔斯博士。他的职业生涯始于海军军官，负责核推进工作。但后来他从海上转向太空，再也没有回头。他在NASA有着漫长的职业生涯，包括担任NASA总工程师、代理局长以及戈达德太空飞行中心主任。随后，2019年，他成为NRO主任，并且是首位担任这一职位、经参议院确认的人。这确实是为了传达参议院对NRO使命的重视程度，以及他们认为对这一对国家至关重要的庞大项目所需的监督。我有幸协助了那次提名程序，那——那真的很有意思。\n斯科尔斯博士和我的同事Kari Bingen，在太空政策方面都是非常厉害的专家，她马上也会在这里自我介绍，所以我把自己定位为台上的通才，尝试将他们即将为更广泛的通才听众介绍的那些真正精妙的能力解释清楚。\n不过首先，NRO为我们带来了一段视频，展示他们的工作。（播放视频演示。）Kari A. Bingen：我叫Kari Bingen。我是CSIS这里的航空航天安全项目主任。斯科尔斯博士，能邀请到您是我们的荣幸。\n我被那段视频深深触动了。请大家想一想。国家侦察办公室是一个已经存在了60多年的组织，但在相当长的一段时间里，它仿佛并不存在。直到1992年，政府才承认它的存在。因此，能够坐在这里看到这样一段视频，真是非常、非常壮观。\n我还想特别强调一点，国家侦察办公室主任来到CSIS，公开谈论这个组织及其使命，是极其罕见的事情。我们有机会在一些行业协会活动之类的场合交谈过，但这里的听众不同。因此，我们非常感谢您出来面向更广泛的听众进行交流并让我们了解情况。\n我很幸运，在2018年至2019年前后认识了斯科尔斯博士，当时您正准备经历您的\n艾米丽提到的确认流程。当时我在国防部，我们对此进行了思考，希望确保首位经参议院确认的局长不是政治人物；专注于技术可靠性、稳健管理，并且在工作中非常不偏不倚。因为当你考虑采购和运营卫星时，那根本不是一件政治性的事情。所以我们非常荣幸，斯科尔斯博士答应了，你知道，他愿意承担这项使命，继续为国家服务。所以我们非常感谢你。\n\n我们大约有30分钟的问答环节，艾米丽和我将与斯科尔斯博士一起进行，随后我们会向现场和线上观众开放提问。如果你在线上，请前往活动网站。那里有一个按钮，点击即可提问。我们会在这里看到问题。另外，如果你在现场，这里有一个二维码供现场观众使用。所以我们非常鼓励大家提问。\n\n那么，下面我把时间交还给艾米丽。\n\n哈丁女士：先生，现在轮到您了。请向我们介绍一下国家侦察局（NRO）以及您所服务的使命。如果要向一个从未听说过NRO的人解释你们的工作，您会如何描述？\n\n克里斯·斯科尔斯博士：当然。首先，感谢邀请我来这里。你们不必称呼我斯科尔斯博士；请叫我克里斯。我会很感激。\n\n正如之前提到的，NRO已经存在了大约60年，确切地说是60多年。在其中大约一半的时间里，我们是秘密机构。当时我们保持秘密是有重要原因的。但在这60多年里，我们的根本使命一直没有改变。我们的工作是从太空的视角收集地球上正在发生的事情，并将这些信息提供给分析人员、决策者、作战人员、应急响应人员，以及任何需要这些信息的人。在这60多年里，我们一直这样做。\n\n在此期间，我们持续进行创新，始终与时代同步。很多时候，我们引领技术发展，因为各国和人们都希望阻止我们看到或了解他们正在做什么，所以我们必须保持领先。我们拥有技术，也需要保持领先，这样才能让我们的系统既经济实惠又具备能力。同时，正如你所说，艾米丽，我们也利用现有的能力。如果已经存在，为什么要重新创造？借助强大的商业产业，我们正在充分利用这一点。\n\n过去30年真正不同的另一件事是，NRO过去拥有一支相对封闭的员工队伍，而现在我们实际上会公开招聘并吸纳人员。如果要这样做，你就必须告诉人们你的存在——（笑声）——否则他们根本找不到你。\n\n所以我们随着时间不断调整，推动创新，推动技术发展，充分利用这些资源。你知道，我们一直保持着领先于竞争对手的地位，可以这么说。\n\n哈丁女士：所以，您的角色既是情报机构，也是国防机构。关于员工队伍这一点，如果我没理解错，NRO曾经从中央情报局和情报界其他部门直接抽调人员，同时也从国防部抽调人员。但现在你们进行公开招聘，需要更加广泛的人才来源。那么，您能否谈谈作为情报机构和国防机构所承担的双重角色？\n\n斯科尔斯博士：当然可以。你知道——如果你在运营\n在太空中，你可以看到整个地球。因此，这些信息应该提供给任何需要它的人，无论是情报界还是国防部。当然，在一定边界内，由一个组织来负责这项工作是高效的，而这些边界是我们需要进一步审视的。这正是国家侦察局所发挥的作用。真正的区别在于：你需要多快获得数据，需要多少数据，以及数据的多样性有多大。你知道，在20世纪60年代，我们成立的时候，从天上投下胶卷桶就足够了。那时世界变化没有那么快，需求也没有那么迫切。当时只有一个对手，即苏联，是我们必须应对的对象。随着发展，我们需要越来越快地获取信息，今天依然如此。人们希望即时获得信息，就像他们可以——你知道，就像通过推特、X或其他平台获取信息一样，对吧？而我们的工作，就是更快地交付所需的信息，这意味着要采用与20世纪60年代不同的探测手段。当时主要是胶卷舱返回地面，带回所观测目标的精美照片。如今，我们必须进行更广泛的观测，因为技术更加复杂。\n\n因此，我们的根本任务始终没有改变。而且，我们很好地服务于国防部和情报界，并在民间机构需要时为其提供服务。我认为，这是对资源的高效利用。这一视角能够从整体上充分利用整个地球，并让我们迅速分发这些信息，还能让我们与所有相关群体协调，交付最终所需的信息。\n\n哈丁女士：所以，在请卡丽谈谈一些代际变化之前，我想起了老电视剧《24小时》，每当他们说“把卫星转到我的手机上”时，我都会笑。（笑声。）当时我想，要是我们真的能做到就好了——但我们正越来越接近你所说的那种实时观测。\n\n不过，我想请你解释一下“从天上投下胶卷桶”，因为这确实是过去发生过的事情。那是什么意思？\n\n斯科尔斯博士：20世纪60年代，要拍摄照片并将其传回地球非常困难。我的意思是，如今我们有了手机，可能所有1990年以后出生的人都不知道胶卷是什么。而当时，最有效的方法就是把相机放到太空中；让胶卷装在相机里；拍照；拍完所有照片后把胶卷卷起来；把它放进一个——本质上是再入飞行器的——桶里，然后用降落伞把它投回地球。如果你有机会，可以去YouTube上看看。那是CORONA回收胶卷舱的视频。飞机会在空中将其捕获，然后胶卷会被送去冲洗，影像也会被分发。\n\n不过，我认为还有一件事应该提到。国家侦察局最早一颗真正具备能力、能够交付成果的卫星叫作GRAB。它大约20英寸大小。按今天的标准，它会被视为一颗立方体卫星。那颗卫星确实会直接向地面传输信息，但传输的不是图像，而是定位雷达所在位置的信息。\n\n哈丁女士：这是一次巨大的飞跃。让我们谈谈如今的飞跃吧。\n\n宾根女士：有趣的是，正如\n你说到CORONA，NRO历史学家发布过一段非常精彩的历史，讲述CORONA及其他项目。我们CSIS团队中的一员克里斯蒂娜·诺德比实际上还找到了一套NRO漫画系列。\n\n斯科莱塞博士：是的。\n\n宾根女士：确实是一套讲述NRO历史的漫画系列。所以我向大家推荐阅读这套漫画。不过，从投放胶卷罐到建造这些非常庞大、精密的系统。现在，随着威胁不断演变，技术也在演变。你提到，我们正处于天基架构的代际变革时期。你能详细谈谈——真正解释一下——这意味着什么吗？\n\n而且你一直在媒体上谈论NRO正在推进的卫星扩展架构。请谈谈你们在那里正在做什么。\n\n斯科莱塞博士：当然。如今有很多事情正在发生。\n\n你知道，我首先要说的是，就在我们现在交谈之际，我们拥有从立方体卫星到你所说的大型精密卫星、大小不一的卫星。这使我们能够以尽可能最佳的方式提供所需信息。我们也在与商业实体合作，让它们填补空白并接手部分任务，这样我们就能专注于那些极其困难、需要我们另行完成的事情。\n\n而扩展架构正是应对所有这些问题的一种方式。我们需要持续覆盖或快速重访。通过进入更高轨道可以获得持续覆盖，但这意味着你需要大得多的孔径，才能完成任务、实现目标。或者，你可以扩展架构，发射更多卫星，使得在用户所需的合理时间内，总有一颗卫星经过某个区域。这就是我们采用扩展架构所做的事情。这得益于一个非常强大的商业产业，它正在建造数百乃至数千颗卫星，使我们能够以低成本获取这些卫星、调整它们以供我们使用，并搭载所需的任何传感器，以在要求的任何重访时间内获取所需信息。因此，它赋予我们这种能力，使我们能够以非常高的速度进行重访、提供信息，并将其直接传送给用户。\n\n它带来的另一项优势是，可以保护这一架构。我们都知道，有些国家——比如俄罗斯和中国——已经开发出能够摧毁卫星的反卫星武器（ASAT）。如果你在轨道上有一百颗卫星，那么至少需要一百枚导弹才能将它们全部摧毁，而要应对这种情况就会复杂得多。因此，它赋予了我们过去没有的韧性。\n\n它带来的另一个优势，是让我们有机会以更高的速率进行更新，从而不断改进技术，无论是技术本身取得进展、威胁发生变化，还是任务在一定程度上发生变化。这使我们能够以比过去快得多的节奏推进。\n\n宾根女士：有意思的是，我们航空航天领域的很多人往往专注于卫星。但当你发展到拥有数百颗卫星的程度时，我们所采用的一些人工流程——确保分析师查看传来的每一张图像——对我来说，那种模式就是——你无法维持这种模式\nmodel。因此，您能否也谈谈——将这种代际变革思维应用到我们在地面端所做的事情上，我指的是任务规划、处理、分析，以及人工智能技术等工具将带来什么？\n斯科莱塞博士：是的，当然。情况正在发生变化。之所以已经发生变化，是因为我们可以利用现有系统更快地交付信息，前提是我们能够非常迅速地将这些数据压缩成所需的产品。但您说得对，一旦转向大规模部署的架构，您谈论的就不再是少数几颗卫星，而是从几颗到几十颗，再到如今的数百颗卫星，许多事情都必须改变。我们正处于这一过程之中。\n因此，运行大规模部署的架构意味着，不再可能让控制中心里的某个人说“我知道这颗卫星在做什么”。所以，我们必须让机器来帮助我们完成这项工作。\n我们需要人工智能、机器学习和自动化流程来帮助我们做到这一点。幸运的是，在这方面，Kepler能帮助我们。不过，在对卫星进行任务规划、告诉它们该做什么，以及确定哪颗卫星最适合执行任务或哪种组合最合适时，我们正借助先进的处理技术，包括人工智能和机器学习中的一些技术，来完成这些工作。\n然后，您说得对，我们现在也已经在传回大量数据，尤其是星座中的某些部分，因此我们在这方面已经抢占先机，能够从大型数据库中提取出我们的职能主管或用户所需的信号，让他们可以非常迅速地使用。我们也将把这一做法应用于大规模部署的架构。\n哈丁女士：是的。那么我们继续谈谈创新这一主题。Kari提到了人工智能。我们在这里研究过轨道上处理的可能性，这样就不必向地面站发送海量数据；您可以在轨道上判断这些图像中哪些最重要，并更快地将其传回，或者传送到更直接的地面站。因此，请谈谈您对这种星座未来发展的看法，以及我们在不久的将来应该期待哪些有趣的新设备。\n斯科莱塞博士：（笑。）嗯，当然，是的，我们今天已经在进行轨道上处理，但还需要做得更多。随着现有先进处理器的发展，这将使我们能够完成更多工作，从而交付所需的信息。不过，我认为真正的价值将来自融合各种数据集，以交付一种产品。无论是在轨道上完成，还是在地面上完成——在地面上可能更容易——但您想做的是把所需的信息传到地面，这样就能在那里进一步加快处理。因此，随着未来的发展，我们会更多地使用轨道上处理。\n而且，您知道，处理芯片已经变得更小、更强大，也更容易获得，这将真正使我们能够开展这项工作。我们只是需要弄清楚如何让它在太空中运行。\n哈丁女士：细节，细节。（笑。）我们知道您会做到的。\n我们刚才在休息室里聊了一点量子传感，我觉得等它真正实现时一定非常酷。您愿意谈谈这方面目前进展到什么程度吗？\n斯科莱塞博士：嗯，我们都还处于——您知道，早期阶段的\n开展量子传感，尤其是远程量子传感。其优势在于，你利用基础物理学来进行探测，这样就能更快地获取信息，因为所需的处理不那么多。你会知道其中的差异。要伪造这些东西非常困难，因为你会察觉到。这是其中的一点。它的应用范围从通信（这可能是发展得最成熟的领域）到测量地球，或者任何行星的引力差异。因此，它有广泛的应用，我们正在逐步推进所有这些工作。由于这属于非常早期的研究，在这一特定领域，我们确实有一个先进技术小组，正是他们在负责这项工作。他们不仅与工业界合作，也与学术界合作，以开发这些能力。我们还建立了一个研究实验室，使我们能够——我们刚才谈到——更加公开一些。我们的研究实验室可以开展非机密研究。很多工作都是非常基础性的研究，因此我们需要有机会开展这些工作。事实上，下周我们将在国家侦察局举行一次非机密会议，讨论我们所需要的技术，并为其他人提供机会，在非机密环境中分享他们的想法，建立我们所需的关系。我们通常会举办机密会议，但这次是为了进一步扩大开放范围。\n\n哈丁女士：那么，请谈谈国家侦察局在这类合作中作为“快速跟随者”的角色。我们看到商业部门或学术部门实际上正在推动科学边界的发展，而国家侦察局则发现自己可以利用这些发现，并试图与这些团体建立合作。您如何设想推动国家侦察局进入这种合作伙伴角色？\n\n斯科莱塞博士：这是一个很自然的角色。我的意思是，事情一开始就是这样。如果你读过历史书，就会发现当时有很多类似的情况。看看国家侦察局的一些先驱者，他们来自工业界，是研究人员，比如埃德温·兰德，就是宝丽来相机的发明者。你不会——你不会想到这一点。还有理查德·加温，他既是IBM的研究人员，也是大学教授。因此，与处于技术前沿的各界人士合作，是我们与生俱来的特质。合作是双向的。他们在开发各种能力，无论是新的数学模型还是新技术，而我们希望参与其中，与他们一起工作。这也是我们设立这些非机密交流机会的原因之一。我们以前也有过这样的活动，但都处于更严格的限制之下。这样一来，范围就能扩大，我们可以邀请更多人参加。同时，我们也一直在提出要求并推动技术发展，而这些技术最终会出现在另一端，即商业领域或其他领域。因此，我会说，建立这种关系并使其发挥作用，是我们与生俱来的特质，我们也在继续这样做。现在有趣的是，外界拥有的能力比国家侦察局刚成立时多得多。当时，正如你们中的一位所说，主要是由政府运营的。那时，太空基本上由政府主导，而其他大多数领域则是各自独立的技术，而不是太空\n技术。情况已经改变，因此现在我们又获得了这项额外的益处。哈丁女士：所以，在我把话题交给卡里，让她谈谈我们面临的一些政府间挑战之前，我想请您稍微谈一下硬目标，以及您如何利用这些合作伙伴关系、如何利用技术进步，去应对那些真正困难的情报问题——有些地方您无法派人到地面上去，但可以从太空中看到。斯科尔斯博士：嗯，你以为自己能从太空看到它们——（笑声）——因为它们会设法隐藏起来，而这就要求我们去做刚才谈到的所有事情：扩散式架构；现在你无法躲藏，因为你一直处于被观察之中；更强的持续性，同样，你无法躲藏，因为我们一直在观察。但还有许多其他事情。比如伪装，如果你想想的话。可以采用很多技巧。这意味着我们必须去研究非常不同的现象学。我们已经开发、并且正在开发能够挫败这类活动的能力。而且，量子传感就是其中之一。你无法真正躲避基本物理定律。哈丁女士：这是给国家侦察局（NRO）想出的绝佳口号：你无法躲避基本物理定律。（笑声。）卡里？（笑。）宾根女士：我想谈一个我在五角大楼工作时处理过的问题，我知道你们每天都在应对，那就是第10编/第50编的问题。对于不了解这一点的人来说，第10编规定的是国防部的权限/任务；第50编规定的是情报界的权限和任务。而且，如果从国防部的角度来看，他们开始——他们正在把更多任务放到太空中。GMTI，也就是地面移动目标指示，就是一个很好的例子。这项任务长期以来一直由飞机执行。但随着对手在阻止我们进入方面的能力越来越强，这把我们推得越来越远；那些飞机很脆弱，所以我们要转向太空。但那是一项战场态势感知任务，并不一定是传统意义上的情报任务。然而，你们都在为我们的国家建设这项能力。所以，你刚才谈到了这种代际变化。随着越来越多这样的任务转移到太空，这个群体如何应对和思考这些问题？这已经不是照常办事——也不是我们过去指派、处理和分析这些系统的传统方式——因为最重要的是迅速把信息直接交给作战人员？所以，你们如何思考正在显现出来的这些问题？我想，未来可能还会有更多问题出现。斯科尔斯博士：是的。你知道——你知道，这确实是个很好的问题，而你给了我一个让我开始思考的方向。你知道，建造卫星并从太空这一有利位置开展工作，并不在乎第10编还是第50编。关键在于它落地之后由谁使用，对吧？所以，我实际上把这些问题看作以数据为中心的问题。我们必须以用户——而且用户可以是任何人——所需要的速度和特性来交付数据。所以，显然，GMTI需要实时获得某个物体在哪里以及它将前往何处的信息，对吧？我们需要把这些数据交付到那里。其他系统也会提供数据。\n这些数据必须融合成一个实际使用的产品。这正是当初将其转移到机载平台时遇到的同一个问题，因为现在你突然开始对其进行远程感知，而飞机上的操作员不一定能够在没有设备辅助的情况下看到地面上的目标。所以，我们正在设法弄清楚如何把所有这些数据传输下来，以及如何以这样的方式对其进行整合，使我们能够在这个特定案例中，把作战人员所需的信息交付给他们，从而以高可靠性持续跟踪那个目标。我认为这更多是一个以数据为中心的问题。我们可以收集数据。这些数据可以用于第10条、第50条规定的用途，也可以用于民用领域，只要有需要的地方都可以使用。关键在于，最终如何融合这些数据，前提是你能够以他们所需的质量、时间以及所需的时间频率交付数据，并且能够提供判别能力来探测目标。\n\n宾根女士：那么，关于地面移动目标指示器（GMTI），现在你面临着这样一个操作挑战：我们必须跟踪这些车辆，而且要通过多颗卫星对它们进行跟踪。这些卫星彼此之间要交接。因此，你现在必须能够实时完成所有这些工作。\n\n你还面临着另一个维度：你收集的是电光彩色图像、雷达图像和信号；你必须实时将所有这些信息融合在一起。不过，我的假设是，这些技术挑战是可以解决的。你们的工程师在那里做得非常出色，我希望你能谈谈这一点。\n\n但有些挑战在于——你知道，当你进行这类任务交接，或者必须在信号采集系统和成像系统之间进行指示与排队时，会遇到政策方面的挑战，也会遇到文化方面的挑战。那么，你能否谈谈其中的一些问题？\n\n斯科莱塞博士：当然。是的。总的来说，我们习惯于处理困难的事情，通常是困难的技术问题。政策问题往往更加棘手——（笑）——正如你所熟知的。你说得对；我的意思是，我们正在努力解决技术挑战，而且进展良好。其中一些已经解决了。有些仍有工作要做。\n\n至于政策问题，我们仍在努力处理所有这些事项。这些问题既包括日常性的——我们如何确保提供给用户、即作战人员的信息是准确的，以及谁来负责——我的意思是，谁将在现场完成这项工作；所谓日常性，是指你本来就会期待我们去做这些事情——其中一些则更为复杂，正如你所说的，我们如何以一种能够让所有这些数据以可用格式传输下来的方式，整合所有这些不同的系统，以便将它们组合起来，从而——从而交付所需的产品。\n\n我们正在继续努力处理这些问题。同样，我们在那里取得了很大进展。我们正在逐步推进。我们把各种卫星的原型放置到那里。这让我们能够了解它们可以做什么，也让我们能够参加演习，在其中测试各种功能。我们还在进行桌面演练和推演，以便测试\n事物的边缘，然后说，这个怎么样？接着，你知道，好，现在我们需要调整X、Y或Z。这帮助我们识别出，是否存在需要着手解决的政策领域或空白？从卫星的建造来看，那里确实没有空白。从把数据传回来看，除了必须确保获取到数据之外，也确实没有什么空白。但把所有数据整合起来，并最终使其成为一个可行的产品，这正是我们正在着手做的事情。我们进展顺利。在完成这项工作的过程中，这些演习给了我们非常大的帮助。当然，那只是天上所有设施中的一小部分。你知道，到今年大约12月——从去年6月到今年12月——我们可能已经发射了100颗卫星。因此，我们正从演示阶段进入运行阶段，届时我们将真正能够开始以更具运行性的方式测试所有这些东西。\n\n宾根女士：所以，这对政策制定界来说是一项挑战，要把政策和授权确定下来，以便随着这些运行能力上线，用户能够无缝使用。\n\n哈丁女士：而那是所有挑战中最难的一个。\n\n宾根女士：天哪，是啊。（笑声。）\n\n哈丁女士：当然。我喜欢听一位非常聪明的工程师分析一个非常复杂的问题。你让它听起来如此直接而合乎逻辑。比如，我们做这个，然后做那个，然后它就奏效了。要是政策也能如此直接就好了。你用了“平淡”这个词，我觉得非常有趣，因为即使是NRO平淡的一天——（笑声）——仍然是相当令人兴奋的一天，毕竟你们正在开展如此了不起的工作。\n\n我想提醒观众发送你们的问题。我们已经收到了一些在线提问——实际上，网上来了很多问题。但请扫描二维码并把问题发来，我们会请斯科尔斯博士、克里斯，听听他对这些问题的看法。不过在此之前，我想谈谈情报领域的信任，这是一个让我非常关心的话题。在情报界工作了很长时间之后，我现在有幸更多地公开谈论情报领域的工作，努力揭穿一些最严重的神话和误解。而你开始承担一种更公开的角色，让NRO走到聚光灯下，我认为这是谈论这些问题的绝佳机会。\n\n在保密与透明之间取得平衡。你如何设想NRO未来的发展方向？在这个开放的新时期，我们看到这种方式取得巨大情报成功的一个例子，就是拜登政府决定公开大量有关俄罗斯即将入侵乌克兰的信息。其中一部分是卫星图像，另一部分是其他情报。但能够指向任何人都无法忽视的证据，证明这件事即将发生，我认为这确实让全世界为欧洲将要发生的一场地面战争做好了准备，而这样的战争我们已有数十年未曾见过。所以，请谈谈情报界如何能够更多地在光明中运作，以及美国人民为什么应当为你们所做的工作感到真正自豪。\n\n斯科尔斯博士：我的意思是，你知道，我们的工作就是提供信息。而我们——再说一次，你知道——我们收集光子和电子，并需要把它们转化为一种产品；在某些方面，这比判断意图要容易得多。我们的工作就是提供\n来自卫星的明确、清晰数据。所以它可能是一张图像或一个信号。我们获取这些数据并确保其有效。因此，我们在检查校准情况，检查卫星指向和传输数据的能力，以及所有这些方面。所以，这确实是关于正在发生什么的客观证据。你所提到的这一点，正是我们如何与商业合作伙伴开展非常有效合作的一个例子。通过结合我们需要用来保护信息的系统并使用商业图像，我们能够共享所有这些数据，并能够说——嗯，其实不是我们。国家侦察局（NRO）不会这么做。但因为我们具备这种能力，我们能够将这些信息作为非机密信息提供出去，从而可以与所有人共享。 \n与此同时，我们也在审视自己的产品，并思考：究竟哪些内容确实需要列为机密？我的意思是，这是一个越来越常被提出的广泛问题。我们也参与回答这个问题。\n哈丁女士：嗯，当然。\n宾根女士：如果我可以插一句关于商业领域的话——我知道你也准备要问一些相关问题了。\n哈丁女士：不，你请说。 \n宾根女士：本周早些时候，我们在战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）发布了一份名为《淘金热：2024年商业遥感全球排名》的报告。我们从奥运会中获得灵感。该报告是我们与美国地理空间情报基金会、泰勒地理空间引擎和泰勒地理空间研究所合作完成的。但我们想评估这些全球可用的商业遥感系统，其中一个原因是想了解竞争状况。我们的结果令人惊讶：在电子成像、雷达、高光谱等不同类别的所有获奖者中，这一领域发展得非常快。领奖台上大约一半的系统是在过去三到四年内发射的。中国在其如今对外商业销售的技术方面取得了惊人的进展。因此，对我们来说，这背后有一个更广泛的战略叙事：从政策和监管角度，作为客户和投资者，政府能做些什么，以确保我们的企业在未来几年继续保持领先？所以这一切其实是想回到你的话题上。我的意思是，国家侦察局正在购买数据，并利用这些商业能力。你认为这将走向何方？国家侦察局在其中扮演什么角色——尤其是在商业领域的可持续性方面？ \n斯科莱塞博士：所以，我们的职责是购买这些卫星传回的像素或数据，并将其交付给相关机构，主要是我们的合作伙伴国家地理空间情报局（NGA）和国家安全局（NSA），但范围也更广。如今，我认为，我们与报告中提到的所有美国供应商都有合作关系。他们涵盖的范围包括：我们刚开始相互了解，他们可能有一种新的能力或新想法，希望将其投入运行，这属于早期阶段；也包括演示项目，例如我们目前正在与雷达领域合作的项目，事实证明进展非常顺利；还包括合同，即我们明确表示需要他们的数据，他们无疑填补了某个空缺，并且可以从国家手段、政府开发的系统中接替这一职能。因此，我们覆盖了这一整个范围。我们希望与商业领域合作，共同分享我们所需要的内容。我们通过我们的\n合作伙伴，再次是在NGA和NSA。也就是说，这些是我们需要的事项，这样他们就能了解我们的进展。然后，如果他们能够开发出来，我们就会与他们合作开发——正如我所说，我们的范围从“我正在考虑”，到“我有一些想做的示例”，再到“你知道，我们已经相当深入，希望你们真正试用一下”，再到“是的，它正在运行，而且我们——你知道——可以依靠它”。这让我们有机会去帮助培育那个社区。\n\n另外，你知道，自大约2018年、我想是这个时间段开始这项工作以来，我们已经把预算增加了大约50%。所以，你知道，这显示出我们对这项工作的真正承诺。这也显示出政府的承诺，对吧？因为NRO可以提出任何建议，但还有许多其他组织必须同意这确实具有价值。所以，你知道，美国政府正在表明这一点。随着我们继续前进，我们希望进一步扩大它。\n\nBingen女士：这是一个很好的需求信号。\n\nScolese博士：是的。\n\nHarding女士：是的。我只想再推荐一下Kari团队的精彩报告《2024年商业遥感全球排名》，我们亲切地称之为“卫星奥运会”。这是一份非常好的报告。你可以快速浏览，看看谁在什么领域取得了什么成果。你也可以看到，作为一个整体社区，我们仍然需要填补哪些空白。我认为，报告中真正给我留下深刻印象的一点，是与盟友的合作，以及这种与盟友的合作有多么重要。\n\n这实际上引出了我们的第一个问题，来自CSIS实习生Ianni Nikas：NRO将如何与国际盟友开展合作？\n\nScolese博士：嗯，我们正在与国际伙伴开展更多合作。我们一直以来都与国际社会保持某种关系。而且，这种关系正在不断发展，就像我刚才提到的商业合作正在发展一样。我们的伙伴拥有很强的能力。我们希望加以利用。他们也希望与我们合作。所以，这是一种——这是一种很好的关系。因此，我们正在大幅拓展这种关系。我认为，我们正在这样做这一事实，会让那些想阻止我们开展行动的人感到担忧。\n\n想想看，你知道，现在——通常我们从佛罗里达州和加利福尼亚州发射。过去四五年里，我们与新西兰的伙伴合作，从那里发射。我们从英国发射过。我们还从弗吉尼亚州的沃洛普斯岛发射过。所以，你知道，这体现了国际伙伴关系。它也体现了我们发射能力的多样化，这一点我们——你知道——可以谈谈。因为通常火箭上会有一个小标志，所以我不能说它不是NRO的。（笑声。）但是，你知道，我的意思是，这只是表明我们的国际合作已经扩展得多么广泛。\n\nHarding女士：是的。我们有一个来自《航空周刊》的Vivienne Machi提出的问题，我实际上要先把它交给Kari，因为我认为这既涉及你在商业航天领域的背景，也涉及你在政府领域的经历。\n\nBingen女士：我还以为今天我是主持人。\n\nHarding女士：（笑。）别想这么轻易脱身。所以，周二在CSIS，美国商业遥感公司的代表谈到了与美国政府合作时当前固有的挑战。您\n最近有人分享说，NRO如今正与更广泛的业界合作伙伴开展合作，其中包括初创企业和新航天公司。对于商业航天公司，NRO需要哪些是它目前还无法自行完成的？此外，从监管和政策角度看，要与这些供应商合作，还需要做些什么？所以我之所以让卡里先回答这个问题，是因为她曾经处在这个等式的两端。\n\n宾根女士：天哪，我还以为自己会是提问者。（笑声。）不，这是个很棒的问题。报告中我们强调的领域之一，就是整合这一概念。而且，克里斯，你今天的讨论中相当多地提到了这一点：你知道，只要商业能力被视为一个独立的东西，它就会成为成本中心，或者人们就不愿意使用它。但如果你能把它引入进来——你知道，从你正在考虑的那些初步尝试开始，一直到你已经建立深度合作关系的那些能力——如果你能获得这些能力，对它们进行试验，将它们整合进你的任务周期、你的行动，以及分析人员所使用的平台中，那么对他们来说，这就会更加无缝、更加一体化。他们也会看到商业领域所带来的价值。但我认为，就政府方面而言——我先为借用你的一些发言道个歉——你知道，政府提出的一些要求，商业市场可能并不要求，或者要求的方式不同。比如某些安全方面的要求、供应链方面的要求。是的，商业部门也关心这些，但政府对此确实非常、非常重视。不过我先把话题交给你。\n\n斯科莱塞博士：我认为卡里已经说得很到位了。\n\n哈丁女士：我就知道她会说得很好。\n\n斯科莱塞博士：所以我想从商业部门的角度补充的唯一一点是，你知道，我们一直在谈论空间系统、运载器，还有——嗯，我们一直在谈论提供数据的航天器。这比那些更广泛。所以卡里所说的一切，我们当然都需要，但还要把范围扩大。商业发射已经极大地帮助了我们。商业航天器让我们能够完成许多事情。它促成了我们的分布式架构。此外，还有我们所需要的数据中心，因为我们总是在谈论太空。云服务对于帮助我们拆分地面系统至关重要，这样我们就不会只有一个地面系统负责运行一切。现在我们可以进行拆分。\n\n在数据处理、数据科学和人工智能方面的发展，也都是我们可以加以利用的领域。而且，你知道，这些领域与商业部门和政府方面的需求之间有很多交集。所以我只是想把卡里所说的范围扩大，将这些也包括进去。这些就是我们可以着手利用的一些东西。\n\n哈丁女士：很好。好的。\n\n来自佛罗里达国际大学的豪尔赫·卡洛斯·阿里亚斯·德尔加多提问：作为一名工程师以及NRO主任，您职业生涯中最精彩的经历之一是什么？\n\n斯科莱塞博士：（笑。）嗯，作为NRO主任，我想我不能告诉你。（笑声。）\n\n不过，你知道，实际上，作为一个确实涵盖所有领域的人，你知道，我们——在我的整个职业生涯中，我一直非常幸运。我有机会做过一些真正很酷的事情——建造过观测地球、甚至飞出太阳系的卫星。你知道，那真是很酷。但你知道，另一件让我确实非常、非常幸运的事情是\n拥有的，就是与你合作，懂吗，都是非常优秀的人。你知道，都是杰出的人才。我每天都看到他们来上班。我在我们的实习生和其他所有人身上都看到了这一点。你知道，他们是一个团队。我待过的每个组织都是一个团队。人们怀着相同的目标努力，交付我们被要求交付的任何东西，并想出新颖而巧妙的方法去完成它。所以我会说，这就是我职业生涯真正的亮点：优秀的人做着伟大的事情。\n\n哈丁女士：那么，关于这一点，我们收到了几个问题，正是关于这个问题的。如果我想在国家侦察办公室工作，你们在寻找什么？你们希望你们的人具备什么？\n\n斯科尔塞女士：嗯，我们显然希望有聪明、敬业的人才——各个领域的人才。因为，我的意思是，我们必须建造、设计和操作卫星。所以显然，你知道，那包括我们的工程师、科学家和数学家。但我们还必须知道它们的成本，否则我们拿不到资金，也不知道资金流向哪里，那就糟糕了。我们需要合同官员。我们需要懂得如何招聘人员的人。所以基本上，我们需要各个学科的人来这里——来国家侦察办公室工作。但我们真正最想要的，是那些真正具有创新精神、真正致力于去做以前从未做过的事情并将其变为现实的人。我的意思是，这确实就是我们的工作：去交付所需的信息，因为技术会发生变化，而我们必须跟上它。但更重要的是，只要有人想伤害美国或我们的盟友，就会有人能够向我们发起挑战，无论是通过技术手段，还是用更好的方式隐藏事物。我们需要保持领先。因此我们总会很忙。我们总会不断创新。所以在国家侦察办公室，永远不会有无聊的一天。（笑声。）\n\n宾根女士：你能——我要接着回答豪尔赫的问题。我知道你不能告诉我们细节，但给我们一点提示。比如，真正处于前沿的是什么？国家侦察办公室正在做什么让你感到兴奋？对于那些想来国家侦察办公室工作的年轻电气工程师或社会科学家来说，你们在哪些方面真正突破了极限？他们将要做什么？\n\n斯科尔塞博士：很酷的东西。（笑声。）你知道，这非常困难。我的意思是，从系统角度来说，我们交付的东西是什么，这很难讲。但从技术角度来说，你绝对可以谈一些。我的意思是，想想我们在光学方面的需求。我是说，你们都知道我们在观察地球，对吧？我的意思是，你拍一张照片，我们一直在拍照片。必须有镜子。必须有镜头。必须有类似这样的东西。你不必以完全相同的方式制造它们。做这件事有很多很多不同的方法。我的意思是，看看哈勃太空望远镜，再看看詹姆斯·韦布，对吧？哈勃是一个大型整体式镜面。詹姆斯·韦布是一个更大的分段式镜面，对吧？我们需要做这类事情。你知道，想想我们刚才谈到的处理能力。想想把一块英伟达芯片、一个人工智能系统送入太空，对吧？这些都是我们必须去思考的事情。你知道，我们使用的探测器需要越来越灵敏。这意味着，你知道，固态——固态物理学对我们正在做的事情绝对至关重要。我们希望把东西做得更小。所以\n这是必要的。再想想我们必须着手使用的材料。现在你去谈论这些事情。你必须有经过抗辐射加固的材料。它们必须能够承受火箭上相对颠簸的旅程。它们必须能够部署。所以我们需要那里具备大量不同的能力。我们的运行环境相当严苛。再想想地面上，我们必须建立可靠的网络，而且还希望这些网络能够抵御网络攻击。因为那是另一份会不断带来麻烦的“礼物”，对吧？我们将不断地必须着手应对这个问题。再想想编程。我的意思是，我们总是——你知道，任何当过工程师或科学家的人，或者如今大概任何人，都编过程序。但我们必须获得值得信赖的可靠程序，对吧？那么我们该如何着手做到这一点？所以，这些就是我们正在从事的一些领域，至于我们如何把它们全部整合起来，这里先不谈。我们需要真正找到非常、非常优秀，而且愿意去做这些事情的人。哈丁女士：这是我最喜欢情报界以及与情报界合作的地方之一：人们会说这不可能，你做不到。然后他们会说，好，等着瞧。（笑声。）我们会想出办法的。我们会让它实现。所以我们有两个问题，主题都是与盟友合作，尤其是那些不属于“五眼”体系的盟友。来自 Tawas USA 的吉姆·施文克问道：您能分享一下关于与不属于“五眼”体系的盟友伙伴合作的想法吗？特别是目前有更多盟国太空司令部可供合作，能够通过它们的工业基础增强供应链和韧性。然后是来自我们 CSIS 的实习生雅各布·布拉德利的问题：您是否觉得我们与盟友和伙伴之间的信息共享和技术共享能力有所不足？也就是说，一个问题是我们如何开展合作，另一个问题是我们如何共享信息。斯科莱塞博士：是的。所以，你知道，我们的供应链现在是全球性的。这一点毫无疑问。而且，你知道，我们希望与盟友合作，因为我们可以对那里发生的事情抱有更高的信心和信任。所以，毫无疑问，我们正在各个层面开展合作，在可能的地方共享数据。你提到，目前有许多太空司令部。更重要的是，太空中有很多卫星——都在那里。我们越能协调在太空中的行动，尽管太空非常广阔——（笑）——我们仍然面临一些必须处理的挑战，以避免发生碰撞。我们共享的信息越多——而且我们确实在共享——通常，我们会与太空司令部合作，由他们负责太空管理，以完成这项工作。但这也包括所有希望在太空中负责任地开展行动的盟友和伙伴太空发展机构，这样我们就能够在那里开展行动。我认为这基本涵盖了这些。还有另一个问题是什么，关于挑战？哈丁女士：共享信息。斯科莱塞博士：是的。我的意思是，我们确实共享信息。现在，再说一次，这不是国家侦察局的职责。那不是我们的工作。我的意思是，我们提供信息。但我确实知道，我们会共享。你得问——哈丁女士：你答应过的，我们确实共享。斯科莱塞博士：\n是的，我们确实会共享。你得——你得去问问我在国家地理空间情报局和国家安全局的同事，了解这是怎么做到的。\n\n哈丁女士：好的。在我把话交还给卡里，让她做一些总结性发言之前，再听取一位观众的问题。我们本可以再涵盖15,000个话题，所以如果今天这一小时里还有什么我们大致上忘记了的，请提出来。\n\n但《国防日报》的卡尔·比泽克问道：关于自去年12月以来将发射的100颗卫星，这些是否全部为国家侦察局所有？这些新卫星将使你们能够做什么？\n\n斯科尔斯博士：答案是肯定的。如果你——如果你的意思是，国家侦察局将负责运行这些卫星，那么它们确实归国家侦察局所有。它们将做什么？它们是扩展式架构的一部分，用于以很高的速度获取地球的相当高分辨率图像。\n\n哈丁女士：以很高的速度。这就是“你无法躲藏”的那部分。是的，我们一直在——这就是所谓持续监视的理念。你可以持续获取地球上某个特定地点正在发生什么的图像，这一点非常重要，尤其是当你像我一样，是一名坐在办公桌前、试图弄清楚某件事是否已经发生的情报分析员时。这是无价的。但现在把话交给卡里，请她提出最后的问题并发表看法。\n\n宾根女士：嗯，我只是——你知道，当你退一步思考时，我的意思是，这个群体长期以来一直只有很少——很有限的库存，以满足各种不同需求。现在我们正走到这样一个阶段：你谈论的是数十颗到数百颗卫星，在很多方面，供应已经不再是问题。我开始想，天哪，这更像优步，而不是你知道的那种传统业务模式，对吧？（笑声。）那么，今后我们该如何思考这些不同类型的模式？或者说，我们将会如此严重地依赖你提到的处理技术、人工智能、自动化以及所有这些东西。\n\n我们就要结束了——这里我会让你作最后发言，但我想提几件事。克里斯，你在一开始提到过，你知道，这是国家侦察局。你们为高级领导层提供国家级情报、为作战人员提供战术情报，在这方面做了大量工作。我记得曾经——在我们组织国会代表团前往伊拉克和阿富汗时，那些处于前线边缘的人们正在接收来自国家侦察局卫星的数据。但你们在那里也确实承担着一项民用任务，因为你们还在支持如此广泛的客户，其中一些并不属于你们传统的国家安全圈。所以我想把这个问题抛给你。\n\n另外，我还想提到，本着更加透明、谈论该机构工作内容的精神，我们计划在这次讨论之后，继续与贵机构的领导团队探讨国家侦察局任务的各个方面。因此，我们非常期待未来在这座大楼里接待国家侦察局的其他领导人。不过，好吧，最后的问题是：提醒我们一下，除了国家安全之外，你们还做些什么？另外，展望未来，未来10到20年国家侦察局会是什么样子？让我们以此作结。\n\n斯科尔斯博士：好的。这问题问得相当不错。所以我还是要回到这一点：一切都关乎我们交付的数据，以及让合适的用户能够获取这些数据。所以对于民用群体来说，我的意思是，如果你想想其中一些\n他们所面临的挑战，以及他们需要快速、可靠信息的场景，是发生自然灾害时。通常情况下，你的通信能力有限，在各处移动的能力也有限。你首先想知道灾情有多严重，以及有哪些路线可以通行，以便把资源送到需要资源的民众和社区手中。如果你拥有数据，并且能以所需的速度提供这些数据，就可以迅速做到这一点。这是国家侦察局（NRO）所做工作的一部分，同时也支持其他活动。你知道，我们在为气候变化之类的问题提供数据方面所发挥的作用有限。但我想说，这正是我们参与其中的一个完美例子。\n\n你知道，未来10年或20年会是什么样子？你知道，我总会想起那些预测未来的人。阿瑟·C·克拉克有句很好的话；如果你不知道，可以去查查。这通常涉及预测错误。但我相信，国家侦察局仍将需要了解这个星球上正在发生什么、人类在做什么，以及这个星球在发生什么。我们将需要继续适应，以应对这些情况；我们谈到的一些事情也将继续存在。但我唯一确知的是，在那里——或者说在外面；也许也在上面——从事这些工作的人，将会是不同的一代人。我们需要继续吸纳真正出色的人才加入其中，确保能够提供10年或20年后所需的信息，而那时所需的信息将不同于今天所需的信息。\n\n宾根女士：艾米丽和我都很幸运，曾经能够走进您领导的机构，在那里参观。正如您所说，那些工程师、科学家以及在那里工作的其他人，我的意思是，他们都很高兴在那里工作。使命和技术确实令人振奋。所以，感谢您所展现的领导力，以及您为保障国家安全所做的一切。\n\n我们还想感谢今天为本次活动提供支持的一些工作人员——克里斯蒂娜·诺德比、肯德拉·拉法夫，以及我们这里出色的直播和广播团队。\n\n再次感谢您，斯科莱塞博士。\n\n斯科莱塞博士：谢谢。\n\n哈丁女士：谢谢。（掌声。）\n\n（结束。）\n\n标签\n北美，\n太空与航空航天，\n技术，\n国防与安全，以及\n国防战略与能力\n\n项目与计划\n国际安全项目\n航空航天安全项目\n情报、国家安全与技术项目\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-10-03T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2912","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国国家科学基金会是国家安全的关键支柱——现在不是解除武装的时候","title_en":"The NSF Is a Key National Security Pillar—Now Is Not the Time to Disarm","summary_cn":"CSIS评论文章指出，美国国家科学基金会（NSF）成立75年来一直是美国科技竞争力和国家安全的关键支柱。面对中国在科技领域的加速投入，作者认为NSF在基础研究、应用研究、人才培养和技术商业化方面发挥着不可替代的作用，其新设的技术、创新与伙伴关系理事会（TIP）正推动区域创新引擎和AI测试平台等举措。文章警告预算短缺、人员削减和领导层不确定性可能削弱该机构应对中国挑战的能力，呼吁加强而非削弱国家创新能力。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary argues that the National Science Foundation (NSF), created 75 years ago, remains a key pillar of U.S. technological competitiveness and national security. Amid China's accelerating science and technology investments, the authors contend that NSF plays an irreplaceable role in basic and applied research, workforce development, and technology commercialization, with its new Directorate for Technology, Innovation and Partnerships (TIP) spearheading initiatives such as Regional Innovation Engines and AI testbeds. The article warns that budget shortfalls, potential workforce reductions, and leadership uncertainty could cripple the agency's ability to meet the challenge from China, urging the United States to strengthen rather than weaken its national innovation capabilities.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:47:06.016427+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/nsf-key-national-security-pillar-now-not-time-disarm","content":"The NSF Is a Key National Security Pillar—Now Is Not the Time to Disarm\nPhoto: VanderWolf Images via Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nSujai Shivakumar\nand\nJulie Heng\nPublished May 1, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nSeventy-five years ago, Congress created the National Science Foundation (NSF) with a sweeping mandate: “to promote the progress of science; to advance the national health, prosperity, and welfare; and to secure the national defense.” That last clause, often overlooked, has never been more relevant than today.As China aggressively accelerates its investments in science and technology and increasingly masters the most advanced technologies, the United States finds itself in a new era of strategic competition, one increasingly defined not by military strength alone, but by technological capability. President Xi Jinping understands the contest and has made no secret of China’s ambitions, repeatedly calling science and technology the “main battlefield” of international competition. China has executed this strategy with the focus and resources of a real war: It includes massive research and development (R&D) spending, coordinated industrial policy, and anticompetitive trade practices backed by a scale of STEM talent development unmatched anywhere in the world. As a direct result, China’s R&D system is becoming more coordinated, more intense, and more aligned with national security objectives. And those who think China cannot innovate are unburdened by the facts. China has become the world’s top producer of science and engineering publications and patents, and excels at the rapid commercialization of innovative products.President Donald Trump has identified that “as our global competitors race to exploit these technologies, it is a national security imperative for the United States to achieve and maintain unquestioned and unchallenged global technological dominance.” However, the United States has not sufficiently recognized that its expansive innovation capacity is key to its ability to compete with China. Without intelligence-informed research, realistic threat assessments, and reliable early commercialization pathways for our innovations, the United States cannot maintain global leadership in science and technology.This is where the NSF must be recognized for what it truly is: a key pillar of our technological competitiveness and, therefore, of our national security. Maintaining and expanding this strategic capability requires stable and substantial support for both basic and applied research and, in the process, training the future workforce, all of which are fundamental to U.S. innovation and technological superiority.Unfortunately, this role is not widely appreciated. For decades, the NSF has been the quiet engine of U.S. power, catalyzing discoveries and inventions that have defined the modern world, from wireless communications to MRI machines to GPS, Doppler radar, the detection of explosives, and supercomputers. It has de-risked emerging technologies long before private capital would engage, and it has delivered unmatched returns on public investments. It was a fraction of a $4.5 million NSF award that supported two Stanford graduate students with an unlikely idea that gave us Google, transforming how the world works and lives.This was not an accident: It is what the NSF does. Some 94 percent of the NSF budget is allocated to research projects, facilities, and STEM education. NSF-funded research spans all 50 states, and nearly 2,000 academic, public, and private institutions receive merit-based support. Indeed, some estimates show that the NSF funds 24 percent of all federally funded research at U.S. universities. In addition to academic research, the NSF also funds small business innovation, supports work at national laboratories across the country, and develops the workforce of tomorrow. Through its Advanced Technological Education program, for example, the NSF has granted over 500 community colleges a combined $1.5 billion towards workforce readiness, including agricultural tech training.In fact, recognizing the critical importance of strengthening the national security-innovation link, the NSF recently launched its first new directorate in over 30 years: the Directorate for Technology, Innovation and Partnerships (TIP), an idea that originated in the first Trump administration and was underutilized during the Biden years, but now could be revitalized with a national security focus.TIP’s mission is clear: expand the United States’ capacity to translate research into national advantage. TIP is already spearheading initiatives like the Regional Innovation Engines, local hubs for economic growth through technologies like advanced semiconductors in Central Florida, aerospace and robotics in El Paso, and advanced agriculture in Fargo. It has launched national AI-ready testbeds, including the National Quantum Virtual Laboratory, which accelerates the path from lab to market; SECURE Analytics, a data project that protects U.S. research from foreign interference and espionage; and entrepreneurship fellowships that ensure the United States’ brightest minds have the support to build at home rather than abroad.But the NSF’s goals to accelerate technology development and prepare the workforce of tomorrow are now under threat. Budget shortfalls, potential workforce reductions, and leadership uncertainty—including the resignation of NSF Director Sethuraman Panchanathan on April 24—could cripple the agency’s ability to meet the pressing challenge from China.Abandoning what is unquestionably the main battlefield of the twenty-first century would be a strategic mistake of historic proportions. China is actively testing America’s will to lead in science and technology. Meanwhile, other countries are rapidly moving to poach U.S. talent by luring the country’s best minds with offers of stable positions and serious funding.Certainly, the NSF can do more to transparently align public R&D spending with national security goals. Its governance structure has faced criticism for being siloed, but there’s real opportunity for its directorates to better coordinate strategic investments across the agency and to partner more proactively with national security R&D counterparts in other federal agencies. Now is the time to strengthen—not weaken—our national innovation capabilities, the path to our prosperity and our security.At last month’s Endless Frontiers Retreat, Science Advisor to the President Michael Kratsios emphasized the urgent need for research security, early-stage commercialization, and strong public-private partnerships. What better place to underscore these priorities than at a retreat named after a visionary report that laid the groundwork for the creation of the NSF? The report underscored that uncoordinated scientific excellence is not enough; strategic leadership and sustained investment at scale are required. Quite simply, we cannot afford to fall behind in today’s tech competition—like in any race, it will be very hard to catch up.The NSF is an agency for technological competitiveness and a key to U.S. national security. Now is not the time to disarm. The United States has always thrived when it meets emerging threats with bold vision and strategic investment.Sujai Shivakumar is the director and senior fellow of Renewing American Innovation at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Julie Heng is a research associate with Renewing American Innovation at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nTechnology and Innovation,\nAmerican Innovation,\nEconomic Security,\nGeopolitics and International Security,\nGlobal Markets,\nStrategic Capital, and\nTechnology\nImage\nSujai Shivakumar\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Renewing American Innovation\nImage\nJulie Heng\nAdjunct Fellow (Non-resident), Renewing American Innovation\nPrograms & Projects\nRenewing American Innovation\nEconomic Security and Technology\nR&D Infrastructure & Innovation","content_cn":"NSF是国家安全的关键支柱——现在不是解除武装的时候\n照片：VanderWolf Images via Adobe Stock\n评论文章\n作者\nSujai Shivakumar\n和\nJulie Heng\n发表于2025年5月1日\n远程可视化\n\n75年前，美国国会赋予国家科学基金会（NSF）一项广泛使命：“促进科学进步；增进国家健康、繁荣与福祉；并维护国家防务。”最后这一条常被忽视，但它从未像今天这样具有现实意义。随着中国大力加快对科学技术的投资，并日益掌握最先进的技术，美国正进入一个新的战略竞争时代。这个时代的定义越来越不只是军事力量，而是技术能力。习近平主席理解这场竞争，也毫不掩饰中国的雄心，多次称科学技术是国际竞争的“主战场”。中国以真正战争般的专注和资源执行这一战略：包括巨额研发（R&D）投入、协调一致的产业政策，以及由全球无可匹敌的STEM人才培养规模支撑的反竞争性贸易做法。直接结果是，中国的研发体系正变得更加协调、更加高强度，也更加符合国家安全目标。那些认为中国无法创新的人，不过是没有事实负担。中国已成为全球科学与工程论文及专利产出最多的国家，并擅长快速实现创新产品的商业化。\n\n唐纳德·特朗普总统指出：“当我们的全球竞争对手竞相利用这些技术时，美国实现并维持不容置疑、无人挑战的全球技术主导地位，是一项国家安全要务。”然而，美国尚未充分认识到，其广泛的创新能力是其与中国竞争的关键。没有情报支持的研究、现实的威胁评估，以及可靠的创新早期商业化路径，美国就无法维持在科学技术领域的全球领导地位。\n\nNSF正是应当被这样认识的机构：它是我们技术竞争力的关键支柱，因此也是国家安全的关键支柱。维持并扩大这一战略能力，需要对基础研究和应用研究提供稳定且充足的支持，同时培养未来劳动力；这些都是美国创新和技术优势的根本。\n\n遗憾的是，这一作用并未得到广泛认可。数十年来，NSF一直是美国实力的无声引擎，催化了定义现代世界的发现和发明，从无线通信、磁共振成像设备，到GPS、多普勒雷达、爆炸物探测和超级计算机。早在私人资本愿意介入之前，它就已为新兴技术降低风险，并为公共投资带来无可匹敌的回报。NSF一笔450万美元资助中的一小部分，曾支持斯坦福大学两名研究生的一个不太可能实现的想法，这个想法后来造就了谷歌，改变了世界的工作和生活方式。\n\n这并非偶然：这正是NSF的职责所在。NSF预算约94%用于研究项目、设施和STEM教育。NSF资助的研究遍及美国50个州，近2,000\n学术、公共和私营机构都能获得基于绩效的支持。事实上，一些估计显示，NSF资助了美国大学所有联邦资助研究的24%。除了学术研究，NSF还资助小企业创新，支持全国各地国家实验室的工作，并培养未来的劳动力。例如，通过其先进技术教育计划，NSF已向500多所社区学院拨款总计15亿美元，用于劳动力准备工作，包括农业技术培训。\n\n事实上，认识到加强国家安全与创新之间联系的关键重要性，NSF最近推出了30多年来首个新局：技术、创新与伙伴关系局（TIP）。这一构想源于特朗普第一届政府时期，在拜登任期内未得到充分利用，但现在可能在国家安全导向下重新焕发生机。\n\nTIP的使命很明确：扩大美国将研究成果转化为国家优势的能力。TIP已经在牵头推进区域创新引擎等计划，在当地建设经济增长中心，推动中佛罗里达州的先进半导体、埃尔帕索的航空航天与机器人技术，以及法戈的先进农业等技术。它还启动了面向人工智能的全国测试平台，包括国家量子虚拟实验室，该实验室加快了从实验室到市场的进程；SECURE Analytics数据项目，保护美国研究免受外国干预和间谍活动影响；以及创业奖学金，确保美国最优秀的人才能够获得支持，在国内而非海外创业发展。\n\n但NSF加速技术发展、培养未来劳动力的目标如今正受到威胁。预算缺口、可能的人员削减以及领导层不确定性——包括NSF主任塞图拉曼·潘查纳坦于4月24日辞职——可能会削弱该机构应对中国紧迫挑战的能力。\n\n放弃这个无疑是二十一世纪主战场的领域，将是具有历史性规模的战略错误。中国正在积极测试美国在科学和技术领域领导世界的意志。与此同时，其他国家正迅速采取行动，通过向美国最优秀的人才提供稳定职位和大量资金，挖走美国人才。\n\n当然，NSF还可以做得更多，使公共研发支出与国家安全目标之间的衔接更加透明。其治理结构因各自为政而受到批评，但各部门确实有机会更好地协调全机构范围内的战略投资，并更主动地与其他联邦机构中负责国家安全研发的对口部门开展合作。现在是加强而非削弱国家创新能力的时候了——这是我们实现繁荣与安全的道路。\n\n在上月举行的“无尽前沿”务虚会上，总统科学顾问迈克尔·克拉齐奥斯强调了研究安全、早期商业化以及强有力的公私合作的迫切必要性。还有什么地方比在一份以富有远见的报告命名的务虚会上更适合强调这些优先事项呢？正是那份报告为创建NSF奠定了基础。报告强调，缺乏协调的科学卓越并不足够；还需要战略领导和持续的大规模投资。简而言之，我们无法承受在当今的技术\n竞争——就像任何比赛一样，要追赶上来将会非常困难。NSF 是一个促进技术竞争力的机构，也是美国国家安全的关键。现在不是解除武装的时候。美国一直在面对新出现的威胁时，以大胆的愿景和战略性投资蓬勃发展。Sujai Shivakumar 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）“重振美国创新”项目的主任兼高级研究员。Julie Heng 是 CSIS“重振美国创新”项目的研究助理。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS 是一家私立、免税机构，专注于国际公共政策问题。其研究具有非党派性，不为任何专有利益服务。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n技术与创新，\n美国创新，\n经济安全，\n地缘政治与国际安全，\n全球市场，\n战略资本，以及\n技术\n图片\nSujai Shivakumar\n“重振美国创新”项目主任兼高级研究员\n图片\nJulie Heng\n“重振美国创新”项目兼职研究员（非驻所）\n项目与计划\n重振美国创新\n经济安全与技术\n研发基础设施与创新","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-05-01T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3624","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"核武器与外交政策：与众议院情报委员会主席迈克·特纳的对话","title_en":"Nuclear Weapons and Foreign Policy: A Conversation with HPSCI Chairman Mike Turner","summary_cn":"2024年6月20日，战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）举办活动，邀请美国众议院情报委员会主席迈克·特纳讨论核武器与外交政策。特纳重点警告俄罗斯正在研发核反卫星武器，称其若在低地球轨道引爆将造成灾难性后果，并批评拜登政府应对迟缓。他还谈及中国核力量扩张、对乌援助及情报界改革等议题。","summary_en":"On June 20, 2024, CSIS hosted House Intelligence Committee Chairman Mike Turner to discuss nuclear weapons and foreign policy. Turner warned that Russia is developing a nuclear anti-satellite weapon, calling its potential detonation in low Earth orbit catastrophic, and criticized the Biden administration's slow response. He also addressed China's nuclear expansion, aid to Ukraine, and intelligence community reforms.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:36:22.542228+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/nuclear-weapons-and-foreign-policy-conversation-hpsci-chairman-mike-turner","content":"Nuclear Weapons and Foreign Policy: A Conversation with HPSCI Chairman Mike Turner\nPhoto: CSIS\nTranscript\n— June 20, 2024\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Transcript\n237kb\nImage\nPhoto: CSIS\nNuclear Weapons and Foreign Policy: A Conversation with HPSCI Chairman Mike Turner\nJune 20, 2024 • 11:00 am – 12:00 pm EDT\nHosted by Project on Nuclear Issues\nThis transcript is from a CSIS event hosted on June 20, 2024. Watch the full video here.Heather Williams: Right. Good morning. Thank you all for joining us. I’m Heather Williams. I’m the director of the Project on Nuclear Issues, affectionately known as PONI, here at CSIS. And we are really excited to have you join us today for a conversation with Congressman Mike Turner, Chairman of the House Permanent Subcommittee on Intelligence.I’d actually like to begin by first thanking Chairman Turner for so much of his time. Immediately before this event, he spent an hour meeting with the PONI nuclear scholars who are also in the room today. And this is a group of next-generation nuclear experts. And Chairman Turner kindly shared his views on a lot of – a lot of the issues that we’re going to hear more about, along with some professional advice. And so just really grateful for all the time that you’ve given us today.Before we begin today’s event, I do need to share with you our building safety precautions. Overall, we feel secure in our building. As a convener, we have a duty to prepare for any eventuality. I will be your responsible safety officer at this event. And please follow my instructions if the need arises. And finally, please familiarize yourself with the emergency exit pathways. They’re probably going to be behind you or at the sides there.So PONI and the Aerospace Security Project are really honored to have Representative Michael Turner with us today to talk about a variety of strategic challenges, including the Russian ASAT threat, along with the role of nuclear weapons in U.S. foreign policy. And I know all of you in this room know, this conversation is coming at a really important moment for U.S. policymakers as they face crucial decisions about nuclear modernization and also about the future of arms control and the role of nuclear weapons in U.S. foreign policy. But also, the American public is reengaging with nuclear weapons in a way that we really haven’t seen since the Cold War. So, this is a very important moment for this conversation.The 2023 bipartisan Strategic Posture Commission highlighted a, quote, “sense of urgency” – a sense of urgency around strategic issues. And just before we start the conversation, I want to highlight three of those. First is the sense of urgency around the U.S. nuclear enterprise and modernization. The Strategic Posture Commission report found that current modernization plans are, quote, “necessary but not sufficient.” And they recommended the current modernization program should be supplemented to ensure U.S. nuclear strategy remains effective in a two nuclear peer environment.More recently, just a – just a week or two ago, National Security Council Senior Director Pranay Vaddi stated that absent a change in the trajectory of adversary arsenals, we may reach a point in the coming years where an increase from the current deployed numbers is required. The report also called for the U.S. to address concerns from allies regarding extended deterrence, where there’s an ongoing debate among allied countries on their nuclear options and the credibility of the U.S. deterrent.A second important trend from the Strategic Posture Commission report is about the worsening security environment and adversaries’ build-up of strategic systems. While America prioritized arms control and nuclear reductions, our adversaries moved in the opposite direction and took advantage of that moment. To highlight just one example, which the Chairman was involved in reviewing, was the Russian ASAT threat, which the administration acknowledged could be a violation of the 1967 Outer Space Treaty. China continues to expand its nuclear arsenal, including production of fissile material, and the Department of Defense anticipates China’s nuclear arsenal will reach 1,000 operational nuclear warheads by 2030.A final topic requiring a sense of urgency is in the search for solutions, potentially to include arms control. After a meeting in November of last year, China has declined for follow-on talks with the United States on this topic, and Russia has backed out of several arms control commitments, including suspension of the New START treaty, violations of the INF Treaty, and de-ratification of the Comprehensive Test Ban Treaty. So overall, these raise really important questions, many of which are time-sensitive, on the role of U.S. nuclear weapons in foreign policy and strategy. And that’s why we are so thrilled to have Chairman Turner here today to offer his insights and thoughts, to include the potential role for the IC in addressing these new strategic challenges.To quickly go through Chairman Turner’s biography, Congressman Michael Turner was first elected to Congress in 2002. In Congress Turner is a senior member of the House Armed Services Committee and has previously served as the lead Republican of the Tactical Air and Land Forces Subcommittee, as well as the Strategic Forces Subcommittee. The subcommittees oversee Army and Air Force acquisition programs, all Navy and Marine Corps aviation programs, nuclear weapons, missile defense, and space systems. After serving on the House Permanent Select Committee on Intelligence since 2015, Congressman Turner was elected as the chairman of the House Permanent Select Committee on Intelligence. The committee has oversight of the nation’s 17 intelligence agencies. He was the president of the NATO Parliamentary Assembly, and he now serves as the vice chairman of the Defense and Security Committee of the NATO Parliamentary Assembly. An additional fun fact about Chairman Turner is that he was mayor of Dayton, Ohio, during the Dayton Peace Accords. So, bringing a lot of expertise is an understatement to today’s session.But this will be moderated by Kari Bingen, who is director of the Aerospace Security Project and senior fellow at the International Security Program here at CSIS. Representative Turner will provide his keynote address followed by a fireside chat with Kari and questions from the audience. If you would like to submit a question you should have a QR code either on your chair or there’s a giant one right behind me. Please just scan this on your phone and submit questions and Kari will field them as they come in.So, with that, Chairman Turner, I’d like to turn it over to you for your remarks. Thank you. (Applause.)Representative Michael Turner (R-OH): Well, thank you, and good morning. I want to thank CSIS for giving me this opportunity, for Kari Bingen to be leading this and for her leadership on the issue of nuclear weapons and the risks that our nation faces.The Space Age began when Russia launched Sputnik in 1957. The eyes of the world turned to the sky and wondered how space and technology would now change life on Earth. The Space Age will end when Russia launches its nuclear anti-satellite weapon into orbit. General Saltzman, chief of staff of the United States Space Force, has referred to the potential launch date of Russia’s nuclear anti-satellite weapon as day zero, because from that day no one can count on space the next day. From that day forward, the assumption on Earth must be, in order to preserve our economic, social, and military structures, that we must have an alternative to space.Right now, there isn’t one. Trillions and trillions of dollars and time that we don’t have will be required to build duplicative and redundant systems just to preserve what we have accomplished in the Space Age. For some things, no alternative exists. The United Nations Outer Space Treaty – entered into force in 1967, signed by the Russian Federation, the United States, and the United Kingdom – declared, “States shall not place nuclear weapons or other weapons of mass destruction in orbit or on celestial bodies or station them in outer space in any other manner.” The treaty was entered into based upon the promise to mankind of advances on planet Earth that might arise from the exploration and utilization of space. The signatories could not have imagined a world of today where agriculture, medicine, commercial transactions, communications, maritime navigation, international security, and even our ability to tell time is space-dependent.Similarly, we cannot imagine a world not space-dependent. On day zero, at the end of the space age, we will have to. The Biden administration, only after having been challenged by a group of bipartisan members of the House Intelligence Committee, reluctantly declassified that Russia is developing a nuclear antisatellite weapon intended to be placed in orbit in outer space. No additional information has been released.News reports have speculated that the weapon is past development, it exists and is preparing to launch. Additional news reports have speculated that Russia already has a satellite in orbit as a test. First phase of Russia’s antisatellite nuclear weapon system. Without confirming or denying the accuracy of any of these reports, the questions they raise must be answered by the Biden administration immediately, regardless of Russia’s timing or the possible immediate impact of this evolving threat. This crisis is the Cuban Missile Crisis in space. And the administration is failing. The advances that mankind has made during the space age are at risk, and the administration is sleepwalking into an irreversible day zero.Dr. Plumb, former assistant secretary of defense for space policy, testified before the House Armed Services Committee that Russia’s antinuclear satellite – Russia’s nuclear antisatellite weapon, if detonated in low Earth orbit, would indiscriminately decimate all satellites within low Earth orbit, and would render that orbit in space unusable for likely at least a year. Mankind would be unable to repopulate low Earth orbit satellites during this period, and manned space exploration would be deadly. This threat would mean that our economic, international security, and social systems come to a grinding halt. This would be a catastrophic and devastating attack upon Western economic and democratic systems. Vladimir Putin knows this. Checkmate.Day zero can be avoided. Imagine how different the world would have been if President Kennedy had allowed Khrushchev to place nuclear weapons in Cuba. Europe would not be free. The United States would have been too fearful to challenge Russia and Europe with nuclear weapons just off the coast of Florida. Just as Khrushchev could have held the United States hostage with nuclear threats from Cuba, Vladimir Putin will hold the world’s space assets hostage to counter attempts to stop him from reassembling the Soviet Union.There is precedent for Russian-United States nuclear weapons control treaties, to include the dismantling of destabilizing weapons, inspection regimes, and prohibitions against deployment. But such treaties are negotiated through strength, something the Biden administration seems incapable of showing. In order to avoid day zero, the Biden administration must immediately declassify all known information concerning the status of Russia’s nuclear antisatellite weapons program. Vladimir Putin thrives in secrecy. Putin’s plans and weapons programs must be fully disclosed by the administration and understood by the world. In addition, the United States and its NATO allies must join together to declare the resolve to enforce the U.N. Outer Space Treaty. I call on the Biden administration to do so.There are risks in confronting Russia. The Biden administration is incredibly reluctant to take any action that would appear to be escalatory. However, Russia is the escalatory aggressor. Escalation has already occurred. Now the United States must stand with our allies to stop day zero and preserve space as the U.N. Outer Space Treaty intended, for the betterment of all mankind. Thank you. (Applause.)Kari A. Bingen: Chairman Turner, those were incredibly sobering remarks for all of us. I do want to start out by saying that I was fortunate to work with you for many years on the House Armed Services Committee and when you were chairman of the Strategic Forces Subcommittee. And what really struck me was how often you were right in the middle and leading on tackling really tough issues, complex issues – missile defense, New START, nuclear modernization – and I always appreciated your and the committee’s bipartisan approach.You took your briefings together, you took your trips together to get firsthand knowledge, and you were voracious in consuming information to make sure you really had all aspects of the issue understood.And I’ve seen you take that same approach with the House Intelligence Committee, and we’ll talk more about what the intelligence committee is doing. So, I will start on the Russia nuclear anti-satellite threat, but we’ll also cover nuclear deterrence, other international security issues, and the Intelligence Committee’s priorities.I will remind everyone here in person scan the QR code if you have questions, and for folks online go to the event web page and you can also submit questions there.So, again, incredibly sobering remarks about day zero. You see all the intelligence, all the threat assessments. Russia has long had an ability to detonate nuclear weapons in space via their ballistic missiles.What makes this threat today different and why is it so significant, and why did you take that unusual step back in February to raise this serious national security threat? You took flak from members in your own party, from the other side of the aisle. Why did you do that?Rep. Turner: Well, first off, I want to begin by thanking Kari because in addition to being an incredible expert on all of these issues you have always been a mentor and an educator on these issues. It’s great that you’re here at CSIS because you’re continuing that. Thank you for including me and to be able to have a dialog and a discussion about these issues.One of the aspects of your work is not just the policy that you’ve helped to effect, but the fact that you really want to educate others to ensure the dialogue on nuclear weapons and deterrence. I mean, this is the greatest destructive force known to man and the fact that you advocate for, you know, knowledge, understanding, and policy debates really is extraordinary. So, thank you for doing that.First off, there’s bipartisan, you know, overwhelming work of the House Intelligence Committee to call on the administration to make public that this threat was emerging. The fact that it took the House Intelligence Committee to do so I think shows you some of the – really, the waste of time that the administration has had – the fact that they don’t have a response to this.You’ve raised the issue of that, you know, Russia could take an ICBM and explode it into space and, perhaps, destroy that the satellites that are there and, you know, why is this different.Well, it’s different enough that they are undertaking to do this, right? The administration says that they’re developing this and what I call on them is to tell the world what is the status of this, and development – does that mean that this is drawing on a – you know, on a drafting board?Does this mean this is a research project in a lab? Does this mean that it’s something in a manufacturing facility? Does it mean that there’s nuclear warheads that have been created? Is there a satellite? Is there a missile?All of these are the natural resulting questions from the administration and they’re avoiding this discussion because they’re avoiding having to admit that they’re not really doing anything.The outcome that was different in the Cuban missile crisis is that we had President Kennedy. We need leadership by the administration, and we certainly need a dialog worldwide to understand and call out, really, what are the Russians doing and what should the response be.Ms. Bingen: Well, and that leads into my next question here. There is likely exquisite intelligence sources and methods involved in collecting this kind of information. So, you’re calling for the declassification of intelligence.What are – and you just highlighted some, but what are some of the specifics that the American public, that policymakers and others, need to understand? What are we missing? But then also how do you weigh that against protecting those sensitive sources and methods?Rep. Turner: Right. This should not be permitted to go into orbit, period, and the administration does not even have that on their to-do list. Part of informing what would be the to-do list is the issue of what is this – what do they mean by development, that Russia is developing an anti-satellite nuclear weapon?Now, obviously, you know, they’ve been engaged with the intelligence committees and, certainly, we’re aware of what this evolving threat looks like. The administration, though, I believe is reticent to have the discussion because they don’t want to admit that they’re not doing anything, and we are sleepwalking into what will be an irreversible day zero effect.The aspect of this being in space is not just, well, it’s there in space; it’s not destructive unless they use it. No, it’s destructive on day one – as General Saltzman said, day zero, which is the next day, because suddenly we will not be able to count on any of the systems that are there. And if we can’t count on those systems, we’re going to have to construct alternative systems or literally it would be catastrophic economically, militarily, communication, to society.Ms. Bingen: So given everything you know, you think there are ways to convey more information about this program without revealing sensitive sources and methods.Rep. Turner: Absolutely.Ms. Bingen:OK. And then all of this leads into the question of: Now what? So, we have this insight. The U.S. government has sought to increase international pressure on Russia with other international partners – a U.N. resolution in April sponsored by the U.S. and Japan reaffirming the Outer Space Treaty, which Russia vetoed, and China abstained. So, you know, the question, I think, now becomes – and you were hinting at this – is, what actions do you think we are going to be willing to take to uphold the Outer Space Treaty and prevent this from happening?Rep. Turner: Right. So, I liken the administration going to the U.N. Security Council on this issue as the principal of a school going to the student body to have a labor dispute with their teachers. The aspect here is that the administration needs to exert leadership. We have allies. We have – I mean, the understanding by the world of what this weapon means and what its outcomes would result in needs to be discussed by the administration as they garner what would be worldwide support in opposition to Russia placing a nuclear weapon in space in violation of the space weapons treaty.Ms. Bingen: China now has a lot to lose in space. Is there room for us to work with China and perhaps others?Rep. Turner: Absolutely. I don’t think – I don’t think that the administration has done enough to inform, which is why I’m calling for the administration to inform both our other adversaries and allies as to what this threat is. You can’t garner support for something that you’re not discussing, and I firmly believe that the administration is avoiding discussing this topic because they don’t want the gap between what they should be doing and what they are doing to be publicly discussed.Ms. Bingen: Well, if I think about the DIME framework, we’re pursuing diplomatic and international pressure options. Are economic sanctions on the table? Should military options be on the table? You oversee intelligence capabilities. What should be on the table here?Rep. Turner: Well, I think all of it. I think – the biggest sign, I think, would be – I mean, this is so catastrophic. Secretary of Defense Austin took – you know, in front of the Armed Services Committee said this would be catastrophic. Well, if this is catastrophic, there should be someone every day in the administration that gets up to make certain that this isn’t occurring. And there is no one who has that responsibility. And there’s no one who’s executing that to-do list. This is something they’re aware of. This is something that they’ve informed us that Russia is undertaking. But this is not a priority of the administration. This needs to be a priority.Ms. Bingen: And how well is the intelligence community, I guess I’ll say, postured to understand the intricacies of this threat?Rep. Turner: Well, I think enough so that the administration was willing to come forward and say that Russia is developing an antisatellite nuclear weapon. I mean, the administration has confirmed this. This is not speculative. This is not something that people are, at this point, having differing opinions. There probably are differing opinions as to what needs to be done, but that’s the dialogue that we need to be having. What should be done? And the administration is not having that debate within itself, or with the American public, or worldwide with our allies.Ms. Bingen:  Well, and it’s interesting, as chairman of the Intelligence Committee, I just think you see – you see everything. You have this global picture of the security environment. This is obviously one very sobering and consequential threat here. But there are a lot of other things happening in the world, I’m assuming, are consuming a lot of folks’ bandwidth as well. So, you know, how do you – if you can take a step back for us and look at that global security environment, what else are you worried about? But then also, how do you see this threat in the context of everything else happening right now in the Middle East, with China, technology advancements, et cetera? Rep. Turner: Well, I don’t know. I think I’d start with if there’s something that’s going to touch every human being on the planet, that’s probably something that should be at the top of the list. And I think that’s one that I think the administration, in focusing, needs to understand that there should not be tolerated that this be in orbit. I honestly think that there may be portions of the administration who believes that we can tolerate this in space. As General Saltzman said, you can’t tolerate this in space because the next day, day zero, you have to assume that none of the space capabilities that we have are available the next day. And you’re going to have to plan for alternative uses, alternative sources, which we – which we don’t have. Which is what I mentioned in the speech, you know, trillions and trillions of dollars and certainly time we don’t have.But there are threats, obviously. You look at both on the nuclear side – China increasing its nuclear weapons inventory, North Korea doing the same, Russia fielding exotics, which – and both China and Russia moving to hypersonics. Even in the nuclear threat that’s not space, you have the capabilities gaps between what the United States is currently doing and what our adversaries are certainly doing. And that creates a vulnerability. And then, of course, you have the conflicts of Russia’s aggression against Ukraine, and Israel-Gaza, and the, really, Iranian influence with Hezbollah, Hamas, and the Houthis, that all needs to be addressed with respect to Iranian aggression.Ms. Bingen: It’s interesting, to your point here, in some respects it shouldn’t surprise us that Russia is doing this, given all the other modernization efforts it has underway in its nuclear forces – whether it be the cruise missile, the undersea vehicle, the ICBMs. You know, why not space? But, man, it’s at the extreme end of the space threat spectrumRep. Turner: Right. If you take Avangard, their hypersonic; Skyfall, their nuclear weapons cruise missile that orbits the Earth; Poseidon, the underwater unmanned missile that can surface, with the intention of destruction of coastal cities; all of those would have been not even imaginable 15 years ago. But it certainly shows, as you were just indicating, an intent on the part of Russia to significantly invest in nuclear weapons capabilities. And we need to be able to respond.Ms. Bingen: And if I can pivot a bit here to talking specifically on nuclear modernization, you know, I would observe there are few members of Congress that really understand nuclear weapons and nuclear deterrence. How do you explain this to other members of Congress, of what those threats are, why do we need to modernize our nuclear weapons and infrastructure, and what really is at stake?Rep. Turner: Right. I think, you know, the most common analogy that people use is just your own personal vehicle. You know, I have a 1964 Cadillac convertible. I wouldn’t use it to commute every day. You upgrade, you look to increase technology, if you think of how many different iPhones you’ve thrown away and replaced. But yet, we look at decades-old technology as our nuclear weapons deterrent and assume that, you know, we have a nuclear weapon, we don’t – we don’t need to modernize. Well, of course we do. They decay. Their capabilities and their technology age. And our adversaries’ capabilities increase, which results in vulnerabilities.It really should be a continuous process, not something we park in a garage and come back decades later and say, let’s kick the tires and see what needs to be done. If, you know, we have the resolve that nuclear weapons are necessary in order to deter our adversaries, and certainly our nuclear adversaries, then in that commitment it’s going to require that we invest and we modernize.Ms. Bingen: And when I think about it, that nuclear deterrence really is the foundation for our conventional forces and other decisions that we make in our security apparatus. So, on modernization, we’re fortunate at CSIS to have a phenomenal group of junior scholars. So, I’m going to weave in a question here from Joseph Rodgers.In its final report, the bipartisan Strategic Posture Commission established by Congress concluded that the existing nuclear program of record, which is – which is the current modernization program that’s being pursued, is necessary but not sufficient. Can you share some congressional perspectives on this “necessary but not sufficient” conclusion? What investments are needed? And what pace of activity is needed?Rep. Turner: Right. So go back to the iPhone again. You know, when you – when you get a new iPhone, you’re increasing the functionality. You’re not just getting one that works better or that is going to do the same things only more reliable. Our modernization is really looking at our nuclear weapons, the inventory, as doing the same things and only, you know, modernizing it to make it do those things better but not looking at additional things that we need to do. Now, one thing that was really interesting about the nuclear posture commission is that they, really also looked at our need to have an enhanced missile defense capability, because we have not availed ourselves of missile defense as a part of the overall equation of deterrence and defense, both with modernization and with missile defense on a defensive capability, having our adversaries question whether or not they’re even able to hit their targetMs. Bingen: Well, and you – in both your roles on the Intelligence Committee as well as Armed Services for a long time, you’ve been out to the nuclear weapons labs, the production facilities, strategic command. What is your view of the state of the weapons complex and the industrial base where we’re at right now to deliver? And you know, some of these modernization programs that are running behind schedule over cost, can our nation afford what’s ahead of us here in modernization?Rep. Turner: Well, I mean, obviously, we can’t afford not to.Ms. Bingen: Yeah.Rep. Turner: But you know, certainly, the systems that we have now are working. We have deterrence. We have nuclear weapons capabilities that are deterring our adversary, and that’s – adversaries – and that’s, of course, why. You know, we don’t have people in – looking to have conflicts with the United States.But at the same time, they’re looking at the next 10, 15 years of how do they change that calculus where their capabilities increase vulnerabilities as we stay stagnant. And that delta, that change of our adversaries’ capabilities, we’ve not – I mean, you and I have not lived in a world where Russia and China had greater weapons capabilities than we have had. That’s a scary place to be. And that’s why the Nuclear Posture Commission called on that we need to do more than we’re doing, because if we’re running in place and our adversaries are running a marathon we’re currently losing.Ms. Bingen: Well, and you know – what we didn’t have before that you’re really seeing now is it’s this two-peer nuclear challenge, is China from a few hundred to, what, a thousand, 1,500 trajectory that they’re on with their nuclear forces. How well postured is the intelligence community to collect and analyze on these threats – the two-peer challenge, perhaps the risk of opportunism by one of the parties? How do you look at the IC’s ability here?Rep. Turner: Well, my serious concern, which you and I have discussed, is the issue that the capabilities that Russia and China are seeking are what we’d call first-strike capabilities, capabilities where it would cause them to change their calculus and look at whether or not they could undertake an attack on the United States which would prevent us to have an ability to respond and that would significantly diminish our ability to be able to deter them by having those first-strike capabilities.But I think on the intelligence side, what’s really important is that there’s a lot of things that publicly we know; we’re just not having the public discussion. Right now, anyone can pull out their iPhone and they can Google “Chinese ICBM missile silo expansion,” and you will get a space picture of the silos that China is building to put in new nuclear weapons. Then, if you Google “U.S. response to China’s expansion of nuclear weapons,” you’re not going to find anything that’s going to be helpful. That’s where our dialogue and discussion need to be. That’s why it’s so important what you’re doing here at CSIS is taking actually what is occurring, saying it needs to be placed in the dialogue so that we can have a policy response, and then in that, you know, real action on the part of the United States to respond to these threats.Ms. Bingen: Yeah. I want to shift to the topic of extended deterrence. And you do a lot of engagement with our allies and partners. You are – you were the president of the NATO Parliamentary Assembly and still heavily involved in a leadership role there. I don’t think, you know, if you look at the broad American public, that we fully – or, fully appreciate the concept of extended deterrence. And the premise is that our allies don’t develop nuclear weapons, you know, we don’t proliferate nuclear capability, in exchange for us extending a nuclear umbrella to them. So with all of the threats that we’ve discussed – with China, with what North Korea’s doing; you know, it has even been striking to see South Korea and Japan start having a bit of a nuclear dialogue here – how – in all your discussions, how confident are our allies and partners in our nuclear umbrella? How is their thinking shaping our nuclear policy, shaping what – how Congress is viewing these issues?Rep. Turner: So, we had this discussion with your nuclear scholars here at CSIS, and what’s interesting is, is, you know, you have to – you have to go to the next step on what extended deterrence policy was.So extended deterrence was where the United States, through its nuclear umbrella, said: We will cover other allies who don’t develop nuclear weapons and provide them our umbrella. If they’re attacked, we will – we have – we will pledge both in defense and our nuclear umbrella as a response if they do not develop nuclear weapons themselves. The intention was not just that our allies not develop nuclear weapons, although we were being for nonproliferation. That certainly was a win that our allies not become nuclear states. But it was also intended to be a disincentive for our adversaries to expand their nuclear weapons, because if our allies – those who are on our team – built up their nuclear weapons, the thought was that our adversaries would have to build up their nuclear weapons. And so, you would have this proliferation not just with our allies, but our adversaries would have more because they’d have to counter-deter both us and our now-nuclear allies.The problem is that that didn’t happen. China is expanding. Russia is expanding. North Korea is expanding. Iran is continuing to march toward the ability to make a nuclear weapon.So the extended deterrent umbrella has failed to cause the response from our adversaries that we intended and has been because of the enormity of the expansion, as you just described – that the multiplication that China has undertaken in its expansion has caused our nonnuclear allies to then wonder would the United States utilize its nuclear weapons as a deterrence umbrella to protect them, and I think it’s a valid question for them to have as they look to, now, what is an increasing threat from – as South Korea looks not only at North Korea but also at China.Ms. Bingen: Well, and then that leads into the topic of arms control and risk reduction. New START expires 2026. China thus far, I think, has declined arms control talks with the U.S.So what is the future of arms control and do you see a path forward for bipartisan consensus not just on, I’ll say, arms control and strengthening nonproliferation but perhaps like New START originally struck is there was this greater deal or compromise between, yes, we will undertake arms control in New START; at the same time we have a need to modernize our nuclear deterrent.Do you see something like that playing out here, going forward?Rep. Turner: I mean, Ronald Reagan’s life – excuse me, Ronald Reagan’s line of peace through strength is not just a political slogan. The reality is that you’re never going to get anyone to the bargaining table, whether it’s in a business transaction or nuclear weapons negotiations, where they don’t believe – if they don’t believe that they have anything to gain from you.You don’t sit across from someone to negotiate where you believe that voluntarily, unilaterally, the other side is already giving up what you might want from them and that’s the situation we’ve placed ourselves in.We have – Russia and China are expanding their nuclear weapons. They’re expanding their nuclear weapons capabilities. We have something we want from them. But they don’t believe that we have either the will or the interest or the means currently to undertake going in any direction, even expansion, that would be a benefit to them to restrain us, and so we’re stalled in any opportunities for nuclear weapons reduction negotiations.I am for arms control. It has proven to work. It has concluded many times. You know, as I said in my speech the dismantling of a destabilizing weapons inspection regime so that – you know, we had numerous individuals both on the Russian and the American side who knew each other well and knew their facilities well. All of that has gone by the wayside and, certainly, we’re less safe because of it.Ms. Bingen: And to your point on Reagan, President Reagan also believed that we could pursue a nonproliferation agenda, an arms control agenda, but do it through, as you said, a position of strength while also making sure that we had a strong nuclear deterrent.Here’s an interesting question from another one of our junior scholars, Lachlan MacKenzie: Nuclear myths – what is one misperception about nuclear weapons or nuclear risks that you wish you could clarify for the American public?Rep. Turner: I truly believe that the American public think that we have a missile defense system in place that is operational and would defend us against China and Russia and, unfortunately, as we’ve gone through the Obama administration and then the short term of the Trump administration to the Biden administration you have policies that have been anti-missile defense.Remember, missile defense when it was first proposed by Ronald Reagan it was viewed as escalatory, that it was destabilizing, and that your adversary would think we need more nuclear weapons because you have missile defense – that it wouldn’t work and that it was too costly.Well, what we’ve seen is it’s actually de-escalatory, not escalatory. We saw that in Iran shooting 300 missiles at Israel that we worked collectively with them using missile defense technology to take those down.Imagine if Israel had been at the receiving end of 300 missiles the response that would have had to have gone to Iran. Instead, it was de-escalatory. It works. You can see in every aspect of what we have proven in technology. You and I worked on that on Capitol Hill including with the deal with Israel for shared technology for the Iron Dome.And then, of course, the cost is proven in that you don’t have the destructive force that you’re able to eliminate through missile defense technology.Ms. Bingen: And that was a great point that the Strategic Posture Commission in a bipartisan manner emphasized as well, was to recommend we expand our missile defenses not just against the strategic missile threats, but also these more coercive cruise missiles and other threats that can now target the homeland.Rep. Turner: We can protect the homeland, and we should.Ms. Bingen: NATO. So, you’re a senior leader in the NATO Parliamentary Assembly. NATO Summit will be in D.C. in a few weeks, marking the 75th anniversary. Can you share your thoughts on where NATO is at today, where it needs to go in the future. And I’m going to weave in a question from the audience, from Natalie Grishin, the Ukrainian Culture Association of Ohio. Got to get the Ohio piece in here: Thank you, Chairman Turner, for a clear and to-the-point presentation. What do you think the White House administration position on the Ukraine path to NATO should be in the coming NATO Summit? So maybe just also hit on your assessment of the situation Ukraine, and where that goes from here.Rep. Turner: This is an exciting NATO Summit, because the success of the summit has already occurred before everybody convenes. And that success is the expansion of NATO to include Finland and Sweden, and the fact that they will be at the table full members, which shows the failure of Putin’s policies of attempting to push NATO back. He’s actually pushed people into NATO. Both Sweden and Finland come to NATO with some of the highest levels of military capabilities of all of the NATO countries. So they’re increasing the capabilities of NATO.Their joining and sitting at that table I think is a reflection of really the success of NATO as an alliance and the failure of Putin’s aggression, and the real calculus that there is a risk here. Sweden and Finland didn’t join NATO because of Ukraine. They joined – Finland and Sweden joined NATO because they knew that other countries were next. And that’s why we’re supporting Ukraine. That’s why we need to continue to support Ukraine.I think at this summit, there should be just a, you know, recommitment to the Bucharest statement. In Bucharest, the NATO stated Ukraine will be a member of NATO. That is the strongest statement – both Georgia and Ukraine received that – strongest statement that NATO has made in aspiring nations that want to join NATO. And that, I think, is a – is a standing commitment by NATO. And you’re seeing it in the billions of dollars of weapons and capabilities that are being provided to Ukraine to defend itself against Russia.Ms. Bingen: And you were one of the leading voices on the Hill articulating the need to pass that legislation to ensure that Ukraine does receive the security assistance and munitions from the United States and others. So, thank you for that.Technology and emerging technology. Several areas are emerging. We’ve talked space. There’s cyber, hypersonics, AI, biotech, all with warfighting and intelligence community benefit, but also with security risk, when you look at what, perhaps, China is doing in these areas. So how do you think about these technologies and what the intelligence community needs to do to maintain its advantage?And I’m also going to weave in that – a question from Alexander Givin on nuclear: nuclear weapons have shaped how conflict is perceived and conducted since their conception. So how do you see artificial intelligence and cyber interacting with nuclear weapons? So broader emerging technology question as well as nuclear with AI and cyber.Rep. Turner: Right. So, AI is one of the most important developments because of what it is able to provide as an analytical capability and assistance. I had an opportunity to sit with Henry Kissinger just shortly before he passed. And he was – he had just written a book the year before on AI. He was still continuing to publish on artificial intelligence. And he was talking about the importance of AI in strategic planning.But he went the next step to say, as policy we should never implement AI where there’s not a requirement that AI inform us of how it’s coming to conclusions that it’s doing, that it’s – that it’s offering to us, or the advancement of human knowledge will stop, and computers and machine learning will advance. He was very adamant about that connectivity. And I think the human element in utilizing AI is going to be incredibly important. And I don’t think AI should ever be used with respect to any decision making with respect to nuclear weapons, or even as we look to the prosecution of conflicts. Obviously, strategy, the assessment, discernment, it’s going to be incredibly important. But decision making should remain with the human factor.Our biggest threat in AI is that our adversaries get there first. China, with its surveillance society where it has implemented this surveillance culture to be able to further suppress its population, has attempted to export that. They’ve also coupled that with cyber efforts to access and download data to get an understanding of other countries, populations, and their activities. The utilization of AI for an authoritarian regime is probably the biggest threat that we have seen in the longest time to mankind and to the issue of freedom and liberty.Ms. Bingen: Really appreciate that. In other technology areas, hypersonics. And I don’t want to steal your thunder, but you and I have talked about this. But as it relates to Title 10, which is Department of Defense authority, and Title 50, which is an intelligence community authority, you are one of the few members that see that total picture. So, when we’re looking at these different technology areas, what concerns you about red? What is blue doing about it? Talk to us about, I guess, some of the tension that you see there, but also how do you bring greater integration across Title 10, DOD, and Title 50, the intelligence community?Rep. Turner: Right. So further on your description, you know, on the intelligence world side we look at our adversaries. What are our adversaries doing? On the national security and defense side, we plan what we’re going to do. Sometimes those don’t match, and the information doesn’t get from one side to the other. And there was a period, obviously, where we were much more advanced than our adversaries. So, the – an understanding of what adversaries were doing was important, but at the same time it didn’t inform the gap between our capabilities and theirs.And that evolution of that gap, that identification of it, is, I think, embodied most in the – in the issue of hypersonics, where the United States was vastly ahead, technology was stolen, China and Russia now have hypersonics programs fielded. Hypersonic weapons are in the hands of our adversaries. We do not have, currently, a hypersonics weapon that has been delivered or even capable of being deployed. And this is public. If you look at what you know that our adversaries are doing in hypersonics, and then what you know the United States is doing, even if we accomplish everything that we have on the drawing board tomorrow in our hypersonics programs, we still fall short of where they’re currently performing.And so that needs to be resolved as to never, you know, allowing ourselves again to be setting – first off, abandoning a program, and then setting the bar lower than what we know our adversaries are already capable of. And this is – I think, if there was one thing that occurred in the last several years of China, Russia, North Korea, Iran, that’s probably the most haunting that people should focus on is – and we all got to see it. President Xi is standing next to Vladimir Putin. He went to Moscow to show his support of this, you know, unlimited friendship, of no limits with Vladimir Putin.And what we would call a hot-mic moment, where they’re not doing their presentation, but the mic is open, President Xi is caught. And he says to Vladimir Putin, what we are bringing about – we, meaning them together – is a change that hasn’t happened in 100 years. Pause on that for a second. We know what happened 100 years ago. That’s World War I and World War II. That is the fight between authoritarianism and democracy. And that’s what they’re saying. They’re saying, we, together – they want to re-prosecute the conflict between authoritarianism and democracy. And they believe that they will win. They certainly can win if we don’t rise to the occasion of making certain that they do not have capabilities for which we cannot respond.Ms. Bingen: And you just had that happen again this week with Vladimir Putin visiting North Korea with Kim Jong-un. So, the question comes from Rachel Oswald at CQ Roll Call: How worried should the U.S. be, that Russia may now provide North Korea with technological assistance to improve North Korea’s long-range ballistic missiles and their ability to directly target the United States?Rep. Turner: Well, that they have that ability now. Certainly, the cooperation between Russia and North Korea would enhance their ability to do so. I think we’ve all sort of felt intuitively that China, Russia, North Korea, Iran, are working together in both their development of capabilities and in their threats to the United States. These symbolic meetings, I think, are – should allow us to focus on this is a threat that has already been occurring. Ms. Bingen: And I’m going to just jump around here with some additional questions from the audience. This is from one of our aerospace interns here at CSIS, Annalise Johnson, who happens to be an Ohio State University student: How do you foresee continued sanctions against Russia affecting their space and nuclear program? And then what level of engagement with Russia should the U.S. strive to have?Rep. Turner: This is a great question, because the question is: Would sanctions significantly impact Russia’s path, as stated by the administration, for developing a nuclear antisatellite weapon? Well, I think we should certainly try. I think we need to try every – you do not hear the administration saying this is such a red line, this is such a change and catastrophic dynamic that places at risk all of the accomplishments that we have in integrating space in the economy, our international defense, our communications, that that red line is so great that we are going to implement an additional regime of financial restrictions, of sanctions, and even look to what are we going to do with our allies in NATO to make such a strong statement as to have Russia understand that this is – this is not something that is going to be accepted.Ms. Bingen: OK. And you briefly mentioned Iran at the outset here, but I want to come back to this. Paul Tervo from the Institute for Science and International Security: Iran’s recent IAEA-confirmed expansion of its nuclear enrichment plants, the Institute for Science and International Security estimates that the time it would take for Iran to now produce a nuclear weapon has been reduced dramatically, under a third of a month. So how do you think Iran’s capacity and capability here will influence Middle East policy going forward?Rep. Turner: Well, really, all roads lead to Iran when you look at the instability in the – in the Middle East: their franchises – Hamas, Hezbollah, and the Houthis; areas in which they have challenged, you know, Saudi Arabia, our other allies; and what we’ve seen, of course, in the conflict that has unfolded with Israel. These still relate back to the destabilizing effect of Iran, and it needs to be countered by the administration.Ms. Bingen: This is a question from Polly Keim from the National Nuclear Security Administration, NNSA. This is interesting: What do you think we should conceal versus reveal to assure allies and deter adversaries? And I recall we’ve had these discussions in the space domain as well as others is, you know, under what circumstances would you reveal a capability versus, obviously, protecting the exquisite nature or the performance of that system. So how do you think about conceal versus reveal?Rep. Turner: Well, intelligence is gathered so you can impact the outcome; it is not so we be casual observers. If you’re not using intelligence to impact the outcome, the intelligence has no value. We’re not doing intelligence merely to inform ourselves. And in this instance, you know, as I’ve called for the administration to declassify the status of this program, there’s no risk to means, methods, and techniques to actually call Russia’s program as to what is its stage in development. And then from that we can inform: What is our to-do list? What do we need to do? What do our allies need to do? How do we need to work together as the world, Western economic community to address this?Ms. Bingen: Well, and then a question from Ajay Gokul, who’s a McHenry fellow pursuing a master’s at Georgetown University: What role does India play here? So how can the U.S. leverage India to play a constructive role, particularly on the nuclear proliferation or nuclear nonproliferation front? You know, they have an interesting historical relationship with Russia, but also increasingly adversarial relationships with China and with Pakistan. These are all nuclear states. So, what role do you see India playing going forward?Rep. Turner: Well, I do think is important, because if you look at India and if you look at Africa, their development has occurred not because they went back and put in infrastructure that we had in the ’70s and ’80s; it’s because they jumped forward in infrastructure and technology. Their ability to establish communications systems, economic infrastructure, energy, all of it is dependent upon space. Africa and India today are what they are today because of space. If space – if the space-age advancement ceases to exist, Africa and India will become isolated.Ms. Bingen: I want to come back to your role as Chairman of the Intelligence Committee. A great credit to you and to Ranking Member Himes for your Beyond the SCIF series, where, rather than do everything in classified form you’ve both been very deliberate about getting out publicly to talk about things that the committee is concerned about.Related to that, I want to hit on this issue of, I’ll say, trust in the intelligence community. You know, in recent years there’s been this drumbeat of media reports and commentaries that suggest that trust in the intelligence community has eroded, and frankly trust in U.S. national security institutions writ large. I got to see the best of what the military intelligence community is doing. I know you’ve seen plenty of that in your role. So how do you think about how can we build trust in our intelligence institutions given the vital security role that they play?Rep. Turner: Right. Well, we give our intelligence community exquisite tools that have great risks if they are used against the American public and can be destabilizing to a democracy. Of the things that we have seen, and certainly of the discourse that we’ve had in the public of the most egregious violations, they’re largely resident in the Department of Justice and in the FBI, which are not an intelligence community generator but intelligence user. And I do think we need significant focus on reforming the way in which the Department of Justice and the FBI marry themselves and utilize intelligence.When we did the renewal of the Foreign Surveillance Act Section 702, we put in 54 reforms targeting largely DOJ and FBI and their utilization of intelligence, and making certain that the court system is involved in those oversight, and that they are restrained and restricted. That is going to be a continuing issue. And I think the American public see it as they look at the news stories that clip by them, that both in inequality in the manner in which justice is pursued, but also in the manner in which this information is utilized. And that is a real issue.Ms. Bingen: And you just passed your intelligence community – your Intelligence Authorization Act, your IAA, out of committee. So, congratulations on that. You mind just spending a minute here talking about the committee’s priorities? But also, you’ve hinted at there are some broader intelligence community reforms that your committee might be tackling.Rep. Turner: Right. I think, well, one of the biggest concerns that we all have right now is the administration’s reticence to share intelligence with allies. Ukraine has been forced to fight Russian aggression on their own soil with one arm tied behind their back. The administration has been slow to give them the type of lethal weapons that can make a difference on the battlefield. Very slow to give them authority to actually use those within Russia to attack systems that are actively sending missiles and attacking Ukraine, but also the weapons infrastructure where they’re quadrupling their output of missiles to attack Ukraine. And they’re hesitant to provide intelligence as to what Russia is doing within Russia for Ukraine.In the beginning, the administration refused to give Ukraine any intelligence as to where the Russians were – where the Russians were in Ukraine. The House and our Intelligence Committee had to push. The administration ultimately changed their position. And the effect was so dramatic that they ran to the microphone to claim credit for changing their own policy. So instead of acknowledging that the policy was wrong, they wanted credit for the change on the battlefield as a result of them lifting their own restrictions.Ms. Bingen: So, I know we’re running out of time here. I want to end – well, we’ve had a pretty sobering conversation across a whole range of issues. I want to end on a bit of a note of optimism here. Earlier this morning you talked to our next-generation nuclear scholars here at CSIS. So, I’m curious what you told them, what do you want these young scholars thinking about as they embark on their careers? And, you know, nuclear weapons, nuclear antisatellite abilities, these are pretty dark subjects to talk about. What makes you then optimistic about our future going forward?Rep. Turner: Right. Most nations, when they talk about a vulnerability, or they talk about an adversary, talk about actually actions against an adversary. For us, it really is a to-do list. As we look to what our risks are, we have to inform our to-do list and then accomplish it. Russia’s economy is the size of Italy and, yet, it dominates our defense policy discussions as to whether or not our huge economy and ingenuity and capability will even be applied to respond to some of the things that they’re accomplishing.We have great capability and, as long as we do the to-do list, there’s nothing we’re going to be able – not be able to accomplish. There are many nations who have wish lists. We have to-do lists. We just have to fill out the to-do list and get to work.Ms. Bingen: And you have a phenomenal group of young nuclear scholars that want to engage in these issues and work on them for the betterment of security, going forward. So –Rep. Turner: They’re incredibly bright. It was amazing to see not just what they’re currently doing but what they want to do in the future, and their questions were so incredibly informed.The next generation coming up, especially since they’ve seen what has been occurring, are absolutely dedicating – dedicated to a strong United States because they know it translates into freedom and liberty, and without it we’re all at risk.Ms. Bingen: Well, Chairman Turner, thank you so much.I will say it is very rare for us to get the chairman of a full committee to CSIS, not just to do this kind of public discussion on a whole range of topics but also for you to spend time with our young scholars. So, thank you for that.Thank you for bringing greater awareness and urgency to the Russian nuclear anti-satellite threat. It is absolutely a day zero – I mean, we are back to the 1950s if this does go forward and the threat that it poses is just incredibly grave.So, thank you very much for your leadership and all that you are doing on the Hill internationally and in educating the American public.For folks here in the audience we will have a lunch reception out in the foyer here, so it’s an opportunity to network and to continue the conversation.Again, Mr. Chairman, thank you.Rep. Turner: Thank you. (Applause.) (END.)\nTags\nNuclear Issues, and\nDefense and Security\nPrograms & Projects\nProject on Nuclear Issues\nAerospace Security Project\nCongressional Affairs at CSIS\nInternational Security Program\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"核武器与外交政策：与众议院永久情报特别委员会主席迈克·特纳的对话\n照片：CSIS\n文字记录\n— 2024年6月20日\n可下载内容\n下载文字记录\n237kb\n图片\n照片：CSIS\n核武器与外交政策：与众议院永久情报特别委员会主席迈克·特纳的对话\n2024年6月20日 • 美国东部时间上午11:00 – 中午12:00\n由核问题项目主办\n本文字记录来自CSIS于2024年6月20日主办的一场活动。点击此处观看完整视频。希瑟·威廉姆斯：好的，早上好。感谢大家参加。​​我是希瑟·威廉姆斯。我是CSIS核问题项目（亲切地称为PONI）的主任。我们非常高兴今天能邀请各位参加与众议员迈克·特纳的对话，他是众议院永久情报特别委员会主席。\n实际上，我想先感谢特纳主席抽出这么多时间。在本次活动开始前，他刚刚花了一个小时与今天也在场的PONI核问题学者会面。这是一群新一代核问题专家。特纳主席非常友善地分享了他对许多——许多我们稍后将进一步讨论的问题的看法，以及一些职业建议。因此，我们真的非常感谢您今天为我们付出的所有时间。\n在今天的活动开始前，我需要向大家说明我们大楼的安全注意事项。总体而言，我们认为大楼是安全的。作为活动召集方，我们有责任为任何突发情况做好准备。在本次活动中，我将担任负责安全的工作人员。如果有需要，请遵循我的指示。最后，请熟悉紧急出口通道。它们可能就在你们身后，或者在两侧。\n因此，PONI和航空航天安全项目非常荣幸今天能邀请到众议员迈克尔·特纳，与我们讨论各种战略挑战，包括俄罗斯的反卫星武器威胁，以及核武器在美国外交政策中的作用。我知道，在座各位都清楚，这场对话正值美国决策者面临关键时刻，他们必须就核力量现代化、军备控制的未来以及核武器在美国外交政策中的作用作出重要决定。同时，美国公众也正以一种自冷战以来我们真正未曾见过的方式重新关注核武器。因此，这是一个非常重要的对话时刻。\n2023年两党战略态势委员会强调了战略问题上的一种“紧迫感”——一种紧迫感。在开始对话之前，我想强调其中三点。第一，是围绕美国核事业和现代化的紧迫感。战略态势委员会报告指出，当前的现代化计划“必要但并不充分”。他们建议，应在当前现代化计划基础上予以补充，以确保美国核战略在两个核大国对手并存的环境中仍然有效。\n最近，就在一两周前，国家安全委员会高级主任普拉奈·瓦迪表示，如果对手核武库的发展轨迹不发生变化，那么在未来几年内，我们可能会达到这样一个时点：需要将当前的已部署数量增加。该报告还呼吁美国解决盟友对延伸威慑的担忧；盟国之间目前正就其核选项以及其可信度展开持续辩论。\n美国威慑力量。\n\n战略态势委员会报告提出的第二个重要趋势，是安全环境日益恶化以及对手不断扩充战略系统。美国优先推进军备控制和核裁减之际，我们的对手却朝着相反方向发展，并利用了这一时机。仅举一个例子——主席曾参与审查这一问题——那就是俄罗斯的反卫星武器威胁；政府承认，这可能违反1967年《外层空间条约》。中国继续扩大其核武库，包括生产裂变材料；国防部预计，到2030年，中国的核武库将达到1,000枚可作战核弹头。\n\n最后一个需要紧迫感的话题，是寻求解决方案，其中可能包括军备控制。去年11月的一次会晤之后，中国拒绝与美国就这一议题举行后续会谈；俄罗斯也退出了多项军备控制承诺，包括暂停履行《新削减战略武器条约》、违反《中导条约》以及取消对《全面禁止核试验条约》的批准。因此，总体而言，这些问题提出了许多非常重要的问题，其中很多都具有时效性，涉及美国核武器在外交政策和战略中的作用。这也是我们今天非常高兴能邀请特纳主席到场、分享他的见解和思考的原因，其中包括情报界在应对这些新的战略挑战方面可能发挥的作用。\n\n简要介绍一下特纳主席的履历：迈克尔·特纳众议员于2002年首次当选国会议员。在国会，特纳是众议院军事委员会的资深成员，曾担任战术空军与陆军部队小组委员会以及战略力量小组委员会的共和党首席成员。这些小组委员会负责监督陆军和空军的采购项目、海军和海军陆战队的所有航空项目、核武器、导弹防御和太空系统。自2015年起担任众议院常设情报特别委员会委员后，特纳众议员当选为众议院常设情报特别委员会主席。该委员会负责监督美国17个情报机构。他曾任北约议会大会主席，目前担任北约议会大会防务与安全委员会副主席。关于特纳主席的另一个有趣事实是，在《代顿和平协议》签署期间，他曾担任俄亥俄州代顿市市长。因此，说他为今天的会议带来了丰富的专业经验，绝不为过。\n\n不过，本次会议将由卡里·宾根主持，她是航空航天安全项目主任，也是这里CSIS国际安全项目的高级研究员。特纳众议员将发表主旨演讲，随后与卡里进行炉边谈话，并回答观众提问。如果您想提交问题，您的椅子上应该有一个二维码，或者就在我身后有一个巨大的二维码。请用手机扫描并提交问题，卡里会在问题传来时进行筛选。\n\n那么，特纳主席，现在请您发表讲话。谢谢。（掌声。）\n\n众议员迈克尔·特纳（俄亥俄州共和党）：谢谢大家，早上好。我要感谢CSIS给我这次机会，感谢卡里·宾根主持本次活动，也感谢她在核武器问题以及我国面临的风险方面发挥的领导作用。\n\n太空\n太空时代始于1957年俄罗斯发射“伴侣号”卫星。当时，全世界的目光转向天空，并思考太空和技术将如何改变地球上的生活。太空时代将在俄罗斯把其核反卫星武器发射入轨时结束。美国太空军参谋长萨尔茨曼将军把俄罗斯核反卫星武器可能的发射日期称为“归零日”，因为从那天起，没有人能够指望第二天太空仍然可用。从那天起，为了维护我们的经济、社会和军事结构，地球上的前提必须是：我们必须拥有太空之外的替代方案。眼下，我们没有替代方案。为了仅仅保住我们在太空时代取得的成果，将需要数万亿美元和我们并不拥有的时间来建造重复和冗余系统。有些事情根本不存在替代方案。联合国《外层空间条约》——该条约于1967年生效，由俄罗斯联邦、美国和英国签署——宣告：“各国不得将核武器或其他大规模毁灭性武器放置在轨道上或天体上，也不得以任何其他方式将其驻留在外层空间。”该条约的缔结，是基于这样一种对人类的承诺：探索和利用太空可能会带来地球上的进步。签署国不可能想象如今这样一个世界：农业、医疗、商业交易、通信、海上导航、国际安全，甚至我们计时的能力，都依赖于太空。\n\n同样，我们也无法想象一个不依赖太空的世界。在太空时代结束的归零日，我们将不得不去想象。拜登政府只有在受到众议院情报委员会一个两党成员团体质询后，才不情愿地解密披露：俄罗斯正在研制一种核反卫星武器，意图将其部署到外层空间的轨道上。此后没有发布更多信息。新闻报道推测，该武器已经完成研发，现已存在并正在准备发射。另有新闻报道称，俄罗斯已经有一颗卫星在轨道上作为测试。俄罗斯核反卫星武器系统的第一阶段。在不确认或否认任何这些报道准确性的情况下，它们提出的问题必须由拜登政府立即回答，无论俄罗斯的时间安排如何，也无论这一不断演变的威胁可能产生怎样的即时影响。这场危机就是太空中的古巴导弹危机。而政府正在失败。人类在太空时代取得的进步正处于危险之中，而政府正梦游般走向一个不可逆转的归零日。\n\n前国防部太空政策助理部长普拉姆博士在众议院军事委员会作证时表示，俄罗斯的反核卫星——俄罗斯的核反卫星武器——如果在近地轨道引爆，将不加区别地摧毁近地轨道内的所有卫星，并可能使这一太空轨道至少一年无法使用。在此期间，人类将无法重新部署近地轨道卫星，载人太空探索也将变得致命。这一威胁意味着，我们的经济、国际安全和社会系统将陷入停顿。这将是对西方经济和民主体系的一次灾难性、毁灭性攻击。弗拉基米尔·普京知道这一点。将军。归零日可能是\n避免这种情况。想象一下，如果肯尼迪总统允许赫鲁晓夫在古巴部署核武器，世界会变得多么不同。欧洲将不会获得自由。美国会因佛罗里达海岸近在咫尺的核武器而过于恐惧，不敢用核武器挑战俄罗斯和欧洲。正如赫鲁晓夫本可以利用来自古巴的核威胁扣押美国一样，弗拉基米尔·普京也将把世界的太空资产扣为人质，以对抗阻止他重建苏联的努力。\n\n俄美核武器控制条约已有先例，包括拆除破坏稳定的武器、建立检查机制以及禁止部署。但这类条约要通过实力来谈判，而拜登政府似乎无力展现这种实力。为了避免“零日”，拜登政府必须立即解密所有已知的、关于俄罗斯核反卫星武器计划状况的信息。弗拉基米尔·普京在秘密中如鱼得水。政府必须全面披露普京的计划和武器项目，并让全世界了解。此外，美国及其北约盟友必须共同宣布执行《联合国外空条约》的决心。我呼吁拜登政府这样做。\n\n与俄罗斯对抗存在风险。拜登政府极不愿意采取任何看似会升级局势的行动。然而，俄罗斯才是升级局势的侵略者。升级已经发生。现在，美国必须与我们的盟友站在一起，阻止“零日”，并按照《联合国外空条约》的意图，为全人类的福祉维护太空。谢谢。（掌声。）\n\n卡里·A·宾根：特纳主席，刚才的讲话对我们所有人来说都令人极为警醒。我想首先说，我很幸运，多年来曾在众议院军事委员会以及您担任战略力量小组委员会主席时与您共事。真正令我印象深刻的是，您多么经常亲自参与并领导应对那些真正棘手、复杂的问题——导弹防御、《新削减战略武器条约》、核力量现代化——我一直很欣赏您和委员会所采取的两党合作方式。\n\n你们一起听取简报，一起出访，以获取第一手信息；你们如饥似渴地吸收信息，以确保真正理解这一问题的方方面面。\n\n我也看到您在众议院情报委员会采取同样的方法，我们稍后会更多讨论情报委员会正在开展的工作。因此，我将从俄罗斯核反卫星威胁谈起，但我们还将讨论核威慑、其他国际安全问题以及情报委员会的重点工作。\n\n我要提醒现场的每个人，如果有问题，请扫描二维码；在线的朋友可以前往活动网页，也可以在那里提交问题。\n\n所以，再次谈到“零日”，刚才的讲话确实令人极为警醒。您看到了所有情报、所有威胁评估。俄罗斯长期以来一直具备通过弹道导弹在太空引爆核武器的能力。\n\n如今这一威胁有何不同，为何如此重大？您为什么在二月采取不寻常的行动，提出这一严重的国家安全威胁？您遭到了本党成员以及另一党派成员的抨击。您为什么要这样做？\n\n特纳议员：首先，我想先感谢卡里，因为在\n除了在所有这些问题上都是一位令人难以置信的专家之外，你一直也是这些问题上的导师和教育者。你现在来到CSIS并继续做这些事情，真是太好了。感谢你让我参与进来，使我们能够就这些问题进行对话和讨论。\n\n你工作的一方面，不仅仅是你帮助推动实施的政策，还在于你确实希望教育他人，确保围绕核武器和威慑的对话得以持续。我的意思是，这是人类已知最具毁灭性的力量，而你倡导知识、理解和政策辩论，这确实非同寻常。所以，感谢你这样做。\n\n首先，众议院情报委员会以两党一致、压倒性的行动，要求政府公开这一威胁正在出现。事实是，必须由众议院情报委员会来推动这件事，我认为这说明政府浪费了多少时间——事实是，他们对此没有应对方案。\n\n你提出过这样一个问题：俄罗斯可以发射一枚洲际弹道导弹，将其送入太空并引爆，也许摧毁那里的卫星；那么，这有什么不同呢？\n\n嗯，不同之处足以让他们着手实施这件事，对吧？政府表示他们正在开发这种武器，而我要求他们告诉全世界，这件事目前处于什么状态，以及“开发”究竟意味着什么——这是否意味着它还只是停留在图纸上？\n\n这是否意味着它是实验室里的一个研究项目？这是否意味着它已经进入某个制造设施？这是否意味着核弹头已经被制造出来？有卫星吗？有导弹吗？\n\n所有这些，都是政府表态自然会引发的问题，而他们回避这场讨论，是因为他们不愿承认自己其实什么也没做。\n\n古巴导弹危机之所以出现不同的结果，是因为当时我们有肯尼迪总统。我们需要政府展现领导力，当然也需要在全球范围内进行对话，以了解并真正揭示俄罗斯人在做什么，以及应当作出怎样的回应。\n\nBingen女士：嗯，这就引出了我的下一个问题。收集这类信息，很可能涉及极其敏感的情报来源和方法。因此，你呼吁解密情报。\n\n你刚才已经强调了一些，但美国公众、政策制定者和其他人需要了解的具体内容有哪些？我们遗漏了什么？另外，你如何在保护这些敏感来源和方法与公开信息之间进行权衡？\n\nTurner众议员：是的。这绝不能被允许进入轨道，绝对不能，而政府甚至没有把这件事列入待办清单。要明确待办清单应包含什么，其中一部分就是要弄清楚：俄罗斯正在开发反卫星核武器，这究竟是什么——他们所说的“开发”到底意味着什么。\n\n现在，显然，他们一直在与情报委员会合作，当然，我们也了解这一不断演变的威胁是什么样子。不过，我认为政府不愿展开讨论，是因为他们不想承认自己什么也没做，而我们正在梦游般走向一个不可逆转的“第零天”效应。\n\n这件事发生在太空，其意义不只是“好吧，它在太空中；它并不具破坏性”\n除非他们使用它。不，这是第一天就具有破坏性的——正如萨尔茨曼将军所说，是第零天，也就是第二天，因为突然之间，我们将无法再依赖那里现有的任何系统。如果我们不能依赖这些系统，就将不得不构建替代系统，否则在经济、军事、通信以及社会层面都将真正造成灾难。\n\n宾根女士：那么，根据你所知道的一切，你认为有没有办法在不透露敏感来源和方法的情况下，传达有关这一计划的更多信息？\n\n特纳众议员：当然。\n\n宾根女士：好的。那么这一切都引出了一个问题：现在怎么办？我们已经获得了这一洞见。美国政府一直在寻求与其他国际伙伴一道增加对俄罗斯的国际压力——4月份由美国和日本发起的一项联合国决议重申《外层空间条约》，俄罗斯对此行使了否决权，而中国弃权。所以，我认为现在的问题是——你刚才也暗示了这一点——我们认为自己将愿意采取哪些行动来维护《外层空间条约》，并阻止这种情况发生？\n\n特纳众议员：是的。所以，我把政府就这一问题前往联合国安理会，比作一所学校的校长去找学生群体，与教师就劳资纠纷进行交涉。这里的关键在于，政府需要发挥领导作用。我们有盟友。我的意思是，世界需要了解这种武器意味着什么，以及它将造成什么后果；政府需要对此进行讨论，同时争取全世界反对俄罗斯将核武器部署到太空、违反太空武器条约的支持。\n\n宾根女士：中国现在在太空领域有很多利益需要维护。我们是否有空间与中国以及其他国家合作？\n\n特纳众议员：当然。我不认为——我不认为政府已经做了足够的通报，这也是为什么我呼吁政府向我们的其他对手和盟友都说明这一威胁是什么。你无法为一件不讨论的事情争取支持，而我坚信，政府正在回避讨论这一话题，因为他们不希望公众讨论他们应该做什么与实际正在做什么之间的差距。\n\n宾根女士：那么，如果我考虑DIME框架，我们正在寻求外交和国际压力选项。经济制裁是否在考虑之列？军事选项是否应该在考虑之列？你负责监督情报能力。这里应该把什么列入考虑范围？\n\n特纳众议员：我认为全部都应该考虑。我认为——我想，最大的信号应该是——我的意思是，这太灾难性了。国防部长奥斯汀在武装部队委员会面前表示，这将是灾难性的。那么，如果这具有灾难性，就应该每天都有政府中的某个人站出来，确保这种事情不会发生。但现在没有人承担这项责任，也没有人在执行那份待办事项清单。这是他们知情的事情。这是他们已经告知我们俄罗斯正在推进的事情。但这不是政府的优先事项。这必须成为优先事项。\n\n宾根女士：那么，情报界，我姑且这么说，在理解这一威胁的复杂性方面准备得如何？\n\n特纳众议员：我认为已经足够了，足以让政府愿意站出来说俄罗斯正在研发\n一种反卫星核武器。我的意思是，本届政府已经确认了这一点。这不是推测。这也不是说人们在此时对此有不同意见。对于需要采取什么措施，可能确实存在不同意见，但这正是我们需要进行的对话。应该怎么做？而本届政府并没有在内部、与美国公众，或在全球范围内与我们的盟友展开这场辩论。\n\n宾根女士：嗯，有意思的是，作为情报委员会主席，我只是觉得，你看到的是——你看到了一切。你掌握着安全环境的全球图景。这显然是一个非常令人警醒且影响深远的威胁。但世界上还有许多其他事情正在发生，我想这些事情也在消耗许多人的大量精力。所以，你知道，如果你能退一步，从全球安全环境的角度来看，还有什么让你担忧？另外，在当前中东局势、中国、技术进步等一切都在发展的背景下，你如何看待这一威胁？\n\n特纳议员：嗯，我不知道。我想我会先说，如果有一件事将影响地球上的每一个人，那它可能应该排在首要位置。我认为，本届政府在关注这一问题时，需要明白，不能容忍这种武器进入轨道。老实说，我认为政府内部可能有人认为，我们可以容忍这种武器存在于太空中。正如萨尔茨曼将军所说，你不能容忍它存在于太空中，因为第二天，也就是第零天，你必须假定我们拥有的所有太空能力在第二天都无法使用。你将不得不为替代用途、替代来源做规划，而我们没有这些。这就是我在演讲中提到的：数万亿美元、数万亿美元，以及我们当然没有的时间。\n\n但显然还有其他威胁。看看核领域：中国正在增加其核武器库存，朝鲜也在这样做，俄罗斯正在部署一些新型武器，而中国和俄罗斯都在转向高超音速武器。即使是在非太空领域的核威胁方面，美国目前正在做的事情与我们的对手显然正在做的事情之间也存在能力差距。这造成了脆弱性。当然，还有俄罗斯对乌克兰的侵略、以色列与加沙的冲突，以及伊朗通过真主党、哈马斯和胡塞武装施加的影响——所有这些都需要从应对伊朗侵略的角度加以处理。\n\n宾根女士：有意思的是，正如你所说，鉴于俄罗斯在核力量方面正在开展的其他所有现代化工作——无论是巡航导弹、无人水下航行器，还是洲际弹道导弹——俄罗斯这样做在某种程度上并不应让我们感到意外。那为什么不涉及太空呢？但这确实处于太空威胁谱系的极端一端。\n\n特纳议员：对。如果你看看“先锋”号高超音速武器、“天空坠落”号核武器巡航导弹（它会绕地球运行），以及“波塞冬”号水下无人导弹（可以浮出水面，意图摧毁沿海城市），这些武器在15年前甚至都无法想象。但正如你刚才所指出的，这确实表明俄罗斯有意在核武器能力方面进行大规模投资。而我们需要……\n能够作出回应。宾根女士：如果我在这里稍微转换一下话题，具体谈谈核现代化，你知道，我会指出，国会议员中真正了解核武器和核威慑的人并不多。你如何向其他国会议员解释这些威胁是什么、我们为什么需要实现核武器和核基础设施现代化，以及究竟有哪些利害关系？特纳众议员：对。我想，你知道，人们最常用的类比就是你自己的私家车。你知道，我有一辆1964年的凯迪拉克敞篷车。我不会每天开它通勤。你会升级，会寻求提高技术；想想你扔掉并更换过多少部不同的iPhone。但另一方面，我们却把几十年前的技术当作核武器威慑，并且以为，我们有核武器，就不需要现代化。嗯，当然需要。它们会老化。它们的能力和技术会过时。而我们的对手能力不断增强，这就造成了脆弱性。\n\n这本应是一个持续的过程，而不是把它停放在车库里，几十年后再回来，说，让我们踢踢轮胎，看看需要做些什么。如果我们确实认定核武器对于威慑我们的对手，当然也包括核对手，是必要的，那么这种承诺就要求我们进行投资并实现现代化。\n\n宾根女士：当我思考这一点时，我认为核威慑确实是我们常规部队以及安全体系中其他决策的基础。因此，谈到现代化，我们很幸运，CSIS拥有一批出色的青年学者。所以，我要把约瑟夫·罗杰斯提出的一个问题融入进来。\n\n国会设立的两党战略态势委员会在其最终报告中得出结论：现有的核计划记录——也就是目前正在推进的现代化计划——是必要的，但还不够充分。你能否分享一些国会方面对“必要但不充分”这一结论的看法？需要进行哪些投资？活动需要以什么速度推进？特纳众议员：对。所以再回到iPhone这个例子。你知道，当你买到一部新iPhone时，你是在增加功能。你不是仅仅买一部运行得更好、或者能做同样事情但更可靠的手机。我们的现代化实际上是在把核武器和库存看作执行同样的任务，只是进行现代化，让它更好地完成这些任务，却没有考虑我们还需要完成的其他任务。战略态势委员会有一点非常有意思：他们还认真研究了我们增强导弹防御能力的需要，因为我们一直没有把导弹防御作为威慑和防御整体方程的一部分加以利用；通过现代化以及在防御能力方面部署导弹防御，让我们的对手怀疑他们是否甚至能够击中目标。\n\n宾根女士：嗯，而且你——无论是在情报委员会还是长期担任武装部队委员会成员期间，你都去过核武器实验室、生产设施和战略司令部。你如何看待目前武器体系和工业基础的状况，以及我们现在交付这些现代化项目的能力？你知道，其中一些现代化项目正在运行\n进度落后、成本超支，面对我们在现代化方面即将承担的开支，我国能负担得起吗？\n特纳众议员：嗯，我的意思是，显然，我们负担不起不去做的代价。\n宾根女士：是的。\n特纳众议员：但是你知道，当然，我们现在拥有的系统仍在运转。我们拥有威慑力量。我们拥有能够威慑对手——对手们——的核武器能力，这当然就是原因。你知道，我们没有人会——没有人在寻求与美国发生冲突。\n但与此同时，他们正在考虑未来10年、15年，如何改变这种局面：当我们的能力停滞不前时，他们的能力却不断增强我们的脆弱性。而这种差距，也就是我们对手能力的变化，我们——我的意思是，你和我从未生活在这样一个世界里：俄罗斯和中国拥有比我们更强大的武器能力。这是一个令人恐惧的处境。这就是为什么《核态势委员会》呼吁我们必须做得比现在更多，因为如果我们在原地踏步，而我们的对手正在跑马拉松，那么我们目前正在输掉这场比赛。\n宾根女士：嗯，而且你知道——我们以前没有、而现在真正看到的是这种双对手核挑战：中国正沿着一条轨迹发展，其核力量从几百枚增加到什么程度？一千枚、1500枚。情报界在收集和分析这些威胁方面准备得有多充分——这种双对手挑战，以及其中一方可能伺机而动的风险？你如何看待情报界在这方面的能力？\n特纳众议员：嗯，我最担心的问题——也是你我讨论过的——是俄罗斯和中国正在寻求的能力，是我们所谓的先发制人打击能力，即这种能力会促使它们改变判断，考虑是否能够对美国发动攻击，使我们无法作出回应，并且凭借这种先发制人打击能力，显著削弱我们威慑它们的能力。\n但我认为，在情报方面，真正重要的是，很多事情我们其实已经公开知晓；只是我们没有进行公开讨论。现在，任何人都可以拿出自己的手机，在谷歌上搜索“中国洲际弹道导弹发射井扩建”，你就会看到一张太空图像，显示中国正在建设、用于部署新型核武器的发射井。然后，如果你搜索“美国对中国扩充核武器的回应”，你不会找到任何有帮助的信息。这正是我们需要进行对话和讨论的地方。这就是为什么你们在战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）所做的工作如此重要：把实际发生的事情提出来，说明它需要被纳入对话，这样我们才能作出政策回应，并且在此基础上，由美国采取真正的行动来应对这些威胁。\n宾根女士：是的。我想把话题转向延伸威慑。你与我们的盟友和伙伴进行了大量接触。你曾——你曾任北约议会大会主席，如今仍在那里担任重要领导职务。我不认为，如果你看看广大的美国公众，我们已经充分——或者说，充分理解了延伸威慑这一概念。其前提是，我们的盟友不发展核武器；你知道，我们不扩散核能力；作为交换，我们向他们延伸提供核保护伞。因此，面对我们讨论过的所有威胁——来自中国的威胁，以及——\n这是朝鲜所为；你知道，看到韩国和日本开始在核问题上展开一些对话，甚至也令人感到震撼——那么，在你们所有的讨论中，我们的盟友和伙伴对我们的核保护伞有多大信心？他们的思考如何塑造我们的核政策，又如何影响国会看待这些问题的方式？\n\n特纳议员：所以，我们刚刚与贵方在战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）的核问题学者讨论了这个问题。有趣的是，你知道，你必须在延伸威慑政策的基础上再向前迈一步。\n\n所谓延伸威慑，就是美国通过其核保护伞表示：我们将保护那些不发展核武器的其他盟友，为他们提供我们的保护伞。如果他们遭到攻击，而他们自身不发展核武器，我们将——我们已经——承诺进行防御，并以我们的核保护伞作为回应。其目的不仅是让我们的盟友不发展核武器，尽管我们当时确实支持防止核扩散。我们的盟友不成为核国家，这当然是一项胜利。但这也旨在遏制我们的对手扩充核武器，因为如果我们的盟友——那些与我们站在一起的人——增强了他们的核武器，按照当时的设想，我们的对手也将不得不增强他们的核武器。这样一来，不仅我们的盟友会出现核扩散，我们的对手也会拥有更多核武器，因为他们必须同时对我们和如今拥有核武器的盟友实施反威慑。\n\n问题在于，事情并没有这样发展。中国正在扩充。俄罗斯正在扩充。朝鲜正在扩充。伊朗仍在继续朝着具备制造核武器的能力迈进。\n\n因此，延伸威慑保护伞未能促使我们的对手作出我们原本希望的反应；而且正如你刚才所描述的，扩充的规模如此巨大——中国在扩充过程中进行的倍增，已经导致我们的非核盟友开始怀疑，美国是否会动用核武器作为威慑保护伞来保护他们。我认为，当韩国现在不仅着眼于朝鲜，也着眼于中国所带来的日益增长的威胁时，他们提出这个问题是完全合理的。\n\n宾根女士：那么，这就引出了军备控制和降低风险的话题。《新削减战略武器条约》将于2026年到期。到目前为止，我认为中国拒绝了与美国举行军备控制谈判。\n\n那么，军备控制的未来是什么？你是否看到两党能够就此形成共识的前进道路——不仅是在军备控制和加强防扩散方面，我想说，也可能像《新削减战略武器条约》最初达成时那样，存在一项更大的协议或妥协：是的，我们将根据《新削减战略武器条约》实施军备控制；与此同时，我们也需要实现核威慑力量现代化。\n\n你认为未来会出现类似这样的局面吗？\n\n特纳议员：我的意思是，罗纳德·里根的一生——抱歉，罗纳德·里根所说的“以实力求和平”，并不只是一句政治口号。现实是，无论是在商业交易中还是在核武器谈判中，如果对方不相信能从你这里得到任何好处，你永远不可能让任何人坐到谈判桌前。\n\n如果你认为对方已经自愿、单方面地放弃了你可能想从他们那里得到的东西，你就不会坐在对方面前谈判；而这正是我们把自己置于的境地。\n\n我们有——俄罗斯和中国正在\n他们正在扩充核武器。他们正在扩充核武器能力。我们有一些想从他们那里得到的东西。但他们不相信我们目前有意愿、兴趣或手段采取任何方向的行动，甚至是扩充，而这本可以促使他们约束我们，因此我们在核武器削减谈判的任何机会方面都陷入了停滞。\n\n我支持军备控制。事实证明它是有效的。它已经多次达成成果。正如我在演讲中所说，拆除一个破坏稳定的武器检查机制——你知道，我们在俄罗斯和美国双方都有许多人彼此熟悉，也熟悉对方的设施。所有这些都已成为过去，当然，我们因此变得不那么安全了。\n\n宾根女士：至于您提到的里根总统，里根总统也相信我们可以推进防扩散议程和军备控制议程，但正如您所说，要通过实力地位来实现，同时确保我们拥有强大的核威慑力量。\n\n我们的另一位青年学者拉克兰·麦肯齐提出了一个有趣的问题：核神话——关于核武器或核风险，有哪一种误解是您希望能够向美国公众澄清的？\n\n特纳众议员：我确实相信，美国公众以为我们已经部署了一套投入运行的导弹防御系统，可以保护我们免受中国和俄罗斯的攻击。但遗憾的是，从奥巴马政府，到特朗普政府的短暂任期，再到拜登政府，我们一直奉行反导弹防御政策。\n\n请记住，导弹防御最初由罗纳德·里根提出时，被认为会升级局势、破坏稳定，并且对手会认为，因为我们拥有导弹防御，所以他们需要更多核武器——认为它不会奏效，而且成本太高。\n\n然而，我们看到的实际情况是，它确实能够降级局势，而不是升级局势。伊朗向以色列发射300枚导弹时，我们与以色列共同使用导弹防御技术将其拦截，这就说明了这一点。\n\n想象一下，如果以色列遭到300枚导弹袭击，必然会对伊朗作出怎样的反应。相反，局势得到了降级。它确实有效。我们在技术方面所验证的一切都能说明这一点。你和我曾在国会山共同参与这项工作，包括与以色列达成共享技术的协议，用于“铁穹”系统。\n\n当然，成本方面也得到了证明，因为通过导弹防御技术，你能够消除原本会造成的破坏性力量。\n\n宾根女士：战略态势委员会也以两党一致的方式特别强调了这一点，建议我们扩大导弹防御，不仅应对战略导弹威胁，也应对这些更具胁迫性的巡航导弹和其他如今能够瞄准美国本土的威胁。\n\n特纳众议员：我们能够保护美国本土，而且我们应该这样做。\n\n宾根女士：北约。您是北约议会大会的高级领导人。几周后，北约峰会将在华盛顿特区举行，纪念北约成立75周年。您能否分享一下您对北约当前状况以及未来发展方向的看法？我还要穿插一个来自观众的问题，提问者是俄亥俄州乌克兰文化协会的娜塔莉·格里申。必须把俄亥俄州这一点写进来：特纳主席，感谢您进行了清晰而切中要点的演讲。您认为\n白宫方面对于乌克兰加入北约的路径，在即将举行的北约峰会上应持什么立场？所以，也请谈谈您对乌克兰局势的评估，以及从现在起局势将如何发展。\n\n特纳众议员：这是一次令人振奋的北约峰会，因为峰会的成功其实早在所有人召开会议之前就已经实现了。这一成功就是北约扩大，将芬兰和瑞典纳入其中；而且事实上，他们将作为正式成员出席会议，这显示出普京试图遏制北约的政策已经失败。他实际上把人们推入了北约。瑞典和芬兰加入北约时，其军事能力在所有北约国家中都处于最高水平之列。因此，他们正在增强北约的能力。\n\n我认为，他们加入北约并坐在那张桌旁，反映了北约作为一个联盟真正取得的成功，也反映了普京侵略的失败，以及人们认识到这里确实存在风险这一现实考量。瑞典和芬兰加入北约并不是因为乌克兰。他们加入——芬兰和瑞典加入北约，是因为他们知道其他国家会成为下一个目标。这就是我们支持乌克兰的原因。这就是我们需要继续支持乌克兰的原因。\n\n我认为，在这次峰会上，应该只是再次重申《布加勒斯特声明》。在布加勒斯特，北约声明乌克兰将成为北约成员。这是北约对希望加入北约的国家所作出的最强声明——格鲁吉亚和乌克兰都得到了这一声明——也是北约对有意加入的国家作出的最强声明。我认为，这是北约持续作出的承诺。你们也能从向乌克兰提供的数十亿美元武器和能力中看到这一点，这些武器和能力正被用于帮助乌克兰抵御俄罗斯。\n\n宾根女士：您是国会山上最早明确阐述必须通过那项立法的主要声音之一，以确保乌克兰确实能够从美国及其他国家获得安全援助和弹药。所以，感谢您所做的一切。\n\n技术与新兴技术。有几个领域正在发展。我们已经谈过太空。还有网络、超高音速、人工智能、生物技术；这些领域都能为作战和情报界带来益处，但如果看看中国可能正在这些领域采取的行动，它们也带来安全风险。那么，您如何看待这些技术，以及情报界需要做些什么来保持其优势？\n\n我还要把亚历山大·吉文关于核问题的一个问题结合进来：自核武器诞生以来，核武器塑造了人们对冲突的看法和冲突的进行方式。那么，您如何看待人工智能和网络技术与核武器之间的互动？也就是说，这是一个更广泛的新兴技术问题，同时也是一个关于人工智能和网络技术与核武器的问题。\n\n特纳众议员：好的。人工智能是最重要的发展之一，因为它能够提供分析能力和辅助。我有机会在亨利·基辛格去世前不久与他会面。当时，他——他在前一年刚刚出版了一本关于人工智能的书。他当时仍在继续发表有关人工智能的文章。他谈到了人工智能在战略规划中的重要性。\n\n但他进一步表示，作为一项政策，我们绝不应在没有要求人工智能向我们说明其得出结论的方式、它是如何形成并向我们提出这些结论的情况下实施人工智能，否则人类知识的进步将会停止，而计算机和机器学习将会不断发展。他非常坚定地强调这种关联。我认为，人类因素在\n利用人工智能将变得极其重要。我不认为人工智能应该被用于有关核武器的任何决策，甚至在我们考虑冲突的实施时也不应如此。显然，战略、评估和判断将变得极其重要。但决策应当由人来作出。我们在人工智能方面最大的威胁是我们的对手抢先一步。中国拥有一个监控社会，通过实施这种监控文化来进一步压制其民众，并试图将其出口。他们还将其与网络行动结合起来，以访问和下载数据，从而了解其他国家、人口及其活动。对一个威权政权而言，利用人工智能可能是我们很长时间以来所见到的、对人类以及自由和权利问题的最大威胁。\n\n宾根女士：非常感谢。在其他技术领域，还有高超音速。我不想抢你的风头，但你我曾谈过这个问题。就《美国法典》第10编（国防部权限）和第50编（情报界权限）而言，你是少数能够看到全貌的议员之一。因此，当我们审视这些不同的技术领域时，红方让你担忧什么？蓝方在做什么？请谈谈，我想，你在那里看到的一些紧张关系，以及你如何在第10编、国防部和第50编、情报界之间实现更大程度的整合？\n\n特纳议员：是的。接着你刚才的描述，在情报领域，我们关注我们的对手。他们在做什么？在国家安全和国防领域，我们规划我们要做什么。有时这两者并不匹配，信息也无法从一方传到另一方。显然，曾有一段时期我们远远领先于我们的对手。因此，了解对手在做什么很重要，但与此同时，这并没有说明我们与他们之间能力的差距。\n\n而这种差距的演变及其识别，我认为最集中地体现在高超音速问题上。美国曾遥遥领先，但技术被窃取，中国和俄罗斯如今已经部署了高超音速项目。高超音速武器已经落入我们的对手手中。我们目前没有任何已经交付、甚至具备部署能力的高超音速武器。这是公开信息。如果你看看你所知道的我们的对手在高超音速方面正在做什么，再看看你所知道的美国正在做什么，即使我们明天完成高超音速项目中图纸上的全部计划，我们仍然落后于他们目前的水平。\n\n因此，我们必须解决这个问题，绝不能再次让自己陷入这样的境地：首先放弃一个项目，然后把标准定得低于我们已知对手已经具备的能力。这一点——我认为，如果说过去几年中国、俄罗斯、朝鲜和伊朗发生的一件事最令人难以忘怀、最值得人们关注，那就是——我们都看到了。习近平主席站在弗拉基米尔·普京旁边。他前往莫斯科，展示对这种“无限友谊”、与弗拉基米尔·普京“不设上限”的关系的支持。\n\n还有一个我们所谓的“麦克风未关”时刻，当时他们并没有在做他们\n演讲，但麦克风是开着的，习近平主席被听到了。他对弗拉基米尔·普京说，我们正在带来的——我们，指他们双方共同——是一场百年来未曾发生过的变革。请对此稍作停留。我们知道100年前发生了什么。那就是第一次世界大战和第二次世界大战。那是威权主义与民主之间的斗争。他们就是这么说的。他们说，我们共同——他们想要重新进行威权主义与民主之间的冲突。而且他们相信自己会获胜。如果我们不挺身而出，确保他们不具备我们无法应对的能力，他们当然可能获胜。\n\n宾根女士：本周弗拉基米尔·普京与金正恩访问朝鲜时，你们又看到这种情况发生了。所以，CQ Roll Call的Rachel Oswald提问：美国应该多么担心俄罗斯现在可能向朝鲜提供技术援助，以改进朝鲜的远程弹道导弹，以及其直接瞄准美国的能力？\n\n特纳议员：他们现在就已经具备那种能力了。当然，俄罗斯与朝鲜之间的合作会增强他们这样做的能力。我想，我们都凭直觉感到，中国、俄罗斯、朝鲜和伊朗正在共同合作，既在发展能力方面，也在对美国进行威胁方面。我认为，这些象征性的会晤——应该让我们聚焦于这样一个事实：这是一种已经在发生的威胁。\n\n宾根女士：我现在要跳着问一些观众提出的其他问题。这是我们CSIS的一名航空航天实习生Annalise Johnson提出的，她恰好是俄亥俄州立大学的学生：你预计持续对俄罗斯实施的制裁将如何影响其太空和核计划？然后，美国应当寻求与俄罗斯保持何种程度的接触？\n\n特纳议员：这是一个很好的问题，因为问题在于：正如政府所说，制裁是否会显著影响俄罗斯发展核反卫星武器的道路？我认为，我们当然应该尝试。我认为我们需要尝试一切——你不会听到政府说，这是如此不可逾越的红线，这是如此巨大的变化和灾难性动态，使我们在将太空融入经济、国际防务和通信方面所取得的全部成就都面临风险；这条红线如此严重，以至于我们将实施额外的金融限制和制裁制度，甚至考虑我们要与北约盟友采取什么行动，作出如此强有力的声明，让俄罗斯明白，这——这不是会被接受的事情。\n\n宾根女士：好的。你在开头简短提到了伊朗，但我想回到这个问题。科学与国际安全研究所的Paul Tervo提问：伊朗近期扩建核浓缩设施的情况已得到国际原子能机构确认。科学与国际安全研究所估计，伊朗现在生产核武器所需的时间已大幅缩短，不到一个月的三分之一。那么，你认为伊朗在这方面的能力和实力将如何影响未来的中东政策？\n\n特纳议员：嗯，当你审视中东地区的不稳定时，所有道路实际上都通向伊朗：他们扶持的代理组织——哈马斯、真主党和胡塞武装；他们发起挑战的领域，在这些领域他们已经……\n……沙特阿拉伯以及我们的其他盟友；当然，还有我们在以色列冲突中所看到的情况。这些仍然都与伊朗的破坏稳定作用有关，政府需要对此加以遏制。\n\n宾根女士：这是国家核安全管理局（NNSA）的波莉·凯姆提出的问题。这很有意思：为了确保盟友安心并威慑对手，您认为我们应该隐瞒什么、披露什么？我记得，我们在太空领域以及其他领域也讨论过这些问题：在什么情况下应该披露某项能力，而在什么情况下显然要保护该系统的精妙性或性能。那么，您如何看待隐瞒与披露之间的取舍？\n\n特纳众议员：情报之所以被收集，是为了影响结果；并不是让我们成为随意旁观的人。如果你不利用情报去影响结果，那么情报就没有价值。我们开展情报工作，不只是为了让自己了解情况。在这件事上，正如我呼吁政府解密该项目的状态一样，说明俄罗斯项目究竟处于什么发展阶段，并不会危及手段、方法和技术。然后，我们就可以据此明确：我们的待办事项是什么？我们需要做什么？我们的盟友需要做什么？作为一个世界性的西方经济共同体，我们需要如何共同应对？\n\n宾根女士：那么，来自阿贾伊·戈库尔的问题。他是麦克亨利学者，正在乔治城大学攻读硕士学位：印度在这里发挥什么作用？美国如何借助印度发挥建设性作用，尤其是在核扩散或核不扩散方面？众所周知，印度与俄罗斯有着有趣的历史关系，但与中国和巴基斯坦的关系也日益对立。这些国家都是核国家。那么，您认为印度今后将发挥什么作用？\n\n特纳众议员：我确实认为这很重要，因为如果你看看印度，再看看非洲，它们的发展之所以能够发生，并不是因为它们回头去建设我们在七八十年代拥有的基础设施；而是因为它们在基础设施和技术方面实现了跨越式发展。它们建立通信系统、经济基础设施和能源体系的能力，全部都依赖于太空。今天的非洲和印度之所以成为今天的样子，是因为太空。如果太空——如果太空时代的进步不复存在，非洲和印度就会陷入孤立。\n\n宾根女士：我想回到您担任情报委员会主席这一角色上。您和首席少数党议员希姆斯推出“超越保密隔离区”（Beyond the SCIF）系列，值得高度赞扬。你们没有把所有事情都以机密形式处理，而是非常有意识地公开谈论委员会所关注的问题。\n\n与此相关，我想谈谈这个问题——我姑且称之为对情报界的信任。近年来，媒体报道和评论一直在反复指出，人们对情报界的信任已经减弱，坦率地说，对美国国家安全机构整体的信任也在减弱。我有机会看到军事情报界正在开展的最佳工作。我知道，您在自己的职位上也见过很多这样的工作。那么，鉴于情报机构所发挥的关键安全作用，您认为我们应该如何建立对这些机构的信任？\n\n特纳众议员：是的。我们赋予情报界卓越的\n如果这些工具被用于美国公众身上，会带来极大风险，并可能 destabilize a democracy（破坏民主的稳定）。就我们所看到的情况，以及当然包括我们在公共场合讨论过的最严重违规行为而言，它们大多存在于司法部和联邦调查局，而这两个机构并不是情报共同体的生成者，而是情报使用者。我确实认为，我们需要重点改革司法部和联邦调查局彼此结合并利用情报的方式。\n\n在我们续期《外国情报监视法》第702条时，我们提出了54项改革，主要针对司法部和联邦调查局及其对情报的使用，并确保法院系统参与监督，同时对他们加以约束和限制。这将是一个持续存在的问题。我认为，美国公众在浏览那些从眼前掠过的新闻报道时，也能看到司法追究方式存在不平等，以及这些信息的使用方式存在不平等。这确实是一个问题。\n\n宾根女士：而且你们刚刚把情报共同体——你们的《情报授权法》，即IAA——提交委员会审议并通过了。所以，恭喜你。你能否花一分钟谈谈委员会的优先事项？不过，你也暗示过，你的委员会可能会着手进行一些更广泛的情报共同体改革。\n\n特纳众议员：是的。我认为，我们现在共同面临的最大担忧之一，是本届政府不愿与盟友分享情报。乌克兰被迫在自己的领土上独自抗击俄罗斯的侵略，仿佛一只手被绑在背后。本届政府迟迟不愿向他们提供能够改变战场局势的致命武器。它也迟迟不愿授权他们在俄罗斯境内实际使用这些武器，攻击那些正在主动向乌克兰发射导弹、发动袭击的系统，以及武器基础设施——俄罗斯正在将其导弹产量提高四倍，以攻击乌克兰。但他们仍不愿向乌克兰提供有关俄罗斯在俄罗斯境内所作所为的情报。\n\n起初，本届政府拒绝向乌克兰提供任何有关俄军所在位置的情报——也就是俄军在乌克兰境内的位置。众议院和我们的情报委员会不得不施加压力。本届政府最终改变了立场。其影响如此显著，以至于他们冲到麦克风前，宣称应为改变自己的政策而获得功劳。因此，他们没有承认这一政策是错误的，反而想要把战场局势因解除自身限制而发生的变化归功于自己。\n\n宾根女士：我知道我们这里时间不多了。我想结束——嗯，我们已经就一系列问题进行了相当令人警醒的讨论。我想以一丝乐观结束。今天早些时候，你与这里CSIS的下一代核领域学者进行了交流。所以，我很好奇你对他们说了什么；你希望这些年轻学者在开启职业生涯时思考什么？而且，你知道，核武器、核反卫星能力，这些都是相当阴暗的话题。是什么让你对我们未来的发展仍然感到乐观？\n\n特纳众议员：是的。大多数国家在谈论一种脆弱性，或者谈论一个对手时，讨论的都是针对对手的实际行动。对我们而言，这确实是一份待办事项清单。当我们审视\n我们的风险在于，我们必须明确我们的待办事项，然后完成它。俄罗斯的经济规模与意大利相当，然而，它却主导着我们的防务政策讨论：我们这个庞大的经济体、创造力和能力是否会被用于应对他们正在完成的一些事情。我们拥有强大的能力，只要完成待办事项，就没有什么是我们无法完成的。许多国家有愿望清单，而我们有待办事项清单。我们只需填完待办事项清单，然后开始工作。\n\n宾根女士：你们还有一群非常出色的年轻核问题学者，他们希望参与这些议题并为未来安全的改善而努力。所以——\n\n特纳众议员：他们非常聪明。看到他们目前正在做的事情，以及他们未来想做的事情，真是令人惊叹；他们的问题也非常有见地。新一代人正在成长，尤其是因为他们亲眼目睹了正在发生的一切，他们绝对致力于建设一个强大的美国，因为他们知道这意味着自由和权利；没有它，我们所有人都将面临风险。\n\n宾根女士：特纳主席，非常感谢您。我想说，我们很少有机会邀请一个全委员会主席来到战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS），不仅就广泛议题进行这样的公开讨论，还能请您抽出时间与我们的年轻学者交流。所以，感谢您。感谢您提高人们对俄罗斯核反卫星威胁的认识，并强调其紧迫性。如果这件事继续推进，这绝对是一个“零日”事件——我的意思是，我们将回到20世纪50年代，而它所带来的威胁极其严重。因此，非常感谢您的领导，以及您在国会山、国际事务和教育美国公众方面所做的一切。\n\n对于在场的各位，我们将在这里的门厅举行午餐招待会，这是一个建立联系并继续交流的机会。再次感谢您，主席先生。\n\n特纳众议员：谢谢。（掌声。）（结束。）\n\n标签\n核问题，以及\n防务与安全\n项目与计划\n核问题项目\n航空航天安全项目\nCSIS国会事务\n国际安全项目\n防务与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-06-20T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2371","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"执行命令颂：为何执行命令应比现在更受重视","title_en":"Ode to the EXORD: Why Execute Orders Should Matter More Than They Do","summary_cn":"美国战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）发布报告指出，五角大楼当前对军事行动的管理正使其走向战略破产，并在大国战争中增加作战人员的风险。报告认为，任务不断累积与风险叠加侵蚀了美国的军事力量余量，而通过执行命令（EXORD）进行的行政式行动管理是问题的一部分。作者主张应重新聚焦EXORD，对现有任务进行定期全面评估，明确风险如何在不同任务、地域和时间之间被承担与转移，以校准和优先配置军事力量服务于政策目标。","summary_en":"A CSIS report argues that the Pentagon's current management of operations is leading it toward strategic insolvency and increasing risks to warfighters in a great-power war. A slow accumulation of missions and aggregating risk has depleted U.S. military margins. The administrative management of operations through execute orders (EXORDs) contributes to the problem. The author contends that renewed focus on EXORDs could help the Department of Defense calibrate, prioritize, and align military power with policy aims, including regular validation of strategic risk and clarity on how risk is assumed and transferred across missions, geography, and time.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:14:50.997569+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/ode-exord-why-execute-orders-should-matter-more-they-do","content":"Ode to the EXORD: Why Execute Orders Should Matter More Than They Do\nPhoto: Hulton Archive/Getty Images\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nReport\nby\nKrista Auchenbach\nPublished September 23, 2025\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Full Report\n1289kb\nThe Pentagon’s current management of operations is leading it toward strategic insolvency and increasing risks to warfighters in the event of great-power war. A slow but steady accumulation of mission and aggregating risk has depleted the United States’ margins of military power. This condition is both dangerous and insidious, and the administrative management of operations—through execute orders (EXORDs)—contributes to part of the problem. EXORDs are the instrument to capture the president’s direction, as commander-in-chief, to use military forces and effects. As the geopolitical environment changes, the Department of Defense (DoD) must take actions to calibrate, prioritize, and align the military element of power to serve strategic aims of policy.An area ripe for greater examination is how currently assigned missions are assessed against future needs. Competition for resources and growing threats demand regular and comprehensive validation of strategic risk, as well as explicit understanding of how those risks are assumed and transferred across missions, geography, and time. The absence of such clarity and codification can inhibit commanders and lower echelons from taking deliberate, risk-informed initiative. Moreover, it impedes the assessment, adaptation, and oversight necessary to oversee the employment of forces and effects to achieve strategic aims at acceptable costs. Despite progress, this report argues that a renewed focus on EXORDs could serve as part of the solution to better position the DoD for the precarious global security environment ahead.  This report is made possible by general support to CSIS. No direct sponsorship contributed to this report.\nTags\nDefense and Security\nImage\nKrista Auchenbach\nFormer Visiting Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nRelated Content\nImage\nDefense Budgets in an Uncertain Security Environment\nCommentary\nby\nSeamus P. Daniels\n— September 16, 2025\nImage\nDoD’s Shifting Homeland Defense Mission Could Undermine the Military’s Lethality\nReport\nby\nMelissa Dalton\n— April 22, 2025\nImage\nGlobal Security Forum 2024: Gathering Strength in a Gathering Storm\nApril 24, 2024 • 8:30 am – 5:00 pm EDT\nHosted by International Security Program","content_cn":"《致EXORD颂歌：为何执行命令的重要性应高于现状》\n图片：Hulton Archive/Getty Images\n目录\n目录\n报告\n作者\nKrista Auchenbach\n发表于2025年9月23日\n可下载内容\n下载完整报告\n1289kb\n五角大楼当前的行动管理正使其走向战略性资不抵债，并在大国战争发生时增加作战人员面临的风险。任务风险与累积风险缓慢但持续地增加，已经耗尽美国军事力量的余量。这种状况既危险又隐蔽，而通过执行命令（EXORD）进行的行动行政管理也助长了部分问题。EXORD是记录总统作为三军统帅对使用军事力量和军事效应所作指示的工具。随着地缘政治环境变化，国防部（DoD）必须采取行动，校准、确定优先级并协调军事力量要素，以服务于政策的战略目标。\n\n一个亟待进一步审视的领域，是如何根据未来需求评估当前分配的任务。资源竞争和不断增长的威胁要求定期、全面地验证战略风险，并明确了解这些风险如何在任务、地域和时间之间被承担和转移。缺乏这种清晰度和规范化，可能会阻碍指挥官及较低层级采取深思熟虑、以风险为依据的主动行动。此外，这还妨碍开展评估、调整和监督，而这些对于以可接受的成本监督力量和军事效应的运用、实现战略目标是必需的。尽管已经取得进展，本报告认为，重新聚焦EXORD可以成为解决方案的一部分，从而更好地使国防部应对前方脆弱的全球安全环境。\n\n本报告得以完成，离不开对CSIS的普遍支持。本报告没有任何直接赞助方。\n标签\n国防与安全\n图片\nKrista Auchenbach\n国防与安全部前访问研究员\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n相关内容\n图片\n不确定安全环境下的国防预算\n评论\n作者\nSeamus P. Daniels\n—2025年9月16日\n图片\n国防部不断变化的本土防御任务可能削弱军队的杀伤力\n报告\n作者\nMelissa Dalton\n—2025年4月22日\n图片\n2024年全球安全论坛：在风暴汇聚之际凝聚力量\n2024年4月24日•美国东部时间上午8:30–下午5:00\n由国际安全项目主办","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-09-23T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2550","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"巴拿马港口诉讼重塑美洲大国竞争：穆利诺主义付诸实践","title_en":"Panama’s Port Lawsuits Reshape Great Power Competition in the Americas: The Mulino Doctrine in Action","summary_cn":"2025年7月30日，巴拿马审计长弗洛雷斯就巴拿马港口公司合同向最高法院提起两起诉讼，指其存在严重违规并造成损失。文章认为此举是CSIS所称“穆利诺主义”的体现，即小国以法律主权而非选边站队应对中美围绕运河港口的博弈，并分析了贝莱德收购案、中远海运介入及三种可能走向。","summary_en":"On July 30, 2025, Panama's comptroller general filed two lawsuits against the Panama Ports Company contract, citing irregularities and losses. The commentary frames this as the \"Mulino Doctrine\" in action: a small state using legal sovereignty rather than choosing sides amid U.S.-China competition over canal ports, and outlines the BlackRock deal, COSCO's demands, and three scenarios.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:27:55.195840+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/panamas-port-lawsuits-reshape-great-power-competition-americas-mulino-doctrine-action","content":"Panama’s Port Lawsuits Reshape Great Power Competition in the Americas: The Mulino Doctrine in Action\nPhoto: LUIS ROBAYO/AFP via Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernandez\nand\nRyan C. Berg\nPublished August 4, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nOn July 30, 2025, as the world’s largest infrastructure deal teetered on the brink of collapse, Panama’s Comptroller General Anel Flores walked into the Supreme Court and filed two lawsuits that redefined great power competition in the Western Hemisphere. The lawsuits demanded that the decades-old contract between Panama and Panama Ports Company (PPC), a subsidiary of Hong Kong-based Hutchison Port Holdings, be rendered null and void over the “many irregularities” found during a months-long audit. The timing was notable. Just days earlier, China had effectively torpedoed BlackRock’s $22.8 billion bid to acquire CK Hutchison’s global port empire by demanding that state-owned COSCO shipping receive a stake in the consortium—and not just any stake, but “veto rights” over important decisions.Rather than chaos, Panama’s actions followed a deliberate and coordinated approach, which CSIS has called in previous research the “Mulino Doctrine.” As global powers maneuvered for control of two key ports at the strategic approaches to the Panama Canal, Panama took initiative through a fundamental expression of sovereignty: the rule of law. Critics of President José Raúl Mulino’s approach may now view these actions as a sophisticated application of national independence.Previous CSIS research delineated sovereignty as strategic practice versus sovereignty as vocal—but ultimately performative—gestures. Panama’s recent actions constitute the former.China’s Checkmate MoveTo understand Panama’s legal gambit, one must first grasp the scope of what China was on the cusp of accomplishing. In March 2025, BlackRock announced its historic deal to acquire 43 ports across 23 countries from CK Hutchison Holdings—199 berths overall. The crown jewels in this unprecedented deal were Panama’s Balboa and Cristóbal terminals. Donald Trump, setting the goal of “reclaiming the Panama Canal,” announced after the BlackRock deal became public, “The Panama Canal was built by Americans, for Americans, not for [others].”But Beijing had other plans. After an initial silence, the deal was met with increasing criticism in China’s state-owned outlets, with the Global Times and Ta Kung Pao criticizing Li Ka-shing, the nonagenarian business magnate whose family owns the CK Hutchison conglomerate, for showing insufficient loyalty and selling out the Chinese people. In addition to freezing Li out of any deals with Chinese state-owned enterprises (SOEs), Beijing ordered its State Administration for Market Regulation to intervene, delaying the deal on the grounds that it challenges the Belt and Road Initiative. In early July, rumors circulated that the exclusive period of negotiations between Hutchison and BlackRock would close at the end of the month without a resolution, unless BlackRock acceded to one of Beijing’s conditions. The Wall Street Journal revealed Beijing’s alleged terms: If the consortium bidding on Hutchison Port Holdings did not include COSCO Shipping, a Chinese SOE, there would be no deal. By July 28, 2025, CK Hutchison allowed BlackRock’s period of exclusive negotiations to expire and announced it would invite “a major strategic investor from the PRC”—widely understood to be COSCO after the Wall Street Journal and Bloomberg reported on the internal dynamics of the deal.In a single maneuver, China had transformed what the Trump administration had celebrated as a U.S. victory into a potential Chinese strategic triumph. The original consortium led by BlackRock would be forced to accommodate China’s state-owned shipping giant as a “significant member” or watch the deal collapse entirely. Furthermore, according to Bloomberg’s reporting of internal discussions, COSCO even demanded “veto rights” over critical decisions of the consortium. As a formerly sanctioned and blacklisted SOE, COSCO works closely with the People’s Liberation Army Navy and has a long track record of collaborating closely in China’s civil-military fusion strategy. On the other hand, CK Hutchison maintains a record of occasional independence from Beijing.This is where many traditional analysts would predict Panama’s capitulation. Small states, the conventional wisdom suggests, must choose sides when great powers collide—“the strong do what they can, while the weak suffer what they must,” to put it in Thucydides’ famous phrasing. Instead, Panama chose litigation.The Anatomy of Legal SovereigntyComptroller General Anel Flores filed two separate lawsuits on July 30, 2025, against the Panama Ports contract, citing serious irregularities and harm to the country’s interests during a months-long audit. But this was no bureaucratic exercise. “The contract was bad, one-sided and abusive, against the interests of the country,” Flores declared. “Panama is the true owner of the ports.”The legal challenge rests on devastating findings: The audit reportedly revealed evidence of multiple breaches of contract that amounted to more than $300 million in losses to Panamanian state coffers. More fundamentally, the audit found that PPC failed to get proper approvals for its 2021 no-bid contract extension, with tax breaks under the contract costing the country $1.3 billion.The key lies in the timing. Rather than opposing the BlackRock deal or capitulating to Chinese pressure, Panama exercised strategic patience and was thereby able to create a third option: legal sovereignty that transcends great power preferences. By subjecting the arrangements enjoyed by Hutchison Port Holding’s subsidiary to Supreme Court review, Panama intends for its most strategic assets to remain under conditions that serve its national interests, regardless of which global consortium ultimately prevails.Strategic Patience in ActionIn President Mulino’s candid response to the legal action at a press conference, he stated, “I do not at the moment see the continuation of the Panama Ports contract, amended or not.” However, he went on to say: “We will wait for the verdict.” This may reflect indecision but could also be strategic patience.Consider the calculus from Panama City’s perspective: If the BlackRock-Mediterranean Shipping Company consortium prevails, Panama enters negotiations from a position of legal strength. If China’s COSCO secures participation, especially under the terms Bloomberg reports it allegedly demands, Panama has shown its willingness to challenge unfavorable arrangements that could impinge on sovereignty. If the deal collapses entirely, Reuters reports that Panama’s President José Raúl Mulino considered that, “Public-private partnerships could take over two key ports near the Panama Canal if courts invalidate a contract with Hong Kong-based CK Hutchison’s . . . to operate them.”This marks a shift from more reactionary approaches to what former Secretary of State Henry Kissinger called “creative diplomacy”—the art of turning constraints into opportunities. The Mulino administration absorbed domestic criticism, at great cost to the president’s popularity and approval ratings, for security cooperation with the United States, while simultaneously preparing legal challenges to economic arrangements that potentially compromised national interests. This maneuver demonstrates that sovereignty requires institutional capacity, not just political will.The Hegseth Visit in ContextSecretary of Defense Pete Hegseth’s April 2025 visit to Panama now appears in a different light. The security cooperation memorandum signed during that visit sparked protests, with opposition figures denouncing alleged U.S. “militarization.” Yet the agreement was modest—formalizing consultation mechanisms that had existed informally for decades.The real significance lay in Panama’s management of the domestic blowback. Rather than vocal or even theatrical nationalism, the Mulino administration weathered criticism while maintaining its strategic course. In light of the shifting strategic context, this patience could be seen not as weakness but preparation, establishing that Panama would cooperate where cooperation served its interests while reserving the right to challenge arrangements that did not.The lawsuits against Hutchison Port Holdings’ subsidiary show strategy: Panama demonstrated it could sign security agreements with Washington while simultaneously challenging commercial arrangements with Hong Kong—based on legal analysis and national interest rather than ideological posturing.A Small States GuidePanama’s approach offers lessons for small states worldwide seeking to maneuver amid increasing great power competition. “Against this backdrop, Panama appears determined to regain control over its key port assets,” as one analysis notes. But this determination manifests through institutional mechanisms rather than dramatic confrontations, especially not against great powers.The contrast with traditional approaches is stark. Where other small states might have resorted to the rhetoric of nationalization or symbolic gestures, Panama deployed its Comptroller General’s Office, the Supreme Court, and its constitutional framework.Panama’s move represents what Klemens von Metternich understood about balance-of-power politics: The goal of smaller states should not be to defeat great powers but to maintain autonomy among them. Sovereignty is not a slogan but a practice requiring difficult choices about which relationships to prioritize and how to manage competing pressures. The Mulino Doctrine recognizes this complexity. Panama’s legal challenge creates options rather than commitments, flexibility rather than binary choices.Several Scenarios EmergeThe legal challenge is the latest twist in the global port sale saga, where the two Panamanian terminals lie at the heart of geopolitical competition, regulatory scrutiny, and the exercise of sovereignty. The outcome may provide an answer as to whether small states can maintain strategic autonomy in an era of intensifying great power competition.Following the latest moves by China, three principal scenarios emerge:Panama’s Supreme Court validates the contracts, Panama renegotiates from a position of strength, and any future arrangement reflects enhanced national interests rather than inherited colonial dynamics.Panama’s Supreme Court annuls the contracts, forcing all parties—BlackRock, Mediterranean Shipping Company, COSCO, or other bidders—to compete for new arrangements under Panamanian legal standards.Legal uncertainty will prevail for a while, providing Panama with permanent leverage over any global consortium and ensuring continuous alignment with national interests. French shipping giant CMA CGM is the latest to express interest in acquiring a stake in CK Hutchison’s global ports business, after exclusive talks between the Hong Kong conglomerate and a BlackRock-Terminal Investment Limited consortium expired. The multiplication of bidders only strengthens Panama’s position.In each scenario, Panama has an opportunity to emerge stronger. This is the essence of the Mulino Doctrine: using institutional mechanisms to create strategic options rather than accepting predetermined outcomes. The key insight is that strategic autonomy requires institutional capacity, not just political will. Panama can challenge arrangements that compromise its interests because it possesses the legal institutions and administrative competence to do so credibly. Other small states facing similar pressures from great power competition should note that Panama’s approach works not because of moral authority or international sympathy, but because of institutional sophistication backed by legal precision. Panama has transformed a crisis of external pressure into an opportunity for institutional assertion.The test of any doctrine lies in its practical application under pressure. The world is watching. And Panama is offering a valuable example.Carlos Ruiz-Hernández is a senior associate (non-resident) with the Americas Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Ryan C. Berg is director of the Americas Program and head of the Future of Venezuela Initiative at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAmericas, and\nCentral America\nImage\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernandez\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Americas Program\nImage\nRyan C. Berg\nDirector, Americas Program\nPrograms & Projects\nAmericas Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nEconomic Development and Trade: Supply Chains, Trade Linkages and Nearshoring\nStrategic Competition: China, Russia and Other Extra-Hemispheric Powers\nRelated Content\nImage\nThe Mulino Doctrine: How Panama Is Redefining Small State Strategy\nCommentary\nby\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernandez\n— June 16, 2025\nImage\nChinese Ports in Panama Come Under New Management\nCritical Questions\nby\nCSIS Scholars\n— March 6, 2025\nImage\nPanama: From Zoned Out to Strategic Opportunity\nCommentary\nby\nAndrew Sanders\nand\nRyan C. Berg\n— January 16, 2025","content_cn":"巴拿马港口诉讼重塑美洲大国竞争：穆利诺主义付诸实践\n照片：LUIS ROBAYO/法新社，Getty Images供图\n评论\n作者\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernandez\n和\nRyan C. Berg\n发表于2025年8月4日\n远程可视化\n2025年7月30日，全球最大基础设施交易濒临崩溃之际，巴拿马总审计长阿内尔·弗洛雷斯走进最高法院，提起两起诉讼，重新定义了西半球的大国竞争。这些诉讼要求宣布巴拿马与巴拿马港务公司（PPC）之间已有数十年历史的合同无效。PPC是总部位于香港的和记港口控股公司的子公司；在为期数月的审计中发现了“诸多违规行为”。时机颇为引人注目。就在几天前，中国实际上挫败了贝莱德以228亿美元收购和记黄埔全球港口帝国的竞标，要求国有企业中远海运在财团中获得股份——而且不是任意股份，而是对重要决策拥有“否决权”。与其说巴拿马的行动造成了混乱，不如说它遵循了一种有意且协调的做法，CSIS在此前的研究中将其称为“穆利诺主义”。当全球大国竞相控制巴拿马运河战略通道附近的两个关键港口时，巴拿马通过主权的一项根本体现——法治——采取了主动。何塞·劳尔·穆利诺总统这一做法的批评者如今或许会将这些行动视为对国家独立的成熟运用。CSIS此前的研究将主权界定为战略实践，而非口头表达但最终只是表演性的姿态。巴拿马最近的行动属于前者。\n中国的将军一着\n要理解巴拿马的法律策略，首先必须把握中国当时即将完成的行动规模。2025年3月，贝莱德宣布了一项具有历史意义的交易，将从和记黄埔控股收购分布于23个国家的43座港口——总计199个泊位。这笔前所未有交易中的皇冠明珠，是巴拿马的巴尔博亚港和克里斯托瓦尔港码头。唐纳德·特朗普以“收回巴拿马运河”为目标，在贝莱德交易公开后宣布：“巴拿马运河是美国人建造的，为美国人服务，不是为[其他人]服务的。”\n但北京另有打算。在最初保持沉默后，中国国营媒体对这笔交易的批评日益增加。《环球时报》和《大公报》批评九旬商界巨头李嘉诚——其家族拥有和记黄埔集团——表现出不够的忠诚，出卖了中国人民。除将李嘉诚排除在与中国国有企业（SOE）的任何交易之外，北京还命令国家市场监督管理总局介入，以该交易挑战“一带一路”倡议为由拖延交易。7月初，有传言称和记与贝莱德之间的排他性谈判期将在月底结束而无法达成解决方案，除非贝莱德接受北京的一项条件。《华尔街日报》披露了北京据称提出的条件：如果竞购和记港口控股的财团不包括中远海运这一中国国有企业，交易就不会达成。截至2025年7月28日，和记黄埔任由贝莱德的排他性谈判期到期，并宣布将邀请“一家来自中华人民共和国的主要战略投资者”——在《华尔街日报》和彭博社报道后，外界普遍认为这家投资者就是中远海运\n报道称交易的内部动态。中国仅通过一次行动，就将特朗普政府曾大肆庆祝的美国胜利，转变成一场潜在的中国战略胜利。由黑石集团牵头的原财团将被迫接纳中国国有航运巨头，成为“重要成员”，否则就眼看交易彻底破裂。此外，据彭博社对内部讨论的报道，中远海运甚至要求对财团的关键决策拥有“否决权”。作为一家曾受制裁并被列入黑名单的国有企业，中远海运与中国人民解放军海军密切合作，并长期积极参与中国的军民融合战略。另一方面，和记黄埔则有时表现出对北京的独立性。许多传统分析人士会据此预测巴拿马将屈服。传统观点认为，当大国发生冲突时，小国必须选边站——用修昔底德著名的话说，“强者可以为所欲为，弱者只能承受苦难”。然而，巴拿马选择了诉讼。\n\n法律主权的剖析\n\n2025年7月30日，审计署署长阿内尔·弗洛雷斯针对巴拿马港口合同分别提起两项诉讼，理由是在持续数月的审计中发现了严重违规行为，并损害了国家利益。但这并非一项官僚主义作业。弗洛雷斯宣称：“这份合同糟糕、片面且具有滥用性，违背国家利益。巴拿马才是港口的真正所有者。”\n\n这场法律挑战建立在令人震撼的调查结果之上：据报道，审计发现了多起合同违约证据，给巴拿马国库造成的损失超过3亿美元。更根本的是，审计发现，巴拿马港口公司未能为其2021年无招标合同延期取得适当批准，而合同中的税收减免使该国损失了13亿美元。\n\n关键在于时机。巴拿马没有反对黑石集团的交易，也没有屈服于中国的压力，而是采取了战略耐心，从而创造出第三种选择：超越大国偏好的法律主权。通过将和记港口控股子公司所享有的安排提交最高法院审查，巴拿马意在确保其最具战略意义的资产始终处于符合国家利益的条件之下，无论最终哪一个全球财团胜出。\n\n战略耐心的实践\n\n总统穆利诺在一次新闻发布会上坦率回应这项法律行动时表示：“我目前看不到巴拿马港口合同继续存在的可能，无论是否进行修订。”然而，他随后又说：“我们将等待判决。”这可能反映出犹豫不决，但也可能是战略耐心的体现。\n\n从巴拿马城的角度来看，可以这样权衡：如果黑石集团—地中海航运公司财团胜出，巴拿马将凭借法律优势地位进入谈判。如果中国的中远海运获得参与资格，尤其是在彭博社报道其据称要求的条件下，巴拿马已经表明，它愿意挑战可能侵害主权的不利安排。如果交易彻底破裂，路透社报道称，巴拿马总统何塞·劳尔·穆利诺曾考虑：“如果法院裁定与总部位于香港的和记黄埔签订的运营合同无效，公私合作伙伴关系可以接管巴拿马运河附近的两个关键港口……”\n\n这标志着巴拿马从更具反应性的……\n迈向前国务卿亨利·基辛格所称的“创造性外交”——将约束转化为机遇的艺术。穆利诺政府为与美国开展安全合作而承受了国内批评，这对总统的民望和支持率造成了高昂代价；与此同时，政府准备对可能损害国家利益的经济安排提出法律挑战。这一举措表明，主权需要制度能力，而不仅仅是政治意愿。\n\n海格塞思访巴背景\n\n美国国防部长皮特·海格塞思于2025年4月访问巴拿马，如今看来意义有所不同。访问期间签署的安全合作备忘录引发了抗议，反对派人士指责所谓的美国“军事化”。然而，该协议并不激进，只是将数十年来以非正式方式存在的磋商机制正式化。\n\n真正的重要性在于巴拿马如何处理国内反弹。穆利诺政府没有采取高调甚至戏剧化的民族主义姿态，而是在维持战略方向的同时经受住了批评。鉴于战略环境不断变化，这种耐心可以被视为并非软弱，而是准备工作，表明巴拿马会在合作符合自身利益的地方开展合作，同时保留挑战不符合其利益的安排的权利。\n\n针对哈钦森港口控股公司子公司的诉讼展现了这一战略：巴拿马证明了自己既能与华盛顿签署安全协议，也能同时依据法律分析和国家利益，而非意识形态姿态，挑战与香港有关的商业安排。\n\n小国指南\n\n巴拿马的做法为世界各地寻求在大国竞争日益加剧之际周旋的小国提供了经验。“在这一背景下，巴拿马似乎决心重新掌控其关键港口资产，”一份分析指出。但这种决心通过制度机制体现出来，而不是通过戏剧性的对抗，尤其不是与大国对抗。\n\n这与传统做法形成鲜明对比。其他小国可能诉诸国有化 rhetoric或象征性举措，而巴拿马则动用了审计署、最高法院及其宪法框架。\n\n巴拿马的举动体现了克莱门斯·冯·梅特涅对均势政治的理解：较小国家的目标不应是击败大国，而应是在大国之间保持自主。主权不是口号，而是一种实践，需要就优先发展哪些关系以及如何管理相互竞争的压力作出艰难选择。穆利诺主义认识到了这种复杂性。巴拿马的法律挑战创造了选择，而不是承诺；带来了灵活性，而不是二元选择。\n\n几种情景浮现\n\n这项法律挑战是全球港口出售传奇的最新转折点，巴拿马的两座码头正处于地缘政治竞争、监管审查和主权行使的核心。其结果或许将回答这样一个问题：在大国竞争日益激烈的时代，小国能否保持战略自主。\n\n随着中国采取最新行动，三种主要情景浮现：巴拿马最高法院确认这些合同有效，巴拿马以强势地位重新谈判，任何未来安排都体现增强后的国家利益，而不是承袭的殖民动态。\n\n巴拿马最高法院宣布这些合同无效\n合同，迫使所有各方——贝莱德、地中海航运公司、中远集团或其他竞标者——按照巴拿马法律标准争夺新的安排。法律上的不确定性将在一段时间内持续存在，使巴拿马能够持续对任何全球财团施加影响，并确保其始终与国家利益保持一致。法国航运巨头达飞海运集团（CMA CGM）是最新一家表示有意收购和记黄埔全球港口业务股份的公司，此前这家香港企业集团与贝莱德—Terminal Investment Limited财团之间的排他性谈判已经到期。竞标者的增加只会进一步巩固巴拿马的地位。\n\n在每一种情形下，巴拿马都有机会变得更加强大。这正是穆利诺主义的本质：利用制度机制创造战略选项，而不是接受预先确定的结果。关键洞见在于，战略自主需要的是制度能力，而不仅仅是政治意愿。巴拿马能够挑战损害其利益的安排，因为它拥有可以可信地做到这一点的法律制度和行政能力。其他面临大国竞争类似压力的小国应当注意，巴拿马的方法之所以奏效，并不是因为道德权威或国际同情，而是因为有法律精准性支撑的制度成熟度。巴拿马已将外部压力造成的危机转变为制度主张的机遇。\n\n任何主义的检验都在于其在压力下的实际运用。全世界都在关注。而巴拿马正在提供一个有价值的范例。\n\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernández是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心美洲项目的高级副研究员（非驻任）。Ryan C. Berg是美洲项目主任，也是CSIS委内瑞拉未来倡议负责人。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作，该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n美洲，以及\n中美洲\n图片\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernandez\n高级顾问（非驻任），美洲项目\n图片\nRyan C. Berg\n美洲项目主任\n项目与计划\n美洲项目\n地缘政治与外交政策\n经济发展与贸易：供应链、贸易联系与近岸外包\n战略竞争：中国、俄罗斯及其他半球外大国\n相关内容\n图片\n穆利诺主义：巴拿马如何重新定义小国战略\n评论文章\n作者\nCarlos Ruiz-Hernandez\n— 2025年6月16日\n图片\n巴拿马的中国港口迎来新管理者\n关键问题\n作者\nCSIS学者\n— 2025年3月6日\n图片\n巴拿马：从被分区隔离到战略机遇\n评论文章\n作者\nAndrew Sanders\n和\nRyan C. Berg\n— 2025年1月16日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-08-04T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-325","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"从石油美元到代币美元：AI时代的经济治国术","title_en":"From the Petrodollar to the Tokendollar: Economic Statecraft in the AI Era","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表评论文章，作者本杰明·詹森提出，美国应效仿20世纪70年代石油美元体系，构建以美元计价AI代币结算的“代币美元”架构。文章主张联合盟友建设涵盖能源、芯片、基础设施、模型与应用的完整AI技术栈，通过稳定币、期货市场与AI基础设施债券等工具，将美元确立为代币生成的默认计价单位，并借助小堆核电与数据中心外交拓展全球南方影响力。","summary_en":"In a CSIS commentary, Benjamin Jensen argues that the United States should build a 'tokendollar' architecture, analogous to the 1970s petrodollar, making the dollar the default unit of pricing for AI token generation. He urges a coalition of free nations to construct the full AI stack—energy, chips, infrastructure, models, and applications—linked to U.S. futures markets and dollar-backed stablecoins, and to use small modular reactors and data center diplomacy to expand influence in the Global South.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:56:43.888655+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/petrodollar-tokendollar-economic-statecraft-ai-era","content":"From the Petrodollar to the Tokendollar: Economic Statecraft in the AI Era\nPhoto: Aleksandra Sova/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nPublished August 25, 2026\nMoney defines power and, through it, international order. When the dollar came off the gold standard in the early 1970s, the United States built a new financial architecture that addressed commodity price inflation, energy flows, and global trade. Oil was traded largely in dollars. Saudi surpluses were recycled through U.S. banks and Treasury markets. U.S. firms sold goods, services, and technology to the Gulf. Security ties reinforced financial ties. A 1979 U.S. Government Accountability Office report described the U.S.-Saudi Joint Commission as a mechanism to strengthen political ties, support Saudi industrialization, recycle petrodollars, and expand flows of U.S. technology. The resulting petrodollar locked in U.S. dollar dominance and through it a larger security architecture.Now the United States should lead a coalition of free nations to build the global technology stack and make the dollar the default unit of pricing for token generation, the default commodity of the AI era. This pricing should be connected to U.S. futures markets and pegged cryptocurrencies to maximize liquidity. Furthermore, the stack should showcase U.S. technology designed to accelerate development in the Global South, including small modular reactors (SMRs) and next-generation infrastructure that supports building more data centers overseas to relieve domestic pressure in the United States. This combination of economic statecraft and infrastructure development represents an entirely new approach to diplomacy and development. The combination of economic statecraft and infrastructure development builds on earlier calls to link the dollar to compute and represents an entirely new approach to diplomacy and development.Tokenpolitik: Aligning AI Stack Construction with U.S. DiplomacyThe world is entering a new era of great power competition defined by tokenpolitik and by which side has the deepest AI stack. NVIDIA’s Jensen Huang describes the AI stack as a “five-layer cake”: energy, chips, infrastructure, models, and applications, with every application pulling on each layer beneath it down to the power plant. This stack is what powers “AI factories” that turn energy into tokens, making algorithmic intelligence a measurable industrial output.Tokens are the basic units of data an AI model processes and generates. They represent characters, numbers, pixels, and even small slices of audio files that have been decomposed to match against patterns and draw inferences to predict the best reply to a prompt. As a result, tokens determine context limits and cost estimates for AI projects. The more the economy comes to rely on AI applications, the more token usage and pricing will become a central driver of growth and productivity.In strategic terms, the side that can produce more trusted tokens per watt and per dollar will have the advantage in great power competition. That makes AI diplomacy increasingly aligned with infrastructure development. To date, the United States has had an advantage here based on deep private sector capital investments and leading frontier developers, but China is closing the gap. Its lead in energy and infrastructure investments in the Global South through initiatives such as the Belt and Road Initiative and Digital Silk Road represents a new foreign policy challenge.The race to bring AI to the Global South will be one of the decisive diplomacy and development challenges of the next generation because many states still lack the power systems, data centers, cloud infrastructure, and financing needed to benefit from AI at scale. The International Energy Agency projects that global data center electricity consumption could more than double by 2030, with AI as a central driver. Countries that cannot secure affordable and reliable power will rent intelligence from someone else. Countries that build AI factories on Chinese finance, equipment, and technology standards will enter a different technology order.This reality creates a new need to build AI factory corridors with allies and strategic partners globally, including in the Global South. Here, China has sought to outflank the United States, Europe, and other free economies for over a generation. These corridors need to account for the whole AI stack, and especially power. The U.S. government should help private sector companies by prioritizing key corridor projects and aligning different government authorities to support advanced SMRs, grid upgrades, secure fiber optic networks, workforce training, and other infrastructure upgrades required to create hyperscale clusters.Policy is already moving in this direction, though still in pieces. The White House executive order on promoting the export of the American AI technology stack calls for a coordinated national effort to export full-stack U.S. AI packages. The Department of Commerce’s American AI Exports Program asks industry-led consortia to deliver integrated offerings that include AI-optimized hardware, data pipelines, models, cybersecurity, and applications.Promoting the export of the AI stack is an interagency effort. The State Department can align allies and partner governments, including existing efforts linked to Pax Silica, to identify key corridors. Agencies such as the Development Finance Corporation (DFC) and Export-Import Bank of the United States (EXIM) can invest in supporting infrastructure and incentivize private sector firms in the United States to build the AI stack globally. At the same time, the Department of Energy can lead SMR and grid diplomacy while the Department of the Treasury, the Department of Commerce, and even the Department of Homeland Security, through the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA), can play a role. The AI stack will therefore form a network of infrastructure that creates power and influence, and through it, a new international order.The TokendollarTo secure its central position in a new international order, the United States should advocate for tokens to clear in dollars. Similar to the petrodollar, the tokendollar would support demand for U.S. dollars, lower borrowing costs, and larger deficits over the next generation, economic benefits whose importance is only increasing owing to the size of government debt and increased borrowing costs.Furthermore, tokendollar contracts traded on futures markets would also let firms hedge future AI costs. AI infrastructure bonds could finance data centers against contracted token capacity. Insurance markets could price outage risk, cyber risk, and performance risk. Universities and national labs could receive token-dollar research credits. The U.S. government could even champion a new form of security cooperation, supporting allies with surge access to AI inference through tokendollar contract vehicles.This new financial order could be linked to stablecoins. The United States should use a dollar-backed stablecoin to settle tokendollar contracts. The GENIUS Act created a federal stablecoin framework and requires 100 percent reserve backing with liquid assets such as dollars or short-term Treasuries, thus providing a framework for linking digital financing to tokendollar contracts.The Next Chapter of Economic StatecraftThe tokendollar can become the key architecture for a new era of AI diplomacy and development that links finance, infrastructure, technology standards, and deeper public–private sector coordination. It will require concerted effort over the next two years to come to fruition.The Trump administration should create a National AI Stack and Tokendollar Initiative chaired by the National Security Council and National Economic Council. The effort should be led by the Department of State but pull in representatives from multiple agencies and even private sector firms to identify priority corridors, align financing, establish standards, and anticipate likely Chinese countermoves. The team will also need technical experts from the Department of the Treasury, the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, the Securities and Exchange Commission, the Department of Commerce (i.e., the National Institute of Standards and Technology), and industry to create trusted token-generation metrics. These metrics should price verified AI work rather than raw output.The Department of State, through EXIM and the DFC, will need to develop full-stack packages that support U.S. firms currently building AI stacks globally. These efforts would expand on existing initiatives such as EXIM’s ExportAI Initiative and integrate key Department of Energy efforts linked to SMRs and modernizing grid infrastructure globally.Most importantly, the president and key members of his cabinet will need to conduct behind-the-scenes negotiations with key firms and states to set conditions for the announcement of the tokendollar as a new settlement contract. Like the emergence of the petrodollar, the most important deliberations will be secret and involve intermediaries from the private sector working alongside diplomats and senior officials.Just as the petrodollar linked U.S. financial power to the energy system of the twentieth century, the tokendollar should serve as the architecture for a new AI era. The U.S. government should support U.S. firms building the AI stack by aligning key government support and creating a financial framework for setting token contracts in dollars and linked stablecoins.Benjamin Jensen is director of the Futures Lab and a senior fellow for the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nArtificial Intelligence,\nDefense and Security,\nEconomic Security,\nGeopolitics and International Security,\nGlobal Markets,\nInternational Development, and\nTechnology\nImage\nBenjamin Jensen\nDirector, Futures Lab and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nCompetition Lab\nDefense and Security\nFutures Lab\nRelated Content\nImage\nTokenpolitik: How the United States Can Compete with China to Build the Global AI Stack\nCommentary\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nYasir Atalan\n— August 3, 2026\nImage\nEconomic Warfare and Military Power\nBrief\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nJacob Clayton\n— May 26, 2026","content_cn":"从石油美元到代币美元：人工智能时代的经济治国术\n图片：Aleksandra Sova/Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n发表于2026年8月25日\n货币定义权力，并由此定义国际秩序。20世纪70年代初美元脱离金本位后，美国建立了一套新的金融架构，应对大宗商品价格通胀、能源流动和全球贸易。石油交易大多以美元计价。沙特的盈余资金通过美国银行和财政部市场进行再循环。美国企业向海湾地区销售商品、服务和技术。安全关系强化了金融关系。美国政府问责局1979年的一份报告将美沙联合委员会描述为一种机制，用于加强政治联系、支持沙特工业化、回流石油美元，并扩大美国技术的流动。由此形成的石油美元锁定了美元的主导地位，并通过美元构建了更广泛的安全架构。如今，美国应领导一个自由国家联盟，建设全球技术栈，并让美元成为代币生成定价的默认单位，即人工智能时代的默认商品。这种定价应与美国期货市场相连接，并与锚定型加密货币挂钩，以最大限度提高流动性。此外，这一技术栈应展示旨在加速全球南方发展的一批美国技术，包括小型模块化反应堆（SMR）以及支持在海外建设更多数据中心的新一代基础设施，从而缓解美国国内压力。这种经济治国术与基础设施发展的结合，代表了一种全新的外交与发展方式。经济治国术与基础设施发展的结合，建立在此前将美元与算力挂钩的呼吁之上，代表了一种全新的外交与发展方式。\n代币政治：让人工智能技术栈建设与美国外交保持一致\n世界正进入一个由代币政治以及哪一方拥有最深厚的人工智能技术栈所定义的大国竞争新时代。英伟达的黄仁勋将人工智能技术栈描述为一块“五层蛋糕”：能源、芯片、基础设施、模型和应用，每个应用都会向下牵动其下方的每一层，直至发电厂。这一技术栈驱动着“人工智能工厂”，将能源转化为代币，使算法智能成为一种可衡量的工业产出。\n代币是人工智能模型处理和生成的数据基本单位。它们代表字符、数字、像素，甚至是经过分解、用于与模式匹配并进行推断以预测对提示词最佳回复的小段音频文件。因此，代币决定了人工智能项目的上下文限制和成本估算。经济越依赖人工智能应用，代币使用量和定价就越会成为增长与生产率的核心驱动力。\n从战略角度看，能够以每瓦、每美元生产更多可信代币的一方，将在大国竞争中占据优势。这使人工智能外交日益与基础设施发展保持一致。迄今为止，美国凭借私营部门的大规模资本投资和领先的前沿开发商，在这方面一直占据优势，但中国正在缩小差距。中国通过“一带一路”和“数字丝绸之路”等倡议，在全球南方的能源和基础设施投资方面处于领先地位，这代表了一个新的外交政策挑战。\n这场竞赛正走向\n将人工智能带到全球南方，将成为下一代面临的决定性外交与发展挑战之一，因为许多国家仍然缺乏大规模受益于人工智能所需的电力系统、数据中心、云基础设施和融资。国际能源署预计，到2030年，全球数据中心的用电量可能增加一倍以上，人工智能是其中的核心驱动力。无法获得负担得起且可靠电力的国家，将不得不从他国租用智能。那些依靠中国的融资、设备和技术标准建设人工智能工厂的国家，将进入一个不同的技术秩序。\n\n这一现实带来了新的需求：与全球各地的盟友和战略伙伴，包括全球南方国家，共同建设人工智能工厂走廊。在这方面，中国已经试图在一代人的时间里从美国、欧洲和其他自由经济体的侧翼实现超越。这些走廊需要考虑完整的人工智能技术栈，尤其是电力。美国政府应通过优先支持关键走廊项目，并协调不同政府部门，为先进小型模块化反应堆、電网升级、安全光纤网络、劳动力培训以及创建超大规模集群所需的其他基础设施升级提供支持，从而帮助私营部门企业。\n\n政策已经朝这一方向发展，尽管目前仍是零散推进。白宫关于促进美国人工智能技术栈出口的行政命令，呼吁开展协调一致的全国性努力，出口完整技术栈的美国人工智能方案。商务部的“美国人工智能出口计划”要求由行业牵头的联合体提供一体化方案，其中包括人工智能优化硬件、数据管道、模型、网络安全和应用程序。\n\n促进人工智能技术栈出口是一项跨部门工作。国务院可以协调盟友和伙伴政府，包括与Pax Silica相关的现有工作，以确定关键走廊。发展金融公司（DFC）和美国进出口银行（EXIM）等机构可以投资支持性基础设施，并激励美国私营部门企业在全球建设人工智能技术栈。与此同时，能源部可以领导小型模块化反应堆和电网外交；财政部、商务部，甚至国土安全部（通过网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA））也可以发挥作用。因此，人工智能技术栈将形成一个能够创造电力与影响力的基础设施网络，并由此形成一种新的国际秩序。\n\n代币美元\n\n为确保其在新国际秩序中的中心地位，美国应倡导以美元清算代币。类似于石油美元，代币美元将在下一代支撑对美元的需求、降低借贷成本并扩大赤字；鉴于政府债务规模和借贷成本上升，这些经济利益的重要性只会日益增加。\n\n此外，在期货市场交易的代币美元合约还将使企业能够对冲未来的人工智能成本。人工智能基础设施债券可以根据签约的代币容量为数据中心融资。保险市场可以为停机风险、网络风险和性能风险定价。大学和国家实验室可以获得代币美元研究额度。美国政府甚至可以倡导一种新形式的安全合作，通过代币美元合同工具，为盟友提供对人工智能推理能力的激增式访问。\n\n这一新的\n金融秩序可以与稳定币挂钩。美国应使用由美元支持的稳定币来结算代币美元合约。《GENIUS法案》建立了联邦稳定币框架，并要求以美元或短期美国国债等流动资产提供100%的储备支持，从而为将数字融资与代币美元合约连接起来提供了框架。\n\n经济治国术的新篇章\n\n代币美元可以成为人工智能外交与发展新时代的关键架构，将金融、基础设施、技术标准以及更深入的公私部门协调联系起来。要实现这一目标，未来两年需要共同努力。\n\n特朗普政府应创建由国家安全委员会和国家经济委员会主持的“国家人工智能堆栈与代币美元倡议”。这项工作应由国务院牵头，但应吸纳多个机构乃至私营企业的代表，以确定优先走廊、协调融资、制定标准，并预判中国可能采取的反制措施。该团队还需要财政部、商品期货交易委员会、证券交易委员会、商务部（即国家标准与技术研究院）以及业界的技术专家，以创建可信的代币生成指标。这些指标应对经验证的人工智能工作而非原始输出进行定价。\n\n国务院通过进出口银行（EXIM）和国际开发金融公司（DFC），需要制定全栈方案，支持当前正在全球建设人工智能堆栈的美国企业。这些努力将扩展现有举措，例如进出口银行的ExportAI倡议，并整合能源部与小型模块化反应堆（SMR）及全球电网基础设施现代化相关的关键工作。\n\n最重要的是，总统及其内阁核心成员需要与关键企业和国家进行幕后谈判，为宣布代币美元作为一种新的结算合约设定条件。正如石油美元的出现一样，最重要的磋商将是秘密进行的，并涉及私营部门中介与外交官和高级官员协同工作。\n\n正如石油美元将美国的金融实力与20世纪的能源体系联系起来一样，代币美元应成为新时代人工智能的架构。美国政府应通过协调关键政府支持，并创建以美元及与美元挂钩的稳定币设定代币合约的金融框架，来支持建设人工智能堆栈的美国企业。\n\nBenjamin Jensen是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心未来实验室主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员。\n\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且不受专有利益影响。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2026年战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n人工智能，\n国防与安全，\n经济安全，\n地缘政治与国际安全，\n全球市场，\n国际发展，以及\n技术\n\n图片\nBenjamin Jensen\n未来实验室主任兼高级研究员，\n国防与安全部\n项目与计划\n竞争实验室\n国防与安全\n未来实验室\n相关内容\n图片\nTokenpolitik：美国如何与中国竞争以构建全球人工智能技术栈\n评论\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n和\nYasir Atalan\n— 2026年8月3日\n图片\n经济战与军事力量\n简报\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n和\nJacob Clayton\n— 2026年5月26日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-08-25T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-341","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"关于打击跨国网络犯罪的总统备忘录：对产业界和政府的影响","title_en":"The Presidential Memo on Combating Transnational Cybercrime: Implications for Industry and Government","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心评论文章分析美国政府新发布的总统备忘录，该备忘录授权私营企业直接打击网络犯罪组织。作者指出，备忘录在授权依据、企业法律保护和目标范围等关键细节上仍不明确，并暴露了政府与私营部门在风险偏好上的分歧。文章认为，国家行为体与犯罪团伙界限日益模糊，企业可能不愿针对贩毒集团等目标，并建议建立目标白名单、提供法律保护、制定应急计划并明确国会监督管辖。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary analyzes a new presidential memorandum authorizing private companies to directly combat cyber-enabled criminal organizations. The authors note that key details remain unclear, including authorities, legal protections for companies, and target sets, exposing diverging risk appetites between the administration and the private sector. They argue the line between state and criminal actors is increasingly blurred, and that companies may be reluctant to target cartels. Recommendations include creating a whitelist of potential targets, establishing legal protections, developing contingency plans, and clarifying congressional oversight jurisdiction.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:57:33.093527+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/presidential-memo-combating-transnational-cybercrime-implications-industry-and-government","content":"The Presidential Memo on Combating Transnational Cybercrime: Implications for Industry and Government\nPhoto: bephoto/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nNikita Shah\nand\nEmily Harding\nPublished August 20, 2026\nThe Trump administration expressed an early desire for the United States to adopt a more aggressive posture against adversaries in cyberspace and to unleash the private sector as part of this fight. Last week, the administration published a presidential memorandum that makes this intent a policy by authorizing private companies to directly combat cyber-enabled criminal organizations in cyberspace. In clear alignment with the 2026 U.S. National Cyber Strategy, the memorandum reflects a hardened posture by the United States in cyberspace, amounting to a renewed effort to deter, degrade, and disrupt its adversaries. However, it lacks key details that will make or break the effort, including important questions around authorities, legal protections for companies, and target sets, exposing diverging risk appetites between the administration and private sector.The Scope of the Cybercrime ProblemBy some estimates, the United States loses over $20 billion annually to cybercrime. Designated a national security threat in the 2023 U.S. National Cyber Strategy, cybercrime undermines the U.S. economy and society and public trust in institutions. Some of the victims of these crimes are big businesses with the capability to return fire, and many are champing at the bit to do so. These companies own and operate vast infrastructure, and in some cases, effectively possess state-grade intelligence capabilities. They argue that they should not be kept on the sidelines when they could help.As the problem has grown, so has the appetite for widening the slate of tools available to retaliate. The most recent cyber policy discussions have fiercely debated the right vehicle for harnessing private sector capabilities, up to and including cyber “letters of marque”—a concept going back to the 1800s whereby Congress could authorize private shipowners to combat vessels and capture their goods.In some ways, this initiative is not particularly new. Private actors have long signed contracts with the federal government for offensive cyber operations, whether providing capabilities, access, or effects (meaning physical or cognitive outcomes from cyber operations) within their own infrastructure. Some of the bigger companies have already taken independent action to combat criminal actors in cyberspace: Google recently set up a threat disruption unit to “actively shape the outcome of adversary behaviors,” while Microsoft has a Digital Crimes Unit that legally disrupts and dismantles adversary infrastructure. But these efforts have been legally limited. The new memorandum is a qualified, initial step toward unleashing these industry partners to do far more.The Cybercrime Threat Landscape and Diverging Private Sector Risk AppetitesSome of the key targets outlined in the memo reflect where private sector offensive cyber operations could provide much-needed speed and scale of effect. This includes targeting traditional cybercrime groups, such as ransomware groups, that are usually associated with serious and organized crime; less sophisticated cyber criminals that conduct high-volume cybercrime and cyber-enabled fraud; and cyber scam compounds in East Asia that have proliferated in recent years. This is where the private sector has been pushing to take more action—specifically, against low-risk actors—and it is well-established to do so for defensive purposes, such as dismantling cybercriminal infrastructure, taking down malicious websites, monitoring for fraudulent accounts and other abuses of company services, and seizing cryptocurrencies. But this is where their authorities would stop—the memo expressly forbids going after state-associated groups.However, the line between state and criminal actors has become increasingly blurred over the last five to 10 years in cyberspace, with different degrees of association. Some criminal groups are directly state-funded, others state-directed, others coerced, and so on. The Russian cyber ecosystem in particular has extensive crossover between criminal groups and state-sponsored activity, but North Korean state actors also conduct cybercrime to evade sanctions, and moonlighters in the Chinese cyber ecosystem often conduct cybercrime on the side for personal financial gain. Even the cyber scam compounds are thought to have explicit ties to, or at least tacit approval from, the Cambodian and Thai ruling elite. Parsing the distinction could take months of painstaking intelligence work to clarify, risking missing an operational window between when companies discover malicious activity and when the U.S. government gives its approval to act.There is also a real risk of divergence between the Trump administration and private sector regarding risk appetite. In policy discussions, companies have persistently raised their concerns at legal liability risk—primarily the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act, which criminalizes unauthorized access to computer systems—particularly where an operation may result in an unintended consequence or harm. Moreover, these same companies have also raised serious concerns about the risk of retribution against their staff, particularly from cartels. This is where the program’s scope risks being a problem.Critically, looking at who this administration includes within the term “transnational criminal organizations” effectively asks the private sector to deploy its capabilities against “narco-terrorists,” including Mexican cartels that have been known to use spyware, malware, and cryptocurrencies, among other technologies and services, to enable their activities. However, “narco-terrorism” is already considered by many in the foreign policy and national security communities as an overexpansion of national security authority and capabilities, having resulted in legal challenges and deaths. These concerns will only be compounded by involving private companies, who might consider targeting cartels with cyber operations to be beyond their acceptable risk thresholds. Some companies may sign up for the program for patriotic reasons, then reconsider whether they believe narco-terrorists fall within the bounds of national security threats.Key Challenges and Implications for the U.S. GovernmentUnder which authorities these industry actors would operate is still up for debate. Traditionally, offensive cyber operations are either authorized under Title 10 (military, and specifically Cyber Command) or Title 50 (intelligence community operations). This ensures that U.S. offensive cyber operations do not adversely impact the U.S. government’s statutory functions and goals, and that they adhere to the legal requirements in U.S. code. This program reports into the Department of Justice (DOJ) and Department of Homeland Security (DHS). While the FBI (under the DOJ) and the DHS’s Office of Intelligence and Analysis have Title 50 authorities, neither has Title 10. Plus, some of these activities could be construed more as law enforcement actions than military activities. Getting legal authorities right is critical to the program’s success, as it is to reassuring companies of minimized legal risk.Related to authorities, some mechanism for deconfliction will need to be in place before these operations start. This will be vital to prevent companies from inadvertently disrupting U.S. intelligence, military, or allied cyber operations against the same target sets, noting that the program includes both surveillance operations and cyber effects operations. This will need to take place in a way that does not break classification rules, and if done correctly, could bring about more effective visibility for the U.S. government into how threat actors are exploiting cyberspace.The memo also offers an opportunity to transform the United States’ overly rigid offensive cyber supply chains, which comprise the various private firms that provide capabilities or technical exploits to the U.S. government. The current system has long been considered stagnant in locking out boutique firms or start-ups—costing the United States important agility and strategic advantage relative to China in particular, which has successfully built up its own ecosystem of private suppliers. Containing language that explicitly enables participation by “smaller, more agile companies” compared to the usual defense primes, the memo sends an important signal to the market and may help stimulate much-needed competition, especially where the program can reduce barriers to entry for small start-ups.RecommendationsIt is a pivotal moment for security in cyberspace. State actors are ramping up activity, as we have seen with the likely Iranian attacks on U.S. water facilities, while high-profile cybercriminal incidents are impacting more sectors across the U.S. economy and society, such as schools. U.S. government actors have their hands full and should make use of industry’s talent and hunger to help. Addressing these recommendations will help make this program a success:U.S. government interlocutors should remove the ambiguity around which cyber actors are in and out of bounds by creating a whitelist of potential targets and sharing the intelligence with participating companies.Congress should establish an initial blanket legal protection for companies that sign up for this program, then reevaluate what legal protections are healthy and necessary after one year.The DOJ and FBI should have contingency plans in place for cases in which companies find themselves targeted by organized crime or cartels, including measures to protect the physical and cyber safety of employees.The White House should ensure Congress is on board with the program and work with congressional leadership to establish which committees have jurisdiction over these activities. Is it the Homeland Security Committees, via DHS, or the Justice Committees via DOJ, or the intelligence committees because of the potential overlap with intelligence operations? If the answer is “all of the above,” how will members of Congress prevent topics falling through the jurisdictional seams?Similarly, the White House, preferably the Office of the National Cyber Director, should set up metrics for evaluating whether the executive branch governance of this program is adequate or logical, and, at the end of year one, establish whether the DHS and DOJ are the right points of oversight or whether shifting oversight to the director of national intelligence would make more sense.The DOJ and DHS should ensure that smaller actors have a chance to participate, as intended, by lowering the bar for smaller firms to engage with the government and helping them navigate these new regulations and contracts.Finally, the White House should use this program to enhance U.S. deterrence in cyberspace by ensuring that public-facing officials talk about the intent to amplify U.S. capabilities to hunt and disrupt cyber actors.ConclusionCybercrime and cyber-enabled fraud pose long-standing threats to U.S. national security and the economy. The intention for the private sector to conduct offensive cyber operations against such actors will be welcomed by many in the policymaker and operational spheres and builds upon what some companies have already been doing. The program will not be without obstacles, though, not least in the form of operational and legal hurdles and the challenge of aligning between private sector and administration risk appetites. The sensible way forward would be through a pilot program, such as seizing criminal cryptocurrency wallets, which cyber specialists have already suggested.Unleashing the prowess and unique capabilities of the U.S. private sector could yield much longer-term benefits for U.S. offensive cyber supply chains and capability pipelines—a much-needed transformation that would ultimately strengthen the United States’ longer-term strategic advantage in cyberspace relative to peer competitors such as China. This approach may even go so far as to help reestablish U.S. deterrence in cyberspace: Enhancing the scale, speed, and sophistication of U.S. operations against cybercriminal actors will send important signals to state adversaries, too.Nikita Shah is s senior fellow with the Intelligence, National Security, and Technology Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Emily Harding is director of the Intelligence, National Security, and Technology Program and vice president of the Defense and Security Department at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nCybersecurity\nNikita Shah\nSenior Fellow, Intelligence, National Security, and Technology Program\nImage\nEmily Harding\nVice President, Defense and Security Department; Director, Intelligence, National Security, and Technology Program\nPrograms & Projects\nIntelligence, National Security, and Technology Program\nDefense and Security\nRelated Content\nImage\nA Playbook for Winning the Cyber War\nCould the United States win a cyber war? This series explores the future of cyber warfare, examining how the United States and its adversaries—Russia, China, and Iran—wage war in the cyber domain.","content_cn":"关于打击跨国网络犯罪的总统备忘录：对行业和政府的影响\n图片：bephoto/Adobe Stock\n评论文章\n作者\n尼基塔·沙阿\n和\n艾米丽·哈丁\n发表于2026年8月20日\n特朗普政府早期就表达了希望美国在网络空间对对手采取更具进攻性的姿态，并释放私营部门力量参与这场斗争的意愿。上周，政府发布了一份总统备忘录，通过授权私营公司在网络空间直接打击网络犯罪组织，将这一意图确立为政策。该备忘录与《2026年美国国家网络战略》明确一致，反映出美国在网络空间采取了更强硬的姿态，意味着重新努力威慑、削弱和扰乱其对手。然而，备忘录缺少一些将决定这项努力成败的关键细节，包括有关授权、企业法律保护和目标范围的重要问题，从而暴露出政府与私营部门在风险承受意愿上的分歧。\n网络犯罪问题的范围\n据一些估计，美国每年因网络犯罪损失超过200亿美元。网络犯罪在《2023年美国国家网络战略》中被认定为国家安全威胁，破坏美国经济和社会，并侵蚀公众对机构的信任。这些犯罪的受害者中，有些是具备还击能力的大型企业，其中许多企业都迫不及待地想这样做。这些公司拥有并运营着庞大的基础设施，在某些情况下实际上具备国家级情报能力。他们认为，既然自己能够提供帮助，就不应被置于场外。\n随着问题日益严重，人们也越来越渴望扩大可用于报复的工具范围。最近的网络政策讨论激烈争论着利用私营部门能力的适当方式，甚至包括网络“私掠许可证”——这一概念可追溯至19世纪，当时国会可以授权私人船主打击船只并夺取其货物。\n从某些方面看，这项举措并不是特别新鲜。长期以来，私人行为者一直与联邦政府签订合同，开展进攻性网络行动，无论是在自身基础设施内提供能力、访问权限还是行动效果（即网络行动产生的物理或认知结果）。一些大型公司已经采取独立行动打击网络空间中的犯罪行为者：谷歌最近成立了一个威胁扰乱部门，以“积极塑造对手行为的结果”；而微软拥有一个数字犯罪部门，在法律框架内扰乱并拆解对手基础设施。但这些努力在法律上受到限制。新备忘录是朝着释放这些行业合作伙伴、让其开展更多行动迈出的有条件的初步一步。\n网络犯罪威胁态势与私营部门风险承受意愿的分歧\n备忘录列出的一些关键目标，反映出私营部门的进攻性网络行动可以在哪些方面提供急需的速度和规模效应。这包括针对传统网络犯罪团伙，例如通常与严重有组织犯罪相关的勒索软件团伙；针对实施大规模网络犯罪和网络赋能欺诈、技术不那么复杂的网络犯罪分子；以及针对近年来在东亚大量涌现的网络诈骗园区。这正是私营部门一直推动采取更多行动的领域——具体来说，\n针对低风险行为者——出于防御目的采取此类行动已有充分先例，例如拆除网络犯罪基础设施、关闭恶意网站、监控欺诈账户及其他滥用公司服务的行为，以及查扣加密货币。但他们的权限也就到此为止——备忘录明确禁止针对与国家有关联的团体。 然而，在过去5至10年间，网络空间中的国家行为者与犯罪行为者之间的界线日益模糊，关联程度各不相同。一些犯罪团体由国家直接资助，另一些受国家指挥，还有一些受到胁迫，情况不一。俄罗斯网络生态系统尤其存在犯罪团体与国家支持活动之间的大量交叉，但朝鲜国家行为者也会实施网络犯罪以规避制裁，而中国网络生态系统中的“兼职者”也常为个人经济利益私下从事网络犯罪。甚至有人认为，网络诈骗园区与柬埔寨和泰国的统治精英存在明确联系，或至少得到他们的默许。厘清这种区别可能需要数月艰苦的情报工作，从而有可能错过这样一个行动窗口：公司发现恶意活动与美国政府批准采取行动之间的时间窗口。 私营部门与特朗普政府在风险偏好方面也确实存在分歧的风险。在政策讨论中，公司一直提出对法律责任风险的担忧——主要涉及《计算机欺诈与滥用法》，该法将未经授权访问计算机系统定为犯罪——尤其是在某项行动可能导致意外后果或伤害的情况下。此外，这些公司还对员工遭到报复的风险表示严重担忧，尤其是来自贩毒集团的报复。这正是该项目范围可能带来问题的地方。 关键在于，考察本届政府将哪些对象纳入“跨国犯罪组织”这一术语，实际上就是要求私营部门动用其能力对付“毒品恐怖分子”，其中包括已知会使用间谍软件、恶意软件和加密货币等技术与服务来开展活动的墨西哥贩毒集团。然而，在外交政策和国家安全界，许多人已经认为“毒品恐怖主义”是对国家安全权限和能力的过度扩张，并已导致法律挑战和人员死亡。让私营公司参与其中，只会加剧这些担忧；这些公司可能认为，以网络行动为手段针对贩毒集团超出了其可接受的风险阈值。一些公司可能出于爱国原因加入该项目，随后重新考虑自己是否认为毒品恐怖分子属于国家安全威胁的范围。 美国政府面临的关键挑战与影响 这些行业参与者将依据哪些权限开展行动，仍在争论之中。传统上，进攻性网络行动要么依据第10篇（军事部门，具体而言是网络司令部）授权，要么依据第50篇（情报界行动）授权。这确保美国的进攻性网络行动不会对美国政府的法定职能和目标产生不利影响，并且遵守《美国法典》中的法律要求。该项目由司法部（DOJ）和国土安全部（DHS）负责。虽然联邦调查局（隶属司法部）和\nDHS 的情报与分析办公室拥有第 50 条授权，而两者都没有第 10 条授权。此外，其中一些活动可能更多地被解释为执法行动，而非军事活动。正确确定法律授权对于该项目的成功至关重要，这对于让企业相信其法律风险已降至最低也同样重要。\n\n与授权相关的是，在这些行动开始前，需要建立某种冲突协调机制。这对于防止企业无意中干扰美国情报、军事或盟国针对同一目标集开展的网络行动至关重要，需要注意的是，该项目既包括监视行动，也包括网络影响行动。这需要以不违反保密规则的方式进行；如果处理得当，还可能让美国政府更有效地了解威胁行为者如何利用网络空间。\n\n该备忘录还提供了一个机会，可以改造美国过于僵化的进攻性网络供应链。这些供应链由向美国政府提供能力或技术漏洞利用工具的各类私营企业组成。长期以来，现行体系一直被认为因排斥精品企业或初创企业而停滞不前，使美国相对于中国尤其如此地失去了重要的灵活性和战略优势；中国已成功建立起自己的私营供应商生态系统。备忘录中的措辞明确允许“规模更小、更灵活的企业”参与，而不是仅限于通常的国防主承包商，这向市场发出了重要信号，并可能帮助激发急需的竞争，尤其是在该项目能够降低小型初创企业进入壁垒的领域。\n\n建议\n\n这是网络空间安全的关键时刻。正如伊朗很可能袭击美国供水设施所显示的那样，国家行为者正在加大活动力度；与此同时，备受关注的网络犯罪事件正影响美国经济和社会中越来越多的部门，例如学校。美国政府各部门已事务繁重，应当利用业界的人才和积极性来提供帮助。落实以下建议将有助于使该项目取得成功：\n\n美国政府的对接人员应通过建立潜在目标白名单并与参与企业共享情报，消除哪些网络行为者属于允许范围、哪些不属于允许范围的歧义。\n\n国会应先为签署加入该项目的企业确立一项初步的全面法律保护措施，然后在一年后重新评估哪些法律保护措施是稳妥且必要的。\n\n司法部和联邦调查局应为企业遭到有组织犯罪团伙或贩毒集团攻击的情况制定应急预案，包括保护员工人身和网络安全的措施。\n\n白宫应确保国会支持该项目，并与国会领导层合作，确定哪些委员会对这些活动拥有管辖权。是由国土安全部负责的国土安全委员会，还是由司法部负责的司法委员会，抑或是因为可能与情报行动重叠而由情报委员会负责？如果答案是“以上全部”，国会议员将如何防止相关议题从管辖权的缝隙中遗漏？\n\n同样，白宫最好由国家网络总监办公室牵头，应建立评估行政部门对该项目的治理是否充分或合理的指标，并在一年结束时\n第一，确定国土安全部（DHS）和司法部（DOJ）是否是合适的监督部门，或者将监督转交给国家情报总监是否更为合理。司法部和国土安全部应通过降低小型企业参与政府事务的门槛，并帮助它们应对这些新法规和合同，确保较小的参与者能够按预期获得参与机会。最后，白宫应利用这一项目增强美国在网络空间的威慑力，确保面向公众的官员谈及扩大美国追捕和扰乱网络行为者的能力。\n\n结论\n\n网络犯罪和网络赋能的欺诈长期以来一直对美国国家安全和经济构成威胁。让私营部门对这些行为者开展进攻性网络行动的意图，将受到政策制定和行动领域许多人士的欢迎，并建立在一些公司已经开展的工作之上。不过，该项目不会毫无障碍，尤其是运营和法律方面的障碍，以及协调私营部门与政府风险承受意愿方面的挑战。明智的前进方式应是开展试点项目，例如没收犯罪分子的加密货币钱包——网络专家已经提出了这一建议。\n\n释放美国私营部门的强大力量和独特能力，可能为美国进攻性网络供应链和能力管线带来更长期的收益——这是一项急需的转型，最终将相对于中国等同级竞争对手，强化美国在网络空间的长期战略优势。这种方法甚至可能有助于重新建立美国在网络空间的威慑力：扩大美国针对网络犯罪行为者的行动规模、速度和复杂程度，也将向国家级对手发出重要信号。\n\n尼基塔·沙阿是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）情报、国家安全与技术项目的高级研究员。艾米丽·哈丁是情报、国家安全与技术项目主任，也是CSIS防务与安全部副总裁。\n\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私营、免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n\n网络安全\n\n尼基塔·沙阿\n\n情报、国家安全与技术项目高级研究员\n\n图片\n\n艾米丽·哈丁\n\n防务与安全部副总裁；情报、国家安全与技术项目主任\n\n项目与计划\n\n情报、国家安全与技术项目\n\n防务与安全\n\n相关内容\n\n图片\n\n赢得网络战争的行动手册\n\n美国能赢得网络战争吗？本系列探讨网络战的未来，考察美国及其对手——俄罗斯、中国和伊朗——如何在网络领域发动战争。","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-08-20T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3592","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"新闻发布会：预览澳美部长级磋商","title_en":"Press Briefing: Previewing the Australia-United States Ministerial Consultations","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心于2024年8月1日举办新闻发布会，预览第34届澳美部长级磋商（AUSMIN）。CSIS专家Charles Edel、Kathryn Paik、Cynthia Cook及澳大利亚国立大学Rory Medcalf参与讨论。会议预计聚焦防务合作、部队态势、后勤预置、关键矿产、海底电缆、太空及太平洋岛屿地区合作，并涉及AUKUS进展与地区安全环境。","summary_en":"CSIS hosted a press briefing on August 1, 2024, previewing the 34th Australia-United States Ministerial Consultations (AUSMIN). CSIS experts Charles Edel, Kathryn Paik, Cynthia Cook, and ANU's Rory Medcalf discussed expected agenda items including defense cooperation, force posture, logistics pre-positioning, critical minerals, undersea cables, space, Pacific Islands engagement, and AUKUS progress amid a contested strategic environment.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:31:05.985837+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/press-briefing-previewing-australia-united-states-ministerial-consultations","content":"Press Briefing: Previewing the Australia-United States Ministerial Consultations\nPhoto: DANIEL SLIM/AFP via Getty Images\nTranscript\n— August 1, 2024\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Transcript\n208kb\nImage\nPhoto: DANIEL SLIM/AFP via Getty Images\nPress Briefing: Previewing the Australia-United States Ministerial Consultations\nAugust 1, 2024 • 4:00 – 5:00 pm EDT\nThis transcript is from a CSIS press briefing hosted on August 1, 2024.Samuel Cestari: Thank you, Don. Hello, everybody, and welcome to the CSIS press briefing, “Previewing the Australia-United States Ministerial Consultations,” also known as AUSMIN. We’re pleased to have four of our experts with us today to share their perspectives and expertise as it relates to AUSMIN, its context, and the issues likely to be addressed.Just a couple of housekeeping notes before we get started. Each of our speakers will offer several minutes of introductory remarks, after which we’ll turn to your questions. We’ll also be distributing a transcript of today’s call to all participants tomorrow morning, eastern time. The transcript will also be made available on CSIS.org.So, with that, why don’t we go ahead and get started? I’ll turn first to Charles Edel, CSIS senior advisor and Australia chair. Charlie, over to you.Charles Edel: Great. Thanks, everyone, for joining us today. And thank you, Sam, for helping organize this. In addition to welcoming my CSIS colleagues, Kathryn and Cynthia, I’m especially pleased that Rory Medcalf is joining us today very early in Australia. Rory is, of course, one of the foremost analysts on the Indo-Pacific region. He’s president of the National Security College at the Australian National University. And much to our delight, is now an adjunct non-resident fellow with the Australia chair at CSIS.Next week, on August 6th, we’ll see the U.S. secretaries of state and defense hosting their Australian counterparts, the Australian ministers for foreign affairs and defense, for the 34th iteration of AUSMIN, the annual Australia-U.S. Ministerial Consultation. When Antony Blinken, Lloyd Austin, Penny Wong, and Richard Marles meet in Annapolis, Maryland, they will take stock of how well Australia and the U.S. have set themselves – have set themselves and each other up to compete effectively in a more challenging and increasingly contested strategic environment.As we’re heading towards election years in both countries, both delegations will be focused on what the two governments have accomplished so far. There will be some announcements of new initiatives, but I’d expect the focus to be on the steady and ongoing progress made thus far. The bottom line here is that AUSMIN will continue to showcase a deepening and broadening relationship between the United States and Australia that spans the globe and has increasing strategic relevance and weight in the Indo-Pacific region. The announcements and statements will reflect that, with deliverables spanning the defense, diplomatic, economic, energy, and development portfolios.These government leaders have now met multiple times. They know each other well and hold each other in high regard. So they are likely to spend their time not simply running through the fact sheet of deliverables, but also genuinely exchanging views on regional and global developments and discussing strategies for how to respond to those developments. Because the alliance has tasked itself with a growing list of initiatives, this year’s AUSMIN is likely to highlight the steady progress that’s been made over the past several years. This is important. Ambition is not the main challenge for the U.S. Australia relationship. Steady progress towards a number of strategic goals is the name of the game.I’ll make a couple of remarks on the general state of the U.S.-Australia relationship and the context in which this year’s AUSMIN is taking place, lay out what to expect this year, and conclude with several questions that the two sides are seeking answers to and that are likely to sharpen moving forward.So AUSMIN itself will be held on August 6th. And Foreign Minister Wong and the Australian deputy prime minister will have their own bilateral meetings ahead of time with their counterparts in Washington. Then they’ll move the show to Annapolis, home, of course, of the U.S. Naval Academy, for the 2+2.The deputy prime minister will stay on the next day and will be joining us here at CSIS for a conversation about the future of the U.S.-Australian alliance. That event will be broadcast live, and I encourage all of you on this call now to submit any questions you might have for Defense Minister Marles.In foreign policy, they say the travel itineraries reflect agendas and priorities. You can see the truth of that in the travel schedules of the four principals. Lloyd Austin and Tony Blinken will have just returned from trips to the Indo-Pacific region, where they delivered some very big news in the upgrading of the U.S.-Japan alliance in Tokyo reflected in modernizing the command structure of the U.S. Forces in Japan, and the trip to Manila where the U.S. pledged $500 million in new military funding to help modernize the Philippines military. Blinken will continue his travel with stops in Singapore and Mongolia. And of course, he met with Penny Wong in Tokyo as part of the Quad ministerial meeting and in Vientiane, Laos, for the ASEAN Regional Forum meetings.The Australian contingent will also be coming to AUSMIN fresh off engagements across the Pacific. Richard Marles was just in Washington for NATO and then in London, and the foreign minister will come into Washington after travels to Tokyo, Seoul, and Vientiane. And she’ll be heading to Tonga shortly thereafter for the Pacific Island(s) Forum leaders meeting.The point here is that all four will have had multiple engagements with each other and with their counterparts in Northeast and Southeast Asia. And at a moment when things seem to be in flux – elections and changes of government across the region, increasing cooperation between authoritarian actors, maturing relationships among democratic states across the region, and increasing pressure from China on display most clearly at Second Thomas Shoal in the Philippines but also around and in Taiwan – this will heighten the importance of their working together to provide more ballast to a region that continues to be destabilized by Beijing’s assertive activity.Lloyd Austin consistently refers to the Australian-American relationship as, quote, “the unbreakable alliance.” It might be that, but it’s also increasingly a multifaceted alliance with an expansive agenda. The bilateral relationship is in remarkably strong shape and is building on Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese’s state visit to Washington last October. There, the leaders committed to expanding fields of cooperation broadening beyond the defense and economic areas, to include climate and clean energy. You can see that in the work on AUKUS; on climate change, clean energy, and critical minerals; and on the synchronization of diplomatic efforts with other capitals, be they in Tokyo, Manila, or Port Moresby.A couple of things to look for in AUSMIN. First, given the deterioration of the security environment globally in Europe, the Middle East, and in the Indo-Pacific, both Australia and the United States will be eager to stress their concern and their cooperation. And I expect there will be much made of the challenges to regional and global stability. Again, following on the commitments made during the prime minister’s state visit to Washington, I would expect that many discussions at AUSMIN will be framed around implementing previous initiatives, including critical minerals, undersea cables, space, and cooperation in Southeast Asia and the Pacific Island region. Beyond that, I expect that most of the substantive announcements will be on defense initiatives, including on progress around force posture, testing of logistics including the pre-positioning of fuel and munitions, attention to capability development – where I’m sure Cynthia will expand on – and deepening cooperation with third parties. There will probably be a nod towards AUKUS, but I do not think that will get pride of place. For more on AUKUS, look towards engagement and possible announcements around its third anniversary in September.Now, before handing it off to my colleagues, I’d like to emphasize how much work is now being undertaken across an increasingly broad array of areas. The alliance remans in remarkably strong shape and enjoys bipartisan support in both countries. When the four principals meet in Annapolis, they’ll have much to congratulate each other on. But while the affection is real and the history runs deep, the increasing cooperation we’ll see is in many respects not simply a reflection of shared values, but a reaction to a growing number of challenges and a shared sense that we’re playing catchup across a number of fronts.In the year ahead, which will come with its fair share of political churn in both countries, there will be critical discussions on whether we have been able to grow our defense industrial bases quickly enough, whether we’ve figured out how to produce sufficient material for the energy transition and to keep our supply chain secure, and whether we’ve demonstrated that we can deliver what the region is demanding of us both. On all of these, I believe there’s a clear direction of travel for the Australian-American relationship. The question increasingly is the speed of delivery.With that, let me turn, in fact, to Sam and to my very talented colleagues.Mr. Cestari: Thank you, Charlie.Next we have Kathryn Paik, senior fellow with the CSIS Australia Chair. Kathryn, over to you.Kathryn Paik: Thanks Sam. And thanks, Charlie, for that outline at the beginning.You know, Charlie outlined some of the broad strokes of the relationship, and then the framing around the 2024 AUSMIN meeting. I’ll take a few minutes just to drill down into the Pacific islands region, which is an area of keen focus for both Australia and the United States, and a topic that I expect will be discussed in detail next week.You know, I agree with Charlie that this year’s meeting is likely to be less focused on just a run sheet of deliverables and more likely to be talking frankly and openly with each other on regional and global developments. It will be very much about taking stock of progress thus far and having an honest exchange about where there remains work to be done. And on both these ledgers, progress made and work to be done, there’s a lot to consider in the Pacific region.So current status in the Pacific, you know, this has long been a strategic priority for the – for Australia. But U.S. commitment in the region has not always been as consistent. But over the last few years, we’ve seen a real positive trajectory of U.S. engagement in the Pacific, which has also been accompanied by a real shift in the way that the U.S. and Australia are cooperating in the region together.The Solomon Island Security Agreement back in 2022 brought home for both countries that pushing back on China’s campaign to gain a foothold in the Pacific would take a focused, coordinated effort by all likeminded partners in the region. Add to this the catastrophic threat climate change poses Pacific region, and it’s clear that the challenges facing the Pacific are immense and more than any one partner can take on.So this coalescence of two factors – the U.S. desire to ramp up engagement along with Australia’s push to have more U.S. involvement in the Pacific – has resulted in an absolute explosion of collaboration and initiatives across the Pacific islands. In addition, we’ve seen a real alignment in the efforts to bring in other likeminded partners beyond the usual suspects, such as New Zealand and Japan, but also others such as the EU, U.K., South Korea, India, Canada, just to name a few.At AUSMIN last year, we saw U.S. and Australia making not just statements on the commitment to the Pacific islands region and the Pacific Islands Forum, PIF, as a regional institution, but also several specific focus areas, such as meeting critical MDA needs with increased Coast Guard deployments, cooperative action with the Pacific Islands to strengthen maritime law enforcement and rule of law. There was also a strong commitment to work through the informal coordination mechanism of Partners in the Blue Pacific to deliver on initiatives focused on climate action, humanitarian, warehousing, and cyber security. So I’d expect this year’s AUSMIN will be somewhat a check in on progress of these focus areas, as well as identifying new or continuing areas of concern.On the topic of areas of concern, one country that is likely to make the talking points is going to be Solomon Islands. So the Solomon’s election earlier this year resulted in a change of prime minister from Sogavare to Manele, though not a change in party. And the government’s strong relationship with China has continued. Australia’s been really engaging fulsomely with the new prime minister, Prime Minister Manele, who has presented himself as a much more diplomatic and evenhanded statesman and his predecessor. Such when he visited Australia for his first overseas trip, but then followed that immediately with a trip to Beijing.Despite these continued challenges, though, the U.S. has a real opportunity with the Solomons and the new prime minister there to re-engage. And Australia and the U.S. may discuss how the U.S. might strengthen its engagement on the ground there, especially with a new embassy in Honiara now up and running. Additionally, the U.S. and Australia are likely to review and perhaps double down on recent successes, such as the launch of the Pacific Connect, which is a partnership with Google that, with further international funding, could realize connectivity for some of the most far-flung islands in the Pacific. Or following through on newer announcements, such as the recent U.S.-Australia commitment to helping Pacific banking that was made back in early July.Another potential area of conversation is Papua New Guinea. Earlier this year, Australia finalized its policing agreement with the country. And the U.S. is moving forward with its implementation of a DCA that was signed last year. Both the U.S. and Australia have an interest in ensuring that these agreements are aligned and reinforcing in their efforts to bolster PNG law enforcement and defense capabilities.One topic that is unlikely to make the readout but could be raised in private is New Caledonia, the French territory currently embroiled in protests against what many see as efforts by the French government to stifle progress towards independence. Given the pressure by the PIF and the Melanesian countries specifically to allow for another referendum on independence, this issue has put Australia, also a PIF member, in a sensitive position. Australia has yet to come out in support of the calls for a new referendum, in contrast to New Zealand.Lastly, I should note that closely on the heels of AUSMIN we have the annual PIF Leaders Meeting in Tonga on the horizon in late August. Although the U.S. is not a PIF member, we are a dialog partner. And the PIF strives to build out its dialog partner architecture. And showing up sincerely – and showing up and sincerely engaging will be a critical piece to demonstrate a continued commitment to the region. Last year, we sent U.S. ambassador to the U.N. Linda Thomas-Greenfield. And I would expect that a senior representation will be heading out this year as well.So overall, a ton potentially on the table to discuss with the Pacific. And with that, I’ll turn it back to Sam. Thanks.Mr. Cestari: Kathryn, thank you.(Gives queuing instructions.)After our next two speakers, we will turn to your questions and answer them as best as we can.Our next speaker is Cynthia Cook, director of the CSIS Defense Industrial Initiatives Group and senior fellow with our International Security Program. Cynthia, the floor is yours.Cynthia Cook: Thanks, Sam. And thanks, Kathryn and Charlie, for your remarks.I would say that, reflecting on where we are, there is a really strong and positive amount of energy around defense industrial cooperation right now, both broadly in the U.S. and specifically on this bilateral partnership. It’s really a topic that never goes away. Industrial cooperation connects to capability development, economic development, and jobs. What’s different now is the growing understanding of the importance of industrial capacity and international partnerships as part of the allied strategic strength. The push towards U.S.-Australian collaboration gets support from every single U.S. strategic document, from the National Security Strategy on down. All of these highlight the importance of allies and partners to the United States.Now, we know that China’s manufacturing base is the largest in the world. It’s been argued that their industrial base is on a wartime footing. They’re sending dual use material to Russia, which is using it in its own defense production to build capabilities that they’re using against Ukraine. China is also working to own a variety of supply chains and have held key – withheld key supplies, like critical materials, to make a strategic point. The threat is real. The threat is recognized by senior leaders. And we just need continued attention to counter it. The U.S. undersecretary of defense for acquisition and sustainment has said that production is deterrence. And to counter China, we need to produce with our allies and partners. And that includes Australia.The strong relationship means that there are some really important commitments already in place. Partnership structures, like AUKUS, which is a test bed for cooperation on advanced technology and will yield some interesting advances. Australia has its own sovereign guided weapons and explosive ordnance enterprise, which includes the Australian subsidiaries of U.S. companies, which is another form of collaboration. And these need to be continued. Furthermore, given the lengthy list of administrative, regulatory, legal, and cultural barriers that get in the way of collaboration, they should be used as test beds to see where the sticking points are to try to fix those. Every single time we collaborate, that’s a way of learning more about the way forward.So hopefully the leaders in AUSMIN will keep this focus on industrial cooperation. It’s not – in my experience, it has not been as typical for senior leaders to be talking about this. It was not typical in the past, and there is a lot of focus on it now. So we’re hoping – I am hoping to see a continued focus on this and some new announcements in terms of fixing challenges across the domain. We know that ITAR is being worked. Australia has updated its export control regime. The U.S. is working to do the same with ITAR. These challenges don’t – these regulatory regimes don’t stop cooperation, collaboration, they just slow it down. And we can’t afford to – we can’t afford those delays anymore.Earlier this week, the Defense Innovation Board issued a new report called, “Optimizing Innovation Cooperation with Allies and Partners.” And they highlight the risks of focusing on the support of regulations that reduce the risk of accidentally releasing classified information or allowing export to a – of protected technology to a partner. And say that the real risk is that on day one of a conflict we have failed to properly integrate and align with the nations that underpin our military strength. So I will close with that. Clearly we do need to do more. And working with Australia is a terrific way forward for the U.S.Mr. Cestari: Perfect. Thank you, Cynthia.And after our next speaker, we will turn to your questions.(Gives queuing instructions.)Our next speaker is Rory Medcalf, nonresident adjunct fellow with the CSIS Australia Chair. Rory, over to you.Rory Medcalf: Look, thanks, Sam, and good to be on the call.So to reinforce, I think, some of the views we’ve heard with a perspective from Down Under, from the – from the Australian side, look, I agree there will be a big focus this year on consolidation of the fairly rapid gains, intensification that the – that the alliance has had in recent years. There will still be momentum, but there’s going to be a very clear eye now on the elections – on, obviously, the U.S. elections, but also, importantly, elections in this country. There’s bipartisan support for the alliance, no question of that, but there are interesting questions about what the next Australian Parliament will look like and the possibility, for example, of a hung parliament and a minority government where independent parliamentarians and perhaps the Greens Party might seek to have some influence is an important consideration because, particularly with the Greens Party, the commitment to the alliance is weaker.But going to the practicalities of the moment, I think there’s no question that the alliance has made big gains in recent years. These are principals, leaders who are meeting with very strong political and leadership chemistry, and very shared strategic assessments of the risks that our interests and values face. I’m sure that in the closed-door conversations there will be a very – a very frank exchange of views on the China challenge, but more broad than that as well – so North Korea, the Russian aggression in Ukraine, the situation in the Middle East, the particularly subregions of the Indo-Pacific where China is making encroachments but we’re pushing back together, particularly the South Pacific.I think that looking to the – you know, the all-important joint statement that will come out of this, you know, I don’t anticipate any spectacular new news, but there doesn’t have to be. There needs to be indications of progress – whether it’s on AUKUS, whether it’s on the force posture initiatives bilaterally, whether it’s also on the much broader security agenda that we saw, for example, in last year’s communique or last year’s joint statement and we are also seeing in the way that both the United States and Australia are working the web of Indo-Pacific partners – you know, Japan, South Korea, the Philippines, and others. I think for the sustainability of the alliance, particularly with the younger generation of Australians, it is important that AUSMIN is not solely about military deterrence. And so a focus of what both countries can do to build the resilience of the Indo-Pacific in development, in governance, in particularly helping partner countries in the South Pacific and Southeast Asia including with technology and the energy transition, all of that will be important. And there will be a weather eye on what the future holds, including if there’s, you know, potentially a change of government in either country or both.I’ll pause there.Mr. Cestari: Perfect. Thank you, Rory. I really appreciate it. And thank you to all of our speakers for sharing your thoughts here today.So why don’t we open it up for questions now? I’ll turn it over to our operator, Don, to open up the line. Don, why don’t we go ahead with our first question.Operator: OK.(Gives queuing instructions.)First, we’re going to the line for David Brunnstrom. Please go ahead.Q: Yeah. Hi, everybody. Thank you very much for doing this.I was wondering if you could talk a little bit – you mentioned just at the end there it will be important to see indications of progress in areas of particularly AUKUS. Can I ask what sort of indicators and progress you will be looking for? How are things going? So I’m trying to get my head around what happens next, and I think we’ve got a deadline coming up for defense trade exemptions. But I think, Charles, you mentioned there could be announcements in September, and I’m wondering why – I mean, OK, there’s an anniversary – what sort of announcements we might expect then.And also, there was an article – well, actually, an editorial in The London Times a few days ago which was suggesting that AUKUS could come up for review as part of the U.K. Defence Review, and I was wondering if that causes any sort of or might cause any sort of worries and could be a subject for discussion. Many thanks.Dr. Edel: All right. Sam, I’ll –Mr. Cestari: Charlie, would you like to take that? OK. Yeah, go right ahead.Dr. Edel: Yeah. I’ll kick off, and then, you know, happy to have Rory or anyone else jump in on this, too – although, David, shame on you because you’ve just asked four questions, so I’ll do my best to kind of sort through them here. (Laughs.)So, first, I think AUKUS needs to continue to show progress, both so that we can see that we’re making progress towards the strategic objectives but also incremental gains on the ground. Look, there are a couple of things that I would look at.So, one, some of these are very unsexy announcements, but they are indeed progress. So I would look to where the State Department ends up landing on kind of export-control reforms. It’s slated to kind of come in mid-August; my guess is it will be pushed back until beginning of September to see if we do have a new regime on that front.I would look very carefully at things that head into and around Perth, Australia, at the same time. It’s long been rumored that we want to see progress towards maintenance of an SSN in Australia with a submarine tender. So I would look for that at some point soon.But I think the thing that is right in front of us, perhaps more important than anything else of the big goals, is making sure that we can make steady progress on Pillar 1, at least, towards actually pulling it off, in particular the buildout of SRF-West – that is, Submarine Rotational Forces-West – out of west coast of Australia. That has to come online. The buildout of the infrastructure both on base but also out into the community to support was going to be a very different presence by both the U.S. military but also to civilians who help support and maintain the submarines that are there. That submarine rotational presence should be coming online by 2027. They need to show progress towards the buildout of that infrastructure.So I would be looking for all of those. There’s always a rumor that there will be more announcements particularly in the Pillar 2 bucket, so there might indeed be some in early September but I’m waiting to see what those are.And, finally, to your question on what to make of the new government in London, and especially with regards to the call that it has made for a Defence Strategic Review to review both spending priorities and objectives, that is, I would say, perfectly natural for a – for a new government to do. It’s what the Australian government did when Labor won two years ago. It’s what any new administration would do, in Washington as well. This makes eminent sense. It does not strike me that AUKUS is under threat in London. But also, you now have a Labour government who has not – who has not had to make positive movements forward on defense budget, nor on AUKUS. So the meeting of defense ministers, whenever that happens – and I suspect it will happen sometime soon – strikes me as a real opportunity to both showcase the progress that AUKUS has made, but also to put the argument to Labour about why this is in their strategic interest.A final point here I would just note is that while in opposition, the British Labour Party made statements of broad support both for AUKUS and for the U.K.’s continued presence in the Indo-Pacific. I don’t expect that will change now that they’re in power, but the way that that manifests itself, I think, will take a little while to unfold.Over.Mr. Medcalf: Look, I might just jump in, Charlie, if you don’t mind. I’ll just add an Australian perspective on – firstly, on that U.K. dimension. I think it’s fair to say that there is a high level of confidence and comfort in Australia with the position that the new Starmer Labour government In London will be taking on AUKUS. Already at a ministerial level there’s a sense that the key players get it, and have got it for some years. They understand that even if the United Kingdom can’t realistically sustain a large strategic presence of its own in the Indo-Pacific, AUKUS is in its interests – is in British interests, including for shared capability.And just as an interesting aside to that, if we were, for example, to face a future where, let’s say, there’s a – you know, there’s a Trump administration in the United States, and you have a Labor government in Australia and Labour government in Britain, it makes very good sense for those very pragmatic Labor governments to work as closely together as possible on ensuring that the politics doesn’t get in the way of AUKUS.Just on that other point about, if you like, putting facts in the water and ensuring that AUKUS momentum is sustained materially, I echo Charlie’s point about a U.S. submarine maintenance, SSN maintenance, occurring in western Australia sooner rather than later. And in fact, there have already been a significant number – I think, 37 – Australian Navy personnel who’ve been training, embedded with U.S. submarine tenders since the start of the year, in preparation for that. And it’s an open secret in the Australian media that there will be a maintenance activity on an American SSN at the Australian base, HMAS Stirling, before the end of the year. So that’s a very tangible signal, as is the fact that there are Australian submariners already studying, already completing courses in the U.S. nuclear Navy training system.Mr. Cestari: Thank you, Rory.Kathryn or Cynthia, anything you’d like to add there?Dr. Cook: I would highlight – this is Cynthia. I would highlight, in terms of sustainment, that there was, in fact, a recent AUSMIN Defense Advanced Capability Committee meeting in D.C. in June. And the Regional Sustainment Framework was one of the topics that came up there. This is a new strategy by the U.S. to expand the number of countries it is doing sustainment with, to ensure that these capabilities are available, you know, in a contested environment. So one of the things that might happen is the announcement of a specific project for the – or, a particular partnership project between the U.S. and Australia in support of the RSF. And it might be the submarine one.Mr. Cestari: Thank you, Cynthia.And so at this time, I’ll give one extra moment if anyone would like to join the queue.(Gives queuing instructions.)Right now, it doesn’t seem we have anyone in the queue. So I guess we have covered everything. So that seems to be all of our questions right now. I want to thank all of our speakers and participants for joining us for this timely call. Please feel free to reach out for anything we can do to help you resource looking ahead to the AUSMIN consultations. And we’re here to help, so don’t hesitate to reach out. As mentioned at the top of the call, we will have a transcript distributed to all of you and it will be available on CSIS.org. So with that, I hope everybody has a good rest of your day. And thanks for joining us.(END.)\nTags\nGlobal Governance and Institutions, and\nAllies and Partners","content_cn":"新闻简报：预览澳大利亚—美国部长级磋商\n照片：DANIEL SLIM/法新社，Getty Images\n文字记录\n— 2024年8月1日\n可下载内容\n下载文字记录\n208kb\n图片\n照片：DANIEL SLIM/法新社，Getty Images\n新闻简报：预览澳大利亚—美国部长级磋商\n2024年8月1日 • 美国东部时间下午4:00–5:00\n本文字记录来自于2024年8月1日举办的CSIS新闻简报。Samuel Cestari：谢谢你，Don。大家好，欢迎参加CSIS新闻简报“预览澳大利亚—美国部长级磋商”，也称为AUSMIN。今天很高兴有四位专家与我们一起，分享他们对AUSMIN、其背景以及可能讨论议题的看法和专业知识。开始之前，先说明几项事务安排。每位发言人将先作几分钟的开场发言，之后我们将回答大家的问题。明天早上（美国东部时间），我们还会向所有参与者分发今天通话的文字记录。文字记录也将发布在CSIS.org上。那么，我们现在就开始吧？首先请CSIS高级顾问兼澳大利亚主席Charles Edel发言。Charlie，请。\n\nCharles Edel：太好了。感谢大家今天参加，也谢谢你，Sam，协助组织此次活动。除了欢迎我的CSIS同事Kathryn和Cynthia之外，我尤其高兴Rory Medcalf今天也参加我们，而此时澳大利亚还是清晨。Rory当然是印太地区最杰出的分析人士之一。他是澳大利亚国立大学国家安全学院院长。令我们非常高兴的是，他现在还是CSIS澳大利亚主席项目的兼职非驻会研究员。\n\n下周，8月6日，美国国务卿和国防部长将与澳大利亚对应官员——澳大利亚外交部长和国防部长——举行第34届AUSMIN，即年度澳大利亚—美国部长级磋商。Antony Blinken、Lloyd Austin、Penny Wong和Richard Marles在马里兰州安纳波利斯会面时，将评估澳大利亚和美国在更加严峻、竞争日益激烈的战略环境中，如何为自身以及彼此有效开展竞争做好了准备。\n\n随着两国都将迎来选举年，双方代表团都将重点关注两国政府迄今取得的成果。届时会宣布一些新倡议，但我预计重点将放在迄今取得的稳步且持续的进展上。这里的底线是，AUSMIN将继续展示美国与澳大利亚之间不断深化和拓展的关系；这种关系遍及全球，并在印太地区具有日益重要的战略意义和分量。相关公告和声明将体现这一点，成果将涵盖国防、外交、经济、能源和发展等领域。\n\n这些政府领导人如今已经多次会面。他们彼此熟悉，并高度重视对方。因此，他们可能不会只是逐项浏览成果清单，还会真正就地区和全球发展交换意见，并讨论如何应对这些发展的策略。由于联盟已为自己设定了越来越多的倡议，今年的AUSMIN很可能会重点介绍\n过去几年取得的稳步进展。这一点很重要。雄心并不是美澳关系面临的主要挑战。朝着若干战略目标稳步推进，才是关键所在。\n\n我将就美澳关系的总体状况以及今年澳美部长级磋商举行的背景谈几点看法，说明今年可以期待什么，并以双方正在寻求答案、且可能在未来进一步凸显的几个问题作结。\n\n澳美部长级磋商本身将于8月6日举行。黄英贤外长和澳大利亚副总理将提前在华盛顿分别与各自对口官员举行双边会谈。随后，他们将把活动移师安纳波利斯——这里当然是美国海军学院所在地——举行“2+2”会谈。\n\n副总理将在第二天继续留在当地，并在这里的战略与国际问题研究中心参加一场关于美澳联盟未来的对话。该活动将进行现场直播，我鼓励现在参加此次电话会议的各位提交你们可能想向马勒斯国防部长提出的问题。\n\n在外交政策领域，人们常说，出访行程反映了议程和优先事项。四位主要官员的出访安排就体现了这一点。劳埃德·奥斯汀和安东尼·布林肯刚刚结束了对印度—太平洋地区的访问：他们在东京宣布了升级美日联盟的一项重大消息，体现为对驻日美军指挥结构进行现代化改造；在马尼拉，美国承诺提供5亿美元新的军事资金，帮助菲律宾军队实现现代化。布林肯将继续出访，前往新加坡和蒙古。当然，他还在东京举行的四方安全对话部长级会议期间，以及在老挝万象举行东盟地区论坛会议期间，与黄英贤会面。\n\n澳大利亚代表团也将在结束太平洋地区的一系列活动后参加澳美部长级磋商。理查德·马勒斯刚刚在华盛顿参加北约相关活动，随后前往伦敦；外长则将在访问东京、首尔和万象后抵达华盛顿。此后不久，她还将前往汤加，参加太平洋岛国论坛领导人会议。\n\n这里的要点是，四位官员都将已经与彼此以及东北亚和东南亚地区的对口官员进行过多次接触。而在当前这个局势似乎正在变化的时刻——整个地区举行选举并发生政府更迭，威权行为体之间的合作日益加强，地区各民主国家之间的关系日趋成熟，中国施加的压力不断增加，这一点在菲律宾第二托马斯浅滩表现得最为明显，同时也体现在台湾周边及岛内——他们开展合作的重要性将进一步提升，从而为一个继续受到北京强势行动破坏稳定的地区提供更多支撑。\n\n劳埃德·奥斯汀一贯将澳美关系称为，引用原话，“牢不可破的联盟”。它或许确实如此，但它也日益成为一个议程广泛、涉及多个层面的联盟。双边关系目前状况极其稳固，并且正在澳大利亚总理安东尼·阿尔巴尼斯去年10月对华盛顿进行国事访问的基础上不断发展。当时，两国领导人承诺扩大合作领域，在防务和经济领域之外，纳入气候与清洁能源。这一点可以从双方在澳英美三方安全伙伴关系（AUKUS）方面的工作中看到；在……方面\n气候变化、清洁能源和关键矿产；以及与其他首都同步开展外交努力，无论是在东京、马尼拉还是莫尔兹比港。\n\n关于AUSMIN，有几件事值得关注。首先，鉴于欧洲、中东和印度洋—太平洋地区的全球安全环境恶化，澳大利亚和美国都将积极强调自身的担忧与合作。我预计，会议将大量讨论地区和全球稳定面临的挑战。再次，继总理对华盛顿进行国事访问期间作出的承诺之后，我预计AUSMIN上的许多讨论将围绕落实此前的倡议展开，包括关键矿产、海底电缆、太空，以及与东南亚和太平洋岛国地区的合作。除此之外，我预计大多数实质性宣布将集中于防务倡议，包括部队态势方面的进展、后勤测试（包括预置燃料和弹药）、能力建设方面的关注——我相信辛西娅会对此作进一步阐述——以及深化与第三方的合作。会议可能会提到AUKUS，但我不认为它会占据首要位置。关于AUKUS的更多信息，可关注其9月迎来成立三周年之际的接触与可能宣布的事项。\n\n现在，在把话题交给我的同事们之前，我想强调，目前正在越来越广泛的领域开展大量工作。联盟目前的状况非常稳固，并在两国都享有两党支持。当四位领导人在安纳波利斯会面时，他们将有许多事情可以相互祝贺。但尽管彼此的情谊是真挚的，历史渊源也很深厚，我们将看到的日益加强的合作，在许多方面并不仅仅是共同价值观的体现，也是对不断增多的挑战以及一种共同认识的回应——我们在许多方面都在奋起直追。\n\n在即将到来的一年里，两国都将经历相当程度的政治变动。届时将进行关键讨论：我们是否能够足够迅速地扩大国防工业基础；是否已经找到生产能源转型所需充足物资并确保供应链安全的方法；以及我们是否已经证明，能够交付本地区要求我们双方提供的成果。关于所有这些问题，我相信澳美关系的发展方向是明确的。问题越来越在于交付的速度。\n\n说到这里，实际上让我转向萨姆以及我才华横溢的同事们。\n\n塞斯塔里先生：谢谢你，查理。\n\n接下来是凯瑟琳·派克，CSIS澳大利亚主席项目的高级研究员。凯瑟琳，请。\n\n凯瑟琳·派克：谢谢你，萨姆。也谢谢查理在开头对总体情况的概述。\n\n查理刚才概述了双边关系的一些总体轮廓，以及围绕2024年AUSMIN会议的框架。我想用几分钟深入谈谈太平洋岛国地区。这是澳大利亚和美国都高度关注的领域，我预计下周将对此进行详细讨论。\n\n我同意查理的看法，即今年的会议可能不会只关注一份交付事项清单，而更可能彼此坦诚、公开地讨论地区和全球发展。这将很大程度上是对迄今进展的盘点，以及进行一次\n坦诚交流哪些方面仍有工作要做。而在这两本账——已经取得的进展和仍待完成的工作——上，太平洋地区都有很多问题需要考虑。\n\n那么，太平洋地区目前的状况是怎样的？众所周知，这长期以来一直是澳大利亚的战略优先事项。但美国对该地区的承诺并不总是连贯一致。不过在过去几年里，我们看到美国对太平洋的参与呈现出真正积极的轨迹，这也伴随着美国和澳大利亚在该地区合作方式的真正转变。\n\n2022年的所罗门群岛安全协议让两国都认识到，要遏制中国谋求在太平洋站稳脚跟的行动，需要该地区所有志同道合的伙伴采取集中协调的努力。再加上气候变化对太平洋地区造成的灾难性威胁，很明显，太平洋面临的挑战极其巨大，绝非任何一个伙伴能够独自应对。\n\n因此，这两个因素的汇聚——美国希望加强参与，以及澳大利亚推动美国更多介入太平洋——导致太平洋各岛屿之间的合作与倡议出现了绝对的爆发式增长。此外，我们还看到，为引入除新西兰和日本等通常伙伴之外的其他志同道合伙伴，各方的努力出现了真正的协调；这些伙伴还包括欧盟、英国、韩国、印度、加拿大，仅举几例。\n\n在去年的AUSMIN会议上，我们看到美国和澳大利亚不仅就致力于太平洋岛国地区以及作为区域机构的太平洋岛国论坛（PIF）发表了声明，还确定了几个具体重点领域，例如通过增加海岸警卫队部署来满足关键的海上领域感知（MDA）需求，与太平洋岛国开展合作行动，以加强海上执法和法治。双方还强烈承诺，通过“蓝色太平洋伙伴”这一非正式协调机制，落实聚焦气候行动、人道主义、仓储和网络安全的倡议。因此，我预计今年的AUSMIN会议将在一定程度上检查这些重点领域的进展，同时确定新的或持续存在的关切领域。\n\n说到关切领域，有一个国家很可能会成为讨论要点，那就是所罗门群岛。因此，所罗门群岛今年早些时候举行的选举导致总理从索加瓦雷更替为马内莱，但执政党并未改变。政府与中国的牢固关系也延续了下来。澳大利亚一直在与新总理马内莱总理进行非常充分的接触；与其前任相比，马内莱把自己展现为一位更具外交性、也更加公允的政治家。他先是把访问澳大利亚作为自己的首次海外出访，随后却立即前往北京。\n\n不过，尽管这些挑战持续存在，美国仍有机会借助所罗门群岛及其新总理重新开展接触。美国和澳大利亚可能会讨论美国如何加强在当地的参与，尤其是美国驻霍尼亚拉新大使馆现已投入运作。此外，美国和澳大利亚可能会评估并或许进一步加码近期的成功举措，例如启动“太平洋互联”（Pacific Connect）项目。这是与谷歌合作的一项伙伴关系；如果获得进一步的国际资金支持，就可能为太平洋一些最偏远的岛屿实现连接。\n或者落实近期宣布的事项，例如美国和澳大利亚最近于7月初作出的帮助太平洋地区银行业的承诺。\n\n另一个潜在的会谈领域是巴布亚新几内亚。今年早些时候，澳大利亚与该国最终敲定了警务协议，而美国正在推进落实去年签署的《防务合作协议》（DCA）。美国和澳大利亚都希望确保这些协议相互协调、相互强化，以加强巴新执法和防务能力。\n\n有一个议题不太可能出现在会后公报中，但可能会在私下提出，那就是新喀里多尼亚。这个法国属地目前正陷入抗议活动，许多人认为这些抗议反对的是法国政府压制其走向独立的努力。鉴于太平洋岛国论坛（PIF）以及美拉尼西亚国家尤其施压，要求允许再次举行独立公投，这一问题使同为PIF成员的澳大利亚处于敏感位置。澳大利亚尚未公开支持再次举行公投的呼吁，这一点不同于新西兰。\n\n最后，我还要指出，AUSMIN结束后紧接着就是即将于8月下旬在汤加举行的年度PIF领导人会议。虽然美国不是PIF成员，但我们是对话伙伴，而PIF正努力完善其对话伙伴体系。真诚地出席、真诚地参与，将是展示美国持续致力于该地区的关键一环。去年，我们派出了美国驻联合国大使琳达·托马斯-格林菲尔德。我预计今年也会有高级别代表前往。\n\n总的来说，与太平洋地区的讨论议题可能非常多。下面我把时间交还给萨姆。谢谢。\n\n塞斯塔里先生：凯瑟琳，谢谢你。（说明提问顺序。）\n\n在接下来的两位发言者发言后，我们将回答大家的问题，并尽力作答。\n\n下一位发言者是辛西娅·库克，她是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）国防工业倡议小组主任，也是国际安全项目高级研究员。辛西娅，请发言。\n\n辛西娅·库克：谢谢，萨姆。也谢谢凯瑟琳和查理的发言。\n\n我想说，回顾我们所处的位置，目前围绕国防工业合作确实有一股非常强劲且积极的势头，这既体现在美国整体层面，也具体体现在这一双边伙伴关系上。这确实是一个始终不会消失的话题。工业合作与能力发展、经济发展和就业相互关联。现在的不同之处在于，人们越来越认识到，工业产能和国际伙伴关系是盟国战略实力的一部分，其重要性正在不断上升。推动美澳合作得到了美国每一份战略文件的支持，从《国家安全战略》往下的各类文件都强调盟友和伙伴对美国的重要性。\n\n现在，我们知道中国的制造业基础是世界上最大的。有人认为，其工业基础已经处于战时状态。他们正在向俄罗斯提供军民两用材料，而俄罗斯正将这些材料用于自身的国防生产，以建设用于对抗乌克兰的能力。中国还在努力掌控各种供应链，并扣留关键供应品，例如关键材料，以此传达战略信号。这一威胁是真实存在的，也得到了高级领导人的认可。我们只是需要继续\n对此予以反制。美国国防部负责采购与保障的副部长曾表示，生产就是威慑。为了制衡中国，我们需要与盟友和伙伴共同生产，其中包括澳大利亚。牢固的关系意味着已经落实了一些非常重要的承诺。像AUKUS这样的伙伴关系机制，就是先进技术合作的试验平台，并将带来一些有趣的进展。澳大利亚拥有自己的主权制导武器和爆炸性军械产业，其中包括美国公司的澳大利亚子公司，这也是另一种合作形式。这些合作需要继续下去。此外，鉴于妨碍合作的行政、监管、法律和文化障碍清单很长，我们应当把这些合作当作试验平台，看看症结在哪里，并努力加以解决。每一次合作，都是进一步了解前进方向的机会。\n\n因此，希望AUSMIN的领导人能够继续关注工业合作。根据我的经验，高层领导过去并不经常谈论这个问题。过去并不常见，而现在对此的关注很多。因此，我们希望——我希望——看到对这一问题的持续关注，并在解决各领域挑战方面有一些新的宣布。我们知道，ITAR正在推进。澳大利亚已经更新了自己的出口管制制度。美国也在努力对ITAR采取同样的做法。这些挑战并不会——这些监管制度不会阻止合作与协作，只是会拖慢进度。而我们再也无法承受——我们再也无法承受这些延误。\n\n本周早些时候，国防创新委员会发布了一份新报告，题为《优化与盟友和伙伴的创新合作》。报告强调了这样一种风险：过于关注支持那些旨在降低意外泄露机密信息或允许向伙伴出口受保护技术风险的监管规定。报告指出，真正的风险在于，冲突第一天，我们未能与支撑我军实力的国家实现适当整合和协调。因此，我就以此作结。显然，我们需要做得更多。与澳大利亚合作，是美国向前迈进的绝佳方式。\n\nCestari先生：非常好。谢谢你，Cynthia。\n\n在下一位发言者发言后，我们将回答大家的问题。（介绍提问排队规则。）\n\n下一位发言者是Rory Medcalf，CSIS澳大利亚主席项目的非常驻兼职研究员。Rory，请。\n\nRory Medcalf：谢谢，Sam，很高兴参加这次电话会议。\n\n为了呼应我们听到的一些观点，我想从南半球、从澳大利亚方面谈谈。首先，我同意今年将高度关注巩固联盟近年来相当迅速取得的成果和强化势头。势头仍将持续，但现在会非常明确地关注选举——显然是美国大选，但同样重要的是澳大利亚的大选。毫无疑问，联盟获得两党支持，但下届澳大利亚议会将呈现何种面貌，以及例如出现悬峙议会和少数派政府的可能性，都是值得关注的问题；届时，无党籍议员以及可能的绿党或许会寻求施加某种影响，这一点尤其需要考虑，因为，特别是绿党\n政党，对联盟的承诺较弱。不过，回到当前的实际情况，我认为毫无疑问，联盟近年来取得了重大进展。这些是原则坚定的领导人，他们举行会晤时展现出非常强的政治和领导层默契，并且对我们的利益和价值观所面临的风险有着高度一致的战略评估。我相信，在闭门会谈中，他们将就中国带来的挑战进行非常——非常坦率的意见交流，但议题也会更广泛——包括朝鲜、俄罗斯在乌克兰的侵略、中东局势，以及印太地区中国正在进行渗透而我们正共同予以反制的若干次区域，尤其是南太平洋。\n\n我认为，着眼于——你知道的——这次会议将发布的重要联合声明，我不期待有什么轰动性的新消息，但其实也不必如此。需要看到的是进展迹象——无论是在“奥库斯”方面，还是在双边部队态势倡议方面，抑或是在更广泛的安全议程方面；例如，我们在去年的公报或去年联合声明中看到的议程，以及我们还看到美国和澳大利亚如何与印太伙伴开展合作——你知道的，日本、韩国、菲律宾以及其他国家。我认为，为了联盟的可持续性，尤其是对澳大利亚年轻一代而言，重要的是，AUSMIN不能只关注军事威慑。因此，两国能够采取哪些行动来增强印太地区在发展、治理方面的韧性，尤其是帮助南太平洋和东南亚的伙伴国家，包括提供技术支持和推动能源转型，这些都将十分重要。人们也会密切关注未来会怎样，包括如果任一国家或两国都可能发生政府更迭。\n\n我先说到这里。\n\nCestari先生：非常好。谢谢你，Rory。我非常感谢你，也感谢今天所有发言者分享你们的看法。\n\n那么，我们现在为什么不开始提问呢？我把时间交给我们的接线员Don，请他接通线路。Don，我们开始第一个问题吧。\n\n接线员：好的。\n（说明排队提问的操作指引。）\n\n首先，我们接通David Brunnstrom的线路。请提问。\n\n问：是的。大家好。非常感谢你们安排这次活动。\n\n我想请问，你们能否谈谈一些情况——你刚才最后提到，看到一些领域取得进展的迹象，尤其是“奥库斯”方面。你们会关注哪些指标和进展？目前进展如何？我正在努力理清接下来会发生什么，我想我们即将迎来国防贸易豁免的截止日期。但我记得，Charles，你提到9月份可能会有一些公告，我想知道为什么——我的意思是，好的，确实有周年纪念——届时我们可能会期待什么样的公告？\n\n另外，几天前《伦敦时报》有一篇文章——实际上是一篇社论——暗示“奥库斯”可能会作为英国国防审查的一部分接受评估。我想知道，这是否会引起某种担忧，或者可能引起某种担忧，并成为讨论议题。非常感谢。\n\nEdel博士：好的，Sam，我——\n\nCestari先生：Charlie，你愿意回答这个问题吗？好的。是的，请继续。\n\nEdel博士：\n埃德尔：是的。我先开始，然后，你知道的，也很欢迎罗里或其他任何人加入进来——不过，大卫，你问了四个问题，真是让你惭愧，所以我会尽力在这里把它们梳理一下。（笑。）那么，首先，我认为“澳英美三方安全伙伴关系”需要继续展示进展，这样我们不仅能看到我们正在朝着战略目标取得进展，也能看到实地的增量成果。听着，有几件事我会关注。\n\n第一，其中一些是非常不起眼的公告，但它们确实代表着进展。因此，我会关注国务院最终会如何处理出口管制改革。这项改革原定于8月中旬出台；我的猜测是，它会被推迟到9月初，届时我们才能知道这一领域是否会建立新的制度。\n\n与此同时，我会非常仔细地关注那些涉及澳大利亚珀斯及其周边的事项。长期以来一直有传闻称，我们希望看到澳大利亚通过潜艇支援舰推进维护一艘核动力攻击潜艇（SSN）的进展。因此，我会期待这方面很快有所行动。\n\n但我认为，眼下摆在我们面前、或许比其他任何重大目标都更重要的事情，是确保我们至少能够在支柱一上稳步推进，真正把它落实，尤其是建设西部潜艇轮换部队（SRF-West）——即“西部潜艇轮换部队”——其基地位于澳大利亚西海岸。这项部署必须上线。基础设施建设不仅要在基地内进行，也要延伸到社区，以支持一种截然不同的存在：既包括美国军方，也包括帮助支持和维护驻留潜艇的平民人员。这种潜艇轮换部署应在2027年前后上线。他们需要展示在基础设施建设方面取得的进展。\n\n因此，我会关注所有这些方面。一直有传闻称，尤其是在支柱二领域还会有更多公告，因此9月初确实可能会有一些，但我正在等待看看具体会是什么。\n\n最后，关于你问到的如何看待伦敦的新政府，尤其是它提出进行国防战略审查、重新审视支出重点和目标的问题，我要说，这对一个新政府而言完全是自然而然的做法。两年前工党赢得选举后，澳大利亚政府就是这么做的。在华盛顿，任何新一届政府也都会这么做。这完全合乎情理。在我看来，“澳英美三方安全伙伴关系”并未受到伦敦方面的威胁。但现在你面对的是一个工党政府，它还没有——还没有——被迫在国防预算或“澳英美三方安全伙伴关系”上采取积极的前进措施。因此，国防部长会议无论何时举行——我猜应该很快就会举行——在我看来，都是一个真正的机会：既可以展示“澳英美三方安全伙伴关系”取得的进展，也可以向工党阐明为什么这符合他们的战略利益。\n\n最后我想指出的是，英国工党在野时曾发表声明，广泛支持“澳英美三方安全伙伴关系”，也支持英国继续留在印度洋—太平洋地区。我不认为他们现在执政后这一点会改变，但这种支持将如何体现出来，我认为还需要一段时间才能逐渐显现。\n\n完。\n\n梅德卡夫先生：查理，如果你不介意的话，我想插一句。我想先从澳大利亚的角度补充一下——首先，关于英国这一层面。我认为可以公平地说\n澳大利亚对伦敦新一届斯塔默工党政府将对AUKUS采取的立场抱有高度信心和安心感。部长级层面已经有一种共识，即关键参与者理解这一点，而且多年来一直理解。他们明白，即便英国实际上无法在印度洋—太平洋地区维持大规模的战略存在，AUKUS也符合其利益——符合英国利益，包括共享能力方面的利益。\n\n顺便提一个有趣的旁题，如果我们比如说面临这样一种未来：美国出现特朗普政府，而澳大利亚有工党政府、英国也有工党政府，那么，对这些非常务实的工党政府来说，尽可能紧密地合作，确保政治不会妨碍AUKUS，是非常有道理的。\n\n就另一个问题而言，也就是如果你愿意说的，在水中注入事实并确保AUKUS的势头在实质上得以维持，我赞同查理关于美国潜艇维护——SSN维护——应尽早在西澳大利亚进行的观点。事实上，已经有相当数量——我想是37名——澳大利亚海军人员自年初以来一直在接受训练，并被安排与美国潜艇补给舰一起工作，为此做准备。澳大利亚媒体中有一个公开的秘密，那就是在今年年底之前，澳大利亚基地斯特灵海军基地（HMAS Stirling）将会对一艘美国SSN开展维护活动。因此，这是一个非常切实的信号；同样切实的信号还包括，澳大利亚潜艇艇员已经在美国核海军训练体系中学习，并且已经完成课程。\n\n塞斯塔里先生：谢谢你，罗里。\n\n凯瑟琳或辛西娅，你们还有什么想补充的吗？\n\n库克博士：我是辛西娅。我想强调，就持续保障而言，最近确实在华盛顿特区举行了一次AUSMIN国防高级能力委员会会议，时间是在六月。区域持续保障框架是会上提出的议题之一。这是美国的一项新战略，旨在扩大与其开展持续保障合作的国家数量，以确保这些能力在受到争夺的环境中依然可用。因此，可能发生的一件事是，宣布美澳之间一个具体项目，或者说，一个支持RSF的特定伙伴关系项目。而这个项目可能就是潜艇项目。\n\n塞斯塔里先生：谢谢你，辛西娅。\n\n现在，如果还有人想加入发言队列，我再给大家一点时间。（说明排队发言的方式。）\n\n目前看来没有人排队发言。所以我想，我们已经涵盖了所有内容。目前看来，这就是我们现在的全部问题。我要感谢所有发言者和参与者参加这次及时的电话会议。如果有什么我们可以做的，帮助你们为即将举行的AUSMIN磋商做好资源准备，请随时联系我们。我们随时愿意提供帮助，所以不要犹豫，尽管联系我们。正如会议开始时所说，我们会向大家分发文字记录，届时也会在CSIS.org上提供。就这样，希望大家接下来一天过得愉快。感谢大家参加。（结束。）\n\n标签\n全球治理与机构，以及\n盟友与伙伴","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-08-01T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-996","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"新闻发布会：预览特朗普与习近平峰会","title_en":"Press Briefing: Previewing the Trump-Xi Summit","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心于2026年5月7日举办新闻发布会，预览特朗普总统与习近平即将举行的峰会。CSIS专家Scott Kennedy、Bonny Lin、Edgard Kagan等参与讨论。会议涉及美中贸易休战、关税、技术限制、台湾问题、AI风险管理以及两国相对实力变化等议题。","summary_en":"CSIS hosted a press briefing on May 7, 2026, previewing the upcoming Trump-Xi summit. CSIS experts Scott Kennedy, Bonny Lin, and Edgard Kagan discussed the U.S.-China trade truce, tariffs, technology restrictions, Taiwan, AI risk management, and shifting relative power dynamics between the two countries.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:24:40.333804+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/press-briefing-previewing-trump-xi-summit","content":"Press Briefing: Previewing the Trump-Xi Summit\nTranscript\n— May 7, 2026\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Transcript\n290kb\nThis transcript is from a CSIS press briefing hosted on May 7, 2026.\nImage\nPhoto: Thomas Peter-Pool/Getty Images\nPress Briefing: Previewing the Trump-Xi Summit\nMay 7, 2026 • 10:00 – 11:00 am EDT\nSofia Chavez: Hello, everyone, and welcome to this CSIS press briefing previewing the upcoming summit between President Trump and Xi Jinping. I’m Sofia Chavez. I’m the media relations manager here at CSIS. And I’m joined today by a great lineup of CSIS scholars who will share their expectations and thoughts of what to look for going into this highly anticipated summit, what we can expect coming out of the summit, and much more.Just a couple of housekeeping notes before we get started. Each of our speakers will offer several minutes of introductory remarks, after which we’ll turn to your questions. If you do want to ask a question, make sure to type it in the Q&A window, or you can raise your hand to ask it verbally. We’ll also be distributing a transcript of today’s call shortly after its conclusion. The transcript will also be made available on CSIS.org.With that, let’s go ahead and get started. I’ll turn first to Scott Kennedy, senior advisor and trustee chair in Chinese business and economics at CSIS. Go ahead, Scott.Scott Kennedy: Thanks, Sofia. Good morning, everybody. And welcome to the briefing. I’m talking to you from Palo Alto, where I just arrived yesterday, after two-and-a-half weeks in Beijing where I talked to a lot of Chinese officials, businesses, foreign governments, about the state of U.S.-China relations and China’s economy. So my remarks will reflect some of that experience.I wanted to make three points. The first is that the six-week delay in the summit has actually been important because they – both sides have been able to further develop the agenda and the details for what the two sides will discuss when Presidents Trump and Xi meet next week. I summarize those as the five Bs and the three Ts. The five Bs, which the U.S. is emphasizing Boeing, beef, beans, the Board of Trade, and the Board of Investment. And the three Ts, which are what China’s emphasizing. The first T is, of course, Taiwan. But then also tariffs and technology. I would add to that one additional item that looks like it will be more important than it otherwise would have been six weeks ago. And that is about AI and the management of risks around AI. So I think we’ll actually have a relatively fulsome agenda, even though the amount of time for the meeting is relatively short.The second point I want to make is that there’s still some big questions that are not going to be answered until they actually meet. And that is, how much of that agenda that I just described will they get through? And, because, again, they don’t have much time and it’s not clear that they’ve reached a common landing point on any of those items. The second is whether there will be a joint statement from the two sides, or whether they will follow past practice and have separate statements. In a lot of the meetings that the two sides have had over the last years there have been no document, no paper trail, and one hopes that from this meeting there would be. And so that’ll be important to see.And then this last question of what will happen is what will President Trump do beyond his meetings with President Xi? Will he visit any American companies? Will he visit any universities? It doesn’t sound like he’s going to do any of those things. It looks like maybe the Temple of Heaven and possibly the Great Wall. I think that shows a relatively constrained view of what the President could do in going to China. I think the last point I want to make is that China and Xi Jinping come into this meeting in a much stronger place than the United States. China has goals that they would like – to extend the ceasefire, to reduce tech restrictions on the imports of semiconductors, and lower tariffs.But even if they don’t get much on any of those things, as long as there’s not a blow up in the meeting and President Trump doesn’t go away and look to re-escalate, China basically comes out stronger. The relative position to the U.S. over the past year has improved, following their pushback against the liberation day tariffs and their successful efforts to get President Trump and the administration to back up multiple times over the last year. Also, as a result of the IEEPA decisions and the lowering of U.S. tariffs as a result of the Supreme Court’s ruling, the challenges that the U.S. now has gotten itself into in the Middle East, all of that has improved China’s relative position.In addition, it’s clear that the U.S. is not pushing for significant adjustments of Chinese industrial policy, the role of the state and society, or other major issues, and it’s not doing so in combination with allies and like-minded countries. The U.S. is really just pushing for its own self-interest, and the U.S. is not investing sufficiently in its – the foundations of its economic power.We’ve cut massive – you know, funding massively to various areas of science and technology, and that’s really where the competition is going to be won or lost. And so I think as long as the trajectory goes in the way it has been for the last year, China comes out of this relatively stronger, and the U.S. will be able to claim some progress but, really, China has the right to be confident that they are doing very well bilaterally.Let me stop there.Ms. Chavez: Great. Thank you, Scott.Next, we have Bonny Lin, director of the China Power Project and senior advisor at CSIS. Bonny, please go ahead.Bonny Lin: Thank you, Sofia, and great to be here today.So Scott mentioned he just returned from China. Edgard and I just returned from Taiwan and Japan, and I want to focus the little time I have here by discussing how Taiwan may play into the Trump-Xi meeting.And it seems like as Scott said, in addition to trying to extend the trade truce and seeking overall stability in U.S.-China relations, Taiwan is probably top of mind in terms of China’s priorities, and we know that China has been signaling for some time that Taiwan is a core issue that will be discussed during President Trump’s visit to China and during his meeting with Chinese leader Xi Jinping, and we’ve seen China do quite a bit of work to prepare for this discussion.So I’ll lay out some of the preparation and what I think could be asked or could be hinted at during the meeting.So as many of you are aware, during the February 2026 phone call between President Trump and Xi, Xi emphasized that Taiwan was the most important issue in U.S.-China relations, and he subtly urged the United States to not provide arms to Taiwan. I think the readout was some – did not specifically say not provide arms, but it was clear that that was a direction that he was pushing President Trump.And then last month after President Trump postponed his trip to China to focus on the Iran war, China green lighted the visit of Taiwan’s largest opposition party leader, the Kuomintang – KMT – Chairperson Cheng Li-wun’s visit to China and she paid a visit in early April.During her visit, she departed significantly from the typical KMT positions and echoed almost everything Beijing wanted to hear. She endorsed China’s view of the 1992 consensus. She expressed opposition to Taiwan independence.She expressed opposition to foreign interference on cross-strait issues, usually a code word for arms sales and other U.S. support for Taiwan. She echoed Xi Jinping’s points that both sides of the Taiwan Strait are part of the Chinese nation and one family.So in other words, Cheng’s views were very different and are very different from the views of Taipei, of the Taiwan government, that has had to deal with the reality of relentless and escalating Chinese military and political economic coercion and threats.Her views are also very different than a lot of views within her own party, the KMT, where many within the KMT are wary of Cheng’s positions and believe that she’s not taking seriously the PRC threat.But what her visit means is that it set up Beijing to be in a very strong position when it comes to Taiwan right before the Trump-Xi meeting and today, during the – China’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs press briefing, the foreign ministry spokesperson, the very first question he answered, posed by a – Chinese media, of course, was – and it was very pointed in noting that the United States must fulfill its international obligations to China on Taiwan and those obligations are, quote, “a necessary prerequisite for the stable, sound, and sustainable development of China-U.S. relations.”So in other words, China is signaling very clear about a week or so right before the Trump-Xi meeting that Taiwan – that getting Taiwan right will be very critical to all the other things that Scott was talking about in terms of the overall U.S.-China relationship.So I think this puts Xi Jinping in a strong position to potentially share with President Trump that, look, there’s a leader in a major political party in Taiwan that we can work with and wants to work with China. All the United States needs to do is rein in Taipei and stop working with those separatists.So, within this context, I think Xi could suggest a couple of things. He could subtly hint that maybe President Trump should change his statement that the United States opposes Taiwan independence. The United States has typically stated that the United States does not support Taiwan independence. So switching from not support to oppose is quite critical.Another possibility is suggesting that maybe President Trump might want to change his statement that the United States supports peaceful unification with Taiwan. In the past, the United States has typically stated that the U.S. supports peaceful resolution of the Taiwan question and also opposes any unilateral changes to the status quo from either side. Xi could again hint or maybe be very explicit again that the United States should not provide arms sales to Taiwan.I’ve not seen any reports in prior meetings that she has specifically raised the issue of – this issue to President Trump in terms of Taiwan President William Lai’s transits to the United States. But China is likely also to use this meeting with President Trump as well as potential follow-on meetings, the potential for three additional meetings this year between the two leaders to make it clear that it is not in U.S. interests to allow Taiwan President William Lai to transit the United States.And just for context, aside from during the COVID years, in the past almost two decades, Taiwan presidents have typically transited the United States one or two times per year. President Lai did not transit the United States in 2025, and it remains to be seen if there’s room for him on the diplomatic calendar transit this year.And I would note that the U.S. position on Taiwan that President Trump currently has going into the meeting – of course, we still have a week left – could be strengthened a lot more if Taiwan is able to pass a special defense budget. We had heard that there were rumors it could pass today in Taipei, and it seems like it did not happen.And one last point before I close. Related to the Taiwan issue is how other close U.S. allies and partners will be discussed or maybe omitted from the Trump-Xi meeting and whether President Trump and his team might engage with key U.S. allies immediately before or after his trip to China, even, for example, in the form of a phone call.I think most U.S. allies and partners are closely watching these dynamics and would benefit from more stable U.S.-China relations. But at the same time, many of them, particularly Japan, are still the target of growing Chinese coercion. And we’re really seeing anti-Japan rhetoric heat up in China now, and it might be possible that Xi Jinping may even raise up to President Trump what he views as problematic Japanese militarization.So, I’ll wrap up here. Thank you, Sofia.Ms. Chavez: Thank you, Bonny.Next, we have Edgard Kagan, senior advisor and Freeman chair in China studies at CSIS. Edgard, please go ahead.Edgard D. Kagan: Hi. Thank you very much for joining the call. And I will say I was very flattered to have been described as a scholar by Sofia. I’m still working up to that from diplomat, and so I’m grateful for the opportunity to speak with you.It’s hard for me to add much to what Scott and Bonny have laid down. I’ll just say that this visit is critical for both leaders. In both countries there is a strong recognition that the U.S.-China relationship is the most important bilateral relationship in the world. And both leaders are going to be very eager to show their publics and also the international community their ability to manage this relationship skillfully.I think that the agenda that Scott laid out is correct. I would – and you know, Bonny’s, I think, terms are spot on. I would just add that, you know, clearly one of the big differences from when this visit was first being discussed is Iran, and the fact that for the president Iran is obviously a, you know, tier-one issue that is absorbing a huge amount of his time. And I think it’s clear the administration believes that China can be helpful in encouraging Iran and pushing Iran to reach an agreement, agree to the terms which the U.S. believes are necessary.I think from China, while in many ways it has worked very hard to manage expectations on Iran, including most recently by having the Iranian foreign minister, Araghchi, there, and, you know, issuing a statement that it wants the straits reopen. My take is this is very much an effort to sort of deflect and manage potential pressure from the president. It’s a sign that they realize this is coming down the pike. I think that in looking at this it’s worth keeping in mind that China has a number of interests, as they always do. One is, very obviously, they have been spared some of the immediate impact by their very significant petroleum reserves, in particular, more broadly, ability to generate energy in a variety of different ways.But they’re still very worried about the impact of this in their economy, both directly, but also – and I think Bonny and her team put out a very good paper on this at the end of April – because the Chinese economy has become even more dependent on exports, And the exports are going to regions where – the export growth has been in regions that are being very affected by higher energy prices and also higher prices for things like fertilizer. So the Chinese are affected by the prospect of a global economic slowdown. So they’ve got interests on both sides. On one hand, they clearly don’t want the U.S. to be too successful. On the other hand, if the straits remain closed that has very significant implications. And I think they will also be keeping a very close eye on what the Gulf states are saying and doing, because that’s also a very important set of partners for them.I think that on the economy, the only thing I would add to what Scott said is just that it’s very clear that for the administration the economic issues were the most important ones, until the Iran conflict. And now Iran has become extremely important as well. But a huge amount of legwork has been done on the economic issues. I agree completely, it’s not completely clear what’s going to come out of it. But I think both sides want to show that they’re able to manage the economic relationship in a more stable and predictable way that gets benefits. I think the Chinese want stability and predictability. They’re willing to give some benefits in order to get that. And so we like the benefits and we’re willing to offer a little bit more on stability and predictability.In terms of the dynamics of the summit, I just think it’s important to recognize that the Chinese clearly want this summit. And they’ve been willing to be quite accommodating to the White House on rescheduling. They’ve been very clear that they want it. And I think that that reflects their sense that the best way to manage the relationship with the United States is to emphasize the strong personal relationship between Chairman Xi and President Trump. And China, as the second-largest country in the world, has a lot at stake in managing its relationship with the largest democracy and the third-largest country in the world. The U.S., obviously, wants it as well for the reasons that Scott and Bonny laid out.I think, as Bonny said, I just like to build on one more thing, which is the region is nervous about what’s going to come out of this. And, you know, all of the U.S. allies and partners are going to be watching very, very closely to see both the specifics of any documents that come out. And I would be very, very surprised if there’s a joint statement. There hasn’t been a joint statement in a long time. It’s very, very hard to do. And if I were advising the administration, I would say it’s probably not worth the effort. But I think there will probably be statements from both sides. Countries in the region, the broader Indo-Pacific as well as around the world, will be watching very closely. They’re going to be looking to see what issues are mentioned, what issues are not mentioned, the difference between how the Chinese talk about them, how we talk about them.And I think that that’s going to be, you know, something that’ll be very important. However, I would also stress that there’s a Goldilocks issue at stake, which is virtually every country in the region wants to avoid a really bad relationship between the U.S. and China. Nobody really thinks they have a stake in a really good relationship either. I mean, I think across the Indo-Pacific is my experience has been nobody wants a G-2. So what countries will be looking for is a sense that, you know, there isn’t conflict and that there’s some sort of positive ability to work together, but also that they’re not so close that all of a sudden it’s going to look like all the important decisions for the region are made in Beijing and Washington, rather than their own capitals.So, with that, let me pass it on to Phil.Ms. Chavez:  Great. Thank you, Edgard.And I’ll quickly mention that after Phil – sorry – we’ll turn to your questions. If you want to ask a question, just as a reminder, you can type it in the Q&A window or raise your hand to ask it verbally.So, without further ado, our last speaker is Philip Luck, director of the economics program and Scholl chair in international business at CSIS. Phil, go ahead.Philip Luck: Great. Thank you so much. And it’s a real honor to be rounding out this fantastic group. So I’ll speak a little bit about the economics of the relationship and where we are coming into this in terms of the issues between the countries, the potential leverage, or perceived leverage, and then potential discussion points. But I think the previous speakers did a much better job than I can discussing the third point.To the first, you know, just as a very broad overlay, you know, I think while I’m very happy that the leaders are meeting and they’ll have this discussion, there are some issues that we simply shouldn’t expect, that they discuss, that are very important. So some of the big structural issues – overcapacity in the PRC economy, the structural imbalances between their lack of consumption and our overconsumption, the broad discussion around the things we are acceptable to trade with one another. Outside of very narrow areas around the margins, I don’t expect those to be a large area of discussion. You know, as Scott and Edgard, and Bonny pointed out, I think the discussions will be much more focused on these sort of short-term/near-term issues and sort of just building that discussion going forward.In terms of – I mean, one thing that we’re seeing, and I think this is important for U.S. interests going forward, is, you know, not unsurprisingly we’re seeing both sides ratcheting up the pressure in very quiet ways in the lead up to the meeting. You know, on the PRC side, as Scott mentioned in terms of his beans and beef and other things, you know, there’s continued lack of demand, or the demand below what you’d normally expect, for U.S. agricultural products in the PRC. You know, they’ve certainly not fulfilled what Secretary Bessent suggested they had agreed to, which was sort of, you know, enormous purchases of U.S. soybeans. You know, beef exports are still much below expectations, same for sorghum and corn as well. And there’s increasing concern that investments in deepwater ports in Brazil makes it so that there really is a much better alternative to U.S. agriculture going forward in a serious way.So this will be – continue to be – I would expect this to continue to be a leverage point that the PRC uses. And in some sense, U.S. agricultural interests should sort of think of this as the norm going forward, you know, but for some broad change in the dynamic. On the U.S. side, we’re seeing reports that, you know, export licenses are being delayed in ways that are not, you know, headline news, but are clearly – or, from my perspective, you know, indicative of the administration trying to build a little bit of pressure on their side without, you know, jeopardizing the discussion overall. So, again, I think that’s, in some sense, you know, a positive change, relative to the, sort of, you know, Truth Social discussions we had in the past.Lastly, to the discussion of sort of what they will discuss, again, I think that’s going to be largely dependent on, sort of, these pressure points. As Scott mentioned, I think agricultural interests and market access will be very much on the table. And they’ll hope to be getting some progress there. And, again, as all three mentioned, I think Iran, of course, is a huge area of focus. The one thing I’ll note on Iran, which is just a broader economic perspective here. You know, I think while potentially the U.S. administration feels more acute political pressure to get a resolution, this is an enormous issue for both sides. And in some ways, economically, a larger issue for China. Not only just in terms of the direct access to fossil fuels through the strait, but the broader supply chain implications as well.You know, the United States is a relatively closed economy in a lot of ways. We are – our economy is driven by domestic demand and domestic consumption. That is not the case of the PRC economy. It is a throughput in supply chains and an exporter to the world. A global recession, which is certainly not off the table if this issue proceeds for much longer, would be really dramatic for the PRC economy while they’re having, you know, continued issues in the domestic economy through continued stress in the residential market. You know, they really are doubling down on external demand. And if that external demand wavers – because of both oil prices, throughput to enormous supplies in copper and aluminum, and other issues – this could be really, really dramatic for the Chinese economy.So I think on this issue, one silver lining is both sides really do have aligned incentives, which is not something that is often the case in this relationship. So I certainly hope that there’s some progress there as well.I’ll stop there. Thank you.Ms. Chavez: Thank you, Phil, and thank you to all of our speakers.Just a quick note that our colleague Steve Morrison, director of the CSIS Global Health Policy Center, had a conflict and could not join today’s call. But if you do have questions related to the health agenda of the summit, please don’t hesitate to reach out and we’ll connect you with an appropriate scholar.So, at this time, we’ll go ahead and open it up for questions. Just one last reminder, if you do want to ask a question, you can type it in the Q&A window or raise your hand to ask it verbally. And thank you to the folks that have already submitted questions.We got a few that are on – in a similar vein, so I’ll just combine them into one: How do you think the Iran war will come up in the presidential summit and what do you think will be achieved on that topic? And also, just a second part here, in what way does the war in Iran influence China’s ambitions and planning regarding Taiwan?So, I know Phil touched on this briefly. I’ll just open up to the group in case anyone has other thoughts.Mr. Kagan:  Sure. This is Edgard. I can go and then defer to people who probably know more than I.But I would say that it’s very important, very important because this is clearly top of mind for the president, and, you know, while a huge amount of the legwork that’s been done on the economic side and in fact much of the legwork really has been done by Secretary Bessent and his team and also Ambassador Greer and his team, the reality is that right now, Iran is critical for the U.S. and the Chinese know that.And as Phil just said and I agree with completely, the Chinese have a lot at stake. They’ve actually been fairly successful at trying to suggest that they’re more insulated; it doesn’t affect them directly.I don’t buy that, and I think that that’s one of the reasons why they have gone ahead with scheduling, that they’ve been relatively eager to do this, is because they know that they have a lot at stake.My sense is that, you know, the president is going to be asking them to use their leverage with Iran, to push Iran to accept an agreement which is acceptable to the U.S. This will include, I think, restrictions on their nuclear program and also reopening the Straits of Hormuz.I think the Chinese have made clear they’d like the Strait of Hormuz reopened and I think they’re going to be more reluctant to be seen siding with the U.S. At the same time, they’re also going to be reluctant to cross the president on something that’s so important to them, as well as something where they have quite a bit at stake.So I think that this is the wild card. I think that we’re going to see a lot of things happen in the next week. I would just say from my own experience working on U.S.-China visits and exchanges, often there’s an effort to sort of freeze the agenda two or three weeks out before a visit to try and minimize distractions.That’s clearly not going to be the case this time, and I think that’s going to be a real challenge for both sides as they try and finalize the prep and then actually move into the meetings.Thank you.Dr. Lin:  If I could jump in after Edgard here.So I very much agree with what Edgard says. I think the one point of, perhaps, common consensus or commonality between the United States and China is that both sides want the Strait of Hormuz to be open.But aside from that, as Edgard pointed out, I think the Chinese have a different position on Iran’s nuclear use. I think it’s important to point out that during the meeting between Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi and his Iranian counterpart Araghchi in Beijing, I think, yesterday, May 6th, China explicitly defended Iran’s right to develop civilian nuclear energy.And also in that meeting, China criticized threats of force and sanctions against Iran regarding its nuclear program. I think that’s a veiled threat saying that China at least publicly will oppose any additional U.S. use of force against Iran.So I think, as Edgard as well as Phil and others have mentioned, this is interesting that China is meeting with the Iranians before meeting with Trump. On one hand, it showcases support to Iran, that China is going to provide these – support to Iran prior to meeting with Trump. On the other hand, I think it also sets China up in a strong position before it talks to President Trump, understanding what Iran wants and what Iran is not willing to do.I think it’s also important to note that we did also see China’s Ministry of Commerce implement a blocking ban on U.S. sanctions against five Chinese firms that were importing Iranian oil. And one of the firms, Hengli, is a huge petrochemical firm within China.So we do see at least several instances in which China is showcasing that it wants to stand up against the United States and potentially to support Iran. But again, it will really depend on what is the nature of the meeting that President Trump has with Xi. And I think this is something that we’ll need to watch very closely because I don’t think China is going to agree to most of what or try to push for most of what the United States wants. But again, China does want the Strait of Hormuz opens.So, one other thing I will note is I’m not sure how much political cost or capital China is willing to take in order to actually get the Strait of Hormuz open. Traditionally, when China has sought to intervene or provide or help settle regional disputes, they’ve just been advocating for political and diplomatic negotiations, but they haven’t really bared too much cost. So, again, this might be, to Edgard’s point, a lot of talk, but not too much follow-on action.Dr. Kennedy: This is Scott Kennedy. I’ll just add to that. I don’t disagree with anything Bonny or Edgard has said.I would just note that as this is an issue because of the U.S. actions in the Persian Gulf and this is something that the U.S. needs from China to do, so a big question will be, what will the U.S. be willing to give China in return for potential support? I do agree that there’s also a relatively low ceiling on what China is likely to be able or interested in doing, but it will certainly want to horse trade across if the U.S. asks China for something specific.The other thing is I would agree that having been in China, yes, its economy is slow. Higher energy prices don’t help China in the short term. But longer term, the challenges of global oil markets and fossil fuels benefits China’s strength in renewables, in batteries, electric vehicles, wind, solar. And around the world governments, companies are all recognizing that they need to further their own energy transitions and China is well placed to exploit that trend.Ms. Chavez:  Great. Thank you all.One more question here from Chris Buckley for Bonny specifically: Can you explain more how Xi Jinping is likely to raise the issue of U.S. arms sales to Taiwan at the summit? And how is President Trump likely to respond? Is presidential approval of the next round of arms sales likely to be much delayed or is that unlikely given sentiment in Congress, etc.?Bonny, if you want to start, and then, of course, if anyone has thoughts as well.Dr. Lin: That’s a really good question. I don’t purport to be in Xi Jinping’s head, so I don’t exactly know how he will raise the issue. But he already raised it to President Trump February of this year during the phone call. So, I think it’s definitely an issue that will probably be raised again.I don’t know if he’s going to explicitly ask the United States to stop selling arms to Taiwan or if he’s going to use the language that he used in September, which is, I’m forgetting the exact term, but it was be more cautious in the arms sales, meaning less arms sales or where he doesn’t explicitly ask don’t provide the arms. That’s one way.You know, I think we could probably think that President Trump’s response will probably be quite similar to his response in February, in which he might say something like, we’ll wait and see. You know, I think President Trump understands that Taiwan is a very important issue for China. It’s an issue that an arms sales is absolutely critical for Taiwan’s defense, but also arms sales is quite beneficial to the U.S. economy and U.S. defense firms.So this is not something that I think President Trump will likely say no to. But President Trump probably will get a sense from the Chinese that if there is another large package in between some of the summits, that maybe it’s possible the Chinese could be more hesitant to meet again.But again, I think it’s important to remind folks that the United States provided a very large arms sales package in December 2025. And here we are five months, six months later, and President Trump is meeting with President Xi. So we don’t have a pattern in which a large U.S. arms sales to Taiwan during the second Trump term has prevented a meeting yet between the two leaders. So we’ll need – we’ll need to see how it plays out.Mr. Kagan:  And this is Edgard. I would just add, I mean, based on my own experience, having been involved in the preparation in some of the meetings going back to the late ’90s, this is very standard on the part of the Chinese. They always try and say before the meeting, like, oh, Taiwan is the critical issue. Taiwan’s the core issue. You have to be careful on Taiwan. So, you know, I would just – putting it in the historical context, I have no doubt that it will come up. I agree with Bonny. I think that they’re going to be very careful at having Chairman Xi asked for something that they know they’re not going to get. But I think that this is going to be part – put in the context of this, is critical for the overall U.S.-China relationship. And that is really the terms in which they’ve cast it, going back almost 30 years. So, you know, that I think there will be more same.And then in terms of specific asks, you know, they recognize that they have to be careful on this, in part because President Trump has a very, very clear understanding of leverage. And the way he performs in meetings, like, he definitely understands when there’s something that the other side wants. So I think that they’re going to be very careful on how they do this, but I think that they also want to create a broader framework where the U.S. has a stake in the relationship and therefore that creates some atmospheric pressure on how the U.S. behaves with regard to Taiwan. At least that’s their theory of the case. Over.Ms. Chavez:  Great. Thank you.We’re going to move on to a question specifically for Phil, and then if anyone has other thoughts: Do you anticipate the upcoming Trump-Xi summit touching on the future of the global payments landscape or trade settlement architecture? In particular, how are policymakers thinking about the growing push by some countries to diversify away from dollar-based settlement systems? And do you see trade settlement mechanisms becoming a more explicit agenda item?Phil, if you want to start on that one.Dr. Luck:  Yeah. It’s a fantastic question. Something we’ve been tracking quite closely. I defer to my colleagues who may know more on this. I don’t expect that to come up in this meeting. I would imagine this to be something that’s going to be coming up with – hopefully, with U.S. and close partners in sort of a parallel line of effort to the Chinese line of effort. This is becoming a much larger issue. I mean, the challenge that the U.S. has is that there’s quite a bit of difference in approaches across U.S. and allied economies. You know, Europe is a very different place than we are, as other economies are as well. You know, I think the Europeans have, really, in their 20th sanctions package, laid to bear just how big this challenge was, with their blocking of Russian crypto-backed stablecoins, and showing, just to be candid, how ineffective the entity-based approach that the U.S. has is for the scale and scope of the challenge.Again, China is taking a slightly different approach. You know, they’re trying to promote their structures, but in a slower and more centralized way, as you might expect. Again, I don’t expect that, but it’s a huge issue. I would imagine it to be something I would hope that would be coming into the G-7 presidency of the U.S. next year. But I don’t expect it in this. But, please, I would love to be corrected if others think otherwise.Dr. Kennedy:  This is Scott. I’ll just add I think Phil is actually quite right. I don’t expect this topic to be discussed by the two leaders, even though President Trump has, you know, said before that he’d be very much opposed to others shifting away from using the dollar, and China’s efforts to internationalize the renminbi and develop an alternative payment system, you know, goes against what he’s been suggesting. But I don’t think – I think that agenda is already too full and there’s really nothing that the U.S. can really suggest to the Chinese.I mean, the Chinese are most worried about potential financial sanctions, which this administration and previous ones have used against individual institutions and banks. And they want to reduce their vulnerability to those kinds of sanctions. And if the U.S. and China are going to escalate further down the road, the area of finance would be the be one potential area. That said, the efforts to internationalize the renminbi more broadly are highly restricted, because China is not willing to open its capital account. And I don’t see any signal from my time in Beijing that suggests that they have reconsidered that basic, fundamental decision.Ms. Chavez:  Great. Thank you, Phil and Scott.Another question for the group here, from Christian Wehrmann: What meaningful leverage would either side actually possess going into negotiations? Would Beijing enter talks believing Trump prioritizes economic wins over strategic competition?I’ll just open that up to the group for anyone’s thoughts.Mr. Kagan: So this is Edgard. I’ll start off, and, you know, then pass it off. I think that both sides believe that they have leverage. Both sides believe that the other side needs something from them. And, frankly, both sides are right. I think the Chinese clearly want this summit to happen. And I think that that’s something that I would just note is quite a change from what they were saying last fall, particularly before the Busan meeting. So I think that that’s a reflection of the fact that they see benefit to doing this, an advantage to doing this. At the same time, I think that, you know, they’re a little bit nervous because they know that direct engagement carries risks. So both sides, I think, have worked very hard to try and prepare an agenda.I think on the economic side, my understanding is that they’re – it’s not completely finalized, but it’s relatively close. And I think that both sides are relatively satisfied. I think the wild card is Iran. But even on that, I think that, you know, the U.S. clearly sees China as potentially helpful on Iran, and wants to try and use the visit to get pressure. I think that the Chinese are going to try and manage it in a way that requires them to give as little as possible, while also recognizing that they have significant interests in a resolution that reopens the Strait of Hormuz, and that allows them to simultaneously preserve their relationships with key Gulf countries as well as Iran.And I just noted that there was also a question about fentanyl. And I apologize for not having mentioned that earlier. I think fentanyl is important. It’s something that has a direct domestic impact. And, frankly, I think it’s one of the success stories of some of the U.S.-China cooperation going back a number of years, because there really does seem to have been a drop in fentanyl-related deaths in the U.S. that is almost certainly linked to it being harder to get precursors. So I think that they’re going to try and expand the cooperation. They’re going to try, first of all, to make sure that it continues. I think it’s worth keeping in mind that they want to try and make clear this is critical for the relationship, and it’s got to – improved cooperation has to continue. And then obviously they’ll be looking to try and expand it as well, because this is something that does touch on key domestic equities. Over.Dr. Kennedy:  This is Scott. I think there’s a question about the two sides’ relative power to each other – relative to each other, which involves both leverage and other forms of power. But more specific – but there’s narrow kinds of leverage. And what we’ve seen, I think it was mentioned by Edgard at the beginning, is both sides have been picking up some chips which they might be able to negotiate on during the meeting next week.We’ve seen the U.S. Commerce Department issue letters to several semiconductor tool companies to tell them to stop providing equipment to one Chinese semiconductor manufacturer, Hua Hong and their subsidiary Huali. There’s in Congress some draft bills, like the MATCH Act and others which might impose further restrictions on China. That’s obviously not the administration doing it, but those things wouldn’t move forward without the administration’s support.And then we see the Chinese actually getting more chips. They had a new rule that they issued early in April on supply chains, another rule on opposition to others using extraterritorial justifications for restrictions on China. Slowing, in some cases, rare earths licenses for exports. The blocking – restrict notice in response to the oil companies that Bonny mentioned. And then, blocking the Meta-Manus deal. So I’m not sure if – how either side will use these, but they’re a reminder to both that they – that each has enormous has enormous – has enormous leverage over the other, and managing their interdependence in a way that doesn’t lead to escalatory spirals is something that both sides will want to see if they can find a pathway through those issues.Dr. Lin: If I could jump in here, too, so I think from China’s perspective President Trump has been a pretty hard president for China to actually read and understand where – what exactly he wants. I think there is a general understanding that he does want some economic wins from this meeting.But I don’t think the Chinese necessarily believe the economic wins are completely different or separate from strategic competition, because if they have read how President Trump characterizes the U.S. National Security Strategy and National Defense Strategy, I think they recognize that U.S. economic strength is a key component of how President Trump thinks about U.S. strength overall and also vis-à-vis China, right?President Trump has characterized U.S.-China relations as China – as one of the United States’ most important economic competitors. I think also within China there is also still deep sense of suspicion about the United States.So even if they might assess that President Trump himself in the summit, which I don’t even know it’s correct or not, prioritizes economic wins, I don’t think the Chinese would believe that the United States is not set on still competition with China. And that makes for linking to one of the other questions in terms of what China might be able to trade for – for example, for Taiwan.I don’t think the Chinese are going into this meeting thinking that if they can give President Trump a significant investment deal that President Trump is going to trade Taiwan for that. I don’t – I hope they’re not that naive to think that President Trump will trade Taiwan and I don’t think President Trump will.So I think it’s really important to point out that, yes, President Trump did go in with some economic priorities, but as Edgard and others have mentioned, there are also Iran priorities, and then the Chinese also want to bring in Taiwan.But these three issues are not necessarily tradable. It’s not like, for example, if we have a really good trade – a really good trade or investment deal that Iran will – the Iran issue will somehow also be linked on to.I think these are three separate things, at least in the minds of the U.S., and they’re not really linked to each other.Ms. Chavez: Thank you, Bonny.I’m going to bring – Bonny touched on this a bit – but a question from David Smith for the group: How likely is it that President Trump will agree to weaken support for Taiwan in return for a deal?Mr. Kagan: I think Bonny’s kind of just addressed that.My view is that, you know, the president understands leverage. This is my experience of being in meetings with him. He has a very, very acute sense of how to use it and, you know, I think that Bonny is exactly right. I mean, these things aren’t completely fungible and interchangeable.There’s – and both countries have equities on both sides of each of these major issues. So I think that the president is going to want to signal an ability to work together with Xi Jinping and I think it’s just worth noting – I mean, I think – and many people have heard him say this and you’ve seen this in posts. You’ve heard him talk about this in a variety of fora.He has tremendous confidence in his personal relationship with Xi Jinping and that’s very much how he looks at international relationships, and, ultimately, a lot of it does come down to the dynamics between leaders.And so he’s going to want to show that he’s able to use that relationship to get tangible benefits for the United States. Obviously, Xi Jinping will want to do the same thing and show that he’s able to get tangible benefits for China.And I think it’s worth keeping in mind for China the prospect of greater stability, greater predictability in terms of particularly economic actions that the administration may be taking but also on things having to do with export controls, technology, and regional issues, those – that is valuable.And so I think that the idea that there’s going to be a trade where, you know, the president sort of sacrifices U.S. interests linked to Taiwan in order to get other things I think is unlikely, based on my own experience of how he operates.Ms. Chavez: Great, thank you.Another question here from Audrey Clark: Do you think 301 port fees or shipbuilding priorities will be discussed?Maybe for Phil and others.Dr. Luck: Yeah. I haven’t heard any indication that that’s on the table. You know, again, I think this is an issue that’s in some sense a little bit more thorny than they’ll be able to – or, I guess, at the very least I don’t imagine any sort of outcomes from a discussion like that. You know, again, relative to some of the – many of the issues I think they’re going to resolve in some sense are resolving issues that have arisen since January of 2025, right? This is – we’ve been in this sort of – and I’m glad that we’re out of the cycle of escalate to deescalate in large part, but we are still in the process of sort of unwinding some of that escalation.So I don’t see that, again, in part because I think the basic incentives and basic objectives of both sides are almost entirely cross purpose. But again, very much value being corrected by some of my colleagues.Dr. Kennedy: This is Scott. I would say if it does – if that kind of topic comes up, I think it comes up by the Chinese in the context of not just the port fees, but the U.S. actions to try and restrict Chinese management of ports elsewhere around the world, particularly in Panama. And I think the Chinese position that they would articulate would be these are market actors providing critical services for global trade and they are not potential coercive weapons of the Chinese state; and the U.S. should stop engaging in those kinds of acts, which will raise prices and hurt the global economy. I’m not saying I agree with the Chinese position at all, but I do think that’s the kind of statement that, if they touch upon this, that the Chinese would bring up.Mr. Kagan: This is Edgard. I agree completely with Scott and Phil.And I would just note there’s a practical issue. These meetings, first of all, the translation is almost always done consecutively because the Chinese tend to be more confident in that. Now, I don’t know for sure; they may do simultaneous. But if it’s consecutive, right away whatever the length of the meeting is, basically, halved.And you know, there is a – there is a recognition, like, you know, when you’re preparing for this stuff, every part of the administration, every part of the government – and this is true on the Chinese side as well – has their pet rock, that they want to get it. But the reality is that, you know, one of the things you got to do is you got to recognize who you’re writing for, and I don’t think either of these – certainly in the interactions I’ve seen with Xi Jinping, I’ve never seen him look closely at notes. The president certainly prides himself on not needing that. So I think that the idea that they’re going to get into this level of detail on issues that aren’t really at the center of the relationship, however important they are – and I don’t mean to suggest that these things don’t matter. But I think for both of them these are things that are derivative of the broader bilateral relationship, and so that’s what they’re going to focus on. So I would be very surprised.Now, the truth is you never know, and it could be that, you know, one of them raises this because they just heard something about it. But my instinct is that they’re going to focus on the broader relationship. And I think it’s worth keeping in mind there is the potential, at least – and I think it’s clear that both sides are very much assuming – there will be a number of further interactions. This is an interesting year because China’s hosting APEC and the U.S. is hosting the G-20, and there’s also talk about a possible visit – a return visit by Chairman Xi. So I think all – they’re going to be working in this. They’re going to be working at the broader relationship, at figuring out what they can do together and laying down markers about what red lines are, but also, you know, trying to make sure that they’re establishing the framework that will then allow, I think, more detailed discussions like these to take place.Over.Ms. Chavez: Great. Thank you.And we’re just about to hit time here in a couple minutes, so I think we have time for just one more question. I’ll say in advance sorry to those whose questions we couldn’t get to. Of course, feel free to reach out via email at [email protected] or phone and we’ll be happy to connect you with one of our experts.So just one last question to round it out for the group: What is the biggest difference in the Trump administration’s approach to China between the first term and the second? And are the Chinese approaching President Trump differently?I’ll open that last one up to the group.Dr. Kennedy: Yeah. This is Scott Kennedy. I guess my view is that during the first Trump administration – I’ll focus mostly on economics – is the administration has relatively limited ambitions on economics in the first term. And it basically was trying to use unilateral pressure, via the Section 301 investigation, to impose tariffs on China to get it to increase purchases of American products and engage in regulatory reform. So instead of using patient diplomacy and the WTO to get China to align its policies with its international commitments, using unilateral pressure to do so. This time, in the second Trump administration, the U.S. is not using pressure to get China to align with that international system. It has tried to break down that system, replace it with an alternative, and then engage with – use pressure against China to get it to basically engage in managed trade, and limit the relationship in areas the U.S. finds threatening. So much more ambitious approach by the second Trump administration than the first.I think the Chinese response is even more dramatically different, in that they in the first administration really just tried to keep things relatively calm and respond with proportional reactions to U.S. pressure, but never escalate and really try to keep things calm. This time, once the U.S. announced its liberation day tariffs, the Chinese responded forcefully and really pushed back to try and achieve escalatory dominance. Which they did achieve. And that’s the position we’re in right now. And so China has really felt, in some ways, itself liberated by its now willingness to use its economic tools in ways that it wouldn’t have done so during – that it didn’t do so during the first Trump administration.Dr. Luck: This is Phil Luck – oh, sorry. Real quick. Sorry. This is Phil Luck.So just I completely agree with everything Scott just said, just adding an extra layer of, sort of, what the further implications of those changes have been. To Scott’s point, I mean, whether you want to think of it as escalation dominance or just something else, you know, the administration has clearly been chastened, to some degree, with China’s utilization of its control over critical minerals, specifically rare earths. That has obviously spurred a lot of momentum on DOD, and State, and others, but that’s – those are all long-term things. So the United States has to deal with this in the short term or medium term. That also has reverberated to other allies. I mean, one thing we haven’t really noted is, you know, our European allies, our Japanese allies, and many others, are also very curiously watching this because they’re very curious how this will evolve, this relationship, over the next year.The other thing I would note is, because in the first term, you know, the Trump administration really fairly narrowly targeted the PRC – it raised tariffs, you know, by the end of the term by about – to about $400 billion worth of goods. And while there were steel and aluminum tariffs on other countries, relatively low. And, you know, at the beginning of this administration they, of course, ratcheted tariffs up along China. But because of the sort of escalation and deescalation pattern, one thing we really have now is we have tariff rates that are not very dissimilar on China versus our other close allies. So the average rate now in May of 2026 is about 30 percent on China. It’s 20 percent on Korea, 19 percent on Japan, and, you know, down the line.That’s a way different – you know, tariff rates on China we’re about double that of our allies about this time last year. So this escalation/deescalation pattern, and the way both sides have sort of adjusted their behavior, has not just had impacts on the bilateral relationship but has had impacts on sort of how other countries are being treated by and interacting in this global system as well. Sorry, Bonny. Over to you.Dr. Lin: No worries. Thank you. I’ll just jump in very quickly on the security side. So we see actually quite a bit of similarity between what’s happening now and the first Trump administration, whereas when it comes to China there’s an emphasis on greater dialogue on the military-to-military side. And there’s also, at the same time, continued significant efforts from the Pentagon to deepen our capabilities, our exercises, our cooperation across the board with U.S. allies and partners in the Indo-Pacific.I think it’s important to note that, despite the fact that President Trump is going to China and there’s lots of pomp and display associated with this visit, the United States is quietly doing significant work with some of our closest partners in the region, in many ways making lots of – or, carving out new areas of cooperation and making significant – (inaudible) – that we didn’t see before. So that’s another area of continuity between what we’re seeing now and the first Trump administration.I will point out one thing, and I hope Edgard can also jump in here, I think China’s approach towards the Trump administration, or towards the U.S. in general, has changed. It’s been, you know, almost 10 years since when President Trump was first elected. And China has become, from their perspective, more powerful and more confident. So we have a China much more willing to push back against the United States now, because of that growing capability, than, say, 10 years ago. But, Edgard, over to you to add more to that.Mr. Kagan: Yeah, look, I agree with the with what’s been laid out, but with a couple of caveats. I think the first is at the beginning of the second Trump administration I think the Chinese expected a continuation of the first, which, in their mind, became more and more aggressive against China over time. And so I think that one of the things that, in my view, has been very interesting – I think you saw this in the run up to the Busan meetings and you’ve seen it now, and then at the meetings following that – is I think the Chinese are starting to believe that there’s least a prospect that the president is less hardline against China than they had originally feared. And that there’s room to work with him in ways that I don’t think they necessarily believed would be the case, particularly in the first six months of last year.The other thing I think it’s worth highlighting is that one of the reasons why China is able to be more assertive vis-à-vis the U.S. is the reaction and the results of steps that were taken in the first administration. However one feels about it, the reality is there’s a lot fewer exports from China to the U.S., at least directly. And China has become less dependent on the U.S. economically than it was before. You can disagree about what the final demand is and other things, and I think those are very legitimate disagreements, but there’s no question that China has more flexibility. And also, China has been working very hard to develop tools.So I think that from their perspective they are intrigued at the possibility that this administration is less hardline or hawkish than they had initially expected. And I think that one of the things that you will be able to judge, and I’m certainly looking for, is the degree of warmth that the visit shows, and the degree to which they come out of it thinking that there is an ability to work somewhat productively with the U.S. on a variety of issues. So my sense is they’re actually more confident, yes, but they also are more hopeful that perhaps the president is more willing to work with them than they had initially believed. And I think that’s part of what’s underlying what they’re doing right now. Over.Ms. Chavez: I think we’ve just reached time here. So I want to thank our speakers and all of our participants for joining this timely call. Please don’t hesitate to reach out if we can be a resource on anything else. We’re here to help. And. as I mentioned at the top of the call, we will have a transcript distributed to all of you, and it will also be made available on our website at CSIS.org. So, with that, I hope everyone has a great rest of the day, and thanks for joining us.(END.)\nPrograms & Projects\nTrump-Xi 2026 Summits","content_cn":"新闻简报：特朗普—习近平峰会前瞻\n\n文字记录\n— 2026年5月7日\n\n可下载内容\n下载文字记录\n290kb\n\n本文字记录来自于2026年5月7日举办的CSIS新闻简报会。\n\n图片\n照片：Thomas Peter-Pool/Getty Images\n\n新闻简报：特朗普—习近平峰会前瞻\n2026年5月7日 • 美国东部时间上午10:00–11:00\n\nSofia Chavez：大家好，欢迎参加本次CSIS新闻简报会。本次简报会将前瞻特朗普总统与习近平即将举行的峰会。我是Sofia Chavez，是CSIS这里的媒体关系经理。今天与我一起的还有CSIS一批优秀的学者，他们将分享对这次备受期待的峰会的预期和看法，包括在峰会前我们应关注什么、峰会结束后我们可以期待什么，以及更多内容。\n\n开始之前，先简单说明几项事务安排。我们的每位发言人将先进行几分钟的开场发言，之后我们会转入提问环节。如果你想提问，请务必在问答窗口中输入问题，或者你可以举手口头提问。我们也会在今天的通话结束后不久分发本次通话的文字记录。文字记录也将在CSIS.org上提供。\n\n那么，我们现在开始。我首先请CSIS中国商业与经济高级顾问、受托人主席Scott Kennedy发言。请开始，Scott。\n\nScott Kennedy：谢谢你，Sofia。大家早上好。欢迎参加本次简报会。我现在在帕洛阿尔托，是昨天刚到这里的，此前我在北京待了两个半星期，期间与许多中国官员、企业以及外国政府讨论了美中关系和中国经济的状况。因此，我的发言将反映其中一些经历。\n\n我想谈三点。第一，峰会推迟六周实际上很重要，因为双方能够进一步制定议程和细节，为特朗普总统和习近平总统下周会面时双方将讨论的内容做准备。我把这些概括为五个B和三个T。五个B是美国所强调的：波音（Boeing）、牛肉（beef）、豆类（beans）、贸易委员会（Board of Trade）和投资委员会（Board of Investment）。三个T则是中国所强调的。第一个T当然是台湾（Taiwan）。此外还有关税（tariffs）和技术（technology）。我还要再加上一项，看起来它的重要性将高于六周前原本的预期，那就是人工智能以及对人工智能相关风险的管理。所以我认为，尽管会谈时间相对较短，我们实际上将有一份相当充实的议程。\n\n我想说的第二点是，仍有一些重大问题要等到双方真正会面时才能得到答案。那就是，他们将能完成我刚才描述的议程中的多少内容？因为同样地，他们没有太多时间，而且目前还不清楚他们是否已在这些事项中的任何一项上达成共同的落脚点。第二个问题是，双方是否会发表联合声明，还是会遵循过去的做法，分别发表声明。在过去几年双方举行的许多会晤中，都没有文件、没有书面记录，人们希望这次会晤能够留下这样的记录。因此，这一点值得关注。\n\n最后一个关于将发生什么的问题是：特朗普总统在与习近平总统会面之外还会做什么？他会访问哪些美国公司？他会访问哪些\n大学？听起来他不会做这些事情中的任何一件。看起来可能会去天坛，也可能会去长城。我认为，这表明总统访问中国时能够做什么，视野相对受限。我想说的最后一点是，中国和习近平是在一个比美国强得多的位置上进入这次会晤的。中国有一些希望实现的目标——延长停火，减少对半导体进口的技术限制，以及降低关税。但即使这些事情都没有取得多少成果，只要会晤没有爆发冲突，而且特朗普总统不在离开后寻求重新升级局势，中国基本上都会变得更强。过去一年，中国相对于美国的地位有所改善，这得益于其对“解放日”关税的反击，以及过去一年中成功多次促使特朗普总统和政府退让。此外，由于IEEPA相关裁决，以及最高法院裁决导致美国关税下调，再加上美国如今在中东陷入的种种困境，所有这些都改善了中国的相对地位。\n\n此外，很明显，美国并没有推动对中国的产业政策、国家与社会的作用，或其他重大问题进行重大调整，而且也没有与盟友和理念相近的国家一道这样做。美国实际上只是在推动自身利益，同时也没有充分投资于其经济实力的基础。\n\n我们大幅削减了——你知道，对科学和技术各个领域的资金进行了大规模削减，而竞争最终将在这些领域决出胜负。因此我认为，只要事态轨迹继续像过去一年那样发展，中国从这次会晤中相对而言会更强，而美国能够宣称取得了一些进展，但实际上，中国有理由相信自己在双边关系中表现得非常好。就说到这里。\n\n查韦斯女士：很好。谢谢你，斯科特。\n\n接下来是中国力量项目主任、CSIS高级顾问Bonny Lin。Bonny，请开始发言。\n\nBonny Lin：谢谢，索菲娅。今天很高兴来到这里。\n\n斯科特提到他刚从中国回来。Edgard和我刚从台湾和日本回来，我想利用这里有限的时间，讨论台湾可能如何影响特朗普与习近平的会晤。\n\n正如斯科特所说，除了试图延长贸易休战、寻求美中关系总体稳定之外，台湾可能是中国优先事项中最受关注的问题。我们知道，中国一段时间以来一直在发出信号，表明台湾是特朗普总统访华期间以及他与中国领导人习近平会晤时将讨论的核心问题，我们也看到中国为这场讨论做了相当多的准备。\n\n因此，我将介绍其中一些准备工作，以及我认为会晤期间可能提出或暗示的内容。\n\n正如许多人所知，在2026年2月特朗普总统与习近平通话期间，习近平强调台湾是美中关系中最重要的问题，并含蓄地敦促美国不要向台湾提供武器。我认为，通话后的说明只是说了一些——并没有明确说不要提供武器，但很明显，这是他向特朗普总统施压的方向。\n\n随后，上个月在总统\n特朗普推迟了访华行程，以专注于伊朗战争；中国为台湾最大反对党领导人、中国国民党（KMT）主席郑丽文访华亮了绿灯，她于4月初访问了中国。在访问期间，她明显偏离了国民党一贯的立场，几乎回应了北京想听到的一切。她支持中国对“九二共识”的看法。她表示反对台独。她表示反对外国干涉两岸问题，这通常是对军售及美国对台湾其他支持的代称。她呼应了习近平关于台湾海峡两岸同属中华民族、是一家人的论点。换句话说，郑丽文的观点与台北、与台湾政府的观点大相径庭，而且现在仍然大相径庭；台湾政府不得不面对中国持续升级的军事、政治和经济胁迫与威胁这一现实。她的观点也与其所在政党国民党内部的许多看法非常不同，国民党内许多人对郑丽文的立场持谨慎态度，认为她没有认真对待中华人民共和国的威胁。但她的访问意味着，在特朗普与习近平会面前夕，北京在台湾问题上处于非常有利的地位；而今天，在中国外交部的新闻发布会上，外交部发言人回答的第一个问题，当然是由中国媒体提出的，问题非常明确地指出，美国必须履行其对中国在台湾问题上的国际义务，而这些义务是“中美关系稳定、健康和可持续发展的必要前提”。换句话说，大约就在特朗普与习近平会面前一周左右，中国发出了非常明确的信号：处理好台湾问题，对于斯科特所谈到的中美总体关系中的其他一切事务都将至关重要。因此，我认为这使习近平处于有利位置，他可能会对特朗普总统说：你看，台湾有一个主要政党的领导人，我们可以合作，她也希望与中国合作。美国要做的只是约束台北，不再与那些分裂分子合作。因此，在这一背景下，我认为习近平可能会提出几件事。他可能会含蓄地暗示，也许特朗普总统应该改变美国反对台独的表述。美国通常的表述是，美国不支持台独。因此，从“不支持”改为“反对”相当关键。另一种可能是暗示，也许特朗普总统可能想改变美国支持与台湾和平统一的表述。过去，美国通常表示，美国支持和平解决台湾问题，也反对任何一方单方面改变现状。习近平可能再次暗示，或者也许会非常明确地表示，美国不应向台湾提供军售。我还没有看到此前会晤的任何报道显示，她曾就台湾总统赖清德过境美国问题，特别向特朗普总统提出过这一议题。但中国很可能也会利用这次与特朗普总统的会晤，以及可能的后续会晤，利用再举行三次额外的\n今年两位领导人之间的会晤，以明确表明允许台湾总统赖清德过境美国并不符合美国利益。仅作背景说明，除新冠疫情期间外，在过去近二十年里，台湾总统通常每年过境美国一到两次。赖总统在2025年没有过境美国，目前还要看今年的外交日程中是否有他的过境安排。我要指出的是，特朗普总统目前在会晤前对台湾的立场——当然，我们还有一周时间——如果台湾能够通过一项特别防务预算，可能会得到大幅加强。我们听说有传言称该预算今天可能在台北通过，但看来并未发生。还有最后一点，在我结束之前。与台湾问题相关的是，其他亲密的美国盟友和伙伴将如何在特朗普与习近平的会晤中被讨论，或者可能被省略；以及特朗普总统和他的团队是否可能在他访问中国前后立即与美国的关键盟友接触，例如通过电话。我认为，大多数美国盟友和伙伴都在密切关注这些动态，并将受益于更加稳定的美中关系。但与此同时，他们中的许多人，尤其是日本，仍然是中国不断加剧胁迫的目标。我们现在确实看到中国国内的反日言论正在升温，习近平甚至可能向特朗普总统提出他所认为的日本军事化问题。所以，我就说到这里。谢谢你，索菲娅。\n\nChavez女士：谢谢你，邦妮。\n\n接下来是埃德加德·卡根，CSIS中国研究高级顾问兼弗里曼主席。埃德加德，请开始。\n\n埃德加德·D·卡根：大家好。非常感谢各位参加此次电话会议。我还要说，索菲娅称我是学者，让我感到非常受宠若惊。我仍在从外交官成长为学者的路上，因此感谢有机会与大家交流。\n\n我很难再补充太多斯科特和邦妮已经阐述的内容。我只想说，这次访问对两位领导人都至关重要。在两国，人们都强烈认识到，美中关系是世界上最重要的双边关系。两位领导人都非常希望向本国公众以及国际社会展示他们有能力娴熟地处理这一关系。\n\n我认为，斯科特列出的议程是正确的。而且，你知道，我认为邦妮的措辞也非常准确。我只想补充一点，显然，与最初讨论这次访问时相比，最大差异之一就是伊朗，以及对总统而言，伊朗显然是一个一级问题，占用了他大量时间。我认为很明显，本届政府相信中国能够在鼓励伊朗、推动伊朗达成协议、同意美国认为必要的条款方面发挥作用。\n\n从中国方面看，尽管它在许多方面都非常努力地管理外界对伊朗问题的预期，包括最近让伊朗外长阿拉格齐到访，并发表声明称希望海峡重新开放，但我的看法是，这在很大程度上是为了转移和管理来自总统的潜在压力。这表明他们意识到这件事即将到来。我认为，在审视这一点时，值得牢记的是，中国有一个\n诸多利益一如既往。其中一个非常明显的利益是，他们凭借非常可观的石油储备，尤其是更广泛地说，凭借以各种不同方式生产能源的能力，避免了一些直接冲击。但他们仍然非常担心这对其经济的影响，既有直接影响，也有——我认为邦妮和她的团队在4月底就此发表了一篇非常好的论文——因为中国经济变得更加依赖出口，而出口将流向那些受到能源价格上涨以及化肥等商品价格上涨严重影响的地区。因此，中国人受到全球经济放缓前景的影响。所以他们在双方都有利益。一方面，他们显然不希望美国过于成功。另一方面，如果海峡继续关闭，那将产生非常重大的影响。我认为，他们还会密切关注海湾国家的言论和行动，因为那也是对他们而言非常重要的一组伙伴。\n\n我认为，在经济问题上，我唯一想补充斯科特所说的是，很明显，对本届政府而言，经济问题一直是最重要的问题，直到伊朗冲突发生。现在伊朗也变得极其重要。但在经济问题上已经做了大量基础工作。我完全同意，目前还不完全清楚会取得什么结果。但我认为，双方都希望表明，他们能够以更加稳定、可预测且能够带来收益的方式管理经济关系。我认为，中国人希望稳定和可预测性。为了获得这些，他们愿意提供一些利益。因此，我们喜欢这些利益，也愿意在稳定性和可预测性方面多做一点让步。\n\n就峰会的动态而言，我只是认为，必须认识到，中国人显然希望举行这次峰会。而且，在重新安排时间方面，他们一直愿意对美国白宫相当配合。他们已经非常明确地表示希望举行峰会。我认为，这反映出他们的判断：管理对美关系的最佳方式，是强调习近平主席与特朗普总统之间牢固的个人关系。中国作为世界第二大国家，在处理与世界上最大民主国家、也是世界第三大国家美国的关系方面，有很大的利益攸关。出于斯科特和邦妮所阐述的原因，美国显然也希望举行峰会。\n\n我认为，正如邦妮所说，我只想再补充一点，那就是该地区对这次峰会将产生什么结果感到紧张。美国所有盟友和伙伴都将非常、非常密切地关注，看看最终发布的任何文件具体写了什么。如果有联合声明，我会感到非常、非常意外。已经很久没有联合声明了。这非常难以做到。如果让我给本届政府提供建议，我会说，这可能不值得投入精力。但我认为，双方可能都会发表声明。该地区国家，包括更广泛的印太地区以及世界各地的国家，都将密切关注。他们会寻找其中提到了哪些问题、没有提到哪些问题，以及中方谈论这些问题的方式与我们的谈论方式之间的差异。\n\n而且我认为，\n这将会是非常重要的一件事。不过，我还要强调，眼下存在一个“金发姑娘”问题，也就是说，实际上该地区几乎每个国家都希望避免美中关系变得非常糟糕。但没有哪个国家真的认为自己会从非常良好的美中关系中获益。我的意思是，根据我在印度洋—太平洋地区的经验，没有哪个国家希望出现“G-2”格局。因此，各国希望看到的是：双方不存在冲突，并且具备某种积极合作的能力；但双方也不能过于接近，以至于突然看起来该地区所有重要决定都是在北京和华盛顿作出的，而不是在它们各自的首都作出的。所以，有了这些，我把话题交给Phil。Ms. Chavez：太好了。谢谢你，Edgard。我还要简短说明一下，Phil发言之后——抱歉——我们会转入提问环节。如果你想提问，提醒一下，你可以在问答窗口中输入问题，或者举手进行口头提问。那么，不再耽搁，我们最后一位发言者是Philip Luck，他是CSIS经济项目主任兼Scholl国际商务讲席。Phil，请开始。Philip Luck：太好了。非常感谢。能够为这个出色的团队作最后总结，我深感荣幸。我会谈一谈两国关系的经济层面，以及在两国议题、潜在或被认为存在的杠杆作用和可能的讨论点方面，我们目前正处于什么位置。不过，我认为前面的发言者在第三点上的讨论比我能做得好得多。首先，作为一个非常宽泛的概述，我很高兴两国领导人能够会面并进行讨论，但有些问题我们不应指望他们会讨论，尽管这些问题非常重要。因此，一些重大的结构性问题——中华人民共和国经济中的产能过剩、两国之间的结构性失衡，即中国消费不足与美国过度消费之间的失衡，以及围绕哪些领域适合双方开展贸易的广泛讨论——除了一些非常狭窄的边缘领域外，我不认为这些会成为讨论的重点。正如Scott、Edgard和Bonny所指出的，我认为讨论将更加集中于这类短期或近期问题，以及仅仅是推动未来进一步开展这种讨论。就我的意思而言，我们看到的一件事，而且我认为这对美国今后的利益很重要，就是毫不意外地，在会晤前夕，双方都在以非常低调的方式逐步加大压力。在中华人民共和国一方，正如Scott提到的大豆、牛肉和其他产品一样，中国对美国农产品的需求持续不足，或者说低于通常预期的水平。当然，他们并没有履行Bessent部长所说双方已经达成的协议，也就是大规模购买美国大豆。牛肉出口仍远低于预期，高粱和玉米也是如此。而且，人们越来越担心，巴西对深水港的投资意味着，今后在相当程度上，美国农业确实会面临一个更好的替代来源。因此，我预计这将继续成为中华人民共和国所利用的一个杠杆点。而在某种意义上，美国农业\n利益相关方应该把这视为今后的一种常态，明白吗，除非动态出现某种广泛变化。在美国方面，我们看到有报道称，出口许可证正以一些并非头条新闻所报道的方式被拖延，但显然——或者从我的角度看——这表明政府试图在自己这边施加一点压力，同时又不危及整体讨论。所以，我认为，相对于我们过去在 Truth Social 上进行的那类讨论，这在某种意义上是一个积极的变化。\n\n最后，谈到他们将讨论什么，我认为，这在很大程度上将取决于这些压力点。正如斯科特提到的，我认为农业利益和市场准入将会是非常重要的议题。他们也希望在那里取得一些进展。同样，正如三位发言者都提到的，我认为伊朗当然是一个重点领域。关于伊朗，我要指出的一点，是从更广泛的经济角度来看。你知道，尽管美国政府可能感受到更为紧迫的政治压力，需要取得解决方案，但这对双方都是一个极其重大的问题。在某些方面，从经济角度看，这对中国是一个更大的问题。不仅仅是因为通过海峡直接获得化石燃料，还因为更广泛的供应链影响。\n\n你知道，美国在很多方面都是一个相对封闭的经济体。我们的经济由国内需求和国内消费驱动。但中华人民共和国经济并非如此。它是供应链中的一个中转环节，也是面向世界的出口方。如果这一问题持续更长时间，全球经济衰退当然并非不可能发生，那么这将对中华人民共和国经济造成极其剧烈的冲击；与此同时，中国国内经济还在持续承受住宅市场不断加剧的压力。你知道，他们确实在进一步依赖外部需求。如果外部需求减弱——无论是因为油价、铜和铝等大量供应的流通，还是其他问题——这都可能对中国经济造成非常、非常剧烈的冲击。\n\n所以，我认为，在这个问题上，一个可取之处是，双方确实拥有一致的利益，这在这种关系中并不常见。因此，我当然也希望那里能够取得一些进展。\n\n我就说到这里。谢谢。\n\n查韦斯女士：谢谢你，菲尔，也感谢我们所有的发言者。\n\n简单说明一下，我们的同事史蒂夫·莫里森是 CSIS 全球卫生政策中心主任，他临时有事，无法参加今天的电话会议。不过，如果你们有与峰会卫生议程相关的问题，请不要犹豫，随时联系我们，我们会为你们联系合适的学者。\n\n那么现在，我们开始提问。最后再提醒一次，如果你想提问，可以在问答窗口中输入问题，或者举手口头提问。也感谢已经提交问题的各位。\n\n我们收到的几个问题比较相近，所以我把它们合并成一个：你们认为伊朗战争将如何在总统峰会上被提及？你们认为在这个议题上将取得什么成果？另外，还有第二部分：伊朗战争以何种方式影响中国围绕台湾的抱负和规划？\n\n我知道菲尔刚才对此作了简要提及。我现在就把这个问题交给各位，\n以防有人还有其他想法。卡根先生：当然。我是埃德加德。我可以先说，然后再请教那些可能比我了解得更多的人。\n\n但我要说，这非常重要，非常重要，因为这显然是总统当前最关心的事情。而且，正如你们所知道的，经济方面已经完成的大量准备工作，事实上其中很大一部分确实是由贝森特部长及其团队，以及格里大使及其团队完成的；现实情况是，此刻伊朗对美国至关重要，而中国人知道这一点。\n\n正如菲尔刚才所说的，我完全同意，中国有很多利益攸关。他们实际上相当成功地试图让人觉得自己受到的隔离更多；这不会直接影响他们。\n\n我不相信这一点，而且我认为，这也是他们继续安排会晤、相对积极地推动此事的原因之一，因为他们知道自己有很多利益攸关。\n\n我的感觉是，总统将要求他们运用对伊朗的影响力，推动伊朗接受一项对美国而言可以接受的协议。我认为，这将包括限制其核计划，以及重新开放霍尔木兹海峡。\n\n我认为，中国已经明确表示希望霍尔木兹海峡重新开放，而我认为，他们会更不愿意被看作是在支持美国。同时，对于一件对他们如此重要、且他们自身也有相当多利益攸关的事情，他们也会不愿意违逆总统。\n\n所以我认为，这就是一个变数。我认为，未来一周我们将看到很多事情发生。我只想根据自己参与美中访问和交流工作的经验说，通常在访问前两三周，双方会设法冻结议程，以尽量减少干扰。\n\n这次显然不会如此，而我认为，当双方试图敲定准备工作、随后真正进入会谈时，这将是一个真正的挑战。\n\n谢谢。\n\n林博士：如果可以的话，我想接着埃德加德的话说。\n\n我非常同意埃德加德所说的。我认为，美国和中国之间或许存在的一个共同共识或共同点是，双方都希望霍尔木兹海峡保持开放。\n\n但除此之外，正如埃德加德指出的，我认为中国在伊朗核能利用问题上的立场不同。我认为有必要指出，在中国外交部长王毅与伊朗外长阿拉格齐在北京举行会晤时——我想是在昨天，5月6日——中国明确维护了伊朗发展民用核能的权利。\n\n而且，在那次会晤中，中国批评了针对伊朗核计划的武力威胁和制裁。我认为，这是一种含蓄的威胁，表明中国至少在公开场合将反对美国进一步对伊朗使用武力。\n\n所以我认为，正如埃德加德、菲尔以及其他人所提到的，中国在与特朗普会面之前先与伊朗人会面，这很有意思。一方面，这展示了对伊朗的支持，即中国将在与特朗普会面前向伊朗提供这些——对伊朗的支持。另一方面，我认为，这也使中国在与特朗普总统交谈前处于有利地位，因为它了解伊朗想要什么，以及伊朗不愿意做什么。\n\n我认为还需要注意的是，我们也确实看到中国商务部实施了一项阻止禁令\n关于美国制裁五家进口伊朗石油的中国企业。其中一家企业恒力，是中国境内一家大型石化企业。因此，我们确实看到至少有几起事例表明，中国正在展示其希望对抗美国，并有可能支持伊朗。但话说回来，这将真正取决于特朗普总统与习近平会面的性质。我认为，这是我们需要密切关注的事情，因为我不认为中国会同意美国想要的大部分内容，或试图推动美国想要的大部分内容。但话说回来，中国确实希望霍尔木兹海峡开放。\n\n所以，我还要指出一点：我不确定中国为了真正让霍尔木兹海峡开放，愿意承担多少政治代价，或动用多少政治资本。传统上，当中国寻求介入或提供帮助以解决地区争端时，他们只是倡导政治和外交谈判，但并没有真正承担太多代价。所以，回到埃德加德所说的，这可能只是很多口头表态，但后续行动并不会太多。\n\n肯尼迪博士：我是斯科特·肯尼迪。我只想对此补充一点。我不反对邦妮或埃德加德所说的任何内容。\n\n我只是想指出，由于这是一个由美国在波斯湾的行动引发的问题，也是美国需要中国去做某些事情的问题，因此一个重大问题将是：美国愿意给予中国什么，来换取其潜在的支持？我同意，中国可能能够或有兴趣采取的行动也存在相对较低的上限，但如果美国向中国提出具体要求，中国当然会希望进行交易。\n\n另一点是，我也同意，既然我去过中国，是的，中国经济正在放缓。能源价格上涨在短期内无助于中国。但从更长期来看，全球石油市场和化石燃料面临的挑战，有利于中国在可再生能源、电池、电动汽车、风能和太阳能方面的实力。在世界各地，各国政府和企业都认识到，他们需要进一步推进自身的能源转型，而中国已经做好了利用这一趋势的准备。\n\n查韦斯女士：很好。谢谢大家。\n\n这里还有一个问题，来自克里斯·巴克利，专门问邦妮：你能否进一步解释，习近平可能会如何在峰会上提出美国对台军售问题？特朗普总统可能会如何回应？鉴于国会中的情绪等因素，总统批准下一轮军售是否可能被大幅推迟，还是不太可能？\n\n邦妮，如果你愿意先开始，当然，如果其他人也有想法，欢迎补充。\n\n林博士：这是一个非常好的问题。我不敢说自己能洞悉习近平的想法，所以我确切不知道他会如何提出这个问题。但今年2月，他已经在与特朗普总统的通话中提出过此事。因此，我认为这绝对是一个可能会再次被提出的问题。\n\n我不知道他是否会明确要求美国停止向台湾出售武器，还是会使用他9月份所用的措辞。我记不清确切的说法了，但大意是，在军售问题上要更加谨慎，也就是说减少军售，或者不明确要求停止提供武器。这是一种方式。\n\n你知道，我认为我们大概可以推测，特朗普总统的回应可能会与他2月份的回应非常相似，他可能会说类似这样的话：我们等着看。你知道，我认为总统\n特朗普明白台湾对中国是一个非常重要的问题。这是一个问题，即军售对台湾的防务绝对至关重要，但军售也对美国经济和美国防务企业相当有利。因此，我认为这不是特朗普总统可能会拒绝的事情。但特朗普总统可能会从中方那里感受到，如果在某些峰会之间再进行一笔大规模军售，那么中方或许会更犹豫是否再次会面。但我还是认为，重要的是要提醒大家，美国在2025年12月提供了一笔非常大规模的军售。而现在五六个月过去了，特朗普总统正在与习近平主席会面。因此，我们还没有形成这样的模式：在特朗普第二任期内，美国对台大规模军售已经阻止了两国领导人之间的会晤。所以我们需要——我们需要看看事情会如何发展。\n\n卡根先生：这是埃德加德。我只想补充一点，我根据自己的经历——从20世纪90年代末开始参与筹备一些会晤——这对中方而言非常标准。他们总是在会晤前试图这样说：哦，台湾是关键问题。台湾是核心问题。你们在台湾问题上必须谨慎。所以，我只是想把它放在历史背景下来看：我毫不怀疑这个问题会被提及。我同意邦妮的看法。我认为，他们会非常谨慎，不会让习近平主席提出他们明知得不到的要求。但我认为，这将成为其中一部分——放在这一背景下，即这对美中整体关系至关重要。这确实是他们近30年来一直赋予它的条件。所以，我认为还会有更多相同的内容。\n\n至于具体要求，他们认识到必须对此保持谨慎，部分原因是特朗普总统对杠杆有着非常、非常清晰的理解。而且，他在会晤中的表现方式是：他绝对能察觉对方想要什么。因此，我认为他们会非常谨慎地处理这一问题，但我也认为，他们希望建立一个更广泛的框架，让美国在这段关系中拥有利益，从而对美国如何对待台湾形成某种氛围压力。至少他们的理论是这样。完毕。\n\n查韦斯女士：很好。谢谢。\n\n我们接下来要提一个专门给菲尔的问题，如果其他人还有想法也请提出：你预计即将举行的特朗普—习近平峰会会涉及全球支付格局或贸易结算架构的未来吗？特别是，政策制定者如何看待一些国家日益推动摆脱以美元为基础的结算体系？你是否认为贸易结算机制会成为议程中更明确的一项？\n\n菲尔，如果你愿意，可以先回答这个问题。\n\n卢克博士：好的。这是一个非常棒的问题。我们一直在密切跟踪。我把这个问题留给可能更了解情况的同事。我不认为这次会晤会涉及这个问题。我想，这应该是美国与亲密伙伴共同推进的事项，希望能够与中方的努力形成某种平行的工作线。这个问题正在变得重要得多。我的意思是，美国面临的挑战在于，美国内部在做法上存在相当大的差异。\n以及盟国经济体。你知道，欧洲与我们是非常不同的地方，其他经济体也是如此。我认为，欧洲人在其第20轮制裁方案中，通过阻止俄罗斯加密货币支持的稳定币，真正揭示了这一挑战有多么严峻，也坦率地表明，美国针对实体的做法对于这一挑战的规模和范围是多么无效。再次强调，中国采取了略有不同的方法。你知道，他们试图推广自身的体系，但正如你所预期的那样，速度更慢、更加集中化。再次强调，我并不指望会这样，但这是一个巨大的问题。我想，这应该是我希望在明年美国担任七国集团主席国期间能够提上议程的事情。但我不指望这次会讨论。可是，如果其他人有不同看法，我很乐意被纠正。\n\n肯尼迪博士：我是斯科特。我只想补充一点，我认为菲尔实际上说得很对。我不认为两国领导人会讨论这个话题，尽管特朗普总统此前曾表示，他非常反对其他国家减少使用美元，而中国推动人民币国际化并发展替代性支付体系的努力，显然与他的主张背道而驰。但我不认为——我认为议程已经排得太满，美国确实没有什么可以向中国提出的。\n\n我的意思是，中国人最担心的是潜在的金融制裁，而本届政府及历届政府都曾对个别机构和银行实施过这类制裁。他们希望降低自身对这类制裁的脆弱性。如果美国和中国未来进一步升级对抗，金融领域可能会成为一个潜在领域。话虽如此，更广泛地推动人民币国际化的努力受到高度限制，因为中国不愿开放资本账户。在我任职北京期间，我没有看到任何信号表明他们重新考虑了这一基本且根本性的决定。\n\n查韦斯女士：很好。谢谢你们，菲尔和斯科特。\n\n这里还有一个问题要请大家回答，来自克里斯蒂安·韦尔曼：双方在进入谈判时，实际上各自拥有怎样的有意义的筹码？北京会不会带着这样的判断进入谈判：特朗普优先考虑经济成果，而不是战略竞争？\n\n我把这个问题交给大家，欢迎任何人发表看法。\n\n卡根先生：我是埃德加德。我先开始，然后再交给其他人。我认为，双方都相信自己拥有筹码。双方都认为对方需要从自己这里得到一些东西。坦率地说，双方都是对的。我认为，中方显然希望这次峰会举行。我想特别指出，这与他们去年秋天所说的情况相比是一个相当大的变化，尤其是在釜山会晤之前。因此，我认为，这反映出他们认为举行峰会有益、有利可图。同时，我认为，他们也有些紧张，因为他们知道直接接触会带来风险。所以我认为，双方都非常努力地试图准备一份议程。\n\n我对经济方面的理解是，他们——虽然还没有完全敲定，但已经比较接近了。我认为双方总体上都比较满意。我认为，变数在于伊朗。但即便如此，我认为，美国显然认为中国可能在伊朗问题上发挥帮助作用，并希望中国能够\n并试图利用这次访问施加压力。我认为，中国人会试图以一种要求他们尽可能少作出让步的方式来处理，同时也认识到，他们在达成一个能够重新开放霍尔木兹海峡的解决方案方面有重大利益，而且这也能让他们同时维持与海湾主要国家以及伊朗的关系。\n\n我刚刚注意到，还有一个关于芬太尼的问题。很抱歉之前没有提到这一点。我认为芬太尼很重要。这是一个会直接影响国内的问题。坦率地说，我认为这是美中多年来一些合作的成功案例之一，因为美国与芬太尼有关的死亡人数似乎确实有所下降，而这几乎可以肯定与前体物质变得更难获取有关。所以我认为，他们会试图扩大合作。首先，他们会努力确保合作继续下去。我认为值得记住的是，他们希望明确表示，这对两国关系至关重要，而改善合作必须继续。然后，显然他们也会寻求扩大合作，因为这是确实涉及国内关键利益的事情。以上。\n\nDr. Kennedy：我是 Scott。我想，有一个问题涉及双方彼此相对的实力——彼此之间的相对实力，其中既包括杠杆，也包括其他形式的权力。但更具体地说——存在一些较为狭窄的杠杆。我们看到的情况，我想 Edgard 在开始时提到过，就是双方都在拿到一些筹码，可能能够在下周的会晤期间就这些筹码进行谈判。\n\n我们看到，美国商务部向几家半导体设备公司发函，要求它们停止向一家中国半导体制造商华虹及其子公司华力提供设备。国会中还有一些法案草案，例如 MATCH Act 以及其他法案，可能会对中国施加进一步限制。这显然不是政府在做，但如果没有政府的支持，这些措施不会推进。\n\n然后我们看到，中国实际上也在获得更多筹码。他们在四月初发布了一项关于供应链的新规定，另有一项规定反对其他国家以域外管辖为理由对中国实施限制。在某些情况下，他们放缓了稀土出口许可证的发放。还有针对 Bonny 提到的石油公司的封锁限制通知。然后，阻止 Meta-Manus 交易。所以我不确定——双方会如何利用这些筹码，但这些事情提醒双方：彼此对对方都拥有巨大——巨大——巨大的杠杆，而以一种不会导致升级螺旋的方式管理相互依存关系，是双方都希望看看能否找到一条途径解决这些问题的事情。\n\nDr. Lin：如果可以的话，我也想插一句。我认为，从中国的角度来看，President Trump 是一位让中国很难真正解读、很难理解他究竟想要什么的总统。我认为，大家普遍理解的是，他确实希望从这次会晤中取得一些经济上的胜利。\n\n但我不认为中国人一定相信，这些经济上的胜利与战略竞争完全不同或彼此分离，因为如果他们读过 President Trump 如何描述美国《国家安全战略》和《国防\n战略方面，我认为他们认识到，美国经济实力是特朗普总统如何看待美国整体实力以及美国相对于中国的实力的关键组成部分，对吧？特朗普总统将美中关系描述为——中国是美国最重要的经济竞争对手之一。我认为，中国国内对美国也仍然存在很深的疑虑。\n\n因此，即使他们可能评估认为，特朗普总统本人在峰会上——我甚至不知道这种判断是否正确——优先考虑经济成果，我不认为中国人会相信美国不打算继续与中国竞争。这也关联到其他问题之一，即中国可能能够用什么来交换——例如，用什么来交换台湾。\n\n我不认为中国人参加这次会议时会认为，如果他们能给特朗普总统一项重大投资协议，特朗普总统就会拿台湾来交换。我不——我希望他们不会天真到认为特朗普总统会拿台湾来交换，我也不认为特朗普总统会这么做。\n\n所以我认为，必须指出的是，是的，特朗普总统确实带着一些经济优先事项参加了会谈，但正如埃德加德和其他人所提到的，还有伊朗方面的优先事项，而中国人也希望把台湾问题带进来。\n\n但这三个问题未必可以交易。比如说，如果我们达成了一项非常好的贸易——一项非常好的贸易或投资协议，并不意味着伊朗问题也会以某种方式被附带联系起来。\n\n我认为，至少在美国人看来，这是三个彼此独立的问题，它们并没有真正相互关联。\n\n查韦斯女士：谢谢你，邦妮。我想把——邦妮刚才已经谈到了一点——戴维·史密斯向小组提出的一个问题带进来：特朗普总统会在多大程度上同意削弱对台湾的支持，以换取一项协议？\n\n卡根先生：我认为邦妮刚才已经基本回答了这个问题。我的看法是，你知道，总统理解杠杆。这是我与他一起参加会议时的经验。他对如何使用杠杆有非常、非常敏锐的感觉，而且，我认为邦妮完全正确。我的意思是，这些事情并不是完全可以互换、等价交换的。\n\n双方在这些重大问题中的每一个问题上都有各自的利益。因此，我认为总统会希望发出信号，表明他有能力与习近平合作；我认为值得指出的是——我的意思是，我想很多人都听他这样说过，也在他的帖子中看到过这一点。你们也听到他在各种场合谈论过这一点。\n\n他对自己与习近平的个人关系抱有极大的信心，而他看待国际关系的方式也正是如此；归根结底，其中很大一部分确实取决于领导人之间的互动。\n\n所以，他会希望展示自己能够利用这种关系，为美国争取到切实的利益。显然，习近平也会希望做同样的事情，展示自己能够为中国争取到切实的利益。\n\n我认为，对中国来说，前景中的更大稳定性和更强可预测性——尤其是在政府可能采取的经济行动方面，但也包括出口管制、技术和地区问题——这些都是有价值的。\n\n因此，我认为，所谓交易，即总统在与台湾相关的问题上牺牲美国利益，\n为了获得其他东西，我认为这是不太可能的，这是基于我自己对他行事方式的经验判断。\n\nChavez女士：太好了，谢谢。\n\n这里还有Audrey Clark的问题：你们认为301条款港口费用或造船优先事项会被讨论吗？也许请Phil和其他人回答。\n\nLuck博士：是的。我没有听到任何迹象表明这件事在议程上。你知道，再说一次，我认为这在某种意义上是一个更加棘手的问题，他们可能无法——或者，我想，至少我不认为那样的讨论会产生任何结果。你知道，再说一次，相对于一些——我认为他们将在某种意义上解决的许多问题，都是在解决自2025年1月以来出现的问题，对吧？这就是——我们一直处于这种状态，而我很高兴我们在很大程度上已经摆脱了“升级再降级”的循环，但我们仍在逐步消解其中一些升级。\n\n所以我再次不认为会讨论这个问题，部分原因是我认为双方的基本激励和基本目标几乎完全背道而驰。不过，我也非常重视我的一些同事对我进行纠正。\n\nKennedy博士：我是Scott。我想说，如果这个问题——如果这类话题出现，我认为它会由中方提出，背景不仅是港口费用，还有美国试图限制中国在世界其他地方管理港口的行动，尤其是在巴拿马。我认为中方会阐述的立场是：这些是为全球贸易提供关键服务的市场参与者，并不是中国国家可能用来胁迫的武器；美国应该停止从事这类行为，因为这些行为会抬高价格并损害全球经济。我完全不是说我同意中方的立场，但我确实认为，如果他们触及这个问题，中方会提出类似这样的说法。\n\nKagan先生：我是Edgard。我完全同意Scott和Phil的看法。\n\n我还想指出一个实际问题。首先，这些会议几乎总是采用交替传译，因为中国人往往对此更有信心。现在，我不能确定；他们也可能采用同声传译。但如果是交替传译，那么无论会议时长是多少，基本上都会立刻减半。\n\n而且，你知道，人们认识到，当你在准备这些事情时，政府的每个部门、行政机构的每个部分——中方也是如此——都有自己的“心头石”，他们想把它放进去。但现实是，你知道，你必须认识到你是在为谁写东西，而据我所见，在我与习近平的互动中，我从未见过他仔细查看笔记。总统当然以不需要笔记为傲。所以我认为，他们不可能就那些并非真正处于双边关系核心的问题展开如此细致的讨论，无论这些问题多么重要——我不是说这些事情无关紧要。但我认为，对他们双方来说，这些都是更广泛双边关系的派生问题，因此他们会关注更广泛的关系。所以我会非常惊讶。\n\n不过，事实是你永远无法确定，也可能其中一方只是刚听到有关某事的消息，于是提出了这个问题。但我的直觉是，他们会聚焦于更广泛的关系。而我\n我认为值得牢记的是，至少存在这种可能性——而且我认为很明显，双方都非常确信——还会有多次进一步互动。今年很有意思，因为中国将主办亚太经合组织（APEC）会议，而美国将主办二十国集团（G-20）峰会，同时也有人谈到一次可能的访问——习近平主席的回访。因此，我认为，他们都将围绕这些问题开展工作。他们将致力于更广泛的双边关系，弄清楚双方能够共同做些什么，并就哪些是红线划定界限；但同时，你知道，他们也会努力确保建立起一个框架，从而使我认为像这样的更具体讨论能够展开。完毕。\n\nChavez女士：太好了。谢谢。\n\n我们再过几分钟就到时间了，所以我想我们只来得及再回答一个问题。对于那些我们没能回答的问题，我先向你们表示抱歉。当然，欢迎通过电子邮件[email protected]或电话联系我们，我们很乐意为你们联系一位专家。\n\n最后一个问题，请大家共同回答：特朗普政府在第一任期和第二任期处理中美关系的方式，最大的不同是什么？中方是否以不同方式对待特朗普总统？我把最后一个问题交给各位。\n\n肯尼迪博士：好的。我是斯科特·肯尼迪。我想，我的看法是，在特朗普第一届政府时期——我主要谈经济问题——政府在第一任期内对经济问题的抱负相对有限。基本上，它试图通过301条款调查施加单边压力，对中国征收关税，以促使中国增加购买美国商品并进行监管改革。因此，它没有采用耐心外交和通过世界贸易组织（WTO）促使中国使其政策与国际承诺保持一致，而是采取单边压力来实现这一目标。这一次，在特朗普第二届政府时期，美国并不是利用压力促使中国与那个国际体系保持一致。它试图瓦解该体系，以一种替代体系取而代之，然后与中国接触——利用对华压力，基本上迫使中国参与有管理的贸易，并限制美国认为具有威胁性的领域中的双边关系。因此，特朗普第二届政府采取的方式比第一届政府更具雄心。\n\n我认为，中方的回应甚至有更加显著的不同，因为在第一届政府时期，中方实际上只是试图让局势保持相对平静，并对美国压力作出相称的反应，但从不升级局势，真正努力维持平静。这一次，在美国宣布“解放日”关税后，中国作出了强硬回应，真正进行了反击，试图取得升级主导权。它确实做到了。我们现在所处的就是这种局面。因此，在某些方面，中国确实感到自己获得了解放，因为如今它愿意以一些在特朗普第一届政府时期不会采取——也确实没有采取——的方式使用经济工具。\n\nLuck博士：我是菲尔·勒克——哦，抱歉。很快补充一下。我完全同意斯科特刚才所说的一切，只是想再增加一层，谈谈这些变化进一步带来的影响。正如斯科特所说，我的意思是，无论你想把它称为升级主导权，还是其他什么，你知道，本届政府显然在某种程度上已经受到了教训，\n以及中国利用其对关键矿产资源，特别是稀土的控制。这显然推动了国防部、国务院以及其他部门采取大量行动，但这些——这些都是长期事项。因此，美国必须在短期或中期内应对这一问题。这也波及了其他盟友。我的意思是，有一件我们还没有真正提到的事，那就是我们的欧洲盟友、日本盟友以及许多其他国家，也都在非常密切地关注这一点，因为他们非常想知道，未来一年这种关系将如何发展。\n\n我还想指出另一点：因为在第一届任期内，你知道，特朗普政府对中华人民共和国的针对性确实相当狭窄——它提高了关税，到任期结束时，大约涉及价值4000亿美元的商品。虽然对其他国家也征收了钢铁和铝关税，但相对较低。而且，你知道，在本届政府开始时，他们当然提高了对华关税。但由于这种升级与降级交替的模式，我们现在真正拥有的一点是，对中国的关税税率与对其他亲密盟友的税率并没有太大差异。因此，2026年5月，目前对华平均税率约为30%。对韩国是20%，对日本是19%，依此类推。\n\n这与以往大不相同——你知道，大约在去年这个时候，对华关税税率约为我们盟友的两倍。因此，这种升级/降级模式，以及双方调整自身行为的方式，不仅对双边关系产生了影响，也影响了其他国家在这一全球体系中受到对待以及与之互动的方式。抱歉，邦妮。请你继续。\n\n林博士：没关系，谢谢。我只想非常快地从安全层面补充一点。我们实际上看到当前发生的事情与特朗普第一届政府时期有不少相似之处；在涉及中国时，双方都强调加强军事层面的对话。同时，五角大楼仍在持续大力推进，在印度洋—太平洋地区全面深化我们与美国盟友和伙伴的能力建设、演习及合作。\n\n我认为需要注意的是，尽管特朗普总统要访问中国，而且这次访问伴随着诸多仪式和展示，但美国正悄然与该地区一些最亲密的伙伴开展重要工作，在许多方面开辟大量新的合作领域，并取得重大——（听不清）——这些都是我们以前没有看到的。因此，这是我们目前看到的情况与特朗普第一届政府之间的另一个连续性领域。\n\n我想指出一点，也希望埃德加德能够在这里补充：我认为，中国对特朗普政府，或者总体上对美国的态度，已经发生了变化。自特朗普总统首次当选以来，已经将近10年了。从中国的角度看，中国已经变得更加强大、更有信心。因此，现在中国更愿意对美国进行反制，凭借其不断增长的能力，相比之下，10年前并不是这样。但是，埃德加德，请你继续补充。 \n\n卡根先生：是的，请看，我同意刚才所阐述的观点，但有几点需要说明。我认为，首先是在特朗普第二届政府开始时，中国方面预计会\n前者在他们看来，随着时间推移，变得越来越针对中国、越来越强硬。因此我认为，在我看来，非常有意思的一点是——我想你们在釜山会议前夕看到了这一点，现在也看到了这一点，之后的会议上同样如此——我认为，中国人开始相信，至少存在这样一种可能：总统对中国的强硬程度低于他们最初担心的程度。而且，有空间以一些方式与他合作，我认为他们起初并不一定相信会有这样的可能，尤其是在去年上半年的头六个月里。\n\n我认为值得强调的另一点是，中国之所以能够在对美关系上表现得更加强势，原因之一在于第一届政府时期所采取措施的反应和结果。无论人们对此有何看法，现实是，中国对美国的出口至少在直接层面上已经少了很多。而且，与之前相比，中国在经济上对美国的依赖已经降低。你可以不同意最终需求是什么，以及其他一些问题；我认为这些都是非常合理的分歧，但毫无疑问，中国拥有了更大的灵活性。此外，中国一直在非常努力地开发各种工具。\n\n所以我认为，从他们的角度来看，这届政府不像他们最初预期的那样强硬或鹰派，这种可能性令他们感到兴趣。我认为，你们将能够据此判断，而我当然也在关注的一点，是这次访问所展现出的热情程度，以及他们在访问结束后在多大程度上认为，能够在各种问题上与美国开展一定程度的富有成效的合作。因此我的感觉是，他们确实更加自信了，是的，但他们也更加希望，总统或许比他们最初认为的更愿意与他们合作。我认为，这正是他们目前所作所为背后的部分原因。以上。\n\n查韦斯女士：我想我们现在已经到了时间。所以我要感谢各位发言人和所有参与者参加这次及时的电话会谈。如果我们还能在其他方面提供帮助，请随时联系我们。我们随时愿意提供协助。正如我在电话会谈开始时提到的，我们会向大家分发文字记录，也会将其发布在我们的网站 CSIS.org 上。好了，希望大家在今天剩下的时间里一切顺利，感谢大家参加。（结束。）\n\n项目与计划\n特朗普—习近平2026年峰会","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-05-07T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3389","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"原子计划2024：双同等对手环境下的战内威慑","title_en":"Project Atom 2024: Intra-War Deterrence in a Two-Peer Environment","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心核问题项目发布报告，邀请专家针对俄罗斯与中国近乎同时使用战场核武器的情景，制定竞争性应对策略，聚焦战略目标、盟友保证、军事回应和非动能回应四大主题。报告显示专家在威慑常规侵略和非动能回应上存在共识，但在盟友保证的优先性、核与常规回应选择及“胜利”定义上存在分歧。","summary_en":"CSIS's Project on Nuclear Issues released a report inviting experts to develop competing strategies for a scenario involving near-simultaneous battlefield nuclear use by Russia and China, focusing on strategic objectives, allied assurance, military responses, and non-kinetic responses. The strategies show agreement on deterring conventional aggression but disagreement on allied assurance priorities, nuclear versus conventional responses, and the meaning of winning.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:15:53.951270+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/project-atom-2024-intra-war-deterrence-two-peer-environment","content":"Project Atom 2024: Intra-War Deterrence in a Two-Peer Environment\nPhoto: Flashmovie via Adobe Stock\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nReport\nby\nHeather Williams,\nReja Younis,\nLachlan MacKenzie,\nChristopher A. Ford,\nRebecca Davis Gibbons,\nAnkit Panda,\nMelanie W. Sisson,\nand\nGregory Weaver\nPublished November 18, 2024\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Full Report\n1713kb\nThere is a growing risk that the United States and its allies could face scenarios in which one or more adversaries might resort to nuclear weapons use in a regional conflict. In response to these growing risks, U.S. decisionmakers are revisiting the concept of intra-war deterrence, which is about influencing enemy actions during an ongoing conflict. The risks of deterrence failure have been a focal point in the testimony of recent U.S. Strategic Command (STRATCOM) commanders, including General Anthony Cotton, who said, “We must be ready if deterrence fails” in testimony in February 2024.To assist in this thinking and to develop actionable insights for the U.S. policy and strategy communities, the Project on Nuclear Issues (PONI) invited a group of experts to develop competing strategies for responding to strategic deterrence failure. The project’s current contributors were each asked to respond to a scenario involving near-simultaneous battlefield nuclear use by Russia and China. The strategies focused on four specific themes: strategic objectives, assurance to allies, military responses, and non-kinetic responses. The strategies demonstrate agreement on key issues, such as the importance of deterring conventional aggression and the relevance of non-kinetic responses to adversary nuclear use. But the strategies also highlight important areas of disagreement about the relative importance and feasibility of assuring allies, at least relative to other strategic objectives; the advisability of a nuclear versus conventional response to deterrence failure; and what “winning” in a strategic deterrence failure scenario would look like.This publication was made possible by generous support from the Defense Threat Reduction Agency.\nThis report is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nNuclear Issues,\nDefense and Security, and\nDefense Strategy and Capabilities\nImage\nHeather Williams\nDirector, Project on Nuclear Issues and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nReja Younis\nAssociate Fellow, Project on Nuclear Issues\nImage\nLachlan MacKenzie\nAssociate Fellow, Project on Nuclear Issues\nChristopher A. Ford\nProfessor of International Relations and Strategic Studies, Graduate School of Defense and Strategic Studies, Missouri State University\nImage\nRebecca Davis Gibbons\nSenior Associate (Non-resident), Project on Nuclear Issues\nAnkit Panda\nStanton Senior Fellow, Nuclear Policy Program, Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\nMelanie W. Sisson\nFellow, Strobe Talbott Center for Security, Strategy, and Technology, Foreign Policy Program\nImage\nGregory Weaver\nSenior Associate (Non-resident), Project on Nuclear Issues\nPrograms & Projects\nProject on Nuclear Issues\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"原子项目2024：双对等对手环境中的战时威慑\n图片：Flashmovie，来自Adobe Stock\n目录\n目录\n报告\n作者\nHeather Williams、\nReja Younis、\nLachlan MacKenzie、\nChristopher A. Ford、\nRebecca Davis Gibbons、\nAnkit Panda、\nMelanie W. Sisson\n和\nGregory Weaver\n发布于2024年11月18日\n可下载内容\n下载完整报告\n1713kb\n美国及其盟友面临这样一种情景的风险正在上升：一个或多个对手可能会在地区冲突中诉诸使用核武器。为应对这些日益增长的风险，美国决策者正重新审视战时威慑这一概念，其核心是在持续冲突期间影响敌方行动。近期美国战略司令部（STRATCOM）司令的证词聚焦于威慑失败风险，其中包括Anthony Cotton将军；他在2024年2月的证词中表示：“如果威慑失败，我们必须做好准备。”为协助这一思考并为美国政策和战略界形成可操作的见解，核问题项目（PONI）邀请了一组专家制定应对战略威慑失败的相互竞争战略。该项目的现有撰稿人均被要求回应一个涉及俄罗斯和中国近乎同时在战场使用核武器的情景。这些战略聚焦于四个具体主题：战略目标、对盟友的保证、军事回应以及非动能回应。这些战略表明，在关键问题上存在共识，例如威慑常规侵略的重要性，以及对手使用核武器后非动能回应的相关性。但这些战略也凸显了若干重要分歧：相对于其他战略目标而言，向盟友提供保证的重要性和可行性；以核手段还是常规手段回应威慑失败是否可取；以及在战略威慑失败情景中，“获胜”将呈现何种样貌。本出版物得益于国防威胁降低局的慷慨支持。\n本报告由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策议题的私人、免税机构。其研究不偏袒任何党派，也不具有专有性。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n标签\n核问题、\n国防与安全，以及\n国防战略与能力\n图片\nHeather Williams\n核问题项目主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员\n图片\nReja Younis\n核问题项目副研究员\n图片\nLachlan MacKenzie\n核问题项目副研究员\nChristopher A. Ford\n密苏里州立大学国防与战略研究研究生院国际关系与战略研究教授\n图片\nRebecca Davis Gibbons\n核问题项目高级副研究员（非驻地）\nAnkit Panda\n卡内基国际和平基金会核政策项目Stanton高级研究员\nMelanie W. Sisson\n外交政策项目Strobe Talbott安全、战略与技术中心研究员\n图片\nGregory Weaver\n核问题项目高级副研究员（非驻地）\n项目与计划\n核问题项目\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-11-18T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2272","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"量子传感与未来战争：保持竞争力的五项关键改革","title_en":"Quantum Sensing and the Future of Warfare: Five Essential Reforms to Stay Competitive","summary_cn":"该CSIS评论文章指出，量子传感技术可能颠覆潜艇、隐形战机和导弹等依赖隐身生存的平台，改变常规与核威慑格局。文章认为美国在量子传感领域投资分散、缺乏统一国家战略，中国和俄罗斯正推进相关军事应用。作者提出五项改革建议：设立联合量子技术转化办公室、实施针对性产业政策保障关键矿产与制造基础、加强实验与实战化测试、推动集成与反量子作战、培养量子科技人才。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary argues that quantum sensing could undermine the survivability of submarines, stealth aircraft, and missiles, reshaping conventional and nuclear deterrence. It contends that U.S. investments in quantum sensing are fragmented and lack a coherent national strategy, while China and Russia are advancing military applications. The authors propose five reforms: establishing a Joint Quantum Technologies Transition Office, pursuing industrial policy for critical minerals and manufacturing, prioritizing experimentation and ruggedization, integrating quantum sensing and counter-quantum operations, and cultivating quantum talent.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:10:26.432624+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/quantum-sensing-and-future-warfare-five-essential-reforms-stay-competitive","content":"Quantum Sensing and the Future of Warfare: Five Essential Reforms to Stay Competitive\nPhoto: da-kuk/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nJahara Matisek,\nKatrina Schweiker,\nand\nMorgan Bazilian\nPublished October 9, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nQuantum sensing is primed for a breakout that will radically change both conventional and nuclear warfare, requiring essential reforms for the Department of Defense (recently renamed to the Department of War) to maintain a competitive advantage. Submarines, stealth bombers, and advanced fifth-generation fighters form the backbone of the United States’ deterrent posture through their survivability and ability to penetrate adversary defenses undetected. Quantum sensing technologies detect atomic-scale interactions in gravity, magnetism, and light, which could nullify weapon systems that rely on stealth and invisibility. Just as radar transformed the air war over Europe in World War II, so too will quantum sensing alter air and undersea warfare.Unfortunately, the United States faces this challenge with fragmented investments and no coherent vision. Researchers in the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, services, national labs, academia, and industry are chasing breakthroughs without a national strategy or unifying technical vision, making it harder to advance quantum technology for both commercial products and military weapon systems. The Department of War should create a coherent vision and plausible path to achievement that includes a willingness to take risks on mid- to long-term technologies, so that even modest efforts, such as quantum sensing trials in space, will not continue to struggle for funding and traction. Meanwhile, China tests seabed sensors to track submarines, and Russia is building quantum navigation to counter electronic warfare.The first country that operationalizes quantum technologies for defense applications will eliminate the comparative advantage of submarines and stealth aircraft, reshaping nuclear deterrence and conventional warfighting alike. This is not science fiction. Prototypes are already being tested, defense ministries are funding scaled programs, and alliances are placing quantum development high on their priority lists. As first-generation quantum sensing systems edge toward military use, we must consider: What happens if China gets there first, and U.S. submarines and stealth aircraft lose their invisibility?Quantum’s Military PromiseQuantum sensing turns exquisitely small physical effects into useful signals. Quantum magnetometers can map minute changes in Earth’s magnetic field to potentially enable the tracking and targeting of an undersea target, such as a submarine. Quantum gravimeters can spot density anomalies underground and around the seabed to reveal tunnels, shafts, or large objects. Cold-atom inertial sensors and advanced clocks could keep platforms on course in GPS-denied environments for days rather than hours. Quantum-enhanced optical techniques can improve detection and geolocation capabilities without electromagnetic emissions. These sensing technologies have massive military implications.Operational value comes from pairing classical sensors and quantum technology. Hybrid stacks, such as quantum plus acoustic, electromagnetic, or optical inputs, filtered by machine learning, can find the signal amid the noise, reduce false alarms, and convert faint signals into targeting-quality tracks. This is already visible in first-look trials: The Royal Navy tested quantum magnetometers for small, submerged objects; China experimented with drone-mounted quantum magnetometers to track undersea movements from the air; U.S. scientists explored GPS-free navigation kits based on atom interferometry; and British researchers demonstrated gravity sensors to detect voids and tunnels. These developments contribute to the concept of Integrated Sensing and Communications as an organizing frame: distribute sensors, move timing and processing closer to the edge, and fuse outputs across platforms so a single weak quantum return becomes militarily useful when combined with traditional data streams.The near-term payoff is not a science-fiction-style radar that “kills stealth” in a single leap. It is better cueing, more resilient navigation, and more persistent wide-area search, especially in the undersea and littorals, where many military systems are vulnerable to saturation or deception. Quantum must be treated as a force multiplier for what the joint force already does: find, fix, and shoot, while staying oriented in highly contested domains with a jammed and saturated electromagnetic spectrum.The Defense Intelligence Assessment 2025 Worldwide Threat Assessment notes China and Russia are expanding quantum communication networks while investing in quantum magnetometers, gravimeters, and inertial navigation systems. These investments are developing operational tools to find, track, and target once invisible weapon systems. Whether or not these investments prove decisive, the trend is clear: adversaries seek to shrink the uncontested maneuver space for U.S. submarines and stealth aircraft, a cornerstone of extended deterrence.More Quantum, More ProblemsWhile advances in quantum technology are showing promise, there are still many challenges to be overcome before those advantages can be realized on the battlefield. These challenges do not negate quantum’s military value but serve as a reminder of the contested nature of current quantum terrain. Every revolutionary technology, from radar to GPS to stealth, matured through complex engineering phases before transforming combat. Quantum sensing is now in that phase: fragile but advancing quickly, with each iteration bringing it closer to rugged, deployable systems. Countries investing and experimenting the most will own the decisive edge in the post-stealth battlespace.Extreme sensitivity is both an advantage and a liability. Sensors that can detect the faintest magnetic or gravitational changes also absorb vibration, thermal drift, electromagnetic clutter, and the noise of their own host platforms. On moving steel hulls and fast jets, background interference overwhelms weak signals. Magnetometers throw spurious tracks near power systems, gravimeters lose stability in turbulence, and inertial packages drift without constant recalibration.This fragility has slowed the transition from laboratory to the battlefield. Devices that perform well in controlled conditions will malfunction when exposed to the motion, temperature swings, and background noise of real-world military operations. Size, weight, power, and cost constraints add another layer of difficulty, as few systems can meet the rugged demands of combat.Other oft-touted applications face similar limits. “Unhackable” quantum communications remain vulnerable to hardware exploits and require major infrastructure investments in repeaters and memory. Claims that quantum radar will “expose advanced stealth aircraft and missiles” or be a “submarine killer” should be treated with skepticism, because it will have limited range and be susceptible to photon loss, atmospheric interference, and noise. More likely, quantum radar will evolve into hybrid systems, paired with conventional radar and signal processing, to improve detection in certain niche environments rather than revolutionize air defense outright.The gap between promise and practice is that quantum devices can detect what traditional sensors miss, but most prototypes are too fragile for combat conditions. Until engineers solve ruggedization and error correction at scale, quantum sensing will remain more of a vulnerability in theory than a military advantage.Winning the Post-Stealth EraQuantum sensors will keep pushing beyond the lab to real-world military applications. The implications will be stark in the areas of seabed warfare, stealth aircraft, and missiles. As quantum sensors improve, platforms designed around invisibility and stealth will have to adapt to increased odds of being detected. Militaries will need to learn to “fight in the light.” Navies will need to invest in real-time magnetic signature reduction to slip past quantum magnetometers. Air forces will need to rethink stealth coatings and shift tactics to account for new radar and LiDAR signatures. Electromagnetic warfare, on prominent display in the Russia-Ukraine War, will need to expand to target quantum receivers. Faster, more precise detection will compress decisionmaking timelines, raising the risks of miscalculation and escalation.Such challenges to U.S. military primacy mean the United States needs a national technology strategy informed by the National Defense Strategy to synchronize investments, accelerate ruggedization, and integrate quantum sensing into joint warfighting concepts. The new administration’s research and development budget priorities elevate quantum in priority, but there are at least five essential reforms to ensure the United States achieves those goals and maintains an economic and military edge.First, the Department of War must establish clear ownership of quantum sensing technology development. The Office of the Secretary of War should establish a Joint Quantum Technologies Transition Office, modeled on the Joint Hypersonics Transition Office, to synchronize research and development strategies and budgets across services to avoid duplication and gaps and enforce common standards. This office should anchor a National Quantum Sensing Strategy, led by the National Security Council and resourced through the standard five-year planning cycle. Strategy without budget is theater; budget without strategy is drift. A joint office focused on quantum technology transition would break service stovepipes, ensuring that federally- and industry-funded research leads to deployable capability.Second, the United States should employ a focused industrial policy to develop a capable and resilient industrial base. Quantum hardware depends on secure access to minerals such as silicon, aluminum, copper, niobium, gallium, germanium, tungsten, and rare earths like yttrium, europium, and erbium. The United States currently relies on fragile, adversary-exposed supply chains for several of these materials. A Defense Production Act Title III program should underwrite domestic refining, allied processing, and device-grade feedstock. Beyond raw materials, the Department of War, Department of the Interior, and Department of Commerce should work together to establish a Trusted Quantum Foundry network to produce low technology readiness level and low-rate initial production fabrication of magnetometers, gravimeters, and inertial sensors with export-controlled design kits, radiation-hard packaging, and thermal management modules. Without a well-powered and resourced industrial base that can deliver ruggedized parts, quantum tech will not be combat-ready.Third, experimentation and ruggedization should become the priority in technology development. Department of War metrics should shift from budget execution to leading indicators of combat performance like mean-time-between-failure, calibration holdover, clutter rejection, and tolerance to real-world vibration and electromagnetic noise. Linked experimentation campaigns, run at Nellis Air Force Base, Eglin Air Force Base, and the Atlantic Undersea Test and Evaluation Center, would generate reference datasets and force prototypes onto operational platforms early. Coupling ruggedization and campaign testing with modular open system architecture standards as the down-select gates for continued funding will ensure that breakthroughs in physics are matched by breakthroughs in engineering at speed to field capable weapon systems.Fourth, integration and counter-quantum operations are essential. Given the most likely near- and mid-term applications and recent space-based demonstration of quantum key distribution, the Department of the Air Force should lead quantum research and development. The Air Force should appoint a Technology Executive Officer for Quantum to establish a Quantum Task Force, with personnel contributions across the joint force, to spiral-integrate GPS-free navigation kits, magnetic anomaly mapping, and quantum-assisted electronic warfare payloads onto aircraft, submarines, and satellites. Each service should be required to field at least one quantum modality per year on an operational platform, such as quantum detection on a U.S. Army Patriot battery. At the same time, the Joint Force must prepare to fight in a post-stealth environment by investing in counter-quantum measures: real-time degaussing, decoy signatures, noise injection, and deception playbooks that scramble adversary sensors. Without a dual focus on integration and denial, quantum sensing will be a one-sided battlefield.Finally, the United States should cultivate technical and scientific talent because quantum sensing requires specialists in atomic physics, photonics, cryogenics, and precision instrumentation—skills currently in short supply. Existing research investments in quantum must be augmented in the near-term by launching fellowships and lab residencies and speeding the clearance process, so scientists contribute to war readiness in months, not years. Visa policies should be reformed to recruit global experts into U.S. labs rather than ceding key global talent to Beijing. Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, National Science Foundation, and service labs should also sponsor prize challenges focused on ruggedization, like a week-long inertial navigation stability on moving aircraft or vibration-tolerant gravimetry from drones, with awards tied to operational demonstrations.Quantum sensing will not render U.S. weapon systems obsolete overnight, but it will erode survivability and deterrence over time. The United States has no time to indulge in fragmented efforts. It must impose a national strategy, secure the critical materials and a quantum industrial base, run experimentation campaigns that prove feasibility under operational stress, integrate quantum sensing into joint warfighting concepts, and develop talent to sustain momentum. The advantage will not go to the country with the most elegant physics, but to the one that fields first and adapts fastest. Without decisive reforms, the United States risks watching adversaries define the post-stealth era on their own terms.Lt. Col. Jahara “FRANKY” Matisek, PhD, is a U.S. Air Force command pilot, nonresident research fellow at the U.S. Naval War College and the Payne Institute for Public Policy, and a visiting scholar at Northwestern University. Col. Katrina Schweiker, PhD, is a U.S. Air Force physicist and military fellow with the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Morgan D. Bazilian, PhD, is a senior associate (non-resident) with the Energy Security and Climate Change Program at CSIS and the director of the Payne Institute for Public Policy and professor at the Colorado School of Mines.The views expressed are those of the authors and do not reflect the official position of the U.S. Naval War College, U.S. Air Force, or Department of Defense.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nTechnology,\nAmerican Innovation,\nCritical Minerals, and\nDefense Industrial Base\nJahara Matisek\nCommand Pilot, U.S. Air Force; Nonresident Research Fellow, U.S. Naval War College and Payne Institute for Public Policy; and Visiting Scholar, Northwestern University\nImage\nKatrina Schweiker\nFormer Military Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nMorgan Bazilian\nFormer Senior Associate (Non-resident), Energy Security and Climate Change Program\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nEnergy Security and Climate Change Program\nEmerging Technologies","content_cn":"量子传感与战争的未来：保持竞争力所需的五项关键改革\n图片：da-kuk/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nJahara Matisek、\nKatrina Schweiker\n和\nMorgan Bazilian\n发表于2025年10月9日\n远程可视化\n量子传感正蓄势待发，可能彻底改变常规战争和核战争，因此国防部（最近更名为战争部）必须进行关键改革，以保持竞争优势。潜艇、隐形轰炸机和先进的第五代战斗机构成了美国威慑态势的骨干，依靠的是它们的生存能力以及在不被侦测的情况下穿透对手防御的能力。量子传感技术能够探测引力、磁场和光中的原子尺度相互作用，这可能使依赖隐身和不可见性的武器系统失效。正如雷达改变了第二次世界大战期间欧洲上空的空战一样，量子传感也将改变空中和水下作战。\n不幸的是，美国正以分散的投资和缺乏连贯的愿景来应对这一挑战。国防高级研究计划局、各军种、国家实验室、学术界和工业界的研究人员都在追逐突破，却没有国家战略或统一的技术愿景，这使得量子技术更难同时推进商业产品和军事武器系统。战争部应制定连贯的愿景和切实可行的实现路径，包括愿意在中长期技术上承担风险，从而避免即使是太空量子传感试验这样规模不大的努力继续为资金和发展势头而苦苦挣扎。与此同时，中国正在测试海床传感器以追踪潜艇，俄罗斯则在构建量子导航系统以应对电子战。\n第一个将量子技术应用于国防的国家将消除潜艇和隐形飞机的比较优势，重塑核威慑和常规作战。这不是科幻小说。原型机已经在测试，国防部门正在资助规模化项目，各联盟也将量子开发列为高度优先事项。随着第一代量子传感系统逐渐走向军事应用，我们必须思考：如果中国率先实现这一目标，而美国潜艇和隐形飞机失去隐身能力，会发生什么？\n量子的军事前景\n量子传感将极其微小的物理效应转化为有用信号。量子磁力计可以绘制地球磁场的细微变化，从而有望实现对水下目标（如潜艇）的追踪和瞄准。量子重力仪能够发现地下和海床周围的密度异常，从而揭示隧道、竖井或大型物体。冷原子惯性传感器和先进时钟可以在没有GPS的环境中让平台保持航向数天，而不是数小时。量子增强光学技术能够在不产生电磁辐射的情况下提升探测和地理定位能力。这些传感技术具有巨大的军事意义。\n作战价值来自经典传感器与量子技术的结合。混合式系统（例如量子输入加上声学、电磁或光学输入，再由机器学习进行过滤）能够在噪声中找到信号、减少误报，并将微弱信号转换为可用于瞄准的高质量航迹。这一点已经在初步试验中显现：英国皇家海军\n测试用于探测小型水下目标的量子磁力计；中国试验将量子磁力计搭载在无人机上，从空中追踪水下活动；美国科学家探索了基于原子干涉测量的无GPS导航套件；英国研究人员则展示了用于探测空洞和隧道的重力传感器。这些发展促成了将综合传感与通信作为组织框架的理念：分布传感器，将计时和处理移至更靠近边缘的位置，并跨平台融合输出，使单个微弱的量子回波在与传统数据流结合后具备军事效用。\n\n近期收益并不是科幻式雷达通过一次飞跃就“摧毁隐身能力”。而是更好的目标提示、更具韧性的导航，以及更持久的广域搜索，尤其是在水下和濒海区域，因为许多军事系统容易受到饱和或欺骗。必须将量子技术视为现有联合作战力量的力量倍增器：在电磁频谱受到干扰和饱和的高度对抗领域中，继续保持态势感知，同时完成发现、定位和打击。\n\n《国防情报评估：2025年全球威胁评估》指出，中国和俄罗斯正在扩大量子通信网络，同时投资于量子磁力计、重力仪和惯性导航系统。这些投资正在发展用于发现、跟踪和瞄准曾经不可见武器系统的作战工具。无论这些投资是否最终证明具有决定性作用，趋势都很明确：对手试图压缩美国潜艇和隐身飞机不受阻碍的机动空间，而这正是延伸威慑的基石。\n\n量子越多，问题越多\n\n尽管量子技术的进步显示出希望，但在这些优势能够在战场上实现之前，仍有许多挑战需要克服。这些挑战并不否定量子技术的军事价值，但提醒人们当前量子领域具有对抗性。从雷达到GPS再到隐身，每一项革命性技术都经历了复杂的工程阶段，才最终改变作战方式。量子传感如今正处于这一阶段：脆弱但发展迅速，每次迭代都使其更接近坚固、可部署的系统。在后隐身战场空间中，投资和试验最多的国家将掌握决定性优势。\n\n极高的灵敏度既是优势也是负担。能够探测最微弱磁场或重力变化的传感器，也会吸收振动、热漂移、电磁杂波以及自身搭载平台的噪声。在移动的钢制舰体和高速喷气机上，背景干扰会压倒微弱信号。磁力计在电力系统附近会产生虚假航迹，重力仪在湍流中失去稳定性，而惯性组件若没有持续重新校准就会发生漂移。\n\n这种脆弱性减缓了从实验室向战场的过渡。在受控条件下表现良好的设备，一旦暴露于现实军事行动中的运动、温度波动和背景噪声，就可能发生故障。尺寸、重量、功率和成本限制又增加了一层困难，因为很少有系统能够满足战斗环境的严苛要求。\n\n其他经常被大肆宣传的应用也面临类似限制。“不可破解”的量子通信仍容易受到硬件漏洞利用的攻击，并且需要在中继器和存储器方面进行重大基础设施投资。关于量子雷达将“暴露先进隐身\n“飞机和导弹”，或成为“潜艇杀手”，都应持怀疑态度，因为其射程有限，并且容易受到光子损失、大气干扰和噪声的影响。更可能的是，量子雷达将发展成混合系统，与常规雷达和信号处理相结合，以改善某些利基环境中的探测能力，而不是彻底革新空防。\n\n承诺与实践之间的差距在于，量子设备能够探测到传统传感器遗漏的目标，但大多数原型机对于作战环境而言过于脆弱。在工程师以规模化方式解决加固和纠错问题之前，量子传感在理论上更多仍是一种脆弱性，而非军事优势。\n\n赢得后隐身时代\n\n量子传感器将继续走出实验室，应用于现实世界的军事领域。其影响将在海床战、隐身飞机和导弹等方面尤为明显。随着量子传感器不断改进，围绕不可见性和隐身性设计的平台将不得不适应更高的被探测概率。各国军队需要学会“在光明中作战”。海军需要投资于实时磁特征降低技术，以避开量子磁力计。空军需要重新思考隐身涂层，并调整战术，以应对新的雷达和激光雷达特征。在俄乌战争中得到突出展示的电磁战，也需要扩大范围，将量子接收器作为攻击目标。更快速、更精确的探测将压缩决策时间线，提高误判和升级的风险。\n\n这些对美国军事主导地位的挑战意味着，美国需要制定一项以《国防战略》为指导的国家技术战略，以协调投资、加速加固，并将量子传感整合进联合作战概念。新政府的研发预算重点提升了量子技术的优先级，但至少还需要五项关键改革，以确保美国实现这些目标，并保持经济和军事优势。\n\n首先，战争部必须明确量子传感技术开发的责任归属。战争部长办公室应设立联合量子技术转型办公室，仿照联合高超音速转型办公室，以协调各军种之间的研发战略和预算，避免重复和空白，并执行统一标准。该办公室应成为国家量子传感战略的支点，由国家安全委员会牵头，并通过标准的五年规划周期提供资源。没有预算的战略只是作秀；没有战略的预算则会漂移。一个专注于量子技术转型的联合办公室将打破军种壁垒，确保联邦和工业界资助的研究转化为可部署能力。\n\n其次，美国应采取有针对性的产业政策，发展有能力且具韧性的工业基础。量子硬件依赖于对硅、铝、铜、铌、镓、锗、钨等矿产，以及钇、铕和铒等稀土的安全获取。目前，美国在其中几种材料上依赖脆弱且暴露于对手影响的供应链。《国防生产法》第三章计划应为国内精炼、盟友加工和器件级原料提供资金支持。除原材料之外，战争部、内政部和\n商务部应共同建立可信量子铸造厂网络，以生产技术成熟度等级较低、初始生产速率较低的磁力计、重力仪和惯性传感器，并配备受出口管制的设计套件、抗辐射封装和热管理模块。没有一个资金和资源充足、能够交付加固部件的工业基础，量子技术就无法达到作战准备状态。第三，试验和加固应成为技术发展的重点。战争部的指标应从预算执行转向作战性能的领先指标，例如平均故障间隔时间、校准保持时间、杂波抑制能力，以及对现实振动和电磁噪声的耐受性。在内利斯空军基地、埃格林空军基地和大西洋水下测试与评估中心开展的联动试验活动，将生成参考数据集，并推动原型机尽早登上作战平台。将加固和战役测试与模块化开放系统架构标准结合起来，并将其作为继续资助的筛选门槛，将确保物理学上的突破能够同步转化为工程上的突破，从而快速部署具备作战能力的武器系统。第四，集成和反量子作战至关重要。鉴于最有可能的近期和中期应用，以及近期基于太空的量子密钥分发演示，空军部应牵头开展量子研发。空军应任命一名量子技术执行官，组建量子特别工作组，由联合部队各单位提供人员，以螺旋式集成无GPS导航套件、磁异常测绘和量子辅助电子战载荷，并将其部署到飞机、潜艇和卫星上。应要求每个军种每年在一个作战平台上部署至少一种量子能力，例如在美国陆军爱国者导弹连上部署量子探测能力。与此同时，联合部队必须通过投资反量子措施，为在后隐身环境中作战做好准备：实时消磁、诱饵特征、噪声注入，以及扰乱敌方传感器的欺骗作战手册。如果不同时关注集成与拒止，量子传感将成为单方面的战场。最后，美国应培养技术和科学人才，因为量子传感需要原子物理、光子学、低温学和精密仪器方面的专家，而这些技能目前供不应求。现有的量子研究投资必须在近期通过启动奖学金和实验室驻留项目、加快安全审查流程来扩大规模，使科学家能够在数月而非数年内为战争准备作出贡献。应改革签证政策，将全球专家吸引到美国实验室，而不是把关键的全球人才拱手让给北京。国防高级研究计划局、国家科学基金会和各军种实验室还应赞助专注于加固的悬赏挑战，例如在移动飞机上进行为期一周的惯性导航稳定性测试，或利用无人机开展抗振动重力测量，并将奖励与作战演示挂钩。量子传感不会在一夜之间使美国武器系统过时，但它会随着时间推移削弱生存能力和威慑力。美国没有时间沉溺于支离破碎的努力。它必须实施国家战略，确保\n关键材料和量子工业基础，开展实验活动，以证明其在作战压力下的可行性，将量子传感融入联合 warfighting 概念，并培养人才以保持发展势头。优势不会属于拥有最优雅物理学的国家，而会属于最先部署并适应最快的国家。如果不进行果断改革，美国可能会眼睁睁地看着对手按照自己的方式定义隐身时代之后的时代。\n\nJahara“FRANKY”Matisek中校，博士，美国空军指挥飞行员、美国海军战争学院和Payne公共政策研究所的非驻院研究员，以及西北大学访问学者。Katrina Schweiker上校，博士，美国空军物理学家，华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）国防与安全部军事研究员。Morgan D. Bazilian博士是CSIS能源安全与气候变化项目的高级助理（非驻院），也是Payne公共政策研究所所长及科罗拉多矿业学院教授。\n\n文中观点仅代表作者个人，不反映美国海军战争学院、美国空军或国防部的官方立场。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不受专有利益影响。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n技术，\n美国创新，\n关键矿产，以及\n国防工业基础\n\nJahara Matisek\n美国空军指挥飞行员；美国海军战争学院和Payne公共政策研究所非驻院研究员；西北大学访问学者\n图像\n\nKatrina Schweiker\n国防与安全部前军事研究员\n图像\n\nMorgan Bazilian\n能源安全与气候变化项目原高级助理（非驻院）\n\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n能源安全与气候变化项目\n新兴技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-10-09T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3625","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"人工智能的真正风险","title_en":"A Real Risk for Artificial Intelligence","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表评论文章，讨论人工智能带来的风险认知问题。作者认为，关于AI导致大规模失业、产生自我意识或“杀手机器人”的恐惧被夸大，类似历史上对自动化和新技术的焦虑。文章指出，真正的风险在于基于推测性危害制定过度限制性监管，可能扼杀创新，并以欧盟数字技术监管拖累经济增长为例，呼吁政策重点应放在利用AI加速创新和公平分配收益上。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary discusses perceptions of AI risk. The author argues that fears of mass unemployment, AI sentience, or killer robots are exaggerated and resemble historical anxieties about automation. The real risk, it contends, is restrictive regulation based on speculative harms that could stifle innovation, citing EU digital technology regulation as a drag on growth. It calls for policy to focus on using AI to accelerate innovation and equitably distribute its benefits.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:37:40.952098+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/real-risk-artificial-intelligence","content":"A Real Risk for Artificial Intelligence\nPhoto: Shuo/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nJames Andrew Lewis\nPublished June 11, 2024\nRemote Visualization\nArtificial intelligence (AI) is not really new. The technology has been around since the 1950s and AI tools are already essential for business. Manufacturing, finance, and research companies that interact with consumers all use AI to manage customers and boost productivity. AI tools will reshape economies, but this will be an iterative process as innovators find better ways to use AI to gain a competitive advantage, similar to the way it took years to reap the benefits of the internet. AI is a tool that will make other tools more productive.The first wave of excitement over AI peaked in the 1980s. Now, “generative AI,” or GenAI, has triggered the latest round of attention. ChatGPT is a well-known example of generative AI. It is trained on huge databases and can produce text, software, and video without human intervention. AI’s reliance on scraping the web for data challenges existing laws regarding intellectual property, and some worry that there may not be enough new data left to scrape. Generative AI can seem human, since it can interact directly with people, but this is an illusion. Meta’s chief AI scientist even said that AI is not as smart as a cat and can’t even figure out how to load a dishwasher.Paradoxically, AI generates both exuberance and gloom. There is an AI financial bubble (although it may be about to pop). Investments in AI technologies have quadrupled in the last year, but this has been accompanied by an anxious narrative.One common fear is that AI will lead to mass unemployment. This is wrong. That automation will cause some jobs to disappear is a concern that goes back to the first days of industrialization in the early nineteenth century. English workers attacked machines and factories that they believed threatened their jobs. People tend to confuse the risk of automation with a failure of social policy—an inability to equitably distribute the results of greater productivity. Many know that Keynes wrote in 1930 that automation creates more jobs than it costs. Decades later, he is still right. Jobs will change, but more new jobs will be created, and with them, more wealth and leisure. Another fear is that AI will “awaken,” become sentient, and take over the world, perhaps destroying all human life in the process. This has been the plot of many films over the last century, and one should regard these fears with a high degree of skepticism. Elon Musk said that AI is “one of the biggest risks to the future of civilization,” while physicist Stephen Hawking warned that “the development of full artificial intelligence could spell the end of the human race.” The Center for AI Safety compared the risks of AI to those of pandemics or nuclear war. In fact, most researchers are divided on whether AI will ever have the independent capability for the planning, subterfuge, and independent thought needed to become an existential threat.There are similar fears about “killer robots,” autonomous devices that can take independent action in combat without human supervision. Progress in the development of autonomous devices will naturally lend itself to military applications and make weapons more precise and agile. Air defense systems are examples of automated weapons developed due to humans being too slow to react to an incoming hypersonic missile. Weapons will be automated, but killer robots do not exist and may never exist. They face the same problems as self-driving cars—an inability to deal with the complexity of physical environments. There are already malicious uses for AI, although none are lethal. AI is used to amplify the effect of “fake news,” adding to the political turmoil the internet created. Societies are already seeing AI-generated “deepfakes” of world leaders saying things they never really said. President Biden joked about this in a speech about the new U.S. executive order on AI, which cautions about the possible misuse of AI to develop new bioweapons. But these fears reflect some deeper social anxieties and resemble the fears about witchcraft in the seventeenth century that were widely endorsed by many elites and experts.Improbable scenarios reflect misconceptions about warfare. The relationship between technological advantage and military success is complex, and other factors are more important than technology. Similar concerns about bias and social effects reflect beliefs in the existence of systemic patterns of behavior and bias that remain a subject of debate. The unspoken assumption in most cases is that societies will build tools and lose control of them.Endorsement by elites does not guarantee veracity. The worthies of the 1600s were persuaded by flimsy evidence that old people had carnal relations with Satan and communicated with him using cats. One possible explanation is that periods of rapid, extensive, and apparently unmanageable change create anxiety, and some external phenomena like witchcraft or AI provide an outlet for this. While this explanation is unsatisfactory, fears about AI safety, bias, or dominance are in fact cousins to the conspiracy theory about vaccines, just more socially acceptable. Why democratic societies have become more risk averse in the last thirty years is a longer discussion, but risk-averse societies are prone to perceiving danger where there is none.The discussion around AI has centered on the need for ethical guidelines to ensure that it is used in a principled manner. The United Kingdom hosted an AI Safety Summit. The European Union passed an AI act to regulate artificial intelligence (prompting even French president Macron to complain of overreach and risk to innovation), and the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development issued guidelines on managing the risks of AI. All of these guidelines recognize the need to balance any potential risk of AI systems against the risk of losing the economic and social benefits the new technology will bring.However, a focus on potential dangers and defining ethical use without the benefit of experience could inevitably harm AI’s potential for economic growth and the benefits to research and innovation it will bring. The trend is to put in place restrictive rules for AI based on predictions of potential harm, not actual harm. Many governments and researchers underestimate how easy it is to choke innovation, which requires an unusual willingness to take risks. By erecting obstacles and creating unreasonable compliance burdens (unreasonable when they address unobserved or hypothetical fears), governments will decrease the amount of new products and services AI could help create and deter companies, especially small, new companies, from entering the field. Pledges by decisionmakers to respect the need for innovation do not compensate for regulatory risk. Therein lies the dilemma. Since the 1990s, EU regulations for digital technologies have harmed Europe’s economic growth by creating bureaucratic obstacles for innovators and investors. The GDP of countries like France and Germany grew at a quarter of the rate of the United States’ and China’s because its regulations slowed economic digitization. This is why the European Union lacks both tech giants and unicorns. The new rules for AI could lock Europe out of further growth and innovation, and if they are copied, the United States and other countries in the rest of the world could join it in losing the benefits.The motives behind these anxious predictions are open to question. It could be a desire for public attention by making extreme statements. There could be commercial motivations, and there is speculation that big companies that currently lead in AI want to use regulation to put potential competitors at a disadvantage. AI also comes at a time of anxiety over the worsening state of international affairs. There is a broad and changed perspective on technological progress. The internet, the last big technological change, came at a time of rosy millennial and utopian predictions about how it would transform the world into a place where war had ended and all countries were on the path to becoming market democracies. This was wishful thinking, and while the predictions for AI are gloomier, they are no more accurate. A more accurate conclusion is that humans are bad at predicting technological futures. Predictions that automation would cost jobs date back two centuries to the start of the industrial era, predictions of killer robots and electronic brains dominating human life to the 1920s, and after 1945, there were predictions of computer intelligence creating existential threats. Nuclear weapons unleashed a torrent of predictions about the end of human life, which were emotionally satisfying but statistically wrong. Why societies are impelled to make pronouncements of doom and why they are wrong requires a separate analysis. It may be that these societal fears reflect an ability to observe, but not control, powerful and seemingly impersonal trends created by technology.AI is just another step in the process of replacing human labor with machines that began more than two centuries ago. Automation continues to improve standards of living and quality of life. Without dismissing these concerns, since they reflect deeper social fears, emphasizing risk misses the point. Societies that run away from technology will forfeit the chance for improvement. The challenges of AI for policymakers are finding how best to use it to accelerate innovation and productivity, and how to equitably deliver the improvements in the standard of living that are the result to all citizens. This is the AI policy problem that needs to be solved.James A. Lewis is the Pritzker Chair and director of the Strategic Technologies Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nTechnology\nImage\nJames Andrew Lewis\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Economic Security and Technology Department\nPrograms & Projects\nEmerging Technologies\nInnovation and Digital Transformation\nStrategic Technologies Program\nEconomic Security and Technology","content_cn":"人工智能面临的真实风险\n图片：Shuo/Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nJames Andrew Lewis\n发表于2024年6月11日\n远程可视化\n人工智能（AI）其实并不新鲜。这项技术自20世纪50年代就已存在，而人工智能工具对于企业来说已经不可或缺。制造业、金融业以及与消费者互动的研究公司都在使用人工智能来管理客户并提高生产率。人工智能工具将重塑经济，但这将是一个反复迭代的过程，因为创新者会不断寻找更好的方式来利用人工智能获取竞争优势，这与互联网花费多年才带来收益的过程类似。人工智能是一种能让其他工具提高生产率的工具。20世纪80年代，第一波对人工智能的兴奋达到顶峰。如今，“生成式人工智能”（即GenAI）引发了最新一轮关注。ChatGPT是生成式人工智能的一个知名例子。它接受海量数据库的训练，无需人类干预即可生成文本、软件和视频。人工智能依赖抓取网络数据，这对现有的知识产权法律构成挑战，也有人担心可能已经没有足够的新数据可供抓取。生成式人工智能可以直接与人互动，因此看起来仿佛具有人性，但这是一种错觉。Meta的首席人工智能科学家甚至表示，人工智能还没有猫聪明，甚至不知道如何把碗碟放进洗碗机。具有讽刺意味的是，人工智能同时带来了狂热与悲观情绪。人工智能领域存在金融泡沫（尽管泡沫可能即将破裂）。过去一年，对人工智能技术的投资翻了两番，但与此同时也伴随着一种焦虑的叙事。一个常见的担忧是，人工智能将导致大规模失业。这种担忧是错误的。自动化会导致一些工作消失，这是一个可以追溯到19世纪初工业化初期的担忧。英国工人曾攻击他们认为会威胁自身工作的机器和工厂。人们往往把自动化风险与社会政策失败混为一谈——也就是无法公平分配生产率提高所带来的成果。许多人知道，凯恩斯曾在1930年写道，自动化创造的就业岗位多于它所造成的岗位损失。几十年后，他仍然是对的。工作岗位会发生变化，但会创造出更多新的工作岗位，并由此带来更多财富和闲暇。另一个担忧是，人工智能会“觉醒”、产生意识并接管世界，甚至可能在此过程中毁灭全人类。过去一个世纪里，许多电影都以此为情节，因此人们应当对这些担忧抱有高度怀疑。埃隆·马斯克曾说，人工智能是“对文明未来最大的风险之一”，而物理学家史蒂芬·霍金警告说，“完整人工智能的发展可能意味着人类的终结”。人工智能安全中心将人工智能的风险与大流行病或核战争的风险相提并论。事实上，大多数研究人员对于人工智能是否最终会具备成为生存性威胁所需的规划、欺骗和独立思考能力，意见并不一致。人们还对“杀手机器人”抱有类似担忧，即能够在没有人类监督的情况下于战斗中采取独立行动的自主设备。自主设备的发展进步自然会推动其军事应用，并使武器更加精确和灵活。防空系统就是自动化武器的例子，这类武器的开发源于人类反应来袭高超音速导弹的速度太慢。武器将实现自动化，但杀手机器人确实\n不存在，也可能永远不会存在。它们面临着与自动驾驶汽车相同的问题——无法应对物理环境的复杂性。人工智能已经被用于恶意用途，尽管目前还没有造成致命后果。人工智能被用来放大“假新闻”的影响，加剧互联网造成的政治动荡。社会已经看到由人工智能生成的世界领导人“深度伪造”视频，其中他们说着从未真正说过的话。拜登总统在一次关于美国新的人工智能行政命令的演讲中拿此开玩笑；该行政命令提醒人们注意滥用人工智能研发新型生物武器的可能性。但这些担忧反映了更深层的社会焦虑，也类似于17世纪人们对巫术的恐惧——当时许多精英和专家都广泛认同这种恐惧。不太可能发生的情景反映了人们对战争的误解。技术优势与军事成功之间的关系很复杂，其他因素比技术更为重要。对偏见和社会影响的类似担忧，反映了人们相信存在系统性的行为模式和偏见，而这些仍是争论的主题。在大多数情况下，未明说的假设是：社会会制造工具，最终却失去对它们的控制。\n\n精英的认可并不能保证真实性。17世纪的显要人物被薄弱的证据说服，认为老年人与撒但发生肉体关系，并通过猫与他交流。一种可能的解释是，快速、广泛且看似无法控制的变化时期会制造焦虑，而巫术或人工智能之类的外部现象则为这种焦虑提供了出口。虽然这种解释并不令人满意，但人们对人工智能安全、偏见或支配地位的担忧，实际上与关于疫苗的阴谋论是近亲，只是前者在社会上更容易被接受。为什么民主社会在过去30年里变得更加规避风险，是一个更长的话题，但规避风险的社会往往会在并不存在危险的地方察觉到危险。\n\n围绕人工智能的讨论集中在需要制定道德准则，以确保其以有原则的方式使用。英国举办了人工智能安全峰会。欧盟通过了一项人工智能法案来监管人工智能（甚至促使法国总统马克龙抱怨监管越界并威胁创新），经济合作与发展组织则发布了管理人工智能风险的指南。所有这些指南都承认，需要在人工智能系统的任何潜在风险与失去这项新技术将带来的经济和社会利益的风险之间取得平衡。\n\n然而，在缺乏经验的情况下，专注于潜在危险并界定合乎道德的使用方式，可能不可避免地损害人工智能促进经济增长的潜力，以及它将为研究和创新带来的益处。当前的趋势是根据潜在危害的预测，而非实际危害，为人工智能制定限制性规则。许多政府和研究人员低估了扼杀创新的容易程度，而创新需要一种非同寻常的冒险意愿。政府设置障碍并制造不合理的合规负担（当这些负担针对未被观察到的或假设性的恐惧时，就是不合理的），将减少人工智能能够帮助创造的新产品和服务数量，并阻止企业，尤其是小型新企业，进入这一领域。决策者承诺尊重创新的必要性，并不能抵消监管风险。\n\n困境就在于此。自20世纪90年代以来，欧盟针对\n数字技术通过为创新者和投资者制造官僚障碍，损害了欧洲的经济增长。法国和德国等国家的GDP增速仅为美国和中国的四分之一，因为其监管规定拖慢了经济数字化进程。这就是为什么欧盟既没有科技巨头，也没有独角兽企业。人工智能的新规可能会把欧洲排除在进一步增长和创新之外；如果这些规定被照搬，美国以及世界其他国家也可能一道失去相关利益。\n\n这些焦虑预测背后的动机值得质疑。动机可能是通过发表极端言论来吸引公众注意。也可能存在商业动机，有人推测，目前在人工智能领域领先的大型企业希望利用监管使潜在竞争者处于不利地位。人工智能的发展也正值国际事务状况恶化、焦虑加剧之时。人们对技术进步的看法广泛而且发生了变化。互联网是上一次重大的技术变革，当时正值乐观的千禧年主义和乌托邦式预测盛行，人们认为互联网将把世界变成一个战争已经结束、所有国家都走向市场民主的地方。这是一厢情愿的想法；尽管关于人工智能的预测更加悲观，但它们并不比那些预测更准确。更准确的结论是，人类不擅长预测技术的未来。关于自动化将导致失业的预测，可以追溯到两个世纪前工业时代的开端；关于杀手机器人和电子大脑支配人类生活的预测，可以追溯到20世纪20年代；1945年之后，又出现了关于计算机智能制造生存威胁的预测。核武器引发了大量关于人类生命终结的预测，这些预测在情感上令人满足，但在统计上却是错误的。社会为何会被驱使发表末日预言，以及这些预言为何错误，需要另行分析。或许，这些社会恐惧反映了人们能够观察由技术创造的强大且看似不受个人意志支配的趋势，却无法控制这些趋势的能力。\n\n人工智能只是用机器取代人类劳动这一进程中的又一步，而这一进程始于两个多世纪前。自动化持续改善着生活水平和生活质量。在不否定这些担忧的前提下——因为它们反映了更深层的社会恐惧——过分强调风险反而忽视了问题的关键。逃避技术的社会将失去改善的机会。人工智能给政策制定者带来的挑战在于：如何最好地利用它来加速创新和提高生产率，以及如何公平地把由此带来的生活水平改善惠及所有公民。这正是需要解决的人工智能政策问题。\n\nJames A. Lewis是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心战略技术项目的普利兹克主席兼主任。\n\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家私人、免税机构，专注于国际公共政策问题。其研究不带党派色彩，也不为任何专有利益服务。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人的观点。\n\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n技术\n\n图片\nJames Andrew Lewis\n高级\n顾问（非驻任）\n经济安全与技术部\n项目与计划\n新兴技术\n创新与数字化转型\n战略技术项目\n经济安全与技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-06-11T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2624","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"重新评估任务授权之必要","title_en":"Reevaluating the Need for Mission Authorization","summary_cn":"文章质疑美国商业航天“任务授权”框架的必要性，认为其源于一种未经检验的信念，而非针对可衡量结果。作者指出，发射、频谱、遥感等已有监管，外空条约第六条并未提供合规蓝图，也无国家公开批评美国履约不足。文章主张放弃任务授权，转而围绕具体、可衡量的目标设计规则，如第三方责任保险或安全风险，避免过度监管抑制商业航天创新。","summary_en":"The commentary questions the need for a U.S. 'mission authorization' framework for commercial space activities, arguing it rests on an axiomatic belief rather than measurable outcomes. Launch, spectrum, and remote sensing are already regulated, Outer Space Treaty Article VI offers no compliance blueprint, and no nation has publicly criticized U.S. compliance. The author urges abandoning mission authorization in favor of rules anchored to specific, measurable goals, such as third-party liability insurance or safety risks, to avoid overregulation that could stifle commercial space innovation.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:30:44.042469+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/reevaluating-need-mission-authorization","content":"Reevaluating the Need for Mission Authorization\nPhoto: Flavio Coelho via Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nClayton Swope\nPublished July 14, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nAgrippa’s Trilemma, named after an ancient Greek philosopher, points to the difficulty of proving a truth without resorting to assumptions. The trilemma posits that there are only three ways to prove a truth—circular reasoning, dogmatic belief, or a never-ending series of “why” questions—none of which is a satisfying analytical anchor. But rather than a justification for giving up on critical reasoning, the trilemma encourages an ongoing reevaluation of conventional wisdom.Such a reevaluation is needed for mission authorization, as it will reveal that mission authorization is based on an axiomatic belief that a new process is needed for its own sake and not to achieve a measurable outcome. Mission authorization is a concept around new federal rules for certain space activities, such as in-space servicing, assembly, and manufacturing, which currently are not directly subject to existing U.S. regulatory or licensing processes. Continued pursuit of mission authorization will produce, at best, rules that serve no useful purpose or, at worst, regulations that harm the commercial space sector. If new rules for private sector space activities are needed, rather than continuing the quest for mission authorization, policymakers should identify the outcomes they want to achieve and figure out how best they can use the tools of government to achieve them.The most recent chapter of the mission authorization story started in 2015, when the Commercial Space Launch Competitiveness Act (CSLCA) directed the Office of Science and Technology Policy (OSTP) to produce a report assessing current and future commercial space activities and propose a way to authorize and supervise those activities, using existing legal authorities. Published in 2016, the report recommended the creation of an oversight framework, later coined mission authorization, for certain private-sector space activities. Rather than delineate which specific activities required new regulations, mission authorization would cover any space activity not already subject to regulation, including even future ones no one had invented yet.Working under the assumption that the United States needed this new process, policymakers and commercial space stakeholders have tried but failed to reach consensus on a way forward for mission authorization. The lack of agreement speaks to the difficulty in developing a framework that works for a diverse variety of private sector space activities, and because space stakeholders simply do not agree on what mission authorization should do. A legislative proposal from the Biden administration tried to cover all the bases, specifying many reasons to regulate, like space sustainability, international obligations, national security, foreign policy, and other national interests. On the other hand, an alternative framework proposed by the House of Representatives effectively ruled out any regulation as part of mission authorization.Though stakeholders today have divergent views about the nature and scope of mission authorization, a frequently stated aim has been to ensure the United States meets its obligations under Article VI of the Outer Space Treaty (OST), which specifies signatories must authorize and provide continuing supervision of their non-government space activities. In addition to this goal, the CSLCA framed mission authorization around promoting and minimizing the regulatory burdens on industry and prioritizing space safety using existing legal authorities.Today, many types of commercial space activities, such as launch and re-entry, spectrum use, and remote sensing, are already subject to regulation or review. Moreover, issuing its first orbital debris mitigation rules around 20 years ago, the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) uses its “public interest” statutory authority for rulemaking aimed at minimizing the creation of space debris. However, given the Supreme Court’s Loper Bright decision to end Chevron deference, some have voiced concerns that the FCC’s ability to issue rules on space debris could be successfully challenged in court. But currently, these existing regulations provide the government opportunities for significant oversight of commercial space activities.Advocates for mission authorization presume these existing regulatory processes are not sufficient to meet the nation’s OST obligations—and this speaks to the existential problem with mission authorization: it's not based on outcomes. Motivated by the belief that a new authorization and supervision process is needed, success is the enactment of the new process itself and not achieving a specific measurable outcome. Compounding the problem, while the treaty establishing the International Maritime Organization requires signatories to enact specific national regulations and members of the International Civil Aviation Organization are expected to adopt certain standards for international air travel, the OST neither provides a compliance blueprint nor even defines “authorization and continuing supervision.”While the United States should hold high standards for itself, overly strict interpretations of international agreements can actually harm U.S. interests. Furthermore, no nation—not even China, Iran, or Russia—has publicly criticized the United States for not living up to its authorization and supervision obligations, though China has complained about U.S. commercial space operators and reiterated that the U.S. government bears responsibility for their actions.Some have also argued that mission authorization is needed to address regulatory ambiguity for certain commercial space activities, like in-space servicing, assembly, and manufacturing, and that this ambiguity could stifle investment and create uncertainty for insurers of novel space activities. These arguments are based on anecdotes—a shaky foundation for regulation—and not data-driven evidence. If anything, the actual data reveals significant ongoing private investment in space. According to Space Capital, in the United States, nearly $158 billion was invested in over 1,000 different companies in the space economy over the last ten years. Certainly, new government regulation requiring the purchase of third-party liability insurance for space activities would create a market for space insurance products—and add a new cost of doing business in space that would probably be hard for startups to afford and could end up stifling innovation.Because mission authorization is oriented around creating a process, without a focus on outcomes, it risks doing either nothing or doing more harm than good. A light-touch authorization process covering all novel space activities would serve no practical purpose. Industry supports this approach—one that imposes no actual new requirements on private sector space activities other than more paperwork—but has consistently opposed actual new regulation on space activities, such as on private human spaceflight, and advocated for reforming existing regulations and cutting existing regulatory red tape.On the other end of the spectrum, giving authority to federal entities to develop regulations for an extensive list of reasons, without a clear focus on the problem regulation would seek to solve, risks giving a well-intentioned bureaucracy carte blanche to develop more and more regulations, potentially slowing the growth of the commercial space sector. Because U.S. export control policies—including U.S. interpretations of its Missile Technology Control Regime commitments—were developed in exactly this way, restricting opportunities for U.S. companies to compete with foreign competitors, policymakers are now pursuing export control reforms. Policymakers could give an entity broad statutory authority for regulating space activities, similar to the authorities granted to the Federal Aviation Administration over U.S. navigable airspace, but with so many unknowns—both for future space activities themselves and their associated risks—this approach cedes authority to regulate a lot of things that have not been fully thought out yet.There are undeniably space activities today that fall through regulatory gaps, though that does not automatically mean they need regulating. Part of the issue around creating any new space regulations is that it is difficult to understand the risks associated with many novel space activities because they are so new. Not all space activities do or will carry the same risks, either. Better understanding those risks will be important to develop an effective outcome-based regulatory approach. If new rules for private sector space activities are needed, they should be designed around specific outcomes—to provide a warm and fuzzy feeling about compliance with an intentionally vague treaty provision, and as a response to anecdotes, are not good reasons for new rules.Goals should be measurable so that metrics can be collected and assessed to determine a regulation’s efficacy. Impactful regulations follow this best practice and are anchored around achieving specific, measurable goals. In order to reduce the risk of fires, particularly ones that could quickly spread across an urbanized area, Boston enacted an ordinance in 1630 that prohibited thatched roofs and wooden chimneys. A federal law requiring all new cars manufactured after 1967 to include seat belts aimed for a measurable outcome—to reduce highway-related automobile accident fatalities. Seeking to reduce the prevalence of acid rain, Congress passed a law in 1990 creating a trading system for power plants emitting sulfur dioxide.The same approach—one anchored to measurable outcomes and not an ambiguous objective to authorize and supervise—would certainly work for designing new rules for commercial space activities. For one, the government may decide to use regulation for limiting its liability, which arises from another international space agreement, the Liability Convention, from damages to third parties caused by U.S. private sector space actors. Without a requirement to purchase liability insurance, however, agreement by private sector space operators to cover their own liability—many of whom would not have deep enough pockets to cover significant damage claims against them—would not be worth much. For example, the United Kingdom and the United Arab Emirates generally require operators to obtain a certain level of third-party liability coverage for space activities, beyond just launch, licensed in their countries.Though some elements of space operations are already regulated to maximize safety, policymakers may also want new rules to further reduce safety risks and decide the best way to do that would be to grant an agency new authority. The Biden administration’s proposal took this route, except that it cast the net very wide, providing broad authorities to regulate. A narrower provision of authority, clearly anchored on the problem new regulations seek to solve, rather than the all-encompassing approach of mission authorization, would help a regulator develop effective rules that also match legislative intent. Moreover, a targeted regulatory approach—that does not try to cover activities no one has even thought of yet—would likely be more tailored to encouraging continued innovation.Despite the passage of 10 years since OSTP called for the creation of a mission authorization framework, there is no sign that mission authorization is happening anytime soon. But this is not a bad thing. The idea of mission authorization is based on a dogmatic belief that a new process is needed for its own sake and not because it would solve a specific problem or achieve a measurable goal. Continuing down this path risks creating rules and regulations that waste time—and in the worst case, actually hold back the U.S. commercial space sector. It is time to end the quixotic quest for mission authorization and go back to square one, figuring out exactly what outcomes the government can help achieve to advance U.S. interests in space.Clayton Swope is the deputy director of the Aerospace Security Project and a senior fellow in the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSpace and Aerospace\nImage\nClayton Swope\nDeputy Director, Aerospace Security Project and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nAerospace Security Project\nDefense and Security\nRelated Content\nImage\nMission Authorization: Decoding the Space Policy Dilemma\nCritical Questions\nby\nClayton Swope\n— December 20, 2023","content_cn":"重新评估任务授权的必要性\n照片：Flavio Coelho via Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nClayton Swope\n发表于2025年7月14日\n远程可视化\n\n以古希腊哲学家命名的阿格里帕三难困境指出了在不诉诸假设的情况下证明真理的困难。三难困境认为，证明真理只有三种方式——循环论证、教条式信念，或无休止地追问“为什么”——而这些都不能成为令人满意的分析支点。但三难困境并不是放弃批判性推理的理由；相反，它鼓励人们持续重新评估传统观念。\n\n任务授权正需要这样一次重新评估，因为这将揭示：任务授权建立在一种公理式信念之上，即需要一个新流程本身就是目的，而不是为了实现可衡量的结果。任务授权是围绕某些太空活动制定新的联邦规则这一概念，例如在轨维修、组装和制造；目前，这些活动并不直接受现有美国监管或许可流程的约束。继续推进任务授权，最好的结果是制定出毫无实际用途的规则，最坏的结果则是出台损害商业航天行业的监管措施。如果确实需要针对私营部门太空活动制定新规则，政策制定者就不应继续追求任务授权，而应明确他们希望实现的结果，并找出如何最好地利用政府工具来实现这些结果。\n\n任务授权故事最近一章始于2015年，当时《商业航天发射竞争力法案》（CSLCA）指示科学与技术政策办公室（OSTP）编写一份报告，评估当前及未来的商业太空活动，并利用现有法律授权提出一种授权和监督这些活动的方式。该报告于2016年发布，建议为某些私营部门太空活动建立一个监督框架，后来被称为任务授权。任务授权不会明确哪些具体活动需要新规，而是涵盖任何尚未受到监管的太空活动，甚至包括那些尚无人发明的未来活动。\n\n政策制定者和商业航天利益相关者是在美国需要这一新流程的假设下开展工作的，他们曾试图就任务授权的前进方向达成共识，但未能成功。缺乏共识说明，为种类繁多的私营部门太空活动制定一个有效框架十分困难，也说明航天领域的利益相关者根本无法就任务授权应发挥什么作用达成一致。拜登政府提出的一项立法方案试图面面俱到，列出监管的诸多理由，例如太空可持续性、国际义务、国家安全、外交政策和其他国家利益。另一方面，众议院提出的另一套框架实际上排除了将任何监管作为任务授权组成部分的可能性。\n\n尽管如今利益相关者对任务授权的性质和范围看法不一，但一个经常被提出的目标是确保美国履行《外层空间条约》（OST）第六条规定的义务。该条规定，签署国必须对本国非政府实体的太空活动进行授权并持续监督。除这一目标外，CSLCA\n围绕促进产业发展、减轻监管负担，并利用现有法律权限优先保障航天安全来构建任务授权框架。如今，许多类型的商业航天活动，例如发射与再入、频谱使用和遥感，已经受到监管或审查。此外，大约20年前发布首套轨道碎片减缓规则时，联邦通信委员会（FCC）运用其法定的“公共利益”权限制定规则，旨在最大限度减少太空碎片的产生。然而，鉴于最高法院在Loper Bright案中的裁决终结了Chevron尊重原则，一些人担心，FCC制定太空碎片规则的能力可能会在法院受到成功挑战。但目前，这些现有法规为政府对商业航天活动进行重大监督提供了机会。\n\n任务授权的倡导者假定，这些现有监管程序不足以履行美国依据《外层空间条约》（OST）承担的义务——这也揭示了任务授权的根本问题：它并不以结果为基础。其出发点是认为需要新的授权和监督程序，而成功的标准是新程序本身的制定，而不是实现某一具体、可衡量的结果。更复杂的是，虽然确立国际海事组织的条约要求签署国制定具体的国内法规，国际民用航空组织的成员也被期望采纳某些国际航空旅行标准，但《外层空间条约》既没有提供合规蓝图，甚至也没有定义“授权和持续监督”。\n\n尽管美国应当对自己设定高标准，但对国际协议作出过于严格的解释实际上可能损害美国利益。此外，没有任何国家——甚至中国、伊朗或俄罗斯——公开批评美国未履行其授权和监督义务，尽管中国曾抱怨美国商业航天运营商，并重申美国政府应对其行为负责。\n\n一些人还认为，任务授权是为了解决某些商业航天活动（如在轨服务、组装和制造）的监管模糊性，而这种模糊性可能抑制投资，并给新型航天活动的保险公司带来不确定性。这些论点建立在轶事之上——这是监管的薄弱基础——而非数据驱动的证据。如果说数据说明了什么，那就是太空领域的私人投资正在持续大量进行。据Space Capital统计，过去十年，美国在太空经济领域的1000多家不同企业中投资了近1580亿美元。当然，要求为航天活动购买第三方责任保险的新政府监管措施，会为航天保险产品创造市场，并增加一项在太空开展业务的新成本；这项成本对初创企业而言可能难以承受，最终可能扼杀创新。\n\n由于任务授权的导向是建立一个程序，而不是关注结果，因此它可能无所作为，或弊大于利。一个涵盖所有新型航天活动、采取轻触式监管的授权程序不会产生任何实际作用。产业界支持这种做法——这种做法不会对私营部门的航天活动施加任何实际的新要求\n除了增加更多文书工作之外——但始终反对对空间活动实施实际的新监管，例如对私人载人航天的监管，并主张改革现有监管规定、削减现有监管繁文缛节。另一方面，如果以广泛列举的理由授权联邦实体制定法规，却没有明确聚焦于这些法规试图解决的问题，就有可能让一个出于善意的官僚机构获得制定越来越多法规的全权委托，从而可能放缓商业航天部门的增长。由于美国的出口管制政策——包括美国对其《导弹技术控制制度》承诺的解释——正是以这种方式制定的，限制了美国公司与外国竞争者竞争的机会，政策制定者目前正在推动出口管制改革。政策制定者可以赋予某个实体广泛的法定权力来监管空间活动，类似于授予联邦航空管理局监管美国可通航空域的权力；但由于存在太多未知因素——既包括未来空间活动本身，也包括其相关风险——这种做法会把监管许多尚未得到充分考虑的事项的权力交出去。\n\n不可否认，如今有些空间活动处于监管空白之中，但这并不自动意味着它们需要受到监管。制定任何新的空间法规所面临的部分问题在于，由于许多新颖的空间活动太过新颖，因此很难理解与之相关的风险。并非所有空间活动现在或将来都会带来相同的风险。更好地理解这些风险，对于制定有效的、以结果为基础的监管方法将十分重要。如果确实需要针对私营部门空间活动制定新规则，那么这些规则应围绕具体结果来设计——为了让人们对遵守一项故意含糊的条约条款产生一种温暖而安心的感觉，或为了回应一些轶闻而制定规则，都不是制定新规则的充分理由。\n\n目标应当是可衡量的，以便收集和评估指标，从而确定一项法规的有效性。有影响力的法规遵循这一最佳实践，并围绕实现具体且可衡量的目标来制定。为了降低火灾风险，尤其是那些可能迅速蔓延至城市化地区的火灾，波士顿于1630年颁布了一项条例，禁止使用茅草屋顶和木制烟囱。一项要求1967年以后生产的所有新车配备安全带的联邦法律，旨在实现一个可衡量的结果——减少与高速公路相关的汽车事故死亡人数。为了减少酸雨的普遍程度，国会于1990年通过一项法律，为排放二氧化硫的发电厂建立了交易制度。\n\n同样的方法——以可衡量的结果为基础，而不是以授权和监督这一含糊目标为基础——当然适用于为商业空间活动设计新规则。首先，政府可能会决定利用监管来限制其责任；这种责任源于另一项国际空间协定《责任公约》，涉及美国私营部门空间活动参与者对第三方造成的损害。然而，如果没有购买责任保险的要求，私营部门空间运营商同意承担自身责任——其中许多人没有足够的财力来支付针对他们提出的重大损害索赔——就没有多大意义。例如，英国和美国……\n阿拉伯联合酋长国通常要求运营商为在其本国获得许可的太空活动购买一定额度的第三方责任保险，而不仅仅是发射活动。尽管太空运营的某些要素已经受到监管，以最大限度地提高安全性，但政策制定者也可能希望制定新规则，进一步降低安全风险，并认为实现这一目标的最佳方式是赋予某个机构新的权力。拜登政府的提案采取了这一路径，但其监管范围非常广泛，赋予了广泛的监管权限。与包罗万象的任务授权方式相比，将授权范围限定得更窄，并明确围绕新规试图解决的问题展开，将有助于监管机构制定既有效又符合立法意图的规则。此外，有针对性的监管方式——不试图涵盖甚至还没人想到的活动——可能更有利于鼓励持续创新。\n尽管距美国总统行政办公室科技政策办公室（OSTP）呼吁建立任务授权框架已经过去了10年，但没有迹象表明任务授权会在近期实现。但这并不是坏事。任务授权的理念建立在一种教条式信念之上，即设立一个新流程是为了流程本身，而不是因为它能够解决具体问题或实现可衡量的目标。继续沿着这条道路走下去，可能会造成浪费时间的规则和监管——在最坏的情况下，甚至会阻碍美国商业航天部门的发展。现在是结束这场不切实际的任务授权追求、回到原点的时候了：明确政府能够帮助实现哪些成果，以推进美国在太空中的利益。\n克莱顿·斯沃普是航空航天安全项目副主任，也是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心防务与安全部的高级研究员。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私立、免税机构。其研究不具有党派性，也不具有专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n太空与航空航天\n图片\n克莱顿·斯沃普\n航空航天安全项目副主任兼防务与安全部高级研究员\n项目与计划\n航空航天安全项目\n防务与安全\n相关文章\n图片\n任务授权：解读太空政策困境\n重要问题\n作者\n克莱顿·斯沃普\n— 2023年12月20日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-07-14T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3479","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"燃烧的地区——10月7日一周年","title_en":"A Region Aflame - October 7 A Year Later","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心中东项目于2024年10月7日举办研讨会，主持人乔恩·奥尔特曼与布鲁金斯学会的纳坦·萨克斯、CSIS的丹·拜曼及里士满大学的达娜·埃尔库尔德对谈。讨论聚焦以色列社会在10月7日袭击后的创伤与脆弱感、公众对谈判与人质协议的态度、内塔尼亚胡的政治处境，以及以色列在加沙和黎巴嫩的战略目标与“胜利”定义的模糊性。","summary_en":"The CSIS Middle East Program hosted an October 7, 2024 discussion marking one year since the Hamas attack. Moderator Jon Alterman was joined by Natan Sachs of Brookings, Dan Byman of CSIS, and Dana El Kurd of the University of Richmond. They examined Israeli society's trauma and vulnerability, public support for hostage negotiations, Netanyahu's political standing, and the unclear definition of victory in Gaza and Lebanon.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:22:39.455586+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/region-aflame-october-7-year-later","content":"A Region Aflame - October 7 A Year Later\nPhoto: CSIS\nTranscript\n— October 7, 2024\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Transcript\n224kb\nImage\nPhoto: CSIS\nA Region Aflame - October 7 A Year Later\nOctober 7, 2024 • 4:00 – 5:00 pm EDT\nHosted by Middle East Program\nThis transcript is from a CSIS event hosted on October 7, 2024. Watch the full video here.Jon B. Alterman: Good afternoon and good evening. I'm Jon Alterman, senior vice president, the Brzezinski Chair of Global Security and Geostrategy, and the director of the Middle East Program at CSIS. We're convening today to discuss the solemn events of October 7th, a day that kicked off a tremendous amount of violence and suffering in the Middle East a year ago, creating deepening trauma for communities throughout the region.To discuss these events, but more importantly, to analyze their impacts on the current and future trajectory of the region, I'm joined by three colleagues. Our goal today is to have an interactive conversation from different perspectives to understand how the events of October 7th last year helped shape the region and where the region's going.I'm joined first by my colleague, Natan Sachs, director of the Center for Middle East Policy and a senior fellow of the Foreign Policy Program at the Brookings Institution. I am also joined by my friend and colleague, Dan Byman, who is a senior fellow with the Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program at CSIS, a professor at Georgetown University, and the director of its Security Studies Program. I am also joined by Professor Dana El Kurd, an assistant professor of political science at the University of Richmond and a senior non-resident fellow at the Arab Center in Washington. All of us come to this with a tremendous amount of focus, but also with very different backgrounds and perspectives. I'm really interested in understanding how we can learn from each other as we look forward to the future of the region. Dana, why don't you start?Dana El Kurd: Thank you so much for having me. I had a question for Natan on Israeli society and how it's metabolizing some of these changes and shifts in the conflict. Yesterday, Prime Minister Netanyahu said that Israel was defending itself on seven fronts against “the enemies of civilization.\" Could you tell us more about where Israeli society stands on this? What's public opinion showing about their appetite for negotiations or continued conflict? I'm trying to understand the societal component of all of this.Natan Sachs: Thanks, Dana. It's good to be with you all despite the day. Today, especially, is an extremely solemn day—certainly in Israel—and a day of real profound mourning. Not to diminish in any way anyone else’s suffering, but of course in Israel, the events from a year ago are still very much being processed. People have referred to Israelis, Palestinians, and now Lebanese as being in some post-traumatic state. The truth is they're not post-traumatic. They're very much still in the midst of a trauma, and that's very much true of Israelis as well, who are still living and reliving the events a year ago. Israelis sometimes refer to today as day 367 of October, in part because some of the hostages are still in captivity, and in part because the war continues. Israeli society, writ large, still feels a very profound sense of vulnerability in a way that would surprise its neighbors that view it as very strong and powerful.Israelis, especially given the events of October 7th, feel extremely vulnerable. There’s a lot of fear on a very personal basis—something that is often a fuel of civil wars. I used to say that Palestinians and now Lebanese certainly feel the same way in different ways, and that is very dangerous. That kind of fear fuels these kinds of conflicts. This sense that if you do not defend yourself, strike first, and protect yourself with everything you have, your loved ones could suffer a fate like that of October 7th a year ago. That means that Israeli society is very risk averse.The idea of negotiations is certainly open. There's a clear majority of Israelis in favor of negotiating—even with Hamas—for a ceasefire in return for the hostages, despite the severe costs in their mind. But there is also a deep aversion to negotiating over anything that they think would risk Israel again, including a Palestinian state at this stage. There are fears that a Palestinian state in the West Bank would mean that the major civilian populations in central Israel would face the same kind of crisis as those who neighbored Gaza.You mentioned Netanyahu. His rhetoric is often bellicose. I often find it offensive. He is deeply unpopular in Israel and is seen as responsible for October 7th for being the one at the helm. It's not that Israelis are against the aims of the war, especially the two clear aims in Gaza—which are bringing down Hamas as the governing power there, which has mostly been achieved, and returning the hostages—or degrading the rest of the Iranian-backed “Axis of Resistance,” particularly Hezbollah. However, you see a lot of disunity over his tactics, means, and what as many Israelis think is him prioritizing his own political fate.I can think of an example from today, then I'll end there to let others speak. Today, there are two main ceremonies commemorating October 7th in Israel. One is state sponsored. Then, the families immediately objected to the idea that this government, the same government that was the government on October 7th, would organize it. So, there is a second ceremony that they call “the national one” or other names, organized mostly by hostage families and bereaved families from October 7th.Today, on a real day of grieving—and I think we see it abroad too—one of the first casualties of war is empathy and appreciation for the sanctity of life, writ large any life. That is certainly true from my vantage point. I see it in people’s inability to accept a mourning for October 7th with demonstrations in front of memorial services, but not to evade the question, I think that's very true of Israeli society when looking at Gaza or others. They see the strategic objectives—sometimes correctly—but have a deficit of empathy still stemming from that trauma. It'll take a long time to recover from it, if it begins to recover. Only then can we think about productive negotiations, hopefully sooner than later.Daniel Byman: Natan, can I push you a little bit on the Netanyahu question? A question I often get asked and frankly don't know the answer to is when you look at Israel's continuing operations, especially in Gaza, where it does seem that they've hit diminishing returns, at least militarily? How much of the war is continued because of Netanyahu's own political agenda versus a broader strategic concept?Dr. Sachs: It's a very hard thing to answer, in part because it's within the mind of one man. He's so analyzed, reviled by so many, and adored by others that I'm usually rather skeptical of anyone who claims to know the true answer to what Netanyahu is exactly thinking. Let me partially evade your question but say the more important point here. If a leader, during a time of war and the deepest crisis a country has faced with many ramifications in terms of economics, international standing, and more, has lost the trust and the faith of at least half the population, who are not sure that he is conducting things only for the best of the country and not his own political survival, he cannot function properly.There is certainly the very strong and just suspicion that his own political survival is important, at least in part. I think he's come to the stage of leadership where he truly believes that any other leader would be extremely damaging to Israel. “The state is I,” the old French adage, right? That is a very dangerous stage of leadership. Not coincidentally, he's a very long-standing leader, and that's characteristic of it.Dr. Alterman: Dan, let me ask you a question about Israeli strategy. I don't know the extent to which we can talk about it being Netanyahu's strategy or the Israeli government’s strategy. A year into this war, I've lost track of what a discrete concept of victory is on the Israeli side. As the war has spread into Lebanon, I don't know what victory in Lebanon looks like in any real military terms. As somebody who's thought a lot about fighting irregular warfare, is there something unique about this? Is there something misguided about the way Israelis are conceptualizing this?Dr. Byman: I think we can go theater by theater and get different answers but let me really focus first on Gaza and then on Lebanon. In Gaza and in general, the goal seems to be to destroy capacity. With Hamas in Gaza, the goal is to make it so they have no military ability, but defining that in a very expansive sense. You could say Hamas as a military organization is shattered compared to what it was a year ago, but I think Israel wants to ensure that not only are they not a threat today, but they will also never be a threat. That's an exceptionally difficult bar, right? I don't actually know how you do that on a practical level. In Gaza, Israel seems to want to eradicate or destroy Hamas, but not do what the United States would say is necessary, which would be a mix of having lots of troops there so you can keep Hamas down and at the same time putting in place some sort of political structure that replaces Hamas.As far as I can tell, Israel is kind of in whack-a-mole mode right now; it has a presence that goes after Hamas leadership. Hamas occasionally pops up, whether it's in a traditional stronghold or some part of Gaza where Israel has moved its presence away, and then Israel tries to suppress that presence there. Then, Hamas pops up somewhere else. This can go on for a long time. As Natan said, Israeli society is willing to bear significant costs and deal with very low risk thresholds, but to me, that’s not a winning strategy for the long term. I don't know what Israel gains or has gained in the last three or four months, and probably longer than that, from its presence in Gaza. Lebanon is very different, but there's a big question mark. Going back to capacity, Israel's goal is to make sure not only that Hezbollah won’t threaten it, but also that Hezbollah is unable to threaten it. Given Hezbollah’s vast rocket and missile arsenal, its skilled fighters, and its Iranian backing, that's exceptionally difficult.Dr. Alterman: And the porosity of the borders.Dr. Byman: And the porosity of its borders. The assumption is that whatever Hezbollah loses, Iran will eventually rebuild. In the last month, Israel has done a sustained series of operations against Hezbollah's leadership. Its military rank and file, its rocket launchers, and, more broadly, its arsenal have been far more effective than I would've said was likely. If you had asked me six months ago, I would've said that Israel would not be able to succeed to the degree it is. The question to me is, can Israel force Hezbollah to make significant concessions through force rather than through a broader deterrent threat? This is going to be difficult because Hezbollah can simply return. We saw this after 2006 where Hezbollah was supposed to leave the Israel-Gaza border and effectively didn't. As always, Hezbollah is a much harder problem than Hamas. There's a genuine question mark about what Israel's going to do in the long term there.Dr. Alterman: Especially with the vacuum in Lebanese politics, which Hezbollah has been filling, and the vacuum in Palestinian politics, where the Palestinian Authority (PA) has been unable to move in.Dr. Byman: That’s the huge question: who takes over? Dana, can I ask you to guide us on that one? Talk us through some of the Palestinian politics on all this.Dr. El Kurd: To understand the current situation in Palestinian politics, I want to preface by noting polling that just came out a few days ago from the Palestinian Center for Policy and Survey Research about, similar to what Natan was describing, the really vivid fear that Palestinians are living in during the aftermath of the last year of the war on Gaza. To Palestinians, this has been described as a genocide, and there are vivid fears this conflict will continue in the West Bank. There are fears of ethnic cleansing also happening in the West Bank. In the latest polling, 77 percent feared that the West Bank would be next in this level of violence.This past year has been an unfathomable level of violence to Palestinians. The way this is described, perhaps a little bit in the media, is as if Gaza is some sort of periphery, given the fragmentation of the Occupied Palestinian Territories. However, it's a sizable portion of the Palestinian body politics. For Palestinians, it's not seen as something happening on the periphery. Current Palestinian politics are based on this very real and growing fear that levels of violence will continue to escalate, and in the aftermath of October 7th, Palestinians are struggling with this kind of policy of fragmentation. There are many Palestinian stakeholders, such as Palestinians in Gaza, the West Bank, and elsewhere with different positionalities, but there isn't really a framework to incorporate all those different Palestinian stakeholders.In the aftermath of October 7th and the level of destruction we saw in Gaza, there's an urgency to resolve this fragmentation issue that limits the ability and capacity of Palestinians to negotiate and put up a united front. There are many discussions happening in Palestinian civil society and attempts to organize to push beyond the Palestinian Authority/Hamas binary, and to largely push beyond the Oslo framework.I could talk more about what those initiatives look like, but I think it's also important to note that we find ourselves in this kind of situation within Palestinian politics because even prior to October 7th and this last year, Hamas, Fatah, and the Palestinian Authority have had very little legitimacy in the Palestinian political space. This was some of the polling from before. Whether it's from the Palestinian Center or the Arab Barometer, a lot of the polling shows that neither Hamas nor Fatah achieved more than a 30 percent approval rating. In the latest polling, people were asked if parliamentary elections were held, which group would win? Fatah received 36 percent and Hamas only received 34 percent.There is this palpable lack of legitimacy for all ongoing actors. That is the problem when we talk about what happens now, in the aftermath, and on whatever day after is supposed to come. Who will govern? That is the biggest problem. If we don't move beyond this legitimacy issue for all the ongoing structures and the political parties involved in those structures, then we'll continue to have similar issues, like spoilers and other actors taking advantage of this vacuum. I could speak more about some of the civil society initiatives if we go in that direction.Dr. Sachs: I'd love to hear about those initiatives. The Palestinian Authority, led by Abbas, has been very lacking in legitimacy for a long time, and probably deservedly so, but it also seems almost impossible to see a clear alternative. Again and again in the polls I see Fatah and Hamas rated very, very low, but no one else is able to rival them. What are the chances of a third actor coming about? Or the chances of Fatah rejuvenating with different actors coming in or back in, and giving it a new energy that it used to have?Dr. El Kurd: The problem, as you mentioned, is that these political parties have been stagnant. Part of the push from Palestinian civil society is to start the discussion about moving beyond the Palestinian Authority/Hamas framework, moving beyond the Oslo framework, and trying to possibly revitalize the Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO) as an umbrella organization. These are the kinds of discussions and attempted initiatives that are ongoing. They also have to do with reviving party politics within these parties. Fatah has not organically fallen apart on its own in this stagnating way. Instead, it has to do with the fact that Fatah hasn't allowed young cadres to rise up in the party or to impact the party for quite some time. That's just one example.Some of the initiatives that I was alluding to have taken off after October 7th. There's the Palestinian National Conference, which is this attempt to revive the PLO and bring together actors from all the different political parties, as well as those outside those political parties. Polling shows that there's a lot of support for national unity governments in Palestinian society. In fact, in the latest polling, it was the biggest priority for what should happen and what the Palestinian Authority should take part in after the war in Gaza, whenever it ends. But as you mentioned, the seriousness of these political parties to actually reinvigorate politics within their parties and allow for new faces to emerge is a problem that still remains.The second thing is international buy-in for some of these initiatives. If there isn't support for an umbrella organization—whether it’s the PLO or we call it something else—that might incorporate Hamas as a junior partner, as has been discussed by some of the people involved in this initiative, or if there isn't support for Palestinian input on whatever the “day after” is going to be, and there's just continued discussions of different configurations of the status quo, then I worry that some of these initiatives don’t necessarily go very far.If Palestinian politics remains as it is, where there isn't a lot of serious international support and engagement with some of the Palestinian civil society initiatives, we're going to see more armed tactics. We're going to see more armed conflict and the devolvement into violence. But if it is taken seriously in an ideal scenario, then we might see a revival of some sort of national unity government or umbrella organization that has the legitimacy to negotiate and return to nonviolent methods of conflict resolution.Dr. Alterman: Dana, could I ask you and Natan a question? As I look at Palestinian-Israeli politics, each one seems to have a blocking third, whether it's Otzma Yehudit and other nationalist parties in Israel or Hamas with strong 30 percent support. You have religiously inflected groups that believe that this is not just between people, but that it's between people and God, and that it's about reclaiming holy land. It's one thing when you have small minorities that you have to manage. It feels to me, and correct me if I'm wrong, that you have these mirror images of about a third of the people on each side who are deeply nationalistic and religious and feel that it's national suicide to negotiate along the lines that Dana talked about. First, am I right in thinking that there is this religious nationalist blocking third on each side? Second how might the two-thirds, who are not in the blocking third, deal with the 30 percent who are? I'm not completely confident that it's a mirror image, but it feels very similar.Dr. Sachs: On the Israeli side, I agree and disagree. The good news is that if you look at the religious national element, the ones who would put religion deeply intertwined with and inherent to the national cause, it's significantly less than 30 percent. If you look at Smotrich and Ben-Gvir, the two figureheads of the far right today, they have much less than 30 percent.Dr. Alterman: They’re at 15 percent, right?Dr. Sachs: At best, and now their polling is actually down. Many of the voters, especially of Ben-Gvir, are not necessarily of the religious national ilk. Technically, religious nationalists, or modern Orthodox, are slightly more than 10 percent of the population. Not all of them are necessarily hardcore nationalists, although most are. That's the good news. Of course, there are the ultra-Orthodox who are a very different group. They are not necessarily as nationalist, although in recent years, they are much more so than in the past. But their leadership is more complex than that. That's the good news. The good news is the blocking third is significantly less than a third of that kind. That's on the religious side.There are two main impetuses for hawkishness among Israelis towards the Palestinian issue. One is the religious aspect. For someone like Smotrich, it's not a question of expediency or security. It's a question, as you said, of divine purpose—the whole purpose of this whole thing. It is about the land of Israel and the State of Israel within it. It has very little to do with other considerations.There's a second consideration, security, and that often coincides with the national one. Even people who are attached to the national or religious idea, but less so, can be very attached to the security question. It is significantly more than 30 percent. It's easily more than half of the population who are now extremely risk averse and have lost patience with the suggestion that they should be taking profound risks with their family because people will be angry at them abroad.Dr. Alterman: Last spring, a Pew poll last spring asked Israelis, \"Would a two-state solution deliver security?” Among Jewish Israelis, the number was 19 percent.Dr. Sachs: Exactly. Let me stress this point. If you somehow convince Israelis magically that there would be a Palestinian state, but there would be zero added risk security-wise, which is impossible, although there are very big differences to what kind of risk you add, I think you could muster a majority with the right leadership, not today, and not in the next year or two or five, but eventually you could do it. That proposition is extremely hard to sell to Israelis, especially today. A two-state solution, or a Palestinian state, at this moment in time among Israelis is a non-starter. A non-starter. Once you introduce demilitarization, which was always part of the negotiations, and spell out what demilitarization might mean, it looks slightly different. With time, the right leadership, and a lot of luck, it may be easier to do.To summarize, there's a big difference between religious or nationalist claims, which are strong. By the way, they're prevalent among many people who are not national religious. Many people on the left, even, feel a deep attachment to the land, et cetera. They just believe they should compromise on it, but it is a compromise for them. That is on one side. The second side is a security question. Especially after October 7th a year ago, that is an overwhelming argument, but it's one that our world of structured diplomacy and geopolitics can deal with at least. That is something one can discuss in some context. Not in the midst of a trauma, but if, hopefully, one can move past it and start recovering from it, it's not impossible to imagine.Dr. Alterman: Dana, how do you feel about the blocking third?Dr. El Kurd: We need to understand the polling around Hamas in a similar way as Natan described. This is not necessarily support for a religious view of the conflict. Support for Hamas does not necessarily equal a religious view of the conflict or a lack of space to compromise. I'm sure there are some elements of that among people who support Hamas. But whenever we see Hamas gaining in polls, it's usually support that's gained because of the failure of its opposite. There is a large element of that in the polling that we have to take into consideration. Another thing that we have to take into consideration is that the Palestinian stakeholders I was describing at the beginning are not just people in Gaza and the West Bank. Even the polls that we do have are more limited, and they don't get the full scope of where Palestinian politics stands today.That being said, Hamas, especially in the aftermath of 2017, has tried to emphasize its role as a national movement and its willingness to compromise around national issues. They know that's where Palestinian society is at. They can't gain as much traction with religiously inflicted narratives as much as they can with nationally motivated narratives. You're right that there is some element in Palestinian society that is zero-sum, and the ongoing violence really increases this. The violence creates the conditions for zero-sum understandings of the conflict. It's less so because of Hamas or Islamic Jihad, and more because of how people are understanding, absorbing, and processing the last year.That means that the space for negotiations is much smaller. Since before October 7th, a lot of us have been saying that if things went on that path and mass violence becomes the norm, the space for any kind of nonviolent resolution to the conflict becomes very narrow. So, I don't think it's necessarily just Hamas as a block. I think it's people who view this conflict as zero-sum as a result of the last year. That portion of the population has definitely increased.Dr. Alterman: Thank you.Dr. Byman: Jon, can I interrupt to ask you a question on the region? A very impressive Israeli diplomatic success is that the Arab partners that Israel has worked with, in some cases for years like Egypt, and in some cases more recently, have somewhat publicly stuck by Israel, at least in their actions. We are a year into this. No one here is saying the end is in sight. Is the support, or at least the quiet cooperation of Arab states, towards Israel something that's going to continue in the future? Is there a breaking point for them?Dr. Alterman: There's cooperation on Iran issues, because under the current government of Iran and its behaviors, they feel they have a common cause with Israel. There's growing frustration on the Palestinian issue, not because they support Hamas, but to a country. All the governments in the region are hostile to Hamas as a popularly elected, Islamically inflected political movement that, as far as they're concerned, wants to push them from power in their own countries. But there is a sense in the region that Israelis believe that Arab lives are particularly cheap, and that's offensive. There is concern with public opinion that I see manifested. There's a certain sense in the public of a profound lack of Israeli respect, which I think frankly some Arab governments also practice with their own people, but this doesn't help legitimate what governments want to do with Israel.The other piece of this that's really important—I would be interested in hearing Dana's perspective on this as well—is that, as a democracy, Israel has a pretty relentless timeline. It has an independent press and people are saying, \"Okay, so where's this going? That was last week. What about this week? Where's this going? When is this going to end?\" There's an insistence on accountability and on the refinement of policy. When it comes to the issue of cooperation or normalization with Israel, a lot of the leaders in the region think they have 40 years to play this out. For them, it doesn't matter what happens this week. They can park the issue for one year, five years. As far as they're concerned, their people aren't fighting and dying. They have other things going on. There's some frustration in the Gulf. They feel their main issue is diversifying their economies and attracting foreign investment, and this war is taking all the attention away from the good things they're doing and getting people agitated, on top of raising risks in the region.But rather than breaking anything irretrievably, they’re likely to park things for quite some time. Don't forget, this is a region where the Saudis and the Iranians, after long being deeply hostile for years and years and years, threatening each other, shutting down embassies, and everything else, the Iranian president met with the Saudi foreign minister last week. People said, “Yeah, that's the right thing to have happen.” You can accelerate or you can deepen cooperation, but everyone has a much longer timeframe, and the people at the wheel now are likely to be the same people there in the future. Sultan bin Abdulaziz was the foreign minister of Saudi Arabia for 48 years. I'm not saying the current foreign minister is going to be there for 40 years, but it's certainly possible. The crown prince expects to be guiding Saudi foreign policy for decades to come. The ruler of the UAE, Mohamed bin Zayed, expects to be there for a long time. Everybody is playing a longer strategic game.You can talk about Netanyahu's politics and Netanyahu's survival. The only survival most leaders in the Arab world are worried about is biological, not political. That gives you a different amount of freedom and a different perspective on a lot of these issues. I want to ask Dana a question about regional politics, but before I do that, I just want to tell the viewers that you can send questions into the panel through the CSIS web page or the YouTube page. Dana, how do you see the regional picture? How do you see the rebirth of Arab interests in Palestine? Is any of this consequential for Palestinians or for regional politics? How does it look from the regional perspective?Dr. El Kurd: When we discuss Palestine and the region, there's been a lot of focus on the Axis, Iranian-backed militias, and that kind of framing for how Palestine might impact the region and vice versa. But understanding how Palestine intersects with regional politics must take a wider approach to understand how Arab societies, not just related to these particular militia groups, view Palestine. It is really important for understanding regional opposition and dissent. Palestine as an issue area is a gateway to dissent because it represents this lack of accountability that you mentioned.I agree with you. Arab governments have this long view of their role in the conflict, but given ramifications of events like the Arab Spring, it may be too comfortable for them to assume that Palestine won't play a larger role in regional dynamics, especially in terms of oppositional politics and the way Arab societies, publics, civil societies, and protest movements are absorbing the information of the last year of war and using it to channel their own grievances. That’s one component. Protests might move in a direction people don't suspect. Another thing is the expansion of the war with no end in sight lends credence to the ideas and narratives of the Axis with the different actors, whether it's Hezbollah or Hamas. It increases the degree to which Arab societies view this as a zero-sum conflict, and that can only escalate the likelihood of regional destabilization. Even if particular Arab governments have a long view of the ongoing crisis and possibly think they can return to normalization at some point, it's not as simple as that. There are a lot of risks in this kind of strategy.Dr. Alterman: Am I correct in feeling that Arab governments have been restrictive in an unprecedented way about protests over Palestine? Under what circumstances would they be less restrictive in your judgment?Dr. El Kurd: As I said, especially in the aftermath of the Arab Spring, the issue area of Palestine has become more of a hot topic to Arab governments. They're much more wary of allowing those kinds of protests and that kind of dissent. In the past, it used to be kind of a safety valve. It was one of the only issue areas in some cases in which people could express some political dissent, but that quickly led and metastasized to other issue areas. Arab governments have been very restrictive as a result of that decade of learning about the role of Palestine in the aftermath of the Arab Spring.They have allowed it at this moment in a very limited sense, and it really depends on a country-by-country basis. It's only been allowed if you are expressing humanitarian concern and fear for people in Gaza. If that transforms into some kind of critique of government policy or the government's approach to any of these issues, including normalization, that has been very swiftly shut down across the region. This is not a very good long-term or winning strategy for them.Dr. Alterman: Let me ask another regional question. You talked about the way this war is spreading. One way it hasn't yet spread profoundly, but may spread, is in the Israel-Iran direct confrontation. Iran has lobbed missiles at Israel, and Israel has fired back, but we haven't seen anything that I would call normal war between the two. Natan, how do you see the Israel-Iran axis unfolding? Do you see it playing out into a direct war? Do you see it playing out in an indirect war in which Iran works through Palestinian groups? What should we be looking for? What should we be worried about?Dr. Sachs: That's a good question. This is almost the flip side of what Dana was saying, but I don't think this contradicts it at all. A very prevalent Israeli perspective pits the two views of the Middle East against each other. One, a Palestinian-centric view that when you speak about Middle East peace, that's almost synonymous with Israeli-Palestinian peace as if there's no other problem in the region, which is obviously incorrect—although the conflict is very emotive in the Arab world. That would be a worldview. Netanyahu wrote a chapter in a book back in the early nineties called the “Myth of Palestinian Centrality”. The flip side of this view revolves around the theme of Iran versus Israel and Iran versus some of the Sunni Arab States. The Palestinian issue is useful for Iran as a ticket into the Sunni-dominated world, because they can raise the mantle that the main Sunni states are not.Dr. Alterman: So it's a Sunni Arab issue for a Shiʿite power?Dr. Sachs: Exactly, for a Shiʿite power.Dr. Alterman: Non-Arab, non-Sunni.Dr. Sachs: They are the only ones raising the banner truly. Pitting the two views against each other is wrong because both can be true. Both the centrality of the Palestinian issue, certainly in Arab, but also in Muslim politics writ large in the Islamic world. I used to live in Indonesia and most people couldn't point to Palestine if you asked them to, but it was an emotive issue even all the way there, and it certainly is now. On the hand, if you look at this war, it is not coincidental that all the cases of actual fighting have been surrounding the Axis of Resistance and nowhere else. It has been seven fronts, as the Israelis like to say. It has been focused on Hamas, Hezbollah, the Houthis, militias in Iraq and in Syria, and Iran itself. All of these are precisely the Axis of Resistance that Iran leads. Hamas is certainly not a Shiʿite organization, but nonetheless align more loosely with the Iranian Axis.Therefore, there were also those in Israel who very early on, as early as October 11th, 2023, proposed not invading or pushing into Gaza in retaliation first, but going north first. A lot of what we see now, perhaps what Dana was referencing or perhaps the assassination of Nasrallah, was considered seriously on October 11th and was pushed back by someone in the Israeli leadership or by President Biden. Eventually Netanyahu decided against it. That worldview says that at the end of the day, this is fundamentally an Iran-Israel fight. Of course, Hamas has its own calculations. It's not a tool of Iran, but it is backed by it. The stronger enemy was Hezbollah, the main tool of Iran, and therefore the war should go there.This was a long way of prefacing. This lends itself to the current Israeli campaign, which given the successes Dan described, and despite the cost of life in Lebanon, has very widespread support in Israel. Especially because, second only to the hostages, there is a feeling that the residents of northern Israel, the tens of thousands of Israelis who evacuated after October 8th when Hezbollah joined the fight, have to return home. To do that, they have to feel a sense of safety, not only in agreement with Hezbollah or Lebanon more broadly, but a sense that Hezbollah is not able to carry out an October 7th of their own on the border, which Hezbollah said it would, at least in terms of invading Israel. A retaliation by Israel for the last ballistic missile fired from Iran is likely. I can't tell you what it is.There is a lot of effort by the United States to limit it and to try to move towards de-escalation. There is a real chance that Israel, including Netanyahu, see their gains in Lebanon and a true degrading of Hezbollah and its capacity. Therefore, they might see an opening to de-escalation since at the end of the day, they will need to deal with Lebanon to bring back their civilians in the north, which is the political imperative domestically.Dr. Alterman: Though arguably, all the big bets and risks that Netanyahu has taken for the last six months, in Netanyahu's eyes, have worked out better than everybody told him they would. Whether it's going to Rafah, going to Lebanon, or assassinating Nasrallah, all that could really go badly hasn't gone badly.Dr. Sachs: That's true. It's a very strong sense in Israel, and that's Netanyahu's political line as well. It's a line that says, “Look, I withstood all this pressure. You can't imagine what pressure. Look at the opposition leaders or anyone else in Israel. Who else could have withstood this pressure? Therefore, I am the only one able to conduct this long term.” He's gained some popularity, and he's risen in the polls somewhat, but he is not leading in the polls. He would still likely draw at best, and a draw is a victory of sorts for him, as we've seen in recent years, but he would draw at best. He could easily lose elections. More importantly from his perspective, he may be thwarting elections. They're only scheduled for two years from now. Given that he also managed to bring someone else into the coalition, he could easily try to maneuver politics that way, or he could go a completely different route, which is to say, “My big successes mean I can chance elections.” I don't know what the next move is with Iran. We are in a very different territory than we were after April 13th when Iran fired directly at Israel for the first time in history. Actually fighting from Iran and sustaining direct, overt Israeli responses on Iran. We're in a very different territory. Part of what we're seeing today, the Israeli calculation and response is based on whether that equation can stand. It is based on whether Iran can now calculate that regardless of Iran and Israel exchanging some fire, the rules of the game are that the great powers, especially the United States, come in and quiet things down. While the Israelis say, “No, the rules have to be before that. You dare not shoot at Israel. That must be the rules of the game.” This is very dangerous, but all the dire warnings of many of these things have come, at most, to partial fruition. In Lebanon, we have not yet seen the all-out war that we fear.Dr. Alterman: Dan, let me ask you. You've thought about Hezbollah, Hamas, and irregular warfare more generally. Are you surprised that Iran hasn't more aggressively advanced the Axis of Resistance against an Israeli onslaught, or is this what you would've thought would happen?Dr. Byman: I've certainly been surprised by how much Israel has been able to degrade Hezbollah. Iran was always a relatively weak actor in terms of its own capabilities. Its conventional military forces are quite limited, it's limited economically, and most of its systems are not advanced. Yes, it has these rocket missile forces, but this has been an education for many people in the importance of air defense. For many years, Israel has been working on developing a truly comprehensive air defense system against small, short-range stuff from Gaza, but also, the big, ballistic missiles that are hard to shoot down from Iran. We knew before this that they had made a lot of progress.I'm a little less surprised that Iran has been cautious, but there are some question marks. This has not just been provocative for Iran; it's been embarrassing for Iran. Israel has not only degraded its major proxy, Hezbollah, but it's done significant operations at the heart of the regime in Iran itself. Iran's responses have been lackluster, and one possibility is Iran backs away, right? It doesn't have to necessarily fight. If Israel has a mid-level response, Iran may say, “We'll strike another day,” but do nothing in reality.Dr. Alterman: As they did after the April 13 strikes.Dr. Byman: Exactly. But Iran might also move towards international terrorism here. We've seen that in the past with attacks in Bulgaria and Argentina. This is a way of responding when you can't respond through traditional military means. But I will say, there's still a big question about what Hezbollah is going to be able to do with the remainder of its arsenal. Is it unable to fight? Has it simply taken so many losses that it can't use this effectively? Or is it actually trying to avoid further escalation? I'm not quite sure what Hezbollah would be fearing, so I do think the degradation is quite real.I did want to throw in one point that is a little different, and I'd like to get Dana's or anyone else’s views on this. It is about the Palestinian Authority as a partner for Israel. From a counter-terrorism point of view, the PA in the West Bank has been incredibly important on a day-to-day basis in helping Israel ensure its security in the West Bank, going after Hamas remnants, and—in the eyes of critical Palestinians—being a handmaiden of the Israeli occupation. There seems to be a fairly dramatic shift in Israeli policy in terms of disregarding the PA leadership and doing major operations in the West Bank with no concern for their political standing.That's a very significant shift. Thank goodness that hasn't been as bloody as Gaza or Lebanon, but it's certainly causing a lot of casualties, and it also raises the long-term question of what the West Bank is going to look like.Dr. El Kurd: This is reflected in some of the polling I mentioned earlier. This is why the Palestinian public fears that the levels of violence we're seeing in Gaza might be replicated in some way. In fact, we are seeing some tactics replicated in the West Bank, where we have the razing of infrastructure and attacks on hospitals. The Palestinian Authority is absolutely not seen as a deterrent in its relationship with Israel. As you mentioned, for most Palestinians, it's seen as a subcontractor of occupation. There was this element that they might be able to negotiate with Israel or, as the Israeli partner, might be able to engage the Israeli state in some way. As you mentioned, that's not happening.To be honest, this is merely a continuing trend from what we were already seeing. Especially after the Unity Intifada time frame, between May and August of 2021, this has been the case. There have been extensive operations in the West Bank against different militant groups that have popped up unrelated to Hamas, and there have been extensive operations in urban centers like Nablus. The Palestinian Authority has not been able to act as some sort of partner with Israel, and that already damaged its role and legitimacy with the Palestinian public long before last year.This only puts the last nail in the coffin for the Palestinian Authority as some sort of legitimate actor that represents Palestinians in the West Bank. I am concerned that if the international community or the United States doesn't lend credence to some of the other attempts to create legitimacy in Palestinian politics, then that vacuum is going to be filled by other types of political action.Dr. Alterman: There are too many PhDs sitting in this room for me not to ask a question about grading. We have a question here: how would you grade the Biden administration's performance toward this conflict? The Biden administration had a theory of the case. It's been very active diplomatically with Israel and Arab states. It has chosen what to engage on, how to engage, and what to say. Understanding that GPAs are now rising, and average grades are somewhere above A-minus at many leading schools, how would you grade the administration's strategic performance? Not that it can control the situation, but it can control what it does.Dr. Sachs: I'm going to let the active professors take that first.Dr. Byman: Dana, I'll start but ask you to correct me. Leverage on the primary actor, which is Israel, is exceptionally weak. That's because, as Natan said at the beginning, the stakes for most Israelis. There is widespread support across party lines that this is an existential moment and that the country is facing a wide range of threats and that a very strong response is required.With that in mind, I think the administration has set a number of red lines, the vast majority of which have been violated. Then, it sets them again, and they get violated or ignored. That looks foolish. It's hard. I don't have great answers for how you do this. It would require a reconsideration of how the United States has approached Israel and changing it dramatically from the U.S. response for the last 50 years, where the United States really effectively doesn't support Israel as much.If you look at the latest confrontation with Iran, the United States is trying to have it both ways. It's saying, “Don't escalate, but we're going to provide you intelligence, we're giving you military support. We're going to stand by you 100 percent.” That's not the biggest threat against escalation. In fact, one could argue it's exactly the opposite. It's basically giving a green light. In that sense, I would say the United States has certainly not done well. It looks foolish to many Arab audiences, and it looks weak.At the same time, the Biden administration scored a lot of points with Israelis, which was their primary audience. Especially early in the conflict, there was a genuine sense of empathy by the President that went a long way and gained some goodwill where there was a lot of concerned suspicion. I'm sympathetic because I don't have great ideas myself for how the United States should change its approach to the conflict, but I can't look around and say the United States has been incredibly successful.Dr. Alterman: Professor Byman, is that a B?Dr. Byman: It depends on what standard you want to use, but yeah, I'll put it in the B range. I think that's fair.Dr. Alterman: Dana?Dr. El Kurd: Wow. It's so generous. I guess I'm a hard grader. I recently wrote something that the editors then titled “Washington Isn't Learning Any Lessons.”Dr. Alterman: It's an interesting piece in Foreign Policy.Dr. El Kurd: It came out today. The empathy that you mentioned that was shown to an Israeli audience and won the Biden administration points on that front was matched by a complete lack of empathy towards Palestinians and towards Arabs, generally. That has harmed the Biden administration here in this country as well.I am particularly concerned and frustrated with the fact that, whatever the diplomatic behind-the-scenes is happening, as you said, there were all these red lines that were crossed. Any ideas that we have about what the “day after” might look like in the Palestinian Territories, or how we might get back to a framework to resolve this conflict in some way, have not deviated very much from reconfigurations of the original status quo that led us here. It seems like the Biden administration hasn't been able to absorb the lessons of its first three years in office, which led to the point where October 7th happened and where this war unfolds. There have been no new ideas about where we go from here. That's really the biggest concern.Obviously, all the red lines that were crossed during the last year, the lack of response, and the lack of capacity to try to condition aid in some way are very harmful with Arab, Palestinian, and global audiences. The biggest concern is: where do we go from here? Have the United States’ decision-makers thought to themselves, “We have to try something other than what we've been trying?” I don't see a lot of evidence of that and that's why I would grade them much more poorly.Dr. Sachs: I wanted to make two points. First, when you're thinking about the success of U.S. strategy, the question is about goals. The U.S. administration and Biden himself early on were not just trying to gain Israeli solidarity. He genuinely identified with the two main Israeli goals, which were that Hamas should not be allowed to govern and poses a threat from the Gaza Strip and the return of the hostages, including American hostages. Those were the main goals.A lot of difficult that people have is with the duality of the United States’ position. On one hand, it's the guarantor of the world order and seen as the one that should restore equilibrium. When something goes wrong almost anywhere, but certainly in the Middle East, people say, \"Who in Washington is in charge of this? Who in Washington is fixing this problem?\"On the other hand, the United States, and certainly President Biden, see themselves as an ally of Israel. Therefore, the administration has, at times, felt that if it aired grievances with Netanyahu, regarding a hostage deal or ceasefire deal, it would be understood as if Hamas is not an obstacle for it. This would cause Hamas, and Sinwar in particular, to harden their positions. They're playing this dual game.The second point I wanted to make is that, very early on, even during the very first speech that Biden gave, the administration correctly identified their main geopolitical, strategic goal and unique position in minimizing the risk for all-out war. They were correct in understanding that it is a role that only the United States can play.Now, we're much closer to an all-out war right now than we were just a few months ago, but nonetheless, on this, I think their grade is pretty high. Recall that, not only did President Biden, very early on, issue a stern warning to any state and any organization, which everyone understood as Iran and Hezbollah, from joining the fight, but he also moved a carrier group and then a second carrier group.Then, on April 13th and14th, the United States Central Command coordinated a historic event. It was not just a historic Iranian attack; it was a joint coalition led by CENTCOM in which Arab states defended Israel actively. That happened again now. Whether good or bad, that is very much in service of the goal that Biden identified. That could come to naught, but it offsets a lot of the frustration that they and others have felt on the Gaza front.Dr. Alterman: You've been an adjunct instructor at Georgetown. What would you give the administration?Dr. Sachs: I would go to the dean at the time, Dan Byman, and ask him. It depends on which realm. On the regional realm, it might be an A-minus. On the Gaza front, it's much harder to decide because of exactly what Dan said. They have a very difficult partner on the Israeli side. They also have an impossible player here, which is Sinwar. Sinwar has veto on this all the time.Israelis complain vociferously against Netanyahu and his prioritization of things and the lack of prioritization of a hostage deal. Criticism that I share. But what Israelis say that they are assuming, of course, is that Sinwar is worse by far. 100 times over, and I'm not trying to make some equivalence or something. That's not my point. My point is that the administration is trying to play a game where both of these very difficult actors have veto. The result has been lacking. Unlike everyone else on Twitter and everywhere else, I am not as smart to know what all of the answers would've been.Dr. Alterman: We are going to have to leave it there. I want to thank my colleagues, Dana El Kurd, Dan Byman, and Natan Sachs. Thank you all for joining us. There is a theme page on the CSIS homepage that has a lot of our work on the conflict and current assessments, including a new report that Dan published, some things that I have published. I've really learned a lot from this conversation. I hope you have too. We will put a transcript up quickly and we look forward to seeing you all again very soon. Thank you much for joining us.\nTags\nMiddle East,\nGeopolitics and International Security, and\nTerrorism and Irregular Warfare\nPrograms & Projects\nMiddle East Program\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nEgypt, Israel, and the Levant\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nInternational Security Program\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"一个燃烧的地区——10月7日一年后\n照片：CSIS\n文字记录\n——2024年10月7日\n可下载内容\n下载文字记录\n224kb\n图像\n照片：CSIS\n一个燃烧的地区——10月7日一年后\n2024年10月7日 • 美国东部时间下午4:00–5:00\n由中东项目主办\n本文字记录来自CSIS于2024年10月7日举办的一场活动。点击此处观看完整视频。乔恩·B·奥尔特曼：下午好，晚上好。我是乔恩·奥尔特曼，CSIS高级副总裁、布热津斯基全球安全与地缘战略讲席教授兼中东项目主任。今天我们召开这场讨论会，探讨10月7日这一庄严事件。一年前的这一天引发了中东地区大量的暴力与苦难，加深了整个地区各个社群的创伤。要讨论这些事件，更重要的是分析它们对该地区当前及未来走向的影响，今天我邀请了三位同事参加。我们今天的目标，是从不同角度进行互动式对话，了解去年的10月7日事件如何帮助塑造了这个地区，以及该地区将走向何方。\n\n首先与我一同出席的是我的同事纳坦·萨克斯，他是中东政策中心主任，也是布鲁金斯学会外交政策项目高级研究员。我还邀请了我的朋友兼同事丹·拜曼，他是CSIS战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目高级研究员、乔治城大学教授，以及该校安全研究项目主任。我还邀请了达娜·埃尔·库尔德教授，她是里士满大学政治学助理教授，也是华盛顿阿拉伯中心的高级非驻会研究员。我们所有人都以高度专注的态度参与这次讨论，但我们的背景和观点也截然不同。在展望该地区未来时，我非常希望了解我们如何彼此学习。达娜，你先开始吧？达娜·埃尔·库尔德：非常感谢邀请我。我想问纳坦一个关于以色列社会的问题：以色列社会正在如何消化这些变化，以及冲突中的一些转变。昨天，内塔尼亚胡总理说，以色列正在七条战线上对抗“文明的敌人”。你能否进一步告诉我们，以色列社会对此的立场是什么？民意调查显示，他们对谈判或持续冲突的意愿如何？我试图理解这一切中的社会层面。纳坦·萨克斯：谢谢你，达娜。尽管是在这样的一天，能和大家在一起仍然很好。尤其是今天，在以色列当然是极其庄严的一天，也是一个真正深切哀悼的日子。并不是要以任何方式贬低其他任何人的苦难，但当然，在以色列，一年前发生的事件仍在被人们深刻消化。人们曾将以色列人、巴勒斯坦人，如今还有黎巴嫩人，称为处于某种创伤后状态。事实是，他们并非处于创伤后状态。他们仍深深处于创伤之中；以色列人也是如此，他们仍在经历并反复重温一年前的事件。以色列人有时把今天称为“十月的第367天”，部分原因是一些人质仍被关押，部分原因是战争仍在继续。总体而言，以色列社会仍然感到一种非常深刻的脆弱感，这种感受会让那些将以色列视为非常强大、有实力的邻国感到意外。以色列人，尤其是鉴于10月7日的事件，感到\n极其脆弱。人们有着非常个人化的恐惧——这往往是内战的燃料。我过去常说，巴勒斯坦人如今当然也包括黎巴嫩人，只是方式不同，却有着同样的感受，而这是非常危险的。这种恐惧会助长此类冲突。人们有这样一种感觉：如果你不保护自己、不先发制人、不用你拥有的一切保护自己，那么你的亲人可能会遭遇像一年前10月7日那样的命运。这意味着，以色列社会非常厌恶风险。谈判当然是可以考虑的。明显多数的以色列人赞成谈判——即使是与哈马斯谈判——以停火换取人质，尽管在他们看来代价极其高昂。但对于任何他们认为会让以色列再次面临风险的事情，他们也深深反感进行谈判，包括在现阶段建立巴勒斯坦国。他们担心，西岸的巴勒斯坦国将意味着以色列中部的主要平民人口会面临与加沙附近居民同样的危机。你提到了内塔尼亚胡。他的言辞往往带有好战色彩。我常常觉得这令人反感。他在以色列极不受欢迎，并被视为对10月7日负责，因为他是当时掌舵的人。并不是说以色列人反对战争目标，尤其是加沙的两个明确目标——推翻哈马斯在那里作为统治力量的地位，这一点基本已经实现；以及让人质获释——或者削弱伊朗支持的“抵抗轴心”其余部分，尤其是真主党。然而，你会看到人们在他的战术、手段，以及许多以色列人认为他把自己的政治命运置于优先地位这几点上存在严重分歧。我可以举一个今天的例子，然后就说到这里，让其他人发言。今天，以色列有两个主要的10月7日纪念仪式。一个由国家主办。随后，遇难者家属立即反对由这个政府来组织，因为正是同一个政府在10月7日执政。因此，还有第二场仪式，他们称之为“全国性的”仪式，或使用其他名称，主要由人质家属和10月7日遇难者家属组织。今天，在这样一个真正哀悼的日子里——我认为我们在国外也能看到这一点——战争最先夺走的东西之一，就是同理心以及对生命神圣性的尊重，不分对象，任何生命都一样。从我的角度看，情况确实如此。我看到，人们无法接受对10月7日的哀悼，却在纪念仪式前举行示威；但不回避这个问题，我认为，以色列社会在看待加沙或其他地方时确实如此。他们看到了战略目标——有时判断是正确的——但仍然缺乏同理心，这种缺失仍源于那场创伤。如果它开始恢复，也将需要很长时间才能从中恢复。只有到那时，我们才能考虑富有成效的谈判，希望这一天能早日到来。丹尼尔·拜曼：纳坦，我能否就内塔尼亚胡的问题再追问你一点？我经常被问到一个问题，说实话我也不知道答案：当你审视以色列持续不断的军事行动，尤其是在加沙的行动时，至少从军事角度看，它们似乎已经出现边际收益递减，这场战争有多少是因为内塔尼亚胡自己的政治议程而继续，又有多少是出于更广泛的战略构想？萨克斯博士：这是一个非常难回答的问题，部分原因在于答案存在于一个人的头脑之中。他被如此深入地分析，被许多人痛恨，也被另一些人崇拜，以至于我通常对任何一个\n声称知道内塔尼亚胡究竟在想什么的真正答案。让我部分回避你的问题，但说一下这里更重要的一点。如果一位领导人在战争期间、在一个国家所面临的最深重危机之际——这场危机在经济、国际地位以及其他方面都有诸多影响——失去了至少一半人口的信任和支持，而这些人不确定他这样做是否只是为了国家的最大利益，而不是为了自己的政治生存，那么他就无法正常履职。当然，有非常强烈且合理的怀疑认为，他自己的政治生存至少在一定程度上很重要。我认为，他已经走到了这样一个领导阶段：他确实相信，任何其他领导人都会对以色列造成极其严重的损害。“国家就是我”，那句古老的法国格言，对吧？这是一个非常危险的领导阶段。并非巧合的是，他是一位任职时间极长的领导人，而这正是这种情况的特征。\n\n阿尔特曼博士：丹，让我问你一个关于以色列战略的问题。我不知道我们在多大程度上可以把它称为内塔尼亚胡的战略，或者以色列政府的战略。这场战争进行了一年之后，我已经弄不清以色列方面所谓明确的胜利概念是什么了。随着战争蔓延到黎巴嫩，我不知道从任何真正的军事意义上说，在黎巴嫩取得胜利会是什么样子。作为一个对打击非正规战争思考很多的人，这件事有什么独特之处吗？以色列人构想这件事的方式是否存在某种误导？\n\n拜曼博士：我认为，我们可以逐个战区讨论，并得到不同的答案，但让我先重点谈谈加沙，然后谈黎巴嫩。在加沙，总体而言，目标似乎是摧毁作战能力。对于加沙的哈马斯，目标是让他们不再具备任何军事能力，但这是以一种非常宽泛的意义来界定的。你可以说，与一年前相比，哈马斯作为一个军事组织已经被击溃，但我认为以色列希望确保的并不只是他们今天不构成威胁，而是他们今后永远不会构成威胁。这是一个极其难以达到的标准，对吧？实际上，我不知道在实践层面该如何做到这一点。在加沙，以色列似乎想要铲除或摧毁哈马斯，但又不愿做美国认为必要的事情，即派驻大量部队，以便压制哈马斯，同时建立某种取代哈马斯的政治结构。\n\n据我所知，以色列目前有点处于“打地鼠”模式；它保持军事存在，追击哈马斯领导层。哈马斯偶尔会在某处冒头，不管是在传统据点，还是在以色列撤走军事存在的加沙某个地区，然后以色列就试图压制那里的存在。接着，哈马斯又会在别处冒头。这种情况可能会持续很长时间。正如纳坦所说，以色列社会愿意承受巨大的代价，也愿意接受极低的风险阈值，但在我看来，这不是一种长期的制胜战略。我不知道在过去三四个月，甚至可能更长时间里，以色列从其在加沙的军事存在中获得了什么，或者说已经获得了什么。黎巴嫩的情况非常不同，但仍然存在一个巨大的问号。回到作战能力这一点，以色列的目标是不仅确保真主党不会威胁它，还要确保真主党无法威胁它。鉴于真主党庞大的火箭和导弹武器库、训练有素的战士以及伊朗的支持，这极其困难。\n\n阿尔特曼博士：还有渗透性\n边界的渗透性。拜曼博士：以及其边界的渗透性。假设是，无论真主党失去什么，伊朗最终都会重建。过去一个月，以色列持续对真主党领导层展开一系列行动。其军事基层人员、火箭发射器，以及更广泛而言，其武器库，都比我原先认为可能达到的效果有效得多。如果你六个月前问我，我会说以色列不可能取得如今这样的成功。对我来说，问题在于，以色列能否通过武力，而不是通过更广泛的威慑威胁，迫使真主党作出重大让步？这将很困难，因为真主党可以简单地卷土重来。2006年之后我们就看到过这种情况：真主党本应离开以色列—加沙边境，但实际上并没有。像往常一样，真主党是一个比哈马斯棘手得多的问题。以色列长期打算在那里做什么，确实是个大问号。\n\n奥尔特曼博士：尤其是在黎巴嫩政治真空之中——真主党一直在填补这个真空；还有巴勒斯坦政治真空，巴勒斯坦民族权力机构（PA）一直无法进入其中。\n\n拜曼博士：这就是最大的疑问：谁来接管？达娜，我能请你来引导我们讨论这个问题吗？请你谈谈这其中的一些巴勒斯坦政治情况。\n\n埃尔·库尔德博士：要理解巴勒斯坦政治的当前局势，我想先提到几天前刚公布的一项民调。这项民调来自巴勒斯坦政策与调查研究中心，内容与纳坦所描述的情况相似，显示在过去一年加沙战争的余波中，巴勒斯坦人正生活在一种极其真切的恐惧之中。对巴勒斯坦人而言，这被描述为一场种族灭绝，而且他们强烈担心冲突会在约旦河西岸继续。他们还担心约旦河西岸也会发生种族清洗。在最新民调中，77%的人担心约旦河西岸会成为下一个遭受这种程度暴力的地方。\n\n过去一年对巴勒斯坦人来说，是难以想象的暴力程度。媒体对这一情况的描述，也许多少带有一种把加沙视为某种边缘地区的意味，因为被占领的巴勒斯坦领土处于碎片化状态。然而，加沙是巴勒斯坦政治共同体中相当大的一部分。对巴勒斯坦人而言，这并不是发生在边缘地区的事情。当前的巴勒斯坦政治建立在一种非常现实且不断加剧的恐惧之上：暴力程度将继续升级；而在10月7日之后，巴勒斯坦人正努力应对这种碎片化政策。巴勒斯坦有许多利益相关方，例如加沙、约旦河西岸以及其他地方的巴勒斯坦人，他们的立场各不相同，但目前确实没有一个框架能够纳入所有这些不同的巴勒斯坦利益相关方。\n\n在10月7日之后，以及我们在加沙看到的破坏程度之下，解决碎片化问题变得十分紧迫，因为这种碎片化限制了巴勒斯坦人进行谈判和形成统一立场的能力与容量。巴勒斯坦公民社会正在展开许多讨论，也在尝试组织起来，以推动超越“巴勒斯坦民族权力机构/哈马斯”二元框架，并在很大程度上超越奥斯陆框架。\n\n我可以进一步谈谈这些倡议的具体样貌，但我认为还必须指出，我们之所以在巴勒斯坦政治中陷入这种局面，是因为即使在10月7日之前、在过去这一年之前，哈马斯、法塔赫和巴勒斯坦\n权威机构在巴勒斯坦政治空间中的合法性一直非常有限。这是之前的一些民调结果。无论是巴勒斯坦中心还是阿拉伯晴雨表，许多民调都显示，哈马斯和法塔赫的支持率都没有超过30%。在最新的民调中，人们被问及：如果举行议会选举，哪个集团会获胜？法塔赫获得36%，而哈马斯仅获得34%。\n\n所有现有行动者都明显缺乏合法性。这就是我们在讨论现在、战后以及所谓“次日”将会发生什么时所面临的问题。谁来治理？这是最大的问题。如果我们不能超越所有现有结构以及参与这些结构的各个政党的合法性问题，那么我们将继续面临类似的问题，例如破坏者和其他行动者利用这一真空。我还可以谈谈一些公民社会倡议，如果我们要朝这个方向继续讨论的话。\n\n萨克斯博士：我很想听听这些倡议。由阿巴斯领导的巴勒斯坦权力机构长期以来一直严重缺乏合法性，而且可能确实如此，但似乎几乎不可能看到一个明确的替代方案。在一次又一次的民调中，我看到法塔赫和哈马斯的评价都非常、非常低，但没有其他人能够与它们匹敌。第三方行动者出现的可能性有多大？或者，法塔赫通过不同的行动者加入或回归而重新焕发生机、获得它过去曾拥有的新活力的可能性有多大？\n\n库尔德博士：正如你所提到的，问题在于这些政党一直停滞不前。巴勒斯坦公民社会推动的一部分工作，是开始讨论如何超越巴勒斯坦权力机构—哈马斯框架，超越奥斯陆框架，并尝试让巴勒斯坦解放组织（PLO）作为一个伞形组织重新焕发生机。这些讨论和尝试中的倡议正在持续进行。它们还涉及在这些政党内部恢复党派政治。法塔赫并不是以这种停滞的方式自然而然地瓦解的。相反，问题在于，长期以来法塔赫一直不允许年轻干部在党内崛起，也不允许他们影响该党。这只是一个例子。\n\n我之前提到的一些倡议是在10月7日之后启动的。其中有巴勒斯坦全国会议，这是一次试图重振巴解组织、汇集所有不同政党行动者以及这些政党之外人士的努力。民调显示，巴勒斯坦社会非常支持民族团结政府。事实上，在最新的民调中，这是人们认为加沙战争结束后——无论何时结束——应该发生什么以及巴勒斯坦权力机构应参与什么的首要优先事项。但正如你所提到的，这些政党是否真正认真地重振党内政治并允许新面孔出现，仍然是一个问题。\n\n第二件事是国际社会是否支持其中一些倡议。如果没有人支持一个伞形组织——无论是巴解组织，还是我们称之为其他名称的组织——而这个组织可能会把哈马斯纳入其中，作为初级伙伴，正如参与这项倡议的一些人士所讨论的那样；或者，如果不支持巴勒斯坦人参与所谓“次日”将会是什么的安排，而只是继续讨论“\n不同的现状配置，那么我担心这些倡议中的一些未必能走得很远。如果巴勒斯坦政治维持现状，在这种情况下，国际社会并没有对一些巴勒斯坦公民社会倡议提供大量认真支持和参与，我们将看到更多武装策略。我们将看到更多武装冲突，并逐渐演变为暴力。但如果在理想情景下，这些倡议得到认真对待，那么我们或许会看到某种民族团结政府或伞式组织复兴，该组织拥有谈判的合法性，并重新采用非暴力的冲突解决方式。\n\n艾特曼博士：达娜，我可以问你和纳坦一个问题吗？当我审视巴勒斯坦—以色列政治时，双方似乎各自都有一个阻碍性的第三方：以色列这边是“犹太力量党”和其他民族主义政党，另一边则是拥有强大30%支持率的哈马斯。你们都有一些带有宗教色彩的团体，他们认为这不只是人与人之间的事情，而是人与上帝之间的事情，而且关系到收复圣地。当你面对的是必须加以管理的少数小群体时，这是一回事。但在我看来——如果我错了请纠正我——双方似乎各有大约三分之一的人，他们深度民族主义化且信奉宗教，认为按照达娜所说的路线进行谈判等同于民族自杀。首先，我这样认为是否正确：双方确实各有这样一个由宗教民族主义者组成的阻碍性三分之一？其次，那些不属于这个阻碍性三分之二的人，可能如何应对那30%的人？我并不完全确信双方是镜像对应的，但感觉非常相似。\n\n萨克斯博士：在以色列这一方，我既同意也不同意。好消息是，如果你观察宗教民族主义因素，也就是那些会把宗教与民族事业深度交织并视为其内在组成部分的人，他们明显少于30%。如果你看看斯莫特里赫和本-格维尔——如今极右翼的两位领袖人物——他们的支持率远低于30%。\n\n艾特曼博士：他们是15%，对吧？\n\n萨克斯博士：最多是这样，而现在他们的民调实际上还在下滑。许多选民，尤其是本-格维尔的支持者，并不一定属于宗教民族主义阵营。从技术上说，宗教民族主义者，或现代正统派，占人口略高于10%。他们并不全都是强硬民族主义者，尽管大多数是。这是好消息。当然，还有极端正统派，他们是一个非常不同的群体。他们未必像民族主义者那样，尽管近年来他们比过去民族主义得多。但他们的领导层比这更复杂。这是好消息。好消息是，这种类型的阻碍性三分之一明显少于人口的三分之一。这是宗教方面的情况。\n\n以色列人对巴勒斯坦问题持强硬立场，主要有两个推动因素。其一是宗教因素。对斯莫特里赫这样的人来说，这不是权宜之计或安全问题，而是正如你所说的神圣目的——整件事的全部目的。这关乎以色列土地以及其中的以色列国，与其他考量几乎没有关系。\n\n第二个考量是安全问题，而它往往与民族因素相重合。即便是那些认同民族或宗教理念、但认同程度较低的人，也可能非常看重安全问题。这个比例明显超过30%，轻易就超过一半的\n如今，这部分民众极度厌恶风险，也已经对这样一种建议失去了耐心：他们应该为了让国外的人不生气，而让自己的家庭承担深重的风险。Alterman博士：去年春天，皮尤的一项民调询问以色列人：“两国方案能带来安全吗？”在以色列犹太人中，回答“能”的比例是19%。Sachs博士：没错。让我强调这一点。如果你能以某种方式神奇地说服以色列人：将会有一个巴勒斯坦国，但在安全风险方面完全不会增加任何风险——尽管这是不可能的，虽然你增加何种风险之间存在很大差异——我认为，在有正确领导的情况下，你可以争取到多数人的支持，不是今天，也不是未来一两年或五年内，但最终你可以做到。这个主张对以色列人来说极难推销，尤其是在今天。两国方案，或者建立一个巴勒斯坦国，在当前这个时间点对以色列人而言是完全无法启动的。完全无法启动。一旦你引入非军事化——这一直是谈判的一部分——并阐明非军事化可能意味着什么，情况看起来就会略有不同。随着时间推移，在正确的领导和很大的运气下，这件事或许会更容易做到。\n\n总结来说，宗教或民族主义诉求之间存在很大差异，而这些诉求都很强烈。顺便说一句，许多并非民族宗教主义者的人也普遍持有这些诉求。甚至许多左翼人士也对这片土地有着深厚的依恋，等等。他们只是认为自己应该在这件事上妥协，但对他们而言，这是一种妥协。这是第一方面。第二方面是安全问题。尤其是在一年前的10月7日之后，这成为压倒性的论点，但至少这是我们的结构化外交和地缘政治世界能够处理的问题。这是可以在某种背景下讨论的事情。不是在创伤尚未平复之时，但如果希望人们能够走出创伤并开始恢复，那么这并非不可想象。Alterman博士：Dana，你如何看待这个阻碍性的第三点？El Kurd博士：我们需要以一种类似于Natan所描述的方式来理解围绕哈马斯的民调。这不一定是对冲突宗教观点的支持。支持哈马斯不一定等同于对冲突持宗教观点，也不一定意味着没有妥协空间。我相信，在支持哈马斯的人当中，确实有一些人属于这种情况。但每当我们看到哈马斯在民调中上升时，这通常是因为其对手的失败而获得的支持。我们必须把民调中的这一重要因素考虑进去。我们还必须考虑到，我在一开始描述的巴勒斯坦利益相关者并不仅仅是加沙和约旦河西岸的人。即便我们现有的民调也更加有限，无法完整涵盖当今巴勒斯坦政治的状况。\n\n话虽如此，哈马斯，尤其是在2017年之后，曾试图强调其作为民族运动的角色，以及其在民族问题上妥协的意愿。他们知道巴勒斯坦社会的立场所在。与宗教驱动的叙事相比，他们无法通过这种叙事获得同样多的支持，却可以通过民族动机驱动的叙事做到这一点。你说得对，巴勒斯坦社会中确实存在某种零和思维，而持续不断的暴力确实加剧了这种情况。暴力制造了以零和方式理解冲突的条件。这与其说是因为哈马斯或伊斯兰圣战组织，不如说是因为\n人们正在理解、吸收并处理过去这一年。这意味着谈判的空间要小得多。在10月7日之前，我们中的许多人一直在说，如果事情沿着那条路发展，大规模暴力成为常态，那么任何形式的非暴力冲突解决方案的空间都会变得非常狭窄。所以，我不认为这必然只是作为一个整体的哈马斯。我认为，这是因为过去这一年，有些人把这场冲突视为零和博弈。那部分人口确实增加了。\n\nAlterman博士：谢谢。\n\nByman博士：Jon，我能打断一下，问你一个关于该地区的问题吗？以色列取得的一项非常引人注目的外交成功是，以色列合作的阿拉伯伙伴——有些像埃及这样已经合作多年，有些则是最近才开始合作——至少在行动上，在某种程度上公开地支持以色列。现在已经过去一年了。这里没有人说终点已经在望。阿拉伯国家对以色列的支持，或者至少是那种安静的合作，未来还会继续吗？对他们来说是否存在一个临界点？\n\nAlterman博士：在伊朗问题上存在合作，因为在伊朗现政府及其行为的影响下，他们觉得自己与以色列有共同目标。在巴勒斯坦问题上，挫败感正在加剧，这并不是因为他们支持哈马斯，而是因为对一个国家而言。该地区所有政府都敌视哈马斯这个民选的、带有伊斯兰色彩的政治运动；在他们看来，哈马斯想把他们赶下台，夺取他们自己国家的权力。但该地区有一种看法，即以色列人认为阿拉伯人的生命特别廉价，而这令人反感。我看到公众舆论对此感到担忧，并表现了出来。公众中存在一种以色列极度缺乏尊重的感觉。坦率地说，我认为一些阿拉伯政府对自己的人民也有这种做法，但这无助于使各国政府希望与以色列开展的行动获得正当性。\n\n其中另一个非常重要的方面——我也很想听听Dana对此的看法——是，作为一个民主国家，以色列有一个相当紧迫的时间表。它拥有独立的媒体，人们会说：“好吧，那这要走向哪里？那是上周的事。这周呢？要走向哪里？什么时候会结束？”人们坚持要求问责，并要求不断完善政策。谈到与以色列合作或实现关系正常化的问题，该地区许多领导人认为，他们有40年的时间来慢慢处理。对他们来说，这周发生什么并不重要。他们可以把这个问题搁置一年、五年。在他们看来，他们的人民没有在战斗，也没有在死亡。他们还有其他事情要做。海湾地区存在一些挫败感。他们认为自己的主要任务是使经济多元化并吸引外国投资，而这场战争吸走了所有注意力，让人们忽视他们正在做的好事，还让人们变得激动不安，并增加了该地区的风险。\n\n但他们不太可能让任何事情彻底破裂，而是很可能把这些问题搁置相当长一段时间。别忘了，这是一个这样的地区：沙特人和伊朗人多年来一直深度敌对，彼此威胁，关闭大使馆，等等；但就在上周，伊朗总统还与沙特外交部长会面。人们说：“是的，这正是应该发生的事。”你可以加快，也可以\n深化合作，但每个人的时间跨度都长得多，现在掌舵的人很可能未来仍是那里的人。阿卜杜勒阿齐兹·本·苏丹曾担任沙特阿拉伯外交大臣48年。我不是说现任外交大臣会在那里任职40年，但这当然有可能。王储预计将在未来几十年继续指导沙特的外交政策。阿联酋统治者穆罕默德·本·扎耶德预计也会在那里任职很长时间。每个人都在进行一场更长期的战略博弈。你可以谈论内塔尼亚胡的政治和他的生存问题。阿拉伯世界大多数领导人所担心的唯一生存问题是生物学意义上的生存，而不是政治生存。这让你在许多问题上拥有不同程度的自由，也有不同的视角。我想问达娜一个关于地区政治的问题，但在此之前，我想告诉观众，你们可以通过CSIS网页或YouTube页面向小组发送问题。达娜，你如何看待地区局势？你如何看待阿拉伯利益在巴勒斯坦问题上的复兴？这些对巴勒斯坦人或地区政治有任何实质影响吗？从地区视角看，情况如何？\n\n埃尔·库尔德博士：当我们讨论巴勒斯坦和该地区时，人们大量关注“轴心”、伊朗支持的民兵，以及这种关于巴勒斯坦可能如何影响地区、反之亦然的框架。但要理解巴勒斯坦如何与地区政治交织，必须采取更广泛的方法，了解阿拉伯社会如何看待巴勒斯坦，而不仅仅是关注这些特定的民兵组织。这对于理解地区反对和异议非常重要。巴勒斯坦作为一个议题，是异议的门户，因为它代表了你所提到的这种缺乏问责。\n\n我同意你的看法。阿拉伯政府对自己在冲突中的角色有着长期视角，但鉴于阿拉伯之春等事件的后果，他们若想当然地认为巴勒斯坦不会在地区动态中扮演更大的角色，可能会过于安逸，尤其是在反对派政治以及阿拉伯社会、公众、民间社会和抗议运动如何吸收过去一年战争的信息，并利用这些信息疏导自身不满方面。那是一个组成部分。抗议可能会朝着人们意想不到的方向发展。另一件事是，战争的扩大且看不到尽头，使“轴心”及其不同参与者——无论是黎巴嫩真主党还是哈马斯——所提出的观点和叙事更具可信度。这提高了阿拉伯社会将其视为零和冲突的程度，而这只会增加地区不稳定的可能性。即使某些阿拉伯政府对持续的危机抱有长期看法，并可能认为它们最终可以恢复正常化，也不会那么简单。这种战略存在许多风险。\n\n奥尔特曼博士：我的感觉是否正确：阿拉伯政府在限制有关巴勒斯坦的抗议方面采取了前所未有的严厉措施？在你看来，什么情况下它们会放松限制？\n\n埃尔·库尔德博士：正如我所说，尤其是在阿拉伯之春之后，巴勒斯坦议题对阿拉伯政府而言变得更加敏感。他们更加警惕允许此类抗议和此类异议。过去，这曾经是一种安全阀。在某些情况下，这是人们能够表达一些\n政治异议，但这很快蔓延并扩展到其他议题领域。由于经历了那十年、认识到巴勒斯坦在阿拉伯之春之后所扮演的角色，阿拉伯政府一直非常严格地限制相关表达。他们目前只是在非常有限的意义上允许这种表达，而且确实因国家而异。只有当你表达对加沙人民的人道主义关切和担忧时，才被允许这样做。如果这转变为某种对政府政策或政府处理任何这些问题的方式（包括正常化）的批评，那么这种表达就在整个地区被迅速压制。这对他们来说并不是一种非常好的长期策略，也不是制胜策略。\n奥尔特曼博士：让我再问一个地区性问题。你谈到了这场战争扩散的方式。这场战争尚未深度扩散、但可能扩散的一种方式，是以色列与伊朗的直接对抗。伊朗向以色列发射了导弹，以色列也进行了还击，但我们还没有看到我会称之为两国之间正常战争的情况。纳坦，你如何看待以色列—伊朗轴线的发展？你认为它会演变成直接战争吗？你认为它会演变成间接战争，即伊朗通过巴勒斯坦团体开展行动吗？我们应该关注什么？我们应该担心什么？萨克斯博士：这是个好问题。这几乎是达娜所说内容的另一面，但我不认为这与她的观点有任何矛盾。一种非常普遍的以色列视角，是把对中东的两种看法对立起来。一种是以巴勒斯坦为中心的看法：当你谈论中东和平时，那几乎等同于以色列—巴勒斯坦和平，仿佛该地区没有其他问题，这显然是不正确的——尽管这场冲突在阿拉伯世界极具情感色彩。这会构成一种世界观。内塔尼亚胡在20世纪90年代初曾在一本书中写过一章，题为“巴勒斯坦中心地位的神话”。这种观点的另一面围绕伊朗与以色列、以及伊朗与一些逊尼派阿拉伯国家之间的对立展开。巴勒斯坦问题对伊朗而言，是进入逊尼派主导世界的一张入场券，因为他们可以举起主要逊尼派国家并未举起的旗帜。奥尔特曼博士：所以，这是一个什叶派国家所面对的逊尼派阿拉伯问题？萨克斯博士：没错，对于一个什叶派国家而言。奥尔特曼博士：非阿拉伯、非逊尼派。萨克斯博士：他们是真正举起这面旗帜的唯一一方。把这两种观点对立起来是错误的，因为两者都可能为真。巴勒斯坦问题的核心地位，不仅在阿拉伯政治中，而且在整个伊斯兰世界的穆斯林政治中，确实如此。我过去住在印度尼西亚，如果你让大多数人指出巴勒斯坦在哪里，他们可能都做不到，但即使在那里，这也是一个具有情感色彩的问题，而现在更是如此。另一方面，如果你审视这场战争，所有实际发生战斗的地方都围绕着“抵抗轴心”，而不是其他地方，这并非巧合。正如以色列人喜欢说的那样，这是七条战线。战事集中在哈马斯、真主党、胡塞武装、伊拉克和叙利亚的民兵组织，以及伊朗本身。这些恰恰都是由伊朗领导的“抵抗轴心”成员。哈马斯当然不是什叶派组织，但无论如何，它与伊朗轴心的结盟更为松散。因此，以色列国内也有人很早就提出，早在2023年10月11日，就不应首先为了报复而入侵或推进到加沙，而应先向北方行动。我们现在看到的许多情况，也许正是达娜所指的……\n或者说，暗杀纳斯鲁拉，这一方案在10月11日被认真考虑过，但后来被以色列领导层中的某个人或拜登总统推迟了。最终，内塔尼亚胡决定放弃。这种世界观认为，说到底，这从根本上是一场伊朗与以色列之间的斗争。当然，哈马斯有自己的盘算。它不是伊朗的工具，但得到了伊朗的支持。更强大的敌人是黎巴嫩真主党，它是伊朗的主要工具，因此战争应该转向那里。\n\n这是一个很长的铺垫。当前以色列的行动正适合这种思路。鉴于丹所描述的那些成果，尽管黎巴嫩付出了生命代价，这场行动在以色列获得了非常广泛的支持。尤其是因为，除了人质问题之外，还有一种感觉：以色列北部的居民——也就是10月8日真主党加入战斗后撤离的数万名以色列人——必须回家。要做到这一点，他们必须感到安全，不仅要与真主党或更广泛意义上的黎巴嫩达成协议，还要确信真主党没有能力在边境对他们发动属于自己的“10月7日”式袭击，至少真主党曾表示会入侵以色列。以色列很可能会针对伊朗上次发射的弹道导弹进行报复。我无法告诉你具体会是什么。\n\n美国正在投入大量努力限制这种报复，并试图推动局势降级。以色列，包括内塔尼亚胡在内，确实有可能认为自己在黎巴嫩取得了成果，真正削弱了真主党及其能力。因此，他们可能会看到降级的机会，因为说到底，他们需要与黎巴嫩打交道，让北部的平民回归，而这在国内是一个政治上的必要条件。\n\n阿尔特曼博士：不过可以说，内塔尼亚胡在过去六个月里押下的所有重大赌注、承担的所有风险，在他看来，都比所有人告诉他的结果要好。无论是进攻拉法、进军黎巴嫩，还是暗杀纳斯鲁拉，那些本可能真正酿成灾难的事情都没有变成灾难。\n\n萨克斯博士：确实如此。在以色列，这种感觉非常强烈，这也是内塔尼亚胡的政治主线。这条主线是：“看，我顶住了所有这些压力。你们无法想象那是什么样的压力。看看以色列的反对派领导人，或者以色列的任何其他人。还有谁能顶住这种压力？因此，只有我能够长期坚持下去。”他的支持率有所上升，在民调中的排名也有所提高，但他并没有领先。他仍然很可能至多打成平局，而正如我们近年来所看到的，对他而言，平局在某种程度上就是胜利；但他最多只能打成平局。他完全可能输掉选举。更重要的是，从他的角度来看，他可能正在阻挠选举。选举原定在两年后举行。鉴于他还成功地让另一个人加入了执政联盟，他完全可以尝试从这个方向操弄政治；或者他也可以走一条完全不同的路，即说：“我的重大成功意味着我可以冒险举行选举。”\n\n我不知道下一步对伊朗会采取什么行动。我们现在所处的局面，与4月13日之后大不相同——当时伊朗历史上首次直接向以色列开火。如今，我们实际上正处于这样一种局面：伊朗方面在作战，同时持续遭受以色列直接、公开的回应。我们现在处于完全不同的领域。我们今天看到的部分情况，以色列的计算和回应，取决于这种方程式能否维持。它取决于伊朗现在是否能够\n可以推断，无论伊朗和以色列之间是否发生了一些交火，游戏规则都是大国，尤其是美国，会介入并平息事态。而以色列人会说：“不，规则必须在那之前就确立。你绝不能向以色列开火。这才必须是游戏规则。”这非常危险，但许多这类可怕警告，充其量只是部分成为现实。在黎巴嫩，我们还没有看到所担心的全面战争。\n\n阿尔特曼博士：丹，让我问问你。你一直在思考真主党、哈马斯以及更普遍意义上的非正规战争。伊朗没有更积极地推动“抵抗轴心”对抗以色列的猛攻，这让你感到意外吗？还是说，你原本就认为事情会这样发展？\n\n拜曼博士：以色列能够在多大程度上削弱真主党，我确实感到意外。就自身能力而言，伊朗一直是一个相对弱小的参与者。其常规军事力量相当有限，经济实力也有限，而且其大多数系统都不先进。没错，它拥有这些火箭和导弹部队，但这让许多人认识到防空的重要性。多年来，以色列一直在努力发展真正全面的防空系统，既要应对来自加沙的小型短程武器，也要应对来自伊朗、难以击落的大型弹道导弹。在此之前，我们就知道他们已经取得了很大进展。\n\n伊朗采取谨慎态度，我没那么意外，但这里仍有一些疑问。这对伊朗来说不仅具有挑衅性，也令人难堪。以色列不仅削弱了其主要代理人真主党，还在伊朗境内政权核心地带开展了重大行动。伊朗的回应乏善可陈，一种可能是伊朗退缩，对吧？它不一定非要参战。如果以色列采取中等程度的回应，伊朗可能会说：“我们改天再打击”，但实际上什么也不做。\n\n阿尔特曼博士：就像他们在4月13日的袭击之后所做的那样。\n\n拜曼博士：没错。但伊朗也可能转向国际恐怖主义。过去我们曾看到过这种情况，例如在保加利亚和阿根廷发动的袭击。当你无法通过传统军事手段回应时，这是一种回应方式。但我要说，真主党剩余的武器库究竟还能发挥什么作用，仍是一个重大问题。它是已经无力作战了吗？还是仅仅遭受了太多损失，无法有效使用这些武器？抑或它实际上是在试图避免进一步升级？我不太确定真主党会害怕什么，所以我确实认为其能力遭到削弱是实实在在的。\n\n我确实想补充一点，这有些不同，我想听听达娜或其他人的看法。这涉及巴勒斯坦权力机构作为以色列的合作伙伴。从反恐角度看，西岸的巴勒斯坦权力机构在日常层面一直极其重要，帮助以色列确保西岸的安全，追剿哈马斯残余势力，同时——在持批判态度的巴勒斯坦人眼中——充当以色列占领的帮凶。以色列政策似乎发生了相当剧烈的转变：无视巴勒斯坦权力机构领导层，在西岸开展重大行动，完全不顾其政治地位。\n\n这是一个非常重大的转变。谢天谢地，那里的流血程度还没有达到加沙或黎巴嫩的程度，但当然已经造成了大量伤亡，也提出了一个长期问题：什么\n约旦河西岸将会变成什么样。埃尔·库尔德博士：这反映在我之前提到的一些民调中。这就是为什么巴勒斯坦民众担心，我们在加沙看到的暴力程度可能会以某种方式在约旦河西岸重演。事实上，我们正在约旦河西岸看到一些类似的战术，包括夷平基础设施和袭击医院。巴勒斯坦权力机构与以色列的关系绝对没有被视为一种威慑。正如你所提到的，对大多数巴勒斯坦人来说，它被视为占领的分包商。曾经有这样一种因素：他们或许能够与以色列谈判，或者作为以色列的伙伴，或许能够以某种方式与以色列国家接触。正如你所提到的，这并没有发生。\n\n坦率地说，这只是我们已经看到的持续趋势。尤其是在2021年5月至8月的“团结起义”时期之后，情况一直如此。约旦河西岸针对不同武装组织展开了大规模行动，这些组织的出现与哈马斯无关；在纳布卢斯等城市中心也开展了大规模行动。巴勒斯坦权力机构一直无法作为以色列的某种伙伴采取行动，而早在去年之前，这已经严重损害了它在巴勒斯坦民众中的作用和合法性。\n\n这只是给巴勒斯坦权力机构作为代表约旦河西岸巴勒斯坦人的某种合法行为体盖棺定论。我担心，如果国际社会或美国不认可巴勒斯坦政治中其他一些建立合法性的尝试，那么这个真空将会被其他类型的政治行动填补。\n\n奥尔特曼博士：这个房间里坐着太多博士了，我不能不问一个关于评分的问题。我们这里有一个问题：你们会如何评价拜登政府应对这场冲突的表现？拜登政府有一套自身的判断框架。它在外交上积极处理与以色列和阿拉伯国家的关系。它选择参与什么、如何参与以及说些什么。考虑到如今平均绩点正在上升，而且许多顶尖学校的平均成绩都在A减以上，你们会如何评价政府的战略表现？并不是说它能够控制局势，但它可以控制自己的行动。\n\n萨克斯博士：我先让在职教授们回答这个问题。\n\n拜曼博士：达娜，我先开始，但请你纠正我。对于主要行为体以色列而言，美国的影响力极其薄弱。这是因为，正如纳坦在开头所说的，对大多数以色列人来说，利害关系是如此之大。跨越党派界限的广泛支持认为，这是一个关乎生存的时刻，国家正面临各种各样的威胁，必须作出非常强硬的回应。\n\n考虑到这一点，我认为政府划定了若干红线，其中绝大多数都被违反了。随后，它再次划定这些红线，而它们又被违反或无视。这看起来很愚蠢。这很难。我没有很好的答案告诉你们应该如何做。这需要重新考虑美国对待以色列的方式，并进行剧烈改变，不同于美国过去50年的回应方式，因为在那段时间里，美国实际上并没有那么支持以色列。\n\n如果你看看最近与伊朗的对抗，就会发现美国试图两面讨好。它一方面说：“不要升级局势”，另一方面又会向你提供情报，向你提供军事\n支持。我们会百分之百站在你们这边。”这并不是阻止局势升级的最大威胁。事实上，可以说恰恰相反。这基本上是在亮绿灯。从这个意义上说，我会说美国的表现肯定不好。对许多阿拉伯观众来说，美国显得很愚蠢，也显得软弱。与此同时，拜登政府赢得了很多以色列人的支持，而以色列人正是他们的主要受众。尤其是在冲突初期，总统表现出的真切同理心产生了很大作用，在当时存在许多担忧和猜疑的情况下，赢得了一些善意。我能理解这一点，因为我自己也没有什么高明的想法，无法说明美国应如何改变其处理这场冲突的方式，但我不能环顾四周后说美国取得了极大的成功。\n\nAlterman博士：拜曼教授，这算B吗？\n\n拜曼博士：这取决于你想采用什么标准，但，是的，我会把它放在B的范围内。我觉得这样公平。\n\nAlterman博士：达娜？\n\n埃尔·库尔德博士：哇，真慷慨。我想我是个严格的评分者。我最近写了一篇文章，编辑后来给它起了标题《华盛顿没有吸取任何教训》。\n\nAlterman博士：这是《外交政策》上的一篇有趣文章。\n\n埃尔·库尔德博士：今天刚刚发表。你提到的、向以色列受众展现的同理心，以及拜登政府因此在这一方面赢得的支持，与其对巴勒斯坦人以及总体而言对阿拉伯人完全缺乏同理心形成了鲜明对比。这也损害了拜登政府在这个国家的声誉。\n\n我尤其担忧和沮丧的是，无论幕后正在进行怎样的外交活动，正如你所说，所有这些红线都被越过了。我们对于“第二天”巴勒斯坦领土可能是什么样子，或者我们如何回到某种框架、以某种方式解决这场冲突的任何想法，都没有偏离多少导致我们走到今天这一步的原有现状，只是在重新配置它。拜登政府似乎没能吸取其执政头三年的教训，而正是那三年导致了10月7日事件的发生，也导致了这场战争的展开。对于我们接下来该何去何从，没有任何新想法。这才是真正最大的担忧。显然，过去一年中所有被越过的红线、缺乏回应，以及缺乏尝试以某种方式对援助附加条件的能力，都在阿拉伯、巴勒斯坦和全球受众中造成了很大伤害。最大的担忧是：我们接下来该何去何从？美国的决策者是否曾对自己说过：“我们必须尝试一些不同于一直以来所尝试的做法？”我看不到很多证据，因此我会给他们低得多的评分。\n\n萨克斯博士：我想提出两点。首先，当你思考美国战略是否成功时，问题在于目标。美国政府以及拜登本人在早期并不只是想获得以色列的团结支持。他确实认同以色列的两个主要目标：不允许哈马斯治理加沙地带并构成威胁，以及让包括美国人质在内的人质获释。这些就是主要目标。\n\n很多人的困难在于美国立场的双重性。一方面，它是世界秩序的保障者，被视为应当恢复平衡的一方。当几乎任何地方出问题时，当然尤其是在中东，人们\n说：“华盛顿谁负责这件事？华盛顿谁在解决这个问题？”\n\n另一方面，美国，当然还有拜登总统，都把自己视为以色列的盟友。因此，政府有时会觉得，如果就人质协议或停火协议向内塔尼亚胡表达不满，外界会理解为哈马斯并不是阻碍。这会导致哈马斯，尤其是辛瓦尔，立场更加强硬。他们在玩这种两面游戏。\n\n我想提出的第二点是，很早以前，甚至在拜登发表的第一次演讲中，政府就正确地确定了其主要的地缘政治和战略目标，以及其在降低全面战争风险方面的独特地位。他们正确地认识到，这一角色只有美国能够发挥。\n\n现在，我们比几个月前更接近全面战争，但尽管如此，在这一点上，我认为他们的得分相当高。请记住，拜登总统不仅很早就向任何可能加入战斗的国家和组织发出严厉警告——所有人都明白这是指伊朗和真主党——而且还调动了一个航母编队，随后又调动了第二个航母编队。\n\n随后，在4月13日和14日，美国中央司令部协调了一次历史性行动。那不仅是一次历史性的伊朗袭击，也是一次由中央司令部领导的联合行动，阿拉伯国家在其中积极保卫以色列。现在又发生了同样的事情。无论好坏，这都在很大程度上服务于拜登确定的目标。这可能最终一无所获，但它抵消了他们以及其他人在加沙问题上所感到的许多挫败感。\n\nAlterman博士：您曾在乔治城大学担任兼职讲师。您会给本届政府打多少分？\n\nSachs博士：我会去找当时的院长丹·拜曼，向他请教。这取决于哪个领域。在地区层面，可能是A-。在加沙问题上则更难评判，因为正如丹所说，他们在以色列方面有一个非常难以合作的伙伴。他们在这里还有一个不可能应对的参与者，那就是辛瓦尔。辛瓦尔一直拥有否决权。\n\n以色列人强烈批评内塔尼亚胡及其对各项事务的优先排序，也批评他没有把人质协议置于优先位置。我也认同这些批评。但以色列人所说的——当然，他们的假设是——辛瓦尔糟糕得多。糟糕100倍，而且我不是要把两者等量齐观或类似什么的。我的重点不是这个。我的重点是，政府试图进行一场博弈，而这两个极难应对的行为者都拥有否决权。结果一直不尽如人意。不同于推特上以及其他各处的所有人，我没有聪明到知道所有答案会是什么。\n\nAlterman博士：我们不得不就此结束了。我要感谢我的同事Dana El Kurd、Dan Byman和Natan Sachs。感谢各位参加。CSIS主页上有一个专题页面，汇集了我们关于这场冲突和当前评估的大量工作，其中包括丹发表的一份新报告，以及我发表的一些文章。这次谈话让我学到了很多。我希望你们也一样。我们会很快发布文字记录，也期待很快再次见到大家。非常感谢各位参加。\n\n标签\n中东，\n地缘政治与国际安全，以及\n恐怖主义与非正规战争\n\n项目与计划\n中东项目\n战争、非正规威胁与恐怖主义项目\n埃及、以色列和黎凡特\n地缘政治与外交政策\n国际安全项目\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-10-07T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3563","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"将优惠贷款重新引入发展工具箱","title_en":"Reintroducing Concessional Loans into the Development Toolbox","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心繁荣与发展项目学者丹尼尔·伦德等人撰写报告，指出全球债务高企、现行多边债务架构应对乏力，美国发展援助几乎全部为赠款。报告回顾美国从贷款转向赠款的历史及中国贷款的影响，建议在保留赠款的同时重新引入优惠贷款，用于有机构能力和政治可行性的项目，以补充赠款、应对债务危机并对冲中国影响力。","summary_en":"CSIS Prosperity and Development Project scholars Daniel Runde and colleagues argue that soaring global debt and an ineffective multilateral debt architecture call for revisiting U.S. lending. Reviewing the shift from loans to grants and China's lending role, they recommend reintroducing concessional loans alongside grants for countries with sound institutions and political feasibility, to complement aid, address debt crises, and counter Chinese influence.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:29:18.020553+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/reintroducing-concessional-loans-development-toolbox","content":"Reintroducing Concessional Loans into the Development Toolbox\nPhoto: Oleg via Adobe Stock\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nReport\nby\nDaniel F. Runde,\nRafael Romeu,\nand\nAustin Hardman\nPublished August 20, 2024\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Full Report\n248kb\nIntroductionGlobal indebtedness has soared to unsustainable levels and requires a long-term solution, not an endless series of bailouts. The process for helping indebted countries is inefficient and does not serve U.S. interests. To be part of the solution for countries facing a debt crisis, the United States must refresh its lending practices and learn from the mistakes and successes that China has made in its lending to the Global South. It is time to seriously reconsider how the United States may be a part of the solution. Is there a role for concessional loans in the U.S. development portfolio? Is the current U.S. strategy of funding foreign assistance exclusively through grants always the right one? Tethering development finance to U.S. foreign policy objectives while producing fiscally sustainable outcomes is a component of great power competition with China that has yet to be satisfactorily assessed.Between 2010 and 2022, government borrowing increased by almost 4 times in Asia and the Pacific, 3 times in Africa, 2.5 times in Europe and Central Asia, and 1.6 times in Latin America and the Caribbean. The Covid-19 pandemic, Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, and rapid rate hikes by the Federal Reserve and other major central banks have worsened the fiscal outlook for many deeply indebted low- and middle-income economies, particularly commodity-exporting countries vulnerable to market volatility. According to the International Monetary Fund (IMF), global public debt hit a record $97 trillion in 2023, with developing countries shouldering a disproportionate amount relative to their share of the global economy.As emerging markets default on their loans in record numbers, the institutions of the current multilateral debt architecture—including the IMF, Group of 20 (G20), and Paris Club—have struggled to provide comprehensive or timely debt service relief, concessional financing, and debt restructuring. At least 53 economies—including Argentina, Egypt, Ghana, Nigeria, Pakistan, and Sri Lanka—either have defaulted on some of their debts or have debt levels that the IMF considers unsustainable. Despite representing only 5 percent of the global economy, these countries are home to approximately 20 percent of the world’s population and face the highest borrowing costs. This underlying “debt tsunami” poses a major threat to global financial stability and undermines efforts to finance lagging UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).Debt service payments now account for critical percentages of national budgets in the developing world, preventing government spending on essential public services and other investments needed to attract business. According to a June 2024 UN Trade and Development report, approximately 48 countries encompassing a population of 3.3 billion people spend more on interest payments than on education or health. In 2022, low-income nations disbursed $49 billion more to their external creditors than they received in foreign aid.\nDebt service payments now account for critical percentages of national budgets in the developing world, preventing government spending on essential public services and other investments needed to attract business.\nThe Evolution of Development Financing: From Loans to GrantsDebt has long been recognized as a powerful tool for economic growth and innovation when used judiciously. The U.S. foray into development loans can be traced back to President Franklin D. Roosevelt’s Good Neighbor policy in the late 1930s, at which time the United States began extending development loans to Latin American countries through the U.S. Export-Import Bank (EXIM). Over the decades, institutions such as the U.S. Trade and Development Agency (USTDA), EXIM Bank, the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation (DFC), the Inter-American Development Bank (IADB), and the World Bank Group have played significant roles in providing extensive lending programs aimed at fostering global development. For instance, in 2023 alone, the World Bank Group disbursed nearly $73 billion for various projects in sectors such as infrastructure, health, and education. Similarly, the recently constituted DFC has been instrumental in supporting economic development in emerging markets, committing $7.4 billion in financing in 2022 across diverse sectors, including renewable energy, healthcare, and technology.Despite the potential benefits of debt, its use in development circles has often been contentious, with many policymakers favoring grants over loans due to concerns about overindebtedness and financial stability. This shift in perspective was significantly influenced by the 2000 Meltzer Commission’s recommendations, which advocated replacing up to 50 percent of World Bank and other development agency loans with grants. The commission argued that grant funding, particularly for critical needs such as sanitation and potable water, would prevent poor countries from falling into unsustainable debt. According to then U.S. secretary of the treasury Paul O’Neill, the philosophy of “more grants, less loans” was justified by the belief that the World Bank, by lending instead of donating funds to fight poverty, had driven poor countries “into a ditch.”The Meltzer testimony recommended that international finance institutions use grants to improve the quality of life in low-income countries through humanitarian assistance efforts. The commission proposed that the grants be paid directly to service providers on evidence of completion from independent auditors. Grants would bypass corrupt governments, and auditing results would improve performance. The administration under President George W. Bush went on to start one of the largest aid programs in half a century, the President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) program, which remains grant funded.The Meltzer Commission report came just a few years after the IMF and World Bank launched the Heavily Indebted Poor Countries (HIPC) Initiative in 1996. The initiative aimed to ensure that no poor country faces an unmanageable debt burden and offered significant debt relief to qualifying countries. Debt forgiveness was seen as one of the reasons many African countries began to move along a positive economic trajectory. During this time, influential actors such as the Catholic Church and U2 lead singer Bono advocated for debt relief. They supported and contributed to the Jubilee 2000 campaign, which urged the elimination of debt for many low-income nations. This activism swayed the opinions of several politicians and resulted in the approval of funds for international debt forgiveness by the Republican-led 106th Congress.In 2005, the HIPC Initiative was supplemented by the Multilateral Debt Relief Initiative (MDRI), providing 100 percent relief on eligible debts from institutions such as the IMF, World Bank, IADB, and African Development Fund. There was hope that this forgiveness would resolve the issue entirely. However, many debtor countries continued irresponsibly borrowing after the HIPC Initiative. Despite the significant debt relief provided by the HIPC and MDRI Initiatives, countries such as Bolivia, Haiti, Honduras, and Nicaragua continued to face fiscal challenges. Honduras and Nicaragua saw their debt levels rise again due to continued borrowing and economic mismanagement, while Bolivia and Haiti struggled with persistent poverty and political instability, which undermined the benefits of debt relief. The initiatives brought about a moral hazard as the massive wave of forgiveness offered only temporary relief without addressing the underlying issues of fiscal discipline and economic sustainability within debtor nations.The roots of the transition to a grants-based development strategy were planted before the HIPC and MDRI Initiatives or the Meltzer Commission. The “New Directions” strategy, embodied in the Foreign Assistance Act of 1973, marked a shift in thinking from large capital projects such as infrastructure and policy reform to technical assistance and meeting basic human needs. As a result, infrastructure investments as a percentage of total investment by multilateral development banks (MDBs) such as the World Bank and Asian Development Bank declined for decades before seeing a resurgence in the early 2000s. Projects in higher-income developing countries such as Brazil, Uruguay, and Venezuela were phased out as the new bottom-up approach was implemented and projects were allocated solely to nations with the lowest incomes. Loans represented about a third of total military and economic assistance until a decline in this figure began in the 1980s. By 2001, loans represented a mere 1 percent of aid. The decline was, in part, due to a response to the debt issues occurring at the time.Today, the U.S. development portfolio is almost entirely grant based, with foreign aid in fiscal year (FY) 2022 totaling an estimated $70.4 billion, or about 1 percent of total spending. Of this aid, 25 percent was for humanitarian activities, 21 percent was for health programs, 18 percent was for peace and security assistance, 18 percent was for economic development assistance, and the remaining 18 percent was for program support, human rights, and multisector assistance, among other purposes. Most of this aid is implemented by nongovernmental organizations rather than foreign governments, emphasizing a model that prioritizes grants over loans to minimize the risk of debt crises in developing countries. The United States remains the largest foreign aid donor globally, accounting for around 29 percent of total official development assistance from donor governments in 2023.China’s Emergence as a Major Lender and Its Use of Predatory LoansLoan-based financing of the Global South did not stop when the United States transitioned away from lending. Instead, countries sought loans from alternative sources, including China and commercial creditors. With immense borrowing needs and limited access to development finance, emerging markets have increasingly resorted to more expensive private sector and Chinese lenders operating outside of the official Paris Club regime, creating a more diffuse credit landscape with a far riskier profile.\nLoan-based financing of the Global South did not stop when the United States transitioned away from lending. Instead, countries sought loans from alternative sources, including China and commercial creditors.\nThe Paris Club, an informal group of official creditors from major economies, aims to find sustainable solutions to payment difficulties that debtor countries experience. It provides a platform for restructuring sovereign debt and operates based on consensus and shared principles of fairness, ensuring that all participating creditors agree to similar terms and that the debtor nation is treated equitably. This coordinated approach helps prevent disorderly defaults and maintains stability in the global financial system.China’s disregard for the Paris Club’s principles and practices poses significant challenges. China’s lending is often opaque, more expensive, and stringent, with undisclosed terms and conditions. This lack of transparency and coordination can lead to debt traps, in which borrowing countries find themselves unable to service their debts, potentially leading to a loss of sovereignty over key assets. This approach undermines the cooperative framework the Paris Club established and creates a riskier credit environment for emerging markets.China’s emergence as the largest official lender to developing countries has proved the main stumbling block in the escalating sovereign debt crisis, with estimates of Chinese debt exposure ranging up to $1.5 trillion. Beijing’s unusual lack of transparency and reluctance to forgive debt have paralyzed the IMF’s ability to provide technical assistance and implement financial stabilization programs that hinge on financial assurances from existing creditors. Emergency programs such as the G20’s Common Framework—developed in late 2020 as a channel for low-income countries in need of rapid debt relief—have been plagued by delays, coordination issues, and enforcement challenges. These issues are in large part due to China’s reluctance to provide significant debt relief concessions, as well as concerns that some creditors, especially China, are pursuing more favorable side deals than others. Additionally, there are concerns that IMF bailouts or hard currency inflows from aid or remittances would flow directly into Chinese pockets. As multilateral debt talks stall, borrowers in distress have waited years for a restructuring deal without receiving a cent from the IMF.\nChina’s emergence as the largest official lender to developing countries has proved the main stumbling block in the escalating sovereign debt crisis, with estimates of Chinese debt exposure ranging up to $1.5 trillion.\nChina’s emergence as a major lender has been transformative for global development finance. As of 2017, China had become the world’s largest official lender, surpassing the World Bank, IMF, and Paris Club combined. The China Overseas Development Finance Database details 1,099 Chinese development finance commitments abroad, amounting to $498 billion between 2008 and 2021. This volume of lending equaled 83 percent of the World Bank’s sovereign lending during that time frame, making China’s direct foreign investments some of the most active worldwide. In China’s $80 billion annual development portfolio, the vast majority of loans are targeted toward low-income countries, with significant concentrations in Southeast Asia, Africa, and South America. Today, more than 5 percent of global gross domestic product (GDP) consists of China’s outstanding claims. However, many of China’s loans are not categorized as development loans or are classified as concessional loans. From 2013 to 2018, 49 percent of Chinese foreign assistance, which totaled an average of $7 billion annually, took the form of concessional loans.Chinese loans, almost all of which come from official government or state-controlled channels, have drawn significant criticism for being opaque and predatory. Even though China’s lending is under state control, it is decentralized and managed by various institutions that are hesitant to accept losses on their loans. Since 2000, there have been 150 documented cases of default, debt restructuring, or other credit events involving Chinese loans to developing nations. The proportion of countries defaulting on these loans has more than doubled, exceeding 10 percent of all nations since 2016. For some nations, up to 50 percent of their foreign loans originate from China, with the majority spending over a third of their government revenue on servicing this foreign debt. Notably, Sri Lanka and Zambia have already defaulted, unable to meet even the interest payments on loans used for constructing ports, mines, and power plants. This rapid increase in defaults demonstrates the risks developing countries take due to China’s lending practices.China’s lending strategy often involves high interest rates and stringent terms. Many of these loans are not reported in the most widely used official debt statistics, creating a hidden debt problem. This means debtor countries and international institutions alike have an incomplete picture of how much countries around the world owe China and under what conditions. For instance, an analysis discovered that Zambia owed $6.6 billion to Chinese state-owned banks, roughly double the amount previously thought. This systematic underreporting complicates global efforts to manage and restructure debt effectively.Chinese lending is also widely considered transactional, driven by a desire to secure access to critical resources such as oil, minerals, and other commodities. Some experts believe Beijing’s willingness to finance infrastructure projects, even in high-risk environments, reflects this strategy. The approach has led to increased debt burdens for borrowing countries and has been criticized as a form of “debt trap diplomacy.” Countries such as Ghana, Pakistan, Sri Lanka, and Zambia have faced severe economic consequences, including defaults and significant reductions in essential services, due to their inability to manage high debt repayments.China’s ascent as a major creditor leveraging state-owned enterprises for its lending activities has introduced a significant challenge into the global economic order and the existing debt crisis. Its lending terms often include nondisclosure agreements and confidentiality clauses, making it difficult for other creditors to gauge the full extent of a country’s indebtedness to China. This development has major consequences for global debt restructuring initiatives. China’s parallel system of development finance challenges the traditional Western approach to providing assistance and negotiating debt relief, which has dominated since the end of World War II. As China’s influence continues to grow, its lending practices and the resultant debt burdens on developing countries remain critical issues for the international community, especially the United States and its allies.Debt Damages Foreign AssistanceDue to the severity of the debt crisis, there are now a dozen key strategic developing countries—including Kenya, Laos, Mongolia, Pakistan, and Zambia—devoting more resources to paying interest on their debt to China than to public goods and services key for development, such as education and healthcare. Development assistance does not exist in a vacuum.The use of foreign aid grants during periods of crisis and capital outflows is highly detrimental to aid effectiveness. Grants, which are non-debt-creating flows, are counted as reserves at central banks once they are converted to local currency and spent on staff or supplies inside the recipient country. Foreign assistance flows are deposited in developing country commercial banks so that they may be spent on aid projects. These deposits are created by the developing country’s central bank, a state-owned enterprise that accepts foreign grant dollars as central bank reserves and creates domestic currency deposits for the local commercial bank. Hence, the counterpart to this grant-funded local currency spending is central bank reserves used to stabilize the currency when domestic bondholders, Eurobond holders, foreign direct investment (FDI) investors, China, and commercial lenders seek to liquidate their investments in a crisis-stricken country. IMF programs attempt to stabilize the currency with whatever dollars are available in order to buy time to make fiscal adjustments. This practice invariably leads to currency devaluation, usually some form of capital controls, and budget cuts. Hence, the current institutional framework necessarily involves using U.S. government grants and remittances to meet central bank reserve requirements instead of using the international purchasing power of the aid grants to fund development.Policy tools such as budget cuts, devaluation, inflation, and multiple exchange rate mechanisms enable governments to convert foreign aid into reserves, thereby reducing the aid’s international purchasing power and impact. This weakens the impact of U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID) grants, which are meant to be spent on goods and services to stimulate development but are instead leveraged for outflows funding debt repayments as countries undertake structural adjustment programs. IMF research suggests an increase in overall aid (comprising both loans and grants) tends to reduce domestic revenues, though loans and grants have differing effects. While the borrowing country’s capacity to repay external creditors and rebuild reserves is closely monitored, insufficient effort is made to mitigate the risk that fiscal adjustments will simply allow redirection of central bank reserves to repaying Chinese lenders, commercial creditors, and local bondholders before devaluations and reserve depletion.Since U.S. taxpayers fund reserves from aid flows, ignoring the use of foreign assistance to repay China could jeopardize critical foreign assistance budgets. As a result, the purchasing power and impact of aid grants diminish. More needs to be done to build the financial and institutional capacity of recipient countries to strengthen their ability to manage aid funds transparently. Providing technical assistance to improve their fiscal policies and debt management practices can reduce reliance on foreign aid for debt repayment. Negotiating debt-for-development swaps, for instance, can ensure that aid funds are used for their intended purposes.The money and resources granted to a low-income country can alter how it chooses to spend government revenue. Regardless of whether the assistance is from government to government, any time dollars are deposited in the central bank of a country in debt distress, those dollars are at a higher risk of being allocated to service the debt. Grants introduce budget volatility and dependency; they may be canceled from one year to the next. When a government receives increased aid, it has several options: it can lower revenues, raise expenditures, cut domestic borrowing, or adopt a mix of these strategies. This dynamic holds true whether the aid supports the budget directly or funds specific projects. When aid finances programs that otherwise would be funded domestically, the government must adjust its budget accordingly. Over a given period, increased aid can result in lower, higher, or unchanged revenues. Foreign assistance should not be used to pay debt.Upgrading the Development ToolboxThe United States’ choice to shift away from loans was intended to mitigate the risk of overindebtedness and maintain financial stability in developing countries. This change aimed to provide more sustainable and predictable aid without burdening recipient nations with debt; however, the developing world today is not the same as it was 30, 20, or even 10 years ago. There is now a pressing need for a refined approach to development. Grants may not be as attractive as they once were. The pervasiveness of China’s Belt and Road Initiative (BRI) demonstrates the Global South’s willingness to take on debt in exchange for infrastructure. These nations aim to graduate from relying on foreign aid to becoming self-sufficient, with robust economic growth driven by private enterprise. Additionally, many developing countries now have more financial resources from taxes and access to capital markets, allowing them to finance much of their own development. Various U.S.-led infrastructure initiatives are underway, but much work must be done to “enable a better offer.”In the world of development finance, concessional loans emerge as an instrument for facilitating sustainable growth and institutional reform in developing countries and markets. Concessional loans can provide a structured approach to development finance, ensuring that recipient countries have both the incentive and the means to enhance their economic and governance frameworks.Earning a Seat at the TableIn light of the merits of issuing loans through a geopolitical lens, how can the United States best counter China’s BRI? Insofar as the United States uses grants, it has no voice at the debt restructuring negotiation table (or creditor committees). Providing countries with aid through loans not only finances development projects but also offers crucial flexibility and crisis mitigation options through mechanisms such as debt restructuring and forgiveness. In the event of economic or financial turmoil, a country facing difficulties repaying its debts can negotiate with its creditors for restructuring or even partial forgiveness of its obligations. This ability to negotiate can help stabilize the economy by freeing up resources for essential public spending and investment, thereby preserving the nation’s development trajectory. One key element facilitating this process is creditor committees, which bring together representatives from different lenders, including multilateral institutions, governments, and private creditors, to negotiate unified responses. By acting collectively, these committees can enforce debt restructuring decisions across all creditors, ensuring that the burdens of economic crises are shared equitably. The United States should seek to have a seat at the table given the size of its investments in developing countries.This unified response is enabled by instruments such as collective action clauses (CACs). CACs are provisions included in sovereign debt contracts that allow a supermajority of bondholders to agree to a restructuring plan that binds all bondholders, even those who did not vote for the plan. These provisions are crucial for preventing holdout creditors from derailing a restructuring deal or seeking preferential treatment, which could exacerbate a country’s financial distress. Including CACs and creditor committees also helps integrate a diverse range of creditors, such as China, commercial lenders, and Eurobond holders, into coordinated relief efforts. Their participation is significant, as these creditors may not adhere to established norms such as those of the Paris Club, which has a long-standing history of helping developing countries manage their debts in times of crisis. By acting as a majority creditor, institutions such as the World Bank and IMF can wield significant influence within creditor committees, steering decisions toward sustainable outcomes. This practice ensures that all parties contribute to an equitable resolution, allowing developing nations to recover from crises more swiftly, avoid default, and maintain their path toward sustainable growth and development.Enhancing multilateral coordination is vital to support sustainable development. Multilateral institutions should strengthen frameworks to include collective action clauses and improve transparency, especially regarding Chinese debt. U.S. institutions and international financial institutions (IFIs) should leverage their influence as lenders within creditor committees to steer debt restructuring efforts toward sustainable outcomes. This approach ensures that restructuring is not only effective but also fair and transparent, helping to stabilize the global financial system.Improving InstitutionsLoans tend to improve the institutions of recipient countries. There is evidence that an increase in the degree of concessionality of assistance is good for growth in countries with poor policy and institutional environments and low GDP per capita. By contrast, countries that have better policy environments or are richer seem to absorb less concessional (but larger) aid flows more effectively. However, it is inconclusive whether concessionality helps growth in highly indebted countries. A high degree of loan concessionality improves aid effectiveness in poor countries and in countries with a poor policy environment. These results hold true in different specifications of the econometric model and are robust to different estimation techniques. However, the results of empirical analysis provide only weak evidence that more concessionality leads to higher growth in countries with high levels of debt. These results hold for concessional loans as well as grants, with the level of concessionality varying across loans (the highest level of concessional lending is a grant).Providing foreign aid to developing countries in the form of loans rather than grants can offer substantial benefits extending beyond immediate financial assistance. One key advantage is that loans can drive long-term institutional improvements, as in the argument for the nontraditional gains from trade related to free trade agreements (FTAs). This theory posits that FTAs compel signatory nations to enhance their economic and regulatory institutions, fostering better governance and stronger economic foundations. Similarly, development loans can instigate institutional reforms, particularly in areas such as corruption control, fiscal discipline, and governance transparency. For instance, loans from institutions such as the World Bank and IMF often come with conditions that require recipient nations to implement specific reforms. These conditions may include measures to improve fiscal management and governance.\nProviding foreign aid to developing countries in the form of loans rather than grants can offer substantial benefits extending beyond immediate financial assistance.\nBy imposing these reforms, development loans can catalyze broader institutional change, fostering a more stable and accountable governance structure. For example, the World Bank’s support for Rwanda through loans includes conditionality and support for reforms aimed at enhancing governance, transparency, and anticorruption measures. Beyond direct conditionality, loan-based financial support can drive governments to reform financial systems, deepen domestic markets, improve institutional oversight, and address systemic issues such as corruption, fiscal mismanagement, and governance inefficiencies. Grants provide financial relief but may not foster the same level of sustainable development as loans tied to institutional improvements.Addressing CounterargumentsThere are a number of counterarguments to concessional loans that need to be addressed:Issuing loans would exacerbate the current debt crisis.There is a serious concern that the United States, by becoming a lender, would add to the problem. The worst policy prescription the United States could pursue would be to replicate what China has done elsewhere in the world or issue loans to nations already overindebted to China.As previously discussed, funds intended for development may be appropriated for debt relief. Development agencies have already become a player in the debt crisis, but they do not have a voice at the table. Such agencies need a seat at the table if they are going to be a part of the solution. China is not interested in nursing indebted countries back to health but, rather, in maximizing its payoff. If development agencies do not want countries to depend on China, but rather to graduate to self-sufficiency and have access to U.S. creditors, then they must help these countries by offering an alternative source for loans.\nIf development agencies do not want countries to depend on China, but rather to graduate to self-sufficiency and have access to U.S. creditors, then they must help these countries by offering an alternative source for loans.\nA forgiven loan is the same as a grant.Taking out a loan provides a sense of ownership and accountability for the country incurring sovereign debt. Borrowing responsibly, unlike receiving grants, builds a credit history that eventually leads to greater market access. One of the issues that hinders emerging economies is the absence of robust financial institutions. Domestic lending channels can eventually develop sophisticated finance institutions in those nations. The examples of Singapore and South Korea demonstrate how loans from the United States can help develop a finance sector capable of surpassing this point of friction and achieving greater capacity. It is difficult to imagine a country that is a long-term development success story that did not develop robust financial sector capabilities. Loans can accelerate needed institutional change and develop financial institutions that can then serve other purposes in alignment with development goals. Unlike grants, loans can incorporate conditions for debt forgiveness, aligning with strategic goals even if circumstances change. Denominating foreign assistance as a loan provides options to resolve thorny issues. If desired, debt forgiveness may be tethered to milestones regarding democracy, human rights, transparency, good governance, and other pertinent metrics. The IMF, for example, tethers its lending to strict conditionality that aligns with its mission and its institutional risk management goals. More research and case studies are needed regarding the efficacy of loans versus grants and concessional versus nonconcessional financing. Although it would be useful to know which mode of foreign assistance is least wasteful and most effective at institutional development and what conditions affect efficacy, the geopolitical angle makes a clear distinction between the two instruments. China issues debt to expand its influence in the Global South, but the United States lacks a formidable alternative. Although allocating more resources to infrastructure projects, regardless of financial instrument, might be an important component of responding to the BRI, there is also a clear demand for loans.The United States and its allies already issue loans through MDBs.The U.S. contribution to MDBs is not a sufficient substitute for the United States offering its own lending instruments. The United States is not as influential in such multilateral institutions as it once was. These institutions are, in fact, multilateral by design; executing U.S. policy is not their mandate. Hence, the United States has found itself constrained in its ability to use multilateral institutions to advance policy preferences in a competitive environment, which reflects the multilateral nature of these institutions. Reassessing how to manage influence in such institutions is undoubtedly an important piece of a larger conversation on U.S. diplomacy, but lending agencies where the United States must compete for influence, such as the IMF, are not reliable tools for U.S.-specific goals.Viewing the debt crisis through a U.S.-China prism is inappropriate.The foremost priority of the Global South is its own prosperity, not the U.S.-China rivalry, which explains the Global South’s willingness to accept Chinese-owned debt. While U.S. strategy should not be overly reactionary to China, U.S. motives are, in part, driven by the great power competition with China. Beyond the immediate debt crisis undermining U.S. foreign aid and indirectly funding Chinese interests, there are few alternatives to Chinese loans. This arena is yet another soft power void China has filled because the United States has failed to lead. China’s approach has shown imperial tendencies, as evidenced by its seizing of ports for military purposes. Beyond the geostrategic implications of inaction, there are opportunity costs. By reentering the loan business, the United States can establish a foundation in emerging markets, fostering future diplomatic relations and economic growth by playing a more prominent role in resolving the debt crisis. The United States is spending tens of billions annually, and more countries and higher-income developing partners can be reached through loans. Hence, adding this tool to the U.S. foreign assistance toolbox expands the envelope of aid and the geographic reach of U.S. assistance. A Nuanced Approach to LoansGiven the insufficiency of MDBs, the Global South’s demand for infrastructure investments, geopolitical competition, and the ineffectiveness of foreign aid amid persistent debt crises, it is time to strategize how, when, and where to reintroduce loans, alongside grants, as a tool for international development. Whereas grants are indispensable for humanitarian aid and for sectors where revenue generation is not feasible, such as health and education, loans may well have a role in the U.S. development policy arsenal to help accomplish particular foreign policy objectives. The critical question is how to introduce loans effectively, ensuring benefits for developing countries, U.S. taxpayers, and diplomatic partnerships while countering China’s influence in the global arena. To achieve these goals, several considerations should be weighed to ensure the appropriate use of loans in development assistance.Deploy loans on a case-by-case basis.A systematic overhaul would take far too long, unnecessarily forfeit the practical and procedural expertise acquired in the current grant-based system, and severely weaken foreign aid in the short to medium term. Critically, loans should not be a one-size-fits-all solution; instead, they should be deployed on a highly customized case-by-case basis to ensure that the specific economic conditions and developmental needs of each country are adequately addressed. An effective loan-based strategy in an emerging market may not be appropriate for a low-income country.Additionally, in a scenario where donating to a middle-to-high-income country may come at the expense of programs needed in lower-income countries, perhaps a loan to the middle- or high-income country would help resources go further. Foreign assistance to higher-income countries should not detract from resources available to poorer nations. A more balanced approach might look like the immediate humanitarian needs that grants address, with long-term growth potential facilitated by loans.Prioritize significant sectors.Loans should be considered for projects that are economically viable and have potential to generate revenue, thereby enabling the recipient country to service the debt without compromising its financial stability. A win would be a project delivering on the developmental goals of the recipient country and the strategic interests of the United States, as well as creating a return on investment. Such projects could include the infrastructure, renewable energy, and technology sectors, which support workforce development, job creation, FDI, and other contributors to economic growth and stability.Evaluate country characteristics.Assessing the recipient country’s institutional capacity to manage and use loans effectively is crucial. This assessment includes evaluating governance structures, financial management systems, and transparency measures. Grants and loans perform better in countries with strong institutions. The United States does not want to make the same mistake China did with some of its BRI loans by overissuing loans to high-risk nations. A nation that has quality institutions or demonstrates a strong positive trajectory may be a good target recipient for loans.Consider political feasibility.Loans may be reserved for instances where grants might have political costs. Loans could be a useful tool in moments where the size and timeline of an endeavor may seem too large of an undertaking with grants. For example, aid to Ukraine is detracting from aid allocated elsewhere while donor fatigue compounds. A lend-lease model, similar to the Lend-Lease Act for the United Kingdom during World War II, could be employed. This model provides substantial long-term support to a trusted ally through loans rather than grants, emphasizing strategic partnership and future repayment. Framing aid to Ukraine in this way, with the Ukraine Democracy Defense Lend-Lease Act, has helped sustain its defense while aligning long-term repayment with broader geopolitical stability. In instances where a large-scale aid effort is required, loans can be used to sustain support without overwhelming immediate budgets or political capital.Do not abandon grants.An approach to development that includes loans should not spiral into something unintended. Issuing loans must not be mistaken for a prelude to a new iteration of the HIPC Initiative. The U.S. development apparatus has spent decades acquiring valuable technical expertise regarding grants. It would be unwise for development agencies to abandon or heavily reduce grant-based projects, but there are adjustments that, if pursued, would foster some of the benefits of loans.Again, a major concern with grant-based development projects is that the central banks of indebted nations will convert the funds to local currency and use this to pay off loans owed to China. To help counteract this, the United States should disburse money as projects are completed, not up front, monitoring disbursements pursuant to project completion. ConclusionThe U.S. government should consider issuing loans in order to complement existing grant mechanisms, help nations navigate the Beijing-bolstered debt crisis, and act in the most strategic sense. Loans should be designed with concessional terms, including extended durations, low interest rates, and grace periods, to ensure that they remain manageable for recipient countries. Loans and grants can supplement each other: grants can address immediate humanitarian needs and critical infrastructure, while loans can fund revenue-generating projects that build long-term economic growth. This combined approach ensures that urgent issues are addressed without exacerbating debt burdens while also promoting sustainable development and financial independence in recipient countries.\nThe U.S. government should consider issuing loans in order to complement existing grant mechanisms, help nations navigate the Beijing-bolstered debt crisis, and act in the most strategic sense.\nChina is exploiting nations by swapping debt for equity. This new era of mercantilism must be stopped. Reintroducing loans as a development tool, when done thoughtfully and strategically, can enhance the effectiveness of U.S. foreign assistance, foster sustainable development, create strong diplomatic ties, and effectively counter China’s growing influence in the developing world.Daniel F. Runde is a senior vice president, director of the Project on Prosperity and Development, and holds the William A. Schreyer Chair in Global Analysis at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Rafael Romeu is a senior associate (non-resident) with the Project on Prosperity and Development at CSIS. Austin Hardman is a research assistant with the Project on Prosperity and Development at CSIS.This paper was made possible by support provided by DevTech Systems, Inc., and was informed by two roundtables of experts held on June 6, 2024, and July 10, 2024.\nThis report is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nChina,\nInternational Development,\nEconomic Security, and\nGlobal Governance and Institutions\nImage\nDaniel F. Runde\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Office of the President\nImage\nRafael Romeu\nSenior Associate (Non-resident), Project on Prosperity and Development\nImage\nAustin Hardman\nFormer Research Assistant, Project on Prosperity and Development\nPrograms & Projects\nProject on Prosperity and Development\nGlobal Development","content_cn":"将优惠贷款重新纳入发展工具箱\n图片：Oleg，来自 Adobe Stock\n目录\n目录\n报告\n作者\nDaniel F. Runde、\nRafael Romeu\n和\nAustin Hardman\n发表于2024年8月20日\n可下载内容\n下载完整报告\n248kb\n引言全球债务规模已飙升至不可持续的水平，需要长期解决方案，而不是无休止的一系列纾困。帮助负债国家的流程效率低下，也不符合美国利益。为了成为面临债务危机国家解决方案的一部分，美国必须更新其贷款实践，并从中国向全球南方国家贷款的错误与成功中汲取经验。现在是认真重新考虑美国如何成为解决方案一部分的时候了。美国发展融资组合中是否存在优惠贷款的作用？当前美国完全通过赠款为外国援助提供资金的战略是否总是正确的？将发展融资与美国外交政策目标挂钩，同时实现财政可持续的结果，是与中国开展大国竞争的一项组成部分，但这一点迄今尚未得到令人满意的评估。2010年至2022年间，亚洲和太平洋地区的政府借款增加了近4倍，非洲增加了3倍，欧洲和中亚增加了2.5倍，拉丁美洲和加勒比地区增加了1.6倍。新冠肺炎疫情、俄罗斯入侵乌克兰，以及美联储和其他主要央行快速加息，恶化了许多负债严重的低收入和中等收入经济体的财政前景，尤其是容易受到市场波动影响的商品出口国。根据国际货币基金组织（IMF）的数据，2023年全球公共债务达到创纪录的97万亿美元，发展中国家承担的债务相对于其在全球经济中的份额而言不成比例。当新兴市场以创纪录的数量违约时，当前多边债务架构中的机构——包括国际货币基金组织、二十国集团（G20）和巴黎俱乐部——一直难以提供全面或及时的偿债减免、优惠融资和债务重组。至少有53个经济体——包括阿根廷、埃及、加纳、尼日利亚、巴基斯坦和斯里兰卡——要么已经拖欠部分债务，要么债务水平被国际货币基金组织认为不可持续。尽管这些国家仅占全球经济的5%，却约占世界人口的20%，并面临最高的借款成本。这场潜在的“债务海啸”对全球金融稳定构成重大威胁，并削弱了为联合国可持续发展目标（SDGs）中进展滞后的目标提供资金的努力。偿债支出如今占发展中国家国家预算的关键比例，使政府无法为基本公共服务以及吸引企业所需的其他投资支出。根据联合国贸易和发展会议2024年6月发布的一份报告，约有48个国家、涉及33亿人口，其利息支出超过教育或医疗支出。2022年，低收入国家向外部债权人支付的金额比其获得的外国援助多490亿美元。\n偿债支出如今占发展中国家国家预算的关键比例，使政府无法为基本公共服务以及吸引企业所需的其他投资支出。\n发展融资的演变\n融资：从贷款到赠款债务长期以来一直被认为是促进经济增长和创新的有力工具，只要使用得当。美国涉足发展贷款可以追溯到20世纪30年代末富兰克林·D·罗斯福总统的睦邻政策，当时美国开始通过美国进出口银行（EXIM）向拉丁美洲国家提供发展贷款。数十年来，美国贸易与发展署（USTDA）、进出口银行、美国国际开发金融公司（DFC）、美洲开发银行（IADB）以及世界银行集团等机构，在提供旨在促进全球发展的广泛贷款项目方面发挥了重要作用。例如，仅在2023年，世界银行集团就为基础设施、医疗卫生和教育等领域的各类项目拨付了近730亿美元。同样，最近成立的DFC在支持新兴市场经济发展方面发挥了重要作用，2022年承诺提供了74亿美元融资，涵盖可再生能源、医疗保健和技术等多个领域。\n\n尽管债务具有潜在益处，但其在发展领域的使用一直颇具争议，许多政策制定者由于担心过度负债和金融稳定问题而倾向于赠款而非贷款。这种观念转变受到2000年梅尔泽委员会建议的重大影响，该委员会主张将世界银行及其他发展机构的贷款中最多50%替换为赠款。委员会认为，赠款资金，尤其是用于卫生设施和饮用水等关键需求的资金，可以防止贫困国家陷入不可持续的债务。时任美国财政部长保罗·奥尼尔认为，“更多赠款、更少贷款”的理念是合理的，因为世界银行不是捐赠资金来抗击贫困，而是通过贷款使贫困国家“陷入困境”。\n\n梅尔泽委员会的证词建议，国际金融机构通过人道主义援助工作使用赠款，以改善低收入国家的生活质量。委员会提议，赠款应在独立审计师证明项目完成后直接支付给服务提供者。赠款将绕过腐败政府，而审计结果将改善绩效。乔治·W·布什总统领导的政府随后启动了半个世纪以来规模最大的援助项目之一——总统防治艾滋病紧急救援计划（PEPFAR），该项目至今仍由赠款资助。\n\n梅尔泽委员会报告发布前几年，国际货币基金组织和世界银行于1996年启动了重债穷国（HIPC）倡议。该倡议旨在确保没有贫困国家面临无法管理的债务负担，并向符合条件的国家提供大幅债务减免。债务免除被视为许多非洲国家开始走上积极经济发展轨道的原因之一。在此期间，天主教会和U2乐队主唱博诺等有影响力的人士倡导债务减免。他们支持并参与了“禧年2000”运动，该运动呼吁免除许多低收入国家的债务。这场行动主义影响了数位政治家的观点，并促成共和党主导的第106届国会批准用于国际债务减免的资金。\n\n2005年，HIPC倡议又得到\n多边债务减免倡议（MDRI）为来自国际货币基金组织、世界银行、泛美开发银行和非洲开发基金等机构的合格债务提供100%的减免。人们曾希望，这种免除能够彻底解决这一问题。然而，许多债务国在重债穷国倡议之后仍继续不负责任地借款。尽管重债穷国倡议和多边债务减免倡议提供了大规模债务减免，玻利维亚、海地、洪都拉斯和尼加拉瓜等国仍继续面临财政挑战。由于持续借款和经济管理不善，洪都拉斯和尼加拉瓜的债务水平再次上升；而玻利维亚和海地则苦于持续的贫困和政治不稳定，这削弱了债务减免带来的益处。这些倡议造成了道德风险，因为大规模的债务免除只能带来暂时的缓解，却没有解决债务国财政纪律和经济可持续性方面的根本问题。\n\n向赠款为基础的发展战略转型的根源，在重债穷国倡议、多边债务减免倡议以及梅尔泽委员会之前就已埋下。1973年《对外援助法》中体现的“新方向”战略，标志着思维从基础设施和政策改革等大型资本项目转向技术援助和满足基本人类需求。因此，世界银行和亚洲开发银行等多边开发银行（MDB）的基础设施投资占总投资的比例，在数十年间持续下降，直到21世纪初才重新上升。随着新的自下而上的方法得到实施，巴西、乌拉圭和委内瑞拉等较高收入发展中国家的项目逐步退出，项目被专门分配给收入最低的国家。直到20世纪80年代这一比例开始下降之前，贷款约占军事和经济援助总额的三分之一。到2001年，贷款仅占援助的1%。这一降幅部分是对当时出现的债务问题的回应。\n\n如今，美国的发展援助组合几乎完全以赠款为基础，2022财政年度（FY 2022）的对外援助总额估计为704亿美元，约占总支出的1%。其中，25%用于人道主义活动，21%用于卫生项目，18%用于和平与安全援助，18%用于经济发展援助，剩余18%用于项目支持、人权和多部门援助等其他目的。这些援助大多由非政府组织而非外国政府实施，凸显了一种优先采用赠款而非贷款的模式，以尽量降低发展中国家债务危机的风险。2023年，美国仍是全球最大的对外援助捐助国，约占捐助国政府提供的官方发展援助总额的29%。\n\n中国作为主要贷款方的崛起及其掠夺性贷款的使用\n\n当美国逐渐停止放贷时，全球南方基于贷款的融资并未停止。相反，各国开始从包括中国和商业债权人在内的替代来源获得贷款。由于借款需求巨大且获得发展融资的渠道有限，新兴市场越来越多地转向成本更高的私营部门和中国贷款方；这些贷款方在官方巴黎俱乐部机制之外运作，形成了一个更加分散的信贷格局，其远\n风险更高的特征。\n全球南方国家的贷款融资并未因美国不再提供贷款而停止。相反，各国转而寻求其他来源的贷款，包括中国和商业债权人。\n巴黎俱乐部是一个由主要经济体官方债权人组成的非正式组织，旨在为债务国面临的偿付困难寻找可持续的解决方案。它为主权债务重组提供平台，并基于共识和公平共享原则运作，确保所有参与债权人同意类似条款，并确保债务国得到公平对待。这种协调方式有助于防止无序违约，维护全球金融体系的稳定。中国无视巴黎俱乐部的原则和做法，带来了重大挑战。中国的贷款往往缺乏透明度、成本更高且条件苛刻，条款和条件未被公开。这种缺乏透明度和协调的做法可能导致债务陷阱，使借款国发现自己无力偿还债务，并可能因此丧失对关键资产的主权。这种做法削弱了巴黎俱乐部建立的合作框架，并为新兴市场创造了风险更高的信贷环境。中国成为发展中国家最大的官方贷款方，已被证明是不断升级的主权债务危机中的主要绊脚石，据估计，中国债务敞口最高达1.5万亿美元。北京罕见的不透明以及不愿免除债务，使国际货币基金组织无法提供技术援助，也无法实施依赖现有债权人提供金融保证的金融稳定计划。诸如二十国集团“共同框架”这样的紧急计划——该框架于2020年末制定，旨在为需要快速债务减免的低收入国家提供渠道——一直受到拖延、协调问题和执行挑战的困扰。这些问题很大程度上源于中国不愿作出实质性的债务减免让步，以及人们担心一些债权人，尤其是中国，正寻求比其他债权人更有利的单独协议。此外，人们还担心，国际货币基金组织的纾困资金或来自援助、汇款的硬通货流入会直接进入中国口袋。随着多边债务谈判陷入停滞，陷入困境的借款方在多年等待重组协议的过程中，却未从国际货币基金组织获得一分钱。\n中国成为发展中国家最大的官方贷款方，已被证明是不断升级的主权债务危机中的主要绊脚石，据估计，中国债务敞口最高达1.5万亿美元。\n中国成为主要贷款方，给全球发展融资带来了变革性影响。截至2017年，中国已成为世界最大的官方贷款方，超过世界银行、国际货币基金组织和巴黎俱乐部的总和。中国海外发展融资数据库详细记录了2008年至2021年间中国在海外作出的1099项发展融资承诺，总额达4980亿美元。这一贷款规模相当于同期世界银行主权贷款的83%，使中国的直接外国投资成为全球最活跃的投资之一。在中国每年800亿美元的发展融资组合中，绝大多数贷款投向低收入国家，主要集中在东南亚、非洲和南美洲。如今，全球国内生产总值中超过5%由\n产品（GDP）由中国的未偿债权构成。然而，中国的许多贷款并未被归类为发展贷款，或被分类为优惠贷款。2013年至2018年，中国对外援助总额平均每年为70亿美元，其中49%采取优惠贷款的形式。中国贷款几乎全部来自官方政府或国有控制渠道，因其不透明且具有掠夺性而受到重大批评。尽管中国的放贷受国家控制，但其体系是去中心化的，由多个机构管理，而这些机构不愿接受其贷款损失。自2000年以来，已有150起涉及中国向发展中国家提供贷款的违约、债务重组或其他信贷事件得到记录。自2016年以来，未能偿还这些贷款的国家比例已增加一倍以上，超过所有国家的10%。对一些国家而言，其外国贷款最多有50%来自中国，其中大多数国家将超过三分之一的政府收入用于偿还这笔外债。值得注意的是，斯里兰卡和赞比亚已经违约，甚至无法支付用于建设港口、矿山和发电厂的贷款利息。这种违约的快速增加表明，发展中国家因中国的放贷做法而承担了风险。中国的放贷策略往往涉及高利率和严格条款。许多此类贷款未被纳入最广泛使用的官方债务统计数据，从而造成隐藏债务问题。这意味着债务国和国际机构都无法完整了解世界各国欠中国多少钱，以及所欠债务的条件。例如，一项分析发现，赞比亚欠中国国有银行66亿美元，约为此前估计金额的两倍。这种系统性少报使全球有效管理和重组债务的努力更加复杂。中国的放贷也被广泛认为具有交易性质，受到获取石油、矿产和其他大宗商品等关键资源的愿望驱动。一些专家认为，北京即使在高风险环境中也愿意为基础设施项目提供融资，反映了这一战略。这种做法导致借款国的债务负担增加，并被批评为一种“债务陷阱外交”。加纳、巴基斯坦、斯里兰卡和赞比亚等国家由于无力管理高额债务偿还，面临严重的经济后果，包括违约和基本公共服务的大幅削减。中国通过国有企业开展放贷活动，崛起为主要债权人，这给全球经济秩序和现有债务危机带来了重大挑战。其贷款条款通常包括不披露协议和保密条款，使其他债权人难以评估一个国家对中国负债的全部规模。这一发展对全球债务重组倡议具有重大影响。中国平行的发展融资体系挑战了传统的西方援助提供和债务减免谈判方式；自第二次世界大战结束以来，这种方式一直占据主导地位。随着中国影响力持续扩大，其放贷做法以及由此给发展中国家造成的债务负担，仍是国际社会，尤其是美国及其\n盟友。债务损害外国援助由于债务危机的严重性，目前有十几个关键的战略性发展中国家——包括肯尼亚、老挝、蒙古、巴基斯坦和赞比亚——将更多资源用于支付对华债务利息，而不是用于教育和医疗等对发展至关重要的公共产品和服务。发展援助并非存在于真空之中。在危机和资本外流时期使用外国援助赠款，会严重损害援助的有效性。赠款属于不产生债务的资金流，一旦兑换为当地货币并在受援国境内用于支付人员或物资费用，就会被中央银行计入储备。外国援助资金流入发展中国家的商业银行，以便用于援助项目。这些存款由发展中国家的中央银行创造；中央银行是国有企业，接受外国赠款美元作为央行储备，并为当地商业银行创造本币存款。因此，与这种赠款资助的本币支出相对应的，是中央银行储备；当国内债券持有人、欧洲债券持有人、外国直接投资（FDI）投资者、中国以及商业贷款机构试图在受危机冲击的国家清算其投资时，这些储备用于稳定货币。国际货币基金组织（IMF）的项目试图利用一切可获得的美元稳定货币，以争取时间进行财政调整。这种做法不可避免地导致货币贬值，通常还会采取某种形式的资本管制以及削减预算。因此，当前的制度框架必然要求使用美国政府赠款和汇款来满足中央银行储备要求，而不是利用援助赠款的国际购买力来资助发展。预算削减、货币贬值、通货膨胀和多重汇率机制等政策工具，使政府能够将外国援助转化为储备，从而降低援助的国际购买力和影响。这削弱了美国国际开发署（USAID）赠款的作用；这些赠款本应花在商品和服务上以刺激发展，却反而在各国实施结构调整计划时被用作资金外流，以偿还债务。国际货币基金组织的研究表明，增加总体援助（包括贷款和赠款）往往会减少国内收入，尽管贷款和赠款的影响有所不同。虽然对借款国偿还外部债权人和重建储备的能力进行了密切监测，但在降低财政调整仅仅使中央银行储备被重新引导、用于在货币贬值和储备耗尽之前偿还中国贷款机构、商业债权人及本地债券持有人这一风险方面，所做努力不足。由于美国纳税人通过援助资金为储备提供资金，忽视利用外国援助偿还中国债务，可能会危及关键的外国援助预算。因此，援助赠款的购买力和影响都会减弱。应进一步建设受援国的金融和制度能力，增强其透明管理援助资金的能力。提供技术援助以改善其财政政策和债务管理实践，可以减少对外国援助偿还债务的依赖。例如，谈判债务换发展协议，可以确保援助资金用于其\n预期用途。授予低收入国家的资金和资源可能会改变其决定如何支出政府收入的方式。无论援助是政府对政府提供的，只要美元存入一个陷入债务困境国家的中央银行，这些美元就面临更高的风险，可能被用于偿还债务。赠款会引入预算波动和依赖性；它们可能从一年到下一年被取消。当政府获得增加的援助时，有几种选择：降低收入、增加支出、减少国内借款，或采取这些策略的组合。无论援助是直接支持预算，还是为具体项目提供资金，这种动态都成立。当援助为原本应由国内资金支持的项目提供融资时，政府必须相应调整预算。在特定时期内，增加援助可能导致收入减少、增加或保持不变。外国援助不应被用于偿还债务。\n\n升级发展工具箱\n美国选择摆脱贷款，旨在降低过度负债的风险，并维护发展中国家的金融稳定。这一改变旨在提供更加可持续和可预测的援助，同时不让受援国背负债务；然而，今天的发展中国家已经不同于30年前、20年前甚至10年前的情况。现在迫切需要一种更完善的发展方式。赠款可能已不像过去那样具有吸引力。中国“一带一路”倡议（BRI）的广泛存在表明，全球南方国家愿意以承担债务为代价换取基础设施。这些国家希望摆脱对外国援助的依赖，转而实现自给自足，由私人企业推动强劲的经济增长。此外，许多发展中国家如今拥有更多来自税收的财政资源，并能够进入资本市场，从而可以为自身发展提供大部分资金。美国主导的各种基础设施倡议正在推进，但要“提供更好的方案”，仍有大量工作要做。\n\n在发展融资领域，优惠贷款成为促进发展中国家和市场可持续增长与制度改革的一种工具。优惠贷款可以为发展融资提供一种结构化方式，确保受援国既有动力，也有手段来改善其经济和治理框架。\n\n赢得谈判桌上的一席之地\n鉴于从地缘政治角度看发放贷款的优点，美国如何才能最好地应对中国的“一带一路”倡议？只要美国使用赠款，它在债务重组谈判桌上（或债权人委员会中）就没有发言权。通过贷款向各国提供援助，不仅能为发展项目融资，还能通过债务重组和债务减免等机制，提供关键的灵活性和危机缓解选项。如果发生经济或金融动荡，一个难以偿还债务的国家可以与债权人协商重组债务，甚至部分免除其债务。这种谈判能力有助于通过释放资源用于必要的公共支出和投资来稳定经济，从而维护国家的发展轨迹。促进这一进程的一个关键要素是债权人委员会，它们汇集了来自不同贷款方的代表，包括多边机构，\n各国政府和私人债权人进行谈判，以形成统一的应对措施。通过集体行动，这些委员会可以在所有债权人中执行债务重组决定，确保经济危机的负担得到公平分担。鉴于美国在发展中国家的投资规模，美国应争取在谈判桌上占有一席之地。\n\n这种统一应对可通过集体行动条款（CACs）等工具实现。CACs是纳入主权债务合同中的条款，允许占多数的债券持有人同意一项重组计划，并使该计划对所有债券持有人具有约束力，即使其中一些人未投票支持该计划。这些条款对于防止拒不合作的债权人破坏重组协议或寻求优惠待遇至关重要，否则可能加剧一国的金融困境。纳入CACs和债权人委员会还有助于将包括中国、商业贷款机构和欧洲债券持有人在内的各类债权人纳入协调一致的救助行动。他们的参与意义重大，因为这些债权人可能不遵循既定规范，例如巴黎俱乐部的规范；巴黎俱乐部长期以来一直帮助发展中国家在危机时期管理债务。通过作为主要债权人采取行动，世界银行和国际货币基金组织等机构可以在债权人委员会中发挥重大影响力，引导决策走向可持续的结果。这种做法确保各方都为公平解决方案作出贡献，使发展中国家能够更迅速地从危机中复苏、避免违约，并保持可持续增长与发展的轨道。\n\n加强多边协调对于支持可持续发展至关重要。多边机构应强化框架，纳入集体行动条款并提高透明度，尤其是有关中国债务的透明度。美国机构和国际金融机构（IFIs）应利用其作为债权人委员会中贷款方的影响力，引导债务重组工作走向可持续的结果。这种做法确保重组不仅有效，而且公平透明，有助于稳定全球金融体系。\n\n改善机构\n贷款往往能够改善受援国的机构。证据表明，在政策和制度环境较差且人均GDP较低的国家，提高援助的优惠程度有利于经济增长。相比之下，政策环境较好或更加富裕的国家，似乎能够更有效地吸收优惠程度较低（但规模更大）的援助流入。然而，优惠程度是否有助于高负债国家增长，尚无定论。较高程度的贷款优惠性能够提高贫困国家以及政策环境较差国家的援助有效性。这些结果在计量经济学模型的不同设定下仍然成立，并且对不同的估计技术具有稳健性。然而，实证分析结果仅提供了微弱证据，表明在债务水平较高的国家，提高优惠程度会带来更高的增长。这些结果既适用于优惠贷款，也适用于赠款，因为不同贷款的优惠程度有所不同（优惠性贷款的最高形式就是赠款）。\n\n以贷款而非赠款的形式向发展中国家提供外国援助，其益处可能远远超出即时的财政……\n援助。贷款的一项关键优势在于，它们能够推动长期的制度改进，正如关于自由贸易协定（FTAs）相关贸易非传统收益的论述所指出的那样。该理论认为，自由贸易协定会迫使签署国改善其经济和监管制度，从而促进更好的治理和更坚实的经济基础。同样，发展贷款也能促成制度改革，尤其是在腐败控制、财政纪律和治理透明度等领域。例如，世界银行和国际货币基金组织（IMF）等机构提供的贷款通常附带条件，要求受援国实施具体改革。这些条件可能包括改善财政管理和治理的措施。\n以贷款而非赠款的形式向发展中国家提供外国援助，其好处可以远远超出即时的资金援助。\n通过施加这些改革，发展贷款能够催化更广泛的制度变革，促进建立更加稳定且负责任的治理结构。例如，世界银行通过贷款支持卢旺达，其中包括附加条件以及旨在加强治理、透明度和反腐败措施的改革支持。除了直接的附加条件之外，基于贷款的金融支持还可以推动政府改革金融体系、深化国内市场、加强制度监督，并解决腐败、财政管理不善和治理低效等系统性问题。赠款能够提供财政缓解，但可能无法像与制度改进挂钩的贷款那样促进同等程度的可持续发展。应对反驳意见关于优惠贷款，有一些反驳意见需要加以回应：发放贷款会加剧当前的债务危机。一个严重的担忧是，美国成为贷款方后，可能会使问题更加严重。美国能够采取的最糟糕的政策方案，是复制中国在世界其他地区所做的事情，或者向已经对中国负债过重的国家发放贷款。如前所述，原本用于发展的资金可能会被挪用于债务减免。发展机构已经成为债务危机中的参与者，但它们在决策桌上没有发言权。如果这些机构要成为解决方案的一部分，就需要在决策桌上占有一席之地。中国无意帮助负债国家恢复健康，而是要使自身收益最大化。如果发展机构不希望各国依赖中国，而是希望这些国家走向自给自足并获得美国债权人的支持，那么它们就必须通过提供替代性贷款来源来帮助这些国家。\n如果发展机构不希望各国依赖中国，而是希望这些国家走向自给自足并获得美国债权人的支持，那么它们就必须通过提供替代性贷款来源来帮助这些国家。\n被免除的贷款与赠款相同。借款能够使承担主权债务的国家产生一种所有权感和问责意识。与接受赠款不同，负责任地借款能够建立信用记录，最终带来更广泛的市场准入。阻碍新兴经济体发展的问题之一，是缺乏健全的金融机构。国内贷款渠道最终可以在这些国家发展出复杂的金融机构。新加坡和韩国的例子表明\n美国贷款如何帮助发展出能够突破这一摩擦点并实现更大能力的金融部门。很难想象一个长期发展成功的国家没有发展出强大的金融部门能力。贷款可以加速必要的制度变革，并发展金融机构，使其随后能够配合发展目标服务于其他用途。与赠款不同，贷款可以纳入债务豁免条件，即使情况发生变化，也能与战略目标保持一致。将对外援助定性为贷款，为解决棘手问题提供了选项。如果需要，可以将债务豁免与民主、人权、透明度、善治及其他相关指标的里程碑挂钩。例如，国际货币基金组织将其贷款与严格的条件性挂钩，这些条件与其使命和机构风险管理目标相一致。关于贷款与赠款、优惠融资与非优惠融资的有效性，还需要更多研究和案例分析。尽管了解哪种对外援助方式最不浪费、在制度发展方面最有效，以及哪些条件会影响其有效性，将很有帮助，但地缘政治角度使这两种工具之间存在明确区别。中国通过发放债务来扩大其在全球南方的影响力，但美国缺乏一个有力的替代方案。无论采用何种金融工具，为基础设施项目分配更多资源可能是应对“一带一路”倡议的重要组成部分，但贷款同样存在明确需求。美国及其盟友已经通过多边开发银行发放贷款。美国对多边开发银行的贡献，并不足以替代美国提供自身的贷款工具。美国在这些多边机构中的影响力已不如从前。事实上，这些机构在设计上就是多边的；执行美国政策并非其职责。因此，在竞争环境中，美国发现自己利用多边机构推进政策偏好的能力受到限制，这反映了这些机构的多边性质。重新评估如何管理在此类机构中的影响力，无疑是关于美国外交的更大讨论中的重要组成部分，但在国际货币基金组织等机构中，美国必须竞争影响力，这些贷款机构并不是实现美国特定目标的可靠工具。用美中棱镜审视债务危机是不恰当的。全球南方的首要优先事项是自身繁荣，而不是美中竞争，这解释了全球南方为何愿意接受中国拥有的债务。虽然美国战略不应对中国作出过度反应，但美国的动机部分受到与中国进行大国竞争的驱动。除了当前债务危机削弱美国对外援助并间接资助中国利益之外，中国贷款几乎没有替代方案。由于美国未能发挥领导作用，中国填补了这一领域的又一个软实力空白。中国的做法已表现出帝国主义倾向，其为军事目的夺取港口就是证明。除了不作为的地缘战略影响之外，还存在机会成本。通过重新进入贷款业务，美国可以在新兴市场建立基础，促进未来的外交关系和经济\n通过在解决债务危机方面发挥更突出作用来促进增长。美国每年支出数百亿美元，并且可以通过贷款覆盖更多国家和收入较高的发展中伙伴。因此，将这一工具加入美国对外援助工具箱，能够扩大援助规模以及美国援助的地理覆盖范围。\n\n对贷款采取更细致入微的方法\n\n鉴于多边开发银行能力不足、全球南方对基础设施投资的需求、地缘政治竞争，以及在持续债务危机中对外援助成效不彰，现在是时候制定战略，研究如何、何时以及在哪里重新引入贷款，并将其与赠款并列作为国际发展的工具。赠款对于人道主义援助以及无法产生收入的领域（如卫生和教育）不可或缺；而贷款很可能在美国发展政策工具箱中发挥作用，帮助实现特定的外交政策目标。关键问题在于如何有效引入贷款，确保发展中国家、美国纳税人和外交伙伴关系都能受益，同时遏制中国在全球舞台上的影响力。为实现这些目标，应权衡若干因素，以确保在发展援助中恰当使用贷款。\n\n根据具体情况部署贷款。\n\n系统性改革耗时过长，也会不必要地放弃当前以赠款为基础的体系所积累的实践和程序专业知识，并在短期至中期内严重削弱对外援助。至关重要的是，贷款不应成为一刀切的解决方案；相反，应高度定制化地根据具体情况逐案部署，以确保充分应对每个国家的具体经济状况和发展需求。在新兴市场中有效的贷款战略，可能并不适用于低收入国家。\n\n此外，如果向中高收入国家提供赠款可能会牺牲低收入国家所需的项目，那么，也许向中高收入国家提供贷款能让资源发挥更大作用。对高收入国家的对外援助不应挤占可用于较贫困国家的资源。更平衡的方法可以是：由赠款满足眼前的人道主义需求，同时通过贷款促进长期增长潜力。\n\n优先考虑重要领域。\n\n应考虑将贷款用于经济上可行且具有创收潜力的项目，从而使受援国能够偿还债务而不损害其金融稳定。理想的项目应同时实现受援国的发展目标和美国的战略利益，并创造投资回报。这类项目可以包括基础设施、可再生能源和技术领域；这些领域能够支持劳动力发展、创造就业、吸引外国直接投资（FDI），以及推动经济增长和稳定的其他因素。\n\n评估国家特征。\n\n评估受援国有效管理和使用贷款的制度能力至关重要。这项评估包括考察治理结构、财务管理体系和透明度措施。在制度健全的国家，赠款和贷款的表现更好。美国不希望重蹈中国在部分“一带一路”（BRI）贷款上的覆辙，向高风险国家过度发放贷款。一个国家如果\n拥有高质量制度或展现出强劲积极发展轨迹的国家，可能是贷款的良好目标受援国。\n\n考虑政治可行性。在赠款可能带来政治成本的情况下，可以保留贷款。对于某些项目而言，如果其规模和时间跨度显得过于庞大，赠款可能难以承担，此时贷款可以成为有用工具。例如，对乌克兰的援助正在挤占原本分配给其他地区的援助，而捐助者疲劳情绪也在加剧。可以采用类似二战期间《租借法案》对英国实施的租借模式。这一模式通过贷款而非赠款，为值得信赖的盟友提供大量长期支持，强调战略伙伴关系和未来偿还。以这种方式构建对乌克兰的援助，并制定《乌克兰民主防御租借法案》，有助于维持其防御能力，同时使长期偿还与更广泛的地缘政治稳定保持一致。在需要大规模援助行动的情况下，可以利用贷款维持支持，而不会压垮即时预算或政治资本。\n\n不要放弃赠款。包含贷款的发展方式不应演变成意料之外的结果。发放贷款不能被误认为是新一轮重债穷国倡议（HIPC Initiative）的前奏。美国发展机构用了数十年积累有关赠款的宝贵技术专长。发展机构放弃或大幅削减以赠款为基础的项目是不明智的，但如果采取某些调整，就能促进贷款带来的一些益处。再次强调，以赠款为基础的发展项目面临的一个主要问题是，负债国家的中央银行会将资金兑换成本币，并用这些资金偿还欠中国的贷款。为帮助应对这一问题，美国应在项目完成后再拨付资金，而不是预先拨付，并根据项目完成情况监督资金拨付。\n\n结论\n美国政府应考虑发放贷款，以补充现有的赠款机制，帮助各国应对北京助推的债务危机，并采取最具战略意义的行动。贷款应以优惠条件设计，包括延长偿还期限、低利率和宽限期，以确保受援国能够负担。贷款和赠款可以相互补充：赠款可以应对紧迫的人道主义需求和关键基础设施建设，而贷款可以资助创收项目，促进长期经济增长。这种综合方式既能确保紧迫问题得到处理而不加重债务负担，也能推动受援国实现可持续发展和财政独立。\n\n美国政府应考虑发放贷款，以补充现有的赠款机制，帮助各国应对北京助推的债务危机，并采取最具战略意义的行动。\n\n中国正通过以债换股剥削各国。这个新的重商主义时代必须被制止。以审慎且具有战略性的方式重新将贷款作为发展工具，可以提高美国对外援助的有效性，促进可持续发展，建立强有力的外交关系，并有效遏制中国在发展中世界日益增长的影响力。Daniel F. Runde是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）高级副总裁、繁荣与发展项目主任，并担任William A. Schreyer全球分析主席。\n在华盛顿特区，Rafael Romeu 是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）繁荣与发展项目的高级副研究员（非驻会）。Austin Hardman 是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）繁荣与发展项目的研究助理。本论文得到了 DevTech Systems, Inc. 提供的支持，并参考了于2024年6月6日和2024年7月10日举行的两场专家圆桌会议。\n本报告由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）编制。CSIS 是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS 不持有具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n中国，\n国际发展，\n经济安全，以及\n全球治理与机构\n图片\nDaniel F. Runde\n高级顾问（非驻会），总裁办公室\n图片\nRafael Romeu\n高级副研究员（非驻会），繁荣与发展项目\n图片\nAustin Hardman\n前研究助理，繁荣与发展项目\n项目与计划\n繁荣与发展项目\n全球发展","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-08-20T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2141","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"言辞与现实：菲律宾、东盟与南海","title_en":"Rhetoric vs. Reality: The Philippines, ASEAN, and the South China Sea","summary_cn":"文章分析菲律宾将于2026年接任东盟轮值主席国之际的南海政策。作者指出，马科斯总统虽将推动南海行为准则列为优先事项，但更多是言辞姿态；东盟峰会上多数成员国表态谨慎，而马尼拉实际进展来自与美国的防务合作，包括新设“菲律宾特遣队”及联盟战备行动计划，并拓展与加拿大、日本、新西兰、德国、印度等国的防务伙伴关系。文章认为菲律宾将采取务实平衡策略，以主席国身份强调准则进程，同时培育能产生实际效果的小多边安全网络。","summary_en":"The commentary examines the Philippines' South China Sea policy as it prepares to chair ASEAN in 2026. While President Marcos has made finalizing the Code of Conduct a stated priority, the author argues this is largely rhetorical: ASEAN members remained cautious at the summit, and Manila's tangible security progress comes from expanding defense partnerships, notably with the United States, including the new Task Force Philippines and an Alliance Readiness Action Plan, plus agreements with Canada, Japan, New Zealand, Germany, and India. Manila is expected to balance ASEAN diplomacy with minilateral security cooperation that consensus-based mechanisms cannot deliver.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:54:18.861767+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/rhetoric-vs-reality-philippines-asean-and-south-china-sea","content":"Rhetoric vs. Reality: The Philippines, ASEAN, and the South China Sea\nPhoto: Cheng Yiheng/Xinhua/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nMonica Sato\nPublished November 7, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nWith the conclusion of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) Summit in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia, the Philippines is preparing to assume the chairmanship of the grouping in 2026. Its new role as ASEAN chair comes at a historic time, coinciding with the tenth anniversary of the 2016 arbitral award ruling amid a period of heightened tensions with China in the South China Sea. Philippine President Ferdinand “Bongbong” Marcos Jr. has repeatedly identified finalizing the long-stalled Code of Conduct (COC) for the South China Sea as his priority for the chairmanship. However, this emphasis appears to be largely rhetorical. Manila’s actions suggest that meaningful progress in addressing ongoing security challenges will depend less on ASEAN consensus and more on its expanding network of defense partnerships, particularly with the United States.This year’s ASEAN Summit underscored the enduring limitations of ASEAN’s collective approach to the South China Sea. Most member states issued cautious statements and avoided directly addressing recent developments, including China’s declaration of a nature reserve at the Philippine-claimed Scarborough Shoal, its deployment of buoys, and its continued ramming and use of water cannons against Philippine vessels. As the 2025 chair, Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim reiterated that disputes should be resolved within ASEAN and warned that the involvement of “outside forces” would only heighten tensions. While President Marcos publicly agreed with this sentiment, his administration continues to pursue partnerships beyond the bloc to deter further Chinese escalation at sea.In contrast to Prime Minister Anwar’s restrained tone, President Marcos delivered remarks described by Philippine media as “strong.” Without naming China directly, he criticized the “dangerous maneuvers and coercive use of tools and equipment to interfere with or obstruct legitimate and routine Philippine activities in [our own] maritime zones and airspace.” His comments at the 20th East Asia Summit were even more pointed. In the presence of Chinese Premier Li Qiang, he condemned Beijing’s declaration of a nature reserve at Scarborough Shoal, asserting that the feature “has long been part of Philippine territory.” At a subsequent press conference, President Marcos maintained that his statements were not provocative but a reflection of the reality that the Philippines is facing in the South China Sea and added that if ASEAN “fails [to resolve issues], at least we should keep finding a way to manage the tensions . . . with some level of modus vivendi for all involved.”President Marcos’s remarks illustrate Manila’s evolving diplomatic policy of reaffirming ASEAN centrality in principle while simultaneously widening its strategic engagement with other partners. Although the Philippines remains supportive of the COC process, the slow pace of negotiations has reinforced a growing consensus in Manila that ASEAN alone cannot deliver meaningful security outcomes in the South China Sea, especially when tensions now can directly threaten the safety and livelihoods of Filipino citizens.Developments at the ASEAN Defence Ministers’ Meeting Plus demonstrated where Manila’s tangible security progress is being made—nearly a week after the main ASEAN Summit. U.S. Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth and Philippine Secretary of National Defense Gilberto Teodoro jointly announced the creation of Task Force Philippines and the completion of an Alliance Readiness Action Plan. Modeled after Task Force Ayungin, which was revealed last year as a U.S. military unit focused on allied response to potential crises in the South China Sea, it offers a step beyond current initiatives coordinated by the Joint U.S. Military Assistance Group. Under the jurisdiction of the U.S. Indo-Pacific Command, Task Force Philippines will reportedly have a wider mandate than its predecessor, covering other missions like disaster relief across the archipelago, but should still be expected to focus on the South China Sea. The task force will include around 60 permanent personnel and be led by a one-star general or flag officer, allowing for greater flexibility in responding to contingencies.Secretary Teodoro emphasized that Manila and Washington must not only strengthen their alliance but “add more allies”—a goal the Philippines has already begun to actively pursue. The Philippines and Canada signed a Status of Visiting Forces Agreement (SOVFA) over the weekend, Ottawa’s first such arrangement in the Indo-Pacific. And on the sidelines of the 2025 Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation Summit, South Korean President Lee Jae Myung described the Philippines as a “special friend” and called for closer cooperation between the two countries, while South Korea’s Hanwha Ocean promised to support the Philippine Navy’s emerging submarine program.These developments add to a growing list of Manila’s recent defense partnerships, including a reciprocal access agreement with Japan, a SOVFA with New Zealand, a defense cooperation agreement with Germany, and several defense agreements with India. Additionally, the Philippines and France are in the process of negotiating their own visiting forces agreement. These arrangements highlight Manila’s effort to build a network of security partners capable of supporting deterrence and capacity-building outside ASEAN.Looking ahead, the Philippines faces a delicate balancing act. Its ASEAN chairmanship offers an opportunity to reaffirm the grouping’s role in maintaining regional stability, yet it also compels Manila to navigate the bloc’s internal divisions, particularly among members seeking to avoid direct confrontation with China. President Marcos’s emerging approach appears to be pragmatic. The Philippines will likely use its chairmanship to highlight ASEAN’s commitment to peaceful dispute resolution through its continued emphasis of the COC without expecting breakthroughs on the state of negotiations. At the same time, Manila will cultivate its network of “minilateral” defense partnerships capable of generating operational outcomes that ASEAN’s consensus-based mechanisms cannot.The Philippines’ 2026 ASEAN chairmanship will likely be both symbolic and strategic. It marks a decade since Manila’s legal victory at The Hague, underscoring its enduring commitment to a rules-based maritime order. At the same time, it represents an opportunity to reconcile ASEAN diplomacy with security realities for the Philippine government in the South China Sea. President Marcos’s challenge now will be to uphold ASEAN principles while advancing Philippine national interests. If successful, the Philippines could position itself as both a defender of ASEAN centrality and a driver of multilateral security cooperation—the success of which will shape not only Manila’s chairmanship but also Southeast Asia’s trajectory in navigating great power competition at sea.Monica Sato is a research associate with the Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAsia,\nSoutheast Asia, and\nGeopolitics and International Security\nImage\nMonica Sato\nResearch Associate, Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative\nPrograms & Projects\nAsia Maritime Transparency Initiative\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"修辞与现实：菲律宾、东盟与南中国海\n照片：Cheng Yiheng/新华社/盖蒂图片社\n评论\n作者\nMonica Sato\n发表于2025年11月7日\n远程可视化\n随着东南亚国家联盟（东盟）峰会在马来西亚吉隆坡落幕，菲律宾正准备于2026年担任该组织轮值主席国。菲律宾担任东盟主席的这一新角色正值一个历史性时刻，既逢2016年仲裁裁决十周年，也处于与中国在南中国海紧张关系加剧的时期。菲律宾总统费迪南德·“邦邦”·马科斯多次表示，完成久拖未决的《南中国海行为准则》（COC）是其担任主席期间的优先事项。然而，这一强调似乎主要停留在修辞层面。马尼拉的行动表明，应对持续的安全挑战能否取得实质进展，与其说取决于东盟共识，不如说更多取决于其不断扩大的防务伙伴网络，尤其是与美国的伙伴关系。\n今年的东盟峰会凸显了东盟在南中国海采取集体方针的长期局限。大多数成员国发表了谨慎声明，避免直接谈及近期事态发展，包括中国在菲律宾声称拥有主权的斯卡伯勒浅滩宣布设立自然保护区、部署浮标，以及继续冲撞菲律宾船只并使用水炮。作为2025年轮值主席国，马来西亚总理安瓦尔·易卜拉欣重申，争端应在东盟内部解决，并警告“外部力量”的介入只会加剧紧张局势。尽管马科斯总统公开表示赞同这一观点，但其政府仍在寻求该集团之外的伙伴关系，以遏制中国在海上的进一步升级行动。\n与安瓦尔总理克制的语调形成对比，马科斯总统发表了被菲律宾媒体称为“强硬”的讲话。他没有直接点名中国，而是批评“危险的机动，以及强制使用工具和设备，干扰或阻碍菲律宾在（我们自己的）海域和空域开展的合法且例行的活动”。他在第20届东亚峰会上的言论更加尖锐。在中国国务院总理李强在场的情况下，他谴责北京在斯卡伯勒浅滩宣布设立自然保护区，声称该地“长期以来一直是菲律宾领土的一部分”。在随后举行的新闻发布会上，马科斯总统坚持认为，他的言论并非挑衅，而是反映了菲律宾在南中国海面临的现实；他还补充说，如果东盟“未能（解决问题），至少我们应继续寻找管理紧张局势的方式……为所有相关方维持某种程度的共处模式”。\n马科斯总统的言论展现了马尼拉不断演变的外交政策：原则上重申东盟的中心地位，同时扩大与其他伙伴的战略接触。尽管菲律宾仍支持《行为准则》进程，但谈判进展缓慢，已在马尼拉强化了一种日益增长的共识，即东盟单独无法在南中国海带来有意义的安全成果，尤其是在当前紧张局势可能直接威胁菲律宾公民安全和生计的情况下。\n东盟国防部长会议—扩大会议的进展表明，马尼拉正在哪里取得切实的安全进展——这距离东盟主峰会结束已近一周。美国\n美国国防部长皮特·赫格塞思与菲律宾国防部长吉尔贝托·特奥多罗联合宣布成立“菲律宾特遣部队”，并完成一项联盟战备行动计划。该计划以去年公布的“阿云津特遣部队”为蓝本；“阿云津特遣部队”是美国军方专注于盟友应对南海潜在危机的单位。它比目前由美国联合军事援助小组协调的倡议更进一步。在美国印太司令部管辖下，据报道，“菲律宾特遣部队”的任务范围将比其前身更广，涵盖整个群岛的灾害救援等其他任务，但预计仍将重点关注南海。该特遣部队将包括约60名常驻人员，并由一名一星将军或旗官领导，从而能够更灵活地应对突发事态。\n\n特奥多罗部长强调，马尼拉和华盛顿不仅必须加强联盟，还要“增加更多盟友”——菲律宾已经开始积极追求这一目标。菲律宾和加拿大于周末签署了《访问部队地位协定》（SOVFA），这是渥太华在印太地区达成的首个此类安排。在2025年亚太经济合作组织峰会期间，韩国总统李在明将菲律宾称为“特殊朋友”，并呼吁两国加强合作；与此同时，韩国韩华海洋公司承诺支持菲律宾海军正在推进的潜艇项目。\n\n这些进展加入了马尼拉近期防务伙伴关系不断扩大的清单，其中包括与日本签署的互惠准入协定、与新西兰签署的《访问部队地位协定》、与德国签署的防务合作协定，以及与印度签署的多项防务协议。此外，菲律宾和法国正就各自的访问部队协定进行谈判。这些安排凸显了马尼拉正努力构建一个安全伙伴网络，使其能够在东盟之外支持威慑与能力建设。\n\n展望未来，菲律宾面临着微妙的平衡任务。担任东盟轮值主席国为重申该组织在维护地区稳定方面的作用提供了机会，但也迫使马尼拉应对集团内部的分歧，尤其是那些寻求避免与中国直接对抗的成员之间的分歧。马科斯总统正在形成的方针似乎是务实的。菲律宾很可能会利用其主席国任期，继续强调《南海行为准则》（COC），以突出东盟致力于和平解决争端，同时不期待谈判现状取得突破。与此同时，马尼拉将培育其“多边小组”防务伙伴网络，使其能够产生东盟基于共识的机制无法实现的行动成果。\n\n菲律宾2026年的东盟轮值主席国任期可能兼具象征意义和战略意义。这标志着马尼拉在海牙取得法律胜利十周年，彰显其对基于规则的海洋秩序的持久承诺。同时，这也代表着一个机会，使菲律宾政府能够在南海问题上调和东盟外交与安全现实。马科斯总统如今面临的挑战，将是在推进菲律宾国家利益的同时维护东盟原则。如果成功，菲律宾或许能够将自己定位为东盟中心地位的捍卫者和多边安全合作的推动者——这一成功\n这不仅将塑造马尼拉的主席任期，也将塑造东南亚在海上应对大国竞争的走向。莫妮卡·佐藤是华盛顿战略与国际问题研究中心亚洲海事透明度倡议的研究助理。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）发布。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性，不代表任何专有利益。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人的意见。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n亚洲、\n东南亚，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全\n图片\n莫妮卡·佐藤\n亚洲海事透明度倡议研究助理\n项目与计划\n亚洲海事透明度倡议\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-11-07T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-465","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"俄罗斯对伙伴援助的吸收上限：制度僵化与外部支持的局限","title_en":"Russia’s Cap on Partner Aid: Institutional Rigidity and the Limits of External Support","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）简报探讨中国、伊朗和朝鲜对俄援助为何未能充分发挥效力。作者提出“潜在能力”与“实现能力”的区分，认为俄罗斯能较顺畅地吸收现成装备类物资援助，却难以消化需要机构、组织或理念变革的支持，如中国提供的侦察情报目标数据和朝鲜派兵。俄军制度僵化、指挥文化集中、腐败与不透明限制了伙伴援助转化为战斗力，俄罗斯需要比乌克兰多得多的援助才能取得相当效果。","summary_en":"A CSIS brief examines why aid from China, Iran, and North Korea has not fully translated into Russian combat power. Distinguishing potential capacity from realized capacity, the authors argue Russia absorbs familiar equipment aid with relative ease but struggles with support requiring institutional, organizational, or conceptual change, such as Chinese ISR-cued targeting data and North Korean troop deployments. Russian institutional rigidity, centralized command culture, corruption, and opacity limit the conversion of partner aid into battlefield effect, meaning Russia needs far more aid than Ukraine to achieve comparable results.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:03:34.393156+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/russias-cap-partner-aid-institutional-rigidity-and-limits-external-support","content":"Russia’s Cap on Partner Aid: Institutional Rigidity and the Limits of External Support\nPhoto: Kremlin Press Service/Anadolu/Getty Images\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nBrief\nby\nWesley Bailey\nand\nDaniel Byman\nPublished July 31, 2026\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Brief\n833kb\nThe IssueChinese, Iranian, and North Korean support has proven essential to Russia’s war machine, yet Russia absorbs this foreign support unevenly, limiting both its impact and Russia’s military effectiveness. Russia metabolizes material aid such as familiar equipment with relatively low friction, simply replacing previous tools. However, Moscow’s war machine struggles to incorporate support that requires institutional, organizational, or conceptual change. Chinese-provided ISR-cued (intelligence, surveillance, and reconnaissance) targeting and North Korean troop deployment are the primary examples of this phenomenon. When inputs require simple reequipment, such as Chinese-supplied military components or Iranian Shahed drone production, Russia encounters relatively little difficulty. In contrast, when inputs require force reorganization, as is the case for Chinese-provided ISR-cued targeting data, Russia stalls, unable to adapt to the challenges posed by language, training, and narrower interoperability. Russian institutional rigidity restricts its ability to properly use partner support, and its recent innovations have merely worked around this constraint without solving it.IntroductionRussia faces mounting battlefield setbacks as Ukraine strikes deeper into its territory; even Russian President Vladimir Putin reportedly conceded that Russian forces are not advancing “as quickly as . . . [Moscow] would like.”1 As of mid-2026, Russia has suffered 1.4 million battlefield casualties from the war in Ukraine, including as many as 450,000 deaths.2 Moreover, Ukraine has gained the initiative, launching over 400 strikes 100 km or more into Russian territory since the beginning of 2026.3 These Russian setbacks have occurred despite continued support from Russia’s partners—principally China, Iran, and North Korea—which have offered both rhetorical support and continued material aid.Western discussions of support for Russia usually center on questions of munition quantities, the identification of components, and the types of satellite access these countries provide, among other forms of assistance. Yet the crucial issues surrounding how—or even if—Russia has been able to utilize partner aid remain unexplored. In situations where Russia has fallen short of success despite significant partner aid, the question becomes: Are these failures the result of isolated mistakes—independent of partner support—or is the Russian war machine struggling to operationalize certain types of partner aid? Aid receipt differs by potential capacity (the arrival of an input) and realized capacity (the ability to exploit that input). If Russia is unable to make use of partner-provided battlefield aid—that is, unable to convert potential capacity into realized capacity—the possession of that aid loses significance.Russia receives substantial and increasing partner aid—an estimated 90 percent of Russia’s microelectronics came from China in 2023, and North Korea has sent an estimated 15 million rounds of 152 mm artillery shells as of March 2026, for example—providing the country with significant potential capacity. However, Russia often stalls at realized capacity, unable to exploit the aid its partners provide.4 This is partially due to the quality of components—for example, North Korean artillery shells have frequently been of poor quality.5 The primary impediment to realizing the aid capacity Russia’s partners provide, though, is institutional rigidity. Although Russia has adapted since the onset of the Ukraine war, important institutional constraints remain, including limitations in Russian satellite imagery processing capacity and the scope and nature of its bilateral partnerships. These factors constrain Russia’s ability to fully exploit some of the aid it receives, such as space-based ISR and additional foreign forces.This brief examines the aid provided by Russia’s partners both in terms of its potential capacity and its realized capacity, in the context of the Ukraine war and Western aid to Ukraine, in order to determine the strategic impact of current aid supplies within the conflict. Examining Ukraine’s incorporation of foreign aid reveals that the key enabler to increasing realized aid capacity is innovation, particularly operational and institutional. The analysis below demonstrates that, absent significant institutional change—which seems unlikely given the lack of pressure for reform from Russia or its partners—Russia will need substantially larger amounts of aid to achieve its goals than Ukraine will to reach its.Russia’s Successes and FailuresRussia’s institutional rigidity is structural and self-reinforcing, encompassing both civil and military institutions. Constant bureaucratic infighting has made reform difficult, and a culture steeped in conservatism and nostalgia discourages innovation or adaptation. Decisionmaking is concentrated to the point that rarely is someone outside the presidential administration empowered to propose even the basic trade-offs reform requires. Moreover, a pervasive fear of exposing the war’s realities, and thus undermining Putin, discourages honest analysis.6 The defense industrial base is also plagued by poor management, corruption, and dependence on foreign technology—especially Western—meaning that it cannot provide substitutes for high-end imported components at scale. This administrative culture makes it hard for the military to break with the past, despite the battlefield clearly demanding adaptation.7\nRussian military institutions reward loyalty over competence and treat decentralization, delegation, and initiative as threats.\nRussia’s military culture reinforces this broader institutional rigidity. The military’s educational and training institutions remain outdated, single-service focused, and oriented toward memorization over independent thought, producing officers averse to innovation, disinclined to delegate, and even prone to persisting with failing courses of action.8 Together, these patterns reinforce Russia’s Soviet-style centralized command culture, which is rooted in a lack of trust between echelons. Overall, Russian military institutions reward loyalty over competence and treat decentralization, delegation, and initiative as threats.The changing role of Starlink—SpaceX’s low-earth orbit constellation that Ukraine was granted access to for military communication—in frontline coordination and drone relay within the Ukraine war reveals the impact of Russia’s institutional rigidity. Russian reliance on the U.S.-based Starlink for frontline communications and drone operations was documented by Ukrainian intelligence, Western analysts, and U.S. lawmakers as a structural vulnerability for years before Moscow took notice. When SpaceX deactivated illicit Russian access to Starlink in February 2026, the long-standing domestic fallback—the commercial Yamal satellite network owned by Gazprom—proved unable to cover the front line or even to handle drone video, leading Russian units to refer to it as unstable. In one intercepted radio transmission, a Russian serviceman stated, “We’ve hit a wall again because our country doesn’t want to spend money on anything or do anything useful for the front . . . Starlink was invented by one person—why couldn’t we have made something similar in all this time?”9Although Russia’s dependence on Starlink was a known vulnerability, it was unprepared for the loss of the network and did not adapt quickly when it occurred. As of mid-2026, Gazprom’s Yamal network comprises a mere four functioning geostationary satellites—in contrast to Starlink’s 10,803 operational satellites—and has proven unable to replace the capabilities previously enabled by Starlink.10 The gap between this clearly unmet need and a working substitute had simply been ignored for years, with ambition to address it only recently surfacing. Russia’s own “Starlink-like” constellation, Rassvet, has proven inadequate for replacing Starlink for military communication.11 After an initial scheduled launch in 2025, Russia launched a batch (16) of its Rassvet satellites in March 2026; within three months, however, one had already failed. Far behind its goal of 156 satellites by the end of 2026, Russia has been slow to build the network it has needed since 2022 and does not appear to be making substantive progress as of 2026.12 Its other satellite systems previously used for military communication—Blagovest, Meridian, and Strela—lack the capacity and satellite density to meet Russia’s wartime communication needs.In the face of mounting setbacks and largely in response to Ukrainian innovation, Russia has adopted specific, gradual changes. For example, Ukraine’s Drone Line initiative, which links drone operators from multiple units to share tactical lessons, inspired the creation of Russia’s Rubicon Unit, which was established by an August 2024 directive from Defense Minister Andrey Belousov. In fact, the Rubicon Unit’s creation was only possible because of Chinese material support, specifically the provision of fiber-optic cables.13 Russia’s only domestic fiber-optic plant, located in the city of Saransk, was damaged in a series of Ukrainian strikes in April 2025 and remains offline. This created a need: Russia had no way of supplying fiber-optic cables to its forces at a time when they were needed to enable innovation (for example, to establish the Rubicon Unit). China stepped in, albeit at a 150 percent price hike, and Chinese fiber-optic imports have since become a key input in Russia’s war machine.14Rubicon has become the prime example of successful Russian innovation and was instrumental in pushing back Ukrainian troops from Kursk. It is an elite strike unit developed by pulling experts from line units to concentrate unmanned aerial systems (UAS) and technology-enabled expertise. In response to Ukrainian tactics successfully disabling Russian drones, Rubicon’s members employed fiber-optic drones to strike Ukrainian supply lines along narrow roads, making resupply and rotation prohibitively costly.15 Rubicon’s performance in Kursk thus illustrates how Russia is able to effectively realize the potential offered by Chinese aid, at least when that aid is directed toward an existing material-use case. Without fiber-optic drones, Russia would likely not have been able to take back Kursk on the timeline it did. The loss of domestic capacity for fiber-optics made Chinese aid decisive.Recovered-weapon analysis conducted by the Ukrainian Main Directorate of Intelligence (GUR) finds that the bulk of the foreign parts in Russian systems are Western made, primarily entering Russia through China.16 Consequentially, over 50 percent of components used for AI, such as integrated circuits, come from U.S.-based companies.17 China acts as the middleman, rerouting Western components to Russia via intermediary firms and the domestic manufacturing of Western parts.18 Russia’s reliance on China for common high-priority (CHP) items on the G7 Common High Priority Items List—for example, computer numerical control (CNC) machines for metal cutting and shaping, semiconductors, integrated circuits, and lithium-ion batteries—has increased substantially each year. Chinese CHP imports made up 54.3 percent of what Russia received in 2022, and rose to 84.6 percent in 2025, representing 4.21 billion of the 4.98 billion U.S. dollars’ worth of CHP imports to Russia.\nChina enables Russia to maintain the status quo rather than innovate.\nThese Chinese-supplied Western components fit into the Russian system with little friction: Most Russian systems that rely on Western components can simply keep doing so because of Chinese support. In fact, by ensuring that these Western components reach Russia through its sanctions-evasion networks and domestic manufacturing, China enables Russia to maintain the status quo rather than innovate. China supports Russia’s rigidity even in the face of short-term losses to its own domestic market. If China provided Russia with exclusively Chinese-made components—such as Huawei semiconductors or Chinese CNC machines—Russia would be required to redesign most of its systems to accommodate the different parts.\nRemote Visualization\nWhen aid shifts from material components of technology to wholesale systems, on the other hand, Russia has shown itself to be less able to operationalize aid than Ukraine. For example, Russia employs Chinese-supplied space-based ISR technology less effectively than Ukraine uses the Western ISR it receives. As both sides receive ISR aid from their partners, comparing how Russia and Ukraine integrate externally provided ISR capabilities offers an illustrative case for understanding aid-realization capabilities. Companies from China have provided Russia with satellite imagery since at least 2023. In January 2023, the U.S. Treasury sanctioned the Chinese firm Spacety China and its Luxembourg subsidiary for providing satellite imagery of Ukraine to Terra Tech, a Russian company supporting operations conducted by the Wagner Group, a Russian proxy force.19Separately, Beijing Yunze sold two Chang Guang satellites to Nika-Frut, a front company tied to Yevgeny Prigozhin, for over $30 million. The GUR has also accused Chinese firms such as Xiamen Victory Technologies of supplying imagery to Russia since 2022.20 Enabled by space-based ISR, high-elevation drone-based ISR, human and agent networks inside Ukraine, signals intelligence, and other sources, Russia conducts strikes deep into Ukrainian territory. The primary driver of this capability is space-based ISR. Russia’s own space-based imaging fleet—comprising approximately three optical and three synthetic-aperture-radar satellites—is too sparse to service a multi-axis battlefield, making Chinese commercial imagery particularly valuable.21 However, while Russia has purchased a wealth of important targeting input, and used much of it, its capacity to exploit the data is structurally constrained.Space-based ISR is only as useful as its ability to enable target identification and facilitate strike launches, and Russia has proven unable to employ these capabilities effectively. As early as November 2022, a senior NATO air commander assessed that Russia lacked “anything like the ISR led strike capabilities of NATO Air Forces, nor the targeting processes to exploit them.” There is no evidence that Russia has meaningfully bridged that gap.22 Furthermore, on a sensor-rich battlefield, the bottleneck is data processing, not data collection.23 Russia may be fielding an AI-based system for processing drone video feeds, but nothing in Russian military discourse suggests it is doing the same with satellite imagery—that is, space-based ISR. Russian forces may be collecting the data, but they lack processing capabilities.24 Russia maintains a limited ability to process and exploit Chinese-provided satellite imagery as timely, validated targeting. Satellite imagery is difficult to process for a range of reasons. For example, object detection from space presents significant challenges, including the low pixel resolution of objects, as the greatest value of processing satellite imagery for targeting lies in identifying relatively small targets. Crucially, Russia does not own satellites that could provide sufficient data for training imagery-based models, nor does it appear to be acquiring or developing such technology. This processing need demands technical adaptation, which is constrained due to Russia’s institutional rigidity.Meanwhile, Ukraine has already integrated satellite imagery processing into its situational awareness and battlefield management system, Delta. Delta is an integrated system that combines real-time ISR data—principally from satellite imagery, drone video feeds, radar, and frontline units—onto an interactive digital map, allowing users to quickly identify enemy positions or track friendly forces. The full system famously compressed the average time between detecting a target to relaying data for a strike—the sensor-to-shooter delay—from 72 hours to mere minutes.25 One component of Delta, known as Avengers, handles the difficult computational task that underlies processing satellite imagery. Avengers develops specialized AI models for detecting small objects in low-resolution, poor-quality imagery.\n“There is effectively no company-level command; command is exercised at the battalion level.”\nIn contrast to its stalled space-based ISR data usage, Russia has recently displayed increased aptitude in utilizing drone-based sensor feeds. The challenge posed by drone-enabled strikes is different, centering on decisionmaking latency issues rather than data processing. Although the Russian military has detailed plans to field an advanced AI-enabled command-and-control (C2) system to reduce strike-approval latency, Russian regimental command staff recently identified strike-approval latency as the primary delay between detection and engagement, suggesting the improvement has not been made force wide.26 Regarding the command structure, staff stated, “there is effectively no company-level command; command is exercised at the battalion level,” meaning decisions must be filtered through multiple layers of command before they can be implemented, slowing down kill chains.27Indeed, within the Russian military, the battalion is the main tactical subunit and the lowest echelon with a staff, which is why strike authorization sits there.28 With multiple subunits existing below the battalion level—but lacking ultimate decisionmaking capabilities—fire authorization sits at a relatively high level, causing noticeable sensor-to-shooter latency, particularly in drone-enabled strikes. In the initial full-scale invasion in 2022, for example, Russia’s Strelets sensor-to-shooter network was fielded but rarely used due to a combination of a lack of trained personnel, misconfiguration, and patchy availability.29 Recent innovations in Glaz/Groza—Russia’s military targeting software which automatically extracts coordinates from drone video streams and performs ballistic calculations and fire adjustments—have reportedly compressed the drone kill chain from hours to minutes, but the gain is incremental in that it works around existing authorization structures rather than relocating decisionmaking authority, concentrating the improvement in select units rather than reforming the Russian force more broadly.30Like China, North Korea has provided important material aid that slots neatly into Russia’s defense industrial base—specifically, 15 million artillery shells as of March 2026 and 120 multiple launch rocket systems as of February 2025. Although there were initial quality issues, Russia has been able to use these munitions and weapons systems effectively, freeing up Russian factory workers that would otherwise be building munition stores.31 This input requires little if any institutional change for Russia.Deployed North Korean soldiers, in contrast, have proven difficult to integrate. In fall 2024, North Korea began sending troops to Russia, with late-2025 estimated troop numbers ranging from 11,000 to 15,000. North Korean troops have rotated in and out of the front lines, and a number were initially sent to Kursk to help regain territory during the 2024 Kursk offensive and remain there.32 North Korean troops have experienced high casualties, though they are broadly comparable to those of Russian forces. Estimates range from 2,300 to 6,000 North Korean casualties as of October 2025, exceeding 50 percent of the estimated stationed North Korean force. Russia has experienced a similar casualty rate (64 percent), according to the high end of international estimates.33 Adding to integration difficulties, poor communication between North Korean soldiers and Russian forces has resulted in battlefield mishaps. On December 14, 2024, for example, North Korean forces fired at a Chechen “Akhmat” Spetsnaz battalion vehicle, killing eight Chechen personnel in a friendly fire incident.34 There is no evidence Russia has resolved the communication barriers, or that there is a clear doctrinal place for these foreign troops within the Russian army; they allegedly operate as independent brigades.35Iranian-supplied Shahed drones offer the clearest case of an input Russia absorbed with minimal institutional friction. On June 8, 2022, Russian officials reportedly visited Kashan Airfield in Iran to inspect the Shahed-129 and Shahed-191 unmanned aerial vehicles.36 By July 11 of that year, the White House revealed that Iran was preparing to provide Russia with drones for use in Ukraine.37 The first transfer began on August 5, 2022.38 Payment moved through the same opaque channels that characterize these partnerships. In August of 2022, Russia flew a plane to Iran with cash and Western missiles Russia had acquired in Ukraine—a U.S. Javelin, a Stinger, and a British NLAW—reportedly intended for Iranian reverse-engineering.39 In February 2023, Tehran and Moscow began planning to build a drone factory in Russia, and the Russian Geran drone series, adapted from the Shaheds and incorporating more resilient navigation and other enhancements, soon replaced the Iran-supplied drones in Ukraine. Russia ultimately localized nearly 90 percent of its Geran production, completing a 6,000-unit contract ahead of schedule and driving down per-unit cost with limited Iranian input.40 The Ukraine CaseRussia’s failures to process space-based ISR and translate it into opportunities for strikes, as well as its inability to integrate foreign troops into its military, contrast sharply with Ukraine’s successes. On June 4, 2026, Ukraine’s Unmanned Systems Force dismantled Russian Shahed-launch infrastructure at the occupied Donetsk Airport using commercial, real-time satellite imagery fed straight to operators’ devices.41 Coordinates arrived within roughly 15 minutes of satellite capture and bypassed central review, compressing the sensor-to-shooter kill cycle by as much as 90 percent. This was the first documented case of Ukraine closing a kill chain using commercial satellite imagery delivered directly to frontline operators—the first visible instance of an institutional arrangement that had been being built for years. Specifically, a decade of volunteer-built processing and battle management software has given Ukraine the processing capacity to take advantage of the wealth of data space-based ISR provides. In contrast, while Russia has received the same class of input, it has not been able to convert it to measurable success.\nRussian military scholars conceded that the Russian military’s efforts were 1.5–2 years behind projections.\nUkraine’s success has been facilitated by the fact that it has shed its Soviet shell to a larger extent than Russia. Ukraine’s command structure benefits from its innovators and its ability to broadly reform its force. Beginning in 2014, volunteers have been building tools that pushed decisionmaking to frontline operators. For example, Ukrainian army SOS—a public volunteer initiative supporting the Ukrainian army—developed Kropyva, which turned cheap tablets into automated fire-control terminals, cutting the time from order to strike by up to tenfold; 90–95 percent of artillery units have adopted it as their primary system.42 Aerorozvidka’s Delta, begun in 2016 and adopted force wide by August 2024, scaled that logic—bottom-up rather than centrally managed, accessible to frontline troops and senior commanders alike through tiered access, and designed to give end users what they need rather than letting a commander micromanage the force.43 The result is an observe-decide-strike loop increasingly closed at the lowest tactical levels, with release authority for the dominant small-UAS and artillery kill chain sitting at the company, platoon, and even squad levels.44Russia has tried to build a similar advantage but has yet to relocate authority, stalling implementation. In fact, by early 2025 even Russian military scholars conceded that the Russian military’s efforts were 1.5–2 years behind projections.45 Moreover, the Russian answer to Ukrainian Aerorozvidka’s Delta—Svod, a Delta-class situational awareness system—remains underdeveloped and insufficiently diffused across the Russian military.46 Based on its demonstrated ability to create drone units, Russia should be able to convert Svod into a useful, largely diffused system, though on a timeline that lags behind the war’s demands and reflects the institutional rigidity that places the country firmly behind Ukraine in software procurement overall.Ukraine’s institutions are not frictionless. The country carries its own absorption costs—corruption, procurement opacity, and dependence on donor timelines, to name a few—and even its strongest systems show this strain.47 For example, Ukraine currently fields a fragmented landscape of 550-plus unmanned system types, many not integrated into Delta, and embodies a volunteer-led supply model that has proven agile but ultimately unsustainable.48 Despite these challenges, Ukraine has been able to successfully convert forms of foreign aid similar to those Russia receives from China and North Korea, while Russia has been unable to match Ukraine’s level of aid realization.Ukraine’s command structure emerges as an explanatory variable for the varying results—this structure was built deliberately over time, not through quick battlefield innovation. Between 2014 and 2022, Ukrainian volunteers introduced 11 situational awareness and battle management systems with direct lines to frontline operators, thereby keeping development tied to real military needs. Once that process was established and in response to the role of drones and other UAS in the conflict, Ukraine stood up a dedicated Unmanned Systems Force as a separate military branch in 2024 and pushed authority toward networked, volunteer-rooted units. In addition, Ukraine’s donors have spent years building absorptive capacity into the Ukrainian military through NATO training and interoperability testing.49 Delta passed NATO interoperability testing at the 2024 Coalition Warrior Interoperability Exploration, Experimentation, Examination Exercise (CWIX24) and was integrated into the Polish TOPAZ artillery system by July 2024.50 Together, a volunteer-built ecosystem and years of donor institution-building solidified Ukrainian absorptive capacity, a combination Russia has been unable to quickly replicate.\nNorth Korean troops appear to operate exclusively within their own brigades and are minimally incorporated into the Russian command structure.\nUkraine employs foreign volunteer aid in token amounts, with foreign troops arriving as individuals or in small groups, not as units by partner nations. There is no official count of how many foreign volunteers serve in Ukraine’s armed forces, and estimates vary widely, though most put the figure at half of Russia’s roughly 15,000 North Korean troops, or possibly fewer.51 Before deployment, these forces receive roughly six weeks of training and are deployed only after they have a level of familiarization with Ukrainian communications, command structure, and battlefield conditions. Moreover, foreigners are matched with Ukrainian units before arrival.52 There is real friction—language barriers remain, and the foreign troops still do not have extensive experience operating within a Ukrainian command structure—but the apparent ad hoc deployment of North Korean troops diverges sharply from the training and force integration process Ukraine has built over several years.53 Troops that are successful in the International Legion are sent to real Ukrainian units and contribute directly to battlefield operations, whereas North Korean troops appear to operate exclusively within their own brigades and are minimally incorporated into the Russian command structure. Partnership Limits: Why Russia’s Partners Cannot Lift the CeilingIn 2026, Ukraine innovates from the bottom up, enabling more effective absorption of aid, but that was not always the case. High-intensity war exposed Ukraine’s Soviet-era institutional rot, and conditional external aid supplied the additional necessary pressure to fix it. In contrast, although China, Iran, and North Korea supply hardware, components, munitions, and imagery, they attach none of the conditions that drove Ukraine’s reforms. Russia’s partners neither demand institutional change as a condition of their support nor exert the existential pressure that pushed Ukraine to convert inputs into effect. Russia, unlike Ukraine, does not face an existential threat to its survival.Western aid to Ukraine comes with stipulations that have strengthened Ukraine’s anti-corruption institutions and enforcement and helped spur innovation.54 The United States has sent Ukraine $127 billion of direct aid since Russia’s 2022 invasion, imposing multiple restrictions on the use of that aid.55 Among these restrictions are stipulations that Ukraine demonstrate clear progress in anti-corruption measures, and corruption reduction further frees up funds. Moreover, conditions vary by aid type and frequently include some oversight and monitoring mechanisms. Military assistance, for example, is accompanied by intensive end-use monitoring: The U.S. Department of Defense stood up the Security Assistance Group-Ukraine within months of the invasion to track shipments to the front—having learned from the seven years it delayed in creating an equivalent body in Afghanistan—and Congress mandated itemized accounting of every defense article transferred.56Of that total, the roughly $45 billion in direct budget support the United States provided was routed through the World Bank and governed by statutory safeguards, including a required U.S.-Ukraine memorandum of understanding on third-party monitoring and a separate auditable account.57 Revealing the enduring nature of these stipulations, in late summer 2023, the U.S. Department of State issued a formal démarche warning that continued budget support depended on Ukraine’s progress on anti-corruption measures and financial transparency, clearly messaging that misappropriation would jeopardize all U.S. aid.58 Crucially, these conditions tracked the same benchmarks as Ukraine’s EU accession bid—independent anti-corruption prosecutors, reformed state-company boards, and judicial overhaul—meaning that pressure came from multiple channels, encouraging the changes Kyiv needed and reinforcing one another. This partnership structure externally incentivized Ukraine—a country still informed by its post-Soviet governance architecture—to direct its newly provided resources honestly. Although corruption remains a challenge within the Ukrainian military—as illustrated by President Zelensky’s attempt to weaken two anti-corruption bodies and the recent dismissal of Minister of Defense Mykhailo Fedorov, a staunch critic of corruption—Ukraine has followed a far more positive trajectory than Russia in reducing corruption and strengthening accountability, granting Ukraine an advantage in the war.59Russia’s partners, in contrast, have provided aid without governance conditions—with each partner controlled by an authoritarian government, there exists little incentive to stipulate governance reform. Ukraine’s partnerships require eventual repayment and compliance with anti-corruption and transparency benchmarks, while Russia’s partnerships rely on direct reciprocity and often embrace illicit practices. Russia’s partners typically expect some combination of immediate payment for services rendered, reciprocal military cooperation, or technological assistance. More consequentially, the Russian bilateral partnerships move through channels purposefully built to evade scrutiny, thereby denying transparency and likely spreading corrosive practices. These pathways encourage shadow trade and sanctions evasion networks, reinforcing opaque governance models and shared illicit financial practices that further weaken accountability across aligned states.60 The conditionality present in Western aid to Ukraine structures beneficial reform, but no equivalent pressure has been placed on Russia—its partners have no interest in institutional transparency.Although Russia’s partnerships reinforce opaque and illicit practices rather than curb them, more consequential in the long term is that the partnership limits prevent embedded external access, a crucial driver of reform. The same opacity and mutual suspicion that throttle the flow of aid also foreclose the embedded, multiyear institution-building that would be required to raise Russia’s absorptive ceiling. A partner can hand over imagery, components, or even a two-week training rotation without ever touching the recipient’s command structure. What a partner cannot do, absent sustained access and trust, is sit inside the force and reform where strike authority sits. In the past, Russia has shown a great deal of restraint in allowing China access to sensitive systems. Although clear mutual suspicion exists—leaked Russian counterintelligence documents show deep-seated distrust, and Russian military journals entertain conspiracies reflecting suspicion of China—recent classified documents reveal that integration between the two countries is growing.61 Despite the growing ties, however, it is unlikely either side will grant the other the level of embedded access reforming a command structure requires, because each continues to treat the other as an intelligence target.\nNeither Russia nor its partners want the transparency and intrusion such institution-building demands.\nThe Western model raised Ukraine’s absorptive capacity because donors were allowed in—conditioning aid, embedding trainers, and running interoperability testing over years. Russia’s partners are barred from playing that role due to mutual distrust. Neither Russia nor its partners want the transparency and intrusion such institution-building demands, so the aid arrives without the accompanying push to reform that would enable Russia to convert the portion of that aid that requires systematic reform into combat power; without reform of the kind seen in Ukraine, Russia remains unable to convert the potential capacity of the aid it receives into fully actualized capability. The partnership limits thus bind Russia twice—once through the inputs it receives, and again, more consequentially, through whether those inputs can be converted into combat capability.There is some evidence that Russia’s partners have attempted to address institutional challenges affecting Russian military adaptation. Under a July 2025 agreement, for example, Russian Defense Minister Andrey Belousov approved Chinese training for roughly 200 Russian personnel in China on drone operations, electronic warfare, counterdrone tactics, and, in at least one case, drone-directed mortar fire.62 Ukrainian intelligence has since confirmed that 180 Russian troops received training in China. While a clear indication of growing military collaboration—particularly as Ukrainian intelligence has also reported 550 Chinese troops training in Russia—this expanded bilateral exchange is unlikely to resolve Russia’s institutional difficulties. The exchange reportedly centers more on Russia providing Chinese troops with knowledge drawn from battlefield experience and less on China providing Russian troops with training.63Furthermore, a prominent investigation indicates that China’s military cooperation with Russia is deepening—developing a joint air- and missile-defense system and even making plans to potentially disrupt the Starlink constellation.64 Crucially, this investigation found that Russia decided to share its coveted S-500 air defense system with China, alongside early-warning radar technologies. Yet even this integration—the highest level of cooperation evident since before the Sino-Soviet split in the late 1950s—takes the form of joint development programs and traded battlefield data; there is no evidence to suggest that China is pushing for governance reform, nor that it is being granted the embedded access demanded by Ukraine’s Western donors.65Fundamentally, Ukraine’s reform agenda is driven by the existential imperative of survival—if Russia were to succeed in its original goals, Ukraine would lose its sovereignty. In contrast, should Russia fail, Ukraine cannot realistically threaten Russia’s sovereignty; at best, Ukraine would take back the territory it has lost since 2014. This existential threat has created necessary conditions for reform within the former Soviet state. If Ukrainian officials wastefully deploy partner-given resources, Ukraine risks losing the war, and thus its sovereignty. Russia can use its resources wastefully with far less consequence, and it has far more resources to waste. Looking ForwardRussia has learned to adapt in the years since its February 2022 invasion of Ukraine. The development of Rubicon suggests that the military has begun to shed its rigid way of war; the Svod command-and-control system has demonstrated a real capacity to absorb failure and adjust under pressure; and a substantial body of scholarship documents bounded but genuine adaptation across assault tactics, drone employment, and electronic warfare.66 These adaptations are real, and, over time, they do diffuse throughout the force—drone units that looked exceptional in 2023 are now a routine feature. The constraint is the speed of that diffusion.Russia’s institutional rigidity means innovation surfaces at the margins, in elite units and with exceptionally capable operators, and then works its way outward slowly, through a force structure built to resist the lateral spread of lessons rather than accelerate it. Rubicon reveals this constraint in real time: a genuine adaptation that has not yet been replicated across the force and, following the historical pattern, likely will diffuse only gradually. Partner aid arrives faster than Russian forces can reorganize to exploit it, so the inputs that demand the most institutional change sit unused or, at best, underused while the relevant adaptation slowly generalizes. Russia will continue adapting, but the rate at which it does so is set by the rigidity of its institutions, and that rate is too slow to convert high-demand partner aid into battlefield effect on the timeline the war imposes.As the Ukraine war drags on, Russia undoubtedly will continue to find innovations that work on the margins, slowly metabolizing these edge innovations as shown by its force-wide adoption of drone tactics since 2023. This suggests that some partner aid—especially Chinese—will grow more useful as the war continues. The July 2025 training agreement may be an early indicator that partner aid is becoming increasingly useful, though Russia’s resulting innovations characteristically concentrate competence in select units rather than driving reform across the broader force.The supply side, moreover, suggests continued improvement. The Qianfan Low Earth Orbit satellite system (Figure 2), a system China has been building out to compete with Starlink—granting it the ability to produce (and then sell) more and higher quality ISR data to Russia—was paused in 2025 due to thruster and gyroscope failures, though this pause was resolved in April 2026, with subsequent launches running every two to three weeks.67 China is targeting 140-plus orbital launches in 2026, and Guowang and Qianfan combined already comprise roughly 350-plus satellites.68 Though still far behind Qianfan’s first-generation target of 1,300, the constraint is easing, with processing capacity remaining as the primary bottleneck.69 Russia continues to rely heavily on purchasing imagery from Chinese satellite companies and, given its delay in establishing its own competitive satellite constellation, that is unlikely to change any time soon.\nRemote Visualization\nInstitutional rigidity caps how much partner aid Russia can convert into combat power. A Soviet-era command culture that discourages innovation slows Russia’s ability to process satellite imagery, integrate foreign troops, and adopt new drone tactics across the force—and the corruption and opacity baked into authoritarian systems make those constraints difficult to overcome through reform. Russia’s adaptation at the edge—in elite units and among its most capable operators—will likely continue, and some inputs—especially Chinese hardware—will likely grow more useful over time. However, absent the institutional reform that Russia’s command culture resists and its partnerships do not demand, Russia will need substantially more aid than Ukraine to achieve comparable effect. This gap is unlikely to close while the inputs keep flowing but the structure that converts them does not change.Wesley Bailey is a former intern with the Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Daniel Byman is the director of the Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program at CSIS.This brief is made possible by general support to CSIS. No direct sponsorship contributed to this brief.\nPlease consult the PDF for references.\nCSIS briefs are produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nRussia,\nDefense and Security,\nDefense Strategy and Capabilities,\nGeopolitics and International Security, and\nUkraine War\nWesley Bailey\nFormer Intern, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nImage\nDaniel Byman\nDirector, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nRelated Content\nImage\nRussian Blood and Treasure: The Ballooning Costs of Putin’s War\nBrief\nby\nSeth G. Jones\nand\nRiley McCabe\n— July 1, 2026\nImage\nResilience Through Linkage: Russia, Iran, and Aspirations for North–South Trade\nReport\nby\nMaria Snegovaya,\nTina Dolbaia,\nand\nNicholas Fenton\n— April 16, 2026\nImage\nHow Russia Is Building a Sovereign Drone Ecosystem for AI-Driven Autonomy\nReport\nby\nKateryna Bondar\n— April 13, 2026\nImage\nUnderstanding the Military AI Ecosystem of Ukraine\nReport\nby\nKateryna Bondar\n— November 12, 2024","content_cn":"俄罗斯对伙伴援助的上限：制度僵化与外部支持的局限\n图片：克里姆林宫新闻服务部/Anadolu/Getty Images\n目录\n目录\n简报\n作者\nWesley Bailey\n和\nDaniel Byman\n发表于2026年7月31日\n可用下载\n下载简报\n833kb\n问题所在\n事实证明，中国、伊朗和朝鲜的支持对俄罗斯的战争机器至关重要，但俄罗斯对这些外国支持的吸收并不均衡，从而限制了其影响以及俄罗斯的军事效能。对于熟悉的装备等物资援助，俄罗斯能够以相对较低的摩擦将其消化，只需替换原有工具。然而，俄罗斯的战争机器难以吸收那些要求制度、组织或观念发生变化的支持。中国提供的由ISR（情报、监视与侦察）引导的目标指示，以及朝鲜部队的部署，是这一现象的主要例子。当投入只要求简单重新装备时，例如中国供应的军事部件或伊朗的“沙赫德”无人机生产，俄罗斯遇到的困难相对较少。相比之下，当投入要求重组部队时，例如中国提供的由ISR引导的目标指示数据，俄罗斯便陷入停滞，无法适应语言、训练和较窄互操作性所带来的挑战。俄罗斯的制度僵化限制了其正确利用伙伴支持的能力，而其近期的创新只是在绕开这一约束，并未解决它。\n引言\n随着乌克兰对俄罗斯境内更深处发动打击，俄罗斯在战场上遭遇越来越多的挫折；据报道，即便是俄罗斯总统弗拉基米尔·普京也承认，俄军推进得没有“……[莫斯科]所希望的那么快”。1 截至2026年年中，俄罗斯在乌克兰战争中已遭受140万战场伤亡，其中死亡人数多达45万。2 此外，乌克兰已取得主动权，自2026年初以来向俄罗斯境内100公里或更远处发动了超过400次打击。3 尽管俄罗斯的伙伴——主要是中国、伊朗和朝鲜——持续提供支持，包括口头支持和持续的物资援助，但俄罗斯仍遭遇了这些挫折。\n西方关于支持俄罗斯的讨论通常集中在弹药数量、部件识别，以及这些国家提供的卫星接入类型等其他形式的援助上。然而，围绕俄罗斯如何——甚至是否——能够利用伙伴援助的关键问题仍未得到探讨。在俄罗斯获得了大量伙伴援助却仍未能成功的情况下，问题就变成：这些失败是孤立错误的结果——与伙伴支持无关——还是俄罗斯的战争机器难以将某些类型的伙伴援助转化为作战能力？援助接收因潜在能力（某项投入的到达）和实现能力（利用该投入的能力）而有所不同。如果俄罗斯无法利用伙伴提供的战场援助——也就是说，无法将潜在能力转化为实现能力——那么拥有这些援助的重要性就会丧失。\n俄罗斯获得的伙伴援助数量可观且不断增加——例如，2023年俄罗斯的微电子产品估计有90%来自中国，截至2026年3月，朝鲜已估计向俄罗斯运送了1500万发152毫米炮弹——这为该国提供了显著的潜在能力。然而，俄罗斯往往在实现能力上陷入停滞，无法利用其\n合作伙伴提供的援助。4 这部分原因在于组件的质量——例如，朝鲜炮弹的质量经常很差。5 不过，实现俄罗斯合作伙伴所提供援助能力的主要障碍是制度僵化。尽管自乌克兰战争爆发以来俄罗斯已经进行了调整，但重要的制度性约束仍然存在，包括俄罗斯卫星图像处理能力的局限，以及其双边伙伴关系的范围和性质。这些因素限制了俄罗斯充分利用所获部分援助的能力，例如天基情报、监视与侦察（ISR）能力以及额外的外国部队。本简报在乌克兰战争和西方对乌克兰援助的背景下，从潜在能力和实际实现能力两个方面考察俄罗斯合作伙伴提供的援助，以确定当前援助供应在冲突中的战略影响。考察乌克兰对外国援助的整合情况表明，提高实际援助能力的关键推动因素是创新，尤其是作战和制度创新。下文分析表明，如果没有重大的制度变革——鉴于俄罗斯及其合作伙伴缺乏改革压力，这种变革似乎不太可能发生——俄罗斯将需要远多于乌克兰的援助，才能实现其目标。\n俄罗斯的成功与失败俄罗斯的制度僵化具有结构性，并且会自我强化，涵盖民政和军事机构。持续不断的官僚内斗使改革困难重重，而根深蒂固的保守主义和怀旧文化抑制了创新或适应。决策权高度集中，以至于总统行政当局之外的人很少被赋予权力，甚至无法提出改革所需的基本权衡方案。此外，对暴露战争现实、进而削弱普京地位的普遍恐惧，也阻碍了坦诚分析。6 国防工业基础同样饱受管理不善、腐败以及对外国技术（尤其是西方技术）的依赖之苦，这意味着它无法大规模提供高端进口组件的替代品。这种行政文化使军方难以摆脱过去，尽管战场已经明确要求进行适应。7\n俄罗斯军事机构奖励忠诚而非能力，并将权力下放、授权和主动性视为威胁。\n俄罗斯的军事文化强化了这种更广泛的制度僵化。军队的教育和训练机构仍然过时，专注于单一军种，并且重视死记硬背而非独立思考，培养出的军官厌恶创新、不愿授权，甚至容易坚持执行已经失败的行动方案。8 这些模式共同强化了俄罗斯苏联式的集中指挥文化，这种文化根植于各层级之间缺乏信任。总体而言，俄罗斯军事机构奖励忠诚而非能力，并将权力下放、授权和主动性视为威胁。Starlink——SpaceX的低地球轨道星座，乌克兰获准将其用于军事通信——在乌克兰战争中的前线协调和无人机中继方面所扮演角色的变化，揭示了俄罗斯制度僵化的影响。乌克兰情报部门、西方分析人士和美国议员记录了俄罗斯依赖总部位于美国的Starlink进行前线通信和无人机行动的情况，作为\n这种结构性脆弱性已经存在多年，直到莫斯科注意到它。2026年2月，当SpaceX停用了俄罗斯的非法Starlink访问后，长期存在的国内备用方案——由俄罗斯天然气工业股份公司（Gazprom）拥有的商业亚马尔卫星网络——事实证明无法覆盖前线，甚至无法处理无人机视频，导致俄罗斯部队称其不稳定。在一段被截获的无线电通信中，一名俄罗斯军人说：“我们又碰壁了，因为我们的国家不想在任何事情上花钱，也不想为前线做任何有用的事……Starlink是一个人发明的——这么长时间里，我们为什么就不能造出类似的东西？”9尽管俄罗斯依赖Starlink这一点早已是众所周知的脆弱环节，但俄罗斯没有为该网络的丧失做好准备，事发后也没有迅速适应。截至2026年年中，俄罗斯天然气工业股份公司的亚马尔网络仅有四颗仍在运行的地球静止轨道卫星——相比之下，Starlink有10,803颗在运行的卫星——事实证明，它无法取代Starlink此前提供的能力。10这一明显未被满足的需求与可用替代方案之间的差距，多年来一直被忽视，直到最近才出现解决这一问题的意愿。俄罗斯自己的“类Starlink”星座“黎明”（Rassvet）已被证明不足以取代Starlink用于军事通信的功能。11在2025年首次按计划发射后，俄罗斯于2026年3月发射了一批（16颗）黎明星；然而，不到三个月，其中一颗就已经失效。俄罗斯远未达到2026年底拥有156颗卫星的目标，自2022年以来一直迟缓地建设这一所需网络，截至2026年也没有显现出实质性进展。12俄罗斯此前用于军事通信的其他卫星系统——“布拉戈韦斯特”（Blagovest）、“子午线”（Meridian）和“斯特列拉”（Strela）——缺乏满足俄罗斯战时通信需求所需的容量和卫星密度。面对不断增加的挫折，并主要是对乌克兰创新的回应，俄罗斯采取了具体而渐进的改变。例如，乌克兰的“无人机线”倡议将多个部队的无人机操作员连接起来，分享战术经验，这启发了俄罗斯“鲁比孔”部队的创建；该部队依据国防部长安德烈·别洛乌索夫于2024年8月发布的一项指令成立。事实上，“鲁比孔”部队的创建之所以成为可能，仅仅是因为中国提供了物资支持，具体来说是光纤电缆。13俄罗斯唯一的国产光纤工厂位于萨兰斯克市，2025年4月遭到乌克兰一系列袭击而受损，至今仍处于停运状态。这造成了一个需求：在需要利用光纤电缆推动创新（例如建立“鲁比孔”部队）之时，俄罗斯没有办法向其部队供应光纤电缆。中国介入了，尽管价格上涨了150%，此后中国光纤进口已成为俄罗斯战争机器的一项关键投入。14“鲁比孔”已成为俄罗斯成功创新的首要范例，并在将乌克兰部队从库尔斯克击退的过程中发挥了重要作用。这是一支精锐突击部队，通过从一线部队抽调专家，集中无人航空系统（UAS）及技术赋能的专业知识而组建。为应对乌克兰成功瘫痪俄罗斯无人机的战术，“鲁比孔”成员使用光纤无人机，沿狭窄道路打击乌克兰补给线，使补给和轮换的成本高得令人难以承受。15因此，“鲁比孔”在库尔斯克的表现说明了俄罗斯如何能够有效实现潜力\n至少在这种援助用于现有材料使用场景时，中国援助发挥了作用。如果没有光纤无人机，俄罗斯很可能无法按实际时间线夺回库尔斯克。国内光纤产能的丧失使中国援助变得至关重要。乌克兰国防部情报总局（GUR）对缴获武器进行的分析发现，俄罗斯系统中的外国部件大多为西方制造，主要经由中国进入俄罗斯。16 因此，人工智能所使用的部件中，超过50%（例如集成电路）来自美国公司。17 中国充当中间人，通过中介公司以及在国内制造西方部件，将西方部件转运至俄罗斯。18 俄罗斯对七国集团《共同高优先级物项清单》中的常见高优先级（CHP）物项——例如用于金属切削和成形的计算机数控（CNC）机床、半导体、集成电路和锂离子电池——的依赖逐年大幅增加。中国CHP进口占俄罗斯2022年所获CHP物项的54.3%，到2025年升至84.6%，相当于俄罗斯CHP进口总额49.8亿美元中的42.1亿美元。\n中国使俄罗斯得以维持现状，而不是进行创新。\n这些由中国供应的西方部件能够以很少的摩擦融入俄罗斯系统：大多数依赖西方部件的俄罗斯系统可以简单地继续这样运作，因为有中国的支持。事实上，通过确保这些西方部件经由其规避制裁网络和国内制造渠道进入俄罗斯，中国使俄罗斯得以维持现状，而不是进行创新。即使短期内会损害中国自身国内市场，中国仍支持俄罗斯的僵化。如果中国向俄罗斯提供的完全是中国制造的部件——例如华为半导体或中国数控机床——俄罗斯将不得不重新设计其大多数系统，以适应不同的零部件。\n远程可视化\n另一方面，当援助从技术的材料部件转向完整系统时，俄罗斯已表现出比乌克兰更弱的援助运用能力。例如，俄罗斯对中国提供的天基情报、监视与侦察（ISR）技术的运用不如乌克兰对其所获得的西方ISR的运用有效。由于双方都从合作伙伴那里获得ISR援助，对比俄罗斯和乌克兰如何整合外部提供的ISR能力，为理解援助实现能力提供了一个具有说明性的案例。至少自2023年以来，中国公司一直向俄罗斯提供卫星图像。2023年1月，美国财政部制裁了中国企业长光卫星技术有限公司（Spacety China）及其卢森堡子公司，原因是它们向俄罗斯公司Terra Tech提供乌克兰的卫星图像；Terra Tech支持俄罗斯代理部队瓦格纳集团开展的行动。19 此外，北京云泽向与叶夫根尼·普里戈任有关联的空壳公司Nika-Frut出售了两颗长光卫星，金额超过3000万美元。GUR还指控厦门胜利科技等中国公司自2022年以来向俄罗斯供应图像。20 借助天基ISR、高空无人机ISR、乌克兰境内的人类和特工网络、信号情报及其他来源，俄罗斯得以对乌克兰纵深地区实施打击。这种能力的主要驱动力是天基ISR。俄罗斯自己的天基成像卫星群由大约三颗\n光学卫星和三颗合成孔径雷达卫星——数量过于稀少，无法服务于多轴战场，这使中国商业影像尤其具有价值。21 然而，尽管俄罗斯购买了大量重要的目标定位输入并使用了其中很大一部分，但其利用这些数据的能力受到结构性限制。天基情报、监视与侦察（ISR）的效用，取决于其识别目标并促成发起打击的能力，而俄罗斯已证明其无法有效运用这些能力。早在2022年11月，一名北约高级空军指挥官就评估称，俄罗斯“完全没有类似北约空军那样由ISR引导的打击能力，也没有利用这些能力的目标定位流程”。没有证据表明俄罗斯已实质性地弥合这一差距。22 此外，在一个传感器密集的战场上，瓶颈在于数据处理，而不是数据收集。23 俄罗斯可能正在部署一种基于人工智能的系统来处理无人机视频流，但俄罗斯军事论述中没有任何迹象表明其对卫星影像——即天基ISR——也采取同样做法。俄军可能正在收集数据，但缺乏处理能力。24 俄罗斯维持着有限能力，能够将中国提供的卫星影像处理并利用为及时且经过验证的目标定位信息。卫星影像由于多种原因而难以处理。例如，从太空进行目标检测面临重大挑战，包括目标像素分辨率低；对目标定位而言，处理卫星影像的最大价值就在于识别相对较小的目标。关键是，俄罗斯没有拥有能够提供足够数据来训练基于影像模型的卫星，也似乎没有在获取或开发此类技术。这种处理需求要求进行技术适应，但由于俄罗斯的制度僵化，这种适应受到限制。\n\n与此同时，乌克兰已经将卫星影像处理整合进其态势感知和战场管理系统“Delta”。Delta是一个综合系统，将实时ISR数据——主要来自卫星影像、无人机视频流、雷达和前线部队——汇集到一张交互式数字地图上，使用户能够迅速识别敌方阵地或跟踪己方部队。该系统以广为人知的方式，将从发现目标到传递打击数据之间的平均时间——即从传感器到射手的延迟——从72小时压缩到了几分钟。25 Delta的一个名为“复仇者”（Avengers）的组件，负责处理卫星影像所需的困难计算任务。Avengers开发专门的人工智能模型，用于在低分辨率、低质量影像中检测小型目标。\n\n“实际上不存在连级指挥；指挥是在营级实施的。”\n\n与天基ISR数据使用陷入停滞形成对比的是，俄罗斯最近表现出利用基于无人机的传感器数据流的能力有所提高。无人机赋能打击带来的挑战有所不同，核心是决策延迟问题，而不是数据处理问题。尽管俄军已制定详细计划，要部署一种先进的人工智能赋能指挥与控制（C2）系统，以缩短打击批准延迟，但俄罗斯团级指挥参谋最近仍将打击批准延迟认定为从发现到交战之间的主要延误，这表明这种改进尚未在全军范围内实现。26 关于指挥结构，参谋人员表示：“实际上不存在连级指挥；指挥是在营级实施的”\n“在营级”，意味着决策必须经过多层指挥机构的筛选后才能实施，从而拖慢杀伤链。27事实上，在俄罗斯军队中，营是主要战术分队，也是最低一级的编制单位，这正是打击授权设在该级的原因。28 营级以下存在多个分队，但它们缺乏最终决策能力，因此火力授权处于相对较高的层级，造成明显的传感器到射手延迟，尤其是在无人机支援的打击中。例如，在2022年最初的全面入侵中，俄罗斯的“射手”传感器到射手网络虽然已部署，但由于缺乏受训人员、配置错误以及可用性不稳定等因素，很少被使用。29 据报道，俄罗斯的Glaz/Groza系统——一种能够自动从无人机视频流中提取坐标、进行弹道计算并调整火力的军事目标定位软件——的最新创新已将无人机杀伤链从数小时压缩到数分钟，但这种提升是渐进式的，因为它是在现有授权结构周围运作，而不是重新配置决策权；改进集中于少数部队，而非更广泛地改革俄罗斯军队。30\n\n与中国一样，朝鲜提供了能够顺利嵌入俄罗斯国防工业基础的重要物资援助——截至2026年3月提供了1500万发炮弹，截至2025年2月提供了120套多管火箭炮系统。尽管最初存在质量问题，俄罗斯仍能够有效使用这些弹药和武器系统，从而解放原本要生产弹药储备的俄罗斯工厂工人。31 这种投入几乎不需要俄罗斯进行任何制度性改变。\n\n相比之下，部署的朝鲜士兵被证明难以整合。2024年秋，朝鲜开始向俄罗斯派遣部队；截至2025年末，估计派驻兵力为1.1万至1.5万人。朝鲜部队在前线轮换进出，其中一些最初被派往库尔斯克，以帮助在2024年库尔斯克攻势期间夺回领土，并一直留在那里。32 朝鲜部队伤亡惨重，但总体上与俄军伤亡相当。截至2025年10月，对朝鲜伤亡人数的估计为2300至6000人，超过估计驻扎朝鲜部队总数的50%。据国际估计的高位数据，俄罗斯也经历了类似的伤亡率（64%）。33 进一步增加整合难度的是，朝鲜士兵与俄军之间沟通不畅，导致战场事故。例如，2024年12月14日，朝鲜部队向一辆车臣“阿赫马特”特种部队营的车辆开火，在一次误伤事件中造成8名车臣人员死亡。34 没有证据表明俄罗斯已经解决了沟通障碍，也没有证据表明这些外国部队在俄军中拥有明确的条令定位；据称它们以独立旅的形式行动。35\n\n伊朗提供的“沙赫德”无人机是俄罗斯以最小制度摩擦吸收的一项投入的最清晰案例。据报道，2022年6月8日，俄罗斯官员访问了伊朗卡尚机场，检查“沙赫德-129”和“沙赫德-191”无人机。36 同年7月11日，白宫披露伊朗正准备向俄罗斯提供用于乌克兰的无人机。37 第一\n转移始于2022年8月5日。38 资金通过同样不透明的渠道流动，这些渠道正是此类伙伴关系的特征。2022年8月，俄罗斯向伊朗派出一架飞机，携带现金以及俄罗斯在乌克兰获得的西方导弹——一枚美国“标枪”导弹、一枚“毒刺”导弹和一枚英国NLAW导弹——据报道，这些武器是供伊朗进行逆向工程的。39 2023年2月，德黑兰和莫斯科开始筹划在俄罗斯建造一座无人机工厂，俄罗斯的“天竺葵”无人机系列由“沙赫德”无人机改造而来，并加入了更具韧性的导航系统及其他改进，很快取代了伊朗供应的无人机，投入乌克兰战场。俄罗斯最终实现了其“天竺葵”生产近90%的本土化，提前完成了6,000架的合同，并在伊朗有限参与的情况下，通过降低单架成本来压低整体成本。40 乌克兰案例俄罗斯未能处理天基ISR并将其转化为打击机会，也无法将外国部队整合进其军队，这与乌克兰的成功形成鲜明对比。2026年6月4日，乌克兰无人系统部队利用直接传送到操作员设备上的商业实时卫星图像，拆除了被占领的顿涅茨克机场的俄罗斯“沙赫德”无人机发射基础设施。41 坐标在卫星拍摄后大约15分钟内送达，绕过了中央审查，使从传感器到射手的杀伤周期压缩了最多90%。这是有记录以来乌克兰首次利用直接交付给前线操作员的商业卫星图像闭合杀伤链——也是一个多年来一直在构建的制度安排首次显现。具体而言，十年来由志愿者构建的处理和战场管理软件，使乌克兰具备了利用天基ISR所提供海量数据的处理能力。相比之下，尽管俄罗斯获得了同类输入，但一直无法将其转化为可衡量的成功。\n俄罗斯军事学者承认，俄军的相关努力落后于预测目标1.5至2年。\n乌克兰的成功得益于这样一个事实：与俄罗斯相比，它在更大程度上摆脱了苏联式外壳。乌克兰的指挥结构受益于其创新者以及广泛改革军队的能力。自2014年起，志愿者一直在构建将决策下放给前线操作员的工具。例如，乌克兰军队SOS——一个支持乌克兰军队的公众志愿倡议——开发了Kropyva，将廉价平板电脑变成自动化火力控制终端，使从下达命令到实施打击的时间最多缩短十倍；90%至95%的炮兵部队已将其作为主要系统。42 Aerorozvidka的Delta项目始于2016年，并于2024年8月前在全军范围内采用，它扩大了这一逻辑——采取自下而上而非集中管理的方式，通过分级权限让前线部队和高级指挥官都能使用，并旨在向最终用户提供其所需，而不是让指挥官对部队进行事无巨细的微观管理。43 结果是，观察—决策—打击循环越来越多地在最低战术层级闭合，主导性小型无人机系统和炮兵杀伤链的发射授权下放到连、排，甚至班一级。44 俄罗斯也曾试图建立类似优势，但尚未重新下放权力，导致实施陷入停滞。事实上，到2025年初，甚至俄罗斯军事学者也承认，俄军的相关努力落后于预测目标1.5至2年。45 此外，俄罗斯\n作为对乌克兰空中侦察部队Aerorozvidka的Delta——一种Delta级态势感知系统——的回应，Svod在俄罗斯军队中的发展仍然不足，扩散也不充分。46 根据俄罗斯已经展示出的组建无人机部队的能力，它应当能够将Svod转化为一个有用且广泛普及的系统，不过其进度会落后于战争的需求，也反映出该国体制僵化，使其在软件采购整体上明显落后于乌克兰。\n乌克兰的制度并非毫无摩擦。这个国家也承担着自身的吸收成本——腐败、采购不透明以及对捐助方时间表的依赖，仅举几例——即便是其最强大的系统也显现出这种压力。47 例如，乌克兰目前拥有一个由550多种无人系统组成的碎片化格局，其中许多并未整合进Delta；同时，它体现了一种由志愿者主导的供应模式，这种模式已被证明灵活敏捷，但最终难以持续。48 尽管面临这些挑战，乌克兰仍成功地转化了某些类似于俄罗斯从中国和朝鲜获得的外国援助，而俄罗斯却未能达到乌克兰实现援助效能的水平。\n乌克兰的指挥结构成为解释不同结果的一个变量——这种结构是经过长期有意建设的，而不是通过战场上的快速创新形成的。2014年至2022年间，乌克兰志愿者引入了11个态势感知和作战管理系统，这些系统与前线操作人员保持直接联系，从而使开发始终与实际军事需求相结合。该进程建立起来后，鉴于无人机及其他无人驾驶航空系统在冲突中的作用，乌克兰于2024年组建了专门的无人系统部队，作为一个独立军种，并将权力下放给网络化、植根于志愿者的部队。此外，乌克兰的捐助方多年来通过北约培训和互操作性测试，帮助乌克兰军队建立吸收能力。49 Delta在2024年联盟战士互操作性探索、试验、检验演习（CWIX24）中通过了北约互操作性测试，并于2024年7月整合进波兰TOPAZ炮兵系统。50 志愿者建设的生态系统与多年捐助方的制度建设共同巩固了乌克兰的吸收能力，而俄罗斯无法迅速复制这种组合。\n朝鲜部队似乎只在其自己的旅内行动，并且仅被最低限度地纳入俄罗斯指挥结构。\n乌克兰以象征性规模使用外国志愿援助，外国部队以个人或小团体形式抵达，而不是由伙伴国家派遣成建制部队。没有官方统计显示有多少外国志愿者在乌克兰武装部队中服役，估计数字差异很大，不过大多数估计认为，这一数字约为俄罗斯约15,000名朝鲜部队的一半，或可能更少。51 在部署前，这些部队接受约六周的训练，只有在熟悉乌克兰通信、指挥结构和战场条件达到一定程度后才会被部署。此外，外国人员在抵达前就会与乌克兰部队匹配。52 确实存在摩擦——语言障碍依然存在，外国部队在乌克兰指挥结构内行动的经验也仍然有限——但朝鲜部队那种明显临时性的部署方式，与乌克兰经过多年建立的训练和部队整合流程大相径庭。53 那些部队是\n在国际军团中表现成功的人员会被派往真正的乌克兰部队，直接参与战场行动；而朝鲜军队似乎只在自己的旅内行动，几乎没有融入俄罗斯的指挥结构。\n\n伙伴关系的局限：俄罗斯的伙伴为何无法突破上限\n\n2026年，乌克兰自下而上进行创新，从而能够更有效地吸收援助，但情况并非一直如此。高强度战争暴露了乌克兰苏联时代的制度腐败，而附带条件的外部援助提供了修复这些问题所需的额外压力。相比之下，尽管中国、伊朗和朝鲜提供硬件、零部件、弹药和影像资料，但它们没有附加任何推动乌克兰改革的条件。俄罗斯的伙伴既不要求制度变革作为支持条件，也没有施加那种促使乌克兰将投入转化为实际效果的生存压力。与乌克兰不同，俄罗斯并不面临关乎自身存续的生存威胁。\n\n西方对乌克兰的援助附带了多项规定，这些规定强化了乌克兰的反腐机构及其执法，并助推了创新。54 自俄罗斯于2022年入侵以来，美国已向乌克兰提供1270亿美元的直接援助，并对这笔援助的使用施加了多项限制。55 这些限制包括要求乌克兰证明其反腐措施取得明确进展，而减少腐败又会进一步释放资金。此外，条件因援助类型而异，并且经常包括某些监督和监测机制。例如，军事援助伴随着严格的最终用途监测：美国国防部在入侵发生后数月内成立了“乌克兰安全援助小组”，以追踪运往前线的物资——此前它花了七年时间才在阿富汗建立一个同等机构，并从中吸取了教训——而国会要求对每一件转移的国防物资进行逐项核算。56\n\n在这笔总额中，美国提供的约450亿美元直接预算支持经由世界银行转交，并受到法定保障措施的约束，包括一份要求签署的美乌第三方监测谅解备忘录，以及一个单独的可审计账户。57 这些规定具有持久性，2023年夏末，美国国务院发布正式外交照会，警告称持续的预算支持取决于乌克兰在反腐措施和财务透明度方面取得的进展，明确传达了挪用资金将危及美国的全部援助。58 更为关键的是，这些条件采用了与乌克兰申请加入欧盟相同的基准——独立的反腐检察官、改革后的国有企业董事会以及司法改革——这意味着压力来自多个渠道，推动基辅所需的变革并相互强化。这种伙伴关系结构从外部激励乌克兰——一个仍受其后苏联治理架构影响的国家——诚实地使用新获得的资源。尽管乌克兰军队内部的腐败仍是一个挑战——泽连斯基总统曾试图削弱两个反腐机构，最近又解除了坚定反腐批评者、国防部长米哈伊洛·费多罗夫的职务——但乌克兰在减少腐败和加强问责方面的轨迹远比俄罗斯积极，使乌克兰在\nwar.59相比之下，俄罗斯的伙伴提供援助时不附带治理条件——由于每个伙伴都由威权政府控制，因此几乎没有动力规定治理改革。乌克兰的伙伴关系要求最终偿还，并遵守反腐败和透明度基准；而俄罗斯的伙伴关系依赖直接互惠，并且往往接纳非法做法。俄罗斯的伙伴通常期待以下几项的某种组合：立即支付所提供服务的费用、相互军事合作，或技术援助。更重要的是，俄罗斯的双边伙伴关系通过专门构建来规避审查的渠道运作，从而拒绝透明度，并可能传播腐蚀性做法。这些路径助长影子贸易和规避制裁网络，强化不透明的治理模式以及共同的非法金融做法，进一步削弱各结盟国家的问责制。60西方对乌克兰援助中存在的条件性促成了有益的改革，但没有对俄罗斯施加同等压力——其伙伴对制度透明度毫无兴趣。尽管俄罗斯的伙伴关系强化了不透明和非法做法，而不是遏制它们，但从长期来看，更重要的是，伙伴关系的限制阻碍了外部嵌入式访问，而这正是改革的关键驱动力。同样的不透明和相互猜疑既阻滞了援助流动，也阻断了提高俄罗斯吸收上限所需的、嵌入式的多年制度建设。一个伙伴可以移交影像资料、部件，甚至安排为期两周的轮训，却始终不接触受援方的指挥结构。在缺乏持续访问和信任的情况下，伙伴无法做到的是进驻部队内部，改革打击授权所在的环节。过去，俄罗斯在允许中国接触敏感系统方面表现出极大克制。尽管双方显然存在相互猜疑——泄露的俄罗斯反情报文件显示出根深蒂固的不信任，俄罗斯军事期刊也提出反映对中国猜疑的阴谋论——但近期的机密文件显示，两国之间的整合正在加深。61然而，尽管联系日益紧密，双方都不太可能赋予对方改革指挥结构所需程度的嵌入式访问权限，因为双方仍将对方视为情报目标。\n\n俄罗斯及其伙伴都不希望承受这种制度建设所要求的透明度和介入。\n\n西方模式提高了乌克兰的吸收能力，因为捐助方获准进入其中——以援助为条件，嵌入培训人员，并在多年间开展互操作性测试。由于相互不信任，俄罗斯的伙伴被禁止扮演这一角色。俄罗斯及其伙伴都不希望承受这种制度建设所要求的透明度和介入，因此援助抵达时并没有伴随推动改革的压力，而这种改革本可使俄罗斯将那部分需要系统性改革才能转化为战斗力的援助转化为战斗力；如果没有乌克兰所经历的那类改革，俄罗斯仍无法将其获得援助的潜在能力转化为完全实现的作战能力。因此，伙伴关系的限制对俄罗斯形成双重束缚——一次是通过其获得的投入，另一次则更为重要，是通过这些投入能否转化为作战能力。已有一些证据表明，俄罗斯的伙伴曾试图解决\n影响俄罗斯军事适应的制度性挑战。例如，根据2025年7月达成的一项协议，俄罗斯国防部长安德烈·别洛乌索夫批准为约200名俄罗斯人员在中国接受无人机作战、电子战、反无人机战术以及至少一例无人机引导迫击炮射击的培训。62 此后，乌克兰情报部门确认，有180名俄罗斯军人接受了中国培训。尽管这清楚表明军事合作正在加强——尤其是乌克兰情报部门还报告称有550名中国军人在俄罗斯接受训练——但这种扩大的双边交流不太可能解决俄罗斯的制度性困难。\n据报道，这种交流更多集中于俄罗斯向中国军队提供源自战场经验的知识，而较少涉及中国向俄罗斯军队提供培训。63此外，一项引人注目的调查显示，中国与俄罗斯的军事合作正在深化——双方正在发展联合防空和导弹防御系统，甚至制定了可能干扰“星链”卫星群的计划。64 至关重要的是，这项调查发现，俄罗斯决定与中国分享其备受觊觎的S-500防空系统以及预警雷达技术。然而，即使是这种整合——这是自20世纪50年代末中苏分裂前以来所见的最高级别合作——也采取联合研发项目和交换战场数据的形式；没有证据表明中国正在推动治理改革，也没有证据表明中国获准获得乌克兰西方捐助方所要求的嵌入式访问权限。65\n从根本上说，乌克兰的改革议程由生存这一关乎存亡的紧迫要求所驱动——如果俄罗斯实现其最初目标，乌克兰将失去主权。相比之下，即使俄罗斯失败，乌克兰也不可能现实地威胁俄罗斯的主权；乌克兰充其量只能收回自2014年以来失去的领土。这一生存性威胁为这个前苏联国家的改革创造了必要条件。如果乌克兰官员浪费性地部署伙伴提供的资源，乌克兰就有输掉战争、进而失去主权的风险。俄罗斯可以浪费性地使用其资源，后果要小得多，而且它拥有更多可供浪费的资源。\n展望未来\n自2022年2月入侵乌克兰以来的这些年中，俄罗斯已经学会适应。Rubicon项目的发展表明，军方开始摆脱其僵化的战争方式；Svod指挥与控制系统展现出吸收失败并在压力下调整的实际能力；大量学术研究记录了突击战术、无人机运用和电子战方面有限但真实的适应。66 这些适应是真实存在的，而且随着时间推移确实会在整个部队中扩散——2023年看起来不同寻常的无人机部队，如今已成为常规配置。制约因素在于这种扩散的速度。\n俄罗斯的制度僵化意味着，创新会在边缘地带、精锐部队和能力异常出众的操作人员中出现，然后缓慢地向外扩散，通过一种旨在抵制经验横向传播而非加速传播的部队结构。Rubicon实时揭示了这一制约：一种真实的适应尚未在全军范围内复制，按照历史模式，很可能只会逐步扩散。伙伴援助抵达的速度快于俄罗斯军队重组并加以利用的速度，因此，那些要求俄罗斯军队进行\n大多数制度性变革处于闲置状态，或至多未被充分利用，而相关适应过程则缓慢地普及开来。俄罗斯将继续适应，但其适应速度取决于制度的僵化程度，而这一速度过于缓慢，无法在战争施加的时间表内，将高需求的伙伴援助转化为战场效果。随着乌克兰战争持续，俄罗斯无疑将继续寻找在边际上奏效的创新，并逐步吸收这些边缘创新，正如其自2023年以来在全军范围内采用无人机战术所显示的那样。这表明，一些伙伴援助——尤其是来自中国的援助——将随着战争持续而变得更加有用。2025年7月的训练协议可能是伙伴援助日益有用的早期迹象，尽管俄罗斯由此产生的创新一如既往地将能力集中于少数部队，而不是推动更广泛军力范围内的改革。\n此外，供应方面也显示出持续改善的迹象。Qianfan低地球轨道卫星系统（图2）是中国为与Starlink竞争而不断建设的一套系统——这赋予中国生产（并随后向俄罗斯出售）更多、更高质量ISR数据的能力——该系统因推进器和陀螺仪故障于2025年暂停，但这一暂停已于2026年4月解决，之后的发射每两到三周进行一次。67 中国计划在2026年进行140多次轨道发射，而Guowang和Qianfan合计已经拥有约350多颗卫星。68 尽管这仍远低于Qianfan第一代1300颗卫星的目标，但限制正在缓解，加工能力仍是主要瓶颈。69 俄罗斯继续严重依赖从中国卫星公司购买图像；鉴于其在建立自身具备竞争力的卫星星座方面存在延误，这种情况短期内不太可能改变。\n远程可视化\n制度僵化限制了俄罗斯将伙伴援助转化为战斗力的程度。苏联时代形成的、抑制创新的指挥文化，减缓了俄罗斯处理卫星图像、整合外国部队以及在全军范围内采用新无人机战术的能力；而威权体系中根深蒂固的腐败和不透明性，使得这些限制难以通过改革克服。俄罗斯在边缘领域——精锐部队和最有能力的操作人员中——的适应很可能会继续，一些投入——尤其是中国硬件——也可能随着时间推移变得更加有用。然而，如果没有俄罗斯指挥文化所抵制、其伙伴关系也不要求的制度改革，俄罗斯将需要比乌克兰多得多的援助，才能取得可比的效果。只要投入持续流入，而将其转化的结构不发生变化，这一差距就不太可能缩小。Wesley Bailey曾是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目的实习生。Daniel Byman是CSIS战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目主任。本简报得到了对CSIS的一般性支持，因此得以完成。没有任何直接赞助资助本简报。\n请参阅PDF以获取参考文献。\nCSIS简报由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且不受专有权限制。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，文中表达的所有观点、立场和结论\n在本出版物中，应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n俄罗斯，\n国防与安全，\n国防战略与能力，\n地缘政治与国际安全，以及\n乌克兰战争\nWesley Bailey\n战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目 前实习生\n图片\nDaniel Byman\n战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目主任\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目\n相关内容\n图片\n俄罗斯的鲜血与财富：普京战争不断膨胀的代价\n简报\n作者\nSeth G. Jones\n和\nRiley McCabe\n— 2026年7月1日\n图片\n通过联动增强韧性：俄罗斯、伊朗与南北贸易的抱负\n报告\n作者\nMaria Snegovaya，\nTina Dolbaia，\n和\nNicholas Fenton\n— 2026年4月16日\n图片\n俄罗斯如何为人工智能驱动的自主性构建主权无人机生态系统\n报告\n作者\nKateryna Bondar\n— 2026年4月13日\n图片\n了解乌克兰的军事人工智能生态系统\n报告\n作者\nKateryna Bondar\n— 2024年11月12日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-07-31T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2525","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"向华盛顿特区派遣国民警卫队并非解决犯罪问题的正确方案","title_en":"Sending the National Guard into D.C. Is the Wrong Solution to a Crime Problem","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）发表评论文章，分析特朗普总统向华盛顿特区部署800名国民警卫队并联邦化特区警察的决定。作者认为军队不适合执法，可能适得其反，建议加强特区警察力量、提升联邦财产实体安全，并指出此举可能分散国防部对大国竞争的关注。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary analyzes President Trump's deployment of 800 National Guard troops to Washington, D.C., and the federalization of the city's police force. The authors argue military forces are unsuited for law enforcement, recommend strengthening the D.C. police instead, and warn the move may divert the Defense Department's focus from great-power competition.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:25:11.142637+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/sending-national-guard-dc-wrong-solution-crime-problem","content":"Sending the National Guard into D.C. Is the Wrong Solution to a Crime Problem\nPhoto: Andrew Harnik/Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nMark F. Cancian\nand\nChris H. Park\nPublished August 12, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nDeploying the National Guard to patrol the streets of Washington, D.C., is the wrong solution to a perceived crime problem. Military forces are unsuited for law enforcement and can easily make the problem worse. A better approach would be to strengthen the D.C. police, the right tool for fighting crime.Q1: What has the president directed?A1: On August 11, President Trump announced that 800 National Guard troops would deploy to combat crime in Washington, D.C., while the federal government takes control of the city’s police force. Over the weekend, the administration had already dispatched FBI agents and other federal authorities to patrol the streets of D.C. These steps follow the president’s warning last week about “federalizing the city” to address crime, issued after a Department of Government Efficiency staff member was assaulted.Q2: Why are military forces unsuited for law enforcement?A2: The reasons are threefold. First, military forces are less familiar than police with the nuances of citizens’ rights and the conditions under which force is permissible (see Figure 1, which compares military training with that of the police). National Guard training focuses on combat—how to use weapons and fight—while police training focuses on handling crime and the law.\nRemote Visualization\nSecond, military forces have the wrong attitude about civilians. Law enforcement is trained to see civilians as citizens who deserve protection, except in the most extreme circumstances. Military personnel are taught to treat civilians as potential threats and to always be ready to respond. Crowd control—in other words, dealing with unruly citizens—is the primary law enforcement training the National Guard receives.Finally, military personnel are untrained in the complexities of gathering evidence and building a case that will stand up in court. Indeed, nearly half the Police Academy’s 27-week curriculum is dedicated to criminal procedure. Instead, military personnel are trained to remove the immediate threat, which often involves actions that would prejudice a court case and undermine the rationale for their actions (removing criminals from the streets).Q3: Does the president have the authority to take these actions?A3: Probably. The president’s orders to use the D.C. National Guard in law enforcement appear to be legally sound, though this will likely be tested in the courts. D.C. is different from the rest of the country because the federal government has much more control.Much of the discussion about using the military for law enforcement turns on the Posse Comitatus Act (PCA), a law that greatly restricts the use of federal troops in law enforcement. It was passed in 1878 after Reconstruction as a reaction to federal forces occupying and administering the former Confederate states and has been reaffirmed for 150 years. Americans have always been wary of the military’s involvement in civilian affairs. Military forces being involved in domestic law enforcement looks too much like martial law and military occupation.The law provides exceptions for “cases and under circumstances expressly authorized by the Constitution or Act of Congress.” Presidents have sent in military forces to settle labor disputes, and courts have affirmed this presidential authority: the “strong arm of the national government may be put forth . . . to compel obedience to its laws.”Using the National Guard raises separate legal questions because of its state connection. State governors command the National Guard in peacetime, and the National Guard is typically the first force outside of the police that communities call on. The courts have generally held that the PCA does not apply to the National Guard when not in federal service.The president has direct control over the D.C. National Guard at all times because the district does not have a governor. The Department of Justice has long opined that the D.C. National Guard can operate without being subject to the PCA, but this claim has not been tested in the courts.Q4: Can the president federalize the D.C. police?A4: Yes. The president of the United States can use D.C.’s municipal police force, the Metropolitan Police, for “federal purposes” for up to 30 days, after which he needs a vote of Congress.The administration stated that it has federalized D.C. police to “[maintain] law and order in the Nation’s seat of Government.” Under this broad mandate, the president will institute law enforcement approaches over the next month that he believes mayors of other major cities should implement. While there surely will be disagreements on the merits of specific reforms or practices, the Metropolitan Police is far better equipped than the military to address criminal activity in the city and engage with its 700,000 residents and 27 million annual tourists.One specific duty identified in the executive order was the protection of national monuments and Federal property, a responsibility typically carried out by specialized agencies. D.C. has a plethora of federal law enforcement agencies as a result of hosting senior elements of the federal government. The list includes national agencies like the U.S. Marshals Service, Secret Service, and FBI, as well as agencies with defined jurisdictions, such as the Capitol Police over congressional grounds, Cathedral Police over the Washington National Cathedral, or Park Police over national parks. The D.C. police could reinforce these existing agencies. Q5: Won’t the military role be limited to support, and not law enforcement?A5: Maybe. Initial reports had the National Guard providing support to the police, such as transportation and communications. This is similar to what troops have long done on the southwest border in support of the U.S. Border Patrol. It is not inappropriate but appears unnecessary. First, the D.C. police have 3,100 officers with 600 civilians in support. Adding 800 military support personnel likely exceeds operational needs and could result in a lot of standing around and boredom.If the problem is a lack of support, the civilian economy can provide communications, transportation, and all manner of services at a much lower cost than the military.Further, the mission could grow. In a Fox News interview, Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth stated that the National Guard will “not be involved in law enforcement functions” while also asserting that they will have “wide latitude . . . [to take] necessary action to protect citizens of D.C. and to protect themselves.” Troops have accompanied Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) on raids in other parts of the country, so such an expansion of the National Guard’s mission in D.C. is possible.Q6: Are there better ways for the president to fight crime in D.C.?A6: Yes. The first action should be bringing the police up to full strength, despite the president’s statements that D.C. has enough police. D.C. Mayor Muriel Bowser has set a goal of 4,000 officers. The city has fallen far short of that target for years, and the number of uniformed officers has been on a downward trajectory. The House recognized the centrality of the police by passing H.R. 2096, Protecting Our Nation’s Capital Emergency Act, by a bipartisan vote.\nRemote Visualization\nConservatives argue that shortfalls result from anti-police attitudes as a result of the George Floyd murder and subsequent riots. District officials argue that the recruiting environment is difficult and that this challenge is one shared by police departments across the country. Regardless, the federal government could help. The Army turned its recruiting crisis around, and might help the D.C. police do the same.If the concern is the protection of federal property, physical security could be enhanced. After 9/11, the federal government spent a huge amount of money on upgrading physical security. Similar measures could be adopted again. Physical security has the advantage that it is on duty 24/7 and does not require expensive personnel.Q7: Looking ahead, what are the implications for D.C.?A7: Sending in the troops is a dramatic political statement about the importance of an issue, and the Trump administration has frequently done that.During his first administration, the president deployed more than 5,000 National Guard troops in response to demonstrations in D.C. protesting the death of George Floyd. As one of his first actions as commander-in-chief this January, the president deployed additional U.S. troops to the southern border. He sent 4,000 National Guard troops and 700 Marines to Los Angeles in June to contain protests against immigration raids.The president will not be dissuaded from making that statement here. However, the troops should be replaced quickly with more effective measures, like expanding the number of police. As in carpentry or auto mechanics, it’s important to use the right tools to get the desired effects.Further, relying on troops for temporary nonmilitary assignments may divert focus from Secretary Hegseth’s stated priority of increasing lethality, particularly with an eye on China. The Department of Defense cannot have it both ways. Either its primary focus is warfighting, or it is a temporary help agency for other parts of the federal government.Mark F. Cancian (Colonel, U.S. Marine Corps Reserve, ret.) is a senior adviser with the International Security Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Chris Park is a research associate for the Arleigh A. Burke Chair at CSIS.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nDefense and Security, and\nGeopolitics and International Security\nImage\nMark F. Cancian\nSenior Adviser, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nChris H. Park\nResearch Associate, Arleigh A. Burke Chair in Strategy\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nDefense 360","content_cn":"向华盛顿特区派遣国民警卫队是解决犯罪问题的错误方案\n照片：Andrew Harnik/Getty Images\n关键问题\n作者\nMark F. Cancian\n和\nChris H. Park\n发表于2025年8月12日\n远程可视化\n部署国民警卫队在华盛顿特区街道上巡逻，是解决所谓犯罪问题的错误方案。军队不适合执法，而且很容易使问题恶化。更好的做法是加强特区警察力量，他们才是打击犯罪的正确工具。Q1：总统下达了什么指令？A1：8月11日，特朗普总统宣布，将部署800名国民警卫队士兵，以打击华盛顿特区的犯罪，同时联邦政府接管该市警察部队。周末期间，本届政府已经派遣联邦调查局特工和其他联邦机构人员在特区街道上巡逻。这些措施是在总统上周警告要通过“联邦化该市”来处理犯罪问题之后采取的，此前一名政府效率部工作人员遭到袭击。Q2：为什么军队不适合执法？A2：原因有三点。首先，与警察相比，军队对公民权利的细微之处以及何种情况下可以使用武力不够熟悉（见图1，该图比较了军队与警察所接受的训练）。国民警卫队的训练重点是战斗——如何使用武器和作战——而警察训练重点是处理犯罪和法律。\n远程可视化\n其次，军队对平民持有错误的态度。执法人员受训将平民视为应受保护的公民，只有在最极端的情况下例外。军人则被教导把平民视为潜在威胁，并始终准备作出反应。人群控制——换句话说，应对不守规矩的公民——是国民警卫队接受的主要执法训练。最后，军人没有接受过收集证据和构建能够经受法庭审理的案件所需的复杂训练。事实上，警察学院为期27周的课程中，近一半用于刑事诉讼程序。相反，军人接受的训练是消除眼前的威胁，这往往涉及会对法庭案件产生不利影响、并削弱其行动理由（将罪犯清出街头）的行为。Q3：总统有权采取这些行动吗？A3：大概有。总统命令动用华盛顿特区国民警卫队执法，在法律上似乎站得住脚，不过这很可能会在法院受到检验。特区不同于美国其他地区，因为联邦政府拥有更大的控制权。关于使用军队执法的大部分讨论，都围绕《武装部队警务法》（PCA）展开；该法严格限制联邦军队参与执法。该法于1878年重建时期结束后通过，当时是对联邦军队占领并管理前邦联州的反应，并在过去150年间得到重申。美国人一直对军队介入民事事务持谨慎态度。军队参与国内执法，看起来过于类似戒严和军事占领。该法律为“宪法或国会法案明确授权的案件和情形”规定了例外。总统曾派遣军队解决劳资纠纷，法院也确认了这种总统\n权限：“可以动用……国家政府的强力……迫使人们服从其法律。”由于国民警卫队与州的联系，使用国民警卫队会引发不同的法律问题。州长在和平时期指挥国民警卫队，而国民警卫队通常是社区在警察之外首先求助的力量。法院通常认定，国民警卫队不在联邦服役时，PCA不适用。\n\n由于哥伦比亚特区没有州长，总统始终直接控制哥伦比亚特区国民警卫队。司法部长期以来一直认为，哥伦比亚特区国民警卫队可以在不受PCA约束的情况下行动，但这一主张尚未在法院得到检验。\n\nQ4：总统能否将哥伦比亚特区警察联邦化？\n\nA4：能。美国总统可以将哥伦比亚特区的市警察部队——大都会警察——用于“联邦目的”，最长30天，之后需要国会投票批准。\n\n政府表示，它已经将哥伦比亚特区警察联邦化，以“维护国家政府所在地的法律与秩序”。在这一广泛授权下，总统将在未来一个月实施他认为其他大城市市长也应采取的执法方式。尽管人们肯定会就具体改革或做法的优点产生分歧，但大都会警察比军队更有能力应对该市的犯罪活动，并与其70万居民和每年2700万游客打交道。\n\n行政命令中确定的一项具体职责是保护国家纪念碑和联邦财产，这项责任通常由专门机构承担。由于联邦政府高级部门设在此地，哥伦比亚特区拥有大量联邦执法机构。名单包括美国法警局、特勤局和联邦调查局等全国性机构，也包括管辖范围明确的机构，例如负责国会大院的国会警察、负责华盛顿国家大教堂的教堂警察，或负责国家公园的公园警察。哥伦比亚特区警察可以为这些现有机构提供增援。\n\nQ5：军方的作用不会仅限于支援，而不会参与执法吗？\n\nA5：也许。最初的报道显示，国民警卫队将为警方提供支援，例如运输和通信。这类似于军队长期以来在西南边境支持美国边境巡逻队所做的工作。这并不不恰当，但似乎没有必要。首先，哥伦比亚特区警察有3100名警员，另有600名文职人员提供支援。增加800名军事支援人员很可能超出行动需要，并可能导致大量人员无所事事、百无聊赖地等待。\n\n如果问题在于缺乏支援，民用经济可以以远低于军方的成本提供通信、运输和各种服务。\n\n此外，任务可能会扩大。在接受福克斯新闻采访时，国防部长皮特·赫格塞思表示，国民警卫队“不会参与执法职能”，同时又声称他们将拥有“广泛的自由裁量权……[采取]保护哥伦比亚特区公民及保护自身所必需的行动”。军队曾在美国其他地区陪同移民与海关执法局（ICE）开展突袭，因此，国民警卫队在哥伦比亚特区的任务出现这种扩展是有可能的。\n\nQ6：总统是否有更好的方式打击哥伦比亚特区的犯罪？\n\nA6：\n是的。第一步应当是让警察队伍达到满员状态，尽管总统声称哥伦比亚特区拥有足够的警察。哥伦比亚特区市长穆里尔·鲍泽设定了4,000名警员的目标。多年来，该市一直远未达到这一目标，身着制服的警员人数也一直呈下降趋势。众议院通过两党投票通过了H.R. 2096《保护我国首都紧急法案》，认可警察的核心地位。\n远程可视化\n保守派认为，人员短缺源于乔治·弗洛伊德被杀及随后骚乱所导致的反警察态度。特区官员则认为，招募环境十分困难，而这一挑战是全国各地警察部门共同面临的。无论如何，联邦政府本可以提供帮助。陆军扭转了自身的招募危机，或许也能帮助哥伦比亚特区警察做到同样的事。如果担忧的是联邦财产的保护，那么可以加强实体安保。9·11事件后，联邦政府投入巨额资金升级实体安保。类似措施可以再次采用。实体安保的优势在于全天候值守，并且不需要昂贵的人力。\nQ7：展望未来，这对哥伦比亚特区意味着什么？A7：出动军队是对某一问题重要性的戏剧性政治宣示，而特朗普政府经常这样做。在其第一届政府任期内，总统曾部署5,000多名国民警卫队员，以应对哥伦比亚特区抗议乔治·弗洛伊德死亡的示威活动。今年1月，作为其担任三军统帅后的首批行动之一，总统向南部边境增派了美国军队。6月，他向洛杉矶派遣了4,000名国民警卫队员和700名海军陆战队员，以遏制反对移民突袭行动的抗议活动。\n总统不会被劝阻，不在这里作出这一宣示。不过，应当迅速用更有效的措施取代军队，例如扩大警察队伍规模。正如木工或汽车修理一样，使用正确的工具以达到预期效果十分重要。\n此外，依靠军队执行临时性的非军事任务，可能会分散国防部长赫格塞思所宣称的提升杀伤力这一优先事项的注意力，尤其是着眼于中国。国防部不能两头兼顾。它的首要重点要么是作战，要么就是成为联邦政府其他部门的临时援助机构。\n马克·F·坎西安（美国海军陆战队预备役上校，退役）是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心国际安全项目的高级顾问。克里斯·帕克是CSIS阿利·A·伯克主席项目的研究助理。\n《关键问题》由战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家私人、免税机构，专注于国际公共政策问题。其研究具有非党派性且不具专有性。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应被理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2025 战略与国际研究中心。保留所有权利。\n标签\n国防与安全，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全\n图片\n马克·F·坎西安\n国防与安全部高级顾问\n图片\n克里斯·H·帕克\n阿利·A·伯克战略主席项目研究助理\n项目与计划\n国防与\n安全\n防御360","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-08-12T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3026","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"共同威胁：当今地缘政治环境下的印太联盟与责任分担","title_en":"Shared Threats: Indo-Pacific Alliances and Burden Sharing in Today’s Geopolitical Environment","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）韩国问题专家车维德向美国参议院外交关系委员会作证，阐述印太联盟体系的历史作用与改革方向。他提出联盟现代化四原则，建议重新定义责任分担、扩大基地准入、加强日韩澳新等盟友在台海等双情景下的协作，并推动印太四国参与全球治理。","summary_en":"CSIS Korea Chair Victor Cha testified before the Senate Foreign Relations Committee on U.S. alliances in the Indo-Pacific, outlining their historical role and needed reforms. He proposed four principles for alliance modernization, redefining burden-sharing, expanding access and basing, enhancing allied cooperation on dual contingencies, and formalizing Indo-Pacific-4 participation in global governance.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:55:36.462902+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/shared-threats-indo-pacific-alliances-and-burden-sharing-todays-geopolitical-environment","content":"Shared Threats: Indo-Pacific Alliances and Burden Sharing in Today’s Geopolitical Environment\nPhoto: CSIS\nCongressional Testimony\nby\nVictor Cha\nPublished March 26, 2025\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload Testimony\n986kb\nVictor Cha testified before the Senate Foreign Relations Committee regarding shared threats between the U.S. and allies in the Indo-Pacific.\nImage\nVictor Cha\nPresident, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Korea Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nKorea Chair\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nCongressional Affairs at CSIS\nCongressional Testimony on Asia\nWatch Full Testimony\nRemote Visualization\nChairman Risch, Ranking Member Shaheen and distinguished members of the committee, it is an honor to appear before this committee on the topic of U.S. alliances in the Indo-Pacific region. The views represented in this testimony are my own and not those of any employer or institution with which I am affiliated.The Indo-Pacific is of terrible importance to U.S. economic and security interests, and our alliances help to preserve those interests and achieve our goals there. But these alliances are in need of reform and modernization given the challenges posed by the new geostrategic environment.I will speak briefly about the historical importance of these security institutions and ways to modernize them going forward. I request that my full written statement be submitted for the record.Origins of the Alliance SystemWhen the United States created a network of bilateral alliances in Asia in the immediate postwar period, architects like John Foster Dulles acknowledged that this system was different from the multilateral structure built in Western Europe, but policymakers at the time probably had no inkling of how successful this alliance system would become and how well it would serve U.S. interests.Each of the bilateral alliances at that time with Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, Philippines, Thailand, Australia, and New Zealand (ANZUS) afforded the United States an immense amount of power and influence, unlike anywhere else in the world. The alliance system created incredibly loyal allies like Japan, Australia, and South Korea, the latter two of which have fought with the United States in every war since World War I and the Korean War, respectively.Through this alliance system, the United States won the Cold War in Asia: It created prosperity and economic development in the region beyond anyone's imagination; it fostered growth and democracy. The importance of democratic values in the alliance system cannot be undervalued considering that at the outset of the alliance system's creation in 1951, it looked very much like communism would win the day. The CCP victory in China in 1949, the communist North Korea attack in 1950, and communist insurgencies in Southeast Asia lent credence to the domino theory -- that soon, all these distant, post-colonial states would be falling behind the iron curtain. But that did not happen. The U.S. alliance system in Asia prevailed.Today’s Geopolitical EnvironmentToday, the pacing threat from China crosses many dimensions, including security, economic, information, and technology. But it is complicated and enhanced by a growing group of illiberal actors operating in concert with one another to undermine the U.S. position. These actors are well known to you: Russia, Iran, and North Korea and their conduit states, Cuba, Iraq, Syria, and Venezuela. Table 1, for example, shows the range of support provided by these actors to Russia’s war in Ukraine. China supplied almost 100% of the microelectronics for Russia’s missiles, tanks, and aircraft. Iran contributed significantly to Russia’s supply of attack drones, close-range ballistic missiles, and glide bombs among other weapons used in the war. North Korea has provided 11,000 to 12,000 troops, hundreds of ballistic missiles, and may account for as much as 50% of Russia’s ammunition today. Outside of these actors, the supply of like-minded and capable partners dwindles in comparison with a growing number of “hedge” states like Brazil, India, Indonesia, Saudi Arabia, South Africa, and UAE who cannot be relied upon to side with the United States depending on the issue.\nRemote Visualization\nReforming the Indo-Pacific AlliancesThe United States must meet these challenges and win, not by going it alone but with the support of our allies. There is no real alternative. The alliance system has proven to be a winning formula in the past, and it will do so again, but not without reform and modernization.For some in the current administration, the imperative to update and modernize these alliances stems from the judgment that they are costly economically to the United States. If we use trade and defense-spending as two metrics of allied burden-sharing, there are no Indo-Pacific allies that are judged to be carrying their fair share.\nRemote Visualization\nFour principles should drive the mindset of alliance modernization: 1) Indo-Pacific allies are capable and can contribute much more to networked capabilities than in the past; 2) Change is difficult, but Indo-Pacific allies must manage the domestic-political resistance to doing things differently; 3) Allies need to accept increased burden-sharing as a given in the modernization effort; and 4) Allied contributions to successful deterrence with the United States are a critical pre-requisite to their realizing commercial opportunity with China – that is, without security, there is no commercial opportunity, there is only commercial coercion by China.In the Indo-Pacific region, the United States has 24 persistent bases and access to 20 other military sites. By contrast, China has only one overseas base in the world in Djibouti. These positions host 88,500 active-duty servicemembers, including about 60,000 in Japan and about 28,500 in Korea. This presence affords the United States rapid response capabilities to military contingencies; contributes to successful deterrence against adversarial threats to the homeland and to allies/partners; and provides the physical assurance of the U.S. security umbrella that is critical not just to peace, but also to profitable commerce and investment that drives the region’s growth.Congress’ role in maintaining this alliance network is critical in its appropriation of funds for basing activities; its legislation of policies and requirements related to basing; and its oversight of the executive branch’s policies related to basing and alliance relations.Burden-SharingFour principles should undergird the reform of burden-sharing arrangements with allies. One, allied governments must recognize that traditional monetary burden-sharing algorithms are legacies of a time one-half century ago when U.S. capabilities dwarfed those of smaller partners. Host-nation support arrangements where the U.S. annual contribution is larger than that of the partner no longer makes sense given the partner’s wealth.Two, host-nation support agreements probably can and should take account of allied contributions to defense and security outside of, but on behalf of, the bilateral security partnership.Three, aside from the monetary aspects, burden-sharing should be defined as the reorientation of costs and capabilities to the broader regional deterrence and defense mission in the Indo-Pacific. While this is already the case for some alliances, it is not the case for all and needs to be so.Four, a critical element of reform is to avoid surprises. It is imperative to create a suitable runway and plan that gives host nations the political space to make potentially paradigm-shifting adjustments to the alliance.Current SMA arrangements for Japan and South Korea adhere to a legacy algorithm of incremental annual increases of around 2% and 5-8% respectively. The newly concluded South Korea agreement ($1.05 billion in 2025 and $1.14 billion in 2026) expires in 2030; the Japan agreement (about $1.5 billion in 2025) expires in 2026 with each agreement covering about 40-50% (Korea) and 75% (Japan) of the non-personnel costs of stationing USFK and USFJ (covering labor, utilities, training, construction, purchase of local supplies).Negotiation of new agreements, following a “cost-plus-50” model for example, will be difficult for host nations to swallow given the need for legislative ratification (unlike in the United States). In devising a more equitable formula for cost-sharing that acknowledges the economic capacity of allied governments’ substantial wealth, the United States and allied governments could agree to include other non-SMA contributions as part of the ledger of allied contributions in future agreements. Table 2 gives examples of past non-SMA costs borne by Japan and Korea.\nRemote Visualization\nGreater burden-sharing by allies could also acknowledge the out-of-theater contributions by allies to security. But this would also require that current burden-sharing agreements be revised to reinstitute allied support for U.S. military assets outside of the host nation (e.g., South Korea). For example, Table 3 enumerates Australia, Japan and South Korea’s contributions to Ukraine.\nRemote Visualization\nThis more comprehensive definition of burden-sharing by allies should be documented by the administration through legislation like the Allied Burden Sharing Report Act and the NATO Burden Sharing Report Act.Access, Basing and Overflight (ABO)The United States will increasingly operate in a contested basing environment. Some of the challenges are internal in terms of our own resource constraints. Some of them relate to the strategy of the adversary (i.e., A2/AD). And some of the challenges are political and internal to those particular host countries. Yet securing these privileges are critical to giving the U.S. military resilience in terms of staying power, resupply/repair, and storage, not to mention the critical physical presence that undergirds credible deterrence.Here I cannot offer recommendations that you have not already heard. In general, we need to push for U.S. use of allied bases in contingencies where the ally is not directly threatened. Given growing reach and capabilities of the adversary, the United States must do more to harden forward presence infrastructure as well as increase air and missile defense systems. There could be more burden-sharing of the latter with allies, including additional THAAD systems in the region.In the case of Japan, major advances in defense spending and the creation of the joint operational command (JJOC) are commendable and have been supported by the public. There is an expectation that the United States would pre-consult with Japan on pre-positioning of additional capabilities, and that Tokyo would gain support of local communities. The Japanese government could build on this momentum to improve access arrangements for the alliance to make deterrence more credible and defenses more capable in a Taiwan contingency. This requires an initiative from the top to engage in a whole-of-government dialogue internally and with the United States (in the 2+2 ministerial talks) on revisions to SOFA which govern limited use access to JSDF and civilian facilities other than permanent U.S. bases in Japan. The purpose would be to streamline the national and local approval process for U.S. access to dispersed positions in the Southwest (Nansei) island chain (Miyako, Ishigaki, Yonaguni) in advance of an armed-attack situation. Tokyo must also work with local governments to enhance their understanding of the role they play in national defense (something better understood in Tokyo). Such reforms should not be seen as a favor or concession to the United States, but as necessary to enhance Japan’s national defense and security in the Nansei island chain closest to Taiwan.In the case of South Korea, U.S. access to bases, the addition of new non-kinetic capabilities, and the addition of kinetic capabilities would be generally uncontroversial. The key is that they are framed as directed toward the North Korean threat. The framing of capabilities as directed to a Taiwan contingency, however, becomes more problematic given the traditional South Korean reluctance to become entrapped in a U.S.-China conflict. Domestic political factors matter here to a degree in that conservative governments in Seoul tend to be a shade more open to discussions about Taiwan contingencies. It is also noteworthy that both progressive and conservative governments have broken new ground with summit-level statements equating freedom of navigation and stability of the Taiwan straits with Korea’s security. Still, the resistance is real. The United States could engage the next Korean government in a broad reorientation of U.S. forces in Korea moving from a peninsular mission to a regional one. The range of options here could span removing troops from the peninsula to elsewhere or increasing the presence in Korea with a portion each committed to peninsular defense and regional defense.However, I would like to draw your attention to one important strategy to optimize U.S. ABO options. If the strategy for maintaining access rests on complementing and/or reforming large, main legacy operating base infrastructures, which are put at risk by the adversary’s anti-access capabilities, with a network of smaller, dispersed, and concealed arrangements in the Indo-Pacific, then a major threat to this plan is not just China’s ability to locate and militarily target such arrangements, but the political and commercial pressure that it can put on the hosts to withhold cooperation with the United States. Indeed, commercial pressure is probably the ideal tool for China to use given the nature of these small and dispersed ABO arrangements – that is, a host nation is more likely to succumb to intense Chinese economic pressure to withhold cooperation with the United States over a discrete access arrangement than over a large military base. China’s use of economic coercion against U.S. allies is well-established. Since 2008, it has used economic coercion against 18 governments and over 500 companies (including U.S. primes). The purpose of the coercion is expressly political, not trade-related. The most well-known case related to access capabilities is probably the 2016-2017 commercial assault on Korean companies for the emplacement of a THAAD battery in Seongju, South Korea, which did at least $7.5 billion dollars of damage.An important component of an ABO strategy must be to complement the military access arrangements with a counter-coercion strategy designed to deter Chinese economic coercion. This strategy, otherwise known as “collective resilience” would take the form of a multilateral economic deterrence mechanism that leverages the combined trade capabilities of like-minded partners to signal to China that they cannot economically coerce any one member of the group. Like-minded partners trade with China in 575 finished and intermediary goods valued at $59.61 billion upon which China is highly dependent, and for which China does not have alternative sources.1 A collective economic deterrence strategy has proven to work in Europe where the anti-coercion instrument (ACI) introduced in 2023 seems to have slowed China’s coercive practices against other individual states as it had done to Lithuania. The United States should encourage Indo-Pacific allies to do the same expressly with the understanding that there is an important security rationale related to ABO.Opportunistic AggressionThe war in Ukraine has made Indo-Pacific allies realize that war is a real possibility in Asia and that the unthinkable is possible. There is more proactive thinking in the region about dual contingencies and opportunistic aggression than I have ever experienced before. For example, we have had inquiries in the last three years from both Taiwan and South Korea about gaming out dual contingencies at the Track II level. An updated alliance structure should capitalize on this new thinking to proactively consider new ways of deterring opportunistic aggression. These could include, but not be limited to:Reorienting of the U.S. military presence on the Korean peninsula to a regional deterrence and defense mission (either by drawdown or supplementing);Training of USFK both on and off the peninsula;Collective defense declaration among the U.S., Japan, and South Korea and a bilateral security declaration between Japan and South Korea (2025 marks the 60th anniversary of normalized relations);Increased South Korean defense spending and independent military capabilities to deter North Korean opportunistic aggression during a Taiwan contingency;Consideration of rear area support roles played by South Korea in a Taiwan contingencyUse of the UNC contributing nations’ framework on the Korean peninsula to enhance peacetime deterrence These are all politically sensitive issues in South Korea and would be topics to be taken up with the government once the political impeachment crisis in that country is resolved. Any reorientation of the U.S. presence on the peninsula, however, would require hard thinking about the retention of some ground troop presence to provide the traditional “tripwire” deterrent against North Korean adventurism; and would necessitate a planned, phased process that would minimize the negative political and economic externalities that would result from an abrupt change. Complete withdrawal of the troop presence would beg the question of cost (losing the South Korean subsidy), as well as alternative locations that would still be close enough to the fight to be effective. Increasing the troop presence (with a portion each trained for peninsular and regional defense) could mute potential South Korean self-help responses (e.g., proactive discussions of nuclearization), but would require a difficult Korean political choice to acknowledge its role in a Taiwan fight.New Areas of Allied CooperationUpdating of the alliances should include other new areas of cooperation outside of the military realm. A strong precedent has already been set by the previous two administrations for allied cooperation on economic security, export controls, and supply chains. Under the current administration, new opportunities have been unearthed in areas including shipbuilding and strategic energy cooperation with Japan and South Korea, in particular.In Secretary Rubio’s confirmation hearing before this body, he observed that eight decades after the end of the second World War, America is being called upon again to create a free world out of chaos, but he also noted the voters elected President Trump because they want a strong America with a “prudent” foreign policy. In this regard, the State Department should encourage our allies in the Indo-Pacific and Europe to play more active political roles in sustaining global governance as traditional institutions like the UN Security Council have underperformed and groupings like BRICS aim to disrupt the current rules-based order. I have two specific recommendations here. First, the United States should encourage more direct security dialogue and military planning among European and Asian allies that builds on the Trump and Biden administrations’ previous efforts to build multilateral coalitions on functional issues like export controls, sanctions, and pandemic preparedness. The war in Ukraine, China’s assertiveness, and North Korea’s supply of Russia have brought the two theaters together in unprecedented ways and I have been personally impressed by the level of interest and sophistication in the European discussions on security of the Indo-Pacific.Finally, the State Department would do well to promote and formalize the “Indo-Pacific-4” framework’s (Japan, South Korea, Australia, New Zealand) participation in global governance institutions. Regular participation in the NATO leader’s summits have led to greater cooperation across Europe and Asia in countering Chinese coercion. Moreover, the Group of Seven (G7) has become the de facto organization in which leading like-minded states coordinate policies, set sanctions, and define new rules and norms in the absence of functioning UN mandates. Indo-Pacific and European actors like Australia, South Korea and Spain are proven high performers in many of the pressing tasks identified in recent G7 leaders’ summits like economic resilience and security, digital competitiveness, food security, climate change, labor security, and development, and therefore could contribute much to burden-sharing and global governance.2Please consult the PDF for references.","content_cn":"共同威胁：当今地缘政治环境下的印太联盟与负担分担\n照片：CSIS\n国会证词\n作者\nVictor Cha\n发表于2025年3月26日\n可下载文件\n下载证词\n986kb\nVictor Cha 在参议院外交关系委员会作证，谈及美国与印太盟友面临的共同威胁。\n图片\nVictor Cha\n地缘政治与外交政策部主任兼韩国主席\n项目与计划\n韩国主席\n地缘政治与外交政策\nCSIS国会事务\n关于亚洲的国会证词\n观看完整证词\n远程可视化\nRisch主席、Shaheen首席少数党委员以及委员会各位尊敬的成员，我很荣幸就美国在印太地区的联盟问题出席本委员会作证。本证词所代表的观点仅为我个人观点，不代表我所隶属的任何雇主或机构。印太地区对美国的经济和安全利益具有极其重要的意义，而我们的联盟有助于维护这些利益并在那里实现我们的目标。但鉴于新的地缘战略环境所带来的挑战，这些联盟需要改革和现代化。我将简要谈谈这些安全机制的历史重要性，以及今后实现现代化的方式。我请求将我的完整书面陈述列入记录。\n联盟体系的起源\n当美国在战后初期于亚洲建立双边联盟网络时，约翰·福斯特·杜勒斯等设计者承认，这一体系不同于在西欧建立的多边结构；但当时的决策者可能完全没有想到，这一联盟体系会取得如此成功，并如此有效地服务于美国利益。当时美国与日本、韩国、台湾、菲律宾、泰国、澳大利亚和新西兰（ANZUS）建立的每一个双边联盟，都赋予美国巨大的力量和影响力，这是世界其他任何地方都无法比拟的。联盟体系造就了日本、澳大利亚和韩国这样极其忠诚的盟友；其中后两个国家分别自第一次世界大战和朝鲜战争以来，在每一场战争中都与美国并肩作战。通过这一联盟体系，美国赢得了亚洲的冷战：它在该地区创造了超乎任何人想象的繁荣与经济发展；促进了增长和民主。鉴于联盟体系在1951年开始建立时，共产主义看起来很可能会取得胜利，因此民主价值观在联盟体系中的重要性怎么强调都不为过。1949年中国共产党在中国取得胜利、1950年共产主义朝鲜发动进攻，以及东南亚的共产主义叛乱，都为多米诺骨牌理论增添了可信度——即这些遥远的后殖民国家很快都会落入铁幕之后。但这并没有发生。美国在亚洲的联盟体系取得了胜利。\n当今的地缘政治环境\n如今，来自中国的步步紧逼的威胁跨越多个维度，包括安全、经济、信息和技术。但一个不断壮大的非自由主义行为体群体正彼此协同行动，试图削弱美国的地位，使这一威胁变得更加复杂并不断增强。你们对这些行为体并不陌生：俄罗斯、伊朗和朝鲜，以及它们的通道国家古巴、伊拉克、叙利亚和委内瑞拉。例如，表1展示了这些行为体提供的支持范围\n到俄罗斯在乌克兰的战争。中国供应了俄罗斯导弹、坦克和飞机所需微电子产品的近100%。伊朗为俄罗斯供应攻击型无人机、近程弹道导弹和滑翔炸弹等战争中使用的武器作出了重大贡献。朝鲜已提供1.1万至1.2万名士兵、数百枚弹道导弹，如今可能占俄罗斯弹药供应量的高达50%。除这些行为体外，志同道合且有能力的伙伴供应日益减少，相较之下，巴西、印度、印度尼西亚、沙特阿拉伯、南非和阿联酋等“摇摆”国家越来越多；根据议题不同，不能指望它们站在美国一边。\n远程可视化\n改革印太联盟美国必须应对这些挑战并取得胜利，这不能靠单打独斗，而要依靠盟友的支持。没有真正的替代方案。联盟体系过去已证明是一套制胜公式，未来也将再次如此，但前提是进行改革和现代化。对现政府中的一些人而言，更新和现代化这些联盟的必要性源于这样一种判断：它们在经济上对美国代价高昂。如果以贸易和国防支出作为盟友分担负担的两个指标，那么没有任何印太盟友被认为承担了其应有的份额。\n远程可视化\n四项原则应当推动联盟现代化的思维方式：1）印太盟友具备能力，相较过去能够为网络化能力作出更多贡献；2）改变很困难，但印太盟友必须处理以不同方式行事所面临的国内政治阻力；3）盟友需要接受，在现代化努力中增加负担分担是既定要求；4）盟友与美国共同实现成功威慑，是其实现对华商业机会的关键先决条件——也就是说，没有安全，就没有商业机会，只有中国实施的商业胁迫。在印太地区，美国拥有24个常驻基地，并可使用另外20个军事设施。相比之下，中国在海外仅有一个基地，位于吉布提。这些阵地驻有88,500名现役军人，其中约60,000人在日本，约28,500人在韩国。这一存在使美国具备对军事突发事件的快速响应能力；有助于成功威慑针对美国本土及盟友/伙伴的敌对威胁；并提供美国安全保护伞的实体保障，这不仅对和平至关重要，也对推动该地区增长的盈利性商业和投资至关重要。国会在维持这一联盟网络方面发挥着关键作用，包括拨付基地活动资金；制定与基地有关的政策和要求；以及监督行政部门有关基地和联盟关系的政策。\n负担分担\n四项原则应当支撑对盟友负担分担安排的改革。第一，盟国政府必须认识到，传统的货币负担分担算法是半个世纪前的遗产；那时美国的能力远远超过较小伙伴。鉴于伙伴国的财富，如今东道国支持安排中美国年度贡献大于伙伴国贡献的做法已不再合理。第二，东道国支持协议可能可以\n并且应当考虑盟国在双边安全伙伴关系之外、但代表该伙伴关系所作的防务和安全贡献。三、除货币层面外，负担分担应定义为将成本和能力重新导向印度—太平洋地区更广泛的威慑与防务使命。对于一些联盟而言，这已经是事实，但并非所有联盟都如此，而这种情况需要改变。四、改革的一个关键要素是避免意外。必须制定合适的准备期和计划，为东道国提供政治空间，以便对联盟作出可能改变范式的调整。\n\n目前日本和韩国的特别措施协定（SMA）安排遵循一种传统算法，年度增幅分别约为2%和5%至8%。新近缔结的韩国协议（2025年10.5亿美元，2026年11.4亿美元）将于2030年到期；日本协议（2025年约15亿美元）将于2026年到期；每项协议分别覆盖驻韩美军和驻日美军非人员驻扎成本的约40%至50%（韩国）和75%（日本），包括劳务、公用事业、训练、建设和购买当地供应品。\n\n例如，按照“成本加50%”模式谈判新协议，对东道国而言将难以接受，因为这些协议需要立法批准（美国则不需要）。在制定一种更公平的成本分担公式、承认盟国政府的经济能力和雄厚财富时，美国和盟国政府可以同意，在未来协议中将其他非SMA贡献纳入盟国贡献账目。表2列举了日本和韩国过去承担的非SMA成本实例。\n\n远程可视化\n\n盟国进一步分担负担，也可以承认盟国在战区之外对安全所作的贡献。但这也要求修订现有的负担分担协议，恢复盟国对东道国之外美国军事资产的支持（例如韩国）。例如，表3列举了澳大利亚、日本和韩国对乌克兰的贡献。\n\n远程可视化\n\n盟国这种更全面的负担分担定义，应由政府通过《盟国负担分担报告法》和《北约负担分担报告法》等立法予以记录。\n\n准入、基地和飞越（ABO）\n\n美国将在越来越多的受争议基地环境中开展行动。其中一些挑战来自内部，即我们自身的资源限制；另一些与对手的战略有关（即反介入/区域拒止，A2/AD）；还有一些挑战具有政治性质，源于相关东道国的内部情况。然而，确保这些特权对于赋予美军韧性至关重要，包括持续作战能力、补给/维修和储存能力，更不用说支撑可信威慑的关键实体存在。\n\n在这里，我无法提出你们尚未听过的建议。总的来说，我们需要推动在盟国未受到直接威胁的突发情况下使用盟国基地。鉴于对手不断扩大的打击范围和能力，美国必须进一步强化前沿部署基础设施，并增加空中和导弹防御系统。后者可以更多地由盟国分担，包括在该地区增设“萨德”系统。\n\n就日本而言，防务支出的重大增长以及创建\n联合行动指挥部（JJOC）的努力值得称道，并得到了公众的支持。人们期待美国就预先部署新增能力事先同日本协商，并期待东京获得地方社区的支持。日本政府可以借此势头改善联盟的准入安排，使威慑在台湾突发事态中更加可信、防御能力更强。这需要高层发起倡议，在政府内部以及与美国（通过“2+2”部长级会谈）展开全政府层面的对话，讨论修订《驻日美军地位协定》（SOFA）；该协定规范了美国对日本自卫队设施以及永久性美国基地以外的民用设施的有限使用权。目的是简化在遭遇武装攻击之前，美国进入西南（南西）岛链（宫古、石垣、与那国）分散部署地点所需的国家和地方审批程序。东京还必须与地方政府合作，加深它们对自身在国防中所扮演角色的理解（东京对此理解得更好）。此类改革不应被视为对美国的恩惠或让步，而应被视为加强日本在距离台湾最近的南西岛链上的国防与安全所必需的措施。\n\n在韩国，美国进入基地、增添新的非动能能力以及增添动能能力，通常都不会引起争议。关键在于，这些措施要被表述为针对朝鲜威胁。然而，将这些能力表述为针对台湾突发事态，则会变得更棘手，因为韩国一向不愿被卷入美中冲突。在一定程度上，国内政治因素在这里很重要，因为首尔的保守派政府往往对讨论台湾突发事态更为开放一些。还值得注意的是，进步派和保守派政府都通过峰会级声明开辟了新局面，将航行自由和台湾海峡稳定等同于韩国安全。尽管如此，阻力确实存在。美国可以推动下一届韩国政府广泛重新调整驻韩美军的定位，从半岛任务转向区域任务。这里的选项范围可以从将部队撤出半岛、部署到其他地方，到增加在韩驻军规模，并将其中一部分分别用于半岛防御和区域防御。\n\n不过，我想提请你注意一项优化美国ABO选项的重要战略。如果维持准入的战略，是依靠在印度洋—太平洋地区建立由规模较小、分散且隐蔽的安排组成的网络，来补充和/或改革那些受到对手反准入能力威胁的大型主要传统作战基地基础设施，那么这一计划面临的主要威胁，不仅是中国定位并以军事手段打击此类安排的能力，还有中国可以对东道国施加的政治和商业压力，迫使其拒绝与美国合作。事实上，鉴于这些规模较小且分散的ABO安排的性质，商业压力很可能是中国最理想的工具——东道国更可能屈服于中国强大的经济压力，就某一项具体准入安排拒绝与美国合作，而不是在涉及大型军事基地时这样做。中国使用经济胁迫\n针对美国盟友的经济胁迫已经确立。自2008年以来，中国已对18个政府和500多家公司（包括美国主要承包商）实施经济胁迫。胁迫的目的明确是政治性的，而非与贸易有关。与准入能力相关的最著名案例，可能是2016年至2017年间因在韩国城州部署一套“萨德”电池而对韩国企业发动的商业打击，这至少造成了75亿美元的损失。ABO战略的一个重要组成部分，必须是以反胁迫战略补充军事准入安排，旨在遏制中国的经济胁迫。这一战略也称为“集体韧性”，其形式将是一种多边经济威慑机制，利用志同道合伙伴的综合贸易能力向中国发出信号：它们不能在经济上胁迫该集团中的任何一个成员。志同道合的伙伴与中国进行575种制成品和中间产品贸易，价值596.1亿美元；中国高度依赖这些商品，且没有替代来源。1 集体经济威慑战略已被证明在欧洲奏效：2023年推出的反胁迫工具（ACI）似乎减缓了中国对其他单个国家实施的胁迫行为，就像其对立陶宛所做的那样。美国应明确鼓励印度—太平洋盟友采取同样做法，并理解其中存在与ABO相关的重要安全理由。机会主义侵略俄乌战争使印度—太平洋盟友意识到，亚洲发生战争是真实可能，难以想象的事情也可能发生。该地区对双重突发事件和机会主义侵略的主动思考，比我以往任何时候经历的都更多。例如，过去三年中，台湾和韩国都曾询问，是否可以在二轨层面推演双重突发事件。更新后的联盟结构应利用这种新思维，主动考虑威慑机会主义侵略的新方式。这些方式可以包括但不限于：重新调整美国在朝鲜半岛的军事存在，将任务转向区域威慑与防御（通过缩减规模或予以补充）；对驻韩美军进行半岛内外的训练；美国、日本和韩国之间发表集体防御声明，以及日本和韩国之间发表双边安全声明（2025年是两国关系正常化60周年）；增加韩国防务支出和独立军事能力，以在台湾突发事态期间威慑朝鲜的机会主义侵略；考虑韩国在台湾突发事态中承担后方支援角色；利用联合国军（UNC）出兵国框架，加强朝鲜半岛的和平时期威慑。这些都是韩国的政治敏感议题，待该国的政治弹劾危机解决后，应与政府讨论。无论如何，任何对美国在半岛存在的重新调整，都需要认真考虑保留一定规模的地面部队，以提供传统的“绊线”威慑，防止朝鲜冒险主义；并且需要一个有计划、分阶段的进程，以尽量减少突然变化所导致的负面政治和经济外部影响。完全撤出驻军将会\n成本问题（失去韩国补贴），以及仍足够接近战场、能够发挥作用的替代地点。增加驻军数量（其中一部分分别接受半岛防御和地区防御训练）可以压低韩国可能采取的自助回应（例如主动讨论核武装）的声音，但这将要求韩国作出艰难的政治选择，承认其在台海冲突中的作用。\n\n盟友合作的新领域\n联盟更新应包括军事领域之外的其他新合作领域。前两届政府已经在经济安全、出口管制和供应链方面的盟友合作上树立了有力先例。在现政府任内，已经发掘出包括与日本和韩国开展造船及战略能源合作在内的新机遇。\n\n在鲁比奥国务卿于本机构举行的提名听证会上，他指出，第二次世界大战结束八十年后，美国再次被要求从混乱中创造一个自由世界，但他也指出，选民之所以选举特朗普总统，是因为他们希望美国强大，并奉行“审慎”的外交政策。在这方面，鉴于联合国安理会等传统机构表现不佳，而金砖国家等集团意图扰乱当前以规则为基础的秩序，国务院应鼓励我们在印太地区和欧洲的盟友发挥更积极的政治作用，以维持全球治理。我在此提出两项具体建议。第一，美国应鼓励欧洲和亚洲盟友开展更直接的安全对话和军事规划，在特朗普和拜登政府此前努力围绕出口管制、制裁和大流行病防范等职能性议题建立多边联盟的基础上继续推进。乌克兰战争、中国日益强硬的姿态以及朝鲜向俄罗斯提供物资，以前所未有的方式将两个战区联系起来；欧洲在印太安全问题讨论中表现出的兴趣程度和专业素养也给我留下了深刻印象。\n\n最后，国务院应积极推动并正式确立“印太四国”框架（日本、韩国、澳大利亚、新西兰）参与全球治理机构。定期参加北约领导人峰会，已经促成欧洲和亚洲在应对中国胁迫方面开展更多合作。此外，在联合国授权机制无法正常运作的情况下，七国集团（G7）已成为事实上的组织，由主要志同道合国家协调政策、制定制裁措施并确立新的规则和规范。澳大利亚、韩国和西班牙等印太及欧洲参与者，在近期七国集团领导人峰会确定的许多紧迫任务上都已证明表现出色，例如经济韧性与安全、数字竞争力、粮食安全、气候变化、劳工安全和发展，因此可以为分担负担和全球治理作出重大贡献。2请查阅PDF以获取参考资料。","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-03-26T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2293","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"权力转移：美国对外援助的下一阶段","title_en":"Shifting Power: The Next Phase of U.S. Foreign Assistance","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）评论文章，为三部分系列的第一篇。作者Hadeil Ali与Andrew Friedman分析特朗普第二任期削减对外援助、解散美国国际开发署（USAID）后的形势，梳理对殖民权力结构、本地化议程落实不力及官僚低效的批评，指出仅约2.1%的援助资金直接或间接流向本地组织，主张新援助模式应以受援社区为中心、真正转移权力与决策权。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary, the first in a three-part series. Authors Hadeil Ali and Andrew Friedman analyze the landscape after the second Trump administration's cuts to U.S. foreign assistance and the dismantling of USAID. They review criticisms of colonial power dynamics, poorly implemented localization, and bureaucratic inefficiency, noting that only about 2.1 percent of donor funds reach local organizations, and argue that a new model must center affected communities and genuinely shift power and decisionmaking.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:11:18.066497+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/shifting-power-next-phase-us-foreign-assistance","content":"Shifting Power: The Next Phase of U.S. Foreign Assistance\nPhoto: MIGUEL GARCIA SAAVED/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nHadeil Ali\nand\nAndrew Friedman\nPublished October 7, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nThis analysis is Part I of a three-part series covering the need and urgency to center rightsholders in the future of U.S. foreign assistance. In a moment of tremendous flux, these commentaries offer analysis and recommendations for a new model rooted in local consultation and input from those most impacted by foreign assistance. Read Part II of the series here. IntroductionThe beginning of the second Trump administration rapidly entailed cuts to U.S. foreign assistance, including the dismantling of the U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID). The administration cited budgetary and programmatic inefficiencies, the spread of a “woke” agenda, and the lack of return on investment to U.S. strategic interests. In announcing the dissolution of USAID, Secretary of State Marco Rubio described that the administration’s goal was to make “America safer, stronger, and more prosperous.” This rollback is not unique to the United States. Other major Western donors have decreased their official development assistance (ODA) in favor of other priorities.Traditional recipients of U.S. foreign assistance have not stood back and waited for a return to large-scale foreign assistance. Instead, they have worked to imagine what a post-foreign assistance world could look like. “We are called to build systems that do more than respond to crises—we must build systems that generate resilience, produce equity, and amplify dignity.” These are the words of President John Dramani Mahama at the historic Africa Health Sovereignty Summit in Accra this past June. While the comment was intended to specifically address the need for Africa to drive the best vision for its own health ecosystem, the underlying sentiment is the need for a new model that is equitable, just, and sustainable.For decades, the U.S. foreign assistance ecosystem has suffered deep structural critiques from the design of its programs to the efficiency of the overall model and the resistance to a more local agenda. The recent USAID closure and international drawback of ODA have injected new urgency into a conversation around the inefficiencies of the previous model from both Western and Global South countries. President Trump’s U.S.-Africa policy approach, rooted in “trade, not aid,” has been framed through peace, stability, and mutual prosperity. This framing has resonated with some across the Global South who have perceived the aid and development structure historically as extractive and colonial.Areas of CriticismColonial Power Dynamics: International nongovernmental organizations (NGOs) and donor agencies have often designed and implemented programs without genuine community consultation, missing key opportunities to ensure a localized lens. While this issue can be attributed to an operational structure, the real question is whether organizations have the full intent and willingness to shift the power structures. David Beasly, the former executive director of the World Food Programme (WFP), has said that the success of aid programs and institutions like WFP depends on their not existing anymore. The challenge is whether these entities are designed to address the root causes and end the cycle of continuous aid.Poorly Defined and Implemented Localized Agenda: U.S. commitments on localization have spanned administrations of both parties. Locally led development was an “underlying principle strongly reflected in the Bush administration’s creation of the Millennium Challenge Corporation (MCC) and the President’s Emergency Program for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR), even though it was not codified or articulated as a stand-alone policy by the administration.” Subsequent administrations have been far more explicit in their commitments, with the Obama administration setting a 30 percent goal, the Biden administration setting a 25 percent goal that would balloon to 50 percent by 2030, and the first Trump administration pioneering the “journey to self-reliance” that worked with local partners from government, civil society and the private sector to reduce dependency on U.S. assistance and improve its capacity to “solve its own development challenges.” There have also been major international efforts, including the Paris Declaration on Aid Effectiveness and the Grand Bargain agreement to adopt a locally led development agenda, but progress has been limited.Despite these efforts, only 2.1 percent of donor funds currently reach local organizations directly or indirectly. The limited progress can be attributed to several factors. What is considered a local actor has often been defined by Western donors and entities, which prevents an inclusive and holistic approach. The challenge is not just in the definitions, but also in the accountability structures. Typically, aid bureaucracies are accountable to donor governments for the usage of taxpayer money, not to the recipient communities, creating a deep imbalance of power. The resistance to localization is also rooted at times in the framing of local actors as corrupt and incapable and international or Western organizations as efficient and capable, creating a false dichotomy of where the knowledge and capabilities sit.Bureaucratic Inefficiencies and Complexities: Foreign assistance programs have been criticized for having too many components, objectives, and stakeholders, making them hard to implement and evaluate. From a budgetary standpoint, USAID and other similar organizations have come under attack for the overhead and indirect costs. USAID’s implementing partners can charge 30–50 percent of budgets as overhead. The high operational costs of international operations—including the salaries and benefits of expatriate staff—have undermined cost-effectiveness and perceptions of value. Global studies have suggested that local organizations could deliver programming that is 32 percent more cost-efficient than international organizations that bring large overhead and salary costs. This issue has been used to discredit development institutions and highlight how much Western entities benefit at the expense of resources reaching the most vulnerable. The story is a nuanced one, but what remains true is the opportunity to build a leaner and more efficient system.While it came at the expense of institutional dismantling, budget cuts, resource depletion, and the rapid and chaotic nature led to untold pain, including deaths, this moment creates an opportunity to view aid differently and consider aid recipients as true partners with vision, priorities, and agency.This moment of tremendous flux creates an existential question for development practitioners. That is, will the same model move forward, content to do “less with less,” or will a new model take hold that aims to maximize the available resources through a new approach? While the authors would posit the importance of the latter, either effort requires considerable consultation and input from those most impacted by foreign assistance.Less with LessThe impacts of U.S. foreign assistance cuts were immediately felt; however, the long-term impacts are still taking shape. Recent survey data from the International Foundation for Electoral Systems puts a fine point on the current and future gap. Of the civil society organizations surveyed, 87 percent had already been able to find some new source of funding; however, only 5 percent reported that the funding was able to fill the gap. More than a third of the organizations surveyed did not believe they would be able to provide the same services two to three years down the line. Further polling from Accountability Lab has more than half of respondents have laid off employees, making the same level of work difficult to envision, if not untenable. In short, no new funding stream is coming to fill the hole left by U.S. retrenchment.That reality, where less work is feasible, requires prioritization. When not all issues can be addressed, it is necessary to determine which issues will take precedence. The realities of prioritization have always been a complex element of international development. Mixed constituencies, including the populations of donor states, and various industries and ecosystems of implementing nonprofits, companies, consultants, and government agencies, muddle the needs, demands, and priorities of impacted populations. This issue is far starker when the total funding is both far smaller and far less predictable.A renewed effort to center the development priorities of local groups and host states protects this low and unpredictable funding. To start with, it improves development outcomes where they are most needed, in areas local populations understand to be the most impactful. Additionally, they are protected against foreign assistance’s unpredictable future because programming that addresses local priorities is the most likely to be taken up by host governments if assistance funding should falter. While a large development project whose selection and design were driven by international consultants may be discontinued if donor country funding priorities change, smaller projects that are asked for and codesigned with local populations are far more likely to be prioritized and funded locally should international funding fall away.In one representative example, USAID and Ghana worked with 10 representatives of the Ghanaian government to fund, pilot, and design a case management system that would improve the efficiency of the Ghanaian judiciary. The system was not only needed, with previous iterations of Ghanaian court management done in an inefficient, analog manner, but was lauded by individuals affiliated with the Ghanaian courts. When the program ended, the Ghanaian government took over management of the system, continuing its important work.New ModelIt is outside the scope of this article to suggest what a new model for foreign assistance might look like. Many discussions and research efforts are currently ongoing to build and develop capabilities that improve the lives of individuals across the globe through ODA, remittances, private sector intervention, and others. However, the vision of a new model necessarily envisions improved outcomes or greater return on investment. Given trends across the globe, the likely reality is that ODA will not return to previous highs for the foreseeable future. That means that any new model would likely have less funding than previous iterations of foreign assistance delivery.Taken together, this asks for improved results despite a smaller budget. In contrast to the “less for less” reality above, a new model, in whatever its form, would ask for at least “the same for less” or, more likely, “more for less.” This cannot be accomplished using the same approaches as before.Despite the challenges outlined above, localization remains one of the most effective means for improving efficiency and development outcomes. As mentioned previously, it is estimated that funneling foreign assistance through local organizations is 32 percent more efficient than utilizing international NGOs. Another analysis estimates that “redirecting emergency aid through local organizations, such as local churches, can cut costs by half and produce better results.”Previous efforts have failed in part because they have focused on shifting funding, not shifting power and decisionmaking. The strategy determinations have still largely been made in donor countries. As one analyst described, “it is about what they are willing to give away, and when, and on what terms.” Without an agenda that truly takes into account the needs and priorities of the most impacted populations, the localization agenda will continue to be rife with inefficiencies and redundancies, as well as significant administrative burdens, all of which stand in the way of full realization of the savings and efficiencies that could come through large-scale localization. Whether the goal is improved prioritization or the greater efficiency of localization, local actors should be a part of planning from the start and at all stages.Hadeil Ali is the chief of staff of the Global Development Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Andrew Friedman is a senior fellow in the Human Rights Initiative at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nImage\nHadeil Ali\nChief of Staff, Global Development Department\nImage\nAndrew Friedman\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Human Rights Initiative\nPrograms & Projects\nBuilding Sustainable and Inclusive Democracy\nHuman Rights Initiative\nGlobal Development\nDiversity and Leadership in International Affairs Program","content_cn":"权力转移：美国对外援助的下一阶段\n照片：MIGUEL GARCIA SAAVED/Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nHadeil Ali\n和\nAndrew Friedman\n发表于2025年10月7日\n远程可视化\n本分析是三篇系列文章的第一部分，讨论未来美国对外援助需要并迫切要将权利持有者置于核心位置。在剧烈变化的时刻，这些评论为一种新模式提供分析和建议：该模式植根于地方协商，并听取受对外援助影响最深的人们的意见。点击此处阅读本系列第二部分。\n引言\n第二届特朗普政府上台之初，便迅速削减美国对外援助，包括拆解美国国际开发署（USAID）。政府以预算和项目效率低下、“觉醒”议程扩散，以及对美国战略利益缺乏投资回报为由。在宣布解散USAID时，国务卿马可·鲁比奥表示，政府的目标是让“美国更安全、更强大、更繁荣”。这种回撤并非美国独有。其他主要西方捐助方也减少了官方发展援助（ODA），转而支持其他优先事项。\n美国对外援助的传统受援方并没有袖手旁观，等待大规模对外援助回归。相反，他们一直在设想一个后对外援助世界可能是什么样子。“我们被召唤去建设不仅能应对危机的体系——我们必须建设能够产生韧性、实现公平并放大尊严的体系。”这是总统约翰·德拉马尼·马哈马今年六月在阿克拉举行的历史性非洲卫生主权峰会上的讲话。虽然这番话具体是针对非洲需要为自身卫生生态系统确立最佳愿景，但其 underlying sentiment 是需要一种公平、公正且可持续的新模式。\n几十年来，从项目设计到整体模式的效率，再到对更加地方化议程的抗拒，美国对外援助生态系统一直遭受深刻的结构性批评。近期USAID关闭以及国际社会对ODA的撤回，使西方国家和全球南方国家都更加迫切地重新讨论旧模式的低效问题。特朗普总统以“贸易，而非援助”为核心的美国对非政策路径，被赋予和平、稳定和共同繁荣的框架。这一框架在全球南方一些地区引起共鸣，因为他们历来认为援助和发展结构具有攫取性和殖民性。\n批评领域\n殖民主义权力动态：国际非政府组织（NGO）和捐助机构往往在没有真正与社区协商的情况下设计并实施项目，错失了确保地方化视角的关键机会。虽然这一问题可以归因于运营结构，但真正的问题在于，这些组织是否有充分的意图和意愿去改变权力结构。世界粮食计划署（WFP）前执行主任戴维·比斯利曾表示，援助项目以及WFP等机构取得成功的标志，在于它们最终不再存在。挑战在于，这些实体的设计是否旨在解决根本原因，并终结持续援助的循环。\n定义和实施不佳的地方化议程：美国关于地方化的承诺已经涵盖了\n两党政府。地方主导的发展是“布什政府创建千年挑战公司（MCC）和总统防治艾滋病紧急救援计划（PEPFAR）中强烈体现的一项基本原则，尽管该原则并未被该政府编纂或表述为一项独立政策。”后续政府在其承诺方面明确得多：奥巴马政府设定了30%的目标，拜登政府设定了25%的目标，并计划到2030年扩大到50%；第一届特朗普政府则率先提出“走向自力更生”，与来自政府、民间社会和私营部门的地方伙伴合作，减少对美国援助的依赖，并提升其“解决自身发展挑战”的能力。国际社会也开展了重大努力，包括《援助成效巴黎宣言》和旨在采纳地方主导发展议程的《大交易》协议，但进展有限。尽管开展了这些努力，目前只有2.1%的捐助方资金直接或间接到达地方组织。进展有限可归因于若干因素。何谓地方行动者，往往由西方捐助方和实体界定，这阻碍了包容且全面的方法。挑战不仅在于定义，也在于问责结构。通常，援助官僚机构对捐助国政府负责，负责纳税人资金的使用，而不是对受援社区负责，从而造成严重的权力失衡。对本土化的抵制有时还源于这样的框架：将地方行动者视为腐败且无能，将国际或西方组织视为高效且有能力，从而制造出一种关于知识和能力归属何处的错误二分法。\n\n官僚效率低下与复杂性：外国援助项目一直受到批评，被认为包含过多组成部分、目标和利益相关者，难以实施和评估。从预算角度看，美国国际开发署（USAID）及其他类似组织因管理费用和间接成本而受到抨击。USAID的实施伙伴可将预算的30%至50%计作管理费用。国际行动的高昂运营成本——包括外籍员工的薪酬和福利——削弱了成本效益和价值认知。全球研究表明，地方组织开展项目的成本效率可能比承担高额管理费用和薪资成本的国际组织高32%。这一问题被用来诋毁发展机构，并凸显西方实体如何以最脆弱群体无法获得资源为代价从中获益。事实颇为复杂，但可以确定的是，我们有机会建立一个更精简、更高效的体系。\n\n尽管这一时刻付出了机构解体、预算削减和资源枯竭的代价，其快速且混乱的性质带来了难以估量的痛苦，包括死亡，但这一时刻创造了以不同方式看待援助的机会，并将援助接受者视为拥有愿景、优先事项和行动能力的真正伙伴。\n\n这一巨大动荡的时刻给发展领域从业者提出了一个关乎生存的问题。也就是说，是继续沿用同一模式，满足于“用更少做更少”，还是会有一种旨在通过新的\n方法？尽管作者会提出后者的重要性，但无论采取哪种努力，都需要与受外国援助影响最大的人群进行大量协商并听取他们的意见。\n\n以更少应对更少\n美国削减对外援助的影响立即显现；然而，长期影响仍在逐步形成。国际选举制度基金会近期的调查数据明确指出了当前及未来的缺口。在接受调查的公民社会组织中，87%的组织已经找到了一些新的资金来源；然而，只有5%的组织表示这些资金能够填补缺口。超过三分之一的受访组织认为，两到三年后它们将无法提供同样的服务。问责实验室的进一步调查显示，超过一半的受访者已经裁员，这使得维持同等工作水平变得难以想象，即便不是完全无法持续。简而言之，没有新的资金渠道会来填补美国收缩留下的空缺。\n\n在工作量较少可行的现实下，必须进行优先排序。当无法处理所有问题时，就有必要确定哪些问题应优先处理。优先排序的现实一直是国际发展的复杂要素。多元的利益群体，包括捐助国民众，以及执行援助项目的非营利组织、公司、顾问和政府机构所处的各种行业与生态系统，使受影响群体的需求、要求和优先事项变得混乱。当总体资金既大幅减少又远不具可预测性时，这一问题更加突出。\n\n重新努力将发展重点置于当地组织和东道国的优先事项之上，可以保护这笔低水平且不可预测的资金。首先，这会在最需要的地方改善发展成果，也就是当地民众认为影响最大的领域。此外，这些项目还能抵御外国援助不可预测的未来，因为如果援助资金出现动摇，解决当地优先事项的项目最有可能被东道国政府接手。一个由国际顾问主导选择和设计的大型发展项目，可能会因捐助国资金优先事项改变而被终止；而那些由当地民众提出要求并与其共同设计的小型项目，如果国际资金消失，更有可能在当地获得优先支持和资金。\n\n在一个具有代表性的例子中，美国国际开发署与加纳政府的10名代表合作，为加纳司法系统资助、试点并设计了一套案件管理系统，以提高加纳司法机构的效率。该系统不仅确有必要——此前加纳法院管理以低效的纸笔方式进行——还获得了与加纳法院有关人士的高度赞誉。项目结束后，加纳政府接管了该系统的管理，继续开展其重要工作。\n\n新模式\n\n提出外国援助新模式应是什么样子，超出了本文的范围。目前，许多讨论和研究工作正在进行之中，旨在通过官方发展援助、汇款、私营部门干预等方式，建设和发展能够改善全球各地个人生活的能力。然而，新模式的愿景必然要求取得更好的成果或实现更高的投资回报。鉴于全球各地的趋势，可能的现实是，官方发展援助将\n在可预见的未来不会回到以往的高点。这意味着，任何新模式获得的资金都可能少于以往几轮外国援助交付模式。综合来看，这要求在预算更少的情况下取得更好的成果。与上述“少给少得”的现实相反，无论新模式采取何种形式，都至少要求“少花钱办同样的事”，更可能是“少花钱办更多的事”。这无法通过沿用以前的做法来实现。\n\n尽管面临上述挑战，本地化仍然是提高效率和发展成果的最有效手段之一。如前所述，据估计，通过本地组织输送外国援助，比利用国际非政府组织高效32%。另一项分析估计，“通过本地组织（如当地教会）重新分配紧急援助，可以将成本削减一半，并取得更好的成果。”\n\n以往的努力之所以失败，部分原因在于它们着眼于转移资金，而不是转移权力和决策权。战略决策仍主要在捐助国作出。正如一位分析人士所说，“关键在于他们愿意放弃什么、何时放弃，以及以什么条件放弃。”如果没有真正考虑受影响最严重人群的需求和优先事项的议程，本地化议程将继续充斥低效和重复，以及沉重的行政负担；所有这些都阻碍了充分实现大规模本地化可能带来的节省和效率。无论目标是改善优先事项排序，还是提高本地化效率，地方行动者都应从一开始并在所有阶段参与规划。\n\nHadeil Ali 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）全球发展部主任。Andrew Friedman 是CSIS人权倡议项目的高级研究员。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私立免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n图片\nHadeil Ali\n全球发展部主任\n图片\nAndrew Friedman\n人权倡议项目主任兼高级研究员\n项目与计划\n建设可持续且包容的民主\n人权倡议\n全球发展\n国际事务多元化与领导力项目","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-10-07T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2596","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"潮水转向：美国太平洋收缩的国家安全影响","title_en":"Shifting Tides: The National Security Implications of the United States’ Pacific Drawdown","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）报告，作者Charles Edel、Kathryn Paik与John Augé。报告指出，尽管美国自称“太平洋大国”，但USAID裁撤、对外援助暂停、对太平洋岛国加征关税及气候议题降级，使地区国家质疑美国承诺，为中国扩大影响力创造空间。报告建议通过多边机制、国会立法及强化与澳日等伙伴合作，维持美国在该地区的存在与信誉。","summary_en":"A CSIS report by Charles Edel, Kathryn Paik, and John Augé. It argues that despite U.S. claims to be a 'Pacific power,' the dismantling of USAID, the pause in foreign assistance, tariffs on Pacific island economies, and the deprioritization of climate change have led regional states to doubt U.S. commitment, opening space for China. It recommends sustained multilateral engagement, congressional legislation, and deeper cooperation with partners such as Australia and Japan to preserve U.S. presence and credibility.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:30:14.807630+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/shifting-tides","content":"Shifting Tides: The National Security Implications of the United States’ Pacific Drawdown\nPhoto: DeAgostini via Getty Images; Safin Hamid/AFP via Getty Images; Dahkareo Pritchett via DVIDS\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nReport\nby\nCharles Edel,\nKathryn Paik,\nand\nJohn Augé\nPublished July 22, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Full Report\n1758kb\nThis page was updated on August 14, 2025, to adjust the USAID cuts as a share of GNI in select Pacific Island Countries.For the last decade, the United States has maintained that it is a “Pacific power,” and multiple administrations have affirmed the importance of the vast Pacific Islands region to U.S. national security. Increased engagement with the Pacific, which began under the first Trump administration, has broad bipartisan support as a security priority. As the first 100 days of the second Trump administration drew to an end, rhetoric coming out of the administration echoed this trend, with Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth visiting Guam and Hawaii on his first trip to the Indo-Pacific, during which he stressed the critical nature of Pacific U.S. territories at the “tip of America’s spear” of deterrence.However, Pacific leaders have consistently stated that they look to actions, and not just words, as proof of a nation’s commitments. Here, recent cuts to the United States Agency for International Development (USAID), the pausing of U.S. foreign assistance, enacting tariffs on small and vulnerable island economies, and deprioritizing climate change—the top priority for the region—have had a decidedly negative impact.Regardless of U.S. intent, the perception across the Pacific of the United States as a disinterested and mercurial partner undercuts the country’s ability to build meaningful and lasting partnerships in the Pacific and simultaneously opens space for China to displace U.S. influence and presence in this critical region.In a region that has quietly become a stage for great power competition, this perception of U.S. disinterest not only opens new entry points for Beijing’s influence but also puts wind in the sails of a new narrative: that it is China, not the United States, that is the committed partner and protector of global rules and norms. While it is too soon to understand the full implications of the United States’ recent actions, early signs in the Pacific suggest that the trajectory is counter to U.S. interests. Reversing this trend will require quick action, sincere engagement, and creative diplomacy. An Emerging Stage for Great Power CompetitionThe United States has long engaged with the Pacific Islands region for trade, access to Asia, and security purposes. While U.S. engagement in the Pacific was arguably deepest during World War II—a period that proved without a doubt the strategic importance of the region—U.S. engagement both predates that war and has persisted since. Building and maintaining strong relationships with the many nations that make up this broad swath of the globe has always been in the national interest of the United States, but maintaining this hard-fought presence and influence requires demonstrating the value of such a partnership. Yet, in the post–World War II era, Washington’s attention to the region was periodic at best—and dominated primarily by defense and security agreements.\nRemote Visualization\nIslands in the Pacific Ocean.\nPhoto: comersis.com/Wikipedia/via CC BY-SA 4.0\nOver the last decade, China has increased both its interest in and its capacity to deepen relationships across what it views as a highly strategic region—one in which a permanent Chinese presence would significantly alter the U.S. and allied strategic calculus. Largely in response, the United States began to ramp up its Pacific engagement under the first Trump administration with the hosting of three Pacific leaders at the White House and the establishment of a team to renegotiate the Compacts of Free Association (COFA) with the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM), the Republic of the Marshall Islands (RMI), and the Republic of Palau. The agreement has direct security benefits for the United States as it allows for unilateral U.S. defense access across the north Pacific, from the Philippines to Hawaii. The Biden administration accelerated this trend, twice hosting the Pacific Islands Forum (PIF) leaders for a summit at the White House, launching the first-ever U.S. government strategy for the Pacific Islands, opening several new embassies, increasing USAID presence across the Pacific, and completing the critical COFA agreements.An overarching characteristic of this reengagement was relationship building, demonstrating a growing appreciation in Washington for the importance the Pacific places on consensus, mutual respect, and dialogue—also called the “Pacific Way.” But building and maintaining relationships—a prerequisite for influence—requires not just sincere engagement, but also follow-through. As 2024 ended, several commitments from the recent U.S.-PIF summits were incomplete, and 2024 concluded with many Pacific nations still skeptical that the United States was truly back as a committed partner—and still questioning the value of U.S. engagement versus that of an ever-present and willing China. Soft Power in the Service of Hard PowerWhile the United States has never been the largest donor in the Pacific, it has become increasingly clear in recent years that meeting the region’s needs while effectively countering China’s influence requires coordinated and sustained efforts by all likeminded allies and partners.The United States was the fifth-largest aid provider to the Pacific between 2008 and 2022, behind Australia, China, Japan, and New Zealand, according to the Lowy Institute’s Pacific Aid Map. In that period, the United States spent $3.4 billion and committed $4.4 billion to the region, although much of this was through the COFA agreements. Beyond COFA, U.S. assistance has been a more limited, but still critical, component of funding to the vulnerable region. For example, in 2022, the Solomon Islands received the most of any non-COFA country from the United States at $24.6 million, but that was still dwarfed by Australia’s $116 million and China’s $40 million spent in the Solomon Islands that same year. In 2023, outside of COFA funds, the United States provided $57.6 million in development assistance to the region. This money went toward a range of areas, including supporting education, infrastructure, climate initiatives, disaster response, and more.\nRemote Visualization\nTraditionally, USAID has been the primary implementer of U.S. foreign assistance programs, but select programs are also executed by the U.S. Department of State. For example, in 2014, the department implemented roughly $4.4 billion of the total $12 billion allocated for economic assistance (USAID implemented $5.4 billion). On 30 June, USAID as an independent agency was officially shuttered. Responsibility for USAID-related development now falls under the Department of State; however, the Trump administration plans to cut 90 percent of USAID programs, and currently almost all USAID and State Department foreign assistance programming has been indefinitely paused pending a full review (the initial 90-day review deadline has been pushed back twice). Cutting 90 percent of projects will entail eliminating 5,800 out of 6,200 multi-year USAID contract awards globally, resulting in a $54 billion reduction, along with cutting 4,100 out of 9,100 State Department grants globally, leading to a $4.4 billion decrease in programmatic funding.Programs with a climate change nexus—considered a top national security threat for the Pacific Islands—are most likely to be axed. For example, a $2.5 million grant for the Pacific Islands Ambassador Self-Help Small Grants Program, which included tackling climate change, has already been terminated, as was a $905,487 grant to support the Pacific Islands Emergency Management Alliance. Taken in combination with the U.S. withdrawal from the Paris Agreement, U.S. credibility as a global leader on a climate change or clean energy has been significantly undercut.Given the extent to which USAID programming was a central part of U.S. engagement in the Pacific, small island nations have been hit particularly hard by these pauses and cuts. While specific figures on the amount of funding cuts per region are not available, the overall effects to the Pacific are significant. According to the Center for Global Development, these cuts amount to 100 percent of USAID country programming for Papua New Guinea, Vanuatu, Palau, Fiji, and the Solomon Islands. Even in the Compact states, some critical funding lies outside of COFA and has been affected. For example, the Palau Red Cross Society has cited the loss of nearly $600,000 in humanitarian aid due to USAID cuts. As one local project manager in Papua New Guinea stated shortly after the initial announcement in late January, the team was “devastated” by the freeze of HIV and health related projects.\nRemote Visualization\nThe effects are not limited to recipient countries: Fellow donor partners and allies are affected as well. Countries such as Australia, which has a deep appreciation of the importance of development assistance to its own national security, are racing to assess the extent of the fallout and recalibrate their own work in the region. The logic behind these changes is that U.S. foreign assistance spending should be in support of U.S. national security objectives. That premise, in and of itself, is not flawed. However, the method of first fully halting and then proceeding with a full review not only deprived partners of valued programming—with some particularly harmful results—but also fed into the narrative of the United States as a fickle, unreliable partner. Such measures weaken, not bolster, U.S. national security by diluting U.S. presence, influence, and relationships around the world.  Tariffs: The One-Two PunchWhile halting the majority of USAID and State Department foreign assistance programs will no doubt have a larger tangible effect on the everyday lives of Pacific Islanders, the “Liberation Day” tariff announcements also caused shock and confusion in a region that has long looked to the United States as global economic leader with which countries sought closer trade ties. The high rates on these small and often struggling economies—where in some islands over 25 percent of the population lives below the poverty line (see Figure 4)—appeared disconnected with both reality and common sense. The United States has a trade surplus with all but two of the Pacific Islands—Fiji ($208 million trade deficit) and Tuvalu ($168,000 trade deficit), the latter of which has a population of around 11,000—and yet almost none escaped liberation day unscathed.\nRemote Visualization\nThe U.S. government has framed these tariffs as both an attempt to level the playing field and as a tool for negotiation. Neither of these justifications makes sense for the Pacific. As small island states with limited markets and opportunities, trade plays an outsized role in bolstering small economies—and yet, overall, U.S.-Pacific trade is almost insignificant on the U.S. ledger. U.S. two-way trade with the Pacific Islands totals around $1 billion, just a small fraction of the more than $7 trillion in global trade conducted by the United States. In other words, there is no “leveling of the playing field” between, for instance, Tuvalu and the United States.\nRemote Visualization\nNeither does the idea that tariffs are a bargaining chip with the Pacific hold strategic logic. Not only do Pacific countries have little to bargain with, but after the imposition of tariffs, it remains unclear what benefit they would accrue from entering such negotiations. Even factoring in ramped-up U.S. engagement in recent years, the United States remains far behind other countries in terms of what it contributes to the region (see Figure 1), a point which is further underscored by Washington’s exiting from the Paris Agreement and shuttering of most of its development assistance programming. Rather than offering limited inducements, many of these pragmatic countries will increasingly look to other partners and markets. As Fijian Prime Minister Rabuka recently stated, “The world is bigger than the United States. . . . We don’t have anything to counter with so we will have to weather the storm and roll with the punches.”\nRemote Visualization\nImplications for U.S. National SecurityU.S. engagement with the Pacific has never simply been blind support for partners and allies, nor even solely of bolstering these small democratic states. It has always had a strong strategic rationale, and that is as true now as it was in World War II.And yet U.S. actions are not just leaving space for China to fill. They are also elevating the narrative of China as a responsible, predictable global partner. As the United States eliminates every argument for the Pacific to look to it over China, Beijing has ramped up its carrot-and-stick diplomacy throughout the region. In a side-by-side comparison, the United States was already less present and offering less than China by almost every metric. There had, however, remained in the Pacific a persistent discomfort with the manner of China’s engagement, with the sense that, ultimately, Chinese interest, at the expense of the Pacific, would always win out. But as the United States reduces programming in the region and China demonstrates its ability to evolve into a reliable partner, this narrative is shifting.Beijing has been quick to capitalize on negative perceptions of Washington and amplify its own messaging of China as the great global stabilizer. Shortly after the global U.S. tariffs were announced, China’s ambassador to Papua New Guinea, Yang Xiaoguang, called U.S. tariffs “economic bullying” and emphasized that China, as Papua New Guinea’s “most reliable partner,” would remain consistent and open to trade with the island nation. That same week, China’s ambassador to Fiji reinforced the same message, emphasizing that China remained committed to enhancing trade and investment ties with Fiji, despite external trade and tariff tensions.China has been focusing much of its Pacific ramp-up on Fiji. Total two-way trade between Fiji and China increased from just $145.2 million in 2010 to $532.2 million in 2023, and in 2024 the two agreed to a $300 million infrastructure development project through the Belt and Road Initiative to construct 22 bridges and improve roads on Vanua Levu. China announced in April 2025 that it would establish direct flights between Shanghai and Nadi, Fiji. In making the announcement, Chinese ambassador to Fiji Zhou Jian noted that the U.S. trade war could present an opportunity for enhancing China’s trade with Fiji. The subtext, of course, is that China is ready and willing to strengthen engagement across a range of sectors in contrast to perceived U.S. withdrawal. Further reinforcing this message, in late May 2025, China hosted the third China-Pacific Island Countries Foreign Ministers’ Meeting in Xiamen, Fujian Province, bringing together senior officials from 11 Pacific Island countries. At this meeting—the first time the Pacific Foreign Ministers have gathered in China—China announced plans to launch over 100 climate-related projects for the region.As a Republican professional staff member of the U.S. House Committee on Armed Services said at an April 2025 committee hearing on military posture and national security challenges in the Indo-Pacific, “we have built very strong relationships…and this has been the greatest thing that has really frustrated China, it’s now surrounded by a group of nations that don’t want to be bullied by China, so they’re looking to us for support . . . and now we are trying to out bully China . . . and show [our partners] that we can punch them just as hard as China can punch them.” The drivers behind this multifaceted push by China are clear. For Beijing, the Pacific presents an exceptionally low-cost, high-yield opportunity. For relatively small monetary commitments, China can pursue major strategic, diplomatic, and economic gains. Strategically, Chinese activity in the Pacific has the dual benefit of pursuing its own potential foothold while forcing the United States, Australia, and others to block Chinese influence everywhere at once. Some have likened this to a game of whack-a-mole, where the United States along with other likeminded countries must work to maintain presence everywhere, whereas China only needs to find one security foothold to drastically alter the calculus of its adversaries. Whatever the game is called, it is clear that it is being played to Beijing’s advantage. And with the United States looking like it might be stepping away from the region, the cost-benefit calculus for China just became that much more compelling.RecommendationsIn the Pacific, where relationships are the currency of influence, the perception of the United States as a disinterested partner is steadily increasing. Making up this perception gap will require careful diplomacy—and real action—that is aimed at the Pacific, instead of relegating the region to collateral damage in sweeping executive orders. To ensure it does not lose further ground in this critical arena, the United States should embrace the opportunity to emphasize its commitment to the region through the following actions:Embrace the use of soft power for hard objectives. As the Department of State completes its review of foreign assistance, it should ensure that proper weight is placed on the importance of aligning with Pacific priorities, as that is the most effective way to build and maintain these critical relationships.The business-minded, transactional approach to international relations risks promoting a zero-sum mentality, which hampers effective diplomacy by deprioritizing the significant gains that can be made through relationship building. Framing an effective strategy for the Pacific region must, as with all national strategies, be aimed at protecting U.S. national security interests. However, creating a national-security centered strategy and working with Pacific partners to meet their critical health, infrastructure, and maritime security are not mutually exclusive—in fact, doing the latter directly bolsters the effectiveness of the former.As one senior U.S. official put it to the authors, much of America’s engagement constitutes “soft power in the service of hard power,” and is a recognition that building trust and relationships in the Pacific is ultimately tied to broader strategic goals.Prioritize follow-through on existing commitments. Even as the Pacific waits for the Trump administration to finish its review of foreign assistance, concern remains that other agreements—even those already funded by Congress—may be in peril. Ensuring full and proper implementation of the COFA agreements and the much heralded Tuna Treaty will be critical as both are viewed as baselines of U.S. commitment to the region. Additionally, attention should be given to following through on promises made, such as opening new embassies in Tonga, the Solomon Islands, Vanuatu, and Kiribati—the first three of which have been officially opened but still lack a resident ambassador. This may be a tough sell amid a State Department push to reduce diplomatic presence globally, but in a region where China has more outposts than the United States and Chinese diplomats often outnumber U.S. ones by 10 to 1, it must remain a top priority for the department.Promote a realistic trade agenda. Even outside of back-and-forth tariffs, there are other ways the United States can help bolster Pacific economic growth. Despite having small and somewhat narrow economies, Pacific nations possess niche markets and business opportunities. Yet, most Pacific embassies in the United States are too small to host economic councilors or trade attaches. Supporting the establishment of a Pacific trade representative in the United States—perhaps in multiple locations such as Washington, D.C., and San Francisco, California—would not only allow Pacific countries to have a central node through which to engage economically with the United States, but would also allow U.S. businesses access to valuable markets and opportunities abroad.To further enable U.S. industrial engagement in the Pacific, the U.S. government should reauthorize and reconfigure the Development Finance Corporation (DFC) to be better fit-for-purpose to meet its strategic mandates. Allowing the DFC to work in middle income countries, for example, and streamlining processes for working with state-owned companies would go a long way toward bolstering the U.S. toolkit as it looks for ways to counter massive Chinese inroads in the economic and infrastructure space in the Pacific.Pursue new opportunities based on administration priorities. Some Trump administration priorities are well aligned with Pacific concerns and provide the opportunity for specialized and welcome engagement. Drug trafficking, a top priority for the Trump administration, is a growing threat in the Pacific and is poised to quickly overwhelm small and fragile Pacific communities and governments. With its vast law enforcement and transnational crime assets, the United States is well positioned to provide training and assistance on an issue of top concern to all countries involved.Relatedly, problems such as drug trafficking are amplified by the immense challenge Pacific Islands have in monitoring their vast maritime domains. Collectively, the Pacific Islands’ exclusive economic zones are over a third bigger than that of the United States, even though their collective land mass is just a fraction of the United States’. Using foreign assistance programming to bolster Pacific security professionals and architecture and leveraging exceptional assets such as the U.S. Coast Guard to bolster Pacific maritime domain awareness has the dual benefit of meeting U.S. and Pacific security needs and further displacing China from entering the security sector across the Pacific.Capitalize on interest from partners and allies. U.S. policymakers have long understood that the United States cannot go it alone in the Pacific. The region is too vast, the challenges too broad, and U.S. attention is often needed elsewhere. At the same time, more and more partners around the world see the Pacific Islands region as one that directly affects their own strategic interests. In its effort to have partners and allies do more for their own security, this interest should be welcomed and encouraged. Bolstering work through multilateral groupings not only coordinates and spread loads resources, it maximizes each partners’ strengths in the service of collective objectives. The Quad is particularly suited for working in the Pacific, given its focus on health, infrastructure, and connectivity, all top priorities for Pacific nations. Following through on the Quad Indo-Pacific Maritime Domain Awareness initiative, which has a particular work stream aimed at bolstering Pacific nations’ ability to monitor their vast maritime domains, would be an excellent way to meet both Pacific and U.S. security priorities.The trilateral U.S.-Australia-Japan partnership has had success targeting critical infrastructure projects such as financing the East Micronesia Cable. This grouping should double down on these efforts by pinpointing and pursuing strategically critical infrastructure projects such as ports, airfields and roads, where China is already making significant inroads.Take action at the congressional level. The importance of the Pacific Islands region remains an area of strong bipartisan agreement in Washington, as demonstrated when the Congress succeeded in passing COFA funding legislation in the spring of 2024, even during a highly partisan election season. As the Trump administration continues to craft its strategy for the Indo-Pacific, the time is ripe for Congress to signal its priorities to the administration and move forward with legislation that protects remaining U.S. engagement and programming in the region. For example, Congress should pass the Pacific Partnership Act, a bipartisan bill that requires the administration to create a Strategy for Pacific Partnership, consult more closely with regional organizations such as the Pacific Islands Forum, and collaborate with other regional development partners such as Australia and Japan. Congress should leverage its ability to provide oversight on the executive branch by requiring follow-through on expanding critically needed diplomatic presence in the region and ensure that the Coast Guard—one of the United States’ most effective tools for countering Chinese influence in the security space—continues to be present and engaged in the region.Strategic PartnershipsAfter Trump’s tariff announcements on Liberation Day, Prime Minister James Marape of Papua New Guinea diplomatically stated, “Our trading partners in Asia—including China, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, and others—have treated Papua New Guinea with respect, honor, and fairness. These are our closest neighbors and represent proximity markets that continue to offer growing opportunities for our producers.” And, in case the diplomatic niceties obscured his point too much, he concluded, “We will continue to strengthen our trade relations in Asia and the Pacific, where our produce is welcomed. If the U.S. market becomes more difficult due to this tariff, we will simply redirect our goods to markets where there is mutual respect and no artificial barriers.”Real influence in the Pacific depends on meaningful relationships, and meaningful relationships are built through partnership, mutual respect, and joint purpose. This can seem harder than making unilateral decisions on U.S. foreign policy, but it is a more long-term—and ultimately more effective—way to do business.As the State Department foreign assistance review reaches its conclusion, now is the time to push forward with initiatives that can strengthen U.S. presence, influence, and reach in this critical region. Doing so is not hard, nor is it necessarily expensive. But it requires commitment.Charles Edel is a senior adviser and the inaugural Australia Chair at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Kathryn Paik is a deputy director and senior fellow with the Australia Chair at CSIS. John Augé is the program manager for the Australia Chair at CSIS.This piece was made possible due to the generosity of Pratt Industries and general funding to CSIS.Note: This paper was updated on July 23, 2025, to adjust the framing of assistance implemented by the U.S. Department of State and USAID in 2014.\nThis report is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAustralia, New Zealand, and Pacific, and\nGeopolitics and International Security\nImage\nCharles Edel\nSenior Adviser and Australia Chair\nImage\nKathryn Paik\nDeputy Director and Senior Fellow, Australia Chair\nImage\nJohn Augé\nProgram Manager, Australia Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nAustralia Chair\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"潮汐变幻：美国在太平洋收缩的国家安全影响\n图片：DeAgostini via Getty Images；Safin Hamid/AFP via Getty Images；Dahkareo Pritchett via DVIDS\n目录\n目录\n报告\n作者\nCharles Edel、\nKathryn Paik、\n以及\nJohn Augé\n发表于2025年7月22日\n远程可视化\n可用下载\n下载完整报告\n1758kb\n本页面于2025年8月14日更新，以调整美国国际开发署援助削减额占部分太平洋岛国国民总收入的比例。\n过去十年，美国一直坚称自己是一个“太平洋国家”，历届政府也都确认广阔的太平洋岛国地区对美国国家安全的重要性。对太平洋加强参与始于特朗普第一届政府，并获得两党广泛支持，成为一项安全优先事项。随着特朗普第二届政府的前100天接近尾声，政府传出的言论延续了这一趋势。国防部长皮特·赫格塞思在其首次印太之行中访问了关岛和夏威夷，并强调太平洋地区美国领土处于威慑“美国长矛尖端”的关键地位。\n然而，太平洋领导人一贯表示，他们看重的是行动，而不仅仅是言辞，将其视为一个国家承诺的证明。在此背景下，近期美国国际开发署（USAID）遭到削减、美国暂停对外援助、对规模小且脆弱的岛国经济体征收关税，以及将气候变化——该地区的首要关切——置于次要地位，都产生了明显的负面影响。\n无论美国的意图为何，整个太平洋地区都将美国视为一个漠不关心且反复无常的伙伴，这种观感削弱了美国在太平洋建立有意义且持久伙伴关系的能力，同时也为中国取代美国在这一关键地区的影响力和存在打开了空间。\n在这个悄然成为大国竞争舞台的地区，对美国漠不关心的这种观感不仅为北京的影响力开辟了新的切入点，也推动了一种新的叙事：坚定承诺、维护全球规则与规范的伙伴和保护者是中国，而不是美国。虽然现在还无法了解美国近期行动的全部影响，但太平洋地区的早期迹象表明，这一轨迹与美国利益背道而驰。扭转这一趋势需要迅速行动、真诚接触和富有创意的外交。\n大国竞争的新兴舞台\n美国长期以来一直出于贸易、进入亚洲以及安全等目的与太平洋岛国地区互动。尽管美国在太平洋的参与或许在第二次世界大战期间最为深入——那一时期毫无疑问地证明了该地区的战略重要性——但美国对太平洋的参与早于那场战争，并一直延续至今。与组成这片广阔区域的众多国家建立并维持牢固关系，一直符合美国的国家利益；但要维持这一来之不易的存在和影响力，就必须证明这种伙伴关系的价值。然而，在二战后的时代，华盛顿对该地区的关注充其量只是周期性的，而且主要由防务与安全协议主导。\n远程可视化\n太平洋上的岛屿。\n图片：comersis.com/Wikipedia/经 CC BY-SA 4.0\n在过去十年中，中国增加了\n既包括对深化其在这一高度战略性地区关系的兴趣，也包括相应能力——在中国永久存在将显著改变美国及其盟友的战略判断。作为对此的主要回应，美国在特朗普第一届政府时期开始加强其太平洋参与：在白宫接待三位太平洋领导人，并成立团队，与密克罗尼西亚联邦（FSM）、马绍尔群岛共和国（RMI）和帕劳共和国重新谈判《自由联系协定》（COFA）。该协定为美国带来直接的安全利益，因为它允许美国单方面获得横跨北太平洋、从菲律宾到夏威夷的防务通行权。拜登政府加速了这一趋势，两次在白宫接待太平洋岛国论坛（PIF）领导人举行峰会，启动美国政府首个太平洋岛国战略，开设数个新大使馆，扩大美国国际开发署（USAID）在太平洋地区的存在，并完成了关键的《自由联系协定》。\n\n此次重新接触的一个总体特征是关系建设，这表明华盛顿日益认识到太平洋地区重视共识、相互尊重和对话的重要性——这也被称为“太平洋之道”。但建立和维护关系——影响力的先决条件——不仅需要真诚参与，也需要持续落实。2024年结束时，近期美国—太平洋岛国论坛峰会作出的若干承诺尚未完成；2024年也在这样的局面下落幕：许多太平洋国家仍怀疑美国是否真正回归并成为坚定的伙伴，同时仍在质疑美国参与的价值是否胜过一个始终存在且意愿积极的中国。\n\n服务于硬实力的软实力\n\n尽管美国从未是太平洋地区最大的援助方，但近年来越来越明显的是，要在满足该地区需求的同时有效应对中国的影响力，需要所有志同道合的盟友和伙伴采取协调一致且持续的努力。\n\n根据洛伊研究所的《太平洋援助地图》，2008年至2022年间，美国是太平洋地区第五大援助提供方，排名落后于澳大利亚、中国、日本和新西兰。在此期间，美国向该地区支出34亿美元，并承诺提供44亿美元，尽管其中很大一部分是通过《自由联系协定》提供的。除《自由联系协定》外，美国援助虽然规模较为有限，但仍是这个脆弱地区资金来源中的关键组成部分。例如，2022年，所罗门群岛从美国获得的资金在所有非《自由联系协定》国家中最多，达到2460万美元，但这仍远低于澳大利亚当年在所罗门群岛支出的1.16亿美元和中国支出的4000万美元。2023年，除《自由联系协定》资金外，美国向该地区提供了5760万美元的发展援助。这些资金用于多个领域，包括支持教育、基础设施、气候倡议、灾害响应等。\n\n远程可视化\n\n传统上，美国国际开发署一直是美国对外援助项目的主要执行机构，但部分项目也由美国国务院执行。例如，2014年，国务院执行了经济援助总额120亿美元中的约44亿美元（美国国际开发署执行了54亿美元）。6月30日，美国国际开发署作为一个独立机构被正式关闭。\n如今，与美国国际开发署（USAID）相关的发展事务由国务院负责；然而，特朗普政府计划削减90%的USAID项目，目前几乎所有USAID和国务院的对外援助项目都已无限期暂停，等待全面审查（最初的90天审查期限已被推迟两次）。削减90%的项目将意味着在全球范围内取消6,200项多年期USAID合同授予中的5,800项，导致资金减少540亿美元；同时削减全球9,100项国务院拨款中的4,100项，使项目资金减少44亿美元。具有气候变化关联的项目——气候变化被视为太平洋岛国面临的首要国家安全威胁——最有可能被砍掉。例如，一项250万美元的太平洋岛国大使自助小额赠款计划拨款（其中包括应对气候变化的内容）已经被终止；一项金额为905,487美元、用于支持太平洋岛国应急管理联盟的拨款也已终止。再加上美国退出《巴黎协定》，美国作为全球气候变化或清洁能源领导者的信誉已受到严重削弱。\n\n鉴于USAID项目在美国参与太平洋事务中曾发挥核心作用，这些暂停和削减对小岛国的打击尤其严重。虽然目前没有各地区具体削减金额的数据，但对太平洋地区的总体影响十分重大。据全球发展中心称，这些削减相当于巴布亚新几内亚、瓦努阿图、帕劳、斐济和所罗门群岛100%的USAID国别项目资金。即使在《自由联系协定》国家，一些关键资金也不属于《自由联系协定》（COFA）范围，同样受到了影响。例如，帕劳红十字会表示，由于USAID削减，其人道主义援助资金损失了近60万美元。正如巴布亚新几内亚一名当地项目经理在1月底最初宣布后不久所说，团队因艾滋病毒和卫生相关项目被冻结而感到“ devastated ”。\n\n远程可视化\n\n影响并不局限于受援国：其他捐助伙伴和盟友也受到影响。澳大利亚等国家深刻认识到发展援助对自身国家安全的重要性，目前正争分夺秒地评估后果的程度，并重新调整其在该地区的工作。推动这些变化的逻辑是，美国对外援助支出应服务于美国国家安全目标。这一前提本身并没有错。然而，先全面叫停、再进行全面审查的做法，不仅剥夺了伙伴方所重视的项目（并造成了一些尤其有害的后果），还助长了美国是一个反复无常、不可靠伙伴的叙事。这些措施削弱而非加强了美国国家安全，因为它们稀释了美国在世界各地的存在、影响力和关系。\n\n关税：一记组合拳\n\n虽然暂停大多数USAID和国务院对外援助项目无疑会对太平洋岛国居民的日常生活产生更大的实际影响，但“解放日”关税公告同样在该地区引发了震动和困惑。长期以来，该地区一直将美国视为全球经济领导者，各国也寻求与其建立更紧密的贸易关系；而针对这些规模较小且往往陷入困境的经济体所设的高税率——其中一些\n在这些岛屿中，超过25%的人口生活在贫困线以下（见图4）——这似乎既与现实脱节，也与常识不符。美国与太平洋岛国中的两个以外的所有国家都保持贸易顺差——斐济（贸易逆差2.08亿美元）和图瓦卢（贸易逆差16.8万美元），后者人口约为11,000人——然而，几乎没有哪个国家能够毫发无损地度过解放日。\n远程可视化\n美国政府将这些关税描述为既是为了创造公平竞争环境，也是为了进行谈判的工具。这两种理由对太平洋地区都说不通。作为市场和机会有限的小岛国，贸易在支撑小型经济体方面发挥着超乎寻常的作用——然而，总体而言，美太贸易在美国的账面上几乎微不足道。美国与太平洋岛国的双边贸易总额约为10亿美元，仅占美国进行的逾7万亿美元全球贸易的一小部分。换言之，例如图瓦卢与美国之间不存在所谓的“创造公平竞争环境”。\n远程可视化\n认为关税可以作为与太平洋地区讨价还价筹码的想法同样缺乏战略逻辑。太平洋国家不仅没有多少可用于谈判的筹码，而且在征收关税之后，它们通过参与此类谈判能够获得什么好处仍不清楚。即使考虑到近年来美国不断加强的参与程度，就其对该地区的贡献而言，美国仍远远落后于其他国家（见图1）；华盛顿退出《巴黎协定》并关闭大部分发展援助项目，更进一步凸显了这一点。这些务实的国家不会满足于有限的诱因，而是会越来越多地转向其他伙伴和市场。正如斐济总理拉布卡最近所说：“世界比美国更大……我们没有任何东西可以反制，因此只能渡过风暴，随机应变。”\n远程可视化\n对美国国家安全的影响美国对太平洋地区的参与从来不只是盲目支持伙伴和盟友，也不仅仅是为了增强这些小型民主国家的实力。它一直有着强有力的战略依据；如今如此，二战时期亦然。然而，美国的行动不仅是在为中国留下可填补的空间，也在强化中国作为负责任、可预测的全球伙伴这一叙事。随着美国消除太平洋地区选择美国而非中国的每一项理由，北京已经在整个地区加大了胡萝卜加大棒式的外交。进行并列比较后可以发现，按几乎每项指标计算，美国原本就不如中国活跃，提供的也更少。不过，太平洋地区始终对中国参与该地区的方式感到不安，普遍认为，最终中国的利益总会以牺牲太平洋地区为代价而胜出。但随着美国减少在该地区的项目，而中国展现出发展成为可靠伙伴的能力，这一叙事正在转变。北京迅速利用人们对华盛顿的负面观感，并放大中国是全球伟大稳定者的自身宣传。美国全球关税宣布后不久，中国驻巴布亚新几内亚大使杨晓光称美国关税是“经济霸凌”，并强调，\n中国作为巴布亚新几内亚“最可靠的伙伴”，将继续保持一贯立场，并对与这个岛国开展贸易持开放态度。\n\n同一周，中国驻斐济大使再次强调了同样的信息，指出尽管面临外部贸易和关税紧张局势，中国仍致力于加强与斐济的贸易和投资关系。中国一直将其在太平洋地区的大部分拓展重点放在斐济。斐济与中国之间的双边贸易总额从2010年的仅1.452亿美元增至2023年的5.322亿美元；2024年，两国还通过“一带一路”倡议达成了一项3亿美元的基础设施开发项目，用于在瓦努阿莱武岛修建22座桥梁并改善道路。中国于2025年4月宣布，将开通上海与斐济楠迪之间的直航。在宣布这一消息时，中国驻斐济大使周剑指出，美国的贸易战可能为加强中国与斐济的贸易提供机会。当然，其潜台词是，与外界所认为的美国撤出形成对比，中国已经准备好并愿意在一系列领域加强接触。为进一步强化这一信息，2025年5月下旬，中国在福建省厦门市主办了第三届中国—太平洋岛国外交部长会议，来自11个太平洋岛国的高级官员齐聚一堂。在这次会议上——太平洋各国外交部长首次在中国聚会——中国宣布计划为该地区启动100多个气候相关项目。\n\n正如美国众议院军事委员会一名共和党专业工作人员在2025年4月委员会关于印太地区军事态势和国家安全挑战的听证会上所说：“我们已经建立了非常牢固的关系……这确实是最让中国感到沮丧的事情：现在它被一群不想受中国欺凌的国家包围着，所以它们向我们寻求支持……而现在我们正试图在欺凌中国方面胜过它……向我们的伙伴表明，我们可以像中国打击他们一样有力地打击中国。”\n\n中国这一多层面的推动行动背后的驱动因素是显而易见的。对北京而言，太平洋地区提供了一个成本极低、收益极高的机会。通过相对较小的资金投入，中国就能谋求重大的战略、外交和经济收益。从战略上看，中国在太平洋地区的活动具有双重好处：既谋求建立自己的潜在立足点，又迫使美国、澳大利亚及其他国家同时在各处阻止中国的影响力。有人将此比作“打地鼠”游戏：美国和其他志同道合的国家必须努力维持在各处的存在，而中国只需找到一个安全立足点，就能彻底改变对手的盘算。不管这个游戏叫什么，显然它正朝着有利于北京的方向进行。而随着美国看起来可能正在逐步离开该地区，对中国而言，这种成本效益盘算刚刚变得更加具有吸引力。\n\n建议\n\n在太平洋地区，关系是影响力的货币，而美国被视为一个漠不关心的伙伴，这种观感正在稳步加深。弥合这一认知鸿沟将需要谨慎的外交——以及真正的行动——这些行动应针对太平洋，而不是在一系列影响广泛的行政命令中把该地区降为附带损害。为确保不在这一关键领域进一步失去阵地，美国应当接受\n通过以下行动强调其对该地区的承诺：\n\n将软实力用于实现硬目标。随着国务院完成对对外援助的审查，应确保充分重视与太平洋地区优先事项保持一致的重要性，因为这是建立和维护这些关键关系的最有效方式。\n\n以商业思维为导向、注重交易的国际关系方式，有可能助长零和心态；这种心态会将通过建立关系可以取得的重大收益置于次要地位，从而妨碍有效外交。与所有国家战略一样，制定太平洋地区的有效战略必须以保护美国国家安全利益为目标。然而，制定以国家安全为中心的战略，并与太平洋伙伴合作，满足其关键的卫生、基础设施和海上安全需求，并非相互排斥——事实上，后者会直接增强前者的有效性。正如一位美国高级官员对作者所说，美国参与事务的很大一部分构成了“服务于硬实力的软实力”，这也表明，在太平洋地区建立信任和关系，最终与更广泛的战略目标息息相关。\n\n优先落实现有承诺。即使太平洋地区仍在等待特朗普政府完成对外援助的审查，人们仍担心其他协议——即便是那些已经获得国会拨款的协议——可能面临危险。确保《自由联系协定》（COFA）和备受赞誉的《金枪鱼条约》得到全面、适当的实施，将至关重要，因为二者都被视为美国对该地区承诺的基准。此外，还应重视兑现已经作出的承诺，例如在汤加、所罗门群岛、瓦努阿图和基里巴斯开设新大使馆——其中前三个国家的大使馆虽已正式开馆，但仍没有常驻大使。在国务院推动全球范围内减少外交存在的背景下，这一主张可能很难获得支持；但在一个中国的驻外据点多于美国、中国外交官人数常常以10比1超过美国外交官的地区，这必须仍是国务院的首要任务。\n\n推动现实可行的贸易议程。即使不考虑相互加征关税，美国还可以通过其他方式帮助促进太平洋地区的经济增长。尽管太平洋国家经济规模较小且相对单一，但它们拥有利基市场和商业机会。然而，美国多数太平洋国家大使馆规模过小，无法设立经济参赞或贸易专员。支持在美国设立太平洋贸易代表——或许可在华盛顿特区和加利福尼亚州旧金山等多个地点设立——不仅能让太平洋国家拥有一个与美国开展经济交往的中心节点，也能让美国企业获得海外有价值的市场和机会。\n\n为了进一步推动美国在太平洋地区的产业参与，美国政府应重新授权并重新调整美国国际开发金融公司（DFC），使其更适合履行其战略任务。例如，允许DFC在中等收入国家开展工作，并简化与国有企业合作的流程，将大大增强美国的工具箱，使其能够寻找抵消中国在经济和基础设施领域大举渗透的方法\n太平洋地区。根据政府的优先事项寻求新的机遇。特朗普政府的一些优先事项与太平洋地区的关切高度契合，为开展有针对性且受欢迎的合作提供了机会。打击毒品贩运是特朗普政府的首要优先事项之一，而毒品贩运正在太平洋地区构成日益严重的威胁，并且很可能迅速压垮太平洋地区规模小且脆弱的社区和政府。凭借广泛的执法和跨国犯罪打击资源，美国完全有能力就这一所有相关国家都高度关切的问题提供培训和援助。\n\n与此相关的是，太平洋岛国在监测其广阔海域方面面临巨大挑战，这放大了毒品贩运等问题。太平洋岛国的专属经济区总面积比美国的大三分之一以上，尽管这些岛国的陆地总面积加起来仅为美国的一小部分。利用对外援助项目加强太平洋地区的安全专业人员和安全架构，并利用美国海岸警卫队等卓越力量提升太平洋地区的海域态势感知，具有双重好处：既能满足美国和太平洋地区的安全需求，又能进一步阻止中国进入整个太平洋地区的安全领域。\n\n利用伙伴和盟友的兴趣。美国政策制定者长期以来一直明白，美国无法独自应对太平洋地区。该地区幅员过于辽阔，挑战过于广泛，而美国的注意力往往还需要投入其他地方。与此同时，世界各地越来越多的伙伴认为，太平洋岛国地区会直接影响其自身的战略利益。在推动伙伴和盟友为自身安全承担更多责任的努力中，应当欢迎并鼓励这种兴趣。通过多边集团加强合作，不仅能够协调并分担资源，还能最大限度地发挥每个伙伴的优势，以服务于共同目标。鉴于四方安全对话聚焦于医疗、基础设施和互联互通，而这些都是太平洋国家的首要优先事项，四方安全对话尤其适合在太平洋地区开展工作。落实四方安全对话“印太海域态势感知”倡议——该倡议设有专门工作流，旨在增强太平洋国家监测其广阔海域的能力——将是同时满足太平洋地区和美国安全优先事项的绝佳方式。\n\n美国—澳大利亚—日本三边伙伴关系在针对关键基础设施项目方面已取得成功，例如为东密克罗尼西亚海底电缆提供融资。该集团应通过确定并推进港口、机场和道路等具有战略重要性的关键基础设施项目，加倍推进这些努力，因为中国已经在这些领域取得了显著进展。\n\n在国会层面采取行动。太平洋岛国地区的重要性仍然是华盛顿两党强烈共识的领域。2024年春季，即使正值党派对立严重的选举季，国会仍成功通过了《自由联系协定》资金立法，这就证明了这一点。随着特朗普政府继续制定其印太战略，现在正是国会向政府表明其优先事项，并推动立法以保护美国在该地区尚存的参与和项目的时机。例如，国会应通过《太平洋伙伴关系法》；这是一项两党法案，要求政府制定一项太平洋战略\n伙伴关系，加强与太平洋岛国论坛等区域组织的磋商，并与澳大利亚、日本等其他区域发展伙伴开展合作。国会应利用其对行政部门进行监督的能力，要求切实扩大该地区极其需要的外交存在，并确保海岸警卫队——美国在安全领域遏制中国影响力最有效的工具之一——继续在该地区开展存在和参与。\n\n战略伙伴关系\n\n在特朗普于“解放日”宣布关税后，巴布亚新几内亚总理詹姆斯·马拉佩以外交辞令表示：“我们在亚洲的贸易伙伴——包括中国、印度尼西亚、马来西亚、菲律宾以及其他国家——一直以尊重、荣誉和公平的态度对待巴布亚新几内亚。这些是我们最亲近的邻国，也是临近市场，继续为我们的生产商提供不断增长的机会。”而且，万一外交上的客套话过于掩饰了他的观点，他最后总结道：“我们将继续加强与亚洲和太平洋地区的贸易关系，我们的农产品在那里受到欢迎。如果由于这项关税，美国市场变得更加困难，我们只会把商品转向那些相互尊重且没有人为壁垒的市场。”\n\n在太平洋地区，真正的影响力取决于有意义的关系，而有意义的关系是通过伙伴关系、相互尊重和共同目标建立起来的。这似乎比单方面决定美国外交政策更难，但这是开展事务更长期——并最终更有效——的方式。\n\n随着国务院对对外援助的审查接近尾声，现在正是推进各项倡议的时机，以加强美国在这一关键地区的存在、影响力和触达范围。这样做并不困难，也不一定昂贵。但它需要承诺。\n\n查尔斯·埃德尔是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）的高级顾问兼首任澳大利亚主席。凯瑟琳·派克是CSIS澳大利亚主席项目的副主任兼高级研究员。约翰·奥热是CSIS澳大利亚主席项目经理。\n\n本篇文章得以完成，得益于普拉特工业公司的慷慨支持以及CSIS的一般性资金。\n\n注：本文于2025年7月23日更新，以调整对美国国务院和美国国际开发署于2014年实施的援助的表述框架。\n\n本报告由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）编制。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不为专有。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n\n© 2025年战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n\n澳大利亚、新西兰和太平洋地区，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全\n\n图片\n\n查尔斯·埃德尔\n高级顾问兼澳大利亚主席\n\n图片\n\n凯瑟琳·派克\n副主任兼高级研究员，澳大利亚主席项目\n\n图片\n\n约翰·奥热\n项目经理，澳大利亚主席项目\n\n项目与计划\n\n澳大利亚主席项目\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-07-22T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-597","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"两种战略打击的较量：结束乌克兰战争的竞赛","title_en":"A Tale of Two Strategic Attacks: The Race to End the War in Ukraine","summary_cn":"CSIS评论文章分析乌克兰战争正演变为两种消耗性胜利理论的竞赛：乌克兰通过无人机、人工智能与低成本远程打击，配合网络与电子战，持续攻击俄炼油厂、出口终端和油轮，试图削弱俄财政收入并动摇精英支持；俄罗斯则加大对乌关键基础设施和政治经济中心的导弹与无人机打击，以迫使乌方让步。文章引用ACLED数据说明乌方打击次数激增，并指出俄弹道导弹产量大增而乌拦截率骤降，建议西方在制裁规避、情报支持和拦截弹联合生产三方面加大援助。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary argues that the war in Ukraine has become a race between two attritional theories of victory. Ukraine is maximizing drone production and AI-enabled deep strikes on Russian refineries, export terminals, and tankers to threaten Moscow's oil revenue and elite cohesion, while Russia escalates missile and drone punishment strikes on Ukrainian critical infrastructure and political-economic centers to force concessions. Citing ACLED data, the piece notes a sharp rise in Ukrainian strikes and a steep drop in Ukraine's ballistic missile interception rate as Russian production surged, and recommends Western action on sanctions evasion, intelligence support, and interceptor coproduction.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:08:30.401457+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/tale-two-strategic-attacks-race-end-war-ukraine","content":"A Tale of Two Strategic Attacks: The Race to End the War in Ukraine\nPhoto: Ivan Antypenko/Suspilne Ukraine/JSC \"UA:PBC\"/Global Images Ukraine/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nYasir Atalan\nPublished July 15, 2026\nThe war in Ukraine is now a race between two competing, attritional theories of victory. Ukraine is focused on maximizing drone production alongside novel use of AI and low-cost, high payoff deep strikes integrated with cyber and electronic warfare designed to hold Russia’s oil and gas, and by proxy its economy, at risk. Russia is trying to increase its punishment campaign, targeting critical infrastructure and major political and economic centers like Kyiv to force a negotiation that favors the Kremlin’s interests. While the battlefield still matters, these dueling deep strike campaigns are the lines of effort most likely to force a settlement over the next six months.Ukraine’s Energy BlitzUkraine is executing strategic attacks against Russian energy infrastructure designed to impose economic costs and fracture elite support for the Kremlin. The campaign targets the financial engine of the state by striking refineries, export terminals, and storage facilities well beyond the front line. This operational design bypasses the stalemate on the ground to threaten the revenue streams funding the war effort. As oil profits decline and domestic fuel shortages rise, the strikes compel Russian power brokers to absorb the physical consequences of the conflict. By holding these critical economic nodes at risk, Kyiv seeks to alter the decision calculus of regime insiders and create political friction within the capital.Ukraine’s launches of air and drone strikes in Russia have increased significantly over the war, according to data from ACLED. In 2023, there were around 580 strikes or strike events; this number increased to 7,244 in 2024. Of these events, 901 aimed at energy infrastructure, such as fuel storage, oil depots, refineries, and pipelines. The data ends in June 2025, but in the first half of 2025 alone, there were 6,176 air and drone strikes, suggesting that the total number of attacks in 2025 may have nearly doubled the 2024 total. Russia suffers from these attacks due to its dependence on oil and gas: Just before the full-scale invasion, taxes and export tariffs related to oil and gas comprised 45 percent of Russia’s federal budget revenues.Ukraine has also extended its campaign from fixed infrastructure to maritime energy logistics. Aerial and Sea Baby drones have been actively used against tankers in Russia’s shadow fleet. Ukraine drone strikes also recently attacked 90 Russian vessels, which led to the suspension of shipping in the Sea of Azov.Russia’s Punishment CampaignRussia’s firepower strike strategy operates as a deliberate punishment campaign designed to substitute for stagnant ground offensives by breaking the political resolve of both Kyiv and its international backers. By launching large salvos of ballistic missiles and drones against critical infrastructure, Russia seeks to exhaust Ukrainian air defenses and inflict paralyzing psychological strain on the civilian populace. This pressure creates a cruel mathematical reality where sheer munitions volume overcomes interception capabilities to directly shape the strategic environment. The Kremlin calibrates these bombardments to attempt to force Ukrainian leadership into premature concessions and secure a negotiated settlement that cements Russian interests regardless of localized battlefield realities.While Ukraine has proved agile in fielding new classes of long-range strike drones and cruise missiles, the key variable to watch in these dueling strike campaigns is ballistic missiles. As documented in the CSIS Russian Firepower Strike Tracker, Ukraine has successfully intercepted drones and cruise missiles since the start of the war: Ukraine often intercepts long-range one-way attack drones like the Shahed over 85 percent of the time. However, ballistic missiles are another story. Between 2023 and 2025, Russia increased the number of ballistic missiles it fired at Ukraine, and by extension its production capacity, by over 700 percent. However, the share of ballistic missiles Ukraine was able to intercept and neutralize dropped by 36 percent. Worse still, the current interception rate in 2026 is only 10.2 percent, a drop of over 200 percent from the beginning of the war. This coercion drives increasing pressure on the Ukrainian president and his supporters to find an off-ramp to the war despite recent battlefield gains.\nImage\nBenjamin Jensen\nDirector, Futures Lab and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nYasir Atalan\nDeputy Director and Data Fellow, Futures Lab, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nFutures Lab\nDefense and Security\nRussian Firepower Strike Tracker: Analyzing Missile Attacks in Ukraine\nRemote Visualization\nThe trend of decreasing success of intercepting ballistic missiles appears to be getting worse in 2026. To date, since January 1, 2026, Russia has fired 167 ballistic missiles at Ukraine. Yet, the past trend shows that Russia generally ramps up its ballistic launches toward the end of the year. If this trend holds, Moscow is on track to fire up to 840 ballistic missiles in 2026 (Figure 2).\nRemote Visualization\nFor Ukraine to successfully defend against Russian attacks, it must increase the rate at which air defenses intercept ballistic missiles. If only 10.2 percent of 840 ballistic missiles are intercepted, that leaves 754 devastating strikes that could alter the balance of resolve. Kyiv needs to limit the number of ballistic missile strikes that reach their target and buy time for its attacks on Russian energy infrastructure to change the decision calculus in Moscow.How Ukraine’s International Backers Can HelpUkraine’s international partners can address the increased use of Russian ballistic missile strikes to support the Ukrainian deep strike campaign along three lines of effort: revisiting sanctions evasion, increasing intelligence support, and accelerating coproduction.Revisit Sanctions Evasion: Since the start of the war, Russia has found ways to bypass sanctions and acquire important electronic components that support its long-range strike campaign. Recent reporting documents how Russia uses a special military intelligence unit, the 20th Directorate, to buy foreign electronics and evade sanctions. To choke off the supply of foreign electronics feeding the Russian war machine, the United States and its allies must aggressively target the global networks orchestrating parallel trade. Russian military intelligence units like the 20th Directorate continually exploit gaps in customs enforcement, lax jurisdictions and shell companies to procure critical dual-use components. Washington should combat this illicit pipeline by rapidly declassifying and passing actionable intelligence on these front operations directly to partner enforcement agencies. Armed with this precise targeting data, the Treasury Department can then expand secondary sanctions against the specific third-country intermediaries and foreign financial institutions facilitating these covert supply chains. Synchronizing active intelligence sharing with expanded economic coercion will fracture the procurement hubs enabling Moscow and steadily degrade the sustainment capacity of the Russian defense industrial base.Increase Intelligence Support: International partners can blunt the lethal math of the Russian salvo campaign by expanding intelligence sharing to support Ukrainian deep strikes. Western agencies should provide actionable targeting packages that support attacking the ballistic missile threat at its source. High-priority target sets include mobile Iskander launchers, manufacturing plants deep inside Russian territory, and critical supply chain vulnerabilities like rocket motor production facilities. Disrupting these nodes before missiles enter the logistical pipeline reduces the immediate burden on Ukraine’s strained air defense network. Shifting a portion of Kyiv’s long-range strike capacity toward active ballistic missile launch platforms and component bottlenecks creates operational friction, forces Moscow to pull assets further back, and slows the overall generation of Russian firepower.Accelerate Coproduction: In 2024, Germany, Spain, Romania, and the Netherlands signed an almost $6 billion contract to manufacture Patriot interceptors (PAC-2) at a new facility run by defense company MBDA in southern Germany. During the July NATO summit, Under Secretary of Defense Michael Duffey signaled a willingness to explore additional coproduction in Europe outside of Germany. These efforts are in addition to calls to license Patriot production in Ukraine, an effort which, while crucial, could take years to materialize.Therefore, European states should tap European Investment Bank Group funding for additional production lines, both at the MBDA plant and in other areas. Accelerating PAC-2 production would allow European states to transfer additional Patriot interceptors to Ukraine without incurring significant long-term risk.Benjamin Jensen is director of the Futures Lab and senior fellow in the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Yasir Atalan is deputy director and data fellow in the Futures Lab at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nUkraine,\nRussia,\nUkraine War,\nDefense and Security, and\nGeopolitics and International Security\nRelated Content\nImage\nRussian Firepower Strike Tracker: Analyzing Missile Attacks in Ukraine\nThis digital series—featuring scholars from the Futures Lab and across CSIS—explores the challenge of modern firepower strikes and their central role in coercive campaigns and competitive strategy\nImage\nRussia’s Massed Strikes: The Strategy of Coercion by Salvo\nCritical Questions\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nYasir Atalan\n— September 9, 2025\nImage\nThe New Salvo War\nBrief\nby\nBenjamin Jensen,\nYasir Atalan,\nand\nErik Tiersten-Nyman\n— July 31, 2025","content_cn":"两种战略攻击的故事：结束乌克兰战争的竞赛\n图片：Ivan Antypenko/Suspilne Ukraine/JSC \"UA:PBC\"/Global Images Ukraine/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n和\nYasir Atalan\n发布于2026年7月15日\n\n乌克兰战争如今已成为两种相互竞争的消耗型胜利理论之间的竞赛。乌克兰专注于最大限度提高无人机产量，同时创新运用人工智能，以及将低成本、高收益的纵深打击与网络战和电子战相结合，旨在使俄罗斯的石油和天然气，并间接使其经济，处于风险之中。俄罗斯则试图加大惩罚行动，瞄准关键基础设施以及基辅等主要政治和经济中心，以迫使对方进行有利于克里姆林宫利益的谈判。尽管战场仍然重要，但这两场相互对抗的纵深打击行动，是未来六个月内最有可能迫使各方达成解决方案的行动主线。\n\n乌克兰的能源闪电战\n\n根据ACLED的数据，乌克兰在战争期间对俄罗斯发动的空袭和无人机袭击显著增加。2023年约有580次袭击或袭击事件；2024年这一数字增至7,244次。其中，901起针对能源基础设施，例如燃料储存设施、石油仓库、炼油厂和管道。数据截至2025年6月，但仅2025年上半年就发生了6,176次空袭和无人机袭击，这表明2025年的袭击总数可能接近2024年的两倍。俄罗斯之所以受到这些袭击的影响，是因为其依赖石油和天然气：在全面入侵前夕，与石油和天然气相关的税收及出口关税占俄罗斯联邦预算收入的45%。\n\n乌克兰还将行动范围从固定基础设施扩大到海上能源物流。空中无人机和“海上宝贝”无人机一直被积极用于攻击俄罗斯影子船队中的油轮。乌克兰无人机袭击最近还攻击了90艘俄罗斯船只，导致亚速海航运暂停。\n\n俄罗斯的惩罚行动\n\n俄罗斯的火力打击战略是一场蓄意实施的惩罚行动，旨在通过打破基辅及其国际支持者的政治意志，取代停滞不前的地面攻势。俄罗斯向关键基础设施发射大规模弹道导弹和无人机齐射，试图耗尽乌克兰的防空力量，并给平民施加令人瘫痪的心理压力。这种压力造成了残酷的数学现实：单凭弹药数量就能压倒拦截能力，从而直接塑造战略环境。克里姆林宫对这些轰炸进行调整，试图迫使乌克兰领导层过早让步，并达成一项巩固俄罗斯\n无论当地战场现实如何。尽管乌克兰已证明能够灵活部署新型远程攻击无人机和巡航导弹，但在这场相互对抗的打击行动中，需要关注的关键变量是弹道导弹。正如CSIS俄罗斯火力打击追踪器所记录的那样，自战争开始以来，乌克兰一直成功拦截无人机和巡航导弹：乌克兰通常能够拦截超过85%的“沙赫德”等远程单向攻击无人机。然而，弹道导弹则是另一回事。2023年至2025年间，俄罗斯向乌克兰发射的弹道导弹数量，以及由此延伸出的生产能力，增加了700%以上。然而，乌克兰能够拦截并摧毁的弹道导弹占比下降了36%。更糟糕的是，2026年当前的拦截率仅为10.2%，较战争初期下降了超过200%。这种胁迫加大了乌克兰总统及其支持者寻找战争退路的压力，尽管乌克兰近期在战场上取得了进展。\n图片\nBenjamin Jensen\n未来实验室主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员\n图片\nYasir Atalan\n未来实验室副主任兼数据研究员，国防与安全部\n项目与计划\n未来实验室\n国防与安全\n俄罗斯火力打击追踪器：分析乌克兰境内的导弹袭击\n远程可视化\n2026年，拦截弹道导弹成功率下降的趋势似乎正在恶化。截至目前，自2026年1月1日以来，俄罗斯已向乌克兰发射167枚弹道导弹。然而，过去的趋势表明，俄罗斯通常会在年底前增加弹道导弹发射量。如果这一趋势持续，莫斯科预计将在2026年发射多达840枚弹道导弹（图2）。\n远程可视化\n为了成功防御俄罗斯的攻击，乌克兰必须提高防空系统拦截弹道导弹的速率。如果840枚弹道导弹中只有10.2%被拦截，就会留下754次毁灭性打击，可能改变双方意志的平衡。基辅需要限制抵达目标的弹道导弹袭击次数，并为其对俄罗斯能源基础设施的攻击争取时间，从而改变莫斯科的决策计算。乌克兰的国际支持者如何提供帮助乌克兰的国际伙伴可以从三条努力方向入手，应对俄罗斯日益增加的弹道导弹袭击，以支持乌克兰的纵深打击行动：重新审视规避制裁问题、增加情报支持，以及加快共同生产。重新审视规避制裁问题：自战争开始以来，俄罗斯一直设法绕过制裁，获取支持其远程打击行动的重要电子元件。近期报道记录了俄罗斯如何利用一个特别军事情报单位——第20局——购买外国电子产品并逃避制裁。为了切断流入俄罗斯战争机器的外国电子产品供应，美国及其盟友必须积极打击策划平行贸易的全球网络。俄罗斯军事情报单位（如第20局）不断利用海关执法漏洞、监管宽松的司法管辖区和空壳公司，采购关键的军民两用部件。华盛顿应通过迅速解密并将有关这些前台行动的可操作情报直接传递给合作伙伴，来打击这一非法供应链。\n执法机构。凭借这些精确的目标数据，财政部随后可以扩大对具体第三国中间商和外国金融机构的次级制裁，这些机构正在为这些秘密供应链提供便利。将积极的情报共享与扩大经济胁迫同步起来，将瓦解为莫斯科提供采购便利的枢纽，并持续削弱俄罗斯国防工业基础的维持能力。\n\n增加情报支持：国际伙伴可以扩大情报共享，以支持乌克兰实施纵深打击，从而削弱俄罗斯齐射行动的致命算术。西方机构应提供可执行的目标数据包，支持在弹道导弹威胁源头发动攻击。高优先级目标包括机动式“伊斯坎德尔”发射车、俄罗斯境内纵深的制造工厂，以及火箭发动机生产设施等关键供应链脆弱点。在导弹进入后勤管道之前扰乱这些节点，可以减轻乌克兰紧张的防空网络所承受的即时负担。将基辅部分远程打击能力转向现役弹道导弹发射平台和部件瓶颈，将制造作战摩擦，迫使莫斯科把资产进一步后撤，并减缓俄罗斯整体火力的生成速度。\n\n加快联合生产：2024年，德国、西班牙、罗马尼亚和荷兰签署了一份近60亿美元的合同，在德国南部由防务公司MBDA运营的新设施生产“爱国者”拦截弹（PAC-2）。在7月的北约峰会上，国防部副部长迈克尔·达菲表示愿意探索在德国以外的欧洲地区开展更多联合生产。这些努力是在呼吁授权在乌克兰生产“爱国者”的基础上进行的；后者虽然至关重要，但可能需要数年才能实现。因此，欧洲国家应利用欧洲投资银行集团的资金，在MBDA工厂及其他地区增设生产线。加快PAC-2生产，将使欧洲国家能够向乌克兰转移更多“爱国者”拦截弹，同时无需承担重大的长期风险。\n\n本杰明·詹森是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）未来实验室主任兼防务与安全部高级研究员。亚西尔·阿塔兰是CSIS未来实验室副主任兼数据研究员。\n\n《评论》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性，不受专有利益影响。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n\n© 2026年战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n乌克兰，\n俄罗斯，\n乌克兰战争，\n防务与安全，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全\n\n相关内容\n图片\n俄罗斯火力打击追踪器：分析乌克兰遭受的导弹袭击\n该数字系列邀请未来实验室及CSIS各部门的学者参与，探讨现代火力打击的挑战，以及其在胁迫行动和竞争战略中的核心作用\n\n图片\n俄罗斯的大规模打击：齐射胁迫战略\n《关键问题》\n作者\n本杰明·詹森\n和\n亚西尔·阿塔兰\n——2025年9月9日\n\n图片\n新的齐射\n战争简报\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen、\nYasir Atalan、\n以及\nErik Tiersten-Nyman\n— 2025年7月31日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-07-15T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1032","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"利用美国对永久战争的厌恶：伊朗叙事在Bluesky上的传播","title_en":"Tapping into America’s Distaste for Forever Wars: The Spread of Iranian Narratives on Bluesky","summary_cn":"CSIS未来实验室报告分析Bluesky平台上疑似与伊朗相关的信息行动。研究团队基于约9012条帖文，运用BERTopic与Claude模型识别出三大叙事主题：将伊朗军事与领导层描绘为受害却成功、把冲突框定为以色列的选择性战争、以及放大美国及其盟友内部围绕冲突的分歧。分析发现第三类主题传播效果最佳，平均获150次转发、470次点赞，并识别出19个核心账号活跃于15个社群。报告强调研究不构成对伊朗国家归属的定论，并建议美国与平台合作开展视觉反制宣传、恢复国家支持账号标注制度，并部署智能体人工智能检测和清除虚假信息网络。","summary_en":"This CSIS Futures Lab report analyzes suspected Iran-aligned information operations on Bluesky. Using roughly 9,012 posts and tools including BERTopic and Claude, researchers identified three core narrative themes: portraying Iran's military and leadership as victimized yet successful, framing the conflict as an Israeli war of choice, and amplifying divisions within the United States and among allies. The third theme performed best, averaging 150 reposts and 470 likes, and network analysis found 19 core accounts active across 15 communities. The report stresses that findings do not constitute definitive attribution to the Iranian state, and recommends visual counter-messaging, a state-sponsored post labeling regime, and agentic AI to detect and dismantle propaganda networks.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:25:40.951478+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/tapping-americas-distaste-forever-wars-spread-iranian-narratives-bluesky","content":"Tapping into America’s Distaste for Forever Wars: The Spread of Iranian Narratives on Bluesky\nPhoto: Bilal Ulker/Adobe Stock\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nReport\nby\nJose M. Macias III\nand\nNico Vacca\nPublished May 4, 2026\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Full Report\n555kb\nRemote Visualization\nIntroductionThe United States and Israel have made battlefield gains in their conflict against Iran, but the United States is struggling to counter Iranian propaganda. Operational successes have removed Iran’s authoritarian supreme leader, dismantled its defense leadership apparatus, and degraded its missile capabilities. However, the opportunity cost of military success for the United States is the loss of ground in the information war for the hearts and minds of American audiences and Western audiences more broadly. While Iran is losing on the battlefield, it is competing effectively in the information space through an aggressive, multiplatform disinformation campaign.Analysis by the Futures Lab of more than 9,000 Bluesky Social posts finds that messages seemingly designed to exacerbate public divisions, which compose 23 percent of posts in the dataset, are the highest performing, averaging 150 reposts, 470 likes, and 28 replies per post. These same posts have been viewed by an estimated 293,666 users and are statistically significantly associated with a higher sharing volume, with an estimated 41 percent increase compared to other posts.1 In addition, network and association analysis identifies 19 core accounts spreading Iranian war narratives and finds them to be active in 15 communities, with 11 communities estimated to be relying heavily on a singular account and 3 communities interacting with more than a single account.2Iran’s disinformation playbook has successfully targeted Israel in prior conflicts with Hamas and Hezbollah. Following the October 7 attacks, Microsoft reported that Iran increased cyber operations against Israel. Moreover, Iranian influence operations rose from roughly six in 2021 to eleven in October 2023 alone, and Iranian actors repeatedly reused old footage while falsely presenting it as Israeli attacks. As a result of this, Meta reportedly closed Iran-linked accounts that pushed narratives on religious divisions within Israel, liberal and conservative critiques of Israel’s war with Hamas, and criticism of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu in likely efforts to inflame tensions within Israel. Following the 2025 U.S.-Israeli strikes on Iranian nuclear facilities, narratives from Iranian accounts generated over 126,000 engagements and an estimated 224 million views. Now Iran’s disinformation machine appears to be targeting Western audiences to undermine support for the U.S.-Israeli conflict with the Iranian regime.Research from Microsoft, Cyabra, and The New York Times shows Iran’s wider social media influence campaign aimed at the United States. To evaluate a portion of this campaign, the Futures Lab leveraged open-source data from Bluesky Social, BERTopic, and Claude’s Sonnet 4.6 model to classify narratives on Bluesky feeds of suspected Iranian disinformation accounts. The research team identified three core themes that underpin Iran’s disinformation campaign: (1) portraying the Iranian military and leadership as both victimized and successful; (2) framing the conflict as an Israeli war of choice; and (3) using rhetoric to amplify divisions surrounding the conflict within the United States and among its allies and partners. Applying statistical analysis, the research team identified that the third theme is positively associated with larger reach. The research team also used network analysis, which showed that the 19 core accounts are deeply embedded in 15 Bluesky communities. To counter these narratives, the U.S. government, working with social media platforms, should craft a visual counter-messaging campaign to deflect AI fakes; establish a labeling regime for state-sponsored posts; and use agentic artificial intelligence (AI) to systemically detect and dismantle propaganda networks.A Refined PlaybookIranian information operations follow a familiar asymmetric playbook: When direct competition with U.S. and Israeli military power is unfavorable, the objective shifts from battlefield parity to a sustained campaign to erode support for the conflict by raising the associated political costs. Russian and Chinese disinformation campaigns established the playbook that Iran has now adapted. Russian campaigns generally focus on amplifying divisions within society and eroding trust in Western democratic institutions. Chinese campaigns have generally aimed to shape positive perceptions of the Chinese Communist Party and suppress narratives on sensitive topics that may delegitimize the regime, such as Taiwan, the South China Sea, and Xinjiang, but both countries use overlapping tactics. Recent monitoring by Graphika found that the Iran conflict has produced a crowded online threat environment marked by information warfare, including deepfakes and other AI-generated media, disinformation, cyberattacks, and coordinated inauthentic activity across platforms. A 2024 Microsoft report on Iranian cyber-enabled influence activity during the Israel-Hamas war showed how Iran used false personas and manipulated content to turn a regional conflict into a source of political pressure in Western countries. Advancements in AI are accelerating this process by increasing the scale and iteration cycles for disinformation campaigns. Iran is applying an established asymmetric information warfare doctrine, accelerated by AI, to a domestic American audience that is already divided over the conflict.Research Design and MethodsTo analyze narratives aligned with Iranian strategic interests likely contributing to Iran’s information war, the research team conducted a combined content and network analysis, identifying patterns of messaging, engagement, and amplification. The unit of study was defined as Bluesky users. The researchers narrowed the scope of the study to Bluesky, using the R programming language, because of its free, open-access application programming interface (API). This platform choice also narrowed the audience under observation. As of July 2025, Bluesky web traffic was concentrated most heavily in the United States (about 50 percent), followed by Japan (about 6 percent), the United Kingdom (about 5 percent), Germany (about 5 percent), and Brazil (about 4 percent). With 70 percent of the platform’s user base consisting of Western audiences, the analysis should not be understood to cover all regions of the world.Next, the research team reviewed existing research to identify patterns of likely Iranian disinformation accounts and to draw data from their Bluesky feeds. Specifically, previous research has shown that Iranian disinformation accounts focus on policy topics spanning anti-Israel, pro-Palestinian, and anti–Trump administration narratives. More recent research has identified accounts using AI deepfakes and multilingual campaigns, as well as Iranian state media pushing a narrative of false battlefield success through exaggerated and fabricated claims of strikes on U.S. military bases, U.S. aircraft, and Israeli cities. Based on these tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs), the researchers analyzed the Bluesky discover feed and selected accounts that showed overlapping TTPs. These TTPs included bot-like posting behavior, sharing links to Iranian websites flagged by the U.S. Department of Justice, persistent post or repost activity in short time periods, limited organic engagement, rapid shifts across opportunistic narratives, and other likely inauthentic tendencies consistent with prior reporting. While these TTPs do not establish definitive Iranian attribution, they identify a high-confidence sample of suspicious accounts consistent with previously reported Iranian-linked influence activity.The researchers then queried the posts on each user’s feed between February 28, 2026, and March 31, 2026. This included posts by the core users, reposts, and data on the original authors of those posts—including reposts, likes, and comment history—to construct a dataset for narrative, sentiment, and network analysis. To note, not all posts in the data were generated by the core accounts. An estimated 58 percent come from the 19 core accounts, while the remainder are reposts by the core users from accounts that appeared on their feeds.To classify the narratives of the suspected Iranian disinformation accounts, the team combined BERTopic sentence transformers with Claude’s Sonnet 4.6 large language model.3 BERTopic was used to identify key topics in posts while Sonnet analyzed the narrative themes found within. The research team then analyzed BERTopic’s and Sonnet’s groupings to create 10 narrative clusters and consolidated them into three major themes, which are presented in this paper. Research also shows that negative or hateful content can overperform and go viral on social media. To include measures of negative and toxic content, the team leveraged vaderSentiment compound scores on a scale of -1 (Negative) to 1 (Positive) and Detoxify toxicity scores on a scale of 0 (Not Toxic) to 1 (Toxic) to quantify post sentiments and levels of toxicity that could spread the Iranian narratives further.4 In addition, the team constructed directed interaction edge lists linking non-core interactors to suspected core accounts using post-level likes and repost metrics, and then analyzed and visualized the spread using NetworkX and R.5 Tabulations across 9,012 posts are shown in Table 1. The dataset was further analyzed using a regression model, as discussed in the results section and shown in the appendix. The final dataset includes the key topics of posts and narrative categories from the 19 core user feeds that were used to establish the three core themes unpacked in this paper. The 19 core accounts and users sharing and engaging with their content remain anonymous in this paper.This study does not claim definitive attribution of the selected Bluesky accounts to the Iranian state, the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC), or any coordinated foreign influence operation. Instead, it analyzes accounts selected because their posting behavior, narrative themes, link-sharing patterns, and amplification behavior resemble tactics documented in prior research on Iranian-linked influence operations. The findings therefore should not be read as conclusive proof that these accounts were part of an Iranian state-backed influence operation.Results\nRemote Visualization\nTheme 1: A Victimized but Successful Military and LeadershipThe first theme that emerged centers on portraying Iranian offensive and defensive operations as evidence of military strength, capability, and strategic legitimacy. The spread of the first theme is depicted in Table 1. The Bluesky posts were on average reposted 45 times, liked 138 times, and commented on by at least seven users. In addition, an estimated 30 percent of these posts included images, and nearly 40 percent had a video embedded. However, further analysis shows that Theme 1 posts are associated with significantly lower repost volume. Regression results in Table 1A (see the appendix) show the performance of the three core narrative themes compared to all others in the dataset as a baseline; the coefficients show that Theme 1 posts are associated with significantly lower repost volume, with about 35 percent fewer reposts compared to the baseline.6The data includes posts highlighting Iranian battlefield successes, including both real operations and fabricated or exaggerated military achievements. This is consistent with research reports spotlighting AI deepfakes depicting successful strikes on a U.S. military base and the U.S. embassy in Saudi Arabia. In addition, this theme is supported by Iran-nationalist and pro-regime posts, including claims that Iran is on the morally correct side of the conflict. Prior reporting also documented AI-generated content depicting Ayatollah Ali Khamenei as a martyr in the face of Western aggression, as well as depicting global pro-Khamenei protests. Recurring content also justified Iran’s deterrent posture by portraying military strength and nuclear capability as legitimate expressions of sovereignty and necessary means of self-protection against foreign aggression.This theme also incorporates narratives of Western weakness to reinforce the core claim of Iranian effectiveness. The data included posts depicting U.S. decline, Western failure, and the inability of U.S. and Israeli militaries to impose decisive costs, framing Iran as resilient and strategically effective. Three days before the initial U.S. strikes, Graphika detected a coordinated social media campaign that warned of the high cost of war while fabricating or exaggerating claims of Iranian capabilities. This same logic continued after the conflict began, relying on a fabricated stream of content focused on U.S. military casualties, declining morale, and the suffering of servicemembers and military families. For Western audiences, the narrative function appears to frame war with Iran as producing visible failure and pain for its adversaries, thereby reinforcing the perception of Iranian battlefield credibility.Theme 2: Israel’s War of ChoiceThe second theme centers on narratives portraying Israel as the principal aggressor in the conflict and the driving force behind U.S. involvement in the conflict. The spread of the second theme is depicted in Table 1. The Bluesky posts were on average reposted 54 times, liked 142 times, and commented on by at least seven users. In addition, an estimated 31 percent of the posts included images, and 30 percent had a video embedded. In Table 1A (see the appendix), the regression results show that Theme 2 posts are associated with higher repost volumes, with an estimated 20 percent more reposts compared a baseline of other themes.7 Evidence from recent reporting, also captured in the dataset, points to three distinct but overlapping discursive patterns that define this theme.First, Israel is portrayed as responsible for drawing the United States and its allies into conflict with Iran. In one example from Clemson University that mirrors posts examined in this study’s dataset, a Bluesky post questions why the United Kingdom is eager to fight in “Israel’s war” while showing an AI image of Trump stuck in Iran with Netanyahu looking on from above. This reflects a real critique of the conflict but creates a narrative in which Western governments are acting in Israel’s interests rather than their own. This messaging is likely designed to resonate with Western audiences already skeptical of a war with Iran or close alignment with Israel, who would be receptive to claims that their governments are sacrificing national autonomy.The second discursive pattern is built on human suffering and atrocity framing possibly intended to intensify anti-Israel sentiment. This line of messaging may be designed to strengthen Iran’s sovereignty by portraying Western governments as betraying their principles by aligning themselves with states willing to wage war with impunity. One example highlighted in external research appears to have amplified a real incident at Gandhi Hospital in Tehran. The posts framed the evacuation and damage from nearby strikes as a direct attack on a neonatal ward, contributing to widespread harm and fear among the Iranian public. In this study’s dataset, this narrative was reflected with posts centered on the Minab school strike, which formed 6.8 percent of BERTopic’s non-noise data. Even where the underlying events were real, posts used civilian suffering to support a broader moral narrative portraying Israel as genocidal.The third pattern challenges Israel’s legitimacy through an explicitly ideological anti-Zionist frame. Posts in this cluster repeatedly describe Israel as a terrorist, apartheid, or genocidal state and use the destruction in Gaza following Hamas’s October 2023 attack to support that narrative. These posts use toxic and hateful stylized spellings to depict Israel, such as “Jizzrael,” “zi0,” “sionazi,” and “IsraHell.” The regression results in Table 1A (see the appendix) highlight that a one unit increase in positive sentiment score, as measured by VADER, is associated with an estimated 36.6 percent decrease in reposts.8 This outcome means that posts that are more negative in sentiment perform better on the platform. There is an inverse outcome for toxicity, as a one unit increase in toxicity score is associated with an estimated 62.4 percent decrease in expected reposts.9 The results show that negative sentiment is estimated to support the spread of a post narrative compared to toxicity. Taken together, this messaging frames Israel as an illegitimate and destabilizing actor whose influence over Western governments and role in civilian suffering justified broader hostility toward both Israel and its allies.Theme 3: Exacerbating Public Divisions over the ConflictThe third theme captures narratives that are likely designed to make the conflict politically corrosive within the United States and other Western countries. This theme generated the strongest engagement on Bluesky. Posts in this category averaged 150 reposts, 470 likes, and 28 replies, with roughly 28 percent including images and 26 percent including video. The regression results from Table 1A (see the appendix) also highlight that posts in Theme 3 are associated with an estimated 41 percent more reposts compared to a baseline of other themes.10 Rather than persuading audiences that Iran is in the right, this messaging likely sought to make continued support for the conflict feel costly, deceptive, and contrary to national interests using several discursive patterns. The first depicts the conflict as the product of manipulative and self-interested U.S. leadership disconnected from the public. This aligns with a narrative reflected in an open letter by Iran’s president to the American public, which asks whose interests the war actually serves. It strikes at the core of recent polling, which finds that 61 percent of Americans disapprove of the conflict.One example of this theme contained in the study’s data exploits the partisan divide surrounding the conflict by framing the conflict as a distraction measure. Specifically, similar posts in the data mirror reported posts that argue that the conflict serves as a distraction from the Epstein files and the people involved with the scandal. Additional messaging suggests that the president started the conflict to “distract us from the real issues.” There is also a separate effort that personalizes Iran’s disinformation campaign by targeting the U.S. president’s family to inflame further partisan tensions; in one example, posts spread disinformation suggesting that Barron Trump bought oil futures shortly before the conflict. These narratives frame the conflict as the product of corrupt leadership serving private or political interests and not the American people.A second narrative pushed within this theme emphasizes broader American institutional and elite dysfunction by targeting American institutions and corporations. Foreign influence campaigns use major U.S. companies to deepen internal division and undermine confidence in the U.S. government itself. This includes Iranian groups targeting institutions such as tech companies, banks, and other high-visibility entities with cyberattacks, as well as opportunistically claiming responsibility for server outages. The effect is to pair visible disruption at iconic U.S. companies and institutions closely associated with American power and prosperity with narrative amplification, reinforcing the perception that the conflict is producing disorder for the United States beyond the battlefield.A third narrative translates the conflict into direct economic and human costs for Western audiences. Messaging focuses on disruption in the Strait of Hormuz, which the accounts use to frame the war as an immediate burden for the American public rather than a distant military operation. The internal dataset shows posts targeting pocketbook issues meant to make the conflict feel immediate to Americans. These include emphasizing rising gas and energy prices, food costs, inflation, and trade-offs between war spending and healthcare or Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) funding. Through this view, the conflict becomes a source of household insecurity and further evidence that U.S. and Israeli leaders are acting recklessly and against their own publics’ interests. On the human cost side, this pressure campaign has also involved large-scale disinformation efforts using AI-generated videos of U.S. flag-draped coffins, servicemembers suffering and regretting the war, and children pleading with parents not to fight.Finally, the last framing extends beyond the United States, likely intending to widen divisions across Western allies. The internal dataset included a distinct BERTopic cluster centered on Australian political hashtags, suggesting the presence of an Iranian-linked disinformation campaign in Australian social media circles. Clemson research also identified a parallel network of IRGC-affiliated accounts posing as users from England, Scotland, and Ireland while pushing domestic political content tailored to those audiences, along with a Spanish-language cluster posing as users in the Americas, including accounts claiming to be in Texas, California, Venezuela, and Chile. The internal dataset complements this finding with accounts posting in Spanish and Portuguese. Taken together, these patterns suggest that the campaign sought not only to deepen divisions within the United States, but to weaken public support for the conflict across the broader Western coalition and turn the conflict into a source of friction for the West.Accounts Are Embedded Within 15 CommunitiesThe core 19 accounts and their Iran-aligned narratives mainly circulate within standalone groups of users (network communities) and rarely bridge over into other communities. Their embeddedness represents a challenge for social media platforms to identify them at scale, but agentic AI could flag accounts for review by humans based on their community structure. Figure 1 shows a repost network in which everyday users are linked to the 19 core user accounts based on observed repost interactions. The network structure indicates that the 19 core users are connected to a set of 15 distinct network communities on Bluesky with strong internal density and few ties to other users, with some core accounts occupying more central network positions and receiving higher repost exposure than others.\nRemote Visualization\nIn addition, the analysis finds that post amplifications are concentrated in a small set of core accounts. Table 2 captures repost exposure and shows that it is highly uneven across the network. Core accounts 5 and 6 received the largest repost exposure (weighted_in = 5,489 and 5,301), followed by a second tier including core account 15 (2,470), core account 10 (2,268), and core account 1 (1,934). This concentration indicates that narrative circulation is not evenly distributed across all 19 core accounts. Instead, a small number of accounts absorb a disproportionate share of repost activity and therefore carry more distributional weight in the observed network period. Breadth and structural position suggest different roles across core accounts. Comparing repost breadth (in_degree) with exposure (weighted_in) shows that some accounts are broad and deep amplifiers, while others are narrower but still represent high-volume nodes.11 For example, core account 5 combines the highest exposure with the widest repost base (3,048 distinct reposting accounts), whereas core account 10 has high exposure with a narrower repost base (587). Page rank values reinforce this hierarchy, with top accounts occupying more central network positions.12 The edge betweenness is near zero for most top nodes, meaning that leading accounts function less as bridges between communities and more as primary endpoints of amplification within their own community structures.13\nRemote Visualization\nAdditional network association-rule analysis (see Table 1A in the appendix) shows that amplification is highly segmented by community. Several communities map almost one-to-one to specific core accounts, with near-perfect confidence. For example, the analysis shows with 100 percent confidence that accounts in community 9 are likely to engage with core account 5, accounts in community 3 with core account 15 (0.999), accounts in community 6 with core account 1 (0.999), and accounts in community 11 with core account 12 (0.999). This pattern indicates that much of repost behavior is not random across the network but concentrated within distinct community-account pairings. The lift values reinforce that these are strong associations above baseline prevalence. High-lift rules—such as community 14 to core account 9 (lift = 68.8), community 12 to core account 8 (59.6), and community 8 to core account 4 (57.1)—suggest unusually strong affinity between specific communities and target core accounts.14 At the same time, some communities split attention across two accounts, such as community 7 (core account 18 at 0.522 confidence; core account 19 at 0.478) and community 4 (core account 2 at 0.771; core account 16 at 0.263), indicating mixed amplification pathways rather than a single dominant target. The association rule findings suggest that digital communities share one to two core accounts spreading Iranian narratives that amplify the content. Removing accounts that are spreading likely disinformation narratives therefore requires systematic deployment to find and target accounts for review by human moderators to disrupt actors that feed similar posts in digital communities. RecommendationsAs the conflict ebbs and flows between the United States, Iran, and Israel, the United States needs to start fighting back in the information space. This report proposes three recommendations aimed at countering Iranian narratives by punching back with visuals and videos to counter-message, creating transparency around posts, and using agentic AI to counter disinformation accounts at scale.Expand Counter-Messaging with Visuals and VideoTo deny Iran the American psyche and push back against Iranian narratives, the White House should reach across the aisle to garner bipartisan support for a messaging campaign. In this manner, the administration could gain additional support and buy-in from local political and corporate elites that could join the administration in a truthful, coordinated messaging campaign. The statistical analysis conducted as part of this study shows that, within the analyzed dataset, posts with video content are estimated to have performed better on Bluesky. Any U.S. agency supporting the mission in Iran could use this to their advantage to (1) counter-message using videos or images and (2) counteract the fake AI videos using confirmed combat footage suitable for a wider audience. This will help communicate to the U.S. and wider Western public in a manner that will spread further than text posts and deflate the deepfakes. The Trump administration has experience sharing such visuals, in recent months sharing videos of U.S. strikes that targeted suspected drug smuggling boats and destroyed Iran’s navy in under 10 days.Bring Back Post Labeling for State-Sponsored Accounts and PostsIn addition, to dissuade engagement and increase transparency around Iranian-backed accounts, the U.S. government should establish a partnership with social media platforms to request a proactive response by industry to label state-sponsored propaganda posts. Research has shown that users are less likely to engage with content on social media platforms when they are better informed about its source. The labeling regime could take different forms but should balance the need to counter-message with First Amendment rights protecting freedom of speech on online platforms. For example, this could be accomplished using a combination of verified account badges and digital signatures for videos. To combat the speed of dissemination, posts could be labeled with warnings that indicate a story may be rapidly evolving, unverified by major news sources, or under dispute.Require Industry to Deploy AI Agents to Take Down Disinformation AccountsThe age of AI is here, and the U.S. government should work with social media platforms to deploy agentic AI not only for counter-messaging but also to identify accounts, posts, and bot farms for review and shut them down. Research highlights that the U.S. government uses AI to defend against propaganda but limits offensive actions during peacetime and is therefore disadvantaged. With the conflict between the United States and Iran on pause, now is an opportune window for social media platforms to coordinate with the Department of War to detect and dismantle troll farms and disinformation accounts and free digital communities from Iranian propaganda.ConclusionInformation operations shape beliefs, exploit social fractures, and leave behind distrust that outlasts conflicts themselves. This research presents a likely pro-Iranian campaign on Bluesky by users organized around narratives of Iranian military resilience, anti-Israel framing, and division across Western publics. This reflects a strategic calculation that losses on the battlefield can be offset in the information space and shape public opinion against policy implementation. The significance extends beyond this war. By amplifying tensions that already exist, Iran is attempting not only to weaken support for the current conflict, but also to erode the political resolve and alliance cohesion the United States and its allies will need long after the fighting stops. That is why the United States should fight back through counter-messaging, transparent post labeling, and AI deployed at scale. This is the first of many fights to come to protect the American public from foreign influence during kinetic conflict. Thus, what the United States does today will shape the future of information warfare for its allies and partners; it’s imperative that the United States shields its public and seizes the narrative.Jose M. Macias III is an associate data fellow in the Futures Lab within the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Nico Vacca is a research intern in the Futures Lab.The authors wish to thank Kelsey Hartman for her outstanding review and analytical copyediting in this white paper.This report is made possible by general support to CSIS. No direct sponsorship contributed to this report.Please see the PDF for the footnotes and appendix.\nThis report is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nMiddle East,\nIran,\nAmericas,\nArtificial Intelligence,\nCivil Society,\nCybersecurity,\nDefense and Security,\nGeopolitics and International Security, and\nTechnology\nImage\nJose M. Macias III\nAssociate Fellow, Futures Lab\nNico Vacca\nResearch Intern, Futures Lab\nPrograms & Projects\nFutures Lab\nDefense and Security\nLatest Analysis: War with Iran\nCompetition Lab\nAI and the Future of Conflict\nRelated Content\nImage\nBeyond Bullets and Bombs: The Rising Tide of Information War in International Affairs\nCommentary\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nDivya Ramjee\n— December 20, 2023","content_cn":"利用美国对旷日持久战争的厌恶：伊朗叙事在 Bluesky 上的传播\n照片：Bilal Ulker/Adobe Stock\n目录\n目录\n报告\n作者\nJose M. Macias III\n和\nNico Vacca\n发表于 2026 年 5 月 4 日\n可下载内容\n下载完整报告\n555kb\n远程可视化\n引言美国和以色列在与伊朗的冲突中已取得战场上的进展，但美国正努力遏制伊朗的宣传。军事行动上的成功已经清除了伊朗的威权最高领袖，瓦解了其国防领导机构，并削弱了其导弹能力。然而，对美国而言，军事成功的机会成本，是在争取美国受众以及更广泛的西方受众的心灵与思想的信息战中失去阵地。伊朗虽在战场上失利，却通过一场激进的多平台虚假信息宣传活动，有效地在信息空间展开竞争。未来实验室对 9,000 多条 Bluesky Social 帖子的分析发现，似乎旨在加剧公众分裂的信息占数据集的 23%，是表现最好的内容，平均每条帖子获得 150 次转发、470 次点赞和 28 条回复。这些帖子估计已被 293,666 名用户浏览，并在统计上与更高的分享量显著相关；与其他帖子相比，预计增加 41%。1 此外，网络与关联分析确定了 19 个传播伊朗战争叙事的核心账号，并发现它们活跃于 15 个社群中；据估计，其中 11 个社群严重依赖单一账号，3 个社群与不止一个账号互动。2 伊朗的虚假信息剧本此前已成功将以色列作为目标，应用于与哈马斯和真主党的冲突中。10 月 7 日袭击事件之后，微软报告称伊朗增加了针对以色列的网络行动。此外，伊朗的影响行动从 2021 年的大约 6 次增加到仅 2023 年 10 月就达 11 次；伊朗行为者还反复重新使用旧 footage，却虚假地将其呈现为以色列的袭击。据报道，Meta 关闭了与伊朗有关联的账号，这些账号推动有关以色列境内宗教分歧、自由派和保守派对以色列与哈马斯战争的批评，以及对总理本雅明·内塔尼亚胡的批评的叙事，可能是为了煽动以色列内部的紧张局势。在 2025 年美国和以色列袭击伊朗核设施之后，来自伊朗账号的叙事产生了超过 126,000 次互动，预计浏览量达 2.24 亿。如今，伊朗的虚假信息机器似乎正将西方受众作为目标，以削弱对美国—以色列与伊朗政权冲突的支持。微软、Cyabra 和《纽约时报》的研究显示，伊朗开展了针对美国的更广泛社交媒体影响行动。为评估这场行动的一部分，未来实验室利用来自 Bluesky Social 的开放源数据、BERTopic 以及 Claude 的 Sonnet 4.6 模型，对疑似伊朗虚假信息账号的 Bluesky 信息流中的叙事进行分类。研究团队确定了支撑伊朗虚假信息行动的三个核心主题：（1）将伊朗军队和领导层描绘成既受害又成功；（2）将冲突描述为以色列主动选择的战争；以及（3）利用言辞放大美国国内及其盟友之间围绕冲突的分歧，和\n合作伙伴。通过应用统计分析，研究团队发现，第三个主题与更大的传播范围呈正相关。研究团队还使用了网络分析，结果显示，19个核心账户深度嵌入15个Bluesky社群。为应对这些叙事，美国政府应与社交媒体平台合作，制定视觉化反信息传播活动，以抵消人工智能伪造内容；建立国家支持帖子标注制度；并利用代理式人工智能（AI）系统性地侦测并瓦解宣传网络。\n\n一套经过完善的行动方案\n\n伊朗的信息行动遵循一种熟悉的不对称行动方案：当与美国和以色列军事力量进行直接竞争处于不利地位时，目标就从战场上的势均力敌转向持续开展行动，通过提高冲突相关的政治成本来削弱对冲突的支持。俄罗斯和中国的虚假信息行动确立了这一行动方案，如今伊朗已加以调整并采用。俄罗斯的行动通常集中于放大社会内部的分歧，并侵蚀人们对西方民主制度的信任。中国的行动通常旨在塑造对中国共产党（中共）的正面看法，并压制可能使该政权失去合法性的敏感议题叙事，例如台湾、南海和新疆，但两国使用的策略存在重叠。Graphika近期的监测发现，伊朗冲突造成了一个拥挤的线上威胁环境，其特征包括信息战——其中包含深度伪造和其他人工智能生成媒体——虚假信息、网络攻击，以及跨平台协调开展的不真实活动。微软2024年关于以色列—哈马斯战争期间伊朗网络赋能影响活动的报告显示，伊朗如何利用虚假身份和被操纵的内容，将一场地区冲突转化为西方国家政治压力的来源。人工智能的进步正通过扩大虚假信息行动的规模和迭代周期，加速这一进程。伊朗正将一种由人工智能加速的、已确立的不对称信息战 doctrine 应用于一个已经因该冲突而分裂的美国国内受众。\n\n研究设计与方法\n\n为分析与伊朗战略利益一致、可能助推伊朗信息战的叙事，研究团队开展了内容分析与网络分析相结合的研究，识别信息传播、互动和扩散模式。研究单位被定义为Bluesky用户。研究人员使用R编程语言，将研究范围缩小至Bluesky，因为该平台提供免费、开放访问的应用程序编程接口（API）。这一平台选择也缩小了所观察的受众范围。截至2025年7月，Bluesky的网页流量最集中于美国（约50%），其次是日本（约6%）、英国（约5%）、德国（约5%）和巴西（约4%）。由于该平台70%的用户群体由西方受众构成，因此不应将此分析理解为涵盖世界所有地区。\n\n接下来，研究团队审阅了现有研究，以识别可能属于伊朗虚假信息账户的模式，并从其Bluesky信息源中提取数据。具体而言，既有研究表明，伊朗虚假信息账户关注的政策议题涵盖反以色列、支持巴勒斯坦以及反特朗普政府等叙事。较新的研究\n发现了一些使用人工智能深度伪造和多语种活动的账户，以及伊朗国家媒体通过夸大和捏造袭击美国军事基地、美国飞机和以色列城市的说法，推动战场上虚假成功的叙事。基于这些战术、技术和程序（TTP），研究人员分析了 Bluesky 的发现动态，并选出了表现出重叠 TTP 的账户。这些 TTP 包括类似机器人的发帖行为、分享被美国司法部标记的伊朗网站链接、在短时间内持续发帖或转发、有限的自然互动、在机会主义叙事之间快速转变，以及其他与先前报道一致、可能不真实的倾向。尽管这些 TTP 无法确定性地证明伊朗归属，但它们识别出了一批高可信度的可疑账户，这些账户与此前报道的伊朗关联影响活动相符。\n\n随后，研究人员查询了每个用户动态中 2026 年 2 月 28 日至 2026 年 3 月 31 日期间的帖子。这包括核心用户发布的帖子、转发内容，以及这些帖子的原作者数据——包括转发、点赞和评论历史——以构建用于叙事、情感和网络分析的数据集。需要注意的是，数据中的并非所有帖子都由核心账户生成。据估计，其中 58% 来自 19 个核心账户，其余则是核心用户从出现在其动态中的账户转发的内容。\n\n为对疑似伊朗虚假信息账户的叙事进行分类，团队将 BERTopic 句子转换器与 Claude 的 Sonnet 4.6 大型语言模型相结合。3 BERTopic 用于识别帖子中的关键主题，而 Sonnet 则分析其中的叙事主题。研究团队随后分析 BERTopic 和 Sonnet 的分组结果，创建了 10 个叙事集群，并将其整合为三大主题，本文将对此进行介绍。研究还表明，负面或仇恨内容可能在社交媒体上获得超额传播并走红。为纳入对负面和有毒内容的测量，团队采用 vaderSentiment 的复合得分（范围为 -1（负面）至 1（正面））以及 Detoxify 的毒性得分（范围为 0（无毒）至 1（有毒）），以量化帖子情感和毒性水平，从而评估这些内容可能进一步传播伊朗叙事的程度。4 此外，团队利用帖子层面的点赞和转发指标，构建了将非核心互动者连接至疑似核心账户的有向互动边列表，随后使用 NetworkX 和 R 对传播进行分析和可视化。5 表 1 展示了对 9,012 条帖子的汇总统计。研究团队还使用回归模型对数据集进行了进一步分析，相关内容将在结果部分讨论，并在附录中展示。最终数据集包括 19 个核心用户动态中的帖子关键主题和叙事类别，这些内容用于确立本文深入解析的三大核心主题。本文未公开 19 个核心账户及分享和参与其内容的用户身份。\n\n本研究并未声称所选 Bluesky 账户明确归属于伊朗政府、伊斯兰革命卫队（IRGC）或任何协调一致的外国影响行动。相反，研究分析的是这样一批账户：之所以选中它们，是因为其发帖行为、叙事主题、链接分享模式和放大行为，与既有关于伊朗关联影响行动的研究中记录的策略相似。研究结果\n因此，不应将其解读为这些账号属于伊朗国家支持的影响力行动的确凿证据。\n\n结果\n远程可视化\n主题1：受害但成功的军事力量与领导力\n出现的第一个主题，围绕着将伊朗的进攻和防御行动描绘为军事力量、能力和战略合法性的证据展开。第一主题的传播情况如表1所示。Bluesky上的帖子平均被转发45次，获赞138次，并至少有7名用户评论。此外，估计有30%的帖子包含图片，近40%的帖子嵌入了视频。然而，进一步分析显示，主题1的帖子与显著较低的转发量相关。表1A（见附录）中的回归结果，以数据集中所有其他帖子作为基线，展示了三个核心叙事主题的表现；系数显示，主题1的帖子与显著较低的转发量相关，与基线相比，转发量约少35%。6\n这些数据包括突出伊朗战场胜利的帖子，其中既有真实行动，也有捏造或夸大的军事成就。这与相关研究报告一致，后者重点关注描绘成功袭击美国军事基地和美国驻沙特阿拉伯大使馆的人工智能深度伪造内容。此外，这一主题还得到伊朗民族主义和亲政权帖子的支持，包括声称伊朗在冲突中站在道德正确一方的说法。此前的报道还记录了人工智能生成的内容，将阿亚图拉·阿里·哈梅内伊描绘成面对西方侵略的烈士，以及描绘全球亲哈梅内伊抗议活动的内容。反复出现的内容还通过将军事力量和核能力描绘为主权的合法体现，以及抵御外国侵略、保护自身所必需的手段，为伊朗的威慑立场辩护。\n这一主题还纳入了有关西方弱点的叙事，以强化伊朗有效性的核心主张。数据包括描绘美国衰落、西方失败，以及美国和以色列军队无法施加决定性代价的帖子，将伊朗塑造成坚韧且在战略上有效的一方。在最初的美国打击发生前三天，Graphika发现了一场协调一致的社交媒体行动，该行动警告战争代价高昂，同时捏造或夸大有关伊朗能力的说法。冲突开始后，这种逻辑仍在延续，依靠一股捏造的内容流，聚焦于美国军方伤亡、士气下降，以及军人和军人家庭所遭受的痛苦。对于西方受众而言，这一叙事的功能似乎是将与伊朗的战争框定为给其对手带来显而易见的失败与痛苦，从而强化伊朗在战场上的可信度。\n主题2：以色列选择发动的战争\n第二个主题围绕着将以色列描绘为冲突中的主要侵略者，以及推动美国介入冲突的驱动力的叙事展开。第二主题的传播情况如表1所示。Bluesky上的帖子平均被转发54次，获赞142次，并至少有7名用户评论。此外，估计有31%的帖子包含图片，30%的帖子嵌入了视频。在表1A（见附录）中，回归结果显示，主题2的帖子与更高的转发量相关，估计多出20%的\n转发量与其他主题的基线进行了比较。7 数据集中还收录了近期报道的证据，这些证据指出了定义该主题的三种彼此不同但相互重叠的话语模式。首先，以色列被描绘成将美国及其盟友拖入与伊朗冲突的责任方。克莱姆森大学的一个例子与本研究数据集中考察的帖子相呼应：一条 Bluesky 帖子在展示一张特朗普被困在伊朗、内塔尼亚胡从上方俯视的 AI 图片时，质疑英国为何热衷于参与“以色列的战争”。这反映了对冲突的真实批评，但构建出这样一种叙事：西方政府是在为以色列的利益行事，而非为自身利益行事。这类信息很可能旨在引起那些已经对与伊朗开战或与以色列紧密结盟持怀疑态度的西方受众的共鸣；他们会接受这样的说法，即本国政府正在牺牲国家自主权。第二种话语模式建立在人类苦难和暴行框架之上，可能意在强化反以色列情绪。这种信息线索可能旨在通过将西方政府描绘成因与那些可以有恃无恐发动战争的国家结盟而背叛自身原则，从而增强伊朗的主权。外部研究中强调的一个例子似乎放大了德黑兰甘地医院发生的一起真实事件。相关帖子将疏散和附近袭击造成的损害描述为对新生儿病房的直接攻击，在伊朗公众中造成了广泛的伤害和恐惧。在本研究的数据集中，这一叙事通过围绕米纳卜学校遭袭的帖子体现出来，该主题占 BERTopic 非噪声数据的 6.8%。即使底层事件是真实的，这些帖子也利用平民苦难来支持一种更广泛的道德叙事，将以色列描绘成种族灭绝国家。第三种模式通过明确的意识形态反犹太复国主义框架挑战以色列的合法性。该聚类中的帖子反复将以色列描述为恐怖主义国家、种族隔离国家或种族灭绝国家，并利用哈马斯 2023 年 10 月袭击后加沙遭到的破坏来支持这一叙事。这些帖子使用有毒且充满仇恨的变体拼写来描绘以色列，例如“Jizzrael”“zi0”“sionazi”和“IsraHell”。表 1A（见附录）中的回归结果强调，VADER 测量的正面情绪得分每增加一个单位，转发量估计减少 36.6%。8 这意味着，情绪更负面的帖子在该平台上的表现更好。毒性则呈现相反结果：毒性得分每增加一个单位，预期转发量估计减少 62.4%。9 结果显示，与毒性相比，负面情绪被估计更有助于帖子叙事的传播。综合来看，这些信息将以色列描述为一个不合法且破坏稳定的行为体；其对西方政府的影响以及在平民苦难中的作用，被视为对以色列及其盟友抱持更广泛敌意的正当理由。主题 3：加剧公众对冲突的分歧第三个主题涵盖了可能旨在使冲突在美国及其他西方国家内部造成政治腐蚀的叙事。该主题在 Bluesky 上获得了最强的互动。此类帖子平均获得 150 次转发、470 个赞和 28 条回复，其中约 28% 包含图片，26%\n包括视频在内。附录中的表1A回归结果还凸显出，与其他主题的基线相比，主题3中的帖子预计多获得41%的转发。10 与其说是说服受众相信伊朗是正义的一方，这些信息更可能是通过若干话语模式，让人觉得继续支持这场冲突代价高昂、具有欺骗性，并且违背国家利益。\n\n第一种模式将冲突描绘成与公众脱节、操纵性强且谋求私利的美国领导层的产物。这与伊朗总统致美国公众的一封公开信中所反映的叙事一致，该信询问战争实际上服务于谁的利益。它直击近期民调的核心；民调发现，61%的美国人不赞成这场冲突。研究数据中包含的一个该主题示例，利用围绕冲突的党派分歧，将冲突描述为一种转移注意力的措施。具体而言，数据中的类似帖子呼应了已有报道中的帖子，后者认为冲突是为了转移人们对爱泼斯坦文件及涉入该丑闻人员的注意力。其他信息则暗示，总统发动冲突是为了“让我们不要去关注真正的问题”。此外，还有一项单独行动，通过针对美国总统家人来煽动更深的党派紧张局势，使伊朗的虚假信息行动个人化；其中一个例子是，帖子传播虚假信息，声称巴伦·特朗普在冲突发生前不久买入了石油期货。这些叙事将冲突描述为腐败领导层服务于私人或政治利益、而非美国人民利益的产物。\n\n该主题推动的第二种叙事，通过针对美国机构和企业，强调更广泛的美国制度与精英失灵。外国影响行动利用美国大型企业加深内部对立，并削弱人们对美国政府本身的信心。这包括伊朗组织对科技公司、银行及其他高知名度实体等机构发动网络攻击，并伺机声称对服务器宕机负责。其效果是，将美国标志性企业和与美国实力及繁荣密切相关的机构所遭受的显著破坏，与叙事放大结合起来，强化这样一种观感：这场冲突正在战场之外给美国造成混乱。\n\n第三种叙事将冲突转化为西方受众可以直接感受到的经济和人力成本。相关信息聚焦于霍尔木兹海峡的中断，相关账号借此将战争描述为美国公众眼下的负担，而非一场遥远的军事行动。内部数据集显示，一些帖子瞄准民生经济问题，意在让美国人切身感受到冲突的迫近。这些内容包括强调不断上涨的汽油和能源价格、食品成本、通货膨胀，以及战争支出与医疗保健或补充营养援助计划（SNAP）资金之间的取舍。通过这种视角，冲突成为家庭不安全感的来源，也进一步证明美国和以色列领导人行事鲁莽，违背了本国公众的利益。在人力成本方面，这场施压行动还包括大规模虚假信息活动，利用人工智能生成的美国国旗覆盖的棺材、遭受痛苦并后悔参战的军人，以及苦苦哀求的儿童\n父母不要争斗。最后，这一框架延伸到美国之外，可能意在扩大西方盟友之间的分歧。内部数据集包含一个以澳大利亚政治标签为中心的独立 BERTopic 聚类，这表明伊朗关联的虚假信息行动可能存在于澳大利亚社交媒体圈。克莱姆森大学的研究还发现了一个平行网络，其中隶属于 IRGC 的账号伪装成来自英格兰、苏格兰和爱尔兰的用户，同时发布针对这些受众定制的国内政治内容；此外，还有一个西班牙语聚类，伪装成美洲地区的用户，包括声称位于得克萨斯州、加利福尼亚州、委内瑞拉和智利的账号。内部数据集通过发布西班牙语和葡萄牙语内容的账号补充了这一发现。综合来看，这些模式表明，该行动不仅试图加深美国内部的分歧，还试图削弱西方更广泛联盟中公众对冲突的支持，并将冲突变成西方内部摩擦的来源。\n\n账号嵌入15个社群之中\n\n核心的19个账号及其亲伊朗叙事主要在彼此独立的用户群体（网络社群）内部传播，很少跨越到其他社群中。它们的嵌入性使社交媒体平台难以大规模识别这些账号，但智能体式人工智能可以根据账号的社群结构标记账号，供人工审核。图1展示了一个转发网络，其中普通用户根据观察到的转发互动与这19个核心用户账号相连。网络结构表明，这19个核心用户与 Bluesky 上15个不同的网络社群相连接；这些社群内部密度高，与其他用户的联系很少，其中一些核心账号处于更中心的网络位置，并比其他账号获得更高的转发曝光量。\n\n远程可视化\n\n此外，分析发现，帖文的扩散集中在少数核心账号上。表2记录了转发曝光量，并显示其在网络中高度不均衡。核心账号5和6获得了最大的转发曝光量（weighted_in 分别为5,489和5,301），其次是第二梯队，包括核心账号15（2,470）、核心账号10（2,268）和核心账号1（1,934）。这种集中表明，叙事传播并不是在全部19个核心账号之间均匀分布的。相反，少数账号吸收了不成比例的转发活动，因此在所观察的网络期间承担了更大的分发权重。广度和结构位置表明，各核心账号承担着不同角色。比较转发广度（in_degree）与曝光量（weighted_in）可以看出，一些账号是广泛且深入的扩散者，而另一些账号覆盖面较窄，但仍是高流量节点。11 例如，核心账号5同时拥有最高曝光量和最广泛的转发基础（3,048个不同的转发账号），而核心账号10拥有较高曝光量，但转发基础较窄（587个）。网页排名值进一步印证了这一层级关系，排名靠前的账号处于更中心的网络位置。12 对大多数顶级节点而言，边介数接近于零，这意味着领先账号较少充当社群之间的桥梁，而更多是在自身社群结构内充当扩散的主要终点。13\n\n远程可视化\n\n其他网络关联规则分析（见附录中的表1A）显示，\n放大传播高度按社群分化。多个社群几乎一一对应于特定的核心账户，置信度接近完美。例如，分析以100%的置信度显示，社群9中的账户可能会与核心账户5互动，社群3中的账户与核心账户15互动（0.999），社群6中的账户与核心账户1互动（0.999），社群11中的账户与核心账户12互动（0.999）。这一模式表明，大量转发行为并非在网络中随机发生，而是集中于不同的社群—账户配对之内。提升度值进一步表明，这些关联显著高于基准普及率。高提升度规则——例如社群14指向核心账户9（提升度 = 68.8）、社群12指向核心账户8（59.6），以及社群8指向核心账户4（57.1）——表明特定社群与目标核心账户之间存在异常强的亲和关系。14 与此同时，一些社群会将注意力分散到两个账户上，例如社群7（核心账户18的置信度为0.522；核心账户19为0.478）和社群4（核心账户2为0.771；核心账户16为0.263），这表明存在混合式放大路径，而非单一的主导目标。关联规则分析结果表明，数字社群会共享一到两个传播伊朗叙事、放大相关内容的核心账户。因此，删除那些传播可能含有虚假信息叙事的账户，需要系统性部署，以发现并锁定账户，交由人工审核员审查，从而扰乱在数字社群中传播类似帖子的行动者。建议随着美国、伊朗和以色列之间的冲突起伏变化，美国需要开始在信息空间进行反击。本报告提出三项建议，旨在通过视觉素材和视频进行反击式传播、围绕帖子建立透明度，以及利用智能体式AI大规模应对虚假信息账户，从而遏制伊朗叙事。扩大使用视觉素材和视频开展反向传播为了不让伊朗操纵美国人的心理并反击伊朗叙事，白宫应跨越党派鸿沟，争取两党对一场传播活动的支持。这样一来，政府可以获得地方政治和企业精英的额外支持与认同，让他们加入政府，开展真实且协调一致的传播活动。本研究开展的统计分析显示，在所分析的数据集中，带有视频内容的帖子预计在Bluesky上的表现更好。任何支持伊朗相关任务的美国机构都可以利用这一点：（1）使用视频或图像进行反向传播；（2）使用适合更广泛受众的、经确认的战斗画面，反制虚假的AI视频。这将有助于以一种比文字帖子传播更广的方式向美国及更广泛的西方公众传达信息，并削弱深度伪造。特朗普政府拥有分享此类视觉素材的经验，近几个月来曾分享美国空袭疑似走私毒品船只的视频，并在不到10天内摧毁伊朗海军。恢复对国家支持账户和帖子的标注此外，为劝阻互动并提高围绕伊朗支持账户的透明度，美国政府应与社交媒体平台建立合作关系，要求行业主动采取措施，对国家支持的宣传内容进行标注\n帖子。研究表明，当用户更了解社交媒体平台上内容的来源时，他们与这些内容互动的可能性会降低。标注制度可以采取不同形式，但应在反制信息的需要与保护在线平台言论自由的美国宪法第一修正案权利之间取得平衡。例如，可以结合使用经过验证的账户徽章和视频数字签名来实现这一点。为应对传播速度，帖子可以添加警告，提示某一报道可能正在快速发展、尚未得到主要新闻来源的核实，或存在争议。要求行业部署人工智能代理以取缔虚假信息账户人工智能时代已经到来，美国政府应与社交媒体平台合作，部署具备代理能力的人工智能，不仅用于发布反制信息，还用于识别账户、帖子和机器人农场，以供审查并将其关闭。研究指出，美国政府利用人工智能防御宣传，但在和平时期限制进攻性行动，因此处于不利地位。随着美国与伊朗之间的冲突暂时停火，现在正是社交媒体平台与战争部协调、侦测并瓦解网络水军农场和虚假信息账户、使数字社区摆脱伊朗宣传的有利时机。结论信息行动塑造信念、利用社会裂痕，并留下持续时间超过冲突本身的不信任。本研究展示了Bluesky上一个可能由亲伊朗用户发起的宣传活动，这些用户围绕伊朗军事韧性、反以色列叙事以及西方公众内部的分裂组织起来。这反映出一种战略盘算：战场上的损失可以通过信息空间予以抵消，并塑造公众舆论，使其反对政策实施。其重要性超越了这场战争。伊朗通过放大既有的紧张关系，不仅试图削弱对当前冲突的支持，也试图侵蚀美国及其盟友在战斗停止很久之后仍将需要的政治决心和联盟凝聚力。这就是为什么美国应通过反制信息、透明的帖子标注以及大规模部署人工智能来反击。这是未来众多斗争中的第一场，旨在保护美国公众免受动能冲突期间外国影响。因此，美国今天采取的行动将塑造其盟友和伙伴未来的信息战；美国必须保护其公众并掌握叙事。Jose M. Macias III是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心防务与安全部未来实验室的数据助理研究员。Nico Vacca是未来实验室的研究实习生。作者感谢Kelsey Hartman对本白皮书出色的审阅和分析性文字编辑。本报告得以完成，得益于对CSIS的普遍支持。没有任何直接赞助为本报告提供资金。脚注和附录请参见PDF。\n本报告由战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人、免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2026 by the\n战略与国际研究中心。保留所有权利。\n标签\n中东、\n伊朗、\n美洲、\n人工智能、\n公民社会、\n网络安全、\n国防与安全、\n地缘政治与国际安全，以及\n技术\n图片\nJose M. Macias III\n未来实验室副研究员\nNico Vacca\n未来实验室研究实习生\n项目与计划\n未来实验室\n国防与安全\n最新分析：与伊朗的战争\n竞争实验室\n人工智能与冲突的未来\n相关内容\n图片\n超越子弹与炸弹：国际事务中信息战的浪潮日益高涨\n评论\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n和\nDivya Ramjee\n——2023年12月20日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-05-04T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-455","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"Token政治：美国如何与中国竞争构建全球人工智能栈","title_en":"Tokenpolitik: How the United States Can Compete with China to Build the Global AI Stack","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表评论文章，提出“Token政治”概念，主张美国应制定跨部门大战略，联合国务院、商务部、财政部、DFC、EXIM及盟友与私营企业，在全球南方建设数据中心、电力与算力基础设施，以对抗中国通过产业政策、开源模型和华为打包方案构建的AI栈，避免在人工智能时代的大国竞争中落后。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary proposes \"tokenpolitik,\" a grand strategy urging the United States to coordinate State, Commerce, Treasury, DFC, EXIM, allies, and private firms to build datacenters, power, and compute capacity in the Global South, countering China's industrial policy, open-weight models, and Huawei packaged offerings across the AI stack.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:02:37.066839+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/tokenpolitik-how-united-states-can-compete-china-build-global-ai-stack","content":"Tokenpolitik: How the United States Can Compete with China to Build the Global AI Stack\nPhoto: Kyle Grillot/Bloomberg via Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nYasir Atalan\nPublished August 3, 2026\nThere is a new form of great power competition that is set to define the future of international system. While the United States and China were negotiating behind the scenes to hold formal talks on artificial intelligence, President Xi Jinping made a public speech proclaiming Beijing as the leader for a new global AI order. The race is on to see who will build the network of datacenters, power plants, and models that are redefining power and international relations.That race pits U.S. firms against state industrial policy in China that is actively promoting its national champions to build lower-cost chips and high-performance open-weight models as alternatives to U.S. technology. At the same time, Beijing has accused Washington of “AI hegemonism” amid possible U.S. investigations into how Chinese firms trained their models using knowledge distillation.The Trump administration has identified the magnitude of the challenge and undertaken a number of initiatives to ensure the United States remains the world’s AI superpower. But these efforts have yet to coalesce into a broader grand strategy that shapes how the United States engages with the world beyond its borders.What is missing is a clear concept of “tokenpolitik”: a grand strategy that bridges multiple agencies within the U.S. government with the private sector to create a new economic and security architecture for the twenty-first century. This strategy would focus on AI’s role in defining power and interest in the decades ahead and provide a framework for the U.S. government to back its private sector firms building datacenters, power plants, and research labs. This would allow U.S. firms to curate the data and new models necessary to ensure a free society and free market ushers in the AI era.What Is New Is Old: Infrastructure as StrategyRealpolitik is about power, but power has never existed only in the barrel of a gun, and has often extended to infrastructure. The French-led Suez Canal project changed the geography of trade between Europe and Asia. Britain opposed its construction, then purchased Egyptian government’s 44 percent shareholding in 1875 after the canal became essential to British commerce and imperial communications. The strategic question extended beyond building the canal to who would finance, govern, secure, and benefit from a new commercial artery.The Cold War saw similar examples. The U.S.-backed International Telecommunications Satellite Organization (INTELSAT) and Soviet-sponsored Intersputnik system competed to extend satellite communications across the world. The wider contest involved satellites, earth stations, orbital access, service prices, standards, technical assistance, and relationships with developing countries. Communications infrastructure became a means of organizing international dependence and influence.Artificial intelligence is producing a similar contest. Models are the most visible products, with close models from firms such as Google, OpenAI, and Anthropic competing against open-weight models from China (e.g., Kimi, DeepSeek, Qwen, and GLM). Yet, the deeper struggle concerns the layered system that makes them useful at scale: energy, chips, infrastructure, and applications. Together with models, these layers create a token supply chain that runs the AI applications increasingly common in government and the workplace.Tokens are the language and currency of the new AI order. A token is the fundamental atomic unit that a neural network ingests, processes and generates. In large language models (LLMs), it is the smallest unit of a text, generally a word, and in large audio-language models (LAMs), it becomes the smallest discrete unit of a continuous sound wave. Tokens represent a basic fragment of text, audio, or image translated into mathematical vector representations for a neural network to process. In the context of global competition, these tokens act as the ultimate industrial output of the massive datacenters and power grids dotting the globe. Every operational constraint in modern artificial intelligence, from the real-time cost of running an inference query to the finite memory of a context window, comes down to how efficiently a system parses and predicts these units. They function as the foundational currency of this new technological era.The great power that most effectively generates tokens through a network of state agencies and private sector firms will sit at the commanding heights of a new international system. The primary strategic question of the twenty-first century is who will build the token supply chain and wire key regions of the world for an AI future.China Is Backing Firms Across the Full StackThough China’s approach is often described as state-led, private firms remain central to innovation. The state shapes the environment around them by supplying capital, subsidized compute, infrastructure, research support, diplomatic backing, and protection from foreign pressure.Beijing is deploying industrial-policy tools across the full stack. These efforts include state-sponsored labs alongside major infrastructure investments including the $8 billion National AI Investment Fund in 2025 followed by a broader $295 billion spending plan for AI infrastructure buildout in 2026. Academic research on Chinese government venture capital similarly shows that public investment operates through a distributed system of national and local funds, helping some initially weaker firms attract private follow-on financing. The Chinese Communist Party also uses state-backed efforts to promote chip makers such as CXMT, which soared more than 466 percent on its stock market debut, YMTC, and infrastructure firms including Huawei.Open-weight models provide a second pillar of Beijing’s strategy. They can be downloaded, locally hosted, and more easily optimized for enterprise use cases. RAND research has argued that such models function as instruments of soft power by shaping developer communities, technical education, standards, and downstream applications.Recently, open-weight models emanating from Chinese labs have been shown to perform well in key AI benchmarks, on par with the US frontier AI models. Furthermore, prices per token are cheaper compared to the U.S.-based models. This puts extra pressure on the U.S. closed-source models as global adoption of open-weight models increases. There is now a growing influence of Chinese developers in the open-model economy. In fact, many U.S. enterprises started to experiment with these Chinese AI models, though they host these models on U.S.-based platforms.Huawei provides a physical distribution network for these models. Its established position in telecommunications allows it to offer governments packages combining datacenters, cloud services, accelerators, networking, cooling, software, training, and maintenance. In developing economies, a technically weaker chip can remain attractive when embedded in an affordable package from a supplier that already understands the national network and relevant ministries. China, through Huawei, has been installing these relationships in markets such as Brazil, Indonesia, Kenya, and the Middle East. The diplomatic narrative centered on access, sovereignty, open development, and capacity building in the Global South complements this trend.Washington Has Assembled Many of the PiecesThe United States starts with stronger private companies and deeper capital markets. U.S. investors accounted for more than half of global outgoing AI venture-capital investment in 2025, while infrastructure and hosting attracted the largest share of AI capital. Washington has now moved beyond the export-control-only strategy that dominated the Biden administration’s AI diffusion policy. The Trump administration, in line with the AI Action Plan, established the American AI Exports Program to promote full-stack American artificial intelligence technology packages to international allies and partners. These efforts rely on a mix of initiatives across the Departments of Commerce and State, including Pax Silica, which focuses on building “a secure, resilient, innovation-driven technology ecosystem” with allies and partners.Furthermore, there are now growing number of bilateral agreements with key actors. The U.S.-United Arab Emirates framework links a planned regional AI cluster with security, monitoring, and investment commitments. Commerce has authorized large chip purchases by G42 and Saudi Arabia’s Humain, subject to reporting and security conditions. The U.S. International Development Finance Corporation (DFC) and the Export-Import Bank of the United States (EXIM) provide financing foundations for these initiatives. DFC reports that AI datacenter investment has become the leading request from partner governments and identifies telecommunications, fiber, cloud infrastructure, datacenters, generation, and grids as components of the stack. EXIM’s ExportAI initiative offers export-credit insurance, working-capital guarantees, loan guarantees, and direct lending for U.S. technology.Still, what is missing is an overarching grand strategy that extends efforts such as Pax Silica and the AI Action Plan into a larger theory of victory for the global competition with the Chinese Communist Party. That plan must find additional ways to coordinate efforts across agencies and back U.S. firms that are building the AI stack of the future. Such a strategy will require a more deft approach to token diplomacy.From One-Off Programs to a Grand StrategyThe United States needs tokenpolitik: a grand strategy connecting State, Commerce, Treasury, DFC, EXIM, allied governments, and private firms to make secure, affordable AI capacity available to strategic partners. This strategy should seek to outflank China’s Digital Silk Road and build out the infrastructure that will power economic growth and opportunity in the Global South while also assuring China doesn’t gain a dominant infrastructure foothold.The White House should begin by establishing an international AI infrastructure strategy program jointly accountable to the National Security Council and National Economic Council. The Department of State should identify priority countries, negotiate operating and security arrangements, and lead embassy deal teams. Commerce should assemble private consortia, evaluate markets, coordinate technical standards, and manage licensing. These efforts need to embrace the private sector and bring in leading companies to provide key insights, since these firms will ultimately design, own, and operate the AI stack.This plan should build on Pax Silica to envision regional orders that create a new economic and security architecture. For example, the effort could align U.S. firms with Turkish infrastructure companies and Azerbaijan energy giants to build out an AI cluster in the Caucasus. Combining this with Armenia’s recent bet on AI compute can align with the Trump Route for International Peace and Prosperity (TRIPP) as well as recent normalization of relations between Turkey and Armenia. The United States could work with Japan, including the Japan Bank for International Cooperation and firms such as NEC Corporation, to build out a cluster in Southeast Asia. These efforts would pivot from building the token supply chains envisioned by Pax Silica to promoting a U.S.-led, AI-empowered future economic and security order.With a standing set of strategy guidance managed by the National Economic Council and National Security Council, the emphasis should turn to establishing joint infrastructure projects that support growing the AI stack in the Global South. There is now a risk of a larger global digital divide that could leave the Global South in the dark; DFC should finance host-country infrastructure such as power generation, cable landings, and datacenters required to generate tokens and power AI-led economic growth. EXIM can offer direct loans, loan guarantees, and insurance to U.S. firms so they can compete against China in building this infrastructure in the Global South and regional clusters.Washington should also connect technology policy with financial intelligence. Treasury’s Outbound Investment Security Program already covers certain U.S. investments involving Chinese semiconductors and AI. Commerce collects information through licenses, end-use checks, red-flag guidance, and trusted-operator programs. This data should support an annual Treasury-Commerce assessment of how national funds, local-government vehicles, state banks, public procurement, and private capital finance the Chinese AI stack.In addition, this effort should include leveraging a mix of anti–money laundering authorities and counter-smuggling efforts such as Operation Gatekeeper, which interdicted Chinese technology smuggling. Beijing uses a complex web of shell companies, falsified paperwork, disguised end users, third-country transshipment, bribes, and blackmail to obtain restricted AI hardware. The United States can use these authorities such as those under the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network to combat these tactics and issue AI-specific red flags for banks, payment processors, and even transportation firms and datacenter operators.Congress should work to make an AI strategy durable. It should authorize multiyear DFC-EXIM financing and invest in the electricians, grid engineers, network operators, cooling specialists, and semiconductor workers needed for the buildout. It should streamline duplicative regulation while preserving competition, privacy, cybersecurity, and critical infrastructure protections.A New Era of TokenpolitikOver the next generation, tokens will power economic growth and change the balance of military power. This fact makes tokens the focal point for defining interest in terms of power. Failing to embrace this reality and build a new grand strategy is a recipe for decline. To win the coming race, the United States needs to outmaneuver Chinese state capitalism. A new generation of strategists inside the executive and Congress, alongside broader networks connecting industry, academia, and think tanks, needs to create a blueprint for great power competition based on the source of power and interest for a new era. That plan should guide the diplomacy and development of building a new economic and security architecture.Benjamin Jensen is director of the Futures Lab and a senior fellow for the Defense and Security Department at CSIS. Yasir Atalan is a deputy director and data fellow in the Futures Lab at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nArtificial Intelligence,\nDefense and Security,\nEnergy and Sustainability, and\nStrategic Capital\nImage\nBenjamin Jensen\nDirector, Futures Lab and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nYasir Atalan\nDeputy Director and Data Fellow, Futures Lab, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nAI and the Future of Conflict\nCompetition Lab\nDefense and Security\nFutures Lab\nRelated Content\nImage\nProtecting Our Edge: Trade Secrets and the Global AI Arms Race\nCongressional Testimony\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\n— May 7, 2025\nImage\nAI and Grand Strategy: The Case for Restraint\nReport\nby\nErica D. Lonergan\nand\nBenjamin Jensen\n— January 28, 2026","content_cn":"Tokenpolitik：美国如何与中国竞争，构建全球人工智能堆栈\n图片：Kyle Grillot/Bloomberg via Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n和\nYasir Atalan\n发表于2026年8月3日\n一种注定定义国际体系未来的新型大国竞争正在形成。当美国和中国在幕后谈判、准备就人工智能举行正式会谈时，中国国家主席习近平发表公开演讲，宣称北京将成为新全球人工智能秩序的领导者。一场竞赛已经开始，争夺谁将建设正在重新定义权力与国际关系的数据中心、电厂和模型网络。这场竞赛让美国企业对上了中国的国家产业政策；中国正积极推动本国龙头企业，建设低成本芯片和高性能开放权重模型，作为美国技术的替代方案。与此同时，在美国可能调查中国企业如何利用知识蒸馏训练模型之际，北京指责华盛顿搞“人工智能霸权主义”。特朗普政府已经认识到这一挑战的规模，并采取了一系列举措，以确保美国继续保持世界人工智能超级大国的地位。但这些努力尚未汇聚成更广泛的大战略，来塑造美国如何与国界之外的世界互动。缺失的是对“tokenpolitik”的清晰概念：一种大战略，把美国政府内部多个机构与私营部门连接起来，为二十一世纪创造新的经济与安全架构。这一战略将聚焦人工智能在未来几十年定义权力与利益方面的作用，并为美国政府支持建设数据中心、电厂和研究实验室的私营企业提供框架。这将使美国企业能够策划和组织必要的数据与新模型，确保自由社会和自由市场引领人工智能时代。\n新事物即旧事物：基础设施即战略\n现实政治关乎权力，但权力从来不只存在于枪管之中，而且往往延伸到基础设施。由法国主导的苏伊士运河项目改变了欧亚之间的贸易地理。英国曾反对修建运河，但在运河对英国商业和帝国通信变得不可或缺后，于1875年购入埃及政府持有的44%股份。战略问题不止是修建运河，还包括谁来为这条新的商业动脉提供资金、治理、保障并从中受益。\n冷战时期也出现了类似例子。由美国支持的国际通信卫星组织（INTELSAT）与苏联赞助的国际通信卫星系统（Intersputnik）展开竞争，力图将卫星通信延伸至世界各地。更广泛的较量涉及卫星、地面站、轨道接入、服务价格、标准、技术援助以及与发展中国家的关系。通信基础设施成为组织国际依赖与影响力的一种手段。\n人工智能正在形成类似的竞争。模型是最显眼的产品，Google、OpenAI和Anthropic等公司的封闭模型，与中国的开放权重模型（例如Kimi、DeepSeek、Qwen和GLM）展开竞争。然而，更深层的斗争围绕着使这些模型能够大规模发挥作用的分层系统展开：能源、芯片、基础设施和应用。这些层与模型共同构成一条代币供应链\n这套系统运行着在政府和职场中日益普及的 AI 应用。Token 是新 AI 秩序的语言和货币。Token 是神经网络摄取、处理和生成的基本原子单位。在大型语言模型（LLM）中，它是文本的最小单位，通常是一个词；而在大型音频语言模型（LAM）中，它则成为连续声波的最小离散单位。Token 代表文本、音频或图像的基本片段，这些片段被转换成数学向量表示，供神经网络处理。在全球竞争的背景下，这些 Token 成为遍布全球的大型数据中心和电网所产出的终极工业产品。现代人工智能中的每一项运行约束——从运行一次推理查询的实时成本，到上下文窗口的有限内存——最终都归结为系统解析和预测这些单位的效率。它们是这个新技术时代的基础货币。最有效地通过国家机构和私营部门企业网络生成 Token 的大国，将处于新国际体系的制高点。二十一世纪的首要战略问题是：谁将构建 Token 供应链，并为世界关键地区接入通向 AI 未来的网络。\n\n中国正在全栈支持企业\n尽管中国的做法常被描述为国家主导，但私营企业仍然是创新的核心。国家通过提供资本、补贴计算资源、基础设施、研究支持、外交支持以及保护其免受外国压力，来塑造企业周围的环境。\n\n北京正在全栈部署产业政策工具。这些努力包括国家支持的实验室，以及重大的基础设施投资，其中包括 2025 年规模达 80 亿美元的国家人工智能投资基金，随后是 2026 年规模更大的 2950 亿美元 AI 基础设施建设支出计划。关于中国政府风险投资的学术研究同样表明，公共投资通过由国家和地方基金组成的分布式体系运作，帮助一些最初实力较弱的企业吸引后续私人融资。中国共产党还利用国家支持的举措推动 CXMT 等芯片制造商的发展；该公司在上市首日股价飙升超过 466%；此外还有长江存储（YMTC）以及包括华为在内的基础设施企业。\n\n开放权重模型构成了北京战略的第二根支柱。这些模型可以下载、在本地托管，并更容易针对企业使用场景进行优化。兰德公司（RAND）的研究认为，此类模型通过塑造开发者社区、技术教育、标准和下游应用，发挥着软实力工具的作用。\n\n最近，来自中国实验室的开放权重模型已被证明在关键 AI 基准测试中表现良好，达到与美国前沿 AI 模型相当的水平。此外，其每个 Token 的价格也低于美国模型。随着开放权重模型在全球的采用率不断提高，这给美国的闭源模型带来了额外压力。如今，中国开发者在开放模型经济中的影响力日益增强。事实上，许多美国企业已经开始试验这些中国 AI 模型，尽管它们将这些模型托管在美国平台上。\n\n华为为这些模型提供了实体分发网络。其在电信领域的既有地位使其能够向各国政府提供将数据中心、\n云服务、加速器、网络、冷却、软件、培训和维护。在发展中经济体中，当一款技术较弱的芯片被嵌入由已经了解该国网络和相关部门的供应商提供的可负担方案时，它仍然可能具有吸引力。中国通过华为，正在巴西、印度尼西亚、肯尼亚和中东等市场建立这些关系。围绕准入、主权、开放发展以及全球南方能力建设的外交叙事，与这一趋势相辅相成。\n\n华盛顿已经集齐了许多要素\n美国拥有更强大的私营企业和更深厚的资本市场。2025年，美国投资者占全球对外人工智能风险投资的逾一半，而基础设施和托管服务吸引了最大份额的人工智能资本。如今，华盛顿已经超越了拜登政府人工智能扩散政策中占主导地位的单一出口管制战略。特朗普政府依据《人工智能行动计划》，设立了美国人工智能出口计划，旨在向国际盟友和伙伴推广全栈式美国人工智能技术方案。这些努力依托商务部和国务院的一系列举措，包括“帕克斯硅”（Pax Silica），该倡议专注于与盟友和伙伴构建“安全、有韧性、由创新驱动的技术生态系统”。\n\n此外，与关键参与者之间的双边协议数量也在不断增加。美阿联酋框架将一个规划中的区域人工智能集群与安全、监测和投资承诺联系起来。商务部已批准G42和沙特阿拉伯Humain大规模采购芯片，但须遵守报告和安全条件。美国国际开发金融公司（DFC）和美国进出口银行（EXIM）为这些倡议提供融资基础。DFC报告称，人工智能数据中心投资已成为伙伴政府提出的首要请求，并将电信、光纤、云基础设施、数据中心、发电和电网列为该技术栈的组成部分。EXIM的ExportAI倡议为美国技术提供出口信用保险、营运资金担保、贷款担保和直接贷款。\n\n不过，目前缺少的是一项总体大战略，能够将“帕克斯硅”和《人工智能行动计划》等努力延伸为一套更宏大的胜利理论，以应对与中国共产党的全球竞争。该计划必须寻找更多方式来协调各机构之间的努力，并支持正在构建未来人工智能技术栈的美国企业。这样的战略将需要更加灵活的“代币外交”方式。\n\n从一次性项目走向大战略\n美国需要“代币政治”（tokenpolitik）：一项连接国务院、商务部、财政部、DFC、EXIM、盟国政府和私营企业的大战略，为战略伙伴提供安全、可负担的人工智能能力。这一战略应寻求迂回超越中国的数字丝绸之路，建设推动全球南方经济增长和机遇的基础设施，同时确保中国不会获得占据主导地位的基础设施立足点。\n\n白宫应首先建立一项国际人工智能基础设施战略计划，由国家安全委员会和国家经济委员会共同负责。国务院应确定优先国家，协商运营和安全\n安排，并领导大使馆交易团队。商务部应组建私营财团、评估市场、协调技术标准并管理许可。这些努力需要接纳私营部门，并吸纳领先企业提供关键见解，因为这些公司最终将设计、拥有并运营人工智能堆栈。该计划应以“帕克斯·硅”（Pax Silica）为基础，构想能够创造全新经济与安全架构的区域性订单。例如，这项工作可以协调美国企业、土耳其基础设施公司和阿塞拜疆能源巨头，在高加索地区建设人工智能集群。将此与亚美尼亚近期对人工智能算力的押注相结合，可以与“特朗普国际和平与繁荣路线”（TRIPP）以及土耳其和亚美尼亚关系近期正常化相衔接。美国可以与日本合作，包括日本国际协力银行以及NEC公司等企业，在东南亚建设集群。这些努力将从建设“帕克斯·硅”设想的代币供应链，转向推动由美国主导、人工智能赋能的未来经济与安全秩序。\n\n在由国家经济委员会和国家安全委员会管理的一套常设战略指导方针下，重点应转向建立支持全球南方人工智能堆栈扩张的联合基础设施项目。如今，全球数字鸿沟扩大、令全球南方陷入黑暗的风险正在上升；国际开发金融公司（DFC）应为东道国的基础设施提供融资，例如发电设施、电缆登陆站和数据中心，这些设施是生成代币并推动人工智能主导的经济增长所必需的。美国进出口银行（EXIM）可以向美国企业提供直接贷款、贷款担保和保险，使其能够在全球南方及区域集群建设这些基础设施时与中国竞争。\n\n华盛顿还应将技术政策与金融情报连接起来。财政部的对外投资安全计划已经涵盖涉及中国半导体和人工智能的某些美国投资。商务部通过许可证、最终用途审查、危险信号指南和可信运营商计划收集信息。这些数据应支持财政部与商务部每年评估国家资金、地方政府融资工具、国有银行、公共采购和私人资本如何为中国人工智能堆栈提供资金。\n\n此外，这项工作还应包括综合运用反洗钱权限和反走私行动，例如曾拦截中国技术走私的“守门人行动”（Operation Gatekeeper）。北京利用复杂的空壳公司网络、伪造文件、伪装最终用户、经第三国转运、行贿和勒索来获取受限的人工智能硬件。美国可以利用金融犯罪执法网络（FinCEN）等机构所拥有的权限打击这些手段，并为银行、支付处理商，乃至运输企业和数据中心运营商发布人工智能专项危险信号。\n\n国会应努力使人工智能战略具有持久性。国会应授权多年期DFC-EXIM融资，并投资培养建设所需的电工、电网工程师、网络运营商、冷却专家和半导体工人。国会还应精简重复性监管，同时维护竞争、隐私、网络安全和关键基础设施保护。\n\n代币政治的新纪元\n\n在未来一代人的时间里，代币将推动经济增长并改变军事力量的平衡。这一事实\n使代币成为以权力界定利益的焦点。未能接受这一现实并制定新的大战略，势必导致衰落。要赢得即将到来的竞赛，美国需要在战略上胜过中国的国家资本主义。行政部门和国会中的新一代战略家，以及连接产业界、学术界和智库的更广泛网络，需要制定一份基于新时期权力和利益来源的大国竞争蓝图。该计划应指导外交工作，以及构建新的经济与安全架构的发展工作。Benjamin Jensen是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）未来实验室主任兼防务与安全部高级研究员。Yasir Atalan是CSIS未来实验室副主任兼数据研究员。\n战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作本评论。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私营、免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且非专有性。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n© 2026战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n人工智能、\n防务与安全、\n能源与可持续性，以及\n战略资本\n图片\nBenjamin Jensen\n未来实验室主任兼防务与安全部高级研究员\n图片\nYasir Atalan\n未来实验室副主任兼数据研究员，防务与安全部\n项目与计划\n人工智能与冲突的未来\n竞争实验室\n防务与安全\n未来实验室\n相关内容\n图片\n保护我们的优势：商业机密与全球人工智能军备竞赛\n国会证词\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n——2025年5月7日\n图片\n人工智能与大战略：克制的理由\n报告\n作者\nErica D. Lonergan\n和\nBenjamin Jensen\n——2026年1月28日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-08-03T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2607","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"乌克兰重建会议是在积聚势头还是未达预期？","title_en":"Is the Ukraine Recovery Conference Building Momentum or Falling Short?","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心评论文章评估2025年7月罗马乌克兰重建会议（URC）。会议吸引逾6000名代表，签署200多项协议，承诺约130亿欧元。文章回顾历届URC累计约2000亿美元承诺，指出私营部门参与增强但多数协议尚未落地，乌克兰治理与改革滞后是结构性瓶颈。文章认为URC总体在积聚势头，但成功并非必然，2026年华沙会议或成转折点。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary assesses the July 2025 Ukraine Recovery Conference in Rome, which drew over 6,000 delegates, saw 200-plus agreements signed, and roughly €13 billion committed. Reviewing four URCs that together secured an estimated $200 billion in pledges, it notes growing private-sector engagement but limited implementation, with Ukraine's governance and reform gaps a structural bottleneck. The piece concludes the URCs are building momentum, though success is not guaranteed, and the 2026 Warsaw conference could mark a turning point.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:37:11.118770+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/ukraine-recovery-conference-building-momentum-or-falling-short","content":"Is the Ukraine Recovery Conference Building Momentum or Falling Short?\nPhoto: Antonio Masiello/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nSergiy Tsivkach\nPublished July 18, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nThe recent Ukraine Recovery Conference (URC), held in Rome on July 10–11, marked the fourth and largest such annual gathering of politicians, financiers, businesses, and experts from over 70 countries united in their commitment to support Ukraine’s recovery and future.More than 6,000 delegates attended the conference, which was officially opened by Italian Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky. Delegates received the latest updates on Ukraine’s recovery plans, engaged in business networking, signed agreements, and learned about international opportunities to support Ukraine’s continued resistance to Russia’s full-scale military invasion.Over 200 agreements were signed this year, most of which represent pledges of support or launches of new cooperation efforts. Approximately €13 billion has been committed across key sectors in Ukraine.Once again, high-level participants from governments and the private sector reassured Ukraine of sustained assistance for reconstruction. Notably, this year’s URC had the strongest business participation to date, creating a truly dynamic atmosphere. Substantial private investment projects were presented and entered early development stages. Private sector momentum has built on the foundation laid in London in 2023 and reinforced in Berlin in 2024, now taking a clearer shape in Rome in 2025.Nevertheless, a broader view is needed to determine whether successive URC meetings are securing Ukraine’s sustainable growth or simply functioning as public support campaigns with limited long-term impact on Ukraine’s prosperity.From Pledges to Progress: The Evolution of the URCSince the first Ukraine Recovery Conference in Lugano in 2022—an evolution from the former Ukraine Reform Conference—the URC has become a major international platform for the coordination of Ukraine’s recovery. At each event, international partners have announced significant financial commitments.Based on available data, the URCs in Lugano, London, Berlin, and Rome have secured an estimated $200 billion in pledged commitments to support Ukraine, its economy, and its businesses through grants, loans, guarantees, and programs. In many cases, these multiyear programs have yet to be implemented by international partners and Ukraine.Hundreds of memoranda and agreements have been signed at these four conferences at the government-to-government (G2G), government-to-business (G2B), and business-to-business (B2B) levels. These have been concluded for various but critical purposes such as budgetary support, energy system reconstruction, investment and trade, insurance, municipal support, and humanitarian aid.Every URC event has had its own character and effect, but the overarching goal of Ukraine’s recovery has persisted across all conferences, and the number of delegates has consistently grown year by year—from just over 1,000 in Lugano in 2022 to more than 6,000 in Rome in 2025.The beneficiaries of URC meetings include Ukraine’s national government, international financial institutions (IFIs), municipal and regional governments of Ukraine, international businesses, investors, and, ultimately, Ukrainian citizens.Due to fragmented reporting, it is difficult to provide accurate figures for total commitments announced at URC meetings and their disbursement rates. Instead, estimates and impacts can be assessed through a high-level summary:Ukrainian government agencies have secured major pledges through financial aid, grants, and concessional loans, primarily through bilateral support and the European Union’s Ukraine Facility. Over 15 major agreements lay the foundation for this support, much of which is designed to benefit businesses and citizens.IFIs, including the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD), the European Investment Bank (EIB), the World Bank, and the International Financial Corporation (IFC), have operated as crucial intermediaries to disburse loans, guarantees, and technical assistance for more than 30 signed agreements.Ukrainian municipalities and regions received support through more than 30 projects at URC meetings, including 20 projects in Rome. This is a positive sign that reflects the growing importance of decentralized cooperation between donors, investors, businesses, and local governments.The national defense and security sectors have benefited through at least five publicly disclosed deals, with a total value exceeding $5 billion.Ukrainian citizens have benefited from programs that support education, internally displaced persons, veterans, housing compensation, healthcare, and other needs via more than 15 agreements.Ukraine’s private sector is expected to benefit from leveraged support through investment vehicles, loans, guarantees, and other programs, as well as facilitated B2B cooperation. More than 400 business-related agreements have been signed at the URCs. However, their ultimate impact remains to be seen, and further tracking is essential.Is Private Sector Development the Missing Link?A shift toward supporting the private sector at URC meetings is vital. The London conference was the first to highlight this, with notable developments at the following year’s conference in Berlin. The Rome conference was more business focused than ever before, marking an important step in private sector mobilization in Ukraine’s recovery agenda.All stakeholders understand that rebuilding Ukraine, at an estimated cost of over $500 billion, cannot succeed without the engagement of the private sector at scale. While the public sector is crucial for rapid recovery, private sector activity yields long-term effects and should therefore be urgently prioritized. Current dynamics, however, signal this objective has yet to be realized.Out of the hundreds of G2B and B2B agreements and memoranda signed, relatively few have materialized. Many of those documents are signed at preliminary stages of business projects and are limited by a lack of access to affordable finance and de-risking mechanisms or regulatory clarity. Hence, the future of such initiatives remains vague. This is not merely a financial gap, but a sign of a structural problem for which Ukraine shares responsibility.Ukraine’s ability to conduct governance and economic reforms in extreme conditions remains a significant limiting factor. The judicial system, tax and customs authorities, and enforcement agencies responsible for fighting corruption and economic crime have all been identified as critical priority reform areas for many years. Without substantial progress in these sectors, reconstruction may stall, defending the country may become more difficult, and international support may weaken.Private capital is available to address financial and structural issues, but it requires effective and transparent regulation, fair business practices, and a level playing field across all sectors of Ukraine’s economy. International support is also crucial to the provision of de-risking, guarantees, and blended finance.Nevertheless, the Rome conference has yielded some significant results:A €600 million EU program, to be delivered through the EIB, supporting energy, transport, and business resilience, including small and medium-sized enterprisesThe European Flagship Fund for the Reconstruction of Ukraine, announced by European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen, which pledged an initial €200 million, with a target of €500 million by 2026An IFC and EBRD initiative to develop investment platforms, with the aim to raise around €600 million from private equity and venture capital for critical sectors such as infrastructureThe U.S. Development Finance Corporation’s continued engagement in Ukraine’s reconstruction through its United States–Ukraine Reconstruction Investment Fund and its search for a suitable fund administratorIt seems that institutional investors remain interested in Ukraine, a reassuring signal of confidence to private players.Turning Promises into ActionCritics of the URC conferences argue that now is not the time for such gatherings, arguing that all focus should remain on defending Ukraine, with broader reconstruction plans put aside until hostilities end. As difficult as it is, they may have a point, but only if URC meetings turn into a window-dressing event rather than a genuine and coordinated effort to rebuild Ukraine. For now, the long-term impact of the URCs remains uncertain. By most accounts, the four URC convenings have been a great success, building momentum for Ukraine’s current recovery and future reconstruction, seeking out more tangible results, and attracting growing international attention year after year. Significant progress has been made: Recovery principles were set in Lugano in 2022; early traction and massive pledges in London in 2023 were reinforced in Berlin in 2024; and deal execution began in Rome in 2025—with a stronger private sector focus than ever before. The Ukrainian government and international partners now must direct their attention to closing some gaps: tracking progress, implementing projects, and scaling successful initiatives.Charting the Path Forward for Ukraine’s Economic TransformationDuring the last three years, Ukrainian Prime Minister Denys Shmyhal’s administration succeeded in stabilizing the country’s economy in the face of extremely challenging conditions involving a war and a pandemic. The newly appointed government of Ukraine, led by Yulia Svyrydenko, has all the tools for success. As former first deputy prime minister and minister of economic development and trade, she and her team possess deep knowledge of Ukraine’s economy and its needs. Svyrydenko, serving as the key URC co-organizer for Ukraine, can solidify the success of the URC platform and ensure its long-lasting impact.The next 12 months will be extremely important for Ukraine’s future, as they require the advancement of strategic initiatives, such as the United States–Ukraine Reconstruction Investment Fund, European Flagship Fund for the Reconstruction of Ukraine, and other programs, including private sector activity. The upcoming URC in Warsaw in 2026—the fifth in the series—is likely to become a turning point and will possibly be the first held in a postwar setting. It carries the potential to mark a historic shift: from foundations and promises to practical delivery and the impactful rebuilding of Ukraine. Ukraine has the opportunity to become the next ground zero for capital flows—or it could become a money pit. The country’s future will depend on coordinated action within Ukraine and reinforcement of investor confidence. Ineffectiveness or unfair practices could deter investors more than the war itself. The URC conferences and Ukraine’s recovery are on the right track, but their success is not guaranteed. Persistent challenges must be addressed to achieve a lasting and meaningful impact.Sergiy Tsivkach is an adjunct fellow (non-resident) with the Project on Prosperity and Development at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nEurope,\nUkraine War, and\nInternational Development\nImage\nSergiy Tsivkach\nAdjunct Fellow (Non-resident), Project on Prosperity and Development\nPrograms & Projects\nProject on Prosperity and Development\nGlobal Development\nUkraine Economic Reconstruction Commission\nFour Years Later: Russia’s War in Ukraine","content_cn":"乌克兰复苏会议是在形成势头还是未达预期？\n照片：Antonio Masiello/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nSergiy Tsivkach\n发表于2025年7月18日\n远程可视化\n最近举行的乌克兰复苏会议（URC）于7月10日至11日在罗马举行，这是此类年度会议的第四届，也是规模最大的一届，来自70多个国家的政治家、金融家、企业和专家齐聚一堂，共同致力于支持乌克兰的复苏与未来。超过6,000名代表出席了会议，会议由意大利总理Giorgia Meloni和乌克兰总统Volodymyr Zelensky正式开幕。代表们了解了乌克兰复苏计划的最新进展，开展商业交流，签署协议，并了解支持乌克兰继续抵抗俄罗斯全面军事入侵的国际机会。今年签署了超过200项协议，其中大多数代表着支持承诺或新的合作努力的启动。乌克兰关键领域获得的承诺资金约为130亿欧元。政府和私营部门的高级别与会者再次向乌克兰保证，将持续援助其重建。值得注意的是，今年的URC吸引了迄今为止最强的商业参与，营造出真正充满活力的氛围。大量私人投资项目得到展示并进入早期发展阶段。私营部门的势头建立在2023年伦敦会议奠定、2024年柏林会议巩固的基础之上，如今在2025年罗马会议上呈现出更加清晰的形态。然而，要判断历届URC会议是在确保乌克兰实现可持续增长，还是仅仅充当公共支持宣传活动、对乌克兰繁荣的长期影响有限，仍需要更广泛的视角。\n从承诺到进展：URC的演变自2022年在卢加诺举行首届乌克兰复苏会议以来——该会议由原乌克兰改革会议演变而来——URC已成为协调乌克兰复苏的重要国际平台。每次会议上，国际伙伴都会宣布重大的资金承诺。根据现有数据，卢加诺、伦敦、柏林和罗马举行的URC通过赠款、贷款、担保和项目，为支持乌克兰、其经济及企业，获得了估计总额达2,000亿美元的承诺。在许多情况下，这些多年期项目尚未由国际伙伴和乌克兰实施。在这四次会议上，政府对政府（G2G）、政府对企业（G2B）以及企业对企业（B2B）层面签署了数百份谅解备忘录和协议。这些协议出于各种但至关重要的目的而达成，例如预算支持、能源系统重建、投资与贸易、保险、市政支持和人道援助。每届URC会议都有其自身的特点和影响，但乌克兰复苏这一总体目标贯穿所有会议，代表人数也逐年持续增长——从2022年卢加诺的略多于1,000人增至2025年罗马的超过6,000人。URC会议的受益者包括乌克兰中央政府、国际金融机构（IFIs）、乌克兰各市和地区政府、国际企业、投资者，以及最终的乌克兰公民。由于报告零散，很难准确提供URC会议上宣布的承诺总额及其\n资金拨付率。相反，可以通过高层次摘要来评估估算值和影响：乌克兰政府机构已通过财政援助、赠款和优惠贷款获得重大承诺，主要来自双边支持和欧盟的乌克兰基金。超过15项重大协议为这项支持奠定了基础，其中许多旨在惠及企业和公民。\n\n包括欧洲复兴开发银行（EBRD）、欧洲投资银行（EIB）、世界银行和国际金融公司（IFC）在内的国际金融机构，作为关键中介，已通过30多项已签署协议发放贷款、提供担保和技术援助。\n\n乌克兰的市镇和地区通过URC会议上的30多个项目获得支持，其中包括罗马会议上的20个项目。这是一个积极信号，反映出捐助方、投资者、企业和地方政府之间的去中心化合作日益重要。\n\n国家国防与安全部门通过至少五项公开披露的交易受益，交易总价值超过50亿美元。\n\n乌克兰公民通过15项以上协议获得了教育、境内流离失所者、退伍军人、住房补偿、医疗保健和其他需求方面的项目支持。\n\n预计乌克兰私营部门将通过投资工具、贷款、担保和其他项目获得杠杆支持，同时促进B2B合作。在URC会议上已签署400多项与商业相关的协议。然而，其最终影响仍有待观察，必须持续跟踪。\n\n私营部门发展是缺失的一环吗？\n\n在URC会议上转向支持私营部门至关重要。伦敦会议首次强调了这一点，次年在柏林举行的会议上取得了显著进展。罗马会议比以往更加注重商业，这是乌克兰恢复议程中动员私营部门的重要一步。\n\n所有利益相关方都明白，重建乌克兰预计成本超过5000亿美元，如果没有私营部门的大规模参与，就不可能成功。公共部门对于快速恢复至关重要，但私营部门活动会产生长期影响，因此应被紧急优先考虑。然而，当前动态表明，这一目标尚未实现。\n\n在签署的数百项G2B和B2B协议及谅解备忘录中，真正落地的相对较少。其中许多文件是在商业项目的初步阶段签署的，并受到缺乏可负担融资和去风险机制或监管明确性的限制。因此，此类倡议的未来仍不明朗。这不仅是资金缺口，也是一个结构性问题的表现，乌克兰对此负有共同责任。\n\n乌克兰在极端条件下开展治理和经济改革的能力仍是一个重要限制因素。司法系统、税务和海关部门，以及负责打击腐败和经济犯罪的执法机构，多年来一直被确定为关键优先改革领域。如果这些部门没有取得实质性进展，重建可能停滞，保卫国家可能变得更加困难，国际支持也可能减弱。\n\n私人资本可以用来解决金融和结构性问题，但它需要有效且\n乌克兰经济各领域的透明监管、公平商业惯例以及公平竞争环境。国际支持对于提供去风险措施、担保和混合融资也至关重要。\n\n尽管如此，罗马会议还是取得了一些重要成果：\n\n一项规模为6亿欧元的欧盟计划，将通过欧洲投资银行实施，支持能源、交通和企业韧性，包括中小型企业\n\n欧洲委员会主席乌尔苏拉·冯德莱恩宣布成立的乌克兰重建欧洲旗舰基金，承诺初始投入2亿欧元，目标是在2026年前达到5亿欧元\n\n国际金融公司和欧洲复兴开发银行发起的一项倡议，旨在开发投资平台，为基础设施等关键领域从私募股权和风险投资筹集约6亿欧元\n\n美国国际开发金融公司继续通过其美国—乌克兰重建投资基金参与乌克兰重建，并寻找合适的基金管理人\n\n看来，机构投资者仍然对乌克兰感兴趣，这向私人参与者发出了令人安心的信心信号。\n\n将承诺转化为行动\n\n乌克兰重建会议的批评者认为，现在不是举行此类会议的时候，他们主张所有重点都应继续放在保卫乌克兰上，更广泛的重建计划应推迟到敌对行动结束之后。尽管这很难做到，他们的观点可能有一定道理，但前提是乌克兰重建会议变成徒具形式的活动，而非真正协调一致的乌克兰重建努力。目前，乌克兰重建会议的长期影响仍不确定。根据大多数说法，四次乌克兰重建会议都取得了巨大成功，为乌克兰当前的恢复和未来的重建积蓄了动力，寻求更加切实的成果，并且年复一年吸引了越来越多的国际关注。已经取得了重大进展：2022年在卢加诺确定了恢复原则；2023年在伦敦取得初步进展并获得大规模承诺，2024年在柏林得到进一步强化；2025年在罗马开始执行交易——对私营部门的关注比以往任何时候都更加强烈。乌克兰政府及其国际伙伴现在必须将注意力转向弥合一些缺口：跟踪进展、实施项目以及扩大成功倡议的规模。\n\n规划乌克兰经济转型的前进道路\n\n过去三年中，乌克兰总理杰尼斯·什梅加尔领导的政府在战争和疫情交织、极其困难的条件下成功稳定了国家经济。由尤利娅·斯维里登科领导的乌克兰新政府拥有取得成功的一切工具。作为前第一副总理兼经济发展和贸易部长，她及其团队对乌克兰经济及其需求拥有深厚的了解。斯维里登科作为乌克兰方面乌克兰重建会议的主要共同组织者，可以巩固乌克兰重建会议平台的成功，并确保其产生持久影响。\n\n未来12个月对乌克兰的未来极其重要，因为这段时期需要推进美国—乌克兰重建投资基金、乌克兰重建欧洲旗舰基金等战略倡议，以及包括私营部门活动在内的其他计划。即将于2026年在华沙举行的乌克兰重建会议——该系列会议的第五次——很可能成为一个转折点，并可能是首次在战后环境下举行。它有可能标志着一个\n历史性转变：从基础和承诺转向务实交付，以及对乌克兰进行具有影响力的重建。乌克兰有机会成为资本流动的下一个“零号地带”——也可能成为一个无底洞。该国的未来将取决于乌克兰境内的协调行动以及对投资者信心的强化。无效做法或不公平做法可能比战争本身更令投资者望而却步。乌克兰重建会议（URC）及乌克兰的复苏正走在正确轨道上，但其成功并无保证。必须应对持续存在的挑战，才能实现持久且有意义的影响。Sergiy Tsivkach 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心繁荣与发展项目的兼职研究员（非驻会）。\n\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS 是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私营、免税机构。其研究具有无党派性和非专有性。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人的观点。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n欧洲，\n乌克兰战争，以及\n国际发展\n\n图片\nSergiy Tsivkach\n繁荣与发展项目兼职研究员（非驻会）\n\n项目与计划\n繁荣与发展项目\n全球发展\n乌克兰经济重建委员会\n四年之后：俄罗斯在乌克兰的战争","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-07-18T12:00:00"}},{"id":"news-2066","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"乌克兰和平计划的未竟之处：逐条解析","title_en":"The Unfinished Plan for Peace in Ukraine: Provision by Provision","summary_cn":"CSIS报告逐条分析2025年11月泄露的美俄28点乌克兰和平计划及欧洲反提案。文章指出该计划来源存疑、措辞含糊、假设缺乏依据，难以按原文实施，并将其与2022年伊斯坦布尔公报对比，显示三年半战争后条款更偏向俄罗斯。报告还评估领土、安全保证、军力上限、制裁与选举等条款，认为感恩节前难成协议，但谈判进程或将继续。","summary_en":"This CSIS report analyzes, provision by provision, the leaked 28-point U.S.-Russia peace plan for Ukraine and European counterproposals. It finds the plan of uncertain parentage, vague, contradictory, and unimplementable as written, and compares it with the 2022 Istanbul Communiqué, showing terms now more favorable to Russia after three and a half years of war. It assesses territory, security guarantees, force caps, sanctions, and elections, concluding no deal by Thanksgiving but a process may continue.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:49:10.627828+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/unfinished-plan-peace-ukraine-provision-provision","content":"The Unfinished Plan for Peace in Ukraine: Provision by Provision\nPhoto: Ukrainian Presidency/Handout/Anadolu/Getty Images\nTable of Contents\nTable of Contents\nReport\nby\nMark F. Cancian\nand\nMaria Snegovaya\nPublished November 24, 2025\nAvailable Downloads\nDownload the Full Report\n635kb\nOn November 19, reports leaked that the United States and Russia had negotiated an agreement to end the war in Ukraine. President Trump is now pressuring Ukraine to sign the agreement, but reactions from members of Congress, Europeans, Ukrainians, and commentators have been scathing, calling the plan pro-Russian. Further, the plan has so many loose ends, inconsistencies, and unsubstantiated assumptions that implementation is impossible as written.This is not an agreement, but may be a starting point to get to an agreement. Indeed, the Europeans have put forth their own version of the 28 points while also circulating a previously drafted 24-point proposal. This European version is less pro-Russian, and might be closer to something both sides could agree to. There reportedly is now a 19-point proposal drafted by the United States and Ukraine.This white paper describes the U.S. plan’s uncertain parentage, responses to the plan, compares it with a 2022 attempt at an agreement to show the changes that three and a half years of war have produced, and finally provides a detailed provision-by-provision analysis of the 28-point U.S. plan and the European counterproposal to that plan.The bottom line is that there will be no agreement by Thanksgiving. However, there may be a process through which an agreement will eventually emerge. An “Agreement” of Uncertain ParentageWithin the U.S. establishment, there appears to be confusion about the agreement’s origin. After the leak, the United States first confirmed the document was genuine. However, after several senators condemned the agreement, some of them stated that Secretary of State Marco Rubio had called to say the Russians had submitted the proposal. Indeed, independent observers have noted the likely Russian origin of some text, which reads like a translation by non-native English speakers. The White House then denied Rubio’s alleged denial. Rubio later stated publicly that the agreement is indeed real and was intended as a starting point.Calling it a starting point makes sense. Technical problems in several provisions show the lack of Ukrainian, NATO, European, and military input. The plan assigns roles to NATO, “Europe,” the World Bank, and the G8, but they are just now being consulted and will need to agree to the roles assigned to them. Further, the plan is full of vague and confusing wording, contradictions about who does what, inconsistencies regarding future actions, and unsubstantiated assumptions about the actions of third parties. Perhaps in recognition of this, Trump said on November 22 that this draft was “not my final offer.” On November 24, Secretary Rubio expanded on this, saying, “This is a living, breathing document. Every day, with input, it changes,” With the terms in flux, “plan” is a better term for the document than “agreement.”Although Russian representatives (reportedly led by Kirill Dmitriev, Russia’s top negotiator in peace talks) were involved in drafting the plan, it is unclear whether the Russian government has approved this text. The president’s representative in the negotiations, Steven Witkoff, likely got Russian input (and possibly Ukrainian input), then drafted his own proposal. Putin has said that it provides “the basis of a final peace settlement,” which is far short of a commitment to the draft.The draft did get everyone’s attention, however. It generated an emergency meeting of U.S., Ukrainian, and European representatives in Geneva. Zelensky is scrambling to appear supportive of Trump while not agreeing to a plan that is unfavorable to Ukraine. Keith Kellogg, the president’s special representative for Russia and Ukraine, announced his resignation when the plan was revealed, likely because Witkoff had superseded him during the process.France, Germany, and the United Kingdom, taking the 28-point U.S. plan as a starting point, have offered a 28-point counterproposal, modifying several key provisions to be less pro-Russian. For example, Ukraine would not be required to give up more territory, the cap on Ukrainian armed forces would be higher, and the proposed U.S. control of frozen Russian funds is eliminated. Also in circulation is a 24-point proposal that was developed earlier. The differences between the 24- and 28-point plans likely reflect how the two plans were drafted: the first as an European initiative, the second as a response to the U.S. proposal.Comparison with the Istanbul Communiqué of 2022The U.S. plan did not emerge from a vacuum; it is part of a series of efforts to end the war. To get a sense of the shifts in the parties’ positions on conflict resolution, this new plan can be compared with the Istanbul Communiqué of April 15, 2022, an early attempt to negotiate peace between Ukraine and Russia. Compared with the Istanbul Communiqué, several provisions are more favorable to Russia, reflecting Russia’s modest battlefield success. The comparison shows the changes that three and a half years of war have brought at the cost of over 1.4 million casualties combined.One example of the shift is in security guarantees, which are Ukraine’s primary concern (along with territorial restoration), given Russia’s repeated violations of past agreements. The Istanbul draft offered a somewhat stronger, though still limited, form by including the possibility of “closing the airspace over Ukraine” and providing necessary weapons (Article 5). The new proposal, while purportedly offering NATO-style security guarantees, is even vaguer. It describes only “reliable” but conditional and ambiguous U.S. guarantees that would lapse if Ukraine were to attack Russia, even unintentionally (provisions 5 and 10).The most painful change for Ukraine is that the 28-point plan envisions sweeping territorial concessions, including recognition of Crimea, Luhansk, and the entirety of the Donetsk region, including allegedly the remaining approximately 25 percent currently under Ukrainian control, as de facto Russian; a freeze of the front lines in Kherson and Zaporizhzhia; and the establishment of a demilitarized zone. By contrast, the Istanbul draft treated these areas as unresolved issues: It excluded only Crimea (Article 8) and the Donbas territories listed in Annex 6 (Article 9) from security guarantees, and it did not involve any Western recognition of Russian control. Overall, the Istanbul draft postponed territorial issues instead of codifying Russian control.Both the Istanbul Communiqué and the new plan envision lifting sanctions on Russia, but the new plan is less specific on this point. The new plan also adds provisions for Russia’s return to the G8 and a long-term cooperation agreement with the United States (provision 13).Unlike the Istanbul communiqué, the new plan requires Ukraine to hold elections within 100 days (provision 25), a point reportedly added by the U.S. side. Some provisions are less favorable to Russia. Indeed, some are so unfavorable that Russia will likely find them unacceptable. For example, the new plan would apparently liquidate all frozen Russian assets to finance Ukrainian reconstruction under U.S. direction and create a special U.S.–Russia investment mechanism for joint projects.The new plan calls for an immediate ceasefire. This provision would benefit Ukraine more, given Russia’s ongoing offensive and its recent gains. The Istanbul draft was vague on this point (provision 28 versus Article 11).The Istanbul Communiqué proposed strict limits on Ukraine’s military cooperation with other states (Articles 1 and 2). The new plan is less restrictive. It prohibits NATO membership but has no restrictions on Ukraine’s weaponry (provisions 7 and 8). Provision 6 would cap Ukraine’s Armed Forces at 600,000 personnel, down from roughly 880,000 today. Istanbul’s proposed caps were far lower, with Russia at one point suggesting a ceiling of 85,000.The Istanbul Communiqué required making Russian an official language of Ukraine, with implementation mandated within 30 days of signing (Article 12). The new plan is relatively more balanced in that regard. It calls for both countries to adopt educational and legal measures promoting cultural tolerance, including Ukraine’s alignment with EU standards on religious and minority rights, and the mutual removal of discriminatory practices that impede equal access to Ukrainian and Russian media and education (provision 20).In general, the provisions favorable to Ukraine are less specific than those favorable to Russia.Lastly, several areas of overlap exist between the two plans. Bothallow Ukraine to apply for EU membership (Article 3 vs point 11);reaffirm Ukraine’s nonnuclear status (Article 2m and provision 18);include full judicial amnesty (Article 7 and provision 26) and a ban on Nazi ideology and activity (Article 13 and provision 20c);provide for comprehensive prisoner exchange (Annex 5 versus provision 24a); andenvision a monitoring mechanism. The Istanbul draft proposed a commission with one representative from each disputing party (Article 14). The new version creates a joint U.S.–Ukraine–Russia working group and peace council chaired by Donald Trump, empowered to impose sanctions on violators (paragraphs 15 and 28). Provision-by-Provision Analysis of the U.S. Plan and European CounterproposalsThe analysis below assesses each of the 28 provisions of the draft U.S. plan, as published by the Associated Press on November 21. That plan continues to evolve and now reportedly has only 19 provisions, but those have not been published. This analysis also includes the 24- and 28-point European proposals. Minor wording differences among the versions likely reflect transcription errors.Provision 1: “Ukraine’s sovereignty will be confirmed.”Parties acting: Russia28-point European counterproposal: Small change (Ukraine’s sovereignty will be “reconfirmed”)Analysis: This would be a win for Ukraine because Putin has often stated that Ukraine is not a real country but a part of Russia.Provision 2: “A comprehensive non-aggression agreement will be concluded between Russia, Ukraine, and Europe. All ambiguities of the last 30 years will be considered settled.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine, “Europe”28-point European counterproposal: A slight change in phrasing. “Europe” replaced with “NATO.”Analysis: The sentiment is commendable, but the reach is so broad that it seems unrealistic, even naive. Where will these discussions take place, and how will the agenda be developed? Who speaks for “Europe”?Provision 3: “It is expected that Russia will not invade neighboring countries, and NATO will not expand further.”Parties acting: Russia, NATO28-point European counterproposal: Provision deletedAnalysis: The provision is not surprising. Russia’s neighbors do not want any more invasions, and Russia does not want further NATO expansion. However, it is unclear what “expected” means. It does not sound like a commitment by any of the parties involved.All 32 NATO members must agree to this restriction, and some may be unwilling. Although NATO has no immediate plans to expand beyond its current members, Georgia and Bosnia/Herzegovina have action plans heading toward membership.The European version deletes the entire provision, likely because the first part is useless and NATO does not want Russia dictating its policy.Provision 4: “A dialogue will be held between Russia and NATO, mediated by the United States, to resolve all security issues and create conditions for de-escalation in order to ensure global security and increase opportunities for cooperation and future economic development.”Parties acting: Russia, NATO, the United States28-point European counterproposal: “After a peace agreement is signed, a dialogue between Russia and NATO will convene to address all security concerns and create a de-escalatory environment to ensure global security and increase the opportunity for connectivity and future economic opportunity.”Analysis: As with provision 2, the sentiment is commendable, but implementation will take a lot of work. Where will these discussions take place, and how will the agenda be developed?The use of “mediate” is odd. It implies that the United States is a neutral third party despite being a member of NATO. U.S. allies may be uncomfortable with the idea that the United States can step outside of its alliance responsibilities and be impartial between friends and adversaries.The major change in the European version is the rephrasing to “connectivity and future economic opportunity.” This makes the point that Russia’s connections to the West depend on its own actions.Provision 5: “Ukraine will receive reliable security guarantees.”Parties acting: Unknown28-point European counterproposal: “Ukraine will receive robust Security Guarantees.”Analysis: This is the key issue for Ukraine—security guarantees to prevent Russia from invading again. However, there is no specification about what the security guarantees will be or who will provide them. This provision, a statement of intent, not a guarantee, is too vague for Ukraine to rely on.The European proposal is just as vague but substitutes the stronger adjective “robust.”Provision 6: “The size of the Ukrainian Armed Forces will be limited to 600,000 personnel.”Parties acting: Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: “The size of Ukraine’s military to be capped at 800,000 in peacetime.”Analysis: The U.S. provision softens Russia’s previous demand for disarming Ukraine. Although many commentators have taken this provision as a significant restriction on Ukraine, the reduction will not be a problem for Ukraine to implement. With a ceasefire, it will want to demobilize some of its forces, now estimated at 880,000, and will likely end up below 600,000. Before the war, the Ukrainian armed forces totaled only 209,000 active-duty personnel. Russia will likely want some specificity about what is included in the 600,000 to prevent Ukraine from indulging in creative accounting.The 28-point European counterproposal increases the cap and clarifies that it is in peacetime. The U.S. provision was unclear on that point. The 24-point European proposal does not impose any restrictions on the size of the Ukrainian military.Provision 7: “Ukraine agrees to enshrine in its constitution that it will not join NATO, and NATO agrees to include in its statutes a provision that Ukraine will not be admitted in the future.”Parties acting: Ukraine, NATO28-point European counterproposal: “Ukraine joining NATO depends on consensus of NATO members, which does not exist.”Analysis: The U.S. proposal would be a major disappointment for Ukraine, which views NATO membership as essential to its long-term security and has been rapidly adopting NATO standards and equipment in recent years. In the near term, however, that membership is unlikely given the ongoing conflict and uncertain postwar peace. Although this has been framed as a “permanent” prohibition, national constitutions and NATO agreements can change, though that is unlikely in the foreseeable future.The European version is a major change, allowing Ukraine to join in the future but acknowledging that that will not happen in the near term.Provision 8: “NATO agrees not to station troops in Ukraine.”Parties acting: NATO28-point European counterproposal: “NATO agrees not to permanently station troops under its command in Ukraine in peacetime.”Analysis: This is a key provision for Russia, which does not want more foreign troops on its borders. Some NATO countries have considered sending troops to Ukraine as part of a security guarantee and peacekeeping force. Indeed, the 24-point European proposal would permit Ukraine to “decide on the presence, weapons and operations of friendly forces invited by the government of Ukraine on its territory.”The Trump administration has ruled out putting U.S. boots on the ground, as had the Biden administration. In theory, non-NATO nations could provide peacekeepers, but few countries have the size, skills, neutrality, and interest to take on such a demanding role. India and Turkey are the two most suitable. Although the 28-point European counterproposal appears to prohibit NATO forces in peacetime, there may be a loophole allowing NATO countries’ forces to be stationed in Ukraine during peacetime, but not under NATO command. It would also allow NATO forces in wartime. It may reflect the fact that some NATO forces are already in Ukraine.Provision 9: “European fighter jets will be stationed in Poland.”Parties acting: NATO28-point European counterproposal: No changeAnalysis: Presumably, this means jets from other countries stationed in Poland. That is already the case under NATO’s Operation Eastern Sentry, which was a response to Russian drones crossing into Eastern Europe, including Poland.This is a key provision for Poland, which fears Russian aggression and wants assurances that other NATO countries will have their forces forward deployed.The plan is silent on ground forces in Poland and Eastern Europe, where the United States has many troops. This could indicate that the Trump administration is considering withdrawing these troops, but it could also reflect the plan’s general looseness.Provision 10: “The U.S. guarantee:The U.S. will receive compensation for the guarantee;if Ukraine invades Russia, it will lose the guarantee;if Russia invades Ukraine, in addition to a decisive coordinated military response, all global sanctions will be reinstated, recognition of the new territory and all other benefits of this deal will be revoked;if Ukraine launches a missile at Moscow or St. Petersburg without cause, the security guarantee will be deemed invalid.”Parties acting: The United States, Russia, Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: Deletes the final bullet and adds that the U.S. guarantee “mirrors Article 5”Analysis: Compensation for the United States is a classic Trump provision, reflecting his belief that this is not a U.S. flight and therefore the United States should be paid for its contributions. The form of compensation is unclear, as is its source. It may be part of the compensation under provision 14 or in addition to it.The requirement that neither country invade the other is fundamental to the agreement. Specifying consequences gives the requirements some teeth.The last element is odd. Why is the prohibition against Ukraine launching missiles limited to Moscow or Saint Petersburg, and why would missile launches be allowed elsewhere? Once a ceasefire takes place, all cross-border shooting should stop. It is hard to imagine Russia accepting this element, and the European counter proposal deletes it entirely.Provision 11: “Ukraine is eligible for EU membership and will receive short-term preferential access to the European market while this issue is being considered.”Parties acting: Ukraine, the European Union28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: Ukraine has applied for EU membership and is going through the membership process. This is important for Ukraine, both to strengthen its economy and to link it more closely to Europe and the West.Provision 12: “A powerful global package of measures to rebuild Ukraine, including but not limited to:The creation of a Ukraine Development Fund to invest in fast-growing industries, including technology, data centers, and artificial intelligence.The United States will cooperate with Ukraine to jointly rebuild, develop, modernize, and operate Ukraine’s gas infrastructure, including pipelines and storage facilities.Joint efforts to rehabilitate war-affected areas for the restoration, reconstruction, and modernization of cities and residential areas.Infrastructure development.Extraction of minerals and natural resources.The World Bank will develop a special financing package to accelerate these efforts.”Parties acting: The United States, Ukraine, the World Bank28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: Rebuilding Ukraine after the devastation of the war will be critical to its long-term economic health and political stability. These provisions are a useful step. However, the lack of specificity makes their impact uncertain. Where does the money for the “Ukraine Development Fund” come from?The World Bank will need to agree to its assigned reconstruction role.“Extraction of minerals and natural resources” may refer to the U.S.-Ukraine agreement on mining.Provision 13: “Russia will be reintegrated into the global economy: The lifting of sanctions will be discussed and agreed upon in stages and on a case-by-case basis. The United States will enter into a long-term economic cooperation agreement for mutual development in the areas of energy, natural resources, infrastructure, artificial intelligence, data centers, rare earth metal extraction projects in the Arctic, and other mutually beneficial corporate opportunities. Russia will be invited to rejoin the G8.”Parties acting: The United States, Russia, the G828-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: This has been one of Putin’s goals and an interest for Trump. Putin wants to reconnect with the world economy, get out from under the painful sanctions, and have the G8 recognize Russia as a major power. Trump sees economic benefits in renewing trade with Russia. Provision 14: “Frozen funds will be used as follows:$100 billion in frozen Russian assets will be invested in US-led efforts to rebuild and invest in Ukraine;The US will receive 50% of the profits from this venture. Europe will add $100 billion to increase the amount of investment available for Ukraine’s reconstruction. Frozen European funds will be unfrozen. The remainder of the frozen Russian funds will be invested in a separate US-Russian investment vehicle that will implement joint projects in specific areas. This fund will be aimed at strengthening relations and increasing common interests to create a strong incentive not to return to conflict.”Parties acting: Russia, Europe, the United States28-point European counterproposal: “Ukraine will be fully reconstructed and compensated financially, including through Russian sovereign assets that will remain frozen until Russia compensates damage to Ukraine.”Analysis: The U.S. provision is extraordinary. In effect, the United States is taking control of the frozen Russian funds and using much of them for its own benefit. It would force the Europeans to contribute to this effort, presumably from Russian funds frozen in Europe. Putting the remaining Russian funds into a “U.S.-Russian investment vehicle” gives the United States half of Russia’s money. It is hard to imagine either the Russians or the Europeans agreeing to such a stark diversion of funds.The European proposal eliminates the U.S. effort to take control of Russian funds. Given how strongly Trump feels about compensation, this may become a major sticking point for him. However, it would remove a major stumbling block for Russia.Provision 15: “A joint American-Russian working group on security issues will be established to promote and ensure compliance with all provisions of this agreement.”Parties acting: The United States, Russia28-point European counterproposal: “A joint Security taskforce will be established with the participation of the U.S., Ukraine, Russia, and the Europeans to promote and enforce all of the provisions of this agreement.”Analysis: Virtually every peace agreement has a compliance mechanism, so it makes sense to have a group coordinate implementation. It is unclear how this group will work with the NATO-Russia dialogue in provision 4 or the peace council in provision 27. It is telling that the U.S. proposal for a working group excludes Ukraine, NATO, and all the other entities that are assigned responsibilities in the plan.Not surprisingly, the European counterproposal adds Ukraine, Russia, and the Europeans to this group.Provision 16: “Russia will enshrine in law its policy of non-aggression towards Europe and Ukraine.”Parties acting: Russia28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: This would implement provision 3 (no Russian attacks on neighbors) and 22 (no territorial changes by force). “In law” implies action by the Russian Duma, not just a decree signed by Putin. For this provision to be viable, the final agreement must have a deadline for when the Russian Duma must pass the law. Still, it is hard to take this provision seriously given Russia’s repeated violations of European security.Provision 17: “The United States and Russia will agree to extend the validity of treaties on the non-proliferation and control of nuclear weapons, including the START I Treaty.”Parties acting: The United States, Russia28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changes; uses “Fair Start” in the Associated Press report, but this is likely an errorAnalysis: It is not clear how nuclear treaties ended up in a plan ending a war between Russia and Ukraine. Nuclear treaties are important, and several, such as the Open Skies Treaty, have been suspended. New START is set to expire on February 4, 2026 (and the text may have meant New START rather than the expired START I). Extending the treaties has major policy implications. However, they are presented in an offhanded manner, not even specifying which treaties are affected. Though the text says that the United States and Russia “will agree,” it might just mean that the two countries will begin discussions. This would not affect the Russian saber-rattling that has disconcerted many countries during the war.Provision 18: “Ukraine agrees to be a non-nuclear state in accordance with the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons.”Parties acting: Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: This was Ukraine’s status before the war. Ukraine had nuclear weapons when the Soviet Union broke up but relinquished them in exchange for security assurances from the United States, the United Kingdom, and Russia in the Budapest Agreement. That agreement was ineffective in protecting Ukraine, and some Ukrainians have suggested that nuclear weapons would be an effective security guarantee if NATO membership is unavailable. This provision would rule out that course of action.Provision 19: “The Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant will be launched under the supervision of the [International Atomic Energy Agency], and the electricity produced will be distributed equally between Russia and Ukraine — 50:50.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine, International Atomic Energy Agency28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: The power plant is on the Russian side of the front lines, but the city of Zaporizhzhia and many of the plant’s workers are on the Ukrainian side. This is a sensible provision as everyone needs electricity. The 24-point proposal has Ukraine regaining control of Zaporizhzhia power plant, the Kakhovka Dam, and the Kinburn Spit.Provision 20: “Both countries undertake to implement educational programs in schools and society aimed at promoting understanding and tolerance of different cultures and eliminating racism and prejudice:Ukraine will adopt EU rules on religious tolerance and the protection of linguistic minorities.Both countries will agree to abolish all discriminatory measures and guarantee the rights of Ukrainian and Russian media and education.All Nazi ideology and activities must be rejected and prohibited.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: “Ukraine will adopt EU rules on religious tolerance and the protection of linguistic minorities.”Analysis: The U.S. provision is sensible, given the deep misunderstandings and bitterness that underlie every war, but the language has an anti-Ukraine slant: Ukraine is required to adopt “EU rules” on religious tolerance and minorities, but Russia is not. The Ukrainian constitution already contains provisions for protecting minorities and specifically Russian speakers (Articles 3, 10, 11, 15).The element about eliminating Nazi ideology responds to Russia’s baseless accusation that such ideologies pervade Ukraine. Ukraine would see this provision as insulting.The European counter proposal retains just the first bullet, not requiring domestic educational programs, which both sides would likely regard as intrusive, and eliminating the element about Nazi ideology.Provision 21: “Territories:Crimea, Luhansk, and Donetsk will be recognized as de facto Russian, including by the United States.Kherson and Zaporizhzhia will be frozen along the line of contact, which will mean de facto recognition along the line of contact.Russia will relinquish other agreed territories it controls outside the five regions.Ukrainian forces will withdraw from the part of Donetsk Oblast that they currently control, and this withdrawal zone will be considered a neutral demilitarized buffer zone, internationally recognized as territory belonging to the Russian Federation. Russian forces will not enter this demilitarized zone.”Parties acting: Ukraine, Russia28-point European counterproposal: “Ukraine commits not to recover its occupied sovereign territory through military means. Negotiations on territorial swaps will start from the Line of Contact.”Analysis: The U.S. provision has rightly garnered the most attention because it requires Ukraine to cede the part of Donetsk province that Russia has not yet captured (about 2,500 square miles). In exchange, Ukraine would get a few bits of territory that Russia has occupied near Kharkiv (about 700 square miles). The ceasefire line in Kherson and Zaporizhzhia provinces would be the current front line. Russia would gain about 1,800 square miles. This is purportedly what Trump and Putin discussed at the Alaska summit in June.The legal wording is loose. “De facto” Russian occupation means that it exists on the ground but not in law (de jure). That would allow Ukraine to continue to claim the territory, despite having withdrawn. However, the text also says that the demilitarized zone will be considered “belonging to the Russian Federation.” There is no mechanism for ensuring the demilitarization of this zone.Complicating this is Article 73 of the Ukrainian constitution, which says, “Issues of altering the territory of Ukraine are resolved exclusively by an All-Ukrainian referendum.” That referendum might not support the loss of territory, which has been consistently unpopular in polling.The 28-point European counterproposal starts at the line of contact and does not allow Russia to take over more territory, more reminiscent of the Istanbul Communiqué wording as of April 15, 2022. This mirrors the 24-point agreement that left territorial settlement to be “discussed and resolved after a full and unconditional ceasefire” based in the “current line of control.” This will be a major sticking point for Russia and possibly for President Trump.Provision 22: “After agreeing on future territorial arrangements, both the Russian Federation and Ukraine undertake not to change these arrangements by force. Any security guarantees will not apply in the event of a breach of this commitment.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: The provision says, “after agreeing on future territorial arrangements,” but provision 21 purports to settle territorial arrangements. It is unclear what future territorial arrangement this phrase refers to.The last sentence tells Ukraine that if it tries to regain these territories, it will lose its security guarantees (provisions 5 and 10), though it is not clear exactly what those guarantees are or who will provide them.Provision 23: “Russia will not prevent Ukraine from using the Dnieper River for commercial activities, and agreements will be reached on the free transport of grain across the Black Sea.”Parties acting: Russia28-point European counterproposal: No change.Analysis: These riverine agreements are standard on jointly controlled waterways. Ukrainian grain exports across the Black Sea remained a major point of contention through 2022–23, and the issue escalated in summer 2023 when Russia withdrew from the grain deal and again threatened the safety of shipments. Such access is important for Ukraine, a major food supplier, and for many countries worldwide that rely on its food exports.Provision 24: “A humanitarian committee will be established to resolve outstanding issues:All remaining prisoners and bodies will be exchanged on an ‘all for all’ basis.All civilian detainees and hostages will be returned, including children.A family reunification program will be implemented.Measures will be taken to alleviate the suffering of the victims of the conflict.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: Minor wording changesAnalysis: These actions are necessary at the end of every war. However, such exchanges are not always simple, as has been seen with the return of Israel’s hostages in the Gaza war. In the Korean War of 1950–53, prisoner exchange was the major sticking point because many North Korean prisoners wanted to remain in the South. Here, the return of children might be difficult because many have now spent several years in Russia and been indoctrinated into its culture.Provision 25: “Ukraine will hold elections in 100 days.”Parties acting: Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: “Ukraine will hold elections as soon as possible after the signing of the peace agreement.”Analysis: Ukraine has deferred elections during the state of emergency caused by the war, as provided for by Article 83 of its constitution. Holding new elections after the ceasefire would be reasonable for a democratic country. The U.S. provision does not prohibit President Zelensky from running again, as Russia has required in the past. However, by imposing domestic requirements on Ukraine, the proposal effectively constrains its sovereignty in violation of provision 1, and demands for elections or regime change have long featured in Kremlin rhetoric, associating Ukraine’s legitimacy with the Kremlin’s conditions.There is also a timing problem. Once the agreement is signed, both sides will begin territorial withdrawals. Fighting will continue, as will Ukraine’s state of emergency. By the time the shooting stops, Ukraine may have only 30 to 60 days left on the 100-day deadline to hold elections. That is probably inadequate.The 28-point European counterproposal eliminates this timing problem by saying “as soon as possible.”Provision 26: “All parties involved in this conflict will receive full amnesty for their actions during the war and agree not to make any claims or consider any complaints in the future.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine, others?28-point European counterproposal: “Provision will be made to address the suffering of victims of the conflict.”Analysis: The U.S. provision is morally uncomfortable, effectively saying that military personnel cannot be held accountable for any crimes they have committed. Russian crimes have been widely reported, but there have also been incidents involving Ukrainian forces.To achieve its aim of shielding servicemembers, “all parties” would need to include all organizations that might gain standing to bring court cases, such as NATO, the International Criminal Court, the Council of Europe, and even nongovernmental organizations.The 28-point European counterproposal sidesteps this question of amnesty with a general statement about addressing suffering. This is a major change that may be a stumbling block for Russia.Provision 27: “This agreement will be legally binding. Its implementation will be monitored and guaranteed by the Peace Council, headed by President Donald J. Trump. Sanctions will be imposed for violations.”Parties acting: The United States, Russia, Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: Substitutes “penalties” for “sanctions”Analysis: Presumably, the United States, Russia, and Ukraine will sign this agreement, but others, such as NATO and the World Bank, would also need to sign since they have specified roles.The membership of the peace council is not specified, yet the council has a role in sustaining the agreement. The provision does give President Trump a prominent position. “Sanctions” are unspecified here. It is unclear how this peace council will interact with the U.S.-Russia working group (provision 15) and the NATO-Russia dialogue (provision 4).The 28-point European counterproposal seeks to give the Europeans more latitude in their response.Provision 28: “Once all parties agree to this memorandum, the ceasefire will take effect immediately after both sides retreat to agreed points to begin implementation of the agreement.”Parties acting: Russia, Ukraine28-point European counterproposal: Adds that “ceasefire modalities, including monitoring, will be agreed by both parties under US supervision.”Analysis: The U.S. provision will be militarily difficult to implement as written. Ukrainian and Russian forces will need to withdraw from the territories specified in provision 21 before the shooting stops. Withdrawals under fire are extremely difficult because forces leave their secure defensive positions and become exposed as they move to new positions. A better approach would be to have a ceasefire with a deadline for both sides to conduct their withdrawals, maybe 30 days.The 28-point European counterproposal adds a mechanism to make the ceasefire operate more smoothly.Negotiations ContinueThis is just the beginning of a peace process. The Europeans and Ukraine have put forward a counterproposal and talks continue. Russia has yet to accept any text and will almost certainly propose a set of changes. The United States may have its own changes once this text is fully staffed throughout the U.S. government.Mark F. Cancian (Colonel, U.S. Marine Corps Reserve, ret.) is a senior adviser with the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Maria Snegovaya is a senior fellow with the Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program at CSIS.\nThis report is made possible by general support to CSIS. No direct sponsorship contributed to this report.\nThis report is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nUkraine,\nEurope, and\nDefense and Security\nImage\nMark F. Cancian\nSenior Adviser, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nMaria Snegovaya\nSenior Fellow, Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nDefense 360\nEurope, Russia, and Eurasia Program\nFour Years Later: Russia’s War in Ukraine\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nRelated Content\nImage\nWill Trump’s Peace Plan for Ukraine Succeed?\nCritical Questions\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nand\nYasir Atalan\n— November 21, 2025\nImage\nRussia’s War in Ukraine: The Next Chapter\nBrief\nby\nMax Bergmann\nand\nMaria Snegovaya\n— September 30, 2025\nImage\nTrump Sends Weapons to Ukraine: By the Numbers\nListen to article\nCritical Questions\nby\nMark F. Cancian\nand\nChris H. Park\n— July 15, 2025","content_cn":"乌克兰和平未竟计划：逐条条款\n照片：乌克兰总统府/资料照片/阿纳多卢通讯社/盖蒂图片社\n目录\n目录\n报告\n作者\n马克·F·坎西安\n和\n玛丽亚·斯涅戈瓦娅\n发表于2025年11月24日\n可下载内容\n下载完整报告\n635kb\n11月19日，有报道称美国和俄罗斯已就结束乌克兰战争谈判达成协议。特朗普总统目前正施压乌克兰签署该协议，但国会议员、欧洲人、乌克兰人和评论员的反应都十分尖锐，称该计划亲俄。此外，该计划存在如此多的未决事项、不一致之处和未经证实的假设，以至于按目前的书面内容根本无法实施。这不是一项协议，但可能是达成协议的起点。事实上，欧洲人已提出自己的28点版本，同时也在传阅一份此前起草的24点提案。这一欧洲版本较不亲俄，可能更接近双方都能同意的方案。据报道，目前已有一份由美国和乌克兰起草的19点提案。这份白皮书介绍了美国计划来源的不确定性、各方对该计划的回应，并将其与2022年一次达成协议的尝试进行比较，以展示三年半战争所带来的变化，最后对美国28点计划及欧洲针对该计划的反提案进行逐条详细分析。归根结底，感恩节前不会达成协议。然而，可能会有一个过程，最终促成协议出现。\n\n来源不明的“协议”\n在美国体制内部，关于该协议的来源似乎存在混乱。泄密事件发生后，美国首先确认该文件是真实的。然而，在几名参议员谴责该协议后，其中一些人表示，国务卿马可·卢比奥曾打电话说俄罗斯人提交了这份提案。事实上，独立观察人士注意到，部分文本很可能源自俄罗斯，其行文读起来像是由非英语母语者翻译的。随后，白宫否认卢比奥所谓的否认。卢比奥后来公开表示，该协议确实是真实的，旨在作为起点。称其为起点是有道理的。若干条款中的技术问题表明，起草过程中缺乏乌克兰、北约、欧洲和军方的参与。该计划为北约、“欧洲”、世界银行和八国集团分配了角色，但目前才开始征求它们的意见，而它们需要同意所分配给自己的角色。此外，该计划充斥着含糊且令人困惑的措辞，在谁做什么方面存在矛盾，对未来行动的表述不一致，并对第三方的行动作出未经证实的假设。或许正是出于这一认识，特朗普于11月22日表示，这份草案“不是我的最终报价”。11月24日，卢比奥国务卿进一步说明道：“这是一份活的、不断变化的文件。每天随着意见的输入，它都会发生变化。”鉴于条款仍在变动，与其称其为“协议”，“计划”是对该文件更好的称呼。尽管俄罗斯代表（据报道由俄罗斯和谈中的首席谈判代表基里尔·德米特里耶夫率领）参与了该计划的起草，但俄罗斯政府是否批准了这份文本尚不清楚。总统在谈判中的代表史蒂文·维特科夫很可能获得了俄罗斯方面的意见（也可能获得了乌克兰方面的意见），随后起草了自己的提案。\n普京表示，这为“最终和平解决方案奠定了基础”，但距离承诺接受该草案还相去甚远。不过，这份草案确实引起了所有人的关注。它促成了美国、乌克兰和欧洲代表在日内瓦举行紧急会议。泽连斯基正努力表现出支持特朗普的样子，同时又不愿同意一项对乌克兰不利的计划。总统驻俄罗斯和乌克兰特别代表基思·凯洛格在该计划曝光时宣布辞职，可能是因为在此过程中维特科夫取代了他。法国、德国和英国以美国的28点计划为起点，提出了一份28点反提案，修改了几项关键条款，使其不那么亲俄。例如，乌克兰将不被要求放弃更多领土，对乌克兰武装部队规模的上限将更高，而且拟议中的美国对被冻结俄罗斯资金的控制被取消了。此外，一份早先制定的24点提案也在流传。24点和28点计划之间的差异可能反映了两项计划的起草方式：前者是欧洲倡议，后者是对美国提案的回应。与2022年《伊斯坦布尔公报》的比较 美国的计划并非凭空出现；它是结束战争的一系列努力的一部分。为了了解各方在冲突解决问题上的立场变化，可以将这份新计划与2022年4月15日的《伊斯坦布尔公报》进行比较，后者是乌克兰与俄罗斯之间早期和平谈判的一次尝试。与《伊斯坦布尔公报》相比，其中几项条款对俄罗斯更为有利，反映出俄罗斯在战场上的有限成功。这一比较显示，三年半的战争带来了哪些变化，而代价是双方合计超过140万人员伤亡。一个变化的例子体现在安全保障方面。鉴于俄罗斯一再违反过去的协议，安全保障是乌克兰最主要的关切（与领土恢复并列）。《伊斯坦布尔公报》草案通过纳入“关闭乌克兰上空”的可能性并提供必要武器（第5条），提出了一种力度稍强但仍有限的形式。新提案虽然据称提供类似北约的安全保障，却更加模糊。它只描述了美国“可靠”但有条件且含义不明的保障；如果乌克兰攻击俄罗斯，即使是无意的，这些保障也将失效（第5和第10项）。对乌克兰而言，最痛苦的变化是，28点计划设想了大范围的领土让步，包括承认克里米亚、卢甘斯克以及整个顿涅茨克地区（据称包括目前仍由乌克兰控制的约25%地区）事实上属于俄罗斯；冻结赫尔松和扎波罗热的前线；以及建立非军事区。相比之下，《伊斯坦布尔公报》草案将这些地区视为未解决问题：它仅将克里米亚（第8条）和附件6中列出的顿巴斯领土（第9条）排除在安全保障之外，并不涉及西方承认俄罗斯的控制。总体而言，《伊斯坦布尔公报》草案推迟了领土问题，而不是将俄罗斯的控制正式确定下来。《伊斯坦布尔公报》和新计划都设想解除对俄罗斯的制裁，但新计划在这一点上不够具体。新计划还增加了俄罗斯重返八国集团以及与美国签署长期合作协议的条款（条款\n13）。不同于《伊斯坦布尔公报》，新计划要求乌克兰在100天内举行选举（第25项），据报道这是美方加入的一点。有些条款对俄罗斯不太有利。事实上，有些条款对俄罗斯极为不利，俄罗斯很可能会认为无法接受。例如，新计划显然会在美国指导下清算所有被冻结的俄罗斯资产，为乌克兰重建提供资金，并建立一个美俄特别投资机制，用于开展联合项目。\n\n新计划要求立即停火。鉴于俄罗斯正在持续进攻且最近取得了进展，这一条款对乌克兰更有利。《伊斯坦布尔草案》在这一点上含糊不清（第28项与第11条之比）。\n\n《伊斯坦布尔公报》提议严格限制乌克兰与其他国家的军事合作（第1条和第2条）。新计划的限制较少。它禁止加入北约，但不限制乌克兰的武器装备（第7项和第8项）。第6项将乌克兰武装部队人数上限设为60万人，低于目前约88万人。《伊斯坦布尔公报》提出的上限低得多，俄罗斯一度建议上限为8.5万人。\n\n《伊斯坦布尔公报》要求将俄语定为乌克兰官方语言，并规定在签署后30天内实施（第12条）。新计划在这方面相对更加平衡。它要求两国采取促进文化宽容的教育和法律措施，包括乌克兰按照欧盟宗教和少数群体权利标准进行调整，以及相互取消妨碍平等获取乌克兰和俄罗斯媒体及教育的歧视性做法（第20项）。\n\n总体而言，有利于乌克兰的条款不如有利于俄罗斯的条款具体。\n\n最后，两项计划之间存在若干重叠之处。两者都允许乌克兰申请加入欧盟（第3条对第11项）；重申乌克兰的无核地位（第2m条和第18项）；都包括全面司法特赦（第7条和第26项）以及禁止纳粹意识形态和活动（第13条和第20c项）；规定全面交换战俘（附件5对第24a项）；并设想建立监督机制。《伊斯坦布尔草案》提议成立一个由争议各方各派一名代表组成的委员会（第14条）。新版本则成立一个由美国、乌克兰和俄罗斯组成的联合工作组及和平委员会，由唐纳德·特朗普担任主席，并授权对违规者实施制裁（第15段和第28段）。\n\n美国计划与欧洲反提案逐项分析\n\n以下分析评估了美方计划草案的28项条款，该草案由美联社于11月21日公布。该计划仍在不断演变，据报道目前只有19项，但这些内容尚未公布。本分析还包括欧洲提出的24项和28项方案。不同版本之间的措辞细微差异可能反映了转录错误。\n\n第1项：“乌克兰的主权将得到确认。”\n\n行动方：俄罗斯\n\n欧洲28项反提案：小幅修改（乌克兰的主权将“再次确认”）\n\n分析：这将是乌克兰的一场胜利，因为普京经常表示乌克兰不是一个真正的国家，而是俄罗斯的一部分。\n\n第2项：“俄罗斯、乌克兰和欧洲将缔结一项全面的互不侵犯协议。过去30年的所有模糊问题将被视为已经解决。”\n\n行动方：俄罗斯、乌克兰、“欧洲”\n\n28项欧洲\n欧洲反提案：措辞略有变化。“欧洲”被替换为“北约”。分析：这一意图值得称赞，但范围如此广泛，以至于显得不切实际，甚至天真。这些讨论将在哪里进行，议程又将如何制定？谁代表“欧洲”？条款3：“预计俄罗斯不会入侵邻国，北约也不会进一步扩大。”行动方：俄罗斯、北约28点欧洲反提案：删除该条款分析：这一条款并不令人意外。俄罗斯的邻国不希望再发生入侵，俄罗斯也不希望北约进一步扩大。然而，“预计”意味着什么并不明确。这听起来不像相关任何一方作出的承诺。北约全部32个成员国都必须同意这一限制，而其中一些成员可能不愿意。尽管北约目前没有立即扩大成员范围的计划，但格鲁吉亚和波斯尼亚和黑塞哥维那已有朝向加入成员资格的行动计划。欧洲版本删除了整个条款，可能是因为第一部分毫无用处，而且北约不希望俄罗斯来规定其政策。条款4：“俄罗斯与北约之间将举行由美国斡旋的对话，以解决所有安全问题并创造缓和局势的条件，从而确保全球安全并增加合作和未来经济发展的机会。”行动方：俄罗斯、北约、美国28点欧洲反提案：“和平协议签署后，将召开俄罗斯与北约之间的对话，以处理所有安全关切，营造缓和局势的环境，确保全球安全，并增加互联互通和未来经济机会。”分析：与条款2一样，这一意图值得称赞，但实施将需要大量工作。这些讨论将在哪里进行，议程又将如何制定？使用“斡旋”一词很奇怪。这意味着美国是一个中立的第三方，尽管它是北约成员。美国盟友可能会对这样一种想法感到不安：美国能够置身于其联盟责任之外，在朋友与对手之间保持公正。欧洲版本的主要变化是将措辞改为“互联互通和未来经济机会”。这表明俄罗斯与西方的联系取决于其自身的行动。条款5：“乌克兰将获得可靠的安全保障。”行动方：未知28点欧洲反提案：“乌克兰将获得强有力的安全保障。”分析：这对乌克兰而言是关键问题——安全保障，以防止俄罗斯再次入侵。然而，条款没有说明安全保障将是什么，也没有说明由谁提供。这一条款只是意向声明，而非保障，过于模糊，乌克兰无法依赖。欧洲提案同样模糊，只是替换成了更强的形容词“强有力”。条款6：“乌克兰武装部队的规模将限制在600,000人。”行动方：乌克兰28点欧洲反提案：“乌克兰军队规模在和平时期上限为800,000人。”分析：美国的条款缓和了俄罗斯此前要求解除乌克兰武装的要求。尽管许多评论人士将这一条款视为对乌克兰的重大限制，但削减兵力对乌克兰实施而言不会成为问题。停火后，乌克兰将希望复员部分部队，目前估计\n达到88万人，最终可能降至60万人以下。战前，乌克兰武装部队的现役人员总数仅为20.9万人。俄罗斯可能希望明确说明60万人这一数字包含哪些人员，以防乌克兰进行创造性统计。28点欧洲反提案提高了上限，并澄清该上限适用于和平时期。美国条款在这一点上并不明确。24点欧洲提案不对乌克兰军队规模施加任何限制。\n\n条款7：“乌克兰同意在其宪法中写明不加入北约，北约同意在其章程中加入一项规定，未来不接纳乌克兰。”\n\n行动方：乌克兰、北约\n\n28点欧洲反提案：“乌克兰加入北约取决于北约成员国的共识，而这种共识并不存在。”\n\n分析：美国提案将令乌克兰大失所望。乌克兰认为，加入北约对其长期安全至关重要，而且近年来一直在迅速采用北约标准和装备。然而，在短期内，鉴于冲突仍在持续以及战后和平前景不确定，乌克兰不太可能加入北约。尽管这一禁令被描述为“永久性”禁令，但各国宪法和北约协议都可以改变，不过在可预见的未来，这种改变不太可能发生。\n\n欧洲版本是重大变化，允许乌克兰未来加入北约，但承认这在短期内不会发生。\n\n条款8：“北约同意不在乌克兰驻扎部队。”\n\n行动方：北约\n\n28点欧洲反提案：“北约同意在和平时期不在乌克兰永久驻扎受其指挥的部队。”\n\n分析：这是俄罗斯关注的一项关键条款，因为俄罗斯不希望其边境出现更多外国军队。一些北约国家曾考虑将部队派往乌克兰，作为安全保障和维和力量的一部分。事实上，24点欧洲提案将允许乌克兰“决定由乌克兰政府邀请、驻扎在其领土上的友好部队的存在、武器和行动”。\n\n特朗普政府已经排除派遣美国地面部队的可能性，拜登政府也采取了同样立场。理论上，非北约国家可以提供维和部队，但很少有国家同时具备承担这一要求极高的角色所需的规模、技能、中立性和意愿。印度和土耳其是最合适的两个国家。尽管28点欧洲反提案似乎禁止和平时期的北约部队，但其中可能存在一个漏洞，允许北约国家的部队在和平时期驻扎于乌克兰，只是不受北约指挥。该提案还将允许北约部队在战时进入乌克兰。这或许反映出一些北约部队已经在乌克兰境内。\n\n条款9：“欧洲战斗机将驻扎在波兰。”\n\n行动方：北约\n\n28点欧洲反提案：无变化\n\n分析：这大概意味着来自其他国家、驻扎在波兰的战斗机。在北约“东部哨兵”行动下，事实已经如此；该行动是对俄罗斯无人机穿越东欧（包括波兰）作出的回应。\n\n这是波兰的一项关键条款。波兰担心俄罗斯的侵略，并希望得到保证，即其他北约国家将把部队前沿部署到位。\n\n该计划没有提及波兰和东欧地区的地面部队，而美国在这些地区部署了大量军队。这可能表明特朗普政府正在考虑撤出这些部队，但也可能反映出该计划总体上的\nlooseness.条款10：“美国担保：美国将因提供担保而获得补偿；如果乌克兰入侵俄罗斯，它将失去担保；如果俄罗斯入侵乌克兰，除采取果断的协调军事回应外，所有全球制裁将恢复，对新领土的承认以及本协议的所有其他利益将被撤销；如果乌克兰无正当理由向莫斯科或圣彼得堡发射导弹，安全担保将被视为无效。”\n\n行动方：美国、俄罗斯、乌克兰\n\n欧洲28点反提案：删除最后一项，并补充美国担保“与第5条相呼应”。\n\n分析：美国获得补偿是典型的特朗普式条款，反映了他的信念，即这不是美国的战争，因此美国应因其贡献获得报酬。补偿的形式不明确，其来源也不明确。它可能属于条款14项下的补偿，也可能是在此基础上的额外补偿。\n\n要求两国都不得入侵对方，是协议的基本内容。明确规定后果，可以使这些要求更具约束力。\n\n最后一项很奇怪。为什么禁止乌克兰发射导弹的规定仅限于莫斯科或圣彼得堡，为什么在其他地方发射导弹却被允许？一旦停火，所有跨境射击都应停止。很难想象俄罗斯会接受这一项，而欧洲反提案则将其完全删除。\n\n条款11：“乌克兰有资格加入欧盟，在审议这一问题期间，将获得对欧洲市场的短期优惠准入。”\n\n行动方：乌克兰、欧盟\n\n欧洲28点反提案：措辞略有修改\n\n分析：乌克兰已申请加入欧盟，目前正处于入盟进程中。这对乌克兰很重要，既能增强其经济实力，也能使其与欧洲及西方建立更紧密的联系。\n\n条款12：“一项强有力的全球性措施方案，用于重建乌克兰，包括但不限于：设立乌克兰发展基金，投资于快速增长的产业，包括技术、数据中心和人工智能。美国将与乌克兰合作，共同重建、开发、现代化并运营乌克兰的天然气基础设施，包括管道和储气设施。共同努力修复受战争影响的地区，以恢复、重建和现代化城市及住宅区。基础设施发展。矿产和自然资源开采。世界银行将制定一项特别融资方案，以加快这些努力。”\n\n行动方：美国、乌克兰、世界银行\n\n欧洲28点反提案：措辞略有修改\n\n分析：战争造成严重破坏后，重建乌克兰对其长期经济健康和政治稳定至关重要。这些条款是有益的一步。然而，缺乏具体细节使其影响仍不确定。“乌克兰发展基金”的资金从何而来？\n\n世界银行需要同意承担所分配的重建职责。\n\n“矿产和自然资源开采”可能指美乌采矿协议。\n\n条款13：“俄罗斯将重新融入全球经济：制裁的解除将分阶段并根据具体情况进行讨论和协商。美国将签订一项长期经济合作协议，以实现双方共同发展，在\n在能源、自然资源、基础设施、人工智能、数据中心、北极地区稀土金属开采项目以及其他互惠互利的企业机会等领域。俄罗斯将受邀重新加入八国集团（G8）。\n\n行动方：美国、俄罗斯、G8\n\n欧洲28点反提案：措辞略有修改\n\n分析：这一直是普京的目标，也是特朗普感兴趣的事项。普京希望重新融入世界经济，摆脱痛苦的制裁，并让八国集团承认俄罗斯是一个大国。特朗普认为恢复与俄罗斯的贸易将带来经济利益。\n\n第14条：“冻结资金将按以下方式使用：\n\n1000亿美元被冻结的俄罗斯资产将投资于由美国主导的重建乌克兰并对乌克兰进行投资的努力；\n\n美国将获得该项目50%的利润。欧洲将增加1000亿美元，以提高可用于乌克兰重建的投资金额。被冻结的欧洲资金将被解冻。其余被冻结的俄罗斯资金将投资于一个独立的美俄投资工具，该工具将在特定领域实施联合项目。该基金旨在加强关系、增加共同利益，从而形成不再回到冲突状态的强大激励。”\n\n行动方：俄罗斯、欧洲、美国\n\n欧洲28点反提案：“乌克兰将得到全面重建并获得经济赔偿，包括通过俄罗斯主权资产进行赔偿；这些资产将继续被冻结，直至俄罗斯赔偿对乌克兰造成的损失。”\n\n分析：美国的这项条款非同寻常。实际上，美国正在接管被冻结的俄罗斯资金，并将其中很大一部分用于自身利益。这将迫使欧洲人为这一努力作出贡献，推测资金来源是被冻结在欧洲的俄罗斯资产。将剩余的俄罗斯资金投入“美俄投资工具”，等于让美国获得俄罗斯资金的一半。很难想象俄罗斯人或欧洲人会同意如此明显的资金挪用。\n\n欧洲的提案取消了美国接管俄罗斯资金的努力。鉴于特朗普对赔偿问题的强烈立场，这可能会成为他的一大症结。不过，这将为俄罗斯消除一个主要障碍。\n\n第15条：“将成立一个美俄安全问题联合工作组，以促进并确保遵守本协议的所有条款。”\n\n行动方：美国、俄罗斯\n\n欧洲28点反提案：“将成立一个联合安全工作组，由美国、乌克兰、俄罗斯和欧洲人参与，以促进并执行本协议的所有条款。”\n\n分析：几乎每项和平协议都有合规机制，因此成立一个协调实施工作的团体是有道理的。目前尚不清楚该团体将如何与第4条中的北约—俄罗斯对话或第27条中的和平委员会协作。值得注意的是，美国提议的工作组排除了乌克兰、北约以及计划中被赋予责任的所有其他实体。\n\n不出所料，欧洲的反提案将乌克兰、俄罗斯和欧洲人加入该工作组。\n\n第16条：“俄罗斯将把其对欧洲和乌克兰的不侵略政策写入法律。”\n\n行动方：俄罗斯\n\n欧洲28点反提案：措辞略有修改\n\n分析：这将落实第3条（俄罗斯不得攻击邻国）和第22条（不得进行领土\n以武力改变）。“依法”意味着由俄罗斯国家杜马采取行动，而不仅仅是由普京签署一项法令。要使这一条款可行，最终协议必须规定俄罗斯国家杜马通过该法律的截止日期。不过，鉴于俄罗斯一再违反欧洲安全承诺，很难认真看待这一条款。\n\n条款17：“美国和俄罗斯将同意延长关于核武器不扩散与控制的条约的有效期，包括《第一阶段削减战略武器条约》。”\n\n采取行动的各方：美国、俄罗斯\n\n28点欧洲反提案：措辞略有改动；美联社报道中使用了“Fair Start”，但这很可能是一个错误。\n\n分析：目前尚不清楚核条约为何会出现在一份旨在结束俄罗斯与乌克兰战争的计划中。核条约很重要，其中一些条约（如《开放天空条约》）已经被暂停。《新削减战略武器条约》将于2026年2月4日到期（而文本可能想指的是《新削减战略武器条约》，而不是已经到期的《第一阶段削减战略武器条约》）。延长这些条约具有重大的政策影响。然而，文本只是顺带提及，甚至没有明确说明涉及哪些条约。尽管文本称美国和俄罗斯“将同意”，但这可能只是意味着两国将开始讨论。这不会影响俄罗斯在战争期间令许多国家感到不安的武力恫吓。\n\n条款18：“乌克兰同意根据《不扩散核武器条约》成为无核国家。”\n\n采取行动的各方：乌克兰\n\n28点欧洲反提案：措辞略有改动\n\n分析：战争前乌克兰就是这一地位。苏联解体时，乌克兰拥有核武器，但根据《布达佩斯协议》，乌克兰放弃了核武器，以换取美国、英国和俄罗斯的安全保证。该协议未能有效保护乌克兰，一些乌克兰人提出，如果无法加入北约，核武器将是一种有效的安全保障。这一条款将排除这条道路。\n\n条款19：“扎波罗热核电站将在[国际原子能机构]监督下启动，所产生的电力将在俄罗斯和乌克兰之间平均分配——50:50。”\n\n采取行动的各方：俄罗斯、乌克兰、国际原子能机构\n\n28点欧洲反提案：措辞略有改动\n\n分析：该核电站位于前线俄方一侧，但扎波罗热市以及核电站的许多工作人员位于乌克兰一侧。这是一项合理的条款，因为每个人都需要电力。24点提案则规定乌克兰重新控制扎波罗热核电站、卡霍夫卡大坝和金伯恩沙嘴。\n\n条款20：“两国承诺在学校和社会中实施教育项目，旨在促进对不同文化的理解与宽容，并消除种族主义和偏见：\n乌克兰将采用欧盟关于宗教宽容和保护语言少数群体的规则。\n两国将同意废除所有歧视性措施，并保障乌克兰和俄罗斯媒体及教育的权利。\n所有纳粹意识形态和活动都必须被拒绝并禁止。”\n\n采取行动的各方：俄罗斯、乌克兰\n\n28点欧洲反提案：“乌克兰将采用欧盟关于宗教宽容和保护语言少数群体的规则。”\n\n分析：鉴于根深蒂固的\n构成每场战争根源的误解和怨恨，但其措辞带有反乌克兰倾向：要求乌克兰在宗教宽容和少数群体问题上采纳“欧盟规则”，但没有对俄罗斯提出同样要求。乌克兰宪法已经包含保护少数群体、特别是俄语使用者的条款（第3条、第10条、第11条、第15条）。关于消除纳粹意识形态的内容，是对俄罗斯毫无根据地指控此类意识形态遍布乌克兰的回应。乌克兰会认为这一条款具有侮辱性。\n\n欧洲反提案仅保留第一项，没有要求开展国内教育项目（双方都可能认为这具有侵入性），并删除了关于纳粹意识形态的内容。\n\n第21条：“领土：克里米亚、卢甘斯克和顿涅茨克将被承认为事实上属于俄罗斯，包括得到美国承认。赫尔松和扎波罗热将沿接触线冻结，这将意味着沿接触线事实上予以承认。俄罗斯将放弃其在五个地区以外控制的其他经协商确定的领土。乌克兰军队将撤出其目前控制的顿涅茨克州部分地区，该撤出区将被视为中立非军事区，并在国际上承认为属于俄罗斯联邦的领土。俄罗斯军队不得进入这一非军事区。”\n\n采取行动的各方：乌克兰、俄罗斯\n\n欧洲28点反提案：“乌克兰承诺不通过军事手段收复其被占领的主权领土。关于领土交换的谈判将从接触线开始。”\n\n分析：美国条款理所当然地获得了最多关注，因为它要求乌克兰割让顿涅茨克省中俄罗斯尚未占领的部分（约2,500平方英里）。作为交换，乌克兰将获得俄罗斯在哈尔科夫附近占领的几小块领土（约700平方英里）。赫尔松省和扎波罗热省的停火线将以当前前线为准。俄罗斯将获得约1,800平方英里。据称，这就是特朗普和普京在6月阿拉斯加峰会上讨论的内容。\n\n法律措辞很宽泛。“事实上”被俄罗斯占领意味着这种占领存在于实地，但不具有法律效力（de jure）。这将允许乌克兰继续声称对该领土拥有主权，尽管其已经撤出。然而，文本还称，非军事区将被视为“属于俄罗斯联邦”。没有任何机制来确保该区域的非军事化。\n\n使问题更复杂的是乌克兰宪法第73条，其中规定：“关于改变乌克兰领土的问题，须专门通过全乌克兰公民投票解决。”这次公投可能不会支持失去领土，因为民调一直显示公众不欢迎这一结果。\n\n欧洲28点反提案从接触线开始，不允许俄罗斯接管更多领土，更接近截至2022年4月15日的《伊斯坦布尔公报》措辞。这与24点协议相呼应；该协议把领土解决留待“在以‘当前控制线’为基础的全面且无条件停火之后讨论并解决”。这将成为俄罗斯、也可能成为特朗普总统的主要争议点。\n\n第22条：“在就未来领土安排达成一致后，俄罗斯联邦和乌克兰双方承诺不以武力改变这些安排。任何安全保障在违反这一承诺的情况下均不适用。”\n\n各方\n代理方：俄罗斯、乌克兰\n\n28点欧洲反提案：措辞小幅修改\n\n分析：该条款称“在就未来领土安排达成一致后”，但第21条似乎意在解决领土安排问题。目前不清楚这句话所指的未来领土安排是什么。\n\n最后一句告知乌克兰，如果它试图收复这些领土，就会失去其安全保障（第5条和第10条），不过这些保障具体是什么、由谁提供，并不明确。\n\n第23条：“俄罗斯不会阻止乌克兰将第聂伯河用于商业活动，并将就粮食经黑海自由运输达成协议。”\n\n行动方：俄罗斯\n\n28点欧洲反提案：无变化。\n\n分析：这些河流航运协议是共同控制水道上的标准安排。2022年至2023年期间，乌克兰经黑海出口粮食一直是争议焦点；2023年夏季，俄罗斯退出粮食协议，再次威胁运输安全，问题进一步升级。对乌克兰这个主要粮食供应国而言，这种通行权十分重要；对全球许多依赖其粮食出口的国家而言也是如此。\n\n第24条：“将成立一个人道主义委员会，以解决尚未解决的问题：\n\n所有剩余的战俘和遗体将按照‘全部换全部’的原则进行交换。\n\n所有平民被拘留者和人质都将获释并返回，包括儿童。\n\n将实施家庭团聚计划。\n\n将采取措施减轻冲突受害者的痛苦。”\n\n行动方：俄罗斯、乌克兰\n\n28点欧洲反提案：措辞小幅修改\n\n分析：这些行动是每场战争结束时都必需的。然而，这类交换并不总是简单，正如加沙战争中以色列人质获释一事所显示的那样。在1950年至1953年的朝鲜战争中，战俘交换是主要症结，因为许多朝鲜战俘希望留在南方。在此情况下，儿童的返回可能会很困难，因为许多儿童如今已在俄罗斯生活了数年，并被灌输了俄罗斯文化。\n\n第25条：“乌克兰将在100天内举行选举。”\n\n行动方：乌克兰\n\n28点欧洲反提案：“乌克兰将在和平协议签署后尽快举行选举。”\n\n分析：正如其宪法第83条所规定，乌克兰因战争造成的紧急状态而推迟了选举。停火后举行新的选举，对于一个民主国家而言是合理的。美国的条款并未禁止泽连斯基总统再次参选，而俄罗斯过去曾要求这样做。然而，通过对乌克兰施加国内要求，该提案实际上限制了其主权，违反了第1条；而要求举行选举或更换政权，长期以来一直是克里姆林宫言论的一部分，将乌克兰的合法性与克里姆林宫的条件联系起来。\n\n这里还存在时间安排问题。协议签署后，双方都将开始撤出领土。战斗仍将继续，乌克兰的紧急状态也将持续。到枪声停止时，乌克兰可能只剩下30至60天来完成100天期限内的选举。这可能远远不够。\n\n28点欧洲反提案通过使用“尽快”一词，消除了这一时间安排问题。\n\n第26条：“参与这场冲突的所有各方将因其在战争期间的行为获得全面赦免，并同意不\n以便未来提出任何索赔或考虑任何申诉。”\n\n采取行动的各方：俄罗斯、乌克兰、其他方？\n\n欧洲28点反提案：“将采取措施处理冲突受害者的苦难。”\n\n分析：美国的这一条款在道德上令人不适，实际上是在说，军人不应因其所犯下的任何罪行而被追究责任。俄罗斯的罪行已被广泛报道，但也有涉及乌克兰军队的事件。为了实现保护军人的目标，“所有各方”需要包括所有可能获得诉讼资格、能够提起法庭案件的组织，例如北约、国际刑事法院、欧洲委员会，甚至非政府组织。\n\n欧洲28点反提案以关于处理苦难的一般性声明回避了这一大赦问题。这是一项重大变化，可能成为俄罗斯的绊脚石。\n\n第27条：“本协议具有法律约束力。其实施将由和平委员会监督并予以保障，该委员会由唐纳德·J·特朗普总统领导。违反协议将受到制裁。”\n\n采取行动的各方：美国、俄罗斯、乌克兰\n\n欧洲28点反提案：以“惩罚”替代“制裁”\n\n分析：推测美国、俄罗斯和乌克兰将签署这份协议，但北约和世界银行等其他方面也需要签署，因为协议为它们规定了具体角色。\n\n和平委员会的成员构成并未明确说明，但该委员会在维持协议方面发挥作用。该条款确实赋予特朗普总统一个突出地位。这里没有具体说明“制裁”的内容。尚不清楚该和平委员会将如何与美俄工作组（第15条）以及北约—俄罗斯对话（第4条）互动。\n\n欧洲28点反提案寻求给予欧洲人在作出回应时更大的回旋余地。\n\n第28条：“一旦所有各方同意这份备忘录，双方撤退至商定地点以开始执行协议后，停火将立即生效。”\n\n采取行动的各方：俄罗斯、乌克兰\n\n欧洲28点反提案：补充规定，“包括监测在内的停火方式将由双方在美国监督下商定。”\n\n分析：按照美国条款的书面规定，军事上将很难实施。乌克兰和俄罗斯军队需要在停止交火之前，从第21条规定的领土撤出。在炮火下撤离极其困难，因为部队会离开安全的防御阵地，在向新阵地转移时暴露在外。更好的做法是实行停火，并规定双方完成撤军的期限，例如30天。\n\n欧洲28点反提案增加了一项机制，使停火能够更加顺利地实施。\n\n谈判继续\n\n这只是和平进程的开始。欧洲方面和乌克兰已提出反提案，谈判仍在继续。俄罗斯尚未接受任何文本，并几乎肯定会提出一系列修改意见。一旦这份文本在美国政府内部完成全面协调，美国可能也会提出自己的修改意见。\n\n马克·F·坎西安（美国海军陆战队预备役上校，退役）是华盛顿战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）防务与安全部的高级顾问。玛丽亚·斯涅戈瓦娅是CSIS欧洲、俄罗斯与欧亚项目的高级研究员。\n\n本报告得以完成，得益于\n对CSIS的一般性支持。本报告没有接受任何直接赞助。\n本报告由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人的观点。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n乌克兰、\n欧洲，以及\n国防与安全\n图片\nMark F. Cancian\n国防与安全部高级顾问\n图片\nMaria Snegovaya\n欧洲、俄罗斯与欧亚项目高级研究员\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\nDefense 360\n欧洲、俄罗斯与欧亚项目\n四年之后：俄罗斯在乌克兰的战争\n地缘政治与外交政策\n相关内容\n图片\n特朗普的乌克兰和平计划会成功吗？\n关键问题\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n和\nYasir Atalan\n— 2025年11月21日\n图片\n俄罗斯在乌克兰的战争：下一篇章\n简报\n作者\nMax Bergmann\n和\nMaria Snegovaya\n— 2025年9月30日\n图片\n特朗普向乌克兰提供武器：数据一览\n收听文章\n关键问题\n作者\nMark F. Cancian\n和\nChris H. Park\n— 2025年7月15日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-11-24T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1091","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"联盟修复：新西兰与库克群岛重修关系","title_en":"A Union Restored: New Zealand and the Cook Islands Repair Ties","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表“关键问题”文章，梳理新西兰与库克群岛关系。文章称，库克群岛2025年2月与中国签署全面战略伙伴关系后，新西兰因缺乏磋商而暂停约3000万新西兰元核心资助，双边关系恶化。2026年4月1日，新西兰外长彼得斯访问库克群岛，与总理布朗签署防务与安全宣言，恢复年度资助，并确认新西兰为防务安全首选伙伴。文章还讨论太平洋地区影响力竞争与深海采矿议题。","summary_en":"A CSIS Critical Questions piece examines New Zealand–Cook Islands relations. After the Cook Islands signed a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership with China in February 2025, New Zealand suspended roughly NZ$30 million in core funding over a lack of consultation. On April 1, 2026, Foreign Minister Winston Peters visited the Cook Islands and signed a Defense and Security Declaration with Prime Minister Mark Brown, restoring annual funding and affirming New Zealand as the partner of choice on defense and security. The authors also discuss Pacific influence competition and deep-sea mining.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:27:01.587033+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/union-restored-new-zealand-and-cook-islands-repair-ties","content":"A Union Restored: New Zealand and the Cook Islands Repair Ties\nPhoto: Leo Altman/Adobe Stock\nCritical Questions\nby\nKathryn Paik\nand\nCharles Edel\nPublished April 28, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nOn April 1, 2026, New Zealand Foreign Minister Winston Peters made a one-day visit to the Cook Islands to sign a defense and security declaration with Cook Islands Prime Minister Mark Brown. This seemingly benign statement between the two already uniquely close nations masked a moment of reconciliation that holds much larger geopolitical significance.Q1: What is the unique nature of the New Zealand–Cook Islands relationship?A1: The Cook Islands is a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand, a status which is defined under New Zealand’s 1964 Cook Islands Constitution Act and gives the Cook Islands sovereignty over its own affairs, with some consultation for external affairs and defense, while New Zealand is required to respond to requests for assistance with respect to disasters and defense. While this status technically allows the Cook Islands to conduct its own external affairs, there has historically been close consultation between the Cook Islands and New Zealand on all external matters.Q2: What caused the falling out between the Cook Islands and NZL over the past year?A2: In February 2025, Cook Islands Prime Minister Mark Brown announced that the Cook Islands and China had signed a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership (CSP) during his state visit to Beijing. The CSP established a five-year framework for cooperation between the two countries on several issues, including multiple security-adjacent sectors such as deep-sea mining, infrastructure development, and technology and innovation. New Zealand officials claimed to be “blindsided” by the deal and expressed concern over the perceived lack of consultation regarding the agreement.As a result, the bilateral relationship continued to deteriorate through the end of 2025. While Cook Islands Prime Minister Brown emphasized the importance of this special bilateral relationship at the 60th anniversary of the establishment of Cook Islands free association with New Zealand last August, the prime minister has also repeatedly stressed the self-governing nature of the Cook Islands and the independence of his country to conduct its own external affairs, noting that the country is “not under anyone’s domination or control.” In mid-2025, New Zealand announced that it had suspended millions in core support funding to the Cook Islands due to “lack of trust” in the bilateral relationship. This funding pause, which amounted to almost NZ$30 million by late 2025 (or more than 10 percent of the Cook Islands’ total annual budget), forced an early budgetary overhaul in the Cook Islands to address a potential fiscal shortfall in essential services.At its core, this falling out was a difference of opinions over the extent to which the Cook Islands’ agreements with Beijing were relevant to New Zealand’s own national security. Documents released under New Zealand’s Official Information Act show that for months prior to the final signing, Wellington attempted to get updates on the status and purpose behind the Cook Islands’ intent to sign a CSP with China. The Cook Islands—and also, predictably, China—insisted that the agreements did not explicitly include defense and security components. The lack of transparency raised concern within the New Zealand government that the Cook Islands were not abiding by an inherent expectation for consultation on issues of “mutual interest,” and that the growing relationship with China in sectors such as hydrography and deep-sea mining could have security implications for New Zealand.Q3: How and why did New Zealand and the Cook Islands repair ties?A3: Following months of bilateral tensions, both sides were eager to repair the relationship. As one of only two states in free association with New Zealand (the other being Niue), the Cook Islands is integral to New Zealand’s security settings across the Pacific. The China–Cook Islands deal was in many ways a wake-up call for New Zealand, and Wellington was anxious to mend this rift—but in a way that provided limits to China’s outreach to its near neighbors. In late 2025, Prime Minister Brown also noted that it was vital to “nurture” this special bond between New Zealand and the Cook Islands and cited the allocation of additional funding to “strengthen and support this partnership” as part of the 2026 budget.It was in this context that New Zealand Foreign Minister Winston Peters made a one-day trip to the Cook Islands on April 1, 2026, to meet with Prime Minister Brown and sign a Declaration on Defense and Security, with the aim of providing “clarity” on the contours and scope of this special relationship. For the Cook Islands, this rapprochement will result in the welcome resumption of New Zealand’s annual funding stream to the Cook Islands’ government. To ease Wellington’s security concerns, the declaration affirms that “New Zealand is its partner of choice regarding defense and security matters” and states that “the Partners will not enter into activities, agreements, or arrangements with third parties that would compromise” the principles of this agreement.Q4: What does this mean for the future of Cook Islands–New Zealand relations, and for the broader competition for influence in the Pacific?A4: Tempers between Rarotonga and Wellington have cooled, relations have improved, and, significantly, expectations for future dealings on sensitive issues involving outside players have been clarified. And yet questions remain, both in the context of Cook Islands–New Zealand relationship, and about how China is slowly resetting the goalposts on the extent of its security—or security-adjacent—relationships across the Pacific.While both capitols are promoting messaging that tensions have passed and the two close “cousins” have returned to a usual state of things, there remains a potentially unstable misalignment of how the current Cook Islands government and Wellington view the contours of this relationship. While the New Zealand Foreign Minister Winston Peters declared that the new declaration would put “massive limitations” on the Cook Islands’ agreements with China, Prime Minister Brown recently emphasized to his parliament that the Cook Islands “is a self-governing state. We govern our own affairs,” and that the relationship with New Zealand, while important, does not “define the limits of our engagement with the world.” While there is now a formalized consultative mechanism between the countries on matters of national security, the potential for diverging national interests, particularly on matters where one party might see economic benefit while the other sees security risks, will continue to exist. Nowhere is this clearer than in the drive to acquire resources, which has prompted major players like China and the United States to push for bilateral agreements with the Cook Islands.For Wellington’s part, this “family” spat with the Cook Islands, in concert with other alarming moves from China such as the February 2025 live fire exercise in the Tasman Strait, has prompted New Zealand to consider a more security-centered approach to the Pacific, where it looks to more clearly articulate its own national security concerns in its dealings with Pacific neighbors, while acting to preclude greater Chinese involvement in areas that carry national security risks. Australia has already embraced a more security-oriented strategy in the Pacific, signing explicit security agreements with Nauru, Papua New Guinea, Tuvalu, and Fiji. This is also an approach that the United States has traditionally followed, particularly in Micronesia, with the Compacts of Free Association with Palau, the Marshall Islands, and the Federated States of Micronesia, to which the United States provides economic assistance and federal programs in exchange for unilateral defense access.Finally, deep-sea mining is an issue likely to have consequential effects on the geopolitics and the geoeconomic relationships of the Pacific, as it has already begun to do in the Cook Islands. Both the United States and China have signed agreements with the Cook Islands on further exploration of the minerals in their Exclusive Economic Zones, and yet New Zealand, its closest strategic partner, has not yet developed a clear approach to deep-sea mining. With the explosive growth in the global demand for access to critical minerals, deep-sea mining is both a fast-developing and a highly contested sector—one where the technology, policy, diplomatic, legal, and environmental issues are rapidly evolving. While the international community does not yet have a consensus on how, when, where, and most importantly, if it should proceed with deep-sea mining, it is an issue that will continue roiling the waters of the Pacific, affecting the economic, political, and strategic calculations of Pacific states and the larger partners surrounding them.Kathryn Paik is a deputy director and senior fellow with the Australia Chair at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Charles Edel is a senior adviser and the inaugural Australia Chair at CSIS.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAustralia, New Zealand, and Pacific\nImage\nKathryn Paik\nDeputy Director and Senior Fellow, Australia Chair\nImage\nCharles Edel\nSenior Adviser and Australia Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nAustralia Chair\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nRelated Content\nImage\nNew Zealand’s Wake-Up Call: Beijing’s Reach Reorienting Wellington’s Regional Role\nCommentary\nby\nCharles Edel\nand\nKathryn Paik\n— May 7, 2025\nImage\nDoes the U.S.–Cook Islands Seabed Minerals Announcement Signal a New Front in Great Power Competition?\nCritical Questions\nby\nKathryn Paik\nand\nGracelin Baskaran\n— August 20, 2025","content_cn":"重修于好：新西兰与库克群岛修复关系\n图片：Leo Altman/Adobe Stock\n关键问题\n作者\nKathryn Paik\n和\nCharles Edel\n发表于2026年4月28日\n远程可视化\n2026年4月1日，新西兰外交部长Winston Peters对库克群岛进行了一天访问，并与库克群岛总理Mark Brown签署了一项防务与安全声明。这份看似无害、由两个本已关系独特亲密的国家达成的声明，掩盖了一个和解时刻，而其地缘政治意义要大得多。Q1：新西兰与库克群岛关系的独特性质是什么？A1：库克群岛是一个与新西兰自由联合的自治国家，这一地位由新西兰1964年《库克群岛宪法法案》界定，赋予库克群岛对其自身事务的主权；在外交事务和防务方面需要进行一定协商，而新西兰则必须回应有关灾害和防务援助的请求。尽管这一地位在技术上允许库克群岛自行处理其外交事务，但从历史上看，库克群岛与新西兰一直就所有对外事务保持密切协商。Q2：过去一年中，是什么导致库克群岛与NZL关系破裂？A2：2025年2月，库克群岛总理Mark Brown宣布，在他对北京进行国事访问期间，库克群岛与中国签署了全面战略伙伴关系（CSP）。该CSP为两国在若干议题上开展合作建立了一个为期五年的框架，其中包括深海采矿、基础设施开发以及技术与创新等多个与安全相邻的领域。新西兰官员称，该协议令他们“措手不及”，并对该协议被认为缺乏协商表示担忧。\n\n因此，双边关系持续恶化，直至2025年底。尽管库克群岛总理Brown在去年8月庆祝库克群岛与新西兰建立自由联合关系60周年时强调了这一特殊双边关系的重要性，但总理也一再强调库克群岛的自治性质，以及其国家独立开展自身外交事务的权利，并指出该国“并不受任何人的支配或控制”。2025年年中，新西兰宣布，由于双边关系中“缺乏信任”，已暂停向库克群岛提供数百万美元的核心支持资金。到2025年底，这一资金暂停规模已接近3,000万新西兰元（约占库克群岛年度总预算的10%以上），迫使库克群岛提前调整预算，以应对基本公共服务可能出现的财政缺口。\n\n从根本上说，这次关系破裂源于双方对于库克群岛与北京达成的协议在多大程度上与新西兰自身国家安全相关存在意见分歧。根据新西兰《官方信息法》公布的文件显示，在最终签署前数月，惠灵顿一直试图了解库克群岛有意与中国签署CSP的进展情况及其背后目的。库克群岛——以及同样可预见的中国——坚持称，这些协议并未明确包含防务和安全内容。透明度的缺失引发了新西兰政府内部的担忧，即库克群岛没有遵守一项\n对就“共同利益”问题进行协商的固有期待，以及与中国在水道测量和深海采矿等领域日益发展的关系，可能对新西兰产生安全影响。\n\n问题3：新西兰和库克群岛如何以及为何修复关系？\n\n回答3：经历数月双边紧张关系后，双方都急于修复这一关系。作为仅有的两个与新西兰自由联系的国家之一（另一个是纽埃），库克群岛是新西兰在整个太平洋地区安全布局不可或缺的一部分。中国—库克群岛协议在许多方面为新西兰敲响了警钟，惠灵顿急于弥合这一裂痕——但希望以限制中国向其近邻拓展影响的方式进行。2025年末，布朗总理还指出，培育新西兰与库克群岛之间这一特殊纽带至关重要，并提到在2026年预算中拨出额外资金，以“加强和支持这一伙伴关系”。\n\n正是在这一背景下，新西兰外交部长温斯顿·彼得斯于2026年4月1日对库克群岛进行了为期一天的访问，会见布朗总理并签署《防务与安全宣言》，旨在为这一特殊关系的轮廓和范围提供“明确性”。对库克群岛而言，这一和解将促使新西兰恢复向库克群岛政府提供年度资金。为缓解惠灵顿的安全担忧，该宣言确认“在防务和安全事务方面，新西兰是其首选伙伴”，并规定“双方不会与第三方开展可能损害”本协议原则的活动、签订协议或作出安排。\n\n问题4：这对库克群岛—新西兰关系的未来，以及对太平洋地区更广泛的影响力竞争意味着什么？\n\n回答4：拉罗汤加与惠灵顿之间的怒气已平息，关系有所改善，而且重要的是，对未来处理涉及外部参与者的敏感问题的预期已经得到明确。然而，无论是在库克群岛—新西兰关系的背景下，还是关于中国如何逐步改变其在太平洋地区安全——或安全相关——关系范围的界限，问题依然存在。\n\n尽管两国首都都在传递紧张局势已经过去、这两个亲密“表亲”已恢复常态的信息，但库克群岛现政府与惠灵顿对这段关系轮廓的看法之间，仍存在一种可能不稳定的不一致。尽管新西兰外交部长温斯顿·彼得斯宣称，新宣言将对库克群岛与中国的协议施加“巨大限制”，布朗总理最近却向议会强调，库克群岛“是一个自治国家。我们管理自己的事务”，而且与新西兰的关系虽然重要，却并不“界定我们与世界交往的界限”。尽管两国现已就国家安全事项建立正式的协商机制，但国家利益出现分歧的可能性将持续存在，尤其是在一方可能看到经济利益、而另一方看到安全风险的问题上。在获取资源的推动下，这一点最为明显；这已促使中国和美国等主要参与者推动达成双边协议\n与库克群岛的关系。对惠灵顿而言，这场与库克群岛的“家族”争端，加上中国其他令人担忧的举动——例如2025年2月在塔斯曼海进行的实弹演习——促使新西兰考虑采取一种更以安全为中心的太平洋政策：在与太平洋邻国打交道时，更清楚地阐明自身国家安全关切，同时采取行动，防止中国在涉及国家安全风险的领域进一步介入。澳大利亚已在太平洋地区采取了更具安全导向的战略，与瑙鲁、巴布亚新几内亚、图瓦卢和斐济签署了明确的安全协议。这也是美国传统上采取的做法，尤其是在密克罗尼西亚地区：美国与帕劳、马绍尔群岛和密克罗尼西亚联邦签署了《自由联系协定》，向这些国家提供经济援助和联邦项目，以换取单方面的防务准入。\n\n最后，深海采矿是一个可能对太平洋地缘政治和地缘经济关系产生重大影响的问题，正如它已经开始在库克群岛产生影响。美国和中国都已与库克群岛签署协议，进一步勘探其专属经济区内的矿产资源；然而，新西兰作为其最亲密的战略伙伴，尚未制定明确的深海采矿方针。随着全球对获取关键矿产的需求爆炸式增长，深海采矿既是一个快速发展的领域，也是一个高度争议的领域——其中的技术、政策、外交、法律和环境问题正在迅速演变。尽管国际社会尚未就应当如何、何时、何地以及最重要的是是否应推进深海采矿达成共识，但这一问题将持续搅动太平洋水域，影响太平洋国家及其周边大型伙伴的经济、政治和战略考量。\n\nKathryn Paik是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）澳大利亚讲席副主任兼高级研究员。Charles Edel是CSIS高级顾问及首任澳大利亚讲席。\n\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作；该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策议题的私人免税机构。其研究不具党派性，也不为专有性质。CSIS不持有具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论应被理解为仅代表作者本人。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n澳大利亚、新西兰与太平洋地区\n\n图片\nKathryn Paik\n副主任兼高级研究员，澳大利亚讲席\n\n图片\nCharles Edel\n高级顾问兼澳大利亚讲席\n\n项目与计划\n澳大利亚讲席\n地缘政治与外交政策\n\n相关内容\n图片\n新西兰的警钟：北京的影响力正在重塑惠灵顿的地区角色\n评论\n作者\nCharles Edel\n和\nKathryn Paik\n— 2025年5月7日\n\n图片\n美国—库克群岛海底矿产公告是否预示着大国竞争的新战线？\n关键问题\n作者\nKathryn Paik\n和\nGracelin Baskaran\n— 2025年8月20日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-04-28T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3483","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国必须发挥领导作用援助缅甸洪灾灾民","title_en":"The United States Must Exercise Leadership to Help Myanmar’s Flood Victims","summary_cn":"CSIS学者Derek Mitchell与Jack Myint撰文指出，台风“摩羯”重创缅甸，造成逾300人死亡、约100万人受灾，农业损失严重。由于军政府阻挠与国际援助不足，人道危机加剧。作者呼吁美国通过国际开发署和国务院牵头协调国际救援，与盟友及本地公民社会合作，并借鉴2008年纳尔吉斯风灾的经验，展现道义与战略领导力。","summary_en":"CSIS scholars Derek Mitchell and Jack Myint argue that Typhoon Yagi devastated Myanmar, killing over 300 and affecting about one million people, with severe agricultural losses. The junta's obstruction and insufficient international aid worsen the humanitarian crisis. They urge the United States, through USAID and the State Department, to lead international relief coordination with allies and local civil society, drawing on lessons from Cyclone Nargis in 2008.","tag":"other","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:23:23.513743+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/united-states-must-exercise-leadership-help-myanmars-flood-victims","content":"The United States Must Exercise Leadership to Help Myanmar’s Flood Victims\nPhoto: STR/AFP via Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nDerek Mitchell\nand\nJack Myint\nPublished October 4, 2024\nThe aftermath of Hurricane Helene has caused devastating effects for the residents of western North Carolina, resulting in loss of lives and property, and has elicited a whole-of-government response by the Biden-Harris administration.Massive floods are also devastating nations from sub-Saharan Africa to central Europe to Southeast Asia. In the latter, Typhoon Yagi, a Category 5 storm and the first violent typhoon of the season, hit Southeast Asia and southern China in early September and has left scores dead and millions in need of assistance in the Philippines, Vietnam, Thailand, Laos, southern China, and Myanmar.Amid the widespread devastation, however, Myanmar’s situation is unique as it has exacerbated an already precarious set of conditions in a nation beset by political turmoil, economic instability, and ongoing armed conflict. As international attention fades amid the complexities of Myanmar’s political landscape, the United States has a moral and strategic imperative to lead efforts in supporting the people of Myanmar through this natural disaster.A Devastating ImpactThe storm caused extensive flooding in several regions of central and eastern Myanmar. Entire villages were submerged, and critical infrastructure was destroyed. As of this writing, the floods have resulted in more than 300 deaths and left at least 100 people unaccounted for. An estimated 1 million individuals have been affected across the country, with many seeking refuge in overcrowded shelters lacking basic amenities.Access to essential needs such as food, shelter, clean water, sanitation, and healthcare has been severely compromised, alongside road networks, telecommunications, and electricity infrastructure. The flooding has also heightened health risks, with waterborne diseases like cholera and dysentery threatening vulnerable populations, particularly children and the elderly.Beyond the immediate impact, the losses in Myanmar’s agricultural sector have been staggering. Rice farming, which forms the backbone of the country’s agrarian economy, has been hit hardest, raising concerns about food security in the coming months. The UN World Food Programme estimates that 2.7 million acres of farmland have been inundated, worsening the food insecurity crisis that had affected over 13 million people even before the floods began.A Call for ActionDespite the magnitude of this crisis, international aid has been insufficient. Exceedingly overstretched, the UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (UNOCHA) is operating in Myanmar with only a quarter of its funding requirement. Other countries have vast needs as a result of Yagi, but their relatively coherent national and local governance structures have enabled the mobilization of resources to meet the challenge in a reasonably effective and timely manner. However, the continued assertion of power by Myanmar’s junta (which overthrew the civilian administration of Aung San Suu Kyi after a violent military coup in February 2021) has complicated humanitarian efforts in Myanmar, as aid organizations struggle to overcome significant bureaucratic hurdles and restrictions and navigate around the country’s de facto fractures. This is exacerbated further by widespread violence inside the country, including the junta’s indiscriminate bombing of civilian locations across central Myanmar. While the United Nations and various nongovernmental organizations have called for a coordinated international response, the political complexities in Myanmar have hindered both an effective mobilization of international support and the delivery of ready assistance to those in need.As with the international response to Myanmar’s civil conflict since the 2021 coup, the absence of international leadership threatens to cost thousands of lives. Given the situation, the U.S. government, through the U.S. Agency for International Development and the Department of State, should take a more proactive role in coordinating international support for Myanmar. The United States can do more to rally support among allies and partners from the public and private sectors across Asia, Australia, the European Union, the United Kingdom, and beyond. At President Biden’s recent Quad summit, a meeting of a security grouping comprising Australia, India, Japan, and the United States, the United States missed an opportunity to rally the grouping, limiting its attention to Vietnam’s post-Yagi recovery. Washington can ensure the international community will work not only with international organizations but also with local civil society organizations, which are often best positioned to deliver aid quickly and effectively and ensure assistance gets to those in need and is not siphoned off to empower corrupt local actors. Congress can also do more to raise awareness and promote the importance of urgently addressing this humanitarian disaster. The recently formed House of Representatives Congressional Burma Caucus, for instance, is in a unique position to lead and coalesce congressional support. A bipartisan coalition of Senate leaders with a longstanding interest in Myanmar is also well positioned to sound the alarm on the rising death toll and looming food security crisis facing Myanmar.Myanmar’s history teaches us that ignoring humanitarian crises can have devastating consequences. In 2008, Cyclone Nargis struck, resulting in over 138,000 deaths and displacing millions. The slow international response and the previous military government’s reluctance to accept foreign aid exacerbated the tragedy. It was only through the leadership and creative politicking of the former secretary-general of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN), Surin Pitsuwan, that international aid was effectively coordinated. But out of disaster can come opportunity. Many believe the official mismanagement of Cyclone Nargis was a turning point for international cooperation for reform in Myanmar. In recent years, the international community has been ineffective at best in addressing the tragedy of Myanmar, consigning the country to economic degradation and brutal official violence that has led to the widespread suffering and displacement of Myanmar’s people and transnational harm (e.g., the rise of drug and human trafficking, scam centers, and infectious disease). The United States must learn from Surin’s model to seek common political ground among global stakeholders to achieve an urgent humanitarian objective. Swift action and principled leadership are crucial to mitigating the impact of Typhoon Yagi and preventing further loss of life in Myanmar. Sadly, ASEAN this time appears tragically unable to serve that role. The United States can and should use its unique position to step up instead. The United States must not turn its backs on the people of Myanmar, who need help now more than ever.Derek Mitchell is a senior adviser in the Office of the President at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C., and served as U.S. ambassador to Myanmar from 2012 to 2016. Jack Myint is a fellow (non-resident) with the Southeast Asia Program at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSoutheast Asia, and\nAsia\nImage\nDerek Mitchell\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Office of the President\nImage\nJack Myint\nAdjunct Fellow (Non-resident), Southeast Asia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nSoutheast Asia Program\nAsia Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"美国必须发挥领导作用，帮助缅甸洪灾受害者\n照片：STR/法新社，Getty Images供图\n评论\n作者\n德里克·米切尔\n和\n杰克·敏\n发表于2024年10月4日\n飓风“海伦”过后，对北卡罗来纳州西部居民造成了灾难性影响，导致人员和财产损失，并促使拜登—哈里斯政府作出全政府响应。大规模洪灾也正在摧毁从撒哈拉以南非洲、中欧到东南亚的多个国家。在东南亚，5级风暴、该季首个强烈台风“摩羯”于9月初袭击东南亚和中国南部，已造成菲律宾、越南、泰国、老挝、中国南部和缅甸数十人死亡、数百万人需要援助。\n然而，在广泛的破坏中，缅甸的情况独具特殊性，因为洪灾加剧了这个国家原本就岌岌可危的处境；该国饱受政治动荡、经济不稳定和持续武装冲突的困扰。随着国际社会的注意力因缅甸复杂的政治局势而逐渐减弱，美国在道义和战略上都有责任领导援助缅甸人民应对这场自然灾害的努力。\n毁灭性影响\n这场风暴在缅甸中部和东部多个地区造成大范围洪灾。整个村庄被淹没，关键基础设施遭到摧毁。截至本文撰写时，洪灾已造成300多人死亡，至少100人失踪。据估计，全国有100万人受到影响，许多人被迫在拥挤不堪且缺乏基本设施的避难所寻求庇护。\n食品、住所、清洁饮用水、卫生设施和医疗保健等基本需求的获取受到严重破坏，道路网络、电信和电力基础设施也同样如此。洪灾还加剧了健康风险，霍乱和痢疾等水源性疾病威胁着弱势群体，尤其是儿童和老年人。\n除了直接影响之外，缅甸农业部门遭受的损失也极其严重。稻米种植构成该国农业经济的支柱，受灾最为严重，引发了人们对未来几个月粮食安全的担忧。联合国世界粮食计划署估计，已有270万英亩农田被淹，加剧了洪灾发生前就已影响超过1300万人的粮食不安全危机。\n行动呼吁\n尽管这场危机规模巨大，国际援助仍然不足。联合国人道主义事务协调厅（UNOCHA）的资源极度紧张，目前在缅甸开展工作时仅获得其资金需求的四分之一。其他国家也因“摩羯”台风而面临巨大需求，但这些国家相对连贯的国家和地方治理结构，使其能够调动资源，以相当有效且及时的方式应对挑战。\n然而，缅甸军政府持续攫取权力（该军政府在2021年2月发动暴力军事政变后推翻了昂山素季领导的文职政府），使缅甸的人道主义工作变得更加复杂，因为援助组织努力克服重大的官僚障碍和限制，并在这个国家事实上的分裂格局中寻找行动空间。这种情况又因国内广泛的暴力而进一步恶化，其中包括军政府对\n缅甸中部各地的平民地点。尽管联合国和多个非政府组织呼吁采取协调一致的国际应对措施，但缅甸的政治复杂性阻碍了国际支持的有效动员，也阻碍了向有需要者提供及时援助。正如自2021年政变以来国际社会对缅甸内战的应对一样，国际领导力的缺失可能导致数千人丧生。鉴于当前局势，美国政府应通过美国国际开发署和国务院，在协调国际社会对缅甸的支持方面发挥更加积极的作用。美国可以进一步在亚洲、澳大利亚、欧盟、英国及其他地区，团结公共和私营部门的盟友与伙伴，争取支持。在拜登总统最近举行的四方安全对话峰会上，这一安全机制由澳大利亚、印度、日本和美国组成，但美国错失了动员该机制的机会，仅将注意力局限于越南灾后恢复工作。华盛顿可以确保国际社会不仅与国际组织合作，也与地方民间社会组织合作；这些组织通常最有能力迅速而有效地提供援助，确保援助送达有需要者手中，而不会被挪用来扶持腐败的地方势力。国会也可以进一步提高公众意识，推动紧急应对这场人道主义灾难的重要性。例如，最近成立的众议院国会缅甸核心小组处于独特位置，可以引领并凝聚国会支持。长期以来关注缅甸问题的两党参议院领导人联盟，也非常适合就缅甸不断上升的死亡人数和迫在眉睫的粮食安全危机发出警报。缅甸的历史告诉我们，忽视人道主义危机会带来灾难性后果。2008年，纳尔吉斯气旋袭击缅甸，造成超过13.8万人死亡，并使数百万人流离失所。国际社会迟缓的反应，以及当时军政府不愿接受外国援助的态度，使这场悲剧进一步恶化。最终，正是在东南亚国家联盟（东盟）前秘书长素林·皮素万的领导和灵活斡旋下，国际援助才得以有效协调。但灾难之中也可能孕育机遇。许多人认为，对纳尔吉斯气旋的官方失当处理，成为国际社会合作推动缅甸改革的转折点。近年来，国际社会在应对缅甸悲剧方面至多只能说是无效，使这个国家陷入经济衰退和残酷的官方暴力，导致缅甸人民普遍遭受苦难和流离失所，并造成跨国危害（例如毒品和人口贩运、诈骗中心以及传染病的兴起）。美国必须借鉴素林的模式，在全球利益攸关方之间寻求共同的政治立场，以实现紧迫的人道主义目标。迅速行动和有原则的领导对于减轻雅吉台风的影响、防止缅甸进一步人员伤亡至关重要。遗憾的是，这一次东盟似乎悲剧性地无力承担这一角色。美国能够、也应当利用其独特地位挺身而出。美国绝不能对……置之不理\n缅甸人民，他们现在比以往任何时候都更需要帮助。Derek Mitchell 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）总统办公室的高级顾问，曾于2012年至2016年担任美国驻缅甸大使。Jack Myint 是战略与国际问题研究中心东南亚项目的（非驻会）研究员。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人、免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不涉及专有利益。CSIS 不对具体政策立场表态。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n东南亚，以及\n亚洲\n图片\nDerek Mitchell\n总统办公室高级顾问（非驻会）\n图片\nJack Myint\n东南亚项目兼职研究员（非驻会）\n项目与计划\n东南亚项目\n亚洲项目\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-10-04T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1425","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"解析伊朗在海湾的无人机战役：未来无人机战争的早期教训","title_en":"Unpacking Iran’s Drone Campaign in the Gulf: Early Lessons for Future Drone Warfare","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心分析文章指出，2026年3月美以“史诗之怒”行动后伊朗对海湾国家发动大规模报复，首周无人机占记录打击约71%，阿联酋承受约55%打击量。文章认为无人机已成为现代空中战役核心工具，伊朗以低成本饱和攻击消耗防空资源，并借鉴乌克兰经验建议美军构建廉价无人机拦截层、传感器融合、抗干扰通信与AI蜂群能力。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary analyzes Iran's retaliatory drone campaign against Gulf states after the March 2026 U.S.-Israeli Operation Epic Fury. Drones accounted for about 71 percent of recorded strikes in the first week, with the UAE absorbing roughly 55 percent. The author argues drones are now central to modern air campaigns, using low-cost saturation to exhaust air defenses, and draws on Ukraine's experience to recommend that the U.S. military build cheap interceptor drones, sensor fusion, resilient communications, and AI-enabled swarming.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:31:50.694206+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/unpacking-irans-drone-campaign-gulf-early-lessons-future-drone-warfare","content":"Unpacking Iran’s Drone Campaign in the Gulf: Early Lessons for Future Drone Warfare\nPhoto: alones/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nKateryna Bondar\nPublished March 10, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nThe first week of Iran’s retaliation campaign during Operation Epic Fury demonstrates that drones are no longer auxiliary strike systems but central instruments of modern air campaigns. Their ability to generate sustained pressure at relatively low cost allows actors to impose economic, psychological, and operational strain on adversaries while preserving higher-end missile assets for select targets. The effectiveness of such campaigns lies not only in the drones themselves but in the broader ecosystem that enables their large-scale employment—production capacity, operational doctrine, targeting architecture, and integration with other strike systems.The Middle East crisis escalated in early March 2026 after coordinated U.S.-Israeli strikes under Operation Epic Fury killed Iranian Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei and several senior commanders. Iran responded with a large-scale retaliatory campaign primarily targeting Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) states. Despite damage to parts of its command and control structure, Tehran has rapidly generated sustained strikes using a layered architecture combining drones, ballistic missiles, and cruise missiles against military installations, energy infrastructure, and economic centers.Data from the first week of the campaign (March 1–8) reveals several operational trends, particularly in the use of unmanned systems. Drawing on official reporting from regional ministries of defense and open-source intelligence, this analysis assesses the scale and role of drones in Iran’s retaliation strategy and identifies capability gaps that militaries, including the U.S. military, may need to address as these operational models evolve.Operational Patterns in Iran’s Strike CampaignDrones have constituted the primary tool for sustaining pressure across multiple GCC states in the first week of Iran’s retaliation campaign. These drones—primarily Shahed-series one-way attack drones deployed in large saturation waves—have been used less to inflict direct military damage and more to disrupt infrastructure while forcing defenders to expend costly interceptors against low-cost systems.Geographic Distribution of Drone Strikes Across the GulfAs shown in Figure 1, Iran’s strike campaign has not been evenly distributed across the Gulf. The United Arab Emirates (UAE) absorbed by far the largest volume of attacks in the dataset: 1,422 detected drones and 246 missiles between March 1 and March 8, or 1,668 total recorded strikes. That means the UAE alone accounts for roughly 55 percent of all recorded strikes in the dataset and 66 percent of all detected drones. In operational terms, this suggests that the UAE has functioned as the campaign’s principal target set, likely because of its concentration of commercial hubs, logistics infrastructure, and high-value military and economic assets.\nImage\nKateryna Bondar\nSenior Fellow, Wadhwani AI Center\nPrograms & Projects\nWadhwani AI Center\nEconomic Security and Technology\nLatest Analysis: War with Iran\nRemote Visualization\nSaudi Arabia has faced a similar threat environment, with strikes focusing on energy infrastructure and military facilities supporting U.S. operations. Drone and missile waves have targeted Riyadh, the Al-Kharj region, and the Eastern Province, including repeated attempts to strike Prince Sultan Air Base and the Ras Tanura refinery. This pattern reinforces Iran’s longstanding strategy of using attacks on energy assets and coalition operational hubs as leverage within the Gulf security architecture.Bahrain and Kuwait have experienced some of the highest strike volumes, reflecting both their proximity to Iran and their importance to the U.S. military posture in the region. Bahrain, host of the U.S. Fifth Fleet, intercepted large numbers of drones and missiles during the first week, though parts of the naval headquarters were reportedly hit during the initial barrage. In Kuwait, dense aerial activity has also exposed operational risks: Air defenses mistakenly shot down three U.S. F-15E aircraft after misidentifying them as hostile targets, highlighting the difficulty of maintaining air situational awareness in a saturated drone environment.Even states attempting diplomatic neutrality have been drawn into the campaign. Qatar, despite mediating between Washington and Tehran, experienced repeated strikes targeting Al Udeid Air Base and nearby civilian aviation infrastructure. At the same time, Iran has extended drone operations into the maritime domain, striking port infrastructure in Oman and targeting commercial shipping near the Strait of Hormuz. These attacks indicate a deliberate effort to pressure global energy and trade flows beyond the immediate battlefield.Drones as the Primary Strike AssetAnalysis of these strikes shows that the campaign has displayed a clear functional division between drone-heavy and missile-heavy target sets. As shown on Figure 2, drones account for about 71 percent of all recorded strikes in the dataset, confirming that unmanned systems have been the main delivery mechanism of the retaliation campaign.\nRemote Visualization\nHowever, this aggregated data masks significant variation. The UAE and Saudi Arabia have been primarily drone-centric theaters, with drones accounting for about 85 percent of strikes, while Bahrain has also seen heavy use of drones. In contrast, Kuwait has seen a more even distribution, and Qatar has been missile-dominated, with missiles making up roughly 49 percent and 68 percent of strikes, respectively. This suggests Iran has tailored strike packages to different targets—using drones for persistent, low-cost pressure and missiles where speed, certainty of effect, or signaling against military facilities is prioritized.The temporal pattern is also revealing. The campaign opened with a massive wave on March 1—1206 strikes (867 drones and 339 missiles)—followed by a sharp drop on March 2 and a steadier rhythm for the rest of the week. From March 2 to March 8, daily strikes stabilized between 190 and 392, suggesting a two-phase campaign: an initial shock wave to saturate defenses and signal escalation, followed by a more sustainable attritional phase designed to maintain pressure over time.\nRemote Visualization\nThese data illustrate how Iran is using drones not just as strike assets, but as a campaign management tool. The volume and persistence of drone launches suggest a system optimized for repeated employment across multiple days and multiple theaters. The data points to a model of warfare in which drones are not supplementary to missiles. They are the backbone of the pressure architecture, especially against states where economic disruption, air defense exhaustion, and public alarm can produce strategic effects even without catastrophic physical destruction.The Technological Composition of Iran’s Drone CampaignOpen-source reporting from March 1 to March 8 indicates that Iran’s retaliatory air campaign has relied primarily on long-range one-way attack drones, with confirmed employment of multiple Shahed-family variants. The most substantiated evidence comes from a March 3 briefing in which the UAE Ministry of Defense displayed recovered wreckage and explicitly identified Shahed-136, Shahed-107, and Shahed-238 unmanned aircraft systems (UASs).Reporting also indicates at least one cross-border UAS strike episode outside the Gulf, in Azerbaijan’s Nakhchivan exclave on March 5, since confirmed by official Azerbaijani government statements. Though the specific UAS model remains under investigation, some outlets attribute this incident to the Arash-2 loitering munition. While some reports suggest a first-time operational use of the Hadid-110 (Dalahu) jet-powered loitering munition, these lack the same level of corroborated, wreckage-based attribution seen in the UAE briefing.Overall, the campaign reinforces a “cost-imposition” logic, which includes saturating regional defenses with mass one-way attack salvos while accepting high attrition to force interceptor expenditure and impose episodic disruption.\nRemote Visualization\nBeyond the systems already described, one particularly interesting case suggests the possible involvement of Russian-produced loitering munitions in Iran’s strike campaign. Open-source analysis of drone debris and video footage from the UAE indicates that a Geran-2 drone, a Russian-produced variant derived from the Iranian Shahed-136, may have been used during the March 2026 retaliation.Serial markings suggest the system originated from the Kupol plant in Izhevsk, rather than Iran’s domestic production lines, and incorporated Russian modifications such as the Kometa-M jam-resistant navigation system. The presence of a Russian-manufactured Geran-2 in Iran’s operations would suggest that the previously one-directional drone cooperation, where Iran supplied Shahed systems to Russia after 2022, may be evolving into a more reciprocal exchange of loitering munition technologies between the two countries.Implications for U.S. Military Counter-Drone Strategy and Lessons from UkraineThis analysis reinforces that countering mass drone attacks cannot rely primarily on traditional air defense missiles. Ukraine’s experience shows why. Interceptor drones have become a core layer of air defense because they are cheap enough to use at scale, preserve high-end interceptors for cruise and ballistic missiles, and can be produced in large numbers. Ukrainian officials state that the country produced over 100,000 in 2025 and that the combat success rates of these platforms exceed 60 percent. The Ukrainian Ministry of Defence also reported deliveries approaching 950 anti-Shahed interceptors per day in December 2025. For the U.S. military, the lesson is straightforward—drone defense must begin with a cheap, numerous, drone-against-drone layer rather than with a force structure built around million-dollar missiles.However, hardware alone does not constitute a real advantage. The decisive factor lies in the broader operational infrastructure that integrates drones into a coherent defensive ecosystem. This ecosystem consists of the following components:Cheap mass-produced drone interceptors: Drone-against-drone warfare is becoming a core element of modern air defense. Ukraine’s experience shows that countering large waves of one-way attack drones cannot rely on high-end interceptors alone; it requires systems that can be deployed at scale. Ukrainian forces increasingly use low-cost interceptor drones to counter Shahed-type loitering munitions because they are inexpensive, scalable, and effective over large areas. Unlike traditional interceptors, such as the Patriot missile, which cost around $4 million per shot, interceptor drones used in Ukraine typically cost $2,000 to $4,000, allowing threats to be engaged at a fraction of the cost. The key lesson is that defending against mass drone attacks requires mass on the defensive side as well, meaning the U.S. military must field large numbers of cheap interceptor drones as a first defensive layer.Integration into doctrine and multilayered air defense: Hardware alone does not determine effectiveness. Ukraine’s success stems from integrating interceptor drones into a layered defensive architecture alongside traditional air defense systems, electronic warfare, and mobile ground teams. Drones are assigned to intercept low-cost threats, preserving high-end systems such as Patriot interceptors for cruise and ballistic missiles. The U.S. military should similarly incorporate drone interceptors into doctrine, command structures, and operational planning rather than treating them as ad hoc solutions.Trained operators and dedicated counter-drone units: Another key factor is personnel. Ukraine’s counter-drone effectiveness depends on large numbers of trained operators organized into specialized units responsible for detecting, tracking, and engaging aerial threats. This requires dedicated training pipelines, standardized tactics, and constant exercises that simulate large-scale drone attacks. For the United States, building a counter-drone force will require not just technology procurement but also training programs and unit structures designed specifically for drone defense missions.Sensor fusion and battle management software: Modern counter-drone operations rely heavily on software that integrates radar, acoustic sensors, optical systems, and other inputs into a single operational picture. Ukrainian systems such as Delta illustrate the importance of sensor fusion and flight management software that allows operators to detect, assign, and prosecute targets quickly. Without such systems, even large numbers of interceptor drones cannot be effectively coordinated. The U.S. military therefore needs advanced sensor fusion and battle management platforms that connect sensors, interceptors, and command nodes.Resilient communications: Drone defense networks must function in environments where GPS is degraded and communications are contested. Ukraine’s experience shows the importance of resilient data links, decentralized control, and systems capable of operating even when communications are disrupted. For U.S. forces operating in contested theaters, counter-drone architecture must be designed with redundant communications pathways and degraded-mode capabilities.Onboard AI for detection, engagement, and swarming: AI increasingly enables drones to operate effectively in complex and contested environments. Onboard AI allows interceptor drones to recognize targets, filter sensor data, and navigate despite jamming or degraded navigation signals. It also enables coordinated swarming behavior, allowing multiple drones to share targeting data, distribute tasks, and engage large numbers of incoming threats simultaneously. Rather than pursuing autonomy as an abstract concept, the U.S. military should prioritize AI functions that enhance survivability, targeting accuracy, and operator efficiency while enabling coordinated drone swarms capable of countering mass drone attacks.Continuous iteration and rapid updates: Perhaps the most important lesson from Ukraine is the speed of adaptation. Drone warfare evolves rapidly, with new countermeasures and modifications appearing constantly. Effective counter-drone systems therefore require continuous iteration of hardware, software, tactics, and training. The U.S. military should establish mechanisms that enable rapid updates and operational feedback loops, ensuring that counter-drone capabilities evolve at the same pace as the threats they are designed to defeat. ConclusionThe first week of Iran’s retaliation campaign during Operation Epic Fury shows that drones are no longer auxiliary systems but central instruments of modern air campaigns. Their ability to sustain pressure at relatively low cost allows actors to impose economic, psychological, and operational strain while preserving higher-end missiles for select targets. The effectiveness of such campaigns depends not only on the drones themselves but on the broader ecosystem that enables their use at scale, including production capacity, doctrine, and integration with other strike systems.For the United States, adapting to this environment requires more than acquiring new hardware. It demands integrating drones and counter-UAS capabilities into doctrine, training, procurement, and operational planning. Building an effective defense against mass drone warfare will require coordinated development of interceptor drones, sensor networks, battle management software, resilient communications, and trained operators capable of operating in increasingly AI-enabled battlespaces.Kateryna Bondar is a fellow with the Wadhwani AI Center at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nMiddle East,\nIran,\nDefense and Security,\nArtificial Intelligence, and\nMilitary Technology","content_cn":"解读伊朗在海湾地区的无人机行动：未来无人机战争的早期经验\n图片：alones/Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nKateryna Bondar\n发表于2026年3月10日\n远程可视化\n伊朗在“史诗之怒行动”期间报复行动的第一周表明，无人机已不再是辅助性打击系统，而是现代空中行动的核心工具。无人机能够以相对较低的成本持续施加压力，使行动方得以在保留高端导弹资产用于打击特定目标的同时，对对手施加经济、心理和作战层面的压力。这类行动的有效性不仅取决于无人机本身，还取决于支持其大规模运用的更广泛生态系统——生产能力、作战 doctrine、目标定位架构，以及与其他打击系统的整合。\n2026年3月初，在“史诗之怒行动”下，美以协调发动的打击造成伊朗最高领袖阿亚图拉·阿里·哈梅内伊及数名高级指挥官死亡，中东危机升级。伊朗随后发动大规模报复行动，主要针对海湾合作委员会（GCC）成员国。尽管其部分指挥与控制结构受损，德黑兰仍迅速组织起持续性打击，采用由无人机、弹道导弹和巡航导弹组成的分层架构，攻击军事设施、能源基础设施和经济中心。\n行动第一周（3月1日至8日）的数据显示出若干作战趋势，尤其体现在无人系统的使用上。本分析依据地区国防部的官方报告和开源情报，评估无人机在伊朗报复战略中的规模与作用，并确定随着这些作战模式演变，包括美国军方在内的各国军队可能需要应对的能力缺口。\n伊朗打击行动的作战模式\n在伊朗报复行动的第一周，无人机一直是其在多个海合会成员国持续施压的主要工具。这些无人机主要是“沙赫德”系列单向攻击无人机，以大规模饱和波次投入使用；其目的较少是造成直接军事损害，更多是扰乱基础设施，同时迫使防御方动用昂贵的拦截弹来对付低成本系统。\n海湾地区无人机袭击的地理分布\n如图1所示，伊朗的打击行动并未均匀分布于海湾地区。数据集中，阿拉伯联合酋长国（UAE）遭受的攻击量远超其他地区：3月1日至8日期间，共探测到1,422架无人机和246枚导弹，即记录在案的袭击总数为1,668次。这意味着，单阿联酋就约占数据集中所有记录袭击的55%，以及所有探测到的无人机的66%。从作战角度看，这表明阿联酋一直是此次行动的主要目标集，可能是因为该国集中了商业枢纽、物流基础设施以及高价值军事和经济资产。\n图片\nKateryna Bondar\nWadhwani人工智能中心高级研究员\n项目与计划\nWadhwani人工智能中心\n经济安全与技术\n最新分析：与伊朗的战争\n远程可视化\n沙特阿拉伯也面临类似的威胁环境，袭击重点针对能源基础设施以及支持美国行动的军事设施。无人机和导弹波次瞄准了利雅得、哈立德地区和东部省，其中包括反复试图\n袭击苏丹王子空军基地和拉斯坦努拉炼油厂。这种模式强化了伊朗长期以来的战略：利用对能源资产和联军行动枢纽的攻击，在海湾安全架构中施加杠杆作用。巴林和科威特经历了最高的袭击量之一，这既反映了它们靠近伊朗，也反映了它们对美国在该地区军事态势的重要性。作为美国第五舰队驻地，巴林在第一周拦截了大量无人机和导弹，但据报道，海军总部部分区域在最初的齐射中遭到打击。在科威特，密集的空中活动也暴露了行动风险：防空部队误将3架美国F-15E飞机识别为敌方目标并将其击落，凸显了在无人机饱和环境中维持空中态势感知的困难。即使是试图保持外交中立的国家，也被卷入了这场行动。卡塔尔尽管在华盛顿和德黑兰之间进行调解，却多次遭到袭击，目标包括乌代德空军基地及附近的民用航空基础设施。与此同时，伊朗已将无人机行动扩展到海上领域，袭击阿曼的港口基础设施，并以霍尔木兹海峡附近的商船为目标。这些袭击表明，伊朗有意对即时战场之外的全球能源和贸易流动施压。无人机作为主要打击资产对这些袭击的分析表明，这场行动在目标组合上呈现出无人机主导与导弹主导之间的明确功能分工。如图2所示，无人机约占数据集中所有记录袭击的71%，证实无人系统是此次报复行动的主要投送手段。\n远程可视化\n然而，这些汇总数据掩盖了显著差异。阿联酋和沙特阿拉伯主要是无人机主导的战区，无人机约占袭击的85%；巴林也大量使用无人机。相比之下，科威特的分布更为均衡，而卡塔尔则由导弹主导，导弹分别约占袭击的49%和68%。这表明，伊朗针对不同目标定制了打击组合——利用无人机施加持续、低成本的压力，并在优先考虑速度、效果确定性或向军事设施发出信号时使用导弹。这一时间模式同样耐人寻味。行动于3月1日以大规模攻势开场——共1206次袭击（867架无人机和339枚导弹）——随后3月2日骤降，并在该周剩余时间保持较为稳定的节奏。从3月2日到3月8日，每日袭击稳定在190至392次之间，表明这是一场分为两个阶段的行动：首先是以饱和防御并传递升级信号为目的的初始冲击波，随后是旨在长期维持压力的、更具可持续性的消耗阶段。\n远程可视化\n这些数据说明，伊朗不仅将无人机作为打击资产，还将其作为行动管理工具。无人机发射的数量和持续性表明，这是一套经过优化、可在多个日期和多个战区反复使用的系统。数据指向一种战争模式：无人机并非导弹的补充。它们是压力架构的支柱，尤其针对那些即使没有灾难性的物理\n摧毁。伊朗无人机战役的技术构成\n\n3月1日至3月8日的公开来源报道表明，伊朗的报复性空袭主要依靠远程单向攻击无人机，并已确认使用多个“沙赫德”系列变体。最有力的证据来自3月3日的一场简报会，阿联酋国防部在会上展示了回收的残骸，并明确认定其中包括“沙赫德-136”“沙赫德-107”和“沙赫德-238”无人机系统（UAS）。报道还显示，3月5日，在海湾以外的阿塞拜疆纳希切万飞地发生了至少一起跨境无人机系统袭击事件，阿塞拜疆政府官方声明已证实此事。尽管具体无人机系统型号仍在调查中，但一些媒体将该事件归因于“阿拉什-2”巡飞弹。部分报道称，伊朗首次投入了“哈迪德-110”（达拉胡）喷气动力巡飞弹，但这些报道缺乏阿联酋简报会中那种有残骸佐证的同等程度归属确认。\n\n总体而言，这场战役强化了“施加成本”的逻辑，包括以大规模单向攻击齐射压 saturate 区域防御，同时接受高损耗，以迫使拦截弹消耗并造成阶段性扰乱。\n\n远程可视化\n\n除已描述的系统外，一个特别有趣的案例表明，伊朗的打击行动中可能涉及俄罗斯生产的巡飞弹。对阿联酋无人机残骸和视频 footage 的公开来源分析显示，一架“Geran-2”无人机——一种源自伊朗“沙赫德-136”的俄罗斯生产变体——可能曾在2026年3月的报复行动中使用。\n\n序列标记表明，该系统源自伊热夫斯克的“穹顶”工厂，而非伊朗国内生产线，并采用了俄罗斯的改装，例如“Kometa-M”抗干扰导航系统。在伊朗行动中出现俄罗斯制造的“Geran-2”，将表明此前单向的无人机合作——即伊朗在2022年后向俄罗斯提供“沙赫德”系统——可能正演变为两国之间更加互惠的巡飞弹技术交流。\n\n对美国军方反无人机战略的启示及乌克兰的经验\n\n这项分析进一步表明，应对大规模无人机攻击不能主要依靠传统防空导弹。乌克兰的经验说明了原因。拦截无人机已成为防空体系的核心层，因为其成本足够低，可以大规模使用；能够为巡航导弹和弹道导弹保留高端拦截弹；并且可以大量生产。乌克兰官员称，该国在2025年生产了超过10万架此类无人机，这些平台的作战成功率超过60%。乌克兰国防部还报告称，2025年12月反“沙赫德”拦截无人机的日均交付量接近950架。对美国军方而言，教训很直接——无人机防御必须首先建立在廉价、大量的无人机对无人机层之上，而不是建立围绕百万美元导弹构建的兵力结构。\n\n然而，单靠硬件并不能构成真正的优势。决定性因素在于更广泛的作战基础设施，即将无人机整合进一个协调一致的防御生态系统。该生态系统由以下组成部分构成：廉价的大规模生产拦截无人机：无人机对无人机作战正成为现代防空的核心要素。乌克兰的经验\n这表明，应对大规模单向攻击无人机波次不能仅依赖高端拦截器；还需要能够大规模部署的系统。乌克兰军队越来越多地使用低成本拦截无人机来对抗“沙赫德”式巡飞弹药，因为它们价格低廉、可扩展，并且能覆盖大范围。与传统拦截器（如“爱国者”导弹）不同，后者每发成本约为400万美元，而乌克兰使用的拦截无人机通常成本为2,000至4,000美元，从而可以以极低的成本应对威胁。关键教训是，防御大规模无人机攻击也要求防御方具备规模优势，这意味着美国军方必须大量部署廉价拦截无人机，作为第一道防御层。\n\n纳入作战条令与多层防空体系：单靠硬件并不能决定有效性。乌克兰的成功源于将拦截无人机整合进分层防御架构，与传统防空系统、电子战以及机动地面小组协同运作。无人机被分配去拦截低成本威胁，从而为巡航导弹和弹道导弹保留“爱国者”等高端系统。美国军方同样应将无人机拦截器纳入作战条令、指挥体系和作战规划，而不是将其视为临时性解决方案。\n\n受训操作员与专门的反无人机部队：另一个关键因素是人员。乌克兰的反无人机效能取决于大量受训操作员，他们被编入专门部队，负责探测、跟踪和打击空中威胁。这要求建立专门的训练流程、标准化战术以及持续演练，以模拟大规模无人机攻击。对美国而言，构建反无人机力量不仅需要采购技术，还需要建立专门为无人机防御任务设计的训练项目和部队编制。\n\n传感器融合与战场管理软件：现代反无人机行动高度依赖将雷达、声学传感器、光学系统及其他输入整合为统一作战态势的软件。乌克兰的“Delta”等系统说明了传感器融合和飞行管理软件的重要性，它们能够让操作员迅速探测、分配并追击目标。没有这类系统，即使拥有大量拦截无人机，也无法进行有效协调。因此，美国军方需要先进的传感器融合和战场管理平台，将传感器、拦截器与指挥节点连接起来。\n\n韧性通信：无人机防御网络必须能够在GPS信号减弱、通信受干扰的环境中运行。乌克兰的经验表明，具备韧性的数据链路、分散式控制，以及即使通信中断仍能运行的系统非常重要。对于在受争夺战区行动的美国部队而言，反无人机架构必须配备冗余通信路径和降级模式能力。\n\n用于探测、交战和蜂群协同的机载AI：AI正日益使无人机能够在复杂且受争夺的环境中有效运行。机载AI能够让拦截无人机识别目标、筛选传感器数据，并在遭受干扰或导航信号减弱的情况下进行导航。它还支持协调式蜂群行为，使多架无人机能够共享目标数据、分配任务，并打击大量来袭的\n同时应对威胁。美国军方不应将自主性作为一个抽象概念来追求，而应优先发展能够提高生存能力、瞄准精度和操作人员效率的人工智能功能，同时实现可协同作战的无人机蜂群，以应对大规模无人机攻击。\n持续迭代与快速更新：也许乌克兰带来的最重要教训，是适应的速度。无人机战争发展迅速，新的反制措施和改装不断出现。因此，有效的反无人机系统需要对硬件、软件、战术和训练进行持续迭代。美国军方应建立能够实现快速更新和作战反馈闭环的机制，确保反无人机能力以与其旨在击败的威胁相同的速度演进。\n结论\n伊朗在“史诗之怒”行动中实施报复性行动的第一周表明，无人机不再是辅助系统，而是现代空中战役的核心工具。它们能够以相对较低的成本持续施压，使行为体能够在保留高端导弹用于特定目标的同时，施加经济、心理和作战压力。这类战役的有效性不仅取决于无人机本身，也取决于支撑其大规模使用的更广泛生态系统，包括生产能力、作战理论以及与其他打击系统的整合。\n对于美国而言，适应这一环境不仅仅需要采购新硬件。它还要求将无人机和反无人机系统能力纳入作战理论、训练、采购和作战规划。建立针对大规模无人机战争的有效防御，将需要协调发展拦截无人机、传感器网络、战场管理软件、韧性通信系统，以及能够在日益由人工智能赋能的战场空间中作战的受训操作人员。\nKateryna Bondar 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心瓦德瓦尼人工智能中心的研究员。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究不带党派立场，也不具有专有性质。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人或作者们的观点。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n中东，\n伊朗，\n国防与安全，\n人工智能，以及\n军事技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-03-10T12:00:00"}},{"id":"news-1854","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"不稳定的硬币：稳定币监管、市场结构立法与美国安全风险","title_en":"Unstable Coins: Stablecoin Regulation, Market Structure Legislation, and U.S. Security Risks","summary_cn":"文章分析美国2025年《GENIUS法案》及《CLARITY法案》《RFIA》等市场结构立法在稳定币监管上的不足，认为其造成联邦与州监管碎片化、储备与流动性要求缺失、反洗钱与反恐融资覆盖不全，可能被对手利用规避制裁，削弱美国金融体系公信力与地缘经济领导地位，并呼吁国会尽快修补漏洞。","summary_en":"The commentary analyzes shortcomings in U.S. stablecoin regulation under the 2025 GENIUS Act and market structure bills such as the CLARITY Act and RFIA. It argues that regulatory fragmentation between federal and state regimes, missing reserve and liquidity requirements, and incomplete AML/CFT coverage could be exploited by adversaries to evade sanctions, undermining U.S. financial credibility and geopolitical leadership, and urges Congress to close these gaps quickly.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:42:15.016720+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/unstable-coins-stablecoin-regulation-market-structure-legislation-and-us-security-risks","content":"Unstable Coins: Stablecoin Regulation, Market Structure Legislation, and U.S. Security Risks\nPhoto: Tex vector/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nTaylar Rajic\nand\nPeter Dohr\nPublished January 12, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nThe United States spent the 2010s incrementally upgrading bank-based magnetic-stripe cards with chips. Meanwhile, China leapfrogged the legacy card-based system by rolling out digital wallets and QR-code-based payment systems. Rapid diffusion of Alipay and WeChat Pay drove near-universal urban adoption. Today, those platforms exceed 1 billion monthly users, enabling low-cost transfers and payments and largely disintermediating the banking system. The Chinese government embraced digital payments, generating economic, national security, and geopolitical dividends. Digital payments unlocked the latent potential of China’s rural consumer market, alleviating mobility constraints and diversifying consumption. The Chinese government then embedded this financial activity within systems of data collection, surveillance, and state influence. The increasing international adoption of these digital payments and their extensive interoperability support the government’s push for broader usage of Chinese currency. While the United States stagnated within its financial paradigm, China’s proactive adoption and regulation of revolutionary financial technologies offered massive national benefits.Today’s cryptocurrencies represent a similar inflection point. As financial systems determine geopolitical leverage, sanctions enforcement, data sovereignty, and the global role of the U.S. dollar, competitiveness in this area is essential. However, in the United States, whose banking and payment systems have always been intertwined, the regulatory landscape is struggling to adjust. Concurrently, allies and adversaries alike embrace the new technologies, threatening to erode U.S. financial dominance and economic competitiveness. Without swift legislation to promote institutional adoption and mitigate financial and national security vulnerabilities, the United States risks ceding its global economic leadership while simultaneously exposing itself to perilous threats.The GENIUS Act and Its ShortcomingsSigned into law in July 2025, the Guiding and Establishing National Innovation for U.S. Stablecoins (GENIUS) Act represents Congress’s first comprehensive attempt to establish a U.S. regulatory framework for payment stablecoins—digital assets that an issuer must redeem for a fixed value. It aims to ensure stablecoin safety and stability by enhancing dependability, oversight, and transparency, without limiting prospects for continued innovation.However, in pursuit of these aims, the act could unintentionally compromise the United States’ global competitiveness. It creates regulatory fragmentation by dividing supervision between federal and state regimes, inviting a “race to the bottom” as states dilute requirements to attract issuers. The bill allows state-level regimes to differ and exempts state-issued stablecoins from the federal framework, enabling regulatory competition. Provisions upend host-state oversight and erode state authority to impose consistent standards. These gaps hinder oversight of stablecoin flows and leave the Federal Reserve and the Department of the Treasury with limited leverage.The act leaves major gaps in stablecoin reserve composition and management. It imposes no hard liquidity, concentration, or stress-testing requirements and permits reserves in uninsured bank deposits and credit union shares. It further ties stablecoin risk to banking-sector stress, creating reciprocal exposure. These dynamics could create fire-sale patterns and turn a stablecoin run into stress on the banking system.The act adds risk by leaving key financial details to future rulemaking. The act authorizes regulators to impose “capital buffers that are tailored to the business model and risk profile,” but offers no specifics on buffer design. Inappropriate capital buffer standards could leave stablecoins “information-sensitive,” prone to runs of sudden, self-reinforcing redemptions whenever doubts arise. The act also pushes the development of run-down and wind-down plans for future regulations. These flaws raise the risk that a large stablecoin issuer could become highly leveraged and poorly resolvable, precipitating a rapid, chaotic liquidation rather than orderly recapitalization or restructuring.Together, these financial vulnerabilities create a systemic shock vector. A stablecoin run could cascade into U.S. banking stress, weakening U.S. economic performance relative to strategic competitors. Moreover, this potential for instability threatens U.S. financial system credibility, weakens U.S. agenda-setting power, complicates allied coordination, and could accelerate diversification away from U.S.-linked systems.The act also maintains gaps that have the potential to weaken U.S. national security by allowing adversaries to influence U.S. financial infrastructure, expand gray-zone financial operations, evade sanctions, and launder stolen funds.The act includes a pathway for foreign payment stablecoin issuers (FPSIs) but does not require rigorous screening of foreign governance structures. It delegates judgment of foreign-jurisdiction regimes to the Treasury, which has yet to finalize an oversight framework, leaving key risks unaddressed. Insufficient oversight of ultimate ownership and controlling interests might allow adversaries to influence an FPSI, creating national security risks. Moreover, if the Treasury defines comparability rules loosely, issuers will flock to the most permissive jurisdiction and still access the U.S. market.Additionally, the act overlooks illicit finance opportunities. It imposes anti-money-laundering (AML) and counterterrorist-financing (CFT) requirements but focuses only on stablecoin issuers. Under GENIUS, the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) does not regulate digital-asset intermediaries or decentralized protocols through which digital assets like stablecoins circulate. This creates a gap that malign actors exploit. Adversarial use of stablecoins to evade sanctions has increased substantially in the last year.Financial weaknesses and national security flaws leave the GENIUS Act an incomplete framework for stablecoin regulation with the potential to negatively impact the United States’ position on the global stage.Market Structure Legislation FlawsIn July 2025, the House passed the Digital Asset Market Clarity (CLARITY) Act, which addresses jurisdictional ambiguities between the Securities and Exchange Commission and the Commodity Futures Trading Commission by categorizing digital assets into commodities, investment contracts, and stablecoins. The Responsible Financial Innovation Act (RFIA) of 2025 reflects the Senate Banking Committee’s ongoing effort to develop a more comprehensive market structure that builds on the CLARITY Act.Senator John Kennedy addressed the GENIUS Act’s financial vulnerabilities in his floor remarks and stated that the banking community expects policymakers to amend digital asset market structure legislation to mitigate risks. Similarly, Senator Elizabeth Warren discussed the GENIUS Act’s national security gaps in a letter to Secretary of the Treasury Scott Bessent, concluding that Congress has an opportunity to address risks in market structure legislation.However, rather than alleviating GENIUS Act financial weaknesses, ongoing efforts overlook critical risks and threaten to institutionalize existing vulnerabilities.These bills risk cementing structural financial vulnerabilities and creating space for regulatory arbitrage. The Conference of State Bank Supervisors warns that the RFIA allows “uninsured national trust companies to exercise a broad range of banking powers... while simultaneously avoiding a range of corresponding regulatory mandates.” Separately, economic analysts warn the RFIA invites “issuers of traditional securities to circumvent investor protections” by creating tokens that “function like investments but avoid customary labels.” The current approach imperils “investor protection, market integrity, and the credibility of the securities regulatory framework itself.” This failure extends beyond digital asset markets, threatening to undermine the foundation of U.S. capital markets and the integrity of the U.S. financial system.By failing to resolve financial vulnerabilities, these bills threaten to entrench the systemic shock vector created by the GENIUS Act. This exacerbates problems for U.S. economic competitiveness, and further undermines U.S. financial-system credibility, agenda-setting power, and prospects for allied coordination—all to the benefit of geopolitical adversaries.These bills also risk sustaining the gaps in national security that exist in the GENIUS Act, amplifying adversaries’ ability to compromise U.S. interests.The CLARITY Act obscures the status of certain digital asset service providers (DASPs) relative to the BSA, potentially limiting the government’s ability to detect, deter, and disrupt money laundering, terrorist financing, sanctions evasion, and other financial crimes. In contrast to other persistent gaps, the RFIA, as initially drafted, does mitigate this issue. It clarifies that these DASPs qualify as financial institutions under the BSA. However, it includes broad decentralized finance (DeFi)–related carveouts that limit oversight of other non-DeFi businesses and persons.The CLARITY Act establishes a broad DeFi exclusion covering user-interface provision and DeFi system development and distribution. The RFIA fails to resolve this issue. Via definitional exclusions and functional ambiguity, it exempts transaction-intermediating and custodial platforms from basic AML/CFT safeguards.These market structure bills also fail to enforce baseline issuer monitoring of the stablecoin ecosystem. Currently, only issuers concerned about illicit finance pay the cost to implement ecosystem monitoring services. This poses a significant threat amid rising illicit stablecoin use. Since 2024, most on-chain illicit activity has involved stablecoins. Without widespread monitoring, stablecoins will become an increasingly attractive avenue for criminal behavior.Reinforcing this failure, these bills leave mixers and anonymity tools unaddressed. They fail to close a loophole left by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit’s 2024 Van Loon decision, which holds that immutable smart contracts do not constitute property. This limits the government’s ability to use existing sanctions laws against such contracts used for money laundering or sanctions evasion. These mixers and anonymity tools enable state-linked adversaries to avoid detection and enforcement when using stablecoins for illicit activities, threatening U.S. national security.Last, these market structure bills would allow crypto platforms to evade regulatory accountability by claiming they operate outside the United States. The Treasury’s 2023 Illicit Finance Risk Assessment of Decentralized Finance highlights the vulnerability created by this regulatory gap. The assessment finds the most significant risk associated with virtual assets stems from “virtual asset service providers operating abroad with substantially deficient AML/CFT programs.”Rather than closing GENIUS Act national security gaps, current market structure initiatives risk elevating these threats, further enabling adversaries to impact the U.S. financial ecosystem, circumvent U.S. sanctions, and hide theft.By compounding the GENIUS Act’s financial and national security vulnerabilities, ongoing market structure efforts risk impairing the United States’ ability to outcompete economic rivals and defend against adversarial efforts to jeopardize its national security, thereby destabilizing the country’s geopolitical leadership.Necessary UrgencyRegulatory uncertainty threatens a strategic reallocation of capital to jurisdictions offering more clarity, intensifying current threats to U.S. financial stability.Disparities between the GENIUS Act, the CLARITY Act, and the RFIA have led to fragmented compliance expectations and operational uncertainties. As political divisions delay regulatory implementation, other regions will continue to establish clear frameworks and accelerate cryptocurrency adoption. This clarity could attract investors seeking a stable legislative environment, triggering capital flight risks that undermine the U.S. economy.The European Union passed the Markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation (MiCA) in 2022, instituting uniform EU market rules for crypto assets through multiple implementation phases. The law establishes a cohesive system for licensing and supervising crypto asset service providers and stablecoin issuers, focusing on transparency, disclosure, authorization, and supervision of transactions. The framework aims to support “market integrity and financial stability” and ensure “consumers are better informed” about crypto asset risks.Other regulatory environments have implemented similarly proactive frameworks. Singapore and Hong Kong, for example, each enforce clear and extensive market structure, monitoring, and security legislation.These global efforts to offer clarity and confidence could easily attract investors looking to diversify away from U.S. regulatory uncertainty. This capital flight risk imposes considerable urgency on current crypto legislation initiatives. Policymakers should mitigate financial and national security vulnerabilities in existing frameworks—but it is equally crucial that they do so quickly. If Congress fails to implement necessary changes with urgency, it risks forsaking the United States’ competitive edge in the global crypto arena.Prospects for ProgressCongress retains opportunities to adjust market structure legislation to address the GENIUS Act’s weaknesses. If policymakers approach this effort with recognition of the vulnerabilities in the current landscape of cryptocurrency regulation and an acceptance of the urgency with which changes are required, they can substantially strengthen the framework.Before passing any market structure legislation, policymakers should make the following crucial changes:Apply AML/CFT responsibilities to digital asset service providers.Ensure the Treasury can impose AML obligations on DeFi when DeFi functions like a financial institution.Close the Van Loon sanctions loophole.Require all stablecoin issuers to implement risk-based and operationally feasible ecosystem-wide monitoring, as enabled by existing techniques and commercially available tools.Enforce Treasury oversight requirements for international crypto platforms.Close gaps that are creating structural financial weaknesses.Policymakers should take additional steps to regulate stablecoins, including establishing clear liquidity and concentration requirements, a defined resolution regime, coordinated supervision, rigorous foreign-issuer screening, enhanced AML/CFT coverage, and greater transparency.Policymakers should also acknowledge the urgent necessity of these changes to digital asset legislation to bolster U.S. economic competitiveness, reinforce U.S. national security, and uphold global standards for countering illicit finance.Peter Dohr is a research intern with the Strategic Technologies Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Taylar Rajic is an associate fellow with the Strategic Technologies Program at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nImage\nTaylar Rajic\nAssociate Fellow, Strategic Technologies Program\nPeter Dohr\nResearch Intern, Strategic Technologies Program\nPrograms & Projects\nStrategic Technologies Program\nEconomic Security and Technology","content_cn":"不稳定的货币：稳定币监管、市场结构立法与美国安全风险\n照片：Tex vector/Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nTaylar Rajic\n和\nPeter Dohr\n发表于2026年1月12日\n远程可视化\n美国在2010年代逐步为以银行为基础的磁条卡升级芯片。与此同时，中国通过推出数字钱包和基于二维码的支付系统，跨越了以传统卡片为基础的体系。支付宝和微信支付的迅速普及推动了城市地区近乎全民的采用。如今，这些平台的月活跃用户超过10亿，使低成本转账和支付成为可能，并在很大程度上绕过了银行体系。中国政府拥抱数字支付，获得了经济、国家安全和地缘政治红利。数字支付释放了中国农村消费市场的潜在能力，缓解了流动性限制并使消费多样化。随后，中国政府将这一金融活动嵌入数据收集、监控和国家影响力体系之中。此类数字支付日益走向国际化，其广泛的互操作性支持了政府推动更广泛使用中国货币的努力。在美国停滞于自身金融范式之际，中国主动采用并监管革命性金融技术，带来了巨大的国家利益。如今的加密货币代表着类似的转折点。当金融体系决定地缘政治影响力、制裁执行、数据主权以及美元的全球作用时，在这一领域保持竞争力至关重要。然而，在银行和支付体系一贯相互交织的美国，监管格局正努力适应这一变化。与此同时，盟友和对手都在拥抱新技术，威胁侵蚀美国的金融主导地位和经济竞争力。如果没有迅速出台立法来促进机构采用并缓解金融和国家安全漏洞，美国就有可能让出其全球经济领导地位，同时使自身暴露于危险威胁之下。《GENIUS法案》及其缺陷\n《指导和确立美国稳定币国家创新法案》（GENIUS法案）于2025年7月签署成为法律，代表国会首次全面尝试为支付型稳定币建立美国监管框架——支付型稳定币是发行方必须按固定价值赎回的数字资产。该法案旨在通过增强可靠性、监督和透明度，确保稳定币的安全与稳定，同时不限制持续创新的前景。然而，在追求这些目标的过程中，该法案可能无意中损害美国的全球竞争力。它将监管分割在联邦和州两套制度之间，造成监管碎片化，并诱使各州通过降低要求来吸引发行方，形成“逐底竞争”。该法案允许州级制度彼此不同，并将州发行的稳定币排除在联邦框架之外，从而促成监管竞争。其中的条款颠覆了东道州的监督，并削弱了州政府施加统一标准的权力。这些漏洞妨碍对稳定币流动的监督，使美联储和财政部的杠杆作用受到限制。该法案在稳定币储备的构成和管理方面留下重大漏洞。它没有规定严格的流动性、集中度或压力测试要求，并且\n允许储备存放于无保险银行存款和信用合作社股份中。该法还将稳定币风险与银行业压力联系起来，形成相互暴露。这些动态可能造成抛售模式，并将稳定币挤兑转化为银行体系压力。\n\n该法将关键金融细节留待未来制定规则，从而增加了风险。该法授权监管机构施加“根据业务模式和风险状况量身定制的资本缓冲”，但没有说明缓冲机制的具体设计。不恰当的资本缓冲标准可能使稳定币处于“信息敏感”状态，每当疑虑出现时，就容易发生突然且自我强化的赎回挤兑。该法还推动为未来监管制定挤兑应对和退出计划。这些缺陷增加了大型稳定币发行方高度杠杆化且难以有序处置的风险，可能引发快速、混乱的清算，而不是有序的资本重组或重组。\n\n这些金融脆弱性合在一起，形成了系统性冲击路径。稳定币挤兑可能蔓延为美国银行业压力，削弱美国相对于战略竞争对手的经济表现。此外，这种潜在的不稳定性威胁美国金融体系的信誉，削弱美国设定议程的能力，使盟友协调复杂化，并可能加速摆脱与美国关联的体系。\n\n该法还保留了一些漏洞，可能通过允许对手影响美国金融基础设施、扩大灰色地带金融行动、规避制裁以及洗白被窃资金来削弱美国国家安全。\n\n该法为外国支付稳定币发行方（FPSI）纳入体系提供了路径，但没有要求对外国治理结构进行严格审查。该法将外国司法辖区制度的判断权交给财政部，而财政部尚未最终确定监督框架，导致关键风险未得到处理。对最终所有权和控制性权益监督不足，可能使对手得以影响某个FPSI，造成国家安全风险。此外，如果财政部宽松地界定可比性规则，发行方将纷纷涌向监管最宽松的司法辖区，同时仍能进入美国市场。\n\n此外，该法忽视了非法金融活动的机会。该法施加反洗钱（AML）和反恐融资（CFT）要求，但只针对稳定币发行方。根据《GENIUS法案》，《银行保密法》（BSA）并不监管数字资产中介机构或去中心化协议，而稳定币等数字资产正是通过这些中介机构或协议流通的。这造成了恶意行为者可以利用的漏洞。过去一年中，对手利用稳定币规避制裁的情况大幅增加。\n\n金融弱点和国家安全缺陷使《GENIUS法案》成为一个不完整的稳定币监管框架，并可能对美国在全球舞台上的地位产生负面影响。\n\n市场结构立法的缺陷\n\n2025年7月，众议院通过了《数字资产市场清晰度法案》（CLARITY法案），该法案将数字资产划分为商品、投资合同和稳定币，以解决证券交易委员会与商品期货交易委员会之间的管辖权歧义。2025年的《负责任金融创新法案》（RFIA）体现了参议院银行委员会持续制定更全面市场结构的努力，并在CLARITY法案基础上进一步发展。\n\n参议员约翰·肯尼迪谈到了\n在楼层发言中，他指出了《GENIUS法案》的金融脆弱性，并表示银行界希望政策制定者修改数字资产市场结构立法，以减轻风险。同样，参议员伊丽莎白·沃伦在致财政部长斯科特·贝森特的信中讨论了《GENIUS法案》的国家安全漏洞，最后指出，国会有机会在市场结构立法中应对这些风险。\n\n然而，持续推进的努力非但没有缓解《GENIUS法案》的金融弱点，反而忽视了关键风险，并威胁要将现有脆弱性制度化。\n\n这些法案可能固化结构性金融脆弱性，并为监管套利创造空间。州银行监管者会议警告称，RFIA允许“未投保的全国性信托公司行使广泛的银行业务权力……同时规避一系列相应的监管义务”。此外，经济分析人士警告称，RFIA通过创造“类似投资但避开惯常标签”的代币，诱使“传统证券发行人规避投资者保护”。当前做法危及“投资者保护、市场完整性以及证券监管框架本身的公信力”。这一失败影响的不仅是数字资产市场，还威胁到美国资本市场的基础以及美国金融体系的完整性。\n\n由于未能解决金融脆弱性，这些法案有可能使《GENIUS法案》造成的系统性冲击向量根深蒂固。这加剧了美国经济竞争力面临的问题，并进一步削弱美国金融体系的公信力、议程设置能力以及与盟友协调的前景——而地缘政治对手将从中受益。\n\n这些法案还可能延续《GENIUS法案》中存在的国家安全漏洞，增强对手损害美国利益的能力。\n\n《CLARITY法案》模糊了某些数字资产服务提供商（DASPs）相对于《银行保密法》（BSA）的地位，可能限制政府侦测、威慑和打击洗钱、恐怖主义融资、规避制裁及其他金融犯罪的能力。与其他长期存在的漏洞不同，RFIA按最初起草的版本确实缓解了这一问题。它明确这些DASP属于《银行保密法》规定的金融机构。然而，该法案包含与去中心化金融（DeFi）相关的广泛豁免条款，限制了对其他非DeFi企业和个人的监督。\n\n《CLARITY法案》确立了广泛的DeFi排除范围，涵盖用户界面提供以及DeFi系统的开发和分发。RFIA未能解决这一问题。通过定义排除和功能上的模糊性，它使交易中介平台和托管平台免于遵守基本的反洗钱/打击恐怖主义融资（AML/CFT）保障措施。\n\n这些市场结构法案还未能强制稳定币生态系统发行人进行基线监测。目前，只有担心非法融资的发行人才会承担实施生态系统监测服务的成本。在稳定币非法使用不断增加之际，这构成了重大威胁。自2024年以来，大多数链上非法活动都涉及稳定币。没有广泛的监测，稳定币将成为犯罪行为越来越有吸引力的途径。\n\n这些法案进一步加剧了这一失败，因为它们没有处理混币器和匿名工具问题。它们未能堵住美国第五巡回上诉法院2024年“Van Loon”案留下的漏洞\n该裁决认定，不可变智能合约不构成财产。这限制了政府利用现有制裁法打击被用于洗钱或规避制裁的此类合约的能力。这些混币器和匿名工具使与国家有关联的对手在使用稳定币从事非法活动时能够逃避侦测和执法，威胁美国国家安全。\n\n最后，这些市场结构法案将允许加密平台声称其在美国境外运营，从而逃避监管问责。美国财政部2023年《去中心化金融非法融资风险评估》强调了这一监管漏洞造成的脆弱性。该评估发现，与虚拟资产相关的最重大风险来自“在境外运营、反洗钱/打击资助恐怖主义（AML/CFT）计划存在严重缺陷的虚拟资产服务提供商”。\n\n当前的市场结构倡议非但没有弥补《GENIUS法案》的国家安全漏洞，反而可能加剧这些威胁，进一步使对手能够影响美国金融生态系统、规避美国制裁并掩盖盗窃行为。\n\n通过叠加《GENIUS法案》在金融和国家安全方面的脆弱性，持续推进的市场结构努力可能削弱美国在经济竞争中胜过对手、抵御危害其国家安全的敌对行动的能力，从而动摇美国的地缘政治领导地位。\n\n必要的紧迫性\n\n监管不确定性正威胁着资本向提供更明确规则的司法管辖区进行战略性重新配置，加剧当前对美国金融稳定的威胁。\n\n《GENIUS法案》、《CLARITY法案》和RFIA之间的差异，导致合规预期碎片化和运营不确定性。随着政治分歧延误监管实施，其他地区将继续建立明确框架并加速加密货币的采用。这种明确性可能吸引寻求稳定立法环境的投资者，引发资本外流风险，进而削弱美国经济。\n\n欧盟于2022年通过《加密资产市场监管条例》（MiCA），通过多个实施阶段为加密资产确立统一的欧盟市场规则。该法建立了对加密资产服务提供商和稳定币发行方进行许可和监督的统一体系，重点关注交易的透明度、披露、授权和监督。该框架旨在支持“市场完整性和金融稳定”，并确保“消费者更充分地了解”加密资产风险。\n\n其他监管环境也实施了类似的积极框架。例如，新加坡和香港都执行明确且全面的市场结构、监测和安全法规。\n\n这些全球范围内旨在提供明确性和信心的努力，很容易吸引希望摆脱美国监管不确定性、实现投资多元化的投资者。这种资本外流风险使当前的加密立法倡议具有相当的紧迫性。政策制定者应当缓解现有框架中的金融和国家安全脆弱性——但同样重要的是，他们必须迅速采取行动。如果国会未能紧迫地实施必要变革，就有可能放弃美国在全球加密领域的竞争优势。\n\n进展前景\n\n国会仍有机会调整市场结构立法，以解决《GENIUS法案》的弱点。如果政策制定者以这种方式推进工作，\n认识到当前加密货币监管格局中的漏洞，并接受必须紧迫推进改革这一事实，他们就能大幅强化该框架。在通过任何市场结构立法之前，政策制定者应作出以下关键改变：将反洗钱/打击资助恐怖主义（AML/CFT）责任适用于数字资产服务提供商。确保财政部能够在去中心化金融（DeFi）发挥金融机构作用时，对其施加反洗钱义务。堵住范·卢恩制裁漏洞。要求所有稳定币发行方实施基于风险且在运营上可行的全生态系统监测，并利用现有技术和商业上可用的工具。对国际加密平台执行财政部监管要求。弥补正在造成结构性金融弱点的漏洞。政策制定者还应采取更多措施监管稳定币，包括确立明确的流动性和集中度要求、明确的处置制度、协调一致的监管、严格的外国发行方审查、加强AML/CFT覆盖范围，以及提高透明度。政策制定者还应承认，迫切需要对数字资产立法作出这些改变，以增强美国经济竞争力、巩固美国国家安全，并维护全球打击非法融资的标准。Peter Dohr是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）战略技术项目的研究实习生。Taylar Rajic是CSIS战略技术项目的副研究员。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不涉及专有利益。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人的观点。\n© 2026战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n图片\nTaylar Rajic\n副研究员，战略技术项目\nPeter Dohr\n研究实习生，战略技术项目\n项目与计划\n战略技术项目\n经济安全与技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-01-12T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2014","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美加勒比关系：推迟2025年美洲峰会的代价","title_en":"U.S.-Caribbean Relations: The Cost of Postponing the 2025 Summit of the Americas","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心发表评论，分析多米尼加共和国推迟2025年第十届美洲峰会的影响。文章指出峰会机制影响力下降，地区面临民主倒退、毒品与帮派暴力、海地危机及委内瑞拉紧张局势。作者认为美国在加勒比地区既推动多边安全使命又对委内瑞拉采取单边行动，仍需地区伙伴合作，呼吁重振1994年峰会合作精神。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary examines the Dominican Republic's postponement of the 2025 tenth Summit of the Americas. It notes the summit's declining relevance amid democratic backsliding, drug and gang violence, Haiti's crisis, and tensions with Venezuela. The authors argue that while the United States pursues a multilateral security mission in Haiti and unilateral action against Venezuela, it still needs regional partners, urging a revival of the cooperative spirit of the 1994 summit.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:46:35.263499+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/us-caribbean-relations-cost-postponing-2025-summit-americas","content":"U.S.-Caribbean Relations: The Cost of Postponing the 2025 Summit of the Americas\nPhoto: FRANCESCO SPOTORNO/AFP/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nGeorges A. Fauriol\nand\nMary Speck\nPublished December 8, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nLargely ignored amid news of the U.S. military deployment in the Caribbean was a brief statement from the Dominican Republic (DR), host country for the 10th Summit of the Americas, announcing that it would postpone the triennial gathering, scheduled for early December, citing “the deep differences that currently make productive dialogue difficult.”With Haiti in turmoil and Venezuela facing possible U.S. military intervention, postponing the 10th Summit of the Americas may have seemed the prudent option. Over the past three decades, the high-level gatherings have grown increasingly irrelevant to major hemispheric challenges. None has matched the ambition of the first summit, held in Miami in 1994, which ended with an agreement to establish a “Free Trade Area of the Americas” (FTAA) by the end of 2005.The Miami meeting was a celebration of democracy in a hemisphere that had recently emerged from military rule. Thirty-four elected presidents and prime ministers attended, representing every country except Cuba. “A spirit of cooperation barely imaginable only a short time ago has taken root and blossomed,” the U.S. Department of State proclaimed as it prepared for the second summit in 1997. “Building on our remarkable progress to date,” the statement continued, “we are moving forward toward the hemisphere’s definition of a brighter future.”That optimism and purpose have faded. Dreams of an FTAA are dead and buried. Much of the region looks to China as its major source of trade and foreign investment, while the United States focuses on bilateral agreements, using tariffs to punish ideological rivals, such as the government of Brazil, and to reward allies, such as Argentina and Ecuador. The hemisphere faces a democratic recession, as many Latin American governments limit due process and civil rights to battle organized crime. Cuba is no longer the hemisphere’s sole dictatorship: Nicaragua and Venezuela have also devolved into full-fledged authoritarianism.The Americas also face escalating violence as cocaine traffickers compete for new routes toward lucrative European markets. Gang violence linked to drug and arms trafficking has soared in much of the Caribbean, pummeling island nations best known for their pristine beaches and luxury resorts. In 2024, Turks and Caicos, a small British Overseas Territory with only 43,000 people, suffered the highest murder rate in the Americas.The security challenges that now preoccupy the region demand regional solutions, especially in Haiti, the poorest, most volatile country in the Americas, which shares the island of Hispaniola with the DR.The Haiti CrisisHaiti’s extreme poverty, natural disasters, political instability, and horrific gang violence have combined to generate a humanitarian crisis, displacing more than 1.4 million people, many of whom have fled across the Haiti–DR border.Although Haiti was not officially on the summit agenda, it would have been hard to avoid discussing the collapse of governance right next door to the DR. The United States has backed a new UN-supported security mission to Haiti, the Gang Suppression Force (GSF), which should be bigger, better equipped, and more aggressive than a previous Kenya-led mission.Recent U.S. actions against Venezuela have overshadowed efforts to win international backing for the new Haiti mission. Staffing, equipping, and deploying the new mission will likely take at least six months. Although the United States may provide the bulk of GSF financial support, it has not been willing to put boots on the ground. Nor have the major Latin American nations that supported past interventions, such as Chile and Brazil, offered substantial help so far, either for personnel or financing.Moreover, Haiti faces another looming crisis after February 7, when the Transitional Presidential Council—a temporary body established in April 2024—was scheduled to hand power over to an elected president and legislature. The nine-member council, whose mandate is unlikely to be extended, has yet to agree on a plan for elections or an interim government capable of carrying them out.Polarizing Security PolicyThe theme of the 2025 Summit of the Americas was supposed to be “human security,” defined broadly as citizen security as well as food, water, and energy security. The U.S. airstrikes on 23 suspected drug boats since early September—killing 87 people—have polarized any discussion of security, however, especially when combined with threats to take out targets inside Venezuela.Such actions trigger painful memories in South America, where the United States often backed military governments during the Cold War. The Caribbean Basin has its own history of “gunboat diplomacy” dating back to the late nineteenth century, when the United States cited the 1823 Monroe Doctrine to prevent colonialist European powers from meddling in the Americas.The administration’s new National Security Strategy, issued on December 4, reorients U.S. military resources to the Western Hemisphere, asserting a “Trump Corollary” to the Monroe Doctrine designed to “prevent and discourage mass migration,” ensure that regional governments “cooperate . . . against narco-terrorists,” and prevent “hostile foreign incursion or ownership of key assets.” Secretary Hegseth summed up the new stance when he announced Operation Southern Spear on social media in November, proclaiming, “The Western Hemisphere is America’s neighborhood—and we will protect it.”Some Caribbean leaders—including Dominican President Luis Abinader and Prime Minister Kamla Persad-Bissessar of Trinidad and Tobago—have embraced the U.S. military deployment, promising to collaborate in joint counternarcotics operations. But the issue has split CARICOM, an economic community with 15 Caribbean member states, which issued a statement calling for a “zone of peace” in the region, respect for international law, and support for “sovereignty and territorial integrity.”Latin America’s largest democratic states—Brazil, Mexico, and Colombia—have all warned against U.S. military action, especially threats against the regime of President Nicolás Maduro, which the White House says functions as a narco-terrorist network.Dominican GrowthFor Dominican President Abinader, hosting the high-level gathering in the resort town of Punta Cana was a chance not only to showcase his country’s white-sand beaches but also to celebrate its extraordinary economic development. The DR, which in the 1960s was among Latin America’s poorest countries, has since then enjoyed growth rates averaging more than 5 percent a year, putting it on a convergence track with the hemisphere’s most advanced economies.The DR has its own history of U.S. interventions: President Johnson secured Organization of American States approval for a multinational invasion force, made up mostly of U.S. troops, to halt an April 1965 uprising by young army officers and prevent “another Cuba” in the Caribbean. The country was also occupied by U.S. forces from 1916 to 1924 (as was neighboring Haiti from 1915 to 1934), ostensibly to restore political stability, protect U.S. investments, and forestall European (especially German) intervention to collect foreign debts.The country’s rapid economic growth has largely extinguished any residues of anti-American feeling. A 2020 poll conducted by Vanderbilt University found that more than two-thirds of the Dominican public trusted the U.S. government, the highest level in Latin America, where overall trust in the United States was found to be low (averaging less than 50 percent) and declining.President Abinader, who ranks among the region’s most popular presidents, is positioning his government as a key U.S. ally, offering the U.S. military temporary use of Dominican airports for counternarcotics operations, access which might also assist the deployment to Haiti of the GSF. The DR reported record cocaine seizures in 2024, much of it bound for Europe. Abinader has also won U.S. praise for his anti-corruption efforts, including the appointment of an independent attorney general who launched a multipronged investigation into high-level embezzlement under the previous government dubbed Operation Antipulpo (anti-octopus).Abinader’s tough line on Haitian migration is an issue that helped him win reelection resoundingly in 2024. Dominican authorities have tightened border security by building a wall and deploying additional troops, which now total about 12,000. The government has also ramped up deportations, setting a goal of up to 10,000 a week. Those deported include Haitians who were born in the DR or have lived there most of their lives; many simply return, paying smugglers who bribe border guards to let them across.Corruption among Dominican security forces along the border remains a problem, as Abinader himself has acknowledged. While migrants flow from Haiti into the DR seeking work and security, arms and other contraband goods flow from the DR into Haiti. Despite a UN arms embargo, Haitian gangs are well supplied with U.S.-made guns, which are often smuggled on cargo ships into the DR and then transported across the border into Haiti.Regional Challenges, Regional SolutionsOn a recent tour to the Indo-Pacific, Secretary Hegseth declared, “‘America First’ does not mean ‘America alone,’” echoing President Trump’s own declarations to global finance leaders at Davos in 2018. That should also apply to America’s own increasingly divided Western Hemisphere, especially amid the political storms that are roiling the Caribbean Basin.The United States has opted for multilateral action to restore peace and democratic governance in Haiti by pushing for a UN-supported security mission to suppress the violent gangs that traffic in weapons and drugs, fueling mass migration into the wider region. In contrast, the United States has chosen to act unilaterally against the regime in Venezuela by threatening to strike targets within the country and close its airspace.The United States will need regional allies to bring lasting peace and prosperity to both Haiti and Venezuela, however. Failure in either Haiti or Venezuela will reverberate throughout the Americas, undermining U.S. claims to regional leadership. Worse, as the U.S. experience over the last 25 years shows, military interventions cannot on their own bring about stable, democratic governance.Postponing the 2025 Summit of the Americas may have been a prudent short-run call. But the United States still needs regional partners to address the Caribbean’s and the Hemisphere’s complex long-run economic and political challenges. Reviving the “spirit of cooperation” that all-too-briefly united the region at the 1994 Summit of the Americas is just as important today.Georges Fauriol is a senior associate (non-resident) with the Americas Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Mary Speck is a former senior advisor to the Latin America Program at the United States Institute of Peace.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAmericas,\nThe Caribbean,\nDefense and Security, and\nConflict and Humanitarian Crises\nImage\nGeorges A. Fauriol\nSenior Associate (Non-resident), Americas Program\nMary Speck\nFormer Senior Adviser, Latin America Program, United States Institute of Peace\nPrograms & Projects\nAmericas Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nGovernance and Rule of Law: Democratic Development, Corruption, Migration, Inequality and Human Rights\nRelated Content\nImage\nThe United States Votes to Establish a Haiti Gang Suppression Force: Now What?\nCritical Questions\nby\nGeorges A. Fauriol\nand\nMary Speck\n— October 8, 2025\nImage\nThe Trump Administration Wants to Establish a UN Support Office in Haiti—Now What?\nCommentary\nby\nGeorges A. Fauriol\n— August 25, 2025","content_cn":"美加勒比关系：推迟2025年美洲峰会的代价\n照片：FRANCESCO SPOTORNO/法新社/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nGeorges A. Fauriol\n和\nMary Speck\n发表于2025年12月8日\n远程可视化\n在有关美国向加勒比地区部署军事力量的新闻中，多米尼加共和国（DR）的一份简短声明基本上被忽视了。该国是第十届美洲峰会的主办国，宣布将推迟原定于12月初举行的三年一度会议，理由是“当前存在的严重分歧使富有成效的对话变得困难”。海地陷入动荡，委内瑞拉面临美国军事干预的可能，推迟第十届美洲峰会似乎是审慎之举。在过去三十年中，这些高层会议对半球重大挑战的相关性日益降低。没有一次会议能够匹敌1994年在迈阿密举行的首次峰会的雄心；那次峰会以达成一项协议告终，计划在2005年底前建立“美洲自由贸易区”（FTAA）。\n迈阿密会议是对一个刚刚摆脱军事统治的半球民主制度的庆祝。除古巴外，所有国家均派出了民选总统和总理，共有34位领导人出席。美国国务院在为1997年第二次峰会做准备时宣称：“这种在不久前还难以想象的合作精神已经扎根并蓬勃发展。”“在迄今取得的显著进展基础上，”声明继续说道，“我们正朝着界定半球更加光明的未来迈进。”\n那种乐观与目标感已经消退。建立美洲自由贸易区的梦想已经彻底破灭。该地区许多国家把中国视为贸易和外国投资的主要来源，而美国则专注于双边协议，利用关税惩罚巴西政府等意识形态对手，并奖励阿根廷和厄瓜多尔等盟友。半球正面临民主衰退，因为许多拉丁美洲政府为了打击有组织犯罪而限制正当程序和公民权利。古巴不再是半球唯一的独裁国家：尼加拉瓜和委内瑞拉也已沦为彻头彻尾的威权主义国家。\n随着可卡因贩运者争夺通往利润丰厚的欧洲市场的新路线，美洲还面临日益升级的暴力。与毒品和武器贩运有关的帮派暴力在加勒比大部分地区激增，重创了那些以原始海滩和豪华度假村闻名的岛国。2024年，人口仅4.3万的英属海外领地特克斯和凯科斯群岛遭遇了美洲最高的谋杀率。\n如今令该地区最为忧心的安全挑战需要区域性解决方案，尤其是在海地——美洲最贫穷、最动荡的国家——该国与多米尼加共和国共同占据伊斯帕尼奥拉岛。\n海地危机\n海地的极端贫困、自然灾害、政治不稳定和骇人听闻的帮派暴力共同造成了一场人道主义危机，导致超过140万人流离失所，其中许多人逃过海地—多米尼加共和国边境。\n尽管海地并未正式列入峰会议程，但要避免讨论就在多米尼加共和国隔壁的治理崩溃几乎是不可能的。美国支持一项由联合国支持的海地新安全任务——帮派镇压部队（GSF）；该部队应比此前由肯尼亚领导的任务规模更大、装备更精良、行动更积极。\n美国近期针对委内瑞拉的行动已经\n这些努力未能赢得国际社会对海地新任务的支持。为新任务配备人员、装备并部署到位，可能至少需要六个月。尽管美国可能提供 GSF 财政支持的大部分，但它不愿派遣地面部队。过去支持干预行动的主要拉美国家，如智利和巴西，到目前为止也没有为人员或资金提供实质性帮助。\n\n此外，海地在2月7日之后还面临另一场迫在眉睫的危机。届时，过渡总统委员会——一个于2024年4月成立的临时机构——原定将权力移交给民选总统和立法机构。这个九人委员会的任期不太可能延长，但迄今仍未就选举计划或一个能够组织选举的过渡政府达成一致。\n\n安全政策两极化\n\n2025年美洲峰会的主题原本应是“人类安全”，其定义广泛，包括公民安全以及粮食、水和能源安全。然而，自9月初以来，美国对23艘疑似运毒船发动空袭，造成87人死亡；再加上威胁要打击委内瑞拉境内目标，这使任何有关安全的讨论都陷入两极化。\n\n此类行动在南美触发了痛苦的记忆。在冷战期间，美国经常支持军政府。加勒比盆地也有自己的“炮舰外交”历史，可追溯至19世纪末，当时美国援引1823年的门罗主义，阻止欧洲殖民主义列强干涉美洲事务。\n\n政府于12月4日发布的新《国家安全战略》将美国军事资源重新部署到西半球，声称对门罗主义作出“特朗普附加条款”，旨在“防止和遏制大规模移民”，确保地区政府“合作……打击毒品恐怖分子”，并防止“敌对外国势力入侵或拥有关键资产”。赫格塞思部长在11月通过社交媒体宣布“南方之矛行动”时，总结了这一新立场，宣称：“西半球是美国的邻里——我们将保护它。”\n\n一些加勒比领导人——包括多米尼加总统路易斯·阿比纳德尔以及特立尼达和多巴哥总理卡姆拉·佩尔萨德-比塞萨尔——支持美国的军事部署，并承诺在联合禁毒行动中开展合作。但这一问题使加共体产生分裂。这个拥有15个加勒比成员国的经济共同体发表声明，呼吁该地区成为“和平区”，尊重国际法，并支持“主权和领土完整”。\n\n拉丁美洲最大的民主国家——巴西、墨西哥和哥伦比亚——都警告反对美国采取军事行动，尤其反对针对尼古拉斯·马杜罗总统政权的威胁。白宫称该政权是一个毒品恐怖主义网络。\n\n多米尼加的发展\n\n对于多米尼加总统阿比纳德尔来说，在度假城蓬塔卡纳主办这场高层聚会，不仅是展示本国白沙滩的机会，也是庆祝其非凡经济发展的机会。多米尼加共和国在20世纪60年代曾是拉丁美洲最贫穷的国家之一，此后经济年均增长率超过5%，使其走上了与西半球最发达经济体趋同的道路。\n\n多米尼加共和国有着美国干预的自身历史：约翰逊总统确保组织\n美洲国家组织批准组建一支多国入侵部队，其中大多数为美军，以平息1965年4月年轻军官发动的起义，并阻止加勒比地区出现“另一个古巴”。该国还曾于1916年至1924年被美军占领（其邻国海地也曾于1915年至1934年被占领），表面理由是恢复政治稳定、保护美国投资，并阻止欧洲（尤其是德国）为收取外债而进行干预。该国经济的快速增长已基本消除反美情绪的残余。范德比尔特大学2020年进行的一项民调发现，超过三分之二的多米尼加公众信任美国政府，这是拉丁美洲最高的水平；在该地区，人们对美国的总体信任度较低（平均不足50%），且还在下降。\n\n阿比纳德总统在该地区最受欢迎的总统之列，他正将其政府定位为美国的关键盟友，向美军提供多米尼加机场的临时使用权，用于缉毒行动；这一通道也可能有助于向海地部署GSF。多米尼加共和国报告称，2024年缴获的可卡因创下纪录，其中大部分运往欧洲。阿比纳德还因反腐努力赢得美国赞誉，包括任命一名独立总检察长，对前政府时期的高层挪用公款展开多管齐下的调查，该行动被称为“反章鱼行动”（Antipulpo）。\n\n阿比纳德对海地移民采取的强硬立场，是帮助他在2024年大幅赢得连任的一个议题。多米尼加当局通过修建隔离墙和增派部队来加强边境安全，目前兵力总数约为12,000人。政府还加大了驱逐力度，目标是每周驱逐最多10,000人。被驱逐者包括在多米尼加共和国出生或一生大部分时间都生活在那里的海地人；许多人只是再次返回，向走私者付费，由后者贿赂边境警卫放行。\n\n正如阿比纳德本人所承认的，多米尼加安全部队在边境沿线的腐败仍是一个问题。当移民从海地流入多米尼加共和国寻求工作和安全时，武器及其他违禁品却从多米尼加共和国流入海地。尽管联合国实施武器禁运，海地帮派仍拥有充足的美国制造枪支；这些枪支常被走私者装上货船运入多米尼加共和国，然后越过边境运往海地。\n\n区域挑战，区域解决方案\n\n在最近一次印度太平洋之行中，国防部长赫格塞思宣称：“‘美国优先’并不意味着‘美国孤立’”，呼应了特朗普总统本人2018年在达沃斯对全球金融领袖所作的类似声明。这一点也应适用于日益分裂的美国西半球，尤其是在政治风暴席卷加勒比海盆地之际。\n\n美国选择采取多边行动，通过推动一项由联合国支持的安全任务，来恢复海地的和平与民主治理，以压制那些贩运武器和毒品、助推大规模移民涌入更广泛地区的暴力帮派。相比之下，美国选择对委内瑞拉政权采取单边行动，威胁打击该国境内目标并关闭其领空。\n\n然而，美国需要地区盟友，才能给海地和委内瑞拉带来持久的和平与繁荣。海地或委内瑞拉任何一方的失败都会在整个美洲引发连锁反应，削弱美国对地区领导地位的宣称。更糟糕的是，随着美国……\n过去25年的经验表明，军事干预本身无法带来稳定、民主的治理。推迟2025年美洲峰会或许是一个审慎的短期决定。但美国仍需要地区伙伴来应对加勒比地区和整个西半球复杂的长期经济与政治挑战。重振1994年美洲峰会期间曾短暂团结该地区的“合作精神”，如今同样重要。Georges Fauriol是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心美洲项目的高级副研究员（非驻会）。Mary Speck曾任美国和平研究所拉丁美洲项目高级顾问。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不涉及专有利益。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n© 2025战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n美洲，\n加勒比地区，\n国防与安全，以及\n冲突与人道主义危机\n图片\nGeorges A. Fauriol\n高级副研究员（非驻会），美洲项目\nMary Speck\n前高级顾问，拉丁美洲项目，美国和平研究所\n项目与课题\n美洲项目\n地缘政治与外交政策\n治理与法治：民主发展、腐败、移民、不平等与人权\n相关内容\n图片\n美国投票决定成立海地帮派镇压部队：接下来怎么办？\n关键问题\n作者\nGeorges A. Fauriol\n和\nMary Speck\n— 2025年10月8日\n图片\n特朗普政府希望在海地设立联合国支持办公室——接下来怎么办？\n评论文章\n作者\nGeorges A. Fauriol\n— 2025年8月25日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-12-08T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-977","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美中贸易休战并未解决镓问题","title_en":"The U.S.-China Trade Truce Has Not Solved the Gallium Problem","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心评论指出，尽管2025年10月美中在釜山达成贸易休战，中国暂停了对美镓出口的推定拒绝禁令，但新证据显示中国对美镓出口并未实质性恢复。中国2025年未锻轧镓出口较2024年下降94%，对美出口自2023年以来几乎消失。全球镓价在2026年4月创下每公斤1850美元的历史新高，较2025年初上涨逾200%。文章分析可能原因包括官僚延迟、军民两用担忧、产业战略及维持谈判筹码，并指出美国短期内缺乏替代中国镓供应的可行方案。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary notes that despite the October 2025 Busan trade truce and China's suspension of the presumption-of-denial ban on gallium exports to the U.S., new evidence shows Chinese gallium exports have not meaningfully resumed. China's unwrought gallium exports fell 94 percent in 2025 from 2024, and exports to the U.S. have effectively disappeared since 2023. Global gallium prices hit a record $1,850 per kilogram in April 2026, up over 200 percent since early 2025. Possible explanations include bureaucratic delays, dual-use concerns, industrial strategy, and maintaining leverage. The U.S. has few short-term alternatives to Chinese gallium.","tag":"risk","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:23:18.748541+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/us-china-trade-truce-has-not-solved-gallium-problem","content":"The U.S.-China Trade Truce Has Not Solved the Gallium Problem\nPhoto: Andrew Harnik/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nAidan Powers-Riggs,\nBrian Hart,\nand\nMatthew P. Funaiole\nPublished May 11, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nPresident Donald Trump’s visit to China this week offers an opportunity to revisit trade negotiations on critical minerals. When President Trump met with Chinese leader Xi Jinping last October in Busan, South Korea, the two sides agreed to step back from an escalating economic dispute, including by pausing export restrictions affecting critical minerals such as rare earths and lesser-known critical minerals like gallium, germanium, and antimony.Despite the Busan “trade truce,” new evidence suggests that access to Chinese-origin gallium remains constrained for U.S. industry, creating challenges for critical defense and high-tech production lines.Caught in the CrossfireGallium is a little-known metal with outsized strategic and economic significance due to its importance to advanced technology supply chains. It was first caught up in escalating U.S.-China tensions in 2023, when Beijing launched its opening salvo of export controls targeting strategic minerals, including gallium and germanium.Those initial controls—which required exporters to obtain licenses and disclose end-use information about their clients—caused alarm due to China’s near-total dominance over the world’s supply of gallium. China produces a startling 99 percent of primary gallium globally, largely due to years of supportive industrial policies that pushed competitors out of the market.\nImage\nAidan Powers-Riggs\nAssociate Fellow for China Studies, iDeas Lab\nImage\nBrian Hart\nDeputy Director and Fellow, China Power Project\nImage\nMatthew P. Funaiole\nVice President, iDeas Lab, Andreas C. Dracopoulos Chair in Innovation and Senior Fellow, China Power Project\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nHidden Reach\nGeoeconomics and Trade\nOn China\nRemote Visualization\nWhile most export licenses were initially approved, China escalated its gallium controls further on December 3, 2024, when its Ministry of Commerce (MOFCOM) issued Document No. 46. This unprecedented rule explicitly named the United States as the target of an export ban on dual-use products, creating a presumption of denial for any exporters seeking to sell gallium to U.S. end users.After a year of broad economic escalation, Presidents Trump and Xi met in October 2025 on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) summit, where they notionally agreed to roll back or suspend many of the more extreme measures each had implemented in the preceding year, including on critical minerals. The official White House fact sheet outlining the terms of the deal explained that, among other actions: “China will issue general licenses valid for exports of rare earths, gallium, germanium, antimony, and graphite for the benefit of U.S. end users and their suppliers around the world. The general license means the de facto removal of controls China imposed since 2023” (emphasis added).China did not release a similarly detailed readout, but roughly a week later, MOFCOM issued Document No. 72, which suspended for one year the presumption-of-denial ban on gallium and other dual-use exports to the United States that it had introduced in December 2024. No mention was made of China’s earlier dual-use export licensing requirements first implemented in 2023, but the expectation appears to have been that license approvals would again be granted under normal procedures.No Relief YetDespite the November 2025 policy adjustment after Busan, there is growing evidence that Chinese exports of gallium to the United States have not resumed in a meaningful way, intensifying the shortage of the material for U.S. and global markets.One signal of the growing supply crunch is price behavior. Over the last year, gallium prices outside China have surged to unprecedented levels. Spot prices reported by Fastmarkets, a leading industry price tracker, reached a record high of $1,850 per kilogram in April 2026—an increase of over 200 percent since the start of 2025. Prices within China, meanwhile, have held steady, creating a clearly bifurcated market within and outside of China.\nRemote Visualization\nThis reflects a market with insufficient supply. Gallium is a niche metal, with less than a thousand metric tons consumed globally each year. Any major infusion of supply—as would have been expected if China lifted its export ban—should lessen the supply and demand mismatch and put downward pressure on prices. Instead, prices have continued to rise rapidly as supply tightens.Some of this increase in prices could be attributable to growing demand, especially if companies are hedging against future instability by stockpiling gallium. Yet, Chinese customs data offers additional evidence that China has not resumed significant exports of unwrought gallium. In 2025, China’s exports of unwrought gallium fell 94 percent from 2024 levels, and they have not yet recovered in 2026. Exports to the United States have effectively disappeared in Chinese customs data since 2023.\nRemote Visualization\nThere are discrepancies between Chinese and U.S. customs data, but the U.S. data shows a similar overall trend: Imports of unwrought gallium from China all but stopped after March 2025. Only a single shipment of roughly 3 kilograms arrived in July.While total U.S. imports of unwrought gallium jumped significantly in 2025, the sources shifted dramatically, with China’s share dropping below 5 percent, down from roughly 38 percent in 2022. It is possible that some Chinese-origin gallium is being transshipped through third countries, but China’s customs data suggests that very little unwrought gallium is leaving the country for any market.\nRemote Visualization\nThe remaining U.S. imports came primarily from Germany, Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Slovakia. These countries do not produce primary gallium, but they have facilities that recycle gallium from semiconductor manufacturing waste. The material reaching the United States from these countries is therefore likely derived from scrap recovery rather than new production, and thus ultimately relies on the same Chinese feedstock.\nRemote Visualization\nTrade data should be interpreted cautiously given gallium’s opaque and relatively small market. Customs and trade agencies around the world do not record gallium trade in uniform ways, making direct comparison difficult.Additionally, companies often move material between their affiliated facilities in different countries, inflating the appearance of cross-border trade. For example, Neo Performance Materials, one of the main suppliers serving North American customers, operates facilities in both Canada and the United States, and likely frequently transports material across the border between its facilities.There is also evidence of attempts to circumvent China’s restrictions. In July 2025, a U.S. company reported that Chinese suppliers were successfully shipping gallium by intentionally mislabeling it as other materials and routing it through third countries in order to evade export controls. Given the rapidly rising price of gallium in global markets, incentives for Chinese suppliers to smuggle their product will continue to grow.Chinese authorities have attempted to clamp down on these practices, but enforcement is difficult. Gallium is traded in small quantities and required only in modest volumes for many applications, which makes illicit shipments harder to detect.Even accounting for these complexities, the price surge and trade data both point to the same conclusion: Little Chinese gallium is reaching global markets.Public statements from global companies provide additional insight. AXT, a major producer of gallium arsenide semiconductor substrates with operations in both China and the United States, acknowledged in its Q4 2025 earnings report that while it “has generally received the required permits for exports to countries in Asia and Europe, our U.S. gallium arsenide customers are considered ‘dual use’ customers. . . . As such, no permits for the export of gallium arsenide to the U.S. have yet been approved.”Neo Performance Materials, the primary North American supplier, has reported similar pressures. In its Q4 2025 earnings release, the company stated that Chinese export restrictions continued to limit gallium availability and push prices higher. While Neo has the ability to recycle gallium scrap, the company noted that “despite strong demand, rising prices, and expanding margins, business growth is restrained by limited gallium scrap supply.”Taken together, these indicators suggest that China’s suspension of the presumption-of-denial rule has not translated into a meaningful increase in exports. Some gallium continues to circulate through recycling and indirect channels, but the total volume available to global markets has fallen sharply.Possible ExplanationsWithout direct visibility into the policy process in Beijing, it is impossible to determine the precise reason for the continued delay in gallium export licensing. Still, it is worth considering several plausible explanations:Bureaucratic delay: The simplest explanation is that MOFCOM is struggling to keep up with a massive licensing backlog likely piling up since 2025. China’s export control system is relatively new and may lack the administrative capacity to manage a surge of applications, especially with more stringent requirements for verifying extensive details about end users. However, the longer export licenses remain in limbo, the less convincing this explanation becomes.Dual-use concerns: Beijing may have genuine concerns about the military end uses of gallium. Gallium compounds are widely used in defense electronics, particularly in radar systems and advanced communications equipment. Chinese authorities may be reluctant to approve exports that could ultimately support U.S. military capabilities, even if the formal restriction has been suspended.Industrial strategy: Limiting global gallium supply could provide an advantage to Chinese firms developing technologies based on emerging gallium compounds such as gallium nitride (GaN) or gallium oxide. These materials are increasingly important for power electronics and high-performance semiconductors, which drive strategic industries like AI, robotics, and aerospace. By restricting exports of raw materials, China could tilt the competitive landscape in favor of its domestic manufacturers.Maintaining strategic leverage: A final explanation is that Beijing intends to maintain strategic leverage in its negotiations with the Trump administration. Keeping tight control over gallium exports gives Beijing a low-cost source of bargaining power. Even without an explicit ban, the ability to restrict supply creates uncertainty that can influence diplomatic discussions and potentially help China extract concessions on technology export controls or other strategic issues. Containing the FalloutWhile policy attention in Washington has understandably focused on acute shortages of more high-profile minerals such as rare earths and tungsten, gallium should not be ignored. Gallium plays a critical role in several emerging technologies that are central to U.S. economic and national security interests. GaN semiconductors are widely used in power electronics for data centers, electric vehicles, and renewable energy systems. They also enable high-performance radar and electronic warfare systems used by modern militaries.Demand for these technologies is expected to increase sharply in the coming decade. Planned investments in missile defense and advanced radar systems, such as the Golden Dome homeland defense system proposed by President Trump, will require substantial quantities of gallium to produce.Ongoing conflicts are creating an even more urgent need to replace equipment made with gallium. Experts estimate that a single Terminal High-Altitude Area Defense (THAAD) missile defense battery, for example, requires roughly 77 kilograms of gallium. Public reporting suggests that Iranian strikes have damaged or destroyed several of these systems at military bases across the Middle East in its war with the United States and Israel in early 2026, meaning they will need to be replaced.Unfortunately, the United States has few good options in the short term. Despite rapidly rising demand from strategic sectors, there is simply no immediate alternative to China for gallium. Under the Trump administration, the Pentagon and other agencies have committed millions in new investments in both domestic and overseas gallium production facilities to create stable substitute suppliers. However, these projects are still nascent and will likely not reach full operational capacity before mid-2027, at best. In the meantime, recycling can help offset some shortages, but recoverable gallium is limited to recycled feedstock from pre-consumer scrap—and is ultimately still reliant on Chinese-origin supply.Stockpiling is another potential response, but it creates its own challenges. The United States has launched Project Vault, an ambitious $12 billion effort to create a stockpile for strategic minerals. However, the utility of stockpiling is diminished once a shortage has already materialized. Large new purchases by government buyers in an already tight market could push prices even higher and worsen the shortage.Diplomacy can play a role in the immediate term. President Trump’s visit to China this week provides an opportunity to raise these issues. During the visit and in future U.S.-China trade talks, U.S. negotiators will need to keep critical minerals, including gallium, on the agenda and work to ensure that China abides by its commitments.Aidan Powers-Riggs is an associate fellow with the iDeas Lab at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Brian Hart is deputy director and fellow of the China Power Project at CSIS. Matthew P. Funaiole is vice president of the iDeas Lab, Andreas C. Dracopoulos Chair in Innovation, and senior fellow in the China Power Project at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nChina, and\nCritical Minerals\nRelated Content\nImage\nBeyond Rare Earths: China’s Growing Threat to Gallium Supply Chains\nBrief\nby\nCSIS Scholars\n— July 17, 2025\nImage\nMineral Monopoly: China’s Control over Gallium Is a National Security Threat\nDigital Feature\nby\nMatthew P. Funaiole,\nBrian Hart,\nand\nAidan Powers-Riggs\n— July 18, 2023\nImage\nDe-risking Gallium Supply Chains: The National Security Case for Eroding China’s Critical Mineral Dominance\nReport\nby\nMatthew P. Funaiole,\nBrian Hart,\nand\nAidan Powers-Riggs\n— August 3, 2023","content_cn":"美中贸易休战尚未解决镓问题\n照片：Andrew Harnik/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nAidan Powers-Riggs、\nBrian Hart，\n以及\nMatthew P. Funaiole\n发表于2026年5月11日\n远程可视化\n\n美国总统唐纳德·特朗普本周访问中国，为重新审视关键矿产贸易谈判提供了机会。去年10月，特朗普总统在韩国釜山会见中国领导人习近平时，双方同意缓和不断升级的经济争端，其中包括暂停影响稀土等关键矿产以及镓、锗、锑等较不为人知的关键矿产的出口限制。尽管达成了釜山“贸易休战”，但新证据表明，对于美国工业而言，获取中国来源的镓仍然受到限制，给关键国防和高科技生产线带来了挑战。\n\n夹缝中的镓\n\n镓是一种鲜为人知的金属，但由于其对先进技术供应链的重要性，具有超乎寻常的战略和经济意义。2023年，随着中美紧张局势不断升级，镓首次被卷入其中；当时，北京发起了针对包括镓和锗在内的战略矿产的首轮出口管制。这些最初的管制要求出口商获取许可证，并披露客户的最终用途信息；由于中国几乎完全主导全球镓供应，这些措施引发了警报。中国生产全球初级镓的99%，这在很大程度上源于多年来支持性的产业政策，这些政策将竞争者挤出了市场。\n\n图片\nAidan Powers-Riggs\n中国研究副研究员，iDeas Lab\n图片\nBrian Hart\n中国实力项目副主任兼研究员\n图片\nMatthew P. Funaiole\niDeas Lab副总裁、Andreas C. Dracopoulos创新主席兼中国实力项目高级研究员\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\nHidden Reach\n地缘经济学与贸易\n关于中国\n远程可视化\n\n尽管大多数出口许可证最初都获批，但2024年12月3日，中国进一步升级了镓管制；当天，中国商务部（MOFCOM）发布第46号文件。这项前所未有的规定明确将美国列为两用产品出口禁令的目标，并规定任何试图向美国最终用户销售镓的出口商，原则上都将被拒绝。经过一年广泛的经济升级后，特朗普总统和习近平于2025年10月在亚太经济合作组织（APEC）峰会期间会面；双方原则上同意撤回或暂停此前一年各自实施的许多更严厉措施，其中包括针对关键矿产的措施。白宫发布的官方事实说明概述了协议条款，其中解释说：“中国将为稀土、镓、锗、锑和石墨的出口签发对美国最终用户及其全球供应商有效的一般许可证。一般许可证意味着事实上取消中国自2023年以来实施的管制”（强调为原文所加）。中国没有发布同样详细的说明，但大约一周后，商务部发布第72号文件，将其于2024年12月实施的、针对向美国出口镓及其他两用产品的原则上拒绝禁令暂停一年。文件没有提及中国早在2023年首次实施的两用出口许可要求，但当时的预期似乎是，许可证\n批准将再次按照正常程序授予。尚无缓解迹象尽管2025年11月布桑之后政策有所调整，但越来越多的证据表明，中国对美国的镓出口并未以有意义的规模恢复，加剧了美国和全球市场的材料短缺。\n供应紧张程度不断加剧的一个信号是价格走势。过去一年，中国以外地区的镓价格飙升至前所未有的水平。领先的行业价格追踪机构Fastmarkets报告称，现货价格在2026年4月达到每公斤1,850美元的历史高位——较2025年初上涨超过200%。与此同时，中国境内价格保持稳定，形成了中国境内外明显分化的市场。\n远程可视化\n这反映出市场供应不足。镓是一种利基金属，全球每年消费量不足1,000公吨。如果中国解除出口禁令，按预期应会有大量供应注入，从而缓解供需失衡并对价格形成下行压力。然而，随着供应趋紧，价格反而继续快速上涨。价格上涨的部分原因可能是需求增长，尤其是企业为应对未来不稳定局势而囤积镓，以进行风险对冲。然而，中国海关数据进一步表明，中国尚未恢复大量未锻轧镓出口。2025年，中国未锻轧镓出口量较2024年下降94%，2026年迄今仍未恢复。自2023年以来，中国海关数据中的对美出口实际上已经消失。\n远程可视化\n中国和美国海关数据之间存在差异，但美国数据也显示出类似的总体趋势：2025年3月之后，从中国进口的未锻轧镓几乎完全停止。7月仅有一批约3公斤的货物抵达。尽管美国2025年未锻轧镓进口总量大幅增加，但来源发生了显著变化：中国所占份额降至5%以下，低于2022年约38%的水平。部分中国产镓可能经由第三国转运，但中国海关数据表明，离开中国、流向任何市场的未锻轧镓非常少。\n远程可视化\n美国其余进口主要来自德国、日本、中国台湾、韩国和斯洛伐克。这些国家并不生产原生镓，但拥有从半导体制造废料中回收镓的设施。因此，从这些国家进入美国的材料很可能来自废料回收，而非新生产，最终仍依赖同一批中国原料。\n远程可视化\n鉴于镓市场不透明且规模相对较小，应谨慎解读贸易数据。世界各地的海关和贸易机构并未以统一方式记录镓贸易，这使直接比较变得困难。此外，公司经常在不同国家的关联设施之间转移材料，夸大跨境贸易的表象。例如，服务北美客户的主要供应商之一Neo Performance Materials在加拿大和美国均设有设施，很可能经常在其设施之间跨境运输材料。\n还有证据表明，有人试图规避中国的限制。2025年7月，一家美国\n该公司报告称，中国供应商通过故意将镓误标为其他材料，并经由第三国转运，以规避出口管制，从而成功将镓运出。鉴于全球市场上镓的价格正在迅速上涨，中国供应商走私其产品的动机将继续增强。\n\n中国当局曾试图遏制这些做法，但执法十分困难。镓以小批量交易，许多应用所需的用量也很少，这使得非法运输更难被发现。\n\n即使考虑到这些复杂因素，价格飙升和贸易数据仍指向同一个结论：中国镓进入全球市场的数量很少。\n\n全球企业的公开声明提供了更多线索。AXT是砷化镓半导体衬底的主要生产商，在中国和美国均设有业务。该公司在2025年第四季度财报中承认，尽管其“通常能够获得向亚洲和欧洲国家出口所需的许可证，但我们的美国砷化镓客户被视为‘两用’客户……因此，向美国出口砷化镓的许可证尚未获得批准。”\n\n北美主要供应商Neo Performance Materials也报告了类似压力。在2025年第四季度财报中，该公司表示，中国的出口限制继续限制镓的供应并推高价格。尽管Neo有能力回收镓废料，但该公司指出，“尽管需求强劲、价格上涨且利润率扩大，但由于镓废料供应有限，业务增长受到制约。”\n\n综合来看，这些指标表明，中国暂停“推定拒绝”规则并未转化为出口量的实质性增加。部分镓仍通过回收和间接渠道流通，但全球市场可获得的总量已大幅下降。\n\n可能的解释\n\n由于无法直接了解北京的政策制定过程，不可能确定镓出口许可持续延迟的确切原因。不过，以下几种合理解释值得考虑：\n\n官僚拖延：最简单的解释是，商务部正努力处理自2025年以来可能不断累积的大量许可证积压。中国的出口管制体系相对较新，可能缺乏管理申请激增的行政能力，尤其是在核实最终用户大量详细信息方面的要求更加严格的情况下。然而，出口许可证处于悬而未决状态的时间越长，这一解释就越缺乏说服力。\n\n两用担忧：北京可能确实担心镓的军事最终用途。镓化合物广泛用于国防电子领域，尤其是雷达系统和先进通信设备。即使正式限制已经暂停，中国当局也可能不愿批准那些最终可能支持美国军事能力的出口。\n\n产业战略：限制全球镓供应可能会使中国企业在开发基于氮化镓（GaN）或氧化镓等新兴镓化合物的技术方面获得优势。这些材料对于电力电子和高性能半导体日益重要，而后者推动着人工智能、机器人和航空航天等战略产业的发展。通过限制原材料出口，\n中国可能会使竞争格局向有利于其国内制造商的方向倾斜。\n\n维持战略杠杆：最后一种解释是，北京打算在与特朗普政府的谈判中保持战略杠杆。严格控制镓出口，为北京提供了一种低成本的议价手段。即使没有明确的禁令，限制供应的能力也会制造不确定性，从而影响外交讨论，并可能帮助中国在技术出口管制或其他战略问题上争取让步。\n\n遏制影响\n\n华盛顿的政策关注理所当然地集中在稀土和钨等更受关注的矿产的严重短缺上，但不应忽视镓。镓在若干新兴技术中发挥着关键作用，而这些技术对美国的经济和国家安全利益至关重要。氮化镓半导体广泛用于数据中心、电动汽车和可再生能源系统中的电力电子设备。它们还可用于现代军队使用的高性能雷达和电子战系统。\n\n预计未来十年对这些技术的需求将大幅增加。特朗普总统提出的“金色穹顶”国土防御系统等导弹防御和先进雷达系统的计划投资，将需要大量镓来制造。\n\n持续不断的冲突正造成更加紧迫的需求，即替换使用镓制造的装备。例如，专家估计，一套末段高空区域防御（THAAD）导弹防御系统大约需要77千克镓。公开报道显示，在2026年初美国和以色列与伊朗的战争中，伊朗的袭击损坏或摧毁了中东各地军事基地中的数套此类系统，这意味着它们将需要被替换。\n\n遗憾的是，美国短期内几乎没有好的选择。尽管战略部门的需求正在迅速上升，但眼下镓没有中国之外的即时替代来源。在特朗普政府领导下，五角大楼及其他机构已承诺投入数百万美元，用于国内外镓生产设施的新投资，以打造稳定的替代供应商。然而，这些项目仍处于起步阶段，最早可能也要到2027年年中才能达到全部运营能力。在此期间，回收可以帮助缓解部分短缺，但可回收镓仅限于来自消费前废料的再生原料，而且最终仍依赖中国来源的供应。\n\n囤积库存是另一种潜在应对措施，但也会带来自身的挑战。美国已启动“金库计划”（Project Vault），这是一项耗资120亿美元、旨在建立战略矿产储备的雄心勃勃的努力。然而，一旦短缺已经出现，囤积库存的效用就会降低。在本已供应紧张的市场中，政府买家的大规模新采购可能会进一步推高价格，加剧短缺。\n\n外交可以在短期内发挥作用。特朗普总统本周访问中国，为提出这些问题提供了机会。在访问期间以及未来的美中贸易谈判中，美国谈判人员需要将包括镓在内的关键矿产列入议程，并努力确保中国履行其承诺。\n\n艾丹·鲍尔斯-里格斯是华盛顿战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）iDeas实验室的副研究员。\nBrian Hart 是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）中国力量项目的副主任兼研究员。Matthew P. Funaiole 是 iDeas 实验室副总裁、Andreas C. Dracopoulos 创新主席，以及 CSIS 中国力量项目的高级研究员。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS 是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且不涉及专有信息。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n中国，以及\n关键矿产\n相关内容\n图片\n超越稀土：中国对镓供应链日益加剧的威胁\n简报\n作者\nCSIS 学者\n— 2025 年 7 月 17 日\n图片\n矿产垄断：中国对镓的控制构成国家安全威胁\n数字专题\n作者\nMatthew P. Funaiole、\nBrian Hart，\n以及\nAidan Powers-Riggs\n— 2023 年 7 月 18 日\n图片\n降低镓供应链风险：削弱中国关键矿产主导地位的国家安全理由\n报告\n作者\nMatthew P. Funaiole、\nBrian Hart，\n以及\nAidan Powers-Riggs\n— 2023 年 8 月 3 日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-05-11T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3247","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"Victor Cha在《财富》杂志撰文：韩国经济似乎正在摆脱尹锡悦弹劾危机，别被表象迷惑","title_en":"Victor Cha in Fortune: Korea’s economy looks like it’s shrugging off Yoon’s impeachment crisis. Don’t be fooled","summary_cn":"CSIS的Victor Cha就韩国政治危机发表评论，标题指出韩国经济看似正在摆脱尹锡悦弹劾危机，但不应被这一表象迷惑。","summary_en":"CSIS's Victor Cha comments on South Korea's political crisis; the title argues that the economy may appear to be shrugging off Yoon's impeachment crisis, but this appearance should not be trusted.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:07:42.412397+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/victor-cha-fortune-koreas-economy-looks-its-shrugging-yoons-impeachment-crisis-dont-be","content":"Victor Cha in Fortune: Korea’s economy looks like it’s shrugging off Yoon’s impeachment crisis. Don’t be fooled\nImage\nPhoto: Chung Sung-Jun/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nVictor Cha\nPublished January 17, 2025\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nImage\nVictor Cha\nPresident, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Korea Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nKorea Chair\nSouth Korea's Political Crisis","content_cn":"Victor Cha 在《财富》杂志撰文：韩国经济似乎正在摆脱尹锡悦弹劾危机。别被迷惑\n图片\n照片：Chung Sung-Jun/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nVictor Cha\n发布于 2025年1月17日\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策议题的私人免税机构。其研究不偏袒任何党派，且不具专属性。CSIS 不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论应被理解为仅代表作者本人。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n图片\nVictor Cha\n地缘政治与外交政策部主任兼韩国讲席\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n韩国讲席\n韩国的政治危机","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-01-17T12:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3335","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"欧盟与南方共同市场自由贸易协定的影响是什么？","title_en":"What Are the Implications of the EU–Mercosur Free Trade Agreement?","summary_cn":"文章以问答形式分析欧盟与南方共同市场于2024年12月签署的自由贸易协定。协定若获批将覆盖近8亿人口，削减超过90%双边贸易关税，并涉及农业、政府采购、关键原材料和环保承诺。文章还讨论法国等国反对、批准程序复杂以及该协定在保护主义上升背景下的战略意义。","summary_en":"This Q&A analyzes the EU–Mercosur free trade agreement signed in December 2024. If ratified, it would cover nearly 800 million people, eliminate tariffs on over 90 percent of bilateral trade, and address agriculture, procurement, critical raw materials, and environmental commitments. It also discusses French-led opposition, complex ratification procedures, and the deal's strategic significance amid rising protectionism.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:12:45.866191+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/what-are-implications-eu-mercosur-free-trade-agreement","content":"What Are the Implications of the EU–Mercosur Free Trade Agreement?\nPhoto: EITAN ABRAMOVICH/AFP via Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nFederico Steinberg\nPublished December 6, 2024\nAfter 25 years of negotiations, the European Union and the Southern Common Market, commonly known as Mercosur and comprising Brazil, Argentina, Uruguay, and Paraguay, signed a free trade and cooperation agreement. If ratified, it will create a market of nearly 800 million citizens, reduce consumer prices, and boost investment. This landmark deal also signals that two like-minded continents can still champion rules-based trade liberalization amid rising protectionism, de-globalization, and geopolitical fragmentation.Q1: What has been agreed?A1: Negotiations between the European Union and Mercosur—two of the world’s largest trade blocs—began in 1999. In 2019, the European Commission reached a preliminary agreement with Mercosur countries. At its core, the deal reduced tariffs on manufactured goods in Mercosur countries while liberalizing agricultural trade in the European Union—a sector long protected by European policies.However, the agreement stalled for years due to opposition from France and other EU countries, who demanded environmental guarantees from Brazil and expressed concerns over the potential losses to European farmers. Simultaneously, the protectionist governments of Brazil and Argentina did not prioritize ratification.On December 6, 2024, a new agreement was signed in Montevideo. This landmark deal is the largest ever concluded by the EU and the only one Mercosur has with a major trading bloc—which means that European products will enter its market under much better conditions than U.S. or Japanese products. It eliminates tariffs on over 90 percent of bilateral trade, saving European exporters EUR 4 billion annually while granting South American products preferential access to European markets, particularly for agricultural goods where Mercosur holds a strong comparative advantage.Until now, trade relations between the two regions have underperformed relative to their potential, especially in merchandise trade (and less so in investment). The agreement is set to change this dynamic, opening Mercosur’s highly protected market to European industrial goods. For instance, previous tariffs on automobiles, textiles, and machinery ranged from 14 percent to 35 percent. The agreement also ensures the protection of 357 European geographical indicators, boosting exports of specialty agricultural products like wines and cheeses.Additionally, European companies will gain better access to Mercosur’s public procurement markets, high-value service sectors, and critical raw materials like lithium. In return, the European Union will reduce tariffs on agricultural products and other goods and contribute EUR 1.8 billion through the Global Gateway initiative to support Mercosur’s green and digital transition.Beyond the economic aspects, the deal includes commitments to high labor standards and reinforces adherence to the Paris Climate Agreement. Measures to prevent deforestation—a key demand of the European Union—are also part of the agreement.Overall, the deal seeks to increase trade and investment by creating a predictable economic environment, reducing tariff and non-tariff barriers, and fostering growth and prosperity, particularly for small and medium-sized enterprises.Q2: Why was the agreement possible?A2: After 25 years of stalled negotiations, many doubted the prospects of an EU–Mercosur partnership. Resistance from European farmers, especially in France, Poland, and Ireland, played a major role. These groups feared fierce competition from more competitive Latin American agricultural producers. Proponents, including Spain, Portugal, Germany, and some Nordic countries, struggled to counter this opposition.However, three elements have made the agreement possible. First, the rise of protectionism, exemplified by Donald Trump's reelection, pushed the European Union and Mercosur to act. Both blocs, as advocates of an open, rules-based economic order, saw the agreement as a way to reaffirm their commitment to rules-based free trade. Second, both Brazil’s President Lula da Silva and Argentina’s President Javier Milei have strongly supported the agreement, while Uruguay has always been in favor of a small export-oriented economy. And third, there were important strategic considerations on the EU side, whose leaders were concerned about the rapid expansion of Chinese trade and investment in Latin America. Moreover, the new European Commission, which took office on December 1, wanted to start its mandate by delivering quick results.Q3: What are the next steps?A3: The ratification process poses significant challenges, particularly on the European side. In Mercosur, the agreement must be approved by the national parliaments, but even if some countries fail to ratify it, the deal will still apply to others that do.In the European Union, however, the process is more complex. After the agreement is translated into all EU member state languages, it will go to the European Council for ratification, where EU countries are represented by their trade ministers. A minimum of four states representing at least 35 percent of the EU population could block the agreement. France, Austria, and Poland have stated that they oppose the agreement, but they would need another large country to reject the agreement at the European Council. Italy could join them, but it is also possible that in the next months, the guarantees that the European Commission is offering European farmers would be enough to convince the more skeptical countries. If the agreement is not blocked, it then must be ratified by the European Parliament. This approval only applies to the ratification of those provisions that fall within the exclusive competence of the European Parliament, mainly those related to trade liberalization, and do not require ratification by national parliaments. The rest, those provisions involving aspects of jurisdiction and sovereignty, such as dispute settlement mechanisms or investment protection, must be unanimously approved by national parliaments to enter into force. The provisions of the agreement related to political dialogue and cooperation also require unanimous approval by national parliaments.Therefore, even if the European ratification process runs smoothly, the tariff reductions will take some months to be applied, and the rest of the elements could take more than a year.But in any case, the signing of this partnership agreement demonstrates the strong interest in trade liberalization among countries that favor maintaining an open economic order based on stable and predictable rules in the context of threats to cooperation and potential trade wars.Federico Steinberg is visiting fellow with the Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nEurope,\nAmericas, and\nTrade and International Business\nImage\nFederico Steinberg\nVisiting Fellow, Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nEurope, Russia, and Eurasia Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"欧盟—南方共同市场自由贸易协定有何影响？\n照片：EITAN ABRAMOVICH/法新社，来自盖蒂图片社\n关键问题\n作者\nFederico Steinberg\n发表于2024年12月6日\n经过25年的谈判，欧洲联盟与南方共同市场（通常称为南共市，由巴西、阿根廷、乌拉圭和巴拉圭组成）签署了一项自由贸易与合作协定。如果获得批准，该协定将创建一个拥有近8亿公民的市场，降低消费者价格并提振投资。这项具有里程碑意义的协议还表明，在保护主义、去全球化和地缘政治碎片化加剧之际，两个志同道合的大陆仍能倡导以规则为基础的贸易自由化。\nQ1：达成了什么协议？A1：欧洲联盟与南方共同市场——世界上最大的两个贸易集团——之间的谈判始于1999年。2019年，欧盟委员会与南共市国家达成了初步协议。该协议的核心是降低南共市国家制造业产品的关税，同时放宽欧洲联盟的农产品贸易限制——这一领域长期受到欧洲政策的保护。\n然而，由于法国及其他欧盟国家的反对，该协议多年陷入停滞；这些国家要求巴西提供环境保障，并担心欧洲农民可能遭受损失。与此同时，巴西和阿根廷的保护主义政府并未将批准协议列为优先事项。\n2024年12月6日，一项新协议在蒙得维的亚签署。这项具有里程碑意义的协议是欧盟迄今达成的最大协议，也是南共市与一个主要贸易集团签署的唯一协议——这意味着欧洲产品进入南共市市场时，将享有远优于美国或日本产品的条件。该协议取消双边贸易中超过90%的关税，每年为欧洲出口商节省40亿欧元，同时给予南美产品进入欧洲市场的优惠准入，尤其是农产品；南共市在农产品方面拥有强大的比较优势。\n到目前为止，两个地区之间的贸易关系相对于其潜力一直表现不佳，尤其是在商品贸易方面（投资方面则相对较好）。该协议将改变这一态势，向欧洲工业品开放南共市高度保护的市场。例如，过去汽车、纺织品和机械的关税税率为14%至35%。该协议还确保保护357项欧洲地理标志，促进葡萄酒和奶酪等特色农产品的出口。\n此外，欧洲企业将更好地进入南共市的公共采购市场、高附加值服务部门以及锂等关键原材料市场。作为回报，欧洲联盟将降低农产品及其他商品的关税，并通过“全球门户”倡议提供18亿欧元，支持南共市的绿色和数字化转型。\n除经济方面外，该协议还包括遵守高标准劳工规范的承诺，并强化对《巴黎气候协定》的遵守。防止森林砍伐的措施——这是欧盟的一项关键要求——也被纳入协议。\n总体而言，该协议旨在通过营造可预测的经济环境、减少关税和非关税壁垒以及促进增长与繁荣来增加贸易和投资，尤其是为中小企业创造条件。\nQ2：为什么能够达成这项协议？A2：经过25年陷入停滞的谈判，许多人曾怀疑\n欧盟—南方共同市场伙伴关系的前景。欧洲农民，尤其是法国、波兰和爱尔兰的农民的抵制发挥了重要作用。这些群体担心会与更具竞争力的拉丁美洲农业生产者展开激烈竞争。包括西班牙、葡萄牙、德国和一些北欧国家在内的支持者难以抵消这种反对意见。然而，三个因素促成了协议的达成。第一，保护主义的兴起（唐纳德·特朗普再次当选就是例证）推动欧盟和南方共同市场采取行动。作为开放、基于规则的经济秩序的倡导者，两个集团都认为，该协议是重申其对基于规则的自由贸易承诺的一种方式。第二，巴西总统卢拉·达席尔瓦和阿根廷总统哈维尔·米莱都大力支持该协议，而乌拉圭一直支持这一面向出口的小型经济体。第三，欧盟方面还有重要的战略考量，其领导人担心中国贸易和投资在拉丁美洲迅速扩张。此外，新的欧盟委员会于12月1日就任，希望通过迅速取得成果来开启其任期。\n\nQ3：下一步是什么？\nA3：批准程序面临重大挑战，尤其是在欧洲方面。在南方共同市场，协议必须得到各国议会批准，但即使一些国家未能批准，协议仍将适用于批准协议的其他国家。\n\n然而，在欧盟，程序更为复杂。协议翻译成所有欧盟成员国的语言后，将提交欧洲理事会批准，欧盟各国由其贸易部长代表。至少四个国家、且代表欧盟人口至少35%的国家可以阻止该协议。法国、奥地利和波兰已表示反对该协议，但它们还需要另一个大国在欧洲理事会上拒绝该协议。意大利可能加入它们，但也有可能在未来几个月内，欧盟委员会向欧洲农民提供的保障足以说服那些较为怀疑的国家。如果协议未被阻止，随后还必须由欧洲议会批准。这一批准仅适用于那些属于欧洲议会专属权限范围的条款的批准，主要是与贸易自由化有关的条款；这些条款不需要各国议会批准。其余条款，即涉及管辖权和主权等方面的条款，例如争端解决机制或投资保护，必须得到各国议会的一致批准后才能生效。协议中有关政治对话与合作的条款也需要各国议会的一致批准。\n\n因此，即使欧洲批准程序顺利进行，关税削减也需要几个月时间才能实施，其余要素可能需要一年多时间。\n\n但无论如何，这项伙伴关系协议的签署表明，在合作受到威胁、贸易战可能发生的背景下，那些支持维护基于稳定且可预测规则的开放经济秩序的国家，对贸易自由化抱有浓厚兴趣。\n\n费德里科·斯坦伯格是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚项目的访问研究员。\n\n批判性\n《Questions》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n欧洲、\n美洲，以及\n贸易与国际商业\n图片\nFederico Steinberg\n欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚项目访问研究员\n项目与计划\n欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚项目\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-06T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1978","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国国际开发金融公司重新授权对能源意味着什么","title_en":"What Does the Development Finance Corporation Reauthorization Mean for Energy?","summary_cn":"文章分析FY2026国防授权法案中美国国际开发金融公司（DFC）重新授权至2031年的条款，包括将或有负债上限从600亿提高至2050亿美元、设立50亿美元股权循环基金及机构改革。作者认为这些变化有利于核能等资本密集型能源投资，但股权评分、人才流失和跨部门协调等实施挑战仍存。","summary_en":"This CSIS analysis examines the FY 2026 NDAA provisions reauthorizing the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation through 2031, including raising its contingent liability ceiling from $60 billion to $205 billion and creating a $5 billion equity revolving fund. It argues these changes benefit capital-intensive energy investments, while implementation challenges around equity scoring, staffing, and interagency coordination remain.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:45:29.353603+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/what-does-development-finance-corporation-reauthorization-mean-energy","content":"What Does the Development Finance Corporation Reauthorization Mean for Energy?\nPhoto: Daniel Heuer/Bloomberg via Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nRay Cai\nPublished December 12, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nAfter months of negotiations, congressional lawmakers have released the final draft of the FY 2026 National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA), which includes language to reauthorize the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation (DFC) through 2031. Congress established the DFC through the bipartisan BUILD Act under the first Trump administration; since then, the DFC has taken on a distinctive role in the United States’ evolving international development and strategic engagement agenda—including facilitating nearly $10 billion in energy-related investments worldwide. Yet policymakers and practitioners broadly agree that the DFC can be improved to better keep pace with rapidly evolving global challenges. With the NDAA expected to soon pass with minimal changes, this analysis examines key elements of the reauthorization and their implications for U.S. energy policy at large.Q1: Does the reauthorization expand the DFC’s capacity?A1: The reauthorization strengthens the DFC’s balance sheet and operational capacity to engage across a wider range of projects and markets. By raising the agency’s contingent liability ceiling from $60 billion to $205 billion, Congress provides the DFC greater room for larger and more complex transactions. This expansion aligns with the White House’s FY 2026 budget request, which proposes $3.8 billion in discretionary funding for the agency—a 280 percent increase from FY 2025—and reflects bipartisan support for a more robust and ambitious DFC.The NDAA also authorizes a $5 billion equity revolving fund at the Department of the Treasury, which provides a dedicated capital stream for equity investment—an especially important tool in markets with inadequate access to debt financing. The fund is intended to address the budget scoring constraint, which has hampered DFC’s equity program since its inception by writing off its investments as loss-making grant expenditures. Additional authorities—such as the potential for subordinate debt, up to 100 percent loan guarantees, and more latitude to engage state-owned enterprises and sovereign wealth funds—all broaden the agency’s ability to tailor its approach to market needs.Aside from financial enhancements, the reauthorization also introduces institutional upgrades intended to improve the DFC’s ability to execute deals. Expanded hiring authorities, higher compensation bands, and new training and internship pathways—including a dedicated program at the Foreign Service Institute—could help grow the talent pool and attract industry expertise that has been difficult to source. By tasking the chief risk officer to develop an agency-wide risk framework, Congress also signals intent for the DFC to adopt a more strategic and transparent approach to risk by moving beyond narrow, deal-by-deal assessments toward holistic, portfolio-level decisionmaking.These reforms could be particularly consequential for capital-intensive sectors like energy. Energy investments—especially nuclear and other major infrastructure projects—often require large, blended financing packages and a tolerance for long construction timelines and complex political and regulatory environments. A more capable and risk-tolerant DFC will be better positioned to work across these value chains and throughout the project life cycle.Q2: How does the reauthorization address the DFC’s strategic role?A2: The reauthorization introduces new mechanisms to better align the DFC with U.S. strategic priorities. The establishment of a chief strategic officer reflects congressional interest in ensuring that the agency’s investments are coherent with U.S. foreign policy, economic competitiveness, and national security goals. In addition, the requirement for the DFC CEO to produce a five-year Strategic Priorities Plan—based on guidance from a new advisory council that includes members of key congressional committees—adds a formal process to orient the agency’s work toward priority regions, sectors, and partnerships.Energy value chains lie at the intersection of strategic priorities ranging from developing export markets for U.S. products to building climate resilience. A more strategically guided DFC could be better positioned to support energy investments that advance multiple objectives at once, whether by strengthening supply chains for key technologies, supporting the deployment of emerging energy systems, or enabling projects that reduce dependence on adversarial suppliers. As such, energy fits naturally within this broader strategic push and aligns closely with the priorities recommended by Congress for the two-year period beginning October 1, 2025: securing supply chains for critical minerals, supporting telecommunications investments, and establishing regional offices abroad.Q3: Can the DFC retain its development focus?A3: The NDAA generally maintains the DFC’s core development mandate while allowing targeted flexibility where strategic needs require it. It instructs the DFC to continue prioritizing less-developed countries, while permitting operations in advancing-income and high-income countries with CEO certification and congressional reporting. Restrictions on working in wealthier markets—including a 25 percent cap on the share of total project cost the DFC can support, a 10 percent ceiling on any high-income country within the agency’s overall exposure, and ethics oversight rules—could serve as guardrails as the agency navigates an expanding strategic remit. At the same time, the reauthorization preserves and expands key institutional features that anchor the DFC’s development role. The chief development officer role remains in place, as do the Impact Quotient evaluation system, reporting and transparency requirements, and language reaffirming DFC’s international focus. A new Development Finance Advisory Council could further institutionalize civil-society and development-sector input to ensure that the agency’s activities remain grounded in development impact even as strategic pressures grow.Recognizing that developmental and strategic objectives can reinforce one another is crucial for advancing energy priorities. Many emerging markets face structural barriers ranging from sovereign and currency risks, shallow capital markets, to gaps in physical infrastructure, institutional capacity, and human capital. The preserved development mandate enables the DFC to continue addressing these constraints and creating enabling conditions for productive investment that help increase energy access and market development in developing economies, a stated priority of the administration. It could also help the DFC to partially assume some, but not all, functions traditionally provided by the U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID) including technical assistance and in-country presence. At the same time, measured flexibility to operate in higher-income markets allows the agency to support select projects or sectors that are strategically important but geographically concentrated, which include energy and critical minerals.Q4: What questions remain as the DFC moves into implementation?A4: Even with significant new authorities, important uncertainties remain that will determine how transformative the reauthorization ultimately proves. The equity scoring issue, for instance, is only partially addressed. While the revolving fund could provide a workable bridge solution, its catalytic potential depends on self-sustaining investment returns that could take years to realize. Experts argue that deeper reform of the Federal Credit Reform Act is still necessary to make the DFC’s equity tools comparable to those of peer institutions; such reform would require additional legislative action, which may prove difficult to secure. Similar questions apply to the DFC’s ability to build the talent and on-the-ground presence at the necessary scale; while expanded hiring authorities and new recruitment pathways are promising, the DFC will be recovering from a recent reduction in force that cut its staff from about 700 down to 500.Uncertainty also persists around whether the DFC will be able to meaningfully increase its risk tolerance, source deals, or deploy a broader set of financial instruments—ranging from guarantees to political risk insurance—tailored to varied market conditions. Past experience suggests that internal restraint has often constrained the agency’s ambition more than statutory limits; whether the new risk framework translates into better dealmaking will only become clear as more transactions move forward. Likewise, operational reforms—such as reducing procedural hurdles, improving reporting and Congressional notification (the threshold for which was moderately increased from $10 to $20 million) protocols, and streamlining origination processes—are still needed for the DFC to move efficiently enough to compete with its more agile peers, including Chinese policy banks; these improvements are also necessary for the DFC to partner more effectively with commercial, sovereign, or multilateral financial institutions. To this end, it is also crucial that well-intentioned safeguards do not inadvertently create additional bureaucratic burden.Interagency coordination remains another critical challenge. Developing robust energy and infrastructure project pipelines often depends on complementary capabilities across institutions, including the Department of Energy, U.S. Export-Import Bank, the U.S. Trade and Development Agency, as well as collaboration with the private sector, civil society, other development banks, and partner-country counterparts. Without a more structured, whole-of-government approach that aligns commercial diplomacy, technical assistance, and strategic finance tools, the DFC alone will not be able to support turnkey project development as envisioned. These coordination issues also intersect with broader directional questions, most notably whether the DFC can allocate its now expanded resources in a way that veritably serves both its developmental and strategic mandates. In many ways, the renewed DFC is better-equipped but faces heightened expectations—particularly as the retrenchment of USAID and other development and finance authorities leaves growing gaps in the U.S. international energy engagement apparatus, which the agency may increasingly be called upon to help fill.Ray Cai is an associate fellow in the Energy Security and Climate Change Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.The author would like to thank the experts who shared their insights in CSIS interviews and convenings related to this topic.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nClimate Change,\nEnergy and Sustainability,\nEnergy and Geopolitics,\nGeopolitics and International Security,\nGlobal Markets,\nEconomic Security,\nStrategic Capital,\nTechnology, and\nTrade and International Business\nImage\nRay Cai\nAssociate Fellow, Energy Security and Climate Change Program\nPrograms & Projects\nEnergy Security and Climate Change Program\nEconomic Security and Technology\nRelated Content\nImage\nNavigating a New Energy Investment Paradigm\nReport\nby\nRay Cai\n— November 13, 2025\nImage\nRethinking U.S. International Energy Finance\nCommentary\nby\nRay Cai\n— June 3, 2025\nImage\nU.S. Low-Carbon Leadership\nBrief\nby\nMathias Zacarias,\nRay Cai,\nand\nJoseph Majkut\n— March 6, 2025","content_cn":"发展金融公司重新授权对能源意味着什么？\n图片：Daniel Heuer/Bloomberg via Getty Images\n关键问题\n作者\nRay Cai\n发表于2025年12月12日\n远程可视化\n经过数月谈判，国会议员公布了《2026财年国防授权法案》（NDAA）的最终草案，其中包含将美国国际开发金融公司（DFC）重新授权至2031年的条款。国会通过第一届特朗普政府时期两党支持的《BUILD法案》设立了DFC；此后，DFC在美国不断演变的国际发展与战略参与议程中承担了独特作用，包括推动全球近100亿美元的能源相关投资。然而，政策制定者和从业者普遍认为，DFC仍可加以改进，以更好地跟上迅速变化的全球挑战。鉴于NDAA预计很快将以最小改动获得通过，本文分析重新授权的关键要素及其对美国整体能源政策的影响。Q1：重新授权是否扩大了DFC的能力？A1：重新授权增强了DFC的资产负债表和运营能力，使其能够参与更广泛的项目和市场。国会将该机构的或有负债上限从600亿美元提高至2050亿美元，为DFC开展规模更大、复杂程度更高的交易提供了更大空间。这一扩展与白宫提出的2026财年预算请求相一致：该请求拟为该机构提供38亿美元的可自由支配资金，较2025财年增加280%，也反映出两党对建立更强大、更具雄心的DFC的支持。\nNDAA还授权财政部设立50亿美元的股权循环基金，为股权投资提供专门的资本来源——在难以获得债务融资的市场中，这尤其是一项重要工具。该基金旨在解决预算计分方面的限制；自成立以来，这一限制一直阻碍DFC的股权项目，因为其将投资注销为亏损性赠款支出。其他授权——例如开展次级债务、提供最高100%的贷款担保，以及更大程度地参与国有企业和主权财富基金——都拓宽了该机构根据市场需求调整其方法的能力。\n除金融方面的强化外，重新授权还引入了旨在提升DFC交易执行能力的机构性升级。扩大招聘权限、提高薪酬档位，以及新的培训和实习渠道——包括在外交学院设立专门项目——有助于扩大人才库，并吸引一直难以获得的行业专业知识。通过要求首席风险官制定全机构风险框架，国会还表明有意让DFC采取更具战略性和透明度的风险管理方式，从狭窄的逐笔交易评估转向全面的投资组合层面决策。\n这些改革可能对能源等资本密集型行业产生尤其重大的影响。能源投资——尤其是核能及其他大型基础设施项目——往往需要大规模的混合融资方案，并且要能够承受漫长的建设周期以及复杂的政治和监管环境。能力更强、风险容忍度更高的DFC将更有条件在这些价值链中开展工作，并贯穿整个\n项目生命周期。Q2：重新授权如何应对DFC的战略作用？A2：重新授权引入了新的机制，以更好地使DFC与美国战略重点保持一致。设立首席战略官反映出国会希望确保该机构的投资与美国外交政策、经济竞争力和国家安全目标相协调。此外，要求DFC首席执行官制定一份为期五年的《战略重点计划》——该计划依据由包括国会关键委员会成员在内的新咨询委员会提供的指导——为引导该机构的工作面向重点地区、部门和伙伴关系增添了正式流程。\n\n能源价值链处于多项战略重点的交汇处，这些重点涵盖为美国产品开发出口市场到增强气候韧性等领域。战略导向更明确的DFC或许能更好地支持同时推进多项目标的能源投资，无论是强化关键技术的供应链、支持新兴能源系统的部署，还是促成减少对对手供应商依赖的项目。因此，能源自然契合这一更广泛的战略推动，并与国会建议的、从2025年10月1日开始的两年期重点高度一致：保障关键矿产供应链、支持电信投资，以及在海外设立区域办事处。\n\nQ3：DFC能否保留其发展重点？A3：总体而言，《国防授权法案》维持了DFC的核心发展任务，同时在战略需求要求时允许有针对性的灵活性。该法案指示DFC继续优先关注欠发达国家，同时允许在中等收入和高收入国家开展业务，但需获得首席执行官认证并向国会报告。关于在较富裕市场开展工作的限制——包括DFC可支持的项目总成本份额上限为25%、任何高收入国家在该机构整体风险敞口中的比例上限为10%，以及道德监督规则——可在该机构应对不断扩大的战略职责范围时发挥护栏作用。与此同时，重新授权保留并扩展了巩固DFC发展作用的关键机构特征。首席发展官职位继续保留，影响商数评估体系、报告和透明度要求，以及重申DFC国际重点的措辞也同样保留。新的发展金融咨询委员会还可进一步将民间社会和发展部门的意见制度化，确保即使战略压力增加，该机构的活动仍然立足于发展影响。\n\n认识到发展目标和战略目标可以相互强化，对于推进能源重点至关重要。许多新兴市场面临各种结构性障碍，从主权风险和货币风险、资本市场不完善，到实体基础设施、机构能力和人力资本方面的缺口。保留的发展任务使DFC能够继续应对这些制约因素，并为生产性投资创造有利条件，从而帮助扩大​​发展中经济体的能源获取和市场发展，这也是本届政府明确提出的重点。它还可能帮助DFC部分承担美国国际开发署（USAID）传统上提供的一些（但并非全部）职能，包括技术\n援助和在当地的存在。与此同时，在较高收入市场开展业务的适度灵活性，使该机构能够支持一些在战略上重要但地理上集中的特定项目或部门，其中包括能源和关键矿产。\n\nQ4：随着DFC进入实施阶段，还有哪些问题尚待解决？\nA4：即使获得了重要的新授权，仍有一些关键不确定性，这些不确定性将决定此次重新授权最终能带来多大变革。例如，股权计分问题只得到了部分解决。虽然循环基金可以提供一种可行的过渡方案，但其催化潜力取决于能够自我维持的投资回报，而实现这些回报可能需要数年时间。专家认为，仍有必要对《联邦信贷改革法》进行更深入的改革，才能使DFC的股权工具与同类机构相当；而此类改革还需要立法行动，能否获得支持可能颇为困难。关于DFC能否以必要的规模建设人才队伍和当地存在，也存在类似疑问；虽然扩大招聘授权和新的招募渠道前景可观，但DFC刚经历了一轮裁员，其员工人数已从约700人削减至500人。\n\n围绕DFC能否实质性提高风险承受度、发掘交易，或部署更广泛的金融工具——从担保到政治风险保险——并根据不同市场状况进行调整，不确定性也依然存在。过去的经验表明，内部克制往往比法定限制更大程度地束缚了该机构的雄心；新的风险框架能否转化为更出色的交易能力，只有随着更多交易推进才能明确。同样，运营改革仍然必不可少，例如减少程序障碍、改进报告和国会通知（其门槛已从1000万美元适度提高至2000万美元）流程，以及简化项目发起流程，只有这样，DFC才能足够高效地与包括中国政策性银行在内、更为灵活的同类机构竞争；这些改进对于DFC更有效地与商业、主权或多边金融机构建立合作关系也同样必要。为此，确保善意的保障措施不会无意中造成额外的官僚负担也至关重要。\n\n机构间协调仍是另一项重大挑战。建设稳健的能源和基础设施项目储备，往往取决于各机构之间的互补能力，包括能源部、美国进出口银行、美国贸易与发展署，以及与私营部门、公民社会、其他开发银行和伙伴国家对口机构的合作。如果没有一种更加结构化的全政府方针，将商业外交、技术援助和战略金融工具协调起来，DFC单独将无法按照设想支持交钥匙项目开发。这些协调问题也与更广泛的方向性问题相互交织，其中最突出的是，DFC能否以真正同时服务于其发展和战略使命的方式，配置如今扩大的资源。在许多方面，焕新的DFC装备更为完善，但也面临更高的期待——尤其是在美国国际开发署及其他发展和金融机构的收缩留下日益扩大的美国……\n国际能源参与机制，而该机构可能日益被要求协助填补这一机制。Ray Cai 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心能源安全与气候变化项目的副研究员。作者感谢在与这一主题相关的 CSIS 访谈和会议中分享见解的专家。\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS 是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性，不涉及专有利益。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n气候变化，\n能源与可持续性，\n能源与地缘政治，\n地缘政治与国际安全，\n全球市场，\n经济安全，\n战略资本，\n技术，以及\n贸易与国际商务\n图片\nRay Cai\n能源安全与气候变化项目副研究员\n项目与计划\n能源安全与气候变化项目\n经济安全与技术\n相关内容\n图片\n探索新的能源投资范式\n报告\n作者\nRay Cai\n— 2025 年 11 月 13 日\n图片\n重新思考美国国际能源融资\n评论\n作者\nRay Cai\n— 2025 年 6 月 3 日\n图片\n美国的低碳领导力\n简报\n作者\nMathias Zacarias，\nRay Cai，\n以及\nJoseph Majkut\n— 2025 年 3 月 6 日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-12-12T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1738","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"特朗普政府新国防战略如何论及中国？","title_en":"What Does the Trump Administration’s New National Defense Strategy Say About China?","summary_cn":"2026年1月23日美国国防部发布新版《国防战略》非机密版本。文章分析该战略将本土防御和西半球列为首要优先、威慑中国列第二，措辞较2018年和2022年版明显缓和，未将中国称为战略竞争者，提出在第一岛链建立拒止防御，并寻求扩大与解放军的军事沟通。文章还讨论其对美中关系及特朗普访华的影响。","summary_en":"On January 23, 2026, the U.S. Department of Defense released the unclassified version of its new National Defense Strategy. This analysis notes that homeland defense and the Western Hemisphere rank first, deterring China second, and that the language on China is notably softer than the 2018 and 2022 strategies, avoiding the label of strategic competitor, calling for denial defense along the First Island Chain, and seeking wider military-to-military communications with the PLA.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:38:47.479777+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/what-does-trump-administrations-new-national-defense-strategy-say-about-china","content":"What Does the Trump Administration’s New National Defense Strategy Say About China?\nPhoto: Andrew Harnik/Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nBonny Lin\nand\nBrian Hart\nPublished January 26, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nOn January 23, the U.S. Department of Defense (DOD), recently renamed the Department of War, released the unclassified version of its long-awaited National Defense Strategy (NDS). In line with the broader National Security Strategy (NSS), which the Trump administration released in late 2025, the new NDS signals shifts in U.S. defense strategy and priorities compared to past administrations—including notable differences in how the document discusses the People’s Republic of China (PRC). Nevertheless, the NDS continues to emphasize the importance of deterring China in the Indo-Pacific.Q1: How important is China in the 2026 NDS?A1: The 2026 NDS identifies homeland defense and the Western Hemisphere as the priority region, and it lists deterring China in the Indo-Pacific as the second strategic regional priority. This is in line with the 2025 NSS, which significantly recast U.S. priorities by focusing on the U.S. Homeland and the Western Hemisphere as part of a “Trump Corollary” to the Monroe Doctrine.This is a marked shift from prior U.S. administrations, including the first Trump administration. The 2018 summary of the first Trump administration’s NDS identified long-term competition with China and Russia as the “principal priorities” for the department. Similarly, in assessing the global threat environment, the Biden administration’s 2022 NDS ranked strategic competition with China as the top challenge and Russia’s “acute” threats as the second major challenge to the global security environment.Although the new NDS places China as the second priority, it seeks to recast how U.S.-China relations are framed. Undersecretary of Defense for Policy Elbridge Colby explained in Seoul that the strategy document seeks to end “needless confrontation” and said that the United States “should strive for a stable, peaceful relationship with China.” The new NDS further adds “respectful relations with China” to this list.Accordingly, one of the first actionable items related to China that the strategy document lists is: “open a wider range of military-to-military communications with the People’s Liberation Army (PLA) with a focus on supporting strategic stability with Beijing as well as deconfliction and de-escalation.” If put into practice, this would reverse the trend of limiting contacts and exchanges between the two militaries that the first Trump administration pushed for out of concern that many of these engagements do not benefit the United States. It remains to be seen how wide an opening the DOD will allow and if such opportunities are restricted largely to senior, policy-level exchanges.The NDS also offers China reassurances that the U.S. goal “is not to dominate China; nor is it to strangle or humiliate them.” It adds that protecting U.S. interests “does not require regime change or existential struggle.” Although similar reassurances were part of the talking points that U.S. officials shared with Chinese interlocutors privately, they have not appeared in recent U.S. national security or defense strategies. Their inclusion in the unclassified 2026 NDS shows that the department is taking additional steps to signal the U.S. military’s strategic intentions. This stands in sharp contrast to developments near the end of the first Trump administration, when then Secretary of State Mike Pompeo advocated for the United States to induce change in China, suggesting regime change was desirable or a possibility.Q2: What does the NDS identify as the main threats or challenges from China?A2: The 2026 NDS is very careful in how it explicitly characterizes China. In a clear departure from the 2018 NDS and 2022 NDS, this NDS neither labels China a strategic competitor nor emphasizes long-term competition with China. Much of the language on China is neutral and value-free, describing China as “the second most powerful country in the world.” It does not, for example, list the range of China’s predatory, coercive, or intimidating activities in the Indo-Pacific and beyond that are well-documented in prior strategy documents. It does not directly mention Taiwan or any other potential flashpoint with China. Instead, the focus of the NDS is to establish “a strong denial of defense along the First Island Chain” and to prevent China from dominating the Indo-Pacific region.This does not mean, however, that the NDS is less focused on countering the Chinese military threat. The call for a denial of defense along the First Island Chain seeks to deny potential PRC aggression in most of the contested hotspots—in the Senkaku Islands, South China Sea, as well as the Taiwan Strait. This suggests continuing strong U.S. support to key allies and partners in the region, including Tokyo, Manila, and Taipei.Implicit in many other sections of the NDS are also threats from China that are not explicitly called out. In the document’s section on “homeland and hemisphere,” the direct military threats to the U.S. homeland—“nuclear threats as well as a variety of conventional strike and space, cyber, electromagnetic warfare capabilities”—likely refer to both Chinese and Russian military threats. The statement that “we have seen adversaries’ influence grow from Greenland in the Arctic to the Gulf of America, the Panama Canal, and locations farther south” likely refers to China and Russia.Similarly, in the section on “the simultaneity problem,” the Pentagon notes the possibility of “one or more potential opponents” acting together in a coordinated or opportunistic fashion and later mentions Europe, the Indo-Pacific, and South Korea. This suggests that the department is most worried about opportunistic or coordinated activities between China, Russia, and North Korea.In terms of increasing burden sharing with allies and partners, the NDS calls for more burden sharing in most regions of the world, except for the Indo-Pacific. Within this section, the NDS states that “as the Department rightly prioritizes Homeland defense and deterring China, other threats will persist,” and U.S. allies and partners will need to step up. Instead of naming the entire Indo-Pacific region as needing greater burden sharing, this section only cites a specific region within the Indo-Pacific: the Korean Peninsula. The Pentagon notes that South Korea is more than capable of handling North Korea, and says “updating U.S. force posture on the Korean Peninsula” is consistent with U.S. interests. In other words, the United States seeks more flexibility in utilizing troops and capabilities from South Korea to deal with other regional contingencies involving China, including a conflict over Taiwan. The NDS makes clear that the focus in the Indo-Pacific is China, not North Korea.Q3: How does the NDS fit within broader U.S.-China relations?A3: Released in late January, this strategic document comes about two months before President Trump’s expected visit to China in April 2026. Given the priority President Trump attaches to engaging with PRC President Xi Jinping directly, it is not surprising that the NDS explicitly references this as the first sentence under the section about deterring China. The NDS’s muted characterizations of China likely reflect the Pentagon’s desire to tread carefully and leave more options open for President Trump, depending on how he wants to take U.S.-China relations.Indeed, the NDS has garnered attention from China. One scholar from the China Institute of International Studies writes that the wording changes in the document are being interpreted as a “significant softening of U.S. rhetoric” on China, which offers “potential room for improving the atmosphere of bilateral relations.” However, the same author says that these rhetorical changes do not indicate that the United States has “‘abandoned’ or ‘relaxed’ its efforts to contain China in the Indo-Pacific.” Other Chinese scholars, even before the release of the NDS, have argued that the United States and China can move beyond adversarial competition to a normal relationship.Regardless of whether Chinese strategists truly believe that or not, there will be significant incentives for Beijing to try to sell the idea that President Trump can reset the relationship between the United States and China in a more positive direction. In doing so, Beijing likely hopes that Washington will overlook coercive or problematic activities it is undertaking against U.S. allies and partners. It will be incumbent on Washington to guard against such attempts if it seeks to recast U.S.-China relations.Bonny Lin is director of the China Power Project and senior adviser at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Brian Hart is deputy director and fellow of the China Power Project at CSIS.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAsia, and\nDefense and Security\nImage\nBonny Lin\nDirector, China Power Project and Senior Adviser\nImage\nBrian Hart\nDeputy Director and Fellow, China Power Project\nPrograms & Projects\nChina Power Project\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nOn China\nTrump-Xi 2026 Summits\nRelated Content\nImage\nSurveying the Experts: The State of U.S.-China Relations Entering 2026\nCommentary\nby\nBonny Lin,\nBrian Hart,\nTruly Tinsley,\nand\nLeon Li\n— January 13, 2026\nImage\nThe Geopolitics of Maduro’s Capture: What Does Operation Absolute Resolve Mean for Russia?\nCritical Questions\nby\nHenry Ziemer\n— January 20, 2026\nImage\nU.S.-China Mil-Mil Ties: A Conversation with Chad Sbragia\nPodcast Episode\nby\nBonny Lin\n— December 4, 2025","content_cn":"特朗普政府新的《国家防务战略》如何谈论中国？\n照片：Andrew Harnik/Getty Images\n关键问题\n作者\nBonny Lin\n和\nBrian Hart\n发表于2026年1月26日\n远程可视化\n1月23日，美国国防部（DOD，最近更名为战争部）发布了其期待已久的《国家防务战略》（NDS）非机密版本。与特朗普政府于2025年末发布的更广泛的《国家安全战略》（NSS）一致，新的NDS表明，与历届政府相比，美国防务战略和优先事项发生了变化——包括该文件讨论中华人民共和国（PRC）的方式存在显著差异。不过，NDS仍继续强调在印度太平洋地区威慑中国的重要性。Q1：在2026年NDS中，中国有多重要？A1：2026年NDS将本土防御和西半球确定为优先地区，并将威慑中国在印度太平洋地区的行动列为第二项战略区域优先事项。这与2025年NSS一致；后者通过将美国本土和西半球作为“门罗主义特朗普修正”的组成部分加以关注，大幅重新界定了美国的优先事项。这与包括第一届特朗普政府在内的美国历届政府相比，是一个显著转变。第一届特朗普政府NDS的2018年摘要将与中国和俄罗斯的长期竞争确定为该部门的“主要优先事项”。同样，在评估全球威胁环境时，拜登政府2022年的NDS将与中国的战略竞争列为首要挑战，将俄罗斯的“紧迫”威胁列为全球安全环境面临的第二大挑战。尽管新的NDS将中国列为第二优先事项，但它试图重新塑造美中关系的框架。国防政策副部长埃尔布里奇·科尔比在首尔解释说，该战略文件旨在结束“无谓的对抗”，并表示美国“应努力与中国建立稳定、和平的关系”。新的NDS进一步将“与中国的相互尊重关系”加入这一清单。因此，该战略文件列出的与中国有关的首批可执行事项之一是：“与中国人民解放军（PLA）开展更广泛的军方对军方沟通，重点是支持与北京的战略稳定，以及避免冲突和降级。”如果付诸实施，这将扭转第一届特朗普政府因担忧许多此类接触并不有利于美国而推动的限制两军联系与交流的趋势。国防部将允许多大范围的开放，以及这些机会是否主要限于高级别、政策层面的交流，仍有待观察。NDS还向中国保证，美国的目标“不是支配中国；也不是要扼杀或羞辱中国”。文件补充说，保护美国利益“并不需要政权更迭或生存斗争”。尽管美国官员曾私下向中国对话者传达过类似保证，但这些内容并未出现在近期美国的国家安全或防务战略中。它们被纳入2026年NDS非机密版本，表明该部门正在采取额外措施，传达美国军方的战略意图。这与第一届特朗普政府任期末附近的发展形成鲜明对比。\n政府，当时的国务卿迈克·蓬佩奥主张美国促使中国发生变化，暗示政权更迭是可取的或有可能实现的。\n\nQ2：NDS认定来自中国的主要威胁或挑战是什么？A2：2026年NDS在明确界定中国方面非常谨慎。与2018年NDS和2022年NDS明显不同的是，本次NDS既没有将中国称为战略竞争者，也没有强调与中国的长期竞争。有关中国的大部分措辞都是中性且不带价值判断的，将中国描述为“世界第二强国”。例如，它没有列出中国在印度太平洋地区及其他区域实施的、此前战略文件中已有充分记录的各种掠夺性、胁迫性或恐吓性活动。它没有直接提及台湾，也没有提及任何其他可能与中国发生冲突的引爆点。相反，NDS的重点是沿“第一岛链建立强大的防御拒止”，并防止中国主导印度太平洋地区。\n\n然而，这并不意味着NDS对遏制中国军事威胁的关注有所减弱。呼吁沿第一岛链实施防御拒止，旨在阻止中国人民解放军在大多数存在争议的热点地区实施潜在侵略——包括钓鱼岛、南海以及台湾海峡。这表明美国将继续强力支持该地区的主要盟友和伙伴，包括东京、马尼拉和台北。\n\nNDS的许多其他章节也隐含提到了来自中国的威胁，只是没有明确点名。在文件关于“本土与西半球”的章节中，对美国本土的直接军事威胁——“核威胁，以及各种常规打击能力、太空能力、网络能力和电磁战能力”——很可能同时指中国和俄罗斯的军事威胁。文件称，“我们已经看到对手的影响力从北极的格陵兰岛延伸至美洲湾、巴拿马运河以及更南方的地区”，这很可能也是指中国和俄罗斯。\n\n同样，在“同时性问题”一节中，五角大楼指出，“一个或多个潜在对手”可能以协调或机会主义的方式共同行动，随后提到欧洲、印度太平洋地区和韩国。这表明该部门最担心的是中国、俄罗斯和朝鲜之间的机会主义或协调行动。\n\n在增加与盟友和伙伴的分担责任方面，NDS呼吁世界大多数地区加强分担责任，但印度太平洋地区除外。在这一节中，NDS表示，“由于国防部正确地将本土防御和威慑中国置于优先位置，其他威胁仍将持续”，美国盟友和伙伴需要承担更多责任。该节没有指明整个印度太平洋地区需要增加分担责任，而只列出了印度太平洋地区内的一个具体区域：朝鲜半岛。五角大楼指出，韩国完全有能力应对朝鲜，并表示“更新美国在朝鲜半岛的兵力部署”符合美国利益。换言之，美国希望在处理涉及中国的其他地区突发事态（包括台海冲突）时，能够更灵活地调动驻韩部队和能力。NDS明确表明，印度太平洋地区的重点是中国，而不是朝鲜。\n\nQ3：NDS如何融入更广泛的美中关系？A3：\n该战略文件于一月下旬发布，时间大约比特朗普总统预计于2026年4月访问中国早两个月。鉴于特朗普总统高度重视直接与中华人民共和国国家主席习近平接触，NDS在有关威慑中国的章节下将此作为第一句话明确提及，并不令人意外。NDS对中国的低调表述可能反映出五角大楼希望谨慎行事，为特朗普总统留下更多选择，具体取决于他希望如何处理美中关系。\n\n事实上，NDS已经引起了中国方面的关注。中国国际问题研究院的一位学者写道，文件中的措辞变化被解读为美国对华“言辞显著软化”，这为“改善双边关系氛围”提供了“潜在空间”。然而，同一作者表示，这些修辞上的变化并不意味着美国已经在印太地区“放弃”或“放松”遏制中国的努力。甚至在NDS发布之前，其他中国学者也曾主张，美国和中国可以超越对抗性竞争，走向一种正常关系。\n\n无论中国战略家是否真的相信这一点，北京都有充分动机试图兜售这样一种观点：特朗普总统能够将美中关系重置到更加积极的方向。这样做时，北京很可能希望华盛顿忽视其针对美国盟友和伙伴所采取的胁迫性或有问题的活动。如果华盛顿寻求重新塑造美中关系，就必须防范此类企图。\n\nBonny Lin是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）中国力量项目主任兼高级顾问。Brian Hart是CSIS中国力量项目副主任兼研究员。\n\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不涉及专有利益。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n亚洲，以及\n国防与安全\n\n图片\nBonny Lin\n中国力量项目主任兼高级顾问\n\n图片\nBrian Hart\n中国力量项目副主任兼研究员\n\n项目与计划\n中国力量项目\n地缘政治与外交政策\n关于中国\n特朗普—习近平2026年峰会\n\n相关内容\n图片\n专家调查：进入2026年的美中关系状况\n评论\n作者\nBonny Lin、\nBrian Hart、\nTruly Tinsley，\n以及\nLeon Li\n— 2026年1月13日\n\n图片\n马杜罗被捕的地缘政治： “绝对决心行动”对俄罗斯意味着什么？\n关键问题\n作者\nHenry Ziemer\n— 2026年1月20日\n\n图片\n美中军事关系：与Chad Sbragia的对话\n播客节目\n作者\nBonny Lin\n— 2025年12月4日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-01-26T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3306","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"土耳其在乌克兰谈判中可以扮演什么角色？","title_en":"What Role Can Turkey Play in Ukraine Negotiations?","summary_cn":"文章分析土耳其在结束乌克兰战争的谈判中可能发挥的调解作用。土耳其既是北约成员国并向乌克兰提供无人机等军事装备，又与俄罗斯保持密切关系且未加入西方制裁，因而具备独特的外交空间。作者梳理了安塔利亚会谈、黑海粮食倡议、换俘等斡旋成果，也指出其中立性受质疑、俄方可能反对等制约，并建议美土协调推动停火与战后重建。","summary_en":"This commentary assesses Turkey's potential mediating role in negotiations to end the war in Ukraine. As a NATO member that armed Ukraine while maintaining close ties with Moscow and refusing to join Western sanctions, Turkey occupies a unique diplomatic space. The author reviews its mediation record, including the Antalya talks, the Black Sea Grain Initiative, and prisoner exchanges, while noting constraints such as questioned neutrality and possible Russian opposition, and suggests U.S.-Turkish coordination on cease-fire and reconstruction.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:11:36.879253+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/what-role-can-turkey-play-ukraine-negotiations","content":"What Role Can Turkey Play in Ukraine Negotiations?\nPhoto: OZAN KOSE/AFP/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nYasir Atalan\nPublished December 12, 2024\nRemote Visualization\nThis series—led by the Futures Lab and featuring scholars across CSIS—explores emerging challenges and opportunities likely to shape peace negotiations to end the war in Ukraine. All contributions in the series can be found by visiting Strategic Headwinds: Understanding the Forces Shaping Ukraine’s Path to Peace.Most interstate conflicts conclude with negotiated settlements rather than clear victories by one side. Such settlements often necessitate mediation efforts undertaken by actors perceived as neutral by all parties involved, especially when neither side can unilaterally impose an outcome. In the case of Ukraine, few candidates can play this role, and Turkey is one of them even if it is not entirely neutral in this war. It is a NATO member and was amongst the few providers of military equipment to Ukraine in the early stages of the war. Plus, Ankara has fostered deep, strategic-level defense cooperation with Kyiv beyond the sale of drones. Therefore, Turkey’s relations with Ukraine are more strategic in nature. Despite this, Turkey has close ties with Moscow. Plus, Turkish president Recep Tayyip Erdoğan enjoys a chummy personal rapport with Russian president Vladimir Putin. Likewise, Turkey has refrained from joining Western sanctions against Russia. These factors enable Ankara to sustain good relations with both Ukraine and Russia, allowing it to assume multiple roles—diplomatic, economic, security, and humanitarian—throughout the conflict. Despite constraints, Ankara can play an important role should president-elect Donald Trump decide to pursue a diplomatic track on the war. It is the most powerful NATO littoral state in the Black Sea with close ties with Ukraine and Russia.Assessing Turkey’s Role in Potential NegotiationsSince the beginning of the war, there have been few attempts to mediate the conflict. Turkey, despite facing criticism from the West, has endeavored to walk a fine line to showcase its diplomatic capabilities—a strategy that has yielded tangible results. Firstly, Turkey hosted a significant meeting in Antalya in March 2022, bringing together the foreign ministers of Russia and Ukraine for bilateral talks. Although this meeting was a dead end with no prospect, this was the first high-level engagement between the two nations since the conflict began, highlighting Turkey’s role as an early mediator.A more productive attempt was the Black Sea Grain Initiative. This initiative stemmed from concerns beginning at the onset of the Ukraine war. When the war broke out, it precipitated a global food crisis and grain shortages. On July 22, 2022, Turkey and the United Nations brokered a deal between the warring parties to establish a shipping corridor for Ukrainian grain which was a lifeline for 349 million people. This agreement was crucial in alleviating the food crisis and bolstering Ukraine’s economy during a tumultuous period. Building on its role as a diplomatic facilitator, Ankara hosted the U.S. and Russian spy chiefs in November 2022 to discuss nuclear threats. Likewise, Turkey’s diplomatic efforts were instrumental in a recent prisoner swap between Russia and Ukraine. This deal was warmly welcomed by the international community, leading to the safe return of important prisoners and signaling the effectiveness of Turkey’s mediating role.Turkey’s political positioning is an enabler for such a role. Since the beginning of the war, Turkey’s position was ultimately unique. On the one hand, Turkey strongly backed Ukraine’s self-defense and territorial integrity and supplied Ukraine with unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and other arms. Simultaneously, Turkey has refrained from joining Western sanctions against Russia and has maintained energy ties by continuing the transfer of Russian gas, which has been vital for Russia’s economy. This balancing act stemmed from Turkey’s reading of the global order, viewing it as more multipolar and less Western-centric, which increased the importance of regional frameworks. Such a worldview entailed Turkey maintaining close ties with Russia and Ukraine at the same time.Despite these advantages, several challenges could hinder Turkey’s ability to mediate. First, Turkey’s being a central actor in the Ukraine conflict damages Turkey’s image as a neutral actor. While being part of Black Sea security architecture, and the ultimate gatekeeper with the control of the straits, Turkey is also balancing Russia in other key geographies including Syria and Libya. Put differently, Turkey is ultimately a hedging actor instead of a neutral third party. Second, Russia may oppose Turkey’s mediating role due to its military support for Ukraine, including the provision of Bayraktar TB2 drones. Similarly, Turkey still considers Crimea, Luhansk, and Donetsk as Ukraine’s land, which could attract Russia’s criticism. Third, as a NATO member, Turkey is ultimately aligned with the transatlantic alliance, which Russia views as an adversary. Fourth, the West may also be cautious about endorsing Turkey’s key role in ending the conflict, given uncertainties about Turkey’s future position within the broader West. These concerns can be alleviated through more clarity about Turkey’s place in the post-war security order. Ankara may be more willing to lean into a mediation role if it sees a clear path toward shaping a stable and inclusive regional security framework.Leveraging Turkey’s PositionRegardless of assuming a mediation role, Turkey should advocate for negotiations that can give Ukraine the opportunity to recover itself and build up its arsenal to defend itself and situate Ukraine in a European security architecture. Any position short of this will likely incentivize Russia to continue its aggression. Aware of this, Turkey could leverage its strategic position—balancing against Russia in the Black Sea and acting as a competitor in Syria—to influence the negotiation talks. Turkey should aim to be a key actor in post-war Ukraine reconstruction as well as rebuilding its defense industrial base. Such an approach will both serve a more sustainable settlement and Turkey’s desire to be a key regional power.Meaningful coordination between the United States and Turkey could contribute to establishing a sustained ceasefire in Ukraine. The next Trump administration has some room to leverage Turkey’s position to end the conflict to the benefit of Ukraine. At a basic level, President Trump can leverage his personal relations with President Erdoğan to engage in leader-level diplomacy with President Putin, potentially creating a positive framework for negotiation talks. On a more strategic level, the recent rebel takeover of the Assad regime in Syria presents an opportunity for the United States and Turkey to align their regional interests. By coordinating policies and operations in Syria—particularly regarding the future of the Syrian government and Northern Syria—they could divert Russia’s attention and resources. This shift might influence Moscow’s approach to the Ukraine conflict. With that, the United States can collaborate with Turkey on energy projects to reduce Turkey’s dependence on Russian oil and gas, a factor that could be key to ending the war. Finally, Turkey’s contribution to the negotiations will be limited without clarity on the strategic framework between the West and Turkey regarding the latter’s role in the Western security architecture. Thus, a more European NATO with Turkey playing a more prominent role could help resolve this ambiguity.A diplomatic settlement also requires clarity about Ukraine’s future, the security dynamics of the Black Sea, and their respective roles within Europe’s broader security framework. Achieving this will demand more intensive foreign and security policy coordination among Turkey, the European Union, and the United Kingdom. Such cooperation is essential not only for ensuring sustainable peace in Ukraine but also for effectively counterbalancing Russia’s influence in the shared neighborhoods of Turkey and Europe.Yasir Atalan is a data fellow for Futures Lab in the International Security Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nMiddle East,\nRussia and Eurasia,\nTurkey,\nUkraine,\nTransnational Threats,\nGeopolitics and International Security, and\nUkraine War\nImage\nYasir Atalan\nDeputy Director and Data Fellow, Futures Lab, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nFutures Lab\nStrategic Headwinds: Understanding the Forces Shaping Ukraine’s Path to Peace","content_cn":"土耳其能在乌克兰谈判中发挥什么作用？\n图片：OZAN KOSE/AFP/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nYasir Atalan\n发布于2024年12月12日\n远程可视化\n本系列由未来实验室主导，并汇集CSIS各领域学者，探讨可能塑造结束乌克兰战争的和平谈判的新兴挑战与机遇。访问《战略逆风：理解塑造乌克兰和平之路的力量》可找到本系列的全部文章。大多数国家间冲突以谈判达成的解决方案而非一方的明确胜利告终。这类解决方案通常需要由各方均认为中立的行为体进行调解，特别是在任何一方都无法单方面强加结果时。就乌克兰而言，能够扮演这一角色的候选者寥寥无几，土耳其便是其中之一，尽管它在这场战争中并非完全中立。它是北约成员国，也是战争初期少数向乌克兰提供军事装备的国家之一。此外，安卡拉与基辅建立了超越出售无人机的深层、战略层面的国防合作。因此，土耳其与乌克兰的关系在本质上更具战略性。尽管如此，土耳其与莫斯科关系密切。此外，土耳其总统雷杰普·塔伊普·埃尔多安与俄罗斯总统弗拉基米尔·普京有着亲密的私人关系。同样，土耳其一直未加入西方对俄罗斯的制裁。这些因素使安卡拉能够同时与乌克兰和俄罗斯保持良好关系，从而得以在整个冲突期间承担外交、经济、安全和人道主义等多重角色。尽管存在限制，若当选总统唐纳德·特朗普决定就这场战争寻求外交途径，安卡拉仍可发挥重要作用。它是黑海沿岸实力最强的北约国家，并与乌克兰和俄罗斯保持密切关系。\n评估土耳其在潜在谈判中的作用\n自战争开始以来，调解这场冲突的尝试寥寥无几。尽管面临西方的批评，土耳其一直努力谨慎平衡，以展示其外交能力——这一战略已取得切实成果。首先，土耳其于2022年3月在安塔利亚主持了一次重要会议，召集俄罗斯和乌克兰外交部长举行双边会谈。尽管这次会议毫无进展、没有前景，但这是冲突开始以来两国间首次高级别接触，凸显了土耳其作为早期调解者的作用。\n更具成效的一次尝试是黑海谷物倡议。该倡议源于乌克兰战争爆发之初出现的担忧。战争爆发时，引发了全球粮食危机和谷物短缺。2022年7月22日，土耳其和联合国促成交战双方达成协议，为乌克兰谷物建立航运走廊，这条走廊是3.49亿人的生命线。该协议对于缓解粮食危机并在动荡时期提振乌克兰经济至关重要。在担任外交促成者的基础上，安卡拉于2022年11月接待了美国和俄罗斯的情报部门负责人，讨论核威胁。同样，土耳其的外交努力对最近一次俄罗斯与乌克兰之间的战俘交换至关重要。这项协议受到国际社会热烈欢迎，促成重要囚犯安全返回，并表明土耳其调解作用的有效性。土耳其的政治\n这种定位使其能够承担这样的角色。自战争开始以来，土耳其的立场始终独具特色。一方面，土耳其强力支持乌克兰的自卫和领土完整，并向乌克兰提供无人机（UAV）及其他武器。与此同时，土耳其避免加入西方对俄罗斯的制裁，并通过继续转运俄罗斯天然气维持能源联系，这对俄罗斯经济至关重要。这种平衡之举源于土耳其对全球秩序的判断：它认为全球秩序更加多极化、较少以西方为中心，因而提升了区域框架的重要性。这样的世界观意味着土耳其同时与俄罗斯和乌克兰保持密切联系。\n\n尽管具备这些优势，若干挑战可能阻碍土耳其的调解能力。第一，土耳其作为乌克兰冲突的核心行为体，损害了其作为中立行为体的形象。土耳其既是黑海安全架构的一部分，又因控制海峡而充当最终把关者，同时还在包括叙利亚和利比亚在内的其他关键地区制衡俄罗斯。换言之，土耳其最终是一个对冲型行为体，而非中立的第三方。第二，俄罗斯可能因土耳其向乌克兰提供军事支持，包括提供拜拉克塔尔TB2无人机，而反对土耳其发挥调解作用。同样，土耳其仍将克里米亚、卢甘斯克和顿涅茨克视为乌克兰领土，这可能招致俄罗斯批评。第三，作为北约成员，土耳其最终与跨大西洋联盟保持一致，而俄罗斯将该联盟视为对手。第四，鉴于土耳其在更广泛西方阵营中未来地位存在不确定性，西方也可能谨慎支持土耳其在结束冲突中发挥关键作用。通过更清晰地界定土耳其在战后安全秩序中的位置，这些担忧可以得到缓解。如果安卡拉看到一条明确路径，能够参与塑造稳定且包容的区域安全框架，它或许会更愿意投入调解角色。\n\n利用土耳其的地位\n\n无论是否承担调解角色，土耳其都应倡导进行谈判，使乌克兰有机会恢复元气、扩充用于自卫的武器库，并将乌克兰置于欧洲安全架构之中。任何低于这一目标的立场，都可能激励俄罗斯继续其侵略行为。土耳其意识到这一点，可利用其战略地位——在黑海制衡俄罗斯，并在叙利亚充当竞争者——来影响谈判。土耳其应致力于成为战后乌克兰重建以及其国防工业基础重建中的关键行为体。这种做法既将服务于更可持续的解决方案，也将服务于土耳其成为关键地区大国的愿望。\n\n美国和土耳其之间有意义的协调，可能有助于在乌克兰实现持久停火。下一届特朗普政府有一定空间利用土耳其的地位，以有利于乌克兰的方式结束冲突。在基本层面上，特朗普总统可以利用他与埃尔多安总统的私人关系，与普京总统开展领导人级外交，从而可能为谈判创造积极框架。在更具战略性的层面上，近期叙利亚反政府武装推翻阿萨德政权，为美国和土耳其协调其地区利益提供了机会。通过协调在\n叙利亚——尤其是关于叙利亚政府和叙利亚北部的未来——可能会分散俄罗斯的注意力和资源。这一转变或会影响莫斯科应对乌克兰冲突的方式。与此同时，美国可以与土耳其合作开展能源项目，以降低土耳其对俄罗斯石油和天然气的依赖；这一因素可能是结束战争的关键。最后，若西方与土耳其之间就后者在西方安全架构中的角色缺乏明确的战略框架，土耳其对谈判的贡献将受到限制。因此，一个更具欧洲色彩、且土耳其发挥更突出作用的北约，或可帮助消除这一模糊性。\n外交解决方案还需要明确乌克兰的未来、黑海的安全态势，以及二者在欧洲更广泛安全框架中的各自角色。实现这一目标将需要土耳其、欧盟和英国在外交与安全政策方面开展更密切的协调。这种合作不仅对于确保乌克兰实现可持续和平至关重要，也是在土耳其与欧洲共同周边地区有效制衡俄罗斯影响力所必需的。\nYasir Atalan 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心国际安全项目“未来实验室”的数据研究员。\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策议题的私营免税机构。其研究不偏袒任何党派，也不属专有性质。CSIS 不持有具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论应被理解为仅代表作者本人或作者们的观点、立场和结论。\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n标签\n中东，\n俄罗斯和欧亚地区，\n土耳其，\n乌克兰，\n跨国威胁，\n地缘政治与国际安全，以及\n乌克兰战争\n图片\nYasir Atalan\n国防与安全部未来实验室副主任兼数据研究员\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n未来实验室\n战略逆风：理解塑造乌克兰和平之路的力量","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-12T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-86","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美墨临时贸易协议究竟会涉及什么？","title_en":"What Would an Interim U.S.-Mexico Trade Deal Actually Involve?","summary_cn":"文章分析美墨加协定（USMCA）进入年度审查阶段后，美墨临时贸易协议的可能内容。作者指出，美国可能要求墨西哥在海关程序、知识产权、对华政策、供应链安全等方面作出让步，以换取232条款和301条款关税减免。墨西哥应坚持比例原则，避免以永久性结构让步换取临时关税缓解，并为2027年更艰难的USMCA谈判保留筹码。","summary_en":"This commentary examines what an interim U.S.-Mexico trade deal might entail after USMCA entered annual reviews. The authors argue Washington may demand Mexican concessions on customs, intellectual property, China alignment, and supply chain security in exchange for Section 232 and 301 tariff relief. Mexico should apply proportionality, avoid permanent structural concessions for temporary tariff relief, and preserve leverage for harder USMCA talks in 2027.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:50:02.481992+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/what-would-interim-us-mexico-trade-deal-actually-involve","content":"What Would an Interim U.S.-Mexico Trade Deal Actually Involve?\nPhoto: Hector Vivas/FIFA/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nDiego Marroquín Bitar\nand\nJuan Carlos Baker Pineda\nPublished September 21, 2026\nOn July 1, 2026, the White House let the clock start on the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA) by declining to renew the deal for another 16 years. The agreement did not expire; instead, it entered a phase of annual reviews that could run until 2036, when the agreement would lapse for good if the three governments never agree to extend it. With U.S.-Canada trade talks now in collapse, Mexico is the only USMCA track still moving. What Mexico concedes for tariff relief in a stopgap deal could become the baseline for Canada, and for the harder USMCA fight still to come.Three weeks after the July 1 deadline, U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer laid out the administration’s two-stage plan for the USMCA before the Senate Finance Committee. The first step is reaching separate interim “deals” with Mexico and Canada to clear several trade and nontrade barriers before year’s end, as well as reducing each country’s exposure to U.S. tariffs. Then, the three countries would turn to the harder files: China, rules of origin, labor, and environmental provisions. Those issues that will ultimately decide whether the USMCA survives.The United States and Canada were close to reaching an agreement in August, but negotiations collapsed, with both sides blaming the other. Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney argued that some of the U.S. demands would have compromised Canada’s control over its own policies, while President Trump accused Canada of having taken advantage of the United States and insisted that such a relationship would no longer continue. On August 22, the United States imposed a 50 percent tariff on $20 billion worth of Canadian products; Canada launched retaliatory tariffs on September 8. While regrettable, the collapse in U.S.-Canada negotiations does not decide Mexico’s outcome. Mexico and the United States have continued their bilateral discussions since March 2026, and Mexican officials have publicly expressed optimism that an agreement can still be reached before the U.S. midterm elections of November. The United States’ bilateral relations with its two USMCA partners are different enough economically, politically, and strategically that it could land in a different place with each, although neither of these agreements would amount to the renewal of the USMCA.What An Interim Agreement Would Actually InvolveRecent U.S. deals with other partners, together with Ambassador Greer’s own testimony, give a reasonably clear picture of what the United States will likely ask of Mexico, and what Mexico would look to secure in return. The administration offered few details, but it has signed enough agreements elsewhere to set precedent. These include agreements on reciprocal trade (ARTs) with countries such as Argentina, South Korea, and Japan. ARTs are not submitted to Congress as traditional free trade agreements; they largely rely on authority to the president claims t and are implemented through executive action. While the legality and scope of some of those authorities remain contested among members of Congress and other actors, these arrangements still indicate the type of concessions that Washington seeks from trading partners.Their substance varies widely. Argentina’s ART opened agricultural markets and tightened intellectual property enforcement. South Korea’s committed $150 billion to U.S. shipbuilding and another $200 billion in broader investment. Japan’s accepted a capped tariff rate in exchange for its own investment pledges. An important issue to keep in mind with respect ARTs is that implementation has been uneven, with commitments from U.S. partners being slow-rolled or quietly set aside.Mexico is a different case. Unlike most countries signing ARTs, Mexico already has a comprehensive free trade agreement with the United States and sits inside deeply integrated North American production networks. Whatever emerges from these talks should be understood strictly as temporary tariff relief, not a substitute for the USMCA.Even so, it is difficult to picture the White House cutting existing Section 232 and Section 301 tariffs without asking Mexico for something in return. The results of negotiations with other countries suggest that tariff relief under this administration comes with a price. When the first International Emergency Economic Powers Act tariffs were announced, the United States had already presented Mexico a lengthy list of tariff and nontariff concerns that it wanted to address, reportedly more than 50 items. By the third U.S.-Mexico negotiation round this July, that list had shrunk to 14 issues. What closes the remaining gap, and what Mexico gets in exchange, is the decisive question now, especially with Canada having already rejected the United States’ terms.What Washington Could Ask ForPotential U.S. demands fall along two dimensions. The first is speed: how quickly Mexico can implement them. The second, and more consequential, is depth: how far they reach into Mexico’s control over its domestic policies and its access to the U.S. market.The first dimension includes short-term demands, which Mexico could address relatively quickly, and medium- or longer-term demands, which would require commitments that extend beyond an interim agreement. The second includes structural and nonstructural demands. This is perhaps more important. Some U.S. requests could be relatively easy for Mexico to implement without fundamentally changing its policies; others could have much deeper consequences by limiting Mexico’s access to the U.S. market or its ability to conduct trade relations with other countries.Crossing the two dimensions produces four types of demands:\nImage\nDiego Marroquín Bitar\nFellow, Americas Program\nJuan Carlos Baker Pineda\nResident Fellow, Georgetown Americas Institute\nPrograms & Projects\nAmericas Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nThe USMCA Strategic Initiative: Understanding the Future of North American Trade\nRemote Visualization\nHousekeeping demands are quick to implement and limited in reach. These are the irritants that the United States has already identified and that Mexico could address through administrative or relatively limited regulatory action. These include U.S. industry complaints, such as U.S. cheese producers’ objection to geographical-indication commitments Mexico made to the European Union, along with questions of agricultural market access, customs procedures, intellectual property rights, or specific regulatory barriers. Mexico can settle these without fundamentally changing the bilateral economic relationship, and it should make progress where doing so builds goodwill with the United States.The Trojan horse is quick to implement but has deep reach. These demands are quickly addressed but introduce limits to Mexico’s own policies well beyond the life of an interim deal. Washington may ask Mexico to align some of its trade policies with those of the United States. These could include tariffs, investment screening, export controls, transshipment, supply chain security, or measures directed at nonmarket economies, particularly China. A request of this type can be presented as a narrow technical adjustment and enacted weeks or by decree, but could have long-term and structural consequences for Mexican trade policy.Rebalancing demands are slower to implement but shallow in reach. The United States may ask Mexico to reduce the bilateral trade deficit through additional purchases of U.S. agricultural, energy, or manufactured goods; to facilitate U.S. investment; or to support U.S. positions in organizations such as the World Trade Organization and the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum. These demands could be costly economically, but they would leave the architecture of the USMCA intact. Similar proposals have been part of other agreements negotiated by the administration.Structural demands are slow to implement but have deep reach. They would redraw Mexico’s long-term domestic and foreign policy. Measures could include quantitative or other limits on Mexican exports, new U.S. content thresholds, explicit restrictions on Mexico’s trade relations with third countries, or even changes that shift the trilateral nature of the USMCA toward a set of parallel bilateral deals. These proposals would outlast any interim framework negotiated between the two countries. Prime Minister Carney treated reported U.S. proposals to limit Canada’s ability to negotiate trade deals with other partners as one of his country’s red lines, blaming these demands for the collapse of the U.S.-Canada interim deal.Mexican President Claudia Sheinbaum’s ability to move quickly is not in doubt. Article 131 of Mexico’s Constitution provides the executive with the power to increase, reduce, or eliminate import and export tariffs and to establish restrictions on imports, exports, and transit when necessary to regulate foreign trade. This authority is further reinforced in Mexico’s Foreign Trade Law, which provides the president and the economy ministry with important instruments to establish or modify tariff and nontariff measures. Consequently, some potentially significant commitments could be implemented without lengthy legislative processes. But if the result is to limit Mexico’s ability to determine its own trade policy toward China or another country, the implications are clearly much more significant. Prime Minister Carney considered structural demands as red lines and claims they caused the collapse of the U.S.-Canada interim deal.What Mexico May Look to Secure in ReturnHow easily Mexico can grant concessions should not be confused with their significance or cost. Mexico’s potential for compromise will depend on what the United States offers in exchange.Mexico’s main objective in an interim agreement is tariff relief, particularly applied to Section 232 duties on steel, aluminum, automobiles, and related products. A second priority is resolving existing Section 301 tariff actions that apply to Mexico, one on forced labor and one on excess manufacturing capacity. A third is protection against future U.S. tariff actions. An interim deal that delivers duty relief on today’s tariffs while leaving the door open for tomorrow’s would give Mexico the illusion of protection while exposing the country to additional duties on the very products it is negotiating relief for right now.The governing principle should be proportionality. If the United States requests relatively simple, reversible concessions, Mexico may be able to accept them in exchange for specific tariff relief. However, if the United States expects Mexico to make deeper and permanent structural changes to its policies, Mexico should receive a stronger guarantee in return. Mexico should not accept permanent limitations on its trade policy in exchange for the temporary suspension of tariffs that can be imposed again a few months later under Section 232, Section 301, or another legal authority. The recurring flaw of ARTs with other countries is that the United States has been unwilling to commit against reimposing tariffs it has agreed to lift.This does not mean that Mexico should refuse to make important concessions. There may be areas where greater alignment with the United States serves Mexico’s own interest—particularly if the objective is to strengthen North American production and secure supply chains—but the exchange needs to be balanced. The deeper and more lasting the demand, the stronger the guarantee that would be needed to justify it.The failure of the U.S.-Canada negotiations makes this balance even more important for Mexico. Mexico may decide that some of the concessions rejected by Canada are acceptable if the benefits obtained from the United States justify them, but Canada’s decision creates an unavoidable point of comparison. If Mexico accepts significant or structural concessions that Canada rejected but obtains only limited or temporary tariff relief in return, it could end up with the worst of both worlds: bound by restrictions that Canada was unwilling to accept while still remaining exposed to the next wave of U.S. tariffs. Similarly, if trade with Mexico and Canada ends up governed by two different sets of rules, each product of a separate bilateral bargain, the trilateral nature of USMCA would survive only on paper. An agreement built on common rules does not hold when the parties operate under divergent terms.Buy Certainty, Keep LeverageMexico should approach an interim deal pragmatically, with a clear hierarchy of what matters. Resolving individual irritants, facilitating additional U.S. investment, and accepting deeper North American economic security cooperation are reasonable pieces of a broader bargain. What Mexico cannot afford to do is treat any such deal as final. President Trump is a permanent negotiator and will reopen trade files as economic or political circumstances shift. If an interim deal is reached, Mexico should assume that the United States can return with new demands next year regardless of what was signed and negotiate accordingly.The value of an interim agreement lies precisely in the fact that it does not have to resolve everything. Mexico should use that to buy certainty where certainty is available, settle what can reasonably be settled now, and preserve enough leverage for the harder USMCA conversations that will resume in 2027. But the risk runs the other way, too: Mexico could burn through its negotiating capital in the early rounds and arrive at the decisive ones with nothing left to trade. The benchmark for a good interim deal is not just tariff reduction today; it also needs to be proportional, provide stable market access to the United States, and leave cards for Mexico to negotiate in the future.Diego Marroquín Bitar is a fellow in the Americas Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Juan Carlos Baker Pineda is resident fellow with the Georgetown Americas Institute and one of the architects of the original USMCA.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAmericas\nRelated Content\nImage\nUSMCA Review 2026\nListen to audio brief\nBrief\nby\nDiego Marroquín Bitar,\nChristopher Hernandez-Roy,\nand\nEarl Anthony Wayne\n— August 18, 2025\nImage\nUSMCA Review 2026: Six Scenarios for North America’s Future\nCommentary\nby\nDiego Marroquín Bitar\nand\nWilliam Alan Reinsch\n— March 27, 2026","content_cn":"临时美墨贸易协议究竟会涉及什么？\n照片：Hector Vivas/FIFA/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nDiego Marroquín Bitar\n和\nJuan Carlos Baker Pineda\n发表于2026年9月21日\n2026年7月1日，白宫拒绝将《美国—墨西哥—加拿大协定》（USMCA）再延长16年，由此启动了该协定的计时。协定并未到期；相反，它进入了年度审查阶段，可能持续到2036年——如果三国政府始终未能同意延长协定，届时协定将永久失效。随着美加贸易谈判如今陷入崩溃，墨西哥成为唯一仍在推进的USMCA谈判轨道。墨西哥为在一项过渡性协议中获得关税减免所作出的让步，可能成为加拿大的基准，也可能成为未来更艰难的USMCA斗争的基线。\n在7月1日最后期限过去三周后，美国贸易代表Jamieson Greer在参议院财政委员会面前阐述了政府分两阶段推进USMCA的计划。第一步是在年底前分别与墨西哥和加拿大达成“临时协议”，消除若干贸易和非贸易壁垒，同时降低两国各自对美国关税的暴露程度。随后，三国将转向更棘手的议题：中国、原产地规则、劳工和环境条款。这些问题最终将决定USMCA能否存续。\n美国和加拿大原本接近于8月达成协议，但谈判破裂，双方都指责对方。加拿大总理Mark Carney认为，美国的一些要求会损害加拿大对自身政策的掌控；而特朗普总统指责加拿大占了美国的便宜，并坚称这种关系不会再继续。8月22日，美国对价值200亿美元的加拿大产品征收50%的关税；加拿大于9月8日启动报复性关税。尽管令人遗憾，美加谈判的破裂并不能决定墨西哥的结果。自2026年3月以来，墨西哥和美国一直在继续双边讨论，墨西哥官员也公开表示乐观，认为在11月美国中期选举之前仍可达成协议。美国与其两个USMCA伙伴的双边关系在经济、政治和战略层面存在足够差异，因此美国可能与两国分别达成不同结果，尽管这两项协议都不会等同于续签USMCA。\n临时协议究竟会涉及什么\n美国近期与其他伙伴达成的协议，加上Greer大使本人的证词，已经相当清楚地勾勒出美国可能向墨西哥提出的要求，以及墨西哥将寻求换取的条件。政府没有透露多少细节，但它已在其他地方签署了足够多的协议，形成了先例。其中包括与阿根廷、韩国和日本等国达成的互惠贸易协议（ART）。ART不会像传统自由贸易协定那样提交国会；它们主要依赖总统声称拥有的授权，并通过行政行动实施。尽管国会议员和其他参与者仍对其中一些授权的合法性和范围存在争议，但这些安排仍表明了华盛顿希望贸易伙伴作出何种让步。\n这些协议的实质内容差异很大。阿根廷的ART开放了农业市场，并加强了知识产权\n执法。韩国承诺向美国造船业投资1500亿美元，并追加2000亿美元的更广泛投资。日本接受了有上限的关税税率，以换取其自身的投资承诺。关于ARTs，需要牢记的一个重要问题是，其实施并不均衡，美国合作伙伴作出的承诺要么被拖延落实，要么被悄然搁置。\n\n墨西哥是一个不同的案例。与大多数签署ARTs的国家不同，墨西哥已经与美国签订了全面自由贸易协定，并处于高度一体化的北美生产网络之中。无论这些谈判最终达成什么结果，都应严格理解为临时性的关税减免，而不是USMCA的替代品。\n\n即便如此，很难想象白宫会在不要求墨西哥作出某种回报的情况下，削减现有的第232条和第301条关税。与其他国家的谈判结果表明，本届政府下的关税减免是有代价的。在首次宣布依据《国际紧急经济权力法》征收关税时，美国已经向墨西哥提出了一份冗长的关税和非关税问题清单，希望加以解决，据报道超过50项。到今年7月美墨第三轮谈判时，这份清单已缩减至14项。如何弥合剩余差距，以及墨西哥将以何种方式换取减免，正是当前的决定性问题，尤其是在加拿大已经拒绝美国条款的情况下。\n\n华盛顿可能提出什么要求\n\n潜在的美国要求可沿两个维度展开。第一个是速度：墨西哥能够多快落实这些要求。第二个、也是影响更大的维度，是深度：这些要求将多大程度触及墨西哥对其国内政策的控制权以及其进入美国市场的机会。\n\n第一个维度包括短期要求，墨西哥可以相对迅速地处理；以及中期或长期要求，这些要求需要作出超出临时协议范围的承诺。第二个维度包括结构性和非结构性要求。这或许更为重要。一些美国请求对墨西哥而言相对容易实施，并不需要从根本上改变其政策；另一些则可能产生更深远的后果，限制墨西哥进入美国市场的机会，或限制其与其他国家开展贸易关系的能力。\n\n将这两个维度交叉起来，会产生四类要求：\n\n图片\nDiego Marroquín Bitar\n美洲项目研究员\nJuan Carlos Baker Pineda\n乔治城美洲研究所常驻研究员\n项目与计划\n美洲项目\n地缘政治与外交政策\nUSMCA战略倡议：了解北美贸易的未来\n远程可视化\n\n家务型要求实施迅速，影响范围有限。这些是美国已经指出的那些恼人问题，墨西哥可以通过行政措施或相对有限的监管行动加以处理。其中包括美国业界的投诉，例如美国奶酪生产商反对墨西哥向欧盟作出的地理标志承诺，以及农业市场准入、海关程序、知识产权，或具体监管壁垒等问题。墨西哥可以在不从根本上改变双边经济关系的情况下解决这些问题；凡是能够增进美国善意的领域，墨西哥都应取得进展。\n\n特洛伊木马型要求实施迅速，但影响深远。这些要求可以迅速得到处理，却会对墨西哥自身的\n这些政策的影响可能远远超出临时协议的存续期。华盛顿可能要求墨西哥使其部分贸易政策与美国的贸易政策保持一致。这些政策可能包括关税、投资审查、出口管制、转运、供应链安全，或针对非市场经济体（尤其是中国）的措施。此类要求可以被包装成狭窄的技术性调整，并在数周内或通过法令实施，但可能对墨西哥贸易政策产生长期和结构性影响。\n\n再平衡方面的要求实施较慢，但影响范围较浅。美国可能要求墨西哥通过额外购买美国农产品、能源或制成品来减少双边贸易逆差；便利美国投资；或支持美国在世界贸易组织和亚太经济合作组织等机构中的立场。这些要求在经济上可能代价高昂，但会使《美墨加协定》的架构保持完整。政府谈判达成的其他协议中也曾包含类似提议。\n\n结构性要求实施缓慢，但影响深远。这些要求将重塑墨西哥的长期内政和外交政策。相关措施可能包括对墨西哥出口实施数量限制或其他限制、设定新的美国成分门槛、明确限制墨西哥与第三国的贸易关系，甚至改变《美墨加协定》的三边性质，使其转变为一组平行的双边协议。这些提议的持续时间将超过两国之间谈判达成的任何临时框架。卡尼总理将据报道美国提出的限制加拿大与其他伙伴谈判贸易协议能力的提议视为本国的红线之一，并指责这些要求导致美加临时协议破裂。\n\n墨西哥总统克劳迪娅·辛鲍姆迅速采取行动的能力毋庸置疑。墨西哥宪法第131条赋予行政部门在必要时为规范对外贸易而提高、降低或取消进出口关税，以及对进口、出口和过境设立限制的权力。墨西哥《对外贸易法》进一步强化了这一权力，该法赋予总统和经济部重要工具，以制定或修改关税和非关税措施。因此，一些可能具有重大意义的承诺无需经过漫长的立法程序即可实施。但如果结果是限制墨西哥自行决定对华或对其他国家的贸易政策，其影响显然会重大得多。卡尼总理将结构性要求视为红线，并称这些要求导致了美加临时协议的破裂。\n\n墨西哥可能寻求获得的回报\n\n墨西哥给予让步的难易程度，不应与这些让步的意义或代价混为一谈。墨西哥妥协的可能程度将取决于美国提供何种交换条件。\n\n墨西哥在临时协议中的主要目标是获得关税减免，尤其是针对钢铁、铝、汽车及相关产品的第232条款关税。第二优先事项是解决现有适用于墨西哥的第301条款关税措施，其中一项针对强迫劳动，另一项针对制造业产能过剩。第三是防范未来美国的关税措施。一项临时协议如果能减免当前关税，却为未来关税措施留下空间，就会让墨西哥产生受到保护的错觉，同时使其暴露于\n使该国承担额外关税，而征收对象恰恰是其目前正在谈判争取获得豁免的产品。应遵循的原则是相称性。如果美国要求的是相对简单且可逆的让步，墨西哥或许可以接受，以换取特定的关税减免。然而，如果美国希望墨西哥对其政策作出更深层且永久性的结构性改变，那么墨西哥应获得更强有力的保障作为回报。墨西哥不应以永久限制其贸易政策为代价，换取可能在几个月后依据第232条、第301条或其他法律授权再次实施的关税暂缓征收。美国与其他国家达成的关税休战协议（ART）反复存在的缺陷在于，美国一直不愿承诺不重新征收其已同意取消的关税。\n\n这并不意味着墨西哥应拒绝作出重要让步。在某些领域，与美国进一步协调符合墨西哥自身利益——尤其是在目标是加强北美生产和保障供应链的情况下——但交换必须保持平衡。要求越深入、持续时间越长，就越需要更强有力的保障来证明作出这一让步是合理的。\n\n美加谈判的失败使这种平衡对墨西哥而言更加重要。墨西哥可能会认为，加拿大拒绝的一些让步是可以接受的，只要从美国获得的利益足以证明这些让步值得作出，但加拿大的决定形成了一个无法回避的比较基准。如果墨西哥接受加拿大拒绝的重大或结构性让步，却只换来有限或暂时的关税减免，那么它可能陷入两头不讨好的境地：受制于加拿大不愿接受的限制，同时仍暴露于美国下一轮关税之下。同样，如果墨西哥和加拿大的贸易最终分别受两套不同规则管辖，而每套规则都源于一次单独的双边交易，那么《美墨加协定》的三边性质将只停留在纸面上。建立在共同规则之上的协议，在各方按照相互不同的条款运作时是无法维持的。\n\n买确定性，保留筹码\n\n墨西哥应务实地对待临时协议，明确轻重缓急。解决个别摩擦点、促进更多美国投资，以及接受更深入的北美经济安全合作，都是更广泛交易中合理的组成部分。墨西哥无法承受的是把任何此类协议视为最终协议。特朗普总统是一位长期的谈判者，随着经济或政治环境变化，他会重新开启贸易议题。如果达成临时协议，墨西哥应假定，无论签署了什么，美国明年都可能带着新的要求回来，并据此进行谈判。\n\n临时协议的价值恰恰在于它不必解决所有问题。墨西哥应利用这一点，在可以获得确定性的地方换取确定性，解决目前可以合理解决的问题，并为将于2027年恢复的更艰难的《美墨加协定》谈判保留足够筹码。但风险也可能朝相反方向发展：墨西哥可能在早期回合耗尽谈判资本，到了决定性回合时已经没有可交换的东西。衡量一份良好临时协议的标准，不只是今天降低关税；它还必须相称，为进入美国市场提供稳定保障，并为墨西哥未来谈判留下筹码。\n\n迭戈\nMarroquín Bitar是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心美洲项目的研究员。Juan Carlos Baker Pineda是乔治城美洲研究所的常驻研究员，也是最初《美墨加协定》的设计者之一。\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私营、免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不具专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n© 2026年战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n美洲\n相关内容\n图像\n2026年USMCA审查\n收听音频简报\n简报\n作者\nDiego Marroquín Bitar、\nChristopher Hernandez-Roy、\n和\nEarl Anthony Wayne\n— 2025年8月18日\n图像\n2026年USMCA审查：北美未来的六种情景\n评论\n作者\nDiego Marroquín Bitar\n和\nWilliam Alan Reinsch\n— 2026年3月27日","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-09-21T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3166","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国对乌援助中“失踪”的1000亿美元在哪里？","title_en":"Where Is the Missing $100 Billion in U.S. Aid for Ukraine?","summary_cn":"针对泽连斯基称乌克兰仅收到175亿美元援助中的75亿、引发“1000亿美元失踪”猜测，CSIS高级顾问马克·坎西安撰文澄清：资金并未失踪。自2022年以来国会共拨款1752亿美元，其中约60%花在美国境内，约25%花在乌克兰，15%用于全球。援助涵盖武器替换、美国工业基础、乌克兰安全援助倡议、驻欧美军、人道援助、经济支持及其他机构支出。泽连斯基的75亿可能仅统计已宣布的军事装备。文章强调资金由美国军方、国务院及世界银行等机构管理，并非现金直送乌克兰。","summary_en":"Responding to Zelensky's claim that Ukraine received only $75 billion of $175 billion in U.S. aid, CSIS senior adviser Mark F. Cancian clarifies that no money is missing. Since 2022 Congress appropriated $175.2 billion, with roughly 60 percent spent in the United States, 25 percent in Ukraine, and 15 percent globally. Aid covers equipment replacement, the U.S. industrial base, the Ukraine Security Assistance Initiative, U.S. surge forces in Europe, humanitarian relief, economic support, and other agencies. Zelensky's $75 billion likely counts only announced military equipment. Funds are managed by the U.S. military, State Department, and World Bank, not delivered as cash.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:03:02.532381+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/where-missing-100-billion-us-aid-ukraine","content":"Where Is the Missing $100 Billion in U.S. Aid for Ukraine?\nPhoto: Anton Petrus/Adobe Stock\nCritical Questions\nby\nMark F. Cancian\nPublished February 11, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nOn February 2, President Volodymyr Zelensky of Ukraine said he had only received $75 billion of the $175 billion the United States had spent on Ukraine. The cry went up, what happened to the other $100 billion? Was it lost or stolen? The answer is no. Only part of the aid goes through Ukrainian control. A large part pays for activities as a result of the war but not to Ukraine directly. These include the United States training of Ukrainian forces, global humanitarian assistance, additional costs of U.S. surge forces in Europe, and intelligence support for both NATO and Ukraine.As CSIS has reported before, “aid to Ukraine” is a misnomer because 90 percent of military aid is spent in the United States. Of aid overall, 60 percent is spent in the United States, about 25 percent is spent in Ukraine, and the final 15 percent is spent globally.Q1: What did President Zelensky say that started this controversy?A1: In an interview with the Associated Press on February 2, Zelensky stated:When I hear that America gave Ukraine hundreds of billions [of dollars], 177, to be more precise . . . as the president of a warring country, I can tell you that we received just over 75 . . . We are talking about specific things, because we got it, not with money but with weapons. There is training, there is additional transport, there are not only prices for weapons. There were humanitarian programs, social, etc. . . . $100 billion of these 177, we never received. When it’s said that Ukraine received $200 billion to support the army during the war, that is not true. I don’t know where all the money is.The statement is a bit confusing since he was speaking off the cuff in a video interview. He refers to both $177 billion and $200 billion. He speaks about supporting the “army” where aid helped all elements of the Ukrainian armed forces, and he appears to include only weapons as aid “received.”Q2: How was that interpreted?A2: Many people asked where the “missing” $100 billion went and seized on the statement, “I don’t know where all the money is.”Comment sections of media reports flamed with accusations of corruption and malfeasance. Some online commentators speculated about corruption by the CIA, Ukrainian officials, or former President Joe Biden. Some linked it to viral social media claims alleging a conspiracy involving U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID) payments to celebrities to visit Ukraine. Others cited this as evidence of government waste, suggesting the Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE) investigate and identify cuts. DOGE had previously raised concerns about the U.S. government’s ability to track aid to Ukraine. Members of Congress and Elon Musk, who leads DOGE, amplified posts questioning the misuse of aids.General Keith Kellogg, the Trump administration’s special envoy for Ukraine and Russia, denied these interpretations. He noted that “we have a pretty good accounting of where it is going” and that most appropriated funds are spent in the United States.Nevertheless, the controversy has had an effect. In private conversations, Republican congressional staff members say that the confusion and hint of corruption have poisoned the possibility of further aid, at least in the near term.Q3: So, what happened to the “missing” $100 billion?A3: The short answer is that it is not missing. The funds went (mostly) to activities that arose because of the war, and all are accounted for. Some paid for sending equipment and funds directly to Ukraine. A large part went to activities that arose because of the war but were not spent in Ukraine. To see why this is the case, it is important to look at the funding as a whole.Since 2022, Congress has appropriated $175.2 billion in aid as a result of the war in Ukraine, a slight difference from the $177 billion that Zelensky cites. This includes all funds in the five supplemental appropriations, $900 million in the regular budget but excludes $9 billion in loans. Most has been delivered, and most of the remainder has been obligated through contracts and binding commitments. However, it will take many years for all the contracts to be completed and the money disbursed.In past analyses, CSIS has divided aid to Ukraine into categories to illustrate the different purposes. Figure 1 shows how the $175 billion is laid out in seven categories. It’s all there, the $75 billion Zelensky cites and the “missing” $100 billion.Military equipment: Replacement of equipment sent to Ukraine from U.S. stockpiles (through Presidential Drawdown Authority) and foreign military financing of equipment for Ukraine.U.S. industrial base and general Department of Defense (DOD) spending: Items that enhance U.S. military capabilities, particularly the defense industrial base, but are not directly related to the war in Ukraine.Ukraine Security Assistance Initiative (USAI): Mostly money for Ukraine to procure equipment directly from U.S. manufacturers, also supports maintenance of Ukrainian equipment, training of Ukrainian personnel and units, and Ukraine-related intelligence activities.U.S. forces: Funds for the extra costs of U.S. forces that surged to Europe in response to Russian aggression. Some of these surge forces have returned to the United States, but others remain.Humanitarian aid: Aid to relieve suffering because of the war. Mostly done through nongovernmental organizations in Europe for Ukrainian refugees, though some is spent in the United States and Ukraine. Some of the budget is also spent on global food relief. About $3 billion has gone to international development, unrelated to the war.Economic: U.S. funds are provided to the Ukrainian government through the World Bank to make up for lost tax revenues and to keep governmental services operating.Other U.S. agencies: Funds for activities in U.S. agencies other than the DOD, Department of State, and USAID, for example, for the Department of the Treasury to enforce sanctions and for the Department of Energy to safeguard nuclear materials.\nImage\nMark F. Cancian\nSenior Adviser, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nThree Years Later: Russia’s War in Ukraine\nRemote Visualization\nQ4: So, what did Zelensky mean when he said Ukraine had only received $75 billion?A4: It’s unclear how he did the math, but there are several plausible explanations.Most likely, he added the value of announced military equipment ($34.1 billion for drawdown, $33.2 billion for USAI, and $6.3 billion for Foreign Military Sales). That comes to $73.4 billion, close to the $75 billion Zelensky cited. However, not all that equipment has arrived, so calling it “received” is not accurate. “Committed” would be better.Zelensky might be using calculations done by the Kiel Institute in Germany, which tracks global support for Ukraine. Their number for U.S. military aid through October 31, 2024, is $64.5 billion; adding the $5.9 billion announced since then brings the total to $70.4 billion, close to the CSIS calculation.\nRemote Visualization\nA final approach (Figure 2) includes all aid going directly to Ukraine ($120 billion, yellow bar). This is mostly equipment, but also economic support and the humanitarian funds spent within Ukraine. This goes far beyond the quantity Zelensky cited but does make the point that the other pieces he mentions are quite important.Regardless of how Zelensky did the math, the bottom line is the same: no money is missing. Also important is that most of the money is not delivered directly to Ukraine but handled by trusted agencies, mostly the U.S. military, the Department of State/USAID, and the World Bank. The image of pallets of cash being sent to Ukraine is inaccurate. This doesn't say whether the United States should or should not provide more aid, but it does provide better information for making that decision.Mark F. Cancian (Colonel, U.S. Marine Corps Reserve, ret.) is a senior adviser with the International Security Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Chris Park assisted with the research.Chris Park and Emily Ezratty assisted with the research for this piece.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nRussia and Eurasia,\nUkraine,\nAllies and Partners,\nDefense Strategy and Capabilities, and\nGeopolitics and International Security","content_cn":"美国对乌援助中消失的1000亿美元去哪儿了？\n照片：Anton Petrus/Adobe Stock\n关键问题\n作者\nMark F. Cancian\n发表于2025年2月11日\n远程可视化\n2月2日，乌克兰总统弗拉基米尔·泽连斯基表示，美国在乌克兰问题上花费的1750亿美元中，他只收到了750亿美元。人们随即质问，另外1000亿美元去哪儿了？是丢失了还是被盗了？答案是否定的。只有部分援助经过乌克兰方面的控制。很大一部分资金用于因战争而开展的活动，但并非直接提供给乌克兰。这些活动包括美国对乌克兰军队的训练、全球人道主义援助、美国增派部队驻扎欧洲的额外费用，以及为北约和乌克兰提供的情报支持。正如战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）此前所报告的，“对乌援助”是个误称，因为90%的军事援助都花费在美国境内。就总体援助而言，60%花费在美国境内，约25%花费在乌克兰，最后15%花费在全球各地。Q1：泽连斯基总统说了什么，从而引发了这场争议？A1：在2月2日接受美联社采访时，泽连斯基表示：当我听到美国给了乌克兰数千亿美元——更准确地说是1770亿美元……作为一个处于战争中的国家的总统，我可以告诉你，我们只收到略高于750亿美元……我们说的是具体的东西，因为我们得到的不是钱，而是武器。有培训，有额外运输费用，不仅仅是武器的价格。还有人道主义项目、社会项目等等……这1770亿美元中，有1000亿美元我们从未收到。有人说乌克兰在战争期间收到了2000亿美元来支持军队，这不是真的。我不知道所有这些钱都去哪儿了。这段话有些令人困惑，因为他是在视频采访中即兴发言。他既提到了1770亿美元，也提到了2000亿美元。他谈到支持“军队”，而援助实际上帮助了乌克兰武装部队的所有组成部分；而且，他似乎只把作为援助“收到”的武器计算在内。Q2：这番话是如何被解读的？A2：许多人追问那“消失的”1000亿美元去了哪里，并抓住了“我不知道所有这些钱都去哪儿了”这句话。媒体报道的评论区充斥着关于腐败和渎职的指责。一些网络评论员猜测，中央情报局、乌克兰官员或前总统乔·拜登存在腐败行为。一些人将其与社交媒体上疯传的说法联系起来，声称其中存在一项阴谋：美国国际开发署（USAID）向名人支付费用，让他们访问乌克兰。另一些人以此作为政府浪费的证据，建议政府效率部（DOGE）进行调查并找出可以削减的开支。DOGE此前曾对美国政府追踪对乌援助的能力表示担忧。国会议员以及领导DOGE的埃隆·马斯克放大了质疑援助被滥用的帖子。特朗普政府负责乌克兰和俄罗斯事务的特使基思·凯洛格将军否认了这些解读。他指出，“我们对资金流向有相当完善的记录”，而且大多数获批资金都花费在美国境内。然而，这场争议已经产生了影响。共和党国会工作人员在私下交谈中表示，这种混乱以及腐败的暗示已经毒化了进一步援助的可能性，至少在近期如此。Q3：那么，那“消失的”1000亿美元究竟去哪儿了？A3：简短的答案是\n也就是说，这些资金并没有缺失。资金（大部分）用于因战争而产生的活动，而且都有账可查。其中一部分用于直接向乌克兰运送装备和资金。很大一部分用于因战争而产生、但并未在乌克兰境内支出的活动。要了解为什么会这样，重要的是从整体上看待这些资金。自2022年以来，美国国会因乌克兰战争拨款1752亿美元援助，这与泽连斯基所说的1770亿美元略有差异。其中包括五项补充拨款中的全部资金，以及常规预算中的9亿美元，但不包括90亿美元贷款。大部分资金已经交付，其余大部分也已通过合同和具有约束力的承诺落实。不过，所有合同完成并将资金拨付完毕需要许多年。过去的分析中，CSIS将对乌克兰的援助分为不同类别，以说明其不同用途。图1展示了1750亿美元如何分为七类。资金都在那里，包括泽连斯基所说的750亿美元和那“缺失”的1000亿美元。军事装备：通过总统提取授权（Presidential Drawdown Authority）从美国库存中替换运往乌克兰的装备，以及为乌克兰装备提供的外国军事融资。美国工业基础和国防部（DOD）一般支出：增强美国军事能力、尤其是国防工业基础的项目，但与乌克兰战争没有直接关系。乌克兰安全援助倡议（USAI）：主要用于乌克兰直接向美国制造商采购装备，也用于维护乌克兰装备、培训乌克兰人员和部队，以及开展与乌克兰有关的情报活动。美国部队：用于支付因应对俄罗斯侵略而增派至欧洲的美国部队的额外费用。其中一些增派部队已返回美国，但其他部队仍留在那里。人道主义援助：用于减轻战争造成的苦难的援助。大部分通过欧洲的非政府组织提供给乌克兰难民，但其中一部分在美国和乌克兰境内支出。部分预算还用于全球粮食救济。约30亿美元用于与战争无关的国际发展。经济援助：美国通过世界银行向乌克兰政府提供资金，以弥补税收收入损失并维持政府服务运转。其他美国机构：用于国防部、国务院和美国国际开发署（USAID）以外的美国机构开展活动的资金，例如用于财政部执行制裁，以及能源部保障核材料安全。\n图片\nMark F. Cancian\n国防与安全部高级顾问\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n三年之后：俄罗斯在乌克兰的战争\n远程可视化\n问4：那么，泽连斯基说乌克兰只收到750亿美元时，他是什么意思？答4：目前不清楚他是如何计算的，但有几种合理的解释。最有可能的是，他把已宣布的军事装备价值相加（提取授权340亿美元、USAI 332亿美元，以及外国军售63亿美元）。总额为734亿美元，接近泽连斯基所说的750亿美元。不过，并非所有这些装备都已抵达，因此称其为“已收到”并不准确。称为“已承诺”更合适。泽连斯基可能使用了德国基尔研究所的计算方法，该研究所追踪全球对\n乌克兰。截至2024年10月31日，他们获得的美国军事援助金额为645亿美元；加上此后宣布的59亿美元，总额达到704亿美元，与CSIS的计算结果接近。\n\n远程可视化\n\n最后一种方法（图2）包括所有直接流向乌克兰的援助（1200亿美元，黄色柱）。这主要是装备，也包括经济支持以及在乌克兰境内支出的​​人道主义资金。这远远超出了泽连斯基所引用的数量，但确实说明了他提到的其他部分相当重要。无论泽连斯基是如何计算的，底线都是一样的：没有钱失踪。同样重要的是，大部分资金并不是直接交付给乌克兰，而是由受信任的机构处理，主要包括美国军方、国务院/美国国际开发署以及世界银行。把成托盘现金运往乌克兰的形象并不准确。这并没有说明美国是否应该提供更多援助，但确实为作出这一决定提供了更好的信息。马克·F·坎西安（美国海军陆战队预备役退役上校）是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心国际安全项目的高级顾问。克里斯·帕克协助了研究工作。克里斯·帕克和艾米莉·埃兹拉蒂协助了本文的研究工作。\n\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究是无党派且非专有的。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n\n© 2025年战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n俄罗斯与欧亚大陆，\n乌克兰，\n盟友与伙伴，\n防务战略与能力，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-02-11T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-781","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"谁应塑造发展的未来，以及如何塑造？","title_en":"Who Should Shape the Future of Development, and How?","summary_cn":"这是CSIS关于美国对外援助未来三部分系列文章的第三部分。文章指出，特朗普政府解散美国国际开发署并取消发展赠款，加之全球官方发展援助下降，国际发展领域出现前所未有的动荡。作者基于2025年9月至2026年3月在CSIS举行的圆桌讨论，聚焦公民社会、慈善部门和私营部门三类利益相关方，提出建议，强调应让受影响社区在决策中发挥核心作用。","summary_en":"This is Part III of a CSIS series on the future of U.S. foreign assistance. It notes unprecedented disruption from the Trump administration's dismantling of USAID and cancellation of development grants, alongside declining global ODA. Drawing on CSIS roundtables held from September 2025 to March 2026, the authors focus on three stakeholder groups—civil society, philanthropy, and the private sector—and offer recommendations to center affected communities in decisionmaking.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:16:28.538348+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/who-should-shape-future-development-and-how","content":"Who Should Shape the Future of Development, and How?\nPhoto: Prasanth/Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nAndrew Friedman\nand\nHadeil Ali\nPublished June 10, 2026\nRemote Visualization\nThis analysis is Part III of a three-part series covering the need and urgency to center rightsholders in the future of U.S. foreign assistance. In a moment of tremendous flux, these commentaries offer analysis and recommendations for a new model rooted in local consultation and input from those most impacted by foreign assistance. Read Part I here and Part II here.IntroductionThe last few years have seen unprecedented disruption in the world of international development. At the top of the list is the Trump administration’s dismantling of the U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID) and cancellation of development grants, but these actions are part of a broader pattern. The majority of countries with foreign assistance budgets across the globe have reduced official development assistance (ODA). While these decisions have been particularly impactful and have spawned conversations about the future of development and foreign assistance, the reality is that many of these conversations have been ongoing for decades.Discussions of decisionmaking, incentives, stakeholders, inefficiencies, and constituencies—ultimately, conversations about power—have long been front of mind for practitioners of international development and those most impacted by its efforts. While the upheaval since January 2025 has damaged trust and relationships and caused preventable death and conflict, there now exists a possibility to reimagine international development and make changes that were difficult, perhaps even impossible, just a few years ago.That these conversations are not new does not diminish their necessity, their timeliness, or their difficulty. A true shift of power in decisionmaking and priority setting requires the buy-in and action of stakeholders, the creativity and pragmatism of individuals, and the political will and courage of leaders. This analysis focuses on three stakeholder groups who will play an outsized role in the future of development: civil society, philanthropy, and the private sector. All three groups were part of a series of roundtables held under the Chatham House rule at CSIS from September 2025 through March 2026. The authors propose a list of recommendations for each group below. Each recommendation is necessary to challenge and shift the power dynamic between donors and impacted community, ultimately ensuring greater sustainability, efficiency, and impact of development efforts.Two throughlines have emerged connecting the recommendations for each group:Do not forget the lessons and structures that already exist. Previous work on international development has provided many lessons about what works and what does not, and these should not be forgotten. Additionally, both through previous work and through independent state building and development, many robust structures already exist. Building structures that run in parallel to functional ones is inefficient, unsustainable, and ensures that structural power imbalances endure.Without coordination, efforts are destined to be piecemeal and lack efficiency. A recurring message from the roundtables held at CSIS was the sheer number of ongoing high-level “future of development” and “future of foreign assistance” conversations. One participant, whose organization had conducted a mapping exercise of civil society–led initiatives, counted 39 such efforts. Another participant from the philanthropy sector put the number at 70. The level of expertise and thought on what the future will look like is undoubtedly a boon and could lead to impressive innovation and a true reimagining. However, if such efforts are not coordinated, they are destined to be repetitive, inefficient, and occasionally at cross-purposes. PhilanthropyFoundations and other philanthropic organizations play a key role in the future of development that has greater needs and less ODA. However, even a fully aligned and coordinated philanthropy sector cannot fill the gap left by governments. According to estimates from the Organisation of Economic Co-Ordination and Development, global ODA dropped by $56 billion over two years (2023–2025). By contrast, the entirety of private philanthropic giving in the international development sphere is approximately $17 billion annually. This creates a difficult strategic calculus, where the need is immediate and great, but the necessity of being strategic and pointed is unending.Theme 1: Role ClarityBe clear about philanthropy’s role as an enabler and catalyst, giving civil society the space to lead the design of the reimagined development landscape.Leverage the ability to be nimble, fast, and take risks that governments and multilateral institutions cannot to enable strategic funding and decisionmaking.Build accountability measures to ensure the goals outlined are not only theoretical or aspirational.Support learning efforts to break siloes, facilitate learnings, and act as a helpful knowledge production tool.Theme 2: Funding StructureEvolve funding systems from project-based grants to unrestricted multiyear funding in order to alleviate the administrative burden on local organizations.Further expand participatory grantmaking, placing impacted communities at the center of the process and rebalancing the decisionmaking equation.Invest in more pooled funds for more strategic, impactful, and effective investments.Expand funding to support Global South networks and coalitions to invest in institutional capacity.Theme 3: LocalizationWhile this is not a new conversation in the philanthropic and broader development ecosystem, this moment has presented a renewed interest and opportunity to intentionally prioritize this issue.Address challenges around power dynamics, inclusion, and equity. Organizations’ own internal structures and operating models are key starting points to ensure these concepts are internalized.Act as an institutional bridge between Global North and Global South countries to form equitable partnershipsExamine preconceived notions of “partners” to ensure civil society actors are viewed as experts, and not merely beneficiaries or implementers.Recognize the existing local initiatives and solutions at play and find ways to amplify and support these efforts.There is an immense trust gap in the current development landscape, and philanthropy should determine its role in rebuilding that trust with existing and future partners. Civil SocietyPerhaps no sector has borne the brunt of cuts and attacks more than civil society. According to the Global Aid Freeze Tracker, which has had four rounds of surveys since February 2025, more than 70 percent of civil society organizations responded that they had to lay off some staff, with nearly 40 percent responding that they had to lay off 31 percent or more. The surveys have also demonstrated decreasing confidence in survival, with an ever-greater number of organizations reporting they are at risk of closure. Despite this context, civil society seeks to continue its vital work providing healthcare and education and advocating for greater transparency and accountability, along with countless other functions across the globe.Theme 1: PositioningEstablish a foundational infrastructure that enables the key development prerequisites for a healthy, stable, and prosperous society. Philanthropy, private sector, and other actors must leverage and support civil society and overall local expertise to enable the most durable outcomes.Be realistic and honest about the current rapidly evolving political and financial environment to ensure proactive adaptation of approaches, strategies, and partnerships.Anchor efforts with three key steps: (1) define the root problem, (2) assess whether preexisting solutions are in place, and (3) identify what systemic barriers are preventing the solutions from working.Highlight successful partnerships through storytelling and quantitative data.Strengthen community engagement and accountability measures given the massive trust gap in the sector.Theme 2: Resource DiversificationMove beyond traditional aid and find innovating fundraising solutions to survive the current environmentFrame the work as social profit that advances system-level solutions. In a moment where messaging is key, such a framing would help diversify resources and ensure a focus on long-term resilience The Private Sector While companies have always contributed to development, intentional efforts to partner with local groups and nurture development outside of regular business operations have seldom been a significant focus area. The closure of USAID, reduced ODA, and diminished government capabilities in the international development sector from both the U.S. government and other traditional donors has led to substantial gaps between what the private sector needs continue its work and what is actually being done. Thankfully, multiple models exist for ramping up support for civil society through both the provision of resources and other means.Theme 1: Understand the “What”Align goals in the development space with corporate goals. This can include geographic considerations as well as substantive ones.Develop an understanding of systems and trusted stakeholders that are working or have worked toward these goals.Theme 2: Determine the “How”Work with embedded organizations to develop a model that allows for full independence, thus utilizing trust and relationships without causing them damage.Develop both internal and external messaging that reinforces and improves understanding of the value of the work with diverse partners and stakeholders.Theme 3: Delineate Support Versus Public RelationsWork with trusted interlocutors to necessarily limit control over funds as well as actions of organizations receiving support. Understand it is the role of civil society to push for improvements, and that can involve criticism of action and of companies providing funding.Understand the ecosystem well enough to be responsive to civil society and to be solution-oriented, while seeing the difference between broad, community-wide suggestions and less constructive or less widely held considerations. ConclusionThe above recommendations are not meant to be comprehensive, nor are they meant to exist in a vacuum. While they are organized by stakeholder group, none stand alone, and each group has a role to play in ensuring that the other stakeholders are held accountable. Other groups, including multilateral organizations and governments, will undoubtedly also play significant roles in the future of development should also focus on improving efficiency, sustainability, and the benefit to impacted populations. Without all groups in alignment and ensuring that development is locally owned and informed, development’s impact will remain diminished.Andrew Friedman is director and senior fellow in the Human Rights Initiative at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Hadeil Ali is chief of staff of the Global Development Department at CSIS.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2026 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nHuman Rights, and\nInternational Development\nImage\nAndrew Friedman\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Human Rights Initiative\nImage\nHadeil Ali\nChief of Staff, Global Development Department\nPrograms & Projects\nGlobal Development\nHuman Rights Initiative","content_cn":"谁应塑造发展的未来，以及如何塑造？\n照片：Prasanth/Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nAndrew Friedman\n和\nHadeil Ali\n发表于2026年6月10日\n远程可视化\n本文是三篇系列文章的第三部分，探讨在美国对外援助的未来中，以权利持有者为中心的必要性和紧迫性。在巨变时刻，这些评论文章为一种植根于地方协商、并纳入受对外援助影响最深者意见的新模式，提供分析和建议。点击此处阅读第一部分，点击此处阅读第二部分。\n引言\n过去几年，国际发展领域经历了前所未有的动荡。首要事件是特朗普政府拆解美国国际开发署（USAID）并取消发展援助拨款，但这些行动只是更广泛模式的一部分。全球大多数拥有对外援助预算的国家都削减了官方发展援助（ODA）。尽管这些决定影响尤其深远，并引发了关于发展和对外援助未来的讨论，但现实是，其中许多讨论几十年来一直在持续。\n关于决策、激励机制、利益相关者、低效和支持群体的讨论——归根结底，是关于权力的讨论——长期以来一直是国际发展从业者以及最受其努力影响者关注的重点。自2025年1月以来的动荡损害了信任与关系，并造成了本可避免的死亡和冲突，但如今重新构想国际发展并作出几年前难以、甚至可能不可能实现的改变，已经成为可能。\n这些讨论并非新鲜事，并不减损其必要性、时效性或难度。决策和优先事项设定中的真正权力转移，需要利益相关者的认同与行动、个人的创造力与务实精神，以及领导者的政治意愿和勇气。本文重点关注三类将在发展未来中发挥超常作用的利益相关者：公民社会、慈善界和私营部门。2025年9月至2026年3月期间，这三个群体都参加了在战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）依据查塔姆宫规则举行的一系列圆桌会议。作者在下文为每个群体提出了一系列建议。每项建议都必须用于挑战并改变捐助方与受影响社区之间的权力动态，最终确保发展工作具有更大的可持续性、效率和影响力。\n连接各群体建议的主线有两条：\n不要忘记已经存在的经验教训和结构。以往的国际发展工作已经提供了许多关于什么有效、什么无效的经验，这些不应被遗忘。此外，无论是通过以往工作，还是通过独立的国家建设与发展，许多稳健的结构已经存在。与有效结构并行建设结构既低效又不可持续，并会确保结构性权力失衡持续存在。\n没有协调，各项努力注定会零散推进并缺乏效率。在CSIS举行的圆桌会议上，一个反复出现的信息是，正在进行的高层“发展未来”和“对外援助未来”讨论数量之多。一位所在组织曾开展过一项公民\n由社会主导的倡议，统计到39项此类努力。慈善领域的另一位参与者则认为这一数字为70。对于未来将会怎样，相关专业知识和思考的水平无疑是一项福祉，并可能带来令人瞩目的创新和真正的重新构想。然而，如果这些努力缺乏协调，它们注定会重复、低效，并且偶尔相互冲突。\n\n慈善事业\n基金会和其他慈善组织在发展事业的未来中发挥着关键作用，而这一未来的需求更大、官方发展援助（ODA）更少。然而，即使慈善领域实现了充分一致和协调，也无法填补政府留下的缺口。根据经济合作与发展组织的估算，全球官方发展援助在两年间（2023—2025年）减少了560亿美元。相比之下，国际发展领域私人慈善捐赠总额每年约为170亿美元。这形成了一个艰难的战略权衡：需求迫切且巨大，但采取战略性和精准行动的必要性始终存在。\n\n主题1：角色清晰\n明确慈善事业作为赋能者和催化剂的角色，为民间社会留出空间，引领重新构想的发展格局的设计。\n利用政府和多边机构无法具备的灵活、快速和承担风险的能力，以促成战略性资助和决策。\n建立问责措施，确保所概述的目标不仅停留在理论或愿景层面。\n支持学习工作，打破信息孤岛，促进经验学习，并发挥有益的知识生产工具作用。\n\n主题2：资助结构\n将资助体系从基于项目的拨款转向不受限制的多年期资助，以减轻当地组织的行政负担。\n进一步扩大参与式拨款，让受影响社区处于流程核心，并重新平衡决策方程。\n投资于更多资金池，以实现更具战略性、更有影响力和更有效的投资。\n扩大资助，支持全球南方的网络和联盟，投资于机构能力。\n\n主题3：本地化\n尽管这并不是慈善生态系统及更广泛发展生态系统中的新话题，但当前时刻带来了对这一问题重新关注并有意优先考虑它的机会。\n应对权力动态、包容性和公平方面的挑战。组织自身的内部结构和运作模式是确保这些理念内化的关键起点。\n在全球北方和全球南方国家之间发挥机构桥梁作用，建立公平的伙伴关系。\n审视对“伙伴”的先入之见，确保民间社会行动者被视为专家，而不仅仅是受益者或执行者。\n承认现有的当地倡议和解决方案，并寻找扩大和支持这些努力的方式。\n当前的发展格局中存在巨大的信任鸿沟，慈善事业应确定自身在与现有及未来伙伴重建信任方面的作用。\n\n民间社会\n或许没有哪个领域比民间社会更能承受削减和攻击的冲击。根据“全球援助冻结追踪器”的数据——该追踪器自2025年2月以来已开展四轮调查——超过70%的民间社会组织表示不得不裁减部分员工，其中近40%的组织表示不得不裁减31%或更多员工。这些调查还显示，\n对生存的信心不断下降，越来越多的组织报告称它们面临关闭风险。尽管处于这样的背景下，民间社会仍寻求继续开展其至关重要的工作，提供医疗保健和教育，倡导更大的透明度和问责制，以及在全球范围内履行无数其他职能。主题1：定位建立基础性基础设施，为健康、稳定和繁荣的社会提供关键发展前提。慈善事业、私营部门和其他参与者必须利用并支持民间社会以及当地整体专业知识，以促成最持久的成果。现实而诚实地面对当前迅速变化的政治和金融环境，以确保主动调整方法、战略和伙伴关系。围绕三个关键步骤开展工作：（1）界定根本问题；（2）评估是否已有解决方案；（3）确定哪些系统性障碍阻碍解决方案发挥作用。通过讲故事和定量数据突出成功的伙伴关系。鉴于该领域存在巨大的信任鸿沟，加强社区参与和问责措施。主题2：资源多元化超越传统援助，寻找创新的筹资解决方案，以在当前环境中生存。将工作定位为推动系统层面解决方案的社会利润。在信息传递至关重要的时刻，这种定位将有助于实现资源多元化，并确保关注长期韧性。私营部门虽然企业一直为发展作出贡献，但有意与当地团体建立伙伴关系，并在常规业务运营之外培育发展的努力，很少成为重点领域。美国国际开发署（USAID）关闭、官方发展援助（ODA）减少，以及美国政府和其他传统捐助方在国际发展领域的政府能力减弱，导致私营部门继续开展工作所需的支持与实际正在采取的行动之间出现了巨大缺口。值得庆幸的是，已有多种模式可通过提供资源和其他方式来扩大对民间社会的支持。主题1：理解“是什么”使发展领域的目标与企业目标保持一致。这既可以包括地域方面的考量，也可以包括实质性方面的考量。了解正在或曾经致力于实现这些目标的系统和受信任利益相关者。主题2：确定“如何做”与深度嵌入当地的组织合作，制定一种能够实现完全独立的模式，从而利用信任和关系而不损害它们。制定内部和外部信息传递方案，强化并增进对与多元伙伴和利益相关者合作之价值的理解。主题3：划清支持与公共关系的界限与受信任的对话者合作，必要地限制对资金以及接受支持的组织行动的控制。理解推动改进是民间社会的职责，这可能包括对提供资金的公司及其行动提出批评。充分了解生态系统，以便回应民间社会并以解决方案为导向，同时辨别广泛的、社区层面的建议与建设性较弱或持有范围较小的考量之间的差异。结论上述建议并非意在面面俱到，也并非意在孤立存在。尽管它们按利益相关者群体组织，但没有任何一个\n各自独立存在，并且每个群体都应发挥作用，确保其他利益相关者受到问责。其他群体，包括多边组织和政府，毫无疑问也将在未来的发展中发挥重要作用，同时也应着力提高效率和可持续性，并使受影响人口受益。如果所有群体不能保持一致，不能确保发展由当地拥有并以当地情况为依据，发展的影响力将继续受到削弱。\n\nAndrew Friedman 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）人权倡议项目的主任兼高级研究员。Hadeil Ali 是 CSIS 全球发展部主任办公室主任。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS 是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人、免税机构。其研究具有非党派性，不受专有利益影响。CSIS 不持有具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n\n© 2026 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n人权，以及\n国际发展\n\n图片\nAndrew Friedman\n人权倡议项目主任兼高级研究员\n\n图片\nHadeil Ali\n全球发展部主任办公室主任\n\n项目与计划\n全球发展\n人权倡议","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-06-10T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3291","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"为什么美国上空有如此多不明无人机在飞行？","title_en":"Why Are There So Many Unexplained Drones Flying Over the United States?","summary_cn":"自2024年11月中旬起，美国东海岸多州居民报告夜间出现疑似无人机的不明飞行物，联邦与州当局未能解释或证实，但强调不构成安全威胁。文章分析当局难以识别无人机的原因，包括传统雷达局限、Remote ID执行不足，介绍美国无人机监管规则、可用的反无人机手段，并列举近期其他不明无人机目击事件。","summary_en":"Since mid-November 2024, residents across several East Coast states have reported unexplained nighttime drone sightings that federal and state authorities have been unable to explain or corroborate, though officials stress no security threat. The commentary examines why authorities struggle to identify drones—traditional radar limits and weak Remote ID compliance—reviews U.S. UAS regulations and counter-drone options, and lists other recent sightings.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:10:54.093422+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/why-are-there-so-many-unexplained-drones-flying-over-united-states","content":"Why Are There So Many Unexplained Drones Flying Over the United States?\nPhoto: John Lund/Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nClayton Swope\nPublished December 16, 2024\nRemote Visualization\nSince mid-November 2024, U.S. residents in several East Coast states have reported hovering and low-flying bright lights at night that many observers assess to be uncrewed aerial systems (UASs), commonly called drones. To date, federal and state authorities have been unable to explain or corroborate the sightings, suggesting that some may be attributed to crewed airplanes or helicopters. Despite the inconclusive results of their investigative efforts, government officials have stressed that the sightings do not appear to pose a public safety or national security threat. In the meantime, local residents and lawmakers have expressed mounting concerns, calling on the government to do more to stop the drones and understand their purpose. These are only the latest in a series of drone sightings over the last several years—including over sensitive sites in the United States and allied countries—to confound authorities, raising questions about the ability of the U.S. government to identify and track drones operating in U.S. airspace, as well as assess and respond to UAS threats.Q1: What have people on the East Coast seen in the night sky over the last month?A1: On November 19, 2024, the prosecutor’s office in Morris County, New Jersey, issued a press release stating that county agencies were investigating reports of drone activity, which had been observed by local law enforcement officers the previous evening. Since then, residents in other parts of New Jersey and across several nearby states, including Connecticut, Maryland, Massachusetts, New York, and Pennsylvania, have reported dozens of similarly unexplained UAS sightings. The Maryland governor reported seeing dozens of large drones over his residence on December 12, 2024. On December 13, 2024, a Navy public affairs official confirmed that unidentified drones have repeatedly entered the restricted airspace over Naval Weapons Station Earle in New Jersey. Drones have also been reported near other critical infrastructure and the U.S. Army’s Picatinny Arsenal.The FBI is leading the investigation into the UAS sightings, with support from the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and state and local law enforcement organizations. So far, White House, FBI, and DHS officials have all stated that they have no reason to believe the sightings pose any safety or security threat. Additionally, a Pentagon spokesperson confirmed that the U.S. military is not operating these drones, noting as well that defense officials do not think the drones were sent by a “foreign entity or adversary.” However, lawmakers continue to press federal officials for answers, with senators from New Jersey and New York sending a letter to the FBI, DHS, FAA, and other congressional leaders questioning the federal response during a recent hearing. In addition to expressing frustration about the government’s inability to explain these sightings, many have called on the U.S. military to shoot down the drones.Q2: Why can’t authorities identify the drones responsible for these sightings?A2: The FAA is responsible for integrating UAS operations into the National Airspace System (NAS), which is the air traffic control service managing over 45,000 flights per day across the almost 30 million square miles of U.S. airspace. Drones are difficult to track using traditional radar systems, which best track objects with large radar cross sections and at higher altitudes than ones at which UAS typically operate. Though radar systems sometimes can detect drones, they may mistake those objects for birds since radar alone cannot classify detected objects. That drones can fly erratically and quickly change speeds, as well as operate in large groups or swarms, like many birds, also makes them more difficult to track using traditional radar. Historically, efforts by the U.S. military to identify and track airborne threats to the homeland focus on ballistic missiles and bombers, meaning that sensors and algorithms processing radar data are not tuned to UAS threats. Additionally, not all data from sensors operated by civil agencies, such as the FAA and National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, has been integrated into homeland defense military tracking architectures, meaning that neither military nor civilian officials have the full picture of potential airborne threats in U.S. airspace. In addition to the impacts on drone tracking, the focus on ballistic missiles and bombers and the lack of full military-civil sensor integration partly explains how some Chinese high-altitude balloons flying over the United States during the past several years went undetected, demonstrating what a senior military official called a “domain awareness gap.”To overcome the shortcomings of traditional radar, officials in New Jersey announced they will be using an advanced radar system that works in combination with a heat sensor and camera to track and identify the unknown drones. Additionally, a network of acoustic sensors can be used, as proven in Ukraine, to successfully identify and track drones. Though it would take time to deploy such a system along the East Coast, the deployment of a similar network of acoustic sensors in the United States, particularly around sensitive sites like critical infrastructure, airports, and military facilities, could help identify and track drones in the future.Drones weighing more than 250 grams are required to broadcast identifying information, including location and altitude, velocity, and location of the human controller, as part of an FAA initiative called Remote ID. Certain drones, however, such as ones operated by the U.S. government, are not required to broadcast Remote ID signals. Though local law enforcement organizations may have difficulties accessing Remote ID data, federal officials should have access to this “digital license plate” and information about the drones’ operators and operations. That federal officials do not appear to have gleaned much information about the drones and their operators from Remote ID suggests that many UAS are not complying with the Remote ID rule, which went into effect in April 2024 as part of FAA regulations covering drone operations in U.S. airspace.Q3: How does the United States regulate UAS operations?A3: The FAA, part of the Department of Transportation, is responsible for regulating U.S. civil aviation and ensuring the safe, efficient use of the nation’s airspace, including by both civilian and military operators. Some states and localities place additional rules beyond FAA requirements on drone operators. The FAA requires the registration of any drone that weighs more than 250 grams (about half of a pound). As of October 1, 2024, there are almost 800,000 drones registered with the FAA in the United States. Two sets of FAA rules cover most drones: one for drones that weigh less than 55 pounds, which the FAA classified as a small UAS, and another for drones larger than 55 pounds.According to these rules, small UAS operators cannot fly above 400 feet, require authorization to fly in controlled airspaces, such as near airports, and must see their drones at all times while flying, among other rules. Drone operators can qualify for waivers to many of these individual rules, though operators must comply with more rigorous standards to obtain them. While many quad-copter drones weigh, at most, only a few pounds and can fit in the palm of the hand, other fixed-wing drones classified as small UAS have wingspans approaching eight feet. Some drones used for commercial purposes, such as package delivery, chemical and agricultural product dispensing, and air taxis, weigh more than 55 pounds and are covered under specific FAA rules. Additionally, UAS performing a governmental function, no matter the weight, can operate under special rules. Specifically, the U.S. military operates a variety of drones of different sizes, with the largest having maximum takeoff weights of close to 35,000 pounds and 130-foot wingspans—a longer wingspan than a Boeing 737 passenger jet.Q4: What can the authorities do to stop these drones?A4: There are a number of technologies, such as signal jamming, cyber operations, kinetic weapons, like bullets, and high-energy weapons, that can be used to disable or destroy drones. No private individual or entity in the United States can legally shoot at a drone, as it is prohibited under federal law to shoot at any crewed or uncrewed aircraft. However, there are certain agencies that can lawfully take counter UAS measures. In 2018, DHS was granted authority to counter UAS threats, but that authority is limited to protecting facilities or assets that support the DHS mission, certain events, and other specified special operations. The ability of the U.S. military to respond to drone threats over U.S. soil is also limited. Interpreting their statutory authorities, defense officials have proposed the use of signal jammers, cyberattacks, nets, and “strong, stringy streamers” to stop drones threatening national security facilities in the United States. Typically, local law enforcement agencies also do not have the authority to disable drones, though the New York Police Department has petitioned federal officials to provide it with such authority, citing the prevalence of drone threats to New York City.Ultimately, federal authorities assert that these drone sightings do not signal any threat, so regardless of which agency has the authority to disable drones, the absence of a threat negates any need for action. It remains to be seen why Remote ID cannot be used to glean more information about the drone operators. Unless the drones were being operated by the U.S. government, they should be broadcasting Remote ID signals. If none of the drones involved in the sightings had Remote ID installed or broadcasting, federal authorities might as well state as much so as to clarify that discrepancy. If confirmed, the lack of Remote ID broadcasts would suggest that the success of FAA efforts to integrate UASs into the NAS has been limited.Q5: What other unidentified drone sightings have occurred recently?A5: No matter the resolution to these recent sightings, these recent reports of unidentified drones are only the tip of the iceberg in both the United States and allied nations. Unidentified drones were sighted operating near a U.S. air base in Germany in early December 2024. In November 2024, unexplained drone operations were reported over four U.S. military bases in the United Kingdom, and a Chinese citizen was arrested for flying a drone over Vandenberg Space Force Base in California. Numerous drones were reportedly observed near Langley Air Force Base in Virginia over the past year. In fact, the joint U.S.-Canadian North American Aerospace Defense Command officially reported in October 2024 that there had been around 600 unauthorized drone incursions over U.S. military sites since 2022. Though the sightings over defense installations during the past year and ongoing events over the East Coast may not pose threats to public safety and security, future ones might.What the string of unexplained sightings demonstrates is that the United States has an incomplete picture of drone activity in U.S. airspace, primarily due to the unsuitability of traditional radar to track small, low-flying drones. Significant investments in radar infrastructure and federal efforts, including the creation of the FAA, on aircraft traffic control that began in the 1950s laid the foundation for the nation’s air traffic control system that today provides officials a comprehensive real-time ability to monitor conventional crewed aircraft operating across the entire nation. Investments in UAS surveillance technologies on a national scale will be needed to provide the same capabilities to track drones—Remote ID is not enough because an uncooperative or hostile drone operator can simply disable the broadcast. What these sightings also show is that officials are hesitant to take action to disable drones whose operators and purposes remain opaque. In wartime or a crisis, such hesitation could result in casualties and damage to critical infrastructure, possibly under attack by hostile drones. Civilian and military officials should heed this urgent clarion call to improve and accelerate their capabilities to identify, track, and respond to drone threats over U.S. soil.Clayton Swope is the deputy director of the Aerospace Security Project and a senior fellow in the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSpace and Aerospace\nImage\nClayton Swope\nDeputy Director, Aerospace Security Project and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nAerospace Security Project\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"为什么有这么多无法解释的无人机飞越美国？\n照片：John Lund/Getty Images\n关键问题\n作者\nClayton Swope\n发表于2024年12月16日\n远程可视化\n自2024年11月中旬以来，美国东海岸多个州的居民报告称，夜间看到悬停和低空飞行的明亮灯光，许多观察者判断这些是无人驾驶航空系统（UAS），通常称为无人机。截至目前，联邦和州当局一直无法解释或证实这些目击事件，这表明其中一些可能是有人驾驶的飞机或直升机所致。尽管调查工作未得出确凿结果，政府官员仍强调，这些目击事件似乎不会对公共安全或国家安全构成威胁。与此同时，当地居民和议员的担忧日益加剧，呼吁政府采取更多措施阻止无人机并了解其用途。这些只是过去几年一系列无人机目击事件中的最新一例——包括在美国及其盟国的敏感地点上空发生的事件——它们令当局困惑，并引发了人们对美国政府识别和跟踪在美国领空运行的无人机、以及评估和应对UAS威胁能力的质疑。\n问题1：过去一个月，东海岸的人们在夜空中看到了什么？答案1：2024年11月19日，新泽西州莫里斯县检察官办公室发布新闻稿称，县内机构正在调查无人机活动报告；当地执法人员前一晚观察到了这些活动。此后，新泽西州其他地区以及附近几个州（包括康涅狄格州、马里兰州、马萨诸塞州、纽约州和宾夕法尼亚州）的居民报告了数十起同样无法解释的UAS目击事件。马里兰州州长报告称，2024年12月12日，他在住所上空看到了数十架大型无人机。2024年12月13日，一名海军公共事务官员证实，不明无人机曾多次进入新泽西州埃尔海军武器站上空的限制空域。据报道，其他关键基础设施以及美国陆军皮卡汀尼兵工厂附近也出现了无人机。\n联邦调查局（FBI）正在牵头调查这些UAS目击事件，国土安全部（DHS）、联邦航空管理局（FAA）以及州和地方执法机构提供支持。截至目前，白宫、FBI和DHS官员均表示，他们没有理由相信这些目击事件会构成任何安全或安保威胁。此外，五角大楼发言人证实，美国军方并未操作这些无人机，并指出国防官员也不认为这些无人机是由“外国实体或对手”派遣的。然而，议员们仍继续向联邦官员追问答案；新泽西州和纽约州的参议员致信FBI、DHS、FAA及其他国会领导人，在最近的一次听证会上质疑联邦政府的应对。除了对政府无法解释这些目击事件表示失望外，许多人还呼吁美国军方击落这些无人机。\n问题2：当局为什么无法识别造成这些目击事件的无人机？答案2：FAA负责将UAS运行整合进国家空域系统（NAS）。该系统是空中交通管制服务，每天管理着超过45,000架次航班，覆盖面积近3,000万平方英里的空域。\n美国空域。使用传统雷达系统很难跟踪无人机，因为传统雷达最擅长跟踪雷达截面积较大的物体，以及飞行高度高于无人机系统通常运行高度的物体。尽管雷达系统有时能够探测到无人机，但由于单靠雷达无法对探测到的物体进行分类，这些物体可能会被误认为是鸟类。无人机能够不规则飞行并迅速改变速度，还能像许多鸟类一样以大群或蜂群方式运行，这也使得使用传统雷达跟踪它们更加困难。从历史上看，美国军方识别和跟踪对本土构成的空中威胁时，重点一直放在弹道导弹和轰炸机上，这意味着处理雷达数据的传感器和算法并未针对无人机系统威胁进行调校。此外，由民政机构（如联邦航空管理局和国家海洋和大气管理局）运行的传感器所收集的数据，并非全部都已整合进本土防御军事跟踪架构中，这意味着无论军方还是民间官员，都无法全面掌握美国空域潜在空中威胁的情况。除了对无人机跟踪的影响之外，对弹道导弹和轰炸机的关注，以及军民传感器整合不充分，也是过去几年一些飞越美国的中国高空气球未被发现的部分原因，这表明正如一名高级军方官员所称，存在“领域态势感知缺口”。为克服传统雷达的不足，新泽西州官员宣布，他们将使用一种先进雷达系统，并结合热传感器和摄像机来跟踪和识别身份不明的无人机。此外，正如乌克兰已证明的那样，可以使用声学传感器网络成功识别和跟踪无人机。尽管沿美国东海岸部署这种系统需要时间，但在美国部署类似的声学传感器网络，尤其是在关键基础设施、机场和军事设施等敏感地点周边部署，可能有助于未来识别和跟踪无人机。作为联邦航空管理局一项名为“远程身份识别”的倡议的一部分，重量超过250克的无人机必须广播识别信息，包括位置和高度、速度以及人类操控者的位置。然而，某些无人机（例如由美国政府运营的无人机）不必广播远程身份识别信号。尽管地方执法机构可能难以访问远程身份识别数据，但联邦官员应该能够访问这一“数字车牌”以及有关无人机操作员和运行情况的信息。联邦官员似乎未能从远程身份识别中获取有关这些无人机及其操作员的大量信息，这表明许多无人机系统并未遵守远程身份识别规则。该规则于2024年4月生效，是联邦航空管理局关于美国空域无人机运行规定的一部分。Q3：美国如何监管无人机系统运行？A3：联邦航空管理局隶属于交通部，负责监管美国民用航空，并确保国家空域得到安全、高效的使用，包括民用和军用运营方。除联邦航空管理局的要求外，一些州和地方政府还会对无人机操作员施加额外规定。联邦航空管理局要求所有重量超过250克（约半磅）的无人机进行注册。截至2024年10月1日，已有近80万架无人机注册，其中\n美国联邦航空管理局（FAA）。FAA的两套规则涵盖了大多数无人机：一套适用于重量低于55磅的无人机，FAA将其归类为小型无人机系统（small UAS）；另一套适用于重量超过55磅的无人机。根据这些规则，小型无人机系统操作员不得飞行至400英尺以上；在受控空域（例如机场附近）飞行需要获得授权；飞行时必须始终目视无人机，此外还需遵守其他规定。无人机操作员可以申请豁免其中许多单项规定，但必须符合更严格的标准才能获得豁免。许多四旋翼无人机最多只有几磅重，可以放在手掌中；但另一些被归类为小型无人机系统的固定翼无人机翼展接近8英尺。一些用于商业用途的无人机，例如用于包裹递送、化学品和农业产品喷洒以及空中出租车的无人机，重量超过55磅，适用FAA的具体规定。此外，执行政府职能的无人机系统无论重量如何，都可以依据特别规则运行。具体而言，美国军方操作着各种不同尺寸的无人机，其中最大的无人机最大起飞重量接近35,000磅，翼展达130英尺——比波音737客机的翼展还长。Q4：当局可以采取什么措施来阻止这些无人机？A4：有多种技术可以用来使无人机失效或将其摧毁，例如信号干扰、网络行动、动能武器（如子弹）和高能武器。在美国，任何私人个人或实体都不能合法向无人机射击，因为联邦法律禁止向任何有人驾驶或无人驾驶航空器射击。不过，某些机构可以依法采取反无人机系统措施。2018年，国土安全部（DHS）获授权应对无人机系统威胁，但该权限仅限于保护支持DHS任务的设施或资产、特定活动以及其他明确规定的特别行动。美国军方应对美国领土上空无人机威胁的能力也受到限制。国防官员根据对其法定权限的解释，提议使用信号干扰器、网络攻击、网以及“结实、有韧性的长条带”来阻止威胁美国国家安全设施的无人机。通常，地方执法机构也无权使无人机失效，不过纽约市警察局已请求联邦官员授予其此类权限，并称无人机对纽约市构成威胁的情况十分普遍。最终，联邦当局声称，这些无人机目击事件并不表明存在任何威胁，因此无论哪个机构有权使无人机失效，在没有威胁的情况下都没有采取行动的必要。Remote ID为何不能用于获取更多有关无人机操作员的信息，仍有待 выяс清。如果无人机不是由美国政府操作，它们本应广播Remote ID信号。如果涉事目击事件中的无人机都未安装或广播Remote ID，联邦当局不妨明确说明这一点，以澄清其中的矛盾。如果得到确认，缺少Remote ID广播将表明FAA将无人机系统整合进国家空域系统（NAS）的努力成效有限。Q5：近期还发生过哪些其他身份不明的无人机目击事件？A5：无论近期这些目击事件最终如何解决，这些关于身份不明无人机的最新报告都只是冰山一角。\n在美国和盟国都只是冰山一角。2024年12月初，有人在德国一座美国空军基地附近发现不明身份的无人机。2024年11月，据报道有不明原因的无人机在英国四座美军基地上空活动；一名中国公民因在加利福尼亚州范登堡太空军基地上空驾驶无人机而被捕。据报道，过去一年中，弗吉尼亚州兰利空军基地附近曾观察到大量无人机。事实上，美加联合的北美防空司令部于2024年10月正式报告称，自2022年以来，美国军事设施上空发生了约600起未经授权的无人机入侵事件。尽管过去一年在国防设施上空的目击事件以及东海岸上空持续发生的事件可能不会对公共安全和安保构成威胁，但未来的事件可能会构成威胁。\n\n这一连串无法解释的目击事件表明，美国并未全面掌握美国空域内的无人机活动情况，主要原因是传统雷达不适合跟踪低空飞行的小型无人机。自20世纪50年代开始，对雷达基础设施以及包括成立联邦航空管理局（FAA）在内的联邦航空交通管制工作的重大投资，为美国国家航空交通管制系统奠定了基础；如今，该系统使官员能够全面、实时地监控在全国范围内运行的传统有人驾驶飞机。要获得同等的无人机跟踪能力，就需要在全国范围内投资无人机系统（UAS）监视技术——仅靠远程识别（Remote ID）是不够的，因为不合作或怀有敌意的无人机操作者只需关闭广播即可。这些目击事件还表明，面对操作者和用途仍不透明的无人机，官员们不愿采取措施使其失效。在战争或危机时期，这种迟疑可能导致人员伤亡和关键基础设施受损，而这些设施可能正遭到敌对无人机的攻击。文职和军方官员应当重视这一紧迫的警示，改进并加快在美国领土上空识别、跟踪和应对无人机威胁的能力。\n\n克莱顿·斯沃普是航空航天安全项目副主任，也是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心国防与安全部的高级研究员。\n\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性，不代表任何专有利益。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应被理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2024 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n太空与航空航天\n\n图片\n克莱顿·斯沃普\n航空航天安全项目副主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员\n\n项目与计划\n航空航天安全项目\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-16T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1916","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国为何对尼日利亚发动空袭？","title_en":"Why Did the United States Conduct Strikes in Nigeria?","summary_cn":"2025年12月25日，美国以战斧导弹打击尼日利亚西北部两处疑似伊斯兰国营地，目标或为伊斯兰国萨赫勒省及当地组织拉库拉瓦。文章分析特朗普政府以保护基督徒为由的打击动机，指出尼日利亚暴力根源复杂，涉及农牧冲突与土匪活动，并评估单次打击难以显著削弱恐怖组织威胁，以及后续可能扩大空袭或采取多维支持的前景。","summary_en":"On December 25, 2025, the United States struck two apparent Islamic State camps in northwest Nigeria with Tomahawk missiles, likely targeting Islamic State-Sahel Province or the local group Lakurawa. The analysis examines the Trump administration's stated justification of protecting Christians, notes Nigeria's complex violence drivers including farmer-herder conflict and banditry, and assesses that isolated strikes are unlikely to significantly reduce the terrorist threat, while considering further strikes or multidimensional support.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:43:49.481036+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/why-did-united-states-conduct-strikes-nigeria","content":"Why Did the United States Conduct Strikes in Nigeria?\nPhoto: AUDU MARTE/AFP/Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nAlexander Palmer\nand\nErin Oppel\nPublished December 26, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nOn December 25, 2025, the United States struck two apparent Islamic State camps in northwest Nigeria with Tomahawk missiles. U.S. Africa Command (AFRICOM) announced that it struck “ISIS terrorists in Nigeria” in collaboration with the Nigerian government.Q1: Who did the strikes target?A1: President Trump stated that the attack targeted Islamic State militants in Nigeria’s Sokoto state. President Trump was almost certainly referring to the Islamic State-Sahel Province (ISSP) or Lakurawa, a local group with increasing ties to the Islamic State.ISSP is an Islamic State group active in Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, and Nigeria. The group grew in strength in 2025 and now commands more fighters, controls more territory, and conducts deadlier attacks than it ever has before. Much of ISSP’s recent gains have come in Niger and Nigeria, where it has increased coordination with other terrorist groups and established an operational presence.Much of ISSP’s progress in Nigeria over the past year can be attributed to the emerging terrorist group Lakurawa, a local group that has pledged allegiance to ISSP but remains largely independent. As it has expanded its foothold, Lakurawa has built its own forms of local governance and brutally cracked down on those who do not comply with its religious rules.Q2: Why did the Trump administration target these groups?A2: President Trump publicly linked the strike to alleged systematic killings of Christians in Nigeria, although Nigerian officials and some analysts have pushed back against U.S. government claims that jihadist groups target Christians specifically, emphasizing that these terrorists target all faiths. U.S. AFRICOM Commander General Dagvin Anderson also stated that the military’s goal was to “protect Americans and disrupt violent extremist organizations where they are.” Justifying strikes against Salafi-jihadist groups in Africa based on the threat they pose to the United States is increasingly common for the Trump administration. AFRICOM repeatedly cited the threat from terrorist organizations as a justification for air strikes in Somalia in 2025, for example.A number of overlapping conflicts in Nigeria have led to the deaths of Christians in the country. Competition between majority-Christian farmers and primarily Muslim herders over scarce resources has resulted in massacres. Salafi-jihadist groups have also specifically targeted Christians, but their infiltration into northwest Nigeria is relatively recent and accounts for less of the region’s violence than farmer-herder conflict and banditry.Nor have Muslims escaped the killing. The region’s Salafi-jihadists conduct indiscriminate violence and target Muslims who reject their extreme interpretation of Islam. In August 2025, gunmen massacred at least 13 people at a mosque during a series of attacks in northwest Nigeria. The day before the U.S. strikes, a suicide bomber struck a mosque in the northeastern city of Maiduguri, killing at least five and injuring dozens more.The threat that the Sahel’s Salafi-jihadist groups pose to the United States is uncertain. Experts have repeatedly noted a lack of evidence that the Sahel’s Salafi-jihadist groups have the interest or ability to attack the U.S. homeland. The Defense Intelligence Agency assessed in 2025 that Islamic State and Al Qaeda affiliates in Africa “focus on plotting attacks locally,” although it also noted that some affiliates would probably “advance” plotting elsewhere in 2025. Even so, there is little doubt that the region’s Salafi-jihadist groups threaten regional stability and U.S. facilities in their areas of operation.The violence in Nigeria is part of a broader, Sahel-wide crisis. West Africa’s terrorist groups are currently the world’s most active and increasingly threaten regional governments. In 2025, ISSP conducted a record number of large-scale attacks across the Sahel, moving with freedom across the Mali-Niger border. In 2025, ISSP concentrated its areas of operation in southern Niger for the first time, carrying out nearly twice as many attacks in Niger in 2025 than all of 2024. The group also claimed its first attack in the Dosso region and drew closer to Niamey, Niger’s capital.The violence has also contributed to greater political instability and Russian influence in the region. Increasing insecurity has delegitimized many of the region’s governments and opened the door to coups d’etat, while Russia has sold itself as an alternative to Western partners.The ascendance of West Africa’s Salafi-jihadist groups represents a longer-term threat to the United States, no matter their current focus. Intentions are often more opaque and mutable than capabilities, meaning that the ascendance of Africa’s terrorist organizations should be concerning even if they do not currently seem particularly interested in directly attacking U.S. interests. AFRICOM also assessed that “if ISIS and al-Qaeda groups continue their expansion, they will pose a direct threat to the U.S. homeland.” Finally, Islamic State ideology remains appealing to terrorists across the world and continues to inspire attacks like those perpetrated last New Years in New Orleans and this December in Sydney.Q3: How will these strikes affect the organizations they targeted?A3: Isolated strikes like the ones carried out by the United States last night are unlikely to significantly reduce the terrorist threat in the region or reverse Nigeria’s growing insecurity. Despite the chairman of a local government in Sokoto State stating that U.S. strikes likely killed fighters, the killings will provide at most a temporary setback.The losses from the U.S. attacks last night will only represent a small fraction of ISSP and Lakurawa resources. According to UN estimates, ISSP has between 2,000 and 3,000 fighters and operates across an area covering many hundreds of miles. Lakurawa is much smaller, commanding only about 200 fighters, but it will probably be able to recruit replacements for any members killed. The deaths of a few operatives will do nothing to reverse the gains both groups have made over the past few years. The administration may be counting on the threat of further strikes to deter these groups from further activity in Nigeria, but deterring terrorist groups through punishment strategies is a highly uncertain endeavor. The Trump administration will probably have to conduct many more strikes if it is to establish a credible deterrent.Q4: What will happen next?A4: Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth wrote after the attack that there was “More to come” but did not elaborate. It is possible that the United States is considering increasing its long-range strikes against terrorist groups in Nigeria, as it has in Somalia. The United States could also consider more multidimensional support like military training and development aid that targets the conflict’s varied, complex drivers. However, reversing increases in northern Nigeria’s insecurity will be extremely difficult.Furthermore, Nigeria’s terrorist threat is not isolated to Lakurawa or ISSP, so any successful counterterrorism operation would require a broader scope to eliminate the jihadist threat in the country. In the past three years, the Nigerian military has been spread thin fighting breakoffs of Boko Haram, the Islamic State – West Africa Province (ISWAP), and Jama’tu Ahlis Sunna Lidda’awati wal-Jihad (JAS), which have operated primarily in the northeastern Lake Chad region of Nigeria but have also expanded their areas of operation into central and western Nigeria. Lakurawa and ISSP are relatively nascent threats in Nigeria compared with ISWAP and JAS, which have been entrenched in the country for years.A sustained campaign of counterterrorist strikes could weaken the Salafi-jihadist groups in Nigeria, but the time required would be long and the prospects for success uncertain at best. Degrading terrorist groups through air campaigns is possible, but it takes a long time and is more likely to cause the collapse of new groups rather than more established organizations like ISSP. In addition, ISSP can take advantage of its relative safe havens in Mali, Burkina Faso, and Niger, where the United States lacks a significant military presence following a series of coups d’etat and the expulsion of UN and French forces over the past five years. The best-case scenario absent larger political changes across the Sahel is therefore a shift of Salafi-jihadist violence from Nigeria to other regional states.Salafi-jihadists are only one driver of violence in northern Nigeria. Even if a U.S.-enabled military campaign could degrade them so that they no longer pose a threat within Nigeria, farmer-herder conflicts would persist. A more comprehensive approach including military, legal, and development measures that addresses the root causes of violence, especially the increasing scarcity of land that can support both groups, would stand a better chance of improving security in the country’s north and decreasing support for militant groups. It would also smack of the type of nation-building that President Trump has so vehemently rejected and would be extremely costly, difficult, and uncertain.Alexander Palmer is a fellow in the Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Erin Oppel is an intern with the WITT Program.Special thanks to Phillip Meylan for editing and publication support.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAfrica,\nSub-Saharan Africa,\nCounterterrorism and Homeland Security,\nDefense and Security,\nTerrorism and Irregular Warfare, and\nTransnational Threats\nImage\nAlexander Palmer\nFellow, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nErin Oppel\nResearch Intern, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nStrategy and Statecraft","content_cn":"美国为何在尼日利亚发动袭击？\n照片：AUDU MARTE/AFP/Getty Images\n关键问题\n作者\nAlexander Palmer\n和\nErin Oppel\n发表于2025年12月26日\n远程可视化\n2025年12月25日，美国使用战斧导弹袭击了尼日利亚西北部两个疑似“伊斯兰国”营地。美国非洲司令部（AFRICOM）宣布，其与尼日利亚政府合作，打击了“在尼日利亚的‘伊斯兰国’恐怖分子”。\nQ1：袭击的目标是谁？A1：特朗普总统表示，这次攻击的目标是尼日利亚索科托州的“伊斯兰国”武装分子。特朗普总统几乎可以肯定指的是“伊斯兰国萨赫勒省”（ISSP）或Lakurawa，这是一个与“伊斯兰国”关系日益密切的地方组织。\nISSP是一个活跃于马里、布基纳法索、尼日尔和尼日利亚的“伊斯兰国”组织。该组织在2025年不断壮大，如今拥有比以往更多的战斗人员，控制更多领土，并发动比以往更加致命的袭击。ISSP近期的大部分收益来自尼日尔和尼日利亚，在那里它加强了与其他恐怖组织的协调，并建立了行动存在。\n过去一年，ISSP在尼日利亚取得的大部分进展，都可归因于新兴恐怖组织Lakurawa。该组织是一个已宣誓效忠ISSP、但仍基本保持独立的地方组织。随着势力范围扩大，Lakurawa建立了自己的地方治理形式，并残酷镇压不遵守其宗教规则的人。\nQ2：特朗普政府为何将这些组织作为目标？A2：特朗普总统公开将袭击与尼日利亚境内据称对基督徒的系统性杀戮联系起来，尽管尼日利亚官员和一些分析人士反驳美国政府关于圣战组织专门针对基督徒的说法，强调这些恐怖分子针对所有信仰。美国非洲司令部司令达格文·安德森将军也表示，军方的目标是“保护美国人，并在暴力极端组织所在之处予以破坏”。特朗普政府越来越常以萨拉菲圣战组织对美国构成的威胁为依据，为其在非洲发动袭击辩护。例如，2025年，美国非洲司令部多次以恐怖组织的威胁为理由，为在索马里的空袭辩护。\n尼日利亚境内多场相互交织的冲突导致该国基督徒死亡。以基督徒为主的农民与主要为穆斯林的牧民围绕稀缺资源展开竞争，造成了屠杀。萨拉菲圣战组织也曾专门袭击基督徒，但它们渗透尼日利亚西北部的时间相对较晚，其造成的地区暴力少于农牧冲突和匪患。\n穆斯林也未能逃脱杀戮。该地区的萨拉菲圣战分子实施不分青红皂白的暴力，并袭击拒绝其极端伊斯兰解释的穆斯林。2025年8月，在尼日利亚西北部一系列袭击中，枪手在一座清真寺内屠杀了至少13人。在美国发动袭击的前一天，一名自杀式炸弹袭击者袭击了东北部城市迈杜古里的清真寺，造成至少5人死亡、数十人受伤。\n萨赫勒地区萨拉菲圣战组织对美国构成的威胁尚不确定。专家们反复指出，缺乏证据表明萨赫勒地区的萨拉菲圣战组织有兴趣或能力袭击美国本土。国防情报局在2025年评估认为，“伊斯兰\n国家和基地组织在非洲的分支机构“专注于在当地策划袭击”，不过报告也指出，一些分支机构可能会在2025年“推进”在其他地方的策划。即便如此，毫无疑问，该地区的萨拉菲圣战组织威胁着地区稳定以及其行动区域内的美国设施。尼日利亚的暴力事件是整个萨赫勒地区更广泛危机的一部分。西非的恐怖组织目前是全球最活跃的，并且日益威胁着该地区各国政府。2025年，伊斯兰国萨赫勒省（ISSP）在整个萨赫勒地区发动了创纪录数量的大规模袭击，并在马里—尼日尔边境自由行动。2025年，ISSP首次将其行动区域集中在尼日尔南部，2025年在尼日尔发动的袭击次数几乎是2024年全年总数的两倍。该组织还首次宣称在多索大区发动袭击，并进一步逼近尼日尔首都尼亚美。\n暴力事件还加剧了该地区的政治不稳定和俄罗斯的影响力。日益恶化的不安全局势使该地区许多政府失去合法性，并为政变打开了大门；与此同时，俄罗斯将自己包装成西方伙伴的替代者。\n无论西非萨拉菲圣战组织当前的关注重点是什么，它们的崛起都对美国构成长期威胁。意图往往比能力更加不透明且易变，这意味着，即使非洲恐怖组织目前似乎并不特别有兴趣直接攻击美国利益，它们的崛起仍应令人担忧。美国非洲司令部还评估称，“如果伊斯兰国和基地组织团体继续扩张，它们将对美国本土构成直接威胁”。最后，伊斯兰国意识形态对世界各地的恐怖分子仍具有吸引力，并继续激发袭击，例如去年新年前夕在新奥尔良实施的袭击，以及今年12月在悉尼实施的袭击。\nQ3：这些袭击将如何影响它们所针对的组织？\nA3：像美国昨晚实施的这类孤立袭击，不太可能显著降低该地区的恐怖主义威胁，也不太可能扭转尼日利亚日益恶化的不安全局势。尽管索科托州一名地方政府主席表示，美国的袭击可能杀死了一些武装分子，但这些杀戮至多只能造成暂时性挫折。\n美国昨晚的袭击造成的损失，只会占ISSP和拉库拉瓦资源的一小部分。根据联合国估计，ISSP拥有2,000至3,000名武装分子，活动范围覆盖数百英里的区域。拉库拉瓦的规模小得多，仅指挥约200名武装分子，但它很可能能够为任何被杀成员招募替补。几名行动人员的死亡，丝毫无法扭转这两个组织在过去几年取得的进展。政府可能指望以进一步袭击的威胁，阻止这些组织在尼日利亚继续活动，但通过惩罚策略遏制恐怖组织，是一项高度不确定的事业。如果特朗普政府要建立可信的威慑力，可能必须发动更多袭击。\nQ4：接下来会发生什么？\nA4：国防部长皮特·赫格塞思在袭击后写道，“还会有更多行动”，但没有进一步说明。美国可能正在考虑加强对尼日利亚恐怖组织的远程打击，就像其在索马里所做的那样。美国还可能考虑更多维度的\n例如军事训练和针对冲突多样且复杂驱动因素的发展援助。然而，要扭转尼日利亚北部日益恶化的不安全局势将极其困难。\n\n此外，尼日利亚面临的恐怖主义威胁并不局限于Lakurawa或ISSP，因此任何成功的反恐行动都需要更广泛的范围，以消除该国的圣战主义威胁。在过去三年中，尼日利亚军队一直捉襟见肘地应对博科圣地、伊斯兰国西非省（ISWAP）和Jama’tu Ahlis Sunna Lidda’awati wal-Jihad（JAS）的分裂派别。这些组织主要在尼日利亚东北部的乍得湖地区活动，但也已将行动范围扩展至尼日利亚中部和西部。与ISWAP和JAS相比，Lakurawa和ISSP在尼日利亚构成的威胁相对较新，而ISWAP和JAS已在该国盘踞多年。\n\n持续开展反恐打击行动可能削弱尼日利亚的萨拉菲圣战主义组织，但所需时间将很长，成功前景充其量也不确定。通过空中行动削弱恐怖组织是可能的，但这需要很长时间，而且更可能导致新组织瓦解，而非像ISSP这样的老牌组织崩溃。此外，ISSP可以利用其在马里、布基纳法索和尼日尔的相对安全避风港。过去五年中，这些国家相继发生政变并驱逐联合国和法国部队，此后美国在当地已不再拥有重要军事存在。因此，在萨赫勒地区没有发生更大范围政治变化的情况下，最好的情形是萨拉菲圣战主义暴力从尼日利亚转移到其他地区国家。\n\n萨拉菲圣战主义者只是尼日利亚北部暴力冲突的一个驱动因素。即使美国支持的军事行动能够削弱他们，使其不再在尼日利亚境内构成威胁，农牧民冲突仍将持续。采取更全面的方法，包括军事、法律和发展措施，来解决暴力的根源，尤其是日益稀缺、能够同时满足两群体需求的土地资源，将更有可能改善该国北部的安全状况，并减少对武装团体的支持。这也会带有特朗普总统强烈拒绝的那种国家建设色彩，而且成本极高、困难重重且前景不确定。\n\nAlexander Palmer是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目的研究员。Erin Oppel是WITT项目的实习生。\n\n特别感谢Phillip Meylan为编辑和出版提供支持。\n\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n非洲，\n撒哈拉以南非洲，\n反恐与国土安全，\n国防与安全，\n恐怖主义与不规则战争，以及\n跨国威胁\n\n图片\nAlexander Palmer\n战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目研究员\nErin Oppel\n战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目研究实习生\n\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目\n战略与治国方略","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-12-26T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3577","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"为什么伊朗将会升级局势","title_en":"Why Iran Will Escalate","summary_cn":"哈马斯政治领导人哈尼亚在德黑兰被暗杀后，伊朗面临必须回应以色列的压力。文章分析伊朗的五个主要回应选项，认为其目标并非升级冲突而是恢复现状，但受国内强硬派和威慑需求制约，回应很可能导致局势螺旋升级。作者呼吁美国与以色列准确评估伊朗所受约束，避免将升级行为等同于升级意图。","summary_en":"After Hamas political leader Haniyeh was killed in Tehran, Iran faces pressure to respond to Israel. The article examines five main response options and argues Iran seeks to restore the status quo rather than escalate, yet domestic hardliners and deterrence needs make a calibrated response nearly impossible. It urges the United States and Israel to assess Iran's constraints accurately and avoid treating escalatory behavior as escalatory intent.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:30:28.196427+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/why-iran-will-escalate","content":"Why Iran Will Escalate\nPhoto: Majid Saeedi/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nAlexander Palmer\nPublished August 8, 2024\nRemote Visualization\nThe killing of Hamas political leader Ismail Haniyeh in Tehran on July 31 has pushed Iran’s leaders into a dangerous corner. Tehran has few options but to escalate the conflict, as it did with its attack against Israel in April. That attack led to a chorus of statements labeling Iran’s actions as “destabilizing,” arguing that it was part of a malign regional strategy, and calling for a punitive Israeli counterattack. Policymakers should not see in Tehran’s inevitable response an intent to escalate or further destabilize the region but rather an imperfect attempt to restore the status quo. Such an interpretation risks turning into a self-fulfilling prophecy, contributing to a march toward a regional war that few want.Iran will feel the need to respond to Israel’s attack for both international and domestic political reasons. Its response will have to be more impactful than its April 13 missile and drone attack to satisfy domestic hardliners and Iran’s strategic need to deter further Israeli attacks in its territory. But Iran also wants to avoid a wider war, which leaves it with a nearly impossible task: to respond without kicking off an escalatory spiral.Iran has five main response options. It could do nothing, undertake low-profile actions like cyberattacks, execute one or more targeted killings, launch indirect fire attacks, or direct its proxies to conduct ground incursions into Israel. The first two options are nonstarters. Iran’s supreme leader has already put his reputation on the line, and anything perceived as a nonresponse would be politically unacceptable. Targeted killings against Israelis would be the least escalatory option, but Iran faces both political and practical obstacles. Iran could also conduct indirect fire attacks through its non-state partners like Hezbollah, in conjunction with them, or alone. Finally, Iran could push its proxies to conduct more conventional ground incursions into Israel. This is the most escalatory option possible given memories of October 7, and Iran will not order such incursions if it is serious about avoiding war.Escalation occurs when one or more parties to a crisis increase the intensity or expand the scope of their efforts, violating the unwritten rules of a conflict. In April, for example, Israel escalated (probably unintentionally) by killing several high-level Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps Quds Force personnel in Damascus—an unusual attack given both the rank of those killed and the fact that it targeted a diplomatic facility. Iran escalated in response, launching an unprecedented drone and missile attack against Israeli territory. Israel managed to resolve the crisis without further escalating the situation, but doing so required a highly calibrated strike that conveyed a threat without violating the unwritten rules that have governed Israel and Iran’s conflict for the past several decades.Targeted killings against a Haniyeh-like figure in Israel would probably provide a response without dramatic escalation, but such a figure does not exist. Israel lacks the kind of non-state proxies that would give Tehran a target closely resembling Haniyeh, so Tehran would probably have to escalate the conflict by targeting Israeli political or military officials. Iran also faces practical problems with such an attack—there is no evidence of covert Iranian infrastructure in Israel comparable to what was required to kill Haniyah. Iran could attack Israeli government figures outside of Israel, as Iran and its non-state partners have repeatedly done over the past several decades. But such attacks also risk escalating the conflict by spreading it to new geographical theaters and face practical problems of their own: a targeted killing or bombing of an embassy would probably take time to plan, weakening the deterrent signal that Iran will seek to send.Iran is more likely to respond with indirect fire attacks somewhat comparable to April’s missile and drone attack. But Iran lacks options that do not risk dramatic escalation. Iran’s proxies are already engaged in a gradually intensifying campaign of attacks against Israel, so a response would have to involve an increase in effort to send the desired deterrent signal—thereby continuing the cycle of escalation, possibly even to a catastrophic Israel-Hezbollah war. A direct conventional attack from Iranian forces would also send a clear signal, but Israel’s April attack against Isfahan carried an implicit threat of airstrikes against Iranian nuclear infrastructure. Israel might carry out that threat in response to another Iranian attack against Israel itself, which would constitute another major escalation and step toward wider war.Iran could direct its non-state partners in Syria or Lebanon to conduct ground incursions into Israel itself. Any ground assault’s resemblance to October 7 would make it the most escalatory option available. Ordering a ground incursion would be the option most likely to spark a wider war. Such a decision would suggest that Iran no longer desired a return to the status quo and instead sought escalation to war.Preventing a wider war requires recognizing the pressures that Iran’s leaders are currently under. Sending a deterrent signal while avoiding escalation will require extreme creativity, and Iranian efforts to calibrate a response will probably fail unless Tehran possesses capabilities it has not yet revealed to Israel and the world. If they do, many will conclude that an escalatory Iranian response is continued evidence of destabilizing intent, even if Iran’s intention were to restore the status quo rather than escalate the conflict.None of this is to say that Iran is a benign regime. Its support for non-state groups has destabilized the region and contributed to the horrors of October 7, 2023. Its repression of its own population has been odious. Intelligence agencies will be looking for evidence that Iran is preparing for war, but Iran’s response is extremely unlikely to provide such evidence. Staving off a wider war in the Middle East requires an accurate assessment of the constraints on Iranian behavior, not assumptions that escalatory behavior means escalatory intent. The United States and Israel should respond accordingly, seeking to minimize the damage from Iran’s response and refraining from their own escalatory responses.Alexander Palmer is an associate fellow in the Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Special thanks to Kelsey Hartman and Madison Bruno for editing and publication support.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nMiddle East,\nIran,\nGeopolitics and International Security,\nDefense and Security,\nDefense Strategy and Capabilities,\nCounterterrorism and Homeland Security,\nTransnational Threats, and\nTerrorism and Irregular Warfare\nImage\nAlexander Palmer\nFellow, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nPrograms & Projects\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nInternational Security Program\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"伊朗为何将升级局势\n照片：Majid Saeedi/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nAlexander Palmer\n发表于2024年8月8日\n远程可视化\n7月31日，哈马斯政治领导人伊斯梅尔·哈尼耶在德黑兰遭杀害，这将伊朗领导人推入了一个危险的角落。德黑兰除了升级冲突之外几乎别无选择，就像其4月份对以色列发动袭击时那样。那次袭击引发了一片声明声浪，将伊朗的行动称为“破坏稳定”，认为这是其恶性地区战略的一部分，并呼吁以色列进行惩罚性反击。政策制定者不应将德黑兰不可避免的回应视为意在升级或进一步破坏地区稳定，而应视为一次恢复现状的、不完美的尝试。这种解读有可能变成自我实现的预言，助推走向一场几乎无人希望发生的地区战争。\n伊朗出于国际和国内政治原因，都将感到有必要回应以色列的袭击。其回应必须比4月13日的导弹和无人机袭击更具影响力，以满足国内强硬派的要求，并满足伊朗通过威慑阻止以色列进一步袭击其领土的战略需要。但伊朗也希望避免更大规模的战争，这使其面临一项几乎不可能完成的任务：作出回应，却不引发升级螺旋。\n伊朗有五种主要回应选项。它可以什么都不做，采取网络攻击等低调行动，实施一次或多次定点杀戮，发动间接火力攻击，或指示其代理人对以色列发动地面入侵。前两个选项都行不通。伊朗最高领袖已经把自己的声誉押了上去，任何被视为不回应的做法在政治上都不可接受。针对以色列人的定点杀戮将是最不具升级性的选项，但伊朗面临政治和实际障碍。伊朗也可以通过真主党等非国家伙伴发动间接火力攻击，与它们协同行动，或单独行动。最后，伊朗可以推动其代理人对以色列发动更传统的地面入侵。鉴于人们对10月7日的记忆，这是可能采取的最具升级性的选项；如果伊朗真心希望避免战争，就不会下令发动这种入侵。\n当危机一方或多方提高其行动强度或扩大行动范围，违反冲突中不成文的规则时，升级就会发生。例如，4月份，以色列杀害了数名伊斯兰革命卫队圣城部队的高级人员，可能是无意中升级了局势——鉴于死者的军衔以及袭击针对的是一处外交设施，这属于不寻常的攻击。伊朗随后升级局势，对以色列领土发动了前所未有的无人机和导弹袭击。以色列设法在不进一步升级局势的情况下化解了危机，但这需要一次经过高度精确校准的打击：既传达威胁，又不违反过去几十年来一直支配以色列与伊朗冲突的不成文规则。\n在以色列境内针对一名类似哈尼耶的人物进行定点杀戮，或许能够在不造成戏剧性升级的情况下作出回应，但以色列并不存在这样的人物。以色列缺乏能让德黑兰找到与哈尼耶高度相似目标的非国家代理人，因此德黑兰可能不得不通过针对以色列政治或军事官员来升级冲突。伊朗还面临此类袭击的实际问题——没有证据表明伊朗秘密\n以色列境内的基础设施，其规模可与杀死哈尼亚所需的基础设施相媲美。伊朗可以袭击以色列境外的政府官员，而在过去几十年里，伊朗及其非国家伙伴已多次这样做。但此类袭击也有可能通过将冲突扩展到新的地理战场而使冲突升级，并且自身面临实际问题：策划一次定点杀害或轰炸大使馆可能需要时间，从而削弱伊朗试图传递的威慑信号。\n\n伊朗更有可能采取与4月的导弹和无人机袭击有些类似的间接火力攻击。但伊朗缺乏不会带来剧烈升级风险的选项。伊朗的代理人已经在开展针对以色列、逐渐加剧的袭击行动，因此任何回应都必须加大力度，以传递所期望的威慑信号——从而延续升级循环，甚至可能升级为灾难性的以色列—真主党战争。伊朗军队直接发动常规攻击也会传递明确的信号，但以色列4月对伊斯法罕的袭击暗含着对伊朗核基础设施实施空袭的威胁。以色列可能会在伊朗再次袭击以色列本土时兑现这一威胁，而这将构成又一次重大升级，并朝着更广泛的战争迈进。\n\n伊朗可以指示其在叙利亚或黎巴嫩的非国家伙伴，对以色列本土发动地面入侵。任何地面进攻因类似于10月7日事件，都将成为现有选项中最具升级性的选择。下令发动地面入侵，是最有可能引发更广泛战争的选项。这样的决定将表明，伊朗已不再希望恢复现状，而是寻求升级为战争。\n\n要防止更广泛的战争，就必须认识到伊朗领导人目前承受的压力。在避免升级的同时传递威慑信号，需要极大的创造力；除非德黑兰拥有尚未向以色列和世界展示的能力，否则伊朗校准回应力度的努力很可能会失败。即使伊朗的意图是恢复现状而非升级冲突，许多人也会据此认定，伊朗采取升级性回应继续证明了其具有破坏稳定的意图。\n\n这并不是说伊朗是一个无害的政权。它对非国家组织的支持破坏了地区稳定，并助长了2023年10月7日事件的惨剧。它对本国民众的镇压令人憎恶。情报机构将寻找伊朗准备发动战争的证据，但伊朗的回应极不可能提供这样的证据。要避免中东爆发更广泛的战争，需要准确评估对伊朗行为的约束，而不是假定升级性行为就意味着升级意图。美国和以色列应据此作出回应，力求将伊朗回应造成的损害降至最低，并避免采取自身的升级性回应。\n\n亚历山大·帕尔默是华盛顿战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）战争、不规则威胁与恐怖主义项目的副研究员。特别感谢凯尔西·哈特曼和麦迪逊·布鲁诺为编辑和出版提供支持。\n\n评论文章由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有非党派性和非专有性。CSIS不\n采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2024 战略与国际研究中心。保留所有权利。\n标签\n中东，\n伊朗，\n地缘政治与国际安全，\n国防与安全，\n国防战略与能力，\n反恐与国土安全，\n跨国威胁，以及\n恐怖主义与非常规战争\n图片\nAlexander Palmer\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目研究员\n项目与计划\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目\n国际安全项目\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-08-08T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3233","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国为何应优先考虑火星？","title_en":"Why Should the United States Prioritize Mars?","summary_cn":"文章以问答形式讨论特朗普政府将火星列为太空优先事项的影响。火星与地球最为相似，可能存在过生命，且具有科学、战略与声望价值。美国火星采样返回（MSR）任务因成本超支暂停，中国计划2028年发射天问三号并可能于2031年先带回样本。SpaceX计划2026年发射无人星舰。作者认为若不大幅增加NASA预算，阿尔忒弥斯计划可能需重新定位为火星技术试验场。","summary_en":"This Q&A examines the implications of the Trump administration prioritizing Mars. Mars is the most Earth-like planet, potentially once habitable, and carries scientific, strategic, and prestige value. NASA's Mars Sample Return (MSR) was paused over cost overruns, while China plans to launch Tianwen-3 in 2028 and may return samples around 2031. SpaceX aims to send uncrewed Starships in 2026. The author argues that without a significant NASA budget increase, Artemis may need to be recast as a Mars test bed.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:06:44.607291+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/why-should-united-states-prioritize-mars","content":"Why Should the United States Prioritize Mars?\nPhoto: Stocktrek Images/Getty Images\nCritical Questions\nby\nClayton Swope\nPublished January 23, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nDuring his inaugural address on January 20, 2024, President Donald Trump indicated he will prioritize U.S. space endeavors focused on Mars. While NASA has a long history of Martian exploration, including operating ongoing missions to the Red Planet, the president’s remarks suggest Mars may play a more central role in NASA’s agenda during the coming years. Unless accompanied by a budget increase for NASA, prioritizing Mars missions may impact other NASA activities, such as the Artemis program, NASA’s effort to return humans to the Moon and establish a long-term lunar presence. A Martian pivot may signal recognition of the far greater scientific and strategic value, not to mention the national prestige at stake vis-à-vis China, of Mars than the Moon. Beyond national interests, Mars might ultimately be the linchpin for the long-term survival of humanity, as it’s the most obvious place other than Earth where, with the right technology, humans may be able to survive somewhat independently of our home planet.Q1: What is special about Mars?A1: There is no other planet quite like Earth in the Solar System, though Mars is by far the most Earth-like of the seven other planets. The fourth planet from the Sun, Mars is often called the Red Planet because its solid surface is covered with orange-red iron oxide, a material with a similar composition to rust. Mars is significantly smaller than Earth, with a diameter about half that of Earth and a mass one-tenth in size. A day on Mars is about the same amount of time as on Earth, lasting 24.6 hours. Mars has an atmosphere, albeit a weak one compared to Earth, containing carbon dioxide, argon, and nitrogen, and water ice at its poles. Gravity on Mars is only about 38 percent of Earth’s gravity. In the past, Mars may have had water oceans.No conclusive signs have been found that indicate life originated or currently exists on Mars, though due to its similarities with Earth, scientists believe that it may have once been habitable for some type of life, probably microorganisms. But to date, no scientist has been able to thoroughly examine material from Mars, as no one has yet brought back a sample of the Martian surface for analysis on Earth. So far, all scientific analysis of Mars has been conducted remotely, such as by instruments attached to landers and rovers operating on its surface.Q2: What is the history of Mars exploration?A2: Mars is one of the night sky’s brightest natural objects, clearly visible from Earth by the naked eye. Some of the earliest surviving records of Martian observations date to ancient Egypt. In 1610, Galileo Galilei, an Italian astronomer, made observations of Mars using a telescope. Soon, astronomers had spotted the Martian ice caps and, by the end of the nineteenth century, noticed yellow clouds on Mars. By the 1960s, the United States and the Soviet Union were in a race to send uncrewed spacecraft to Mars, with the United States becoming the first country to successfully reach Mars in 1964 when Mariner 4 conducted a flyby of the planet. The Soviet Union made the first soft landing on Mars in 1971. That same year, the United States successfully placed Mariner 9 in Martian orbit, becoming the first nation to send a spacecraft into orbit around another planet. The United States landed Viking 1 on Mars in 1975, becoming the first successful lander to reach Mars.NASA landed its first Martian rover mission, Pathfinder, on July 4, 1997. Since Pathfinder, the United States has launched a number of Mars rovers, including one that deployed a small helicopter, and orbiters. Over the last 25 years, the European Space Agency (ESA), India, China, and the United Arab Emirates have also executed successful missions to Mars, in most cases placing spacecraft into orbit. China’s Tianwen-1 mission to Mars, launched in 2021, was notable because it involved an orbiter, lander, and rover. Its success marked the first time a country had been able to conduct a soft landing and deploy a rover on its maiden mission to Mars. In total, there are currently about seven active spacecraft in orbit around Mars and two operational rovers on the Martian surface, NASA’s Perseverance and Curiosity rovers. As part of its mission, Perseverance has been collecting and caching samples of the Martian surface for pickup and retrieval by NASA’s future Mars Sample Return (MSR) mission.Q3: What future missions are planned for Mars?A3: For over two decades, NASA has been investigating the possibility of returning samples from Mars to Earth. In 2009, aiming to bring back samples to Earth in the 2020s, NASA began working with ESA on what would eventually become the MSR mission, signing an agreement to work together in 2018. In 2022, the National Academies recommended that “the highest scientific priority of NASA’s robotic exploration efforts this decade should be completion of Mars Sample Return as soon as is practicably possible,” echoing similar recommendations from 2011 and 2003. In November 2023, NASA paused work on MSR after a review board estimated that the mission’s price tag would well exceed what NASA had been expecting.In April 2024, NASA sought ideas from industry to cut costs and accelerate the mission timeline, targeting a return of the samples collected by Perseverance to Earth in the mid-2030s. After narrowing down the revised options to two in early January 2024, NASA said it would take the next 18 months to conduct further assessments and decide on a new plan. Meanwhile, China is accelerating its own Mars plans. In September 2024, China said that it intended to move up the launch for a Mars sample return mission, called Tianwen-3, to 2028. As Chinese space officials have noted, the mission would last about three years, and China would probably be able to return its samples to Earth around 2031, several years before NASA.Additionally, SpaceX is pursuing its own plans to reach Mars, announcing in October 2024 that it will begin launching uncrewed Starship rockets to Mars in 2026, which is the next time on a 26-month cycle when Mars and Earth are closest together in orbit. The goal of the tests is to demonstrate that Starship can successfully reach and land on Mars, paving the way for future crewed missions to the Red Planet, possibly as early as 2028 or 2029. In December 2024, NASA released its updated plans for its Moon to Mars architecture, which incorporates the Moon and Artemis program as key testing opportunities for capabilities intended to land humans on Mars. NASA has not revised its goal set during the Obama administration for sending astronauts to Mars by 2033. In 2021, China set its own goal for landing humans on Mars, aiming also for 2033.Q4: What happens next?A4: Though the United States and several other countries, including China, have demonstrated they can successfully reach Mars and land missions on the Martian surface, the next phases of Martian exploration will prove exceptionally challenging—and expensive. Successfully executing the MSR mission and developing plans to send humans to Mars will take more resources than NASA has heretofore allocated to these initiatives, confronting NASA leaders with difficult budgetary choices. Though NASA links its Moon-focused initiatives to its future plans for Mars, as operating on the Moon is a good Martian test environment, the emphasis on returning humans to the Moon has come at a cost to other initiatives, such as MSR.For fiscal year 2025, NASA had planned to spend nearly a third of its budget on the Artemis program, its plans to return humans to the Moon. Without a significant overall agency-level budget increase, NASA may have to reassess the scope of the Artemis program to free up more resources for Mars. Realigning Artemis primarily as a test bed for technologies and procedures for operating on Mars, and less about establishing a long-term lunar presence, might create the right balance. This could allow NASA to return astronauts to the Moon before China gets humans there for the first time and use the Moon as a proving ground for crewed technologies and systems intended for Mars.In addition to spending more on Mars initiatives, NASA will also have to decide and commit to a Mars plan of action. In the short term, NASA will need to decide what to do with MSR. Right now, it plans to wait 18 more months to decide on a way forward, at a time when China will be hard at work designing and building its own sample return mission hardware. That SpaceX is independently working to send Starships to Mars next year strongly suggests there is an opportunity both to refocus MSR plans around an accelerated timeline and find further cost savings. Ultimately, a successful and accelerated MSR supports efforts to land humans on Mars, as data gleaned from Martian samples will help scientists and engineers better understand and use the Martian environment for future crewed missions.Q5: What is at stake?A5: Simply put, the future of humanity is likely tied to Mars. Though a human could not survive unaided on the Martian surface today—surviving on Mars would take a similar level of equipment as would living on the Moon—critical building blocks are present for creating a more habitable environment. These include chemical compounds, frozen water, sources of energy, such as solar and geochemical, and Earth-like physical characteristics, enumerated earlier, that could be used by as-yet-not-invented technologies to shape Mars into something more conducive to human life. The scale of such an activity would be enormous, but there is no better place than Mars should humans wish to carve out another place to live that is not Earth.Additionally, there is science and national prestige on the line, maybe to a greater extent than during Moon Race 1.0 between the United States and the Soviet Union because the possibilities of Mars are so great. The success of MSR would provide U.S. scientists the chance to look for signs that life once existed on Mars. Conclusive evidence that life originated there might be one of the most important discoveries in human history. The willingness of the new administration and Congress to prioritize funding and resources, as well as NASA’s willingness to reexamine Artemis, will likely determine whether the United States can accomplish a Martian sample return and, probably, whether it can land astronauts on Mars before China. Should China achieve either of these humankind firsts, future generations of Americans would wonder why the United States let up on the accelerator and lost the Mars race.Clayton Swope is the deputy director of the Aerospace Security Project and a senior fellow in the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCritical Questions is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nSpace and Aerospace, and\nTechnology\nImage\nClayton Swope\nDeputy Director, Aerospace Security Project and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nAerospace Security Project\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"为什么美国应优先考虑火星？\n照片：Stocktrek Images/Getty Images\n关键问题\n作者\nClayton Swope\n发表于2025年1月23日\n远程可视化\n在2024年1月20日的就职演说中，唐纳德·特朗普总统表示，他将优先发展以火星为重点的美国太空事业。尽管美国国家航空航天局（NASA）拥有长期的火星探测历史，包括持续执行前往这颗红色星球的任务，但总统的言论表明，在未来几年，火星可能会在NASA的议程中扮演更加核心的角色。除非同时增加NASA的预算，否则优先开展火星任务可能会影响NASA的其他活动，例如阿尔忒弥斯计划——NASA重返月球并建立长期月球存在的努力。火星转向可能表明，人们认识到，与月球相比，火星具有大得多的科学和战略价值，更不用说相对于中国而言所涉及的国家威望了。除了国家利益之外，火星最终可能成为人类长期生存的关键，因为除地球之外，火星是最明显的一个地点；凭借适当的技术，人类或许能够在一定程度上独立于我们的母星生存。\nQ1：火星有什么特别之处？A1：太阳系中没有任何其他行星像地球这样，尽管在其他七颗行星中，火星是最像地球的。火星是距太阳第四近的行星，常被称为“红色星球”，因为其固体表面覆盖着橙红色的氧化铁，这种物质的成分与铁锈相似。火星明显小于地球，直径约为地球的一半，质量约为地球的十分之一。火星上的一天与地球上的一天大致相同，持续24.6小时。火星拥有大气层，尽管与地球相比很稀薄，其中含有二氧化碳、氩气和氮气，且两极有水冰。火星上的重力只有地球重力的约38%。过去，火星上可能曾有水 океan。\n尚未发现任何确凿迹象表明生命起源于火星或目前存在于火星上，但由于火星与地球相似，科学家认为它可能曾经适合某种生命存在，很可能是微生物。然而截至目前，还没有科学家能够彻底检查来自火星的物质，因为尚未有人将火星表面的样本带回地球进行分析。到目前为止，对火星的所有科学分析都是远程进行的，例如通过安装在其表面运行的着陆器和漫游车上的仪器进行分析。\nQ2：火星探测的历史是怎样的？A2：火星是夜空中最明亮的天然天体之一，从地球上用肉眼即可清晰看到。一些现存最早的火星观测记录可追溯至古埃及。1610年，意大利天文学家伽利略·伽利莱使用望远镜观测了火星。不久，天文学家发现了火星的冰盖，并在19世纪末注意到火星上的黄色云层。到20世纪60年代，美国和苏联展开了向火星发射无人航天器的竞赛；1964年，美国成为第一个成功抵达火星的国家，当时“水手4号”飞掠了这颗行星。1971年，苏联完成了火星上的首次软着陆。同年，美国成功将“水手9号”送入火星轨道，成为第一个将航天器送入另一颗行星轨道的国家。1975年，美国将“海盗1号”降落在火星上，\n成为首个成功抵达火星的着陆器。NASA于1997年7月4日让其首个火星车任务“火星探路者号”登陆火星。自“火星探路者号”以来，美国已发射了多辆火星车，其中一辆部署了一架小型直升机，还发射了轨道器。在过去25年中，欧洲航天局（ESA）、印度、中国和阿拉伯联合酋长国也成功执行了火星任务，其中大多数是将航天器送入轨道。中国于2021年发射的“天问一号”火星任务之所以引人注目，是因为它包括轨道器、着陆器和火星车。其成功标志着一个国家首次能够在首次火星任务中实现软着陆并部署火星车。目前总共有大约七个活跃航天器在火星轨道上运行，火星表面有两辆仍在运行的火星车，即NASA的“毅力号”和“好奇号”火星车。作为任务的一部分，“毅力号”一直在收集并封存火星表面样本，以便NASA未来的火星样本返回（MSR）任务取回这些样本。\n\nQ3：未来计划开展哪些火星任务？\nA3：二十多年来，NASA一直在研究将火星样本带回地球的可能性。2009年，为了在2020年代将样本带回地球，NASA开始与ESA合作开展最终成为MSR任务的项目，并于2018年签署了合作协议。2022年，美国国家科学院建议：“本十年NASA机器人探索工作中最高的科学优先事项，应当是在实际可行的情况下尽快完成火星样本返回”，这与其2011年和2003年的类似建议相呼应。2023年11月，NASA在一个审查委员会估计该任务的成本将远超NASA预期后，暂停了MSR的工作。\n\n2024年4月，NASA向业界征集削减成本并加快任务进度的方案，目标是在2030年代中期将“毅力号”收集的样本带回地球。在2024年1月初将修订后的选项缩减为两个之后，NASA表示将用接下来的18个月进行进一步评估，并决定新的方案。与此同时，中国正在加快自己的火星计划。2024年9月，中国表示计划将名为“天问三号”的火星采样返回任务发射时间提前至2028年。正如中国航天官员所指出的那样，该任务将持续约三年，中国或许能够在2031年前后将样本带回地球，比NASA早数年。\n\n此外，SpaceX也在推进自己的火星计划。该公司于2024年10月宣布，将于2026年开始向火星发射无人“星舰”火箭；在26个月的周期中，下一次火星与地球在轨道上距离最近的时间就是2026年。这些测试的目标是证明“星舰”能够成功抵达并着陆火星，为未来载人前往这颗红色星球铺平道路，最早可能在2028年或2029年实现。2024年12月，NASA发布了更新后的“月球到火星”架构计划，将月球和阿耳忒弥斯计划作为测试旨在实现人类登陆火星的相关能力的关键机会。NASA尚未修改奥巴马政府时期制定的目标，即在2033年前将宇航员送上火星。2021年，中国也制定了让人类登陆火星的目标，同样瞄准2033年。\n\nQ4：接下来会发生什么？\nA4：尽管美国和包括中国在内的其他几个国家已经证明它们能够\n成功抵达火星并在火星表面着陆任务后，火星探索的下一阶段将极具挑战性——也极其昂贵。成功执行火星样本返回（MSR）任务并制定将人类送上火星的计划，所需资源将超过NASA迄今为止为这些倡议分配的资源，这将使NASA领导层面临艰难的预算抉择。尽管NASA将其以月球为重点的倡议与未来的火星计划联系起来，因为在月球上开展行动是良好的火星测试环境，但重返月球的人类计划已给其他倡议带来了代价，例如MSR。\n\n对于2025财年，NASA计划将近三分之一的预算用于阿尔忒弥斯计划，即让人类重返月球的计划。在整个机构层面的预算没有显著增加的情况下，NASA可能必须重新评估阿尔忒弥斯计划的范围，以便为火星释放更多资源。将阿尔忒弥斯重新定位为主要用于测试在火星上开展行动的技术和程序，而较少着眼于建立长期月球驻留，或许能实现恰当的平衡。这可能让NASA在中国首次将人类送上月球之前把宇航员送回月球，并将月球用作载人技术和系统的试验场，这些技术和系统旨在用于火星。\n\n除了增加对火星倡议的投入外，NASA还必须决定并落实一项火星行动计划。短期内，NASA需要决定如何处理MSR。目前，它计划再等18个月才决定前进方向，而此时中国将竭力设计和建造自己的样本返回任务硬件。SpaceX正独立推进明年向火星发射星舰，这强烈表明，NASA既有机会围绕加速的时间表重新聚焦MSR计划，也有机会进一步节约成本。归根结底，成功且加速的MSR将支持把人类送上火星的努力，因为从火星样本中获取的数据将帮助科学家和工程师更好地理解和利用火星环境，为未来的载人任务服务。\n\nQ5：利害关系是什么？A5：简单地说，人类的未来很可能与火星息息相关。尽管今天人类无法在没有辅助的情况下于火星表面生存——在火星上生存所需的设备水平将与在月球上生活相近——但那里存在构建更宜居环境的关键基础要素。这些要素包括化合物、冰冻水、能源来源（如太阳能和地球化学能），以及前文列举的类似地球的物理特征；尚未发明的技术或许可以利用这些要素，将火星塑造成更适合人类生存的地方。这种活动的规模将极其庞大，但如果人类希望在地球之外开辟另一个居住地，那么没有比火星更好的地方。\n\n此外，科学和国家声望也正处于利害攸关之中，其重要性或许超过美国与苏联之间的第一次月球竞赛，因为火星的可能性如此巨大。MSR的成功将为美国科学家提供寻找火星上曾经存在生命迹象的机会。确凿证据表明生命起源于火星，可能会成为人类历史上最重要的发现之一。新一届政府和国会是否愿意优先提供资金和资源，以及NASA是否愿意重新审视阿尔忒弥斯计划，很可能将决定美国能否实现一项火星\n样本返回，以及很可能它能否在中国之前将宇航员送上火星。如果中国实现这两项人类首次成就中的任何一项，未来几代美国人都会想知道，美国为什么放松了油门，输掉了火星竞赛。\n\nClayton Swope 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心航空航天安全项目副主任兼防务与安全部高级研究员。\n\n《关键问题》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私营免税机构。其研究具有非党派性且不受专有利益影响。CSIS 不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n\n标签\n航天与航空，以及\n技术\n\n图片\nClayton Swope\n航空航天安全项目副主任兼防务与安全部高级研究员\n\n项目与计划\n航空航天安全项目\n防务与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-01-23T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2498","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"为什么美国在联合国的初级专业人员仍然重要","title_en":"Why U.S. Junior Professionals in the UN Still Matter","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心评论文章指出，美国通过初级专业人员项目向联合国输送人才，近年人数增至147人，有助于提升美国在多边机构中的影响力并对冲中国影响。但2025年撤销法案和2026财年预算请求将相关经费清零，可能大幅削减美国职位。作者建议国会每年拨款3500万美元支持美国人在联合国任职，并鼓励国务院及技术安全机构出资参与该项目。","summary_en":"A CSIS commentary argues that the U.S. Junior Professional Officer program places Americans in the UN, with numbers tripling to 147 in 2023, helping counter Chinese influence. However, the 2025 Rescissions Act and FY 2026 budget request zero out related funding, likely slashing U.S. placements. The authors recommend Congress designate $35 million annually to support Americans in the UN and urge State Department and technical agencies to fund JPO positions.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:23:01.112568+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/why-us-junior-professionals-un-still-matter","content":"Why U.S. Junior Professionals in the UN Still Matter\nPhoto: Michael via Adobe Stock\nCommentary\nby\nAllison Lombardo\nand\nElla Lipin\nPublished August 20, 2025\nRemote Visualization\nOver the last several years, there has been bipartisan support for increasing the number of Americans working in the UN, in part via the Junior Professional Officer (JPO) program. In the last Department of State, foreign operations, and related appropriations bill passed in 2024, Congress explicitly earmarked money for placing U.S. citizens in the JPO program and for the Department of State to enhance the competitiveness of U.S. citizens for leadership positions in the United Nations. The proposal by the current administration to zero out funding for the UN will negatively impact this route for U.S. influence. Instead, the United States should invest in this program with funding from across the U.S. government to have an exponential effect in boosting U.S. numbers and dramatically increasing U.S. influence and employment in key bodies.Global competitors are increasingly vying for multilateral influence—including by ensuring their nationals make up both senior and junior positions at the UN. The most direct way to do that—outside of elections or appointments—is by paying for JPO entry-level positions. These roles are funded directly by donors, essentially allowing countries to pay for junior staff in areas they care about. Position costs vary depending on location and individual family circumstances, but range between $200,000 and $400,000 per year. JPOs act as independent international civil servants who often go on to long careers at UN agencies. Critically, JPOs are retained in the UN system, finding follow-on employment after the end of their one- to three-year term at an average rate of 55 percent or greater, depending on the organization, making it one of the most reliable paths to quickly improve national representation in the UN.According to the UN, in August 2025, there were nearly 400 JPOs from nearly 30 donor countries assigned to 46 different UN departments. In 2010 and again in 2023, the Government Accountability Office (GAO) found that Americans were underrepresented in UN employment generally. Historically, U.S. JPO placements were low. These expert positions were mainly funded by the U.S. Agency for International Development and the Department of State’s Bureau of Population, Refugees, and Migration (PRM), which placed staff into humanitarian agencies like the World Food Programme (WFP), UN High Commissioner for Refugees, or UN Children’s Fund (UNICEF), and Department of State’s Bureau of International Security and Nonproliferation, which placed Americans at the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) to work on nuclear security.But, over the last several years, the number of U.S. JPOs tripled thanks to new U.S. investment. In 2023, the United States had 147 JPOs across the UN system.The first Trump administration created a team in the Department of State’s International Organization Affairs Bureau to support the employment of Americans in the UN and increase U.S. competitiveness, primarily with the People’s Republic of China (PRC). Continuing under the Biden administration, this team became a formal office and took a number of steps to boost the JPO program by funding more U.S. positions and strategically placing them in parts of the UN that had not previously hosted U.S. JPOs, such as the secretariat and key technical and standard setting bodies based in Geneva, where Beijing is heavily boosting its numbers. This new investment in the JPO program was bolstered by advertising the positions more broadly, establishing annual training for U.S. JPOs, and assigning staff to help them with follow-on placements at the UN. Financial support for these efforts began in 2019, and a Republican Congress boosted the funding for JPOs to a $5 million earmark in the FY 2024 International Organizations and Programs (IO&P) account.Unfortunately, the Rescissions Act of 2025 zeroed out the remaining IO&P account, likely dramatically cutting U.S. placements. The FY 2026 Budget Request zeros it out altogether. Plus, this budget stripped the PRM of the majority of its program funding and made severe cuts to the humanitarian aid budget. This means few to no U.S. JPOs going forward.Before this dramatic turn of events, there was bipartisan support in both the executive and legislative branches to boost and sustain this effort. Why?Most overtly, there has been broad agreement on the need to counter rising PRC influence at the UN. There is direct great power competition across several spheres in the multilateral system, including personnel. Beijing clearly sees the opportunity in the JPO program and has dramatically ramped up its investment over the past decade accordingly. The number of Chinese nationals employed by the United Nations nearly doubled from 2009 to 2022, and in a study of just four UN entities, the UN Secretariat, Food and Agriculture Organization, World Health Organization, and International Labour Organization—China had the largest increase of JPOs: from 0 in 2015 to 49 in 2022, with likely dozens more today.Across both the first Trump and Biden administrations, many in the executive branch and on the Hill made the case that strong U.S. candidates for UN leadership positions would advance U.S. interests. The JPO program acts as a feeder of talented staff into the UN bureaucracy and, thus, eventually, into UN leadership roles. This is especially salient with the high number of UN leaders reaching retirement age in the next decade. Even nearly two decades ago, in 2007, the UN identified that over 22 percent of its staff were over 55 and approaching retirement. This demographic challenge means that having qualified candidates in the right place at the right time can easily extend U.S. influence.But does it really matter if an UN employee is an American? Yes. Internal staff help shape UN decisionmaking and norms, and having free-market, pro-democracy Americans involved can matter. This remains particularly true when the alternatives may be authoritarian or statist competitors more influenced or tied to their home governments. For instance, under PRC national Zhao Houlin’s leadership of the International Telecommunications Union (ITU) from 2015 to 2022, historical documents were revised, substituting “Taiwan, Province of China” for the original “Taiwan.” As the Trump administration demonstrated recently with its endorsement of Doreen Bogdan-Martin for another leadership term at ITU, most administrations find Americans in leadership at the UN useful.Even for those who do not believe the United States’ engagement at the UN advances U.S. interests, individual Americans’ contributions can be an easy way to retain some U.S. inroads to a UN that will soldier on even without the United States as an active participant.RecommendationsSupporting Americans working at the UN via the JPO program is a relatively hands-off way to ensure that even as the United States draws down its direct engagements and financial investment in multilateral institutions, it keeps a hand in the future of areas that align with U.S. interests. In order to continue the bipartisan investment in promoting Americans in the UN, it will be critical to increase the funding for JPOs and other efforts and get more U.S. agencies participating in the effort.Congress should designate $35 million a year to support Americans in the UN. Looking at the FY 2026 budget, Congress should make clear that a strong multi-sector JPO program would benefit U.S. interests regardless of the government’s engagement—or disengagement—at the UN. Specifically, congressional appropriators should make clear in their mark-up that at least $35 million a year is needed to support Americans in the UN and to maintain the current number of U.S. JPOs. This funding can be earmarked within the newly requested $2.9 billion America First Opportunities fund, where the president has discretion to provide contributions to international organizations that align with White House priorities.The Department of State should have funding for staff placement at the humanitarian and global health–related agencies that align with the new “International Humanitarian Assistance” account. A pipeline in the humanitarian sector is crucial if the United States is to maintain its leadership of WFP and UNICEF, and if —despite severe budget reductions—the United States will most likely remain the largest bilateral donor. Past anecdotes would suggest that driving reform and shaping priorities is smoother when UN partners are compatriots.Technical and security U.S. government agencies should designate their own funding for JPOs. The United States will fall behind the PRC’s investment in JPOs if it simply maintains the current level of funding. Funding should also be earmarked for JPOs from other departments and agencies, including the technical and security agencies that the Trump administration has exempted from budget cuts. For example, the FAA could fund staff at the International Civil Aviation Organization, Commerce at the International Maritime Organization, the ITU, the World Intellectual Property Organization (WIPO), the Department of Energy at the IAEA, and the Department of Defense at the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons.All U.S. government departments and agencies should encourage JPOs to specialize in areas that align with current U.S. foreign policy. This includes: nuclear weapons, peace and security, humanitarian aid, and key standard-setting bodies such as ITU and WIPO. Senator Risch called attention to these fields in 2020 for China’s efforts to increase its own representation in influential roles.Allison Lombardo is a senior associate (non-resident) with the Humanitarian Agenda and Human Rights Initiative at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C. Ella Lipin servedas deputy assistant secretary with the U.S. Department of State’s Bureau of International Organization Affairs.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nHuman Rights\nImage\nAllison Lombardo\nSenior Associate (Non-resident), Humanitarian Agenda and Human Rights Initiative\nElla Lipin\nFormer Deputy Assistant Secretary, U.S. Department of State Bureau of International Organization Affairs.\nPrograms & Projects\nHuman Rights Initiative\nHumanitarian Agenda\nGlobal Development","content_cn":"为什么联合国中的美国初级专业人员仍然重要\n照片：Michael via Adobe Stock\n评论\n作者\nAllison Lombardo\n和\nElla Lipin\n发表于2025年8月20日\n远程可视化\n\n过去数年间，两党一直支持增加在联合国工作的美国人数量，其中部分通过初级专业人员（JPO）计划实现。在国会于2024年通过的上一份国务院、对外行动及相关拨款法案中，国会明确拨款用于安排美国公民参加JPO计划，并要求国务院提升美国公民竞逐联合国领导职位的竞争力。现政府提出将联合国经费削减为零，这将对美国施加影响的这一途径造成负面影响。相反，美国应利用来自美国政府各部门的资金投资该计划，以产生指数级效果，增加美国人员数量，并大幅提升美国在关键机构中的影响力和就业机会。\n\n全球竞争者正日益争夺多边影响力，包括确保本国国民在联合国担任高级和初级职位。除选举或任命之外，最直接的做法就是为JPO入门级职位提供资金。这些职位由捐助方直接资助，实质上允许各国为其所关心领域的初级工作人员买单。职位成本因地点和个人家庭情况而异，但每年在20万至40万美元之间。JPO以独立国际公务员身份工作，之后往往在联合国机构开启长期职业生涯。关键的是，JPO会被联合国系统留用，在其一至三年任期结束后继续找到工作，平均比例达到或超过55%，具体取决于所属机构，因此这是迅速改善一国在联合国代表性的最可靠途径之一。\n\n据联合国统计，2025年8月，来自近30个捐助国的近400名JPO被派驻联合国46个不同部门。政府问责局（GAO）在2010年和2023年再次发现，美国人在联合国总体就业中所占比例不足。历史上，美国JPO派驻人数较少。这些专家职位主要由美国国际开发署和国务院人口、难民及移民局（PRM）资助，后者将人员派往世界粮食计划署（WFP）、联合国难民事务高级专员公署或联合国儿童基金会（UNICEF）等人道主义机构；国务院国际安全与防扩散局则将美国人派往国际原子能机构（IAEA），从事核安全工作。\n\n然而，得益于美国新的投资，过去数年间美国JPO人数增加了两倍。2023年，美国在联合国系统内拥有147名JPO。\n\n第一届特朗普政府在国务院国际组织事务局组建了一个团队，支持美国人在联合国就业并提升美国竞争力，主要是与中华人民共和国（PRC）竞争。在拜登政府时期，该团队继续运作并成为正式办公室，还采取了一系列措施，通过资助更多美国职位，并将其战略性地安排在联合国此前未接收美国JPO的部门，例如秘书处以及关键技术和标准制定\n总部设在日内瓦的机构，而北京正在大力增加其人数。这项对JPO项目的新投资得到了多方面措施的支持，包括更广泛地宣传这些职位、为美国JPO建立年度培训，以及安排工作人员帮助他们在联合国获得后续任职机会。对这些工作的财政支持始于2019年，而共和党国会将JPO经费提高到2024财年《国际组织与项目》（IO&P）账户中的500万美元专项拨款。不幸的是，2025年《撤销法》将剩余的IO&P账户资金全部清零，可能大幅削减美国的任职安排。2026财年预算申请则彻底将其清零。此外，这份预算削减了PRM项目资金的大部分，并大幅削减了人道援助预算。这意味着今后美国JPO将寥寥无几，甚至不复存在。\n\n在这一事态发生戏剧性转折之前，行政和立法两大分支都曾有两党支持来扩大并维持这项工作。为什么？\n\n最明显的一点是，各方普遍同意有必要遏制中华人民共和国在联合国日益上升的影响力。在多边体系的多个领域，包括人员领域，都存在直接的大国竞争。北京显然看到了JPO项目中的机会，并据此在过去十年大幅增加投入。联合国雇用的中国籍人员数量从2009年到2022年几乎翻了一番；而在仅对联合国秘书处、联合国粮食及农业组织、世界卫生组织和国际劳工组织这四个联合国实体进行的研究中，中国籍JPO人数增幅最大：从2015年的0人增加到2022年的49人，如今可能还要多出几十人。\n\n在第一届特朗普政府和拜登政府任期内，行政部门和国会山的许多人都提出，有能力的美国候选人担任联合国领导职务将推进美国利益。JPO项目为联合国官僚体系输送优秀人才，因此最终也会为联合国领导岗位输送人才。考虑到未来十年将有大量联合国领导人达到退休年龄，这一点尤其重要。甚至早在近二十年前的2007年，联合国就已确定，其员工中超过22%的人年龄在55岁以上并接近退休。这一人口结构挑战意味着，只要在正确的时间把合格的候选人放在正确的位置，就能轻易延伸美国的影响力。\n\n但如果一名联合国雇员是美国人，这真的重要吗？重要。内部工作人员有助于塑造联合国的决策和规范，让信奉自由市场、支持民主的美国人参与其中可能会产生影响。当替代者可能是更受本国政府影响或与其有联系的威权主义或国家主义竞争者时，这一点尤其如此。例如，在中华人民共和国籍官员赵厚林于2015年至2022年领导国际电信联盟（ITU）期间，历史文件被修改，用“中国台湾省”取代了原来的“台湾”。正如特朗普政府最近支持多琳·博格丹-马丁在国际电信联盟担任另一任领导职务所表明的那样，大多数政府都认为美国人在联合国担任领导职务是有用的。\n\n即使有人不认为美国参与联合国能够推进美国利益，个别美国人的贡献也可以成为一种简单方式，让美国在一个即使没有美国积极参与仍将继续运转的联合国中保留一些切入点。\n\n建议\n\n通过JPO项目支持在联合国工作的美国人，是一项\n一种相对不干预的方式，以确保即使美国减少其对多边机构的直接参与和财政投资，它仍能参与塑造符合美国利益领域的未来。为了继续两党对推动美国人在联合国任职的投资，增加对初级专业人员（JPO）及其他相关工作的资助，并让更多美国政府机构参与其中，将至关重要。\n国会应每年拨款3500万美元，用于支持美国人在联合国任职。审视2026财年预算时，国会应明确指出，无论政府参与还是不参与联合国，一个强有力的多部门JPO项目都将有利于美国利益。具体而言，国会拨款委员会应在其标记稿中明确指出，每年至少需要3500万美元来支持美国人在联合国任职，并维持当前美国JPO的数量。这笔资金可以从总统新近要求设立的29亿美元“美国优先机遇基金”中专门拨出；总统可酌情向符合白宫优先事项的国际组织提供捐款。\n国务院应为在人道主义和全球卫生相关机构安排工作人员提供资金，这些机构与新的“国际人道主义援助”账户相契合。如果美国要维持其对世界粮食计划署和联合国儿童基金会的领导地位，并且——尽管预算大幅削减——美国很可能仍将是最大的双边捐助国，那么在人道主义领域建立人才输送渠道至关重要。过去的事例表明，当联合国合作伙伴是本国同胞时，推动改革和塑造优先事项会更加顺畅。\n美国政府的技术和安全机构应为JPO指定各自的资金。如果只是维持当前资助水平，美国在JPO投资方面将落后于中华人民共和国。还应为其他部门和机构的JPO划拨专门资金，包括特朗普政府豁免其预算削减的技术和安全机构。例如，联邦航空管理局可以资助国际民用航空组织的工作人员，商务部可以资助国际海事组织、国际电信联盟和世界知识产权组织（WIPO）的工作人员，能源部可以资助国际原子能机构的工作人员，国防部可以资助禁止化学武器组织的工作人员。\n美国所有政府部门和机构都应鼓励JPO专门研究符合当前美国外交政策的领域。这包括核武器、和平与安全、人道主义援助，以及国际电信联盟和世界知识产权组织等关键标准制定机构。里施参议员曾在2020年针对中国努力增加其在具有影响力职位中的代表性时，特别强调了这些领域。\nAllison Lombardo是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）人道主义议程与人权倡议项目的高级（非驻会）副研究员。Ella Lipin曾任美国国务院国际组织事务局副助理国务卿。\n评论由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）撰写。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且非专有性。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表该\n作者。\n© 2025年战略与国际问题研究中心。保留所有权利。\n标签\n人权\n图片\nAllison Lombardo\n人道主义议程与人权倡议高级助理（非驻）\nElla Lipin\n美国国务院国际组织事务局前副助理国务卿。\n项目与计划\n人权倡议\n人道主义议程\n全球发展","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-08-20T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3326","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"凛冬将至：能源与经济如何影响乌克兰谈判","title_en":"Winter is Coming: How Energy and Economics Will Impact Negotiations in Ukraine","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心评论文章分析能源价格、欧洲经济增长乏力与公众舆论变化将如何影响乌克兰谈判环境。作者指出，俄罗斯持续打击乌克兰能源基础设施，冬季寒潮与能源需求上升可能加大欧洲国内压力；建议美国增加能源出口、暂缓对欧关税，并配合军事支持，使基辅在谈判桌上处于更有利地位。","summary_en":"This CSIS commentary examines how energy prices, sluggish European growth, and shifting public opinion will shape the environment for negotiations over Ukraine. It notes that Russia's sustained strikes on Ukrainian energy infrastructure, combined with a cold winter and rising energy demand, could intensify domestic pressure in Europe. It recommends that the United States increase energy exports and pause tariffs on European partners, alongside military support, to strengthen Kyiv's hand at the negotiating table.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:12:06.508037+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/winter-coming-how-energy-and-economics-will-impact-negotiations-ukraine","content":"Winter is Coming: How Energy and Economics Will Impact Negotiations in Ukraine\nPhoto: Artem Hvozdkov/Getty Images\nCommentary\nby\nBenjamin Jensen\nPublished December 10, 2024\nRemote Visualization\nThis series—led by the Futures Lab and featuring scholars across CSIS—explores emerging challenges and opportunities likely to shape peace negotiations to end the war in Ukraine. All contributions in the series can be found by visiting Strategic Headwinds: Understanding the Forces Shaping Ukraine’s Path to Peace.The climate surrounding any negotiation in Ukraine will be chilly at best, marked by concerns about energy prices, economic growth in Europe, and shifting public opinion on the continent. This confluence of economic and political factors will make it difficult, albeit not impossible to support Ukraine’s desire to take back its territory and hold the Kremlin accountable. As a result, the incoming Trump administration should increase energy exports and pause any tariffs on European partners during the negotiation process. When combined with military strategy to bolster Ukrainian forces defending along the frontline, these measures ensure that Kyiv will have a stronger hand at the negotiating table.How Energy and Economics Shape Support for Negotiations in EuropeThere is a confluence of economic and political factors that could change European support for Kyiv during any negotiation. Energy costs are rising in Europe while the prospect of price volatility linked to conflicts in the Middle East remains high. Uncertainty could produce domestic pressure, especially in Germany, which heads to the ballot box in February. Rising energy costs could also tip broad-based economic stagnation, defined by low GDP growth, into a recession. Even absent a recession, rising costs amidst sluggish growth could increase domestic pressure to end the war and avoid costly military buildups in multiple European states.Historically, January—the earliest any formal negotiation might start—is the coldest month in Europe. This winter there are also concerns that a La Niña weather pattern could bring colder temperatures to Western Europe and increase the chance of an extreme weather event.As a result, there is the possibility that a sudden surge in energy demand could further amplify domestic pressure in Europe at the worst possible time. Over the fall, there has been a statistically significant decline in confidence in the Ukrainian leadership and rising concerns about the level of aid countries are providing to Kyiv. These concerns are particularly strong in right-wing populist parties gaining traction across the continent. The combination of shifting public opinion, cold weather, energy price volatility, and sluggish economic growth will create an environment where European leaders are less likely to pressure Moscow to accept more hardline Ukrainian demands at the negotiating table.How Russia’s Attacks on Energy Infrastructure in Ukraine Compound the ProblemThe economics and politics of energy are also key features of the war in Ukraine. Russia has waged a systematic campaign to destroy Ukraine’s energy infrastructure over the last two years. Moscow is increasing, not decreasing, its use of firepower strikes as a form of countervalue targeting. By punishing the population, the Kremlin seeks to compel Ukrainian leaders to end the war quickly and on terms favorable to Russia.The reality of large-scale blackouts during what could be an unseasonably cold winter will put pressure on Ukrainian leaders. Recent surveys suggest half of Ukrainians want a quick, negotiated end to the war. The population is increasingly pessimistic about the long-term prospects for the country indicative of a mix of war fatigue and the prospect of a frozen conflict similar to 2014–2022. For most, negotiation is a pause more than an end to the war. This reality will almost certainly weigh on Ukrainian leaders as they start negotiations.Supporting Ukraine in Negotiations Will Require More than Military AidMost wars end with some form of negotiation putting a premium on external military support as the combatants seek to turn battlefield gains into bargaining power. In addition to this military logic, diplomatic measures that consolidate support are critical. This is especially true when the majority of states supporting Kyiv are democratic and responsive to domestic concerns about economic growth.As a result, U.S. energy and trade policy towards Europe has a critical, indirect role in setting conditions for any negotiation to end the war in Ukraine. First, the politics of energy exports and diplomatic negotiations are related. The United States can drop some Biden-era energy policies to influence market prices, especially for liquified natural gas. Even if these measures don’t immediately impact prices, they send a signal to markets and European leaders that the United States will be a stable source of energy going forward.Second, if not carefully enacted tariffs could undermine efforts to ensure Ukraine negotiates from a position of strength. While the Trump administration explores using large-scale tariffs, it should consider how these measures could further weigh on European growth and undermine the transatlantic relationship. Yes, the United States deserves fair trade deals, but the world also needs an end to the war in Ukraine that doesn’t encourage other authoritarian states—especially China—to launch gambles to annex territory. The Trump team must strategically sequence new trade policy and multilateral efforts to support negotiations.Even without external pressure, the war in Ukraine was likely headed towards an end in the next nine months. Ukraine is struggling to mobilize enough troops, and the Russian economy is finally on the edge of collapse. But how the war ends and what it says about the free world have yet to be decided.Benjamin Jensen is a senior fellow in the Futures Lab at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nCommentary is produced by the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2024 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nUkraine,\nEconomic Security,\nDefense and Security,\nGeopolitics and International Security,\nEnergy and Geopolitics,\nEnergy and Sustainability,\nGlobal Markets,\nTrade and International Business, and\nUkraine War\nImage\nBenjamin Jensen\nDirector, Futures Lab and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security\nFutures Lab\nStrategic Headwinds: Understanding the Forces Shaping Ukraine’s Path to Peace","content_cn":"冬季将至：能源与经济将如何影响乌克兰谈判\n照片：Artem Hvozdkov/Getty Images\n评论\n作者\nBenjamin Jensen\n发表于2024年12月10日\n远程可视化\n本系列由未来实验室主导，汇集CSIS各领域学者，探讨可能塑造结束乌克兰战争之和平谈判的新兴挑战与机遇。访问“战略逆风：理解塑造乌克兰和平道路的力量”，即可查看本系列的所有文章。乌克兰任何谈判的周边环境充其量将是寒冷的，其特点是人们对能源价格、欧洲经济增长以及欧洲大陆公众舆论变化的担忧。这些经济和政治因素的交汇，将使支持乌克兰收复领土并追究克里姆林宫责任变得困难，尽管并非不可能。因此，即将上任的特朗普政府应增加能源出口，并在谈判过程中暂停对欧洲伙伴征收任何关税。若与增强乌克兰前线防御部队的军事战略相结合，这些措施将确保基辅在谈判桌上拥有更强的筹码。\n能源与经济如何塑造欧洲对谈判的支持\n一系列经济和政治因素可能会在任何谈判期间改变欧洲对基辅的支持。欧洲能源成本正在上升，而与中东冲突相关的价格波动前景仍然很高。不确定性可能产生国内压力，尤其是在德国，该国将于2月举行选举。能源成本上升还可能使广泛存在的经济停滞——其特征是GDP增长低迷——转变为衰退。即使没有衰退，在增长乏力的情况下成本上升，也可能增加欧洲多个国家要求结束战争、避免代价高昂的军事扩张的国内压力。\n从历史上看，1月是欧洲最寒冷的月份，也是正式谈判最早可能开始的时间。今年冬季，人们还担心拉尼娜天气模式可能给西欧带来更低的气温，并增加极端天气事件发生的可能性。\n因此，能源需求突然激增，有可能在最糟糕的时刻进一步放大欧洲的国内压力。整个秋季，对乌克兰领导层的信心出现了统计上显著的下降，人们对各国向基辅提供援助的程度也日益担忧。这些担忧在整个欧洲大陆日益获得支持的右翼民粹主义政党中尤为强烈。公众舆论变化、寒冷天气、能源价格波动和经济增长乏力的共同作用，将营造一种环境，使欧洲领导人不太可能在谈判桌上向莫斯科施压，要求其接受乌克兰更加强硬的要求。\n俄罗斯对乌克兰能源基础设施的袭击如何使问题更加复杂\n能源经济学与政治同样是乌克兰战争的关键特征。过去两年，俄罗斯一直在系统性地摧毁乌克兰的能源基础设施。莫斯科正在增加而非减少将火力打击作为反价值目标的一种形式。克里姆林宫通过惩罚民众，试图迫使乌克兰领导人迅速结束战争，并接受有利于俄罗斯的条件。\n在一个可能异常寒冷的冬季里，大规模停电的现实将使\n对乌克兰领导人的压力。近期调查显示，一半乌克兰人希望通过谈判迅速结束战争。民众对国家长期前景越来越悲观，这表明战争疲劳与类似于2014年至2022年冻结冲突前景的混合影响。对大多数人而言，谈判更像是暂停，而不是结束战争。这一现实几乎肯定会在乌克兰领导人开始谈判时对他们产生影响。\n\n在谈判中支持乌克兰将需要的不仅仅是军事援助\n\n大多数战争最终都会以某种形式的谈判结束，这使得外部军事支持显得尤为重要，因为交战方寻求将战场上的成果转化为谈判筹码。除了这种军事逻辑之外，巩固支持的外交措施也至关重要。当支持基辅的大多数国家都是民主国家，并且需要回应国内对经济增长的关切时，这一点尤其如此。\n\n因此，美国对欧洲的能源和贸易政策，在为结束乌克兰战争的任何谈判设定条件方面发挥着关键的间接作用。首先，能源出口政治与外交谈判相互关联。美国可以放弃拜登时期的一些能源政策，以影响市场价格，尤其是液化天然气的价格。即使这些措施不会立即影响价格，它们也会向市场和欧洲领导人发出信号，表明美国今后将成为稳定的能源来源。\n\n其次，如果关税实施不够谨慎，可能会削弱确保乌克兰以有利地位进行谈判的努力。特朗普政府正在探索使用大规模关税之际，应考虑这些措施可能如何进一步拖累欧洲增长并削弱跨大西洋关系。是的，美国应当获得公平的贸易协议，但世界也需要结束乌克兰战争，而且这一结局不能鼓励其他威权国家——尤其是中国——冒险发动行动以吞并领土。特朗普团队必须在战略上安排新的贸易政策与多边努力的先后顺序，以支持谈判。\n\n即使没有外部压力，乌克兰战争很可能也会在未来九个月内走向结束。乌克兰正在努力动员足够的军队，而俄罗斯经济终于濒临崩溃。但战争将如何结束，以及这对自由世界意味着什么，仍有待决定。\n\n本杰明·詹森是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心未来实验室的高级研究员。\n\n评论由战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）制作。该中心是一家私营、免税机构，专注于国际公共政策问题。其研究具有非党派性且不受专有性限制。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论都应理解为仅代表作者个人。\n\n© 2024 战略与国际研究中心。版权所有。\n\n标签\n乌克兰，\n经济安全，\n国防与安全，\n地缘政治与国际安全，\n能源与地缘政治，\n能源与可持续发展，\n全球市场，\n贸易与国际商业，以及\n乌克兰战争\n\n图片\n本杰明·詹森\n未来实验室主任、国防与安全部高级研究员\n\n项目与计划\n国防与安全\n未来实验室\n\n战略逆风：了解塑造乌克兰和平之路的力量","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-10T12:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1220","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"借来的时间：过度前置发债的经济与金融风险","title_en":"Running on Borrowed Time: The Economic and Financial Risks of Excessive Front-end Debt Issuance | Charting Geoeconomics | CSIS","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心博客文章分析美国国债过度依赖短期国库券发行的风险。文章指出，美国国债已达38.77万亿美元，利息支出占联邦支出12.1%。持续大量发行短期国库券虽可降低借贷成本，但加剧了展期风险，并抽干回购市场流动性，迫使美联储频繁干预。文章警告这可能形成道德风险，导致财政主导，削弱美联储货币政策独立性，建议财政部回归更均衡的期限结构，以维护金融体系稳定。","summary_en":"A CSIS blog post analyzes the risks of the U.S. Treasury's excessive reliance on short-term T-bill issuance. With national debt at $38.77 trillion and interest payments at 12.1 percent of federal spending, persistent T-bill issuance lowers borrowing costs but heightens rollover risk and drains repo market liquidity, forcing frequent Fed intervention. The article warns this creates moral hazard and risks fiscal dominance, undermining Fed independence, and recommends returning to a more balanced maturity profile to preserve financial stability.","tag":"risk","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:31:31.460966+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/charting-geoeconomics/running-borrowed-time-economic-and-financial-risks-excessive-front-0","content":"Running on Borrowed Time: The Economic and Financial Risks of Excessive Front-end Debt Issuance\nPhoto: KTSDESIGN/SCIENCE PHOTO LIBRARY/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nDavid Yang\nPublished April 6, 2026\nAmerica's economic security rests on its “exorbitant privilege” to borrow at low interest costs relative to the size of its debt, which sustains its economic and geopolitical strength. With the national debt at $38.77 trillion at the beginning of 2026, up 67 percent since 2020, and interest payments making up 12.1 percent of federal spending, sound management of this debt and the capital markets that fund it is of critical importance to U.S. resilience to economic and financial shocks. The persistent shift in issuance strategy to short-term Treasuries, starting under the previous administration and continuing in the current, risks limiting the government’s ability to respond to future shocks, clogging a vital artery of the financial system, and constraining monetary policy flexibility.The U.S. Treasury’s principal objective in debt management is to “finance the government at the lowest cost over time”, which involves issuing debt across a range of maturities. T-bills, the shortest-maturity bonds, play a crucial role as a “shock absorber” of government borrowing. Maturing between just one month and one year, the price of T-bills is less sensitive to interest changes than longer maturity bonds, in other words, having a low duration. This makes T-bills an attractive safe-haven asset for investors to store cash on a short-term basis. This dash for T-bills in economic crises means that when the Treasury is in immediate need of a lot of cash, such as to fund emergency stimulus, a surge in T-bill issuance is the most effective way to keep the government liquid. However, although T-bills are price insensitive to rates, their yields—what the government pays in interest—are highly sensitive to short-term interest rate changes. Thus, their frequent refinancing exposes the government to volatile borrowing costs and investors to volatile returns over time, known as rollover risk.\nRemote Visualization\nThis is seen in Figure 1, where the T-bill share of outstanding debt rises during recessions when governments must make fiscal interventions, and gradually declines during expansions as the Treasury returns to a more balanced maturity profile. This cycle maintains the Treasury’s ability to respond to crises like the 2008 financial crisis or the COVID-19 pandemic with countercyclical policy without oversaturating Treasury markets. In 2023, the T-bill share of debt surged in the absence of a recession, instead as a measure to keep the Treasury liquid after it drew down on its cash reserves when the United States temporarily hit its debt ceiling. While outside of a recession, the immediate need for cash necessitated compressing the T-bill “shock absorber”. The issue, however, has been the continued persistent reliance on T-bill issuance to finance the deficit. It is tempting for the Treasury to continue using short-term bonds to finance the national debt. Treasuries remain the predominant global safe-haven asset, as no other country competes with the depth and liquidity of U.S. debt capital markets. However, the “convenience yield” of long-term Treasuries has declined in the face of increased U.S. economic policy uncertainty and fiscal imbalances. In other words, the premium global investors are willing to pay for long bonds’ safety and liquidity is falling. Conversely, T-bills carry a lower “term premium” than long-term debt as there is less risk to holding them over their short maturity, and are less reliant on foreign demand, with just 22 percent foreign ownership compared to 37 percent of longer-term Treasuries (see figure 2). While interest rates remain elevated, it is attractive for the Treasury to lean on T-bill issuance to keep borrowing costs low.\nRemote Visualization\nHowever, excessive and persistent T-bill issuance poses acute risks to government borrowing. The yield sensitivity to rate changes of T-bills creates rollover risk for the government. With debt being refinanced more frequently, the United States’ interest payments, already at all-time highs, become more sensitive to short-term changes in interest rates. Macroeconomic uncertainty stemming from countervailing trade, fiscal, and immigration policies complicates the Federal Reserve’s (Fed) interest rate path. Combined with a potential global energy crisis from conflict with Iran in the Persian Gulf, the potential for upside surprises to inflation and therefore interest rates could cause sudden, sharp rises in the U.S.’ borrowing costs.\nRemote Visualization\nThe Treasury might point to how investors will always demand T-bills for their near-money status, always being attractive to park reserve cash and earn interest on a short-term basis. But it is precisely this substitutability that poses a serious threat to a crucial artery of the financial system.Excessive T-bill issuance risks clogging the financial plumbingThe short maturity and market liquidity of T-bills mean that they are “near money”. Banks can easily liquidate them into cash if needed, but would rather earn their small interest essentially risk-free than hold cash that is eroded away by inflation. However, excessive T-bill issuance draws down on cash in the system, which reduces lending and seizes up key channels of financial activity. That forces the Fed to be a backstop and buy up what the private sector cannot, at the cost of its monetary independence, and creates a moral hazard for the Treasury.The Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR) is the interest rate for the shortest-term loans between financial institutions using Treasuries as collateral, otherwise known as repurchase agreements or “repo”. Although not directly set or targeted by the Fed, SOFR is the effective “market price of money” for over $3T in daily financing volume that feeds into the real economy. As a key short-term interest rate, the Fed works to prevent major deviations between SOFR and its Federal Funds Rate (FFR) target range to ensure effective monetary policy transmission. Under normal market conditions, the Fed achieves this by acting as a lender and borrower of last resort to financial institutions at the ceiling and floor of the FFR target range.\nRemote Visualization\nExcessive T-bill issuance disrupts repo markets by draining liquidity and seizing up lending. Banks buying up all the T-bills cost cash, while they must also meet minimum cash buffer regulations. When cash reserves fall too low, banks stop lending on repo markets to comply with reserve requirements. Yet demand to borrow cash does not fall and indeed grows with more T-bills as hedge funds in highly leveraged Treasury basis trades continually need to borrow to maintain their positions. With high demand for lending meeting little cash supply from banks, the SOFR spikes, causing a crucial lending artery of the financial system to tighten, and raising concern over the Fed’s ability to influence key interest rates. The Fed cannot credibly commit to its dual mandate without control over short-term interest rates, and as such has no choice but to intervene in Treasury markets. The Fed must absorb the Treasury’s excess debt issuance that markets cannot clear.Multiple such episodes have occurred in recent history, most recently in December 2025 when the FOMC decided to reverse their balance sheet reduction of Treasury holdings from COVID (known as Quantitative Tightening). Accelerated T-bill issuance during the summer last year tightened liquidity and caused the SOFR spike above the FFR target range. In response, the Fed announced it would buy $40B of T-bills per month. These “reserve management purchases” (RMP) take T-bills off banks’ books and replace them with reserves. This allows banks to comply with cash buffer requirements, allowing repo lending to function as normal at “ample reserves” and keeping the SOFR within the FFR target range.Just because the Fed stepped in and prevented a crisis does not change the problem: T-bill markets are so oversaturated that the Fed could not reduce its balance sheet below $6.5T before cash reserves in money markets were on the verge of drying up and needed intervention. Compare this to the $3.7T floor the Fed’s balance sheet hit before the September 2019 repo crisis, and it is evident that at current rates of T-bill issuance, the Fed must be not just a backstop, but an active participant in T-bill markets just to keep the financial plumbing going.\nRemote Visualization\nThe Moral Hazard of Unlimited LiquidityEconomists of the Federal Reserve Board have presented a trilemma in central bank balance sheet management. At any time, a central bank can only choose two of three goals: low short-term interest rate volatility, limited market intervention, and a small balance sheet. The repo episodes encapsulate this: to maintain control over short-term interest rates like the SOFR, the Fed must either frequently intervene with operations like RMP or maintain a larger balance sheet.\nRemote Visualization\nSource: Burcu Duygan-Bump and Robert Jay Kahn (2026)\nHowever, this trilemma only contains what is within the Fed’s control. These constraints may always be present, but excessive T-bill issuance only exacerbates them by further draining liquidity, needing more Fed involvement. This may very soon run into another hard constraint: Kevin Warsh, the nominee for Fed Chair, has made clear of his desire to significantly shrink the Fed’s balance sheet, which remains over 50 percent larger than pre-COVID levels.In the face of continued large T-bill issuance, the Fed then must choose between frequent interventions or accept volatility in short-term interest rates. Given the Fed’s ultimate mandate of setting monetary policy in pursuit of price stability and maximum employment, it is unlikely that they will accept losing control of short-term interest rates. This leaves the Fed with no choice but to regularly intervene in markets.The Treasury must not let this create a moral hazard. Price-sensitive private sector buyers are important to ensuring an efficient allocation of debt and reserves. When the Fed is an active market participant, price signals are distorted and do not discipline the Treasury’s borrowing. This encourages more borrowing, which can only be absorbed by the Fed. The Federal Reserve’s institutions are strong, but this risks creating the structural preconditions of “fiscal dominance”—where the Treasury’s borrowing needs take precedence over the Fed’s monetary policy goals: setting short-term interest rates and maintaining an optimally-sized balance sheet.The Treasury must not become dependent on T-bill issuance for the United States’ daily financing needs. Not only does it expose the deficit to greater refinancing volatility, but it creates systemic risks that reduce the U.S. government’s ability to intervene when the economy needs it most. Continued T-bill issuance will tighten repo market liquidity, raising the risk of the financial plumbing seizing up. At the same time, it compresses the borrowing shock absorber before it is most needed to intervene in crises often precipitated by financial market failures. Relying more on the Fed will only serve to entrench moral hazard and further insulate the government from market discipline.Policymakers would be well-advised to monitor stress in repo markets. As T-bill issuance continues, the “floor” of reserves needed to keep repo markets liquid will likely rise; keeping track of the floor will inform the Fed’s appetite to continue interventions like RMP as it potentially turns to further quantitative tightening. Returning issuance to a more balanced maturity profile, while temporarily painful by issuing more long-end debt that will raise yields somewhat, will reduce rollover risk, ease pressure on the financial plumbing, and give the Fed operational space to carry out its monetary policy as it sees fit. On its current path, the United States is running on a short duration of borrowed time.For more analysis on how states interact in a transitioning global economy, check out our blog series, Charting Geoeconomics.\nTags\nTrade and International Business,\nGlobal Markets,\nStrategic Capital,\nEconomic Security, and\nTrade and International Business\nDavid Yang\nFormer Research Intern, Economics Program and Scholl Chair in International Business\nPrograms & Projects\nEconomics Program and Scholl Chair in International Business\nEconomic Security and Technology","content_cn":"靠借来的时间运行：过度发行短端债务的经济与金融风险\n图片：KTSDESIGN/SCIENCE PHOTO LIBRARY/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nDavid Yang\n发表于2026年4月6日\n美国的经济安全依赖于其“过度特权”：相对于债务规模以较低利息成本借款的能力，这一特权支撑着美国的经济和地缘政治实力。2026年初，美国国债达到38.77万亿美元，较2020年增加67%，利息支付占联邦支出的12.1%。因此，稳健管理这些债务以及为其提供资金的资本市场，对于美国抵御经济和金融冲击的韧性至关重要。自上一届政府开始、并在本届政府延续的发行策略持续转向短期国债，这可能限制政府应对未来冲击的能力，堵塞金融体系的一条重要动脉，并约束货币政策的灵活性。\n美国财政部债务管理的首要目标是“随着时间推移以最低成本为政府融资”，这包括发行一系列不同期限的债务。国库券是期限最短的债券，在政府借款中发挥着关键的“减震器”作用。国库券期限仅为一个月至一年，其价格对利率变化的敏感度低于期限更长的债券，换言之，其久期较低。这使国库券成为投资者短期存放现金的理想避险资产。经济危机期间对国库券的争抢意味着，当财政部迫切需要大量现金（例如为紧急刺激措施提供资金）时，大幅增加国库券发行是保持政府流动性的最有效方式。然而，尽管国库券价格对利率不敏感，但其收益率——即政府支付的利息——对短期利率变化高度敏感。因此，频繁再融资使政府面临波动的借款成本，也使投资者随着时间推移面临波动的回报，这被称为再融资风险。\n远程可视化\n图1显示了这一点：当政府必须进行财政干预时，经济衰退期间国库券在未偿债务中的占比会上升；随着经济扩张、财政部恢复更加均衡的期限结构，该占比会逐步下降。这一循环使财政部能够在不让国债市场供给过度饱和的情况下，通过逆周期政策应对2008年金融危机或新冠肺炎疫情等危机。2023年，在没有经济衰退的情况下，国库券债务占比却激增，这是因为美国暂时触及债务上限、财政部消耗现金储备后，需要采取措施保持流动性。尽管当时并非经济衰退，但对现金的即时需求迫使国库券这一“减震器”被压缩。然而，问题在于，财政部持续依赖发行国库券来为赤字融资。\n财政部很容易继续使用短期债券为国债融资。美国国债仍是全球占主导地位的避险资产，因为没有其他国家能够与美国债务资本市场的深度和流动性相匹敌。然而，面对美国经济政策不确定性上升和财政失衡，长期美国国债的“便利收益率”已经下降。换言之，全球投资者愿意为长期债券的安全性和流动性支付的溢价正在下降。\n相反，短期国库券（T-bills）相较于长期债务承担更低的“期限溢价”，因为在其短暂到期期间持有它们的风险较小，而且对外国需求的依赖也较低：外国持有量仅占22%，而长期美国国债的外国持有量为37%（见图2）。在利率维持高位的情况下，财政部倾向于依靠发行短期国库券来保持较低的借款成本，是颇具吸引力的做法。\n远程可视化\n然而，过度且持续发行短期国库券会对政府借款构成严重风险。短期国库券收益率对利率变化的敏感性，为政府带来了再融资风险。由于债务再融资更加频繁，美国的利息支出已经处于历史最高水平，并且对利率的短期变化更加敏感。贸易、财政和移民政策相互制衡所带来的宏观经济不确定性，使美联储（Fed）的利率路径更加复杂。再加上与伊朗在波斯湾发生冲突可能引发全球能源危机，通胀及因此导致的利率出现上行意外，可能使美国的借款成本突然大幅上升。\n远程可视化\n财政部可能会指出，投资者始终会因短期国库券接近货币的属性而对其保持需求：它们始终适合用来存放储备现金，并以短期方式赚取利息。但正是这种可替代性，对金融体系的一条关键动脉构成了严重威胁。过度发行短期国库券有堵塞金融管道的风险。短期国库券期限短且市场流动性高，这意味着它们是“近似货币”。银行可以在需要时轻松将其变现为现金，但与持有会被通胀侵蚀的现金相比，银行更愿意以基本无风险的方式赚取少量利息。然而，过度发行短期国库券会抽走体系中的现金，从而减少放贷并使金融活动的关键渠道陷入停滞。这迫使美联储充当后盾，买入私人部门无法吸收的部分，代价是牺牲其货币独立性，并给财政部造成道德风险。担保隔夜融资利率（SOFR）是金融机构之间以美国国债为抵押进行最短期贷款的利率，这类交易也称为回购协议或“回购”。尽管SOFR并非由美联储直接设定或作为目标，但对于每天超过3万亿美元的融资规模而言，它是有效的“货币市场价格”，并会传导至实体经济。作为关键的短期利率，美联储努力防止SOFR与其联邦基金利率（FFR）目标区间之间出现重大偏离，以确保货币政策有效传导。在正常市场条件下，美联储通过在FFR目标区间的上限和下限为金融机构充当最后贷款人和最后借款人来实现这一点。\n远程可视化\n过度发行短期国库券会抽干流动性并使借贷陷入停滞，从而扰乱回购市场。银行买入全部短期国库券会耗用现金，同时还必须满足最低现金缓冲监管要求。当现金储备降得过低时，银行会停止在回购市场放贷，以遵守储备要求。然而，借入现金的需求并不会下降；随着短期国库券增多，需求实际上还会增长，因为进行高杠杆国债基差交易的对冲基金需要不断借入资金来维持头寸。在银行现金供应很少的情况下，高借贷需求与之相遇，SOFR便会飙升，导致金融体系一条关键的借贷动脉的\n这会导致体系收紧，并引发人们对美联储影响关键利率能力的担忧。如果无法控制短期利率，美联储就无法可信地履行其双重使命，因此只能干预国债市场。美联储必须吸收市场无法消化的财政部超额债券发行。近代历史上曾多次出现这种情况，最近一次是在2025年12月，当时联邦公开市场委员会（FOMC）决定扭转其因新冠疫情而对国债持有量进行的资产负债表缩减（即所谓的量化紧缩）。去年夏季加速发行国库券收紧了流动性，导致担保隔夜融资利率（SOFR）飙升至联邦基金利率（FFR）目标区间之上。作为回应，美联储宣布每月购买400亿美元国库券。这些“储备管理购买”（RMP）将国库券从银行账面上移除，并以储备金取而代之。这使银行能够满足现金缓冲要求，使回购贷款在“充足储备”状态下正常运作，并将SOFR维持在FFR目标区间内。仅仅因为美联储介入并避免了一场危机，并不会改变问题：国库券市场已经过度饱和，以至于在货币市场现金储备濒临枯竭、需要干预之前，美联储无法将其资产负债表缩减至6.5万亿美元以下。将这一数字与2019年9月回购危机前美联储资产负债表触及的3.7万亿美元底部相比，可以明显看出，按照当前国库券发行速度，美联储必须不仅充当后盾，还要成为国库券市场的积极参与者，才能维持金融管道的正常运转。\n远程可视化\n无限流动性的道德风险\n美联储理事会的经济学家提出了央行资产负债表管理中的三难困境。任何时候，央行只能在三个目标中选择两个：较低的短期利率波动、有限的市场干预，以及较小的资产负债表。回购事件体现了这一点：为了维持对SOFR等短期利率的控制，美联储要么必须频繁开展RMP之类的操作进行干预，要么维持更大的资产负债表。\n远程可视化\n来源：Burcu Duygan-Bump 和 Robert Jay Kahn（2026年）\n然而，这一三难困境只包含美联储能够控制的因素。这些约束可能始终存在，但过量发行国库券只会通过进一步抽走流动性、需要美联储更多参与而加剧这些约束。这可能很快触及另一项硬约束：美联储主席提名人凯文·沃什已明确表示希望大幅缩减美联储资产负债表，而该资产负债表目前仍比新冠疫情前大出50%以上。面对持续大规模发行国库券的局面，美联储就必须在频繁干预和接受短期利率波动之间作出选择。鉴于美联储的最终使命是为追求价格稳定和最大就业而制定货币政策，它不太可能接受失去对短期利率的控制。这使美联储别无选择，只能定期干预市场。财政部绝不能让这造成道德风险。对价格敏感的私营部门买家对于确保债务和储备金的有效配置至关重要。当美联储成为积极的市场参与者时，价格信号会遭到扭曲，无法约束财政部的借贷行为。这会鼓励更多借贷，而这些借贷只能由美联储吸收。美联储的制度很强大，但这会带来风险\n这正在创造“财政主导”的结构性前提——即财政部的借款需求优先于美联储的货币政策目标：设定短期利率并维持规模最优的资产负债表。财政部不能变得依赖发行短期国库券来满足美国的日常融资需求。这不仅会使赤字面临更大的再融资波动，还会造成系统性风险，削弱美国政府在经济最需要时进行干预的能力。持续发行短期国库券将收紧回购市场的流动性，提高金融基础设施陷入停滞的风险。与此同时，这会在危机干预最为需要之前，压缩借款冲击缓冲空间，而危机往往由金融市场失灵引发。更加依赖美联储只会固化道德风险，并使政府进一步脱离市场纪律。\n\n政策制定者应密切监测回购市场的压力。随着短期国库券发行持续增加，维持回购市场流动性所需的准备金“底线”可能会上升；跟踪这一底线将有助于了解美联储继续实施诸如RMP等干预措施的意愿，因为美联储可能会转向进一步的量化紧缩。恢复更加均衡的期限结构，虽然通过发行更多长期债务会在短期内造成痛苦并略微推高收益率，但将降低展期风险，缓解金融基础设施压力，并为美联储按照自身判断执行货币政策提供操作空间。按照当前路径，美国正依靠一段借来的短暂时光维持运行。\n\n如需进一步了解各国如何在转型中的全球经济中互动，请查看我们的博客系列《描绘地缘经济学》。\n\n标签\n贸易与国际商务，\n全球市场，\n战略资本，\n经济安全，以及\n贸易与国际商务\n\nDavid Yang\n经济学项目及舒尔国际商务主席前研究实习生\n\n项目与计划\n经济学项目及舒尔国际商务主席\n经济安全与技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-04-06T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2723","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：边境争端威胁泰国政府倒台","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Border Row Threatens to Topple Thai Government | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"CSIS博客文章报道，2025年5月泰柬军队在争议的崇博关口交火，造成一名柬埔寨人死亡，双方随后增兵并收紧边境管控。柬埔寨寻求国际法院裁决，泰国拒绝承认其管辖权。泰国总理佩通坦与洪森通话遭泄露，引发国内政治危机，执政联盟第二大党退出，政府濒临垮台。","summary_en":"A CSIS blog post reports that Thai and Cambodian troops exchanged fire in May 2025 at the disputed Chong Bok pass, killing one Cambodian, with both sides reinforcing troops and tightening border controls. Cambodia seeks an ICJ ruling that Thailand rejects. A leaked call between PM Paetongtarn and Hun Sen triggered a political crisis, with the coalition's second-largest party withdrawing, leaving the government near collapse.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:35:38.839200+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-border-row-threatens-topple-thai-government","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Border Row Threatens to Topple Thai Government\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nGregory B. Poling\nand\nJaphet Quitzon\nPublished June 18, 2025\nThai and Cambodian troops on May 28 exchanged gunfire at the disputed Chong Bok pass where Thailand’s northeast Ubon Ratchathani province borders Cambodia’s Preah Vihear, leaving one Cambodian dead. Both sides have since pulled back from the disputed area and committed to resolve the issue peacefully but have reinforced their military presence elsewhere along the border. The diplomatic fallout, including Thailand’s tightening of border controls, has significantly strained ties. And this week, Thailand’s governing coalition fractured, threatening to topple Prime Minister Paetongtarn Shinawatra’s government. Five days after the border clash, Cambodian prime minister Hun Manet announced that his government would seek a ruling from the International Court of Justice on four disputed border areas. Cambodia won a previous ICJ case over the area surrounding the Preah Vihear temple in 2013, but Thailand does not recognize the court’s jurisdiction. The Thai-Cambodia Joint Boundary Commission (JBC) met in Phnom Penh on June 14 to negotiate border issues but Thailand’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs insisted the commission would not discuss the disputed border areas submitted to the ICJ. The commission is scheduled to meet again in Bangkok in September. The ongoing border row has sparked public protests and threats of economic and diplomatic retaliation on both sides. Cambodian Senate president and former prime minister Hun Sen released an ultimatum on June 17 demanding Thailand restore normal checkpoint operations to avoid a full border closure and import ban. But while the Cambodian government has largely read from the same script, factions within the Thai government and military have not. Prime Minister Paetongtarn held a private phone call with Hun Sen on June 18, which the Cambodian side promptly leaked. In the call, Paetongtarn adopted a deferential tone, calling Hun Sen “Uncle” and vowing to “take care” of “anything he wants.” More damaging, she told Hun Sen that the commander of Thailand’s Second Army Region, which is responsible for the disputed border, is “aligned with the other side” (her political opponents) and blamed the army for “saying things that aren’t beneficial to the country.”The domestic political response has been immediate and devastating. Natthaphong Ruengpanyawut, leader of the opposition People’s Party which hold the most seats in the House of Representatives, called on her to dissolve parliament and step down to avoid a coup. Hours later, the Bhumjaithai Party, the second-largest in Paetongtarn’s coalition, withdrew its support for the government and accused the prime minister of “compromising Thailand’s honor, national dignity, people, and military.” It is unclear if the government can still command a majority in parliament or if other members of the coalition will follow Bhumjaithai’s lead. Paetongtarn’s coalition, barely a year in power, is on the precipice of collapse.Gregory B. Poling is a senior fellow and director for the Southeast Asia Program and the Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative at CSIS. Japhet Quitzon is an Associate Fellow for the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C.For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nAsia, and\nSoutheast Asia\nPrograms & Projects\nAsia Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nSoutheast Asia Program\nImage\nGregory B. Poling\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Southeast Asia Program and Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative\nImage\nJaphet Quitzon\nAssociate Fellow, Southeast Asia Program","content_cn":"东南亚最新动态：边境争端威胁推翻泰国政府\n图片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nGregory B. Poling\n和\nJaphet Quitzon\n发布于2025年6月18日\n5月28日，泰国东北部乌汶府与柬埔寨柏威夏省交界、有争议的Chong Bok山口，泰国和柬埔寨军队交火，造成一名柬埔寨人死亡。此后双方都从争议地区撤军，并承诺和平解决问题，但在边境沿线其他地区加强了军事存在。包括泰国收紧边境管控在内的外交后果，使两国关系严重紧张。本周，泰国执政联盟出现裂痕，威胁要推翻总理佩通坦·钦那瓦的政府。边境冲突五天后，柬埔寨首相洪玛奈宣布，其政府将就四个有争议的边境地区寻求国际法院裁决。柬埔寨曾在2013年就柏威夏寺周边地区赢得一宗国际法院案件，但泰国不承认该法院的管辖权。泰柬联合边界委员会（JBC）于6月14日在金边举行会议，谈判边境问题，但泰国外交部坚持认为，委员会不会讨论已提交国际法院的争议边境地区。委员会计划于9月在曼谷再次开会。持续的边境争端在双方引发了公众抗议以及经济和外交报复威胁。柬埔寨参议院主席、前首相洪森于6月17日发出最后通牒，要求泰国恢复检查站正常运作，以避免全面关闭边境和实施进口禁令。但尽管柬埔寨政府基本上口径一致，泰国政府和军方内部的各派却并非如此。6月18日，佩通坦与洪森进行了一次私人通话，柬方随即将通话内容泄露。通话中，佩通坦采取了恭敬语气，称洪森为“叔叔”，并承诺“照顾”他“想要的任何事情”。更具破坏性的是，她告诉洪森，负责争议边境地区的泰国第二军区司令“与另一方站在一起”（指她的政治对手），并指责军方“说了一些对国家没有益处的话”。国内政治反应迅速而猛烈。拥有众议院最多席位的反对党人民党领导人纳塔蓬·伦潘亚武表示，为避免政变，她应解散议会并下台。数小时后，佩通坦联盟中的第二大党自豪泰党撤回了对政府的支持，并指责总理“牺牲泰国的荣誉、国家尊严、人民和军队”。目前尚不清楚政府是否仍能在议会中掌握多数，或联盟中的其他成员是否会跟随自豪泰党的做法。佩通坦的联盟执政还不到一年，如今已濒临崩溃。Gregory B. Poling是战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目及亚洲海事透明度倡议的高级研究员兼主任。Japhet Quitzon是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目的副研究员。有关该地区更多政治、经济和安全分析，请查看我们的《东南亚最新动态》博客系列。若想听到该地区最新新闻及东南亚专家的观点，请在任意流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚广播》播客。\n标签\n亚洲，以及\n东南亚\n项目与计划\n亚洲项目\n地缘政治与外交政策\n东南亚项目\n图片\nGregory B. Poling\n东南亚项目及亚洲海事透明度倡议主任兼高级研究员\n图片\nJaphet Quitzon\n东南亚项目副研究员","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-06-18T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2519","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：马科斯访印度、苏林访韩国","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Marcos to India, To Lam to South Korea | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"CSIS博客文章报道，2025年8月菲律宾总统马科斯访问印度，双方将关系提升为战略伙伴关系，签署13项协议并首次举行联合海军演习。越南总书记苏林访问韩国，签署10项谅解备忘录，双方同意到2030年将双边贸易额扩大至1500亿美元，并加强国防、基础设施和关键矿产合作。","summary_en":"A CSIS blog post reports that in August 2025 Philippine President Marcos visited India, elevating ties to a strategic partnership with 13 agreements and a first joint naval exercise. Vietnam's To Lam visited South Korea, signing 10 MOUs and agreeing to expand bilateral trade to $150 billion by 2030, while strengthening defense, infrastructure, and critical minerals cooperation.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:24:42.197491+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-marcos-india-lam-south-korea","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Marcos to India, To Lam to South Korea\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nLauren Mai\nPublished August 14, 2025\nLast week, Philippine president Ferdinand Marcos Jr. and Communist Party of Vietnam general secretary To Lam visited India and South Korea respectively. A theme of both visits was the strengthening of economic and defense relationships outside of the U.S.-China rivalry. Diversification of trade ties is receiving increasing attention across the region in the weeks since U.S. president Donald Trump declared revised tariff rates on goods from nearly every country.  Marcos visited New Delhi from August 4 to 8, coinciding with the 75th anniversary of diplomatic ties between the Philippines and India. On August 5, Marcos and Indian prime minister Narendra Modi elevated the Philippines-India relationship to a strategic partnership, to be guided by a plan of action for deepening the bilateral relationship between 2025 and 2029. The two leaders also oversaw the signing of 13 bilateral agreements, with a particular emphasis on defense and maritime cooperation, which they affirmed would play a core role in the strategic partnership. In formally declaring the strategic partnership, the leaders noted concerns regarding “the situation in the South China Sea particularly with regard to coercive and aggressive actions that impact on regional peace and stability,” though they refrained from mentioning China by name.   Coinciding with the visit, the Philippines and India staged joint naval exercises in the South China Sea on August 5-6. This was the first time that the two countries had staged a naval exercise together. Armed Forces of the Philippines chief of staff General Romeo Brawner affirmed that the exercises went well and indicated that Filipino forces would engage India’s military in more joint maneuvers in the future. To Lam, meanwhile, traveled to Seoul from August 10 to 13, becoming the first foreign leader to visit South Korea since President Lee Jae Myung took office in June. The two countries signed 10 memoranda of understanding, including on nuclear and renewable energy, financial policies, and science and technology. The visit’s emphasis was on improving economic relations. Lam confirmed that the two nations agreed to expand bilateral trade to $150 billion by 2030, almost double the $86.8 billion reported in 2024. In addition to trade, the two leaders vowed to strengthen collaboration in national defense, infrastructure, and critical minerals, as well as improving cultural, educational, and people-to-people exchanges The two leaders emphasized the importance of Vietnam and South Korea increasing military ties, hoping that this cooperation improves regional security.  Lauren Mai is a Program Coordinator and Research Assistant for the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nAsia, and\nSoutheast Asia\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nAsia\nSoutheast Asia Program\nImage\nLauren Mai\nProgram Manager and Research Associate, Southeast Asia Program","content_cn":"东南亚最新动态：马科斯访印，苏林访韩\n图片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nLauren Mai\n发布于2025年8月14日\n上周，菲律宾总统费迪南德·马科斯二世和越南共产党中央委员会总书记苏林分别访问了印度和韩国。两次访问的共同主题是加强在美中竞争之外的经济和防务关系。自美国总统唐纳德·特朗普宣布对几乎所有国家商品修订关税税率以来的数周内，贸易关系多元化在整个地区受到越来越多关注。马科斯于8月4日至8日访问新德里，恰逢菲律宾与印度建交75周年。8月5日，马科斯和印度总理纳伦德拉·莫迪将菲印关系提升为战略伙伴关系，并将由一项2025年至2029年深化双边关系的行动计划指导。两位领导人还见证签署了13项双边协议，尤其侧重防务和海事合作；他们确认，这些合作将在战略伙伴关系中发挥核心作用。在正式宣布建立战略伙伴关系时，两位领导人指出，他们关切“南海局势，特别是影响地区和平与稳定的胁迫性和侵略性行动”，但未点名中国。\n\n与此次访问同期，菲律宾和印度于8月5日至6日在南海举行联合海军演习。这是两国首次共同举行海军演习。菲律宾武装部队参谋长罗密欧·布劳纳上将确认演习进展顺利，并表示菲律宾部队未来将与印度军方进行更多联合演练。\n\n与此同时，苏林于8月10日至13日访问首尔，成为韩国总统李在明6月就职以来首位访问韩国的外国领导人。两国签署了10份谅解备忘录，涵盖核能和可再生能源、金融政策以及科学技术等领域。此次访问重点在于改善经济关系。苏林确认，两国同意到2030年将双边贸易额扩大至1,500亿美元，接近2024年报告的868亿美元的两倍。除贸易外，两位领导人还承诺加强在国防、基础设施和关键矿产方面的合作，并改善文化、教育和民间交流。两位领导人强调越南和韩国加强军事联系的重要性，希望这种合作能改善地区安全。\n\nLauren Mai是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目的项目协调员兼研究助理。欲获取更多有关该地区的政治、经济和安全分析，请查看我们的博客系列《东南亚最新动态》。欲了解该地区的最新新闻并聆听东南亚专家的观点，请在任一流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚电台》播客。\n\n标签\n亚洲，和\n东南亚\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n亚洲\n东南亚项目\n图片\nLauren Mai\n东南亚项目项目经理兼研究助理","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-08-14T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3212","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：鲁比奥与南海问题","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Rubio and the South China Sea | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"美国国务卿鲁比奥上任首周即与四方安全对话伙伴及菲律宾、印尼、越南外长通话，聚焦南海海上安全，重申对菲“铁一般”支持并批评中国行为。随后美国海岸警卫队东南亚培训团队与菲律宾海岸警卫队在南菲举办为期两周的多国培训。文章指出地区对美国政策延续性持谨慎乐观，但对经济政策及援助冻结仍感不安。","summary_en":"In his first week, Secretary of State Marco Rubio focused on maritime security and the South China Sea in calls with Quad partners and the Philippines, Indonesia, and Vietnam, reaffirming 'ironclad' support for Manila and criticizing China's actions. A two-week U.S.-Philippine-led multinational maritime training followed. The region remains cautiously optimistic about policy continuity but anxious over economic policy and frozen aid.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:04:59.636221+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-rubio-and-south-china-sea","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Rubio and the South China Sea\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nLauren Mai\nand\nGregory B. Poling\nPublished January 30, 2025\nIn his first week on the job, Secretary of State Marco Rubio made maritime security and the South China Sea a central focus of discussions with Quad partners and three Southeast Asian nations: the Philippines, Indonesia, and Vietnam. This coincides with rising tensions between Philippine and Chinese vessels around Scarborough Shoal and Thitu Island in recent weeks. On January 21, Rubio met with fellow Quad foreign ministers Penny Wong of Australia, S. Jaishankar of India, and Iwaya Takeshi of Japan. The four recommitted to “a Free and Open Indo-Pacific where the rule of law, democratic values, sovereignty, and territorial integrity are upheld and defended...in all domains including the maritime domain.” In a clear swipe at Beijing, they pledged to “strongly oppose any unilateral actions that seek to change the status quo by force or coercion.” Appropriately, Rubio’s first call with a Southeast Asian counterpart was to Philippine secretary of foreign affairs Enrique Manalo. The two discussed “issues of mutual concern,” particularly China’s provocations in the South China Sea dispute, and reaffirmed the United States’s “ironclad support” for the Philippines. Rubio described China’s actions as “dangerous and destabilizing” violations of international law. On January 22, Rubio called Indonesian foreign minister Sugiono. The two emphasized the importance of peace and stability in the South China Sea and agreed to strengthen the U.S.-Indonesia Comprehensive Strategic Partnership. Four days later, Rubio spoke with Vietnam’s deputy prime minister and foreign minister Bui Thanh Son. After celebrating the 30th anniversary of U.S.-Vietnam relations as well as progress under the U.S.-Vietnam Comprehensive Strategic Partnership, Rubio raised concerns over China’s “aggressive” behavior in the South China Sea. Following these calls, the United States Coast Guard Southeast Asia Training Team partnered with the Philippine Coast Guard to host a two-week multinational training session in the southern Philippines to strengthen maritime security in the Indo-Pacific. During the training, representatives from the United States, the Philippines, Indonesia, and Vietnam practiced safe vessel boarding at sea, maritime law, evidence collection and preservation, safety and risk mitigation, and arrest techniques.  The first week of the new administration reinforces a cautious optimism in much of the region that U.S. policies toward security cooperation and the South China Sea will remain consistent. But allies and partners remain anxious as they await details on the administration’s economic policy and assess the impact of frozen U.S. assistance programs. Lauren Mai is a Program Coordinator and Research Assistant for the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. Gregory B. Poling is a senior fellow and director for the Southeast Asia Program and the Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative at CSIS.For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nAsia,\nSoutheast Asia, and\nMaritime Issues and Oceans\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nAsia Program\nSoutheast Asia Program\nImage\nLauren Mai\nProgram Manager and Research Associate, Southeast Asia Program\nImage\nGregory B. Poling\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Southeast Asia Program and Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative","content_cn":"东南亚最新动态：鲁比奥与南海\n照片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nLauren Mai\n和\nGregory B. Poling\n发表于2025年1月30日\n上任第一周，国务卿马尔科·鲁比奥便将海上安全和南海作为与四方安全对话伙伴及三个东南亚国家——菲律宾、印度尼西亚和越南——讨论的重点。与此同时，近几周菲律宾和中国船只在黄岩岛和中业岛周边的紧张局势不断升温。1月21日，鲁比奥会见了澳大利亚外交部长黄英贤、印度外交部长苏杰生和日本外务大臣岩屋毅等四方安全对话国家外长。四方再次承诺维护和捍卫“一个法治、民主价值观、主权和领土完整得到维护和捍卫的自由开放的印太地区……涵盖包括海洋领域在内的所有领域”。四方明确针对北京承诺，将“强烈反对任何试图以武力或胁迫单方面改变现状的行动”。\n鲁比奥与东南亚同行的首次通话恰当地安排给了菲律宾外交部长恩里克·马纳洛。双方讨论了“共同关切的问题”，特别是中国在南海争端中的挑衅行为，并重申美国对菲律宾的“坚定支持”。鲁比奥称中国的行动是违反国际法的“危险且破坏稳定”的行为。\n1月22日，鲁比奥致电印度尼西亚外交部长苏吉奥诺。双方强调南海和平与稳定的重要性，并同意加强美印尼全面战略伙伴关系。四天后，鲁比奥与越南副总理兼外交部长裴青山通话。在庆祝美越关系30周年以及美越全面战略伙伴关系取得进展后，鲁比奥对中国在南海的“侵略性”行为表示关切。\n在这些通话之后，美国海岸警卫队东南亚培训队与菲律宾海岸警卫队合作，在菲律宾南部举办了为期两周的多国培训，以加强印太地区海上安全。培训期间，来自美国、菲律宾、印度尼西亚和越南的代表演练了海上安全登船、海事法律、证据收集与保全、安全与风险缓解以及逮捕技术。\n新政府上任第一周强化了该地区许多国家谨慎的乐观情绪，即美国在安全合作和南海问题上的政策将保持一致。但盟友和伙伴仍在等待政府经济政策的细节，并评估被冻结的美国援助项目所造成的影响，因此依然感到焦虑。\nLauren Mai是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目的项目协调员兼研究助理。Gregory B. Poling是CSIS东南亚项目和亚洲海事透明倡议的高级研究员兼主任。\n如需了解更多有关该地区政治、经济和安全的分析，请参阅我们的博客系列《东南亚最新动态》。若要获取该地区最新新闻并听取东南亚专家的观点，请在任意流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚电台》播客。\n标签\n亚洲，\n东南亚，以及\n海事问题与海洋\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n亚洲项目\n东南亚项目\n图片\nLauren Mai\n东南亚项目项目经理兼研究助理\n图片\nGregory B. Poling\n东南亚项目和亚洲海事透明倡议主任兼高级研究员","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-01-30T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1988","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：东南亚严重洪灾","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Severe Flooding in Southeast Asia | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"自2025年11月中旬以来，三个异常晚季气旋和强化季风系统重创南亚与东南亚，斯里兰卡、印尼、泰国、马来西亚和越南受灾，至少1350人死亡、数百万人流离失所。文章详述印尼苏门答腊、泰国南部、马来西亚北部、斯里兰卡和越南的伤亡与损失，以及美国、联合国和欧盟的援助举措。","summary_en":"Since mid-November 2025, three unusually late-season cyclones and intensified monsoons have devastated South and Southeast Asia, hitting Sri Lanka, Indonesia, Thailand, Malaysia, and Vietnam, killing at least 1,350 and displacing millions. The piece details casualties and damage in Sumatra, southern Thailand, northern Malaysia, Sri Lanka, and Vietnam, plus U.S., UN, and EU relief efforts.","tag":"risk","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:45:42.097167+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-severe-flooding-southeast-asia","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Severe Flooding in Southeast Asia\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nLauren Mai\nPublished December 11, 2025\nSince mid-November, three unusually late-season cyclones and intensified monsoon systems have devastated South and Southeast Asia, impacting Sri Lanka, Indonesia, Thailand, Malaysia, and Vietnam. The intense string of storms have killed at least 1,350 people across the region and have left millions displaced. The La-Niña-intensified storms are the latest disasters to strike the region, following deadly typhoons and an earthquake that devastated the region, particularly the Philippines and Vietnam, just months prior.  Tropical Cyclone Senyar descended on Indonesia’s Aceh, North Sumatra, and West Sumatra provinces from November 22 to 25, triggering deadly floods and landslides across dozens of districts. The floods and landslides have killed over 950 people, with 274 people still missing. Over 1 million people have been displaced with more than 900,000 living in temporary shelters. The Indonesian government, which faces domestic criticism for slow response and refusing outside assistance, has said that Sumatra requires $3.11 billion in reconstruction and recovery funds. President Prabowo Subianto has announced the country will purchase 200 helicopters in 2026 to increase defense and natural disaster preparedness.  Intense monsoon rains have devastated southern Thailand, causing intense flooding across 12 provinces. At least 276 people were killed, 5,000 injured, and over 219,000 residents displaced. The flooding also hit northern Malaysia, killing at least two people and leaving 18,700 displaced. The Thai government announced it had disbursed over $31.3 million in compensation to more than 120,000 households, and that recovery efforts have restored water and electricity to almost all the affected areas. Concurrently, Cyclone Ditwah hit Sri Lanka, killing at least 618 and leaving 209 missing. Central Vietnam has faced relentless downpours since mid-November, which was worsened by Typhoon Koto, killing at least 98 people and leaving 12 missing. The Vietnamese government confirmed that more than 186,000 homes were damaged and 3 million livestock were swept away. Vietnam has faced one of its harshest typhoon seasons in years, with Typhoon Koto marking the country’s 15th to strike the country this year. In 2025, Vietnam’s storms and floods have caused more than 400 deaths, 700 injuries, and over $3.2 billion in economic losses.  On November 17, the U.S. embassy in Vietnam provided $500,000 to deliver immediate relief to families and individuals impacted by the successive typhoons. To further support Vietnam’s recovery, the United Nations has allocated $2.6 million in aid from the UN Central Emergency Response Fund. The Department of State has also extended its condolences to those impacted by the flooding in Indonesia, Malaysia, and Thailand, standing ready to support recovery efforts. The European Union released $2.8 million in emergency assistance to aid the impacted communities in Sri Lanka, Indonesia and Thailand. Lauren Mai is a Program Manager and Research Associate for the Southeast Asia Program at CSIS. For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nAsia, and\nSoutheast Asia\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nAsia\nSoutheast Asia Program\nImage\nLauren Mai\nProgram Manager and Research Associate, Southeast Asia Program","content_cn":"东南亚最新情况：东南亚严重洪灾\n图片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者：Lauren Mai\n发布于2025年12月11日\n自11月中旬以来，三个异常晚季气旋和增强的季风系统席卷南亚和东南亚，重创斯里兰卡、印度尼西亚、泰国、马来西亚和越南。这一连串强烈风暴已造成该地区至少1,350人死亡，数百万人流离失所。受拉尼娜现象增强的风暴，是该地区近期遭遇的最新灾害；就在几个月前，致命台风和一场地震重创了该地区，尤其是菲律宾和越南。热带气旋Senyar于11月22日至25日侵袭印度尼西亚亚齐省、北苏门答腊省和西苏门答腊省，在数十个地区引发致命洪水和山体滑坡。洪水和山体滑坡已造成950多人死亡，仍有274人失踪。超过100万人流离失所，其中90多万人居住在临时避难所。印度尼西亚政府因应对缓慢及拒绝外部援助而面临国内批评；政府表示，苏门答腊需要31.1亿美元的重建和恢复资金。总统普拉博沃·苏比延多宣布，印度尼西亚将在2026年购买200架直升机，以加强国防和自然灾害应对能力。\n强烈季风降雨重创泰国南部，导致12个府发生严重洪灾。至少276人死亡、5,000人受伤，超过219,000名居民流离失所。洪水还袭击了马来西亚北部，造成至少2人死亡，18,700人流离失所。泰国政府宣布，已向超过120,000户家庭发放逾3,130万美元的补偿，并且恢复工作已使几乎所有受影响地区恢复供水和供电。与此同时，气旋Ditwah袭击斯里兰卡，造成至少618人死亡、209人失踪。\n越南中部自11月中旬以来持续遭受暴雨，台风Koto使情况进一步恶化，至少98人死亡、12人失踪。越南政府确认，超过186,000栋房屋受损，300万头牲畜被冲走。越南经历了多年来最严峻的台风季之一，台风Koto成为今年袭击该国的第15个台风。2025年，越南的风暴和洪水已造成400多人死亡、700人受伤，并造成超过32亿美元的经济损失。\n11月17日，美国驻越南大使馆提供了50万美元，用于向受连续台风影响的家庭和个人提供紧急救济。为进一步支持越南的恢复工作，联合国从联合国中央应急基金中拨款260万美元援助。美国国务院还向印度尼西亚、马来西亚和泰国受洪灾影响的人表示哀悼，并准备支持恢复工作。欧盟拨款280万美元紧急援助，用于帮助斯里兰卡、印度尼西亚和泰国受影响的社区。\nLauren Mai是战略与国际问题研究中心东南亚项目的项目经理兼研究助理。\n如需了解该地区更多政治、经济和安全分析，请查看我们的博客系列《东南亚最新情况》。如需收听该地区的最新消息及东南亚专家观点，请在任何流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚广播》播客。\n标签\n亚洲，以及\n东南亚\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n亚洲\n东南亚项目\n图片\nLauren Mai\n东南亚项目项目经理兼研究助理","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-12-11T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3120","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：特朗普在东南亚的防务联盟","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Trump’s Defense Alliances in Southeast Asia | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"特朗普第二任期头30天内，美国防长赫格塞思分别与菲律宾和泰国国防官员通话，重申美菲共同防御条约承诺并讨论美泰防务合作。菲律宾获得5亿美元军援中3.36亿美元豁免于对外援助冻结；美泰随后启动第44届“金色眼镜蛇”联合军演，逾8000人及约30国参与。文章指出地区对美国对外援助不确定性仍感担忧。","summary_en":"In the second Trump administration's first 30 days, Defense Secretary Hegseth called Philippine and Thai counterparts, reaffirming the U.S.-Philippines mutual defense treaty and discussing U.S.-Thailand defense cooperation. The Philippines had $336 million of $500 million in military funding exempted from an aid freeze; the 44th Cobra Gold exercise followed with over 8,000 personnel from about 30 nations. The region remains anxious over aid uncertainty.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:59:18.625888+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-trumps-defense-alliances-southeast-asia","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Trump’s Defense Alliances in Southeast Asia\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nLauren Mai\nPublished February 27, 2025\nWithin the first 30 days of the second Trump administration, the United States has focused on its bilateral defense alliances in Southeast Asia, with U.S. secretary of defense Pete Hegseth holding phone calls with counterpart officials in the Philippines and Thailand. On February 5th, Hegseth and Philippine secretary of national defense Gilberto Teodoro, Jr. held an introductory phone call to discuss the U.S.-Philippines defense alliance. The call, only two weeks after Secretary of State Marco Rubio’s call with Philippine secretary of foreign affairs Enrique Manalo, reiterated the importance of joint efforts reestablishing deterrence in the South China Sea. The two also reaffirmed the United States’s ironclad commitment to the 1951 Mutual Defense Treaty. And two weeks after this phone call, the Philippines announced that $336 million of an allocated $500 million in U.S. military funding had been exempted from Trump’s $5.3 billion pause in foreign aid. The $336 million, allocated to modernize Philippine security forces, is one of three security financing exemptions made by the Trump administration, alongside allocations for Taiwan and Ukraine.   Later in the month, Thailand had its first high-level phone call with the new administration between Hegseth and Thai deputy prime minister and defense minister Phumtham Wechayachai. Both countries acknowledged the long-standing U.S.-Thailand alliance and discussed opportunities to strengthen the bilateral defense relationship. Alongside reaffirming the United States’s commitment to Thailand’s military modernization, Hegseth also discussed possibilities in expanding military exercises and deepening cyber and defense industrial base cooperation. The phone call came right before the United States and Thailand launched the 44th iteration of the Cobra Gold military exercises in Thailand. Cobra Gold, the largest joint and combined exercise in the Indo-Pacific region, is considered a “longstanding symbol of the U.S.-Thailand defense alliance.” This year, the exercises will include over 8,000 personnel from both nations, as well as participants from approximately 30 nations.  As the pieces fall into place over the new administration’s first 30 days, Trump is prioritizing established bilateral defense alliances. And while some Southeast Asian allies can reap the benefits, the region continues to hold its breath over the uncertainty of U.S. foreign aid.Lauren Mai is a Program Coordinator and Research Assistant for the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nAsia, and\nSoutheast Asia\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nAsia\nSoutheast Asia Program\nImage\nLauren Mai\nProgram Manager and Research Associate, Southeast Asia Program","content_cn":"东南亚最新动态：特朗普在东南亚的防务联盟\n图片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nLauren Mai\n发布于 2025年2月27日\n在第二届特朗普政府上任后的前30天内，美国将重点放在其在东南亚的双边防务联盟上，美国国防部长皮特·赫格塞思与菲律宾和泰国的对口官员通了电话。2月5日，赫格塞思与菲律宾国防部长小吉尔伯托·特奥多罗举行了介绍性通话，讨论美菲防务联盟。这次通话发生在国务卿马可·鲁比奥与菲律宾外交部长恩里克·马纳洛通话仅两周之后，重申了共同努力恢复南海威慑的重要性。两人还重申了美国对1951年《共同防御条约》的坚定承诺。在这次通话两周后，菲律宾宣布，在美国为其分配的5亿美元军事资金中，有3.36亿美元获豁免，不受特朗普暂停530亿美元对外援助的措施影响。这3.36亿美元用于菲律宾安全部队现代化，是特朗普政府作出的三项安全融资豁免之一，另外两项拨款分别面向台湾和乌克兰。\n\n当月晚些时候，泰国与新政府进行了首次高层通话，通话双方为赫格塞思和泰国副总理兼国防部长普坦·韦查亚猜。两国都确认了长期存在的美泰联盟，并讨论了加强双边防务关系的机会。除重申美国对泰国军事现代化的承诺外，赫格塞思还讨论了扩大军事演习以及深化网络安全和国防工业基础合作的可能性。这次通话恰好发生在美国和泰国在泰国启动第44届“金色眼镜蛇”军事演习之前。“金色眼镜蛇”是印太地区规模最大的联合与协同演习，被视为“美泰防务联盟的长期象征”。今年的演习将有两国8000多名人员参加，另有约30个国家的参与者。\n\n随着新政府上任前30天的各项部署逐步到位，特朗普正优先重视既有的双边防务联盟。尽管一些东南亚盟友能够从中受益，但该地区仍在对美国对外援助的不确定性中屏息观望。Lauren Mai 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目的项目协调员兼研究助理。\n\n如需了解更多关于该地区的政治、经济和安全分析，请查看我们的博客系列《东南亚最新动态》。如需了解该地区最新消息并聆听东南亚专家观点，请在任何流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚电台》播客。\n标签\n亚洲，以及\n东南亚\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n亚洲\n东南亚项目\n图片\nLauren Mai\n东南亚项目项目经理兼研究助理","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-02-27T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2465","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：台风“卡吉基”更新","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Typhoon Kajiki Updates | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心东南亚项目博客文章，梳理台风“卡吉基”2025年8月的路径与影响。台风8月22日在菲律宾附近生成，经南海掠过中国海南后于8月25日登陆越南中北部，造成越南至少7人死亡、34人受伤、1人失踪，泰国5人死亡、7人失踪，越南逾1万所房屋受灾，160万人受停电影响，农业损失严重。越南总理范明政呼吁大规模灾后恢复，并警告后续仍有山洪和滑坡风险。","summary_en":"A CSIS Southeast Asia Program blog post tracking Typhoon Kajiki in August 2025. The storm formed near the Philippines on August 22, crossed the South China Sea, skirted China's Hainan, and made landfall in north-central Vietnam on August 25, killing at least seven in Vietnam and five in Thailand, injuring 34 and leaving one missing in Vietnam. Over 10,000 Vietnamese homes were affected, 1.6 million people lost power, and agricultural losses were heavy. Prime Minister Pham Minh Chinh called for mass recovery efforts and warned of further flash floods and mudslides.","tag":"risk","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:20:32.600790+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-typhoon-kajiki-updates","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: Typhoon Kajiki Updates\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nLauren Mai\nPublished August 28, 2025\nTyphoon Kajiki began as a tropical depression over the Philippines on August 22. The storm, initially anticipated to be as severe as the devastating Typhoon Yagi in 2024, proceeded to develop into a typhoon after stirring over the South China Sea, skirting China’s Hainan province before hitting Vietnam the hardest.  On August 24, just 24 hours before Kajiki made landfall in Vietnam, the country evacuated over half a million people as it braced for its fifth tropical storm of the year. Over 16,500 soldiers and 107,000 paramilitary forces were on standby to aid disaster relief efforts. Yet the storm diverged from weather forecasts, moving slowly into Ha Tinh province, with heavy rainfall extending as far as Hanoi.  Kajiki struck Vietnam’s north central coast on August 25, bringing severe weather and causing at least seven deaths. An additional 34 Vietnamese citizens were injured and one remains missing. In nearby Thailand, five people were killed and seven people went missing, with an unspecified number of injuries. The storm caused severe flooding and landslides over the course of eight hours, impacting more than 10,000 homes in Vietnam and 180 households in Thailand. Kajiki’s severity also damaged electricity poles, which caused a power blackout in Vietnam’s Ha Tinh and Nghe An provinces, directly impacting 1.6 million people. Vietnam experienced considerable agricultural losses, including damaging over 200,000 acres of rice paddy, killing almost 2,000 livestock and poultry, and felling around 21,000 trees.  In Kajiki’s aftermath, Vietnamese prime minister Pham Minh Chinh has called for mass recovery efforts across the country. After extending condolences to the families of the deceased and missing, Chinh urged local authorities in Thanh Hoa, Nghe An, and Ha Tinh provinces to provide immediate relief. The provincial governments are responsible for providing essential supplies for displaced households as well as repairing damaged schools and hospitals by August 30. But the storm’s destruction is far from over, with Vietnamese officials warning communities of more flash flooding and mudslides to come. Lauren Mai is a Program Coordinator and Research Assistant for the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nAsia, and\nSoutheast Asia\nImage\nLauren Mai\nProgram Manager and Research Associate, Southeast Asia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nAsia\nSoutheast Asia Program","content_cn":"东南亚最新动态：台风“剑鱼”更新\n照片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nLauren Mai\n发布于2025年8月28日\n\n台风“剑鱼”于8月22日在菲律宾上空形成热带低气压。这场风暴最初预计会与2024年造成毁灭性破坏的台风“摩羯”一样严重，随后在南海搅动后发展为台风，掠过中国海南省，之后对越南造成最严重影响。8月24日，即“剑鱼”在越南登陆前仅24小时，越南在准备应对本年度第五场热带风暴之际，疏散了超过50万人。超过16,500名士兵和107,000名准军事部队人员待命，以协助救灾工作。然而，该风暴偏离天气预报，缓慢进入河静省，强降雨范围一直延伸至河内。\n\n“剑鱼”于8月25日袭击越南中北部海岸，带来恶劣天气并造成至少7人死亡。此外，34名越南公民受伤，1人仍然失踪。邻近的泰国有5人死亡、7人失踪，受伤人数不详。这场风暴在8小时内引发严重洪水和山体滑坡，影响越南超过10,000所住房及泰国180户家庭。“剑鱼”的强度还损坏了电线杆，导致越南河静省和乂安省停电，直接影响160万人。越南遭受严重农业损失，包括超过200,000英亩稻田受损，近2,000头牲畜和家禽死亡，约21,000棵树木倒伏。\n\n在“剑鱼”过后，越南总理范明政呼吁在全国开展大规模恢复工作。在向死者和失踪者家属表示慰问后，范明政敦促清化省、乂安省和河静省地方当局立即提供救助。各省政府负责为流离失所家庭提供基本物资，并在8月30日前修复受损学校和医院。但风暴造成的破坏远未结束，越南官员警告社区还将面临更多山洪和泥石流。\n\nLauren Mai 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目的项目协调员兼研究助理。欲了解更多有关该地区的政治、经济和安全分析，请查看我们的博客系列《东南亚最新动态》。如欲获悉该地区最新消息并听取东南亚专家的观点，请在任一流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚广播》播客。\n\n标签\n亚洲，以及\n东南亚\n\n图片\nLauren Mai\n东南亚项目项目经理兼研究助理\n\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n亚洲\n东南亚项目","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-08-28T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3065","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"东南亚最新动态：美国退出公正能源转型伙伴关系","title_en":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: U.S. Withdrawal from JETP | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心东南亚项目博客文章，分析美国2025年3月6日退出公正能源转型伙伴关系（JETP）的影响。文章指出此举涉及美国对越南和印尼逾30亿美元承诺，多为商业贷款。印尼JETP原承诺200亿美元，越南获155亿美元公私融资支持。作者认为美国退出叠加对外援助冻结将削弱其在东南亚的地位，越南可能转向寻求美国对其核电项目的支持。","summary_en":"A CSIS Southeast Asia Program blog post analyzing the U.S. withdrawal from the Just Energy Transition Partnership on March 6, 2025. The move affects over $3 billion in U.S. commitments to Vietnam and Indonesia, mostly commercial loans. Indonesia's JETP had promised $20 billion, while Vietnam received $15.5 billion in public and private finance. The author argues the withdrawal, combined with a foreign assistance freeze, will diminish U.S. standing in the region, and Vietnam may instead seek U.S. support for its nuclear energy program.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:57:04.687113+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/latest-southeast-asia-us-withdrawal-jetp","content":"The Latest on Southeast Asia: U.S. Withdrawal from JETP\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nJaphet Quitzon\nPublished March 13, 2025\nOn March 6, U.S. treasury secretary Scott Bessent withdrew the United States from the Just Energy Transition Partnership (JETP), a platform for developed nations to aid developing nations transition from coal to clean energy. The withdrawal impacts over $3 billion of U.S. commitments in Vietnam and Indonesia, mostly commercial loans. Vietnam and Indonesia, both industrializing nations with significant power needs, have prioritized the development of clean, reliable energy. Both countries have ambitious decarbonization plans, striving for net-zero emissions by 2050.Indonesia’s JETP launched in November 2022, with a promised $20 billion from the United States, Japan, and European partners to aid its green energy transition. According to its Comprehensive Investment and Policy Plan, Indonesia aimedto cap its power sector emissions at 290 million ton CO2eq, boost renewable energy to account for 34 percent of power generation, and hit net zero emissions by 2050. JETP funding would have covered $20 billion of the estimated $235 billion required for Indonesia to meet its net-zero ambitions by 2050. Indonesia’s special envoy for energy and the environment, Hashim Djojohadikusumo, criticized JETP for minimal disbursements of funds, calling it a “failed program.” The United States’ JETP commitments, while small in comparison to Indonesia’s total need for energy financing, were still a meaningful gesture that demonstrated U.S. commitment to Indonesia. Japan and Germany, now co-leaders for the International Partnership Group that manages Indonesia’s JETP, will have to find new funding to fill the gap left behind by the United States. The United States’ deep commitment is perhaps even more important to Vietnam, which is seeking to toe the line between the United States and China as it funds its energy transition. The United States and its partners committed $15.5 billion in public and private finance to support Vietnam’s energy transition. Like Indonesia, Vietnam has ambitious aspirations of reducing peak power sector emissions by up to 30 percent, lowering Vietnam’s coal capacity to 30.2 gigawatts from 37 gigawatts, and achieving 47 percent renewable energy electricity generation by 2030. With the withdrawal of U.S. funding, however, Vietnam will likely seek different avenues of engagement with the United States, perhaps through its pursuit of U.S. funding to aid its ambitious nuclear energy program.The United States’ departure from JETP combined with the sudden freeze on foreign assistance will diminish U.S. standing in the region. Japhet Quitzon is an Associate Fellow for the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C. For more political, economic, and security analysis on the region, check out our blog series, The Latest on Southeast Asia. To hear the latest news on the region and hear from Southeast Asia experts, listen and subscribe to the Southeast Asia Radio podcast on any streaming platform.\nTags\nSoutheast Asia, and\nAsia\nImage\nJaphet Quitzon\nAssociate Fellow, Southeast Asia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nAsia\nSoutheast Asia Program","content_cn":"东南亚最新动态：美国退出 JETP\n图片：MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nJaphet Quitzon\n发布于 2025年3月13日\n3月6日，美国财政部长 Scott Bessent 宣布美国退出公正能源转型伙伴关系（JETP）。该平台旨在由发达国家帮助发展中国家从煤炭转向清洁能源。此次退出影响美国在越南和印度尼西亚超过30亿美元的承诺，其中大多为商业贷款。越南和印度尼西亚都是电力需求显著的工业化国家，已将发展清洁、可靠能源置于优先位置。两国均有雄心勃勃的脱碳计划，力争到2050年实现净零排放。印度尼西亚的 JETP 于2022年11月启动，美国、日本和欧洲伙伴承诺提供200亿美元，以支持其绿色能源转型。根据其《综合投资和政策计划》，印度尼西亚的目标是将电力部门排放量控制在2.9亿吨二氧化碳当量，将可再生能源在发电中的占比提高至34%，并于2050年实现净零排放。JETP 资金原本将覆盖印度尼西亚为实现2050年净零目标所需的估计2350亿美元中的200亿美元。印度尼西亚能源与环境特使 Hashim Djojohadikusumo 批评 JETP 的资金拨付微乎其微，称其为“失败的项目”。美国对 JETP 的承诺，尽管与印度尼西亚的整体能源融资需求相比规模较小，仍是一项有意义的姿态，表明了美国对印度尼西亚的承诺。如今共同领导管理印度尼西亚 JETP 的国际伙伴关系小组的日本和德国，将不得不寻找新的资金来填补美国留下的缺口。\n\n美国的深度承诺对越南或许更为重要；越南在为能源转型筹资时，正试图在美国与中国之间保持平衡。美国及其伙伴承诺提供155亿美元的公共和私人融资，以支持越南的能源转型。与印度尼西亚一样，越南也有宏伟目标：将电力部门排放峰值最多降低30%，将越南的煤电装机容量从370吉瓦降至302吉瓦，并在2030年前实现可再生能源发电占比47%。然而，随着美国资金撤出，越南可能会寻求与美国接触的不同途径，例如寻求美国资金来支持其雄心勃勃的核能计划。\n\n美国退出 JETP，加之突然冻结对外援助，将削弱美国在该地区的地位。\n\nJaphet Quitzon 是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）东南亚项目的副研究员。欲获取该地区更多政治、经济和安全分析，请查看我们的博客系列《东南亚最新动态》。如欲了解该地区最新新闻并聆听东南亚专家观点，请在任一流媒体平台收听并订阅《东南亚电台》播客。\n\n标签\n东南亚，以及\n亚洲\n\n图片\nJaphet Quitzon\n东南亚项目副研究员\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n亚洲\n东南亚项目","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-03-13T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3259","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"聚焦马来西亚：2025年1月16日","title_en":"Spotlight - Malaysia: January 16, 2025 | The Latest on Southeast Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心东南亚项目博客文章，分析马来西亚总理安瓦尔·易卜拉欣面临的政治挑战。文章提及登嘉楼州鞭刑事件冲击其自由民主信誉，宗教争议自2022年以来上升；前总理纳吉布寻求居家服刑复审，政府拟立法允许囚犯居家服刑引发批评；2025年底沙巴州选举及该州领导层腐败指控，成为安瓦尔维持执政联盟的关键考验。","summary_en":"A CSIS Southeast Asia Program blog post on the political challenges facing Malaysian Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim. It cites a caning case in Terengganu undermining his liberal democratic credentials amid rising religious controversies since 2022; former premier Najib Razak's bid for house arrest and a cabinet proposal to allow prisoners to serve sentences at home, which critics see as accommodating Najib; and the late-2025 Sabah state election and corruption allegations there as a critical test for Anwar's coalition.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:07:46.502008+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/latest-southeast-asia/spotlight-malaysia-january-16-2025","content":"Spotlight - Malaysia: January 16, 2025\nPhoto: MOHD RASFAN/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nSophie Lemière\nPublished January 16, 2025\nPrime Minister Anwar Ibrahim of Malaysia finds himself navigating choppy waters as his reformist agenda faces mounting challenges. The recent caning of a man in Terengganu state has cast a dark shadow over Anwar’s liberal democratic credentials, highlighting the growing tension between religious conservatism and progressive ideals in the country. This incident is part of a broader trend of religious controversies that have been on the rise since 2022, testing the limits of Anwar’s ability to balance competing interests within Malaysia’s diverse society.As Anwar’s credibility wanes, the political scene has been further complicated by developments surrounding former prime minister Najib Razak. The court’s decision to hear Najib's request for a review of his bid for house arrest has reignited debates about the alliance between Anwar’s reformist Parti Keadilan Rakyat (PKR) and the former ruling party, the United Malays National Organisation (UMNO), that allowed the now-prime minister to secure power in 2022. Najib, who was sentenced to 12 years in jail in 2022 and received a partial royal pardon in 2024, now seeks to leverage an alleged addendum to the king’s pardon that would allow him to serve the remainder of his sentence under house arrest.The government’s handling of Najib’s case has raised eyebrows, particularly in light of the cabinet’s proposition in November 2024 to introduce legislation allowing prisoners to serve sentences under house arrest. Critics view this move as a thinly veiled attempt to accommodate Najib’s request, further eroding public trust in the administration’s commitment to justice and accountability. Also, the government is believed to have instructed the attorney general to apply a gag order on the addendum. As Malaysia looks ahead to the upcoming state elections in Sabah, scheduled for the end of 2025, Anwar faces yet another critical test of his leadership. The recent leadership crisis in Sabah, marked by corruption allegations against the chief minister’s administration, underscores the fragility of political alliances in this strategically important state. Sabah’s continued support remains crucial for Anwar to maintain his grip on power, making the coming months pivotal for his administration.With the possibility of an early dissolution of the Sabah state assembly looming, Anwar must navigate these turbulent political waters carefully. His ability to address religious sensitivities, manage high-profile legal cases, and secure crucial electoral support will determine his political future.Sophie Lemière is an adjunct fellow (non-resident) with the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nTags\nSoutheast Asia, and\nAsia\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nSoutheast Asia Program\nImage\nSophie Lemière\nFormer Adjunct Fellow (Non-resident), Southeast Asia Program","content_cn":"聚焦——马来西亚：2025年1月16日\n照片：MOHD RASFAN/法新社/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nSophie Lemière\n发表于2025年1月16日\n马来西亚总理安瓦尔·易卜拉欣发现自己正处于风浪之中，因为他的改革议程面临日益加剧的挑战。近期登嘉楼州对一名男子施以鞭刑，给安瓦尔的自由民主信誉蒙上了阴影，凸显出该国宗教保守主义与进步理想之间日益紧张的关系。这起事件是自2022年以来不断增加的宗教争议这一更广泛趋势的一部分，考验着安瓦尔在马来西亚多元社会中平衡各方利益的能力。\n随着安瓦尔的信誉减弱，围绕前总理纳吉布·拉扎克的事态发展进一步使政治局势复杂化。法院决定审理纳吉布要求复核其居家监禁申请的请求，重新点燃了关于安瓦尔改革派人民公正党（PKR）与前执政党巫统（UMNO）之间联盟的争论；正是这一联盟让时任总理得以在2022年掌权。纳吉布于2022年被判处12年监禁，并于2024年获得部分皇家赦免，如今他寻求利用一份据称附加于国王赦免令中的附录，以便在居家监禁下服完余刑。\n政府处理纳吉布案件的方式引发质疑，尤其是考虑到内阁在2024年11月提出引入允许囚犯居家服刑的立法。批评者认为，此举是为满足纳吉布请求而精心掩饰的尝试，进一步侵蚀了公众对政府致力于司法和问责的信任。此外，据认为政府已指示总检察长就该附录申请禁言令。\n随着马来西亚展望定于2025年底举行的沙巴州选举，安瓦尔又面临对其领导力的另一项关键考验。沙巴近期的领导危机——州首席部长政府遭到腐败指控——凸显了这个具有战略重要性的州政治联盟的脆弱性。沙巴持续支持对安瓦尔维持权力至关重要，因此未来几个月对其政府来说将是关键时期。\n随着沙巴州议会可能提前解散，安瓦尔必须谨慎应对这些动荡的政治局势。他能否处理宗教敏感问题、管理备受关注的法律案件并争取关键选举支持，将决定他的政治前途。\nSophie Lemière是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心东南亚项目的兼职（非驻会）研究员。\n标签\n东南亚，以及\n亚洲\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n东南亚项目\n图片\nSophie Lemière\n前兼职研究员（非驻会），东南亚项目","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-01-16T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3288","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"数字时代的民主：水军文化如何刺痛印尼民主","title_en":"Democracy in the Digital Age: How Buzzer Culture is Stinging Indonesia’s Democracy | New Perspectives on Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心博客文章探讨印尼政治水军文化，指出受雇水军通过虚假社交媒体账号放大政治信息、操纵舆论，从2012年雅加达选举到2024年大选不断演变，帮助塑造了普拉博沃的形象并压制批评。文章认为水军文化加剧民主倒退、虚假信息传播和公众信任下降，建议加强社交媒体治理和负责任的技术监管，并指出这对美国利益和东盟数字治理构成挑战。","summary_en":"A CSIS blog post explores Indonesia's political buzzer culture, in which hired individuals use fake social media profiles to amplify political messages and manipulate opinion, evolving from the 2012 Jakarta election to the 2024 presidential race, helping reshape Prabowo Subianto's image and suppress criticism. It argues buzzer culture deepens democratic backsliding, disinformation, and declining public trust, urging stronger social media governance and responsible tech regulation, and noting implications for U.S. interests and ASEAN digital governance.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:10:31.766798+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/new-perspectives-asia/democracy-digital-age-how-buzzer-culture-stinging-indonesias-democracy","content":"Democracy in the Digital Age: How Buzzer Culture is Stinging Indonesia’s Democracy\nBlog Post\nby\nNatania Seah\nPublished December 17, 2024\nPrabowo Subianto, Indonesia’s newest president, was inaugurated on October 20. He won the country’s 2024 elections on a campaign focused on playing up his gemoy persona—as a jovial older uncle and cat-lover. This was a far cry from his previous two presidential campaigns in 2014 and 2019, where he had unsuccessfully tried to appeal to voters as a tough, military strongman. While Prabowo’s recent success may be attributed to his toned-down image, the transformative role of political “buzzers” in pushing Prabowo’s transformation, and shaping the results of Indonesia’s 2024 elections, cannot be understated. Buzzers are recruited individuals managing fake social media profiles, which amplify political messages to influence and manipulate public opinion under clients’ orders. Most political buzzers are employees of professional agencies, offering services tailored to mimic organic social media activity, such as posts, likes, views, and customizable comments. These services are often openly advertised on Instagram or e-commerce sites like Shopee. Such services are neither new nor unique to Indonesia. Yet, the societal entrenchment and increasingly sophisticated tactics of buzzer usage in Indonesian political culture are noteworthy. These factors bear concern for the democratic deterioration, spread of dis/misinformation, and decreasing public trust in the world’s third-largest democracy. Increased social media governance and the promotion of responsible tech regulations are critical in combating the usage of buzzers in Indonesia, lest Indonesia’s current turn toward authoritarianism continues.Origins and implications of buzzer culture in IndonesiaBuzzers for hire have long existed in Indonesia, initially serving as a commercial marketing tool to generate favorable public opinion—from endorsing new products, to boosting a restaurant’s reviews. Indonesia’s buzzer culture took on a political spin in the wake of the highly contested 2012 Jakarta gubernatorial election. Then-candidate Joko “Jokowi” Widodo’s campaign volunteers capitalized on the rise of social media usage among Jakarta’s urban, middle-class voters to flood social media with pro-Jokowi messaging, resulting in eventual victory. The subsequent 2014 presidential campaign featured a more professional approach by both candidates, Jokowi and Prabowo, with the burgeoning use of hired public relations and buzzer agencies. Beyond promotion of their respective candidates, social media campaigns focused heavily on conjuring up ostensibly organic social media posts, memes, and infographics for kampanye hitam, or “black” smear campaigns, against opponents.By the 2017 Jakarta gubernatorial election, buzzers were firmly entrenched as an integral aspect of Indonesian electoral politics. The election marked a significant escalation in the use and impact of buzzers on political discourse: all candidates employed buzzer teams to amplify campaign messaging, while buzzer activity heavily contributed to heightened social, religious, and political polarization. The election pit Chinese Christian incumbent Basuki Tjahaja Purnama, commonly known as Ahok, against Muslim opponents. A targeted disinformation campaign by an army of buzzers featuring manipulated clips of a campaign trail speech by Ahok referencing verses from the Quran eventually contributed to his defeat and imprisonment on blasphemy charges.Buzzers, unlike typical influencers, mislead by posing as legitimate users, interspersing their political messaging with spurious personal posts. They also often act in tandem with influencers and bots as part of a coordinated ecosystem, such as by amplifying influencer posts through views and likes, while bots boost hashtags initiated by buzzers. Buzzers may have connections to traditional media outlets, sometimes paying to secure favorable news coverage for their clients. The use of buzzers distorts Indonesia’s information landscape and hampers the organic discourse critical for democracy. Leading up to Indonesia’s 2019 presidential election, the hashtag #SkandalSandiaga attacking vice presidential candidate Sandiaga Uno proliferated on Twitter. A social network analysis by domestic counter-disinformation company Media Kernels identified a network of bots and buzzers driving the hashtag’s uptick in the smear campaign. Buzzing has morphed into a lucrative industry for Indonesians, particularly youth. It offers a semblance of job security in a country with a 13.9 percent unemployment rate, especially during peak periods like elections. Some buzzer agency owners frame their businesses as entrepreneurial ventures and digital marketing agencies—a testament to how deeply normalized the practice has become. The manipulation of public opinion, by working-class citizens to serve the elite, highlights the cruel irony of Indonesia’s democratic erosion—empowering the disadvantaged while fueling their disenfranchisement.Buzzers, pervasive in Indonesia’s crowded digital space, also manipulate public opinion by silencing criticism and spreading false narratives, posing concerns for Indonesia’s democracy. Once confined to elections, their use is now standard practice for hot-button issues, with politicians sometimes openly acknowledging and justifying their use. This practice impacts not just domestic politics but also foreign policy, public health, and security, as seen in efforts to promote COVID-19 policies, sway international opinion on West Papuan independence, and discredit Corruption Eradication Commission (KPK) investigators as Muslim fundamentalists.Indonesia’s 2024 elections On February 14, Indonesians selected a new president and vice-president, parliament, and local legislators in the world’s largest single-day election. Regional elections to elect governors, mayors, and district heads were held on November 27. Courting the youth vote—particularly through social media—became a cornerstone of these elections, with almost 60 percent of the electorate consisting of Generation Z and Millennial voters and 22.8 percent being first time voters. In an era characterized by the ubiquity of social media, this year’s electorate was more susceptible than ever to political messaging from buzzers. A 2023 survey found that 42.3 percent of respondents believed in some disinformation about the 2024 elections, such as foreign interference. The use of buzzers and misinformation were instrumental in electing Prabowo, allowing him to sanitize criticism such as his past human rights record and the controversial court ruling that allowed Jokowi’s son to run as Prabowo’s vice-president. The viral videos of Prabowo dancing, appealing AI-generated cartoon avatars, and historical retellings of his military days all helped radically engineer Prabowo’s new image, never mind his previous visa ban by the United States for military atrocities. Confronting buzzer culture: paths to reform and accountabilityThe Indonesian government has taken steps to combat buzzers. These include task forces organized by the Ministry of Communication and Informatics that collaborate with digital platforms such as Meta, Google, and TikTok to monitor and prevent the spread of disinformation. The government is also working with non-profit organizations such as MAFINDO, which conduct fact-checking, public education, and advocacy work to strengthen digital literacy. Plans have also been announced to revamp the existing Electronic Information and Transactions law to boost cybersecurity robustness. There are also heartening grassroots efforts to shore up information transparency and healthy political discussion. Youth-led social media initiatives such as What Is Up Indonesia and Bijak Memilih encourage increased youth engagement and interest in Indonesian politics through clear, accurate, and timely information. The Cek Fakta network comprises 6,000 fact-checkers with around 25 media organizations dedicated to combating misinformation.While commendable, these efforts and initiatives achieve little against the government’s ongoing complicity and its selective focus on addressing only the misinformation and buzzer usage that challenge its interests. Furthermore, they are inadequate in combating the pervasiveness of buzzers in Indonesian politics without government acknowledgement of its democratic implications.Indonesia’s buzzer culture holds disturbing ramifications for U.S. interests. Concerns over Indonesia’s democratic backsliding have burgeoned over the years, particularly under former president Jokowi, who weakened democratic norms and institutions and consolidated his power to advance his own interests. Prabowo swept to power in part due to these conditions, with Jokowi’s tacit support. The societal fragmentation, tension, eroded public trust, and weakened democratic processes in which buzzer culture perpetuates are all antithetical to the advancement of the democratic norms championed by the United States. Indonesia’s struggle with digital governance and disinformation are echoed around Southeast Asia. Data governance regimes across the region are highly disparate, and policies initiated by the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN)—such as the forthcoming Digital Economy Framework Agreement (DEFA) and Framework on Digital Data Governance—are expected to be non-binding with no enforcement mechanisms. While this offers the opportunity for greater U.S involvement to promote transparency, accountability, and norms setting, it is likely that such initiatives may meet resistance or a lukewarm response. The poor regulation and governance of digital platforms in the United States also means that Washington is ill-equipped to act as a leader on these issues. The United States has struggled domestically with enacting robust digital governance laws, like a comprehensive federal data privacy law equivalent to the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation. It also lacks a cohesive federal framework to combat disinformation, resulting in a patchwork system of state laws and voluntary guidelines from social media companies. Furthermore, a largely reactive regulatory approach in the United States regarding disinformation is an obstacle for U.S. leadership in digital governance. First Amendment protections often constrain efforts, limiting the government's ability to regulate speech and hindering policymakers from implementing pre-emptive measures.Their ubiquity means that the Indonesian public expects the involvement of buzzers in any public discourse or government communications, and are resigned to having buzzers as an inevitable facet of Indonesian politics. Beyond the warping of accurate information, the most tragic ramification of buzzer culture is its unsaid harm to the freedom of expression and healthy political discussions of the Indonesian public, who deserve transparency, accountability, and better from their elected government.Natania Seah is a former research intern with the Southeast Asia Program at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nTags\nAsia, and\nSoutheast Asia\nNatania Seah\nFormer Research Intern, Southeast Asia Program\nPrograms & Projects\nSoutheast Asia Program\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"数字时代的民主：煽动者文化如何刺痛印度尼西亚的民主\n博客文章\n作者\nNatania Seah\n发表于2024年12月17日\n印度尼西亚新任总统普拉博沃·苏比延多于10月20日宣誓就职。他在2024年大选中获胜，竞选重点是突出其gemoy形象——一位开朗、喜欢猫的年长叔叔。这与他此前在2014年和2019年的两次总统竞选相去甚远；当时，他未能以强硬的军人强人形象赢得选民支持。尽管普拉博沃近期的成功可能归因于其收敛后的形象，但政治“煽动者”在推动普拉博沃形象转变以及塑造印度尼西亚2024年大选结果方面所发挥的变革性作用，绝不能低估。煽动者是受雇管理虚假社交媒体账号的人员，他们按照客户指令放大政治信息，以影响和操纵公众舆论。大多数政治煽动者都是专业机构的员工，提供经过定制、旨在模仿自然社交媒体活动的服务，例如发帖、点赞、观看量和可定制评论。这些服务经常在Instagram或Shopee等电商网站上公开宣传。这类服务既不是新现象，也并非印度尼西亚独有。然而，煽动者使用在印度尼西亚政治文化中的社会根基日益深厚，其策略也日趋复杂，值得注意。这些因素令人担忧：这个世界第三大民主国家正出现民主衰退、虚假信息/错误信息传播以及公众信任下降。加强社交媒体治理并推动负责任的技术监管，对于遏制煽动者在印度尼西亚的使用至关重要，否则印度尼西亚当前转向威权主义的趋势将持续下去。\n印度尼西亚煽动者文化的起源及影响\n印度尼西亚长期以来一直存在可雇佣的煽动者，最初作为商业营销工具，用于营造有利的公众舆论——从为新产品背书，到提升餐馆的评价。随着备受争议的2012年雅加达省长选举，印度尼西亚的煽动者文化开始染上政治色彩。当时的候选人佐科·“佐科威”·维多多的竞选志愿者利用雅加达城市中产阶级选民日益增加的社交媒体使用率，在社交媒体上大量发布支持佐科威的信息，最终取得胜利。随后的2014年总统竞选中，两位候选人佐科威和普拉博沃采取了更专业的方式，并开始大量使用受雇的公关和煽动者机构。社交媒体竞选除了宣传各自的候选人外，还高度重视炮制表面上看似自然产生的社交媒体帖子、表情包和信息图，用于对竞争对手发起kampanye hitam，即“黑色”抹黑运动。\n到2017年雅加达省长选举时，煽动者已经根深蒂固，成为印度尼西亚选举政治不可或缺的一部分。这场选举标志着煽动者在政治话语中的使用及其影响显著升级：所有候选人都雇用了煽动者团队来放大竞选信息，而煽动者活动也在很大程度上加剧了社会、宗教和政治极化。这场选举由华裔基督徒现任省长巴苏基·查哈亚·普尔纳马（俗称阿学）对阵穆斯林竞争者。一场由煽动者大军发起的定向虚假信息运动，传播经过操纵的竞选途中演讲片段；阿学在演讲中提及《古兰经》经文。这场运动最终促成了他的失败，并\n因亵渎罪指控入狱。与典型的影响者不同，网络水军通过伪装成合法用户来误导他人，将政治宣传与虚假的个人帖子穿插在一起。他们还经常与影响者和机器人协同行动，成为协调生态系统的一部分，例如通过浏览量和点赞来放大影响者的帖子，而机器人则推高由水军发起的话题标签。水军可能与传统媒体机构有联系，有时会付费为客户争取有利的新闻报道。水军的使用扭曲了印度尼西亚的信息环境，阻碍了对民主至关重要的有机公共讨论。在印度尼西亚2019年总统选举前夕，攻击副总统候选人桑迪亚加·乌诺的标签#SkandalSandiaga在推特上广泛传播。国内反虚假信息公司Media Kernels进行的社交网络分析发现，一个由机器人和水军组成的网络推动了这一诽谤活动中该标签的热度上升。网络水军活动已演变成印度尼西亚人，尤其是年轻人的一项利润丰厚的产业。在失业率达13.9%的国家，这种工作提供了某种工作保障，尤其是在选举等高峰时期。一些水军机构老板将自己的企业包装成创业项目和数字营销机构——这证明这种做法已经被深度正常化。工薪阶层公民通过操纵舆论为精英服务，凸显了印度尼西亚民主衰退的残酷讽刺——赋予弱势群体权力的同时，也助推了他们被剥夺政治权利。\n\n在印度尼西亚拥挤的数字空间中无处不在的水军，还通过压制批评和传播虚假叙事来操纵舆论，引发了人们对印度尼西亚民主的担忧。他们一度仅限于选举期间使用，如今已成为处理热点问题的常规做法，政治人物有时甚至公开承认并为其使用辩护。这种做法影响的不仅是国内政治，也包括外交政策、公共卫生和安全，例如推动新冠肺炎政策、影响国际社会对西巴布亚独立的看法，以及抹黑反腐败委员会（KPK）调查人员、称其为穆斯林原教旨主义者。\n\n印度尼西亚2024年选举\n2月14日，印度尼西亚人选出了新总统和副总统、议会成员以及地方立法机构成员，这是世界上最大规模的单日选举。11月27日举行了选举州长、市长和县长的地方选举。争取青年选票——尤其是通过社交媒体——成为这些选举的基石，选民中近60%是Z世代和千禧一代，22.8%是首次投票者。在社交媒体无处不在的时代，今年的选民比以往任何时候都更容易受到水军政治宣传的影响。2023年的一项调查发现，42.3%的受访者相信有关2024年选举的一些虚假信息，例如外国干预。水军和虚假信息的使用对于普拉博沃当选起到了关键作用，使他能够淡化诸如其过去的人权记录，以及一项允许佐科威之子以普拉博沃副总统候选人身份参选的争议性法院裁决等批评。普拉博沃跳舞的病毒式视频、讨喜的AI生成卡通形象，以及对其军旅经历的历史性重述，都帮助彻底塑造了普拉博沃的新形象，更不用说他此前曾被美国以军事\n暴行。应对“蜂鸣器”文化：改革与问责之路\n\n印度尼西亚政府已采取措施打击“蜂鸣器”。这些措施包括由通信与信息学部组织的工作组，与Meta、谷歌和TikTok等数字平台合作，监测并防止虚假信息传播。政府还与MAFINDO等非营利组织合作，开展事实核查、公众教育和倡导工作，以加强数字素养。政府还宣布计划修订现有的《电子信息与交易法》，以提升网络安全的稳健性。\n\n一些基层努力也令人鼓舞，它们旨在加强信息透明度和健康的政治讨论。由青年主导的社交媒体倡议，如“What Is Up Indonesia”和“Bijak Memilih”，通过提供清晰、准确且及时的信息，鼓励青年更多参与并关注印度尼西亚政治。“事实核查”（Cek Fakta）网络由6,000名事实核查员组成，约有25家媒体机构致力于打击错误信息。\n\n尽管值得肯定，但面对政府持续的共谋，以及其有选择地只处理挑战自身利益的虚假信息和“蜂鸣器”使用行为，这些努力和倡议收效甚微。此外，在政府不承认“蜂鸣器”对民主所产生影响的情况下，这些措施不足以应对“蜂鸣器”在印度尼西亚政治中的普遍存在。\n\n印度尼西亚的“蜂鸣器”文化对美国利益具有令人不安的影响。多年来，人们对印度尼西亚民主倒退的担忧不断加剧，尤其是在前总统佐科维执政期间；他削弱了民主规范和制度，并巩固自身权力以推进个人利益。普拉博沃上台，部分正是得益于这些条件以及佐科维的默许支持。“蜂鸣器”文化所持续造成的社会分裂、紧张局势、公众信任受损和民主进程削弱，都与美国所倡导的民主规范发展背道而驰。\n\n印度尼西亚在数字治理和虚假信息方面的困境，也在东南亚各地得到呼应。整个地区的数据治理制度高度不一致，而东南亚国家联盟（ASEAN）发起的政策——例如即将出台的《数字经济框架协定》（DEFA）和《数字数据治理框架》——预计将不具约束力，也没有执行机制。虽然这为美国更多参与、推动透明度、问责制和规范制定提供了机会，但此类倡议很可能会遭遇抵制或冷淡回应。\n\n美国对数字平台监管和治理不力，也意味着华盛顿没有能力在这些问题上充当领导者。美国国内一直难以制定强有力的数字治理法律，例如一部相当于欧盟《通用数据保护条例》的全面联邦数据隐私法。美国也缺乏打击虚假信息的统一联邦框架，导致形成由各州法律和社交媒体公司自愿准则拼凑而成的体系。\n\n此外，美国在虚假信息问题上总体上采取被动应对的监管方式，这阻碍了美国在数字治理领域发挥领导作用。第一修正案的保护往往限制相关努力，制约政府监管的能力\n言论，并阻碍政策制定者实施预防性措施。它们无处不在，这意味着印度尼西亚公众期待在任何公共话语或政府沟通中都有“水军”的参与，并且已经听任“水军”成为印度尼西亚政治中不可避免的一面。除了扭曲准确信息之外，“水军”文化最悲惨的后果，是它对印度尼西亚公众的言论自由和健康政治讨论造成了未明言的伤害；这些公众理应从其民选政府那里获得透明度、问责制以及更好的表现。纳塔尼娅·西娅曾是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心东南亚项目的研究实习生。\n标签\n亚洲，以及\n东南亚\n纳塔尼娅·西娅\n前研究实习生，东南亚项目\n项目与计划\n东南亚项目\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-17T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2471","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"日本与菲律宾的国防工业合作","title_en":"Japan-Philippines Defense Industrial Cooperation | New Perspectives on Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"文章分析日本在菲律宾国防现代化中的角色。面对中国在南海的持续压力，菲律宾海警力量薄弱，正推进现代化计划。日本通过放宽防务装备转移原则、提供巡逻艇与雷达、签署互惠准入协定等方式深化合作，并探讨将菲律宾打造为维修保养枢纽，以强化双边安全与产业协同。","summary_en":"This CSIS blog post examines Japan's growing role in the Philippines' defense modernization amid persistent Chinese maritime pressure in the South China Sea. It reviews Japan's eased defense transfer rules, provision of patrol boats and radars, the 2023 Reciprocal Access Agreement, and prospects for making the Philippines a maintenance hub to deepen bilateral security and industrial cooperation.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:21:09.379616+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/new-perspectives-asia/japan-philippines-defense-industrial-cooperation","content":"Japan-Philippines Defense Industrial Cooperation\nBlog Post\nby\nChristopher Kirch\nPublished August 27, 2025\nIn the territorial waters of the South China Sea, the Philippines finds itself under persistent pressure. Beijing’s maritime assertiveness and gray-zone coercion have only grown more aggressive, with the China Coast Guard (CCG) maintaining an almost daily presence inside the Philippines’ exclusive economic zone. While China’s expansion is daunting, Manila has not stood still. In recent years, the Philippines has embarked on some of its most ambitious defense modernization drives in decades, backed by strong political will and growing external support. Japan is playing a growing role in supporting the Philippines and its contributions reflect overlapping security concerns, economic interests, and a shared desire to promote regional stability. Recent milestones include the 2023 Reciprocal Access Agreement (RAA), which allows for the deployment of personnel and assets in each other’s territories, as well as the 2024 transfer of coastal radars and patrol boats. By leveraging its technological and industrial strengths, Japan is positioned to complement the Philippines’ modernization push and help Manila build a more sustainable defense industrial base over time.The Philippines ContextDespite this momentum, capability gaps remain. The CCG, the world’s largest, fields 157 large patrol vessels as of 2023, nearly quadruple its strength a decade ago. By contrast, the Philippine Coast Guard has only 25 major vessels and still struggles with limited surveillance coverage and outdated platforms. Compounding these challenges are budgetary constraints and constitutional limits on defense spending, which slow the pace of procurement.The Philippines is also nearing the end of a 15-year modernization program, which has been uneven in its progress. Earlier phases were hampered by underspending, project delays, and reliance on foreign suppliers. The final phase, however, has seen marked improvements, with new assets arriving at a faster rate and greater clarity on Manila’s priorities. Going forward, the challenge may not be whether the Philippines is doing enough, but how it can strategically manage partnerships and domestic reforms to maximize the impact of its investments. Modernizing its maritime capabilities while cultivating diversified and sustainable defense partnerships may be essential to keeping pace with regional threats.Paving the Way for a Japan-Philippines Industrial PartnershipIn this context, Japan has emerged as a strategic partner willing to support both tracks through defense technology transfers and development assistance. Japan updated the “Three Principles on Transfer of Defense Equipment and Technology” in 2014 to permit defense transfers, legalizing the transfer of Parola-Class Patrol Boats and Teresa Magbanua-Class Vessels to the Philippines. This military hardware was financed by the Official Development Assistance (ODA) programs of the Japan International Cooperation Agency, which broadly interprets maritime security as a form of economic development. In 2024, Japan was the largest ODA provider to the Philippines with 13.23 billion dollars.To bolster this evolving public cooperation, Japanese defense manufacturers can consider offshoring auxiliary industrial operations. The Philippines benefits from Japanese Overseas Security Assistance (OSA) launched in 2023 to enhance the security capabilities of like-minded countries by providing military equipment and infrastructure support. These factors present a nascent commercial opportunity for maintenance, repair, and operations (MRO).Japan has recently constructed the legal and regulatory frameworks for the Philippines to potentially serve as a profitable MRO hub. Japan and the Philippines are negotiating a General Security of Military Information Agreement and an Acquisition and Cross-Services Agreement. Ongoing multi-partisan support for this partnership underlies recent material transfer agreements with the Philippines: a FPS-3ME radar and six Abukuma-class destroyer escorts. Japanese defense firms have an opportunity to provide maintenance to this hardware. Prime Minister Shigeru Ishiba recently noted that Japan and the Philippines “are now partners that are almost like allies,” demonstrating a foundation of diplomatic goodwill and commercial opportunity.Japanese defense firms that enter the Philippines may build long-term lock-in demand for upstream components. The FPS-3ME provided to the Philippines is exclusively developed and manufactured by Mitsubishi Electric, which profits from ongoing operational support and modular upgrades. Overseas markets such as the Philippines provide diversified and predictable revenue streams that could compensate for unpredictable domestic military spending, and opportunities such as these can support the development and competitiveness of Japanese defense firms.In short, enhanced industrial harmonization between Japan and the Philippines can bolster security in the South China Sea. Legal, security, and industrial frameworks between Japan and the Philippines can support economic growth and stability in the region. Bilateral frameworks can also strengthen the institutional foundations for enhanced minilateral networking with the United States, the European Union, and the Quad.ConclusionCrisis creates opportunity. The considerable security tensions in the South China Sea are putting the Philippines under coercive pressure. In this moment, Japan stepping up in partnership may facilitate defense industrial cooperation that can support regional stability. Japan and the Philippines are aligning their potential shared commercial and strategic interests for defense cooperation, thus forging a prospective pathway for a security partnership in the region.Christopher Kirch was an intern with the Japan Chair at the Center for Strategic and International Studies.\nChristopher Kirch\nFormer Research Intern, Japan Chair","content_cn":"日本—菲律宾防务工业合作\n\n博客文章\n\n作者\n\nChristopher Kirch\n\n发表于2025年8月27日\n\n在南海的领海范围内，菲律宾发现自己正承受持续不断的压力。北京的海上强势姿态和灰色地带胁迫只会变得更加咄咄逼人，中国海警（CCG）几乎每天都在菲律宾专属经济区内活动。尽管中国的扩张势头令人担忧，马尼拉并未停滞不前。近年来，在强大的政治意愿和日益增加的外部支持下，菲律宾启动了数十年来最雄心勃勃的一些国防现代化计划。日本在支持菲律宾方面发挥着越来越大的作用，其贡献体现了重叠的安全关切、经济利益以及促进地区稳定的共同愿望。近期里程碑包括2023年的《互惠准入协定》（RAA），该协定允许双方在彼此领土内部署人员和资产；以及2024年向菲律宾移交海岸雷达和巡逻艇。凭借其技术和工业优势，日本有望补充菲律宾的现代化努力，并帮助马尼拉随着时间推移建设更加可持续的国防工业基础。\n\n菲律宾背景\n\n尽管势头良好，但能力差距依然存在。截至2023年，全球规模最大的中国海警拥有157艘大型巡逻舰，规模几乎是十年前的四倍。相比之下，菲律宾海岸警卫队仅有25艘主要舰艇，在监视覆盖范围有限和平台老旧方面仍举步维艰。预算限制以及宪法对国防支出的限制进一步加剧了这些挑战，拖慢了采购进度。\n\n菲律宾也即将结束一项为期15年的现代化计划，该计划进展并不均衡。早期阶段受到支出不足、项目延误以及依赖外国供应商的阻碍。然而，最后阶段已有明显改善，新资产以更快速度到位，马尼拉的优先事项也更加明确。展望未来，挑战可能不在于菲律宾是否做得足够，而在于它如何战略性地管理伙伴关系和国内改革，以最大限度地发挥投资的影响。在培育多元化且可持续的防务伙伴关系的同时，实现海上能力现代化，可能是跟上地区威胁步伐的关键。\n\n为日本—菲律宾工业伙伴关系铺平道路\n\n在这一背景下，日本已成为一个战略伙伴，愿意通过防务技术转让和发展援助支持这两个方面。日本于2014年更新了《防务装备和技术转让三原则》，允许进行防务转让，从而使向菲律宾转让帕罗拉级巡逻艇和特蕾莎·马格巴努阿级舰艇合法化。这些军事装备由日本国际协力机构的官方发展援助（ODA）项目提供资金，该机构将海上安全广义解释为经济发展的一种形式。2024年，日本是菲律宾最大的ODA提供者，援助金额达132.3亿美元。\n\n为加强这一不断发展的公共合作，日本防务制造商可以考虑将辅助性工业业务外包。菲律宾受益于日本于2023年启动的海外安全援助（OSA），该计划旨在通过\n提供军事装备和基础设施支持。这些因素为维护、维修和运营（MRO）带来了初步的商业机遇。日本近期为菲律宾可能成为一个有利可图的MRO枢纽构建了法律和监管框架。日本和菲律宾正在谈判《军事信息总体安全协议》和《获取与交叉服务协议》。对这一伙伴关系持续的跨党派支持，促成了近期与菲律宾签署的实物转让协议：一部FPS-3ME雷达和六艘阿武隈级护航驱逐舰。日本防务企业有机会为这些装备提供维护服务。首相石破茂近期指出，日本和菲律宾“现在是几乎如同盟友的伙伴”，这表明双方拥有外交善意和商业机遇的基础。日本防务企业进入菲律宾后，可能会为上游零部件形成长期锁定需求。提供给菲律宾的FPS-3ME由三菱电机独家开发和制造，该公司可从持续的运行支持和模块化升级中获利。菲律宾等海外市场能够提供多元化且可预测的收入来源，从而弥补不可预测的国内军费开支，而此类机遇有助于支持日本防务企业的发展和竞争力。总之，加强日本与菲律宾之间的产业协调，可以增强南海安全。日本与菲律宾之间的法律、安全和产业框架能够支持该地区的经济增长与稳定。双边框架还可以加强与美国、欧盟和“四方安全对话”开展更广泛小多边网络合作的制度基础。结论危机孕育机遇。南海相当大的安全紧张局势正使菲律宾承受胁迫压力。在这一时刻，日本通过加强伙伴关系，可能促成能够支持地区稳定的防务工业合作。日本和菲律宾正在协调双方在防务合作方面潜在的共同商业和战略利益，从而为该地区的安全伙伴关系开辟一条潜在路径。Christopher Kirch曾在战略与国际问题研究中心日本讲席担任实习生。\nChristopher Kirch\n前研究实习生，日本讲席","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-08-27T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1047","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"赢得消费者：让印度智能电表真正服务于民众","title_en":"Winning Over the Consumer: Making India's Smart Meters Work for People | New Perspectives on Asia | CSIS","summary_cn":"文章指出印度计划到2028年部署2.5亿台智能电表，但计费透明度不足、数字素养偏低和用户参与有限拖慢接受度。作者建议借鉴英国Smart Energy GB模式，在REC Limited下设立常设消费者参与使命，并针对不同数字素养和地区设计差异化外展策略，以建立信任、推动行为改变。","summary_en":"The article notes India aims to deploy 250 million smart meters by 2028, but gaps in billing transparency, low digital literacy and limited user engagement slow acceptance. The author recommends drawing on the UK's Smart Energy GB model to create a permanent consumer engagement mission under REC Limited, with segmented outreach tailored to digital literacy and geography to build trust and drive behavioral change.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:26:07.195489+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/new-perspectives-asia/winning-over-consumer-making-indias-smart-meters-work-people","content":"Winning Over the Consumer: Making India's Smart Meters Work for People\nPhoto: JAMES ARTHUR GEKIERE/BELGA MAG/AFP via Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nAnna Trissa\nPublished May 1, 2026\nIndia aims to deploy 250 million smart meters under the Revamped Distribution Sector Scheme by 2028, making consumer adoption central to power sector reform. However, persistent gaps in billing transparency, low digital literacy, and limited engagement with smart meter tools are slowing user acceptance. India has focused on reaching meter installation targets. However, it must simultaneously focus on whether consumers understand, trust, and actively use the existing smart meters by establishing a national consumer engagement mission and implementing targeted outreach strategies. Together, these measures can build trust, improve consumer experience, and accelerate uniform smart meter adoption across states. Build a Consumer Engagement Mission India currently lacks a streamlined process for building public trust and consumer engagement in smart metering. Attempts have been made such as in February 2026, the Ministry of Power, with support from distribution companies (DISCOMs), launched a two-week campaign titled ‘Smart Meter Pakhwada’, which included community awareness sessions, door-to-door consumer engagements, and live demonstrations of smart meters. However, sustained efforts are needed beyond short-term initiatives if India needs to reach its target of 250 million smart meters by 2028 and engage a population of over 1.4 billion. The United Kingdom’s engagement efforts offer a model for India. Facing consumer resistance during its 2011 smart meter rollout, the UK established Smart Energy GB (SEGB), a government-backed body focused on public engagement. SEGB’s ‘Consumer Engagement Plan 2025’ uses multi-channel campaigns and continuous consumer research. According to its 2025 Annual Report, SEGB’s efforts contributed to 60 percent of smart meter installations. The UK model shows that structured consumer awareness can support smart metering, but though success in India will depend on adapting it to local institutional design. India should establish an equivalent mission under the REC Limited, with a clear mandate, dedicated funding, and measurable engagement targets. The mandate can be to design and implement strategies that improve awareness, trust, and usage of smart meters. For example, the Indian government can look to SEGB’s 2025 consumer engagement plan which focused on campaigns, targeted outreach, and inclusion of microbusinesses. The body’s work is evaluated against a ‘Performance Management Framework’ (PMF), a document that has performance metrics and targets to be achieved every year.In 2025, SEGB ran campaigns highlighting financial and energy-saving benefits of smart meters (flexible tariffs, EV/solar integration) and reassurance campaigns to counter myths. Outreach used digital and social media while retaining television for older audiences. It also used ‘People like Me’ case studies and consumer surveys, and partnered regionally to reach vulnerable groups, including low-income households and those without digital access.India should adapt these lessons to its own context. A permanent consumer engagement mission should focus on outcomes such as increasing app adoption, improving understanding of billing systems, and ensuring that consumers can access detailed consumption data. Activities that were part of Smart Meter Pakhwada, such as door-to-door engagements, demonstrations of smart meters and educational programs could be formalized into this mission. Regular consumer surveys, feedback loops, and behavioral insights should be used to refine communication strategies. Using local and preferred languages in campaigns could help make it more personalized and inclusive for consumers.  Design Targeted Outreach Strategies A uniform communication strategy will not be effective in India’s highly diverse consumer landscape. The proposed framework should implement segmented outreach strategies based on digital literacy, user engagement, and geography.  In regions with low digital literacy, outreach should prioritize in-person engagement through community meetings, demonstrations, and local intermediaries. Research suggests that face-to-face communication is particularly effective in overcoming distrust and correcting misinformation.  To increase user engagement, especially in areas where smart meters have already been installed, but participation remains low; campaigns could focus on digital literacy and adoption. This includes promoting mobile applications, explaining features like consumption tracking and bill breakdowns, and guiding users through payment and recharge processes. Radio and television should complement digital outreach in low-connectivity regions to ensure communication remains inclusive.  Consumer engagement efforts can also vary depending on the geography. Urban deployment may be easier, but residents may still resist adoption or distrust billing changes. Rural areas may face infrastructure constraints, lower awareness of smart meter benefits, and weaker app usage. A one-size-fits-all strategy will therefore not succeed. To implement these successfully, structural differences between the two countries must be considered. The UK's rollout was managed through a relatively centralized energy system with a small number of large suppliers, whereas India's electricity distribution is fragmented across several state-owned DISCOMs with varying states of financial health and technical capacity.  India’s smart meter rollout success could be increased if paired with stronger consumer engagement. Establishing a permanent framework and delivering targeted outreach will help drive genuine behavioral change and ensure the benefits of smart metering reach every household. Anna Trissa is a research intern with the Chair on India and Emerging Asia Economics at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nTags\nIndia,\nAsia, and\nEnergy Markets, Trends, and Outlooks\nAnna Trissa\nIntern, Chair on India and Emerging Asia Economics\nPrograms & Projects\nChair on India and Emerging Asia Economics\nEconomic Security and Technology","content_cn":"赢得消费者：让印度的智能电表为民所用\n照片：JAMES ARTHUR GEKIERE/BELGA MAG/AFP via Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nAnna Trissa\n发表于2026年5月1日\n印度计划在2028年前依据改革版配电部门计划部署2.5亿只智能电表，使消费者采用成为电力部门改革的核心。然而，计费透明度持续存在缺口、数字素养较低，以及对智能电表工具的参与有限，正拖慢用户接受速度。印度一直专注于实现电表安装目标。然而，它必须同时关注消费者是否理解、信任并积极使用现有智能电表，方法是建立国家消费者参与使命，并实施有针对性的外展战略。这些措施结合起来，可以建立信任、改善消费者体验，并加快各邦普遍采用智能电表。\n\n建立消费者参与使命\n\n印度目前缺乏一个精简的流程来建立公众对智能计量的信任并促进消费者参与。印度曾作出一些尝试，例如2026年2月，电力部在配电公司（DISCOMs）的支持下发起了为期两周的“Smart Meter Pakhwada”活动，其中包括社区宣传会议、挨家挨户与消费者互动，以及智能电表现场演示。然而，如果印度要在2028年前达到2.5亿只智能电表的目标，并让超过14亿人口参与进来，就需要在短期举措之外持续开展努力。\n\n英国的参与工作为印度提供了一个范例。在2011年推出智能电表期间面临消费者抵触后，英国成立了Smart Energy GB（SEGB），这是一个专注于公众参与、由政府支持的机构。SEGB的《Consumer Engagement Plan 2025》采用多渠道宣传和持续的消费者研究。根据其《2025年年度报告》，SEGB的工作促成了60%的智能电表安装。英国模式表明，有组织的消费者宣传可以支持智能计量，但印度能否成功，将取决于能否根据当地制度设计进行调整。\n\n印度应在REC Limited旗下建立一个同等机构，赋予其明确的使命、专门资金和可衡量的参与目标。其使命可以是设计并实施提高智能电表认知度、信任度和使用率的战略。例如，印度政府可以参考SEGB的2025年消费者参与计划，该计划重点开展宣传活动、有针对性的外展，并纳入微型企业。该机构的工作依据《绩效管理框架》（PMF）进行评估，这是一份规定绩效指标和年度目标的文件。\n\n2025年，SEGB开展了宣传活动，突出智能电表在财务和节能方面的益处（灵活电价、电动汽车/太阳能整合），并开展安抚性宣传以消除误解。外展活动使用了数字媒体和社交媒体，同时保留电视渠道以覆盖年龄较大的受众。它还采用“People like Me”案例研究和消费者调查，并在地区层面开展合作，以接触包括低收入家庭和无法使用数字服务者在内的弱势群体。\n\n印度应根据自身情况吸收这些经验。一个常设的消费者参与使命应聚焦于提高应用程序使用率、增进对计费系统的理解，以及确保消费者能够获取详细的用电数据等成果。Smart Meter Pakhwada活动中的挨家挨户\n可以将互动活动、智能电表展示和教育项目正式纳入这一使命。应利用定期消费者调查、反馈循环和行为洞察来完善沟通策略。在宣传活动中使用当地语言和消费者偏好的语言，有助于使沟通更加个性化并更具包容性。\n\n设计有针对性的外展策略\n\n统一的沟通策略无法适应印度高度多样化的消费者环境。拟议框架应根据数字素养、用户参与度和地理位置实施分众外展策略。\n\n在数字素养较低的地区，外展应优先通过社区会议、演示活动和地方中间人开展面对面互动。研究表明，面对面沟通对于克服不信任和纠正错误信息尤其有效。\n\n为了提高用户参与度，尤其是在智能电表已经安装但参与率仍然较低的地区，宣传活动可以侧重于数字素养和使用推广。这包括推广移动应用程序，解释用量跟踪和账单明细等功能，并引导用户完成支付和充值流程。在网络连接较弱的地区，应通过广播和电视补充数字化外展，以确保沟通保持包容性。\n\n消费者参与工作也可以根据地理位置而有所不同。城市地区的部署可能更容易，但居民仍可能抵触使用或不信任计费变化。农村地区可能面临基础设施限制、对智能电表益处的认知较低以及应用程序使用率较低等问题。因此，千篇一律的策略不会成功。\n\n要成功实施这些措施，必须考虑两国之间的结构性差异。英国的推广是在相对集中的能源体系下管理的，由少数大型供应商组成；而印度的电力分配则分散在多个国有配电公司（DISCOM）之间，各州的财务状况和技术能力不尽相同。\n\n如果配合更强有力的消费者参与措施，印度智能电表推广的成功率可能会提高。建立永久性框架并开展有针对性的外展，将有助于推动真正的行为改变，并确保智能计量的益处惠及每个家庭。\n\nAnna Trissa 是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心印度及新兴亚洲经济学主席项目的研究实习生。\n\n标签\n印度，\n亚洲，以及\n能源市场、趋势与展望\nAnna Trissa\n实习生，印度及新兴亚洲经济学主席项目\n项目与计划\n印度及新兴亚洲经济学主席项目\n经济安全与技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-05-01T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3150","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"超越监管与去监管的陷阱 | 创新视角 | CSIS","title_en":"Moving Beyond the Regulation/Deregulation Trap | Perspectives on Innovation | CSIS","summary_cn":"文章讨论美国创新政策中的监管问题，认为监管与去监管的二元对立过于简化。作者引用经济学家弗兰克·奈特关于风险与不确定性的区分，主张创新政策的目标应是减少监管不确定性而非简单去监管。文章以《芯片法案》等为例，说明稳定、可预测的监管框架对促进创新和投资的重要性。","summary_en":"The article discusses regulatory issues in U.S. innovation policy, arguing that the regulation/deregulation dichotomy oversimplifies the debate. Drawing on economist Frank Knight's distinction between risk and uncertainty, the author contends that pro-innovation policy should aim to minimize regulatory uncertainty rather than pursue deregulation. Using examples like the CHIPS Act, it highlights that stable and predictable regulatory frameworks are key to fostering innovation and investment.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:01:15.892814+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/perspectives-innovation/moving-beyond-regulationderegulation-trap","content":"Moving Beyond the Regulation/Deregulation Trap\nPhoto: adri/Adobe Stock\nBlog Post\nby\nJulie Heng\nPublished February 20, 2025\nHow can public policies better support innovation across the country? In recent years, agencies like the Commerce Department and National Science Foundation have stepped up their engagement with regional ecosystems across the United States to foster innovation and production of critical emerging technologies, such as semiconductors and biomanufacturing. The CHIPS Act, notably, has been a key driver, spurring an unprecedented push to stimulate chip manufacturing and research in numerous U.S. regions. The resulting activities, however, have also brought about a host of regulatory challenges surrounding issues related to power transmission infrastructure, clean energy permitting, water, and workforce requirements. Often, these discussions are framed as a binary choice: regulation versus deregulation. Many assert that regulation stifles innovation and growth, while deregulation fosters the opposite. While there is no shortage of regulations that need reform, the regulation/deregulation dichotomy often oversimplifies the issue and can be misleading. A more constructive framing for balancing good governance and good business shifts the focus from “regulation versus deregulation” to “risk versus uncertainty.”  While it’s true that industry stakeholders generally push for deregulation to minimize compliance costs, there's a more nuanced reality at play. In the long run, it's the predictability and usefulness of rules, not their mere presence or absence, that drives successful investment and innovation. Businesses and investors often express frustration over arbitrary or unexpected regulatory changes—the sense of \"being denied at the finish line\" after substantial investments in research, development, and planning. This experience is common in areas such as licensing, permitting, intellectual property regimes, and export regulations, as well as their waivers and exceptions.  What firms seek, then, is not necessarily a complete absence of regulation but stability and predictability: firms are willing to live with a reasonable tradeoff between extra costs imposed by regulation in return for certainty in regulation that they can then plan and invest around. In other words, rules for innovation can be valuable—as long as those rules don’t keep changing mid-game. From Regulation/Deregulation to Risk/Uncertainty The economist Frank Knight, in his seminal 1921 work Risk, Uncertainty, and Profit, helpfully distinguished between risk and uncertainty: “Uncertainty must be taken in a sense radically distinct from the familiar notion of Risk... the crucial difference lies in whether the outcome can be calculated and planned for, or whether it is inherently unpredictable.” In other words, risk refers to situations where possible outcomes can be identified and probabilities assigned—think of financial investments, where markets allow for hedging or diversification. Uncertainty, on the other hand, refers to situations where possible outcomes are known, but probabilities are not—such as when political instability confuses policy trajectories or when future technological capabilities are unknown. (In some cases of emergent technological innovation, we may even move into a kind of uncertainty where possible outcomes, not to mention probabilities, cannot be identified.) Knight’s insight is critical for understanding innovation policy: risk can be planned for; uncertainty cannot. Entrepreneurs, investors, and inventors can forecast, manage, insure against, and mitigate risk. But when uncertainty reigns, it becomes far harder for innovators to chart a path forward. When it comes to regulatory reform, then, a pro-innovation goal is not necessarily deregulation but minimizing regulatory uncertainty. Reframing Regulation for Innovation Undoubtedly, there are many opportunities where rethinking regulation can be helpful. There are often confusing and sometimes contradictory directives from federal, state, and local levels, as well as across federal agencies, worthy of reform. Contradictory or onerously overlapping standards for the same process or product can add unnecessary transaction costs, reducing both investment and income. And by no means is predictable bad regulation worth preserving solely for its predictability. Instead, this distinction between risk and uncertainty can guide us towards a more nuanced approach to regulatory reform. Rather than focus solely on whether regulations should exist, we should consider how well-designed regulations can include stability and clarity for innovation. Many lessons can be learned, for example, from how DARPA leverages the Intergovernmental Personnel Act or how the Building Chips in America Act exempts certain projects from undergoing National Environmental Policy Act reviews to speed up federally funded fab construction.  In fact, regulation can serve as a demand signal that channels efforts towards high-priority innovation areas while also protecting firms’ ability to innovate. For example, clear guidance on chemical usage or energy efficiency performance standards can help coordinate efforts across industries such as semiconductor manufacturing, where companies must balance innovation with substantial long-term investments. Similarly, cybersecurity standards can ensure robust safeguards across competitors, while regulatory frameworks in biotechnology can help novel startups thrive despite unpredictability in funding from large pharmaceutical companies.  Furthermore, this risk/uncertainty distinction highlights how good industrial policy can help not only reduce risks for investors but also establish stable and conducive market foundations to foster innovation, ensure fair practices, and address societal goals like sustainability and workforce needs. It's a more comprehensive and nuanced approach to policymaking. Ultimately, many debates over regulation versus deregulation miss a key point: Innovation frequently thrives not necessarily in the absence of regulation but in the presence of clear and predictable regulatory frameworks. As we work to revitalize regional economic development, we need a more sophisticated approach to regulation—one that strikes the balance between providing stability and incentivizing cooperation, while avoiding rigidity that stifles creativity and risk-taking—to serve the public interest. Julie Heng is a research associate of Renewing American Innovation at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS) in Washington, D.C.\nThe Perspectives on Innovation Blog is produced by the Renewing American Innovation Project at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nNorth America,\nAmerican Innovation,\nTechnology,\nTechnology and Innovation,\nEconomic Security,\nTrade and International Business, and\nEnergy and Sustainability\nImage\nJulie Heng\nAdjunct Fellow (Non-resident), Renewing American Innovation\nPrograms & Projects\nAmerica’s Semiconductor Edge\nBiotechnology and Security\nRenewing American Innovation\nEconomic Security and Technology\nSemiconductors and National Security\nEconomic Security Toolkit","content_cn":"超越监管/放松监管的困局\n图片：adri/Adobe Stock\n博客文章\n作者\nJulie Heng\n发表于2025年2月20日\n公共政策如何才能更好地支持全国各地的创新？近年来，商务部和国家科学基金会等机构加大了与美国各地创新生态系统的合作力度，以推动半导体和生物制造等关键新兴技术的创新与生产。值得注意的是，《芯片法案》一直是其中的关键驱动力，促使美国众多地区以前所未有的力度推动芯片制造和研究。然而，由此产生的活动也带来了一系列监管挑战，涉及输电基础设施、清洁能源许可、水资源以及劳动力要求等问题。通常，这些讨论被框定为一种二元选择：监管还是放松监管。许多人认为，监管会扼杀创新和增长，而放松监管则会促进创新和增长。尽管需要改革的法规并不少见，但监管/放松监管的二分法往往过度简化了问题，也可能产生误导。要在善治与良好商业环境之间取得平衡，更具建设性的框架是将关注点从“监管还是放松监管”转向“风险还是不确定性”。虽然行业利益相关者通常确实会推动放松监管，以降低合规成本，但实际情况要复杂得多。从长远来看，推动成功投资和创新的，并不是规则是否存在，而是规则是否具有可预测性和实用性。企业和投资者常常对任意或出人意料的监管变化感到沮丧——在投入大量资金进行研究、开发和规划之后，却有一种“在终点线前被拒之门外”的感觉。在许可、审批、知识产权制度和出口监管等领域，以及这些领域中的豁免和例外情形中，这种经历十分普遍。  因此，企业寻求的未必是完全没有监管，而是稳定性和可预测性：企业愿意接受监管带来的额外成本，只要这些成本能够换来监管上的确定性，使企业可以据此进行规划和投资。换言之，只要创新规则不会在比赛进行到一半时不断改变，这些规则就可能具有价值。从监管/放松监管到风险/不确定性 经济学家弗兰克·奈特在其1921年的经典著作《风险、不确定性与利润》中，对风险和不确定性作了有益的区分：“不确定性必须从根本上区别于我们熟悉的风险概念……关键差异在于，结果是否可以计算并据此规划，还是其本质上不可预测。” 换言之，风险指的是可以确定可能结果并为其分配概率的情形——可以想想金融投资，市场允许人们进行对冲或分散投资。另一方面，不确定性指的是已知可能结果、但无法确定其概率的情形——例如政治不稳定导致政策走向难以判断，或者未来的技术能力尚不可知。（在某些新兴技术创新的情况下，我们甚至可能进入这样一种不确定性状态：可能的结果，更不用说其概率，根本无法确定。） 奈特的洞见对于理解创新政策至关重要：风险\n风险是可以规划的；不确定性则无法规划。企业家、投资者和发明者可以预测、管理、投保应对并减轻风险。但当不确定性占据主导时，创新者就很难规划前进的路径。因此，在监管改革方面，促进创新的目标未必是放松监管，而是尽量减少监管不确定性。\n\n重新构思促进创新的监管\n\n毫无疑问，有许多领域可以通过重新思考监管来发挥作用。联邦、州和地方层级之间，以及各联邦机构之间，往往存在令人困惑、有时相互矛盾的指令，这些都值得改革。针对同一流程或产品的相互矛盾或繁琐重叠的标准，可能增加不必要的交易成本，从而同时减少投资和收入。当然，也绝不能仅仅因为糟糕的监管具有可预测性，就认为这种监管值得保留。\n\n相反，风险与不确定性之间的这种区别，可以引导我们采取更细致入微的监管改革方法。与其只关注监管是否应当存在，我们还应考虑设计良好的监管如何为创新纳入稳定性和清晰度。例如，我们可以从DARPA如何利用《政府间人员法》，以及《美国芯片建设法》如何豁免某些项目接受《国家环境政策法》审查、以加快联邦资助的晶圆厂建设中，吸取许多经验。\n\n事实上，监管可以充当需求信号，将努力引向高优先级的创新领域，同时保护企业的创新能力。例如，关于化学品使用或能源效率绩效标准的明确指导，可以帮助协调半导体制造等行业之间的努力；在这些行业中，企业必须在创新与大量长期投资之间取得平衡。同样，网络安全标准可以确保竞争者之间具备强有力的保障措施，而生物技术领域的监管框架则可以帮助新兴初创企业蓬勃发展，尽管大型制药公司提供资金存在不可预测性。\n\n此外，这种风险/不确定性区别凸显了良好的产业政策如何不仅帮助降低投资者的风险，还能建立稳定且有利的市场基础，以促进创新、确保公平实践，并应对可持续性和劳动力需求等社会目标。这是一种更加全面、细致入微的政策制定方法。\n\n归根结底，围绕监管与放松监管的许多争论忽视了一个关键点：创新蓬勃发展，往往并非因为没有监管，而是因为存在清晰且可预测的监管框架。在我们努力振兴区域经济发展之际，需要采取更成熟的监管方式——在提供稳定性、激励合作与避免压制创造力和冒险精神的僵化之间取得平衡——以服务公共利益。\n\nJulie Heng是华盛顿特区战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）“振兴美国创新”项目的研究助理。\n\n《创新视角博客》由战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS）的“振兴美国创新”项目制作。CSIS是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不代表任何专有利益。CSIS不持有特定的政策立场。因此，所有观点、立场以及\n本文所表达的结论应理解为仅代表作者本人。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n北美洲，\n美国创新，\n技术，\n技术与创新，\n经济安全，\n贸易与国际商务，以及\n能源与可持续发展\n图片\nJulie Heng\n重振美国创新项目兼任研究员（非驻留）\n项目与计划\n美国的半导体优势\n生物技术与安全\n重振美国创新\n经济安全与技术\n半导体与国家安全\n经济安全工具包","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-02-20T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2970","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"RAI解读：知识产权如何促进创新","title_en":"RAI Explainer: How IP Rights Promote Innovation | Perspectives on Innovation | CSIS","summary_cn":"CSIS文章论述知识产权对创新的推动作用，认为专利、版权和商业秘密是创新与国家安全的关键驱动力。文章从产权属性、风险投资激励、赋能中小企业及促进下游创新四方面展开，指出私人部门已占美国研发投入约75%，强调稳定可预期的知识产权是技术标准协作和保持美国技术领先的基础。","summary_en":"This CSIS piece argues intellectual property rights are vital drivers of innovation and U.S. economic and national security. It examines how IP functions as property, enables risk-taking and investment, empowers small actors, and facilitates downstream innovation. Noting the private sector now funds about 75% of U.S. R&D, it stresses that secure, predictable IP underpins standards collaboration and U.S. technological leadership.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:50:03.516375+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/perspectives-innovation/rai-explainer-how-ip-rights-promote-innovation","content":"RAI Explainer: How IP Rights Promote Innovation\nPhoto: Yingyaipumi/Adobe Stock\nBlog Post\nby\nChris Borges\nPublished April 17, 2025\nIntellectual Property (IP) rights, such as patents, copyrights, and trade secrets, are more than legal instruments; they are vital drivers of innovation and critical components of U.S. economic and national security strategies. This connection was evident to the U.S. founders, who enshrined IP protections in the U.S. Constitution to promote ingenuity. Likewise, President Lincoln recognized that the patent system “added the fuel of interest to the fire of genius.” In a time where technological progress is expected and innovation taken for granted, the critical role of IP in fostering that innovation and maintaining a technological edge is often overlooked. Discussions on how to spur technological progress and maintain economic competitiveness frequently discount the role of strong IP protections in shaping incentives, securing investments, and enabling breakthroughs. Without a clear understanding of these connections, efforts to foster innovation risk missing a crucial piece of the equation.To inform the debate, it is critical to closely examine the mechanisms through which IP rights stimulate creativity, facilitate investment, support small businesses, and, ultimately, promote innovation.IP Rights are Property RightsAt the most fundamental level, secure IP rights provide a stable and predictable framework that actors in the innovation ecosystem can utilize to make decisions. Property rights for physical goods and spaces are widely considered as essential to the functioning of market economies. Buyers and sellers cannot transact if rights over the property in question are unclear. Would you buy a house if the seller could not also hand over the deed?IP rights similarly transform inventions, artistic works, and other ideas into discrete economic assets that enable commercial transactions. This greatly increases market efficiency by providing economic actors with the security they need to collaborate and appropriately value their innovation. Indeed, a 2021 estimate placed the value of all U.S. patents at just under $3 trillion. By allowing the market to value innovation as an economic good, IP rights incentivize further innovation.Further, numerous U.S. businesses rely on IP as their primary assets and depend on secure IP rights to survive. Though these firms do not necessarily produce consumer-facing end products, they are nonetheless vital components of critical and emerging technology value chains. Several U.S. advanced communications firms, for instance, invest heavily in research and development (R&D) to develop new interoperable technologies such as 5G, Wi-Fi, and Bluetooth, then leverage the IP system to license their innovations to other firms who integrate them into their products for commercialization. Without secure IP rights, these upstream R&D-focused firms could not safely license their innovations, undermining their viability as businesses, as well as their vital contributions to the sophisticated technologies we have come to rely on.IP Rights Enable Risk-Taking and InvestmentInnovation is inherently risky, time-consuming, and capital-intensive. Developing new ideas and bringing them to market requires significant investment, especially in critical and emerging technology fields. Bringing a new drug to market can cost several billion dollars, while the cost of training a frontier artificial intelligence (AI) model doubles every year. Yet success is far from guaranteed—more than 75 percent of startups fail, while less than 14 percent of drugs pass clinical trials and are approved for use.Strong and secure IP rights play a pivotal role in enabling inventors, entrepreneurs, and investors to take on these massive and risky undertakings by providing clear ownership rights to their work. Absent such rights, it would be extremely unlikely for investors and firms to allocate large amounts of capital to fund projects with a high risk of failure, as success would inevitably be met with free riding by competitors. Why take the risks if someone else can simply steal your success? Secure IP rights are essential for promoting investment in innovation, as they enable innovators to recoup their investments should they succeed and, ideally, fund further innovation.The role of IP rights in driving investment is compounded today as the center of gravity of U.S. R&D funding continues to move away from government. While public funding once played a central role in R&D, the private sector has now become the primary investor in innovation. In the early 1980s, public and private contributions to U.S. R&D were roughly equal, but by 2021, private sector investment accounted for 75 percent of total U.S. R&D spending. Meanwhile, federal R&D funding continues to decline, with a 2.7 percent cut in FY2024, including an 11.3 percent reduction in non-defense R&D spending. Put simply, in the twenty-first century, the vast majority of R&D in the United States that drives innovation in critical and emerging technologies is funded by the private sector. Secure IP rights are the foundation that keeps these investments flowing.IP Rights Empower Small Economic ActorsA fundamental component of any healthy innovation ecosystem are small actors such as startups, small and medium-sized enterprises, and individual inventors. These actors account for over 40 percent of U.S. economic activity and create two-thirds of new jobs. They take risks that established companies cannot, driving innovation, technological change, and economic growth. Indeed, many of the most impactful companies today were once startups.Strong and secure IP rights are essential for these small economic actors to continue flourishing. Large firms often have several ways to secure competitive advantages in technology. They can fund their own R&D, quickly scale up development, and monetize their products in adjacent markets.Small firms, in contrast, typically lack the resources necessary to quickly develop their ideas into large-scale marketable products. They often require external financing to support R&D and must engage other companies to manufacture and market their ideas. By transforming their ideas into property that can be assigned and protected, IP rights allow small businesses to both attract investment and form commercial partnerships with other firms with the assurance that their ideas will not be stolen. For this reason, inventors, entrepreneurs, and startups repeatedly emphasize the importance of secure IP rights as a critical path to market entry, growth, and fair participation. Research bears this out: The approval of a startup's first patent can increase its employment growth over the next five years by 36 percent, creating quality jobs for American workers.U.S. history further underscores the importance of strong IP rights for small economic actors. The late 1930s through the 1970s, for instance, was marked by relatively weak IP protections in the United States, as U.S. courts were reluctant to enforce patents in a period of strict antitrust enforcement. During this span, both small innovators and the U.S. innovation ecosystem overall suffered. R&D activity became concentrated in the research labs of large corporations and was principally funded by the government, as investors were hesitant to contribute funds to R&D projects without secure rights over the results of their work. Though innovation did not come to a complete halt, this era of weak patent protection coincided with a noticeable decline in U.S. innovation performance.It was only when patent protections were strengthened in the 1980s through reforms like the Bayh-Dole Act and the establishment of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit that U.S. innovation rebounded. Rather than protecting incumbents, strong IP rights spurred the entry of new R&D-focused firms into high-tech industries like biopharmaceuticals and semiconductors. Private capital flowed into startups, fueling competition, fostering partnerships with larger firms, and reinvigorating the U.S. innovation ecosystem. Indeed, one scholar of economic history described the patent system as the “democratization of invention,” because it empowers inventors from all backgrounds—not just the wealthy and powerful—to invent and protect their ideas.Conversely, weakened and unstable IP rights disproportionately hurt small economic actors. They increase the barriers to attracting investment and make small firms more susceptible to attacks and theft by larger, better-resourced firms that can quickly scale up production and afford scores of lawyers. Without a robust ecosystem of small economic actors, the entire U.S. innovation ecosystem suffers, undermining U.S. economic and national security.IP Rights Facilitate Downstream InnovationDownstream innovation refers to the process by which new inventions or technologies serve as the foundation for subsequent innovations, often leading to the development of entirely new industries, markets, and applications. GPS technology, for example, spurred the creation of navigation systems, ride-sharing platforms, and precision agriculture, among other new technologies, while the invention of the transistor in the mid-twentieth century laid the groundwork for the semiconductor industry, which, in turn, makes modern computing possible—including the AI revolution. Secure IP rights are an essential catalyst for downstream innovation, as they promote the open exchange of technical information. To receive a patent, for instance, an applicant must disclose the technical details of their invention, thus making this information available to the public. This is a key function of patents: In exchange for patent rights, the inventor describes the invention in clear terms, thereby accelerating innovation by allowing others to learn from this information and build on it. (Indeed, the word “patent” stems from the Latin patere, which means “be open.”) Without secure IP protections, inventors might resort to secrecy, which would hinder the diffusion of ideas and slow technological progress.Critically, IP rights also play a key role in facilitating the collaborative and non-linear nature of innovation. Standards development is an important example of this. Technical standards are sets of specifications, guidelines, or protocols that are widely accepted within a particular industry or field of technology. These standards are rarely made by a single firm. Instead, they are typically developed by industry consensus and can include contributions from hundreds of firms. The 5G standard, for instance, encompasses over 25,000 distinct patents owned by various companies. For a standard like this to succeed, owners of patents necessary to implement the standard—standard essential patents (SEPs)—must have confidence in their rights over their work. Without that security, why undertake the cost and risk needed to invent new technologies and contribute them to the standard? In other words, secure, stable, and predictable IP rights lay the foundation necessary for the development of sophisticated technical standards. This secure foundation affords IP owners the assurances needed to cooperate in the development and implementation of standardized technologies and provides the incentives to make those technologies broadly available. Without secure IP rights, IP owners would be less likely to cooperate for fear of having their innovation stolen or dramatically devalued, greatly undercutting innovation and harming consumers everywhere. Think about how different life would be if phones from different manufacturers could not call each other, or if each device required a different power outlet.ConclusionSecure, stable, and predictable IP rights are a cornerstone of the U.S.  innovation ecosystem. They build confidence for investors, empower smaller actors, and catalyze downstream discoveries—ensuring that creative breakthroughs can emerge from any corner and expand into entirely new industries. By aligning incentives and safeguarding ideas, IP protections help the United States maintain its technological leadership and reinforce the very foundations of its economic and national security.Christopher Borges is a program manager and associate fellow with the Economics Program and Scholl Chair for International Business at the Center for Strategic and International Studies in Washington, D.C.\nThe Perspectives on Innovation Blog is produced by the Renewing American Innovation Project at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nIntellectual Property, and\nAmerican Innovation\nImage\nChris Borges\nFellow and Senior Program Manager, Economic Security and Technology Department\nPrograms & Projects\nEconomic Security and Technology\nLeadershIP\nRenewing American Innovation\nStandards and Intellectual Property Rights for Innovation","content_cn":"RAI 解析：知识产权如何促进创新\n图片：Yingyaipumi/Adobe Stock\n博客文章\n作者\nChris Borges\n发表于2025年4月17日\n知识产权（IP）权利，如专利、版权和商业秘密，不仅仅是法律工具；它们还是创新的重要驱动力，也是美国经济和国家安全战略的关键组成部分。美国建国者认识到了这种联系，他们将知识产权保护写入美国宪法，以促进创造力。同样，林肯总统也认识到，专利制度“为天才之火添上了利益之薪”。在技术进步被视为理所当然、创新也被习以为常的时代，知识产权在促进创新和保持技术优势方面的关键作用常常被忽视。关于如何推动技术进步和保持经济竞争力的讨论，往往低估了强有力的知识产权保护在塑造激励机制、保障投资和促成突破方面的作用。如果不清楚地理解这些联系，促进创新的努力就有可能遗漏其中一个关键环节。为了让这场辩论更有依据，关键是要仔细审视知识产权如何激发创造力、促进投资、支持小企业，并最终推动创新。\n\n知识产权是财产权\n\n在最根本的层面上，稳固的知识产权为创新生态系统中的参与者提供了一个稳定且可预测的框架，使其能够据此作出决策。实物和空间的财产权被普遍认为是市场经济运行的基础。如果对相关财产的权利不明确，买卖双方就无法进行交易。如果卖方无法同时交付房契，你会买房吗？\n\n知识产权同样将发明、艺术作品和其他思想转化为明确的经济资产，从而实现商业交易。这通过为经济参与者提供合作和适当评估其创新成果所需的保障，大幅提高了市场效率。事实上，2021年的一项估算显示，美国所有专利的价值略低于3万亿美元。通过让市场能够将创新作为一种经济商品进行估值，知识产权激励了进一步的创新。\n\n此外，众多美国企业将知识产权作为其主要资产，并依赖稳固的知识产权来生存。尽管这些企业不一定生产面向消费者的最终产品，但它们仍然是关键和新兴技术价值链的重要组成部分。例如，几家美国先进通信企业大量投资于研发（R&D），开发5G、Wi-Fi和蓝牙等新的互操作技术，然后利用知识产权体系，将其创新成果授权给其他企业，由后者将这些成果整合到产品中进行商业化。没有稳固的知识产权，这些以上游研发为重点的企业就无法安全地授权其创新成果，从而危及其作为企业的生存能力，也会削弱它们对我们日益依赖的复杂技术所作出的重要贡献。\n\n知识产权促成风险承担和投资\n\n创新本质上具有风险性、耗时长且资本密集。开发新想法并将其推向市场需要大量投资，尤其是在关键和新兴技术领域。将一种新药推向市场可能需要数十亿美元，而培训一款前沿人工智能\n人工智能（AI）模型每年翻一番。然而，成功远非有保障——超过75%的初创企业会失败，而通过临床试验并获批使用的药物不到14%。\n\n强大且安全的知识产权保护通过为发明者、企业家和投资者的成果提供明确的所有权，在使他们能够承担这些规模巨大且风险高昂的事业方面发挥着关键作用。如果没有这类权利，投资者和企业极不可能投入大量资本来资助失败风险很高的项目，因为一旦成功，竞争者必然会搭便车。既然别人可以轻易窃取你的成功，为什么还要承担风险？安全的知识产权保护对于促进创新投资至关重要，因为它使创新者在成功时能够收回投资，并在理想情况下为进一步创新提供资金。\n\n如今，随着美国研发资金的重心继续从政府转移，知识产权在推动投资方面的作用更加凸显。公共资金曾经在研发中发挥核心作用，但如今私营部门已成为创新的主要投资者。20世纪80年代初，公共和私人对美国研发的投入大致相当；但到2021年，私营部门投资已占美国研发总支出的75%。与此同时，联邦研发资金持续下降，2024财年削减了2.7%，其中非国防研发支出减少了11.3%。简而言之，在21世纪，美国推动关键和新兴技术创新的大部分研发资金都来自私营部门。安全的知识产权保护是维持这些投资持续流入的基础。\n\n知识产权赋能小型经济参与者\n\n任何健康的创新生态系统都离不开初创企业、中小型企业和个人发明者等小型参与者。这些参与者占美国经济活动的40%以上，并创造了三分之二的新就业岗位。他们承担着成熟企业无法承担的风险，推动创新、技术变革和经济增长。事实上，如今许多最具影响力的企业都曾经是初创企业。\n\n强大且安全的知识产权保护对于这些小型经济参与者持续蓬勃发展至关重要。大型企业通常有多种方式在技术领域取得竞争优势。它们可以资助自己的研发，迅速扩大开发规模，并在相邻市场将产品变现。\n\n相比之下，小型企业通常缺乏将创意迅速开发成大规模可销售产品所需的资源。它们往往需要外部融资来支持研发，并必须与其他企业合作制造和营销其创意。通过将创意转化为可转让且受保护的财产，知识产权使小企业能够吸引投资，并与其他企业建立商业合作关系，同时确保其创意不会被窃取。因此，发明者、企业家和初创企业一再强调，安全的知识产权保护是进入市场、实现增长和公平参与的关键途径。研究也证实了这一点：初创企业首项专利获批后，未来五年的就业增长率可能提高36%，为美国劳动者创造高质量就业岗位。\n\n美国历史进一步凸显了强大知识产权保护对小型经济参与者的重要性。20世纪30年代末至70年代，\n例如，在美国，知识产权保护相对薄弱，因为在严格执行反垄断法的时期，美国法院不愿执行专利。在此期间，小型创新者以及美国整体创新生态系统都遭受了损失。研发活动集中到了大公司的研究实验室，且主要由政府资助，因为投资者不愿在无法确保其工作成果权利的情况下，为研发项目投入资金。尽管创新并未完全停滞，但这一专利保护薄弱的时代恰逢美国创新表现明显下滑。\n\n直到20世纪80年代，通过《拜杜法案》等改革以及美国联邦巡回上诉法院的成立加强了专利保护，美国创新才得以复苏。强有力的知识产权并非保护既有企业，而是推动了新的、专注于研发的企业进入生物制药和半导体等高科技产业。私人资本流入初创企业，促进了竞争，推动了与大型企业的合作，并重新激发了美国创新生态系统。事实上，一位经济史学者将专利制度描述为“发明的民主化”，因为它赋予所有背景的发明者——而不仅仅是富人和有权势者——发明并保护其创意的能力。\n\n相反，弱化且不稳定的知识产权会不成比例地伤害小型经济主体。它们提高了吸引投资的门槛，使小企业更容易受到资金和资源更充足的大型企业的攻击和窃取，而后者能够迅速扩大生产并负担得起成群的律师。没有强大的小型经济主体生态系统，整个美国创新生态系统都会受到影响，从而削弱美国的经济和国家安全。\n\n知识产权促进下游创新\n\n下游创新是指新发明或新技术成为后续创新基础的过程，通常会促成全新产业、市场和应用的发展。例如，GPS技术推动了导航系统、网约车平台和精准农业等新技术的诞生；而20世纪中叶晶体管的发明为半导体产业奠定了基础，半导体产业进而使现代计算成为可能——包括人工智能革命。安全可靠的知识产权是下游创新不可或缺的催化剂，因为它促进了技术信息的开放交流。例如，要获得专利，申请人必须披露其发明的技术细节，从而使这些信息向公众开放。这是专利的一项关键功能：作为获得专利权的交换，发明者用清晰的语言描述发明，由此允许他人从这些信息中学习并在其基础上继续发展，从而加速创新。（事实上，“patent”一词源自拉丁语patere，意为“开放”。）如果没有安全可靠的知识产权保护，发明者可能会转而保守秘密，这将阻碍思想的传播并减缓技术进步。\n\n至关重要的是，知识产权还在促进创新的协作性和非线性方面发挥着关键作用。标准制定就是一个重要例子。技术标准是指在特定领域内被广泛接受的一组规范、指南或协议，\n技术行业或领域。这些标准很少由单一企业制定。相反，它们通常由行业共识推动制定，并可能包含数百家企业的贡献。例如，5G标准涵盖了由不同公司拥有的超过25,000项独立专利。要使这样的标准取得成功，拥有实施该标准所必需专利的权利人——标准必要专利（SEPs）持有人——必须对其成果所享有的权利充满信心。没有这种保障，为什么还要承担发明新技术并将其贡献给标准所需的成本和风险？换言之，安全、稳定且可预测的知识产权为复杂技术标准的发展奠定了必要基础。这一稳固基础为知识产权所有者提供了合作开发和实施标准化技术所需的保证，并为这些技术得到广泛应用提供了激励。没有安全的知识产权，知识产权所有者将因担心其创新被窃取或价值大幅贬低而不太可能开展合作，从而严重削弱创新并损害世界各地的消费者。想想看，如果不同制造商的手机无法相互拨打，或者每台设备都需要不同的电源插座，生活会有多么不同。结论安全、稳定且可预测的知识产权是美国创新生态系统的基石。它们为投资者建立信心，赋能较小的参与者，并催化下游发现——确保创意突破可以从任何角落涌现，并拓展到全新的产业。通过协调激励并保护创意，知识产权保护帮助美国保持技术领导地位，巩固其经济和国家安全的根基。Christopher Borges是华盛顿特区战略与国际研究中心经济学项目及Scholl国际商业主席的项目经理和副研究员。\n“创新视角”博客由战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）的“重振美国创新项目”制作。该中心是一家专注于国际公共政策问题的私人免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不涉及专有利益。CSIS不采取具体政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的观点。\n© 2025 战略与国际研究中心版权所有。保留所有权利。\n标签\n知识产权，以及\n美国创新\n图片\nChris Borges\n研究员兼经济安全与技术部高级项目经理\n项目与计划\n经济安全与技术\nLeadershIP\n重振美国创新\n创新的标准与知识产权","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-04-17T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2575","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"SBIR与多次获奖的作用 | 创新视角 | CSIS","title_en":"SBIR and the Role of Multiple Awards | Perspectives on Innovation | CSIS","summary_cn":"在2025年9月SBIR项目重新授权截止日期临近之际，美国国会正讨论该项目的下一阶段形式。战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）的文章指出，部分重新授权提案试图限制或抑制“多次获奖者”，这可能无意中削弱SBIR的灵活性和有效性。文章认为，限制特定小企业的获奖数量表面上可让更多企业参与，但SBIR已是开放项目，每年约三分之一获奖者为新参与者，且多次获奖者获得的资金份额有限。文章建议国会应鼓励项目管理人淘汰不符合创新使命的企业，而非通过立法施加限制。","summary_en":"As the Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) program reauthorization deadline approaches in September 2025, Congress is debating the next phase of this successful program. A CSIS article notes that some reauthorization proposals would limit or disincentivize 'multiple award winners,' potentially undermining the program's flexibility and effectiveness. While capping awards may seem to broaden participation, SBIR is already open, with about one-third of awardees new each year, and multiple award winners receive a modest share of funding. The article argues Congress should encourage program managers to weed out underperformers rather than impose legislative restrictions.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:28:49.933481+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/perspectives-innovation/sbir-and-role-multiple-awards","content":"SBIR and the Role of Multiple Awards\nPhoto: Adam Glanzman/Bloomberg via Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nCharles Wessner\nand\nSujai Shivakumar\nPublished July 29, 2025\nAs the Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) program reauthorization deadline approaches in September 2025, Congress is debating what form the next phase of this highly successful program should take. While there is bipartisan agreement that SBIR is a critical tool for national innovation and security, some reauthorization proposals would limit or disincentivize “multiple award winners,” unintentionally undermining the program’s flexibility and effectiveness.  On its face, limiting the number of awards for a particular small business may allow more firms to participate in the SBIR program, thereby broadening the nation’s innovation base. But SBIR is already an open program with a remarkable one-third of awardees new to the program each year. Moreover, the share of funding going to multiple award winners is modest, and the number of firms winning high counts of awards is small relative to the program’s scale and longevity. Top-down restrictions and unnecessary administrative burdens, such as those proposed in the INNOVATE Act, would reduce the flexibility and agility of the SBIR program and risk making them less effective as tools to support new technologies and deliver rapid solutions to the mission needs of federal agencies. More fundamentally, rather than seeking to manage the program through legislative restrictions, Congress should encourage agency program managers to weed out firms that do not support SBIR’s innovation mission and justify cases where companies receive multiple awards. The program was designed to rely on program managers—and most have proven successful. If they are not, the managers should be changed, not the program.How Multiple Awards Support Innovation It is worth asking what we expect out of SBIR award winners. One or two awards do not often generate a thriving company on their own. They may, however, contribute to the creation of intellectual property, measured in patent applications, patents awarded, and patent licensing. The award may attract outside investment from private partners or lead to spinouts of the technology in a new firm focused on developing the results of the award. And there are cases where the firm meets the agency mission need with a low-cost invention or meets a specific analytical need for the awarding agency.There are multiple reasons why multiple SBIR awards are essential to accelerating innovation: Building Momentum: Technologies do not surge into the market from the head of Zeus: they require iteration, testing, and adaptation. SBIR’s structure must allow for reinforcement of initial support as small innovative firms develop, especially for nascent high-tech products that are building momentum toward acquisition or commercialization.Supporting Small Pools of Expertise in Strategic Fields: In strategically important emerging technology areas, such as quantum computing or advanced materials, the pool of innovative companies is typically small and specialized. As a result, multiple awards often concentrate by necessity among the few technically capable firms. Rather than evidence of failure, this reflects the reality of emerging sectors: these firms are often the only ones with the depth of talent and familiarity with agency missions to meet the research and development (R&D) needs of groundbreaking technologies. SBIR helps grow these pools.Preventing Premature Abandonment: Arbitrarily limiting repeat awards could strand promising technologies in a quasi-developed status, leaving them vulnerable to foreign absorption or emulation. If U.S. firms cannot complete development through domestic support, they may be outpaced or overtaken by foreign competitors, including strategic adversaries. In an era of global technology competition, particularly with China, the cost of under-developing strategic technologies is not theoretical—it is a national security vulnerability.Extending the Bridge across the Valley of Death: Promising projects may require additional SBIR awards simply to reach the threshold for commercial uptake; they have done so in the past, with very powerful results. While SBIR Phases I (Feasibility Study) and II (Prototype Development) are well-established and funded within SBIR, Phase III (Commercialization) often lacks a uniform pathway. Some agencies, like the Department of Defense have robust Phase III programs. Others, like the National Institutes of Health, have follow-on awards. But success in Phase I or II does not guarantee the technology’s commercialization. For this reason, the program sometimes needs “an additional arch” in its bridge over the Valley of Death. This can be achieved through more formal initiatives but is probably best handled by encouraging optional follow-on funding at the discretion of program managers. Again, this is a management choice, not a structural issue for Congress.Support Excellence, Not Arbitrary Headcounts To fully exploit SBIR’s potential in today’s hyper-competitive environment, Congress should maintain an open and competitive program and incentivize active agency management, not impose arbitrary caps. The best way to prevent abuse or inefficiency in a program like SBIR is not through rigid legislation, but effective program management. When individual firms receive repeat awards, program managers should be able to justify why. Managers should also be empowered to exclude underperformers or justify continued support where warranted. Legislative caps simply introduce blunt restrictions into a program designed for technical judgment and adaption to changing—and sometimes pressing—national needs. The key questions are: What are the mission needs? How well does the proposal meet those needs? And how well did the proposing firm do previously? If answers are positive and the quality is high, the award should go forward. If a new firm presents a promising approach, it should win as well. There is no panacea, just multiple low-cost shots on goal. Some will score. At the end of the day, SBIR’s purpose is to identify and support excellence in innovation. The goal is not to just hand out starter grants but to help develop technologies that advance U.S. economic and national security interests. Instead of limiting participation, Congress should expand the program’s capacity to support success—wherever it arises—and take meaningful steps to ensure projects can reach the finish line. But above all, the fundamental condition for SBIR success is Congressional support for the R&D budgets that fund it. That is the lifeblood of the program—and the long-term source of American innovation and security.The authors would like to thank Julie Heng, research associate with Renewing American Innovation, for her contributions to this piece.\nThe Perspectives on Innovation Blog is produced by the Renewing American Innovation Project at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), a private, tax-exempt institution focusing on international public policy issues. Its research is nonpartisan and nonproprietary. CSIS does not take specific policy positions. Accordingly, all views, positions, and conclusions expressed in this publication should be understood to be solely those of the author(s).\n© 2025 by the Center for Strategic and International Studies. All rights reserved.\nTags\nAmerican Innovation,\nStrategic Capital, and\nTechnology and Innovation\nImage\nCharles Wessner\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Renewing American Innovation\nImage\nSujai Shivakumar\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Renewing American Innovation\nPrograms & Projects\nRenewing American Innovation\nEconomic Security and Technology\nR&D Infrastructure & Innovation","content_cn":"SBIR与多次获奖的作用\n图片：Adam Glanzman/Bloomberg via Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nCharles Wessner\n和\nSujai Shivakumar\n发表于2025年7月29日\n随着小企业创新研究（SBIR）计划重新授权的截止日期临近（2025年9月），国会正在辩论这一极其成功的计划下一阶段应采取何种形式。尽管两党都同意SBIR是推动国家创新与安全的关键工具，但一些重新授权提案将限制或抑制“多次获奖者”，无意中削弱该计划的灵活性和有效性。表面上看，限制某家小企业获得的奖项数量，可能会让更多企业参与SBIR计划，从而扩大国家的创新基础。但SBIR本来就是一个开放的计划，每年有三分之一的获奖者是首次参与该计划。此外，流向多次获奖者的资金占比不高，而获得大量奖项的企业数量，相对于该计划的规模和持续时间而言也很少。像《创新法案》（INNOVATE Act）所提议的自上而下的限制和不必要的行政负担，将降低SBIR计划的灵活性和敏捷性，并可能使其作为支持新技术、快速解决联邦机构任务需求的工具变得不那么有效。更根本地说，与其试图通过立法限制来管理该计划，国会不如鼓励机构项目经理淘汰那些不支持SBIR创新使命的企业，并要求对企业获得多次奖项的情况作出说明。该计划的设计就是依靠项目经理——而大多数项目经理已证明自己是成功的。如果他们不成功，应更换的是经理，而不是计划。\n多次获奖如何支持创新\n值得思考的是，我们希望SBIR获奖者取得什么成果。一两个奖项通常无法单独催生一家蓬勃发展的企业。不过，它们可能有助于知识产权的创造，这可以通过专利申请、获授专利以及专利许可来衡量。奖项可能吸引私人合作伙伴的外部投资，或促成技术从原企业中分拆出来，成立一家专注于开发获奖成果的新公司。在另一些情况下，企业可能通过低成本发明满足机构的任务需求，或满足授奖机构的特定分析需求。\n多次SBIR奖项对于加速创新至关重要，原因有很多：\n积累势头：技术不会凭空突然进入市场：它们需要迭代、测试和适应。随着小型创新企业不断发展，SBIR的结构必须允许对最初的支持进行强化，尤其是对于那些正朝着采购或商业化方向积累势头的新兴高科技产品。\n支持战略领域中的小规模专业力量：在量子计算或先进材料等具有战略重要性的新兴技术领域，创新企业群体通常规模较小且高度专业化。因此，多次奖项往往出于必要而集中于少数具备技术能力的企业。这并不是失败的证据，而是新兴行业的现实：这些企业往往是唯一拥有足够人才深度、并熟悉机构使命，能够满足突破性技术研发（R&D）需求的企业。SBIR帮助\n扩大这些资金池。\n\n防止过早放弃：武断地限制重复授奖可能会使有前景的技术停留在准成熟状态，使其容易被外国吸收或仿效。如果美国企业无法通过国内支持完成开发，它们可能会被包括战略对手在内的外国竞争者赶超或取代。在全球技术竞争时代，尤其是与中国的竞争中，对战略技术开发不足的代价并非理论上的——这将成为国家安全漏洞。\n\n跨越“死亡之谷”延伸桥梁：有前景的项目可能需要额外的SBIR资助，才能达到商业采用的门槛；过去它们确实这样做过，并取得了非常显著的成果。SBIR第一阶段（可行性研究）和第二阶段（原型开发）已经得到确立，并在SBIR框架内获得资助；而第三阶段（商业化）往往缺乏统一的路径。一些机构，如国防部，拥有完善的第三阶段项目；另一些机构，如国立卫生研究院，则提供后续资助。但在第一阶段或第二阶段取得成功，并不能保证技术实现商业化。因此，该项目有时需要在跨越“死亡之谷”的桥梁上增加“一道拱”。这可以通过更加正式的倡议来实现，但最好的处理方式可能是鼓励项目经理酌情提供可选的后续资助。再次强调，这是管理层面的选择，而不是国会需要解决的结构性问题。\n\n支持卓越，而非武断的人数限制\n\n为了在当今竞争极其激烈的环境中充分发挥SBIR的潜力，国会应当维持一个开放且具有竞争性的项目，并激励机构积极进行管理，而不是施加武断的上限。防止SBIR这类项目出现滥用或低效的最佳方式，不是通过僵化的立法，而是通过有效的项目管理。当个别企业获得重复资助时，项目经理应当能够说明理由。管理者也应被授权排除表现不佳者，或在有充分理由时说明继续支持的依据。立法上限只会给一个旨在依靠技术判断并适应不断变化、且有时十分紧迫的国家需求的项目，增添生硬的限制。\n\n关键问题是：任务需求是什么？提案在多大程度上满足这些需求？提出该提案的企业过去表现如何？\n\n如果答案是肯定的，且质量很高，就应当继续授奖。如果一家新企业提出了有前景的方法，它也应当获胜。没有万能药，只有多次低成本的射门机会。有些会进球。\n\n归根结底，SBIR的宗旨是发现并支持创新领域的卓越成果。其目标不仅是发放启动资金，更是帮助开发能够推进美国经济和国家安全利益的技术。国会不应限制参与，而应扩大项目支持成功的能力——无论成功出现在哪里——并采取切实措施，确保项目能够走到终点。\n\n但最重要的是，SBIR成功的根本条件，是国会支持为其提供资金的研发预算。这是该项目的命脉，也是美国创新与安全的长期源泉。\n\n作者谨向Renewing American Innovation的研究助理Julie Heng致谢，感谢她对本文的贡献。\n\n《Perspectives on Innovation Blog》由Renewing American Innovation Project制作。\n战略与国际问题研究中心（CSIS），一个专注于国际公共政策问题的私人、免税机构。其研究具有无党派性且不涉及专有内容。CSIS不采取具体的政策立场。因此，本出版物中表达的所有观点、立场和结论均应理解为仅代表作者的意见。\n© 2025 战略与国际问题研究中心。版权所有。\n标签\n美国创新、\n战略资本，以及\n技术与创新\n图片\nCharles Wessner\n美国创新再生项目高级顾问（非驻外）\n图片\nSujai Shivakumar\n美国创新再生项目主任兼高级研究员\n项目与计划\n美国创新再生\n经济安全与技术\n研发基础设施与创新","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-07-29T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1468","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"将知识产权转化为收入：中国在全球知识产权格局中地位的变化","title_en":"Translating IP Into Revenue: China’s Changing Place in the Global IP Landscape | Trustee China Hand | CSIS","summary_cn":"CSIS博客文章分析中国在全球知识产权格局中的角色转变。中国历史上是知识产权净支付方，但近十年本土企业获得的许可收入持续增长，收支比从十年前的1:20改善至约1:4至1:5。文章以华为、宁德时代及生物医药领域的对外授权为例，说明中国正从知识产权使用者转向供应者，但整体仍为净支付方，创新进展并不均衡。","summary_en":"This CSIS blog post examines China's shifting role in the global intellectual property landscape. Long a net payer of IP fees, China has seen rising licensing receipts over the past decade, with its receipts-to-payments ratio improving from about 1:20 to roughly 1:4-1:5. Citing Huawei, CATL, and biotech out-licensing deals, the author argues China is moving from IP user toward supplier, though it remains a net payer and its innovation progress is uneven.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:33:01.626364+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/blogs/trustee-china-hand/translating-ip-revenue-chinas-changing-place-global-ip-landscape","content":"Translating IP Into Revenue: China’s Changing Place in the Global IP Landscape\nPhoto: TOBIAS SCHWARZ/AFP/Getty Images\nBlog Post\nby\nRyan Featherston\nPublished March 5, 2026\nTrustee Chair in Chinese Business and Economics  >  Trustee China HandChina’s innovation drive has made enormous strides in the past few decades, but progress has been uneven and is incomplete, a key theme of the program’s recent report, The Power of Innovation. This pattern is reflected in Chinese efforts to become creators of intellectual property (IP) and leverage those rights into greater technology leadership and revenue.China is infamous for its IP theft. It still may be a significant issue, though over the last decade American companies doing business in China have identified IP theft as of decreasing relevance to their own businesses. That change in illicit behavior may also be reflected in the world of IP royalty and licensing payments.From an international perspective, China has historically been a net payer when it comes to IP. Becoming the world’s factory was accompanied by expanding licensing fees to create products both for use in China and for export to the rest of the world. But in the last decade, Chinese companies have started to receive more payments for their own IP, a sign of growing creativity and technological acumen (see Figure 1). Although there’s been a drop from the peak of 2022 (which could be for a variety of reasons), the overall trend of China receiving more IP income is clear.\nRemote Visualization\nAnother way to look at China’s changing position in international IP is to compare how much the country earns from others relative to what it pays, its IP receipts-to-payments ratio. As Figure 2 shows, while China still pays around $4-5 for every one dollar it receives, this ratio has shifted in the country’s favor compared to a decade ago, when China earned only $1 for every $20 it paid out.\nRemote Visualization\nIn comparative terms China appears at first glance to still have a profile similar to other emerging economies, such as India and Brazil (Figure 3), all with ratios well under 1.0. However, this comparison understates China’s scale: in absolute terms the country received more in IP payments than South Korea in 2024. Moreover, China’s low ratio also reflects the sheer volume of  manufacturing occurring on by both domestic and foreign-owned factories; they all  import a lot of components for which they have to pay royalties, boosting China’s overall payment figures. That is why in 2024 China was the third largest payer, behind only Ireland and the United States putting it far ahead of countries with similarly low ratios.\nRemote Visualization\nAlthough China does not publish IP receipts by industry, its global patent filings provide some clues of which industries are driving this new revenue. China’s largest PCT patent categories were for digital communication, computer technology, and machinery with large increases in areas like pharmaceuticals, and medical technology (see Figure 4).\nRemote Visualization\nTelecom giant Huawei serves as an instructive microcosm of the “digital communication” industry. The company received $630 million in patent licensing revenue in 2024, mostly from 4G and 5G devices followed by automotive and consumer electronics – roughly half of what Ericsson earned in patent licensing in 2024 ($1.27 billion), a third of Nokia's ($2 billion), and about 10 percent of Qualcomm's ($5.57 billion). Huawei also reported that its IP payments are still larger than what it receives, reflecting both sides of China’s current IP story – increasing receipts but still a net payer. Another example is battery maker CATL, which has been expanding licensing of is IP through its “license royalty service” (LRS) model. This is the model behind CATL’s cooperation with global firms such as Ford, Tesla, and GM – partially in response to U.S. restrictions and scrutiny as well as the attractiveness of CATL’s leading IP in lithium-iron phosphate batteries. The Ford plant in Michigan is emblematic of this kind of strategy – Ford owns and operates the plant while paying licensing fees to CATL for LFP technology.Pharmaceuticals is another striking area where rapid shifts are occurring. In the first three months of 2025, 32% of out licensing biotech deal value occurred in China, verses 21% reported in 2024 and 2023. Global firms like Pfizer, AstraZeneca, and Bristol Myers Squibb have all made licensing deals with Chinese firms. This shift is likely not yet visible in the IMF data and is another example of how China’s innovation is reversing the direction of flow for IP.None of these examples are exhaustive accounting, but they each give a unique perspective into how China’s shift towards supplying more of the world’s IP is playing out.Related Trustee Chair ActivitiesScott Kennedy, The Power of Innovation: The Strategic Value of China’s High-Tech Drive, CSIS Report, March 2, 2026.Event: “China’s Great Tech Leap Forward and the Implications for the United States,” March 2, 2026.Scott Kennedy, Wang Qiuyang, and Mingda Qiu, “Show Me the Receipts: China’s Rising IP Payments to the United States,” Trustee China Hand Blog Post, August 19, 2019.\nImage\nTags\nChina,\nAsia,\nAsian Economics, and\nTechnology and Innovation\nImage\nRyan Featherston\nAssociate Fellow, Trustee Chair in Chinese Business and Economics\nPrograms & Projects\nChinese Business and Economics\nEconomic Security and Technology","content_cn":"将知识产权转化为收入：中国在全球知识产权格局中不断变化的地位\n照片：TOBIAS SCHWARZ/AFP/Getty Images\n博客文章\n作者\nRyan Featherston\n发布于2026年3月5日\n中国商业与经济学受托人讲席 > 受托人中国观察\n中国的创新驱动在过去几十年取得了巨大进展，但发展并不均衡，且尚未完成——这是该项目近期报告《创新的力量》的一个关键主题。这一模式也反映在中国努力成为知识产权（IP）创造者，并利用这些权利获得更强技术领导地位和更多收入的过程中。\n\n中国因知识产权盗窃而臭名昭著。它可能仍是一个重大问题，尽管在过去十年中，在中国开展业务的美国公司认为知识产权盗窃与其自身业务的相关性正在下降。此类非法行为的变化也可能反映在知识产权特许权使用费和许可付款领域。\n\n从国际视角看，谈到知识产权，中国历来是净付款方。成为世界工厂伴随着不断扩大的许可费用，用于生产既供中国使用、也出口至世界其他地区的产品。但在过去十年，中国企业开始因其自身知识产权获得更多付款，这表明其创造力和技术能力不断增强（见图1）。尽管较2022年的峰值有所下降（原因可能多种多样），但中国获得更多知识产权收入的总体趋势是明确的。\n\n远程可视化\n\n观察中国在国际知识产权领域地位变化的另一种方式，是比较该国从其他国家获得的收入与其支付金额的相对关系，即知识产权收入与付款比率。如图2所示，尽管中国每获得1美元仍要支付约4至5美元，但与十年前相比，这一比率已向有利于中国的方向转变；当时中国每支付20美元，仅获得1美元。\n\n远程可视化\n\n从比较角度看，中国乍看之下仍与印度和巴西等其他新兴经济体具有相似特征（图3），其比率都远低于1.0。然而，这一比较低估了中国的规模：按绝对值计算，2024年中国获得的知识产权付款多于韩国。此外，中国的低比率也反映出国内和外资工厂进行制造活动的庞大规模；它们都进口大量需支付特许权使用费的零部件，从而推高了中国的总体付款数字。这就是为什么2024年中国是第三大付款国，仅次于爱尔兰和美国，远远领先于比率同样偏低的国家。\n\n远程可视化\n\n尽管中国不公布按行业划分的知识产权收入数据，但其全球专利申请为哪些行业正在推动这类新收入提供了一些线索。中国最大的PCT专利类别是数字通信、计算机技术和机械，在制药和医疗技术等领域也有大幅增长（见图4）。\n\n远程可视化\n\n电信巨头华为是“数字通信”行业一个颇具启发性的缩影。该公司2024年获得了6.3亿美元的专利许可收入，主要来自4G和5G设备，其次是汽车和消费电子产品——约为爱立信2024年专利许可收入（12.7亿美元）的一半，诺基亚（20亿美元）的三分之一，以及高通（55.7亿美元）的约10%。华为还报告称\n其知识产权支付额仍大于其收到的金额，反映出中国当前知识产权故事的两面——收入不断增加，但仍是净支付方。另一个例子是电池制造商宁德时代（CATL），该公司一直通过其“许可费服务”（LRS）模式扩大知识产权许可。这一模式正是宁德时代与福特、特斯拉和通用汽车等全球企业开展合作的基础——部分原因是应对美国的限制与审查，同时也源于宁德时代在磷酸铁锂电池领域领先知识产权的吸引力。福特在密歇根州的工厂体现了这种战略——福特拥有并运营该工厂，同时向宁德时代支付磷酸铁锂技术许可费。\n\n制药业是另一个正在发生快速转变的显著领域。2025年前三个月，中国占生物科技对外许可交易价值的32%，而2024年和2023年的报告比例为21%。辉瑞、阿斯利康和百时美施贵宝等全球企业都已与中国企业达成许可交易。这一转变可能尚未反映在国际货币基金组织的数据中，也是中国创新正在扭转知识产权流动方向的又一例证。\n\n这些例子都不是穷尽式的统计，但每一个都从独特角度展现了中国转向为世界提供更多知识产权这一趋势是如何展开的。\n\n相关受托人主席活动\nScott Kennedy，《创新的力量：中国高科技发展的战略价值》，CSIS报告，2026年3月2日。\n活动：“中国的伟大科技跃进及其对美国的影响”，2026年3月2日。\nScott Kennedy、Wang Qiuyang和Mingda Qiu，《给我看看收据：中国对美国知识产权支付的增长》，受托人中国手册博客文章，2019年8月19日。\n\n图片\n\n标签\n中国，\n亚洲，\n亚洲经济，以及\n技术与创新\n\n图片\nRyan Featherston\n受托人主席中国商业与经济项目副研究员\n项目与计划\n中国商业与经济\n经济安全与技术","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-03-05T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-2827","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Bing News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"2025年CSIS-CSDS印太跨大西洋对话","title_en":"2025 CSIS-CSDS Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific | CSIS Events","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）与布鲁塞尔治理学院安全、外交与战略研究中心（CSDS）自2020年起共同举办印太跨大西洋对话，2025年5月27日举行第五届会议。活动聚焦特朗普重返白宫后美国盟友面临的安全与经济不确定性，探讨欧洲与印太盟友如何应对中俄伊朝等威权国家协作带来的挑战，议题涵盖外交、军事协调与防务技术。","summary_en":"CSIS and the Centre for Security, Diplomacy and Strategy (CSDS) at the Brussels School of Governance have co-organized the Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific since 2020. The fifth meeting, held on May 27, 2025, examined how U.S. NATO and Indo-Pacific allies navigate the evolving geopolitical landscape amid U.S.-China competition, covering diplomacy, military alignment, and defense technology.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:42:10.987465+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/events/2025-csis-csds-transatlantic-dialogue-indo-pacific","content":"Breadcrumb\nEvents\n2025 CSIS-CSDS Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific\nMay 27, 2025 • 2:00 – 3:45 pm EDT\nBrought to you by\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nWith President Trump’s return to the White House in 2025, U.S. allies and partners face a bevy of questions about future security and economic scenarios arising in Europe and the Indo-Pacific. How should U.S. allies deter further aggression in Europe and contend with contingencies in Asia? What will be the resolution to U.S. efforts to rebalance trade and to negotiate reciprocal tariffs? How should allies and partners in Europe and the Indo-Pacific meet the new challenges arising from the cohesion of autocratic actors including China, Iran, Russia and North Korea?CSIS and the Centre for Security, Diplomacy and Strategy (CSDS) at the Brussels School of Governance (BSoG) of the VUB have been co-organizing the Transatlantic Dialogue on Indo-Pacific since 2020. Please join us for the Dialogue's 5th meeting at CSIS to explore how America's NATO and Indo-Pacific allies navigate the evolving geopolitical landscape in 2025. Experts, practitioners, and scholars in the CSIS-CSDS Dialogue will discuss key issues in diplomacy, military alignment, and defense technology in an era of U.S.-China competition.This event is made possible through general support to CSIS.\nTags\nEurope,\nNATO,\nAsia,\nSoutheast Asia,\nKorea,\nJapan,\nAustralia, New Zealand, and Pacific, and\nGeopolitics and International Security\nWelcoming Remarks\n2:00 pm\nVictor Cha, President, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Korea Chair, CSIS\nLuis Simón, Director, Centre for Security, Diplomacy and Strategy (CSDS)\nKeynote Address\n2:10 pm\nThomas Wright, Senior Fellow, Foreign Policy, Strobe Talbott Center for Security, Strategy, and Technology, Brookings Institute; Former Special Assistant to the President and Senior Director for Strategic Planning, National Security Council\nPanel Discussion\n2:30 pm\nMax Bergmann, Director, Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program and Stuart Center, CSIS\nBenedetta Berti, Director of Policy Planning, North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)\nGregory Poling, Director and Senior Fellow, Southeast Asia Program and Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative, CSIS\nMichito Tsuruoka, Professor, Faculty of Policy Studies, Keio University; Deputy Director, Keio Center for Strategy (KCS)\nModerator: Eva Pejsova, Japan Chair, Centre for Security, Diplomacy and Strategy (CSDS)\nAdjourn\n3:45 pm\nHosted By\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nContact Information\nMinchi Hyun\nSpecial Assistant to the President, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Program Manager, Korea Chair\n[email protected]\nImage\nVictor Cha\nPresident, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Korea Chair\nImage\nLuis Simón\nDirector, Center for Security, Diplomacy, and Strategy (CSDS)\nImage\nThomas Wright\nSenior Fellow, Foreign Policy, Strobe Talbott Center for Security, Strategy, and Technology, Brookings Institute; Former Special Assistant to the President and Senior Director for Strategic Planning, National Security Council\nImage\nEva Pejsova\nJapan Chair, Center for Security, Diplomacy, and Strategy (CSDS)\nImage\nMax Bergmann\nDirector, Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program and Stuart Center\nImage\nBenedetta Berti\nDirector, Policy Planning, Office of the Secretary General, NATO\nImage\nMichito Tsuruoka\nProfessor, Faculty of Policy Studies, Keio University; Deputy Director, Keio Center for Strategy (KCS)\nImage\nGregory B. Poling\nDirector and Senior Fellow, Southeast Asia Program and Asia Maritime Transparency Initiative\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"面包屑导航\n活动\n2025年CSIS-CSDS跨大西洋印太对话\n2025年5月27日 • 美国东部时间下午2:00–3:45\n由以下部门主办\n地缘政治与外交政策\n随着特朗普总统于2025年重返白宫，美国盟友和伙伴面临一系列问题：欧洲和印太地区未来可能出现哪些安全与经济情景？美国盟友应如何遏制欧洲的进一步侵略，并应对亚洲的突发事态？美国为重新平衡贸易和谈判对等关税所作努力将如何收场？欧洲和印太地区的盟友与伙伴应如何应对由包括中国、伊朗、俄罗斯和朝鲜在内的专制行为体凝聚而产生的新挑战？自2020年以来，战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）与布鲁塞尔自由大学（VUB）布鲁塞尔治理学院（BSoG）的安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）一直共同组织跨大西洋印太对话。欢迎参加在CSIS举行的第五次对话会议，探讨美国在北约和印太地区的盟友如何应对2025年不断变化的地缘政治格局。CSIS-CSDS对话的专家、实务人士和学者将讨论美国与中国竞争时代外交、军事协同和防务技术方面的关键问题。本活动得到了对CSIS的综合支持。\n标签\n欧洲、\n北约、\n亚洲、\n东南亚、\n韩国、\n日本、\n澳大利亚、新西兰及太平洋地区，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全\n欢迎致辞\n下午2:00\nVictor Cha，地缘政治与外交政策部主任兼韩国主席，CSIS\nLuis Simón，安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）主任\n主题演讲\n下午2:10\nThomas Wright，布鲁金斯学会斯特罗布·塔尔博特安全、战略与技术中心外交政策高级研究员；前总统特别助理兼国家安全委员会战略规划高级主任\n小组讨论\n下午2:30\nMax Bergmann，欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚项目及斯图尔特中心主任，CSIS\nBenedetta Berti，北大西洋公约组织（北约）政策规划主任\nGregory Poling，东南亚项目及亚洲海事透明度倡议主任兼高级研究员，CSIS\nMichito Tsuruoka，庆应义塾大学政策研究学院教授；庆应战略中心（KCS）副主任\n主持人：Eva Pejsova，日本主席，安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）\n散会\n下午3:45\n主办方\n地缘政治与外交政策\n联系方式\nMinchi Hyun\n总统特别助理，地缘政治与外交政策部；韩国主席项目经理\n[email protected]\n图片\nVictor Cha\n地缘政治与外交政策部主任兼韩国主席\n图片\nLuis Simón\n安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）主任\n图片\nThomas Wright\n外交政策高级研究员，布鲁金斯学会斯特罗布·塔尔博特安全、战略与技术中心；前总统特别助理兼国家安全委员会战略规划高级主任\n图片\nEva Pejsova\n日本主席，安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）\n图片\nMax Bergmann\n欧洲、俄罗斯和欧亚项目及斯图尔特中心主任\n图片\nBenedetta Berti\n政策规划主任，秘书长办公室，北约\n图片\nMichito Tsuruoka\n庆应义塾大学政策研究学院教授；庆应战略中心（KCS）副主任\n图片\nGregory B. Poling\n东南亚项目及亚洲海事透明度倡议主任兼高级研究员\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-05-27T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-903","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Bing News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"2026年CSIS-CSDS印太跨大西洋对话","title_en":"2026 CSIS-CSDS Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific | CSIS Events","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）与布鲁塞尔治理学院安全、外交与战略研究中心（CSDS）自2020年起共同举办印太跨大西洋对话，2026年5月26日举行第六届会议。活动探讨欧洲与中东冲突并发背景下美国盟友如何应对印太威慑挑战，以及跨大西洋与跨太平洋伙伴在安全协调和贸易摩擦方面的前景。","summary_en":"CSIS and the Centre for Security, Diplomacy and Strategy (CSDS) at the Brussels School of Governance have co-organized the Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific since 2020. The sixth iteration, scheduled for May 26, 2026, explores how U.S. European and Indo-Pacific allies navigate a deteriorating security environment with simultaneous conflicts in Europe and the Middle East, and the prospects for coordination and trade ties.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:52:21.961217+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/events/2026-csis-csds-transatlantic-dialogue-indo-pacific","content":"Breadcrumb\nEvents\n2026 CSIS-CSDS Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific\nMay 26, 2026 • 2:00 – 3:00 pm EDT\nBrought to you by\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nThe global security environment continues to deteriorate with simultaneous conflicts in Europe and the Middle East complicating U.S. efforts to deter aggression in the Indo-Pacific. How are U.S. allies and partners navigating this increasingly unpredictable environment? What are the prospects for enhancing coordination with each other and the United States to manage security challenges in multiple theaters? To what extent will trade friction affect transatlantic and transpacific ties? CSIS and the Centre for Security, Diplomacy and Strategy (CSDS) at the Brussels School of Governance (BSoG) of the Vrije Universiteit Brussel (VUB) have been co-organizing the Transatlantic Dialogue on the Indo-Pacific since 2020. Please join us at CSIS for the 6th iteration of the Dialogue where experts will explore how America's European and Indo-Pacific allies will navigate the evolving geopolitical landscape in 2026.This event is made possible by general support to CSIS.\nTags\nAsia,\nEurope,\nMiddle East,\nJapan,\nKorea,\nRussia and Eurasia,\nGeopolitics and International Security, and\nDefense and Security\nHosted By\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nContact Information\nMinchi Hyun\nSpecial Assistant to the President, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Program Manager, Korea Chair\n[email protected]\nImage\nVictor Cha\nPresident, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Korea Chair\nImage\nLuis Simón\nDirector, Centre for Security, Diplomacy, and Strategy (CSDS)\nImage\nMax Bergmann\nDirector, Europe, Russia, and Eurasia Program and Stuart Center\nImage\nKristi Govella\nSenior Adviser and Japan Chair\nImage\nEva Pejsova\nJapan Chair, Centre for Security, Diplomacy, and Strategy (CSDS)\nImage\nMichito Tsuruoka\nProfessor, Faculty of Policy Studies, Keio University; Deputy Director, Keio Center for Strategy (KCS)\nImage\nNicholas Szechenyi\nVice President, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Senior Fellow, Japan Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy\nJapan Chair\nKorea Chair\nEurope, Russia, and Eurasia Program\nG7 Summit","content_cn":"面包屑导航\n活动\n2026年 CSIS-CSDS 印太跨大西洋对话\n2026年5月26日 • 美国东部夏令时间下午2:00至3:00\n由以下机构为您呈现\n地缘政治与外交政策\n随着欧洲和中东冲突同时发生，全球安全环境持续恶化，令美国在印太地区威慑侵略的努力更加复杂。美国盟友和伙伴正如何应对这一日益不可预测的环境？它们彼此之间以及与美国加强协调，以应对多个战区安全挑战的前景如何？贸易摩擦将在何种程度上影响跨大西洋和跨太平洋关系？自 2020 年以来，CSIS 与布鲁塞尔自由大学（VUB）布鲁塞尔治理学院（BSoG）的安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）一直共同组织印太跨大西洋对话。请加入我们，在 CSIS 举行第 6 届对话，届时专家将探讨美国的欧洲和印太盟友将如何在 2026 年应对不断演变的地缘政治格局。本活动得益于对 CSIS 的一般性支持。\n标签\n亚洲，\n欧洲，\n中东，\n日本，\n韩国，\n俄罗斯与欧亚，\n地缘政治与国际安全，以及\n国防与安全\n主办方\n地缘政治与外交政策\n联系信息\nMinchi Hyun\n总裁特别助理，地缘政治与外交政策部门及韩国讲席项目经理\n[email protected]\n图片\nVictor Cha\n总裁，地缘政治与外交政策部门及韩国讲席\n图片\nLuis Simón\n安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）主任\n图片\nMax Bergmann\n欧洲、俄罗斯与欧亚项目及 Stuart 中心主任\n图片\nKristi Govella\n高级顾问兼日本讲席\n图片\nEva Pejsova\n安全、外交与战略中心（CSDS）日本讲席\n图片\nMichito Tsuruoka\n庆应义塾大学政策研究学院教授；庆应战略中心（KCS）副主任\n图片\nNicholas Szechenyi\n地缘政治与外交政策部门副总裁兼日本讲席高级研究员\n项目与计划\n地缘政治与外交政策\n日本讲席\n韩国讲席\n欧洲、俄罗斯与欧亚项目\n七国集团峰会","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-05-26T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-589","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"评估李在明政府执政一周年 | The Capital Cable 第138期","title_en":"Assessing One Year of Lee Jae Myung Administration | The Capital Cable #138 | CSIS Events","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）韩国问题项目举办的The Capital Cable第138期活动，于2026年7月16日评估李在明政府执政一周年。李在明2025年6月无过渡期就任，面临对美贸易争端与国内分裂局面。主持人马克·利珀特与CSIS的维克多·车、CNAS的金度妍及伦敦国王学院的拉蒙·帕切科-帕尔多共同回顾其务实外交成效与后续挑战。","summary_en":"The CSIS Korea Chair's Capital Cable #138, held on July 16, 2026, assesses one year of the Lee Jae Myung administration. President Lee took office in June 2025 without transition, inheriting a trade fight with the United States and a fractured domestic landscape. Host Mark Lippert is joined by CSIS's Victor Cha, Duyeon Kim of CNAS, and Ramón Pacheco-Pardo of King's College London to take stock of the administration's pragmatic diplomacy and what comes next.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:08:18.854764+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/events/assessing-one-year-lee-jae-myung-administration-capital-cable-138","content":"Breadcrumb\nEvents\nAssessing One Year of Lee Jae Myung Administration | The Capital Cable #138\nJuly 16, 2026 • 9:15 – 10:00 am EDT\nBrought to you by\nKorea Chair\n, The Capital Cable\nPlease join the CSIS Capital Cable for a look at one year of the Lee Jae Myung administration. President Lee took office in June 2025 with no transition, inheriting a trade fight with the United States, and a fractured domestic landscape recovering from the December 2024 martial law. One year on, Lee's pragmatic diplomacy has stabilized ties with the second Trump administration, steadied relations with Japan and China, and managed international crises, including the Iran conflict, while facing mounting economic issues domestically and a continued deadlock on the Korean Peninsula. Host Mark Lippert is joined by CSIS's Victor Cha, Duyeon Kim, adjunct senior fellow at CNAS and Ramón Pacheco-Pardo, Professor of International Relations at King’s College London and the KF-VUB Korea Chair to take stock of where the Lee administration stands and what comes next.Duyeon Kim is an adjunct senior fellow with the Indo-Pacific Security Program at CNAS based in Seoul. Her expertise includes the two Koreas, nuclear nonproliferation, arms control, East Asian relations and geopolitics, U.S. nuclear policy, and security. She is a columnist for the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists and a Visiting Professor at the Yonsei University Graduate School of International Studies. Ramón Pacheco Pardo is Professor of International Relations at King’s College London and the KF-VUB Korea Chair at the Brussels School of Governance of Vrije Universiteit Brussel. He is also King's Regional Envoy for East and South East Asia, helping to shape and implement the university's strategy for the region. He is also Adjunct Fellow (Non-Resident) with the Korea Chair at CSIS, Scientific Council member at Elcano Royal Institute, Steering Committee member at CSCAP EU, Advisory Committee member at Jeju Forum and Advisory Committee member at the Reset Korea Campaign of JoongAng Ilbo. This event is made possible by general support to CSIS.\nTags\nKorea,\nAsia,\nDefense and Security, and\nTrade and International Business\nHosted By\nKorea Chair\nThe Capital Cable\nAndy Lim\nDeputy Director and Fellow, Korea Chair\n202.644.5651\n[email protected]\nImage\nDuyeon Kim\nAdjunct Senior Fellow, Indo-Pacific Security Program, CNAS\nImage\nRamón Pacheco Pardo\nProfessor of International Relations, King's College London; KF-VUB Korea Chair, Brussels School of Governance, Vrije Universiteit Brussel\nImage\nVictor Cha\nPresident, Geopolitics and Foreign Policy Department and Korea Chair\nImage\nMark Lippert\nSenior Adviser (Non-resident), Korea Chair\nPrograms & Projects\nKorea Chair\nThe Capital Cable\nGeopolitics and Foreign Policy","content_cn":"面包屑导航\n活动\n评估李在明政府执政一周年 | 《资本电缆》第138期\n2026年7月16日 • 美国东部时间上午9:15–10:00\n由韩国主席和《资本电缆》主办\n请参加CSIS《资本电缆》节目，了解李在明政府执政一年来的情况。李总统于2025年6月在没有过渡期的情况下就任，接手了与美国的贸易争端，以及一个正从2024年12月戒严中恢复的分裂国内局势。一年后，李在明的务实外交稳定了与第二届特朗普政府的关系，稳住了与日本和中国的关系，并处理了包括伊朗冲突在内的国际危机，同时国内经济问题日益严重，朝鲜半岛局势也持续僵持。主持人马克·利珀特将与CSIS的维克托·查、CNAS兼职高级研究员金杜妍，以及伦敦国王学院国际关系教授、KF-VUB韩国主席拉蒙·帕切科·帕尔多一道，评估李政府目前所处的位置以及未来走向。\n金杜妍是设在首尔的CNAS印太安全项目兼职高级研究员。她的专业领域包括朝韩两国、核不扩散、军备控制、东亚关系与地缘政治、美国核政策和安全。她是《原子科学家公报》专栏作家，也是延世大学国际研究生院客座教授。\n拉蒙·帕切科·帕尔多是伦敦国王学院国际关系教授，也是布鲁塞尔自由大学布鲁塞尔治理学院的KF-VUB韩国主席。他还是国王学院东亚和东南亚地区特使，协助制定和实施该校在该地区的战略。他同时担任CSIS韩国主席的兼职研究员（非驻会）、埃尔卡诺皇家研究所科学委员会成员、CSCAP欧盟指导委员会成员、济州论坛咨询委员会成员，以及《中央日报》“重置韩国”倡议咨询委员会成员。\n本活动得到了对CSIS的普通支持。\n标签\n韩国、\n亚洲、\n国防与安全，以及\n贸易与国际商业\n主办方\n韩国主席\n《资本电缆》\nAndy Lim\n副主任兼研究员，韩国主席\n202.644.5651\n[email protected]\n图片\nDuyeon Kim\nCNAS印太安全项目兼职高级研究员\n图片\nRamón Pacheco Pardo\n伦敦国王学院国际关系教授；布鲁塞尔自由大学布鲁塞尔治理学院KF-VUB韩国主席\n图片\nVictor Cha\n地缘政治与外交政策部主任兼韩国主席\n图片\nMark Lippert\n韩国主席高级顾问（非驻会）\n项目与计划\n韩国主席\n《资本电缆》\n地缘政治与外交政策","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2026-07-16T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2190","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"新书发布：《选择失败：美国失去阿富汗的二十年历程》","title_en":"Book Launch: Choosing Defeat: The Twenty-Year Saga of How America Lost Afghanistan | CSIS Events","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）战争、非正规威胁与恐怖主义项目于2025年10月31日举办线上讨论，邀请乔治城大学保罗·D·米勒博士介绍其新书《选择失败：美国失去阿富汗的二十年历程》。CSIS专家丹尼尔·拜曼与亚历山大·帕尔默参与对话，探讨阿富汗战争期间美国总统决策内幕、官僚挑战及影响美国政策的关键决策节点。","summary_en":"The CSIS Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program hosted a virtual discussion on October 31, 2025, with Dr. Paul D. Miller of Georgetown University on his new book, Choosing Defeat: The Twenty-Year Saga of How America Lost Afghanistan. CSIS experts Dr. Daniel Byman and Alexander Palmer joined to explore presidential decision-making during the war, key bureaucratic challenges, and pivotal decision points that shaped U.S. policy in America's longest war.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:08:13.086790+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/events/book-launch-choosing-defeat-twenty-year-saga-how-america-lost-afghanistan","content":"Breadcrumb\nEvents\nBook Launch: Choosing Defeat: The Twenty-Year Saga of How America Lost Afghanistan\nOctober 31, 2025 • 4:00 – 5:00 pm EDT\nBrought to you by\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nPlease join the CSIS Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism program on Friday, October 31 from 4:00 p.m. to 5:00 p.m. ET, for a virtual discussion with Dr. Paul D. Miller, Professor of the Practice of International Affairs at Georgetown University's School of Foreign Service and Senior Fellow with the Atlantic Council's Scowcroft Center for Strategy and Security. CSIS experts Dr. Daniel Byman and Alexander Palmer will join Dr. Miller for a conversation on his new book, Choosing Defeat: The Twenty-Year Saga of How America Lost Afghanistan. Choosing Defeat offers a remarkable look inside presidential decision-making during the war in Afghanistan and draws lessons for U.S. security policy and strategic leadership. This discussion will explore the complexities on the ground in Afghanistan, address some of the key bureaucratic challenges identified in Dr. Miller’s book, and analyze the pivotal decision points that shaped U.S. policy during America’s longest war. This event is made possible through general support to CSIS.\nTags\nAfghanistan,\nDefense and Security,\nTerrorism and Irregular Warfare,\nTransnational Threats, and\nGeopolitics and International Security\nHosted By\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nContact Information\nSofia Triana\nProgram Manager, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\n[email protected]\nImage\nDaniel Byman\nDirector, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nImage\nAlexander Palmer\nFellow, Warfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nImage\nPaul D. Miller\nProfessor of the Practice and Co-Chair for Global Politics and Security; and Senior Fellow, Scowcroft Center for Strategy and Security, Atlantic Council\nPrograms & Projects\nWarfare, Irregular Threats, and Terrorism Program\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"面包屑导航\n活动\n新书发布会：《选择失败：美国如何失去阿富汗的二十年历程》\n2025年10月31日 • 美国东部夏令时间下午4:00–5:00\n主办方\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目\n请于10月31日（星期五）美国东部时间下午4:00至5:00，参加CSIS战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目举办的线上讨论会。讨论嘉宾包括乔治城大学外交学院国际事务实践教授、大西洋理事会斯考克罗夫特战略与安全中心高级研究员Paul D. Miller博士。CSIS专家Daniel Byman博士和Alexander Palmer将与Miller博士共同探讨他的新书《选择失败：美国如何失去阿富汗的二十年历程》。\n《选择失败》深入呈现了阿富汗战争期间总统决策的内幕，并为美国安全政策和战略领导力总结经验教训。本次讨论将探究阿富汗当地局势的复杂性，讨论Miller博士书中指出的一些关键官僚体制挑战，并分析塑造美国在这场美国历史上最长战争中政策的关键决策节点。本活动得以举办，得益于对CSIS的一般性支持。\n标签\n阿富汗、\n国防与安全、\n恐怖主义与非常规战争、\n跨国威胁，以及\n地缘政治与国际安全\n主办方\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目\n联系信息\nSofia Triana\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目项目经理\n[email protected]\n图片\nDaniel Byman\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目主任\n图片\nAlexander Palmer\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目研究员\n图片\nPaul D. Miller\n国际政治与安全实践教授兼联合主席；大西洋理事会斯考克罗夫特战略与安全中心高级研究员\n项目与计划\n战争、非常规威胁与恐怖主义项目\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-10-31T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3303","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"直面末日：美中台海冲突中核威慑及其失败的兵棋推演 | CSIS活动","title_en":"Confronting Armageddon: Wargaming Nuclear Deterrence and Its Failures in a U.S.-China Conflict over Taiwan | CSIS Events","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）国防与安全部门与麻省理工学院安全研究项目兵棋推演实验室联合发布报告《直面末日：美中台海冲突中核威慑及其失败的兵棋推演》。研究将核武器纳入现有美中兵棋推演并运行15次，发现中国团队在入侵面临可能威胁中共统治的失败时，核武器使用压力最大。报告认为美国外交比核边缘政策更重要，需准备打赢高端常规战争同时提供体面退路，否则可能引发核灾难。","summary_en":"The CSIS Defense and Security Department, in partnership with the MIT Security Studies Program Wargaming Lab, launched the report 'Confronting Armageddon: Wargaming Nuclear Deterrence and Its Failures in a U.S.-China Conflict over Taiwan.' The study modified an existing U.S.-China wargame to include nuclear weapons and ran it 15 times, finding that pressure for nuclear use peaked when China faced a crisis threatening CCP rule. It concludes that U.S. diplomacy matters more than nuclear brinksmanship and that the U.S. must prepare for high-end conventional war while offering face-saving off-ramps.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:11:14.070403+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/events/confronting-armageddon-wargaming-nuclear-deterrence-and-its-failures-us-china-conflict-over","content":"Breadcrumb\nEvents\nConfronting Armageddon: Wargaming Nuclear Deterrence and Its Failures in a U.S.-China Conflict over Taiwan\nDecember 13, 2024 • 1:30 – 2:30 pm EST\nBrought to you by\nDefense and Security\nPlease join the CSIS Defense and Security Department for the launch of a joint report with the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) Security Studies Program Wargaming Lab titled Confronting Armageddon: Wargaming Nuclear Deterrence and Its Failures in a U.S.-China Conflict over Taiwan by Mark Cancian, Matthew Cancian, and Eric Heginbotham. This event will feature a presentation by the report’s authors and a panel discussion with Kari Bingen, Charles Glaser, and Tong Zhao.This study examines nuclear dynamics in a Chinese invasion of Taiwan, a war that the authors hope will never occur. What creates the greatest pressure for nuclear weapons use in such a conflict? What happens if nuclear weapons are used? To answer these questions, the CSIS-MIT team modified its existing U.S.-China wargame to include nuclear weapons and ran it 15 times.The greatest pressure for nuclear use came when China teams reached a crisis: their invasion was in danger of a defeat that might threaten Chinese Communist Party (CCP) rule. To dissuade China from gambling for resurrection—using nuclear weapons to salvage a failing conventional campaign—U.S. diplomacy was much more important than nuclear brinksmanship. Favorable outcomes were possible, but total victory was unachievable. The United States must therefore be prepared to successfully prosecute a high-end conventional war while at the same time providing face saving off-ramps to the adversary. To do otherwise risks a nuclear holocaust, as indeed occurred in three game iterations.The research for this project was funded by a grant from the Department of Defense. The MIT Wargaming Lab supported the completion of this report and is grateful for generous family foundation support.\nTags\nAsia,\nChina,\nNuclear Issues, and\nWargaming and Scenarios\nHosted By\nDefense and Security\nContact Information\nChris H. Park\nResearch Associate, Arleigh A. Burke Chair in Strategy\n[email protected]\nImage\nMark F. Cancian\nSenior Adviser, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nMatthew F. Cancian\nAssociate Professor, United States Naval War College\nImage\nEric Heginbotham\nPrincipal Research Scientist, MIT Center for International Studies\nImage\nKari A. Bingen\nDirector, Aerospace Security Project and Senior Fellow, Defense and Security Department\nImage\nCharles Glaser\nSenior Fellow, MIT Security Studies Program\nImage\nTong Zhao\nSenior Fellow, Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\nPrograms & Projects\nDefense and Security","content_cn":"面包屑导航\n活动\n直面末日：推演美国与中国因台湾发生冲突时的核威慑及其失效\n2024年12月13日 • 美国东部时间下午1:30–2:30\n由以下机构呈现\n国防与安全\n请参加CSIS国防与安全部举办的活动，发布其与麻省理工学院（MIT）安全研究项目兵棋推演实验室联合完成的报告《直面末日：推演美国与中国因台湾发生冲突时的核威慑及其失效》，作者为Mark Cancian、Matthew Cancian和Eric Heginbotham。本次活动将包括报告作者的介绍，以及Kari Bingen、Charles Glaser和Tong Zhao参与的小组讨论。本研究考察中国入侵台湾情境中的核动态，这是一场作者希望永远不会发生的战争。在此类冲突中，什么会对使用核武器造成最大压力？如果使用核武器会发生什么？为回答这些问题，CSIS-MIT团队修改了其现有的美中兵棋推演以纳入核武器，并进行了15次推演。\n当中国团队陷入危机时，使用核武器的压力最大：其入侵面临失败的危险，而这一失败可能威胁中国共产党（CCP）的统治。为劝阻中国孤注一掷、试图力挽狂澜——即使用核武器挽救一场失败的常规战役——美国外交远比核边缘政策重要。虽然可能取得有利结果，但完全胜利无法实现。因此，美国必须做好成功进行高端常规战争的准备，同时向对手提供保全面子的退出路径。否则就有发生核浩劫的风险，事实上这在三次推演中确实发生了。\n本项目研究由国防部拨款资助。MIT兵棋推演实验室支持完成本报告，并感谢家庭基金会的慷慨支持。\n标签\n亚洲，\n中国，\n核问题，以及\n兵棋推演与情景\n主办方\n国防与安全\n联系信息\nChris H. Park\n战略阿利·A·伯克讲席研究助理\n[email protected]\n图片\nMark F. Cancian\n国防与安全部高级顾问\n图片\nMatthew F. Cancian\n美国海军战争学院副教授\n图片\nEric Heginbotham\nMIT国际研究中心首席研究科学家\n图片\nKari A. Bingen\n航空航天安全项目主任兼国防与安全部高级研究员\n图片\nCharles Glaser\nMIT安全研究项目高级研究员\n图片\nTong Zhao\n卡内基国际和平基金会高级研究员\n项目与计划\n国防与安全","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2024-12-13T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2948","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Bing News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"CSIS 2025全球发展论坛：美国对发展中国家的技术供给——前景与陷阱","title_en":"CSIS 2025 GDF: The U.S. Technology Offer to Developing Countries: The Promise and Pitfalls | 2025 Global Development Forum | CSIS Events","summary_cn":"战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）繁荣与发展项目于2025年4月23日举办2025全球发展论坛小组讨论，主题为美国对发展中国家的技术供给。讨论围绕美国对外援助剧变背景下全球发展议程的不确定性，探讨AI等技术革命对全球南方发展的影响、美国私营部门商业参与的作用、美国技术供给与主要竞争对手（特别是中国）的差异，以及发展中国家如何吸收和部署技术以促进繁荣、经济安全与韧性。","summary_en":"The CSIS Project on Prosperity and Development hosted a 2025 Global Development Forum panel on April 23, 2025, titled 'The U.S. Technology Offer to Developing Countries: The Promise and Pitfalls.' Amid rapid changes to U.S. foreign assistance, the discussion examined the future of U.S. development policy, the implications of technology revolutions such as AI for the Global South, the role of the U.S. private sector and commercial engagement, how the U.S. technology offer differs from competitors including the PRC, and how developing countries can absorb and deploy technologies for prosperity and resilience.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:49:04.930107+00:00","url":"https://www.csis.org/events/csis-2025-gdf-us-technology-offer-developing-countries-promise-and-pitfalls","content":"Breadcrumb\nEvents\nCSIS 2025 GDF: The U.S. Technology Offer to Developing Countries: The Promise and Pitfalls\nApril 23, 2025 • 9:45 – 11:15 am EDT\nBrought to you by\nProject on Prosperity and Development\nImage\nAmidst unprecedented and rapid changes to U.S. foreign assistance, the global development agenda is in flux. These changes raise fundamental questions about the future of U.S. development policy and development finance, and U.S. partnerships with the Global South. While we are in a moment of risk and uncertainty, we are also in a moment of possibility. Multiple technology revolutions are underway – in particular the AI revolution – with far-reaching implications for economic and social development, and for poverty reduction in the Global South.The ability of countries to benefit from these technologies depends in part on a strong U.S. private sector, commercial engagement. What then is the U.S. technology offer to developing countries? What form should it take and how is it differentiating from those of our major competitors, in particular the PRC? What role should the U.S. government and business play in ensuring a robust, long-term approach to technology cooperation? And how can developing countries ensure that they are ready to absorb and deploy these technologies in pursuit of prosperity, economic security, and resilience?This 2025 GDF panel will examine pressing issues at the nexus of developing country priorities, and U.S. leadership in technology and innovation through commercial diplomacy and private sector engagement.\nHosted By\nProject on Prosperity and Development\nImage\nNavin Girishankar\nPresident, Economic Security and Technology Department\nMagdi M. Amin\nFounder Managing Partner, African Renaissance Ventures\nEsimaje Brikinn\nSenior Corporate Affairs Advisor, Middle East & Africa, Chevron International Exploration and Production\nArun Venkataraman\nFormer Assistant Secretary of Commerce for Global Markets, and former Director General, U.S. and Foreign Commercial Service, U.S. Department of Commerce\nHowie Wachtel\nSenior Director for Policy, UN and International Organizations, Microsoft\nPrograms & Projects\nProject on Prosperity and Development\nGlobal Development","content_cn":"面包屑导航\n活动\nCSIS 2025年全球发展论坛：美国向发展中国家提供的技术方案：愿景与陷阱\n2025年4月23日 • 美国东部时间上午9:45–11:15\n由以下机构提供\n繁荣与发展项目\n图片\n在美国对外援助发生前所未有且迅速变化之际，全球发展议程正处于变动之中。这些变化引发了有关美国发展政策和发展融资未来，以及美国与全球南方伙伴关系的根本性问题。虽然我们正处于风险与不确定性之中，但也正处于充满可能性的时刻。多场技术革命正在展开，尤其是人工智能革命，对经济和社会发展以及全球南方减贫具有深远影响。各国能否从这些技术中受益，部分取决于强大的美国私营部门和商业参与。那么，美国向发展中国家提供的技术方案是什么？它应采取何种形式？与我们的主要竞争对手，尤其是中华人民共和国的方案相比，它有何不同？美国政府和企业应在确保采取稳健、长期的技术合作方式方面发挥什么作用？发展中国家又如何确保自身准备好吸收和部署这些技术，以实现繁荣、经济安全和韧性？2025年全球发展论坛小组讨论将通过商业外交和私营部门参与，探讨发展中国家优先事项与美国在技术和创新领域领导力交汇处的紧迫问题。\n主办方\n繁荣与发展项目\n图片\nNavin Girishankar\n经济安全与技术部总裁\nMagdi M. Amin\n非洲复兴创投创始管理合伙人\nEsimaje Brikinn\n雪佛龙国际勘探与生产公司中东及非洲高级企业事务顾问\nArun Venkataraman\n美国商务部前全球市场助理部长、美国商务与对外商业服务前总局长\nHowie Wachtel\n微软联合国及国际组织政策高级总监\n项目与计划\n繁荣与发展项目\n全球发展","publish_source":"csis.org","publish_time":"2025-04-23T00:00:00+00:00"}}];
